     gene            8238..9191
                     /gene="talB"
                     /locus_tag="b0008"
                     /label=talB
     CDS             8238..9191
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="talB"
                     /locus_tag="b0008"
                     /product="bifunctional transaldolase B / transaldolase"
                     /EC_number="2.2.1.2"
                     /label=talB
                     /note="Transaldolase B is an enzyme of the non-oxidative
                     branch of the pentose phosphate pathway. Along with 
                     transketolase, transaldolase creates a reversible link 
                     between the pentose phosphate pathway and glycolysis. It 
                     catalyzes the interconversion of glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate
                     and sedoheptulose-7-phosphate to fructose-6-phosphate and 
                     erythrose-4-phosphate. The reversibility of this reaction 
                     and carbon flux through the pentose phosphate pathway has 
                     been addressed both experimentally (summarized in |CITS: 
                     [6049456]|) and theoretically |CITS: 
                     [6049456][9546650][16176270]|.
                     There are two closely related transaldolases in <i>E. 
                     coli</i>, encoded by <i>talA</i> and <i>talB</i>. Only 
                     transaldolase B has been biochemically characterized. TalB 
                     is a dimer in solution |CITS: [7592346]| and in the crystal
                     structure |CITS: [8805555]|. Mutation of the R300 residue 
                     leads to the formation of catalytically active monomers 
                     |CITS: [9883893]|. Catalytically important active site 
                     residues have been identified by site-directed mutagenesis 
                     |CITS: [11298760]|.
                     Crystal structures of transaldolase B have been determined,
                     confirming the presence of a Schiff-base intermediate at 
                     the active site and leading to a proposed reaction 
                     mechanism |CITS: [8805555][9007983][11298760]|.
                     A <i>talB</i> null mutant has no growth defect on minimal 
                     media with glucose as the carbon source |CITS: [11298760]|.
                     A <i>pfkAB</i> knockout strain (with the 
                     Embden-Meyerhof-Parnas pathway [EMPP] inactivated for 
                     glucose metabolism) subjected to adaptive laboratory 
                     evolution recovered growth to wild-type levels and showed 
                     an improved the Entner-Doudoroff pathway (EDP) for glucose 
                     breakdown due to mutations in <i>ugd-|FRAME: 
                     EG10411|-wbbL-2</i>, <i>|FRAME: EG10787|</i>, <i>|FRAME: 
                     EG11556|</i>, <i>|FRAME: EG12630|</i>, <i>|FRAME: 
                     EG10364|</i>, and <i>|FRAME: EG10164|</i> 
                     |CITS:[36352474]|.
                     TalB: ""<b>t</b>rans<b>al</b>dolase <b>B</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [7592346]|
                     Review: |CITS: [19401148]|"
