     gene            complement(9928..10494)
                     /gene="satP"
                     /locus_tag="b0010"
                     /label=satP
     CDS             complement(9928..10494)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="satP"
                     /locus_tag="b0010"
                     /product="trifunctional acetate/succinate:H(+) symporter / 
                     acetate:H(+) symporter / succinate:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=satP
                     /note="SatP is an acetate:proton symporter that is
                     implicated in acetate homeostasis when <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     is grown aerobically at high glucose concentrations; under 
                     these conditons, SatP is active in acetate transport at the
                     exponential growth stage whereas the acetate permease 
                     |FRAME: YJCG-MONOMER ""ActP""| is more active at the entry 
                     to stationary phase; SatP is implicated in acetate uptake 
                     when <i>E. coli</i> is grown with acetic acid as the sole 
                     carbon and energy source |CITS: [23844911]|.
                     In transport assays, succinate is a competitive inhibitor 
                     for acetate uptake by YaaH; the absence of YaaH is 
                     associated with an increase in the amount of succinate 
                     found (at the 10 hour timepoint) in the culture supernatant
                     of cells grown aerobically with glucose; this increase 
                     appears consistent with a decrease in succinate uptake 
                     |CITS: [23844911]|. SatP contributes to the residual 
                     succinate uptake seen in mutant strains lacking the major 
                     aerobic succinate transporters DctA and DauA |CITS: 
                     [23844911]|.
                     SatP is predicted to have 6 membrane spanning alpha-helices
                     with the C-terminus located in the cytoplasm 
                     |CITS:[16120447]|. SatP is predicted to form a hexameric 
                     ring structure with strong similarity to the proton-gated 
                     urea channel, Ure1, from <i>Helicobacter pylori</i> |CITS: 
                     [26335199]|.
                     SatP is a member of the Acetate Uptake Transporter (AceTr) 
                     family |CITS: [24225317]|
                     <i>satP</i>: <U>s</U>uccinate-<U>a</U>cetate 
                     <U>t</U>ransporter <U>p</U>rotein
                     Related review: |CITS: [27415771]|"
