                     literature search conducted on April 5, 2017.
                     "
                     /translation="MKSVFTISASLAISLMLCCTAQANDHKLLGAIAMPRNETNDLALK
                     LPVCRIVKRIQLSADHGDLQLSGASVYFKAARSASQSLNIPSEIKEGQTTDWININSDN
                     DNKRCVSKITFSGHTVNSSDMATLKIIGDD"
     gene            12163..14079
                     /gene="dnaK"
                     /locus_tag="b0014"
                     /label=dnaK
     CDS             12163..14079
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dnaK"
                     /locus_tag="b0014"
                     /product="bifunctional chaperone protein DnaK / chaperone
                     ATPase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.4.10"
                     /label=dnaK
                     /note="DnaK is a Hsp70 (<u>h</u>eat <u>s</u>hock
                     <u>p</u>rotein 70 kDa) chaperone. It assists in a number of
                     cytoplasmic cellular processes involved in a ""protein 
                     quality control"" network, including folding of nascent 
                     polypeptide chains |CITS: [10458167][10380927]|, rescue of 
                     misfolded proteins |CITS: [2203539][7902351][7900997]| and 
                     protein secretion |CITS: [1628824][8655561]|. The chaperone
                     action of DnaK is powered by ATP hydrolysis and assisted by
                     partner chaperones |FRAME: CPLX0-8191 ""DnaJ""|, |FRAME: 
                     EG12193-MONOMER CbpA|, |FRAME: EG11570-MONOMER DjlA| and 
                     the nucleotide exchange factor |FRAME: CPLX0-8192 
                     ""GrpE""|. The DnaK system contributes to control of the 
                     heat shock response in <i>E. coli</i> through its 
                     interaction with |FRAME: RPOH-MONOMER 
                     ""&sigma;<sup>32</sup>""|, the primary sigma factor 
                     controlling heat shock response during log phase growth 
                     (reviewed in |CITS: [9133661][10322172][10791710]|). The 
                     DnaK chaperone system is also essential for bacteriophage 
                     &lambda; DNA replication and is involved in the formation 
                     and disassembly of the initiation complex at the viral 
                     origin of replication <i>ori</i>-&lambda; |CITS: [2543679] 
                     [2525129][2527744][2525130][2154468][1839120][1361234][8444
                     859][8098531]|. DnaK consists of an N-terminal nucleotide 
                     binding domain (NBD; residues 1-370) |CITS: [7776367]|, a 
                     17 kDa substrate-binding domain (SBD; residues 390-600) 
                     |CITS: [8658133][9609686][10742174][14573869]| and a 10kDa 
                     subdomain of &alpha;-helical structure (residues 510-638) 
                     |CITS: [10048327]|. DnaK functions are mediated by 
                     interdomain allostery. The NBD regulates the substrate 
                     affinity of the SBD in a nucleotide dependent manner - 
                     alternating between an ATP bound state (the T-state) with 
                     low affinity/fast exchange of substrate and an ADP bound 
                     state (the R-state) with high affinity/low exchange rate of
                     substrate. In turn, substrate binding results in 
                     stimulation of the ATPase activity of the NBD |CITS: [8413 
                     631][8310296][7776367][8947566][9223639][9454592][9425082][
                     10876246]|. The molecular mechanism underpinning 
                     interdomain allostery in DnaK is the subject of 
                     considerable research |CITS: [76 
                     22507][11352903][12356325][16384998][16613854][17434124][20
                     078127][21482798][23217711][23708608][26038563][26240360][2
                     6383706][28533394]|. DnaK uses the entropic pulling 
                     mechanism |CITS: [31083504]| to generate the force required
                     for chaperone function |CITS: [39379347]|.
                     Purified DnaK possesses weak ATPase activity and an 
                     autophosphorylating activity |CITS: [6314326][6086613]|. 
                     The co-chaperones, GrpE and DnaJ act to control the flux of
                     substrate through the SBD of DnaK by regulating the 
                     nucleotide bound state (reviews: |CITS: 
                     [16952052][14984054]|. The presence of DnaJ and GrpE 
                     stimulates the ATPase activity of DnaK <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [1826368][7876226]|.
                     DnaK, DnaJ, GrpE and ATP are required to mediate the 
                     efficient folding of misfolded luciferase <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [7937953]|. DnaK, DnaJ and GrpE plus ATP are able to
                     convert misfolded luciferase into an unfolded intermediate 
                     which spontaneously refolds into its native configuration 
                     after release from the chaperone |CITS: 
                     [7900997][20953191]|. DnaK-DnaJ-GrpE acts cooperatively 
                     with |FRAME: EG10157-MONOMER ""ClpB""| to mediate 
                     disaggregation and refolding of thermolabile proteins 
                     |CITS: [10570141][10497158][10601016]|. DnaK-DnaJ-GrpE 
                     cooperates with |FRAME: EG11003-MONOMER ""trigger factor 
                     (TF)""| to chaperone <i>de novo</i> protein folding |CITS: 
                     [11592405][12603737][14726952]|.
                     Characterization of isolated ribosome nascent chain 
                     (RNC):chaperone complexes provides molecular insight into 
                     the coordination of TF and DnaK/DnaJ binding during 
                     chaperone-assisted cotranslational folding of a model 
                     multidomain protein |CITS: [38908370]| (and see |CITS: 
                     [38996455]|).
                     DnaK has a broad substrate specificity and interacts with 
                     ~700 newly synthesized and preexistent proteins; the 
                     combined loss of DnaK/DnaJ and TF results in proteostasis 
                     collapse with extensive protein aggregation; protein flux 
                     to |FRAME: CPLX0-3934 GroEL-ES| is also disrupted despite 
                     significant upregulation of the GroEL-ES system |CITS: 
                     [22832197]|. DnaK binds with the highest affinity to short 
                     hydrophobic peptide segments in extended conformation 
                     |CITS: [8658133]|. DnaK binds to a consensus motif 
                     consisting of a hydrophobic core of 4-5 residues (most 
                     commonly Leu) flanked by basic residues |CITS: 
                     [9145101][9130695]|. Binding sites have been identified in 
                     2258 proteins; in general, most proteins bear at least one 
                     binding site and the number of binding sites is directly 
                     proportional to protein size |CITS: [36539330]|.
                     The DnaK-DnaJ-GrpE chaperone system is involved in 
                     protection against copper-induced protein aggregation under
                     anaerobic growth conditions |CITS: [35289645]|. DnaK 
                     controls the homoeostasis of curli biogenesis at multiple 
                     stages to organize the biofilm matrix |CITS: [30271935]|. 
                     DnaK has a multifunctional role in bacterial amyloid 
                     biogenesis |CITS: [30271935]|.
                     The model substrate SH3 interacts reversibly with the 
                     ADP-bound form of DnaK; multiple, largely unstructured 
                     conformations of SH3 interact relatively weakly with DnaK, 
                     which suggests the possibility of chaperone 
                     surface-mediated protein folding |CITS: [26195753]|.
                     An intact DnaK(OmpA)<sub>2</sub> complex can be released 
                     from native outer membrane vesicles |CITS: [30442809]|. 
                     Modifications of the gene and protein have been studied. 
                     Using a method that distinguishes N-phosphorylation from 
                     O-phosphorylation, Lys304 residue was found to be 
                     phosphorylated <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [35636362]|. A 
                     top-down proteomics study detected many proteoforms of 
                     DnaK, such as N-terminal methionine excision, acetylation, 
                     and C-terminal truncation |CITS: [30972727]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11076019][12207689]|<BR>Related reviews: 
                     |CITS: [6240590][7669342][9476895][22576356][17182732][256 
                     83596][26539440][29735737][31201219][31569820][32446288]|"
                     /translation="MGKIIGIDLGTTNSCVAIMDGTTPRVLENAEGDRTTPSIIAYTQD
                     GETLVGQPAKRQAVTNPQNTLFAIKRLIGRRFQDEEVQRDVSIMPFKIIAADNGDAWVE
                     VKGQKMAPPQISAEVLKKMKKTAEDYLGEPVTEAVITVPAYFNDAQRQATKDAGRIAGL
                     EVKRIINEPTAAALAYGLDKGTGNRTIAVYDLGGGTFDISIIEIDEVDGEKTFEVLATN
                     GDTHLGGEDFDSRLINYLVEEFKKDQGIDLRNDPLAMQRLKEAAEKAKIELSSAQQTDV
                     NLPYITADATGPKHMNIKVTRAKLESLVEDLVNRSIEPLKVALQDAGLSVSDIDDVILV
                     GGQTRMPMVQKKVAEFFGKEPRKDVNPDEAVAIGAAVQGGVLTGDVKDVLLLDVTPLSL
