     gene            complement(16751..16960)
                     /gene="mokC"
                     /locus_tag="b0018"
                     /label=mokC
     CDS             complement(16751..16960)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mokC"
                     /locus_tag="b0018"
                     /product="regulatory protein MokC"
                     /label=mokC
                     /note="Sequence analysis indicates that the |FRAME:
                     G0-9563| gene is a homologue of the <I>hok</I> (host 
                     killing) gene which is responsible for mediating plasmid 
                     stabilization by post-segregational killing (PSK) in 
                     plasmid R1. <I>hok</I> encodes a stable mRNA whose 
                     translation is inhibited by a less stable mRNA encoded by 
                     <I>sok</I>. The stable mRNA from <I>hokC</I> encodes a 
                     polypeptide that induction experiments have shown to be 
                     toxic to <I>Escherichia coli</I> host cells, resulting in 
                     cell growth arrest, morphological changes, and rapid cell 
                     killing. The <I>hokC</I> system is inactive due to 
                     insertion of the insertion element IS<I>186</I> 22 bp 
                     downstream of the gene.
                     Sequence analysis shows that after the removal of the 
                     IS<I>186</I> element sequence, the <I>hokC</I> system 
                     contains regulatory elements described for the 
                     <I>hok/sok</I> system from plasmid R1 including a fold-back
                     inhibition element (<I>fbi</I>), a translational activation
                     element (<I>tac</I>), a promoter, and an overlapping open 
                     reading frame named <I>mokC</I>.
                     Northern analysis using the <I>hokC</I> sysem from ECOR24 
                     which does not contain the IS<I>186</I> element has shown 
                     that the <I>fbi</I> sequence is exonucleolytically removed 
                     during post-transcriptional processing, and that the 
                     <I>hokC</I> locus also encodes a small, less stable 
                     antisense RNA complimentary to the <I>hokC</I> mRNA leader 
                     in the <I>mokC</I> open reading frame. The antisense RNA is
                     homologous to Sok-RNA from plasmid R1, and is
                     believed to be designed to regulate <I>hokC</I> translation
                     by inhibiting translation of the overlapping <I>mokC</I>. 
                     It has been shown that <I>hokC</I> is unable to mediate PSK
                     |CITS:[10361310]|."
                     /translation="MLNTCRVPLTDRKVKEKRAMKQHKAMIVALIVICITAVVAALVTR
                     KDLCEVHIRTGQTEVAVFTAYESE"
                     /go_component="outer membrane-bounded periplasmic space
                     [goid 0030288] [evidence IEA] [PMID 26602691]"
                     /go_component="plasma membrane [goid 0005886] [evidence
                     RCA] [PMID 27510400]"
                     /go_component="plasma membrane [goid 0005886] [evidence
                     IEA] [GO_REF 0000044]"
                     /go_component="membrane [goid 0016020] [evidence IEA]
                     [GO_REF 0000002]"
     gene            complement(16751..16903)
                     /gene="hokC"
                     /locus_tag="b4412"
                     /label=hokC
     CDS             complement(16751..16903)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hokC"
                     /locus_tag="b4412"
                     /product="small toxic polypeptide HokC"
                     /label=hokC
                     /note="HokC is a member of a conserved Hok family of toxin 
                     proteins |CITS: [2693900][2101633]|.
                     Sequence analysis indicates that the <I>hokC</I> gene is a 
                     homologue of
                     the <I>hok</I> (<U>ho</U>st <U>k</U>illing) gene which is 
                     responsible for mediating plasmid
                     stabilization by post-segregational killing (PSK) in 
                     plasmid R1. <i>E. coli</i> has five chromosomal hok-like 
                     genes |CITS: [10361310]|; HokC has similarity to <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |FRAME: EG11130-MONOMER HokD| and the episomal Hok
                     protein |CITS: [2693900]|.
                     <I>hok</I>
                     encodes a stable mRNA whose translation is inhibited by a 
                     less stable mRNA
                     encoded by <I>sok</I>. The stable mRNA from <I>hokC</I> 
                     encodes a
                     polypeptide that induction experiments have shown to be 
                     toxic to
                     <I>Escherichia coli</I> host cells, resulting in cell 
                     growth arrest, morphological
                     changes, and rapid cell killing. The <I>hokC</I> system is 
                     inactive due to
                     insertion of the insertion element IS<I>186</I> 22 bp 
                     downstream of the gene.
                     Sequence analysis shows that after the removal of the 
                     IS<I>186</I> element
                     sequence, the <I>hokC</I> system contains regulatory 
                     elements described
                     for the <I>hok/sok</I> system from plasmid R1 including a 
                     fold-back inhibition
                     element (<I>fbi</I>), a translational activation element 
                     (<I>tac</I>), a promoter,
                     and an overlapping open reading frame named <I>mokC</I>. 
                     Northern
                     analysis using the <I>hokC</I> system from ECOR24 which 
                     does not contain
                     the IS<I>186</I> element has shown that the <I>fbi</I> 
                     sequence is
                     exonucleolytically removed during post-transcriptional 
                     processing, and that the
                     <I>hokC</I> locus also encodes a small, less stable 
                     antisense RNA complimentary
                     to the <I>hokC</I> mRNA leader in the <I>mokC</I> open 
                     reading frame. The antisense RNA is homologous to Sok-RNA 
                     from plasmid R1, and is
                     believed to be designed to regulate <I>hokC</I> translation
                     by inhibiting
                     translation of the overlapping <I>mokC</I>. It has been 
                     shown that <I>hokC</I>
                     is unable to mediate PSK |CITS:[10361310]|.
                     HokC has an N-terminal integral membrane domain, which 
                     resides in the
                     inner membrane, and a periplasmic C-terminal dimerization 
                     domain
                     |CITS: [1943700]|. The C-terminal domain confers the toxic 
                     function |CITS: [1943700]|.
                     HokC has been studied as an experimental anti-cancer 
                     therapeutic agent
                     |CITS: [12824883], [12838323]|. HokC has been used as a 
                     component of an
                     experimental antimicrobial therapy |CITS: [12654662]|, and 
                     as part of an
                     experimental containment system for microorganisms that are
                     modified for
                     possible release into the environment |CITS: [8285679], 
                     [7574584], [9008888],
                     [9732536], [9835581], [11375176]|.
                     HokC-resistant mutants have been isolated |CITS: 
                     [1602968]|.
                     Regulation has been described |CITS: [2693900], [1943701], 
                     [10361310]|.
                     HokC production is regulated post-transcriptionally |CITS: 
                     [2693900], [1943701]|.
                     HokC regulation involves an antisense RNA |CITS: [1943701],
                     [10361310]|.
                     The HokC mRNA is subject to 3' post-transcriptional 
                     processing |CITS: [10361310]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [2101633][9221668][23131729]|."
