     gene            16952..17010
                     /gene="sokC"
                     /locus_tag="b4413"
                     /label=sokC
     misc_RNA        16952..17010
                     /gene="sokC"
                     /locus_tag="b4413"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA antitoxin SokC"
                     /note="<i>sokC</i> encodes a small regulatory RNA that 
                     downregulates the expression of the HokC toxin |CITS: 
                     [1943701]|.
                     The <i>hokC-sokC</i> locus of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 encodes a
                     hok/sok toxin/antitoxin system that appeared to have been 
                     inactivated by the nearby insertion of an IS186 insertion 
                     sequence |CITS: [10361310]|.
                     In the plasmid R1 hok/sok system, Sok antisense RNA binds 
                     with <i>hok</i> mRNA, allowing cleavage by RNase III |CITS:
                     [10473621][1380562]|.
                     SokC interacts with the protein |FRAME: EG12866-MONOMER| 
                     |CITS: [36328526]|.
                     Sof: ""<b>s</b>uppression <b>o</b>f <b>f</b>atality"" 
                     |CITS: [1943701]|<br>
                     SokC: ""<b>s</b>uppression <b>o</b>f <b>k</b>illing 
                     <b>C</b>"" |CITS: [10361310]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9442888][17376733][23131729][32213244]|"
     gene            17489..18655
                     /gene="nhaA"
                     /locus_tag="b0019"
                     /label=nhaA
     CDS             17489..18655
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nhaA"
                     /locus_tag="b0019"
                     /product="bifunctional Na(+):H(+) antiporter NhaA /
                     Na(+):H(+) antiporter"
                     /label=nhaA
                     /note="NhaA is an Na<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> antiporter
                     with a prominent role in sodium ion and alkaline pH 
                     homeostasis. NhaA mediates the active uptake of protons in 
                     exchange for cytoplasmic Na<sup>+</sup> - a crucial 
                     mechanism that supports the maintenance of cytoplasmic pH 
                     under alkali challenge (see |CITS: [21464825]|).
                     Purified, reconstituted NhaA is an electrogenic antiporter 
                     with a stoichiometry of 2H<sup>+</sup>:Na<sup>+</sup> 
                     |CITS: [1645730][8383669]|. NhaA is also capable of 
                     transporting Li<sup>+</sup> and is involved in lithium 
                     detoxification |CITS:[8019504]|; NhaA is a 
                     Li<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> antiporter with a 
                     stoichiometry of 2H<sup>+</sup>:Li<sup>+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [27021484]|. NhaA mediated Na<sup>+</sup> efflux (in an 
                     <i>in vitro</i> system) is stimulated 2000-fold upon an 
                     increase in pH from 6.5 to 8.5 |CITS: [1645730]|; 
                     pH-induced activation is associated with conformational 
                     change |CITS: [10455127][17350999][19396973][21873214]|. 
                     Purified, reconstituted NhaA catalyses 
                     Na<sup>+</sup>:Na<sup>+</sup>, 
                     Na<sup>+</sup>:Li<sup>+</sup> |CITS:[8282121]|, 
                     Ca<sup>2+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> and 
                     Ca<sup>2+</sup>:Na<sup>+</sup> exchange |CITS:[8282122]|. 
                     NhaA has an important role in maintaining antibiotic 
                     tolerance under starvation; <i>nhaA</i> is upregulated 
                     during nutrient starvation and the starvation-induced 
                     ampicillin tolerance levels of an <i>nhaA</i> knockout 
                     strain are significantly reduced over both a 6 and 30 day 
                     period |CITS: [34787438]|.
                     NhaA exists as a dimer within the membrane |CITS: 
                     [10393172][10638764][11258962][15894644][17704177]|. NhaA 
                     monomers are functional for Na<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> 
                     antiport but dimers are important under conditions of 
                     extreme stress, possibly due to increased stability 
                     |CITS:[17635927], [19129192]|. The monomer contains 12 
                     transmembrane (TM) helices with both N and C termini 
                     located in the cytoplasm |CITS:[8943289],[10638764]|. 
                     Crystal structures of the NhaA monomer |CITS: [15988517]| 
                     and dimer |CITS: [25422503]|, both crystallised at low pH 
                     (3.5 - 4) have been reported; see reviews by |CITS: 
                     [19448069]| and |CITS: [24361841]| for a detailed 
                     discussion of NhaA structure. Mass spectrometry analysis of
                     NhaA released from C8E4 (tetraethylene glycol monooctyl 
                     ether) micelles shows binding of cardiolipin and 
                     phospholipids to intact dimers; tandem mass spectrometry 
                     suggests that cardiolipin stabilizes the dimer structure 
                     |CITS: [28077870]| (see also |CITS: [31776461]|).
                     Cells lacking <i>nhaA</i> have impaired capacity to adapt 
                     to high salinity and enhanced Li<sup>+</sup> toxicity 
                     |CITS: [2555351]|
                     <I>nhaA</I> expression is induced by exposure to 
                     Na<sup>+</sup> or Li<sup>+</sup>; varying the pH (6.5 - 
                     8.6) does not increase <i>nhaA</i> expression but does 
                     affect the sensitivity of expression to Na<sup>+</sup> or 
                     Li<sup>+</sup> |CITS:[1657980]|. <I>nhaA</I> transcription 
                     is regulated by |FRAME: PD00474 ""NhaR""| in response to 
                     intracellular Na<sup>+</sup>, and by the global regulator 
                     H-NS; expression is regulated via two promoters, P1 - a 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> dependent, NhaR-Na<sup>+</sup> 
                     responsive promoter active during exponential growth - and 
                     P2, a &sigma;<sup>S</sup> dependent, Na<sup>+</sup> 
                     independent promoter active during the stationary phase 
                     |CITS:[1316901][8932307][11133959]|.
                     NhaA has been extensively mutagenised to study 
                     structure-function relationships |CITS: [8381959][8168494] 
                     [7737413][7592922][8999858][9163515][9756882][11258962][117
                     81101][12475238][14604993][15039449][17284054][18387952][19
                     923224][21907722][22915592][23836890][28330875][30409911][3
                     2973095][34273399]|. Binding of Li<sup>+</sup> and 
                     H<sup>+</sup> is coupled and antagonistic in agreement with
                     the proposed alternative access mechanism for secondary 
                     transporters |CITS: [27021484]|. Binding of lithium to NhaA
                     induces global conformational change resulting from two 
                     movements which affect the cytoplasmic and periplamic 
                     funnels and are consistent with an alternating-access 
                     mechanism of transport |CITS: [29078272]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains other sodium:proton 
                     antiporters including |FRAME: NHAB-MONOMER ""NhaB""| (a 
                     second electrogenic H<sup>+</sup>:Na<sup>+</sup> 
                     antiporter), |FRAME: CHAA-MONOMER ""ChaA""| and the 
                     multidrug efflux transporters |FRAME: CMR-MONOMER ""MdfA""|
                     and |FRAME: YJIO-MONOMER ""MdtM""|. The <i>E. coli</i> K-12
                     mutant strain KNabc (&Delta;<i>nhaA</i> &Delta;<i>nhaB</i> 
                     &Delta;<i>chaA</i>) cannot export Na<sup>+</sup> and is 
                     unable to grow in the presence of of 0.2 M NaCl (or 10 mM 
                     LiCl); membrane vesicles prepared from KNabc cells do not 
                     show any Na<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> antiporter activity 
                     |CITS: [8651921]|.
                     <i>ant</i>(up): increased Na<sup>+</sup>/H<sup>+</sup> 
                     <U>ant</U>iport |CITS: [3033655][7047530]|.
                     Additional reviews: |CITS:[8144493][10207920][11248196][11 
                     415429][12586378][15282168][16544108][22694117][36012428]| 
                     "
