     gene            22391..25207
                     /gene="ileS"
                     /locus_tag="b0026"
                     /label=ileS
     CDS             22391..25207
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ileS"
                     /locus_tag="b0026"
                     /product="multifunctional L-valine--tRNA(Ile) ligase / 
                     L-valyl-tRNA(Ile) deacylase / L-homocysteine cyclase / 
                     isoleucine--tRNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.1.1.5"
                     /label=ileS
                     /note="Isoleucine-tRNA ligase (IleRS) is a member of the
                     family of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, which interpret the 
                     genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their 
                     specific tRNA molecules. The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis. IleRS belongs to the Class I aminoacyl tRNA 
                     synthetases |CITS: [2203971][7647112]|.
                     Specificity determinants within tRNA<sup>Ile</sup> that are
                     important for recognition by IleRS have been identified 
                     |CITS: [789377][336087][411506][1726806][202393 
                     4][8199246][8114089][19233850]|. The presence of the 
                     t<sup>6</sup>A modification at position 37 of 
                     tRNA<sup>Ile</sup><sub>GAU</sub> strongly enhances the 
                     aminoacylation rate by IleRS |CITS: [26337258]|. Residues 
                     within IleRS that interact with tRNA<sup>Ile</sup> have 
                     been identified |CITS: [7669778][8605884]|. IleRS 
                     specifically acylates the 2'-OH and deacylates the 3'-OH of
                     tRNA<sup>Ile</sup> |CITS: [11923317]|. The aminoacyl 
                     linkage between Ile and tRNA<sup>Ile</sup> is the most 
                     stable among the 12 pairs of cognate amino acid-tRNA pairs 
                     measured by |CITS: [24751649]|.
                     The reaction mechanism of IleRS has been studied |CITS: 
                     [6129973]|. Aminoacylation proceeds via the aminoacyl 
                     adenylate pathway |CITS: [764868][323252]|.
                     IleRS contains two zinc atoms per active site |CITS: 
                     [8286369]|. One zinc atom bound near the N-terminal 
                     catalytic core is important for amino acid binding and 
                     utilization |CITS: [7947832]|. The second zinc binding site
                     has been mapped near the C terminus of IleRS |CITS: 
                     [8051111]| and is required for the tRNA binding step in 
                     aminoacylation |CITS: [7488160]|. The C-terminal peptide 
                     containing this site can complement the specific tRNA 
                     binding and catalytic activity of the N-terminal domain 
                     <i>in trans</i> |CITS: [8672449][9184155]|. If separately 
                     expressed, the three domains of IleRS can form an active 
                     complex and complement each other <i>in trans</i> |CITS: 
                     [1429621][1542687]|. The dispensable CP1 domain interrupts 
                     the nucleotide binding domain within IleRS |CITS: 
                     [3306924]|.  IleRS binds one molecule of tRNA per molecule 
                     of enzyme |CITS: [323006]|. Affinity labeling experiments 
                     were used to identify various binding and active sites of 
                     IleRS |CITS: [19557155]|.
                     Binding specificity of IleRS for various aliphatic amino 
                     acids and aminoalkyl adenylates has been determined; none 
                     of the tested isoleucine analogs bind as tightly as 
                     isoleucine itself |CITS: 
                     [782880][795668][325520][3322383]|. Both amino acid 
                     discrimination and proofreading contribute to the 
                     specificity of Ile charging onto tRNA<sup>Ile</sup> |CITS: 
                     [3315663][3281834]|, and experimental data is consistent 
                     with the existence of both a pre- and a post-transfer 
                     editing capability of the enzyme, depending on the amino 
                     acid |CITS: [321008][329276][3297096][1720662]|. The energy
                     costs and trade-offs associated with translational 
                     proofreading has been studied with IleRS |CITS: 
                     [1063397][32354218][35259959]|.
                     The body of tRNA<sup>Ile</sup> appears to modulate the 
                     pre-transfer editing activity of IleRS, while the 3' end of
                     tRNA<sup>Ile</sup> is required for post-transfer editing 
                     |CITS: [25873392]|.
                     Misactivated homocysteine undergoes pre-transfer editing 
                     |CITS: [7024910]|. Although valine is discriminated against
                     at the amino acid recognition stage, IleRS misactivates 
                     valine at a significant rate.  A single point mutation, 
                     G56A, eliminates the ability to discriminate against 
                     valine, and valine is then discriminated against by 
                     hydrolytic editing |CITS: [8146659]|. The presence of 
                     misacylated tRNA was thought to be required for both pre- 
                     and post-transfer editing |CITS: [14596614]|.
                     IleRS appears to be the only class I enzyme for which 
                     pre-transfer editing is important. The rates of hydrolysis 
                     of a pre-transfer amino-adenylate and a misacylated tRNA 
                     are approximately equal for IleRS |CITS: [20498377]|. The 
                     tRNA-dependent pre-transfer editing activity accounts for 
                     approximately 1/3 of the total proofreading activity. The 
                     conserved Tyr59 residue within the tRNA aminoacylation 
                     active site is involved in tRNA-dependent pre-transfer 
                     editing |CITS: [25207837]|. tRNA<sup>Ile</sup> stimulates 
                     pre-transfer editing and decreases the affinity of IleRS 
                     toward amino acids |CITS: [25873392]|. The editing response
                     was initially thought to be triggered by specific bases in 
                     the effector and to not require a tRNA-like structure or 
                     active acceptor hydroxyl groups |CITS: [8610114]|, and was 
                     thought to involve translocation of the misactivated amino 
                     acid from the active site to the editing site |CITS: 
                     [10549284][11284704][11782529][11864608][12515858]|.
                     The post-transfer editing site maps to the CP1 domain, an 
                     insertion that interrupts the Rossman fold 
                     nucleotide-binding domain |CITS: [7669778]|. The isolated 
                     CP1 domain alone has post-transfer editing activity |CITS: 
                     [8900273]|. The editing site is distinct from the active 
                     site for aminoacylation |CITS: [10889024]|. Distinct 
                     residues within the editing site are required for editing 
                     the misactivated adenylate form and the aminoacyl ester 
                     |CITS: [11864608]|.
                     Site-directed mutagenesis identified the isoleucine binding
                     site |CITS: [3282306]|. Labeling studies with 
                     pyridoxal-5'-phosphate identified residues that may be 
                     important for binding of the phosphates of ATP |CITS: 
                     [8031903]|. Pseudomonic acid A (mupirocin) is an inhibitor 
                     of IleRS activity |CITS: [7929087]|.
                     An editing-deficient IleRS mutant strain has a growth yield
                     advantage under specialized conditions, generating 
                     ""statistical proteins"" containing norvaline |CITS: 
                     [15163798]|, but under most conditions, it has a lower 
                     growth rate |CITS: [15647356]|. The isoleucine 
                     mistranslation that occurs in the editing-deficient mutant 
                     strain can provide a modest adaptive effect under oxidative
                     stress conditions |CITS: [38963138]|.
                     Expression of <i>ileS</i> and the downstream gene, 
                     <i>lspA</i>, appear to be tightly coupled |CITS: 
                     [26070674]|.
                     There is a general discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [37224536]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10966471][2183216][15677335][23075299]|"
