     gene            28374..29195
                     /gene="dapB"
                     /locus_tag="b0031"
                     /label=dapB
     CDS             28374..29195
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dapB"
                     /locus_tag="b0031"
                     /product="4-hydroxy-tetrahydrodipicolinate reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.17.1.8"
                     /label=dapB
                     /note="4-hydroxy-tetrahydrodipicolinate reductase,
                     historically called dihydrodipicolinate reductase (DHDPR, 
                     DapB) catalyzes a step in lysine biosynthesis via 
                     diaminopimelate. The substrate for DapB is (4<i>S</i>)-4-h 
                     ydroxy-2,3,4,5-tetrahydro-(2<i>S</i>)-dipicolinate (HTPA) 
                     |CITS: [20503968]|. Given the discovery of HTPA as the true
                     substrate of DapB |CITS: [20503968]|, previous data on the 
                     reaction mechanism of the enzyme, e.g. in |CITS: 
                     [9398235]|, need to be re-evaluated.
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme have been solved |CITS: 
                     [7893645][8873595][9398235]|. The enzyme consists of two 
                     domains; the N-terminal domain binds the dinucleotide 
                     cosubstrate, while the C-terminal domain is thought to bind
                     dihydrodipicolinate |CITS: [7893645]|. Conformational 
                     change upon substrate binding may be essential for 
                     catalysis |CITS: [9092803][9398235]|; a reaction mechansim 
                     was proposed |CITS: [9398235]|. Interactions of DapB with a
                     variety of nucleotide substrates were investigated by 
                     isothermal titration calorimetry and crystallography |CITS:
                     [8873595]|. The enzyme follows an ordered reaction 
                     mechanism, first binding NADPH |CITS: [7893644]|.  The DapB
                     homotetramer appears to bind NADH sequentially with 
                     negative cooperativity between the subunits |CITS: 
                     [18710263]|.
                     Expression of <i>dapB</i> is repressed by lysine |CITS: 
                     [6094578]| via ArgP |CITS: [18502871][21890697]|. Depletion
                     of DapB was found to generate reactive oxygen species 
                     (ROS), inducing oxidative damage; in mutants lacking the 
                     base excision repair (BER) genes, |FRAME: EG10329| and 
                     |FRAME: EG10627|, the lack of DapB resulted in cell death 
                     |CITS: [35130721]|.
                     DapB: ""<b>d</b>i<b>a</b>mino<b>p</b>imelate biosynthesis 
                     <b>B</b>"" |CITS: [4926684]|"
