     gene            42403..43173
                     /gene="fixA"
                     /locus_tag="b0041"
                     /label=fixA
     CDS             42403..43173
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fixA"
                     /locus_tag="b0041"
                     /product="putative electron transfer flavoprotein FixA"
                     /label=fixA
                     /note="FixA and FixB are required for anaerobic carnitine 
                     reduction and are proposed to act in the transfer of 
                     electrons to crotonobetainyl-CoA reductase (CaiA) |CITS: 
                     [12081978]|.  FixA and FixB have similarity to mammalian 
                     electron transfer flavoprotein subunits |CITS: [7473063]| 
                     (ETF-&beta; and &alpha; families, respectively) |CITS: 
                     [8525056]| and were predicted to play an electron transport
                     role in <i>E. coli</i> based on sequence similarity |CITS: 
                     [7473063]|.
                     <i>fixA</i>, <i>fixB</i>, <i>ydiQ</i>, <i>ydiR</i>, 
                     <i>ygcR</i>, and <i>ygcQ</i> form a subsystem based on 
                     genome context methods, and may represent a previously 
                     uncharacterized electron transfer pathway |CITS: 
                     [21775308]|.
                     A <i>fixA</i> or <i>fixB</i> mutant exhibits a defect in 
                     anaerobic reduction of carnitine to &gamma;-butyrobetaine 
                     |CITS: [12081978]|.  Chromosomal insertion mutants in 
                     <i>fix</i> genes have a growth defect under anaerobic 
                     conditions |CITS: [7473063]|.
                     The <i>cai</i> and <i>fix</i> operons are divergently 
                     transcribed in a coordinated fashion |CITS: [9573142]|. The
                     <i>fix</i> operon is expressed under anaerobic conditions 
                     in the presence of carnitine or crotonobetaine |CITS: 
                     [7473063]|.
                     Work by |CITS: [7473063]| was performed with genes from 
                     <i>E. coli</i> strain O44 K74, which was isolated from the 
                     intestine of a rat after a carnitine rich diet and selected
                     for high carnitine metabolism activity."
