     gene            49823..50302
                     /gene="folA"
                     /locus_tag="b0048"
                     /label=folA
     CDS             49823..50302
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="folA"
                     /locus_tag="b0048"
                     /product="dihydrofolate reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.5.1.3"
                     /label=folA
                     /note="The enzyme encoded by <i>folA</i>, dihydrofolate 
                     reductase (DHFR) provides the major dihydrofolate reductase
                     activity in the tetrahydrofolate biosynthetic pathway. It 
                     catalyzes the reduction of dihydrofolate to 
                     tetrahydrofolate via hydride transfer from NADPH to C6 of 
                     the pteridine ring. Tetrahydrofolate is an important 
                     intermediate in the biosynthesis of proteins and nucleic 
                     acids (see pathway |FRAME: PWY-6614|). Because 
                     dihydrofolate reductase is essential for cell division and 
                     growth, it is a target for drug development. It is 
                     susceptible to inhibition by several agents which have 
                     antitumor, antibacterial and antimalarial properties 
                     including the well known drugs methotrexate and 
                     trimethoprim |CITS: [21084197][22024482]|.
                     The <i>folA</i> gene has been characterized |CITS: 
                     [6159575][6444603][7047510]| and the enzyme has been 
                     purified and characterized |CITS: [46][6269632][6315690]|. 
                     <i>E. coli</i> dihydrofolate reductase has been used as a 
                     model enzyme for research on the relationship between 
                     enzyme structure, dynamics and function. Early kinetic 
                     studies of wild-type and mutant enzymes showed that the 
                     apoenzyme exists in at least two different conformational 
                     states, one of which is not catalytically competent |CITS: 
                     [2108144]|. Subsequent kinetic studies of site-directed 
                     mutant enzymes contributed to a mechanistic model of 
                     catalysis |CITS: [9572847][9572848]|. Later kinetic and 
                     biophysical studies analyzed protein conformational changes
                     in relation to catalysis |CITS: 
                     [19419144][22060818][22437714][23705773][25014833]|. 
                     Additional physical, biophysical and kinetic studies have 
                     provided insight into its activity, stability and reaction 
                     mechanism |CITS: [22266402][22942460][22641560][22922383][ 
                     23672258][24252106][24820793][24977791][26407224]|. A study
                     on chaperone-assisted <i>de novo </i> protein folding using
                     DHFR found a novel independent folding unit on DHFR when 
                     it's associated with the ribosome and its nascent protein 
                     chain interacts with |FRAME: EG11003-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [38987455]|.
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme and its kinetic 
                     intermediates have been solved, elucidating the 
                     conformational changes that occur during the catalytic 
                     cycle |CITS: [6815178][6815179][2185835][1998681][9012674] 
                     [26147643][24915100]|. These changes have also been studied
                     in solution using NMR techniques |CITS: 
                     [15609999][14717591][15795383][16973882] 
                     [18690714][20080605][21474759][23614825][23758161]|, 
                     including the dynamics of inhibitor binding |CITS: 
                     [22246400]|. A hyperactive mutant lacking cysteine and 
                     methionine was created and studied both kinetically and 
                     crystallographically |CITS: [16510443]|. The enzyme has 
                     also been used to study protein folding using several 
                     biophysical approaches |CITS: 
                     [15740745][17331539][21554889]|. Using a combination of 
                     enzyme kinetics, X-ray crystallography and NMR spectroscopy
                     the enzyme was shown to undergo conformational modulation 
                     in the presence of substrate, product and cofactor as it 
                     goes through its kinetic cycle |CITS: [26147643]|.
                     Dihydrofolate reductase has also been used to study 
                     mechanisms of drug resistance |CITS: 
                     [22179135][28422217][34011959][35562546]|, and to determine
                     the effect of mutations on protein homeostasis from an 
                     evolutionary perspective |CITS: 
                     [22411825][23219534][25892240]|, including exploring what 
                     impacts fitness landscapes |CITS: [37995212][40120127]|. 
                     The enzyme has also played a role in the development of new
                     biochemical and biophysical methods, including its use in: 
                     defining mechanisms of allosteric regulation |CITS: 
                     [22196731]|; library screening as a reporter protein |CITS:
                     [23423901]|; <i>in vitro</i> translational incorporation of
                     &beta;-amino acids |CITS: [23375097]|; cell imaging and 
                     control of protein function <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [23745575][24598024][25065762]|; protein conformation 
                     algorithm predictions |CITS: [23913260]|; mechanistic 
                     studies of allosteric effector antibodies |CITS: 
                     [23911607]| and active-site trapping antibodies |CITS: 
                     [24617671]|; studying the evolution of protein 
                     conformational dynamics |CITS: [24077226][35984987]|; 
                     quantitative models of drug resistance |CITS: [24980690]|; 
                     thermostability studies |CITS: [24437522]|; and transition 
                     state simulations |CITS: [24860275][24798860]|. 
                     Partially-unfolded FolA is recognized and degraded by the 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-2982| |CITS: [36630366]|.
                     A temperature-sensitive mutation in this gene was 
                     identified that showed potential as a growth-switch for 
                     genetic engineering |CITS: [37642940]|.
                     Adaptive laboratory evolution of <i>E. coli</i> MG1655 in 
                     the presence of trimethoprim resulted in loss-of-function 
                     mutations in <i>folA</i> typically after mutations in 
                     <i>|FRAME: G7002|</i> occurred. The trimethoprim-resistant 
                     &Delta;<i>mgrB</i> mutant strain caused upregulation in 
                     <i>folA</i> transcription that was dependent on PhoP, 
                     though not necessarily due to direct binding of PhoP to the
                     <i>folA</i> promoter |CITS: [34591012]|. Duplication of a 
                     large genomic segment flanked by insertion sequences (IS) 
                     and containing <i>folA</i> occurred more frequently in 
                     &Delta;<i>mgrB</i> and double &Delta;<i>|FRAME: 
                     EG10542|/mgrB</i> strains compared to wild-type. The 
                     <i>folA</i> gene copy number depended on the trimethoprim 
                     pressure and the number of point mutations in the 
                     <i>folA</i> gene or promoter, and was exacerbated by Lon 
                     proteolysis on unstable DHFR mutants, pointing to 
                     proteostasis as another determinant in gene copy number 
                     evolution |CITS: [40073078]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [15139807][18298083][23420416]|"
