     gene            complement(50380..51222)
                     /gene="apaH"
                     /locus_tag="b0049"
                     /label=apaH
     CDS             complement(50380..51222)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="apaH"
                     /locus_tag="b0049"
                     /product="bifunctional pppGpp-hydrolase / diadenosine 
                     tetraphosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.1.40; 3.6.1.41"
                     /label=apaH
                     /note="Diadenosine tetraphosphate (Ap<sub>4</sub>A) is a
                     side product of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, including <i>E.
                     coli</i> |FRAME: LYSU-CPLX LysU|. Diadenosine 
                     tetraphosphatase (diadenosine 5', 5'''-P<sup>1</sup>, 
                     P<sup>4</sup>-tetraphosphate pyrophosphohydrolase, ApaH) 
                     hydrolyzes Ap<sub>4</sub>A and related molecules |CITS: 
                     [6317672]|. ApaH decaps RNA molecules that contain 
                     Ap<sub>4</sub> and Gp<sub>4</sub> caps at the 5' end, 
                     generating a diphosphorylated RNA product |CITS: 
                     [31178354]|. It has also been shown that ApaH can cleave 
                     both methylated and non-methylated Np<sub>n</sub>N caps 
                     |CITS: [32103016]|.
                     Epistasis experiments suggest an RNA decay pathway 
                     involving the consecutive actions of ApaH followed by 
                     |FRAME: G7459-MONOMER RppH|, triggering the degradation of 
                     Np<sub>4</sub>-capped RNAs |CITS: [31178354]|.
                     Substrate specificity and reaction kinetics have been 
                     examined |CITS: [6317672][2986688][35294098]|. ApaH cleaves
                     after the second phosphate group of the adenosine moiety 
                     |CITS: [8286347]|.
                     Hydrolysis of, and potential inhibition by, pppGpp, a 
                     ""Magic Spot Nucleotide"" involved in the stringent 
                     response was observed for ApaH despite the lack of a Nudix 
                     hydrolase domain |CITS: [35294098]|.
                     An <i>apaH</i> mutation leads to elevated levels of 
                     Ap<sub>4</sub>A |CITS: [2544886][2157025][28516732]|, loss 
                     of motility due to a decrease in 
                     &sigma;<sup>F</sup>-mediated gene expression, and decreased
                     expression of cAMP-CRP-regulated genes |CITS: [2544886]|. 
                     An <i>apaH</i> mutant exhibits decreased heat resistance 
                     |CITS: [1935909][28516732]| that can be suppressed by ClpB 
                     overproduction |CITS: [8468292]|, and decreased resistance 
                     to mild acid stress |CITS: [27826291]|. The previously 
                     identified <i>cfcB1</i> mutation |CITS: [2651881]| is an 
                     <i>apaH</i> allele |CITS: [9286857]|. An <i>apaH</i> null 
                     mutant shows a moderately decreased level of persister cell
                     formation |CITS: [18519731]|. The phenotypes of several 
                     <i>apaH</i> point mutations and the effects of a 
                     &Delta;<i>apaH</i> mutation on the proteome were also 
                     tested |CITS: [28516732]|. Both deletion of <i>apaH</i> and
                     overexpression of LysU lead to a large increase in killing 
                     by aminoglycoside antibiotics such as kanamycin |CITS: 
                     [31004054]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>apaH</i> decreases the intracellular 
                     concentration of dinucleotide tetraphosphates |CITS: 
                     [2995325]|, but does not appear to alter the profile of 
                     proteins produced upon heat shock or oxidative damage and 
                     does not increase resistance to H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     |CITS: [3038851]|.
                     The ApaH protein of <i>Salmonella Typhimurium</i> is 
                     required for wild-type invasion of human cells <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [12824172]|.
                     <i>apaH</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     "
