     gene            complement(51609..52430)
                     /gene="rsmA"
                     /locus_tag="b0051"
                     /label=rsmA
     CDS             complement(51609..52430)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rsmA"
                     /locus_tag="b0051"
                     /product="bifunctional 16S rRNA 
                     (A(1518)/A(1519)-N(6)-dimethyltransferase / 16S rRNA 
                     m(6)2A1518,m(6)2A1519 dimethyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.182"
                     /label=rsmA
                     /note="RsmA is the methyltransferase responsible for 
                     dimethylation of 16S rRNA at the two adjacent adenosine 
                     bases A1518 and A1519 |CITS: [383712]|. <i>In vitro</i>, 
                     the enzyme is active on |FRAME: CPLX0-3953 ""30S ribosomal 
                     subunits""|, but not the fully assembled 70S |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3964| |CITS: [383712]|. RsmA may play a role in 
                     biogenesis of the small subunit of the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [18990185]|, providing a checkpoint where binding of RsmA 
                     keeps immature 30S subunits from entering the translational
                     cycle |CITS: [18391965]|. RsmA and the ribosome maturation 
                     factor |FRAME: EG11178-MONOMER RbfA| function together in 
                     checkpointing maturation of the 30S subunit |CITS: 
                     [23387871]|. Methylation of the 16S rRNA by RsmA is 
                     important for release of RbfA from the 30S subunit |CITS: 
                     [31728529]|.
                     The evolutionary relationship and functional divergence of 
                     this enzyme and its homologs in eukaryotes has been studied
                     |CITS: [17031457]|. RsmA as well as the dimethylation 
                     modification it catalyzes are nearly universally conserved,
                     suggesting an important function |CITS: [16620761]|. RsmA 
                     is unable to methylate free 16S rRNA |CITS: [4610401]| and 
                     is only able to methylate 30S ribosomal subunits in their 
                     translationally inactive conformation |CITS: 
                     [16620761][22308031]|. RsmA interacts with the decoding 
                     site of the 30S subunit, and interactions of 30S with RsmA 
                     and |FRAME: EG10506-MONOMER| appear to be mutually 
                     exclusive |CITS: [18391965]|. Lack of RsmA affects the 
                     initiation error rate and the kinetics of translation 
                     initiation |CITS: [31586395]|.
                     Methylation of the two adenosine residues is independent of
                     each other |CITS: [2207142][10512710]|. Recognition of the 
                     3' terminal hairpin of 16S rRNA and methylation activity of
                     RsmA have been studied |CITS: [7538324]|. The loop of helix
                     24 and the length of helix 45 of 16S rRNA appear to be 
                     important for RsmA catalytic activity |CITS: [21142019]|. 
                     Binding of RsmA prevents helix 44 from adopting its 
                     position in the mature 30S subunit and blocks the formation
                     of the decoding site |CITS: [22308031]|. Once the 
                     methylation is complete, RsmA dissociates rapidly from 30S,
                     likely due to a methylation-dependent conformational change
                     in the rRNA |CITS: [22142337]|. Crystal structures of wild 
                     type and the L114P mutant of RsmA |CITS: 
                     [12876362][15136037][22142337]| and cryo-EM structures of 
                     RsmA bound to a nonmethylated 30S ribosomal subunit |CITS: 
                     [22308031][34086932]| have been solved. Site-directed 
                     mutagenesis of conserved residues in the predicted 
                     N-terminal SAM binding motifs result in loss of 
                     methyltransferase activity |CITS: [17890303]|. The L114 
                     residue in the active site specifically affects the ability
                     of the enzyme to perform the second methylation reaction at
                     the same adenine base |CITS: [22142337]|. While the 
                     N-terminal catalytic domain contacts the substrate 
                     adenosine residues, the C-terminal domain contacts helix 27
                     and helix 24 of 16S rRNA |CITS: [22308031]|. Successive 
                     methylation of the two adjacent adenosine residues is 
                     thought to involve successive flipping of the substrate 
                     nucleotides without dissociation of RsmA |CITS: 
                     [34086932]|.
                     RsmA also exhibits cytosine-DNA glycosylase activity and 
                     may play a role in protection of DNA against oxidative 
                     stress |CITS: [19223326]|. The C terminal of RsmA, which is
                     not involved in methylation, is spatially similar to the 
                     H2TH domains of the |FRAME: EG10329-MONOMER 
                     ""DNA-formamidopyrimidine glycosylase MutM""| and the 
                     |FRAME: G6383 ""endonuclease Nei|. Purified RsmA, but not 
                     C-terminally deleted RsmA, exhibited DNA-binding activity 
                     |CITS: [37041652]|.
                     Mutation of <i>rsmA</i> causes resistance to |FRAME: 
                     CPD-26641| (an inhibitor of translation initiation), but no
                     substantial growth defect |CITS: 
                     [4901406][6988670][2157025]|. The lack of methylation at 
                     A1519, but not A1518, appears to be responsible for the 
                     kasugamycin resistance phenotype of an <i>rsmA</i> mutant 
                     |CITS: [10512710]|. An <i>rsmA</i> deletion enhances the 
                     slow growth phenotype of an |FRAME: G7841| mutation |CITS: 
                     [18223068]|. <i>rsmA</i> was also identified as a multicopy
                     suppressor for a cold-sensitive mutant of |FRAME: EG10270|,
                     which encodes an essential GTP-binding protein |CITS: 
                     [9748462]|. The methyltransferase and multicopy suppressor 
                     activities of RsmA are separable, indicating a possible 
                     second function of RsmA |CITS: [17890303]|. High-level 
                     overexpression of RsmA is toxic, and the cells show 
                     increased sensitivity to acid shock |CITS: [17890303]|. An 
                     <i>rsmA</i> deletion strain is cold sensitive and shows an 
                     altered ribosome profile and altered 16S rRNA processing, 
                     while an <i>rsmA</i> point mutant lacking catalytic 
                     activity has a dominant negative effect on growth and 
                     ribosome formation |CITS: [18990185]|. A &Delta;<i>rsmA</i>
                     mutant shows increased susceptibility to aminoglycoside 
                     antibiotics; the phenotype is due to the lack of its 
                     methyltransferase activity, which affects translation 
                     fidelity |CITS: [30082289]|. Overexpression of |FRAME: 
                     EG11178-MONOMER RbfA| in a &Delta;<i>rsmA</i> strain leads 
                     to a severe growth defect and accumulation of 17S rRNA 
                     precursors |CITS: [23387871]|. Deletion of <i>rsmA</i> 
                     results in accumulation of free 30S assembly intermediates;
                     treatment with RsmA indicates it targets inactive 30S 
                     particles and induces large-scale structural remodeling in 
                     its 'proofreading' function of ribosome biogenesis |CITS: 
                     [37653244]|.
                     In a systematic study of rRNA methyltransferases, 
                     <i>rsmA</i> expression was highly induced in early 
                     exponential phase. The &Delta;<i>rsmA</i> mutant of the 
                     Keio collection showed no apparent accumulation of 
                     ribosomal assembly intermediates, but an increased amount 
                     of the 17S rRNA processing intermediate when grown at 
                     20&deg;C. The mutant shows decreased efficiency of 
                     exogenous protein synthesis |CITS: [32174967]|.
                     The RsmA protein binds specifically to its own mRNA and may
                     regulate its own translation |CITS: [2500421]|. Growth rate
                     positively regulates <i>rsmA</i> expression |CITS: 
                     [11844765]|.
                     KsgA: ""<b>k</b>a<b>s</b>u<b>g</b>amycin resistance"" 
                     |CITS: [4901406]|
                     Comment: |CITS: [19006815]|"
