     gene            complement(54755..57109)
                     /gene="lptD"
                     /locus_tag="b0054"
                     /label=lptD
     CDS             complement(54755..57109)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lptD"
                     /locus_tag="b0054"
                     /product="trifunctional lipopolysaccharide assembly
                     protein LptD / lipopolysaccharide transport system - outer 
                     membrane assembly complex / lipopolysaccharide transport 
                     system"
                     /EC_number="7.5.2.5"
                     /label=lptD
                     /note="The LptD
                     (<u><b>l</b></u>ipo<u><b>p</b></u>olysaccharide 
                     <u><b>t</b></u>ransport) protein is an essential outer 
                     membrane (OM) protein which, in complex with LptE, 
                     functions to assemble lipopolysaccharides at the surface of
                     the OM |CITS:[12207697][16861298]|.
                     LptD exhibits &beta; barrel-like characteristics, including
                     a high incidence of aromatic amino acids (13.1%) 
                     |CITS:[12207697]|. Sequence analyses via the algorithm by 
                     Shirmer and Cowan |CITS:[8401221]| and Zhai 
                     |CITS:[12192075]| suggest that LptD is rich in 
                     transmembrane &beta;-strands. LptD contains an N-terminal 
                     periplasmic domain and a C-terminal &beta;-barrel domain 
                     |CITS: [20203010]|. LptD contains four cysteines which form
                     non-consecutive disulfide bonds (Cys31=Cys724 and 
                     Cys173=Cys755) that bridge the N-terminal and C-terminal 
                     domains |CITS: [20566849]|. LptD is fully oxidised <i>in 
                     vivo</i> and formation of at least one correct disulfide 
                     bond is required for function |CITS: [20566849]|. Folding 
                     of LptD involves DsbA and proceeds via an intermediate 
                     stage containing non-native disulfides |CITS: [22936569]|. 
                     LptE is required for maturation to the active protein 
                     |CITS: [20566849][22936569]|. The bi-functional 
                     protease/chaperone |FRAME: G7311-MONOMER BepA| is important
                     for LptD quality control |CITS: [24003122][34463613] |. 
                     |FRAME: MONOMER0-2693 Lipoprotein LptM| promotes LptD 
                     oxidative maturation |CITS: [37821449]|.
                     LptD is involved in determining the level of organic 
                     solvent tolerance in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     |CITS:[7765247][7811102]|.
                     Decreased expression of the <i>lptD</i> gene results in 
                     increased sensitivity to organic solvents such as n-hexane 
                     |CITS:[12724388]|. Depletion of <i>lptD</i> results in 
                     filamentation (2-8 cells long), loss of membrane integrity,
                     and eventual cell lysis, creating filaments of rod-shaped 
                     ghosts |CITS:[12207697]|. LptD depletion (via a CRISPRi 
                     mechanism) is associated with morphological change, 
                     increased cytoplasmic accumulation of LPS, and a marked 
                     upregulation in carbohydrate metabolism; LptD depletion may
                     potentiate the action of colistin |CITS: [37701421]|).
                     An <i>lptD</i> mutation (<i>imp</i>4213) increases OM 
                     permeability making the cell sensitive to many different 
                     antibiotics and to bile salts |CITS: [2547691], 
                     [11520949]|. This phenotype can be partially suppressed by 
                     a null mutation in <i>bamB</i> |CITS: [15851036]| and an 
                     insertion mutation in <i>bamA</i> |CITS: [17163776]|. 
                     <i>imp</i>4213 cells have reduced outer membrane stiffness 
                     and decreased survival time during mechanical perturbation;
                     LPS contributes to the load bearing capacity of the outer 
                     membrane in <i>E. coli</i> cells |CITS: [30022160]|.
                     Expression of <I>lptD</I> occurs from a &sigma;<sup>E</sup>
                     promoter |CITS: [11274153][12207697]| and two 
                     &sigma;<sup>D</sup>-like promoters |CITS:[14981317]|.
                     LptD is the terminal receptor for a number of small 
                     siphoviruses |CITS: [34784345]|.
                     <i>imp</i>: <u><b>i</b></u>ncreased <u><b>m</b></u>embrane 
                     <u><b>p</b></u>ermeability |CITS: 
                     [2547691]|<BR><i>ostA</i>: <u><b>o</b></u>rganic 
                     <u><b>s</b></u>olvent <u><b>t</b></u>olerance |CITS: 
                     [7765247]|"
