     gene            57364..58179
                     /gene="djlA"
                     /locus_tag="b0055"
                     /label=djlA
     CDS             57364..58179
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="djlA"
                     /locus_tag="b0055"
                     /product="co-chaperone protein DjlA"
                     /label=djlA
                     /note="DjlA functions as a co-chaperone with DnaK and is 
                     implicated in regulating synthesis of the colanic acid 
                     capsule.
                     DjlA is located in the inner membrane, with a short N 
                     terminal region in the periplasmic space, a single 
                     transmembrane dimerization domain |CITS: [12655402]|, and a
                     large cytoplasmic domain containing a J-domain with 
                     similarity to the chaperone DnaJ |CITS: [8809778]|. The 
                     J-domain can functionally substitute for the J-domain of 
                     DnaJ |CITS: [11106641]|. Together with the transmembrane 
                     domain, it is essential for stimulation of the synthesis of
                     the colanic acid mucoid capsule via the RcsB/C signal 
                     transduction system |CITS: [9364917][9364918]|. DnaK and 
                     GrpE |CITS: [9364917]| as well as direct interaction of 
                     DjlA and DnaK |CITS: [11106641]| are required for this 
                     function.  In the absence of DnaJ, the J-domain and the 
                     central domain of DjlA, but not the transmembrane domain, 
                     are required for complementation of DnaJ function |CITS: 
                     [11544239]|.  <i>In vitro</i>, a cytoplasmic fragment of 
                     DjlA can stimulate the ATPase activity of DnaK in a way 
                     similar to DnaJ and has co-chaperone activity together with
                     DnaK |CITS: [11106641]|.
                     One study showed that a <i>djlA</i> null mutant has 
                     envelope and growth defects and accumulates suppressor 
                     mutations quickly |CITS: [8809778]|, while a second study 
                     found that a <i>djlA</i> null mutant only has a subtle 
                     defect in the onset of mucoidy |CITS: [9364917]|.  A 
                     <i>djlA dnaJ</i> double mutant can not grow above 37&deg;C 
                     |CITS: [11544239]|.  In a <i>dnaJ</i> mutant, both DjlA and
                     CbpA are required for solubilization of protein aggregates 
                     |CITS: [15489435]|. Strong overproduction of DjlA is lethal
                     |CITS: [9364917]|, while moderate overproduction increases 
                     sensitivity to novobiocin and W7, an anticalmodulin |CITS: 
                     [9819058]|.  <i>djlA</i> mRNA is very unstable and is 
                     either induced or stabilized by cold shock |CITS: 
                     [9364917]|.
                     DjlA: ""<b>D</b>na<b>J</b>-<b>l</b>ike <b>A</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [8809778]|"
