     gene            complement(60358..63264)
                     /gene="rapA"
                     /locus_tag="b0059"
                     /label=rapA
     CDS             complement(60358..63264)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rapA"
                     /locus_tag="b0059"
                     /product="RNA polymerase-binding ATPase and RNAP recycling
                     factor"
                     /label=rapA
                     /note="RapA enables recycling of stalled RNA polymerases
                     (RNAPs) and stimulates RNA synthesis <i>in vitro</i> |CITS:
                     [11751638][18786404]|. The location of the RapA binding 
                     site on RNAP suggests that the ATPase activity of RapA 
                     energizes the backward translocation of RNAP along the DNA 
                     template in an elongation complex |CITS: [25646438]|. RapA 
                     is involved in the transcription cycle through aiding in 
                     transcription termination and RNAP reuse |CITS: 
                     [37406096]|.
                     RapA is a distantly related member of the SNF2 family of 
                     proteins |CITS: [8382805][7651832][9025290]|.
                     RapA interacts with |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX| with 1:1 
                     stoichiometry |CITS: [9614128][10801781]|; this interaction
                     activates the ATPase activity of RapA |CITS: 
                     [9507009][10801781]| allosterically |CITS: [26272746]|. The
                     N-terminal domain of RapA inhibits its ATPase activity 
                     |CITS: [26272746]|. RapA and &sigma;<sup>70</sup> compete 
                     for binding to core RNAP |CITS: [9614128][18786404]| 
                     because their binding sites overlap |CITS: 
                     [25646438][26272746]|. Initial reports that RapA is able to
                     bind RNAP holoenzyme |CITS: [9507009][10801781]| are now 
                     attributed to the presence of core RNAP in the preparation 
                     |CITS: [18786404]|. RapA enables polyadenylation of 
                     RNAP-associated RNA by |FRAME: EG10690-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [21299217]|.
                     Single-molecule fluorescence experiments with N-terminal 
                     His-tagged RapA revealed RapA binding to the RNA polymerase
                     core enzyme that is itself bound nonspecifically to 
                     double-stranded DNA in a kinetically stable post 
                     termination complex (PTC) and removing RNAP from DNA via 
                     its ATPase activity |CITS: [37406096]|. Crystal and 
                     solution structures of RapA alone |CITS: 
                     [18786404][26272746]| and bound to RNAP |CITS: 
                     [25646438][26272746]| have been solved. A spacer domain of 
                     RapA is inserted into the RNA exit channel of RNAP, 
                     narrowing the channel and making it unable to accommodate 
                     the formation of RNA hairpins |CITS: [25646438]|. Cryo-EM 
                     studies of RapA complexed with the RNAP elongation complex 
                     revealed conformational changes of RapA's N-terminal domain
                     (RapA-NTD) that increases access to the RapA activity site,
                     which then enhances ATPase activity; the RapA-NTD 
                     conformational changes also results in contact with the 
                     zinc binding domain of RNAP resulting in the RNAP clamp 
                     closing. RapA complexed with RNAP in a post-termination 
                     complex (RNAP-PTC) along with DNA binding studies revealed 
                     that RapA reduces nonspecific of DNA and RNAP |CITS: 
                     [34774797]|. Using single-molecule assays, RapA was 
                     observed to remove RNAP only from positively-supercoiled 
                     DNA by ""backtracking"" towards the promoter, possibly by 
                     applying torque; RNAP stalled on negatively-supercoiled DNA
                     is not removed by RapA. Inhibition of RapA's ability to 
                     remove RNAP from stalled DNA by the |FRAME: 
                     EG11578-MONOMER| supports the backtracking mechanism |CITS:
                     [35819188]|. Cryo-EM analyses of reconstituted RNAP-PTC 
                     associated with negatively supercoiled DNA and 'trapped' 
                     RapA bound to ADP analog found that the ADP analog promotes
                     conformational changes in RapA that in turn induces large 
                     conformational changes in RNAP causing the DNA to be 
                     released. RapA also controls the RNAP-PTC induced R-loop 
                     formations in the DNA |CITS: [39779919]|.
                     <i>rapA</i> mutants exhibit a growth defect under high salt
                     conditions on solid medium |CITS: [11751638][19580329]| and
                     display reduced RNA polyadenylation |CITS: [21299217]|. 
                     Site-directed mutants in the SWI/SNF domain alter the 
                     ATPase activity of RapA |CITS: [19580329]|. Reports 
                     disagree on whether <i>rapA</i> mutants do |CITS: 
                     [9614128]| or do not |CITS: [10801781]| show increased UV 
                     sensitivity. Biofilms formed by a <i>rapA</i> mutant are 
                     more sensitive to penicillin G than wild type |CITS: 
                     [17664315]|.
                     Transcription of <i>rapA</i> is growth rate-dependent 
                     |CITS: [11567013]|. The <i>rapA</i> gene is not part of the
                     LexA regulon |CITS: [1577702]|.
                     HepA: ""<b>he</b>licaselike <b>p</b>rotein"" |CITS: 
                     [1577702]|
                     RapA: ""<b>R</b>NA polymerase (RNAP)-<b>a</b>ssociated 
                     <b>p</b>rotein with <b>A</b>TPase activity"" |CITS: 
                     [9507009]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [18786392][21419241]|"
