     gene            complement(63429..65780)
                     /gene="polB"
                     /locus_tag="b0060"
                     /label=polB
     CDS             complement(63429..65780)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="polB"
                     /locus_tag="b0060"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA polymerase II / DNA polymerase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.7"
                     /label=polB
                     /note="DNA polymerase II (Pol II) is a combined polymerase
                     and exonuclease involved in replication restart following 
                     UV exposure, translesion synthesis and nucleotide excision 
                     repair.
                     Pol II catalyzes both a DNA polymerase activity and a 3' to
                     5' exonuclease activity |CITS: [4927672][1581388]|. Pol II 
                     is also required for rapid restart of DNA replication 
                     following UV irradiation |CITS: [11929519][10430924]|. In 
                     addition to its role in translesion bypass, discussed 
                     below, Pol II is required for a nucleotide excision repair 
                     pathway that fixes DNA crosslinks |CITS: [10217781]|. Pol 
                     II also plays a role in surviving thymine dimers and 
                     avoiding the mutagenic effects of agents such as peroxide 
                     |CITS: [11809372][7928992]|.
                     Pol II tends to make more errors in replication of AT-rich 
                     sequences, which differs from the behavior of most other 
                     DNA polymerases |CITS: [11733526]|.
                     Pol II allows replication to bypass some kinds of DNA 
                     lesions. Under certain conditions Pol II is required for 
                     bypass of abasic lesions |CITS: [7908652][8798733]|. This 
                     bypass increases when Pol II exonuclease activity is 
                     disabled |CITS: [8798733]|. Pol II can also bypass 
                     3,N(4)-ethenocytosine lesions and carries out mutagenic 
                     bypass of 2-acetylaminofluorene-induced lesions, the latter
                     resulting in -2 frameshifts |CITS: 
                     [16171831][11080171][11356152]|. Pol II and Pol V appear to
                     compete directly to bypass AAF lesion sites |CITS: 
                     [11447256]|. Pol II has also been implicated in bypass of 
                     crosslinking lesions |CITS: [16257273]|.
                     The exonuclease function of Pol II appears to be located in
                     its amino-terminal region |CITS: [8182073]|. A crystal 
                     structure of all of Pol II has been determined to 3 &Aring;
                     resolution |CITS: [8145251]|.
                     <i>In vitro</i>, the DNA polymerase III gamma clamp loader 
                     complex and beta clamp stimulate Pol II activity on ssDNA 
                     template, increasing its processivity several hundred fold 
                     |CITS: [1999435][1534562]|.
                     In mutants lacking Pol II exonuclease activity, mutation 
                     increases, including adaptive mutation |CITS: 
                     [9023362][7644519]|. These exonuclease-deficient strains 
                     especially experience mutations in the lagging strand and 
                     suffer a synergestic increase in mutation in strains with 
                     mutator versions of DNA polymerase III |CITS: [16164549]|. 
                     In mutants lacking the DNA polymerase III gamma complex, 
                     the presence of Pol II is lethal |CITS: [14507374]|.
                     Pol II is regulated by the SOS response and is induced by 
                     the
                     SOS-inducing agents nalidixic acid, peroxide and ethanol 
                     |CITS: [2228959][10522385]|. Pol II fidelity may also 
                     increase during induction of the SOS response |CITS: 
                     [16257273]|. The presence of mitomycin C (MMC) induces 
                     <i>polB</i> gene expression, and RpoS is necessary for the 
                     full expression of it while BarA is only partially 
                     responsible for this induction |CITS:[ 28468910]|."
