     gene            complement(65855..66532)
                     /gene="araD"
                     /locus_tag="b0061"
                     /label=araD
     CDS             complement(65855..66532)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="araD"
                     /locus_tag="b0061"
                     /product="bifunctional L-ribulose-5-phosphate 4-epimerase
                     AraD / L-ribulose-5-phosphate 4-epimerase"
                     /EC_number="5.1.3.4"
                     /label=araD
                     /note="L-ribulose 5-phosphate 4-epimerase catalyzes the
                     third step in the pathway for degradation of L-arabinose, 
                     the epimerization at the C4 carbon to form 
                     D-xylulose-5-phosphate, which enters the |FRAME: 
                     NONOXIPENT-PWY|.
                     The enzyme catalyzes epimerization via a carbon-carbon bond
                     cleavage analogous to a class II aldolase reaction |CITS: 
                     [9548961][10769138]|.  The H97N and other mutants can 
                     catalyze an aldolase reaction |CITS: 
                     [9548961][10769139][11732896]|.  The binding site for 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup>, the physiological metal cofactor, has been
                     identified by sequence similarity and mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [10769139]| as well as in the crystal structure |CITS: 
                     [11732895]|.  Mutagenesis of the predicted 
                     phosphate-binding pocket results in a significant increase 
                     in K<sub>m</sub>, and Asp120 was identified as one of two 
                     key catalytic acid/base residues |CITS: [11732896]|.
                     The enzyme was crystallized |CITS: [8520491]|, and crystal 
                     structures of the wild type |CITS: [11732895]| and a mutant
                     enzyme |CITS: [11732896]| have been solved. Consistent with
                     its aldolase-type reaction mechanism, the structure shows 
                     close overall similarity to that of L-fuculose-1-phosphate 
                     aldolase |CITS: [11732895]|.
                     (Note: Several of the recent studies, including 
                     crystallization of the enzyme, appear to be using the 
                     enzyme from <i>E. coli</i> B/r UP1000 (gene originally 
                     cloned by |CITS: [3549454]|) |CITS: 
                     [10769138][10769139][11732895][11732896]|.)  Most studies 
                     of the enzyme and its genetics were done in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     B/r.
                     Earlier studies of the enzyme from <i>E. coli</i> B/r 
                     described its purification |CITS: [4879898]|, its catalytic
                     mechanism |CITS: [4560420]| and summarized its properties 
                     |CITS: [236468]|. The nucleotide sequence of the coding 
                     region of <i>araD</i> from <i>E. coli</i> K-12 W3110 was 
                     shown to be 97% identical to that of <i>E. coli</i> B/r, 
                     and the deduced amino acid sequences showed 98.7% identity 
                     |CITS: [2251150]|.
                     AraD from <i>E. coli</i> DH1 (a K-12 derivative) forms a 
                     stable homotetramer as determined by size exclusion 
                     chromatography |CITS: [9548961]|.
                     Metabolic engineering work in yeast has utilized <i>E. 
                     coli</i> <i>araD</i> and <i>araB</i>, and <i>Bacillus 
                     subtilis</i> or <i>B. licheniformis araA</i> in the 
                     fermentation of L-arabinose to ethanol |CITS: 
                     [12839792][18263741]|.
                     Transcription of the <i>araBAD</i> operon is induced in the
                     presence of L-arabinose by the transcription factor AraC 
                     |CITS: [1093171][7768852]|.  <i>araBAD</i> expression can 
                     also induced by L-lyxose |CITS: [10913097]|. <i>araD</i> 
                     mutants can not utilize L-arabinose for growth |CITS: 
                     [13829634]| and accumulate L-ribulose 5-phosphate when 
                     incubated with L-arabinose |CITS: [13890280]|."
