     gene            complement(72003..72095)
                     /gene="sroA"
                     /locus_tag="b4762"
                     /label=sroA
     misc_RNA        complement(72003..72095)
                     /gene="sroA"
                     /locus_tag="b4762"
                     /product="small RNA SroA"
                     /note="The SroA RNA was found by a shotgun cloning
                     approach and overlaps with a predicted riboswitch element 
                     in the 5' UTR of |FRAME: EG11574 <i>thiB</i>|.  SroA may 
                     therefore be generated by attenuation of <i>thiBPQ</i> 
                     transcription in rapidly growing cells.  The half life of 
                     SroA in exponentially growing cells is 4 minutes |CITS: 
                     [14602901]|.
                     The <i>sgrR</i> and <i>thiB</i> genes and their intergenic 
                     region are conserved between <i>E. coli</i> and 
                     <i>Salmonella</i> species |CITS: [14602901]|.
                     Likely functional regions of SroA were identified; of the 
                     predicted mRNA targets, testing by electrophoretic mobility
                     shift assay confirmed an interaction with the <i>rhtA</i> 
                     and <i>rplK</i> mRNAs |CITS: [30287822]|. SroA: 
                     ""<b>s</b>mall <b>R</b>N<b>o</b>mics RNA <b>A</b> |CITS: 
                     [14602901]|"
     gene            complement(72131..73786)
                     /gene="sgrR"
                     /locus_tag="b0069"
                     /label=sgrR
     CDS             complement(72131..73786)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sgrR"
                     /locus_tag="b0069"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional dual regulator SgrR"
                     /label=sgrR
                     /note="""""<b>S</b>u<b>g</b>a<b>R</b> transport-related 
                     <b>R</b>egulator"""" SgrR is negatively autoregulated and 
                     coordinately activates transcription of the divergent 
                     operon. This regulator coordinates the response to 
                     glucose-phosphate stress |CITS: 
                     [17209026][15522088][17383224]|. As the intracellular 
                     accumulation of glucose-6-phosphate correlates with SgrR 
                     induction, it has been proposed as its inducer |CITS: 
                     [17209026][15522088][17383224]|. Autoregulation of SgrR is 
                     not affected by phosphosugar stress |CITS: [17209026]|. In 
                     addition, the induction of <i>sgrS</i> expression by the 
                     nonmetabolizable glucose PTS substrate (+/-)-methyl 
                     glucoside is abolished in a <i>sgrR</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [15522088]|.
                     The SgrR protein is a member of a novel family of 
                     regulators and contains an N terminal which is 
                     characteristic of winged-helix DNA-binding transcriptional 
                     regulators and a predicted C-terminal solute-binding domain
                     |CITS: [17209026]|.
                     Inhibition <i>in vivo</i> of SgrR by glutamate has been 
                     shown |CITS:[31578326]|.
                     The SgrR binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"""">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""""http://boltznet.bu.edu"""">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
