     gene            73854..74080
                     /gene="sgrS"
                     /locus_tag="b4577"
                     /label=sgrS
     misc_RNA        73854..74080
                     /gene="sgrS"
                     /locus_tag="b4577"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA SgrS"
                     /note="The small RNA SgrS post-transcriptionally regulates 
                     abundance of |FRAME: PTSG-MONOMER PtsG|, the major glucose 
                     transporter. Expression of SgrS is sufficient to inhibit 
                     translation and cause loss of <i>ptsG</i> mRNA, and thus 
                     loss of the glucose-specific phosphoenolpyruvate 
                     phosphotransferase system (PTS), which enables transport of
                     glucose into the cytoplasm and its concurrent 
                     phosphorylation to glucose-6-phosphate |CITS: [15522088]|. 
                     The secondary glucose transporter ManXYZ was also 
                     identified as a target of SgrS regulation |CITS: 
                     [21245045]|. Two SgrS binding sites, one in the <i>manX</i>
                     ORF and one in the <i>manX-manY</i> intergenic region, are 
                     involved in independent translational repression of the 
                     <i>manX</i> and <i>manYZ</i> gene products |CITS: 
                     [22988087]|. A third target, whose translation is 
                     positively regulated, is the sugar phosphatase YigL |CITS: 
                     [23873911]|. SgrS regulation of different subsets of its 
                     targets was shown to be required to cope with different 
                     stress conditions |CITS: [23873911]|. Additional regulatory
                     targets of SgrS, <i>asd</i>, <i>adiY</i>, <i>folE</i> and 
                     <i>purR</i>, aid recovery from glucose-phosphate stress 
                     |CITS: [26411266]|. SgrS is part of the response to 
                     glucose-phosphate stress. Evidence suggests that the 
                     metabolic root of this stress is the depletion of 
                     glycolytic intermediates |CITS: [23995640]|.
                     The interaction of SgrS with Hfq has been demonstrated 
                     |CITS: [36328526]|.
                     The binding of SgrS to its mRNA targets <i>in vivo</i> is 
                     dynamic and reversible. Formation of SgrS-mRNA complexes is
                     rate-limiting and determines the overall regulation 
                     efficiency of each individual target |CITS: [25792329]|. 
                     The SgrS glucose-phosphate stress regulatory network was 
                     analyzed by single molecule super-resolution microscopy, 
                     resulting in a kinetic model that includes transcriptional 
                     regulation, replication and RNA silencing |CITS: 
                     [33425989]|. The stability of the sRNA-mRNA duplexes, the 
                     relative location of the base pairing sites, and the mode 
                     of Hfq binding influence the regulatory hierarchy |CITS: 
                     [31340077]|. A detailed analysis of the interactions of 
                     SgrS mutants with Hfq and the <i>ptsG</i> target mRNA 
                     showed that SgrS-mRNA annealing is directly facilitated by 
                     Hfq |CITS: [33558533]|.
                     SgrS inhibits translation of <i>ptsG</i> mRNA both directly
                     and indirectly. A 14 nucleotide base-pairing region at the 
                     3' end of SgrS is necessary and sufficient for binding to 
                     the Shine-Dalgarno region of <i>ptsG</i> mRNA |CITS: 
                     [20345651]|, forming a stable duplex. Six critical 
                     nucleotides in the Shine-Dalgarno region of <i>ptsG</i> are
                     particularly important for this binding, as is the action 
                     of Hfq |CITS: [16859494][17264113]|  Base pairing of SgrS 
                     with <i>ptsG</i> mRNA is necessary and sufficient for 
                     translational silencing; the role of Hfq is to stimulate 
                     duplex formation |CITS: [18650387]|, possibly by 
                     influencing a negative interaction of SgrS |CITS: 
                     [20345651]|. The destabilization of <i>ptsG</i> mRNA by 
                     SgrS depends on Hfq |CITS: [15650111][16166379]|, likely 
                     due to Hfq's ability to recruit RNase E |CITS: 
                     [16166379][16549791]|. The polyU tail and a hairpin region 
                     of SgrS are required for interaction with Hfq and for the 
                     Hfq-dependent regulatory function |CITS: 
                     [21788484][22454537]|; the minimal Hfq-binding module has 
                     been defined |CITS: [22454537]|.  SgrS regulation is 
                     dependent on the localization of the <i>ptsG</i> mRNA to 
                     the inner membrane and insertion of the nascent PtsG 
                     peptide into the membrane |CITS: [15650111]|.
                     SgrS was found to regulate the translation of <i>manX</i> 
                     by facilitating the interaction of Hfq with a site near the
                     ribosome binding site, which then inhibits initiation of 
                     translation |CITS: [29294046]|. Repression of <i>manY</i> 
                     translation by SgrS is due to interfering with binding of 
                     ribosomal protein S1 to a translation enhancer within the 
                     5' UTR of the <i>manY</i> mRNA |CITS: [32291821]|. 
                     Activation of <i>yigL</i> expression by SgrS appears to be 
                     due to blocking RNase E from scanning past the 
                     SgrS-<i>yigL</i> mRNA duplex for its cleavage site |CITS: 
                     [30852060]|.
                     It has been demonstrated that SgrS is also able to regulate
                     at the transcriptional level, to the same targets that 
                     regulate at the translational level, and that this 
                     regulation depends on Rho and Rut sites in the target 
                     |CITS: [34592145]|.
                     The 5' end of SgrS contains a 43 amino acid open reading 
                     frame, |FRAME: G0-10617|, which is translated under 
                     conditions of glucose-phosphate stress and regulates the 
                     activity of PtsG |CITS: [18042713]|.
                     Synthesis of SgrS is induced by the non-metabolizable 
                     glucose PTS substrate &alpha;-methyl glucoside (&alpha;-MG)
                     when glucose phosphate accumulates via the transcriptional 
                     regulator SgrR |CITS: [15522088]| and appears to be 
                     regulated by the stringent response |CITS: [28986375]|. 
                     Direct or indirect induction of the Pho regulon partially 
                     suppresses the defect in recovery from glucose-phosphate 
                     stress of an <i>sgrS</i> mutant |CITS: [22427626]|. The 
                     &alpha;-MG growth defect of an <i>sgrS</i> mutant can be 
                     rescued by the addition of glycolytic compounds like 
                     glucose-6-phosphate and fructose-6-phosphate and is 
                     exacerbated by the addition of pyruvate, which causes cell 
                     lysis |CITS: [23995640]|. Unlike in <i>E. coli</i> B, SgrS 
                     expression is not induced by growth on high levels of 
                     glucose in K-12 strains |CITS: [20920177]|. Overexpression 
                     of SgrS in a K-12 strain reduces secretion of acetate 
                     during growth on glucose |CITS: [22107968]| and reduces the
                     growth rate |CITS: [25506402]|. The half life of SgrS is 
                     significantly reduced in <i>pnp</i> and <i>hfq</i> mutants 
                     |CITS: [21527671]|. Conversely, in <i>pnp hfq</i> double 
                     mutants, levels of a slightly shorter form of SgrS increase
                     |CITS: [22355164]|. Overexpression of SgrS in cells grown 
                     on glucose as the sole source of carbon induces <i>IS50</i>
                     transposase expression and <i>Tn5</i> transposition, 
                     apparently due to metabolic stress |CITS: [25506402]|. 
                     Overexpression of SgrS increases biofilm formation and 
                     decreases swarming motility and curli expression |CITS: 
                     [26469694]|.
                     SgrS activity is also regulated at the level of 
                     transcription termination. In normally growing cells, 
                     frequent read-through at the Rho-independent terminator is 
                     observed; this longer form of SgrS does not bind Hfq. 
                     Stress conditions enhance termination at the 
                     Rho-independent terminator |CITS: [26106215]|. The 
                     structure of the terminator appears to be optimal for 
                     generation of functional SgrS |CITS: [28606943]|. Synthetic
                     modified SgrS small RNAs have been engineered to enable 
                     different levels of repression of target genes |CITS: 
                     [31132322]|.
                     Single mutation of <i>sgrS</i> negatively affects the 
                     cellular growth when methyl-alpha-D-glucopyranoside 
                     (&alpha;-MG) is used as a carbon source, but in a double 
                     mutant <i>sgrS</i>-<i>sibE</i>, the <i>sibE</i> mutant 
                     appears to suppress the effect of a <i>sgrS</i> mutant on 
                     growth  |CITS: [35920556]|. Many other mutants also 
                     suppress the effect of the <i>sgrS</i> mutant, such as 
                     <i>ptsG</i>, <i>ptsI</i>, and <i>pgi</i>, among others 
                     |CITS: [35920556]|.
                     SgrS: ""<b>s</b>u<b>g</b>a<b>r</b> transport-related 
                     <b>s</b>RNA"" |CITS: [15522088]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17383224][17383928][18981470][20662934][2 
                     1816203][21843668][23392245][24847473][28289086][30191807][
                     31249814][32213244]|<br> Comment: |CITS: [32367584]|"
     gene            73875..74006
                     /gene="sgrT"
