                     /label=sgrT
     CDS             73875..74006
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sgrT"
                     /locus_tag="b4662"
                     /product="PtsG glucose transporter inhibitor"
                     /label=sgrT
                     /note="<i>sgrT</i> is a conserved open reading frame
                     located within the 5' end of the small RNA gene 
                     <i>sgrS</i>; <i>sgrT</i> is translated under 
                     sugar-phosphate stress (simulated by exposure of cells to 
                     &alpha;-methyl glucoside) and plays a role in the 
                     glucose-phosphate stress response. Ectopic expression of 
                     SgrT inhibits glucose transport and can promote recovery 
                     from sugar-phosphate stress |CITS: [18042713]|; however, it
                     is not capable of rescue when produced from its native 
                     ribosome-binding site |CITS: [19620214]|. Production of 
                     SgrT in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 appears to be low compared to 
                     that in <i>Salmonella</i> Typhimurium; a putative hairpin 
                     structure formed in the translation initiation region of 
                     <i>sgrT</i> may be responsible for repressing translation 
                     |CITS: [19620214]|.
                     A single amino acid substitution (P384R) in the linker 
                     region of |FRAME: PTSG-MONOMER PtsG| (EIIBC<sup>glc</sup>) 
                     suppresses the growth inhibition phenotype associated with 
                     SgrT overproduction |CITS: [21621292]|; SgrT interacts with
                     the unphosphorylated PtsG in crosslinking assays and with a
                     truncated 'EIIC-linker' derivative in fluorescence 
                     complementation assay; SgrT localizes to the membrane in 
                     <i>ptsG</i><sup>+</sup> cells but is present in the 
                     cytoplasm in a &Delta;<i>ptsG</i> strain |CITS: 
                     [24957761]|.
                     Ectopic expression of SgrT inhibits growth on minimal 
                     glucose medium but does not affect growth on mannose, 
                     fructose, trehalose nor on N-acetylglcosamine minimal 
                     medium; SgrT specifically inhibits transport activity of 
                     PtsG; strong inhibition requires the IIC domain of PtsG 
                     |CITS: [28289085]|. Ectopic expression of SgrT promotes a 
                     growth advantage in &Delta;<i>sgrS</i> cells grown in the 
                     presence of &alpha;-methyl glucoside and lactose |CITS: 
                     [28289085]|.
                     SgrT does not appear to be expressed under LB growth 
                     conditions, but can be detected after addition of 
                     &alpha;-methylglucoside (&alpha;MG) |CITS: [19121005]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [18077354][21111023][21621292][21816203][2 
                     4847473][30191807][30810554]|<BR>Comments: |CITS: 
                     [18077354][28289086]|"
