     CDS             83844..84335
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ilvH"
                     /locus_tag="b0078"
                     /product="trifunctional acetolactate synthase / 
                     acetohydroxybutanoate synthase, regulatory subunit / 
                     acetolactate synthase / acetohydroxybutanoate synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.2.1.6"
                     /label=ilvH
                     /note="IlvH is the small regulatory subunit of the
                     bifunctional acetohydroxybutanoate synthase /acetolactate 
                     synthase (IlvI/H, AHAS III) which carries out both the 
                     first step in valine biosynthesis and the second step in 
                     isoleucine biosynthesis. The IlvI/H protein complex 
                     catalyzes the conversion of pyruvate and oxobutanoate into 
                     2-aceto-2-hydroxy-butyrate and the conversion of pyruvate 
                     into 2-acetolactate. Both reactions generate carbon dioxide
                     as a product. |CITS: 
                     [4612003][2675968][3301814][8756689][1512191]|
                     IlvH confers sensitivity to inhibition by valine and is 
                     required for full catalytic activity of acetolactate 
                     synthase III holoenzyme |CITS: [8756689]|.
                     The crystal structure of IlvH has been determined at 1.75 
                     &Aring; resolution.  It forms a dimer with two ferredoxin 
                     domains in each monomer. The valine binding sites can be 
                     tentatively assigned to the interface between the two 
                     N-terminal domains |CITS: [16458324]|.
                     C-terminal and N-terminal <i>ilvH</i> mutants were 
                     constructed and used to determine the minimum activation 
                     peptide necessary to activate IlvI |CITS: [23512804]|. 
                     Mutagenesis of the C-terminal domain of IlvH prevented 
                     interdomain contacts resulting in uninhibited activity and 
                     low affinity for valine |CITS: [21224018]|.
                     Interactions between large and small subunits of different 
                     AHAS isozymes were investigated. IlvM, instead of IlvH, 
                     could be used to activate the catalytic subunit of IlvI 
                     |CITS: [19653643]|.
                     Abundance of IlvH decreased in response to nitrosative 
                     stress |CITS: [35427827]|."
