                     /label=ftsL
     CDS             87519..87884
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftsL"
                     /locus_tag="b0083"
                     /product="bifunctional cell division protein FtsL / FtsLBQ
                     cell division complex"
                     /label=ftsL
                     /note="FtsL is an essential cell division protein that is 
                     present in very small amounts of approximately 25 molecules
                     per cell |CITS: [1332942][1656499]|. Localization of FtsL 
                     to the septal ring is dependent on FtsZ, FtsA, ZipA, FtsK 
                     and FtsQ, but not FtsI |CITS: 
                     [10027987][11703663][11948172]|.  Conversely, FtsL is 
                     required for localization of FtsI to the Z ring |CITS: 
                     [9882665]|.  Although the hierarchy of dependency in the 
                     assembly of cell division proteins is largely linear, 
                     results have shown that assembly of the cell division 
                     machinery is complex |CITS: [16824093]|.
                     FtsL is an integral membrane protein with a small 
                     N-terminal cytoplasmic domain and a large periplasmic 
                     domain |CITS: [1656499][1332942]|.  Both the cytoplasmic 
                     domain and membrane spanning regions are essential for the 
                     role of FtsL in cell division |CITS: [9260951]|; the 
                     cytoplasmic domain is involved in recruiting downstream 
                     cell division proteins |CITS: [20363951]|.
                     The membrane-spanning and periplasmic regions of FtsL are 
                     required for localization to the division septum |CITS: 
                     [10613870]|.  The periplasmic domain contains a leucine 
                     zipper motif |CITS: [1332942]|, which is required for 
                     optimal interaction between FtsL and FtsB |CITS: 
                     [21784946]|.  Mutations in the leucine zipper motif region 
                     affect localization and function of FtsL |CITS: 
                     [10613870]|.  Localization of FtsB and FtsL to the 
                     constriction site is codependent |CITS: [11972052]|.  The 
                     transmembrane helices of FtsB and FtsL appear to be major 
                     contributors to the stability of a 1:1 higher-order 
                     oligomeric complex between FtsB and FtsL |CITS: 
                     [24083359]|.  FtsQ, FtsL and FtsB form a complex <i>in 
                     vivo</i> even in the absence of FtsK and thus independently
                     of their localization to the septal region |CITS: 
                     [15165235]|.  The periplasmic coiled-coil domain, including
                     specific polar amino acids, is required for the interaction
                     of FtsQ with the FtsL/FtsB subcomplex |CITS: 
                     [20363951][34871549][35798142]|. Based on mutational 
                     studies extensive interactions within the periplasmic 
                     domain were found to extend transpeptidase domain of FtsI 
                     away from the membrane; FtsN was also found to bind between
                     FtsI and FtsL helping to link FtsI to FtsL |CITS: 
                     [37524712]|.
                     In a <i>zntA</i> zinc exporter mutant, overexpression of 
                     FtsL increases the sensitivity of the cells to zinc |CITS: 
                     [21708137]|.
                     Selected reviews: |CITS: [20943430][15491352][12626683]|"
