     gene            87900..89666
                     /gene="ftsI"
                     /locus_tag="b0084"
                     /label=ftsI
     CDS             87900..89666
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftsI"
                     /locus_tag="b0084"
                     /product="bifunctional peptidoglycan DD-transpeptidase
                     FtsI / peptidoglycan D,D-transpeptidase"
                     /label=ftsI
                     /note="FtsI (penicillin-binding protein 3, PBP3) is
                     believed to be the primary peptidoglycan (PG) 
                     transpeptidase which functions together with the SEDS 
                     family protein |FRAME: EG10344-MONOMER FtsW| to synthesize 
                     septal peptidoglycan (sPG) during cell division; FtsW-FtsI 
                     are the SEDS-bPBP pair of the divisome. The spatiotemporal 
                     coordination of sPG synthesis in <i>E. coli</i> is 
                     currently described by a 'two-track' model (see |CITS: 
                     [38821027]|).
                     FtsI is an essential cell division protein |CITS: 
                     [1103132]| which is present at low abundance (~100 
                     molecules per cell) |CITS: [9379897]|. Binding of 
                     beta-lactam antibiotics to FtsI inhibits FtsI activity and 
                     is lethal |CITS: [3902760]|. FtsI localizes to the division
                     site; localization is dependent on FtsZ, FtsA, FtsQ, FtsL 
                     and FtsW, but not |FRAME: EG10341-MONOMER FtsN| |CITS: 
                     [9379897][9603865][9882665][11703663][11807049]|. 
                     Subcomplexes of class 2 penicillin binding proteins with 
                     SEDS (shape, elongation, division and sporulation) family 
                     proteins are proposed to be the primary peptidoglycan 
                     synthases in cell elongation and division |CITS: 
                     [27525505]|. FtsI and FtsW (a member of the SEDS family) 
                     may function as the transpeptidase and glycosyltransferase 
                     respectively, for peptidoglycan synthesis within the 
                     divisome |CITS: [27643381]|. FtsI regulates the interaction
                     of FtsW with lipid II; a ternary complex of FtsW-FtsI-MrcB 
                     is the core enzyme of the divisome which interacts with 
                     lipid II substrate |CITS: [28233869]|. Directional movement
                     of FtsI during septal peptidoglycan formation is guided by 
                     FtsZ treadmilling |CITS: [28209899]| (and further |CITS: 
                     [33495624][33504807]|). High-resolution imaging suggests 
                     that the Z-ring proteins and the peptidoglycan-synthesizing
                     machinery are spatially separated during envelope 
                     constriction |CITS: [29193432]| (and further |CITS: 
                     [36180460]|). |FRAME: EG10341-MONOMER FtsN| and |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7934 FtsQLB| are implicated in a mechanism for 
                     activating the septal peptidoglycan synthase FtsWI |CITS: 
                     [37524712]|.
                     FtsI contains a small N-terminal cytoplasmic domain, a 
                     transmembrane helix and a C-terminal periplasmic region 
                     that can be separated into a noncatalytic and a catalytic 
                     domain |CITS: [2677607][9614966]|. The cytoplasmic domain 
                     and transmembrane helix are essential for its role in cell 
                     division |CITS: [9260951][8631709]|. The transmembrane 
                     helix is necessary and sufficient for localization of FtsI 
                     to the Z ring |CITS: [9882665][14702319][15601716]|. The 
                     noncatalytic periplasmic domain is required for recruitment
                     of FtsN |CITS: [14702319]|. The catalytic C-terminal domain
                     contains the transpeptidase activity and is involved in 
                     peptidoglycan synthesis at the division septum |CITS: 
                     [6450748][3531167][9260951]|. Constriction of the Z ring 
                     during cell division requires the transpeptidase activity 
                     of FtsI |CITS: [9012823]|. The C-terminal 349 amino acids 
                     contain the penicillin-binding region |CITS: [6092133]|. A 
                     fraction of FtsI molecules are modified with glycerol and 
                     fatty acids |CITS: [3053665]|. Overproduction of FtsI 
                     suppresses the filamentous phenotype of strains with 
                     mutations in <i>ftsI</i> and <i>ftsH</i> |CITS: [3316193]|.
                     Inactivation of FtsI by binding of beta-lactam antibiotics 
                     or mutagenesis induces the SOS response via the DpiBA 
                     two-component signal transduction system. The resulting 
                     cell division arrest may enable survival of the cells 
                     despite exposure to otherwise lethal antibiotics |CITS: 
                     [15308764]|.
                     Transcription of peptidoglycan biosynthesis genes, 
                     including <i>ftsI</i>, is regulated by |FRAME: 
                     EG10977-MONOMER| |CITS: [36308522]|.
                     Selected reviews: |CITS: [15491352][12626683][9614966][354 
                     08901][37524757][38574821]|<BR>Comments: |CITS: 
                     [30808703]|"
