     gene            94890..96134
                     /gene="ftsW"
                     /locus_tag="b0089"
                     /label=ftsW
     CDS             94890..96134
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftsW"
                     /locus_tag="b0089"
                     /product="bifunctional peptidoglycan glycosyltransferase
                     FtsW / peptidoglycan glycosyltransferase"
                     /label=ftsW
                     /note="The SEDS family protein FtsW is is believed to be
                     the primary peptidoglycan (PG) glycosyltransferase which 
                     functions together with its cognate transpeptidase |FRAME: 
                     EG10341-MONOMER ""FtsI""| (PBP3) to synthesize septal 
                     peptidoglycan (sPG) during cell division; FtsW-FtsI are the
                     SEDS-bPBP pair of the divisome. The spatiotemporal 
                     coordination of sPG synthesis in <i>E. coli</i> is 
                     currently described by a 'two-track' model (see |CITS: 
                     [38821027]|).
                     FtsW is an essential cell division protein that is 
                     conserved in most walled bacteria; the protein is produced 
                     at low levels and localizes to the septum |CITS: 
                     [7961485][9218774][9603865]|. FtsW is an integral membrane 
                     protein belonging to the SEDS (shape, elongation, division,
                     sporulation) family; it is predicted to consist of 10 
                     transmembrane (TM) segments with both the N- and C-terminal
                     end located in the cytoplasm |CITS: [12423747]|. 
                     Subcomplexes of SEDS proteins with class 2 penicillin 
                     binding proteins are proposed to be the primary 
                     peptidoglycan synthases in cell elongation and division 
                     |CITS: [27525505]|. FtsW and FtsI may function as the 
                     glycosyltransferase and transpeptidase respectively, for 
                     peptidoglycan synthesis within the divisome |CITS: 
                     [27643381]|.
                     FtsW is an essential septum-specific peptidoglycan 
                     glycosyltransferase <i>in vivo</i>; cells in which FtsW 
                     activity is inhibited appear unable to complete cell 
                     division |CITS: [33495624]|. FtsW displays directional 
                     movement during sPG synthesis; FtsW and FtsI move together 
                     as a complex; a |FRAME: EG10347-MONOMER FtsZ|-independent, 
                     slow-moving FtsWI synthase is active in sPG synthesis while
                     a FtsZ-dependent, fast-moving complex is inactive |CITS: 
                     [33495624]| (and further |CITS: [33504807]|). The mechanism
                     of activating FtsWI for septal PG synthesis remains an area
                     of active research (see |CITS: 
                     [28419603][36180460][37524712]|).
                     Neither FtsW nor a FtsW-FtsI complex are able to catalyse 
                     lipid II polymerization <i>in vitro</i>; the ternary 
                     complex FtsW-FtsI-|FRAME: CPLX0-3951 MrcB| is the core 
                     enzyme of the divisome which interacts with lipid II 
                     substrate; the interaction of FtsW with lipid II substrate 
                     is regulated by FtsI (PBP3) |CITS: [28233869]|.
                     FtsW is a candidate |FRAME: TRANS-RXN0-286 ""lipid II 
                     flippase""|; FtsW induces transbilayer movement of 
                     fluorophore-labeled lipid II in proteoliposomes and 
                     depletion of FtsW results in reduced Lipid II transport in 
                     isolated bacterial membranes |CITS: [21386816]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [24711460]|). Lipid II flippase function is retained
                     in strains lacking |FRAME: EG10607 <i>rodA</i>| and 
                     depleted for FtsW; |FRAME: G6561-MONOMER MurJ| is the lipid
                     II flippase in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [25013077]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [33495624]|).
                     Localization of FtsW is dependent on |FRAME: 
                     EG10347-MONOMER FtsZ|, |FRAME: EG10339-MONOMER FtsA|, 
                     |FRAME: EG10342-MONOMER FtsQ|, and |FRAME: EG11086-MONOMER 
                     FtsL| but not FtsI. FtsW is required for subsequent 
                     recruitment of FtsI to the cell division site |CITS: 
                     [11807049][16824093]|; artificially targeted FtsI can also 
                     ""back-recruit"" FtsW, and artificially targeted FtsW can 
                     ""back-recruit"" FtsQ, FtsL and FtsB |CITS: [16824093]|. 
                     FtsW interacts directly with FtsN |CITS: [20497333]| and 
                     forms a subcomplex with FtsI |CITS: [20847002]|. 
                     Overproduction of FtsW inhibits cell growth |CITS: 
                     [7961485]|.
                     The periplasmic loop between TM 9 and 10 is required for 
                     recruitment of FtsI to the septum, and certain amino acids 
                     in the periplasmic loop between TM 7 and 8 are essential 
                     for FtsW function |CITS: [15576787]|. The loop between TM 7
                     and 8 contributes to the interaction with FtsI but not with
                     MrcB (PBP1B) |CITS: [28233869]|.
                     FtsW has a role in stabilizing the Z ring |CITS: 
                     [7961485][9006034][9218774]|; FtsW is not required for FtsZ
                     localization to the division site |CITS: [11807049]|. 
                     Overproduction of FtsW can rescue the growth defect caused 
                     by high levels of L-serine in a strain that does not 
                     produce either of the three L-serine deaminases |CITS: 
                     [20729359]|.
                     Transcription of peptidoglycan biosynthesis genes, 
                     including <i>ftsW</i>, is regulated by |FRAME: 
                     EG10977-MONOMER| |CITS: [36308522]|.
                     <i>ftsW</i> has been the subject of a pilot study to 
                     examine genetic interactions involving essential genes 
                     |CITS: [24463526]|.
                     Selected reviews: |CITS: [15491352][12626683][26792999][28 
                     975672][35408901][37524757][38821027]|"
