     gene            96131..97198
                     /gene="murG"
                     /locus_tag="b0090"
                     /label=murG
     CDS             96131..97198
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="murG"
                     /locus_tag="b0090"
                     /product="N-acetylglucosaminyl transferase"
                     /EC_number="2.4.1.227"
                     /label=murG
                     /note="The <I>murG</I> gene codes for the
                     N-acetylglucosaminyl transferase responsible for the final 
                     intracellular step of peptidoglycan subunit assembly. The 
                     transferase is associated with the cytoplasmic face of the 
                     inner membrane. Therefore, the entire peptidoglycan subunit
                     is assembled before transport across the cytoplasmic 
                     membrane |CITS:[1649817][8449890]|.
                     The crystal structures of MurG by itself to a resolution of
                     1.9 &Aring; |CITS:[10892798]| and in a complex with its 
                     substrate UDP-GlcNAc to a resolution of 2.5 &Aring; 
                     |CITS:[12538870]| have been determined by X-ray 
                     crystallography.
                     A temperature sensitive MurG mutant fails to localize to 
                     cell-division sites resulting in cell lysis 
                     |CITS:[17640276]|. Mid-cell localization of MurG during 
                     cell division was dependent upon the presence of a mature 
                     divisome including the proteins FtsZ, PBP3, FtsW, FtsQ, 
                     MreC, and MreD, but did not depend upon MreB 
                     |CITS:[17640276]|. Translocation of Lipid II from the inner
                     to the outer leaflet of the inner membrane is not coupled 
                     to the activity of MurG, but rather is coupled to 
                     transglycosylation of the peptidoglycan subunits on the 
                     periplasmic face of the inner membrane |CITS:[17501931]|. 
                     Reviews:  |CITS:[12455415][11042447]|"
