     gene            98720..99640
                     /gene="ddlB"
                     /locus_tag="b0092"
                     /label=ddlB
     CDS             98720..99640
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ddlB"
                     /locus_tag="b0092"
                     /product="bifunctional D-alanine--D-alanine ligase B / 
                     D-alanine--D-alanine ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.3.2.4"
                     /label=ddlB
                     /note="DdlB is one of two D-alanine&mdash;D-alanine
                     ligases in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [1993184]|. 
                     D-alanine&mdash;D-alanine ligase, along with alanine 
                     racemase, makes up the D-alanine branch of peptidoglycan 
                     biosynthesis.  The enzyme combines two molecules of 
                     D-alanine to form D-alanyl-D-alanine, which is then added 
                     to the growing cell wall precursor.
                     DdlB belongs to the ATP grasp superfamily.  The enzyme is 
                     similar to VanA, a DD-ligase with modified substrate 
                     specificity that occurs in vancomycin-resistant enterococci
                     |CITS: [2266943][1998664]|. D-alanine&mdash;D-alanine 
                     ligase is an antibacterial drug target; it has long known 
                     to be the target of D-cycloserine |CITS: [14188160]|.  
                     Structural and functional studues revealed that it is the 
                     phosphorylated form of D-cycloserine that inhibits DdlB 
                     |CITS: [29208891]|.
                     Crystal structures of DdlB have been solved, and a reaction
                     mechanism was proposed |CITS: 
                     [7939684][7862655][9054558][20383009]|.  Kinetic analysis 
                     of mutants in predicted active site residues has allowed 
                     confirmation and refinement of the proposed reaction 
                     mechanism |CITS: [7893688]|.  The reaction involves rapid 
                     formation of an enzyme-bound D-alanyl phosphate 
                     intermediate |CITS: [10903933]|.  Several active site 
                     mutants have gained ligase functions similar to the VanA 
                     enzyme involved in vancomycin resistance of enterococci 
                     |CITS: [8756703]|.  Crystal structures of wild type and 
                     mutant DdlB in a complex with an inhibitor allowed insights
                     into substrate specificity and reasons for the weaker 
                     inhibition of VanA |CITS: [9054558]|.  Molecular dynamics 
                     simulations of the transition between the open and closed 
                     conformation enabled virtual screening and discovery of 
                     inhibitors of DdlB |CITS: [22803830]|.
                     The second D-alanine substrate (D-Ala<sub>2</sub>) is bound
                     in its zwitterionic state, suggesting that the specificity 
                     of VanA for D-lactate as the second substrate arises from 
                     differences in ligand ionization rather than differences in
                     catalytic behavior |CITS: [9888837]|.
                     A <i>ddlB</i> insertion mutant is lethal due to a polar 
                     effect on the essential cell division genes that lie 
                     downstream in the same transcription unit |CITS: 
                     [9791176]|.
                     A potential RNA G-quadruplex structure, formed by 
                     guanine-rich sequences located in the coding sequence 
                     region of the gene, was identified  for <i>ddlB</i> . This 
                     structure  could regulate the expression of the gene, as 
                     observed for <i>hemL</i> gene expression |CITS: 
                     [31964733]|.
                     "
