     gene            99642..100472
                     /gene="ftsQ"
                     /locus_tag="b0093"
                     /label=ftsQ
     CDS             99642..100472
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftsQ"
                     /locus_tag="b0093"
                     /product="bifunctional cell division protein FtsQ / FtsLBQ
                     cell division complex"
                     /label=ftsQ
                     /note="FtsQ is an essential cell division protein that is 
                     required throughout the process of septum formation; the 
                     protein is present in very small amounts of approximately 
                     22 molecules per cell |CITS: [2007547]|. FtsQ localizes to 
                     the division site; the periplasmic domain of FtsQ is 
                     necessary and sufficient for correct localization |CITS: 
                     [9829918][9882666]|. The cytoplasmic domain and membrane 
                     spanning region determine membrane localization, but are 
                     not otherwise required for FtsQ function |CITS: [8631709]|.
                     Localization to the Z ring is dependent on FtsZ, FtsA, 
                     ZipA, and FtsK, but not FtsI, FtsL and FtsN |CITS: 
                     [9882666][11703663][11948172]|. Conversely, FtsL and FtsB 
                     require FtsQ for localization to the Z ring |CITS: 
                     [10027987][11972052]|. FtsQ, FtsL and FtsB form a complex 
                     <i>in vivo</i> even in the absence of FtsK and thus 
                     independently of their localization to the septal region 
                     |CITS: [15165235]|. However, assembly of the cell division 
                     machinery is cooperative rather than linear, and FtsQ may 
                     play a central role. Different domains of FtsQ were found 
                     to be used for interactions with FtsA, FtsI, FtsL, FtsN, 
                     FtsQ, FtsX, and YmgF |CITS: [15774864][16980443]|. 
                     Premature targeting of FtsQ to the Z ring allows targeting 
                     of FtsL and FtsI to the Z ring even in the absence of FtsA,
                     FtsK, or both. Under similar conditions, FtsN does not 
                     localize to the cell division site, indicating that its 
                     localization requires additional signals. In the absence of
                     FtsA, FtsK can be ""back-recruited"" to the division site 
                     by FtsQ |CITS: [15630023]|. FtsQ may also play a role in 
                     regulating the assembly of FtsI at the division site |CITS:
                     [15342580]|.
                     FtsQ is an integral membrane protein with a small 
                     cytoplasmic domain and a large periplasmic domain |CITS: 
                     [1656499]|. Insertion of FtsQ into the membrane requires 
                     the Sec translocase |CITS: [11415986]| and may be partially
                     dependent on YidC |CITS: [11415986][24272775]|. The 
                     transmembrane segment plays a role in localization of FtsQ 
                     to the cell division site |CITS: [17693520]|. A number of 
                     mutant alleles of <i>ftsQ</i> were analyzed to dissect the 
                     functional domains |CITS: [16980443]| and protein 
                     interactions |CITS: [20079830][20460179][23782448]| of 
                     FtsQ. Three subdomains of the periplasmic domain have 
                     different roles in interactions with other cell division 
                     proteins |CITS: [17185541]|. <i>In vivo</i> site-specific 
                     cross-linking was used to map the periplasmic interaction 
                     interfaces with FtsB and FtsL, disagreeing with results 
                     from previously published bacterial two-hybrid assays 
                     |CITS: [23846696]|. A crystal structure of the periplasmic 
                     domain has been solved, showing an &alpha; domain similar 
                     to POTRA domains that is involved in FtsQ localization and 
                     a C-terminal &beta; sheet domain that is involved in 
                     recruitment of downstream cell division proteins |CITS: 
                     [18312270]|. Local interactions of the FtsQBL complex 
                     components were determined using hydrogen-deuterium 
                     exchange mass spectrometry (HDX-MS). In the FtsQBL complex,
                     the connection between &alpha;- and &beta;-domains of FtsQ 
                     are strengthened and a large area of the &beta;-domain of 
                     FtsQ is stiffened when FtsB interacts with FtsQ at its 
                     C-terminal region; peptides and amino acid residues 
                     important to the structural interactions are also indicated
                     |CITS: [35798142]|.
                     FtsQ is required for localization of heterologous 
                     autotransporter proteins to the cell poles |CITS: 
                     [22375072]|.
                     FtsQ expression is growth rate regulated, and the gene is 
                     transcribed at the time of cell division |CITS: 
                     [2515414][8552633]|. Translation of the <i>ftsQ</i> mRNA is
                     inefficient and may lead to the observed low levels of FtsQ
                     protein in the cell |CITS: [2170339]|. Overexpression of 
                     Blr or YmgF suppresses the growth defect of 
                     <i>ftsQ1</i>(Ts) cells |CITS: [22885295]|.
                     A macrocyclic covalent inhibitor was designed based on a 
                     periplasmic region of FtsB that mediates its binding to 
                     FtsQ when forming the |FRAME: CPLX0-7934| |CITS: 
                     [PAULUSSEN2022]|.
                     FtsQ: ""<b>f</b>ilamentous <b>t</b>emperature 
                     <b>s</b>ensitive <b>Q</b>""
                     Selected reviews: |CITS: [20943430][15491352][12626683]|"
