     gene            100469..101731
                     /gene="ftsA"
                     /locus_tag="b0094"
                     /label=ftsA
     CDS             100469..101731
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftsA"
                     /locus_tag="b0094"
                     /product="cell division protein FtsA"
                     /label=ftsA
                     /note="|FRAME: EG10339-MONOMER FtsA| is an essential cell 
                     division protein which colocalizes with |FRAME: 
                     EG10347-MONOMER| to the septal ring structure; localization
                     is FtsZ-dependent |CITS: [8917533][8955398]|. Both FtsA and
                     |FRAME: G7258-MONOMER| recruit |FRAME: ABC-54-CPLX| |CITS: 
                     [14729705]| and |FRAME: G6464-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [9723913][14663069]|, and thus other cell division proteins
                     downstream, to the Z ring. However, premature targeting of 
                     cell division proteins to mid-cell allows recruitment of 
                     other members of the cell division machinery even in the 
                     absence of FtsA |CITS: [16824093]|. Localization of FtsA is
                     dependent on the membrane potential |CITS: [20566861]|. 
                     Divisome assembly, septal peptidoglycan synthesis and 
                     hydrolysis are coordinated by interactions with FtsA |CITS:
                     [27503875][31175681][32636250]|.
                     The FtsA protein belongs to the actin family of ATPases 
                     |CITS: [7781919]| and is associated with the cytoplasmic 
                     side of the inner membrane |CITS: [2203741]|. It is well 
                     conserved in bacteria, but is conspicuously absent in 
                     certain groups such as the mycobacteria, streptomycetes, 
                     and cyanobacteria.
                     FtsA can self-interact to form multimers both <i>in 
                     vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [11053380][15752196]|. 
                     Subdomain 1C is essential for interaction with other FtsA 
                     molecules, and FtsA self-interaction is important for Z 
                     ring assembly |CITS: [12486713][17986188]|. However, 
                     impairment of self-interaction by FtsA enabled it to carry 
                     out its roles associated with Z-ring assembly as well as 
                     its ability to bypass ZipA, implying that FtsA 
                     polymerization competes with its role in recruiting 
                     downstream cell division proteins |CITS: [22111832]|. 
                     Allosteric changes may influence oligomeric state of a 
                     thermosensitive FtsA mutant |CITS: [25213228]|. 
                     Overproduction of FtsA causes inhibition of cell septation 
                     |CITS: [2198274]|. To enable cell division, FtsA and FtsZ 
                     have to be present at a ratio of about 1:100 in the cell 
                     |CITS: [1400163][1400183]|, although this ratio may be 
                     closer to 1:10 |CITS: [12754232]|. |FRAME: CPLX0-3461| 
                     cleaves the <i>ftsQAZ</i> transcript and decreases the 
                     relative abundance of <i>ftsA</i> to <i>ftsZ</i> mRNA 
                     |CITS: [8760895]|. Depletion of RNase E results in an 
                     altered FtsA/FtsZ ratio |CITS: [16816186]|. The main driver
                     of rate-limitation for the initiation of constriction at 
                     the septum is FtsZ cellular concentration and accumulation 
                     within the Z-ring, rather than FtsN and FtsA; a new model 
                     is proposed |CITS: [39550358]|.
                     FtsA, together with ZipA, supports stabilization of the Z 
                     ring |CITS: [11847116]| via the conserved C-terminal tail 
                     of FtsZ |CITS: [19415799]|. A point mutation in FtsA 
                     (R286W, named FtsA*) can bypass the requirement for ZipA 
                     |CITS: [12634424]| and FtsK |CITS: [16194242]| in cell 
                     division and can suppress the temperature sensitivity of 
                     the <i>ftsQ1</i> allele |CITS: [16194242]|. FtsA* appears 
                     to have an altered interaction with FtsZ |CITS: [17322202]|
                     and can partially disassemble FtsZ polymers <i>in vitro</i>
                     |CITS: [19297332]|. A point mutation in subdomain 1C of 
                     FtsA, <i>ftsA</i>(I143L), can also rescue a variety of 
                     divisome assembly defects, including that of <i>ftsK</i> or
                     <i>zipA</i> deletion mutants, as well as localization and 
                     recruitment mutations in FtsQ |CITS: [16980443]|. Another 
                     mutation in subdomain 1C, E124A, suppresses an <i>ftsN</i> 
                     deletion mutant and certain other cell division mutants 
                     |CITS: [17542921]|.
                     FtsA interacts directly with the C-terminal core domain of 
                     FtsZ |CITS: [9287012][10601211]|. The extreme C terminus of
                     FtsA is required for biological activity, localization to 
                     the Z ring, and for normal self-interaction |CITS: 
                     [11053380]|. A conserved C-terminal amphipathic helix is 
                     required for targeting of FtsA to the membrane and the Z 
                     ring |CITS: [15752196][17501933]|, but appears to serve 
                     only as a generic membrane anchor |CITS: [18186792]|. 
                     Subdomain 1C is sufficient for recruitment of |FRAME: 
                     EG10342-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11529-MONOMER| and |FRAME: 
                     EG10341-MONOMER|, but is not required for localization of 
                     FtsA to the Z ring |CITS: [15387815][15516588]|. If this 
                     subdomain is independently targeted to the Z ring, it can 
                     partially suppress an <i>ftsA</i><sup>ts</sup> mutation; it
                     may therefore constitute an independent domain involved in 
                     the recruitment of other septation proteins |CITS: 
                     [15516588]|. FtsA protofilament-like structures interacting
                     with FtsZ have been observed <i>in vitro</i> via 
                     transmission electron microscopy (TEM) |CITS: 
                     [22761913][28695917]|. A C-terminal membrane targeting 
                     sequence (&Delta;MTS) mutant forms ATP-dependent linear 
                     polymers that coassemble with FtsZ; the FtsA polymers 
                     become unstable in the presence of unassembled FtsZ 
                     polymers suggesting a co-assembled state of FtsA/FtsZ 
                     |CITS: [35104502]|. Quantitative, <i>in vitro</i> 
                     characterization of the interactions between FtsA, FtsZ and
                     FtsN using FtsA* mutant have been carried out |CITS: 
                     [35550516]|. Residues in FtsA were identified as being 
                     required for interaction with FtsZ |CITS: [17501933]|; 
                     subsequent studies with point mutations near the ATP 
                     hydrolysis site (E14R and S84L) and a mutation associated 
                     with temperature-sensitive growth (A188V) indicate that 
                     FtsA promotes ATP-dependent vesicle tubulation, 
                     phospholipid binding and stabilization of FtsZ polymers, 
                     thus affecting Z-ring localization and assembly |CITS: 
                     [37250038]|.
                     Specific residues in subdomain 1c of FtsA preferentially 
                     interact with the FtsN cytoplasmic domain <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [22328664][25496259][25496160]|. Interaction between
                     FtsA and FtsN are essential for promoting the 
                     back-recruitment of late components of the divisome which 
                     enables the cell to bypass FtsEX and FtsK normally required
                     for the process of divisome assembly |CITS: [29967164]|. 
                     FtsA is involved in the recruitment and activation of FtsWI
                     when FtsA is activated by FtsN |CITS: [34453005]|. FtsA 
                     interactions with FtsN's cytoplasmic tail results in FtsA 
                     forming antiparallel double filaments <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [36123441][36347840]|.
                     Using an <i>in vivo</i> crosslinking method, FtsA assembles
                     as antiparallel double-stranded filaments in later stages 
                     of cell division and depends on the divisome asembled to at
                     least the FtsQ-dependent step |CITS: [39041810]|.
                     In the L form strain LW1655F that lacks peptidoglycan, FtsA
                     carries point mutations in conserved residues |CITS: 
                     [16640589]|.
                     FtsA: ""<b>f</b>ilamentous <b>t</b>emperature 
                     <b>s</b>ensitive <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [4583216]|
                     Selected reviews: |CITS: [16824090][15491352][12626683][12 
                     457697][28419603][37503768][37524757]|"
