     gene            complement(101435..101986)
                     /gene="stfZ"
                     /locus_tag="b4840"
                     /label=stfZ
     misc_RNA        complement(101435..101986)
                     /gene="stfZ"
                     /locus_tag="b4840"
                     /product="small antisense RNA StfZ"
                     /note="StfZ is a small antisense regulatory RNA that
                     affects genes related to cell division through the 
                     repression of the expression of the transcriptional 
                     regulator FtsZ, mainly at 42 &deg;C |CITS: [36338044]|. The
                     <i>stfZ</i> locus is transcribed from three promoters, 
                     producing three isoforms of StfZ of nucleotides 366, 474, 
                     and 552 that are produced at similar levels |CITS: 
                     [36338044]|. The three isoforms contain the complementary 
                     sequence to the 5'-region of <i>ftsZ</i> that covers the 
                     ribosome-binding site of <i>ftsZ</i>, the complete 
                     <i>ftsZ-ftsA</i> intergenic region, and a 3'-region of the 
                     <i>ftsA</i> gene. StfZ showed a low level of expression, as
                     it was transcribed at a 1:6 ratio (StfZ:ftsZ) |CITS: 
                     [36338044]|.
                     StfZ, which is expressed in all growth phases, does not 
                     affect the transcription nor the mRNA stability of 
                     <i>ftsZ</i>, and it appears that it does not block the 
                     translation at RBS locations;  however, overexpression of 
                     the regulatory asRNA diminished the production of FtsZ 
                     protein |CITS: [36338044]|.
                     A secondary structure of two loops for the longer isoform 
                     of StfZ was predicted |CITS: [36338044]|."
     gene            101792..102943
                     /gene="ftsZ"
                     /locus_tag="b0095"
                     /label=ftsZ
     CDS             101792..102943
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftsZ"
                     /locus_tag="b0095"
                     /product="cell division protein FtsZ"
                     /label=ftsZ
                     /note="Assembly of FtsZ into a ring structure (the Z ring, 
                     |CITS: [1944597]|) at the future cell division site is the 
                     earliest known event in cell division. FtsZ is the most 
                     highly conserved of the proteins that eventually comprise 
                     the septal ring structure; homologs of FtsZ are nearly 
                     universally present in bacteria as well as in many archaea,
                     some chloroplasts and a few mitochondria |CITS: 
                     [14743312]|.
                     FtsZ is essential |CITS: [2045370]|; it binds GTP and has 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup>-dependent GTPase activity |CITS: 
                     [1528267][1528268]|. Assembly of FtsZ into protein 
                     filaments is dynamic and regulated by GTP hydrolysis |CITS:
                     [8169229]|, resembling tubulin |CITS: [9430638]|. Turnover 
                     of FtsZ within the Z ring is extremely rapid, with the 
                     rate-limiting step for turnover likely to be GTP hydrolysis
                     |CITS: [15317782][14705956]|.
                     The position of the FtsZ ring structure marks the cell 
                     division site and serves as the assembly point to which 
                     other proteins of the cell division machinery are 
                     recruited. Based on studies of mutants, FtsA and |FRAME: 
                     G7258-MONOMER| initially associate with FtsZ forming a 
                     proto-ring, followed by addition of |FRAME: ABC-54-CPLX|, 
                     |FRAME: G6464-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10342-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11086-MONOMER|/|FRAME: G7424-MONOMER FtsB|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10344-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10341-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11529-MONOMER| and |FRAME: G7458-MONOMER|, apparently in 
                     this defined order.
                     FtsZ assembles <i>in vitro</i> into protofilaments that can
                     adopt two conformations: a straight conformation, which can
                     assemble further into two-dimensional protofilament sheets,
                     and a curved conformation, which forms minirings about 23 
                     nm in diameter. The curved protofilaments also assemble 
                     into tubes. GTP is associated with the straight 
                     conformation while GDP favors the curved conformation. The 
                     transition from straight to curved conformation may provide
                     the mechanism for how energy from GTP hydrolysis generates 
                     the force for the constriction of the FtsZ ring in cell 
                     division |CITS: [10613876]|. Models of protofilaments, 
                     their cooperativity related to assembly and their potential
                     for generating a constrictive force have been put forward 
                     |CITS: [19478069]|.
                     Using high-resolution electron microscopy FtsZ 
                     protofilaments were found to condense into suprastructures 
                     <i>in vitro</i> by molecular crowding but with various 
                     arrangements depending on the cations present |CITS: 
                     [19137575]|. FtsZ can form dynamic biomolecular condensates
                     in the presence of the |FRAME: CPLX0-7922| but also by 
                     itself, even when lacking its C-terminal disordered region,
                     though less efficiently; the formation of condensates is 
                     subject to physiological factors |CITS: 
                     [30523075][33581219]|. Subsequent studies of the <i>in 
                     vitro</i> effects of two physiologically relevant factors, 
                     glutamate and lipid surfaces, found that both enhanced the 
                     formation of FtsZ and DNA-bound SlmA condensates on lipid 
                     surfaces |CITS: [36315857]|.
                     The question of how FtsZ itself is positioned precisely at 
                     mid-cell, enabling a normal cell division event, has not 
                     been definitively answered. Both nucleoid occlusion and the
                     Min system appear to play a role in division site 
                     selection; the hypothesis has been discussed in detail 
                     |CITS: [15013745]|. Nucleoid occlusion describes a process 
                     by which the presence of the nucleoid inhibits Z ring 
                     formation at that site. When the nucleoid structure is 
                     perturbed by a block in transcription, nucleoid occlusion 
                     is affected; thus, the process may depend on the specific 
                     organization of the nucleoid |CITS: [15175309]|. Model 
                     results based on the time difference of the appearance of 
                     the division site, the timing of cell division and the 
                     Z-ring existence time between cells with and without a 
                     functioning Min system indicated that the septum formation 
                     rate is affected by the oscillating Min system |CITS: 
                     [25090009]|. Relative expression levels of the different 
                     Min proteins, particularly increasing MinE relative to 
                     MinD, delays FtsZ ring formation resulting in larger cell 
                     sizes |CITS: [37714867]|. FtsZ cellular concentration and 
                     accumulation within the Z-ring, rather than FtsN and FtsA, 
                     is the main driver of rate-limitation for the initiation of
                     constriction at the septum; a new model is proposed |CITS: 
                     [39550358]|.
                     FtsZ exhibits periodic assembly and disassembly cycles of 
                     FtsZ polymers in the Z-ring ('treadmilling') linked to 
                     GTPase activity; the treadmilling guides the directional 
                     movement of the essential |FRAME: EG10341-MONOMER ""septal 
                     transpeptidase FtsI""| which helps direct the distribution 
                     of new septal peptidoglycan |CITS: [28209899]|.
                     The constrictive force for cytokinesis has been attributed 
                     to the Z-ring based on initial <i>in vitro</i> studies with
                     tubular liposomes |CITS: [18420899][23776220]|. The rate of
                     constriction of the Z-ring was not slowed in an FtsZ mutant
                     but was in an FtsI mutant, implicating cell wall synthesis 
                     as a factor in generating constrictive force |CITS: 
                     [26831086]|. Using a temperature-sensitive FtsZ mutant, 
                     cells already in late stage of constriction but not early 
                     stages can complete constriction despite disassembly of the
                     Z-ring upon rapid shift to 42 &deg;C |CITS: 
                     [9209044][35679326]|.
                     Both |FRAME: EG10984 SulA| and |FRAME: EG10596 MinC| are 
                     negative regulators of Z ring assembly |CITS: [8432706]|. 
                     SulA interacts directly with FtsZ |CITS: 
                     [7726836][12808143]| and inhibits GTPase activity and 
                     polymerization <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [9501185][9683493]| 
                     and FtsZ ring formation <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [10931335]|. 
                     MinC also interacts with FtsZ directly and prevents FtsZ 
                     polymerization without inhibiting its GTPase activity 
                     |CITS: [10611296][11673433]|. The |FRAME: CPLX0-8188 
                     ""MinCD proteins""| oscillate between the two cell poles; 
                     this behavior may allow Z ring formation at mid-cell 
                     because the time-integrated concentration of MinCD is 
                     lowest at mid-cell. A conflicting report asserts that MinCD
                     does not block Z ring formation, but instead blocks |FRAME:
                     EG10339-MONOMER| association with the Z ring |CITS: 
                     [10931335]|. MinDE surface waves generated without MinC 
                     regulate the spatial organization of FtsA-anchored FtsZ 
                     (FtsA-FtsZ) cytoskeletal structure when examined <i>in 
                     vitro</i> on supported lipid bilayers and within 
                     microdroplets |CITS: [35798943]|.
                     In studies using GFP-labeled FtsZ, FtsZ outside of the Z 
                     ring was found to move rapidly in a helix-like pattern 
                     along the cell, similar to the movements of the Min 
                     proteins. The presence of a dynamic, helical cytoskeleton 
                     was proposed |CITS: [15242613]|. Supporting this 
                     hypothesis, FtsZ was found to be involved in maintaining 
                     cell shape |CITS: [15466028]|.
                     The role of cytoskeleton proteins, FtsZ and |FRAME: 
                     EG10608-MONOMER|, the nucleoid occlusion system, |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7922|, and the Min system proteins and complexes, 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-8196|, |FRAME: EG10597-MONOMER| and |FRAME: 
                     EG10598-MONOMER| have been studied in relation to wall-less
                     L-form cells in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [17993535][27573111][27573120][39587276]|.
                     Using flow cytometry to differentially detect tetracysteine
                     (TC)-labelled FtsZ and propidium iodide-labelled DNA in 
                     slow-growing cells (chromosome number &le; 2) found 
                     expression of FtsZ was higher after cells entered DNA 
                     replication phase of the cell cycle, confirming previous 
                     results |CITS: [30230648][39015018]|. Under fast-growth 
                     conditions (chromosome number &ge; 4), FtsZ expression 
                     levels were proportional to the DNA content, whereas the 
                     proportionality was more complex under different nutrient 
                     conditions |CITS: [39015018]|. The domain structure of FtsZ
                     has been described |CITS: [14743312]|. GTP binds to the 
                     nucleotide binding site present in the N-terminal domain. A
                     highly conserved central domain is structurally and 
                     functionally homologous to tubulin; it contains the 
                     dimerization domain |CITS: [10231484]|. The C-terminal core
                     domain consists of 12 amino acids essential for FtsA and 
                     |FRAME: G7258-MONOMER| binding |CITS: [10601211]|. The 
                     crystal structure of a C-terminal fragment of FtsZ in 
                     complex with ZipA has been solved |CITS: [10880432]|. The 
                     protein also contains a short N-terminal intrinsically 
                     disordered peptide (IDP) and a much longer C-terminal 
                     intrinsically disordered region (IDR); these are involved 
                     in linking FtsZ to ZipA and FtsA, and are likely involved 
                     in bending the FtsZ protofilaments |CITS: [23714328]|. 
                     Residues in the C-terminus were identified for binding to 
                     ZipA; mutations in residue 373 affected interactions with 
                     both ZipA and FtsA |CITS: [11278571]|. Studies with the 
                     enzyme from <i>Bacillus subtilis</i> have shown that the 
                     C-terminal IDP is crucial for FtsZ assembly <i>in vitro</i>
                     and Z ring formation <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [23692518][25305578]|, and molecular simulations with the 
                     <i>E. coli</i> enzyme support that conclusion. The 
                     simulation suggests that the IDR interacts with the 
                     globular core, causing a bend in the core helix of the GTP 
                     bound FtsZ monomer and enabling polymerization |CITS: 
                     [37158088]|. Co-assembly of FtsZ/FtsA was shown to occur in
                     a study with a C-terminal membrane targeting sequence 
                     (&Delta;MTS) FtsA mutant |CITS: [35104502]|. Studies 
                     focused on the constriction forces required for the FtsZ 
                     protofilaments and ZipA to start the membrane to curve have
                     been carried out |CITS: [37806464]|.
                     Compounds with activity against <i>E. coli</i> FtsZ, with 
                     potential utility as broad-spectrum antimicrobials, have 
                     been isolated, characterized and modelled |CITS: 
                     [15289600][35618784][37634788][40287515]|.
                     Regulation of FtsZ expression and activity is complex and 
                     has been summarized in |CITS: [12051832]|. Under 
                     nutrient-rich growth conditions the inner membrane 
                     glycosyltransferase |FRAME: EG11886-MONOMER OpgH| interacts
                     directly with FtsZ to delay cell division |CITS: 
                     [23935518]|. Negative regulation of FtsZ is achieved 
                     through degradation by the |FRAME: EG10159-MONOMER| of 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3107| |CITS: [19541655]|; protection from 
                     degradation occurs through direct interaction with |FRAME: 
                     G7258-MONOMER| |CITS: [23233671]|. ClpX interacts with the 
                     predicted IDR within the C-terminal interaction site of 
                     polymerized FtsZ |CITS: [35481648]|. Post-transcriptional 
                     regulation of FtsZ mRNA for different growth phases is 
                     related to distinct isoforms of the |FRAME: RNA0-436| 
                     |CITS: [36338044]|.
                     A potential RNA G-quadruplex structure, formed by 
                     guanine-rich sequences located in the coding sequence 
                     region of the gene, was identified  for <i>ftsZ</i>. This 
                     structure could regulate the expression of the gene, as 
                     observed for <i>hemL</i> gene expression |CITS: 
                     [31964733]|.
                     Effects of bacteriophage on FtsZ localization was examined 
                     |CITS: [35967845]|.
                     Selected reviews: |CITS: [12626683][14527275][15491352][18 
                     291013][21119015][26377318][27114427][28500525][28975672][ 
                     31171437][33220539][34351794][35408901][37503768][37524757 
                     ][38821027][39587276]|"
