     gene            103044..103961
                     /gene="lpxC"
                     /locus_tag="b0096"
                     /label=lpxC
     CDS             103044..103961
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lpxC"
                     /locus_tag="b0096"
                     /product="UDP-3-O-acyl-N-acetylglucosamine deacetylase"
                     /EC_number="3.5.1.108"
                     /label=lpxC
                     /note="|FRAME: EC-3.5.1.108| (LpxC) catalyzes the second 
                     reaction and the first committed step in lipid A 
                     biosynthesis. LpxC hydrolyzes 
                     UDP-3-O-(3-hydroxymyristoyl)-N-acetlyglucosamine to 
                     UDP-3-O-(3-hydroxymyristoyl)glucosamine and acetate |CITS: 
                     [8530464][3288280]|. The mechanism of action of LpxC has 
                     been studied, and a high-throughput fluorescent screen as 
                     well as a mass-spectrometry based screen have been 
                     developed to evaluate LpxC activity |CITS: 
                     [15705580][11237337][20019290]|.
                     LpxC is a metalloenzyme that was reported to require bound 
                     zinc for activity |CITS: [10026271]|. Site-directed 
                     mutagenesis has identified zinc binding sites |CITS: 
                     [11148046]|. More recently, LpxC with bound iron was shown 
                     to have higher activity than zinc-bound enzyme |CITS: 
                     [20136146]|. While LpxC has significantly higher affinity 
                     for zinc, <i>in vivo</i> concentrations of readily 
                     available iron are higher that those of zinc. It has been 
                     proposed that LpxC is able to switch between metal ions in 
                     response to their availability |CITS: [20709752]|.
                     LpxC abundance is regulated in response to the cells need 
                     for lipid A |CITS: [8824222]|. This regulation occurs via 
                     degradation by |FRAME: CPLX0-2982|, and requires the 
                     presence of the carboxy-terminus of LpxC which contains a 
                     signal sequence necessary for this FtsH-dependent 
                     degradation |CITS: [10048027][16420369]|. LpxC is degraded 
                     by FtsH in a growth-rate dependent manner, where LpxC is 
                     degraded during slow growth, and stabilized when cells grow
                     rapidly |CITS: [23417489]|. A quantitative model of 
                     phospholipid biosynthesis indicated that acyl-CoA, ppGpp 
                     and levels of saturated but not unsaturated fatty acids 
                     play a role in LpxC stability |CITS: [26929331]|. LpxC is 
                     the target of a proposed |FRAME: CPLX0-7944 
                     ""PldA""|-dependent transenvelope lipid signaling pathway 
                     that detects phospholipid accumulation in the outer 
                     membrane (OM) and regulates LPS production to restore OM 
                     homeostasis; PldA signaling modulates LPS biosynthesis via 
                     |FRAME: ACYLCOASYN-CPLX ""FadD""| mediated production of 
                     acyl-CoA which ultimately leads to stabilization of LpxC 
                     levels and increased LPS production |CITS: [29559571]|. 
                     LpxC is the target of a proposed regulatory pathway for LPS
                     homeostasis involving the three essential membrane proteins
                     |FRAME: EG11506-MONOMER ""FtsH""|, |FRAME: EG12691-MONOMER 
                     ""LapB""| and |FRAME: EG12049-MONOMER ""LapC""| |CITS: 
                     [32291302][32430473]|. LapB is an adaptor protein that 
                     utilizes its transmembrane helix to interact with FtsH and 
                     its cytoplasmic domains to recruit LpxC. LapC is an 
                     anti-adaptor protein, competing with FtsH for LapB to 
                     inhibit LpxC degradation. LapC has overlapping binding 
                     sites for LapB and for lipopolysaccharides (LPS), and thus 
                     LPS levels control formation of the LapC/LapB complex and, 
                     indirectly, determine LpxC protein levels |CITS: 
                     [35931690]|. |FRAME: CPLX0-1163| was also reported to 
                     mediate FtsH-independant degradation of LpxC <i>in 
                     vivo</i>; HslUV-dependant proteolysis of LpxC may be 
                     important at high temperatures |CITS: [33260377]|. 
                     Increased levels of LpxC resulted from overexpression of 
                     <i>lapD</i> and other possible new regulatory factors 
                     |CITS: [37894855]|.
                     <i>lpxC</i> is an essential gene |CITS: [2824434]|. 
                     Mutations in <i>lpxC</i> lead to problems with cell 
                     division, increased permeability to antibacterial agents, 
                     leakage of periplasmic enzymes and reduced abundance of LPS
                     |CITS: [4903169][4922970][1904854][8752330]|.
                     LpxC is a drug target for antimicrobials due to its role in
                     lipid A synthesis |CITS: [8875939]|. Various inhibitors of 
                     LpxC have been discovered |CITS: [12019092 
                     ][10753902][18289052][21194954][23914798][23917427]|. The 
                     molecular determinants of binding and specificity as well 
                     as the catalytic mechanism have been studied with respect 
                     to drug development |CITS: [17176046]|. Spontaneous mutants
                     which were resistant to LpxC inhibitors were isolated and 
                     found to have mutations in <i>fabZ</i> or <i>thrS</i> 
                     |CITS: [23316051]|.
                     The crystal structure of LpxC in complex with 
                     UDP-(3-O-(R-3-hydroxymyristoyl))-glucosamine was determined
                     |CITS: [24108127]|. The crystal structure analysis of LpxC 
                     bound to an inhibitor revealed a backbone flipping of a 
                     loop causing a conformational switch allowing for expansion
                     of the active site and thereby accommodating LpxC 
                     inhibitors with different head groups |CITS: [24117400]|.
                     The transcription of the <i>lpxC</i> gene appears to be 
                     positively regulated by the protein BglG |CITS: 
                     [35397010]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [27235477][33036869][35008618]|"
