     CDS             104192..104704
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="secM"
                     /locus_tag="b0097"
                     /product="SecA translation regulator"
                     /label=secM
                     /note="<i>secM</i> encodes a presecretory protein that
                     regulates production of the protein translocation ATPase 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-8089 SecA| via translation arrest. SecM 
                     functions in the nascent ribsome-associated state to induce
                     translational pausing which affects mRNA folding and 
                     regulates <i>secA</i> translation (see |CITS: [ 
                     29790986]|).
                     Early work observing that production of SecA is coordinated
                     with the protein secretion status of the cell and 
                     implicating 'gene X' (later <i>secM</i>) in SecA regulation
                     includes |CITS: [6751561][2841285][2464580][183 
                     3384][1834634][9020977][9748461][9871110][10986266]|.
                     SecM is difficult to detect in normal cells; <i>secM</i> 
                     encodes a secretory protein that is rapidly degraded by 
                     tail-specific protease following export to the periplasm 
                     |CITS: [1834634][11172723]|. <i>secM</i> translation is 
                     subject to elongation pausing at a position close to its 
                     C-terminus and this arrest is prolonged when |FRAME: 
                     SECE-G-Y-CPLX Sec translocase| function is compromised 
                     |CITS: [11172723]|. SecM translation is arrested at 
                     Pro-166; a conserved 17 residue C-terminal motif (the 
                     arrest motif) contains arrest-essential residues including 
                     Pro-166 and Arg-163; mutations in both RNA and protein 
                     components of the ribosome suppress elongation arrest 
                     |CITS: [11893334]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [11948148][18586934]|). Translation arrest may also be 
                     modulated by other regions in SecM (see |CITS: 
                     [38735477]|).
                     Transient pausing of SecM translation is required for 
                     basal-level synthesis of SecA; translation arrest is 
                     required for upregulation of SecA in response to a 
                     secretion defect and for cold-induction of SecA |CITS: 
                     [15302932]|. SecM translation arrest is relieved by the 
                     pulling force generated at the Sec translocon |CITS: 
                     [14594848]| (see also |CITS: [25908824]|. SecM signal 
                     peptide is implicated in the release of translation arrest 
                     |CITS: [21635582]|.
                     The SecM-stalled ribosome complex has peptidyl-tRNA 
                     (SecM-tRNA<sup>Gly</sup>) at the P-site and 
                     Pro-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> at the A-site of the ribosome and is
                     stalled prior to peptide bond formation |CITS: [16713584]| 
                     (see also |CITS: [38503753]|). The molecular mechanism of 
                     stalling continues to be investigated |CITS: 
                     [16713583][16762832][20676057][21383133][21267063][22853911
                     ][24836001][26670735][30563926][31913464][38503753][3933278
                     4]|. SecM-mediated ribosome arrest is resistant to |FRAME: 
                     SSRA-RNA tmRNA| recruitment |CITS: [16968693]|. |FRAME: 
                     YHES-MONOMER| releases nascent SecM from translational 
                     arrest |CITS: [38661232]|.
                     <i>secM</i>: secretion monitor (see |CITS: [10986266]|) 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [14695511][15063851]|<BR>Related reviews: 
                     |CITS: [18369182][20117091]|"
