     gene            115768..116622
                     /gene="ampE"
                     /locus_tag="b0111"
                     /label=ampE
     CDS             115768..116622
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ampE"
                     /locus_tag="b0111"
                     /product="protein AmpE"
                     /label=ampE
                     /note="In contrast to some gram-negative species
                     (<i>Citrobacter freundii, Enterobacter cloacae, Pseudomonas
                     aeruginosa</i> and others), expression of the <i>ampC</i> 
                     encoded &beta;-lactamase is non-inducible in <i>E. coli</i>
                     K-12 due to the absence of <i>ampR</i> encoding a 
                     transcriptional regulator. Cloned <i>ampC</i> from <i>C. 
                     freundii</i> or <i>E. cloacae</i> is inducible in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> when accompanied by <i>ampR</i> (see |CITS: 
                     [8452343]| and |CITS: [10539994]|).
                     The closely linked genes, <i>ampD</i> and <i>ampE</i>, are 
                     implicated in the AmpR mediated regulation of AmpC in an 
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strain containing cloned <i>C. freundii
                     ampR</i> and <i>ampC</i> genes, but their role is not clear
                     |CITS:[2691840][2607970]|. AmpD was later characterized as 
                     an |FRAME: EG10041-MONOMER| active during peptidoglycan 
                     recycling and its role in the induction of AmpC clarified 
                     (see reviews by |CITS: [9158731][24955547]|) but the 
                     function of AmpE remains unclear. <I>ampE</I> encodes an 
                     inner membrane protein; AmpE does not bind 
                     benzylpenicillin; <i>ampE</i> contains sequence motifs 
                     associated with ATP binding, it also contains 2 of the 3 
                     motifs generally found in &beta;-lactam reactive proteins 
                     |CITS:[2691840][2607970]|. <i>&Delta;ampD &Delta;ampE</i> 
                     cells are more sensitive to ampicillin than the parent 
                     strain and this phenotype can be complemented by cloned 
                     <i>ampD</i> but not by cloned <i>ampE</i> |CITS: 
                     [2691840]|.
                     "
