     gene            complement(116665..118038)
                     /gene="aroP"
                     /locus_tag="b0112"
                     /label=aroP
     CDS             complement(116665..118038)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aroP"
                     /locus_tag="b0112"
                     /product="multifunctional aromatic amino acid:H(+)
                     symporter AroP / L-tryptophan:H(+) symporter / 
                     L-tyrosine:H(+) symporter / L-phenylalanine:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=aroP
                     /note="AroP is an aromatic amino acid permease that is a
                     member of the APC Superfamily of transporters |CITS: 
                     [9889387]|. AroP is referred to as the general aromatic 
                     amino acid permease because it is responsible for the 
                     transport of all three aromatic amino acids, phenylalanine,
                     tyrosine, and tryptophan, across the inner membrane |CITS: 
                     [4919744][9150230]|. Based on the hydrophobicity profile 
                     and distribution of charged amino acid residues, AroP was 
                     shown to have 12 membrane-spanning regions connected by 
                     hydrophilic loops |CITS: [9150230]|. This topographical 
                     model is very similar to that reported for the PheP 
                     permease, a phenylalanine-specific transporter, which has 
                     61% amino acid sequence identity with the AroP permease. 
                     However, despite their high degree of sequence similarity, 
                     the substrate specificities and affinities of the AroP and 
                     PheP permeases differ |CITS: [10735864]|. AroP transports 
                     each of the aromatic amino acids with a K<sub>m</sub> of 
                     less than 1 &mu;M |CITS: [4919744]|. A key residue involved
                     in tryptophan transport is tyrosine at position 103 |CITS: 
                     [10735864]|. Studies with uncouplers of oxidative 
                     phosphorylation and with strains deficient in
                     F<sub>0</sub>F<sub>1</sub>-ATPase indicate that transport 
                     via the AroP system is driven by the proton motive force 
                     |CITS: [4919744], [10735864]|.
                     Expression of <I>aroP</I> is subject to regulation 
                     involving the TyrR protein |CITS: [334742]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>aroP</i> strain shows resistance to |FRAME: 
                     CPD0-2336| |CITS: [36009997]|."
