                     /note="CueO multicopper oxidase is a copper-stimulated 
                     phenoloxidase and ferroxidase |CITS: [11466290]| that plays
                     a role in copper homeostasis |CITS: [11222619]| under 
                     aerobic |CITS: [11399769]| and anaerobic |CITS: [40624827]|
                     conditions. CueO has been observed to offer protective 
                     effects from copper-mediated damage |CITS: [11527384]|. In 
                     the presence of copper, CueO can oxidize and thus 
                     inactivate the iron siderophores 2,3-dihydroxybenzoate and 
                     enterobactin <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [11466290][15317788]|. 
                     A mixture of enterobactin and copper is toxic, and ferric 
                     enterobactin may be the natural substrate of CueO <i>in 
                     vivo</i>; thus, CueO may play a central role as an 
                     interface between copper detoxification and iron 
                     homeostasis |CITS: [15317788]|. The purified protein has 
                     cuprous oxidase activity <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [15516598]|; CueO functions primarily as a cuprous oxidase 
                     <i>in vivo</i>, not as a phenol oxidase |CITS: 
                     [20088522][25679350]|.
                     Purified CueO contains four |CITS: [11527384][11867755]| or
                     six |CITS: [11466290]| atoms of copper per monomer, 
                     although the latter value may result from inaccurate 
                     protein quantification methods. CueO contains a substrate 
                     binding, type 1 ('blue') copper site and a trinuclear 
                     copper center (consisting of type 2 and type 3 copper 
                     sites) where oxygen binding and reduction takes place; it 
                     also contains a methionine rich region which lies over the 
                     type 1 copper site and may interfere with substrate binding
                     |CITS: [11867755]|. Purified CueO binds five copper atoms; 
                     a fifth copper atom binds in the methionine rich region and
                     is important for the copper associated activation of enzyme
                     activity |CITS: [12794077]|. Deletion of the 
                     methionine-rich region results in a significant reduction 
                     in cuprous oxidase activity but an increase in 
                     phenoloxidase activity possibly due to increased access to 
                     the type 1 copper site |CITS: [17804014]|. The 
                     methionine-rich region is able to bind additional Cu(I) 
                     ions (Cu6 and Cu7 sites) |CITS: [21903583]|. The 
                     methionine-rich region may facilitate recruitment of 
                     strongly chelated Cu(I) species like the model substrate, 
                     copper bicinchoninate [BCA-Cu(I) complex] |CITS: 
                     [39378088]|
                     The fifth copper binding site [also called the regulatory 
                     copper site (rCu), the substrate copper site (sCu), type 4 
                     site (T4) or Cu5] has a different role in phenol and 
                     cuprous oxidation; as a phenol oxidase the T4 site is 
                     occupied by Cu(II) and mediates electron transfer from 
                     organic substrates such as 2,6-dimethoxyphenol to the T1 
                     site; as a cuprous oxidase the T4 site must be vacant in 
                     order to dock a Cu(I) substrate, as demonstrated with the 
                     BCA-Cu(I) complex |CITS: [20088522]|.
                     Cu6 and Cu7 function as substrate [Cu(I)] binding oxidation
                     sites; Cu5 acts as an electron transfer site, not a 
                     substrate reaction site |CITS: [25679350]|. Alternately, 
                     Cu5 is the principal substrate-binding site for cuprous 
                     oxidase activity, and Cu6 and Cu7 sites are unnecessary for
                     catalysis <i>in vivo</i> or <i>in vitro</i>; loss of Cu6 or
                     Cu7 coordination does not affect activity of the purified 
                     enzyme (CueO&Delta;Cu6/7) with Cu(I)(Bca)<sub>2</sub> as 
                     substrate; expression of the &Delta;Cu6/7 variant 
                     complements the copper-sensitivity of a 
                     &Delta;<i>cusB</i>&Delta;<i>cueO</i> strain with the same 
                     efficiency as the wild-type enzyme |CITS: [39378088]|.
                     CueO crystallized in the presence of Ag(I) contains Ag(I) 
                     bound at T4 and at the additional sites (sites 6 and 7) in 
                     the Met rich region; binding of Ag(I) inhibits CueO 
                     activity |CITS: [21903583]|.
                     Phenoloxidase activity of CueO was demonstrated against 
                     substrates including p-phenylenediamine |CITS: [11527384]|,
                     2,6-dimethoxyphenol |CITS: [11527384][11682198]|, and iron 
                     siderophores including 2,3-dihydroxybenzoate, enterobactin,
                     and 3-hydroxyanthranilate |CITS: [11466290]|.
                     CueO is translocated to the periplasm via the Tat pathway 
                     |CITS: [11527384][14702305]|. Copper-free, apo-CueO folds 
                     to a translocation compatible form in the cytoplasm; CueO 
                     is transported without bound copper; CueO assembles copper 
                     in the periplasm under copper stress conditions; cofactor 
                     assembly stabilizes the protein |CITS: [27129241]|.
                     CueO is a member of the copper responsive, CueR regulon 
                     |CITS: [10915804]|.
                     CueO is the major form of soluble protein-bound copper in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS:[19205756]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [12829268]|"
