     CDS             complement(135322..137712)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gcd"
                     /locus_tag="b0124"
                     /product="bifunctional quinoprotein glucose dehydrogenase
                     / glucose dehydrogenase"
                     /EC_number="1.1.5.2"
                     /label=gcd
                     /note="Glucose dehydrogenase (Gcd) is a membrane-bound
                     enzyme. Its role in glucose utilization is not fully clear,
                     because under ordinary conditions it exists as an 
                     apoprotein. The enzyme requires the cofactor 
                     pyrroloquinoline quinone (PQQ) for activity, yet <i>E. 
                     coli</i> lacks the ability to synthesize PQQ |CITS: 
                     [HOMMES84][9353919]|. However, <i>E. coli</i> exhibits 
                     chemotaxis towards environmental PQQ |CITS: [8576064]|, and
                     may thus use an externally supplied cofactor. PQQ is 
                     transported across the outer membrane by the TonB-dependent
                     transporter |FRAME: G6762-MONOMER PqqU| |CITS: [36054785]|.
                     Once functional, the enzyme is able to oxidize glucose and 
                     feed electrons into the respiratory chain |CITS: 
                     [3926746]|, but does not generate a proton motive force 
                     |CITS: [8509415]|. The topological structure of Gcd within 
                     the membrane has been investigated and revealed five 
                     N-terminal membrane-spanning segments, with the N-terminus 
                     located in the cytoplasm and the C-terminus is located in 
                     the periplasm |CITS: [8509415]|. The C-terminal periplasmic
                     domain binds PQQ, contains the catalytic activity and is 
                     able to interact with ubiquinone in the cytoplasmic 
                     membrane |CITS: [11604400]|. A ubiquinone-interacting site 
                     is located close to the membrane surface |CITS: 
                     [10354491]|.
                     Further experiments showed that Gcd contains two ubiquinone
                     binding sites |CITS: [14612441]|. In addition to the PQQ 
                     cofactor, a tightly bound ubiquinone-8 molecule is located 
                     very close to PQQ at an estimated distance of 11-13 &Aring;
                     |CITS: [14612441][16216080]| and mediates the electron 
                     transfer from PQQ to the membrane ubiquinone pools |CITS: 
                     [16216080][18550551]|. Menaquinone can substitute for 
                     ubiquinone in this function |CITS: [18708350]|. Bound 
                     ubiquinone and may be involved in the oxidation of glucose 
                     in addition to the intramolecular electron transfer from 
                     PQQ |CITS: [19096097][18708350]|. The residues thought to 
                     interact with PQQ and the bound ubiquinone have been 
                     identified by site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [9705344][10702303][18550551]|. The specificity for 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup> as the metal cofactor has been studied 
                     |CITS: [12686133]|.
                     A <i>gcd</i> insertion mutant has no detectable growth rate
                     defect; however, in a strain with a phosphotransferase 
                     system mutation and in the presence of the PQQ cofactor, a 
                     <i>gcd</i> mutant can no longer utilize glucose as the sole
                     source of carbon |CITS: [2228962]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [36054785]|). Overexpression of Gcd may alter sugar 
                     utilization preferences |CITS: [8573321]|. Expression of 
                     <i>gcd</i> is increased in the presence of glucose and 
                     under aerobic growth conditions |CITS: [8419307]| and may 
                     be repressed by FNR |CITS: [16377617]|.
                     Combined site-directed mutagenesis and homology modeling 
                     studies of <i>E. coli</i> Gcd have suggested that conserved
                     amino acid residues Asp204 and Gly776 are functionally 
                     important |CITS: [20215780]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [12686131]|"
