     gene            complement(142801..143181)
                     /gene="panD"
                     /locus_tag="b0131"
                     /label=panD
     CDS             complement(142801..143181)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="panD"
                     /locus_tag="b0131"
                     /product="aspartate 1-decarboxylase proenzyme"
                     /EC_number="4.1.1.11"
                     /label=panD
                     /note="Aspartate 1-decarboxylase is responsible for the 
                     synthesis of &beta;-alanine, which is needed in the 
                     biosynthesis of pantothenate.  This enzyme is one of a 
                     small class of enzymes that use a covalently bound pyruvoyl
                     prosthetic group.  The pyruvoyl group is thought to act 
                     analogously to a pyridoxal phosphate cofactor by forming a 
                     Schiff base with the amino group of the substrate and then 
                     serving as an electron sink to facilitate the 
                     decarboxylation |CITS: [2197977]|. Pyruvoyl-containing 
                     enzymes are expressed as a zymogen which is processed 
                     post-translationally by a self-maturation cleavage called 
                     serinolysis.  <i>E. coli</i> contains two more such 
                     enzymes, |FRAME: PHOSPHASERDECARB-DIMER| and |FRAME: 
                     SAMDECARB-CPLX|.
                     The PanD proenzyme (&pi; protein) is processed at the 
                     serine residue at position 25, resulting in two subunits, 
                     &alpha; and &beta;, which form a complex that is 
                     enzymatically active.  Autocatalytic processing of purified
                     enzyme preparations occurs slowly at room temperature or 
                     37&deg; C, and at a higher rate at elevated temperatures 
                     |CITS: [9169598]|.  An ester intermediate at Ser25, formed 
                     by an N->O acyl shift, facilitates autoproteolysis |CITS: 
                     [9546220]|.  &beta;-elimination of the ester results in 
                     proteolysis and the formation of dehydroalanine, which 
                     undergoes hydrolysis to form the pyruvoyl group |CITS: 
                     [14633979]|.  Experiments in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [23170229]| and <i>Salmonella enterica</i> |CITS: 
                     [22497218]| have shown that |FRAME: EG12211-MONOMER 
                     ""PanZ""| is a maturation factor that triggers cleavage of 
                     pro-PanD to its mature and active form.  PanZ has 
                     micromolar affinity for both coenzyme A and acetyl-CoA and 
                     nanomolar affinity for the PanD proenzyme.  PanZ is not an 
                     enzyme but a stoichiometric facilitator of PanD activation.
                     Formation of a PanD-PanZ complex stabilizes a conformation 
                     of PanD in which the serine-25 hydroxyl group moves to a 
                     close proximity to the adjacent carbonyl group, thus 
                     facilitating an attack on the G24-S25 peptide bond |CITS: 
                     [22940551][25910242]|. Addition of PanZ to an assay of 
                     &beta;-alanine synthesis by PanD enhances activity 24-fold 
                     |CITS: [22497218]|.  The interaction of PanZ with PanD is 
                     dependent on the presence of CoA; titration experiments 
                     suggest that PanZ, PanD and CoA bind in a 1:1:1 ratio 
                     |CITS: [22940551]|.  Crystal structures of the PanZ-PanD 
                     complex elucidate the CoA dependency of the PanZ-PanD 
                     interaction and the structural basis for activation of PanD
                     by PanZ |CITS: [25910242]|.
                     An S25A mutation eliminates self-cleavage of the &pi; 
                     protein and eliminates enzymatic activity.  A strain 
                     containing this mutant form of PanD absolutely requires 
                     exogenous &beta;-alanine for growth |CITS: [15033515]|. The
                     N45 residue of PanZ forms a hydrogen bond with the PanD 
                     main-chain amide of residue D23, which is located next to 
                     the cleavage site between G24 and S25 |CITS: [25910242]|.
                     A crystal structure of aspartate 1-decarboxylase enabled 
                     identification of the ester intermediate of the 
                     autoproteolytic cleavage reaction.  The active sites are 
                     located between the subunits |CITS: [9546220]|.  The Tyr58 
                     residue may act as the proton donor in the reprotonation 
                     step of the decarboxylase reaction |CITS: [12240302]|. 
                     Crystal structures of the unprocessed precursor as well as 
                     several point mutants reveal conformational constraints of 
                     the autoproteolysis reaction; a mechanism for 
                     self-processing has been proposed |CITS: [14633979]|. 
                     <i>panD</i> mutants are auxotrophic for pantothenate or 
                     &beta;-alanine |CITS: [7037743][8837478]|.  Removal of 
                     &beta;-alanine from the growth media of a <i>panD</i> 
                     mutant leads to depletion of coenzyme A |CITS: [6317688]|. 
                     PanD: ""<b>pan</b>tothenate""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [2197977][9546209]|"
