                     |CITS:[20345943]|. The operon is cryptic under normal 
                     laboratory conditions but when constitutive expression is 
                     induced in a strain that lacks the type 1 fimbrial complex 
                     the <i>yad</i> operon promotes biofilm formation in minimal
                     media on a variety of abiotic surfaces and produces surface
                     fimbrial structures that can be observed microscopically 
                     |CITS: [20345943]|.
                     Constitutive expression of the <i>yad</i> operon results in
                     increased adhesion of cells to xylose; constitutive 
                     expression of the <i>yad</i> operon results in increased 
                     adherence to intestinal epithelial cells and can modulate 
                     the inflammatory reponse of host cells |CITS: [27696649]|. 
                     A strain producing Yad fimbriae outcompetes both the wild 
                     type and a &Delta;<i>yad</i> strain for corn rhizosphere 
                     colonisation |CITS: [27696649]|
                     Expression of the <i>yad</i> operon is negatively regulated
