     gene            complement(153740..154219)
                     /gene="folK"
                     /locus_tag="b0142"
                     /label=folK
     CDS             complement(153740..154219)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="folK"
                     /locus_tag="b0142"
                     /product="
                     2-amino-4-hydroxy-6-hydroxymethyldihydropteridine 
                     diphosphokinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.6.3"
                     /label=folK
                     /note="6-hydroxymethyl-7,8-dihydropterin pyrophosphokinase 
                     (HPPK) catalyzes a reaction in the folate biosynthesis 
                     pathway, the transfer of pyrophosphate from ATP to 
                     6-hydroxymethyl-7,8-dihydropterin |CITS: [1325970]|.  HPPK 
                     is essential in microorganisms, and the enzyme is not 
                     present in mammals; it is therefore a target for the 
                     development of antimicrobial drugs.
                     Various crystal and solution structures of HPPK have been 
                     solved and have elucidated the reaction mechanism and 
                     kinetics.  The protein undergoes conformational changes 
                     during the catalytic cycle.  |CITS: [10378268][10427747][1 
                     0452528][11080626][11311059][11381532][11546767][12578370][
                     14769023][15016362][15018613][15821168][15952765][17029412]
                     [19108643][19924845][26492157][27951655]| The enzyme binds 
                     ATP first, which then enables faster binding of 
                     6-hydroxymethyl-7,8-dihydropterin (HMDP) |CITS: 
                     [10751386]|.  Mg<sup>2+</sup> is important for binding of 
                     both ATP and HMDP |CITS: [12093297]|.  The roles in 
                     substrate binding and catalysis of several amino acid 
                     residues in the flexible loop 3 have been investigated by 
                     site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [12578371][14769023][15952765]|.  Substrate site inhibitors
                     have been synthesized and tested |CITS: 
                     [27094768][33199204]|.
                     Comparative genomics has suggested a functional association
                     between |FRAME: 3-METHYL-2-OXOBUT-OHCH3XFER-CPLX PanB| and 
                     FolK.  The proposed hypothesis is that a side reaction 
                     catalyzed by PanB damages 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate 
                     (CH2-THF), and FolK subsequently recycles the resulting 
                     pterin moiety to folate.  In support of this hypothesis, 
                     overexpression of <i>panB</i> increases the cells' 
                     sensitivity to antifolate drugs sulfathiazole and 
                     trimethoprim.  However, overexpression of <i>panB</i> 
                     increases the intracellular folate pool.  Kinetic modeling 
                     of the folate synthesis pathway suggests that a regulatory 
                     effect, whereby an initial drop in THF concentration upon 
                     PanB overexpression leads to increased expression of 
                     enzymes in the THF synthesis pathway, can explain this 
                     effect |CITS: [27065985]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [18804704][21222642]|
                     "
