                     /locus_tag="b0144"
                     /product="bifunctional glutamyl-Q tRNA(Asp) synthetase /
                     glutamyl-Q tRNA(Asp) synthetase"
                     /label=gluQ
                     /note="Glutamyl-Q tRNA<sup>Asp</sup> synthetase (Glu-Q-RS,
                     GluQ) catalyzes the addition of a glutamate residue to the 
                     queosine-modified wobble base of tRNA<sup>Asp</sup>.
                     The GluQ protein shows similarity to the amino terminal 
                     part of bacterial glutamyl-tRNA synthetases, although it 
                     lacks the tRNA anticodon interaction domain |CITS: 
                     [15003446]|.  The protein is not capable of using 
                     tRNA<sup>Glu</sup> or tRNA<sup>Gln</sup> as a substrate 
                     <i>in vitro</i> or <i>in vivo</i>, but can modify 
                     tRNA<sup>Asp</sup> with glutamate |CITS: 
                     [15096612][15096594]|.  There, glutamate is not esterified 
                     to the 3' terminal adenosine, but the 
                     2-amino-5-(4,5-dihydroxy-2-cyclopenten-1-yl) moiety of 
                     queosine, which occupies the wobble position of the 
                     tRNA<sup>Asp</sup> anticodon |CITS: [15096612][15150343]|. 
                     This tRNA modification is present in the normal <i>E. 
                     coli</i> tRNA pool |CITS: [15096612]|.  The glutamylated 
                     tRNA<sup>Asp</sup> does not bind elongation factor Tu and 
                     may therefore not be involved in ribosomal protein 
                     synthesis |CITS: [15096594]|.
                     Discrimination against other tRNAs that contain queosine at
                     the wobble position is due to the C38 and C32 nucleotides 
                     of tRNA<sup>Asp</sup> |CITS: [18602926]|. Homologs of GluQ 
                     have so far only been found in bacteria |CITS: [16164993]|;
                     functional similarity to the YadB protein of <i>Neisseria 
                     meningitidis</i> suggests a ubiquitous function |CITS: 
                     [15096594]|.
                     Crystal structures of Glu-Q-RS alone |CITS: [15003446]| and
                     in complex with glutamate |CITS: [18602926]| have been 
                     solved at 1.5 and 1.75 &Aring; resolution.  A 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> atom is predicted to be present and 
                     coordinated by cysteine and tyrosine ligands |CITS: 
                     [15003446]|.  A C101S/C103S mutant still contains 0.2 moles
                     of zinc |CITS: [24505021]|.  Comparison with the structure 
                     of GluRS-Glu complex allowed insight into the structural 
                     elements that distinguish the catalytic properties of the 
                     two enzymes |CITS: [18602926]|. Disruption of the 
                     <i>gluQ</i> gene does not cause a detectable phenotype on 
                     rich or minimal growth media |CITS: [15096612]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [22905870][16164993][15138304]|"
