     gene            complement(156636..157091)
                     /gene="dksA"
                     /locus_tag="b0145"
                     /label=dksA
     CDS             complement(156636..157091)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dksA"
                     /locus_tag="b0145"
                     /product="bifunctional RNA polymerase-binding
                     transcription factor DksA / DksA-ppGpp"
                     /label=dksA
                     /note="The DksA protein binds directly to RNA polymerase, 
                     affecting transcript elongation and potentiating 
                     allosterically the effect of the alarmone ppGpp on 
                     transcription initiation |CITS: [15294156][29478808]|. DksA
                     binding to the RNA polymerase (RNAP) secondary channel 
                     interferes with the proper ribonucleotide positioning in 
                     the RNAP active site, decreasing open complex stability but
                     only at promoters with intrinsically unstable open 
                     complexes, such as the ribosomal promoters 
                     |CITS:[25916853]|. DksA together with ppGpp is required for
                     regulation of transcription of ribosomal RNA promoters 
                     |CITS: [15294157]|. DksA and ppGpp independently reduce 
                     transcription of the <i>rrsB</i>p1 promoter. The additions 
                     of both ppGpp and DksA have independent cumulative effects 
                     on transcription inhibition of the <i>rrsB</i>p1 promoter 
                     |CITS:[25916853]|. DksA appears to regulate genes involved 
                     in biofilm formation |CITS:[36326671]|.  DksA, when bound 
                     to ppGp, regulates cell division positively through 
                     transcription; an effect of these regulators on cell length
                     and cell filamentation has been observed |CITS: 
                     [37882534]|. This regulation is carried out indirectly 
                     through the FtsZ protein |CITS: [37882534]|. On the other 
                     hand, if ppGpp is absent, DksA negatively regulates cell 
                     division |CITS: [37882534]|.
                     DksA, together with ppGpp, inhibits full-length pRNA 
                     synthesis, a short RNA product (pRNA) synthesized from 6S 
                     RNA by RNAP:&sigma;70 |CITS:[38692564]|. The synthesis of 
                     pRNA is necessary for the release of RNA 
                     polymerase:&sigma;70 trapped in an inhibitory complex with 
                     6S RNA (6S RNA:RNAP:&sigma;70) |CITS:[36198425][38692564]|.
                     DksA and ppGpp weakly decrease rRNA extension of damaged 
                     DNA during transcription in RNA polymerase mutants |CITS: 
                     [35667439]|.
                     DksA is involved in translational regulation of RpoS |CITS:
                     [12142416]|.
                     DksA is critical for aerobic nitric oxide (NO) 
                     detoxification |CITS:[30366060]|, and it is involved in 
                     efficient acriflavine efflux |CITS:[36745554]|. Under 
                     ampicillin treatment, the activity of DksA appears to 
                     increase |CITS: [33731833]|.
                     DksA is essential for growth in M63 minimal medium with 
                     glycerol at 37&deg;C, but not for growth in LB medium 
                     |CITS: [34923900]|. <i>dksA</i> mutants have pleiotropic 
                     phenotypes. A null mutant is viable |CITS: [2180916]|.  A 
                     <i>dksA</i> mutant exhibited a defect in induction of RpoS 
                     production upon entry into stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [12193624]| or in the presence of ppGpp  |CITS: 
                     [12142416]|.  A <i>dksA</i> mutant exhibited a complex 
                     phenotype with respect to ppGpp regulation |CITS: 
                     [12142416]|.  DksA and the general stress response 
                     regulator RpoS play a critical role in surviving 
                     dehydration. DksA also has an RpoS-independent and 
                     ppGpp-related role in dehydration tolerance 
                     |CITS:[29457688]|.
                     Comparative RNA-seq of a <i>dksA</i> knockout strain and 
                     the wild type indicated that DksA activates the glycogen 
                     biosynthesis regulon and the universal quorum-sensing 
                     autoinducer AI-2 system and represses the citrate regulon, 
                     the pyrimidine biosynthesis genes, and the 
                     flagella-chemotaxis regulon |CITS: [34923900]|.
                     DksA is implicated in DNA repair; <i>dksA</i> mutants 
                     (<i>dksA::kan</i>) show increased sensitivity to compounds 
                     - nalidixic acid, polymyxin B and mitomycin C - which 
                     induce double strand breaks in replicating cells; 
                     inactivation of both DksA and RecN significantly reduces 
                     the ability of cells to survive chromosome breakage 
                     |CITS:[15948952][40352728]|. DksA plays an active role in 
                     the repair of nalidixic acid-induced DNA damage and 
                     interaction between DnaK and RNAP is required for this 
                     repair |CITS: [30779388]|.
                     A <i>dksA</i> mutant exhibited a defect in amino acid 
                     biosynthesis that was suppressed by an <i>rpoB</i> T563P 
                     mutation |CITS: [12142416]|.  A <i>dksA</i> mutation 
                     suppressed the replication defect of a plasmid with 
                     mutations at the origin of replication |CITS: [9068662]|. 
                     DksA overproduction suppressed the heat sensitivity of a 
                     <i>dnaK</i> or grpE280 mutant |CITS: [2180916][22267514]|. 
                     Overproduction also suppressed the heat sensitivity, but 
                     not the anucleate cell morphology, of a mukB106 mutant 
                     |CITS: [7984109]|. Overproduction partially suppressed the 
                     filamentous morphology of a <i>dnaK</i> or grpE280 mutant 
                     |CITS: [2180916]|. Overproduction suppressed phenotypes of 
                     a <i>prc</i> mutant |CITS: [8576052]|.  Overproduction also
                     suppressed the filamentous morphology and growth defect of 
                     a <i>yhhP</i> mutant in low-salt rich medium |CITS: 
                     [10830496]|. The Keio collection <i>dksA</i> mutant is 
                     sensitive to lithium (<90% growth at 100mM Li) |CITS: 
                     [39983623]|.
                     DksA regulates the production of inorganic polyphosphate 
                     (polyP) without the help of ppGpp |CITS:[30745375]|. 
                     Specifically, <i>dksA</i> positively regulates polyP 
                     synthesis upon nutrient limitation |CITS: 
                     [32341074][35482529]|. DksA must bind to RNA polymerase to 
                     activate polyP synthesis |CITS: [32341074]|. On the other 
                     hand, DksA is required for the formation of new replication
                     origins and constitutive stable DNA replication (cSDR), and
                     it promotes the repair of nalidixic acid (Nal)-induced DNA 
                     double-strand breaks (DSBs) |CITS:[30779388]|.
                     DksA: ""dnaK suppressor"" |CITS: [2180916]|.
                     MsmA: ""multicopy suppressor of mukB106"" |CITS: 
                     [7984109]|.
                     The crystal structure of DksA has been solved at 2.0 
                     &Aring; resolution, and it shows similarity to the 
                     structure of GreA, the transcription cleavage factor |CITS:
                     [15294156]|. On the other hand, the X-ray crystal 
                     structures of RNAP has been solved in complex with DksA 
                     alone and with ppGpp at 4.5 &Aring; resolution 
                     |CITS:[29478808]|.
                     Based on overexpression, deletion, transcription profiling,
                     and RNA polymerase mutant experiments, mutual and 
                     antagonistic competition effects between GreA, GreB, and 
                     DksA on RNA polymerase activity are observed when ppGpp is 
                     absent but are more pronounced when DksA and ppGpp are not 
                     present |CITS: [22056927]|. DksA overproduction suppressed 
                     the temperature-sensitive phenotype of a  dnaKJ and a 
                     grpE280 mutants |CITS:[2180916][22267514]|.
                     GreA/B factors contribute significantly to complex 
                     regulation by DksA and ppGpp. Here, GreA has a major role 
                     and GreB has a minor role in the DksA-ppGpp regulatory 
                     network |CITS: [22056927]|. An interaction network of GreA,
                     GreB, DksA, ppGpp, and RNA polymerase has been proposed 
                     |CITS:[22056927]|.
                     The aspartic acid residue D74 located in the coiled-coil 
                     tip of DksA is crucial for function of both positive and 
                     negative control of transcription initiation <i>in 
                     vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i> |CITS:[22200485]|. It is also 
                     key for distinguishing its functions from those of Gre 
                     factors |CITS:[22200485]|. A substitution in this residue 
                     eliminates DksA function without affecting its apparent 
                     affinity |CITS:[22200485]|. Two models have been proposed 
                     for the DksA-RNAP interaction: i) the coiled-coil tip of 
                     DksA, specifically the D74 and A76 residues, interact and 
                     stabilize the &beta;' trigger loop of RNAP, altering its 
                     conformation, and ii) D74 and A76 are affected by the 
                     conformation changing the timing required for RNAP 
                     |CITS:[22200485]|.
                     Expression is increased under low-pH conditions |CITS: 
                     [12107143]|. Increased ppGpp abundance does not induce DksA
                     production |CITS: [12142416]|. The cellular concentration 
                     of DksA is constant under different growth conditions, and 
                     ppGpp serves as the signal of nutrient limitation 
                     |CITS:[15294157]|. DksA is stable and long-lived and 
                     remains constant under log- and stationary-phase growth 
                     conditions  |CITS:[21496125]|. Some regulatory functions of
                     DksA are conserved between <i>E. coli</i> and 
                     <i>Salmonella</i> spp., according to analysis of 
                     transcriptome profiles |CITS: [34923900]|. DksA has a 
                     half-life of 44 +/- 9 minutes |CITS:[21496125]|.
                     Through its PPIase activity, overexpression of <i>dksA</i> 
                     is capable of suppressing the effects caused by a mutant 
                     lacking the periplasmic domain of |FRAME: EG12049-MONOMER 
                     LapC|, including growth and permeability defects, as well 
                     as the reduction in the amount of lipopolysaccharide due to
                     the hyperdegradation of the |FRAME: 
                     UDPACYLGLCNACDEACETYL-MONOMER LpxC| protein |CITS: 
                     [37894855]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [15568992]|"
