     gene            complement(157988..158518)
                     /gene="thpR"
                     /locus_tag="b0147"
                     /label=thpR
     CDS             complement(157988..158518)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="thpR"
                     /locus_tag="b0147"
                     /product="bifunctional RNA 2',3'-cyclic phosphodiesterase
                     / 2'-5' RNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.4.58"
                     /label=thpR
                     /note="ThpR belongs to the family of two-histidine (2H) 
                     phosphoesterase enzymes and has been shown to be an ""end 
                     healing"" cyclic phosphodiesterase (CPDase) that hydrolyzes
                     RNA 2',3'-cyclic phosphodiesters |CITS: [25239919]|.
                     Biophysical characterization of ThpR and comparison to two 
                     other cyclic nucleotide phosphodiesterases from yeast and 
                     <i>Arabidopsis</i> has been reported.  ThpR appears to 
                     exist as a monomer in solution |CITS: [11866446]|.  A 
                     crystal structure of ThpR in complex with 2'-AMP has been 
                     solved at 2 &Aring; resolution, implicating His43 as a 
                     general acid catalyst and His125 as a general base 
                     catalyst.  Arg130 may have a role in stabilizing the 
                     transition state.  Site-directed mutagenesis of these amino
                     acids confirmed their importance for catalytic activity 
                     |CITS: [25239919]|.
                     ThpR was initially identified as a ""2'-5' RNA ligase"" 
                     that catalyzes the ligation and cleavage of 2'-5' linkages 
                     in RNA, a reversible transesterification reaction, <i>in 
                     vitro</i>.  This activity was demonstrated using tRNA 
                     halves prepared with yeast pre-tRNA and splicing enzymes; 
                     the reaction does not require nucleotide triphosphates, as 
                     other known ligases do |CITS: [6347395][8940112]|.  This 
                     reaction is probably not physiologically relevant |CITS: 
                     [25239919]|.
                     Overproduction of ThpR leads to reduced growth rate and the
                     inability to grow at 43&deg;C; deletion of <i>thpR</i> has 
                     no effect on growth under laboratory conditions |CITS: 
                     [8940112]|.
                     ThpR: ""<b>t</b>wo-<b>h</b>istidine 2',3'-cyclic 
                     <b>p</b>hosphodiesterase acting on <b>R</b>NA"" |CITS: 
                     [25239919]|"
                     /translation="MSEPQRLFFAIDLPAEIREQIIHWRATHFPPEAGRPVAADNLHLT
                     LAFLGEVSAEKEKALSLLAGRIRQPGFTLTLDDAGQWLRSRVVWLGMRQPPRGLIQLAN
                     MLRSQAARSGCFQSNRPFHPHITLLRDASEAVTIPPPGFNWSYAVTEFTLYASSFARGR
                     TRYTPLKRWALTQ"
                     /go_component="cytosol [goid 0005829] [evidence IEA] [PMID
