     gene            167062..167952
                     /gene="fhuD"
                     /locus_tag="b0152"
                     /label=fhuD
     CDS             167062..167952
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fhuD"
                     /locus_tag="b0152"
                     /product="multifunctional Fe(3+) hydroxamate ABC
                     transporter periplasmic binding protein / Fe(3+) 
                     hydroxamate ABC transporter / iron (III) hydroxamate ABC 
                     transporter / ferric coprogen ABC transporter / ferrichrome
                     ABC transporter"
                     /EC_number="7.2.2.16"
                     /label=fhuD
                     /note="FhuD is the periplasmic siderophore binding
                     component of an iron(III) hydroxamate ABC transporter. 
                     Purified FhuD binds ferrichrome (<i>K<sub>d</sub></i> = 1 
                     &micro;M), ferric coprogen (<i>K<sub>d</sub></i> = 0.3 
                     &micro;M), ferric aerobactin (<i>K<sub>d</sub></i> = 0.4 
                     &micro;M) and the antibiotic albomycin 
                     (<i>K<sub>d</sub></i> = 5.4 &micro;M). It also binds 
                     ferrichrome A, ferrioxamine B and ferrioxamine E with lower
                     affinity (<i>K<sub>d</sub></i> = 79, 36, and 42 &micro;M, 
                     respectively) |CITS: [8522527]|.
                     Peptide mapping experiments suggest that FhuD binds to 
                     periplasmic loop 2, cytoplasmic loop 7 and the 
                     transmembrane region of FhuB |CITS: [9426146]|.
                     FhuD bound to the ferrichrome analogue, gallichrome, has a 
                     'kidney bean shaped bilobate' structure; the protein 
                     consists of N and C terminal domains joined by a long 
                     &alpha;-helix; the interaction between domains is extensive
                     and mostly hydrophobic; gallichrome is bound in a shallow 
                     groove formed between the two domains; a large portion of 
                     the bound gallichrome molecule remains solvent exposed 
                     |CITS:[10742172], [11805094]|."
