     gene            173097..173441
                     /gene="erpA"
                     /locus_tag="b0156"
                     /label=erpA
     CDS             173097..173441
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="erpA"
                     /locus_tag="b0156"
                     /product="bifunctional iron-sulfur cluster insertion
                     protein ErpA / [Fe-S] cluster insertion protein ErpA"
                     /label=erpA
                     /note="ErpA is an A-type iron-sulfur carrier protein; it
                     can bind both [2Fe-2S] and [4Fe-4S] clusters.  <i>In 
                     vitro</i>, ErpA can transfer a [4Fe-4S] cluster to |FRAME: 
                     EG10370-MONOMER ""apo-IspG""| |CITS: [17698959]|. 
                     Functional redundancy between the three A-type carriers 
                     (ATCs) in <i>E. coli</i>, ErpA (an ATC-I) and IscA and SufA
                     (both belonging to the ATC-II family), has been 
                     investigated |CITS: [19478995]|.  ErpA is required for 
                     maturation of |FRAME: EG11212-MONOMER NsrR|, but not 
                     |FRAME: G7326-MONOMER IscR| |CITS: [23320508]|.  IscA and 
                     ErpA appear to have independent roles in the delivery of 
                     iron-sulfur clusters to individual subunits of the |FRAME: 
                     FHLMULTI-CPLX FHL complex| |CITS: [23558265]|.
                     ErpA and |FRAME: G7748-MONOMER NfuA| directly interact with
                     each other.  The half life of the ErpA Fe-S cluster upon 
                     oxygen exposure is 54 minutes; in the presence of NfuA, its
                     half life increases to 90 minutes.  ErpA also directly 
                     interacts with IscA and SufA as well as with client 
                     proteins |CITS: [29626095]|.
                     <i>erpA</i> is essential for aerobic growth and under 
                     conditions of anaerobic respiration; the conserved cysteine
                     residues are important for <i>in vivo</i> function.  The 
                     growth defect of an <i>erpA</i> null mutant can be 
                     partially rescued by expression of the eukaryotic 
                     MVA-dependent pathway for IPP synthesis (|FRAME: PWY-922|),
                     whose enzymes do not contain Fe-S clusters.  An <i>erpA</i>
                     mutant contains 5-6-fold less quinone than wild type |CITS:
                     [17698959]|.  An <i>erpA</i> null mutant contains no active
                     |FRAME: FORMATEDEHYDROGN-CPLX Fdh-N|, |FRAME: 
                     FORMATEDEHYDROGO-CPLX Fdh-O|, |FRAME: NITRATREDUCTA-CPLX 
                     nitrate reductase| |CITS: [22081393]|, and |FRAME: 
                     FHLMULTI-CPLX FHL| |CITS: [23558265]| and no detectable 
                     hydrogenase 1 or hydrogenase 2 activity |CITS: [22363723]| 
                     due to the absence of the Fe-S cluster-containing small 
                     subunits of these enzymes.  ErpA is also involved in 
                     maturation of |FRAME: CPLX0-7761 aconitase B|, |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-8160| (complex II) |CITS: [29626095]|, |FRAME: 
                     NADH-DHI-CPLX| (complex I) |CITS: [29626095][32777304]|, 
                     |FRAME: EG11595-MONOMER MoaA|, and |FRAME: PD00197 FNR| 
                     |CITS: [33782054]|.
                     <i>erpA</i> is regulated at the level of transcription by 
                     IscR |CITS: [16677314]| and post-transcriptionally by RyhB 
                     |CITS: [23980183][27651365]|.  <i>erpA</i> expression is 
                     repressed by RyhB in response to iron starvation, while 
                     repression of <i>erpA</i> by IscR is alleviated under 
                     iron-limited conditions.  This type of regulatory circuit 
                     is called an ""incoherent network"" |CITS: [27651365]|. 
                     ErpA: ""<b>e</b>ssential <b>r</b>espiratory <b>p</b>rotein 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [17698959]|
                     Review: |CITS: [20467446]|"
