     gene            175724..177241
                     /gene="dgt"
                     /locus_tag="b0160"
                     /label=dgt
     CDS             175724..177241
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dgt"
                     /locus_tag="b0160"
                     /product="dGTP triphosphohydrolase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.5.1"
                     /label=dgt
                     /note="Deoxyguanosinetriphosphate triphosphohydrolase
                     (dGTPase, Dgt) is a unique nucleoside triphosphatase which 
                     specifically hydrolyzes dGTP to deoxyguanosine and 
                     inorganic tripolyphosphate |CITS: [13563461][2826481]|. 
                     Possible physiological roles of the enzyme have only 
                     recently been elucidated.  Dgt may be involved in 
                     replication fidelity via its effect on the cellular pool of
                     dGTP |CITS: [18776019]|.  The level of Dgt expression 
                     correlates with the ability of cells to salvage thymine in 
                     the absence of functional <i>de novo</i> biosynthesis in a 
                     <i>thyA</i> mutant, presumably due to its effect on the 
                     intracellular deoxyribose-1-phosphate pool |CITS: 
                     [21736641]|.
                     dGTPase is able to bind single-stranded DNA with relatively
                     high affinity |CITS: [8397198]|.  Crystal structures of the
                     enzyme have been solved |CITS: [25694425][31019074]|.  
                     Although dGTPase was thought to be a homotetramer based on 
                     a prior measurement of its native molecular weight |CITS: 
                     [2826481]|, the crystal structure showed a homohexameric 
                     conformation.  Subunits with bound ssDNA show significant 
                     conformational changes, including in the active site.  DNA 
                     binding activates the enzyme, lowering the K<sub>m</sub> 
                     for dGTP.  An S34D G37E double mutant abolishes binding and
                     stimulation of enzymatic activity by DNA |CITS: 
                     [25694425]|.  At physiologically relevant GTP:dGTP ratios 
                     (higher than 10:1), GTP appears to act as a competitive 
                     inhibitor of dGTPase |CITS: [31019074]|.
                     Crystal structures of various free and ligand- or 
                     inhibitor-bound forms of the enzyme enabled investigation 
                     of the  substrate selectivity of dGTPase.  dGTP, but not 
                     GTP binding results in conformational changes within the 
                     active site.  Mutagenesis of the active site residues H126,
                     E129, or Y272 to alanine resulted in loss of catalytic 
                     activity.  The binding pocket forms a tight fit with 
                     extensive hydrogen bonding around dGTP |CITS: [31019074]|.
                     The <i>optA1</i> allele of <i>dgt</i>, a promoter mutation 
                     |CITS: [2157212]|, causes increased production of dGTPase, 
                     which makes the cells unable to support growth of phage T7 
                     containing mutations in gene 1.2 |CITS: [2833745]|.  The 
                     wild-type gene 1.2 protein is a specific inhibitor of 
                     dGTPase by binding to the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [2843524][2155228]|.
                     A <i>dgt</i> null mutation has no detectable growth 
                     phenotype in rich medium |CITS: [2157212]|.  Several 
                     <i>dgt</i> transposon insertion mutants were shown to have 
                     a mutator phenotype |CITS: [18776019]|.  When an <i>optA1 
                     gpt</i> double mutant is grown in a medium lacking an 
                     exogenous source of purines, the dGPT pool collapses, and 
                     the cells eventually die in a manner similar to thymineless
                     death |CITS: [24810600]|.
                     OptA: |CITS: [7012382]|
                     Dgt: ""<b>dGT</b>Pase"" |CITS: [2559296]|"
