     gene            complement(182465..185137)
                     /gene="glnD"
                     /locus_tag="b0167"
                     /label=glnD
     CDS             complement(182465..185137)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glnD"
                     /locus_tag="b0167"
                     /product="multifunctional protein-PII 
                     uridylyltransferase/uridylyl-removing enzyme / [protein 
                     PII-1] uridylyltransferase / [protein PII-2] 
                     uridylyltransferase / [protein-PII-UMP] deuridylylation / 
                     [protein-PII-1-UMP] deuridylylation"
                     /label=glnD
                     /note="Uridylyltransferase (UTase) encoded by <i>glnD</i>
                     is a bifunctional protein that catalyzes the uridylylation 
                     as well as the de-uridylylation of |FRAME: PROTEIN-PII|. 
                     Uridylylation and deuridylylation are distinct reactions; 
                     uridylylation involves the transfer of a UMP group (derived
                     from UTP) to PII-1 to form PII-1-UMP and pyrophosphate; 
                     deuridylylation of PII-1-UMP is a hydrolytic reaction that 
                     results in the release of UMP and PII-1 
                     |CITS:[6105849][6130097]|. GlnD may function to sense 
                     cellular glutamine concentration and relay this information
                     via the uridylylation status of PII-1 (see |CITS: 
                     [24296575]|
                     Under limiting nitrogen conditions PII-1-UMP interacts with
                     |FRAME: GLNE-MONOMER glutamine synthetase (GS) 
                     adenylyltransferase| which catalyzes removal of AMP from 
                     (and consequent activation of) |FRAME: 
                     GLUTAMINESYN-OLIGOMER glutamine synthetase|. In the absence
                     of UTase there is very little activation of glutamine 
                     synthetase in response to nitrogen deprivation |CITS: 
                     [26660][2865248][2874557]|. Glutamine inhibits the 
                     uridylylation reaction and stimulates the deuridylylation 
                     reaction; Mg<sup>2+</sup> is the physiologically relevent 
                     metal ion cofactor |CITS: [9737855]|. UTase can use other 
                     nucleotides to modify PII-1 <i>in vitro</i> but PII-1-UMP 
                     is most effective for activating NtrB phosphatase activity 
                     and GlnE GS deadenylylation activity |CITS: [9737855]|. 
                     GlnD can also catalyse the uridylylation and 
                     deuridylylation of the <i>glnK</i>-encoded PII protein 
                     (|FRAME: PII2-CPLX PII-2|) |CITS: [8843440][10231487]|; the
                     deuridylylation of GlnK-UMP by GlnD is very slow |CITS: 
                     [10231487]|.
                     GlnD contains 4 domains: an N-terminal 
                     nucleotidyltransferase (NT) domain associated with 
                     uridylylation activity; a central HD (conserved histidine 
                     (H) and aspartate (D)) domain essential for deuridylylation
                     activity and two C-terminal ACT (aspartokinase, chorismate 
                     mutase, and TyrA ) domains that may be involved in sensing 
                     glutamine |CITS: [20363937]|. The uridylylation and 
                     deuridylylation reactions may |CITS: [9737855]| or may not 
                     |CITS: [20363937]| share the same active site. The central 
                     HD domain may act to pass the signal from the glutamine 
                     sensing ACT domains to the N-terminal NT domain; PII-1 
                     inhibits the uridylylation activity of GlnD by binding to 
                     the HD domain |CITS: [23088522]|. The UTase activity of 
                     GlnD is subject to substrate inhibition by PII-1 |CITS: 
                     [23088566]|. <i>glnD</i> is expressed constitutively at a 
                     low level |CITS: [8412694][9749538]|. ArcA appears to 
                     activate glnD gene expression under anaerobiosis. A 
                     putative ArcA binding site is located 560 bp upstream of 
                     this gene |CITS: [15699038]|."
