     gene            complement(185199..185993)
                     /gene="map"
                     /locus_tag="b0168"
                     /label=map
     CDS             complement(185199..185993)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="map"
                     /locus_tag="b0168"
                     /product="methionine aminopeptidase"
                     /EC_number="3.4.11.18"
                     /label=map
                     /note="All known proteins in <i>E. coli</i> use N-formyl 
                     methionine (N-fMet) as the first amino acid in a peptide 
                     chain. Amino-terminal maturation involves two enzymes, 
                     |FRAME: EG11440-MONOMER| (PDF) which removes the formyl 
                     group, and methionine aminopeptidase (MAP), which catalyzes
                     the removal of the deformylated methionine residue |CITS: 
                     [ColiSalII][3027045]|. Oxidation of N-terminal fMet 
                     prevents hydrolysis by MAP as determined using bottom-up 
                     proteomics |CITS: [39499632]|.
                     MAP interacts directly with the ribosome at the ribosomal 
                     exit tunnel and competes with |FRAME: EG11440-MONOMER| for 
                     overlapping sites |CITS: [23770820]|. MAP does not 
                     interfere with binding of |FRAME: EG11003-MONOMER trigger 
                     factor| or |FRAME: SRP-CPLX SRP| to translating ribosomes 
                     |CITS: [24939037]|.
                     The activity of MAP is dependent on the identity of the 
                     second, third and fourth amino acid residues of the target 
                     protein |CITS: [2682640][16963780][3027045]|; the substrate
                     specificity has been analyzed in detail |CITS: 
                     [20521764][22085501]|. The most preferred amino acid 
                     residue in the position following the fMet is alanine.
                     The <i>map</i> gene encoding methionine aminopeptidase is 
                     essential for growth in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [2544569]|. 
                     <i>E. coli</i> MAP is a type-I enzyme and is a potential 
                     antibiotic target; selective inhibitors have been designed 
                     |CITS: [16142902][19167218][19198897][21575996][22489069][ 
                     25293447][26099541]|.
                     Crystal structures of the protein itself and in complex 
                     with various inhibitors have been reported |CITS: [8471602 
                     ][10387007][15506752][16300729][17120228][17948983][1809332
                     5][18785729]|, and the catalytic mechanism was studied in 
                     co-crystals with various substrate and transition state 
                     analogs |CITS: [10555963][16769889]|.
                     MAP contains two metal binding sites; reports differ on 
                     whether a single metal ion is sufficient for catalysis 
                     |CITS: [11683640][16769889][17948983][20017927]|, or both 
                     are required |CITS: [17929833][19019076]|. The 
                     physiologically relevant metal cofactor of MAP is most 
                     likely Fe<sup>2+</sup> rather than Co<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [18669631][22112844]|. Studies with inhibitors that were 
                     active <i>in vitro</i>, but not <i>in vivo</i>, indicated 
                     that high metal concentrations in <i>in vitro</i> assays 
                     may have led to artefacts |CITS: [15880695][16420038]|. The
                     activity of enzymes containing mutations in predicted 
                     active site and metal-binding residues has been measured 
                     |CITS: [10419973][12755633][14976199][18855426][18952013][ 
                     19019076]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8199241][10708856][24721450][26268344]|"
