     gene            187344..188195
                     /gene="tsf"
                     /locus_tag="b0170"
                     /label=tsf
     CDS             187344..188195
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tsf"
                     /locus_tag="b0170"
                     /product="protein chain elongation factor EF-Ts"
                     /label=tsf
                     /note="EF-Ts is an elongation factor associated with the 
                     ribosome; it functions as the guanine nucleotide exchange 
                     factor for the EF-Tu GTPase.  EF-Ts was first identified in
                     the ""T"" (for transfer) fraction consisting of a stable 
                     (Ts) and an unstable (Tu) component |CITS: [4289971]|.  Two
                     molecules each of elongation factor Ts and |FRAME: 
                     EG11036-MONOMER ""elongation factor Tu""| assemble into a 
                     complex |CITS: [4893410][6681813][8596629]|.  EF-Ts is 
                     essential for growth |CITS: [2498311][16738554]|.
                     EF-Ts interacts with aminoacyl-tRNA-bound EF-Tu and 
                     regulates its affinity for GTP and aa-tRNA ligands.  EF-Ts 
                     may regulate the stability of the EF-Tu-GTP-aa-tRNA ternary
                     complex by facilitating the conformational changes within 
                     EF-Tu that are responsible for binding and release of 
                     aa-tRNAs |CITS: [23539628]|.
                     EF-Ts enhances the rate of dissociation of GDP from EF-Tu 
                     within the EF-Tu-GDP-EF-Ts complex |CITS: 
                     [7016856][6681813]| by a factor of 6x10<sup>4</sup> |CITS: 
                     [11772013]|.  EF-Ts dissociates from the ternary 
                     EF-Tu-GDP/GTP-EF-Ts complex 10<sup>3</sup>-10<sup>4</sup> 
                     times faster than from the binary EF-Tu-EF-Ts complex. 
                     Under conditions resembling those <i>in vivo</i>, the 
                     overall nucleotide exchange rate catalyzed by EF-Ts is 30 
                     s<sup>-1</sup> |CITS: [11772013]|.  The kinetics of the 
                     nucleotide exchange reaction has also been modelled |CITS: 
                     [14715237]|.
                     A crystal structure of the EF-Tu-EF-Ts complex (EF-T) has 
                     been solved at 2.5 &Aring; resolution.  It shows two 
                     subunits each of EF-Ts and EF-Tu, where a coiled-coil motif
                     of EF-Ts appears to be primarily responsible for 
                     dimerization |CITS: [8596629]|.  An EF-Ts mutant lacking 
                     this coiled-coil motif shows near-normal growth, but forms 
                     unstable EF-Tu-EF-Ts complexes in the presence of guanine 
                     nucleotides |CITS: [14622294]|.
                     The N-terminal part of EF-Ts is required for the nucleotide
                     exchange activity |CITS: [7615087]| and appears to 
                     determine the strength of the interaction with EF-Tu |CITS:
                     [9268331]|.  A conformational change in the N terminus may 
                     be essential for binding EF-Tu |CITS: [9390186]|.  Further 
                     site-directed mutagenesis showed that residues within the 
                     N-terminal domain and subdomain C are important for 
                     interaction with EF-Tu |CITS: [9468511]|. Residues that are
                     thought to be involved in the guanine nucleotide exchange 
                     reaction have been mutagenized.  The D80 and F81 residues 
                     are important, but not essential for activity; they appear 
                     to affect the interaction with EF-Tu |CITS: [8765000]|.
                     The mechanism of nucleotide exchange has been investigated.
                      Initial contact of the N-terminal domain of EF-Ts with 
                     helix D of EF-Tu appears to weaken interactions with the 
                     guanine base; contacts with the phosphate binding site, 
                     promoting release of the phosphate moiety, take place later
                     |CITS: [11744709]|.
                     EF-Ts enhances phosphorylation of EF-Tu |CITS: [7782317]| 
                     and can act as a ""steric chaperone"" for EF-Tu mutants 
                     which do not fold correctly |CITS: [11456491]|.
                     In addition to its function as a translation elongation 
                     factor, EF-Ts is a host-supplied component of the phage 
                     Q&beta; RNA replicase complex |CITS: [4624757][764866]|. An
                     EF-Ts mutant that forms unstable EF-Tu-EF-Ts complexes is 
                     unable to form an active Q&beta;-polymerase complex; EF-Ts 
                     may thus be required to stably trap EF-Tu within the 
                     Q&beta;-polymerase |CITS: [14583631]|.  Crystal structures 
                     of the Q&beta; replicase core complex have been solved 
                     |CITS: [20534494][20798060]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [4941237][12932732]|
                     "
