     gene            189359..189916
                     /gene="frr"
                     /locus_tag="b0172"
                     /label=frr
     CDS             189359..189916
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="frr"
                     /locus_tag="b0172"
                     /product="ribosome-recycling factor"
                     /label=frr
                     /note="Ribosome recycling factor (RRF) recycles the
                     ribosome upon translation termination |CITS: 
                     [8183897][9463391]|.  RRF, release factor RF3, and 
                     elongation factor EF-G are involved in this recycling 
                     process |CITS: [4563926][4583357][9233822][9344747]|.  At 
                     termination, RRF and EF-G catalyze release of the 50S 
                     ribosomal subunit from the 70S complex, a GTPase-dependent 
                     process |CITS: [10360176]|; EF-G acts to release RRF from 
                     the ribosome |CITS: [12960150][15228531][15461445]|. 
                     Release factor RF1 inhibits RRF activity |CITS: [9344747]|.
                      RRF with EF-2 and RF3 increase dissociation of 
                     peptidyl-tRNA from the ribosome |CITS: [9451005]|.
                     RRF stimulates translation |CITS: [410018][7016861]|, 
                     inhibits amber readthrough |CITS: 
                     [1100117][7016861][7031641]|, and enables correct 
                     translation initiation of a translationally coupled open 
                     reading frame |CITS: [10899132]|.  Upon inactivation of a 
                     temperature-sensitive allele of <i>rrf in vivo</i>, the 
                     ribosome re-initiates translation downstream of the stop 
                     codon |CITS: [9463391]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> growing exponentially in rich media contain 
                     approximately 16,500 molecules of RRF per cell |CITS: 
                     [10690639]|.
                     RRF has been structurally characterized |CITS: 
                     [10656793][10666636][10811627][11883785]|.  Crystal 
                     structures |CITS: [10811627][11752787]| and NMR studies 
                     |CITS: [11883785][12680764]| are presented.  RRF has 
                     structural similarity to tRNAs |CITS: 
                     [10811627][11752787]|, and was thought to bind to the 
                     ribosome like tRNA |CITS: [11214182][11980724][12138121]|; 
                     however, protection assays |CITS: [12372306]| and cryo-EM 
                     |CITS: [15178758]| indicate that the interaction of RRF 
                     with the ribosome is physically distinct from the 
                     interaction of tRNA with the ribosome.  RRF induces 
                     conformational changes in some functional domains of the 
                     ribosome |CITS: [15178758]|.
                     RRF shows physical interactions with 50S ribosomal 
                     subunits, and shows weaker interaction with 70S ribosomes 
                     and 30S ribosomal subunits |CITS: [11168582][12841582]|. 
                     Interaction with the 50S ribosomal subunit involves RRF 
                     residue R132 |CITS: [11168582]| and is sensitive to some 
                     antibiotics |CITS: [11168582][12841582]|.
                     RRF is essential for viability |CITS: [8183897][9463391]|. 
                     A mutant lacking 25 C-terminal residues is inviable |CITS: 
                     [10214965]|.  A collection of conditional lethal and null 
                     alleles of <i>frr</i> |CITS: [10656793][15380559]| as well 
                     as an amber mutation |CITS: [15090508]| have been 
                     generated.
                     Frr: ""<b>f</b>actor for <b>r</b>ibosome <b>r</b>ecycling""
                     |CITS: [2661533]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8781287][9150873][8861897][12762054]|"
