     gene            191271..191331
                     /gene="ispZ"
                     /locus_tag="b4809"
                     /label=ispZ
     misc_RNA        191271..191331
                     /gene="ispZ"
                     /locus_tag="b4809"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA IspZ"
                     /note="IspZ is a 5' UTR-derived small RNA that is
                     generated by a Rho-dependent mechanism.  Northern analysis 
                     reveals two transcripts of 81 and 60 nt that may be derived
                     from different transcription start sites with a common 3' 
                     end.  IspZ is abundantly expressed in exponential and 
                     stationary phase cells grown in either LB or M9 glucose 
                     medium; abundance was only slightly affected in a 
                     &Delta;<i>hfq</i> background |CITS: [33460557]|.
                     IspZ overexpression leads to decreased levels of the 
                     |FRAME: OXYS-RNA|; IspZ may act as a sponge for OxyS |CITS:
                     [33460557]|."
     gene            191390..192151
                     /gene="ispU"
                     /locus_tag="b0174"
                     /label=ispU
     CDS             191390..192151
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ispU"
                     /locus_tag="b0174"
                     /product="ditrans,polycis-undecaprenyl-diphosphate
                     synthase [(2E,6E)-farnesyl-diphosphate specific]"
                     /EC_number="2.5.1.31"
                     /label=ispU
                     /note="Undecaprenyl pyrophosphate (UPP) synthase (IspU) 
                     catalyzes the condensation reactions resulting in the 
                     formation of UPP, a <i>di-trans,poly-cis</i>-undecaprenyl 
                     pyrophosphate that functions as the lipid carrier for 
                     bacterial cell wall carbohydrates.  To generate UPP, eight 
                     isopentenyl diphosphate molecules are sequentially added to
                     farnesyl diphosphate with <i>cis</i> stereochemistry |CITS:
                     [3897217][3519603][9882662][10217761]|.
                     Under various reaction conditions, the enzyme can produce 
                     C50 intermediates as well as larger C60-C75 products in 
                     addition to UPP.  Triton X-100 activates the reaction by 
                     accelerating a step after the rate-limiting IPP 
                     condensation reactions |CITS: [10978182]|.  Site-directed 
                     mutagenesis of conserved residues identified amino acids 
                     that are required for catalysis and/or play a role in 
                     binding of IPP |CITS: [11076526][15447632]|, and play a 
                     role in product chain length determination |CITS: 
                     [11581264]|.  Molecular dynamics simulations indicate that 
                     IspU is a highly flexible protein |CITS: [21294851]|.  A 
                     conformational change of the enzyme during catalysis is 
                     thought to occur |CITS: [11744728]|.  The length of a 
                     flexible loop region appears to regulate the conformational
                     change |CITS: [14661956]|.  Single-turnover experiments 
                     support a concerted condensation-elimination mechanism for 
                     the enzyme |CITS: [20828539]|.  Substrate specificity has 
                     been studied, and the shape of both the active site and the
                     product are predicted to determine the chain length of the 
                     product |CITS: [15447632]|.  An enzyme containing the W31R 
                     mutation has severely decreased enzymatic activity |CITS: 
                     [29986944]|.
                     Crystal structures of IspU have been solved, revealing a 
                     possible reaction mechanism |CITS: [11581264][12756244][15 
                     044730][15788389][17535895][21294851]|. UPP synthase 
                     appears to be a dimer in solution and in the crystal 
                     structure |CITS: [9882662][12756244]|.
                     <i>ispU</i> is essential in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [10217761][16738554]|.  Mutants with temperature-sensitive 
                     alleles of <i>ispU</i> are sensitive to low osmolarity of 
                     the growth medium and have a cell shape defect when grown 
                     at the restrictive temperature |CITS: [10217761]|. 
                     Replacing wild type <i>ispU</i> with the <i>uppS31</i> 
                     missense allele is lethal; suppressor mutations in 
                     <i>ispH</i> and <i>idi</i> enable growth at low temperature
                     |CITS: [29986944]|.  UPP production and peptidoglycan 
                     synthesis are synergistic; lowering both at the same time 
                     leads to cell lysis |CITS: [31022322]|. Inhibitors of the 
                     enzyme have been investigated as novel antimicrobial agents
                     |CITS: [18295483][20476728][24827744] 
                     [25574764][27571880][30692977][32437130]|. <i>ispU</i> gene
                     expression is repressed by MetJ, which appears to bind a 
                     site upstream of this gene |CITS: [16515535]|.
                     Rth: ""<b>R</b>ER <b>t</b>wo <b>h</b>omologue"" |CITS: 
                     [10217761]|<br>IspU: <b>is</b>o<b>p</b>renoid<br>UppS: 
                     <b>Upp</b> <b>s</b>ynthetase |CITS: [9882662]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10586494][26443724][24799078]|"
