     gene            193033..194385
                     /gene="rseP"
                     /locus_tag="b0176"
                     /label=rseP
     CDS             193033..194385
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rseP"
                     /locus_tag="b0176"
                     /product="trifunctional intramembrane zinc metalloprotease
                     RseP / lipoprotein signal peptide peptidase / signal 
                     peptide peptidase"
                     /label=rseP
                     /note="RseP is an integral membrane, zinc-containing
                     site-2 metalloprotease (S2P) which catalyses hydrolytic 
                     cleavage of peptide bonds within the hydrophobic lipid 
                     bilayer. In response to extracytoplasmic stress, the site-1
                     protease |FRAME: CPLX0-8183 DegS| and RseP catalyse 
                     sequential cleavage of the anti-sigma factor |FRAME: 
                     EG12341-MONOMER RseA| which releases the 
                     RseA-&sigma;<sup>E</sup> complex from the membrane and 
                     ultimately activates the |FRAME: RPOE-MONOMER 
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup>|-mediated stress response |CITS: 
                     [12183368][12183369][11689431]|. RseP degrades remnant 
                     signal peptides that are left in the inner membrane after 
                     their cleavage from preproteins and prolipoproteins by 
                     signal peptidases |CITS: [21810987]|. RseP cleaves the 
                     transmembrane region of |FRAME: EG10292-MONOMER FecR| - the
                     last step in a sequential processing cascade that is 
                     required for |FRAME: PD00440 FecI|-mediated transcription 
                     of the <i>fec</i> operon |CITS: [33865858]|.
                     Purified RseP can cleave the transmembrane sequence of RseA
                     and other engineered membrane proteins with diverse 
                     transmembrane sequences |CITS: [15496982]|. Purifed RseP 
                     can degrade &sigma;<sup>E</sup> and &sigma;<sup>32</sup> 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [11689431]|. RseP cleaves the 
                     remnant signal peptides from a variety of secretory 
                     proteins <i>in vivo</i> including OmpF, LivK, SecM, PhoA 
                     and others; RseP cleaves remnant signal peptides from 
                     proteins that are processed via the Sec and Tat pathways; 
                     RseP cleaves synthetic &beta;-lactamase remnant signal 
                     peptides within the hydrophobic core region |CITS: 
                     [21810987][15496982]|. RseP may cleave small membrane 
                     proteins; RseP cleaves the small toxic membrane protein 
                     |FRAME: MONOMER0-1604 HokB|; RseP-mediated cleavage 
                     suppresses HokB cytotoxicity |CITS: [37409810]|.
                     RseP has four membrane spanning sequences, and both termini
                     are located in the periplasm |CITS: [11750129][11867724]|. 
                     The protein contains a potential zinc-binding motif, a PDZ 
                     domain and a conserved C-terminal sequence that is also 
                     found in human Site-2 Protease |CITS: [11689431]|. An RseP 
                     variant lacking the PDZ domain is able to degrade intact 
                     RseA without any previous site-1 cleavage |CITS: 
                     [14633997]|. Cleavage of intact RseA is inhibited by 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-7648 RseB| and DegS; RseP is able to cleave 
                     full-length RseA in &Delta;<i>degS</i> &Delta;<i>rseB cells
                     |CITS: [15520285]|. </i>RseP contains tandem PDZ domains 
                     (PDZ-N and PDZ-C) which may have different functional roles
                     |CITS: [18945679]|. The tandem PDZ domains act as a size 
                     exclusion filter to discriminate between intact and site-1 
                     cleaved RseA (see |CITS: [24389025][28065260]|). Other 
                     structural elements implicated in substrate discrimination 
                     have been identified |CITS: 
                     [26447507][28256773][33329500]|). Crystal structures of 
                     RseP in complex with the peptide-mimetic batimastat provide
                     insight into substrate discrimination and accomodation 
                     |CITS: [36001659]|.
                     The sequential cleavage of RseA by DegS and RseP has been 
                     reconstituted <i>in vitro</i>; RseP fails to cleave RseA in
                     the absence of DegS |CITS: [19706448]|
                     RseP is an essential protease |CITS: 
                     [11689431][11750129][16738554]|. Overproduction of 
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup> suppresses the lethality of an 
                     <i>rseP</i> null mutation |CITS: [12183368]| (and see 
                     |CITS: [12183369]|).
                     RseP is a member of the site-2 protease (S2P) family |CITS:
                     [16322567]| of intra-membrane cleaving proteases (I-CLips) 
                     |CITS: [11750129]|.
                     <i>rseP</i> is transcribed by E&sigma;<sup>E</sup> 
                     polymerase |CITS: [11689431][11274153]|.
                     RseP is the target of |FRAME: CPD-21519| - a  matrix 
                     metalloproteinase inhibitor and anti-cancer drug |CITS: 
                     [29941590]|
                     Ecf: ""extracytoplasmic function"" |CITS: 
                     [11274153]|<BR><i>rseP</i>: <U>r</U>egulator of 
                     <U>s</U>igma E, <U>p</U>rotease
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [12795200][15101969][18983936][208 
                     36086][23479440][24099006]|."
