     gene            194415..196847
                     /gene="bamA"
                     /locus_tag="b0177"
                     /label=bamA
     CDS             194415..196847
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="bamA"
                     /locus_tag="b0177"
                     /product="bifunctional outer membrane protein assembly
                     factor BamA / outer membrane protein assembly complex"
                     /label=bamA
                     /note="BamA is an essential outer membrane protein that is
                     part of the large multi-protein BAM complex responsible for
                     the assembly and insertion of outer membrane &beta;-barrel 
                     proteins.
                     BamA forms a complex with BamB, BamC and BamD |CITS: 
                     [15851030][16079137]|. BamA interacts directly with BamB 
                     and BamD <i>in vitro</i> whereas BamC requires BamD for its
                     association with BamA |CITS:[16824102]|. Purified, refolded
                     BamA is oligomeric and forms pores when reconstituted into 
                     liposomes or planar lipid bilayers 
                     |CITS:[17090219][16829683]|. The relative stoichiometry of 
                     the Bam proteins (ABC and D) within the complex is believed
                     to be 1:1:1:1 |CITS: [20378773]|.  A species specific 
                     C-terminal OMP signature sequence is important for the 
                     interaction of nascent OMPs with BamA |CITS:[17090219]|.
                     BamA is essential |CITS: 
                     [13129938][15851030][16102012][15951436]|. BamA depleted 
                     strains have defects in the assembly of the outer membrane 
                     proteins OmpA and LamB resulting in reduced overall levels 
                     of these proteins accompanied by the accumulation of 
                     unfolded forms |CITS: [15851030][16824102]|. The outer 
                     membranes of cells depleted for BamA have reduced levels of
                     the OMPs TolC, OmpF/C and OmpA. BamA depletion does not 
                     affect inner membrane proteins or the localisation of 
                     lipopolysaccharide. BamA depletion results in a transient 
                     accumulation of protease sensitive unfolded OMPs in the 
                     periplasm |CITS: [16102012][15951436]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [25976323]| for further details on the phenotype associated
                     with BamA depletion). Overexpression of |FRAME: SECA 
                     ""<i>secA</i>""| suppresses the OMP biogenesis defective 
                     phenotype of a BamA missense mutation |CITS:[15951436]|.
                     A <i>bamA</i> insertion mutation suppresses the bile acid 
                     sensitive phenotype of an |FRAME: EG11569-MONOMER ""LptD""|
                     mutant which has defects in OM permeability. The BamA 
                     suppressor allele is a partial loss of function allele; the
                     mechanism by which suppression occurs is not completely 
                     understood |CITS:[17163776]|.
                     BamA consists of an N-terminal periplasmic domain and a 
                     C-terminal pore-forming membrane domain |CITS: [16829683]|.
                     The periplasmic domain of BamA contains 5 POTRA 
                     (polypeptide transport associated) motifs |CITS: 
                     [14559180]|. The crystal structure of the periplasmic 
                     domain of BamA containing four of the five POTRA motifs has
                     been determined in both a compact |CITS:[17702946]| and 
                     more extended formation |CITS:[19081063], [21070948]| 
                     suggesting apparent flexibility. The POTRA domains have 
                     similar structure (a three stranded &beta;-sheet with 2 
                     overlaid, parallel helices) despite low sequence similarity
                     |CITS: [17702946]|. POTRA domains bind unfolded OMPs 
                     non-specifically |CITS:[18430136]|. The periplasmic |FRAME:
                     EG10985-MONOMER chaperone SurA| interacts with POTRA1 of 
                     BamA |CITS: [20598079][39218969]|. BamA POTRA1 mutants show
                     a defect in the biogenesis of most OMPS except TolC. Two 
                     pathways for the Bam mediated insertion and assembly of 
                     OMPs have been proposed differentiated by their dependence 
                     on SurA-POTRA1 interaction |CITS: [20598079]|
                     The periplasmic domain of BamA may function to scaffold the
                     4 lipoproteins of the Bam complex |CITS:[17702946]|. Cells 
                     expressing BamA constructs lacking POTRA domains 1 or 2 
                     grow poorly upon BamA depletion whereas those containing 
                     constructs lacking POTRA domains 3 or 4 do not survive. 
                     Expression of a BamA construct lacking POTRA motif 5 is 
                     toxic, even under conditions in which wild type BamA is 
                     co-expressed |CITS: [17702946]|.
                     The C-terminal domain of BamA is predicted to form a 
                     &beta;-barrel consisting of 16 transmembrane &beta;-sheets 
                     and 8 periplasmic loops. The &beta;-barrel domain can adopt
                     different conformations defined by the surface exposure 
                     status (and hence protease sensitivity) of a flexible 
                     periplasmic loop (L6). Both a protease sensitive 
                     conformation (BamA<sup>S</sup>) and a protease resistant 
                     (BamA<sup>R</sup>) conformation can be detected in 
                     wild-type cells. BamA &beta;-barrel conformation is 
                     regulated by the the lipoproteins BamDE via POTRA domain 5 
                     of BamA |CITS: [22178970][23479609]|. Molecular dynamics 
                     simulations of an <i>E. coli</i> BamA homology model based 
                     on available crystal structures show the presence of a 
                     lateral opening and a substrate exit pore in the barrel 
                     domain; engineered disulphide cross-links in either of 
                     these regions render BamA non-functional |CITS: [24980798]|
                     (see further |CITS: [32528179]|). Binding of BamCDE affects
                     the conformation of the &beta;-barrel of BamA; in the 
                     crystal structure determined by |CITS: [26744406]| the BamA
                     barrel lumen is occluded from the periplasm suggesting that
                     OMPs may access the Bam complex at a lateral gate |CITS: 
                     [26744406]| (see also |CITS: [30087180][33561734]|).
                     BamA facilitates folding of OmpA into lipid bilayers 
                     containing phosphatidylethanolamine; BamA faciltates 
                     folding and insertion of Skp-bound OmpA <I>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [23641708]|. BamA relieves the kinetic barrier to 
                     OMP folding imposed by membrane lipids; this kinetic 
                     barrier serves to partition unfolded OMPs away from the 
                     inner membrane and into chaperone complexes |CITS: 
                     [24715731]|. BamA mediated folding of tOmpA (the 
                     transmembrane domain of OmpA) <i>in vitro</i> is not 
                     dependent on BamA lateral gating or hybrid BamA-OmpA barrel
                     formation but is dependent on membrane thickness - BamA 
                     accelerates tOmpA folding more effectively in liposomes 
                     with larger acyl chain lengths |CITS: [28919234]|.
                     Transcription is |FRAME: RPOE-MONOMER 
                     &sigma;<sup>24</sup>|-dependent |CITS:[11274153]|.
                     BamA is implicated in contact-dependent growth inhibition; 
                     BamA is the receptor for the CdiA<sup>EC93</sup> effector 
                     from <i>E. coli</i> EC93 |CITS: [18761695]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [28351921][34541845]|.
                     BamA is a target for the identification/development of 
                     novel antibiotics (see |CITS: 
                     [39379361][31747680][40265351]|).
                     Reviews: |CITS:[16264426][18041813][35650668]|.<BR>Comment:
                     |CITS: [17702930]|"
