     gene            196969..197454
                     /gene="skp"
                     /locus_tag="b0178"
                     /label=skp
     CDS             196969..197454
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="skp"
                     /locus_tag="b0178"
                     /product="periplasmic chaperone Skp"
                     /label=skp
                     /note="Skp is a periplasmic chaperone which functions
                     during the biogenesis of outer membrane proteins (OMPs). 
                     Skp prevents folding of |FRAME: EG10669-MONOMER OmpA| in 
                     solution; Skp and lipopolysaccharide (LPS) improve 
                     insertion of OmpA into phospholipid bilayers |CITS: 
                     [12509434]|. Skp prevents |FRAME: EG12180-MONOMER PagP| 
                     aggregation <i>in vitro</i> and accelerates PagP folding 
                     into negatively charged liposomes |CITS: [23796519]|. Skp 
                     interacts with OmpA and PhoE in close proximity to the 
                     inner membrane; Skp interacts with early folding 
                     intermediates of outer membrane proteins |CITS: 
                     [11278858][10455120]|. Purifed Skp prevents the aggregation
                     of lysozyme in a nucleotide independent manner |CITS: 
                     [15304217]|.
                     <i>skp</i>::Tn10 mutation induces the |FRAME: RPOE-MONOMER 
                     sigma E| regulon |CITS: [8878048]|. Mutational inactivation
                     of <i>skp</i> results in decreased abundance of outer 
                     membrane proteins, compared to wild type |CITS: [8730870]|.
                     A <i>skp</i> null mutant exhibits mild membrane defects and
                     is unable to efficiently release OmpA from the inner 
                     membrane |CITS: [10455120]|. Skp partially suppresses the 
                     LamB/OmpA translocation defect of a <i>secA</i> mutant 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [2167239]|. Combined inactivation of
                     <i>skp</i> and <i>degP</i> results in a synthetic heat 
                     sensitivity and associated accumulation of denatured 
                     proteins in the periplasm |CITS: [10455120]|. &Delta;<i>skp
                     surA::kan</i> is synthetically lethal; the combined 
                     inactivation is bacteriostatic and associated with filament
                     formation and reduced levels of envelope proteins |CITS: 
                     [11698367]|. Genetic interaction studies suggest that 
                     |FRAME: EG10985-MONOMER SurA| and |FRAME: CPLX0-2921 
                     DegP|/Skp perform redundant chaperone functions |CITS: 
                     [11698367][17908933]|. Genetic interaction studies suggest 
                     that Skp has a role in the assembly of |FRAME: 
                     EG11569-MONOMER LptD| and |FRAME: EG10302-MONOMER FhuA| 
                     |CITS: [23772069]|. Computational modeling of global OMP 
                     biogenesis suggests Skp and DegP function primarily to 
                     manage the accumulation of unfolded OMPs (uOMPs) in the 
                     periplasm that occurs under stress conditions; Skp-uOMP 
                     interactions are fleeting but highly populated |CITS: 
                     [27482090]|.
                     Skp functions to remove &beta;-barrel OMPs that have 
                     stalled during assembly on the |FRAME: CPLX0-3933 BAM 
                     complex|; Skp functions as a 'sacrificial adaptor protein' 
                     - it is degraded together with its bound substrate by the 
                     protease DegP |CITS: [34969846]|.
                     Skp interacts with OmpA, OmpC, OmpF, LamB, PhoE OmpG, OmpX 
                     and YaeT outer membrane proteins |CITS: 
                     [8730870][9914480][11278858][19382746][24077225]|. Trimeric
                     Skp forms 1:1 complexes with outer membrane proteins |CITS:
                     [17928002]|. Proteomic analyses have identified 30 other 
                     interacting proteins, especially from the outer membrane, 
                     among these FadL and BtuB, and from the periplasm, MalE and
                     OppA |CITS: [19003857]|. Skp interacts with phospholipids 
                     and can penetrate into a phospholipid monolayer <i>in 
                     vitro</i>; purified Skp becomes protease resistant in the 
                     presence of phospholipids and bivalent cations |CITS: 
                     [9914480]|. The interaction of Skp with lipopolysaccharide 
                     is non-specific and does not have biological significance 
                     |CITS: [25809264]|.
                     Crystal structures of Skp have been reported |CITS: 
                     [15304217][15361861][15101556]|. Mature Skp crystallises as
                     a trimer described as having a 'jellyfish' architecture 
                     with a &beta;-barrel body and 3 &alpha;-helical tentacles. 
                     In a Skp/OmpA complex, the OmpA &beta;-barrel is maintained
                     in an unfolded state within the Skp cavity whereas the 
                     folded periplasmic domain of OmpA protrudes outside of the 
                     cavity |CITS: [19181847]|.
                     Skp is periplasmic and soluble |CITS: [1838129][8730870]| 
                     and forms stable homo-trimers |CITS: [15101556]|. <i>In 
                     vivo</i> homomultimeric Skp interaction can be detected in 
                     cross-linking experiments |CITS: [21697552]|. Purifed Skp 
                     is not an obligate trimer under physiological conditions; 
                     Skp may experience a dynamic monomer-trimer equilibrium 
                     |CITS: [26579730]|. The monomer state of Skp 
                     (Skp<sub>A108L</sub>) is intrisically disordered and does 
                     not interact wih a model client protein tOmpA; client 
                     binding stabilizes the trimer and shifts the monomer-trimer
                     equilibrium toward the trimeric state |CITS: [33087350]|.
                     Skp is subject to post-translational processing; a17 kD 
                     precursor (preSkp) is proteolytically processed by excision
                     of a 20 residue N-terminal leader to yield the mature 15 
                     kDa protein |CITS: [2843433][1838129]|.
                     Skp sequesters OMPs of varing size; 1:1 trimeric Skp:OMP 
                     complexes involve expansion of the core Skp cavity to 
                     accomodate larger OMPs; binding of multivalent Skp is 
                     necessary to sequester OMPs that exceed the dimensions of 
                     the Skp cavity. Incubation of Skp with BamA in a 2:1 molar 
                     ratio (Skp concentration in trimer equivalents) decreases 
                     BamA folding whereas a 4:1 molar ratio inhibits BamA 
                     folding <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [27455461]|.
                     Expression of <i>skp</i> is regulated by 
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup> as well as by the CpxAR two-component 
                     system |CITS: [11274153][16336047]|. The |FRAME: RNA0-389 
                     CpxQ| sRNA negatively regulates Skp production when the Cpx
                     stress response system is activated; this regulation may 
                     function to prevent Skp from mis-inserting OMPs into the 
                     inner membrane |CITS: [27048800]|. Skp contributes to the 
                     toxicity of the |FRAME: CPLX0-7655 LamB|(A23D) mutation; 
                     the LamB(A23D) mutant protein remains attached to the inner
                     membrane by an uncleaved signal peptide and Skp likely 
                     promotes folding and insertion of the mutant protein into 
                     the inner membrane |CITS: [27048800]|. Coexpression of 
                     <i>skp</i> improves production of some recombinant proteins
                     and in particular antibody fragments |CITS: 
                     [9555730][10092493][10540244][11676604][11676610][1 
                     8314349]| (and see |CITS: [19173718]|).
                     There was some confusion among the neighboring genes 
                     <i>skp</i> and <i>lpxD (firA)</i> in early studies |CITS: 
                     [3056922][2318304][2167239][1987124]|.
                     HlpA: ""histone-like protein"" /
                     HLP-I: ""histone-like protein I"" /
                     Skp: ""seventeen kDa protein""
                     Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [11051763][14513830][25849907][36613738][39008906]|."
