     gene            200980..201576
                     /gene="rnhB"
                     /locus_tag="b0183"
                     /label=rnhB
     CDS             200980..201576
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rnhB"
                     /locus_tag="b0183"
                     /product="bifunctional RNase HII / RNase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.26.4"
                     /label=rnhB
                     /note="RNase HII (type 2 RNase H) cleaves RNA in RNA-DNA
                     hybrids by nicking DNA 5' to the incorporated 
                     ribonucleotide |CITS: [2172991][23144626]|. RNase HII is 
                     required for removal of misincorporated ribose from DNA 
                     |CITS: [12475934]|. A second RNase with a similar structure
                     and catalytic mechanism is encoded by gene |FRAME: EG10860|
                     which produces RNase HI (type 1 RNase H). RNase HII has 
                     also been reported to have junction RNase activity, 
                     cleaving RNA-DNADNA and RNA-DNARNA duplexes at the 5' side 
                     of the last ribonulceotide at the RNA-DNA junction |CITS: 
                     [18959768]|. <i>In vivo</i> studies show that RNase HII 
                     targets isolated mispaired ribonucleoside monophosphates 
                     within chromosomal DNA in competition with the mismatch 
                     repair system, whereas RNase HI targets a mismatch within 
                     an RNA-DNA heteroduplex. RNase HII efficiently targets a 
                     single mispaired ribonucleoside monophosphate, whereas 
                     RNase HI targets a tract of five ribonucleoside 
                     monophosphates with a mispair |CITS: [22139012]|.
                     Genetic and biochemical evidence suggest that RNase HII 
                     initiates the ribonucleotide excision repair process, 
                     followed by DNA polymerase I |CITS: [24495324]|. In an 
                     <i>E. coli</i> &Delta;<i>rnhB</i> strain expressing 
                     <i>umuC</i>_Y11A (an active site steric gate mutant of pol 
                     V), repair of a significant number of misincorporated 
                     ribonucleotides in DNA is dependent on RNase HI |CITS: 
                     [23144626][24244177]|. <i>In vitro</i> studies suggest that
                     RNase HI may collaborate with RNase HII in the excision of 
                     single ribonucleotides that are misincorporated into DNA, 
                     suggesting a role for RNase HI in ribonucleotide excision 
                     repair |CITS: [25951507]|. Ribonucleotide excision repair 
                     is the main source of leading strand scission during DNA 
                     replication <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [30617079]|.
                     Studies with double RNase HI and HII mutants 
                     (&Delta;<i>rnhAB</i>) found a variety of pleiotropic 
                     phenotypes, such as increased sensitivity to UV, 
                     significant induction of the SOS response, inhibition of 
                     growth, temperature sensitivity, and severe chromosomal 
                     complications. Examination of the synthetic lethal 
                     (colethal) mutants displayed avoidance and repair pathways 
                     allowing survival when RNase H proteins are not available, 
                     such as replication fork progression, recombinational 
                     repair and R-loop prevention and removal |CITS: 
                     [28821455][33693789][37819120]|.
                     The enzyme is a monomer as determined by SDS-PAGE and gel 
                     filtration chromatography |CITS: [10788800]|. The 
                     biochemical properties of <i>E. coli</i> RNase HII have 
                     been compared with those of a psychrotrophic 
                     <i>Shewanella</i> species |CITS: [16650002]|. Kinetic 
                     parameters for recombinant RNase HII purified from <i>E. 
                     coli</i> BL21 have been determined in the presence of 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup> and its pH-dependence provided insight into
                     the reaction mechanism |CITS: [17355881]|.
                     RNase HII is found in bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes. The
                     eukaryotic enzymes are heterotrimers the catalytic subunit 
                     of which is similar to prokarytoic enzymes, although 
                     substrate preferences differ. In humans a mutation in any 
                     of the subunits of RNase H2 can result in Aicardi-Goutieres
                     syndrome, a severe neurological disorder |CITS: 
                     [16845400][21177858]|.
                     Transcription of rnhB is induced by nalidixic acid, but not
                     by mitomycin C, and does not require LexA |CITS: 
                     [11544210]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [19228197][19228198][25753809][35787606][38982189]|"
