     gene            211320..211742
                     /gene="arfB"
                     /locus_tag="b0191"
                     /label=arfB
     CDS             211320..211742
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="arfB"
                     /locus_tag="b0191"
                     /product="bifunctional peptidyl-tRNA hydrolase, ribosome
                     rescue factor / peptidyl-tRNA hydrolase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.1.29"
                     /label=arfB
                     /note="ArfB is a protein involved in the rescue of stalled 
                     ribosomes.  The protein is able to hydrolyze peptidyl-tRNAs
                     generated by translation of non-stop mRNAs or mRNAs 
                     containing clusters of rare codons |CITS: 
                     [21051357][21418110][22857598][22922063]|.  ArfB has 
                     similarity to domain III of |FRAME: EG10761-MONOMER 
                     ""RF1""| and |FRAME: EG10762-MONOMER ""RF2""|, including 
                     the GGQ motif required for peptidyl-tRNA hydrolase 
                     activity, but not to domain II, which contains the stop 
                     codon recognition domain of the release factors |CITS: 
                     [21418110]|.  The crystal structure of the ArfB-ribosome 
                     complex suggests that the ArfB tail functions as a sensor 
                     probing the occupancy of the mRNA entry channel, thereby 
                     distinguishing between stalled and translating ribosomes 
                     |CITS: [22422986]|.
                     ArfB appears to be associated with the 70S ribosome and 
                     polysome fractions.  The unstructured C-terminal tail of 
                     ArfB is required for ribosome binding and peptidyl-tRNA 
                     hydrolase activity |CITS: [21051357][21418110]|.  A crystal
                     structure of ArfB bound to the <i>Thermus thermophilus</i> 
                     70S ribosome has been solved.  The N-terminal domain of 
                     ArfB is bound in the A site of the 50S subunit, and its 
                     C-terminal tail occupies the mRNA entry channel downstream 
                     of the 30S A site, positioning the GGQ motif of ArfB in the
                     peptidyltransferase center |CITS: [22422986]|.  The 
                     solution structure of the GGQ domain has been solved |CITS:
                     [24322300]|.
                     Site-directed mutagenesis identified a set of highly 
                     conserved residues that are required for ArfB function 
                     |CITS: [24322300]|.  Overexpression of <i>arfB</i> rescues 
                     the lethal phenotype of an <i>ssrA arfA</i> double mutant. 
                     The GGQ motif and the unstructured C-terminal tail are 
                     required for suppressor activity |CITS: [21418110]|.
                     ArfB: <b>a</b>lternative <b>r</b>ibosome rescue 
                     <b>f</b>actor <b>B</b>""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [22909071][24706739][24782844][24782856][2 
                     5135187][25446863][25874843]|"
