                     /note="Proline-tRNA ligase (ProRS) is a member of the
                     family of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, which interpret the 
                     genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their 
                     specific tRNA molecules.  The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis.  ProRS belongs to the Class IIA aminoacyl-tRNA 
                     synthetases |CITS: [2203971][1852601][23075299]|.
                     ProRS of <i>E. coli</i> B is a dimer in solution |CITS: 
                     [4886431]|.
                     Specificity determinants within tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> that are
                     important for recognition by ProRS have been identified 
                     |CITS: [7493319][7522561][8643363][7870582][812 
                     7693][9383472][11112540][12903242][18513497]|. The R144 
                     residue within the active site makes a key contact with G72
                     in the acceptor stem of tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> |CITS: 
                     [18513497]|.  Specificity determinants and residues within 
                     ProRS that are important for catalytic activity have been 
                     investigated |CITS: [9062123][11112540][32681406]|.  
                     Aminoacylation of tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> that lacks the 
                     m<sup>1</sup>G37 modification is less efficient than that 
                     of the modified tRNA.  An experimental evolution experiment
                     showed that the growth defect due to |FRAME: CPLX0-3950 
                     TrmD| deficiency can be suppressed by point mutations in 
                     ProRS that enable more efficient aminoacylation of 
                     unmodified tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> |CITS: [34761260]|.
                     Many aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases have been shown to have 
                     editing functions.  ProRS misactivates alanine, but has 
                     both a pre-transfer editing function, hydrolyzing the 
                     non-cognate amino acid before transfer to 
                     tRNA<sup>Pro</sup>, and a post-transfer editing function 
                     that deacetylates mischarged Ala-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> |CITS: 
                     [10922054]|.  Pre-transfer editing is not stimulated by the
                     addition of tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> |CITS: [10922054]| and does 
                     not require a separate editing domain |CITS: [16864571]|.  
                     Pre-transfer editing by itself is not sufficient to prevent
                     formation of Ala-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup>; the enzyme's 
                     selectivity for proline over alanine is 2000-fold |CITS: 
                     [18180290]|.
                     Specificity determinants within tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> that are
                     important for post-transfer editing are primarily located 
                     in the anticodon loop |CITS: [24371276]|.  The C433 |CITS: 
                     [10922054]| and K279 |CITS: [12033945]| residues in ProRS 
                     are critical for the post-transfer editing function.  ProRS
                     contains a large insertion domain (INS) between motifs 2 
                     and 3 of the class II aminoacyl tRNA synthetases; this 
                     domain is independently folded and plays a role in the 
                     editing functions |CITS: [12033945][14530268]|.  A chimeric
                     yeast prolyl-tRNA synthetase engineered to contain this 
                     domain gains a post-transfer editing function |CITS: 
                     [17283340]|.  The specificity of the INS domain for 
                     recognition of mischarged Ala-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> appears to
                     be due to a hydrophobic pocket defined by the residues 
                     I263, L266, K279 and T277, where the methyl side chain of 
                     alanine binds |CITS: [22128149]|.  The K279 residue is 
                     predicted to interact with the phosphate backbone of the 
                     acceptor stem; results from site-directed mutants showed 
                     that a positive charge at residue 279 is required in the 
                     editing active site |CITS: [24450765]|.  Based on hybrid 
                     QM/MM calculations combined with biochemical assays, a 
                     mechanism for Ala-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> hydrolysis was 
                     proposed |CITS: [22458656]|.
                     Molecular dynamics simulations and mutagenesis studies 
                     showed that the INS domain affects motions within the 
                     proline binding loop of the active site |CITS: [22356126]|.
                      Efficient catalysis requires communication between the 
                     aminoacylation and editing domains of ProRS |CITS: 
                     [23731272]|.
                     ProRS exhibits a natural level of mischarging with 
                     cysteine, resulting in Cys-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup>, which is not
                     edited by the enzyme.  The 
                     <i>k</i><sub>cat</sub>/<i>K<sub>m</sub></i> for proline is 
                     369-fold higher than for cysteine |CITS: [12130657]|. 
                     Cys-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> is edited by a stand-alone 
                     hydrolase, |FRAME: EG12454-MONOMER| (YbaK).  ProRS and YbaK
                     interact <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [30418624]|.
                     Synthesis of ProRS is derepressed by starvation for proline
                     |CITS: [4551738]|.  A temperature-sensitive allele of 
                     <i>proS</i> has been isolated |CITS: [6991867]|.
                     There is a general discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [37224536]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10966471][23075299]|"
