     gene            219320..219895
                     /gene="gmhB"
                     /locus_tag="b0200"
                     /label=gmhB
     CDS             219320..219895
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gmhB"
                     /locus_tag="b0200"
                     /product="D-glycero-beta-D-manno-heptose-1,7-bisphosphate 
                     7-phosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.3.82"
                     /label=gmhB
                     /note="<i>gmhB</i> encodes the D,D-heptose
                     1,7-bisphosphate phosphatase of the ADP-heptose 
                     biosynthesis pathway.  GmhB belongs to the superfamily of 
                     haloacid dehalogenase (HAD)-like hydrolases.  A <i>gmhB</i>
                     deletion strain has a partial defect in LPS core synthesis,
                     but is not completely heptoseless |CITS: [11751812]|.
                     GmhB has a narrow substrate range, with activity that is 
                     specific for ketohexose and ketoheptose bisphosphates, and 
                     shows high catalytic efficiency towards its physiological 
                     substrate |CITS: [20050615]|.  Crystal structures of 
                     apo-GmhB and several liganded forms have been solved, 
                     guiding construction of site-directed mutants that provide 
                     insight into substrate recognition elements in the 
                     catalytic site and suggest a reaction intermediate |CITS: 
                     [20050614][20050699]|. Protein-Protein Interaction (PPI) 
                     networks have been examined with algebraic topology 
                     analysis showing that most interacting proteins with GmhB 
                     are related to the critical cellular processes such as 
                     protein translation |CITS: [35560988]|.
                     It has been proposed that the <i>gmhB</i> gene and the 
                     HOL-P phosphatase moiety of <i>hisB</i> evolved via a 
                     paralogous duplication event of an ancestral DDDD 
                     phosphatase-encoding gene |CITS: [15042344]|.
                     GmhB is required for toxicity of colicins A and N |CITS: 
                     [19650773]|.  Loss of function mutations in <i>gmhB</i> 
                     have been identified in <i>E. coli</i> isolates derived 
                     from human infections; the mutations lead to increased 
                     degradation of OmpF by the periplasmic protease DegP and 
                     thereby increased resistance to cefalexin |CITS: 
                     [34460299]|."
