     gene            complement(229084..230442)
                     /gene="mltD"
                     /locus_tag="b0211"
                     /label=mltD
     CDS             complement(229084..230442)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mltD"
                     /locus_tag="b0211"
                     /product="trifunctional membrane-bound lytic murein 
                     transglycosylase D / peptidoglycan lytic 
                     exotransglycosylase / peptidoglycan lytic 
                     endotransglycosylase"
                     /label=mltD
                     /note="<i>mltD</i> encodes a membrane bound lytic murein 
                     transglycosylase which cleaves the &beta;-1,4 glycosidic 
                     bond between <I>N</I>-acetylglucosamine (GlcNAc) and 
                     <I>N</I>-acetylmuramic acid (MurNAc) residues of 
                     peptidoglycan with the concomitant formation of a 
                     1,6-anhydro ring at the MurNAc residue.
                     MltD contains an N-terminal transglycosylase domain and two
                     lysin motif (LysM) repeats at its C-terminus |CITS: 
                     [10843862]|. MltD is an outer membrane lipoprotein |CITS: 
                     [15174130]|. A crystal structure of the LysM-type 
                     peptidoglycan-binding domain (residues 389 to 452) 
                     containing a &beta;&alpha;&alpha;&beta; secondary structure
                     has been reported |CITS: [10843862]|. Purified MltD 
                     (residues 18-452) degrades the purified cell wall sacculus 
                     to produce the following major products (in order of 
                     abundance): tetra-A1 (GlcNAc-1,6-anhydro-MurNAc monomer 
                     with tetrapeptide stem); tri-A1 (GlcNAc-1,6-anhydro-MurNAc 
                     monomer with tripeptide stem); di-A1 
                     (GlcNAc-1,6-anhydro-MurNAc monomer with dipeptide stem) 
                     plus other variations in smaller amounts. The reaction does
                     not generate cross-linked products (suggesting that it may 
                     discriminate for substrate on that basis) but does generate
                     products that are indicative of an endolytic 
                     transglycosylase reaction and some products containing a 
                     reducing end (ie containing a terminal MurNAc residue 
                     rather than a 1,6-anhydro-MurNAc residue) |CITS: 
                     [23421439]|.
                     MltD function is important for peptidoglycan expansion; 
                     overexpression of MltD decreases lysis in 
                     endopeptidase-deficient mutants (&Delta;<i>mepSMH</i>) but 
                     does not restore cell shape. Purified MltD cleaves 
                     uncrosslinked glycan strans preferentially |CITS: 
                     [38422114]|.
                     A mutant containing deletions in <i>mltC</i>, <i>mltD</i> 
                     and <i>mltE</i> has a defect in cell separation, growing as
                     short chains of cells |CITS: [12399477]|. Mutant strains 
                     (MHD79, MHD82) lacking lytic transglycosylase activity are 
                     unable to induce &beta;-lactamase and are more susceptible 
                     to certain high-molecular weight antibiotics which are 
                     normally inactive against Gram-negative bacteria, such as 
                     bacitracin, gallidermin and vancomycin |CITS: [15793119]|. 
                     MltD cellular levels are high in exponential growth phase 
                     and decline as cells enter stationary phase; MltD is a 
                     substrate of the periplasmic adaptor-protease complex, 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-8198 NlpI|-|FRAME: EG10760-MONOMER Prc|; 
                     cellular levels are also regulated (in an unknown manner) 
                     by the stationary-phase sigma factor RpoS |CITS: 
                     [38422114]|.
                     <I>E. coli</I> K-12 contains other lytic murein 
                     transglycosylases including |FRAME: G7457-MONOMER 
                     ""MltA""|, |FRAME: G7410-MONOMER ""MltB""|, |FRAME: 
                     G7533-MONOMER ""MltC""|, |FRAME: EG11373-MONOMER ""MltF""|,
                     |FRAME: G6622-MONOMER ""EmtA""| and |FRAME: EG10950-MONOMER
                     ""Slt70""|. |FRAME: EG11494 ""MltG""|, an inner membrane 
                     protein and potential PG terminase, also has lytic murein 
                     transglycosylase activity.
                     <i>mltD</i>:  membrane-bound lytic transglycosylase 
                     D<br><i>dniR</i>: ""regulator of synthesis of the 
                     dissimilatory nitrite reductase"" |CITS: [1663890]| Related
                     reviews: |CITS: [18266855][22126997]|."
