     gene            complement(232022..232489)
                     /gene="rnhA"
                     /locus_tag="b0214"
                     /label=rnhA
     CDS             complement(232022..232489)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rnhA"
                     /locus_tag="b0214"
                     /product="bifunctional ribonuclease HI / ribonuclease"
                     /EC_number="3.1.26.4"
                     /label=rnhA
                     /note="RNase H carries out the endonucleolytic cleavage of
                     RNA in RNA-DNA hybrids, cleaving near the 3' terminus of 
                     the RNA and then digesting the remainder of the RNA |CITS: 
                     [4572736][8526876]|. Although its physiological functions 
                     are not fully understood, it is involved in DNA replication
                     and repair. Note that gene <i>rnhA</i> is also referred to 
                     by its synonym <i>rnh</i> and its product RNase HI (type I 
                     RNase H) often is referred to simply as RNase H. A second 
                     RNase with a similar structure and catalytic mechanism is 
                     encoded by gene |FRAME: EG10861| which produces RNase HII 
                     (type 2 RNase H).
                     Targets for RNase HI include RNA primers for DNA synthesis,
                     especially longer primers that are cleaved by RNase HI 
                     prior to digestion by the exonuclease activity of DNA 
                     polymerase I. RNase HI does not cleave RNA-DNA substrates 
                     with a single ribonucleotide but appears to influence 
                     repair of single ribonucleotides when |FRAME: CPLX0-3803| 
                     incorporates one into the lagging (Okazaki) strand |CITS: 
                     [6319961][2411935][12417313][36762476]|. RNase HI also acts
                     to block the formation of R-loops and the initiation of DNA
                     replication from sites other than the classical origin of 
                     replication, <i>oriC</i>. Although RNase HI has a role in 
                     the removal of RNA primers of Okazaki fragments, its major 
                     function may be in the control of DNA topology by removing 
                     the RNA moiety of R-loops |CITS: [18554330]|.
                     <i>In vivo</i> studies show that RNase HI targets a 
                     mismatch within an RNA-DNA heteroduplex, whereas RNase HII 
                     targets isolated mispaired ribonucleoside monophosphates 
                     within chromosomal DNA in competition with the mismatch 
                     repair system. RNase HI targets a tract of five 
                     ribonucleoside monophosphates with a mispair, whereas RNase
                     HII efficiently targets a single mispaired ribonucleoside 
                     monophosphate |CITS: [22139012]|. However, <i>in vitro</i> 
                     studies suggest that RNase HI may collaborate with RNase 
                     HII in the excision of single ribonucleotides that are 
                     misincorporated into DNA, suggesting a role for RNase HI in
                     ribonucleotide excision repair |CITS: [25951507]|.
                     Although RNase HI only cleaves RNA in hybrid duplexes, it 
                     binds DNA-DNA, DNA-RNA, and RNA-RNA duplexes |CITS: 
                     [7694232]|. Kinetics of substrate binding has been analyzed
                     |CITS: [1690712]|. Binding occurs in the minor groove of 
                     the duplex, and may involve hydrogen bonds with 2' oxygens 
                     in the RNA |CITS: [1662398][9222496]|. Steric modification 
                     of the minor groove results in a substantial increase in 
                     <i>K</i><sub>m</sub>, whereas modification of the major 
                     groove has no effect and altering the 2'-hydroxyl does not 
                     alter binding but lowers the cleavage rate |CITS: 
                     [7525971]|. A-form (RNA-RNA, RNA-DNA) duplexes are bound 60
                     times more tightly than B-form (DNA-DNA) duplexes, and 300 
                     times more tightly than single-stranded oligonucleotides 
                     |CITS: [9003192]|. Tests with boranophosphate DNA analogs 
                     demonstrate that weaker hybridization yields more rapid RNA
                     degradation |CITS: [10192289]|. Evaluation of duplexes 
                     containing arabinonucleic acids suggests that RNase HI may 
                     recognize RNA-DNA hybrid duplexes in a feature of a minor 
                     groove that has a width intermediate between that seen in 
                     RNA-RNA and DNA-DNA duplexes |CITS: [10852702][10736151]|. 
                     Damaged DNA can disrupt cleavage of hybridized RNA, 
                     directing it to sites adjacent to the damaged nucleotides 
                     |CITS: [11551764]|. Cleavage can also be slowed by the 
                     presence of nearby RNA secondary structure |CITS: 
                     [9218455]|. However, RNase HI appears to be able to induce 
                     hybrid formation and cleavage even with complementary DNA 
                     that exists in stable stem-loops or duplexes |CITS: 
                     [9548746]|. Mutants lacking RNase HI function can undergo 
                     ""constitutive stable DNA replication"" (cSDR), replication
                     in the absence of protein synthesis |CITS: 
                     [2828881][26444226]|. This replication can also take place 
                     without functional DnaA and OriC; <i>rnhA</i> mutation 
                     suppresses the negative effects of loss of DnaA function 
                     |CITS: [11894964][6208456]|. Though RNase HI is not 
                     required for initiation of replication, it serves as a 
                     specificity factor blocking replication from sites other 
                     than OriC |CITS: [6322184][8407851][2993805]|. Indeed, 
                     replication in a double mutant lacking RnhA and OriC starts
                     from multiple origins |CITS: [6387151]|. Though cSDR 
                     normally requires RecA, a <i>lexA</i> mutant can suppress 
                     this requirement, as long as DNA Pol I polymerase activity 
                     is present |CITS: [8226671]|. This mutation can also 
                     suppress the lethality of an <i>rnhA</i>, <i>polA</i> 
                     double mutant |CITS: [9079893]|. Various other genotypes 
                     allowing cSDR have been examined |CITS: [2823060]|. Double 
                     mutants lacking <i>rnhA</i> and the helicase gene 
                     <i>recG</i> are inviable; mutants in <i>recG</i> alone also
                     display cSDR and a requirement for DNA Pol I function 
                     |CITS: [7774596]|. Double mutants in <i>rep</i> and 
                     <i>rnhA</i> are also inviable |CITS: [15716497]|. Plasmid 
                     ColE1 can also undergo cSDR in the absence of RecA |CITS: 
                     [2870052]|. cSDR requires PriA and PriB, but not PriC 
                     |CITS: [7525276][15716497]|.
                     Chromosomal replication profiles of <i>rnhA</i> mutants 
                     analyzed DNA replication initiation from <i>oriK</i> sites 
                     and its implications for genome stability |CITS: 
                     [24164596]|. A mutant lacking <i>rnhA</i> shows increased 
                     expansion of a chromosomal repeat |CITS: [19236706]|. Loss 
                     of <i>rnhA</i> increases stress-induced amplification and 
                     point mutation, whereas overproduction of RNase HI reduces 
                     these |CITS: [23828459]|. An RNase HI-sensitive region also
                     is present at the terminus (Ter) region |CITS: 
                     [30222737][37141391]|
                     RNase HI mutants affect plasmid replication even in the 
                     presence of protein synthesis. pBR322 can replicate in the 
                     absence of DNA Pol I in an <i>rnhA</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [6096862]|. Both pBR322 and ColE1-type plasmids form 
                     concatamers in <i>rnhA</i> mutants |CITS: [3023625]|. Loss 
                     of <i>rnhA</i> allows replication of replication-defective 
                     ColE1, even if the entire origin of replication is deleted 
                     |CITS: [6320195][2828638]|.
                     RNase HI exerts control over the formation of R-loops 
                     (single strands of DNA that loop out when RNA binds to 
                     their cognate strand), probably by degrading the RNA that 
                     induces looping. In the absence of DNA topoisomerase I, DNA
                     gyrase can induce R-loop formation, impairing rRNA 
                     transcript elongation and limiting the induction of heat 
                     shock proteins, and stops growth; overexpressed RNase HI 
                     blocks these effects |CITS: 
                     [7536935][10753935][12725933][15458416][18790862]|. 
                     Overexpressed RNase HI is detrimental at 21 &deg;C, though 
                     combined <i>rnhA</i>, <i>nusG</i> double mutants are lethal
                     at this temperature |CITS: [10610761][12946345]|. 
                     Overexpression of topoisomerase III blocks both the 
                     detrimental effects of a topoisomerase I mutant and those 
                     of RNase HI overexpression at low temperature |CITS: 
                     [10632877]|. RNase HI overexpression is also detrimental 
                     following UV irradiation, which may explain why its 
                     abundance is unaltered even during UV-induced stable DNA 
                     replication |CITS: [3030882][3001028]|.
                     Analysis of chromosome replication in cells lacking RNase 
                     HI supports its role in the processing of R-loops and in 
                     maintaining replication control and orientation |CITS: 
                     [26530381]|. A <i>topA rnhA</i> null mutant shows 
                     segregation and growth defects that can be suppressed by 
                     topoisomerase III |CITS: [18554330][23396913]|. Suppressors
                     of the growth defect in <i>topA rnhA</i> null mutants 
                     reduce replication from R-loops and restore supercoiling, 
                     suggesting a correlation between R-loop dependent 
                     replication in these mutants and the inhibition of gyrase 
                     activity and growth. Overproduction of topoisomerase III 
                     cannot complement the growth defect of these mutants at low
                     temperatures |CITS: [26947024]|.
                     In an <i>E. coli</i> &Delta;<i>rnhB</i> strain expressing 
                     <i>umuC</i>_Y11A (an active site steric gate mutant of pol 
                     V), repair of a significant number of misincorporated 
                     ribonucleotides in DNA is dependent on RNase HI |CITS: 
                     [23144626][24244177]|.
                     Studies with double RNase HI and HII mutants 
                     (&Delta;<i>rnhAB</i>) found a variety of pleiotropic 
                     phenotypes, such as increased sensitivity to UV, 
                     significant induction of the SOS response, inhibition of 
                     growth, temperature sensitivity, and severe chromosomal 
                     complications. Examination of the synthetic lethal 
                     (colethal) mutants displayed avoidance and repair pathways 
                     allowing survival when RNase H proteins are not available, 
                     such as replication fork progression, recombinational 
                     repair and R-loop prevention and removal |CITS: 
                     [28821455][33693789][37819120]|.
                     The enzyme is monomeric as determined by SDS-PAGE and gel 
                     filtration chromatography |CITS: [6296074]|. Two 
                     isoelectric points were found for this protein, 9.0 and 9.6
                     the basis for which is unknown |CITS: [6279646]|. RNase HI 
                     binds to single stranded DNA agarose columns |CITS: 
                     [4566677]|. A method for real time assay of its junction 
                     ribonuclease activity has been described |CITS: 
                     [23608820]|.
                     RNase HI has been subject to extensive structural analysis.
                     A crystal structure to 1.8 &Aring; resolution showed that 
                     RNase HI has two domains, and that its metal binding site 
                     is near a cluster of four acidic residues, a feature 
                     conserved in homologs in other organisms |CITS: [1698262]|.
                     Based on a 1.48 &Aring; resolution structure, RNase HI 
                     comprises a five-stranded beta sheet and five &alpha; 
                     helices |CITS: [1311386]|. A subsequent cocrystal with 
                     divalent magnesium ion shared a similar backbone 
                     conformation with the pure protein with minor differences 
                     in the substrate binding region, and a single coordinated 
                     magnesium |CITS: [8108376]|. Crystal structures of several 
                     active site mutants reveal only localized conformational 
                     change around this magnesium-binding site |CITS: 
                     [8408067]|. A cocrystal with divalent manganese shows two 
                     metal-binding sites, one needed for enzymatic activity, the
                     other possibly involved in inhibition |CITS: [11083878]|. 
                     Indeed, RNase HI is activated by low concentrations and 
                     inhibited by high concentrations of manganese ion |CITS: 
                     [9852071]|. Crystal structures have also been determined 
                     for RNase HI bound to RNA-DNA hybrid substrate |CITS: 
                     [15989951]|. Alteration of the coordinating residues Asp10 
                     and Glu48 to arginines yields a protein that is 
                     structurally similar to wild type as determined by circular
                     dichroism and binds nucleic acids well, but the mutant does
                     not complement a mutant lacking RNase HI activity |CITS: 
                     [11343799]|. Alterations in a ""handle"" region from 
                     residues 84-99 does alter protein conformation and 
                     substrate recognition, and raises <i>K</i><sub>m</sub> 3-5 
                     fold |CITS: [1646812]|. Deletion of the carboxy-terminal E 
                     helix does not affect structure but diminishes activity; 
                     adding a peptide corresponding to this region stimulates 
                     activity in this mutant variant |CITS: [9188727]|. Removal 
                     of a basic helix/loop region that can render homologous HIV
                     RNase H partially active also diminishes enzymatic activity
                     |CITS: [8702700]|. Subsequent crystal structures of native 
                     and mutant RNase HI |CITS: 
                     [8390295][8393706][8381958][8931125][11106164][1 
                     1734003][11790848][11847275][15644213][17761696][17944939][
                     17277445][19791799][20423323][25903123][35234152]| and an 
                     NMR solution structure |CITS: [11041241]| have provided 
                     insight into its catalytic mechanism.
                     Molecular dynamics modeling of various divalent metal ions 
                     in RNase HI has been carried out |CITS: [12889961]|. 
                     Activity is highest with Mg<sup>2+</sup>, for which the 
                     binding affinity is 8.39 x 10<sup>3</sup> M<sup>-1</sup>, 
                     lower than for other metal ions. Compared with other metal 
                     ions, Mg<sup>2+</sup> binding changes the enzyme's 
                     structure to a lesser extent, stabilizing it while 
                     maintaining its flexibility |CITS: [36736006]|.
                     The stereochemistry of the RNase HI reaction has been 
                     described |CITS: [12465033]|. NMR spin relaxation studies 
                     combined with molecular dynamics simulations investigated 
                     side chain dynamics in catalysis and proposed a two state 
                     ('open' and 'closed') kinetic model for handle region 
                     dynamics |CITS: [24219366]|. Additional studies focused on 
                     the effects of replacing amino acid R88 with N88, as is 
                     found in RNH1 in humans, and established its importance to 
                     thermostability and extended a multistate model for 
                     substrate recognition to include additional comformation 
                     states |CITS: [35312304]|.
                     RNase HI folding has been studied as an example of general 
                     protein folding principles |CITS: [14672667][16179479][191 
                     05660][19177560][19501596][19505477][22549943][24639503][25
                     258414][25803034][25311861][26916981][26724535]|. Its 
                     thermostability has also been analyzed |CITS: [1654588 
                     2][17088323][17761696][17944939][24098095][25550198]|. 
                     RNase HI activity is potentially useful as a therapeutic 
                     |CITS: [24523351][25398895][26384424]|.
                     The SOS response is active in <i>rnhA</i> mutants |CITS: 
                     [8389213]|. RNase HI is essential for activation of the 
                     RnlA toxin of the <i>rnlA-rnlB</i> toxin-antitoxin system. 
                     RNase HI binds to RnlA <i>in vivo</i> and enhances its 
                     RNase activity <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [24308852]|. RNase HI
                     binds to the C-terminus of the single-stranded DNA-binding 
                     protein encoded by <i>ssb</i> which stimulates RNase HI 
                     activity |CITS: [25903123]|.
                     DNA containing nucleoside analogs can effect the stability 
                     and activity of RNase HI and may serve as potential 
                     treatments for some diseases |CITS: [35259549][35452799]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9184011][19228197][19228198][25387798][25 
                     753809][31471263][32120891][35787606][36344169][38982189]| 
                     "
