     gene            240030..240608
                     /gene="gmhA"
                     /locus_tag="b0222"
                     /label=gmhA
     CDS             240030..240608
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gmhA"
                     /locus_tag="b0222"
                     /product="D-sedoheptulose 7-phosphate isomerase"
                     /EC_number="5.3.1.28"
                     /label=gmhA
                     /note="Sedoheptulose 7-phosphate isomerase catalyzes the
                     first committed step in the biosynthesis of a core 
                     component of lipopolysaccharide, the isomerization of 
                     D-sedoheptulose 7-phosphate to 
                     D-<i>glycero</i>-D-<i>manno</i>-heptose 7-phosphate. 
                     Crystal structures of GmhA alone and in complex with the 
                     substrate D-sedoheptulose 7-phosphate have been solved. The
                     protein appears to be a tetramer in both the crystal 
                     structures and in solution. A reaction mechanism utilizing 
                     an
                     enediol intermediate has been proposed |CITS: [18056714]|. 
                     Potential active site residues were mutagenized and their 
                     enzymatic activity was measured both <i>in vivo</i> and 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [18056714]|. Mutations in 
                     <i>gmhA</i> confer novobiocin hypersensitivity |CITS: 
                     [4926688][785207][18056714]| and F plasmid conjugation 
                     deficiency |CITS: [785207]|; <i>gmhA</i> mutants were shown
                     to contain lipopolysaccharides lacking heptose |CITS: 
                     [4926688][785207][8631969]|. An IS<i>1</i> insertion in the
                     Shine-Dalgarno sequence of <i>gmhA</i> was isolated as a 
                     suppressor of a &Delta;<i>lapB</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [24722986]|.
                     The LPS biosynthesis pathway |CITS: [16632256]| including 
                     GmhA |CITS: [21922563]| have been explored as a target for 
                     development of new antibacterial compounds. Under selection
                     for reduced susceptibility to tigecycline, mutations in 
                     <i>gmhA</i> appear |CITS: [23843301]|. These mutants show 
                     increased sensitivity to bile salts |CITS: [26851608]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>gmhA</i> mutant shows increased sensitivity to 
                     certain cationic antimicrobial peptides |CITS: [30245684]| 
                     and decreased the rate that ciprofloxacin enters the cell 
                     |CITS: [35568342]|. Overexpression of <i>gmhA</i> increases
                     furfural tolerance |CITS: [24489935]|.
                     LpcA = ""<b>LP</b>S-<b>c</b>ore synthesis"" |CITS: 
                     [4926688]|
                     GmhA = ""<i><b>g</b>lycero-<b>m</b>anno</i>-<b>h</b>eptose 
                     synthesis"" |CITS: [11751812]|
                     Review: |CITS: [12045108]|"
