     gene            complement(242448..242726)
                     /gene="yafQ"
                     /locus_tag="b0225"
                     /label=yafQ
     CDS             complement(242448..242726)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yafQ"
                     /locus_tag="b0225"
                     /product="trifunctional ribosome-dependent mRNA
                     interferase toxin YafQ / RNase / DinJ-YafQ antitoxin/toxin 
                     complex / DNA-binding transcriptional repressor"
                     /label=yafQ
                     /note="YafQ is the toxin of the YafQ-DinJ toxin-antitoxin
                     system and is a BECR-fold toxin in the RelE/ParE 
                     superfamily of type II toxin-antitoxin systems |CITS: 
                     [39998207]|. YafQ interacts with the 50S subunit of the 
                     ribosome and cleaves mRNAs, thereby inhibiting translation.
                     Interaction with its cognate antitoxin, DinJ, abolishes 
                     RNase activity of YafQ |CITS: [19210620]|.
                     A crystal structure of the DinJ-YafQ complex showed that 
                     the proposed active site of YafQ is concealed by DinJ 
                     binding |CITS: [24898247]|.  When not bound by DinJ, YafQ 
                     interacts with the A site of 70S ribosomes containing 
                     target mRNAs |CITS: [26261214]|.
                     YafQ cleaves mRNAs at AAA codons |CITS: [19210620]|. <i>In 
                     vivo</i>, YafQ cleaves preferentially between the second 
                     and third nucleotide of the codon, on the 5' side |CITS: 
                     [22247505]|. Among potential targets, the <i>tnaA</i> 
                     coding region has 16 potential YafQ cleavage sites. YafQ 
                     production reduces levels of |FRAME: TRYPTOPHAN-MONOMER 
                     tryptophanase| and indirectly, |FRAME: RPOS-MONOMER RpoS|, 
                     resulting in reduced levels of indole and increased 
                     persister cell formation |CITS: [25041421]|.
                     A crystal structure of the DinJ-YafQ complex showed that 
                     YafQ adopts the RelE/YoeB toxin family fold of microbial 
                     RNases |CITS: [24898247]|. Several conserved catalytic 
                     residues are not conserved in YafQ |CITS: [17263853]|. 
                     Amino acid residues required for catalytic activity have 
                     been identified by site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [22247505][26261214]|.
                     Surprisingly, overexpression of <i>yafQ</i> in liquid 
                     medium has no deleterious effect, while overexpression on 
                     solid medium inhibits growth |CITS: [19707553]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [37703274]|).
                     Although a <i>yafQ</i> deletion mutant produces biofilms 
                     with wild type appearance, the biofilms show up to a 
                     2,400-fold decrease in survival after exposure to the 
                     antibiotics cefazolin or tobramycin. Overexpression of 
                     <i>yafQ</i> results in increased survival of biofilm cells 
                     under those conditions. The effect is specific to biofilm 
                     growth conditions and the antibiotic used; no effect on 
                     tolerance is seen with deoxycycline or rifampicin and under
                     stationary phase planktonic growth conditions |CITS: 
                     [19307375]|. Overexpression of any one of three RNA 
                     interferase toxins HicA, HigB or YafQ suppresses the 
                     essentiality of |FRAME: RPOE-MONOMER &sigma;<sup>E</sup>| 
                     |CITS: [25917909]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [22060041][31932311]|"
