     gene            complement(242729..242989)
                     /gene="dinJ"
                     /locus_tag="b0226"
                     /label=dinJ
     CDS             complement(242729..242989)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dinJ"
                     /locus_tag="b0226"
                     /product="bifunctional antitoxin/DNA-binding
                     transcriptional repressor DinJ / DinJ-YafQ antitoxin/toxin 
                     complex / DNA-binding transcriptional repressor"
                     /label=dinJ
                     /note="DinJ acts as the antitoxin of the YafQ toxin by
                     binding to YafQ and abolishing its RNase activity. DinJ can
                     be specifically degraded by the Lon and ClpXP proteases 
                     |CITS: [19210620]|. DinJ belongs to the ribbon-helix-helix 
                     (RHH) family of transcription factors |CITS: [24898247]|.
                     DinJ influences the RpoS-regulated general stress response 
                     by repressing transcription of <i>cspE</i> |CITS: 
                     [22026739]|.
                     The <i>dinJ</i> gene was first identified as a chromosomal 
                     locus containing a predicted high affinity operator site 
                     for the LexA repressor |CITS: [8057377]|. While expression 
                     of <i>dinJ</i> does not appear to be LexA-regulated or 
                     damage-induced, and LexA does not bind to its predicted 
                     binding site <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [10760155][11333217]|, 
                     <i>in vivo</i> experiments have shown some association of 
                     LexA with the <i>dinJ</i> promoter |CITS: [16264194]|. LexA
                     was shown to bind to the <i>dinJ-yafQ</i> palindrome 
                     upstream of the translation start site |CITS: [19210620]|. 
                     A knockout mutant of this gene was sensitive to boric acid 
                     exposure compared to most other gene knockouts; a 
                     complementation assay restored its intrinsic boric acid 
                     resistance |CITS: [38466471]|.
                     The deletion of <i>dinJ</i> caused a significant reduction 
                     in viability in soil, with full recovery upon 
                     complementation, confirming its functional role 
                     |CITS:[39905026]|. This finding suggests that stress 
                     modulation and fine-tuned transcriptional regulation are 
                     essential for persistence in soil.
                     Expression of <i>dinJ</i> is lowered in an <i>rpoS</i> 
                     mutant background |CITS: [15454563]|.
                     The DinJ binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [22060041][31932311]|"
