     gene            complement(244845..246557)
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="lfhA"
                     /locus_tag="b0229"
                     /label=lfhA
     CDS             complement(244845..246557)
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="lfhA"
                     /locus_tag="b0229"
                     /product="flagellar system protein, promoterless fragment"
                     /label=lfhA
                     /note="Sequence analysis reveals <I>fhiA</I> and
                     <I>mbhA</I> are two adjacent, divergent pseudogenes which 
                     represent the fused boundaries of a 44 gene cluster 
                     encoding a second flagellar system designated
                     Flag-2 in <I>E. coli</I> strain 042. A few non-flagellar 
                     genes are also located within this region. The
                     Flag-2 system has been deleted in K-12 leaving the 
                     <I>fhiA-mbhA</I> gene cluster promotorless. PCR experiments
                     revealed that 15 out of 73 <I>E. coli</I> strains tested 
                     had the entire Flag-2 cluster, though it
                     is believed to be inactive in these strains due to a 
                     frameshift mutation in the <I>lfgC</I> gene which
                     encodes and FlgC-like proximal rod protein necessary for 
                     production of flagella. The Flag-2 locus has
                     also been identified in a number of other bacteria 
                     including <I>C. violaceum, C. rodentium, Y. 
                     pseudotuberculosis, Y. pestis</I>, and <I>V. 
                     parahaemolyticus</I> |CITS:[15687208]|."
     gene            246559..247314
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="lafU"
                     /locus_tag="b0230"
                     /label=lafU
     CDS             246559..247314
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="lafU"
                     /locus_tag="b0230"
                     /product="flagellar system protein, promoterless fragment"
                     /label=lafU
                     /note="Sequence analysis reveals <i>fhiA</i> and
                     <i>mbhA</i> are two adjacent, divergent pseudogenes which 
                     represent the fused boundaries of a 44 gene cluster 
                     encoding a second flagellar system designated Flag-2 in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> strain 042. A few non-flagellar genes are 
                     also located within this region. The Flag-2 system has been
                     deleted in K-12 leaving the <i>fhiA-mbhA</i> gene cluster 
                     promotorless. PCR experiments revealed that 15 out of 73 
                     <i>E. coli</i> strains tested had the entire Flag-2 
                     cluster, though it is believed to be inactive in these 
                     strains due to a frameshift mutation in the <i>lfgC</i> 
                     gene which encodes an FlgC-like proximal rod protein 
                     necessary for production of flagella. The Flag-2 locus has 
                     also been identified in a number of other bacteria 
                     including <i>C. violaceum, C. rodentium, Y. 
                     pseudotuberculosis, Y. pestis</i>, and <i>V. 
                     parahaemolyticus</i> |CITS: [15687208]|."
     gene            247385..248440
                     /gene="dinB"
                     /locus_tag="b0231"
                     /label=dinB
     CDS             247385..248440
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dinB"
                     /locus_tag="b0231"
                     /product="DNA polymerase IV"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.7"
                     /label=dinB
                     /note="<i>dinB</i> encodes translesion DNA polymerase IV
                     (Pol IV), which belongs to the Y family of specialized DNA 
                     polymerases |CITS: [11515498]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [21622227][30916324]|).
                     Purified Pol IV is a distributive, Mg<sup>2+</sup> 
                     dependent DNA polymerase which lacks proof-reading activity
                     and is able to elongate misaligned DNA template/primer 
                     pairs <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [10488344]|. Pol IV 
                     processivity is stimulated by the |FRAME: CPLX0-3761 
                     ""&beta; sliding clamp""|; interaction with the &beta; 
                     clamp promotes formation of a stable initiation complex 
                     with DNA and increases the processivity of Pol IV <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [10801133][11263491]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [11751576][14633999][14592985]|).
                     <I>dinB</i> mutations suppress phage &lambda; untargeted 
                     mutagenesis |CITS: [2940594][9391106]|. Over-expression of 
                     <i>dinB</i> increases untargeted (ie. lesion-independent) 
                     mutagenesis (-1 frameshifts and single nucleotide 
                     substitutions) |CITS: [9391106][10913093]|. Disruption of 
                     <i>dinB</i> decreases the spontaneous mutation rate in both
                     <i>dnaE</i> mutator strains and in the wild type |CITS: 
                     [11073920]|). Pol IV contributes to the SOS mutator 
                     phenotype of <i>recA730</i> carrying strains |CITS: 
                     [16980447]|.
                     Pol IV is implicated in both error-free and frameshift 
                     benzo(a)pyrene (BaP) translesion synthesis (TLS) |CITS: 
                     [11080171]| and in -1 frameshift mutagenesis induced by 
                     4-nitroquinoline N-oxide (4-NQO) |CITS: [11683261]| (see 
                     also |CITS: [11734560]|). Pol IV is especially proficient 
                     at bypassing N2-dG adducts |CITS: [16407906][18562283]| 
                     (see also |CITS: 
                     [17377496][19948952][21302943][21614131]|). Pol IV is 
                     implicated in the error-free bypass of alkylating DNA 
                     lesions (3-methyladenine and 3-methylguanine) <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [17483416]|. Pol IV catalysed TLS restarts 
                     leading strand synthesis across an N2-dG adduct <i>in 
                     vitro</i>; the method of restart involves sequential 
                     switching from |FRAME: CPLX0-3803 DNA Pol III| to Pol IV 
                     and back to Pol III |CITS: [24957605]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [16168375][18308729][19617571][22544274][26352807]| and 
                     further |CITS: [24825884][35285726]|). Most Pol IV foci 
                     form outside of replisome regions; Pol IV has only 
                     temporary access to the replisome, even after induction of 
                     the SOS response |CITS: [29351274]|.
                     Pol IV can efficiently bypass abasic sites <i>in vitro</i>,
                     but not <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [14599744]|.
                     Pol IV is required specifically for adaptive (stationary 
                     phase) mutation |CITS: [11463382]| (further |CITS: 
                     [16168374][19270270]|; conflicting observations on the role
                     of <i>dinB</i> in growth dependent mutation may be due to 
                     the polar effect of <i>dinB</i> mutations on genes 
                     downstream in the <i>dinB-yafN-yafO-yafP</i> operon |CITS: 
                     [12813093]|. Pol IV makes no significant contribution to 
                     the chromosomal mutation rate under normal growth 
                     conditions |CITS: [15090533] [15231812]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [26460006]|). Pol IV is part of a double strand break 
                     (DSB)-repair-dependent stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) 
                     pathway |CITS: [21808005]| (further |CITS: [23041320][2390 
                     7132][23686288][26100038][28727736][30872406]|).
                     Pol IV exhibits strict 'sugar' selectivity; Phe-13 and 
                     Thr-43 represent the steric and polar filters in Pol IV 
                     which act in tandem to achieve stringent selection for 
                     deoxyribonucleotides over ribonucleotides |CITS: 
                     [31544946]| (see also |CITS: [25684709]|).
                     Pol IV forms a multiprotein complex (MPC) with RecA and 
                     UmuD; the catalytic activity of Pol IV is modulated by 
                     formation of the ternary complex |CITS: 
                     [18158902][23292773]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [20467052][24243543]|). Pol IV interacts with |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-8165| (SSB) |CITS: [22447448]| and this interaction 
                     is implicated in enriching Pol IV near lesion-stalled 
                     replisomes |CITS: [36127468]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [36191223]|).
                     Pol IV physically interacts with the transcription 
                     elongation factor |FRAME: EG10665-MONOMER ""NusA""|; 
                     increased expression of <i>dinB</i> suppresses the 
                     temperature sensitivity of a <i>nusA11</i> strain and 
                     suppression requires Pol IV catalytic activity |CITS: 
                     [18996995]| (see also |CITS: [21326909]|).
                     Polyphosphate kinase is implicated in Pol IV dependent 
                     mutation |CITS: [16045619]|. Hydrolysis of the 
                     pyrophosphate moiety (PPi) is an intrinsic step of the Pol 
                     IV mediated DNA synthesis reaction; cleavage of PPi 
                     contributes to the fidelity of nucleotide incorporation and
                     ensures that the reaction is energetically favourable 
                     without the need for pyrophosphatases |CITS: [29850882]|. 
                     The <i>dinB</i> gene is contained on the F' plasmid in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> strain CC108 (used in the lac reversion 
                     system for measuring mutation rate) |CITS: [11683261]| 
                     (further |CITS: [14559967][29301907]|). The expression of 
                     episomal <i>dinB</I> is 2-3 times higher than the 
                     expression of chromosomal <i>dinB</i> in this strain; there
                     are approximately 250 molecules of DinB/cell from 
                     chromosomal <i>dinB</i> expression and 750 molecules of 
                     DinB/cell from episomal expression. Both chromosomal and 
                     episomal <i>dinB</i> expression is induced 10-fold by 
                     treatment with DNA damaging agents |CITS: [11683261]|. 
                     <i>dinB</i> is carried on the widely used plasmid 
                     F'<sub>128</sub> |CITS: [12511513]|.
                     <i>dinB</i> is part of the SOS regulon |CITS: [6771759][63 
                     50267][7596361][11544210][10522385][11683261][11333217]|. 
                     Pol IV levels increase in late stationary phase; induction 
                     requires the general stress response sigma factor RpoS 
                     |CITS: [14617178]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [20639336][20472798][23589461]|).
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains a second Y family polymerase -
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3925|. Y family DNA polymerases are found 
                     across all three domains of life (see |CITS: [24716551]|) 
                     din: <U>d</U>amage <U>in</U>ducible
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11436305]
                     [15588845]|.<BR>Related reviews: |CITS: [10481906][1048584 
                     2][10753775][11205331][11425512][11459973][12581656][24296 
                     168][27321147]|"
