     gene            248788..249186
                     /gene="yafO"
                     /locus_tag="b0233"
                     /label=yafO
     CDS             248788..249186
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yafO"
                     /locus_tag="b0233"
                     /product="bifunctional ribosome-dependent mRNA interferase
                     toxin YafO / ribosome-dependent mRNA interferase"
                     /label=yafO
                     /note="YafO is the toxin of the YafO-YafN toxin-antitoxin 
                     system, acting as a ribosome-dependent mRNA interferase. It
                     is a a BECR-fold toxin in the RelE/ParE superfamily of type
                     II toxin-antitoxin systems |CITS: [39998207]|. When it is 
                     associated with the ribosome, YafO inhibits protein 
                     synthesis by cleaving mRNAs downstream of the translation 
                     initiation site |CITS: [19617347][19943910]|. YafO alone 
                     does not have endoribonuclease activity |CITS: [19617347]|.
                     An analysis of the targets and site specificity of YafO 
                     showed quite weak cleavage site specificity and limited 
                     codon position preference. The cleavage pattern suggests 
                     that the ribosome dependence of YafO ribonuclease activity 
                     should be reconsidered. Like other <i>E. coli</i> 
                     endoribonuclease toxins, YafO activity does not affect 
                     mature ribosomes, but inhibits ribosome biogenesis, likely 
                     by affecting the translation of a specific set of ribosomal
                     proteins |CITS: [34544284]|.
                     Ectopic expression of <i>yafO</i> causes inhibition of cell
                     growth |CITS: [14594833][19617347][19943910]|. Expression 
                     of <i>yafO</i> is increased by UV irradiation |CITS: 
                     [11333217]|; the <i>dinB</i> promoter is SOS-regulated 
                     |CITS: [19943910]|. The promoter upstream of <i>yafN</i> 
                     appears to be autorepressed by YafN |CITS: [19943910]|.
                     Polar <i>dinB</i> mutations affecting the 
                     <i>dinB-yafNOP</i> operon reduce spontaneous mutagenesis 
                     |CITS: [12813093]|; however, only DinB is required for 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis |CITS: [19837801]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [22060041][31932311]|"
