     gene            join(254316..254386,254387..254717)
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="crl"
                     /locus_tag="b0240"
                     /label=crl
     CDS             join(254316..254386,254387..254717)
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="crl"
                     /locus_tag="b0240"
                     /product="RNA polymerase holoenzyme assembly factor Crl"
                     /label=crl
                     /note="Resequencing of multiple isolates of the MG1655
                     strain has identified several genetic variations compared 
                     to the reference sequence, including an IS1 insertion in 
                     <i>crl</i> in strain ATCC700926 |CITS: [22081388]|, which 
                     is the source of the genome sequence within EcoCyc 
                     (U00096.3).  <a href=""http://ecoliwiki.net/colipedia/inde 
                     x.php/Category:Strain:MG1655#Resequencing_of_MG1655_and_com
                     parison_to_the_reference_strain"">This EcoliWiki page</a> 
                     summarizes these sequence differences. The <i>crl</i> gene 
                     is part of a commonly deleted region in long-term stab 
                     cultures of <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [15375124]|.  A 
                     <i>crl</i> deletion strain has a fitness advantage in 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [18793199]|.
                     An intact version of <i>crl</i> can be seen in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 W3110 <a href=""https://biocyc.org/gene?orgi 
                     d=ECOL316407&id=JW0230"">here</a>.
                     Crl is an RNA polymerase holoenzyme assembly factor |CITS: 
                     [24043782]|.  By tilting the competition between |FRAME: 
                     RPOS-MONOMER &sigma;<sup>S</sup>| and |FRAME: RPOD-MONOMER 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup>| for binding to |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX| 
                     toward &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, more |FRAME: RNAPS-CPLX| 
                     (E&sigma;<sup>S</sup>) is formed, allowing more 
                     transcription from promoters within the &sigma;<sup>S</sup>
                     regulon |CITS: [17332743][9767590]|. Crl is able to enhance
                     both promoter binding and opening by E&sigma;<sup>S</sup> 
                     holoenzyme at certain promoters |CITS: [23867204]|.  Crl 
                     acts by promoting the active conformation of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: [31846423]|. 40% of the 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> regulon is modulated by Crl |CITS: 
                     [30991941]|.
                     The surface-exposed regions of &sigma;<sup>S</sup> that 
                     distinguish it from &sigma;<sup>70</sup> and allow specific
                     recognition by Crl have been identified |CITS: [24043782]|.
                      Converseley, a conserved binding surface including the R51
                     residue in Crl was shown to be critical for activation of 
                     E&sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: [25002538]|. Resonance 
                     assignments for a solution structure of Crl have been 
                     published |CITS: [30806877]|.  A cryo-EM structure of 
                     <i>Salmonella</i> Typhimurium (<i>Sty</i>) Crl in a complex
                     with <i>Sty</i>&sigma;<sup>S</sup>, <i>E. coli</i> RNA 
                     polymerase, and a &sigma;<sup>S</sup> promoter DNA region 
                     shows that Crl not only interacts with domain 2 of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, but also with the clamp-toe region of 
                     the &beta;' subunit of RNA polymerase, tethering 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> to the core RNA polymerase |CITS: 
                     [31484766]|.  A cryo-EM structure of a transcription 
                     activation complex with all-<i>E. coli</i> components shows
                     similar results.  HDX-MS results show that Crl stabilizes 
                     structural elements of &sigma;<sup>S</sup> to promote its 
                     function |CITS: [31846423]|.
                     Crl has been shown to be required in the specific case of 
                     the &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent expression of the 
                     <i>csgBA</i> operon, which encodes the fibronectin-binding 
                     proteins involved in curli formation |CITS: [14978043]|, 
                     and is required for repression of the <i>bgl</i> operon by 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: [12177351]|.  Crl affects 
                     expression of a number of proteins, including Fur |CITS: 
                     [17224606][17224607][17332743][18158608][23867204]|, as 
                     well as the expression of the small RNA SdsN |CITS: 
                     [27166377]|.  In exponential phase, Crl slightly stimulates
                     transcription by &sigma;<sup>S</sup>; in stationary phase, 
                     it controls a subset of &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent genes
                     |CITS: [23867204]|. Crl also affects the partitioning of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> between RssB and RNA polymerase, 
                     thereby protecting &sigma;<sup>S</sup> from RssB-induced 
                     proteolysis |CITS: [17332743]|.
                     <i>In vitro</i>, Crl stimulates transcription in RNA 
                     polymerase holoenzymes containing &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> or &sigma;<sup>70</sup> when it is 
                     present before holoenzyme formation and when sigma factor 
                     concentration is low, suggesting that Crl may have a 
                     general effect on holoenzyme formation |CITS: [16980472]|. 
                     Crl expression increases during the transition from growth 
                     to stationary phase; the protein accumulates in stationary 
                     phase only at 30&deg;C, but not at 37&deg;C |CITS: 
                     [14978043]|.  Crl may represent a thermosensor |CITS: 
                     [14978043]| or an environmental sensor via the indole 
                     concentration |CITS: [17224607]|.  <i>crl</i> expression is
                     significantly downregulated after infection with phage M13 
                     |CITS: [15782232]|.
                     Under nitrogen-limiting conditions, <i>crl</i> is 
                     transcribed from its &sigma;<sup>54</sup>-dependent 
                     promoter, which appears to repress transcription from its 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup>-dependent promoter.  The resulting 
                     transcript does not contain a ribosome binding site; thus, 
                     increased transcription from this promoter in fact leads to
                     decreased levels of Crl protein |CITS: [24474781]|.
                     A potential RNA G-quadruplex structure, formed by 
                     guanine-rich sequences located in the coding region of 
                     <i>crl</i>, was identified.  This structure could regulate 
                     the expression of the gene, as observed for <i>hemL</i> 
                     gene expression |CITS: [31964733]|.
                     Restoration of an intact <i>crl</i> allele in the MG1655 
                     strain that contains an IS1 insertion in <i>crl</i> 
                     promotes dehydration tolerance through modulation of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: [29457688]|.  Disruption of 
                     <i>crl</i> decreases resistance to high hydrostatic 
                     pressure |CITS: [30497599]|.
                     <i>crl</i> was first thought to encode the major curlin 
                     subunit |CITS: [2649795]|, but was later found to play a 
                     regulatory role |CITS: [1357528]|.
                     Crl: <b>c</b>u<b>rl</b>i
                     Reviews: |CITS: [24080089][27180360][32849409]|"
     repeat_region   254728..254748
                     /locus_tag="REPT21"
                     /label=REPt21
                     /rpt_family="REP"
                     /note="REPt21"
     gene            complement(254756..255811)
                     /gene="phoE"
                     /locus_tag="b0241"
                     /label=phoE
     CDS             complement(254756..255811)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="phoE"
                     /locus_tag="b0241"
                     /product="multifunctional outer membrane porin PhoE /
                     transport of hydrophilic solute or ion < 600 Da / transport
                     of a monosaccharide / transport of an organophosphorus 
                     compound / transport of a glycoside / transport of a sugar 
                     / transport of a sugar phosphate / transport of a 
                     monocarboxylate / transport of an aromatic carboxylate / 
                     transport of a trisaccharide / transport of a disaccharide 
                     / transport of an oligopeptide / transport of a nucleotide 
                     sugar / transport of a nucleotide / transport of a 
                     nucleoside / transport of an aldehyde / transport of a 
                     lactone / transport of an amide / transport of an amine / 
                     transport of a hydroxy carboxylate / transport of an 
                     aldonate / transport of S-methyl-5-thio-D-ribose / 
                     transport of an inositol / transport of an amino acid / 
                     transport of a nucleobase / transport of a dicarboxylate / 
                     transport of a sugar alcohol / transport of a short-chain 
                     alcohol / transport of a short-chain carboxylate / 
                     transport of an organosulfur compound / transport of H2O / 
                     transport of an ion / transport of glycerol phosphate"
                     /label=phoE
                     /note="PhoE is a member of the General Bacterial Porin
                     (GBP) family.  These proteins are present in the outer 
                     membranes of Gram negative bacteria, mitochondria, and 
                     plastids. Induced by phosphate limitation, PhoE facilitates
                     efficient diffusion of phosphate and phosphorus-containing 
                     compounds across the outer membrane |CITS:[6289897]|.
                     PhoE specificity is defined by the pore's composition; PhoE
                     is believed to have a preference for small anions due to a 
                     collection of positively charged amino acids near the pore 
                     entrance |CITS: [8988389][6325185]|. Therefore PhoE is not 
                     a
                     specific transport system, rather, it is a water filled 
                     channel allowing for passive diffusion of small molecules 
                     (~600 Da). Structural studies indicate that PhoE is a 
                     trimer of 16-stranded &beta;-barrel monomers with large 
                     loops that fold inside the &beta;-barrel, and the amino 
                     terminus faces the periplasm 
                     |CITS:[1380671][9278284][8752355][6361002][6309736]|. 
                     Charged residues within the constriction zone of the pore 
                     may act as voltage gating sensors |CITS:[9217751]|. PhoE 
                     activity is inhibited by ATP which blocks ion flow through 
                     the porin |CITS:[10037149]|.
                     Targeting of PhoE to the Sec-translocase for transport 
                     across the inner membrane is SecB-dependent 
                     |CITS:[1313884],[16352602]|. Protein cross-linking studies 
                     indicate the transmembrane form of PhoE interacts with Skp 
                     at the periplasmic side of the inner membrane 
                     |CITS:[11278858]|. Protein assembly has been studied 
                     identifying intermediates
                     in the assembly process |CITS:[10848998]|, and LPS has been
                     identified as being important in PhoE folding 
                     |CITS:[9988760],[8896450]|. TolB has been shown to interact
                     with trimeric PhoE |CITS:[9393690]|. The C-terminal 
                     phenylalanine is essential for proper assembly of PhoE in 
                     the outer membrane |CITS:[1848301],[9217252]|. Expression 
                     of <I>phoE</I> is inhibited by &sigma;<sup>S</sup> 
                     |CITS:[15347756]|. High NaCl induces <I>phoE</I>-mediated 
                     acid sensitivity |CITS:[8987702]|. Adaptation to high 
                     osmolarity results in reduced levels of PhoE 
                     |CITS:[2168386]|.
                     PhoE binds human lactoferrin |CITS:[10347162]|."
