     gene            272426..273358
                     /gene="mmuM"
                     /locus_tag="b0261"
                     /label=mmuM
     CDS             272426..273358
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mmuM"
                     /locus_tag="b0261"
                     /product="bifunctional CP4-6 prophage; homocysteine 
                     S-methyltransferase / homocysteine S-methyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.10"
                     /label=mmuM
                     /note="MmuM is an <i>S</i>-methylmethionine:homocysteine 
                     methyltransferase |CITS: [9882684][10026151]| involved in 
                     <i>S</i>-methylmethionine utilization |CITS: [9882684]|. It
                     can also be thought of as a third methionine synthase 
                     enzyme in addition to |FRAME: HOMOCYSMET-MONOMER MetE| and 
                     |FRAME: HOMOCYSMETB12-MONOMER MetH|.
                     Although activity of the enzyme using S-adenosylmethionine 
                     (SAM) as the methyl donor has been measured <i>in 
                     vitro</i>, SAM likely can not serve as an effective 
                     substrate <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [10026151]|.  However, when
                     provided with a heterologous SAM transporter, a <i>metK</i>
                     mutant can utilize externally provided SAM, which includes 
                     the non-physiological enantiomer (R)-SAM, as methyl donor, 
                     apparently by MmuM-catalyzed methylation of homocysteine 
                     |CITS: [23912937]|.
                     Crystal structures of MmuM have been solved.  The protein 
                     is monomeric in solution and forms a compact globular 
                     (&alpha;/&beta;)<sub>8</sub> TIM barrel |CITS: [26564203]|.
                      In the metallated form, Cys229, Cys295 and Cys296 
                     coordinate a zinc ion, which appears to play both 
                     structural and catalytic roles.  Site-directed mutagenesis 
                     of Tyr71, which is located near the metal binding site, has
                     confirmed its role in catalysis.  Substrate binding and 
                     specificity has been modeled; while (<i>S,S</i>)-SAM is 
                     predicted to bind to MmuM, the orientation of the S-methyl 
                     group suggests that it cannot serve as a methyl donor 
                     |CITS: [26564203]|.
                     MmuM abundance is decreased in the presence of methionine, 
                     but not <i>S</i>-methylmethionine |CITS: [9882684]|. Unlike
                     a <i>metE metH</i> double mutant, a <i>mmuM metE metH</i> 
                     triple mutant is unable to utilize 
                     <i>S</i>-methylmethionine as a source of methionine for 
                     growth |CITS: [9882684]|.  Overproduction of MmuM leads to 
                     some selenium tolerance in the presence of 
                     <i>S</i>-methylmethionine in the medium |CITS: [10026151]|.
                     MmuM: ""<i>S</i>-<b>m</b>ethyl<b>m</b>ethionine 
                     <b>u</b>tilization"" |CITS: [9882684]|"
