     gene            complement(284115..284873)
                     /gene="xynR"
                     /locus_tag="b0272"
                     /label=xynR
     CDS             complement(284115..284873)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="xynR"
                     /locus_tag="b0272"
                     /product="bifunctional CP4-6 prophage; DNA-binding
                     transcriptional repressor XynR / XynR-D-xylonate"
                     /label=xynR
                     /note="XynR, which contains a helix-turn-helix motif to
                     bind DNA in its N-terminal domain, appears to belong to the
                     IclR family of transcription factors (TFs) |CITS: 
                     [35880217]|. Based on SELEX screening of <i>Escherichia 
                     coli</i> K-12 W3110, XynR, formerly known as YagI, was 
                     identified as a regulator of xylonate catabolism; it is a 
                     rare single-target TF, and its regulation network is still 
                     fixed within the CR4-6 prophage without significant 
                     cross-talk with the host. It  regulates the bidirectional 
                     transcripts <i>yagA</i>(<i>b</i>) and 
                     <i>yagEF</i>(<i>GH</i>) |CITS: [29087459]|.
                     A single peak of XynR binding was identified within the 
                     spacer of bidirectional transcription units of the 
                     <i>yagA</i> and <i>yagEF</i> operons. However, by 
                     decreasing the cutoff of detection, approximately 30 
                     low-level peaks were detected, which are all located inside
                     open reading frames (ORFs) |CITS: [29087459]|. The level of
                     XynR was detectable in the exponential growth phase, but it
                     decreased to an undetectable level during growth in the 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [29087459]|. Upstream of the 
                     <i>xynR</i> gene, a sequence was found that could work as a
                     transcriptional promoter, and the transcription of the 
                     <i>xynR</i> gene is affected by stress and glucose-lactose 
                     shift |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     A <i>xynR</i> mutant shows increased biofilm formation 
                     compared to wild type, but a lower increase in biofilm 
                     formation when the derepressed conjugative plasmid 
                     R1<i>drd</i>19 is present |CITS: [18344336]|.
                     XynR: <b>xy</b>lo<b>n</b>ate catabolism <b>r</b>egulator 
                     |CITS: [29087459]|
                     <i>xynR</i> was found transcriptionally upregulated in the 
                     presence of copper in Cu-resistant strains evolved 
                     experimentally |CITS:[ 35740118]|.
                     The XynR binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
                     /translation="MPIIQSVERALQILDLFNEQATELKITDISKLMGLSKSTLHSLLK
