     gene            311002..311301
                     /gene="insE1"
                     /locus_tag="b0298"
                     /label=insE1
     CDS             311002..311301
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="insE1"
                     /locus_tag="b0298"
                     /product="IS3 element protein InsE"
                     /label=insE1
                     /note="InsE is one of three proteins coded for by the IS3 
                     insertion element. It is required
                     for transposition of IS3.
                     InsE and InsF are required for transposition by the InsEF 
                     transposase |CITS: [8107082]|.
                     As in many insertion sequences, IS3 codes for three 
                     products, one of which is generated by
                     a -1 frameshift that allows readthrough on the first stop 
                     codon, resulting in a superproduct
                     that includes most of the first gene in the element. This 
                     frameshift occurs in an AAAAG motif
                     in the latter end of the <i>insE</i> gene and yields the 
                     IS3 transposase |CITS: [8107082]|. The IS3 frameshift motif
                     has been used in experiments studying the basis of 
                     regulatory translational frameshifts |CITS: [12970189]|. 
                     The insertion of an IS3 element upstream from a gene can 
                     lead to enhanced transcription of that gene
                     due to a ""Pribnow box"" (CAATTT) and a -35 region (TTGGTC)
                     toward the end of <i>insF</i>,
                     which effectively make IS3 a mobile promoter |CITS: 
                     [6292860]|.
                     IS3 elements have been found in 4-6 copies across many 
                     <i>E. coli</i> genomes |CITS: 
                     [4567154][4567155][2987844][6282688]|.
                     Locations of IS3 elements in the genome correlate with Hft 
                     origin points as well
                     as crossover points for F' factor excision |CITS: 
                     [2553981]|.
                     "
