     gene            325174..327207
                     /gene="betT"
                     /locus_tag="b0314"
                     /label=betT
     CDS             325174..327207
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="betT"
                     /locus_tag="b0314"
                     /product="choline:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=betT
                     /note="BetT is a proton-motive-force-driven choline
                     transporter that belongs to the Betaine Carnitine Choline 
                     Transport (BCCT) family of proteins. Deletion mutants of 
                     <I>betT</I> displayed essentially no choline uptake 
                     activity, but could be complemented with a 
                     <I>betT</I>-encoding plasmid, restoring rapid choline 
                     uptake |CITS: [1956285]|. Choline uptake
                     is completely inhibited by the protonophore carbonylcyanide
                     p-trifluoromethoxyphenyl hydrazone, suggesting that choline
                     transport by BetT is driven by the proton-motive force 
                     |CITS: [1956285]|. Choline is used to synthesize glycine 
                     betaine, which is used by <I>E. coli</I> to overcome growth
                     inhibition caused by osmotic stress |CITS: [3065456]|. The 
                     <I>betTIAB</I> genes are expressed only under aerobic 
                     conditions, and are induced by osmotic stress |CITS: 
                     [1956285]|.  Analysis of <I>betT-lacZ</I> fusions showed 
                     that <I>betT</I> transcription increased 7-10 fold in 
                     response to increases in medium osmolarity. The presence of
                     choline further increases this response |CITS: [3141381]|.
                     <i>betT</i> mRNA decay is controlled by RNase III in 
                     response to osmotic stress. <i>betT</i> mRNA contains two 
                     tandem RNase III cleavage sites - both of which are 
                     required for efficient <i>betT</i> mRNA degradation |CITS: 
                     [24956275]|.
                     BetT is predicted to have 10-12 membrane spanning regions; 
                     it also contains a long hydrophilic C-terminal domain of 
                     approximately 200 amino acid residues |CITS: [1956285]|. 
                     <i>betT</i>: |CITS: [3065456]|"
