     gene            338595..339644
                     /gene="yahK"
                     /locus_tag="b0325"
                     /label=yahK
     CDS             338595..339644
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yahK"
                     /locus_tag="b0325"
                     /product="multifunctional aldehyde reductase,
                     NADPH-dependent / 3-methylbut-2-enal reductase / aldehyde 
                     reductase / isobutyraldehyde reductase / (S)-lactaldehyde 
                     reductase / (R)-lactaldehyde reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.2; 1.1.1.55"
                     /label=yahK
                     /note="YahK is a member of the zinc-containing
                     medium-chain dehydrogenase/reductase family.  The enzyme is
                     a major source of NADPH-dependent aldehyde reductase 
                     activity in the cell.  Catalytic activity of the purified 
                     enzyme was measured with a variety of aldehyde substrates 
                     |CITS: [23093176]|.
                     In a metabolically engineered strain, phenylacetaldehyde 
                     and 4-hydroxyphenylacetaldehyde are reduced to 
                     2-phenylethanol and 2-(4-hydroxyphenyl)ethanol by the 
                     endogenous aldehyde reductases YqhD, YjgB, and YahK |CITS: 
                     [22752168]|.  In a different engineered strain |FRAME: 
                     LACTALD| and |FRAME: CPD-358| were reduced to the 
                     corresponding forms of |FRAME: DL-12-Propanediol 
                     ""propane-1,2-diol""| |CITS: [25007409]|.  Conversely, a 
                     broad survey of aldehyde reductases showed that YahK was 
                     one of several endogenous aldehyde reductases that 
                     contribute to the degradation of desired aldehyde end 
                     products of metabolic engineering |CITS: [25108218]|. 
                     Mutants that change the preference of the enzyme for the 
                     NADPH cosubstrate towards NADH have been constructed |CITS:
                     [24992211]|.
                     An acetate-tolerant mutant of <i>E. coli</i> DH5&alpha;, 
                     DA19, grows faster and produces less acetate on chemically 
                     defined medium than its parent; expression of <i>yahK</i> 
                     is downregulated in DA19 compared to DH5&alpha; |CITS: 
                     [20700606]|.
                     <i>yahK</i> is involved in biofilm formation by a 
                     reduced-genome <i>E. coli</i> strain, but not wild-type 
                     |CITS: [21980953]|.
                     "
                     /translation="MKIKAVGAYSAKQPLEPMDITRREPGPNDVKIEIAYCGVCHSDLH
