     gene            345723..346892
                     /gene="prpC"
                     /locus_tag="b0333"
                     /label=prpC
     CDS             345723..346892
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="prpC"
                     /locus_tag="b0333"
                     /product="2-methylcitrate synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.3.5"
                     /label=prpC
                     /note="2-Methylcitrate synthase (PrpC) catalyzes the
                     Claisen condensation of propionyl-CoA with oxaloacetate, a 
                     reaction in propionate catabolism via the methylcitrate 
                     cycle |CITS: [9325432][9579066][8508809][10348870]|. 
                     Production of the enzyme is induced by growth on 
                     propionate, but not acetate or glucose |CITS: 
                     [9325432][9579066][12473114]|.  Expression of 
                     <i>prpBCDE</i> is regulated by PrpR and catabolite 
                     repression |CITS: [15805526]| and is downregulated in the 
                     presence of a variety of sugars |CITS: [22579471]|.
                     A revertant of a <i>gltA</i> mutant strain produces an 
                     enzyme with citrate synthase activity |CITS: [8508809]| 
                     that was later found to be identical to PrpC |CITS: 
                     [9325432][9579066]|.  The mutation causing the revertant 
                     phenotype is unknown.  Adaptive laboratory evolution 
                     experiments designed to determine whether PrpC can function
                     as a citrate synthase isozyme showed that increased 
                     expression and increased copy number of <i>prpC</i> rescued
                     growth of the <i>gltA</i> mutant |CITS: [25564669]|.
                     <i>prpC</i> and |FRAME: EG10035 <i>aldA</i>| are 
                     synthetically lethal.  Based on the lack of sequence and 
                     structural similarity between AldA and PrpC, it seems 
                     unlikely that PrpC also catalyzes the glycolaldehyde 
                     dehydrogenase reaction that is thought to be the essential 
                     function of AldA.  Potential explanations for the synthetic
                     lethality of <i>aldA</i> and <i>prpC</i> were discussed 
                     |CITS: [26441892]|."
