     gene            346926..348377
                     /gene="prpD"
                     /locus_tag="b0334"
                     /label=prpD
     CDS             346926..348377
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="prpD"
                     /locus_tag="b0334"
                     /product="2-methylcitrate dehydratase"
                     /EC_number="4.2.1.79"
                     /label=prpD
                     /note="2-Methylcitrate dehydratase (PrpD) catalyzes the 
                     stereospecific dehydration of 2-methylcitrate, a reaction 
                     in propionate catabolism via the methylcitrate cycle |CITS:
                     [11782506][12473114]|.
                     PrpD is monomeric in solution |CITS: [11782506]|.  Reports 
                     disagree on whether |CITS: [11782506]| or not |CITS: 
                     [12473114]| PrpD contains an iron-sulfur cluster, and on 
                     the substrate specificity of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [11782506][12473114]|.
                     <i>prpD</i> also encodes the residual aconitase-like 
                     activity, aconitase C |CITS: [11782506]|, which constitutes
                     5% or less of cellular aconitase activity and is observed 
                     in an <i>acnA acnB</i> double mutant |CITS: [9202458]|.  
                     Aconitase C activity does not substitute for aconitase A 
                     and B activities <i>in vivo</i> and appears not to be 
                     reversible, in contrast to true aconitase activity, and is 
                     therefore better described as a dehydratase activity |CITS:
                     [11782506]|.
                     Expression of <i>prpD</i> is induced by propionate, and 
                     protein production is observed during growth on propionate,
                     but not acetate |CITS: [12473114]|.
                     Expression of <i>prpBCDE</i> is regulated by PrpR and 
                     catabolite repression |CITS: [15805526]| and is 
                     downregulated in the presence of a variety of sugars |CITS:
                     [22579471]|.
                     "
