     gene            354510..355169
                     /gene="cynT"
                     /locus_tag="b0339"
                     /label=cynT
     CDS             354510..355169
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cynT"
                     /locus_tag="b0339"
                     /product="bifunctional carbonic anhydrase 1 / carbonic
                     anhydrase"
                     /EC_number="4.2.1.1"
                     /label=cynT
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> encodes two carbonic anhydrases,
                     CynT (carbonic anhydrase 1) and Can (|FRAME: CPLX0-7521|). 
                     The enzymes belong to Clade A and Clade B of the &beta; 
                     class of carbonic anhydrases, respectively |CITS: 
                     [10978542]|.
                     <i>cynT</i> is encoded in an operon with |FRAME: EG10175|, 
                     the gene encoding |FRAME: CYANLY-CPLX|, and its expression 
                     is inducible by cyanate |CITS: [7961413]|.  The biological 
                     role of the CynT carbonic anhydrase is to prevent the 
                     depletion of endogenous bicarbonate due to rapid loss of 
                     CO<sub>2</sub>, the product of the cyanase-catalyzed 
                     reaction, by diffusion out of the cell |CITS: [1740425]|. 
                     The enzyme behaves as an oligomer of two or four subunits, 
                     depending on the presence of bicarbonate |CITS: [1740425]|.
                     Inhibition of CynT activity by |FRAME: CPD0-1626| has been 
                     investigated |CITS: [35899716]|.
                     At atmospheric concentrations of CO<sub>2</sub>, a 
                     <i>cynT</i> mutant is more sensitive to cyanate than a 
                     <i>cynS</i> mutant and can not utilize cyanate as the sole 
                     source of nitrogen, although it produces active cyanase. 
                     However, at a high pCO<sub>2</sub> of 3%, it behaves like 
                     the wild type |CITS: [8444806]|.  After cyanate is 
                     depleted, CynT is degraded rapidly at high pCO<sub>2</sub>,
                     but only slowly at low pCO<sub>2</sub> |CITS: [8083164]|.  
                     Growth inhibition by cyanate in the absence of carbonic 
                     anhydrase is not solely due to depletion of bicarbonate 
                     |CITS: [7768821]|.
                     Induction of <i>cynT</i> expression suppresses phenotypes 
                     of a <i>can</i> mutant |CITS: [14563877]|.  A <i>can 
                     cynT</i> double mutant is only viable under high 
                     CO<sub>2</sub> conditions |CITS: [12784642]|.
                     The product of the <i>cynT</i> gene was originally thought 
                     to be a cyanate permease |CITS: [3049588][2670891]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10978542][11268536][11696553][38506448]|"
                     /translation="MKEIIDGFLKFQREAFPKREALFKQLATQQSPRTLFISCSDSRLV
