     gene            355200..355670
                     /gene="cynS"
                     /locus_tag="b0340"
                     /label=cynS
     CDS             355200..355670
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cynS"
                     /locus_tag="b0340"
                     /product="cyanase"
                     /label=cynS
                     /note="Cyanase is an inducible enzyme in <i>E. coli</i>
                     and was first reported and subsequently studied in
                     <i>E. coli</i> B |CITS: [13775509][6994799]|.  The cyanase 
                     operon may have evolved to function in 
                     detoxification/decomposition of cyanate arising from intra-
                     and extracellular activities |CITS: [2094285]|.
                     It was shown that bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>)
                     is a substrate of the enzyme, and that water does
                     not participate as a substrate |CITS: [3110153]|.  The 
                     initial product carbamate is unstable and spontaneously 
                     decomposes to ammonia and carbon dioxide |CITS: [6994799]|.
                      The enzyme shows competitive substrate inhibition
                     by both substrates |CITS: [3518792]|.
                     The native enzyme from <i>E. coli</i> B has a molecular 
                     weight of ca. 150 kDa and thus appears to be a
                     homodecamer in solution |CITS: [6994799]|.  A crystal 
                     structure of cyanase was solved at 1.65 &Aring; resolution;
                     it
                     showed a homodecamer composed of five dimers.  The 
                     structure allowed identification of the active site, which 
                     is
                     located between dimers and is comprised of residues from 
                     four adjacent subunits |CITS: [10801492]|.
                     The free sulfhydryl group of the single cysteine residue is
                     not required for catalytic activity, but plays a role in
                     stabilizing the decameric structure |CITS: 
                     [3128546][8195141]|.  The single histidine residue is also 
                     required for the
                     stability of the decamer and contributes to its catalytic 
                     properties |CITS: [8195141][7947823]|.
                     A mutant lacking cyanase is more sensitive to cyanate than 
                     the wild type |CITS: [3309165]|; a
                     <i>cynS</i> mutant is not able to use cyanate as the sole 
                     source of nitrogen |CITS: [3034861]|.
                     "
                     /translation="MIQSQINRNIRLDLADAILLSKAKKDLSFAEIADGTGLAEAFVTA
