     gene            complement(357637..358890)
                     /gene="lacY"
                     /locus_tag="b0343"
                     /label=lacY
     CDS             complement(357637..358890)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lacY"
                     /locus_tag="b0343"
                     /product="lactose permease"
                     /label=lacY
                     /note="The lactose permease LacY is a galactoside:proton 
                     symporter, responsible for the uptake of lactose and other 
                     galactosides. LacY is a member of the major facilitator 
                     superfamily (MFS) of transporters |CITS: [8438231]| and is 
                     probably the best characterised secondary transporter. 
                     &beta;-galactoside transport was first described in the mid
                     twentieth century (reviewed in |CITS: [6358502]| and 
                     studies of lactose transport were instrumental in 
                     elucidating and confirming the chemiosmotic theory for 
                     energy transfer in cells |CITS: 
                     [13771349][4920870][4579628]|. The <i>lacY</i> gene was the
                     first transporter gene to be cloned and sequenced |CITS: 
                     [345098][6444453]|. Lactose permease has been solubilised, 
                     purified, reconstituted into liposomes, and shown to 
                     mediate lactose:proton symport with a 1:1 stoichiometry 
                     |CITS: [7000781][7028742][3520229]|.
                     The substrate specificty of LacY has been surveyed - 
                     compounds were assayed as inhibitors of 
                     <i>o</i>-nitrophenyl-&beta;-D-galactopyranoside (ONPG) 
                     uptake |CITS: [336372]| (refer to list of inhibitors 
                     below). Results suggest a specific requirement for the 
                     galactopyranosyl ring of D configuration with a free 
                     hydroxyl group. The size of the aglycone group appeared to 
                     be more important than its chemical nature - an increase in
                     aglycone size was associated with enhanced binding. 
                     Aromatic &alpha; and &beta;-D-galactopyranosides are 
                     high-affinity inhibitors but not all are transported. LacY 
                     transports the D-galactopyranosides lactose and melibiose, 
                     with similar affinities (K<sub>m</sub> = 0.1-1 mM) |CITS: 
                     [336372]|.
                     Purified LacY functions as a monomer |CITS: 
                     [2824513][8202501]|. The secondary structure is 
                     predominantly &alpha;-helical, consisting of 12 
                     transmembrane regions with the N and C termini located in 
                     the cytoplasm |CITS: [6336750][3912174][2164211][7578103]|.
                     Crystal structures of mutant LacY (Cys154 &rarr; Gly) with 
                     and without bound ligand and native LacY have been 
                     obtained. The twelve TM helices are organised into two 
                     bundles of six connected by a long flexible loop. A central
                     hydrophilic cavity contains the sugar binding site and the 
                     residues involved in proton translocation. The structures 
                     are 'inward-facing', that is they are oriented with the 
                     cavity open to the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [12893935][16525509][17881559]|. This is believed to be the
                     conformation of the transporter that is present in 
                     bacterial membranes in the absence of bound substrate 
                     |CITS: [20457922][24872451]|.
                     The introduction of tryptophan replacements for 
                     glycine-glycine pairs (G46/G262 and G46/G370) results in 
                     the formation of a stable outward-facing LacY structure 
                     |CITS: [23671103]|. A crystal structure of the double 
                     tryptophan mutant (G46W G262W) with bound substrate has 
                     been obtained. The substrate is bound within an almost 
                     occluded central cavity that has a narrow opening to the 
                     periplasmic side and is completely sealed on the 
                     cytoplasmic face. Amino acids ligating the sugar are 
                     Glu-126, Arg-144, Trp-151, Glu-269, Asn-272, and His-322 
                     |CITS: [24453216]|.
                     Substrate transport occurs by an 'alternating access 
                     mechanism', in which conformational change of the 
                     transporter allows alternating access of single sugar and 
                     proton binding sites to either side of the membrane 
                     (reviewed by |CITS: [21161516]| and |CITS: [21995338]|. 
                     Sugar binding is associated with opening of a wide 
                     hydrophilic cleft on the periplasmic side of the membrane 
                     and closing of a large cytoplasmic facing cavity |CITS: [1 
                     7172438][17502603][17925435][18319336][19959662][19781551][
                     20457922][20043916][24872451]|. LacY mutants with single 
                     cysteine 154 &rarr; glycine replacements are able to bind 
                     substrate but have no transport activity |CITS: [12627968]|
                     due to constitutive opening of the periplasmic cleft  
                     |CITS: [17925435][18485365]|. Introduction of large thiol 
                     groups into the periplasmic cleft also blocks transport 
                     without affecting substrate binding |CITS: [19128028]|. 
                     Specific lipid-protein interactions affect the 
                     conformational dynamics of LacY; phosphotidylethanolamine 
                     disrupts a charge-relay network (Asp-68, Lys-131, Glu-139 
                     and Lys-335) present on the intracellular side of LacY and 
                     triggers a conformational shift towards the inward facing 
                     state <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [30297844]|.
                     LacY has been extensively mutagenised (reviewed in |CITS: 
                     [11483994]|. A functional Cys-less transporter has been 
                     generated and Cys-scanning mutagenesis has altered every 
                     residue in this transporter to a cysteine. Only six 
                     residues (Glu-126, Arg-144, Glu-269, Arg-302, His-322 and 
                     Glu-325) are absolutely essential for lactose transport 
                     |CITS: [9761772]|. Glu-126 and Arg-144 are essential for 
                     sugar binding |CITS: 
                     [9707556][9888822][22802658][10360937]| and along with 
                     Trp-151 and Glu-269 form the substrate binding/recognition 
                     site |CITS: [12893935][12578349][16525509][17881559]|. 
                     There is a salt bridge between Glu-126 and Arg-144, which 
                     is broken upon substrate binding |CITS: [15272008]|. The 
                     galactopyranosyl ring of the substrate has a direct 
                     stacking interaction with the indole ring of Trp-151 |CITS:
                     [14566061]|. Glu-269 is predicted to participate in 
                     substrate recognition by hydrogen bonding to the C-3 OH 
                     group of the galactopyranosyl ring |CITS: [12660154]|. 
                     Glu-269 may be the primary protonation site |CITS: 
                     [16525509]|. Asn-272 is part of the sugar binding site 
                     |CITS: [24520888][24453216]|.
                     Residues involved in the proton translocation pathway 
                     (Tyr231, Asp240, Arg-302, Lys319, His-322, and Glu-325) are
                     mainly located in the C-terminal helix bundle and are 
                     positioned on the opposite side of the cavity to the 
                     substrate binding site |CITS: 
                     [10984523][11353849][12718531][19689129]|. Protonation is 
                     required for sugar binding |CITS: [18567672][23033496]|. At
                     physiological pH, sustrate binds to fully protonated LacY 
                     |CITS: [19689129]|. Residues involved in proton 
                     translocation do not form a pathway across the membrane but
                     form a highly connected salt bridge cluster |CITS: 
                     [17881559]| - water molecules coordinated within this 
                     cluster may function as the protonation site during 
                     transport |CITS: [19689129]|.
                     Glu-126 and Arg-144 are not required for sugar binding, 
                     though they do play a role in maintenance of secondary 
                     structure |CITS: [12549931]|.
                     LacY has also served as a model for studying the 
                     contribution of membrane lipids and lipid headgroups to 
                     membrane protein assembly |CITS: [7822303][8662750][854728 
                     2][11980707][18779371][16698795][20696931][22969082][233227
                     71][23690595]|. The <i>lacY</i> gene forms part of the well
                     known lactose-inducible <i>lac</i> operon with the 
                     <i>lacZ</i> gene encoding &beta;-galactosidase, the 
                     <i>lacA</i> gene encoding thio-&beta;-galactoside 
                     transacetylase and the <i>lacI</i> gene encoding the 
                     lactose repressor. Inhibitors of ONPG uptake |CITS: 
                     [336372]| (in order of decreasing affinity): |FRAME: 
                     CPD0-2500|, phenyl-&alpha;-D-galactopyranoside, 
                     <i>o</i>-nitrophenyl-&alpha;-D-galactopyranoside, |FRAME: 
                     Alpha-lactose|, |FRAME: MELIBIOSE|, |FRAME: CPD-3561|, 
                     |FRAME: CPD0-1302|, |FRAME: CPD0-1303|, |FRAME: CPD-1099|, 
                     |FRAME: CPD-3565|, methyl-thio-&beta;-D-galactopyranoside, 
                     |FRAME: CPD-9117|, |FRAME: D-GALACTOSYLAMINE|, 
                     <i>o</i>-nitrophenyl-&beta;-D-fucopyranoside, |FRAME: 
                     METHYL-BETA-D-GALACTOSIDE|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [6358502][3004294][8144491][7823021][96346 
                     51][14630326][15950162][16689628][21161516][25568085]|.<br>
                     Comments: |CITS: [9159101][12893929][17251354]|"
