                     /product="bifunctional 3-(3-hydroxyphenyl)propanoate
                     hydroxylase / 3-hydroxy-trans-cinnamate hydroxylase"
                     /EC_number="1.14.13.127"
                     /label=mhpA
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> is able to utilize aromatic acids as 
                     carbon and energy sources.  A meta-cleavage pathway is used
                     for the catabolism of 3-(3-hydroxyphenyl)propionate |CITS: 
                     [6345502]|.  <i>mhpA</i> encodes a flavin-type 
                     monooxygenase |CITS: [9098055]| that was purified and 
                     biochemically characterized only recently |CITS: 
                     [31811039]|.
                     The C terminus of MhpA does not show similarity to other 
                     3-hydroxyaromatic acid hydroxylases; however, it is 
                     required for catalytic activity |CITS: [31811039]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>mhpA</i> mutant is unable to grow with 
                     3-(3-hydroxyphenyl)propionate (3HPP, MHP) as the sole 
                     source of carbon |CITS: [31811039]|.  Enzyme activity is 
                     induced after growth with MHP or hydroxycinnamate (HCA) 
                     |CITS: [3531186]|."
