     gene            complement(390586..391743)
                     /gene="ampH"
                     /locus_tag="b0376"
                     /label=ampH
     CDS             complement(390586..391743)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ampH"
                     /locus_tag="b0376"
                     /product="trifunctional peptidoglycan 
                     DD-carboxypeptidase/peptidoglycan DD-endopeptidase / 
                     peptidoglycan DD-endopeptidase / peptidoglycan 
                     DD-carboxypeptidase"
                     /label=ampH
                     /note="AmpH is a penicillin-binding protein that catalyzes
                     both DD-carboxypeptidase and DD-endopeptidase activities 
                     |CITS: [9324260][22001512]|. AmpH is a bifunctional class C
                     penicillin-binding protein with a probable role in 
                     peptidoglycan remodelling and/or recycling.
                     AmpH binds a range of &beta;-lactams, including penicillin 
                     G |CITS: [9324260]|. Though AmpH is similar to class C 
                     &beta;-lactamases, it has no detectable &beta;-lactamase 
                     activity |CITS: [9324260]|. AmpH may influence or contain a
                     weak DD-carboxypeptidase activity |CITS: [14769329]|. 
                     Purified AmpH cleaves the DD peptide bridge in cross-linked
                     muropeptides <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [22001512]|. Purified 
                     AmpH removes the D-alanine from muropeptides with a 
                     C-terminal D-alanyl-D-alanine dipeptide |CITS: [22001512]|.
                     Purified AmpH binds Bocillin |CITS: [22001512]|.
                     AmpH has a 23-residue amino-terminal signal sequence which 
                     is cleaved in the mature protein |CITS: [22001512]|. AmpH 
                     is membrane-associated |CITS: [22001512]|.
                     Loss of AmpH function has no effect on its own, but can 
                     lead to morphological defects in a strain lacking at least 
                     two other major penicillin-binding proteins |CITS: 
                     [10383966][9324260]|."
