     gene            405600..406508
                     /gene="mak"
                     /locus_tag="b0394"
                     /label=mak
     CDS             405600..406508
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mak"
                     /locus_tag="b0394"
                     /product="bifunctional mannokinase / fructokinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.7; 2.7.1.4"
                     /label=mak
                     /note="In the absence of the usual PTS-mediated route of 
                     fructose uptake, a selected mutation that causes increased 
                     expression of Mak enables phosphorylation of fructose after
                     its facilitated diffusion into the cell via a selected 
                     mutant allele of |FRAME: PTSG-MONOMER PtsG| |CITS: 
                     [10677538][11742072]|.
                     The Mak enzyme appears to be identical to the previously 
                     reported mannokinase activity in <i>E. coli</i>.  The 
                     enzyme was reported to be constitutively expressed and is 
                     able to phosphorylate mannose, fructose and to a lesser 
                     extent glucosamine, D-glucose and 2-deoxyglucose |CITS: 
                     [5342371][4557975]|.
                     Trace Mak activity is present in wild-type cells |CITS: 
                     [11742072]|.  Overexpression of <i>mak</i> rescues the 
                     glucose auxotrophy of a glucokinase mutant, and the Mak 
                     protein functions as a rudimentary glucokinase <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [15157072]|.  When a glucokinase-deficient
                     strain is placed under selective pressure for growth on 
                     glucose, a spontaneous point mutation appears to regenerate
                     a &sigma;<sup>70</sup> consensus sequence in the predicted 
                     promoter region, leading to a 94-fold increase in the 
                     expression |CITS: [16086580]|.  Prior work identified a 
                     spontaneous point mutation that was then thought to be 
                     within the <i>mak</i> coding region (A24D) and that led to 
                     increased and more stable Mak activity |CITS: [11742072]|; 
                     the start of the <i>mak</i> coding region has since been 
                     re-annotated, indicating that this mutation may also have 
                     affected transcription or transcript stability |CITS: 
                     [15157072]|. Review: |CITS: [11361065]|"
                     /translation="MRIGIDLGGTKTEVIALGDAGEQLYRHRLPTPRDDYRQTIETIAT
