     gene            423103..424950
                     /gene="secD"
                     /locus_tag="b0408"
                     /label=secD
     CDS             423103..424950
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="secD"
                     /locus_tag="b0408"
                     /product="trifunctional Sec translocon accessory complex
                     subunit SecD / Sec translocon accessory complex / Sec 
                     Holo-Translocon"
                     /label=secD
                     /note="SecD is an inner membrane protein that is part of
                     the heterotrimeric Sec translocon accessory complex.
                     Early studies characterized the <i>secD</i> locus, composed
                     of <i>yajC-secD-secF</i>, and implicated the products of 
                     <i>secD</i> and <i>secF</i> in protein export 
                     |CITS:[3029032][2170107][8428584][7507921]|. SecD and SecF 
                     are not absolutely essential but are required for efficient
                     protein transport; <i>secDF</i> double null mutants have a 
                     severe protein export defect at 37&deg;C which is 
                     exacerbated at 23 &deg;C; overexpressing SecD and SecF 
                     improves the export of proteins with a defective signal 
                     sequence |CITS: [8313900]|. SecD and SecF are implicated in
                     SecA membrane cycling at SecYEG <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [8548804]| (see further |CITS: [9305629]|).
                     SecD and SecF are implicated in the later, PMF-dependent 
                     stages of protein translocation |CITS: [8112309]|. SecDF 
                     comprises 12 transmembrane helices that form the 
                     proton-conducting channel, and 3 periplasmic domains |CITS:
                     [21562494]| (see also |CITS: [29718185]|). The periplasmic 
                     domains of SecDF mediate the recruitment of the |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3933 Bam complex| to facilitate delivery of outer 
                     membrane proteins (OMPs) to the outer membrane; the 
                     proton-conducting activity of SecDF, which is not required 
                     for SecA-mediated OMP translocation into inner membrane 
                     vesicles <i>in vitro</i>, is required for efficient OMP 
                     maturation <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [33146611]|.
                     SecD and SecF are predicted to be integral inner membrane 
                     proteins containing a large periplasmic domain; the 
                     proteins show sequence similarity particularly in the 
                     C-terminal hydrophobic region |CITS: [2170107]|. Mutants 
                     lacking the periplasmic domain form a stable complex but 
                     display an <i>in vitro</i> protein translocation defect 
                     |CITS: [16077136]|.
                     Strains carrying the <i>secD29</i> mutation (A593V) have a 
                     cold-sensitive phenotype; SecDF-YajC complexes containing 
                     the mutant protein show impaired protein translocation 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [16166550]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [8462840]|).
                     In the Transporter Classification Database |CITS: 
                     [26546518]|, SecD is a member of the 
                     Resistance-Nodulation-Cell Division (RND) Superfamily."
