                     /gene="tsx"
                     /locus_tag="b0411"
                     /product="bifunctional nucleoside-specific channel-forming
                     protein Tsx / outer membrane (deoxy)nucleoside channel"
                     /label=tsx
                     /note="Tsx is an outer membrane porin which is responsible
                     for the uptake of nucleosides and deoxynucleosides at 
                     sub-uM concentrations |CITS: [791677][3276691]|. 
                     Reconstitution of purified Tsx into lipid bilayer membranes
                     suggests that Tsx has a greater affinity for 
                     deoxynucleosides than for nucleosides and that it does not 
                     play a role in the transport of nucleobases |CITS: [2458926
                     ]|. Tsx forms a monomeric &beta;-barrel consisting of 12 
                     &beta; strands |CITS:[15272310]|.
                     In addition to being a channel for (deoxy)nucleosides Tsx 
                     functions as a receptor for bacteriophage and colicins and 
                     transports the antibiotic, albicidin |CITS: 
                     [791677][2191080]|. Tsx is the primary receptor for the 
                     uncharacterized environmental phage isolate U115 |CITS: 
                     [32675236]| (see further |CITS: [34784345]|).
                     Tsx is down-regulated when <i>E. coli</i> is exposed to 
                     ethanol but deletion of <i>tsx</i> does not affect the 
                     survival of cells exposed to ethanol |CITS: [29518576]|."
