     gene            432045..432563
                     /gene="pgpA"
                     /locus_tag="b0418"
                     /label=pgpA
     CDS             432045..432563
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pgpA"
                     /locus_tag="b0418"
                     /product="bifunctional phosphatidylglycerophosphatase A / 
                     phosphatidylglycerophosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.3.27"
                     /label=pgpA
                     /note="<i>E.coli</i> contains three 
                     phosphatidylglycerophosphatases - PgpA, |FRAME: 
                     PGPPHOSPHAB-MONOMER ""PgpB""| and |FRAME: EG11371 ""PgpC""|
                     - which catalyse the dephosphorylation of 
                     phosphatidylglycerol phosphate (PGP) to 
                     phosphatidylglycerol (PG), an essential phospholipid of the
                     inner and outer membrane in <i>E. coli</i>. PgpA and PgpC 
                     appear to be specific for PGP |CITS: [6296050], [21148555]|
                     whereas PgpB is a multifunctional enzyme that is also 
                     active on undecaprenyl diphosphate, phosphatidicic acid and
                     lysophosphatidic acid |CITS: [15778224], [8940025]|.
                     A <i>pgpApgpBpgpC</i> triple mutant is not viable but can 
                     be generated with a complementing plasmid containing any 
                     one of the three phosphatidylglycerophosphatases. A triple 
                     mutant complemented with any one of <i>pgpA, pgpB</i> or 
                     <i>pgpC</i> expressed on a temperature sensitive plasmid is
                     viable at 30&deg;C but not at 42&deg;C |CITS: [21148555]|. 
                     Lipid profiling of these strains showed accumulation of PGP
                     and disappearance of PG when shifted from 30&deg;C to 
                     42&deg;C |CITS: [21148555]|. Overexpression of <i>pgpA</i> 
                     from a plasmid results in increased 
                     phosphatidylglycerophosphatase activity compared to the 
                     wild type |CITS: [21148555]|.
                     PgpA contains a single transmembrane segment and an active 
                     site that faces the cytoplasm |CITS: [21148555]|.
                     "
