     gene            complement(435658..436557)
                     /gene="ispA"
                     /locus_tag="b0421"
                     /label=ispA
     CDS             complement(435658..436557)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ispA"
                     /locus_tag="b0421"
                     /product="trifunctional geranyl diphosphate/farnesyl
                     diphosphate synthase / geranyl diphosphate synthase / 
                     farnesyl diphosphate synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.5.1.1; 2.5.1.10"
                     /label=ispA
                     /note="Farnesyl diphosphate synthase can utilize both
                     prenyl (dimethylallyl) and geranyl diphosphates as 
                     substrates, generating
                     geranyl and farnesyl diphosphate, respectively. Therefore 
                     the enzyme can catalyze two sequential reactions in the 
                     polyisoprenoid biosynthetic pathway |CITS: [3519603]|. 
                     Isoprenoid product chain length specificity determinants 
                     within IspA were identified by random PCR mutagenesis 
                     |CITS: [15721807]|. Substrate selectivity and 
                     stereoselectivity of the enzyme have been investigated 
                     |CITS: [17147392]|.
                     Crystal structures of ternary complexes of the enzyme with 
                     substrate(s) or an inhibitor have been solved, revealing 
                     the contribution of three Mg<sup>2+</sup> ions to substrate
                     binding and catalysis |CITS: [14672944]|.
                     Even though  <i>ispA</i> has been reported to be essential 
                     for growth |CITS: [16738554]|, <i>ispA</i> null mutants are
                     viable, with near-wild type growth yield under anaerobic 
                     conditions and a slight reduction in growth yield under 
                     aerobic conditions. The mutant contains less than 13% and 
                     18%, respectively, of ubiquinone-8 and menaquinone-8 and 
                     40-70% of the wild-type levels of undecaprenyl phosphate. A
                     low level of geranyl diphosphate synthase activity was 
                     detected, which may be due to the activity of other 
                     prenyltransferases |CITS: [2676985][15809342]|. A 
                     short-chain prenyltransferase activity has been isolated 
                     from an <i>ispA</i> mutant strain |CITS: [17630132]|.
                     An enzyme-coupled continuous fluorescence assay was 
                     developed for farnesyl diphosphate synthases from <i>E. 
                     coli</i> and other organisms aimed at facilitating the 
                     screening of inhibitors for this drug target |CITS: 
                     [22085443]|. Photoactive farnesyl diphosphate analogues 
                     have also been designed to aid the study of farnesyl 
                     diphosphate synthases, including the <i>E. coli</i> enzyme 
                     |CITS: [19447628][19954434]|.
                     A mutation of <i>ispA</i> suppressed the cold-sensitive 
                     growth of cells lacking <i>rodZ</i>. The mutation also 
                     improved wild-type cell growth at low temperatures |CITS: 
                     [22003871]|.
                     In metabolic engineering studies, co-expression of the 
                     <i>ispA</i> gene was useful in increasing the production of
                     prenyl alcohols in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [20552672][19129660]|. However, the enzyme has low 
                     solubility and recombinant expression of IspA results in 
                     formation of inclusion bodies. Improved solubility of 
                     His<sub>6</sub>-IspA was achieved by fusing the gene with 
                     the GB1-domain of Streptococcal Protein G. Combining 
                     GB1-IspA expression with recombinant expression of the 
                     Tobacco Etch Virus protease (TEVp) resulted in a 
                     self-cleavage system that produced large amounts of cleaved
                     soluble IspA protein <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [36639146]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11583838][19672590]|"
