     gene            437005..438453
                     /gene="thiI"
                     /locus_tag="b0423"
                     /label=thiI
     CDS             437005..438453
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="thiI"
                     /locus_tag="b0423"
                     /product="bifunctional tRNA uridine 4-sulfurtransferase / 
                     carboxy-adenylated-[ThiS sulfur-carrier-protein] 
                     sulfurtransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.8.1.4"
                     /label=thiI
                     /note="ThiI is a bifunctional enzyme that is required both
                     for the conversion of uridine to 4-thiouridine 
                     (s<sup>4</sup>U) at position 8 in certain tRNAs and for the
                     synthesis of the thiazole moiety of thiamine. The protien 
                     contains several functional domains - an N-terminal 
                     ferredoxin-like domain (NFLD), a THUMP (THioUridine 
                     synthases, RNA Methylases and Pseudouridine synthases) 
                     domain, a PP-loop-containing pyrophosphatase domain (the 
                     catalytic domain), and a rhodanese domain. The THUMP domain
                     is known to specifically interact with tRNAs that are 
                     modified at position 6, 8 or 10 |CITS: [16687654]|. The 
                     PP-loop motif binds ATP, which allows the formation of an 
                     adenylated tRNA intermediate |CITS: [18604845]|, and the 
                     rhodanese domain is involved in sulfur transfer |CITS: 
                     [11443125]|.
                     Mutants in the biosynthesis of s<sup>4</sup>U-modified tRNA
                     were initially identified by their easily selectable 
                     phenotype, e.g. |CITS: [789361]|. In wild-type strains 
                     under near-ultraviolet (UV) light, tRNAs containing 
                     s<sup>4</sup>U8 acquire an intra-tRNA crosslink between U8 
                     and the nearby cytidine base at position 13 |CITS: 
                     [4932656][2578956]|. These damaged tRNAs are poor 
                     substrates for aminoacylation |CITS: [4614866]|, and the 
                     resulting uncharged tRNAs induce the stringent response and
                     subsequent growth arrest. Mutants that are resistant to 
                     exposure to near-UV light were shown to lack s<sup>4</sup>U
                     at position 8 of tRNAs |CITS: [789361]|. High probabilities
                     for tRNA modifications at position 8 or 9 were confirmed 
                     using site-specific profiling approach |CITS: [35155896]|.
                     ThiI was biochemically identified as the ""factor A"" 
                     component required for s<sup>4</sup>U-tRNA biosynthesis 
                     |CITS: [5541999][357427]| and later purified based on 
                     <i>thiI</i>'s ability to complement the <i>nuvA</i> mutant 
                     phenotype |CITS: [9592144]|. The ""factor C"" component was
                     later identified as |FRAME: G7325-MONOMER IscS| |CITS: 
                     [10600118]|. <i>In vitro</i> studies using radiolabelled 
                     sulfur showed transfer of sulfane sulfur from IscS to ThiI;
                     if tRNA is included, the sulfur is incorporated into the 
                     tRNA in a ThiI- and ATP-dependent manner |CITS: 
                     [10753862]|.
                     tRNA isolated from a strain containing the <i>thiI</i> 
                     D189A or K321M mutants lack s<sup>4</sup>U; these amino 
                     acid residues may be required for activating O4 of the U8 
                     base in tRNA by adenylation |CITS: [10595545]|. As of July 
                     2020, the presence of an adenylated tRNA intermediate has 
                     not been shown directly.
                     The C456 |CITS: [10722656]| and C344 |CITS: [11443125]| 
                     residues are critical for the sulfur transfer function of 
                     ThiI; a mechanism for sulfur transfer including the 
                     formation of a tRNA-adenylate intermediate and a disulfide 
                     bond between C456 and C344 during turnover has been 
                     proposed |CITS: [11443125]|. Supporting a 
                     persulfide-disulfide-thiol cycle at C456, ThiI is unable to
                     catalyze more than a single turnover in the absence of 
                     added reductant |CITS: [12510310]|. The presence of an 
                     intra-subunit disulfide bond after a single catalytic 
                     turnover has been shown |CITS: [27293139]|.
                     ThiI was shown to interact with tRNA |CITS: [10753862]|. 
                     The specificity for the U8 base of tRNA appears to be 
                     conferred by the secondary and tertiary structure of tRNA 
                     |CITS: [15037613]|. The N-terminal domain of ThiI 
                     recognizes the acceptor-stem region of tRNA, while the 
                     C-terminal domain appears to induce a conformational change
                     in the full-length tRNA |CITS: [19509301]|.
                     ThiI is required for the posttranslational formation of a 
                     thiocarboxylate modification at the C-terminal glycine 
                     residue of |FRAME: THIS-MONOMER| <i>in vivo</i>. ThiS 
                     isolated from a <i>thiI</i>-deficient strain does not 
                     contain this modification |CITS: [9632726]|.
                     Nuv: ""<b>n</b>ear-<b>UV</b>"" |CITS: [408039]|<br>ThiI: 
                     |CITS: [9592144]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10382260][16547481][26443755][40210524]|"
