     gene            complement(444106..446097)
                     /gene="cyoB"
                     /locus_tag="b0431"
                     /label=cyoB
     CDS             complement(444106..446097)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cyoB"
                     /locus_tag="b0431"
                     /product="trifunctional cytochrome bo3 subunit 1 /
                     cytochrome bo3 ubiquinol:oxygen oxidoreductase / ubiquinol 
                     oxidase (H(+)-transporting)"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.3"
                     /label=cyoB
                     /note="<i>cyoB</i> encodes subunit I of the cytochrome
                     <i>bo</i> complex.  The three prosthetic groups (heme O, 
                     heme B and Cu<sub>B</sub>) are located in subunit 1 and are
                     liganded by conserved histidine residues: His106 and His421
                     for heme B; His419 for heme O; His284, His333 and His334 
                     for Cu<sub>B</sub> (|CITS: [1309808][1309809][83820 
                     47][8218219][8399240][8241181][7983018][8163490]| and 
                     reviewed in |CITS: [11132627]|).  The 3.5&Aring; crystal 
                     structure confirmed these assignments |CITS: [11017202]|. 
                     Subunit 1 contains 15 transmembrane helices |CITS: 
                     [2165491]|; helices 1-12 constitute a transmembrane barrel 
                     |CITS: [11017202]|.
                     Mutation of a highly conserved amino acid (Asp135) in 
                     cytochrome <i>bo</i>3 subunit I appears to decouple proton 
                     pumping from electron transfer-activity, which indicates 
                     that subunit I is likely to be involved in proton 
                     translocation |CITS: [8399242]|. Two polar cavities (the K 
                     and D channels), originating on the cytosolic side of the 
                     membrane and leading to the binuclear center are evident in
                     the crystal structure and may function as proton transfer 
                     channels |CITS: [11017202]|.
                     A potential quinone binding site is located in the membrane
                     domain of subunit I; it may represent the high affinity 
                     binding site (UQ<sub>H</sub>); a low affinity binding site 
                     (UQ<sub>L</sub>) could not be determined |CITS: [11017202]|
                     (see further |CITS: [28235459][28235459]|)
                     Mutation of the histidine residues that coordinate heme B 
                     and heme O result in defective assembly of the complex; 
                     mutation of residues coordinating the Cu<sub>B</sub> center
                     showed no effect on complex assembly |CITS: [17583738]|."
