     gene            complement(446119..447066)
                     /gene="cyoA"
                     /locus_tag="b0432"
                     /label=cyoA
     CDS             complement(446119..447066)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cyoA"
                     /locus_tag="b0432"
                     /product="trifunctional cytochrome bo3 subunit 2 /
                     cytochrome bo3 ubiquinol:oxygen oxidoreductase / ubiquinol 
                     oxidase (H(+)-transporting)"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.3"
                     /label=cyoA
                     /note="CyoA is subunit II of the 4 subunit cytochrome
                     <i>bo</i> complex. CyoA was initially suggested to contain 
                     one of two ubiquinone binding sites; current work suggests 
                     that the complex contains only one binding site located in 
                     subunit I (CyoB) |CITS: 
                     [7961841][11017202][20416270][28235459][28538096]|.
                     CyoA consists of two domains - an N terminal domain 
                     containing two transmembrane helices and a large C-terminal
                     domain located in the periplasm |CITS: [2165491], 
                     [11017202]|.  CyoA is a lipoprotein; during maturation, the
                     protein is modified by attachment of fatty acids and 
                     protease cleavage at C25. The addition of [14C]palmitic 
                     acid to the growth medium results in covalent labeling of 
                     CyoA with the fatty acid. A C25A mutation blocks 
                     lipoprotein processing but the protein retains oxidase 
                     activity suggesting that posttranslational modification is 
                     not essential for assembly or activity of the cytochrome 
                     <i>bo</i> terminal oxidase complex |CITS:[9298948]|.
                     |FRAME: YIDC ""YidC""|, the Sec translocon (|FRAME: 
                     SECE-G-Y-CPLX ""SecYEG""|) and |FRAME: CPLX0-8089 ""SecA""|
                     are required for efficient insertion of cyoA into 
                     proteoliposomes |CITS: [16513637]|. Efficient targeting and
                     assembly of CyoA <i>in vivo</i> requires the |FRAME: 
                     SRP-CPLX ""SRP""|, SecYEG, SecA and YidC. Insertion of the 
                     large periplasmic C-terminal segment requires both the Sec 
                     translocon and SecA whereas YidC is sufficient for 
                     insertion of the N-terminal domain. TM1 of CyoA recruits 
                     the SRP for targeting of nascent CyoA. The SecYEG 
                     translocase operates downstream of YidC during the membrane
                     assembly of CyoA |CITS: [16481320]| |CITS: [18155041]|. 
                     Membrane insertion of the CyoA protein is independent of 
                     the proton-motive force |CITS: [16488430], [18155041]|.
                     The C-terminal periplasmic domain of CyoA contains an 11 
                     stranded anti-parallel &beta; sandwich and three &alpha; 
                     helices. This domain has been engineered to contain a 
                     dinuclear copper center (Cu<sub>A</sub>) that is 
                     characteristic of the cytochrome c oxidase family 
                     |CITS:[8433374][8618822]|.
                     <i>cyoA</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|."
