     gene            complement(447526..449001)
                     /gene="ampG"
                     /locus_tag="b0433"
                     /label=ampG
     CDS             complement(447526..449001)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ampG"
                     /locus_tag="b0433"
                     /product="muropeptide:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=ampG
                     /note="AmpG encodes an anhyhyro-muropeptide transporter
                     required for peptidoglycan recycling. In many 
                     enterobacteria (but not <i>E. coli</i>) AmpG participates 
                     in an |FRAME: EG10040-MONOMER AmpC| induction pathway (see 
                     |CITS: [24955547]|).
                     <i>ampG</i> is required for the induction of &beta;-lactam 
                     resistance in an <i>E. coli</i> strain engineered for 
                     inducible &beta;-lactamase activity (strain SN0301/pNU305) 
                     |CITS: [8231804][7773404]|. <i>ampG</i> mutants are 
                     defective in peptidoglycan recycling and release 
                     muropeptides (GlnNAc-anhydoMurNAc-tetrapeptide and 
                     tetrapeptide) into the culture medium; AmpG is a 
                     muropeptide-specific permease required for peptidoglycan 
                     recycling |CITS: [7925310]| (see also |CITS: [8878601]|. 
                     AmpG transports anhydro-muropeptides specifically and 
                     transport is dependent on the proton motive force |CITS: 
                     [12426329]|. AmpG and the |FRAME: CPLX0-3970 murein ABC 
                     transporter| (OppBCDFMppA) contribute in different ways to 
                     muropeptide recycling and scavenging; AmpG plays a major 
                     role in peptidoglycan recycling; AmpG expression and 
                     substrate specificity appears fine-tuned to match 
                     peptidoglycan turn-over rates |CITS: [37871219]|.
                     AmpG is an inner membrane protein with two large 
                     cytoplasmic loops and 10 transmembrane segments |CITS: 
                     [8231804][15728916]|.
                     Deletion of <i>ampG</i> lowers the intrinsic &beta;-lactam 
                     resistance of <i>E. coli</i> and reduces biofilm formation 
                     |CITS: [29566229]|. The Keio collection <i>ampG</i> mutant 
                     is sensitive to lithium (<90% growth at 200mM Li) |CITS: 
                     [39983623]|.
                     In the Transporter Classification Database |CITS: 
                     [33170213]| AmpG is a member of the Peptide/Acetyl-Coenzyme
                     A/Drug Transporter (PAT) Family within the Major 
                     Facilitator Superfamily
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9158731][18535144]|"
