     gene            450589..451887
                     /gene="tig"
                     /locus_tag="b0436"
                     /label=tig
     CDS             450589..451887
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tig"
                     /locus_tag="b0436"
                     /product="bifunctional trigger factor / peptidyl-prolyl
                     cis-trans isomerase"
                     /EC_number="5.2.1.8"
                     /label=tig
                     /note="Trigger factor (TF) is a ribsome-associated, 
                     ATP-independent chaperone that cooperates with downstream 
                     proteins such as |FRAME: EG10241-MONOMER DnaK| and |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3934 GroEL-GroES| in the folding of newly synthesized
                     cytosolic proteins; TF also interacts with nascent 
                     secretory proteins and cooperates with the |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-8046 SecB| chaperone to usher a subset of proteins on
                     the post-translational secretory pathway.
                     TF is involved in protection against copper-induced protein
                     aggregation under anaerobic growth conditions |CITS: 
                     [35289645]|.
                     TF exhibits peptidyl-prolyl isomerase and protein folding 
                     activity <i>in vitro</i>; TF associates with ribosome bound
                     nascent cytosolic and secretory polypeptide chains <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [2843289][7588623][8633085][9009267]|. 
                     Nascent polypeptides interact first with TF |CITS: 
                     [10458167]| at the ribosome, where TF binds at proteins 
                     L23/L29 at the polypeptide exit site |CITS: 
                     [12226666][12756233][15148364]|. TF is thought to interact 
                     primarily with short nascent chains |CITS: [8521806]| and 
                     its function overlaps with that of DnaK, which interacts 
                     with longer nascent chains downstream of TF |CITS: 
                     [10380927]|.  Although deletion mutants of either 
                     <i>tig</i> or <i>dnaK</i> are viable, their combined 
                     deletion is lethal at temperatures above 30&deg;C |CITS: 
                     [10458167]|. Recruitment of TF occurs <i>in vivo</i> when 
                     the ribosome nascent-chain (RNC) polypeptide is is 
                     approximately 100 amino acids in length. Premature 
                     engagement of TF (through over-expression of <i>tig</i>) 
                     interferes with the co-translational removal of the of the 
                     N-terminal formyl methionine residue |CITS: [22153074]|. TF
                     postpones co-translational folding in model substrates 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and can also unfold ribosome tethered 
                     polypeptides that have acquired their native folded state 
                     |CITS: [22921937]|. TF stabilizes partially folded states 
                     and mediates folding transitions until proteins reach their
                     native structure |CITS: [23831649]|. TF has foldase 
                     activity on protein substrates under tension; at zero force
                     TF stabilizes the unfolded state or protects the partially 
                     folded state, in the presence of force (such as that 
                     present at the ribosomal exit tunnel) TF decreases the 
                     stability of the misfolded state and increases the folding 
                     probability of a substrate construct (protein L) |CITS: 
                     [28939815]|. TF employs a hybrid holding-folding mechanism 
                     of chaperone action; purified TF binds and maintains the 
                     model client protein, glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate 
                     dehydrogenase (GAPDH), in a monomeric form and promotes 
                     folding to a near-native state; following its release the 
                     monomer rapidly tetramerizes and becomes enzymatically 
                     active |CITS: [35840586]|.
                     Characterization of isolated ribosome nascent chain 
                     (RNC):chaperone complexes provides molecular insight into 
                     the coordination of TF and DnaK/DnaJ binding during 
                     chaperone-assisted cotranslational folding of a model 
                     multidomain protein |CITS: [38908370]| (and see |CITS: 
                     [38996455]|). Thermodynamic factors promote TF's 
                     interaction with diverse nascent chain sequences; TF 
                     binding to RNC complexes is entropy driven |CITS: 
                     [38985872]|.
                     TF is a genuine secretory pathway chaperone; it binds 
                     tightly to a subset of secretory preproteins and prevents 
                     their aggregation; TF:secretory protein complexes are 
                     targeted to the SecYEG-SecA translocase; SecB relieves the 
                     inhibitory effect of TF on pre-protein translocation |CITS:
                     [32307852]|.
                     TF rapidly binds to and dissociates from vacant or 
                     translating ribosomes; transient complexes are strongly 
                     stabilised when the nascent peptide displays a TF binding 
                     sequence; TF and |FRAME: SRP-CPLX SRP| exhibit partial 
                     competitive binding to ribosome nascent chain complexes 
                     |CITS: [24939037]|. A nascent polypeptide chain is needed 
                     for TF binding to ribosomes; <i>in vivo</i>, the 
                     interaction between TF and RNC is highly dynamic with rapid
                     binding and unbinding |CITS: [39739798]|. TF affects 
                     cotranslational protein complex assembly; in an engineered 
                     <i>E. coli</i> strain, TF increases the specificity of 
                     interaction between a fully synthesized subunit encoded by 
                     the upstream gene, and a nascent subunit encoded by the 
                     downstream gene, of an operon |CITS: [26405228]|.
                     TF cycles between a dimeric form when free in solution and 
                     a conformationally activated monomer induced by ribosome 
                     binding; TF may remain bound to substrate proteins 
                     post-translationally |CITS: [17051157]|.
                     TF substrates have been identified by TF copurification and
                     TF resolvable aggregation. Many of the substrates are 
                     subunits of multimeric complexes including many ribosomal 
                     proteins and a role for TF in the biogenesis of protein 
                     complexes has been suggested |CITS: [19737520]|. Selective 
                     ribosome profiling suggests that nascent outer membrane 
                     proteins (OMPs) are preferential TF targets <i>in vivo</i>.
                     Consistent with this &Delta;<i>tig</i> cells have decreased
                     OMP levels and phenotypes that are consistent with 
                     disrupted outer membrane integrity |CITS: [22153074]|. 
                     Secretory proteins may be directed into a Sec-mediated 
                     post-translational pathway by means of their preferential 
                     interaction with TF |CITS: [10619852]|.
                     TF consists of three domains |CITS: [15334087]| all of 
                     which interact with the nascent polypeptide during 
                     translation |CITS: [17296610]|. The N-terminal domain 
                     contains the TF signature motif which mediates ribosome 
                     binding |CITS: [9268318][12226666][14656439]|. The middle 
                     domain displays peptidyl-prolyl cis/trans isomerase 
                     (PPIase) activity |CITS: [8612805]| and is dispensable for 
                     the chaperone activity of TF <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [14726952][14729669]|. The C-terminal 
                     domain (CTD) is essential for chaperone activity |CITS: 
                     [16926148][16380200]|. The peptidyl-prolyl cis/trans 
                     isomerase domain (PPD) and CTD of TF form a versatile 
                     cavity that binds the partially folded domains of diverse 
                     clients while the ribosome-binding domain (RBD) acts as a 
                     physical spacer to prevent chaperone binding close to the 
                     exit tunnel |CITS: [38908370]|.
                     Three TF molecules are required to bind unfolded alkaline 
                     phosphatase (PhoA) <i>in vitro</i>. The 3 TF molecules bind
                     PhoA in discrete regions separated by stretches of about 
                     80-90 amino acids. TF contains 4 sites, which bind unfolded
                     PhoA over a distance of about 90&Aring;. TF binds to PhoA 
                     primarily though hydrophobic contacts; interaction between 
                     TF and PhoA is dynamic with fast association and 
                     dissociation rates. TF is a dimer in solution but PhoA 
                     binding results in TF monomerization. TF does not interact 
                     with folded MBP<sub>mut</sub> (an aggregation-prone maltose
                     binding protein variant) but multivalent binding between TF
                     and unfolded MBP<sub>mut</sub> prevents its aggregation 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [24812405]|. TF has insignificant 
                     unfoldase activity in solution but strong unfoldase 
                     activity when bound to the ribosome |CITS: [24812405]|.
                     <i>tig</i> shows differential codon adaptation, resulting 
                     in differential translation efficiency signatures, in 
                     thermophilic microbes. It is therefore predicted to play a 
                     role in the heat shock response.  A <i>tig</i> deletion 
                     mutant is more sensitive than wild-type to heat shock and 
                     osmotic stress |CITS: [24580753]|.
                     A strain containing non-polar deletions of all six 
                     cytoplasmic PPIases (&Delta;6<i>ppi</i>) shows a growth 
                     defect at 37&deg;C in rich media and at 43&deg;C in both 
                     rich and minimal media |CITS: [32545723]|.
                     trigger factor: 'triggers membrane assembly-competent 
                     folding' |CITS: [3299381]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8682200][15837180][20132842]|<BR>Comments:
                     |CITS: [15457244][17122845][23062952][24812390]|"
