     gene            457371..459242
                     /gene="ppiD"
                     /locus_tag="b0441"
                     /label=ppiD
     CDS             457371..459242
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ppiD"
                     /locus_tag="b0441"
                     /product="periplasmic folding chaperone"
                     /label=ppiD
                     /note="PpiD shows sequence similarity to peptidyl-prolyl 
                     cis-trans isomerases (PPIases), but contrary to an earlier 
                     report |CITS: [9670013]|, does not have PPIase activity 
                     |CITS: [19866485]|. PpiD has PPIase-independent chaperone 
                     activity |CITS: [20920237]|.
                     A <i>ppiD</i> null mutation is associated with reduced 
                     levels of outer membrane proteins (OMPs) and induces a 
                     periplasmic unfolding stress response. <i>ppiD</i> is a 
                     multicopy suppressor of a null mutation in <i>surA</i> and 
                     a <i>ppiD surA</i> double mutation is synthetically lethal 
                     |CITS: [9670013]|. Insertional inactivation of both 
                     <i>ppiD</i> and <i>surA</i> does not result in a synthetic 
                     lethal phenotype and insertional inactivation of 
                     <i>ppiD</i> has no discernable effect on OMP levels |CITS: 
                     [16267292][20920237]|. Lack of PpiD impairs the release of 
                     newly synthesized OmpA from spheroblasts |CITS: 
                     [18439025]|. Overexpression of <i>ppiD</i> restores the 
                     viability of a <i>surA skp</i> double null mutant and 
                     results in increased levels of OmpA in the outer membrane 
                     |CITS: [20920237]|.
                     PpiD is predicted to contain an N-terminal transmembrane 
                     helix followed by three periplasmic domains. The second 
                     periplasmic domain (residues 264-357) is a parvulin-like 
                     domain which adopts a parvulin fold but does not catalyse 
                     <i>cis-trans</i> isomerisation of prolyl peptide bonds as 
                     is characteristic of parvulin proteins |CITS: 
                     [9670013][19866485]|.
                     PpiD and PpiD lacking the parvulin domain exhibit chaperone
                     activity <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [20920237]|. PpiD has been 
                     found as a homodimer and homotrimer in the membrane |CITS: 
                     [16079137]|. PpiD forms a complex with |FRAME: 
                     G7321-MONOMER ""YfgM""| |CITS: [21210718]|.
                     PpiD may play a role in bacteriophage T4 infection |CITS: 
                     [38675830]|
                     Regulation has been described |CITS: [9670013]|."
                     /translation="MMDSLRTAANSLVLKIIFGIIIVSFILTGVSGYLIGGGNNYAAKV
