     gene            468422..469708
                     /gene="amtB"
                     /locus_tag="b0451"
                     /label=amtB
     CDS             468422..469708
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="amtB"
                     /locus_tag="b0451"
                     /product="bifunctional ammonium transporter / transport of 
                     ammonium"
                     /label=amtB
                     /note="AmtB, a member of the Ammonia Channel Transporter
                     (Amt) family, facilitates the uptake of ammonium/ammonia 
                     and is important for growth at low external ammonium 
                     concentrations. Under nitrogen rich conditions transport of
                     ammonium by AmtB is blocked by the deuridylylated form of 
                     |FRAME: PII2-CPLX ""PII-2""|, the product of the 
                     <I>glnK</I> gene cotranscribed with <I>amtB</I>. The 
                     specific substrate of the AmtB transporter is controversial
                     - it has been variously proposed that AmtB transports 
                     uncharged ammonia (NH<sub>3</sub>), cationic ammonium 
                     (NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>) and that 
                     NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> deprotonation takes place at the
                     periplasmic binding site prior to NH<sub>3</sub> conduction
                     through the pore (reviewed by |CITS: 
                     [16273393][25483282]|). More recent evidence indicates that
                     following NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> deprotonation, 
                     H<sup>+</sup> and NH<sub>3</sub> follow two separated 
                     pathways across the membrane before recombining to 
                     NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> |CITS: [32662768]| (and see 
                     |CITS: [32840481][35196127]|).
                     Disruption of <I>amtB</I> impairs growth at low 
                     NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> concentration only at pH values 
                     less than 7 suggesting that AmtB transports the uncharged 
                     form, NH<sub>3</sub>; this defect could be complemented by 
                     the cloned <I>amtB</I> gene |CITS:[9618533]|. Whole cell 
                     transport experiments indicate that AmtB mediates transport
                     of the ammonium analogue, methylammonium, and suggest that 
                     transport is dependent on rapid metabolism of the 
                     transported substrate |CITS:[9618533]|. AmtB mediates 
                     NH<sub>3</sub> uniport in an energy independent, 
                     non-concentrative process |CITS: [9618533][15876187]|. AmtB
                     activity is electrogenic |CITS: [30211659][32662768]|; 
                     cytoplasmic and periplasmic water wires form a continuous 
                     path for H<sup>+</sup> movement from its 
                     NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> sequestration site at the 
                     periplasm, to the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [32662768][35084334][35196127]|.
                     AmtB has 11 membrane spanning segments and is trimeric in 
                     the native cell membrane 
                     |CITS:[12023896][15563598][15361618][15568015]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [10931328]|). AmtB has an ammonium binding site 
                     within the periplasmic vestibule |CITS:[16435797]|, a 
                     hydrophobic pore for ammonia conductance 
                     |CITS:[15361618][15563598]|, and a cytoplasmic 
                     reprotonation site |CITS:[17291160]|, though molecular 
                     dynamics simulations suggest the pore may also contain 
                     water raising the possibility of ammonium conductance as 
                     well |CITS:[17351012]| (see also |CITS: [24021113]|). 
                     Residues important in substrate binding, deprotonation and 
                     channel gating have been identified |CITS: 
                     [18362341][19278252]|.
                     Phosphatidylglycerol binding stabilizes the AmtB channel 
                     |CITS: [24899312]|. Specific lipid-AmtB interactions can 
                     allosterically modulate the binding of lipids of different 
                     type <i>in vitro</i>; allosteric lipid-protein interactions
                     may function to define the AmtB-lipid environment <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [29507234]|. Lipid-binding to AmtB 
                     allosterically modulates the interaction between AmtB and 
                     GlnK <i>in vitro</i>; lipid tail length and head group 
                     identity affect the allosteric effects observed |CITS: 
                     [29259178]|.
                     The <I>amtB</I> gene forms an operon with the <I>glnK</I> 
                     gene, and expression of this operon occurs under 
                     nitrogen-limiting conditions and is dependent on the NtrC 
                     regulatory protein |CITS:[8843440]|. GlnK and AmtB have 
                     been shown to quickly and reversibly interact after 
                     ammonium shock |CITS:[11847102]|. This reversible 
                     sequestration of GlnK by AmtB is rapidly responsive to 
                     &mu;M concentrations of ammonia and requires the C-terminal
                     cytoplasmic domain of AmtB |CITS:[11847102]|. The 1:1 
                     interaction is dependent upon ATP and is sensitive to 
                     2-oxoglutarate when MgCl<sub>2</sub> is present 
                     |CITS:[16864585]|. Deuridylylated GlnK is sequestered by 
                     AmtB; deuridylylated GlnK inhibits ammonium flux through 
                     AmtB by protein-protein interaction |CITS:[11847102]|. 
                     Deletion or mutation of <I>amtB</I> prevents 
                     deuridylylation of GlnK due to decreased intracellular 
                     glutamine levels - intracellular glutamine levels being the
                     main effector of the uridyltransferase activity of |FRAME: 
                     GLND-MONOMER ""GlnD""|. Deuridylylation of GlnK and 
                     subsequent GlnK-AmtB association is due to increased 
                     intracellular glutamine levels |CITS:[14668330]|.
                     Crystal structures of the AmtB-GlnK complex have been 
                     determined to resolutions of 1.96 &Aring; |CITS:[17190799]|
                     and 2.5 &Aring; |CITS:[17220269]|. A GlnK trimer, along 
                     with its associated 3 molecules of ADP, associates with the
                     cytoplasmic face of the AmtB trimer by inserting the T-loop
                     of each GlnK subunit into the cytoplasmic pore exit of the 
                     adjacent AmtB subunit |CITS:[17220269]|.
                     <i>E. coli amtB</i> expressed in <i>Xenopus</i> oocytes is 
                     active as both a CO<sub>2</sub> and NH<sub>3</sub> channel 
                     |CITS:[19273840]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS:[15667297],[16273393]|<BR>
                     Comment: |CITS: [15361612]|"
