     gene            complement(469757..470617)
                     /gene="tesB"
                     /locus_tag="b0452"
                     /label=tesB
     CDS             complement(469757..470617)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tesB"
                     /locus_tag="b0452"
                     /product="multifunctional acyl-CoA thioesterase II / 
                     (S)-3-hydroxybutanoyl-CoA thioesterase / 
                     (R)-3-hydroxybutanoyl-CoA thioesterase / acyl-CoA 
                     thioesterase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.2.20"
                     /label=tesB
                     /note="Thioesterase II (TesB) is one of a number of 
                     thioesterases present in <i>E. coli</i>.  The enzyme has 
                     relatively broad substrate specificity, cleaving medium- 
                     and long-chain acyl-CoA substrates |CITS: 
                     [18702504][20547355][24271180]|.  Thioesterase II is one of
                     the three thioesterases (|FRAME: EG10995|, |FRAME: G6244| 
                     and |FRAME: EG11467|) known to support growth on oleate or 
                     conjugated linoleic acid (CLA; |FRAME: 
                     9-CIS11-TRANS-OCTADECADIENOATE|) as the sole source of 
                     carbon, though at a reduced growth rate |CITS: 
                     [18702504][35876515]|.
                     A crystal structure of the enzyme has been solved at 1.9 
                     &Aring; resolution.  TesB appears to be a homodimer in the 
                     crystal structure |CITS: [10876240]|, while data from 
                     non-denaturing polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis suggested
                     a homotetrameric structure |CITS: [1645722]|. The D204 
                     residue was predicted to be in the active site; its 
                     importance was confirmed by kinetic analysis of mutants 
                     |CITS: [10876240]|.
                     Strains either lacking or overproducing <i>tesB</i> have no
                     obvious defect |CITS: [2869026][1645722]|. Overproduction 
                     of TesB relieves inhibition of fatty acid synthesis by 
                     long-chain acyl-ACP molecules that accumulate upon glycerol
                     starvation |CITS: [7910602]|.
                     TesB has been used in the engineered production of 
                     enantiopure |FRAME: CPD-335| and |FRAME: CPD-1843| |CITS: 
                     [19304817]| and in the engineered reversal of the 
                     &beta;-oxidation cycle |CITS: [21832992]|."
