     gene            471904..472017
                     /gene="ffs"
                     /locus_tag="b0455"
                     /label=ffs
     misc_RNA        471904..472017
                     /gene="ffs"
                     /locus_tag="b0455"
                     /product="signal recognition particle 4.5S RNA"
                     /note="The 4.5S RNA and Ffh together form the signal
                     recognition particle (SRP) |CITS: 
                     [2171778][1701272][1279430]| which binds to the signal 
                     sequence |CITS: [1279430]| and targets the nascent protein 
                     to the SRP receptor, FtsY |CITS: [8107852]|. The SRP is 
                     involved in integration of nascent proteins into the 
                     membrane |CITS: 
                     [8521840][8985168][9843943][10397756][10781078]|.
                     The interaction between 4.5S RNA and Ffh |CITS: 
                     [1281314][7518399][8608448][9659905][10556722]|, the 
                     GTP-dependent |CITS: [8107852]| interaction between the SRP
                     and the SRP receptor |CITS: [8107852][10834842][11233986]|,
                     and the GTP hydrolysis cycle |CITS: 
                     [8107852][7660124][9326611][9799502][11735405]| have been 
                     examined in detail. The 4.5S RNA catalyses the interaction 
                     of Ffh and the SRP receptor only when signal sequence is 
                     bound |CITS: [19119234]|.
                     Independently of Ffh, 4.5S RNA binds to elongation factor G
                     (FusA) and to ribosomes |CITS: 
                     [2438050][9914538][10556722][10780448][12022228]|, and 4.5S
                     RNA is proposed to modulate (specifically to release |CITS:
                     [10780448]|) the interaction of elongation factor G with 
                     the ribosome |CITS: [9914538][10780448]|.
                     RNase P processing of the precursor of 4.5S RNA has been 
                     examined |CITS: 
                     [778851][6197186][6199841][8020097][10525416]|. Processing 
                     by other RNases has been observed |CITS: [9501180]|. The 
                     4.5S RNA structure has been studied in detail |CITS: [6366 
                     742][1689715][8608448][10801497][10944346][12777762]|. 4.5S
                     RNA folds into a single hairpin structure containing an 
                     apical GGAA tetraloop that caps one end plus five symmetric
                     or asymmetric internal loops (reviews: |CITS: 
                     [1707519][7518075]|). The effects of pausing on folding 
                     kinetics have been studied |CITS: [36115451]|. The two ends
                     of the 4.5S RNA hairpin have distinct functions in the 
                     targeting cycle |CITS: [23235881]|. 4.5S RNA acts as a 
                     molecular scaffold for movement of the Ffh-FtsY GTPase 
                     complex from the tetraloop region to the distal region. 
                     This movement facilitates docking of the ribosome to the 
                     translocon and hydrolysis of GTP, which in turn stimulates 
                     SRP recycling |CITS: [23235881][23235870]|.
                     Any mutant lacking 4.5S RNA is inviable |CITS: [6208371]|. 
                     Depletion of 4.5S RNA leads to induction of the heat shock 
                     response |CITS: [1688840][1701272]|, lambda phage induction
                     |CITS: [1688840]|, defects in translation |CITS: 
                     [6208371][2433587][7513325]|, buildup of pre-beta-lactamase
                     |CITS: [2171778]|, decreased abundance of maltose binding 
                     protein |CITS: [2171778]|, and cell inviability |CITS: 
                     [6208371]|. Mutants exhibit defects in membrane protein 
                     assembly |CITS: [10781078][11741850]|. The 4.5S RNAdl1 
                     allele is a dominant mutation that causes induction of the 
                     heat shock response, defects in translocation of a subset 
                     of proteins, aberrant cell envelope morphology, and 
                     eventual translation defects and cell inviability |CITS: 
                     [1701272]|. Overexpression of 4.5S RNA suppresses the heat 
                     sensitivity of a ffh-10(Ts) mutant |CITS: [11976293]|. The 
                     inviability of a 4.5S RNA mutant is suppressed by 
                     <i>fusA</i> mutations |CITS: [2438050]|. The phenotypes of 
                     a 4.5S RNA mutant are partially suppressed by mutations in 
                     16S or 23S rRNA |CITS: [11904179]|.
                     The inviability of a 4.5S RNA mutant is functionally 
                     complemented by expression of RNAs from various bacterial 
                     species |CITS: [2512280][1689715]|, including 
                     <i>Haemophilus influenzae</i> |CITS: [9522453]|, by 
                     archaebacterial |CITS: [1999396]| or human SRP7S RNA |CITS:
                     [2171778][1999396]|, and by a <i>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</i> 
                     RNA |CITS: [1370291]|. The viability of a <i>Bacillus 
                     subtilis</i> scRNA mutant is functionally complemented by 
                     expression of <i>E. coli</i> 4.5S RNA |CITS: [1372600]|, 
                     but the <i>B. subtilis</i> sporulation defect is not |CITS:
                     [8535524]|. Exogenous SRP54 protein exhibits binding to 
                     <i>E. coli</i> 4.5S RNA |CITS: [1699948][1702385]|.
                     Regulation has been described |CITS: [8780771]|. 
                     Complementary binding of peptide nucleic acids (PNAs) to 
                     the tetraloop region of the 4.5S RNA has been examined for 
                     its ability to inhibit the interaction of 4.5S RNA with Ffh
                     to form the SRP; the PNAs could be used as antisense 
                     molecular antibiotics |CITS: [38595046]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [1715753][8145649][10068996][11051763][111 
                     23669][32213244]|"
