                     /product="maltose O-acetyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.79"
                     /label=maa
                     /note="Maltose <i>O</i>-acetyltransferase has broad
                     substrate specificity, and is capable of acetylating many 
                     sugars.  The enzyme is not part of the maltose system.
                     Its specific physiological role is not known, but it is 
                     thought that the enzyme prevents the accumulation of free 
                     sugars to high levels through acetylation, playing a 
                     detoxifying role in the cell |CITS: 
                     [1856235][7041741][7012137]|.  Acetylmaltose is excreted 
                     into the culture medium in <i>malB<sup>+</sup> malQ</i> 
                     strains, which accumulate maltose, but cannot metabolize it
                     |CITS: [7012137]|.
                     The enzyme belongs to the hexapeptide repeat family of 
                     proteins.  A crystal structure has been solved at 2.15 
                     &Aring; resolution |CITS: [11717516][12731863]|.  The most 
                     effective substrate is glucose, which is acetylated at the 
                     C6 position; maltose is acetylated at C6 of the nonreducing
                     glucose moiety |CITS: [12731863]|.
                     Mac: ""<b>m</b>altose trans<b>ac</b>etylase"" |CITS: 
                     [1856235]|"
