     gene            complement(475546..475764)
                     /gene="hha"
                     /locus_tag="b0460"
                     /label=hha
     CDS             complement(475546..475764)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hha"
                     /locus_tag="b0460"
                     /product="haemolysin expression-modulating protein Hha"
                     /label=hha
                     /note="Hha affects production of multiple proteins,
                     causing pleiotropic effects on catabolite repression and 
                     other systems; these effects are higher under conditions of
                     higher osmotic strength |CITS: [10322001]|.  The H-NS-Hha 
                     complex plays a role in repressing the expression of DegP 
                     during growth at low osmolarity |CITS: [16143461]|.
                     Hha interacts with H-NS; complex formation does not require
                     DNA |CITS: [10778755][11790731]|.  Hha undergoes intrinsic 
                     conformational changes that influence its interaction with 
                     H-NS |CITS: [15720293]|.  The N-terminal domain of H-NS can
                     be functionally replaced by Hha |CITS: [16030239]|.
                     The <i>tomB-hha</i> operon may encode an antitoxin-toxin 
                     module.  Expression of TomB diminishes the toxicity of Hha 
                     expression.  Both TomB and Hha were found to bind certain 
                     coding and intergenic regions of the genome |CITS: 
                     [18545668]|.
                     Hha binds DNA non-specifically |CITS: 
                     [10778755][12193622]|.  Hha was thought to bind to a 
                     transcriptional silencing region within the <i>hlyC</i> 
                     gene of the hemolysin expression plasmid |CITS: [7798139]|,
                     but specific binding is in fact due to H-NS |CITS: 
                     [12193622]|.
                     RfaH appears to be an antisilencer of the xenogenes 
                     silenced by HNS and HhA+YdgT proteins in a Rho-dependent 
                     way |CITS: [35258322]|
                     Hha affects DNA supercoiling and has effects on 
                     transcription of episomal genes |CITS: [7934907]|.  Hha 
                     also affects transposition |CITS: 
                     [8145648][8076255][8757747]|.
                     Structural characterization by NMR shows a helix-turn-helix
                     motif |CITS: [11854485]|.
                     The Hha protein of pathogenic <i>E. coli</i> strains 
                     affects production of some virulence factors |CITS: 
                     [8698530][12073039]|.
                     <i>hha</i> mutants overproduce haemolysin from a high-copy 
                     recombinant haemolytic plasmid; they show some differences 
                     in periplasmic protein content, but not outer membrane 
                     protein content, compared to wild type |CITS: [2855523]|. 
                     In a <i>hha</i> mutant, <i>hns</i> expression is decreased 
                     compared to wild type |CITS: [15458420]|.  Deletion 
                     mutations and missense mutations have been characterized 
                     |CITS: [11790731]|.  YdgT (Cnu) is a paralog of Hha and can
                     partially compensate for the phenotypes of a <i>hha</i> 
                     mutant |CITS: [15458420]|.  A &Delta;<i>tomB hha</i> double
                     mutant shows decreased biofilm formation |CITS: 
                     [16317765]|.
                     Decreased expression of <i>hha</i> is observed under 
                     conditions of low osmolarity or stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [9580219]|.  Transcription is induced upon biofilm 
                     formation |CITS: [14727089]|.
                     Hha: ""<b>h</b>igh <b>h</b>emolysin <b>a</b>ctivity"" 
                     |CITS: [2855523]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11890540][17116239]|"
