     gene            complement(476710..479859)
                     /gene="acrB"
                     /locus_tag="b0462"
                     /label=acrB
     CDS             complement(476710..479859)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="acrB"
                     /locus_tag="b0462"
                     /product="bifunctional multidrug efflux pump RND permease
                     AcrB / multidrug efflux pump AcrAB-TolC"
                     /label=acrB
                     /note="The RND (resistance-nodulation-division) family
                     |CITS: [8022262][10941792]| protein, AcrB, is the inner 
                     membrane component of a tripartite, proton dependent, drug 
                     efflux pump.
                     AcrB contains 12 potential transmembrane regions and two 
                     large hydrophilic domains; the N and C-terminal halves of 
                     the protein have symmetrical hydropathy profiles |CITS: 
                     [8407802]|. Purified AcrB reconstituted in lipid vesicles 
                     mediates the &Delta;pH driven expulsion of fluorescent 
                     phospholipids; extrusion is inhibited by the antibiotics 
                     erythromycin and cloxacillin and the bile compounds 
                     glycochenodeoxycholate, glycocholate and taurocholate; the 
                     addition of AcrA with Mg<sup>2+</sup> stimulates transport;
                     AcrB-containing proteoliposomes (with an interior-acid 
                     &Delta;pH) show proton efflux when a substrate (positively 
                     charged, negatively charged or uncharged) is added to the 
                     external medium |CITS: [10377390]|.
                     The crystal structure of AcrB has been determined at 3.5 
                     |CITS: [12374972]|, 2.9, 3.0 |CITS: [16946072]|, and 2.8 
                     &Aring; resolution |CITS: [16915237]|. Crystal structures 
                     of AcrB with bound ligands rhodamine 6G, ethidium, 
                     dequalinium, and ciprofloxacin |CITS: [13129936][12738864]|
                     and with the secondary bile acid, deoxycholate |CITS: 
                     [19023693]| have been reported. The crystal structure of 
                     AcrB in complex with a designed protein that binds and 
                     inhibits the pump has been determined at 2.5 &Aring; 
                     resolution |CITS: [17194213]|. The AcrB monomer contains 12
                     transmembrane (TM) &alpha; helices and two large 
                     periplasmic loops - one between TM1 and 2, the other 
                     between TM7 and 8. The AcrB trimer consists of 3 domains: 
                     the transmembrane domain; the periplasmic or porter domain 
                     and the TolC binding domain. The AcrB monomers form an 
                     asymmetric trimer in which each monomer adopts a different 
                     conformation and a functionally rotating, three step 
                     (access, binding, and extrusion), peristaltic pump 
                     mechanism for the export of substrates |CITS: 
                     [16946072][16915237][17194213][18223659][19166984][ 
                     22451937]| has been proposed. Inactivation of any one of 
                     the three protomeric units of the trimer inactivates the 
                     entire complex |CITS: [19060146]|. AcrB interacts 
                     extensively with membrane lipids |CITS: [30509977]|.
                     Substrate molecules are captured by AcrB from the periplasm
                     or the periplasm/inner membrane interface |CITS: 
                     [12426336][20804453]|. Vestibules (openings between 
                     protomers) which are located just outside the external 
                     surface of the membrane bilayer may be important for 
                     substrate entry |CITS: [12374972][21856849]|. AcrB has 
                     multiple entrance channels: CH1 - open to the surface of 
                     the cell membrane; CH2 - open to the periplasm and CH3 - 
                     open to the central cavity of the trimer (see |CITS: 
                     [29317622]| and references within).  AcrB has two multisite
                     drug binding pockets (the proximal or access pocket and the
                     distal or deep pocket) separated by a flexible loop; 
                     transport may involve initial binding to the access pocket 
                     and subsequent transport to the deep pocket by 
                     conformational change |CITS: [22451937]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [22121023]|). Multiple entrances and multiple transport 
                     pathways may function to extend the substrate specificity 
                     of AcrB |CITS: [22121023][29317622]|.
                     AcrB mediated proton transport proceeds by an alternating 
                     access mechanism; proton binding sites within the 
                     transmembrane domain become alternatively accessible to the
                     periplasm or cytoplasm; AcrB adopts a novel antiport 
                     mechanism which involves the coupling of two remote 
                     alternating access mechanisms - one for drugs and one for 
                     protons -  within each AcrB protomer |CITS: [25248080]|. 
                     The crystal structure of AcrB bound by a pyridopyrimidine 
                     derived inhibitor has been determined. The inhibitor binds 
                     tightly to a hydrophobic 'trap' that is located off the 
                     substrate translocation channel and inhibits functional 
                     rotation of the AcrB monomer |CITS: [23812586]|.
                     The intermolecular contacts between AcrB and the 
                     periplasmic adaptor protein AcrA have been mapped using an 
                     <i>in vivo</i> cross-linking approach and the AcrAB complex
                     has been modelled |CITS: [19342493]|.
                     AcrB has been subject to site-directed and randomized 
                     mutagenesis (see |CITS: [17015667][18849422][18936189][194 
                     25588][24007302][22877148][24914123][25182653][27789287][32
                     905396][35311521]|). AcrB may play a role in 
                     contact-dependent growth inhibition (CDI). This activity 
                     can be separated from its function as part of the 
                     AcrAB/TolC multidrug efflux complex |CITS: [18761695]|. 
                     AcrB is the receptor protein for group 1 and group 3 CDI 
                     ionophore toxins |CITS: [39591464]|.
                     <i>acrB</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     <i>acrB</i> is one of 35 efflux-encoding genes that have 
                     been deleted or inactivated in 'Efflux KnockOut-35' 
                     (EKO-35) -  a K-12 BW25133-derived strain that lacks 35 
                     efflux pumps and can be used as a platform to study their 
                     function |CITS: [36065018][36318669]|. Swimming motility is
                     increased in the <i>acrB</i> Keio collection mutant 
                     (BW25113 &Delta;<i>acrB::kan</i>) |CITS: [39529380]|. The 
                     Keio collection <i>acrB</i> mutant is sensitive to lithium 
                     (<90% growth at 300mM Li) |CITS: [39983623]|.
                     <i>acrB</i>: <u>acr</u>iflavine resistance protein B (see 
                     |CITS: [4879570][1097404][8407802]|)
                     Reviews: |CITS: [18781920][12654283][19453279][18644451][1 
                     9026770][31588569]|.<br>Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [24200360][25941524][28044273][27232955][3 
                     8358391]|<br>Comment: |CITS: [16971936]|"
