     gene            complement(479882..481075)
                     /gene="acrA"
                     /locus_tag="b0463"
                     /label=acrA
     CDS             complement(479882..481075)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="acrA"
                     /locus_tag="b0463"
                     /product="trifunctional multidrug efflux pump membrane
                     fusion lipoprotein AcrA / multidrug efflux pump AcrAD-TolC 
                     / multidrug efflux pump AcrAB-TolC"
                     /label=acrA
                     /note="AcrA is the periplasmic lipoprotein component of
                     the AcrAB-TolC and AcrAD-TolC multidrug efflux pumps.
                     An <i>acrA</i> IS2 insertion mutant (strain N43) has 
                     increased susceptibility to acriflavine, mitomycin C, 
                     erythromycin, fusidic acid, novobiocin and ethidium 
                     bromide; <i>acrA</i> encodes a predicted lipoprotein; the 
                     major portion of the protein is located in the periplasm 
                     |CITS: [8407802]|. The AcrA signal sequence is cleaved upon
                     maturation; Cys25 is required for cleavage |CITS: 
                     [10920254]|. An AcrA derivative, lacking the signal peptide
                     and lipid modification site is functional for drug efflux 
                     |CITS: [9878415]|. AcrA forms an inner-membrane associated 
                     trimer; oligomeric AcrA forms complexes with AcrB; AcrAB 
                     complexes can be identified in TolC deficient strains 
                     |CITS: [10894736]|  AcrA interacts with the AcrD RND family
                     permease; AcrD mediated efflux is dependent on the presence
                     of AcrA |CITS: [12426336][15743938]|. AcrA also functions 
                     with |FRAME: ACRF-MONOMER ""AcrF""|; extrachromosomal 
                     expression of AcrEF or AcrF alone improves the solvent 
                     resistance of an AcrB deficient strain (OST5500) which is 
                     hypersensitive to solvents |CITS: [11274125]|. AcrA can 
                     function with |FRAME: YHIV-MONOMER ""MdtF""| for drug 
                     efflux |CITS: [12426337]|. AcrA can interact with MexB from
                     <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i>; AcrA-MexB and TolC form a 
                     partially functional efflux pump |CITS: [18024521]|. 
                     Extragenic suppressor analysis indicates that AcrA 
                     interacts with TolC <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [15491355]|.
                     Purified AcrA (specifically a quadruple methionine 
                     substitution mutant of  AcrA residues 45-312) forms an 
                     elongated, sickle-shaped molecule and comprises a 
                     &beta;-barrel domain, a central lipoyl domain and a 
                     coiled-coil &alpha;-helical domain; the &alpha;-helical 
                     domain exhibits conformational flexibility 
                     |CITS:[16531241]|. Acidic pH induces conformational 
                     rearrangement and oligomerization of purified AcrA |CITS: 
                     [14523004]|.
                     Cryo-EM structures of  the AcrAB-TolC complex (with and 
                     without ligand) show that AcrA forms a hexamer through the 
                     trimerization of two conformationally distinct protomers; 
                     the helical domain of the AcrA protomers interact in a 
                     tip-to-tip manner with TolC; the &beta;-barrel and membrane
                     proximal domains interact with AcrB; the structures suggest
                     that upon ligand binding, conformational change in AcrB is 
                     communicated through AcrA to TolC and this allosteric 
                     effect both seals and opens the export channel |CITS: 
                     [28355133]|.
                     <i>acrA</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     AcrA is a member of the membrane fusion protein (MFP) 
                     family |CITS: [8021163][26546518]|.
                     AcrA specific inhibitors which enhance the activity of 
                     antibiotics have been identified |CITS: [27768847]|. 
                     Exposure of cells to aspirin or to the flavouring compound 
                     vanillin increases AcrA levels and leads to increased 
                     resistance to chloramphenicol and ciprofloxacin |CITS: 
                     [29973719]|.
                     The Keio collection <i>acrA</i> mutant is sensitive to 
                     lithium (<90% growth at 300mM Li) |CITS: [39983623]|. 
                     <i>acrA</i>: <U>acr</U>iflavine resistance protein A (see 
                     |CITS: [4879570][1097404][8407802]|)
                     Related review: |CITS: [26074901]|"
