     gene            complement(485741..486268)
                     /gene="priC"
                     /locus_tag="b0467"
                     /label=priC
     CDS             complement(485741..486268)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="priC"
                     /locus_tag="b0467"
                     /product="replication restart protein PriC"
                     /label=priC
                     /note="PriC is involved in both DNA replication initiation
                     and restart of stalled replication forks.
                     PriC forms a protein complex involved in restart of stalled
                     replication forks |CITS: [2144283]|. It associates with the
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3922| components |FRAME: EG10763-MONOMER 
                     PriA|-|FRAME: EG10764-MONOMER PriB| interact with 
                     bacteriophage ssDNA <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [8366072]| and 
                     has been found in isolated intact primosomes |CITS: 
                     [8663105]|. Though PriC does not contribute to the 
                     stability of the PriA-PriB-DNA complex, in its absence from
                     the phiX174 replication model, priming drops by one third 
                     and replication by one half |CITS: [8663104]|.
                     Based on genetic studies with phiX174 DNA replication 
                     restart proteins expressed <i>in vitro</i>, stalled 
                     replication forks can restart via a mechanism requiring 
                     PriA and either PriB or PriC, or with PriC and |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3931| instead of PriA |CITS: [10835375]|. Whereas 
                     PriA-dependent restart occurs when there are no gaps in the
                     leading strand, PriC-dependent restart requires a gap 
                     |CITS: [15749022]|. This can be blocked if the 5' end of 
                     the lagging strand is near the replication fork, requiring 
                     the unwinding activity of Rep, which was first implicated 
                     to be involved with PriC by |CITS: [9029161]|, or PriA to 
                     allow PriC-mediated restart |CITS: [16079128]|. PriC in 
                     turn stimulates Rep activity at stalled forks |CITS: 
                     [16079128]|. PriC and Rep are also involved in overcoming 
                     replication fork arrest due to UV damage |CITS: 
                     [22636770]|.
                     Using <i>E. coli</i> mutants of |FRAME: EG10237-MONOMER|, 
                     it was found that <i>priC</i> has a limited phenotype, but 
                     a <i>priBC</i> double null suffers a large reduction in 
                     growth and viability |CITS: [10540288]|. Subsequent work 
                     confirmed the existence of a PriC-mediated DNA replication 
                     restart distinct from the PriA/PriB mechanism. The 
                     <i>dnaC809</i> suppression of <i>priA</i> mutant requires 
                     PriC and Rep |CITS: [10835375]|. In some <i>|FRAME: 
                     EG10235|</i> temperature sensitive mutant strains grown at 
                     the permissive temperature of 30 &deg;C, PriC can help with
                     initiation of DNA replication at <i>oriC</i> |CITS: 
                     [15588282]|. The mechanism of PriC-dependent rescue of DNA 
                     replication initiation has been examined and found to occur
                     in cells in which |FRAME: CPLX0-3621 DnaB| (replicative DNA
                     helicase) cannot attach to <i>oriC</i>, such as cells with 
                     mutated <i>dnaC</i> or with mutations in <i>oriC</i>, or in
                     cells in which DnaA-DnaB interactions are inhibited |CITS: 
                     [40439200]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [28320884][29202195]|"
