                     /product="adenine phosphoribosyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.4.2.7"
                     /label=apt
                     /note="Adenine phosphoribosyltransferase is a purine
                     salvage enzyme which catalyzes the transfer of the 
                     ribosyl-5-phosphate group from |FRAME: PRPP| to the 
                     N<sup>9</sup> position of adenine to yield AMP.
                     APRT is one of three <i>E. coli</i> 
                     phosphoribosyltransferases (PRTases) that participate in 
                     purine salvage, Gpt, Hpt and Apt |CITS: [4910918]|.
                     Enzyme activity is induced by growth on purines, 
                     amethopterin, and thymine and is higher in stationary phase
                     cultures.  The enzyme is subject to negative regulation by 
                     uncyclized nucleoside 5'-phosphates and end product 
                     inhibition |CITS: [4328693]|.  Adenine 
                     phosphoribosyltransferase is cytoplasmic |CITS: [365172]|, 
                     but can be found associated with isolated membrane vesicles
                     |CITS: [4328694]|.
                     Apt: ""<b>a</b>denine 
                     <b>p</b>hosphoribosyl<b>t</b>ransferase"" |CITS: [765747]|"
