     CDS             490576..492450
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="htpG"
                     /locus_tag="b0473"
                     /product="bifunctional chaperone protein HtpG / chaperone
                     ATPase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.4.10"
                     /label=htpG
                     /note="HtpG is a member of the Hsp90 (heat shock protein,
                     90kDa) family of molecular chaperones |CITS: [3299380]|. 
                     Purified HtpG is an ATPase |CITS: [8419347][9707442]| (with
                     a k<sub>cat</sub> of 0.011 sec<sup>-1</sup> and a 
                     K<sub>M</sub> for ATP of 250 &micro;M |CITS: [19165152]|). 
                     Purified, immobilised HtpG binds the heat shock alternative
                     sigma factor &sigma;<sup>32</sup> and an unidentified 
                     cyclophilin (peptidyl-prolyl isomerase) |CITS: [8419347]|. 
                     HtpG participates in the folding of newly synthesized 
                     proteins under mild heat shock conditions |CITS: 
                     [10931286]|; purified HtpG suppresses the thermal 
                     aggregation of citrate synthase at 43&deg;C |CITS: 
                     [7706269]| (see also |CITS: [11237871]|).
                     HtpG collaborates with the |FRAME: EG10241-MONOMER 
                     ""DnaK""| chaperone system to reactivate inactive protein 
                     substrates; the ATPase activity of HtpG is essential for 
                     this activity; HtpG and DnaK physically interact <i>in 
                     vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [21525416]| (see 
                     further |CITS: [28013030][29706537]|). HtpG binds partially
                     folded states of a model substrate (Staphylococcal 
                     nuclease) that are transiently sampled from its 
                     thermodynamically stable native state |CITS: [24726919]|.
                     HtpG is a phosphoprotein <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [2647735]|; 
                     the purified protein acquires 1.5 phosphate groups/molecule
                     predominantly on serine and threonine residues |CITS: 
                     [8419347]| but also on a His residue |CITS: [35636362]|. 
                     Purified HtpG is a dimer in solution |CITS: [2647735]| but 
                     oligomerizes at higher temperatures (65&deg;C) |CITS: 
                     [11237871]|. The oligomeric state and activity of the 
                     protein is affected by the presence of divalent cations 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [7782303]|.
                     HtpG is a multi-domain, dimeric protein; interactions 
                     between the domains and their functional significance has 
                     been investigated (see |CITS: 
                     [11606187][11606188][12492485]|). Crystal structures of the
                     C-terminal |CITS: [15274928]|, N-terminal and middle 
                     domains |CITS: [15837196]| have been reported, along with 
                     crystal structures of full length HtpG with and without ADP
                     |CITS: [17055434]| (and see comments by |CITS: 
                     [15837187][17055424][18462669]|). The conformational 
                     dynamics of HtpG during substrate binding and the ATPase 
                     cycle have been studied |CITS: [15837196][17055434][190616 
                     38][18462680][19165152][19427321][21414251][22063096][39663
                     352][38890550]|. Over-expression of <i>htpG</i> results in 
                     filamentation, decreased survival and SDS sensitivity 
                     |CITS: [23260660]|. Over-expression of <i>htpG</i> directly
                     inhibits |FRAME: EG10347-MONOMER ""FtsZ""| activity and 
                     blocks FtsZ-ring formation; FtsZ is degraded more rapidly 
                     in cells lacking HtpG compared to wild-type; purified HtpG 
                     interacts with FtsZ and inhibits FtsZ polymerization in an 
                     ATP-independent reaction; HtpG may affect cell division via
                     a reversible FtsZ-holdase activity |CITS: [31160467]|. HtpG
                     is required for CRISPR activity; this requirement is 
                     mediated by HtpG's effect on |FRAME: EG12634-MONOMER 
                     ""Cas3""| |CITS: [22114197]|
                     <i>htpG</i> is not essential; a <i>htpG</i> deletion mutant
                     (&Delta;<i>htpG1::lacZ</i>) grows poorly at high 
                     temperature (46 &deg;C) |CITS: [3290192]| (see further 
                     |CITS: [9748451][23618614]|. Expression of <i>htpG</i> is 
                     induced by heat shock |CITS: [2078102][1367813]| and acid 
                     shock |CITS: [2199304]|, is repressed by nitrogen 
                     starvation |CITS: [15289551]|, and is dependent on the 
                     growth environment |CITS: [10427031]|. <i>htpG</i> belongs 
                     to the &sigma;<sup>32</sup> regulon |CITS: [15757896]|. 
                     HtpG: ""<b>h</b>igh <b>t</b>emperature <b>p</b>rotein 
                     <b>G</b>"" |CITS: [6442118]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [11407116][30401745]|"
