     CDS             492631..493275
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="adk"
                     /locus_tag="b0474"
                     /product="multifunctional adenylate kinase / UDP kinase /
                     CDP kinase / GDP kinase / dGDP kinase / dCDP kinase / dTDP 
                     kinase / AMP kinase (GTP)"
                     /EC_number="2.7.4.3; 2.7.4.4"
                     /label=adk
                     /note="Adenylate kinase is an essential enzyme required
                     for the biosynthesis of purine ribonucleotides |CITS: 
                     [4243860][166976]| and has a key role in controlling the 
                     rate of cell growth |CITS: [166976][6243627]|. Adenylate 
                     kinase is a relatively small, monomeric protein that 
                     consists of three functionally relevant domains: the 
                     N-terminal nucleotide binding (NMPbind) domain that binds 
                     AMP, followed by the CORE domain that represents the bulk 
                     of the enzyme and encloses the LID domain that binds ATP. 
                     There are estimated to be approximately 10,000 molecules of
                     adenylate kinase present in the cell |CITS: [2997739]|. 
                     Adenylate kinase activity may be inhibited by interaction 
                     with the phosphorylated form of |FRAME: PTSH-MONOMER HPr| 
                     |CITS: [28634232]| and the ribosome |CITS: [28715177]|. 
                     Various crystal |CITS: [2845103][1548697][8451239][7937733 
                     ][8805521][16302237][19805185][28559350]| and solution 
                     |CITS: [10491122][10828981]| structures of the enzyme have 
                     been solved, and a reaction mechanism was proposed and 
                     refined. Based on new data showing activation of adenylate 
                     kinase by direct binding of Mg<sup>2+</sup>, a new kinetic 
                     model for the forward reaction involving a 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup>-activated random bi-bi mechanism has been 
                     proposed. The requirement for binding of Mg<sup>2+</sup> 
                     before binding of AMP to the enzyme explains the apparent 
                     competitive inhibition by AMP |CITS: [19029291]|. The free 
                     energy difference between the open and closed conformation 
                     of adenylate kinase is quite small |CITS: [22212071]|. The 
                     site for the initial interaction of ATP with the enzyme in 
                     the open conformation has been identified by NMR |CITS: 
                     [23339454]|. Analysis of the open-to-closed transition by 
                     X-ray solution scattering and analysis of available crystal
                     structures suggest that the transition initiated by ATP 
                     binding follows an induced fit model |CITS: [28767234]|; 
                     single-molecule FRET analysis shows that domain closure in 
                     the presence of substrate is two orders of magnitude faster
                     than the catalytic reaction |CITS: [29531052]|. A mutant 
                     enzyme trapped in the closed, catalytically active state 
                     was studied |CITS: [28559350]|. The catalytic residues 
                     serve a dual role - in addition to lowering the energy 
                     barrier for the phosphoryl transfer reaction, they also 
                     delay the opening of the enzyme, maintaining it in a 
                     catalytically active, closed conformation for long enough 
                     to enable the subsequent chemical step |CITS: [36802243]|.
                     Energy transfer experiments confirmed stepwise domain 
                     movement/closure upon binding of the substrates |CITS: 
                     [8634252][8639589][8955362]|. The dynamics and energetics 
                     of domain movements upon substrate binding and catalysis 
                     have been further analyzed |CITS: [15382240][15521058][161 
                     39299][16771428][16800615][17217965][17299745]|. Small, 
                     fast motion in the hinge regions facilitates larger, but 
                     slow movement of the lid domain |CITS: [18026087]|. 
                     Molecular dynamics simulations, NMR spectroscopy and QM/MM 
                     calculations of the conformational transition have been 
                     reported |CITS: [18682219][22963267][ 
                     23936827][26009297][28534408][29239025][30615212]|. A low 
                     concentration of urea activates adenylate kinase by 
                     modulating its conformational equilibrium |CITS: 
                     [27705762]|.
                     Many additional studies have used <i>E. coli</i> Adk as a 
                     model protein to analyze folding and conformational 
                     dynamics, often toward the ultimate goal of the rational 
                     design of proteins |CITS: [18676657][18931260][19013178][1 
                     9159290][19219996][19507242][19527659][19673471][19708737][
                     19805185][20471396][20487508][21081091][21081909][21333795]
                     [21689533][21779157][22212071][22685395][22733562][22898349
                     ][24507607][24787383][24940783][25393408][26666584][2872141
                     2][29378407][30088929]|. Site-directed mutants in residues 
                     in the nucleotide binding site |CITS: [2844237][2017434]|, 
                     in the phosphate binding loop |CITS: [2542263][2223776]|, 
                     and in the ""second loop"" |CITS: [2040598][1748642]| have 
                     been kinetically analyzed. Amino acid changes that increase
                     thermal stability of the enzyme have been introduced |CITS:
                     [9668094][9668095]|. The effects of genetic variation in 
                     adenylate kinase on cellular fitness depend on its 
                     catalytic capacity (the product of cellular abundance and 
                     catalytic activity) |CITS: [28812634]|. The effect of 
                     mutations in surface residues that increase local unfolding
                     depend on their location: mutations in the LID domain 
                     decrease the affinity for ATP, while mutations in the 
                     NMPbind domain increase catalytic activity |CITS: 
                     [29875414]|. Conversely, mutations that stabilize the 
                     enzyme show increased substrate inhibition |CITS: 
                     [31097595]|. Real-time dynamics using single-molecule 
                     nanopore analyses indicates both ligand binding and 
                     allosteric binding regulate movement of the LID and NMP 
                     domains and enzymatic function |CITS: [39572546]|. 
                     Adenylate kinase is able to fulfill the role of nucleoside 
                     diphosphate kinase in an <i>ndk</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [8650159]| and participates in the T4 dNTP synthetase 
                     complex |CITS: [15941717]|.
                     Adenylate kinase can combine with |FRAME: PPK-CPLX| to 
                     catalyze the formation of ADP from AMP and 
                     poly(P<sub>i</sub>). This polyP:AMP phosphotransferase 
                     (PAP) activity requires both enzymes acting together |CITS:
                     [11106368]|. Although adenylate kinase is able to catalyze 
                     thiamine triphosphate (ThTP) biosynthesis in vertebrates, 
                     it is not responsible for ThTP biosynthesis <i>in vivo</i> 
                     in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [18215312]|.
                     PlsA: ""defective in <b>p</b>hospho<b>l</b>ipid 
                     bio<b>s</b>ynthesis"" |CITS: [4910856]|
                     DnaW: |CITS: [377019][6290847]|
                     Adk: ""<b>ad</b>enylate <b>k</b>inase"" |CITS: [6243627]| 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [18075575][23543318]|<br>
                     Comment: |CITS: [29895911]|"
