     gene            500370..502022
                     /gene="ushA"
                     /locus_tag="b0480"
                     /label=ushA
     CDS             500370..502022
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ushA"
                     /locus_tag="b0480"
                     /product="multifunctional 5'-nucleotidase / UDP-sugar
                     hydrolase / 5'-ribonucleotidase / UDP-sugar hydrolase / 
                     5'-deoxyribonucleotidase / nitrophenyl phosphatase / 
                     P(1),P(3)-bis(5'-adenosyl) triphosphate hydrolase / 
                     CDP-glycerol hydrolase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.3.5; 3.6.1.45; 3.1.3.89; 3.6.1.29"
                     /label=ushA
                     /note="The <i>ushA</i> gene of <i>E. coli</i> encodes a 
                     bifunctional enzyme with 5'-nucleotidase and UDP-sugar 
                     hydrolase activities. It has relatively broad substrate 
                     specificity, hydrolyzing 5'-ribonucleotides and 
                     5'-deoxyribonucleotides |CITS: [5341265]|, 
                     bis(5'-nucleosidyl)polyphosphates |CITS: [2556371]|, UDP 
                     sugars |CITS: [5337593]|, and CDP-alcohols |CITS: 
                     [18641143]|. It also hydrolyzes the synthetic substrates 
                     bis(<i>p</i>-nitrophenyl)phosphate and <i>p</i>-nitrophenyl
                     phosphate |CITS: [5341265]|.
                     UshA localizes to the periplasm where it is able to 
                     catalyze the degradation of external UDP-glucose to 
                     uridine, glucose-1-phosphate and inorganic phosphate which 
                     are then utilized by the cell. The nucleoside diphosphate 
                     sugar hydrolase activity is specific for UDP sugars |CITS: 
                     [5337593]|.
                     The periplasmic location suggests a general role for this 
                     enzyme in utilization of extracellular nucleotides |CITS: 
                     [1089626]|. UshA is also involved in the degradation of 
                     internally formed nucleotides during degradation of 
                     cellular RNA |CITS: [1104575], [17693717]|. <i>ushA</i> 
                     knockout mutants lack major 5'-nucleotidase activity.  An 
                     <i>ushA</i> <i>aphA</i> double knockout mutant is unable to
                     utilize purine nucleotides as the sole carbon source |CITS:
                     [17693717]|.
                     In addition to its 5'-nucleotidase activity, UDP-sugar 
                     hydrolase activity, and dinucleoside polyphosphate 
                     hydrolase activity, UshA has a novel, highly efficient 
                     CDP-alcohol hydrolase activity. The <i>E. coli</i> enzyme 
                     and its ortholog in <i>Yersinia intermedia</i> have 
                     CDP-alcohol hydrolase activity against CDP-glycerol, 
                     CDP-ethanolamine and CDP-choline. CDP-glycerol hydrolysis 
                     by the <i>Yersinia</i> enzyme produces cytidine, 
                     L-glycerol-3-phosphate and inorganic phosphate |CITS: 
                     [18641143]|.
                     The activities of both the UDP-sugar hydrolase and the 
                     nucleotidase are inhibited by an (unidentifed) 
                     intracellular protein; this inhibition may serve to protect
                     the cytoplasmic nucleotide pool prior to secretion of UshA 
                     to the periplasm |CITS: [5337593][5341266][11802543]|.
                     X-ray structures indicate that UshA is a monomer consisting
                     of a larger N-terminal domain that coordinates two 
                     catalytic divalent metal ions, linked by an &alpha; helix 
                     to a smaller C-terminal domain which provides the substrate
                     specificity pocket for the nucleotides. Substrate binding 
                     is at the interface of the two domains. A large domain 
                     rotation is associated with catalysis |CITS: 
                     [15709736][15215524][11491294][11491293][10331872][ 
                     26724996]|. UshA belongs to the calcineurin superfamily of 
                     dinuclear metallophosphatases and is homologous to the 
                     mammalian ecto-5'-nucleotidases |CITS: [15709736]|.
                     <i>ush</i>: <U>U</U>DP-<U>s</U>ugar <U>h</U>ydrolase |CITS:
                     [4355492]|"
