     gene            complement(502059..502538)
                     /gene="ybaK"
                     /locus_tag="b0481"
                     /label=ybaK
     CDS             complement(502059..502538)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ybaK"
                     /locus_tag="b0481"
                     /product="bifunctional Cys-tRNA(Pro)/Cys-tRNA(Cys)
                     deacylase YbaK / Cys-tRNA(Pro) deacylase"
                     /label=ybaK
                     /note="YbaK hydrolyzes both incorrectly charged 
                     Cys-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> and correctly charged 
                     Cys-tRNA<sup>Cys</sup> <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [15886196]|. 
                     YbaK does not appear to recognize specific tRNAs |CITS: 
                     [24371276]|.  However, YbaK interacts with |FRAME: 
                     PROS-CPLX ProRS| <i>in vivo</i>, and this interaction 
                     enhances YbaK's Cys-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> deacylation 
                     activity.  The interaction with ProRS appears to provide 
                     specificity for hydrolysis of mischarged 
                     Cys-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> |CITS: [30418624]|.
                     YbaK has similarity to the editing active site (INS domain)
                     of ProRS, which is able to edit mischarged 
                     Ala-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> |CITS: [14663147]|.
                     A computational model of YbaK bound to the substrate analog
                     5'-CCA-Cys and application of hybrid quantum 
                     mechanical/molecular mechanics (QM/MM) methods provided 
                     further insight into catalysis.  The reaction mechanism is 
                     proposed to involve cyclization of the substrate Cys and 
                     subsequent cleavage of the Cys-tRNA ester bond.  YbaK 
                     protein backbone atoms play a role in stabilizing the 
                     transition state, and the product is stabilized by the 
                     2'-OH of the tRNA |CITS: [23185990]|.
                     Mutations in the Gly30 residue lead to significantly 
                     reduced Cys-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> deacylation activity, but do
                     not alter substrate specificity, while mutations in the 
                     homologous Ile263 residue of the INS domain of ProRS allow 
                     alteration of its substrate specificity |CITS: [23185990]|.
                      A <i>ybaK</i> deletion mutant is 2-fold more sensitive to 
                     X-radiation than wild type |CITS: [27718375]|.
                     Expression of <i>ybaK</i> is upregulated by the 
                     naphthoquinones plumbagin or juglone |CITS: [23688616]|."
