     gene            complement(504331..506835)
                     /gene="copA"
                     /locus_tag="b0484"
                     /label=copA
     CDS             complement(504331..506835)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="copA"
                     /locus_tag="b0484"
                     /product="Cu(+) exporting P-type ATPase"
                     /EC_number="7.2.2.8"
                     /label=copA
                     /note="CopA is a P-type ATPase that catalyses the efflux
                     of monovalent copper in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS:[10639134],[12351646]|. CopA contributes to copper 
                     tolerance in both aerobic and anerobic conditions 
                     |CITS:[11399769]|.
                     CopA is a member of the P-type ATPase cation transporter 
                     superfamily |CITS:[8151716][24225317]| - ATP pumps 
                     distinguished by the formation of a phosphorylated 
                     intermediate during the catalytic cycle; CopA is a member 
                     of the P<sub>1B</sub> ATPase subgroup of P-ATPases 
                     (reviewed in |CITS: [17219055]|.
                     A <I>copA</I> mutant is sensitive to copper salts |CITS: 
                     [10639134]|.
                     CopA contains conserved ATP binding, phosphorylation and 
                     phosphatase domains and is predicted to have 8 
                     transmembrane regions; CopA contains a membrane embedded 
                     C<sub>479</sub>PC<sub>481</sub> motif; CopA has two 
                     N-terminal metal binding domains (MBDs) - MBD1 and MBD2 - 
                     both of which contain CXXC metal binding motifs |CITS: 
                     [10639134]|. The cysteine residues of the CPC motif are 
                     required for CopA function |CITS: [12351646]| Neither of 
                     the CXXC motifs is required for copper resistance or 
                     transport function but deletion of the N-terminal region of
                     the protein (encompassing residues 8-150) results in loss 
                     of both copper resistance <i>in vivo</i> and copper 
                     transport <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [11500054]|. The two metal
                     binding domains may have distinct functions. MBD2 acts a 
                     regulatory domain, its primary role is to repress ATPase 
                     activity in the absence of copper; MBD1 appears to have a 
                     chaperone-like function |CITS: [25899340]|. MBD1 exists in 
                     both a free floating/diffusible form (see |FRAME: 
                     MONOMER0-4382|) and in a transporter bound state |CITS: 
                     [28107647]|.
                     CopA delivers Cu<sup>+</sup> directly to |FRAME: 
                     G6321-MONOMER ""CusF""| - the periplasmic binding protein 
                     of the <i>cusCFBA</i> encoded |FRAME: CPLX0-1721|; 
                     Cu<sup>+</sup> transfer is achieved through a specific 
                     CopA-CusF interaction and is dependent on ATP hydrolysis 
                     |CITS: [24917681]|
                     Expression of <I>copA</I> is induced by Cu(I), Ag(I) 
                     |CITS:[10639134],[12832075], [15773991]|, or Au(I) 
                     |CITS:[12446701],[12832075]|, though it may 
                     |CITS:[12832075]| or may not |CITS:[11283292]| be involved 
                     in silver resistance and is not involved in gold resistance
                     |CITS:[12832075]|. Expression is controlled by the copper 
                     efflux regulator, <I>cueR</I> |CITS:[10915804],[11167016], 
                     [11136469], [15773991]|. Review: |CITS:[12829268]|
                     "
