     CDS             complement(join(506624..506632,506632..506835))
                     /codon_start=1
                     /ribosomal_slippage
                     /gene="copA"
                     /locus_tag="b0484"
                     /product="soluble Cu(+) chaperone"
                     /EC_number="7.2.2.8"
                     /label=copA
                     /note="CopA(Z) is a small (70 amino acid) cytoplasmic
                     copper chaperone that contributes to copper tolerance 
                     |CITS: [28107647]|.
                     CopA(Z) is generated by -1 programmed ribosomal 
                     frameshifting (-1PRF) of full-length <i>copA</i> mRNA; 
                     expression of <i>copA</i> from a plasmid in a 
                     &Delta;<i>copA</i> strain generates full-size CopA plus 
                     CopA(Z) (9323 Da).  -1PRF in <i>copA</i> mRNA is promoted 
                     by a slippery sequence (SS) (5'CCCAAAGGC 3') and a 
                     predicted frameshifting pseudoknot located just downstream 
                     of the SS. The CopA nascent protein (specifically the 
                     portion residing inside the exit tunnel when the ribsome 
                     reaches the PRF site) is also implicated in modulating the 
                     efficiency of -1PRF during translation of copA <i>in 
                     vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i>. Mutations which disrupt the 
                     SS but preserve the amino acid sequence of full-length CopA
                     abolish CopA(Z) production and result in reduced cell 
                     tolerance to elevated concentrations of copper |CITS: 
                     [28107647]|.
                     -1PRF signatures (SS plus pseudoknot sequences) are present
                     in <i>copA</i> homologs in a wide range of bacterial 
                     species and in the human <i>copA</i> homolog, ATP7B |CITS: 
                     [28107647]|
                     The <i>copA</i> gene generates two transcripts, one of 
                     which, CopA(Z), is truncated and produces a 
                     metallochaperone with a key role in resistance against 
                     copper shock <i>in vivo</i>. CopA(Z) is essential for 
                     producing sufficient amounts of the chaperone to support 
                     the membrane pump |CITS:[28925527]|.
                     CopA(Z) is generated from both transcripts, and the 
                     existence of the short transcript increases production of 
                     CopA(Z) severalfold |CITS:[28925527]|. CopA(Z) is triggered
                     at lower copper concentrations, and it is far more stable 
                     than the full-length <i>copA</i> mRNA |CITS:[28925527]|."
