     gene            507097..508029
                     /gene="glsA"
                     /locus_tag="b0485"
                     /label=glsA
     CDS             507097..508029
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glsA"
                     /locus_tag="b0485"
                     /product="bifunctional glutaminase 1 / glutaminase"
                     /EC_number="3.5.1.2"
                     /label=glsA
                     /note="GlsA (YbaS) is a glutaminase that is highly
                     selective for L-glutamine.  Based on the pH profile of the 
                     enzymatic activity, GlsA was proposed to correspond to the 
                     previously described |FRAME: GLUTAMINA-CPLX| of <i>E. 
                     coli</i> B.  Glutamine binding exhibits positive 
                     cooperativity |CITS: [18459799]|.
                     Together with the antiporter |FRAME: XASA-MONOMER GadC|, 
                     GlsA constitutes an acid resistance system.  GlsA. which is
                     activated by acidic conditions, converts |FRAME: GLN| to 
                     |FRAME: GLT| and neutralizes H+ by producing ammonia, which
                     binds a proton to form ammonium |CITS: [23337585]|. A 
                     crystal structure of unliganded GlsA has been solved at 1.6
                     &Aring; resolution; an active site has been predicted, and 
                     site-directed mutagenesis confirmed the importance of 
                     various conserved residues for catalytic activity.  The S66
                     residue is proposed to act as a catalytic nucleophile, and 
                     the enzyme is predicted to employ a &beta;-lactamase-like 
                     catalytic mechanism |CITS: [18459799]|.
                     Under certain growth conditions, a <i>glsA</i> deletion 
                     mutant shows a reduced growth rate |CITS: [18459799]|. 
                     <i>glsA</i> is required for glutamine-mediated acid 
                     resistance |CITS: [23337585]|.  Expression of <i>glsA</i> 
                     is increased during acid shock |CITS: [12730179]|. 
                     <i>glsA</i> is associated with isobutanol tolerance in an 
                     engineered <i>E. coli</i> strain |CITS: [24203355]|. 
                     Review: |CITS: [NANDAKUMAR03]|"
