     gene            complement(512881..513735)
                     /gene="cnoX"
                     /locus_tag="b0492"
                     /label=cnoX
     CDS             complement(512881..513735)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cnoX"
                     /locus_tag="b0492"
                     /product="chaperedoxin"
                     /label=cnoX
                     /note="CnoX (formerly YbbN) is a chaperedoxin - a protein
                     which functions as a holdase and also provides redox 
                     protection to its substrates through the formation of mixed
                     disulfide complexes.
                     Purified CnoX is a potent holdase for structually distinct 
                     substrates when specifically activated by hypochlorous acid
                     (HOCl); HOCl activates CnoX via chlorination of amino acid 
                     residues located in its tetratricopeptide (TPR) domain 
                     |CITS: [29754824]|. CnoX prevents protein aggregation in a 
                     chaperone deficient strain (&Delta;<i>rpoH</i>) exposed to 
                     HOCl; CnoX does not exhibit refolding activity but is able 
                     to cooperate with the |FRAME: EG10241-MONOMER 
                     ""DnaK""|/J/GrpE system for refolding <i>in vitro</i> and 
                     also with |FRAME: CPLX0-3934 ""GroEL/ES""| for refolding 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i>; transfer of substrates 
                     to these chaperones does not depend on the presence of a 
                     reducing agent |CITS: [29754824]|.
                     CnoX forms mixed disulfide complexes with client proteins 
                     and protects them from oxidation; reduction of the mixed 
                     disulfide complexes is carried out via the |FRAME: 
                     GLUT-REDOX-PWY ""glutathione-glutaredoxin pathway""|; CnoX 
                     protects the proteins |FRAME: THIOREDOXIN-REDUCT-NADPH-CPLX
                     ""TrxB""|, |FRAME: PC00004 ""Crp""|, |FRAME: 
                     CITRATE-SI-SYNTHASE ""GltA""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-2521 
                     ""MoaB""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-8180 ""ClpB""|, |FRAME: 
                     ALPHAGALACTOSID-CPLX ""MelA""|, |FRAME: ADENODEAMIN-MONOMER
                     ""Add""| and |FRAME: PKI-COMPLEX ""PykF""| from 
                     over-oxidation <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [29754824]|.
                     Purified YbbN stimulates the reactivation of reduced or 
                     scrambled RNase A, thus showing protein disulfide isomerase
                     activity; purified YbbN facilitates the refolding of 
                     denatured citrate synthase and &alpha;-glucosidase |CITS: 
                     [16563353]|. YbbN stimulates the folding of malate 
                     dehydrogenase <i>in vitro</i>; YbbN increases the rate (but
                     not the yield) of citrate synthase renaturation by the 
                     DnaK/J/GrpE chaperone system <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [18657513]|.
                     The N terminal domain of the YbbN is similar to 
                     thioredoxins; it contains a cysteine residue (Cys38) in an 
                     SxxC peroxiredoxin-like motif as well as a second cysteine 
                     residue (Cys63); both are titratable with DTNB |CITS: 
                     [16563353]|. YbbN does not form mixed disulfides in cells 
                     subject to a heat stress or after exposure to menadione or 
                     hydrogen peroxide |CITS: [18657513]|. YbbN is an elongated 
                     molecule with a mobile N-terminal Trx (thioredoxin) domain 
                     tethered to a C-terminal domain containing atypical 
                     tetratricopeptide repeat (TPR) motifs; YbbN is monomeric in
                     solution |CITS: [21498507]|.
                     Reduced YbbN appears in a dimer form, while oxidized YbbN 
                     consists of a mixture of tetramers, hexamers, and higher 
                     molecular weight oligomers |CITS: [16563353]|. In a study 
                     of the <i>E. coli</i> complexome, YbbN was found in a 
                     cytoplasmic complex together with |FRAME: G7068-MONOMER 
                     ""YeeN""| and |FRAME: G7089-MONOMER ""YeeZ""| |CITS: 
                     [16858726]|.
                     A YbbN deficient strain displays increased sensitivity to 
                     heat stress but does not display any increased sensitivity 
                     to hydrogen peroxide, UV, osmotic or pH stresses |CITS: 
                     [18657513]|. A &Delta;<i>cnoX</i> mutant is highly 
                     sensitive to hypochlorous acid (HOCl) and <i>cnoX</i> is 
                     induced following exposure to HOCl |CITS: [29754824]| 
                     <i>CnoX</i>: chaperone-redox |CITS: [29754824]|"
                     /translation="MSVENIVNINESNLQQVLEQSMTTPVLFYFWSERSQHCLQLTPIL
                     ESLAAQYNGQFILAKLDCDAEQMIAAQFGLRAIPTVYLFQNGQPVDGFQGPQPEEAIRA
