     gene            537344..538489
                     /gene="glxK"
                     /locus_tag="b0514"
                     /label=glxK
     CDS             537344..538489
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glxK"
                     /locus_tag="b0514"
                     /product="bifunctional glycerate 2-kinase 2 / glycerate
                     2-kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.165"
                     /label=glxK
                     /note="Glycerate kinase II (GKII), encoded by <i>glxK</i>, 
                     catalyzes the phosphorylation of D-glycerate.  The product 
                     of the reaction (with the enzyme from <i>E. coli</i> 
                     Crooke's strain) was initially identified as 
                     3-phosphoglycerate |CITS: [5325263]|.  More recent 
                     biochemical |CITS: [18675808]| and <i>in vivo</i> metabolic
                     assays showed that the product is in fact 
                     2-phosphoglycerate |CITS: [25823014]|.
                     There are two glycerate kinases, known as GKI and GKII, in 
                     <i>E. coli</i>.  GKII is less thermostable and has a 
                     narrower pH optimum than GKI |CITS: [4887503]|.
                     Glycerate kinase II is induced by growth on glycolate as 
                     the carbon source |CITS: [4887503][5325263]|.
                     A <i>glxK</i> mutant is unable to utilize glyoxylate |CITS:
                     [10601204]|.  In contrast, |CITS: [25823014]| state that 
                     deletion of <i>glxK</i> does not result in a growth defect 
                     with glycolate, glyoxylate or glycerate as sole carbon 
                     sources, although the data are not shown.
                     GlxK was shown to allosterically activate |FRAME: CPLX-64|,
                     an enzyme that otherwise has low affinity for its 
                     substrate, in the presence of |FRAME: GLYOX|. Activation by
                     GlxK leads to increased affinity for allantoin |CITS: 
                     [37147430]|. Glyoxylate is a product of allanotin 
                     degradation (see |FRAME: PWY-5705|), and is converted to 
                     |FRAME: 2-PG| in a pathway that involves GlxK (see |FRAME: 
                     GLYCOLATEMET-PWY|)."
