     gene            complement(548674..549396)
                     /gene="lpxH"
                     /locus_tag="b0524"
                     /label=lpxH
     CDS             complement(548674..549396)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lpxH"
                     /locus_tag="b0524"
                     /product="UDP-2,3-diacylglucosamine diphosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.1.54"
                     /label=lpxH
                     /note="UDP-2,3-diacylglucosamine hydrolase (LpxH)
                     catalyzes the fourth step in lipid A synthesis, hydrolyzing
                     UDP-2,3-bis(3-hydroxymyristoyl)glucosamine to yield 
                     2,3-bis(3-hydroxymyristoyl)-&beta;-D-glucosaminyl 
                     1-phosphate (lipid X) |CITS: [12000770]|.
                     LpxH is a peripheral membrane protein whose activity 
                     appears to be stimulated by Mn<sup>2+</sup> <i>in 
                     vitro</i>.  LpxH catalyzes the attack of water on the 
                     &alpha;-P atom of the UDP moiety to form lipid X and UMP 
                     |CITS: [12000770]|.  Although LpxH and |FRAME: 
                     CDPDIGLYPYPHOSPHA-MONOMER| (Cdh) are both able to catalyze 
                     the hydrolysis of UDP-2,3-diacylglucosamine <i>in 
                     vitro</i>, Cdh can not substitute for LpxH <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [12000771]|.  The Cdh substrate CDP-diacylglycerol 
                     is not a substrate for LpxH |CITS: [12000770]|.
                     A crystal structure of a solubilized, catalytically active 
                     mutant of LpxH has been solved at 2 &Aring; resolution. The
                     capping helices covering the active site of the enzyme 
                     appear to be flexible in the absence of the 
                     substrate/product |CITS: [29626094]|.
                     In a simulation of the lipid A biosynthesis pathway, it was
                     necessary to include feedback inhibition of LpxH to obtain 
                     a steady-state outcome that agreed with the observed lipid 
                     A production rate |CITS: [25919634]|.
                     LpxH is essential for growth |CITS: [12000771]|.  Several 
                     <i>lpxH</i> point mutants were isolated in a screen for 
                     mutants that provide resistance to the potential 
                     antibacterial compound sulfonyl piperazine.  The mutations 
                     map to the residues G48, L84, F141, and R149 |CITS: 
                     [25733621]|.
                     LpxH: |CITS: [12000770]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17362200][19815028]|"
