     gene            complement(549399..549893)
                     /gene="ppiB"
                     /locus_tag="b0525"
                     /label=ppiB
     CDS             complement(549399..549893)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ppiB"
                     /locus_tag="b0525"
                     /product="bifunctional peptidyl-prolyl cis-trans isomerase
                     B / peptidyl-prolyl cis-trans isomerase"
                     /EC_number="5.2.1.8"
                     /label=ppiB
                     /note="PpiB is a single-domain peptidyl-prolyl 
                     <i>cis-trans</i>-isomerase (PPIase) that is the main 
                     contributor to PPIase activity in the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [32545723]|.  PPIases catalyze the conformational 
                     isomerization of prolyl residues in peptides; 
                     <i>cis-trans</i> isomerization of prolyl peptide bonds is a
                     slow step in protein folding.
                     PpiB was shown to improve the efficiency of bovine protein 
                     disulfide isomerase as a catalyst of oxidative protein 
                     folding |CITS: [1584784]| and to catalyze the refolding of 
                     denatured type III collagen |CITS: [1606970]|.  Unlike the 
                     eukaryotic cyclophilins, PpiB activity is only inhibited by
                     high concentrations of cyclosporin A |CITS: [1606970]| or 
                     FK506 |CITS: [2007139]|.  Putative substrates of PpiB 
                     activity, including DnaK, PflB and Pta, have been 
                     identified |CITS: [27306110][28752912][32545723]|. 
                     Consistent with its role as the major cytoplasmic PPIase, 
                     PpiB has a large number of substrates |CITS: [32545723]|. 
                     Crystal structures in two different conformations |CITS: 
                     [8601841][9268657]| and a solution structure |CITS: 
                     [21955241]| of PpiB have been determined.  Refolding 
                     kinetics of the protein have been studied |CITS: 
                     [10731429]|.
                     Amino acid residues that are important for PpiB function 
                     have been identified |CITS: [27868330]|.  A mutant in the 
                     potential active site residue R43 was assayed for potential
                     substrate binding and PPIase activity |CITS: [32545723]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>ppiB</i> mutant shows increased swimming and 
                     swarming motility and biofilm formation |CITS: [27306110]| 
                     and a mild cell division defect |CITS: [28752912]|. 
                     Overexpression of PpiB leads to cell filamentation |CITS: 
                     [28752912]|.
                     A <i>ppiB</i> mutant is resistant to the growth inhibiory 
                     effect of bacteriophage T7 Gp3.8, a putative homing 
                     endonuclease |CITS: [25512533]|.  The authors hypothesize 
                     that PpiB may be involved in folding of Gp3.8.  A 
                     &Delta;<i>ppiB</i> strain shows a mild defect in supporting
                     growth of phage LL5 |CITS: [32415154]|.  A strain 
                     containing non-polar deletions of all six cytoplasmic 
                     PPIases (&Delta;6<i>ppi</i>) shows a growth defect at 
                     37&deg;C in rich media and at 43&deg;C in both rich and 
                     minimal media |CITS: [32545723]|.
                     PpiB: ""<b>p</b>eptidyl-<b>p</b>rolyl 
                     <i>cis-trans</i>-<b>i</b>somerase <b>B</b>"""
