     CDS             complement(559304..559936)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fimZ"
                     /locus_tag="b0535"
                     /product="LuxR family transcriptional regulator FimZ"
                     /label=fimZ
                     /note="FimZ is a positive DNA-binding transcriptional
                     regulator that belongs to the LuxR/UhpA family of 
                     transcriptional regulators.   FimZ's role as a regulator of
                     fimbrial gene expression has been studied in <i>Salmonella 
                     enterica</i>; see e.g. |CITS: [19218381]|. FimZ regulates 
                     pili biosynthesis and cell surface extension 
                     |CITS:[36057789]|. FimZ has two active forms; active form I
                     includes essential amino acid residues K106 and D109 to 
                     bind F-type ATPase, which induces constant cell elongation.
                     The active form II requires the amino acid residue D56, a 
                     putative phosphorylation site, to activate the 
                     transcription of its targets |CITS:[36057789]|.
                     The genome-wide transcriptional profile in presence or 
                     absence of FimZ was characterized by microarrays; genes 
                     involved in the SOS response, fimbria formation, and 
                     putrescine biosynthesis were found to be differentially 
                     expressed. However, they are not involved in the regulation
                     of cell elongation mediated by FimZ |CITS:[36057789]|.
                     The FimZ binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The overexpression of <i>fimZ</i> in stationary phase 
                     results in cell elongation |CITS:[36057789]|.
                     <i>fimZ</i> knockout mutant cells have a long axis that has
                     a greater length than that of wild type |CITS:[36057789]|.
                     In a double mutant, <i>phoP</i> <i>phoB</i>, <i>fimZ</i> is
                     overexpressed |CITS:[36057789]|.
                     Overproduction of <i>fimZ</i> leads to moderately increased
                     resistance to kanamycin |CITS: [12618449]| and 2-fold 
                     increased &beta;-lactam resistance via increased 
                     <i>ampC</i> expression |CITS: [12951338]|.
                     <i>fimZ</i> expression is induced at all stages of biofilm 
                     formation |CITS: [17222132]|."
