     gene            566349..566900
                     /gene="ybcL"
                     /locus_tag="b0545"
                     /label=ybcL
     CDS             566349..566900
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ybcL"
                     /locus_tag="b0545"
                     /product="DLP12 prophage; periplasmic protein YbcL"
                     /label=ybcL
                     /note="YbcL from uropathogenic <i>E. coli</i> (strain
                     UTI89) suppresses acute neutrophil migration which is 
                     associated with pathogenicity |CITS: [22966043]|. There are
                     6 amino acid differences between YbcL from <i>E. coli</i> 
                     K-12 MG1655 and  strain UTI89 - one of which (V78T) is 
                     non-conservative. Episomal expression of YbcL 
                     <sub>UTI89(T78V)</sub> in an <i>E. coli</i> UTI189 
                     <i>ybcL</i> deleted strain results in loss of function (ie.
                     lack of suppression of neutrophils in a transepithelial 
                     migration assay) whereas expression of 
                     YbcL<sub>MG1655(V78T)</sub> in the same background results 
                     in a gain-of-function phenotype |CITS: [22966043]|. 
                     Expression of YbcL<sub>UTI89</sub> or 
                     YbcL<sub>MG1655(V78T)</sub> in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655 
                     results in suppression of neutrophil migration levels 
                     similar to those seen in <i>E. coli</i> UTI89 |CITS: 
                     [22966043]|.
                     YbcL from uropathogenic <i>E. coli</i> UTI89 is secreted 
                     from the bacterial periplasm during infection |CITS: 
                     [22966043]|.
                     YbcL has sequence and structural similarity to rat and 
                     human RKIP (Raf kinase inhibitor protein), which modulates 
                     signal transduction pathways |CITS: [11439028]|.
                     A crystal structure of the protein has been solved at 1.8 
                     &Aring; resolution |CITS: [11439028]|.
                     YbcL is periplasmic |CITS: [11439028]|.
                     "
