     gene            566910..567707
                     /gene="ybcM"
                     /locus_tag="b0546"
                     /label=ybcM
     CDS             566910..567707
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ybcM"
                     /locus_tag="b0546"
                     /product="DLP12 prophage; putative DNA-binding
                     transcriptional regulator"
                     /label=ybcM
                     /note="YbcM was predicted to be an AraC-type transcription 
                     factor |CITS: [34428301] [35880217]|, and it contains a 
                     helix-turn-helix motif to bind DNA in its C-terminal domain
                     |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     DNA binding of YbcM was probed in vivo, in glucose M9 
                     minimal medium under oxidative conditions, through 
                     chromatin immunoprecipitation assays (ChIP-exo) |CITS: 
                     [34428301]|. Some binding target genes were found to be 
                     related to the stress response, but the effect of YbcM on 
                     transcription still needs to be studied |CITS: [34428301]|.
                     On the other hand, it was predicted to regulate genes 
                     related to metabolism and processes related to phages 
                     |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     Upstream of the <i>ybcM</i> gene, a sequence was found that
                     could work as a transcriptional promoter, and the 
                     transcription of the <i>ybcM</i> gene is affected by amino 
                     acid starvation |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     <i>ybcM</i> was identified in a screen for genes that 
                     reduce the lethal effects of stress. A <i>ybcM</i> 
                     insertion mutant was more sensitive than wild type to 
                     mitomycin C and other stresses |CITS: [20128927]|. A 
                     knockout mutant for the <i>ybcM</i> gene was more 
                     susceptible to H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> than the 
                     wild-type strain, which showed a survival rate 8-fold 
                     higher than the <i>ybcM</i> deletion strain after treatment
                     with 60 mM H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> in glucose M9 minimal
                     medium |CITS: [34428301]|.
                     The YbcM binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
