     gene            573783..573965
                     /gene="rzoD"
                     /locus_tag="b4510"
                     /label=rzoD
     CDS             573783..573965
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rzoD"
                     /locus_tag="b4510"
                     /product="DLP12 prophage; putative prophage lysis
                     lipoprotein RzoD"
                     /label=rzoD
                     /note="RzoD is a predicted lysis protein of the cryptic
                     lambdoid prophage DLP12 and is part of the lysis cassette. 
                     An <i>rzpD/rzoD</i> mutant is impaired in curli-based 
                     biofilm formation, which may be an indirect effect due to 
                     altered peptidoglycan metabolism |CITS: [21415116]|. 
                     Deletion of the lysis module of DLP12 defective prophage 
                     (<i>essD, rrrD</i>, <i>rzpD/rzoD</i>) induces a 
                     hypervesiculation phenotype (possibly due to an excess of 
                     PG fragments in the periplasmic space); expression of the 
                     module is sensitive to environmental stress factors (low 
                     temperature, high osmolarity and acidic pH) |CITS: 
                     [33673345]|.
                     Expression of the DLP12 lysis cassette is positively 
                     regulated by Hha |CITS: [18545668]|, YbcQ |CITS: 
                     [23378572]|, and the alternative sigma factor |FRAME: 
                     RPOE-MONOMER &sigma;<sup>E</sup>| |CITS: [25998262]|. The 
                     CRISPR-Cas system is implicated in regulating cryptic 
                     prophages; the K12 CRISPR array contains cryptic 
                     prophage-related targeting spacers; expression of RzoD  is 
                     repressed by the CRISPR-Cas system likely via RNA 
                     interference |CITS: [36555835]|.
                     In an <i>rzpD/rzoD</i> deletion mutant, induction of Hha 
                     results in significantly less growth inhibition than in 
                     wild-type |CITS: [18545668]|."
                     /translation="MRKLKMMLCVMMLPLVVVGCTSKQSVSQCVKPPRPPAWIMQPPPD
