     gene            complement(580136..581089)
                     /gene="ompT"
                     /locus_tag="b0565"
                     /label=ompT
     CDS             complement(580136..581089)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ompT"
                     /locus_tag="b0565"
                     /product="bifunctional DLP12 prophage; omptin family outer
                     membrane protease OmpT / protease"
                     /EC_number="3.4.23.49"
                     /label=ompT
                     /note="OmpT is an outer membrane protease with specificity
                     for paired basic residues |CITS: [3056908]|; detailed 
                     studies on
                     substrate specificity have been performed |CITS: 
                     [11327829][11866094][15317797]|.
                     OmpT is active under extreme denaturing conditions and 
                     shows a preference for denatured substrates |CITS: 
                     [7768890]|. It is responsible for hydrolysis of the 
                     antimicrobial peptide protamine |CITS: [9683502]| and is a 
                     virulence determinant in urinary tract infections |CITS: 
                     [14634457]|. OmpT activity is cooperatively modulated by 
                     |FRAME: EG10669-MONOMER OmpA| and lipopolysaccharide <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [40669592]|.
                     OmpT cleaves endonuclease colicin E2 (ColE2), a 
                     bacteriocidal protein, and the associated cognate immunity 
                     protein (Im2) in the presence of BtuB; specifically, OmpT 
                     cleaves the C-terminal DNase domain of ColE2 |CITS: 
                     [18990718]|. OmpT can cleave active human complement 
                     component C3 |CITS: [36533918]|
                     Kinetic parameters of the purified protein have been 
                     determined |CITS: [10651827]|.
                     Based on information from a crystal structure, a novel 
                     catalytic mechanism was suggested |CITS: [11566868]|. 
                     Site-directed mutagenesis studies had previously identified
                     essential active site residues Ser99 and His212 |CITS: 
                     [10692590]|; mutational studies of additional residues 
                     support a novel catalytic mechanism |CITS: [11576541]|.
                     OmpT expression and activity increases in response to heat 
                     shock as well as overexpression of recombinant proteins 
                     |CITS: [10937437][10972933][11056060]|.
                     OmpT is a member of the omptin family of enterobacterial 
                     surface proteases |CITS: [15293449]|."
