     gene            592587..592919
                     /gene="cusF"
                     /locus_tag="b0573"
                     /label=cusF
     CDS             592587..592919
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cusF"
                     /locus_tag="b0573"
                     /product="bifunctional copper/silver export system
                     periplasmic binding protein / copper/silver export system"
                     /label=cusF
                     /note="CusF is a periplasmic binding protein involved in
                     the detoxification of copper and silver ions in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> as part of the CusCFBA copper/silver efflux 
                     system.
                     CusF forms a five-stranded &beta;-barrel and has been 
                     crystallized in its apo form as well as with bound Ag(I) or
                     Cu(I) |CITS: [16060662][17893365][18157124]|. CusF is a 
                     metallochaperone that specifically binds Ag(I) and Cu(I), 
                     but not Cu(II) |CITS: [16964970]| despite earlier evidence 
                     regarding binding of Cu(II) |CITS: [15770503]|.  CusF 
                     transfers metal directly to CusB for export |CITS: 
                     [18847219]|. CusF is a pink copper-binding protein and 
                     binds one copper ion per monomer |CITS: [12813074]|. The 
                     histidine residue at position 58 and the two methionine 
                     residues at positions 69 and 71 are essential for 
                     copper/silver binding |CITS: [12813074][16964970]|. Cu(I) 
                     binding also involves a strong interaction between the 
                     metal ion and the aromatic ring of a tryptophan residue at 
                     position 44 |CITS: [18157124][19381697][22029374]|. The 
                     Trp-44 'capping ligand' serves a protective function, 
                     preventing access of oxygen to the Cu(I) center |CITS: 
                     [39472424]|. CusF is susceptible to methionine oxidation; 
                     the |FRAME: CPLX0-8213| is implicated in maintaining the 
                     reduced form |CITS: [35816552]|.
                     CusF appears in the periplasmic space after induction with 
                     CuCl<sub>2</sub> |CITS: [12813074]|.
                     Comment: |CITS: [18202673]|"
