     gene            592935..594158
                     /gene="cusB"
                     /locus_tag="b0574"
                     /label=cusB
     CDS             592935..594158
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cusB"
                     /locus_tag="b0574"
                     /product="bifunctional copper/silver export system
                     membrane fusion protein / copper/silver export system"
                     /label=cusB
                     /note="CusB is a membrane fusion protein involved in the 
                     detoxification of copper and silver ions in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     as part of the CusCFBA copper/silver efflux system.
                     CusB is monomeric in solution |CITS: [17893146]|. Three 
                     methionine residues (M21, M36, M38) ligate the metal 
                     substrate in a 1:1 stoichiometry |CITS: 
                     [17893146][22812620]|. Crystal structures of CusB in the 
                     absence and presence of silver and copper ions have been 
                     described; CusB consists of 4 major domains: 3 
                     &beta;-strand domains and one &alpha;-helical domain; two 
                     distinct conformations of CusB were found in single 
                     crystals suggesting that CusB exhibits conformational 
                     flexibility |CITS: [19695261]|. The function of a membrane 
                     fusion protein like CusB may be to bring the outer membrane
                     factor, CusC, closer to the resistance-nodulation-division 
                     permease, CusA, consistent with the 'funnel' model of 
                     efflux. Alternatively CusB may act as a switch whereby 
                     metal binding to CusB promotes opening of a CusA channel 
                     and docking of substrate-loaded CusF (reviewed in |CITS: 
                     [21398536]|). Using selenomethionine labeling of active 
                     site methionine residues |CITS: [25313055]| provide <i>in 
                     vitro</i> evidence that loaded CusB activates CusA to 
                     accept copper from CusF. Back-transfer of copper from CusB 
                     to CusF was also observed and this may function as a 
                     regulatory mechanism
                     CusB is the key protein for metal induced assembly of the 
                     CusCBA tripartite complex; CusB can sense and and bind 
                     metal directly in the periplasm; when CusB binds metal, it 
                     increases the assembly kinetics and decreases the 
                     disassembly kinetics of the CusCBA complex |CITS: 
                     [28607072]|.
                     <i>cusB</i> expression is upregulated by indole in a 
                     dose-dependent manner |CITS: [15686558]|.
                     "
