     gene            complement(600227..600880)
                     /gene="nfsB"
                     /locus_tag="b0578"
                     /label=nfsB
     CDS             complement(600227..600880)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nfsB"
                     /locus_tag="b0578"
                     /product="multifunctional NAD(P)H-dependent nitroreductase
                     NfsB / NAD(P)H nitroreductase / quinone reductase / 
                     dihydropteridine reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.6.5.2; 1.5.1.34"
                     /label=nfsB
                     /note="The <i>nfsB</i>-encoded nitroreductase is the minor 
                     oxygen-insensitive nitroreductase present in <i>E. coli</i>
                     K-12.  NfsB reduces a broad range of nitroaromatic 
                     compounds |CITS: [8947835][8755909]|, including the 
                     antibiotics nitrofurazone and nitrofurantoin |CITS: 
                     [338576][7011517][9791100]|.
                     NfsB is a flavin mononucleotide (FMN)-containing protein 
                     and uses both NADH and NADPH as a source of reducing 
                     equivalents |CITS: [7011517][8947835]|.  FAD can substitute
                     for FMN as an effective prosthetic group |CITS: [8947835]|.
                      NfsB catalyzes the reduction of nitrocompounds using a 
                     ping-pong Bi-Bi mechanism |CITS: [8947835]|.  The reduction
                     of nitroaromatic compounds consists of two successive, 
                     two-electron transfer reactions to reduce nitroaromatics to
                     their hydroxylamine derivatives |CITS: [15684426]|.  NfsB 
                     can also act as a ferric reductase and catalyze a 
                     one-electron reduction of hydrogen peroxide, forming 
                     hydroxyl radicals |CITS: [20407804]|.  In the presence of 
                     free flavins, NfsB is able to release ferrous iron from 
                     FtnA and Bfr; NfsB also has a free flavin reductase 
                     activity with NADH as the electron donor |CITS: 
                     [31281172]|.
                     An enzyme with dihyropteridine reductase (DHPR) activity 
                     |CITS: [3060113][1418677]|, pterin-independent NAD(P)H 
                     oxidoreductase activity with potassium ferricyanide as the 
                     electron acceptor |CITS: [3060113]| and dihydrofolate 
                     reductase activity |CITS: [1418677]| has been purified from
                     <i>E. coli</i>; its N-terminal amino acid sequence |CITS: 
                     [3060113]| is highly similar to that of NfsB. Crystal 
                     structures of NfsB alone |CITS: [11020276]|, in complex 
                     with the substrate analog nicotinic acid |CITS: [11491290]|
                     or with nitrofurazone |CITS: [15684426]|, and of several 
                     mutant enzymes |CITS: [17350040]| have been solved.  To 
                     investigate the dynamics of NfsB upon ligand binding, NMR 
                     backbone assignments with and without nicotinic acid were 
                     obtained |CITS: [33423170]|.
                     Mutations in <i>nfsB</i> lead to increased resistance to 
                     nitrofurazone and furazolidone in an <i>nfsA</i> mutant 
                     background |CITS: [338576][9791100]|.  Nitrofurantoin 
                     resistance due to <i>nfsA/nfsB</i> mutations carries a 
                     small fitness cost |CITS: [18544599]|.  An <i>nfsA nfsB</i>
                     double mutant retains 30% of wt nitroreductase activity 
                     under aerobic conditions |CITS: [23795903]|.
                     NfsB has been implicated in the ability to utilize 
                     2,4,6-trinitrotoluene (TNT) as a source of nitrogen |CITS: 
                     [17504490]|.
                     <i>nfsB</i> contains a hotspot of IS<i>5</i> element 
                     insertion |CITS: [9791100]|.  IS<i>5</i> insertion appears 
                     to be targeted to superhelical stress-induced DNA 
                     destabilization (SSID) regions |CITS: [28666002]|. 
                     Bacterial nitroreductases such as NfsB have been used as 
                     prodrug activators for chemotherapy |CITS: 
                     [12052212][19290872][26431849]|.  Mutants that increase its
                     enzymatic activity against prodrugs (e.g. |CITS: 
                     [17350040]|) or to detoxify the anesthetic NBDZ |CITS: 
                     [22445794]| have been identified.
                     NfsB: ""<b>n</b>itro<b>f</b>uran <b>s</b>ensitivity 
                     <b>B</b>"" |CITS: [338576]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [18355273][20504108]|"
