     gene            complement(605710..607950)
                     /gene="fepA"
                     /locus_tag="b0584"
                     /label=fepA
     CDS             complement(605710..607950)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fepA"
                     /locus_tag="b0584"
                     /product="bifunctional ferric enterobactin outer membrane
                     transport complex / ferric enterobactin outer membrane 
                     transporter"
                     /label=fepA
                     /note="FepA is an outer membrane (OM) protein that binds
                     and transports ferric enterobactin (ferric enterochelin) 
                     |CITS: [6215063][148905]|; it also binds and transports 
                     colicins B and D (|CITS: [131121][139147]| and reviewed in 
                     |CITS: [12423783]|). FepA mediated transport across the OM 
                     is energised by the inner membrane proton gradient; energy 
                     transduction is facilitated by the |FRAME: CPLX0-1923| 
                     (reviewed in |CITS: [7654405]|). FepA transports ferric 
                     enterobactin and its degradation products, |FRAME: 
                     CPD-21612 Fe(DHBS)<sub>2</sub>| and ferric 
                     dihydroxybenzoate (FeDHBA<sub>3</sub>) |CITS: [38601941]|. 
                     FepA is a gated outer membrane porin |CITS: 
                     [9157886][1411544]|. FepA is a 22-strand antiparallel 
                     &beta;-barrel with an N-terminal globular domain which 
                     folds into the barrel plugging the pore. The structure 
                     contains several extracellular loops that would extend 
                     beyond the OM bilayer and may be involved ferric 
                     enterobactin binding |CITS: [9886293]|. A 'ball and chain' 
                     mechanism of transport for FepA, in which the globular 
                     domain moves in and out of the channel to facilitate 
                     transport, has been proposed |CITS: [9886293][17056600]|. 
                     The FepA channel is the largest OM channel characterised in
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [7504275]|.
                     Purified FepA binds ferric enterobactin with high affinity 
                     |CITS: [8471822][7734925]|. FepA physically interacts with 
                     TonB |CITS: [8344918]|. FepA binds glucosylated 
                     enterobactin |CITS: [38601941]|. FepA has been extensively 
                     mutagenised to study the transport mechanism |CITS: [22016 
                     87][7947735][7578022][9114029][9335564][9636045][10383757][
                     10998164][11532122][12169616][12779236][17578453]|. 
                     Proteomic analyses indicate that <i>fepA</i> is upregulated
                     when <i>E. coli</i> cells are exposed to sea water |CITS: 
                     [18318713]|. Cultivation with nTiO<sub>2</sub> resulted in 
                     increased production of proteins involved in siderophore 
                     biosynthesis (EntA, EntB, EntC, and EntE), siderophore 
                     recognition (FepA, CirA) and transport (FepB) |CITS: 
                     [35325859]|.
                     FepA translation is negatively regulated by the small RNAs 
                     OmrA and OmrB.  Binding of those small RNAs disrupts a 
                     stem-loop region within the 5' end of the FepA coding 
                     sequence; formation of this stem-loop in the absence of 
                     OmrA/B positively affects translation initiation |CITS: 
                     [28918899]|.
                     The binding of the transcription factor |FRAME: CPLX0-226 
                     CRP| to a predicted CRP DNA binding site around the start 
                     of the <i>fepA</i> gene has been demonstrated; however an 
                     effect of this binding on gene expression has not been 
                     analyzed |CITS: [37114659]|.
                     The class IIb microcin MccM targets the TonB-dependent 
                     siderophore receptors |FRAME: G6414-MONOMER Fiu|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10155-MONOMER CirA|, and FepA |CITS: [39172990]|. FepA is
                     the terminal receptor for a number of small siphoviruses of
                     the <i>Drexlerviridae</i> genus |CITS: [34784345]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [10209114][17186377][12957833]|<BR>Comments: |CITS: 
                     [9886280]|"
