     gene            608193..609395
                     /gene="fes"
                     /locus_tag="b0585"
                     /label=fes
     CDS             608193..609395
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fes"
                     /locus_tag="b0585"
                     /product="bifunctional ferric enterobactin esterase /
                     enterochelin esterase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.1.108"
                     /label=fes
                     /note="Enterochelin (enterobactin, Ent) esterase (Fes)
                     catalyzes the hydrolysis of both enterobactin and ferric 
                     enterobactin.  The enzyme produces the linear 
                     2,3-dihydroxy-N-benzoyl-L-serine trimer as well as the 
                     dimer and monomer |CITS: 
                     [4330269][4565531][150859][8148617]|.  Hydrolysis of ferric
                     enterobactin is less efficient than hydrolysis of 
                     unliganded enterobactin |CITS: [1534808]|.  The catalytic 
                     activity of the Fes ortholog in <i>E. coli</i> CFT073 was 
                     characterized; although the k<sub>cat</sub> for unliganded 
                     enterobactin is higher than that for Fe-Ent, the catalytic 
                     efficiency (k<sub>cat</sub>/K<sub>m</sub>) with the Fe-Ent 
                     substrate is much higher than that for apo-Ent.  Thus, 
                     Fe-Ent appears to be the physiological substrate of Fes 
                     |CITS: [16076215]|.  The protein sequence of the CFT073 
                     enzyme is 96% identical to that of the K-12 enzyme. 
                     Expression of <i>fes</i> is increased by iron limitation 
                     |CITS: [3040679][2974033]|; repression under iron-replete 
                     conditions is mediated by Fur |CITS: [8021177][9573216]|. 
                     <i>fes</i> is more highly expressed at 37&deg;C than at 
                     23&deg;C during growth in minimal media; expression under 
                     iron-depleted conditions is high at both growth 
                     temperatures |CITS: [17526711]|.
                     When grown under conditions of iron limitation, <i>fes</i> 
                     mutants require the presence of added iron or citrate 
                     |CITS: [4565531]|.  A <i>fes</i> mutant is more sensitive 
                     to near-UV radiation |CITS: [8806229]|, hydrogen peroxide 
                     and paraquat |CITS: [27310257]| than wild type and is 
                     highly sensitive to the ferrous iron chelator 
                     2,2'-dipyridyl |CITS: [21097627]|.  A <i>fes</i> insertion 
                     mutant shows a SpoT-dependent increase in the ppGpp pool 
                     |CITS: [15853883]|.  A &Delta;<i>fes</i> mutant is 
                     hypersensitive to the antimicrobial peptide wrwycr |CITS: 
                     [22096151]|.
                     Fes: ""<b>f</b>erric <b>e</b>nterochelin e<b>s</b>terase"" 
                     |CITS: [4565531]|
                     Review: |CITS: [23443998]|
                     "
