     gene            609613..613494
                     /gene="entF"
                     /locus_tag="b0586"
                     /label=entF
     CDS             609613..613494
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="entF"
                     /locus_tag="b0586"
                     /product="apo-serine activating enzyme"
                     /EC_number="6.3.2.14; 6.2.1.72"
                     /label=entF
                     /note="EntF is part of the enterobactin biosynthesis
                     pathway. Enterobactin, a siderophore molecule, is 
                     synthesized in response to iron deprivation by formation of
                     an amide bond between 2,3-dihydroxybenzoate (2,3-DHB) and 
                     L-serine. EntF is an activating enzyme for L-serine and 
                     catalyzes the formation of an L-seryl-AMP-EntF complex. It 
                     activates and binds Ser to itself via a thioester linkage 
                     to bound 4'-phosphopantetheine |CITS: 
                     [1826089][9485415][3040679][1338974]|.
                     EntF is one of three proteins (also EntE and EntB) that 
                     comprises the nonribosomal peptide synthetase (NRPS) 
                     portion of the overall |FRAME: ENTMULTI-CPLX| in <i>E. 
                     coli</i>. EntF contains the complete serine module and a 
                     C-terminal domain that catalyzes the formation of the 
                     trilactone ring |CITS: [9214294][9485415]|. The apo form of
                     EntF serves as a substrate for EntD |CITS: [9485415]|. EntF
                     is a cytosolic protein which can be released by osmotic 
                     shock. Membrane association of EntF has also been 
                     demonstrated |CITS: [10692387]|.
                     In a directed evolution study critical residues of the 
                     binding pocket of EntF were identified, thus enabling 
                     substrate substitution for genetic engineering purposes 
                     |CITS: [39569740]|."
