     gene            624756..625169
                     /gene="entH"
                     /locus_tag="b0597"
                     /label=entH
     CDS             624756..625169
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="entH"
                     /locus_tag="b0597"
                     /product="trifunctional proofreading thioesterase in
                     enterobactin biosynthesis / proofreading thioesterase / 
                     3-hydroxybenzoyl-CoA thioesterase"
                     /label=entH
                     /note="EntH is a thioesterase that is involved in the 
                     biosynthesis of enterobactin |CITS: [17675380]|.  It acts 
                     as a proofreading enzyme that hydrolyzes misacylated EntB 
                     aryl intermediates |CITS: [19193103][19119850]|.  |CITS: 
                     [19193103]| reports that the catalytic activity of EntH is 
                     sensitive to the pattern of hydroxyl groups on the aryl-CoA
                     substrates |CITS: [19193103]|, while |CITS: [24992697]| saw
                     no such effect.  Although EntH can utilize both CoA and 
                     holo-EntB as the substrate thioester, holo-ACP is a very 
                     poor substrate |CITS: [24992697]|. While EntH is not 
                     required for enterobactin synthesis <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [9485415]|, it is required for optimal synthesis <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [17675380]|.  EntH interacts with 
                     phosphopantetheine-modified |FRAME: ENTB-CPLX EntB| <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [17675380]|. Esterase activity of EntH was 
                     first discovered in a high-throughput screen of purified 
                     proteins |CITS: [15808744]|.  Benzoyl-CoA was found to be a
                     good substrate, with a K<sub>m</sub> of 82 &micro;M |CITS: 
                     [17675380]|.
                     Crystal structures of EntH have been determined |CITS: 
                     [16021622][25010423]|.  The quarternary structure is best 
                     described as a dimer of dimers |CITS: [25010423]|.
                     Enzymes with mutations in predicted active site residues 
                     have been purified and kinetically characterized |CITS: 
                     [19193103][24992697][25010423]|.  EntH belongs to the 
                     4-hydroxybenzoyl-CoA thioesterase subfamily of the hot dog 
                     fold superfamily of proteins |CITS: [19473548]|. Structural
                     and functional comparisons between EntH and its paralog 
                     |FRAME: G6912-MONOMER MenI| provided insight into the 
                     substrate specificity and evolution of the hotdog fold 
                     enzymes |CITS: [24992697][25010423]|.
                     EntH production is increased under iron starvation 
                     conditions; overproduction of EntH inhibits production of 
                     enterobactin |CITS: [17675380]|.
                     EntH: ""<b>ent</b>erobactin"" |CITS: [17675380]|"
