     gene            634401..634964
                     /gene="ahpC"
                     /locus_tag="b0605"
                     /label=ahpC
     CDS             634401..634964
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ahpC"
                     /locus_tag="b0605"
                     /product="bifunctional alkyl hydroperoxide reductase, AhpC 
                     component / alkyl hydroperoxide reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.11.1.26"
                     /label=ahpC
                     /note="AhpC is the peroxidase component of alkyl
                     hydroperoxide reductase belonging to the family of typical 
                     2-Cys peroxiredoxins with two conserved redox-active 
                     cysteines. By similarity to the enzyme from <i>Salmonella 
                     typhimurium</i>, AhpC carries out the actual reduction of 
                     the hydroperoxide substrate.
                     Under reduced conditions, AhpC forms a decameric ring 
                     composed of five homodimers that interact in a head-to-tail
                     configuration, with intermolecular disulfide bonds between 
                     Cys47 of one subunit and Cys177 of the second subunit 
                     forming the active site |CITS: [25193562][25372677]|. The C
                     terminus is required for the dimer-to-decamer transition, 
                     anchors the AhpC-AhpF interaction, and undergoes 
                     redox-modulated structural rearrangements |CITS: 
                     [25193562][26584540]|.
                     A proteomics screen showed that AhpC interacts with ATP 
                     |CITS: [22733688]|. AhpC has been found to interact with 
                     thioredoxin |CITS: [15004283]| and the peptidyl-prolyl 
                     cis-trans isomerase PpiC |CITS: [20806779]|. AhpC is 38% 
                     oxidatively modified in exponentially growing cells; this 
                     increases to 63.2% after hypochlorite treatment |CITS: 
                     [18456836]|.
                     A G142V mutation in <i>ahpC</i> leads to increased activity
                     in the reduction of tetralin hydroperoxide to 
                     1,2,3,4-tetrahydro-1-naphthol |CITS: [7644465]|; an L177Q 
                     mutation leads to increased limonene tolerance, likely by 
                     reducing the toxic compound limonene hydroperoxide |CITS: 
                     [25934627]|. Addition of a single phenylalanine residue 
                     converts AhpC from a peroxidase to a disulfide reductase 
                     |CITS: [11588261]| that generates reduced glutathione 
                     |CITS: [18206967]|. Other point mutants confer disulfide 
                     reductase activity without eliminating the peroxidase 
                     activity of AhpC |CITS: [18206967]|. Surprisingly, 
                     different mutant versions of AhpC can channel electrons 
                     into disulfide-reducing pathways via different protein 
                     substrates |CITS: [18456836]|.
                     Protein abundance is increased by aerobic growth in acidic 
                     medium |CITS: [10094700]|, sulfate starvation |CITS: 
                     [8774726]|, and indole |CITS: [10648109]| and decreased in 
                     the presence of the antifouling agent zosteric acid |CITS: 
                     [22176949]|. Overexpression of <i>ahpC</i> confers furfural
                     tolerance |CITS: [24981318]|.
                     An <i>ahpC</i> deletion mutant secretes less enterobactin 
                     and shows reduced growth and reduced accumulation of iron 
                     when grown in low iron media. The defect may be due to an 
                     effect of AhpC on the activity of EntC or on the 
                     availability of chorismate |CITS: [23042987]|.
                     Using a method that distinguishes N-phosphorylation from 
                     O-phosphorylation, His residue 83 was found to be 
                     phosphorylated |CITS: [35636362]|.
                     Ssi8: ""sulfate starvation-induced"" |CITS: [8774726]|"
