     gene            635209..636774
                     /gene="ahpF"
                     /locus_tag="b0606"
                     /label=ahpF
     CDS             635209..636774
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ahpF"
                     /locus_tag="b0606"
                     /product="trifunctional alkyl hydroperoxide reductase,
                     AhpF component / FAD reductase (NADH) / alkyl hydroperoxide
                     reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.11.1.26; 1.5.1.37"
                     /label=ahpF
                     /note="AhpF is the peroxiredoxin reductase component of
                     alkyl hydroperoxide reductase, and is one of the ten most 
                     abundant proteins in |FRAME: TAX-562| |CITS: [9298646]|. 
                     The N-terminal domain of AhpF belongs to the family of 
                     protein-disulfide oxidoreductases, while the C terminus 
                     belongs to the thioredoxin reductase family |CITS: 
                     [11243797]|. By similarity to the enzyme from <i>Salmonella
                     typhimurium</i>, it is thought to channel electrons from 
                     NADH to the AhpC component for reduction of the 
                     hydroperoxide substrate. An internal redox-active disulfide
                     bridge between two cysteine residues is reduced by electron
                     transfer from NADH via the FAD cofactor. This reduction 
                     triggers a cascade of disulfide-exchange reactions, first 
                     intramolecularly, then intermolecularly to the disulfide 
                     cysteine residues of the AhpC component. AhpF consists of 
                     an N-terminal domain (NTD), an FAD-binding domain and an 
                     NADH-binding domain |CITS: [25372677]|. A crystal structure
                     of the catalytic core of AhpF containing the FAD cofactor 
                     has been solved at 1.9 &Aring; resolution |CITS: 
                     [10666639][11243797]|. Crystal structures of the 
                     full-length protein have been solved |CITS: 
                     [25372677][26092085]|. The NTD was identified as the 
                     AhpC-interacting epitope |CITS: [25372677]|; structures of 
                     the NADH- and NAD<sup>+</sup>-bound forms and SAXS studies 
                     show a substatial interdomain rotation and intermediate 
                     conformations that enable electron transfer |CITS: 
                     [26092085]|.
                     Overexpression of AhpF suppresses the formation of 
                     aggregated proteins upon streptomycin treatment and 
                     increases resistance to aminoglycoside antibiotics |CITS: 
                     [23122414]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [11012664]|"
