                     /label=rna
     CDS             complement(639653..640459)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rna"
                     /locus_tag="b0611"
                     /product="bifunctional RNase I / RNase I endoribonuclease"
                     /EC_number="4.6.1.21"
                     /label=rna
                     /note="Ribonuclease I (RNase I) is an endoribonuclease
                     that cleaves phosphodiester bonds between any two 
                     nucleotides in RNA, yielding nucleoside 3'-phosphates and 
                     3'-phosphooligonucleotides |CITS: [SPAHR1961]|. RNase I is 
                     partially responsible for the degradation of total and 
                     ribosomal RNA during both normal and nutrient starvation 
                     conditions, especially during carbon starvation |CITS: 
                     [4358625][1091648][320188]|. RNase I is specifically 
                     required for the breakdown of 23S RNA, though it is not 
                     required for degradation of 16S RNA or very small (4S) RNA 
                     fragments that result from breakdown of larger RNA |CITS: 
                     [1089666][1091648]|. RNase I degradation of the 50S 
                     ribosomal subunit releases the ribosomal proteins L4, L10 
                     and L7/12 in addition to cleaving the 23S RNA to yield a 
                     smaller product |CITS: [115862]|. Polyamines stimulate the 
                     activity of RNase I against synthetic polynucleotides <i>in
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [321440]|.
                     RNase I is primarily a periplasmic protein that can be 
                     released by spheroblasting or treatment of cells with 
                     N-dodecyldiethanolamine. The latter treatment allows 
                     subsequent enhanced breakdown of rRNA by RNase I |CITS: 
                     [5319839][14218066][14257624][4284300][4994433][801034]|. 
                     RNase I appears to remain with the membrane fraction in 
                     disrupted cells |CITS: [765338]| and is associated with the
                     30S ribosomal subunit |CITS: [SPAHR1961][14218066]|. The 
                     gene encoding RNase I was cloned, sequenced, and its 
                     physical map position determined. It has a 23-residue 
                     amino-terminal signal sequence which is cleaved and likely 
                     allows its transport to the periplasm |CITS: 
                     [2253883][1398076]|. A multiply mutated form of RNase I, 
                     termed RNase M, has been characterized from <i>E. coli</i> 
                     strain MRE600 which is deficient in RNase I activity |CITS:
                     [1856166][11780627][26802429]|. The formation of disulfide 
                     bonds in RNase I <i>in vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> has 
                     been investigated |CITS: [17702751][14726535]|. Gene 
                     <i>rna</i> is one of several genes associated with 
                     tolerance to furfural, which is of interest in metabolic 
                     engineering |CITS: [24981318]|. Crystallization of purified
                     native, or recombinant wild-type and mutant RNase I has 
                     been reported |CITS: [8230230][11368513]|. Biophysical 
                     analysis revealed a conformation energy of 11.5 kcal/mol at
                     pH 7.5, and a T<sub>m</sub> of 64 &deg;C at pH 4.0, 
                     reflecting high physical stability |CITS: [11368513]|. 
                     Other crystal structures for RNase I are shown in the PDB 
                     links on this page. They are described in |CITS: 
                     [17702751]| for RNase I at 2.3 &Aring; resolution and in 
                     |CITS: [18305191]| for RNase I in complex with a 
                     decadeoxynucleotide at 1.7 &Aring; resolution.
                     Early studies characterized and mapped RNase 1 mutants 
                     |CITS: [5331244][4860430][4938550][4887525][4915439]|. 
                     Mutants lacking RNase I or other ribonuclease activities 
                     have reduced DNA degradation, possibly due to interaction 
                     between excess RNA and DNA endonuclease I |CITS: [4105037]|
                     and increased extracellular DNA production 
                     |CITS:[20833130]|. RNase I is also required for full 
                     recovery from starvation, as cell viability studies show a 
                     direct correlation between recovery from starvation and the
                     ability to degrade RNA |CITS: [1089666]|. A method has been
                     developed to screen for mutants in RNase I by checking for 
                     a delay in &beta;-galactosidase expression during amino 
                     acid starvation |CITS: [4570595]|. Mutant studies and the 
                     creation of hybrid ribosomes identified helix 41 of 16S 
                     ribosomal RNA as having a crucial role in the specific 
                     inhibition of RNase I by the 30S ribosomal subunit |CITS: 
                     [22109523]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [35787606][38064689]|"
                     /translation="MKAFWRNAALLAVSLLPFSSANALALQAKQYGDFDRYVLALSWQT
                     GFCQSQHDRNRNERDECRLQTETTNKADFLTVHGLWPGLPKSVAARGVDERRWMRFGCA
