     gene            652013..652573
                     /gene="pagP"
                     /locus_tag="b0622"
                     /label=pagP
     CDS             652013..652573
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pagP"
                     /locus_tag="b0622"
                     /product="Lipid A palmitoyltransferase"
                     /label=pagP
                     /note="The outer membrane protein PagP catalyses the
                     transfer of a palmitate chain from phospholipid donor to 
                     the lipid A moiety of lipopolysaccharide (LPS), a 
                     modification that is important for virulence in many 
                     gram-negative bacteria.
                     Purified PagP transfers a palmitate chain from the 
                     <i>sn</i>-1 position of a phospholipid substrate 
                     (phosphatidylcholine substrates and common E. coli 
                     phospholipids) to the amide-linked hydroxymyristate chain 
                     of the proximal glucosamine unit (GlcN I) of disaccharide 
                     lipid A precursors (lipid IV<sub><small>A</small></sub> and
                     Kdo<sub><small>2</small></sub>-lipid 
                     IV<sub><small>A</small></sub>) <i>in vitro</i>; the 
                     expected physiological substrate of PagP is the lipid A 
                     moiety of fully assembled |FRAME: CPD0-939 ""LPS""| in the 
                     outer membrane; PagP is highly selective for a saturated 
                     palmitate chain |CITS: [11013210]|.
                     PagP is an integral outer membrane enzyme |CITS: 
                     [11013210]|. Purified PagP (refolded in detergent micelles)
                     forms an eight-stranded antiparallel &beta;-barrel with a 
                     small amphipathic N-terminal &alpha;-helix on the 
                     periplasmic side of the membrane; the putative catalytic 
                     residues (His-33, Asp-76, and Ser-77) are located on the 
                     exterior edge of the outer membrane |CITS: [12357033]| (see
                     also  |CITS: [15210985]|). PagP forms a 'tilted' barrel 
                     structure with an internal ligand binding pocket that 
                     discriminates between acyl chains with different numbers of
                     carbon atoms |CITS: [15272304]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [17375935][20175558][20853818]|. Both phospholipid 
                     substrate and LPS must gain access to the active site 
                     suggesting that PagP is active when lipid asymmetry is 
                     disturbed; PagP activity <i>in vivo</i> depends on aberrant
                     migration of phospholipids to the outer membrane |CITS: 
                     [15319435]|. PagP contains two regions of weakened 
                     &beta;-strand hydrogen bonding (the crenel and the 
                     embrasure) for controlling lipid diffusion between the 
                     barrel interior and the outer membrane external leaflet 
                     |CITS: [19769329]| (see further |CITS: [20826347]|). PagP 
                     activity is negatively modulated by |FRAME: EG10669-MONOMER
                     OmpA| <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [40669592]|.
                     <i>pagP</i> expression is induced in mature biofilms and in
                     very late stationary-phase planktonic cultures |CITS: 
                     [25139899][30131361]|.
                     <i>crcA</i>: <u>c</u>onfers <u>r</u>esistance to 
                     <u>c</u>amphor |CITS: [8844142]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [12904550]|).
                     Reviews: |CITS: [15120001][16091033]|"
