     gene            652748..652957
                     /gene="cspE"
                     /locus_tag="b0623"
                     /label=cspE
     CDS             652748..652957
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cspE"
                     /locus_tag="b0623"
                     /product="transcription antiterminator and regulator of
                     RNA stability CspE"
                     /label=cspE
                     /note="CspE belongs to the cold shock family of proteins
                     and functions as a transcription antiterminator at 
                     &rho;-independent terminators both <i>in vitro</i> and 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [10884409]|.
                     The nucleic acid melting activity of CspE is required for 
                     its transcription antitermination activity |CITS: 
                     [11756430][12324471]|; the melting activity of CspE point 
                     mutants |CITS: [15001358]| and wild-type |CITS: [16214801]|
                     on DNA substrates has been studied. Purified CspE protein 
                     binds to poly(A) RNA tails and impedes poly(A)-mediated 
                     mRNA decay and internal mRNA cleavage by polynucleotide 
                     phosphorylase and RNase E |CITS: [11390393]|. CspE inhibits
                     the expression of <i>cspA in vitro</i> by increasing pause 
                     recognition by RNA polymerase at the cspA ""cold box"" 
                     |CITS: [10200963]|. Initial results showed that CspE 
                     interacts with nascent RNA in an initial transcribing 
                     complex and inhibits Q-mediated transcription 
                     antitermination at the &lambda; P<sub>R'</sub> promoter 
                     |CITS: [9735283]|.
                     Although CspE did not appear to interact with free RNA 
                     |CITS: [9735283]|, it was later shown to interact with both
                     RNA and DNA; a consensus sequence for selective RNA binding
                     has been determined using an <i>in vitro</i> selection 
                     approach |CITS: [10476034]|. CspE appears to interact 
                     specifically with mRNAs that encode membrane proteins 
                     |CITS: [26225847]| and that are located in the soluble 
                     ribosome-free fraction of the cytoplasm |CITS: [28841711]|.
                     CspE displays the highest affinity towards ssDNA containing
                     six contiguous dT residues |CITS: [17088256]|. CspE is 
                     involved in resistance to camphor-induced chromosome 
                     decondensation |CITS: [8844142][12904550]|, and a model of 
                     how its ssDNA binding activity might affect chromosome 
                     condensation has been proposed |CITS: [17088256]|.
                     Published evidence disagrees on whether the protein behaves
                     like a monomer |CITS: [9735283]| or a dimer |CITS: 
                     [17088256]| in solution.
                     Expression of <i>cspE</i> is constitutive at 37&deg;C 
                     |CITS: [10200963][18177308]| and is transiently induced in 
                     response to cold shock at both the transcript and the 
                     protein levels |CITS: [18177308]|. Expression of 
                     <i>cspE</i> and eight other <i>csp</i> genes during growth 
                     and under different media conditions has been measured 
                     |CITS: [24952137]|. Diverse expression responses of the 
                     cold shock proteins, CspA-I, were found under different 
                     stress conditions, specifically cold, heat, rich vs minimal
                     media and oxidative stress caused by 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. Expression of <i>cspE</i> is 
                     significantly up in cold-shock conditions whereas response 
                     to heat stress is minimal. It is significantly up after 30 
                     min exposure to H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> stress and 
                     throughout the growth cycle in minimal media |CITS: 
                     [40312551]|.
                     <i>cspE</i> is regulated at the posttranscriptional level 
                     by RNase E and predicted temperature-responsive secondary 
                     structures in the 5' UTR |CITS: [18177308]|. |FRAME: 
                     EG10900-MONOMER| appears to inhibit RNase E-dependent decay
                     of <i>cspE</i> mRNA |CITS: [18824515][21685451]|. The 
                     |FRAME: EG12866-MONOMER| binds to the 3' UTR of <i>cspE</i>
                     mRNA and appears to protect it from RNase II degradation 
                     |CITS: [29804828][31761494]|.
                     A <i>cspE</i> deletion strain has no growth defect, but 
                     shows a longer lag period than wild type after dilution 
                     into fresh medium |CITS: [10200963]|. A <i>cspE</i> 
                     insertion mutant is highly sensitive to UV and gamma 
                     radiation |CITS: [11215702]|. Only a quadruple 
                     <i>cspABGE</i> deletion mutant shows cold sensitivity 
                     |CITS: [11298285]|. Global gene expression changes in 
                     response to cold shock in the &Delta;<i>cspABGE</i> strain 
                     |CITS: [15466053]| and in response to a <i>cspCE</i> 
                     deletion and CspE overexpression |CITS: [16547039]| have 
                     been measured. A <i>pagP</i> (<i>crcA</i>) <i>cspE crcB</i>
                     triple null mutant exhibits greater than wild-type 
                     sensitivity to camphor, which causes chromosome 
                     decondensation, and it enhances the nucleoid morphology 
                     phenotype of a topoisomerase IV mutant |CITS: [12904550]|. 
                     Overexpression of <i>pagP</i>, <i>cspE</i>, and <i>crcB</i>
                     together results in resistance to camphor |CITS: 
                     [8844142]|, increased DNA supercoiling and increased 
                     expression of <i>rcsA</i> |CITS: [12904550]|, and 
                     suppresses phenotypes of a <i>mukB</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [7984109][8844142]|, a gyrase mutant and a topoisomerase IV
                     mutant |CITS: [12904550]|.
                     Overexpression of CspE is sufficient to increase 
                     transcription of <i>nusA</i>, <i>infB</i>, <i>rbfA</i>, and
                     <i>pnp</i> |CITS: [10884409]| and leads to higher levels of
                     <i>rpoS</i> mRNA |CITS: [11157932]|. Genes whose expression
                     level responds to overexpression of CspE have been 
                     determined |CITS: [19840122]|.
                     An <i>in-silico</i> comparative study of evolution, 
                     structure and function was carried out for the 9 CspA 
                     family of proteins in <i>E. coli K-12</i>. Despite high 
                     homology (>70% amino acid sequence similarity), it was 
                     determined that 4 paralogous pairs could be identified and 
                     served as the focus for the comparative analyses: 
                     CspA/CspB, CspC/CspE, CspG/CspI and CspF/CspH |CITS: 
                     [37333716]|. A follow-up study conducted mutational 
                     analysis and molecular modeling to determine selection 
                     pressure on the paralogous pairs |CITS: [39779584]|.
                     MsmC: ""<b>m</b>ulticopy <b>s</b>uppressor of 
                     <i><b>m</b>ukB106</i>"" |CITS: [7984109]|
                     CspE: ""<b>c</b>old <b>s</b>hock <b>p</b>rotein <b>E</b>"" 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8022259][17437059][17455763][21045540]|"
