     gene            complement(654707..655672)
                     /gene="lipA"
                     /locus_tag="b0628"
                     /label=lipA
     CDS             complement(654707..655672)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lipA"
                     /locus_tag="b0628"
                     /product="lipoyl synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.8.1.8"
                     /label=lipA
                     /note="Lipoate synthase catalyzes the final step of <i>de 
                     novo</i> lipoate biosynthesis, the insertion of sulfur into
                     the octanoyl side chain of an octanoylated E2 domain to 
                     form the lipoate moiety |CITS: 
                     [8444795][11106496][14700636]|. Lipoate modification of the
                     E2 subunits is important for the function of |FRAME: 
                     PYRUVATEDEH-CPLX| |CITS: [814874][6794598][8444795]|, 
                     |FRAME: 2OXOGLUTARATEDEH-CPLX ""&alpha;-ketoglutarate 
                     dehydrogenase""| |CITS: [814874][6794598][8444795]|, and 
                     the |FRAME: GCVMULTI-CPLX| |CITS: [1655709][8444795]|. 
                     Lipoate synthase is a homodimer containing iron-sulfur 
                     clusters |CITS: [10403368][BUSBY99][10747808][11106496]|. 
                     Coexpression with the <i>isc</i> operon increases the yield
                     of overexpressed soluble LipA |CITS: [12699687]|. LipA 
                     belongs to the radical SAM superfamily of enzymes |CITS: 
                     [11222759]|. The enzyme uses octanoyl side chains (but not 
                     free octanoate) as substrate |CITS: [11106496][14700636]|. 
                     The 5'-dA&middot; radicals, generated from SAM, act 
                     directly on the octanoyl substrate. Two equivalents of 
                     5'-dA&middot; sequentially abstract hydrogen atoms from C6 
                     and C8 of the octanoyl group, preparing it for sulfur 
                     insertion |CITS: [15157071]|. LipA contains two distinct 
                     [4Fe-4S] clusters per polypeptide which are both required 
                     for catalytic activity |CITS: [15362861]|, and one of which
                     may be the source of the sulfur atoms required for 
                     synthesis of the lipoyl group |CITS: [15740115]|. Evidence 
                     for a covalently linked monothiolated reaction intermediate
                     and a partially degraded Fe-S cluster has been obtained 
                     |CITS: [24901788]|. <i>In vitro</i> assays have provided 
                     direct evidence that a [4Fe-4S] cluster in LipA is the 
                     source of the two sulfur atoms. The [4Fe-4S] cluster 
                     carrier protein |FRAME: G7748-MONOMER NfuA| is able to 
                     replace the degraded [4Fe-4S] cluster of LipA in a process 
                     that is not rate-limiting, resulting in catalytic activity 
                     of LipA <i>in vitro</i>. In the absence of NfuA, |FRAME: 
                     G7324-MONOMER IscU| is able to provide a replacement 
                     [4Fe-4S] cluster to LipA |CITS: [29051382]|.
                     LipA and LipB interact directly and independently with the 
                     E2 protein of both |FRAME: PYRUVATEDEH-CPLX| and |FRAME: 
                     2OXOGLUTARATEDEH-CPLX ""&alpha;-ketoglutarate 
                     dehydrogenase""|, but not GcvH of the |FRAME: 
                     GCVMULTI-CPLX| |CITS: [21209092]|.
                     The <i>lip-2</i> and <i>lip-9</i> mutations, which cause 
                     lipoic acid auxotrophy, are <i>lipA</i> alleles |CITS: 
                     [1655709]|; <i>lip-2</i> encodes an S307F change and 
                     <i>lip-9</i> encodes an E195K change |CITS: [8466915]|. The
                     lipoate auxotrophy of a <i>lipA</i> mutant is rescued by 
                     6-thiooctanoate or 8-thiooctanoate |CITS: [8444795]|. 
                     <i>lipA</i> mutants have an extended lifespan |CITS: 
                     [20707865]|. A <i>lipA</i> mutant has a slow growth 
                     phenotype in LB and shows increased resistance to acid, 
                     hydrogen peroxide, heat and osmotic stress compared to wild
                     type |CITS: [27310825]|. A knockout mutant of <i>lipA</i> 
                     abolishes lipoylation on all substrate sites by LipB and 
                     LplA. This is not the case when knockouts of <i>lipB</i> or
                     <i>lplA</i> |CITS: [35648456]|.
                     A PdhR binding site that was predicted to be involved in 
                     regulation of <i>lipA</i> expression has been identified 
                     |CITS: [20718955]|, but was shown to be nonfunctional 
                     |CITS: [24816490]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [29051366][25341020][17936058][16221579][1 
                     5112987][11395404][11153271]|"
