     gene            complement(655881..656834)
                     /gene="ybeF"
                     /locus_tag="b0629"
                     /label=ybeF
     CDS             complement(655881..656834)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ybeF"
                     /locus_tag="b0629"
                     /product="putative DNA-binding transcriptional regulator
                     YbeF"
                     /label=ybeF
                     /note="YbeF was predicted to be a LysR-type transcription
                     factor |CITS:[34428301] [35880217]|. It contains a 
                     helix-turn-helix motif to bind DNA in its N-terminal 
                     domain. YbeF DNA-binding activity was probed in vivo in 
                     glucose M9 minimal medium through chromatin 
                     immunoprecipitation assays (ChIP-exo) |CITS: [34428301]|. 
                     It was predicted to regulate genes related to cell division
                     |CITS: [35880217]|. However, the effect of YbeF on the 
                     transcription of any gene has not yet been demonstrated 
                     |CITS: [34428301]|.
                     The transcriptional response to deletion of YbeF was 
                     characterized by RNA-seq in M9 minimal medium supplemented 
                     by 7 mM l-threonine |CITS:[35508583]|. In the YbeF knockout
                     mutant, the <i>lrhA</i> gene was downregulated and 
                     <i>flhC</i>  was upregulated in comparison with the wild 
                     type, and it was proposed that YbeF could be involved in 
                     flagella biosynthesis|CITS:[35508583]|.
                     YbeF is a paralog of CitR with 30% 
                     identity|CITS:[35508583]|.
                     In systematic studies of oligomerization, it was shown that
                     some members of the LysR family, like YbeF, interact with 
                     other members of the family to form heterodimers, but the 
                     physiological significance of this is unknown |CITS: 
                     [21187915]|.
                     Upstream of the <i>ybeF</i> gene, a sequence was found that
                     could work as a transcriptional promoter, and the 
                     transcription of the <i>ybeF </i> gene is affected by 
                     temperature and conditions that generate general 
                     transcriptional termination |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     "
