     gene            complement(657093..657734)
                     /gene="lipB"
                     /locus_tag="b0630"
                     /label=lipB
     CDS             complement(657093..657734)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lipB"
                     /locus_tag="b0630"
                     /product="trifunctional lipoyl(octanoyl) transferase /
                     octanoyl transferase / lipoyl-protein ligase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.181"
                     /label=lipB
                     /note="LipB is a lipoyl-/octanoyl-ACP:protein transferase
                     that catalyzes the first step of <i>de novo</i> lipoate 
                     biosynthesis |CITS: [9218413][12591875]|. <i>In vitro</i>, 
                     it transfers lipoyl or octanoyl domains from the 
                     corresponding acylated ACP to its target proteins; <i>in 
                     vivo</i>, octanoyl-ACP is the relevant substrate. Lipoate 
                     modification of the E2 subunits is important for the 
                     function of |FRAME: PYRUVATEDEH-CPLX| |CITS: 
                     [814874][6794598][8444795]|, |FRAME: 2OXOGLUTARATEDEH-CPLX 
                     ""&alpha;-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase""| |CITS: 
                     [814874][6794598][8444795]|, and the |FRAME: GCVMULTI-CPLX|
                     |CITS: [1655709][8444795]|.
                     The LipB protein is translated from a TTG translation 
                     initiation codon |CITS: [11072067]|; protein translated 
                     from the downstream ATG is not active |CITS: [12591875]|. 
                     The LipB-catalyzed reaction proceeds via an acyl-enzyme 
                     intermediate. The octanoate group is first transferred from
                     the thiol of ACP to the C169 thiol of LipB, followed by 
                     transfer to a specific lysine residue of E2 subunits |CITS:
                     [16342964]|. Allosteric regulation controls this transfer 
                     of the octanoic acid |CITS: [33846262]|. Substrate 
                     recognition appears to include a structural component, as a
                     biotinyl domain (structurally similar to a lipoyl domain) 
                     is lipoylated if mutated to contain a lipoylation site of 
                     sequence DKA |CITS: [9445386]|. Structural similarity 
                     between lipoylating and biotinylating enzymes, and 
                     implications with respect to the catalytic site, are 
                     discussed |CITS: [11106165]|. LipB recognizes the 
                     4'-phosphopantetheine-tethered acyl chain of the substrate,
                     and its full activity requires a conserved 
                     <sup>71</sup>RGG<sup>73</sup> loop |CITS: [35764173]|.
                     LipA and LipB interact directly and independently with the 
                     E2 protein of both |FRAME: PYRUVATEDEH-CPLX| and |FRAME: 
                     2OXOGLUTARATEDEH-CPLX ""&alpha;-ketoglutarate 
                     dehydrogenase""|, but not GcvH of the |FRAME: 
                     GCVMULTI-CPLX| |CITS: [21209092]|.
                     A <i>lipB</i> null mutant exhibits decreased lipoate 
                     biosynthesis, decreased pyruvate dehydrogenase activity, 
                     and decreased alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase activity 
                     compared to wild type. Growth of a <i>lipB</i> mutant can 
                     be supported  by 6-thiooctanoate or 8-thiooctanoate in 
                     place of lipoate |CITS: [8444795]|. A <i>lipB</i> mutant 
                     also exhibits increased transcription from the major 
                     promoter of the |FRAME: EG10204 ""<i>dam</i>""| 
                     methyltransferase gene, compared to wild type |CITS: 
                     [11072067]|. A heat-sensitive enzyme has been generated 
                     |CITS: [12591875]|. Duplication of the <i>lipB</i> locus 
                     results in resistance to selenolipoic acid |CITS: 
                     [12218038]|. Genetic interactions have been observed 
                     between LplA and the LipB-LipA |FRAME: PWY0-501| pathway: 
                     phenotypes of an <i>lipB</i> mutant are suppressed by 
                     overproduction of LplA, and <i>lplA lipA</i> or <i>lplA 
                     lipB</i> double mutants exhibit growth defects, whereas an 
                     <i>lplA</i> single mutant does not |CITS: [8002607]|. 
                     Missense mutations in <i>lplA</i> that reduce the enzyme's 
                     K<sub>m</sub> for octanoate can scavenge cytosolic 
                     octanoate and thereby suppress the auxotrophic phenotype of
                     a <i>lipB</i> mutant |CITS: [19684135]|. A knockout mutant 
                     of <i>lipB</i> only partially impacts lipoylation by LipA 
                     or LplA at their target lipoylation sites, whereas a double
                     knockout of <i>lipB lplA</i> abolished all lipoylation 
                     sites for LipA, LipB and LplA; supplementation with 
                     exogenous octanoic acids recovered lipoylation in 
                     &Delta;<i>lipB</i> strain |CITS: [35648456]|.
                     LipB appears to be expressed at very low levels; 
                     overexpression from a plasmid decreases the growth rate of 
                     the cell |CITS: [11072067]|.
                     <i>lipB</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [16221579]|"
                     /translation="MYQDKILVRQLGLQPYEPISQAMHEFTDTRDDSTLDEIWLVEHYP
                     VFTQGQAGKAEHILMPGDIPVIQSDRGGQVTYHGPGQQVMYVLLNLKRRKLGVRELVTL
                     LEQTVVNTLAELGIEAHPRADAPGVYVGEKKICSLGLRIRRGCSFHGLALNVNMDLSPF
                     LRINPCGYAGMEMAKISQWKPEATTNNIAPRLLENILALLNNPDFEYITA"
