     CDS             complement(661772..663673)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mrdA"
                     /locus_tag="b0635"
                     /product="bifunctional peptidoglycan DD-transpeptidase
                     MrdA / peptidoglycan DD-transpeptidase"
                     /label=mrdA
                     /note="Penicillin binding protein 2 (PBP2), the product of
                     the <i>mrdA</i> gene is believed to be the primary 
                     peptidoglycan (PG) transpeptidase which functions together 
                     with the SEDS family protein |FRAME: EG10607-MONOMER MrdB| 
                     (RodA) to synthesize PG during cell elongation; MrdB-PBP2 
                     are the SEDS-bPBP pair of the elongasome/Rod system.
                     A model of SEDS-bPBP catalysed peptidoglycan synthesis has 
                     been generated based on the structural, biochemical, 
                     genetic, and computational analyses of a RodA-PBP2 fusion 
                     protein |CITS: [37620344]|.
                     PBP2 displays |FRAME: EG10608-MONOMER ""MreB""|-like 
                     directional motion and has been implicated as the core 
                     transpeptidase of peptidoglycan synthesis during cell 
                     elongation |CITS: [27643381]|. Subcomplexes of class 2 
                     penicillin binding proteins with SEDS (shape, elongation, 
                     division and sporulation) family proteins are proposed to 
                     be the primary peptidoglycan synthases in cell elongation 
                     and division |CITS: [27525505]|. PBP2 and |FRAME: 
                     EG10607-MONOMER ""MrdB""| (a member of the SEDS family) may
                     function as the transpeptidase and glycosyltransferase 
                     respectively, for peptidoglycan synthesis during cell 
                     elongation |CITS: [27643381]|. Purified PBP2-MrdB fusion 
                     proteins are functional peptidoglycan synthases |CITS: 
                     [30335755]|. PBP2 plays a central role in Rod complex 
                     initiation |CITS: [32077853]|.
                     PBP2 is located in the inner membrane; PBP2 is the target 
                     at which mecillinam and 6-aminopenicillanic acid (6-APA) 
                     act to affect cell shape; PBP2 binds benzylpenicillin, 
                     ampicillin, mecillinam and penicillin V; it doesn't bind 
                     cephalosporins; mecillinam binds exclusively to PBP2; PBP2 
                     is present at an estimated 10 to 20 copies per cell |CITS: 
                     [1091862][1103132][319999]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [189683][393164]|). PBP2 is the product of the <i>mrdA</i> 
                     gene; <i>mrdA</i>(Ts) mutants convert from rod shaped, 
                     mecillinam sensitive cells to spherical, mecillinam 
                     resistant cells when grown at 42&deg;C |CITS: 
                     [6451612][6243629]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [6300030][6348028][3894330][3326629][2283038]|). PBP2 has 
                     murein transpeptidase activity; a mecillinam sensitive, 
                     peptidoglycan cross-linking activity can be detected in 
                     membranes containing elevated levels of PBP2 |CITS: 
                     [6297485][3009484]| (see further |CITS: [22606933]|). PBP2 
                     activity is essential; an <i>mrdA::kan</i> mutation cannot 
                     be introduced into a wild-type strain |CITS: [2656638]|; 
                     essentiality can be suppressed by the overproduction of 
                     |FRAME: EG10347-MONOMER| |CITS: [8407846]| (see also |CITS:
                     [2540960][1563353][7815948]|). Mecillinam blocks PBP2 
                     function and induces a futile cycle of peptidoglycan 
                     synthesis and degradation |CITS: [18390656][25480295]| (see
                     also |CITS: [28749938]|)
                     PBP2 has no signal peptide; a stretch of 25 non-ionic amino
                     acids in the N-terminal region likely anchors the protein 
                     in the inner membrane; an active site serine residue 
                     (Ser-330) which binds penicillin is located within a 
                     conserved SXXK motif |CITS: [3533535][2826251][3148617]|. A
                     GFP-PBP2 fusion localizes at the cylindrical part of the 
                     cell membrane and at the site of constriction; PBP2 may be 
                     active at the site of division but does not appear to be a 
                     stable component of the divisome |CITS: [12519203]|. 
                     Affinity chromatography suggests PBP2 interacts with 
                     |FRAME: EG10950-MONOMER ""Slt70""| |CITS: [9158739]| and 
                     with |FRAME: G7457-MONOMER ""MltA""| |CITS: [10037771]| 
                     (see also |CITS: [10542235]|). Inhibition of PBP2 produces 
                     a 'clustered' pattern of murein incorporation |CITS: 
                     [19346310]|. PBP2 is required for MreB rotation; MreB 
                     motion is reduced in an <i>mrdA</i>(Ts) mutant |CITS: 
                     [21903929]|. PBP2 interacts physically with |FRAME: 
                     EG10015-MONOMER ""RodZ""|; RodZ mediates MreB motion by 
                     coupling MreB to PBP2 and/or RodA activity |CITS: 
                     [26396257]|. PBP2 exists in both diffusive and bound 
                     states; PBP2 binds at sites that are independent of MreB 
                     localization; bound PBP2 molecules transition between 
                     persistantly moving and immobile states |CITS: [32077853]|.
                     PBP2 interacts physically with |FRAME: EG10748-MONOMER 
                     ""PBP1a""| <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i>; PBP2 
                     stimulates the glycosyltransferase activity of PBP1a <i>in 
                     vitro</i>; PBP2 has transpeptidase activity in the presence
                     of active PBP1a and contributes to the attachment of new 
                     peptidoglycan to sacculi <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [22606933]|.
                     <i>mrdA</i> and <i>mrdB</i> constitute a single 
                     transcriptional unit - the operon may also include three 
                     other genes - <i>rlpA, rlmH</i> and <i>rsfS</i> |CITS: 
                     [2644207][19838305]|. Transcription of peptidoglycan 
                     biosynthesis genes, including <i>mrdA</i>, is regulated by 
                     |FRAME: EG10977-MONOMER| |CITS: [36308522]|.
                     PBP2 is a class B, high molecular mass penicillin binding 
                     protein |CITS: [1741619][18266856]|. The molecular features
                     required for &beta;-lactam binding (the 'pharmacore' 
                     features) have been defined |CITS: [39585958]|.
                     <i>mrd</i>: <U>m</U>u<U>r</U>ein <U>D</U> |CITS: [6243629]|
                     Related reviews: 
                     |CITS:[9158730][17658458][22203377][28214390]|"
