     gene            complement(665387..666028)
                     /gene="nadD"
                     /locus_tag="b0639"
                     /label=nadD
     CDS             complement(665387..666028)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nadD"
                     /locus_tag="b0639"
                     /product="nicotinate-nucleotide adenylyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.18"
                     /label=nadD
                     /note="Nicotinate-mononucleotide adenylyltransferase
                     (NadD) is an essential enzyme involved in both <i>de 
                     novo</i> biosynthesis and salvage of NAD<sup>+</sup> |CITS:
                     [10894752]|.  Its essentiality makes it a target for the 
                     development of antibacterial agents |CITS: [19716475]|.
                     Both nicotinic acid mononucleotide (NaMN) and nicotinamide 
                     mononucleotide (NMN) are substrates, but the rate of 
                     adenylation of NaMN is at least 20 times faster than that 
                     of NMN |CITS: [10894752]|.
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme alone and in a complex 
                     with deamido-NAD have been solved and show that ligand 
                     binding causes conformational changes in regions 
                     surrounding the active site |CITS: [11796112]|. <i>nadD</i>
                     is essential for growth |CITS: [12142426][16153292]|.  The 
                     <i>nadD72</i> mutant allele was first isolated as a 
                     spontaneous temperature-sensitive mutation that leads to 
                     higher tolerance to fusidic acid. The mutant phenotype is 
                     pleiotropic |CITS: [353503]|. Further characterization of 
                     this mutation showed it to be an allele of <i>nadD</i> with
                     a -1 frameshift mutation that leads to a change in the C 
                     terminus of the NadD protein.  The mutant synthesizes very 
                     little NAD<sup>+</sup> and NADPH at the permissive 
                     temperature and essentially none at the non-permissive 
                     temperature |CITS: [12949168]|.  The newly isolated 
                     <i>nadD74</i> allele causes an amino acid change in the 
                     ATP-binding site of NadD.  On miminal medium, the mutant 
                     shows temperature sensitivity due to a reduction in 
                     NAD<sup>+</sup> synthesis; at the permissive temperature, 
                     NAD<sup>+</sup> synthesis is reduced, but sufficient to 
                     support normal growth |CITS: [12949168]|.  Additional 
                     mutants within the active site and the C terminus of NadD 
                     have been isolated and characterized |CITS: [16153292]|.  
                     Mutants that shift the substrate preference of NadD to NMN 
                     have been designed and tested |CITS: [28455340]|.
                     The <i>nadD</i> coding region contains an inhibitory RNA 
                     polymerase-binding aptamer (iRAP) that promotes 
                     Rho-dependent transcription termination, in particular in 
                     stationary phase and under oxidative stress |CITS: 
                     [28648779]|.
                     FusB: ""<b>fus</b>idic acid tolerance"" |CITS: 
                     [353503]|<br>NadD: |CITS: [10894752]|"
