     gene            complement(667061..667642)
                     /gene="lptE"
                     /locus_tag="b0641"
                     /label=lptE
     CDS             complement(667061..667642)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lptE"
                     /locus_tag="b0641"
                     /product="trifunctional lipopolysaccharide assembly
                     protein LptE / lipopolysaccharide transport system - outer 
                     membrane assembly complex / lipopolysaccharide transport 
                     system"
                     /EC_number="7.5.2.5"
                     /label=lptE
                     /note="LptE is part of the outer membrane LptDE complex
                     that functions to assemble lipopolysaccharide (LPS) at the 
                     cell surface |CITS:[16861298]|.
                     <i>lptE</i> (formerly <i>rlpB</i>) encodes a minor 
                     lipoprotein; <i>in vitro</i> expression of the mature 
                     protein is inhibited by treatment with the signal peptidase
                     II inhibitor, globomycin; the protein incorporates 
                     <sup>3</sup>H-labeled palmitate and <sup>3</sup>H-labeled 
                     glycerol <i>in vitro</i> |CITS:[3316191]|. Purifed LptE 
                     binds LPS specifically |CITS: [20203010]|. LptE binds LPS, 
                     disrupts LPS-LPS interactions and affects LPS aggregation 
                     propensity <i>in vitro</i>. LptE binds and solubilizes 
                     surface bound LPS <i>in vitro</i>. LptE containing the 
                     double mutation R91D K136D is able to bind, but cannot 
                     solubilize, surface bound LPS. These two amino acids may 
                     form part of an LPS interaction site that functions to 
                     disaggregate LPS and aid insertion into the outer membrane 
                     |CITS: [24938785]|. LptE consists of 2 &alpha;-helices and 
                     4 &beta;-strands; two loops (one located between 
                     &beta;-strands 2 and 3, the other between &beta;-strand 4 
                     and &alpha;-helix 2) form a lipid binding site |CITS: 
                     [24938785]|.
                     Exposure to synthetic reactive oxygen species (ROS) 
                     generated from synthetron radiation (X-ray) results in 
                     oxidation of methionine residues in vivo in hundreds of 
                     proteins. The % modification was highest in LptE, |FRAME: 
                     EG12240-MONOMER MdtE| and |FRAME: EG11009-MONOMER TolC| and
                     increased as the X-ray oxidation dose increased |CITS: 
                     [40199130]|.
                     <i>rlpB</i>: <U>r</U>are <U>l</U>ipo<U>p</U>rotein B 
                     |CITS:[3316191]|"
