     gene            complement(667657..670239)
                     /gene="leuS"
                     /locus_tag="b0642"
                     /label=leuS
     CDS             complement(667657..670239)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="leuS"
                     /locus_tag="b0642"
                     /product="trifunctional L-norvaline--tRNA(Leu) ligase / 
                     L-norvaline-tRNA(Leu) deacylase / leucine--tRNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.1.1.4"
                     /label=leuS
                     /note="Leucine-tRNA ligase (LeuRS) is a member of the
                     family of aminoacyl tRNA synthetases, which interpret the 
                     genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their 
                     specific tRNA molecules.  The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis.  LeuRS belongs to the Class IB aminoacyl tRNA 
                     synthetases; apart from sequence motifs within the active 
                     site, the different enzymes show little similarity in their
                     primary amino acid sequences |CITS: [7647112]|. Correct 
                     aminoacylation by LeuRS requires both an activation and an 
                     editing function |CITS: [10966471]|.
                     The CCA acceptor end of tRNA<sup>Leu</sup> is essential for
                     both the aminoacylation and editing reaction |CITS: 
                     [21775341]|.  Measurement of kinetic parameters for both 
                     reactions showed that the rate-limiting step for 
                     posttransfer editing is product release, and that its 
                     active site functions by kinetic partitioning between 
                     hydrolysis and dissociation of misacylated tRNA |CITS: 
                     [22648413]|.
                     LeuRS shows high initial substrate discrimination and 
                     appears to correct mistakes by posttransfer editing alone 
                     |CITS: [3534789]|.  LeuRS editing defects are lethal to the
                     cell |CITS: [16956879]|.  The CP1 (connective polypeptide 
                     1) domain, which splits the nucleotide binding fold |CITS: 
                     [3306924]|, is required for the posttransfer editing 
                     function |CITS: [10828991][11331000][12186547]|. The Thr251
                     residue is a critical specificity determinant |CITS: 
                     [14705941][15110746]|, and two conserved Thr residues 
                     within CP1 play a central role in amino acid editing, 
                     possibly by stabilizing the transition state |CITS: 
                     [16300391]|.  The conserved Arg249 residue confers specific
                     amino acid substrate recognition |CITS: [17311409]|.  The 
                     &beta;-strands as well as the short flexible ""hinges"" 
                     connecting the CP1 domain to the aminoacylation core are 
                     required for editing of mischarged tRNA<sup>Leu</sup> 
                     |CITS: [17474713]|.  The effect of point mutations in the 
                     &beta;-strands on enzyme activity support the hypothesis 
                     that the flexibility and orientation of the &beta;-strands 
                     is essential for function |CITS: [18363380]|.  Mutations in
                     both hinge regions allow mischarged tRNAs to bypass the 
                     posttransfer editing site |CITS: [19258309][19796639]|.
                     The mechanistic details of posttransfer editing have been 
                     elucidated by a series of co-crystal structures of LeuRS 
                     with its substrate in the editing and aminoacylation 
                     conformations.  Translocation of the 3' end of tRNAs from 
                     the aminoacylation to the editing site involves correlated 
                     rotations of four flexibly linked LeuRS domains |CITS: 
                     [22683997]|.  Crystal structures of the editing domain in 
                     complex with non-cognate amino acids showed that amino acid
                     discrimination is based on a lock-and-key mechanism |CITS: 
                     [16277600]|.
                     A pretransfer editing activity can be unmasked by secondary
                     mutations within the CP1 domain |CITS: [16505383]| or by 
                     specifically inhibiting the posttransfer editing function 
                     |CITS: [19940155]|.  Deletion of the entire CP1 domain 
                     results in loss of posttransfer editing and unmasks a 
                     tRNA-dependent editing activity that resides in the 
                     aminoacylation active site |CITS: [19020078]|. Deletion of 
                     the CP2 domain (residues 194-225) leads to loss of leucine 
                     activation, aminoacylation and posttransfer editing 
                     activities of LeuRS |CITS: [18955487]|.
                     In contrast to results with the yeast enzyme, deletion of 
                     the C-terminal domain of <i>E. coli</i> LeuRS nearly 
                     abolishes aminoacylation and editing activity |CITS: 
                     [16774921]|.  The length of the flexible peptide linker 
                     which connects the C-terminal domain to the main body of 
                     the enzyme is critical especially for the aminoacylation 
                     activitiy of the enzyme |CITS: [18155724]|.  The Leu570 
                     residue is important for both amino acid discrimination and
                     tRNA binding |CITS: [17378584]|.  The ""leucine-specific 
                     domain"" (amino acids 572-617) immediately upstream of the 
                     catalytic site is required for aminoacylation activity, but
                     not post-transfer editing |CITS: [17407263]|.
                     <i>leuS</i> is an essential gene |CITS: [16738554]|. 
                     Strains containing an editing-defective mutant allele show 
                     a growth defect when grown on medium containing isoleucine,
                     methionine, valine, or various non-standard amino acids 
                     |CITS: [17890314]|.
                     There is a general discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [37224536]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [14665676][19057010]|."
