     gene            675668..677119
                     /gene="djlC"
                     /locus_tag="b0649"
                     /label=djlC
     CDS             675668..677119
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="djlC"
                     /locus_tag="b0649"
                     /product="co-chaperone DjlC"
                     /label=djlC
                     /note="DjlC, also known as Hsc56 (<u>h</u>eat <u>s</u>hock 
                     <u>c</u>ognate protein with a molecular weight of 56,000), 
                     is a <u>D</u>na<u>J</u>-<u>l</u>ike protein that stimulates
                     the ATPase activity of the chaperone |FRAME: G6357-MONOMER 
                     ""HscC""|.
                     Purified DjlC stimulates the ATPase activity of HscC 
                     (Hsc62) <i>in vitro</i>; DjlC exhibits specificity towards 
                     HscC - it does not activate the ATPase activity of |FRAME: 
                     EG10241-MONOMER ""DnaK""| or |FRAME: EG12130 ""HscA""| 
                     (Hsc66) |CITS: [12054669][12183460]|. The nucleotide 
                     exchange factor |FRAME: CPLX0-8192 ""GrpE""| may |CITS: 
                     [12054669]| or may not |CITS: [12183460]| enhance the 
                     ATPase activity of the DjlC-HscC system.
                     DjlC contains a J-domain sequence (characteristic of DnaJ 
                     homologues) and a C-terminal transmembrane domain |CITS: 
                     [10574456][12054669][12183460]|. DjlC belongs to the 
                     heterogeneous group of C-tail-anchored membrane proteins 
                     (TAMPs) |CITS: [20406421]|. The C-terminal transmembrane 
                     domain is able to target a soluble reporter protein to the 
                     inner membrane where it is inserted in the expected C-out 
                     N-in orientation; mislocalization of the reporter protein 
                     occurs in strains that are lacking or depleted in SRP, 
                     FtsY, DnaK or YidC but not in strains lacking trigger 
                     factor or YajC; the transmembrane domain of the reporter 
                     protein cross-links to Ffh, trigger factor, YidC and YajC 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [29246766]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>djlB</i> &Delta;<i>djlC</i> double knockout 
                     strain shows increased sensitivity to Cd<sup>2+</sup> 
                     (600uM) compared to wild type |CITS: [12183460]|.
                     DjlC is predicted to be a bitopic inner membrane protein 
                     |CITS: [27510400]|.
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [16952052][17919282]|
                     "
                     /translation="MKTCWQILEIESTTQIDIIRQAYLARLPLCHPETDPQGFKALRQA
