     gene            complement(684799..686337)
                     /gene="lnt"
                     /locus_tag="b0657"
                     /label=lnt
     CDS             complement(684799..686337)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lnt"
                     /locus_tag="b0657"
                     /product="apolipoprotein N-acyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.269"
                     /label=lnt
                     /note="Apolipoprotein N-acyltransferase catalyses the last
                     step in lipoprotein maturation: the transfer of a fatty 
                     acid from phospholipid to the amino terminus of a 
                     diacylglycerol prolipoprotein |CITS: [2032623]|. Acylation 
                     is believed to involve formation of a thioester 
                     intermediate between an acyl group from 
                     <i>sn</i>-1-glycerolphospholipid and Cys387 of Lnt; <i>in 
                     vivo</i> the bulk of Lnt exists as a C387-acyl enzyme 
                     intermediate |CITS: [20000742]|. Purified Lnt exists mainly
                     in the non-acylated form; purified Lnt attacks the 
                     <i>sn</i>-1-acyl chain of a phosphoglyceride, becomes 
                     acylated and acylates Apo-Lpp (pro-|FRAME: EG10544-MONOMER 
                     ""murein lipoprotein""|) or a synthetic diacylglycerol 
                     lipopetide <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [21676878]| 
                     Aminoacylation of lipoproteins bound for the outer membrane
                     is required for proper localization of these lipoproteins 
                     via the Lol pathway |CITS: [12198129]|.
                     The enzyme can utilize the phospholipids 
                     phosphatidylethanolamine (PE), phosphatidylglycerol (PG), 
                     or cardiolipin in vitro |CITS: [2032623]|.  A strain (<i>E.
                     coli</i> AH930 <i>pss::kan</i>) lacking |FRAME: 
                     PHOSPHASERSYN-MONOMER| retains apolipoprotein 
                     N-acyltransferase activity, indicating that the enzyme is 
                     not specific for a phosphatidylethanolamine donor <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [2033085]|. PE is the most efficient acyl 
                     donor <i>in vitro</i>; the enzyme prefers unbranched acyl 
                     chains in position <i>sn</i>-1 plus unsaturated chains in 
                     position <i>sn</i>-2; the length of the acyl chain is less 
                     important |CITS: [21676878]|
                     Apolipoprotein N-acyltransferase localizes to inner 
                     membrane or inner-plus-outer membrane fractions |CITS: 
                     [2032623]|.
                     <i>lnt</i> is essential |CITS: [15513925]|. Lnt-depletion 
                     results in major alterations to cell envelope structure; a 
                     contributing factor to this disruption is the retention of 
                     peptidoglycan cross-linked apoLpp in the inner membrane 
                     |CITS: [15513925]|.
                     Lnt is a member of the nitrilase superfamily |CITS: 
                     [11380987]|. Lnt contains a large periplasmic 
                     carbon-nitrogen (C-N) lyase domain with a conserved 
                     Lys335-Cys387-Glu267 catalytic triad; the C-N domain is 
                     anchored to the membrane by a number of transmembrane 
                     helices |CITS: [15513925][17416655]|. Lnt crystalizes as a 
                     monomer; Lnt contains an 8-helix transmembrane domain (MD) 
                     capped by a globular nitrilase-like catalytic domain (ND) 
                     on the periplasmic side; the structures identify a putative
                     portal into and out of the active site pocket and are 
                     consistent with the proposed ping-pong reaction mechanism 
                     |CITS: [28675161][28885614]| (see also |CITS: [28698362]|).
                     The <i>cutE355</i> mutant has a copper-sensitive, 
                     temperature sensitive phenotype (see |CITS: [1938881]|). 
                     CutE contains a short region of amino acids that show 
                     homology to a putative copper-binding motif |CITS: 
                     [1938881]|.
                     <i>lnt</i> functionally complements the heat sensitivity, 
                     copper sensitivity, and apolipoprotein N-acyltransferase 
                     defect of a <i>Salmonella typhimurium</i> SE5312 mutant 
                     |CITS: [8344936]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [7651187]|"
