     gene            complement(687330..687797)
                     /gene="ybeY"
                     /locus_tag="b0659"
                     /label=ybeY
     CDS             complement(687330..687797)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ybeY"
                     /locus_tag="b0659"
                     /product="endoribonuclease YbeY"
                     /label=ybeY
                     /note="YbeY is a metal-dependent single strand-specific 
                     endoribonuclease that is involved in maturation of 16S rRNA
                     |CITS: [23273979]| and in transcription antitermination at 
                     an rRNA promoter |CITS: [23638028]|. RNase R and YbeY 
                     together (but not alone) are able to completely degrade 
                     defective 70S ribosomes generated in a <i>ybeY</i> mutant 
                     |CITS: [23273979]|.
                     In separate reports, YbeY did not appear to be involved in 
                     3' end processing of 16S rRNA; any one of the four 
                     3'->5'-exoribonucleases RNase II, RNase R RNase PH, and 
                     PNPase are able to perform this function at varying 
                     efficiencies |CITS: [23532845][29660326]|.
                     Under high temperature growth conditions, YbeY appears to 
                     be essential for translation. A <i>ybeY</i> null mutant 
                     accumulates ribosomal subunits and shows a low level of 
                     polysomes |CITS: [19181801]|. <i>In vitro</i>, ribosomes 
                     prepared from a <i>ybeY</i> deletion mutant show lower 
                     activity than wild-type due to a defect in the 30S subunit.
                      YbeY may function during ribosome biogenesis; assembled 
                     ribosomes isolated from a <i>ybeY</i> deletion mutant can 
                     not be rescued by addition of purified YbeY |CITS: 
                     [20639334]|. YbeY appears to be involved in the degradation
                     of rRNA during ""apoptosis-like death"" induced by high 
                     levels of nalidixic acid |CITS: [25028428]|.
                     Proteins that interact directly with YbeY include ribosomal
                     protein |FRAME: EG10910-MONOMER S11|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10270-MONOMER Era|, |FRAME: G7319-MONOMER Der|, |FRAME: 
                     G6363-MONOMER YbeZ|, and |FRAME: SPOT-MONOMER SpoT|. The 
                     binding interface between YbeY and S11 has been defined by 
                     mutagenesis; although it differs from the RNase active 
                     site, it is required for rRNA maturation and stress 
                     regulation |CITS: [27834201]|.
                     YbeY shows similarity to metal-dependent hydrolases. A 
                     crystal structure of YbeY has been solved at 2.7 &Aring; 
                     resolution, and together with fluorescence analysis it 
                     shows that YbeY binds a nickel ion coordinated by histidine
                     residues |CITS: [16511207]|. The R59 and H114 residues are 
                     important for <i>in vivo</i> function and <i>in vitro</i> 
                     RNase activity of YbeY |CITS: [20807199][23273979]|.
                     A <i>ybeY</i> null mutant is temperature sensitive and 
                     dramatically decreases the ability to recover from 
                     incubation at 51&deg;C |CITS: [19181801]|. The growth 
                     defect can be partially complemented by overexpression of 
                     several Nus factors |CITS: [23638028]| and the ribosomal 
                     maturation GTPase |FRAME: EG10270-MONOMER Era| |CITS: 
                     [29914987]| and is complemented by orthologs from several 
                     evolutionarily distant bacteria |CITS: [24901994]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>ybeY</i> mutant has pleiotropic defects in 
                     ribosome activity, translational fidelity and ribosome 
                     assembly |CITS: [20807199]|, shows increased resistance to 
                     hydroxyurea and has altered small RNA-mediated gene 
                     expression |CITS: [24511998]|. The Era GTPase is capable of
                     suppressing the slow growth, 16S rRNA processing and 
                     ribosome assembly defect but not the temperature 
                     sensitivity and 16S rRNA stability phenotypes |CITS: 
                     [35663862]|. <i>ybeY</i> is part of the heat shock factor, 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup>, regulon; its expression is induced by
                     heat shock |CITS: [16818608]|.
                     Unlike in <i>E. coli</i>, <i>ybeY</i> is essential in many 
                     other bacteria and is involved in pathogenesis |CITS: 
                     [24901994]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [19379808][23395272][35787606]|"
