                     /label=umpH
     CDS             complement(695030..695782)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="umpH"
                     /locus_tag="b0675"
                     /product="UMP phosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.3.5"
                     /label=umpH
                     /note="UmpH is a ribonucleoside tri-, di-, and
                     monophosphatase with a preference for purines |CITS: 
                     [16430214]|.  The enzyme was found to degrade ""overflow"" 
                     UMP nucleotides and is required for optimal growth in 
                     response to environmental pyrimidine intermediates |CITS: 
                     [23903661]|.  UMP accumulation to levels above the 
                     K<sub>m</sub> of the enzyme, triggered by environmental 
                     conditions or artificially induced by mutations in the 
                     normal feedback regulation of the pyrimidine biosynthesis 
                     pathway, appear to cause the overflow degradation of UMP by
                     UmpH |CITS: [23903661]|.
                     Though UmpH is a member of the <i>nag</i> 
                     N-acetylglucosamine utilization operon, it is a fairly 
                     general ribonucleotide monophosphatase |CITS: 
                     [2190615][16430214][16990279]|.  A type IIA member of the 
                     HAD protein superfamily, UmpH shows peak activity with UMP,
                     but is also a very effective phosphatase with AMP, GMP and 
                     CMP
                     |CITS: [7966317][16430214]|.
                     The structure of UmpH has been solved to 1.8 &Aring; 
                     resolution |CITS: [16430214]|.
                     A <i>umpH</i> null mutant shows somewhat impaired growth 
                     upon addition of orotate, an intermediate in the |FRAME: 
                     PWY0-162 ""pyrimidine biosynthesis pathway""|, to the 
                     growth medium.  However, growth recovers, and the final 
                     culture density is higher than for wild type cells |CITS: 
                     [23903661]|.  Overexpression of <i>umpH</i> induces a 
                     stress response, including increased expression of 
                     <i>rpoH</i>, and enables improved expression of certain 
                     recombinant membrane proteins |CITS: [22609824]|.
                     UmpH: ""<b>UMP</b> degradation"" |CITS: [23903661]| 
                     Comment: |CITS: [23903659]|"
