     gene            complement(695830..697050)
                     /gene="nagC"
                     /locus_tag="b0676"
                     /label=nagC
     CDS             complement(695830..697050)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nagC"
                     /locus_tag="b0676"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA-binding transcriptional dual
                     regulator NagC / NagC-N-acetyl-D-glucosamine 6-phosphate"
                     /label=nagC
                     /note="The NagC, ""<b><i>N</i></b>-<b>a</b>cetyl<b>g</b>lu 
                     <b>c</b>osamine,"" transcriptional dual regulator 
                     participates in regulating the phosphotransferase system 
                     (PTS) |CITS:[18067539]|. Its function is to coordinate the 
                     biosynthesis of the amino sugars, D-glucosamine (GlcN) and 
                     <i>N</i>-acetylglucosamine (GlcNAc) with their catabolism 
                     |CITS: [1766379][11361067][11139621][15066032]|.
                     The specific inducer for NagC is GlcNAc-6-P, the product of
                     GlcNAc transport by the PTS |CITS:[1766379][11361067]|. 
                     NagC is displaced from its DNA targets by interacting with 
                     GlcNAc-6-P |CITS:[1766379]|.  Mutation in the first two 
                     amino acids of the recognition helix of the DNA-binding 
                     motif causes GlcNAc6P to act with NagC as a corepressor 
                     instead of as an inducer |CITS: [31107208]|. Proline at the
                     first position of this helix seems to contribute to the 
                     distinction between the NagC binding sites and suboptimal 
                     sites |CITS: [31107208]|.
                     Based on the structure of |FRAME: PD01896|, models for the 
                     three-dimensional structure of NagC and for the binding of 
                     GlcNAc-6-P were developed |CITS:[18067539]|. This protein 
                     has a helix-turn-helix motif in its N-terminal part |CITS: 
                     [18067539]|.
                     The Nag regulon consists of two divergent operons, 
                     <i>nagE</i> and <i>nagBACD</i>. <i>nagC</i> encodes the 
                     repressor of this regulon. Its localization within the 
                     inducible <i>nagBACD</i> operon is unusual, since most 
                     transcriptional dual regulators are located near the loci 
                     they control |CITS:[1766379]|.
                     The <i>nagC</i> gene is upregulated in response to 
                     ampicillin treatment |CITS:[35196813]|.
                     NagC is present at low levels within the bacterial cell 
                     |CITS:[1766379]|.
                     There appears to be a genetic interaction between NagC and 
                     the PriA/PriB pathway, the pathway that is necessary for 
                     DNA replication restart |CITS: [36326440]|.
                     The consensus sequence for the NagC-binding site has been 
                     determined via combination of native site comparisons, 
                     operator mutagenesis, and selection of DNA-binding 
                     fragments, |CITS:[11139621]|. The dual function of NagC 
                     requires it to bind simultaneously to two operators in 
                     order to form a DNA loop |CITS:[11361067]|. The strictly 
                     conserved TT/AA motif, located in both ends of the center 
                     of symmetry, is probably the major recognition point for 
                     NagC |CITS:[11139621]|.
                     NagC is a member of the ROK (<b>r</b>epressor, <b>O</b>RFs,
                     <b>k</b>inases) (NagC/XylR) family of proteins, which 
                     contains at least two distinct classes of proteins, xylose 
                     repressor (XylR) and a series of glucose/fructose kinases 
                     |CITS:[7952186][12374829 ]|.
                     NagC is similar to Mlc transcriptional dual regulator 
                     |CITS:[15916829]|. However, they have different 
                     specificities for select binding sites |CITS: [11139621]|. 
                     Mutations in <i>nagC</i> have been observed in tolerant 
                     cells to the chemicals glutarate, adipate, putrescine, and 
                     hexamethylenediamine |CITS:[36738854]|. The Keio collection
                     <i>nagC</i> mutant exhibits a 'low level' of resistance to 
                     lithium exposure |CITS: [39983623]|.
                     The NagC binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
