     gene            complement(698267..699067)
                     /gene="nagB"
                     /locus_tag="b0678"
                     /label=nagB
     CDS             complement(698267..699067)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nagB"
                     /locus_tag="b0678"
                     /product="glucosamine-6-phosphate deaminase"
                     /EC_number="3.5.99.6"
                     /label=nagB
                     /note="Glucosamine-6-phosphate deaminase (NagB) catalyzes
                     the second cytoplasmic reaction in the metabolism of 
                     <i>N</i>-acetyl-D-glucosamine.  The deamination of 
                     glucosamine-6-phosphate generates ammonia and 
                     fructose-6-phosphate, which can enter glycolysis directly. 
                     Both <i>N</i>-acetylglucosamine and glucosamine can serve 
                     as the sole source of carbon for <i>E. coli</i>. 
                     Utilization of several amino sugars as carbon sources 
                     converges on NagB; additionally, NagB activity needs to be 
                     regulated to avoid a futile cycle with |FRAME: 
                     L-GLN-FRUCT-6-P-AMINOTRANS-MONOMER GlmS|.  It is therefore 
                     not surprising that a number of regulators have been 
                     identified to date.  The first to be identified was the 
                     compound <i>N</i>-acetyl-D-glucosamine-6-phosphate 
                     (GlcNAc6P), which allosterically activates NagB |CITS: 
                     [4861885][1734962]|.  Both substrate binding and the 
                     allosteric transition are entropy-driven processes |CITS: 
                     [11594728]|.  However, the growth rate on glucosamine is 
                     limited by the supply of substrate to NagB rather than the 
                     absence of GlcNAc6P |CITS: [19700525]|.  Replacement of 
                     NagB with an orthologous enzyme that is not allosterically 
                     regulated has surprisingly little effect on the growth rate
                     or fitness of <i>E. coli</i>; therefore, the physiological 
                     role of allosteric regulation of NagB activity is still 
                     unclear |CITS: [27002132]|.
                     Recently, several enzymes of central metabolism were found 
                     to regulate NagB activity.  Unphosphorylated |FRAME: 
                     PTSH-MONOMER| is an allosteric activator of NagB.  The 
                     specific amino acid residues involved in the interaction 
                     have been modeled |CITS: [28634232]|.  Additional 
                     allosteric activators are |FRAME: NANE-MONOMER NanE| and 
                     |FRAME: URIDYLYL-PII|.  The HPr+U-PII and HPr+NanE pairs 
                     appear to have synergistic effects on NagB, and modeling of
                     their binding sites suggest that they can interact with 
                     NagB simultaneously |CITS: [29229699]|.
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme have been solved |CITS: 
                     [8747459][10378272][11752775]|.  The enzyme is a trimer of 
                     dimers that are linked by an interchain disulfide bridge 
                     |CITS: [8240271]|.
                     Extensive site-directed mutagenesis and analysis of the 
                     mutant enzymes has allowed identification of residues that 
                     play roles in catalysis, coupling binding in the active 
                     site to the allosteric pocket, and binding of the 
                     allosteric effector 
                     <i>N</i>-acetyl-D-glucosamine-6-phosphate |CITS: [1734962] 
                     [7742311][9601045][11513596][11513596][12051945][14678787][
                     16168949][15667206]|. A fluorescently labelled derivative 
                     allowed monitoring of the allosteric transition of the 
                     enzyme |CITS: [16285712]|.  Nanoencapsulation, which keeps 
                     NagB in the T quaternary conformation, was used to study 
                     the allosteric activation process.  The results are 
                     consistent with the Tertiary Two-States model |CITS: 
                     [24787711]|.
                     <i>nagB</i> mutants are unable to grow on 
                     <i>N</i>-acetylglucosamine as the sole source of carbon and
                     energy |CITS: [4866432][4591481][4563983]|.  Several point 
                     mutants that affect 
                     <i>N</i>-acetyl-D-glucosamine-6-phosphate binding, the 
                     active site, and the active site lid <i>in vitro</i> were 
                     tested for their effect on the growth rate with glucosamine
                     or <i>N</i>-acetylglucosamine as the source of carbon and 
                     energy <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [15838023]|. Overexpression of
                     <i>nagB</i> can suppress the defect of a <i>glmS</i> 
                     mutant, showing that glucosamine-6-phosphate deaminase can 
                     act in the biosynthetic direction, supplying 
                     glucosamine-6-phosphate for the synthesis of UDP-GlcNAc. To
                     avoid a futile cycle, proper regulation of both enzymes is 
                     thus essential |CITS: [2687246]|, and it has been 
                     hypothesized that allosteric activation of NagB by 
                     <i>N</i>-acetyl-D-glucosamine-6-phosphate plays this role 
                     |CITS: [19700525]|.
                     NagB: ""<b><i>N</i></b>-<b>a</b>cetyl<b>g</b>lucosamine"" 
