     gene            701549..703213
                     /gene="glnS"
                     /locus_tag="b0680"
                     /label=glnS
                     /note="The  glnS  gene expression could be  repressed  in
                     a direct or indirect way by the OmpR protein. Since, using 
                     gene arrray system,  a repression of this gene was observed
                     as a result of  an up mutation in the ompR gene |CITS: 
                     [14523117]|."
     CDS             701549..703213
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glnS"
                     /locus_tag="b0680"
                     /product="glutamine--tRNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.1.1.18"
                     /label=glnS
                     /note="Glutamine-tRNA ligase (GlnRS) is a member of the
                     family of aminoacyl tRNA synthetases, which interpret the 
                     genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their 
                     specific tRNA molecules.  The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis.  GlnRS belongs to the Class I aminoacyl tRNA 
                     synthetases |CITS: [2203971][7647112]|; apart from sequence
                     motifs within the active site, the different enzymes show 
                     little similarity in their primary amino acid sequences.
                     Substrate recognition and discrimination by GlnRS has been 
                     studied extensively.  Binding of tRNA and ATP to GlnRS
                     is cooperative, and transfer of the aminoacyl adenylate to 
                     the tRNA is the rate determining step |CITS: [7659511]|. 
                     Interactions between the tRNA identity nucleotides and 
                     GlnRS modulate the affinity of the enzyme for glutamine 
                     |CITS: [8692925]|, and the terminal adenosine base of 
                     tRNA<sup>Gln</sup> mediates amino acid recognition
                     |CITS: [9657697]|.  tRNA binding to GlnRS results in 
                     conformational changes in the active site |CITS: 
                     [12737824]|;
                     induced-fit changes in the active site structure by both 
                     amino acid and tRNA binding may contribute to enzyme 
                     specificity |CITS: [15845537]|.  Reports differ on whether 
                     the properties of the amino acid binding site allow the 
                     enzyme
                     to specifically recognize glutamine and select against 
                     glutamate |CITS: [9562563]| or function as  a negative 
                     determinant, binding glutamate in a non-productive 
                     orientation |CITS: [12691748]|.  The glutaminyl adenylate 
                     intermediate is hydrolyzed by the GlnRS-tRNA<sup>Gln</sup> 
                     complex and is analogous to pre-transfer editing
                     reactions catalyzed by editing aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases 
                     like IleRS |CITS: [15845536]|.  A substrate-assisted 
                     catalytic mechanism is supported by single-turnover kinetic
                     analysis |CITS: [16734422]|.  Molecular dynamics 
                     simulations of tRNA binding to GlnRS have been performed 
                     |CITS: [17028132]|.
                     Various crystal structures of GlnRS have been solved |CITS:
                     [2479982][1857417][8011621][8364025][8942633][10881 
                     199][12691748][12737824]|.
                     GlnRS mutants that allow recognition and mischarging of 
                     <i>su<sup>+</sup>3</i> tRNA<sup>Tyr</sup> and others
                     have been identified |CITS: [6382258][2464170][7680483][75 
                     06418][8027995][8555233][8601833][8617245][8917315]|,
                     and their relaxed specificity has been interpreted in light
                     of their location within the crystal structure |CITS: 
                     [2686030][2194108][8942633]|.  Overexpression of wild type 
                     GlnRS also leads to mischarging of <i>su<sup>+</sup>3</i> 
                     tRNA<sup>Tyr</sup> |CITS: [6389180]|; concomitant 
                     overexpression of the cognate tRNA<sup>Gln2</sup> abolishes
                     this effect, indicating that proper balance between the 
                     tRNA and the
                     aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase is critical |CITS: [3144042]|.
                     A ""minimal"" GlnRS enzyme retains catalytic activity 
                     |CITS: [7505222]|.
                     Specificity determinants within tRNA that are important for
                     recognition by GlnRS have been identified; see, for 
                     example,
                     |CITS: [1565639][1396597][8199240][8199248][10860750]| and 
                     references therein.
                     GlnRS enzyme levels increase with increasing growth rate 
                     |CITS: [2578447]|.  GlnRS expression is regulated at the 
                     transcriptional as well as posttranscriptional levels 
                     |CITS: [2578447][2186926][1681509]|.
                     There is a general discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [37224536]|.
                     The tRNA<sup>Gln</sup>/GlnRS pair has been engineered for 
                     incorporation of unnatural amino acids into proteins |CITS:
                     [9294168][9331409]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [9372179][9078373][8643392][8199243][1725262]|."
