     gene            708443..708988
                     /gene="seqA"
                     /locus_tag="b0687"
                     /label=seqA
     CDS             708443..708988
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="seqA"
                     /locus_tag="b0687"
                     /product="negative modulator of initiation of replication"
                     /label=seqA
                     /note="The SeqA protein is a negative modulator of the 
                     initiation of chromosome replication and is involved in 
                     sequestration of |FRAME: G0-10506 <i>oriC</i>|, ensuring a 
                     single round of chromosome replication per cell cycle 
                     |CITS: [8011018][7891562][8901558]|. SeqA binds 
                     hemimethylated GATC sequences in the wake of the advancing 
                     replication fork |CITS: [7664748][15612935][22373925]| and 
                     inhibits open complex formation |CITS: [10469666]|, 
                     delaying reinitiation at the hemimethylated <i>oriC</i> 
                     until it is fully methylated by |FRAME: EG10204-MONOMER 
                     Dam| |CITS: [11080169]|. Regulation of replication by SeqA 
                     is important for survival of replication fork damage |CITS:
                     [16556234]|.  Colocalization of sister origins in rapidly 
                     growing cells |CITS: [17914458]| and cell cycle arrest 
                     during the stringent response |CITS: [19079575]| are 
                     dependent on SeqA.
                     <i>In vitro</i>, SeqA inhibits both the formation of the 
                     pre-priming complex as well as replication from an 
                     established pre-priming complex and alters dependence on 
                     the concentration of DnaA |CITS: [9670030]|. <i>In 
                     vivo</i>, SeqA blocks binding of DnaA to its lower-affinity
                     sites near <i>oriC</i>, but not to the high-affinity sites 
                     |CITS: [17114060]|. The ability of SeqA dimers to form 
                     higher-order complexes is required for origin sequestration
                     |CITS: [15933720][16236133]|. There is disagreement over 
                     whether |CITS: [15186411]| or not |CITS: [16999830]| SeqA 
                     foci represent the location of replication forks. SeqA foci
                     localize to the midcell in newborn cells; after the FtsZ 
                     ring is formed, the SeqA foci migrate towards the cell 
                     quarter sites |CITS: 
                     [9660922][10209749][10886362][11401691]|. MukB protein is 
                     required for correct SeqA localization |CITS: 
                     [9660922][10209749]|.
                     Multiple molecules of SeqA interact with fully methylated 
                     <i>oriC</i> sequences and with hemimethylated <i>oriC</i> 
                     and non-<i>oriC</i> sequences |CITS: [7553853]|. The DNA 
                     binding properties of SeqA have been extensively 
                     investigated |CITS: [10205183][10206949][11122375][1253506 
                     5][12824161][14704346][15151991][15767277]|.
                     SeqA binds specifically and cooperatively to two sites 
                     surrounding the DnaA binding site R1 in fully or 
                     hemimethylated <i>oriC</i> |CITS: [10931282][11254974]|. 
                     Binding is dependent on the alignment of the sites on the 
                     same face of the helix |CITS: [11080170]| and requires at 
                     least three base pairs flanking both sides of the 
                     G<sup>m</sup>ATC sequence |CITS: [12507506]|. 
                     Chromosome-wide binding of SeqA was investigated by 
                     ChIP-Chip |CITS: [20689753][20602746]|.
                     SeqA protein forms a homotetramer; the E9K mutant is 
                     defective in both tetramer formation and hemimethylated DNA
                     binding |CITS: [11457824]|. The N-terminal domain (residues
                     1-59) of SeqA is involved in self-association, while the 
                     C-terminal domain (residues 71-181) contains the specific 
                     DNA binding activity |CITS: [12507506]|. The hinge region 
                     between the two domains is required for aggregation and 
                     formation of SeqA foci |CITS: [17586464]|. Crystal 
                     structures of the C-terminal DNA binding domain in complex 
                     with normal and mismatched hemimethylated GATC sites have 
                     been determined |CITS: [12379844][14704346]|. A crystal 
                     structure of the oligomerization domain shows it to form 
                     dimers that assemble into a helical filament |CITS: 
                     [15933720]|. A crystal structure of a dimeric mutant form 
                     of SeqA in complex with DNA is available |CITS: 
                     [18540078][19304745]|, and a structural model has been 
                     proposed |CITS: [18849124]|. In an <i>aphA</i> null mutant,
                     the membrane contains 6-fold more SeqA protein than the 
                     parental strain |CITS: [9924983]|.
                     SeqA modulates superhelicity of the <i>E. coli</i> 
                     chromosome |CITS: [11263493][11254974][15458410]|. SeqA was
                     found to interact with the C-terminal domain of |FRAME: 
                     EG10686-MONOMER ParC|, a subunit of topoisomerase IV (topo 
                     IV); purified SeqA specifically stimulates topo IV 
                     activity, while excess SeqA inhibits all topoisomerases 
                     |CITS: [14512422]|. Genetic analysis suggests that SeqA 
                     stabilizes sister chromatid cohesion by antagonizing their 
                     topo IV-mediated resolution |CITS: [23990792]|.
                     SeqA stimulates transcription from the methylated or 
                     hemimethylated bacteriophage &lambda; p<sub>R</sub> 
                     promoter |CITS: [11442835][17005979]| and may cooperate 
                     with CII in stimulating transcription from p<sub>I</sub> 
                     and p<sub>aQ</sub> promoters |CITS: [12622820]|.
                     A <i>seqA</i> null mutant is viable, but newly replicated 
                     origins are not sequestered and the frequency of 
                     replication initiation is increased |CITS: 
                     [8011018][7891562]|, leading to a significantly shortened 
                     eclipse period |CITS: [12067334]|. In a <i>recBC</i>(Ts) 
                     background, <i>seqA</i> mutants display chromosomal 
                     fragmentation |CITS: [15531636][24806348]|. <i>seqA</i> 
                     mutants are sensitive to UV irradiation and HU |CITS: 
                     [16556234]|. A non-polar &Delta;<i>seqA</i> mutant shows 
                     synthetic lethality with &Delta;<i>recA</i> at 22&deg;C and
                     a high ratio of <i>ori</i> to <i>ter</i> sequences, 
                     indicating a number of incomplete replication cycles. Loss 
                     of function in LPS biosynthesis enzymes (<i>rfaQGP</i>) 
                     suppresses the <i>ori</i>/<i>ter</i> effects of <i>seqA</i>
                     mutation, but not the chromosomal fragmentation phenotype 
                     |CITS: [19432803]|. The <i>seqA2</i> and <i>seqA4</i> point
                     mutations cause defects in self-association of the SeqA 
                     protein |CITS: [12535065][16236133]|. A microarray analysis
                     of transcription patterns in wild type vs. a <i>seqA</i> 
                     mutant has been performed |CITS: [12682301]|. 
                     Overexpression of SeqA leads to an extended period of 
                     <i>oriC</i> hemimethylation and sequestration as well as 
                     other cell division phenotypes |CITS: [12514137]| and a 
                     mutator phenotype |CITS: [15225322]|. One of the mutations 
                     that suppresses the effect of an <i>hda</i> deletion, 
                     <i>hsm-1</i>, results in upregulated expression of 
                     <i>seqA</i> |CITS: [21299647]|.
                     An <i>in vitro</i> DNA replication cycle reaction system 
                     has been developed with 26 purified replication-associated 
                     <i>E. coli</i> proteins (|CITS: [29036468]|) and shown to 
                     accurately represent regulation of DNA replication 
                     initiation at <i>oriC</i> by SeqA and |FRAME: 
                     EG10204-MONOMER Dam| through consecutive replication cycles
                     |CITS: [39922869]|.
                     SeqA: ""<b>seq</b>uestration protein <b>A</b>""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [7850437][10972793][11092821][11269497][11 
                     263490][11254973][11401686][16753031][17459114][19254745][ 
                     20157337][21698131][24747048][38982189]|"
                     /translation="MKTIEVDDELYSYIASHTKHIGESASDILRRMLKFSAASQPAAPV
                     TKEVRVASPAIVEAKPVKTIKDKVRAMRELLLSDEYAEQKRAVNRFMLLLSTLYSLDAQ
                     AFAEATESLHGRTRVYFAADEQTLLKNGNQTKPKHVPGTPYWVITNTNTGRKCSMIEHI
                     MQSMQFPAELIEKVCGTI"
