     gene            709014..710654
                     /gene="pgm"
                     /locus_tag="b0688"
                     /label=pgm
     CDS             709014..710654
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pgm"
                     /locus_tag="b0688"
                     /product="phosphoglucomutase"
                     /EC_number="5.4.2.2"
                     /label=pgm
                     /note="Phosphoglucomutase is involved in the breakdown of 
                     glycogen and metabolism of galactose and maltose.  Pgm 
                     forms a complex with |FRAME: GLU6PDEHYDROG-MONOMER|, which 
                     uses the product of Pgm, |FRAME: GLC-1-P|, to produce 
                     |FRAME: D-6-P-GLUCONO-DELTA-LACTONE| |CITS: [16858726]|. 
                     Phosphoglucomutase was found to be a modulator of 
                     chromosomal supercoiling.  Its role in mediating chromosome
                     topology may be indirect |CITS: [16135230]|. <i>pgm</i> 
                     mutants are thought to accumulate maltodextrin due to 
                     reversal of the reaction catalyzed by |FRAME: 
                     MALDEXPHOSPHORYL-CPLX|, leading to the Blu phenotype (blue 
                     color when stained with iodine) |CITS: [4942754][8083177]|.
                      Amylose begins to accumulate when the <i>pgm</i> 
                     expression level is below 10% of wild type levels |CITS: 
                     [10191405]|.
                     In a genome-wide screen of single-gene knockout mutants of 
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 (the Keio collection), a <i>pgm</i> 
                     mutant was shown to be defective in swarming and swimming 
                     ability, as expected for the role of this enzyme in energy 
                     production |CITS: [17122336]|.  In another genome-wide 
                     screen of the Keio collection, <i>pgm</i> mutants displayed
                     a glycogen-less phenotype |CITS: [17543954]| as expected 
                     from the role of &alpha;-D-glucose 1-phosphate in glycogen 
                     metabolism (see pathways |FRAME: GLYCOCAT-PWY| and |FRAME: 
                     GLYCOGENSYNTH-PWY|).  A nonsense mutation (Y324*) 
                     suppresses the low-temperature vancomycin sensitivity of 
                     <i>E. coli</i> wild-type; the &Delta;<i>pgm</i> mutant from
                     the Keio collection also displays vancomycin resistance at 
                     15&deg;C |CITS: [26853624]|.
                     Using a method that distinguishes N-phosphorylation from 
                     O-phosphorylation, N-phosphorylation was detected <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [33277485][35636362]|."
