     gene            complement(720444..722492)
                     /gene="kdpB"
                     /locus_tag="b0697"
                     /label=kdpB
     CDS             complement(720444..722492)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="kdpB"
                     /locus_tag="b0697"
                     /product="trifunctional K(+) transporting P-type ATPase
                     subunit KdpB / K(+) transporting P-type ATPase / K(+) 
                     transporting ATPase"
                     /EC_number="7.2.2.6"
                     /label=kdpB
                     /note="KdpB is the largest subunit of a potassium ion
                     importing P-type ATPase; KdpB forms a phosphorylated 
                     intermediate as part of the transport cycle |CITS: 
                     [2522440]|. KdpB contains seven transmembrane (TM) helices 
                     and two large cytoplasmic domains; the first of these is 
                     located between TM 2 and 3 and is known as the actuator- or
                     A-domain; the second is located between TM 4 and 5 and 
                     consists of a phosphorylation- or P-domain and a 
                     nucleotide-binding- or N-domain (see |CITS: [17710642]| and
                     |CITS: [28636601]|).
                     Asp-307, within the conserved DKTGT motif in the P-domain 
                     is the site of transient phosphorylation |CITS: [1474895]|.
                     Two conserved charged residues, D583 and K586, located 
                     within TM 5, establish a dipole which has been implicated 
                     in energy coupling between the KdpB and KdpA subunits 
                     |CITS: [12763849][15938615][17994765]|. Coordination of ATP
                     occurs via the formation of a transient KdpB/KdpC/ATP 
                     complex |CITS: [21711450]|. In K<sup>+</sup> replete media 
                     phosphorylation of Ser-162 within the conserved TGES motif 
                     in the A-domain results in inhibition of ATPase activity 
                     and K<sup>+</sup> transport |CITS: 
                     [28636601][32955430][38622146]|. Cryo-EM structures of the 
                     KdpFABC complex suggest a translocation pathway for 
                     K<sup>+</sup> via KdpB and KdpA half-channels |CITS: 
                     [30478378]| (and further |CITS: [34429416]|)."
