                     /label=phr
     CDS             734963..736381
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="phr"
                     /locus_tag="b0708"
                     /product="deoxyribodipyrimidine photolyase"
                     /label=phr
                     /note="<i>phr</i> encodes the DNA repair enzyme, 
                     deoxyribodipyrimidine photolyase (DNA photolyase; 
                     photoreactivating enzyme or PRE). DNA photolyase mediates 
                     blue-light dependent repair of cyclobutane pyrimidine 
                     dimers (CPDs; most commonly Thy&lt;&gt;Thy) which are 
                     produced by exposure of cells to UV radiation at 200-300nm.
                     Early studies on photoreactivation (PR) were conducted 
                     using <i>E. coli</i> B strains (see for example |CITS: 
                     [13491816][4891197][5366015][4933486][4619712][765805]|). 
                     <i>phr</i> has been cloned |CITS: [369954][6314252]| and 
                     its protein product purified. The active, purified enzyme 
                     is a monomer and repairs UV-irradiated DNA (UV-pBR322) in a
                     light dependent reaction; the enzyme contains two 
                     prosthetic groups - a light harvesting cofactor or antenna 
                     pigment, 5,10-methenyltetrahydrofolate polyglutamate (see 
                     |FRAME: 5-10-METHENYL-THF-GLU-N|), and a catalytic flavin 
                     adenine dinucleotide (FAD) cofactor (active in the 
                     FADH<sup>-</sup> form <i>in vivo</i>) |CITS: 
                     [6314252][6325459][6363715][2895469][7604260]|.
                     Purified DNA photolyase binds to DNA containing cyclobutane
                     pyrimidine dimers |CITS: [3539939]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [3308872][1716150][12169694]|) and mediates repair by 
                     cleaving the cyclobutane ring; the repair reaction involves
                     a number of steps: absorption of a blue-light photon by the
                     antenna pigment; transfer of excitation energy to the 
                     flavin cofactor; electron transfer to the CPD and cleavage 
                     of the ring; electron transfer back to the flavin cofactor,
                     and dissociation of the enzyme from the DNA (see reviews by
                     |CITS: [8337263][8286340]| and references within). The 
                     complete mechanism of CPD photorepair including a critical 
                     cyclic electron-tunneling mechanism within the flavin 
                     cofactor has been resolved (see reviews by |CITS: 
                     [28802828][25830375][25870862]| and references within).
                     Purified Phr contains the flavin cofactor in a 
                     semi-reduced, catalytically inactive state (FADH&deg;) 
                     which can be reduced to the catalytically competent 
                     FADH<sup>-</sup> form in the presence of light and an 
                     external reductant; this process, known as flavin 
                     photoreduction or photoactivation relies on an 
                     intra-protein electron transfer mechanism involving a 
                     triple tryptophan (Trp-382, Trp-359, Try-306) electron 
                     ""nanowire"" (see |CITS: 
                     [12835419][16898706][17696363][20705454][19954157]|). Trp 
                     dependent photoreduction is not biologically relevant <i>in
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [15568802]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> DNA photolyase is a class I photolyase in 
                     the photolyase/cryptochrome (PHR/CRY) family (see |CITS: 
                     [27864885]|.
                     Additional selected reviews: |CITS: [3052275][8492239][157 
                     21603][18682397][26151545]|<BR>Comments: |CITS: 
                     [7604259][22106255]|<BR>Nobel lecture: |CITS: [27337655]|"
