     gene            738283..739026
                     /gene="ybgI"
                     /locus_tag="b0710"
                     /label=ybgI
     CDS             738283..739026
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ybgI"
                     /locus_tag="b0710"
                     /product="radiation resistance protein YbgI"
                     /label=ybgI
                     /note="YbgI is proposed to be a hydrolase-oxidase.  YbgI
                     has structural similarity to proteins with DNA-related 
                     functions |CITS: [14519207]|.  A recent study of the DUF34 
                     protein family suggests that the proteins may function as 
                     metal ion insertases, chaperones, or metallocofactor 
                     maturases rather than GTP cyclohydrolases |CITS: 
                     [34572495]|.
                     YbgI localizes to the cell poles |CITS: [29625546]|. 
                     Crystal structures of YbgI have been solved at 2.2 &Aring; 
                     resolution.  YbgI forms a toroidal complex comprising three
                     sets of homodimers, with metal ions (two per monomer) 
                     coordinated within the ring |CITS: [14519207]|. <i>ybgI</i>
                     insertion mutants were identified in a genetic screen for 
                     genes that are important for survival of exposure to 
                     ionizing radiation (IR).  A <i>ybgI</i> deletion mutant has
                     a substantial decrease in IR survival |CITS: [25049088]|, 
                     but does not appear to have increased sensitivity to UV 
                     damage; it is more sensitive to fosfomycin, carbenicillin, 
                     vancomycin, and amoxicillin |CITS: [29625546]|.  A 
                     proteomics experiment comparing wild type and the 
                     &Delta;<i>ybgI</i> mutant grown on M9 minimal medium showed
                     upregulation of TnaA and AphA and downregulation of YgiW, 
                     YgaU, Dps, and OsmY protein levels |CITS: [29625546]|."
