     gene            758470..759636
                     /gene="sucC"
                     /locus_tag="b0728"
                     /label=sucC
     CDS             758470..759636
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sucC"
                     /locus_tag="b0728"
                     /product="bifunctional succinyl-CoA synthetase subunit
                     beta / succinyl-CoA synthetase"
                     /EC_number="6.2.1.5"
                     /label=sucC
                     /note="SucC is the &beta; subunit of succinyl-CoA
                     synthetase.
                     Trp45 and His50 are required for activity |CITS: 
                     [2548486]|, while Cys47 |CITS: [2548486]| and Cys325 |CITS:
                     [2643599]| are not essential. Single mutations of 
                     tryptophan residues W43, W76 and W248 have little effect on
                     enzyme activity. W76 and W248 may be part of the CoA 
                     binding site |CITS: [2340278]|. Glu197 is essential for 
                     phosphorylation and dephosphorylation of the active site 
                     His246 in the &alpha; subunit |CITS: [11781092]|. SucC was 
                     identified as a putative phosphohistidine acceptor using 
                     chemoproteomics analysis with a stable analogue of 3-pHis 
                     |CITS: [40201162]|.
                     Based on photolabeling, site-directed mutagenesis, and 
                     crystal structures, the nucleotide binding site resides in 
                     the N-terminal ATP-grasp fold of the &beta; subunit |CITS: 
                     [10353839][10625475]|.
                     SucC levels are elevated at low pH |CITS: [12107143]|. The 
                     amount of SucC was increased in a proteomics study in a 
                     &Delta;|FRAME: CPLX0-245 Ahp| mutant |CITS: [37725887]|. 
                     Overexpression of <i>sucC</i> from an ASKA plasmid leads to
                     a more than 3-fold increase in the MIC of hypochlorous acid
                     |CITS: [34072091]|."
