     gene            769652..769765
                     /gene="cydX"
                     /locus_tag="b4515"
                     /label=cydX
     CDS             769652..769765
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cydX"
                     /locus_tag="b4515"
                     /product="bifunctional cytochrome bd-I accessory subunit
                     CydX / cytochrome bd-I ubiquinol:oxygen oxidoreductase"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.7"
                     /label=cydX
                     /note="CydX is small, noncatalytic, single transmembrane, 
                     accessory subunit of |FRAME: CYT-D-UBIOX-CPLX| |CITS: 
                     [23749980][24681096][31604309][31723136]|. A strain lacking
                     <i>cydX</i> is slow growing in aerobic liquid culture and 
                     on plates and has increased sensitivity to 
                     &beta;-mercaptoethanol; a wild-type phenotype can be 
                     restored when <i>cydX</i> is expressed from a plasmid 
                     |CITS: [23749980]|. Membrane extracts from a strain lacking
                     <i>cydX</i> display reduced levels of N N N' 
                     N'-tetramethyl-p-phenylenediamine (TMPD) oxidation |CITS: 
                     [23749980]|. Preparations of CydAB lacking CydX do not 
                     contain the di-heme centre (hemes <i>b<sub>595</sub></i> 
                     and <i>d</i>) suggesting that CydX may be involved in the 
                     assembly or stabilisation of the di-heme active centre 
                     |CITS: [24681096]| (further |CITS: [31604309]|).
                     The <i>cydX</i> open reading frame contains a predicted 
                     transmembrane segment, and the protein can be found in the 
                     membrane fraction |CITS: [19121005][21778229]|. Using a 
                     sucrose fractionation protocol CydX partitions into both 
                     the inner membrane and outer membrane fractions |CITS: 
                     [21778229]|. Topology assays using GFP and PhoA fusion 
                     proteins suggest that CydX is oriented with the C-terminus 
                     in the cytoplasm |CITS: [21778229]|. Translocation of CydX 
                     is impaired in both SecE depleted and YidC depleted <i>E. 
                     coli</i> strains |CITS: [21778229]|.
                     CydX is highly expressed during both exponential and 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [19121005]|. Expression of CydX is 
                     increased during oxygen-limited growth and under envelope 
                     stress induced by SDS/EDTA treatment |CITS: [19734316]|. 
                     CydX and |FRAME: MONOMER0-2817 ""AppX""| are paralogues and
                     may have overlapping functions within <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23749980]|. Analysis of intact protein complexes 
                     released directly from native membranes indicates that CydX
                     and AppX are able to interact simultaneously with CydAB to 
                     form a heterotetramer |CITS: [30442809]|."
                     /translation="MWYFAWILGTLLACSFGVITALALEHVESGKAGQEDI"
