     gene            771305..771733
                     /gene="tolR"
                     /locus_tag="b0738"
                     /label=tolR
     CDS             771305..771733
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tolR"
                     /locus_tag="b0738"
                     /product="trifunctional energy transducing Tol complex
                     motor protein TolR / energy transducing Tol complex / 
                     Tol-Pal Cell Envelope Complex"
                     /label=tolR
                     /note="TolR is an inner membrane component of the Tol-Pal
                     system - a group of interacting proteins which span the 
                     cell envelope in <i>E. coli</i> K-12. The Tol-Pal system 
                     plays a role in outer membrane invagination and septal 
                     peptidoglycan processing during cell division and is 
                     important for maintaining outer membrane integrity via 
                     lipid homeostasis. It also has a role in facilitating phage
                     infection and colicin uptake
                     TolR is an inner membrane protein with a single 
                     transmembrane domain; its large C-terminal region is 
                     located in the periplasm and the N-terminus is in the 
                     cytoplasm |CITS: [8331075][8376353]|. TolR interacts with 
                     TolQ and TolA; the transmembrane domain is important for 
                     this interaction |CITS: [7853390][10419942]|. TolR 
                     homodimers can be detected <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [10419942][11994151]| and homotrimers can be detected <i>in
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [10419942]|. TolR accumulates at the site 
                     of constriction in dividing cells; this localisation is 
                     dependent on |FRAME: EG11529-MONOMER ""FtsN""| |CITS: 
                     [17233825]|.
                     Mutations in <i>tolR</i> render (F-plasmid-containing) 
                     cells tolerant to the group A colicins and prevent 
                     infection by filamentous bacteriophage (reviewed by |CITS: 
                     [1683466]|). The central domain (residues 44-117) of TolR 
                     interacts with colicin A <i>in vivo</i> and with colicins A
                     and E3 <i>in vitro</i> but does not interact with colicin 
                     E1 nor B <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [11703658]| <i>tolR</i> and
                     |FRAME: EG10272 ""<i>exbD</i>""| have 49.7% nucleotide 
                     sequence homology |CITS: [2670903]|. TolR shows protein 
                     sequence similarity with |FRAME: 
                     MOTB-FLAGELLAR-MOTOR-STATOR-PROTEIN ""MotB""| |CITS: 
                     [11722743]|
                     The TolR C-terminal domain is conformationally responsive 
                     to proton motive force |CITS: [17442676]|.
                     <i>tol</i>: colicin <U>tol</U>erant |CITS: 
                     [4167587][4860908]|"
