     gene            771798..773063
                     /gene="tolA"
                     /locus_tag="b0739"
                     /label=tolA
     CDS             771798..773063
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tolA"
                     /locus_tag="b0739"
                     /product="trifunctional energy transducing Tol complex
                     protein TolA / energy transducing Tol complex / Tol-Pal 
                     Cell Envelope Complex"
                     /label=tolA
                     /note="TolA is an inner membrane component of the Tol-Pal
                     system - a group of interacting proteins which span the 
                     cell envelope of <i>E. coli</i> K-12. The Tol-Pal system 
                     plays a role in outer membrane invagination and septal 
                     peptidoglycan processing during cell division and is 
                     important for maintaining outer membrane integrity via 
                     lipid homeostasis. It also has a role in facilitating phage
                     infection and colicin uptake.
                     The TolA protein consists of 3 domains - an N-terminal 
                     domain (TolAI), a central domain (TolAII) that is rich in 
                     &alpha;-helical structure and a C terminal domain 
                     (TolAIII); the N-terminal domain anchors the protein to the
                     inner membrane with the remainder of the protein located in
                     the periplasm |CITS: [2687247][2068069]|.
                     TolA interacts with TolQ and TolR |CITS: [7744737]|. TolA 
                     interacts with the trimeric outer membrane porins, |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7534 ""OmpF""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-7533 ""OmpC""|, |FRAME:
                     CPLX0-7530 ""PhoE""| and |FRAME: CPLX0-7655 ""LamB""| <i>in
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [8978668]|. TolA interacts with the 
                     peptidogylcan associated outer membrane lipoprotein, Pal; 
                     this interaction requires the proton motive force |CITS: 
                     [11115123]|. TolQ and TolR are both necessary for the 
                     pmf-dependent interaction of TolA with Pal |CITS: 
                     [11722743]|. TolA interacts with TolB; disrupting this 
                     interaction results in cells that are hypersensitive to SDS
                     and tolerant to colicin A |CITS: [11994151][12142433]|. 
                     TolA interacts with CpoB |CITS: [11994151]|.
                     TolA accumulates at the site of constriction in dividing 
                     cells; this localisation requires |FRAME: EG11529-MONOMER 
                     FtsN| |CITS: [17233825]| (and further |CITS: [34748387]|). 
                     TolA is the energy-transducing protein of the TolQRA inner 
                     membrane complex which, together with TolB participates in 
                     a ""mobilisation and capture"" mechanism for accumulating 
                     Pal at division sites and promoting outer membrane 
                     invagination |CITS: [31153904][32161270]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [35240126]|).
                     Mutations in <i>tolA</i> render cells tolerant to the group
                     A colicins and prevent infection by filamentous 
                     bacteriophage |CITS: [9335297]|. TolA binds colicins A and 
                     E1 <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [2065649]|. TolA interacts with 
                     gene 3 protein (g3p) of filamentous bacteriophage |CITS: 
                     [10404600][12670988]|. The C-terminal domain of TolA 
                     (TolAIII) interacts with the N-terminal domain of colicin A
                     and the N-terminal domain of g3p |CITS: 
                     [22493500][15701516][16237036]|. TolA interacts with 
                     colicin N |CITS: [19306883]|. Colicin N and TolB bind TolA 
                     at the same site |CITS: [25536997]|. TolA, TolB and proton 
                     motive force are required for release of the immunity 
                     protein (Im9) from colicin E9 |CITS: [19458090]|. 
                     <i>tolA</i> mutants release periplasmic proteins into the 
                     extracellular medium |CITS: [7009581][3312962]|. A 
                     transposon insertion mutation of <i>tolA</i> 
                     (<i>tolA</i>::EZTN) results in a chaining phenotype (in LB 
                     medium lacking NaCl) and cells display abnormal cell 
                     morphology including localised expansion of the periplasm 
                     at the division site and outer membrane blebbing that is 
                     associated with sites of constriction or the cell poles 
                     |CITS: [17233825]|. &Delta;<i>tolA</i> mutants have a 
                     hypervesiculating phenotype; isolated vesicles from this 
                     strain have an altered morphology including a high 
                     proportion with multi-membranous structure |CITS: 
                     [33746922]|.
                     <i>tol</i>: colicin <U>tol</U>erant |CITS: 
                     [4167587][4860908]|<BR><i>lky</i>: <U>l</U>ea<U>ky</U> 
                     |CITS: [7009581]|<BR><i>excC</i>: <U>exc</U>retory |CITS: 
                     [3312962]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [23176500]|"
