     gene            773196..774488
                     /gene="tolB"
                     /locus_tag="b0740"
                     /label=tolB
     CDS             773196..774488
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tolB"
                     /locus_tag="b0740"
                     /product="bifunctional Tol-Pal system periplasmic protein
                     TolB / Tol-Pal Cell Envelope Complex"
                     /label=tolB
                     /note="TolB is a periplasmic component of the Tol-Pal
                     system - a group of interacting proteins which span the 
                     cell envelope of <i>E. coli</i> K-12. The Tol-Pal system 
                     plays a role in outer membrane invagination and septal 
                     peptidoglycan processing during cell division and is 
                     important for maintaining outer membrane integrity via 
                     lipid homeostasis. It also has a role in facilitating phage
                     infection and colicin uptake.
                     TolB is a periplasmic protein |CITS: [7929011]|; TolB 
                     interacts with the peptidoglycan associated lipoprotein, 
                     Pal <i>in vivo</I> |CITS: [7744736][11994151]|. TolB 
                     interacts with the major outer membrane lipoprotein, 
                     |FRAME: EG10544-MONOMER ""Lpp""| and with |FRAME: 
                     EG10669-MONOMER ""OmpA""| in a Pal-dependent manner |CITS: 
                     [9701827]|. Purified TolB interacts with the trimeric outer
                     membrane porins, |FRAME: CPLX0-7534 ""OmpF""|, |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7533 ""OmpC""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-7530 ""PhoE""| and 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-7655 ""LamB""| <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [9393690]|. TolB may form homodimers <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [11994151]|. TolB interacts with TolA; disrupting this 
                     interaction results in cells that are hypersensitive to SDS
                     and tolerant to colicin A |CITS: [11994151]|.
                     TolB accumulates at the site of constriction in dividing 
                     cells; this localisation is dependent on |FRAME: 
                     EG11529-MONOMER ""FtsN""| |CITS: [17233825]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [31153904]|).
                     TolB consists of two domains - an N-terminal domain with an
                     &alpha;&beta; structure and a large C-terminal domain that 
                     adopts a 6-bladed &beta; propeller structure |CITS: 
                     [10545334][10673426]|.
                     Colicin E9 binds to the propeller domain of TolB; colicin 
                     E9 competes with Pal for binding to TolB; colicin E9 can 
                     dislodge Pal from its complex with TolB but this requires 
                     Ca<sup>2+</sup> or Mg<sup>2+</sup> ions |CITS: 
                     [16894158][22310049]|. Colicin A and colicin E9 interact in
                     subtly different ways with TolB |CITS: [19627502]|. TolB, 
                     TolA and proton motive force are required for release of 
                     the immunity protein (Im9) from colicin E9 |CITS: 
                     [19458090]|
                     <i>tolB</i> mutants release periplasmic proteins into the 
                     extracellular medium |CITS: [7009581][3312962]|.
                     Targeting of TolB to the Sec-translocase for transport 
                     across the inner membrane is SecB-dependent 
                     |CITS:[16352602]|.
                     <i>tol</i>: colicin <U>tol</U>erant |CITS: 
                     [4167587][4860908]|<BR><i>lky</i>: <U>l</U>ea<U>ky</U> 
                     |CITS: [7009581]|
                     Comments: |CITS: [22381408]|"
