                     /label=pal
     CDS             774523..775044
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pal"
                     /locus_tag="b0741"
                     /product="bifunctional peptidoglycan-associated outer
                     membrane lipoprotein Pal / Tol-Pal Cell Envelope Complex"
                     /label=pal
                     /note="Pal is an outer membrane component of the Tol-Pal
                     system - a group of interacting proteins which span the 
                     cell envelope in <i>E. coli</i> K-12. The Tol-Pal system 
                     plays a role in outer membrane invagination and septal 
                     peptidoglycan processing during cell division and is 
                     important for maintaining outer membrane integrity via 
                     lipid homeostasis. It also has a role in facilitating phage
                     infection and colicin uptake.
                     Pal is a <U>p</U>eptidoglycan-<U>a</U>ssociated outer 
                     membrane <U>l</U>ipoprotein |CITS: 
                     [6788752][115860][1574003]|.
                     Pal interacts with TolA <i>in vivo</i>; this interaction 
                     requires the proton motive force |CITS: [11115123]|. Pal 
                     interacts with TolB <i>in vivo</I> |CITS: 
                     [7744736][9701827][11994151]|. Pal interacts with OmpA 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [9701827]|. Purified Pal binds 
                     peptidoglycan <i>in vitro</i>; the interaction of Pal with 
                     peptidoglycan and TolB may be exclusive - a TolB-Pal 
                     complex does not appear to be associated with the 
                     peptidoglycan |CITS: [10515919]|. Separate regions of Pal 
                     are implicated in binding TolA, TolB and OmpA; Pal still 
                     interacts with TolB and with OmpA after dissipation of the 
                     proton motive force |CITS: [14731286]|.
                     Pal accumulates at the site of constriction in dividing 
                     cells; this localization is dependent on |FRAME: 
                     EG11529-MONOMER ""FtsN""| |CITS: [17233825]|. Both the 
                     location and abundance of immunolabelled Pal changes 
                     throughout the cell divsion cycle |CITS: [39539348]|. 
                     Recruitment of a chromosomal, fluorescent Pal fusion 
                     protein to the constriction site requires all the Tol 
                     proteins and a functional TolQR motor |CITS: [31153904]|. 
                     Accumulation of Pal at the division site requires the 
                     TolQRA energy-transducing complex and TolB; the Tol system 
                     drives a ""mobilisation and capture"" mechanism for Pal 
                     accumulation at division sites |CITS: [32161270]|. Deletion
                     of <i>pal</i> results in a chaining phenotype (in LB medium
                     lacking NaCl) and cells display abnormal cell morphology 
                     including localised expansion of the periplasm at the 
                     division site and outer membrane blebbing that is 
                     associated with sites of constriction or the cell poles 
                     |CITS: [17233825]|.
                     <i>excC</i>: <U>exc</U>retory |CITS: [3312962]|
                     Review: |CITS: [19519769]|"
