     gene            775054..775845
                     /gene="cpoB"
                     /locus_tag="b0742"
                     /label=cpoB
     CDS             775054..775845
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cpoB"
                     /locus_tag="b0742"
                     /product="cell division coordinator CpoB"
                     /label=cpoB
                     /note="CpoB is a periplasmic protein that mediates
                     physical and functional coordination between the 
                     peptidoglycan (PG) synthase |FRAME: CPLX0-3951 
                     ""PBP1B-LpoB""| and the energy transducing Tol system; this
                     coordination is required to properly synchronize PG 
                     synthesis and outer membrane (OM) constriction during cell 
                     division. CpoB, PBP1B-LpoB and the Tol proteins are 
                     recruited to the septum at the later stages of cell 
                     division and form a higher order complex that spatially 
                     links PG synthesis and OM invagination |CITS: [25951518]|. 
                     CpoB interacts with TolA <i>in vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i>
                     |CITS: [11994151][20816983]|. CpoB forms stable trimers in 
                     solution |CITS: [20455268]|; TolA binding disrupts trimeric
                     CpoB to form CpoB-TolA heterodimers |CITS: [20816983]|
                     CpoB localizes to sites of envelope constriction in 
                     pre-divisional cellls; CpoB interacts with PBP1B <i>in 
                     vivo</i>; CpoB forms a ternery complex with TolA and PBP1B;
                     CpoB forms a ternary compex with LpoB and PBP1B. CpoB is 
                     present at a copy number of 4550 &plusmn; 540 molecules per
                     cell in growing cells. CpoB binding inhibits the 
                     transpeptidase activity of the PBP1B-LpoB complex but does 
                     not affect the transglycosylase activity - this selective 
                     control may be important during cell division |CITS: 
                     [25951518]| (see also |CITS: [30044025]|).
                     Genetic interactions implicate CpoB in PBP1B function; 
                     &Delta;<i>cpoB</i> strains show increased sensitivity to 
                     &beta;-lactams and have minor OM defects; these phentoypes 
                     are exacerbated by the absence of LpoA and are also present
                     in a &Delta;<i>cpoB</i> &Delta;<i>mrcA</i> 
                     &Delta;<i>lpoA</i> triple mutant |CITS: [25951518]|.
                     CpoB contains an N-terminal coiled-coil domain and a 
                     C-terminal tetratricopeptide repeat (TPR) domain which 
                     contains three consecutive repeats |CITS: [20455268]|. The 
                     N-terminal domain consists of 6 chains arranged to form 2 
                     three-stranded parallel coiled-coil bundles |CITS: 
                     [20816983]|
                     <i>cpoB</i> is the last gene in a three gene operon 
                     (<i>tolB-pal-cpoB</i>) |CITS: [8763928]|.
                     Targeting of CpoB to the Sec-translocase for transport 
                     across the inner membrane is SecB-dependent |CITS: 
                     [16352602]|.
                     CpoB: ""<b>c</b>oordinator of <b>P</b>G synthesis and 
                     <b>O</b>M constriction, associated with PBP1<b>B</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [25951518]|"
