     gene            781089..782141
                     /gene="aroG"
                     /locus_tag="b0754"
                     /label=aroG
     CDS             781089..782141
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aroG"
                     /locus_tag="b0754"
                     /product="bifunctional 3-deoxy-7-phosphoheptulonate
                     synthase, Phe-sensitive / 3-deoxy-7-phosphoheptulonate 
                     synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.5.1.54"
                     /label=aroG
                     /note="2-Dehydro-3-deoxyphosphoheptonate aldolase (DAHP 
                     synthase) is involved in the first committed step of the 
                     chorismate pathway, which leads to the biosynthesis of 
                     aromatic amino acids. DAHP synthase catalyzes an aldol 
                     reaction between phosphoenolpyruvate and D-erythrose 
                     4-phosphate to generate 3-deoxy-D-arabino-heptulosonate 
                     7-phosphate (DAHP). |CITS: [13654249]|
                     There are three isozymes of DAHP synthase, each 
                     specifically feedback regulated by tyrosine, phenylalanine 
                     or tryptophan |CITS: [13989507][6167722]|. DAHP synthase 
                     which is sensitive to phenylalanine (DAHP synthase (Phe), 
                     AroG) is encoded by <i>aroG</i>. All three isozymes are 
                     metalloenzymes, and DAHP synthase (Phe) has been shown to 
                     contain 1 mol of iron per mol of tetramer |CITS: [33383]|. 
                     There is a high degree of sequence identity (41%) between 
                     the three isozymes and the polypeptides are nearly 
                     identical in size |CITS: [2903857]|. In wild-type cells 
                     grown in minimal media, DAHP synthase (Phe) makes up about 
                     80% of the total DAHP synthase activity, DAHP synthase 
                     (Tyr) makes up about 20%, and DAHP synthase (Trp) makes up 
                     about 1% |CITS: [8426]|.
                     Mutations in <i>aroG</i> lead to phenylalanine-insensitive 
                     DAHP synthase |CITS: [9023954][7896760][22819948]|.
                     Site directed mutagenesis studies of cysteines showed that 
                     Cys-61 is essential for catalytic and metal binding |CITS: 
                     [1348247]|. The interior region and N-terminus of AroG are 
                     involved in the formation of the feedback inhibition site 
                     as shown by mutagenesis studies |CITS: [12964183]|. 
                     Transcriptional control of <i>aroG</i> is mediated by the 
                     <i>tyrR</i> repressor with the end products of the pathway 
                     acting as co-repressors |CITS: [4887504][6125934]|.
                     The 3D structure of AroG co-crystallized with PEP was 
                     determined, and the active site revealed |CITS: 
                     [10425687]|. The crystal structure of DAHP synthase (Phe) 
                     in complex with Mn<sup>2+</sup> and substrate analog, 
                     2-phosphoglycolate was resolved to 2.0 &Aring; resolution 
                     |CITS: [10926516]|. Also, the crystal structure of DAHP 
                     synthase (Phe) in complex with its inhibitor, 
                     L-phenylalanine, as well as phosphoenolpyruvate, and 
                     Mn<sup>2+</sup> was determined to 2.8 &Aring; resolution 
                     |CITS: [12126632]|.
                     Ligand-detected NMR spectroscopy revealed additional 
                     functional interactions between endogenous metabolites and 
                     AroG |CITS: [27065204]|.
                     "
                     /translation="MNYQNDDLRIKEIKELLPPVALLEKFPATENAANTVAHARKAIHK
