     gene            complement(784287..785435)
                     /gene="galK"
                     /locus_tag="b0757"
                     /label=galK
     CDS             complement(784287..785435)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="galK"
                     /locus_tag="b0757"
                     /product="galactokinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.6"
                     /label=galK
                     /note="Galactokinase (GalK) catalyzes the first step in
                     the Leloir pathway of galactose metabolism |CITS: 
                     [13390972][16590575]|.
                     The substrate specificity of GalK is high; however, a 
                     single amino acid change, Y371H, leads to substantial 
                     kinase activity with a variety of substrates |CITS: 
                     [14612558]|.
                     The 3-D structure of GalK has been modeled |CITS: 
                     [21423888]|.
                     Transcriptional and post-transcriptional regulation of the 
                     <i>gal</i> locus is complex.  A <i>galK</i>-encoding mRNA 
                     species, named mK2, appears to be generated by RNase P 
                     cleavage of a longer transcript |CITS: [24794565]|. 
                     Translation of GalK is independently downregulated by 
                     |FRAME: SPF-RNA| |CITS: 
                     [11804583][12101127][17264113][21292161][22388518]|, likely
                     due to degradation of the <i>galK</i>-specific mK2 mRNA 
                     |CITS: [26045496]| that is generated by RNase P cleavage 
                     |CITS: [24794565]|.  Spot 42 also affects polarity of gene 
                     expression; its binding site overlaps with a putative 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-2441 Rho|-binding site and may enhance 
                     Rho-mediated transcription termination |CITS: [26045496]|. 
                     An earlier report suggested that expression of GalK is 
                     translationally coupled to GalT |CITS: [6754091]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8647345][21292156][25069377]|"
