     gene            complement(785439..786485)
                     /gene="galT"
                     /locus_tag="b0758"
                     /label=galT
     CDS             complement(785439..786485)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="galT"
                     /locus_tag="b0758"
                     /product="bifunctional galactose-1-phosphate
                     uridylyltransferase / 
                     UDP-glucose:alpha-D-galactose-1-phosphate 
                     uridylyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.12"
                     /label=galT
                     /note="Galactose 1-phosphate uridylyltransferase catalyzes
                     an interconversion reaction in galactose catabolism.  The 
                     overall equilibrium constant of the galactose-1-phosphate 
                     uridylyltransferase and UDP-galactose 4-epimerase reactions
                     is 7.4, favoring glucose-1-phosphate formation |CITS: 
                     [4606575]|.
                     The reaction displays ping-pong bi-bi kinetics and a double
                     displacement mechanism involving the formation of a 
                     uridylyl enzyme intermediate |CITS: 
                     [4594499][4606575][321007][229894][3022797]|.  The His166 
                     residues acts as the nucleophile in the active site |CITS: 
                     [380639][2541773][8794735]|.  The enzyme is a metalloenzyme
                     containing both Zn<sup>2+</sup> and Fe<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [7727423]|.  The ions appear to be structural rather than 
                     catalytic components |CITS: [7727423][7669762]|.  Only the 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> binding site is essential for enzymatic 
                     activity |CITS: [10529216]|.  The enzyme shows an absolute 
                     requirement for cysteine |CITS: [14412847]|.
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme has been solved, allowing 
                     identification of the active site and understanding of 
                     substrate binding and catalytic mechanisms |CITS: 
                     [7669762][8794735][9063869]|.
                     The H164 and H166 residues are essential for enzymatic 
                     activity |CITS: [2541773]|.  A point mutation corresponding
                     to Q168R in the <i>E. coli</i> enzyme causes galactosemia 
                     in humans.  Kinetic study of the mutant enzyme showed that 
                     its catalytic defect is due to slow uridylylation and 
                     deuridylation |CITS: [9772178]|.
                     Kinetic characterization of mutant enzymes elucidated the 
                     role of C160 and S161 in catalysis |CITS: [10820011]|. 
                     Review: |CITS: [8647345]|"
