     gene            complement(786495..787511)
                     /gene="galE"
                     /locus_tag="b0759"
                     /label=galE
     CDS             complement(786495..787511)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="galE"
                     /locus_tag="b0759"
                     /product="UDP-glucose 4-epimerase"
                     /EC_number="5.1.3.2"
                     /label=galE
                     /note="UDP-glucose 4-epimerase (GalE) catalyzes a hydride 
                     transfer in the interconversion of UDP-galactose and 
                     UDP-glucose as part of galactose catabolism.  The active 
                     epimerase contains two molecules of NAD<sup>+</sup> per 
                     dimer |CITS: [14235524][1579570][8652544]|.  The monomer 
                     may also be catalytically active |CITS: [9224707]|.
                     The reaction mechanism has been studied extensively.  The 
                     reaction proceeds via an intermolecular hydrogen transfer 
                     |CITS: [4348210]|.  The bound NAD<sup>+</sup> is 
                     transiently reduced in the course of the reaction |CITS: 
                     [4333194][4333195][791990]| and was thought to be 
                     accompanied by a change in protein structure |CITS: 
                     [202302][210796]|.  When uridine nucleotides bind to the 
                     substrate site, it was thought that a protein 
                     conformational change is induced which increases the 
                     chemical reactivity of NAD<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [8241177]|. 
                     Complexes of UDP-glucose 4-epimerase containing NADH and 
                     uridine nucleotide are inactive |CITS: [7989316]|.
                     A number of crystal structures of the enzyme with a variety
                     of ligands have been solved |CITS: [2008433][12977 
                     89][1579570][8611559][8611497][8931134][9174344][9271499][9
                     708982][12019271]|. They revealed that the active site 
                     accommodates a variety of sugars by rearrangement of water 
                     molecules rather than changes in side chain conformation 
                     |CITS: [9174344]|.  The 4'-ketopyranose reaction 
                     intermediate can rotate freely within the active site of 
                     the enzyme |CITS: [9708982]|. The Lys153 residue may play a
                     role in increasing the chemical reactivity of enzyme-bound 
                     NAD<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [8241178]|.  Tyr149 is required for 
                     the general acid-base catalysis, and Ser124 plays a role in
                     mediating proton transfer |CITS: [9271498]| by stabilizing 
                     the <i>anti</i> conformation of the nicotinamide ring 
                     |CITS: [9271499]|. Hydride transfer is not the 
                     rate-limiting step during catalysis |CITS: [11380265]|.  A 
                     Y299C mutant has substrate specificity similar to the human
                     enzyme |CITS: [12019271]|.
                     Transcriptional and translational regulation of the 
                     <i>gal</i> operon is complex and has been studied 
                     extensively |CITS: 
                     [7934815][18374359][18691599][19029898]|.  Expression of 
                     <i>galE</i> is higher than that of promoter-distal genes 
                     |CITS: [2838723]|.  GalE is an endogenously SsrA-tagged 
                     protein |CITS: [11373298]|.  The N-terminal domain of GalE 
                     induces SsrA tagging, possibly by influencing ribosome 
                     pausing |CITS: [21151867]|.
                     When a <i>galE</i> mutant is grown in the presence of 
                     D-galactose, UDP-D-galactose accumulates, and growth is 
                     arrested due to low availability of CTP and UTP, which 
                     results in reduced RNA synthesis |CITS: [19887636]|. 
                     Review: |CITS: [8647345]|"
