     gene            790229..790378
                     /gene="acrZ"
                     /locus_tag="b0762"
                     /label=acrZ
     CDS             790229..790378
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="acrZ"
                     /locus_tag="b0762"
                     /product="multidrug efflux pump accessory protein AcrZ"
                     /label=acrZ
                     /note="<i>acrZ</i> encodes a small membrane protein that 
                     associates with the |FRAME: TRANS-CPLX-201 AcrAB-TolC 
                     multidrug efflux pump| in an AcrB-dependent manner. AcrZ 
                     affects the substrate specificity of the AcrAB-TolC efflux 
                     pump |CITS: [23010927]|. AcrZ and membrane lipids may 
                     modulate the activity of AcrB through allosteric changes 
                     |CITS: [32348749]|.
                     AcrZ with a sequential peptide affinity tag (AcrZ-SPA) 
                     interacts specifically with AcrB. AcrZ is required for 
                     efflux of some AcrB susbtrates (puromycin, chloramphenicol,
                     tetracycline) but not others (erythromycin, rifampicin, 
                     fusidic acid) |CITS: [23010927]|. AcrZ adopts a hydrophobic
                     &alpha;-helical structure that fits into a groove in the 
                     transmembrane domain of AcrB. The N-terminus of AcrZ is 
                     located in the periplasm; the C-terminus is located in the 
                     cytoplasm and interacts with a hydrophilic region of AcrB 
                     near the membrane surface |CITS: [24747401]|.
                     The <i>acrZ</i> open reading frame contains a predicted 
                     transmembrane segment, and the protein can be found in the 
                     membrane fraction |CITS: [19121005]|. Using a sucrose 
                     fractionation protocol, AcrZ partitions into both the inner
                     and outer membrane fractions |CITS: [21778229]|. Topology 
                     assays using GFP and PhoA fusion proteins suggest that the 
                     C-terminus of AcrZ is oriented to the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [21778229]|. Translocation of AcrZ is impaired in both SecE
                     depleted and YidC depleted <i>E. coli</i> strains |CITS: 
                     [21778229]|.
                     A class II Mar/Rob/Sox box and a &sigma;<sub>70</sub> 
                     promoter are located upstream of the <i>acrZ</i> 
                     transcription start site. <i>acrZ</i> expression is induced
                     by paraquat. AcrZ-SPA and AcrB levels increase when MarA, 
                     Rob or SoxS are overproduced from plasmids |CITS: 
                     [23010927]|.
                     <i>acrZ</i> is expressed during both exponential and 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [19121005]|.
                     In growth competition experiments, an <i>acrZ</i> deletion 
                     mutant is more sensitive to cell envelope stress than the 
                     wild type |CITS: [19734312]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [30810554]|"
                     /translation="MLELLKSLVFAVIMVPVVMAIILGLIYGLGEVFNIFSGVGKKDQP
