     gene            790545..791318
                     /gene="modA"
                     /locus_tag="b0763"
                     /label=modA
     CDS             790545..791318
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="modA"
                     /locus_tag="b0763"
                     /product="trifunctional molybdate ABC transporter
                     periplasmic binding protein / molybdate ABC transporter / 
                     transport of molybdate"
                     /EC_number="7.3.2.5"
                     /label=modA
                     /note="ModA is the periplasmic binding protein of an ABC 
                     transporter that mediates the high affinity uptake of 
                     molybdate.
                     Purified ModA binds molybdate and tungstate with similar 
                     high affinities and in a 1:1 ratio; it does not bind 
                     sulfate, phosphate, selenate nor chlorate |CITS: 
                     [8576221][9545596]|. Although an early study |CITS: 
                     [8576221]|, indicated that ModA does not bind chromate, 
                     later work |CITS: [28150901]| reported that purified ModA 
                     binds chromate with high affinity (K<sub>d</sub> approx 100
                     nM). Chromate bound ModA crystallizes as a dimer; chromate 
                     binds to ModA in the same binding pocket and with 
                     coordination by the same residues as those identified for 
                     molybdate and tungstate; purified ModA is effective in 
                     removing chromate from an aqueous solution |CITS: 
                     [28150901]|.
                     Purified, crystallized ModA is monomeric and contains two 
                     &alpha;/&beta; domains; molybdate and tungstate bind at the
                     interface between the two domains |CITS: [9302996]|. The 
                     promoter of <i>modA</i> is predicted to be 
                     &sigma;<sup>28</sup>-dependent and can be transcribed by 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-222| <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [16997971]|. ModA
                     is a potential target for antibacterial drug design |CITS: 
                     [38642264]|."
