     gene            794042..795037
                     /gene="pgl"
                     /locus_tag="b0767"
                     /label=pgl
     CDS             794042..795037
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pgl"
                     /locus_tag="b0767"
                     /product="6-phosphogluconolactonase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.1.31"
                     /label=pgl
                     /note="6-phosphogluconolactonase is an enzyme of the
                     oxidative pentose phosphate pathway.
                     A <i>pgl</i> mutant strain grows only slightly slower than 
                     wild type on glucose as the sole source of carbon |CITS: 
                     [4902810][4552019]|. Growth on glucose may be due to 
                     non-enzymatic hydrolysis of |FRAME: 
                     D-6-P-GLUCONO-DELTA-LACTONE| or a bypass pathway that 
                     involves dephosphorylation and export of gluconolactone, 
                     hydrolysis to gluconate, followed by gluconate re-import 
                     and phosphorylation |CITS: [4552019][15576773][31390539]|. 
                     When grown on maltose medium, strains lacking Pgl activity 
                     turn blue after iodine treatment |CITS: 
                     [4902810][15576773][15766779]|. The phenotype of a 
                     <i>pgl</i> deletion strain can be complemented by 
                     expression of the <i>pgl</i> gene from <i>Pseudomonas 
                     putida</i>, although there is no detectable similarity 
                     between the two genes |CITS: [15766779]|. A metabolic flux 
                     balance experiment revealed that the inefficient growth 
                     when <i>pgl</i> is deleted results solely from the 
                     extracellular bypass mechanism |CITS: [38982839]|.
                     Unlike some other enzymes that participate in the pentose 
                     phosphate pathway, Pgl is robust towards oxidative 
                     inactivation by peroxyl radicals |CITS: [37119864]|. When 
                     all three |FRAME: OXIDATIVEPENT-PWY| enzymes (|FRAME: 
                     GLU6PDEHYDROG-MONOMER|, Pgl and |FRAME: 
                     6PGLUCONDEHYDROG-MONOMER|) are exposed to a peroxyl radical
                     oxidation together, the production of NADPH was lower than 
                     what is generated by non-oxidized enzymes but not during 
                     the initial stages of oxidation |CITS: [38848786]|.
                     A strategy for metabolic engineering of <i>E. coli</i> for 
                     the production of riboflavin included overexpression of 
                     <i>pgl</i>, leading to an increase in riboflavin titer 
                     |CITS: [25027702]|.
                     <i>pgl</i> is part of a genomic region that is deleted in 
                     the <i>E. coli</i> B strain BL21, but is present in the 
                     K-12 strain MG1655 |CITS: [22190455]|.
                     <i>pgl</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     Pgl: ""phosphogluconolactonase"" |CITS: [4902810]|"
                     /translation="MKQTVYIASPESQQIHVWNLNHEGALTLTQVVDVPGQVQPMVVSP
