     gene            806978..807733
                     /gene="bioC"
                     /locus_tag="b0777"
                     /label=bioC
     CDS             806978..807733
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="bioC"
                     /locus_tag="b0777"
                     /product="bifunctional malonyl-acyl carrier protein 
                     methyltransferase / malonyl-CoA methyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.197"
                     /label=bioC
                     /note="BioC is a methyltransferase involved in an early
                     step of biotin biosynthesis |CITS: 
                     [4864413][4877129][3058702]|.  It is thought to methylate 
                     the &omega;-carboxyl group of |FRAME: MALONYL-ACP 
                     ""malonyl-[acp]""| to form |FRAME: 
                     Malonyl-acp-methyl-ester|, which is recognized by the fatty
                     acid synthetic enzymes |CITS: [20693992]|.  The methyl 
                     ester is processed via the fatty acid elongation cycle to 
                     give |FRAME: Pimeloyl-ACP-methyl-esters|, which is 
                     processed into |FRAME: Pimeloyl-ACPs ""pimelyl-[acp]""| by 
                     |FRAME: EG10122-MONOMER ""BioH""| |CITS: [20693992]|. For a
                     long time, neither the exact function of BioC nor the path 
                     to pimelate was understood.  Lemoine et al. (|CITS: 
                     [8830257]|) proposed that BioC may catalyze the stepwise 
                     condensation of malonyl-CoA by addition of acetate units.  
                     |CITS: [2678009]| cites unpublished results showing lack of
                     growth of a <i>bioC</i> mutant on pimelate, but 
                     <sup>13</sup>C tracing indicated that pimelate is not a 
                     precursor of biotin |CITS: [8125118]|. The function of BioC
                     was finally suggested in a 2010 study |CITS: [20693992]|.  
                     Due to difficulties studying the <i>E. coli</i> enzyme 
                     <i>in vitro</i>, the biochemical function of the 
                     <i>Bacillus cereus</i> enzyme was determined; expression of
                     this enzyme complements an <i>E. coli bioC</i> mutant 
                     |CITS: [22965231]|.
                     <i>bioC</i> mutants require biotin for growth |CITS: 
                     [4864413]| and appear to be blocked before the synthesis of
                     pimeloyl-CoA |CITS: [4563978]|.  Monomethyl esters of 
                     malonate, glutarate and pimelate allow growth of a 
                     <i>bioC</i> deletion mutant in the presence of a 
                     heterologous acyl-ACP synthetase that converts these 
                     compounds to ACP thioesters |CITS: [20693992]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [21437340]|"
