     gene            812500..813489
                     /gene="moaA"
                     /locus_tag="b0781"
                     /label=moaA
     CDS             812500..813489
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="moaA"
                     /locus_tag="b0781"
                     /product="GTP 3',8'-cyclase"
                     /EC_number="4.1.99.22"
                     /label=moaA
                     /note="MoaA, a member of the radical SAM superfamily of
                     enzymes |CITS: [11222759]|, participates in the |FRAME: 
                     PWY-6823| pathway. Biochemical characterization of the 
                     enzyme and its reaction mechanism have been performed with 
                     the <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> enzyme, which was shown to
                     catalyze a complex rearrangement reaction that involves the
                     insertion of the C8 carbon of the purine moiety of GTP into
                     the C2'-C3' bond of its ribose moiety |CITS: [1663260 
                     8][23286307][23627491][23848839][24955657][25697423][259413
                     96][32348669]|. Under anaerobic conditions, the A-type Fe-S
                     cluster carrier proteins |FRAME: CPLX0-7617| and |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7908| are involved in insertion of [4Fe-4S] clusters 
                     into MoaA |CITS: [33782054]|.
                     Using an <i>E. coli</i> strain overproducing MoaA and 
                     |FRAME: EG11666-MONOMER MoaC|, |FRAME: PRECURSOR-Z| 
                     (precursor Z) was produced, purified, and chemically 
                     characterized |CITS: [14761975]|. In earlier work, 
                     expression of genes <i>moaABC</i> in a <i>|FRAME: 
                     EG10153|</i> mutant resulted in the production of 
                     ""compound Z"", an oxidized product of precursor Z |CITS: 
                     [9692897]|.
                     Mutants in molybdenum cofactor (MoCo) biosynthesis genes 
                     were initially identified by their chlorate resistance and 
                     hence named ""<i>chl</i>""; the mutant phenotype is due to 
                     the lack of several enzymes that require MoCo for activity,
                     such as nitrate reductase (see e.g. |CITS: [2947896]|). 
                     <i>moaA, |FRAME: EG10153|</i> and <i>|FRAME: EG10152|</i> 
                     mutants are hypersensitive to the base analog 
                     6-hydroxylaminopurine |CITS: 
                     [10852865][12730170][17349664]|.
                     <i>moaA</i>, <i>|FRAME: EG11511|</i>, <i>|FRAME: 
                     EG11596|</i>, <i>|FRAME: EG11598|</i>, <i>|FRAME: 
                     EG10002|</i>, or <i>modC</i> deletion mutants lose the 
                     ability to reduce |FRAME: CPD-4544| (tellurate), which can 
                     be restored by complementation. Although the <i>E. coli</i>
                     tellurate reductase gene and its product remain 
                     uncharacterized, these data suggest that it involves a 
                     molybdoenzyme |CITS: [23475618]|.
                     The molybdate-sensing transcription regulator |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-2541| acts as an activator for six operons including 
                     the <i>moaABCDE</i> operon |CITS: [11092866]|. <i>moaA</i> 
                     expression is posttranscriptionally regulated by a 
                     riboswitch that binds both the molybdenum cofactor as well 
                     as the |FRAME: CPLX0-7956| protein |CITS: 
                     [18363797][23274138]|. Binding of CsrA to the <i>moaA</i> 
                     mRNA appears to be necessary, but not sufficient for 
                     activation of translation |CITS: [23274138]|. The 
                     riboswitch needs to bind K<sup>+</sup> and Mg<sup>+</sup> 
                     to be a stable structure |CITS:[36774787]|.
                     <i>moaA</i> shows differential codon adaptation, resulting 
                     in differential translation efficiency signatures, in 
                     thermophilic microbes. It was therefore predicted to play a
                     role in the heat shock response. A <i>moaA</i> deletion 
                     mutant was shown to be more sensitive than wild-type 
                     specifically to heat shock, but not other stresses |CITS: 
                     [24580753]|.
                     ChlA: ""<b>chl</b>orate resistance <b>A</b>""<br>BisA: 
                     ""<b>bi</b>otin <b>s</b>ulfoxide""
                     MoaA: ""<b>mo</b>lybdenum cofactor biosynthesis"" |CITS: 
                     [1484496]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [1587808][16218869][21528011][23201473][25 
