     gene            814504..814749
                     /gene="moaD"
                     /locus_tag="b0784"
                     /label=moaD
     CDS             814504..814749
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="moaD"
                     /locus_tag="b0784"
                     /product="multifunctional molybdopterin synthase sulfur
                     carrier subunit / molybdopterin synthase 
                     adenylyltransferase/sulfur transferase / molybdopterin 
                     synthase sulfur transferase / molybdopterin synthase 
                     adenylyltransferase / molybdopterin synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.8.1.12; 2.8.1.11; 2.7.7.80"
                     /label=moaD
                     /note="Initially, |FRAME: EG11598-MONOMER (MoeB)| was
                     presumed to catalyze transfer of sulfur atoms to |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-2502| |CITS: [9388540]|, however other evidence 
                     indicates that MoeB activates MoaD by adenylylation of 
                     MoaD's C-terminus. MoaD appears to further enhance MoeB's 
                     activation of |FRAME: G7325-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [11290749][11463785][19946146]|. Surface plasmon resonance 
                     studies suggested that IscS specifically binds to the 
                     putative <i>in vivo</i> MoeB-MoaD complex |CITS: 
                     [21856748]|. It has since been determined that the 
                     carboxy-adenylated MoaD is sulfurated by the IscS via TusA 
                     or YnjE sulfurtransferases |CITS: [21856748][23281480]|. 
                     Purified recombinant MoeB was shown to be a homodimer in 
                     solution |CITS: [11463785]|. It was also shown to form a 
                     MoeB-MoaD heterotetrameric complex. Crystal structures of 
                     the apo, ATP-bound, and MoaD-adenylate forms of the 
                     MoeB-MoaD complex have been determined at 1.7, 2.9 and 2.1 
                     &Aring; resolution, respectively |CITS: [11713534]|. The 
                     interaction between these proteins was also demonstrated in
                     solution |CITS: [17223713]|.
                     MoaD structurally resembles the ubiquitin and ThiS proteins
                     |CITS: [16388576]|. MoaD contains a ubiquitin-like fold and
                     a highly conserved Gly-Gly motif that upon activation forms
                     the thiocarboxylate group. In addition to forming the 
                     molybdopterin synthase complex with MoaE, MoaD also forms a
                     similar complex with MoeB to regenerate its sulfur after 
                     transfer to molybdoptrin. Site-directed mutagenesis studies
                     showed that Gly81 is important in sulfur transfer and is 
                     required for MoaD adenylation |CITS: [17223713]|.
                     MoaD can be used as a fusion tag to promote the hetrologous
                     expression of some, but not all, proteins in <i>E. coli</i>
                     |CITS: [24444081]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [23201473][28098827][32239579]|"
