     gene            complement(825428..826099)
                     /gene="cecR"
                     /locus_tag="b0796"
                     /label=cecR
     CDS             complement(825428..826099)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cecR"
                     /locus_tag="b0796"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional dual regulator CecR"
                     /label=cecR
                     /note="CecR (also known as YbiH) is a TetR-family
                     (subfamily D) transcription factor originally described as 
                     a ""<b>Ce</b>foperazone and <b>c</b>hloramphenicol 
                     <b>R</b>egulator"" [Yamanaka16] |CITS:[27112147][41194554]|
                     and confirmed as a TF through an integrated workflow 
                     comprised of computational prediction, knowledge-based 
                     classification, and experimental validation of candidate 
                     TFs at the genome scale |CITS: [30137486]|. CecR controls 
                     genes involved in sensitivity to cefoperazone and 
                     chloramphenicol |CITS:[27112147]|.
                     CecR functions as an all-helical TetR-like homodimer, and 
                     each protomer contains an N-terminal DNA-binding domain 
                     with a helix-turn-helix (HTH) motif and a C-terminal 
                     ligand-recognition domain |CITS:[41194554]|. The CecR 
                     crystal structure (1.91 &Aring;) shows a C-terminal 
                     effector-binding cavity with an uncommon tunnel-like 
                     architecture (~35 &Aring; long; with volume of ~399 
                     &Aring;<sup><sup>3</sup></sup>) that can accommodate 
                     polyethylene glycol fragments from the crystallization 
                     solution, consistent with a potential ligand-binding site 
                     |CITS:[41194554]|. CecR binds antibiotics, including 
                     cephalosporins and chloramphenicol |CITS:[41194554]|, and 
                     docking places these ligands in the tunnel region 
                     overlapping a PEG fragment and predicts key contributions 
                     from conserved residues (notably Q126 and F74, with 
                     additional contacts involving L109 and potential 
                     contributions from S119/S123) |CITS:[41194554]|.
                     The transcriptional factor binding sites (TFBSs) of CecR 
                     enclose AT-rich inverted repeats separated by 7 nt, 
                     consistent with a homodimeric (or tetrameric) DNA-binding 
                     architecture that facilitates tight DNA binding |CITS: 
                     [30137486]|.
                     Compared to known global TFs, CecR has more intragenic 
                     binding peaks and fewer peaks in putative regulatory 
                     regions, fewer binding peaks overall than CRP, Lrp, Fnr, 
                     and ArcA, binds to more genes with putative functions, and 
                     shows lower expression relative to most global TFs|CITS: 
                     [30137486]|.
                     Wide-scale analyses of TetR-family transcriptional 
                     regulators indicate that cecR (ybiH) is present across 
                     Escherichia and Salmonella species but not across all 
                     surveyed Gram-negative species |CITS:[31606035]|. Another 
                     phylogenomic analysis supports that CecR is broadly 
                     conserved among Enterobacteriales/Enterobacterales members 
                     |CITS:[41194554]|.
                     The transcription of the <i>cecR</i> gene is affected by 
                     aerobic-to-anaerobic shift |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     The YbiH binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
