     gene            826328..827692
                     /gene="rhlE"
                     /locus_tag="b0797"
                     /label=rhlE
     CDS             826328..827692
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rhlE"
                     /locus_tag="b0797"
                     /product="bifunctional ATP-dependent RNA helicase RhlE / 
                     ATP-dependent RNA helicase"
                     /EC_number="5.6.2.7"
                     /label=rhlE
                     /note="RhlE is a ribosome-associated factor that may be
                     involved in ribosome maturation |CITS: [18083833]|.
                     RhlE is a member of the DEAD-box-containing ATP-dependent 
                     RNA helicase family |CITS: [8037924]|. Its ATPase
                     activity is stimulated by both long RNAs and short 
                     oligoribonucleotides |CITS: [15196029]|. Of the three <i>E.
                     coli</i>
                     DEAD-box RNA helicases DeaD, SrmB and RhlE, only RhlE can 
                     unwind substrates with short or no single-stranded 
                     extensions, but the enzyme shows low processivity |CITS: 
                     [15196029]|.
                     RhlE physically interacts with |FRAME: EG10690-MONOMER| 
                     |CITS: [10361280]| and |FRAME: EG10859-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [15554979]|. RhlE can functionally replace RhlB within the 
                     degradosome |CITS: [15554979]|.
                     An <i>rhlE</i> mutant does not have any obvious growth 
                     defect |CITS: [8037924]|. Overexpression of RhlE can 
                     substitute for the essential function of DeaD during cold 
                     shock |CITS: [17557820][18083833]|, but exacerbates the 
                     cold-sensitive growth defect of an <i>srmB</i> mutant 
                     |CITS: [18083833]|. Conversely, the cold-sensitive growth 
                     defect of an <i>rhlE deaD</i> double mutant is enhanced, 
                     while that of an <i>rhlE srmB</i> double mutant is 
                     alleviated |CITS: [18083833]|. Both DeaD and SrmB are 
                     involved in ribosome maturation, indicating that RhlE may 
                     play a similar role |CITS: [18083833]|.
                     <i>rhlE</i> shows differential codon adaptation, resulting 
                     in differential translation efficiency signatures, in 
                     thermophilic microbes. It was therefore predicted to play a
                     role in the heat shock response. An <i>rhlE</i> deletion 
                     mutant was shown to be more sensitive than wild-type 
                     specifically to heat shock, but not other stresses |CITS: 
                     [24580753]|.
                     Using a method that distinguishes N-phosphorylation from 
                     O-phosphorylation, His141 residue was found to be 
                     phosphorylated <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [35636362]|.
                     RhlE: ""<b>R</b>NA <b>h</b>elicase-<b>l</b>ike"" |CITS: 
                     [1931833]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16935881][24635478][25907111]|"
