     gene            complement(850280..851200)
                     /gene="ldtB"
                     /locus_tag="b0819"
                     /label=ldtB
     CDS             complement(850280..851200)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ldtB"
                     /locus_tag="b0819"
                     /product="bifunctional L,D-transpeptidase LdtB / 
                     L,D-transpeptidase"
                     /label=ldtB
                     /note="LdtB is a periplasmic L,D-transpeptidase which
                     cleaves the <i>meso</i>-diaminopimeloyl<sup>3</sup>&rarr; 
                     D-alanine<sup>4</sup> peptide bond in a peptidoglycan 
                     tetrapeptide stem and links the carboxyl group at the 
                     L-center of <i>m</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup> to the side chain 
                     amine of the lysine residue located at the C terminus of 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-8279 ""Braun lipoprotein""| |CITS: 
                     [4918558][17369299]|. This activity anchors Braun 
                     lipoprotein to the peptidoglycan and contributes to the 
                     stability of the cell envelope.
                     Muropeptides obtained from the peptidoglycan of an <i>E. 
                     coli</i> BW25113 &Delta;(<i>|FRAME: G7073 ""ldtA""| ldtB 
                     |FRAME: G6571 ""ldtC""| |FRAME: G6904 ""ldtE""|</i>) 
                     quadruple mutant (BW25113&Delta;4) do not contain 
                     tripeptide stems substituted by a fragment of the Braun 
                     lipoprotein |CITS: [17369299]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [18456808]|). Expression of <i>ldtB</i> in BW25113&Delta;4 
                     restores the appearance of this muropeptide suggesting that
                     LdtB catalyzes the covalent anchoring of Braun lipoprotein 
                     to the peptidoglycan |CITS: [17369299]|. Complementation 
                     analysis suggests that <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains three 
                     L,D transpeptidases (LdtA, LdtB and LdtC) that can catalyse
                     the covalent anchoring of Braun lipoprotein to the 
                     peptidoglycan however the analysis of single deletion 
                     mutants suggests LdtB may be of primary importance 
                     (|CITS:[17369299]| ('results not shown'). An <i>E. coli</i>
                     BW25113 &Delta;(<i>ldtA ldtB ldtC</i>) triple mutant 
                     (BW25113&Delta;3) has increased outer membrane 
                     permeability, is hypersensitive to EDTA, and is resistant 
                     to the toxic effects of 25mM D-methionine |CITS: 
                     [23832002]|.
                     LdtA, B, C and E are all of similar size and share 42-62% 
                     sequence similarity including a putative active site 
                     cysteine located in a conserved motif |CITS: [23832002]|. 
                     Sequence analysis suggests that LdtB may contain 
                     &beta;-barrel structure(s) |CITS:[12192075]|. 
                     Complementation analysis (|CITS:[18456808]| results not 
                     shown) also suggests LdtB can exchange D-ala<sup>4</sup> in
                     peptidoglycan tetrapeptide stems with glycine (|FRAME: 
                     RXN-16665 ""RXN-16665""|) and hydrolyse D-ala<sup>4</sup> 
                     to produce tripeptide stems (|FRAME: RXN-16664 
                     ""RXN-16664""|). <i>E. coli</i> K-12 incorporates 
                     exogenously supplied D-methionine into peptidoglycan stem 
                     tetrapeptides; the percentage of muropeptides that contain 
                     D-methionine is reduced to varying extents in all single 
                     <i>ldt</i> insertion mutants* and is reduced by ~75% in the
                     double <i>ldtD ldtE</i> insertion mutant |CITS: [21792174]|
                     (see also |CITS: [23832002]|).
                     <I>ldtB</I> (formerly <i>ybiS</i>) is transcriptionally 
                     activated by constitutive levels of Rob |CITS:[10850996]|. 
                     <i>ldtB</i> transcription is repressed by CRP <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS:[15520470]| (see also |CITS: [14597652]|). 
                     *<i>ldtF</i> was not included in this analysis
                     "
