     gene            851419..853011
                     /gene="ybiT"
                     /locus_tag="b0820"
                     /label=ybiT
     CDS             851419..853011
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ybiT"
                     /locus_tag="b0820"
                     /product="ATP-binding protein YbiT"
                     /label=ybiT
                     /note="YbiT belongs to the YbiT subfamily of the ABCF
                     family of ATP-binding cassette (ABC) proteins |CITS: 
                     [30597160]|. There are four ABCF family proteins within 
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12: YheS, |FRAME: YJJK-MONOMER ""EttA""|, 
                     |FRAME: UUP-MONOMER ""Uup""| and |FRAME: G6423-MONOMER 
                     ""YbiT""|, all of which are involved in noncanonical 
                     translation of 'hard-to-translate' nascent peptides getting
                     them through the ribosome exit tunnel. YheS represses 
                     poly-acidic sequence-dependent intrinsic ribosome 
                     destabilization, stalling and premature termination |CITS: 
                     [38661232]|.
                     Overexpression of an EQ<sub>2</sub> mutant of YbiT 
                     (carrying substitutions in the predicted ATPase active 
                     sites) of <i>E. coli</i> CFT073 inhibits protein synthesis 
                     |CITS: [30597160]|.
                     Accumulation of the cationic fluorescent dye 
                     3,3'-dipropylthiadicarbocyanine iodide (diS-C3) is greater 
                     in <i>ybiT</i> knockout cells compared to wild type |CITS: 
                     [31438868]|.
                     The binding of the transcription factor |FRAME: CPLX0-226 
                     CRP| to a predicted CRP DNA binding site around the start 
                     of the <i>ybiT</i> gene has been demonstrated; however an 
                     effect of this binding on gene expression has not been 
                     analyzed |CITS: [37114659]|.
                     Using a reconstituted <i>in vitro</i> translation system 
                     and <i>in vivo</i> deletion mutant analyses, the 
                     interdomain linkage and ATPase activity were found to be 
                     essential to rescuing ribosomes arrested by a nascent 
                     peptide with poly-basic sequences |CITS: [38661232]|. 
                     Review: |CITS: [31563533]|"
