     gene            868435..870783
                     /gene="pdeI"
                     /locus_tag="b0833"
                     /label=pdeI
     CDS             868435..870783
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pdeI"
                     /locus_tag="b0833"
                     /product="cyclic di-GMP synthase PdeI"
                     /label=pdeI
                     /note="PdeI was initially predicted to be a
                     c-di-GMP-specific phosphodiesterase |CITS: [26553851]| but 
                     has since been shown to increase c-di-GMP and referred to 
                     as a c-di-GMP synthase |CITS: [39689163]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>pdeI</i> reduces biofilm formation 
                     |CITS: [19460094]|. PdeI is predicted to be an inner 
                     membrane protein consisting of two transmembrane domains 
                     |CITS: [15919996]| that flank a predicted periplasmic CHASE
                     (<u>c</u>yclases/<u>h</u>istidine kinases <u>a</u>ssociated
                     <u>se</u>nsory) domain, followed by a divergent CACHE 
                     domain, a HAMP domain, a degenerate GGDEF domain, and a 
                     C-terminal EAL domain |CITS: [26148715]|.
                     A screen for suppressors of the motility defect of a 
                     |FRAME: EG12252 &Delta;<i>pdeH</i>| mutant identified 
                     activating mutations in <i>E. coli</i>'s alternative 
                     c-di-GMP phosphodiesterases (PDEs), including PdeI. This 
                     supports a model whereby the signaling specificity of PDEs 
                     is the result of environmental signals required for their 
                     activation. The suppressor mutation in <i>pdeI</i> 
                     consisted of a single amino acid substitution in the 
                     N-terminal domain of PdeI and resulted in increased 
                     swimming motility and reduced levels of intracellular 
                     c-di-GMP |CITS: [26553851]|.
                     Using single-cell analyses and a PdeI fusion protein 
                     revealed that PdeI is indeed a membrane protein, that 
                     increased expression of PdeI correlated with elevated 
                     c-di-GMP, and is primarily found at the bottom of biofilms.
                     Additional analysis with a mutation in the divergent GGDEF 
                     domain of PdeI with the major <i>E. coli</i> |FRAME: 
                     EG12252-MONOMER| deleted, suggests PdeI does not function 
                     as a phosphodiesterase but likely functions as a 
                     membrane-associated sensor. Using an ampicillin antibiotic 
                     killing assay, cells with high PdeI levels at the early 
                     stages of biofilm development produce a higher ratio of 
                     persister cells. The results of these studies suggest that 
                     PdeI can sense and integrate environmental signals with 
                     c-di-GMP production to regulate biofilm heterogeneity 
                     |CITS: [39689163]|.
                     In an assay that involves colonization of a monospecies 
                     biofilm with the pathogenic <i>E. coli</i> strain 55989, a 
                     <i>pdeI</i> mutant strain allows a significantly higher 
                     level of colonization by the pathogen than does the wild 
                     type |CITS: [23667443]|.
                     Expression of <i>pdeI</i> is downregulated by CsrA |CITS: 
                     [18713317]|; PdeI protein levels increase with temperature,
                     with highest expression seen at 42&deg;C |CITS: 
                     [26553851]|.
                     PdeI: ""<b>p</b>hospho<b>d</b>i<b>e</b>sterase <b>I</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [26148715]|"
