     gene            complement(872166..873491)
                     /gene="rimO"
                     /locus_tag="b0835"
                     /label=rimO
     CDS             complement(872166..873491)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rimO"
                     /locus_tag="b0835"
                     /product="bifunctional ribosomal protein S12
                     methylthiotransferase RimO / protein methylthiotransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.8.4.4"
                     /label=rimO
                     /note="RimO was identified as the methyltransferase
                     responsible for methylthiolation of the &beta; carbon of 
                     the D88 residue of |FRAME: EG10911-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [18252828]|. RimO belongs to the methylthiotransferase 
                     (MTTase) family of the superfamily of radical-SAM proteins 
                     |CITS: [18252828]| and is the only MTTase known to catalyze
                     methylthiolation of a protein substrate |CITS: [19736993]|.
                     RimO interacts directly with a ribonucleoprotein complex 
                     containing S12; it seems likely that the enzyme is only 
                     able to modify S12 efficiently in the context of the small 
                     ribosomal subunit |CITS: [21169565]|.
                     RimO contains two spectroscopically distinguishable 
                     [4Fe-4S] clusters |CITS: [19736993]|. The reaction is 
                     thought to take place in two steps |CITS: [23991893]|, 
                     however this has not been shown for RimO in <i>E. coli</i>.
                     It is thought that the |FRAME: G7511 YgfZ protein| is 
                     involved in the repair/exchange of the spent cluster of 
                     both RimO and the homologous enzyme |FRAME: G6364 MiaB|. 
                     The <i>in vivo</i> activity of RimO is very low in the 
                     absence of YgfZ |CITS: [36902159]|.
                     A <i>rimO</i> mutant contains the unmodified form of S12 
                     |CITS: [18252828][21169565]| and grows more slowly than the
                     wild-type strain |CITS: [18252828]|. A <i>rimO</i> deletion
                     mutant has an altered transcriptional profile similar to a 
                     <i>ycaO</i> deletion mutant, primarily affecting members of
                     the Fnr and Nar regulons |CITS: [21169565]|.
                     RimO: ""<b>ri</b>bosomal <b>m</b>odification"" |CITS: 
                     [18252828]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [18355719][22178611][25009947][28361051]|"
