     gene            complement(877432..878190)
                     /gene="deoR"
                     /locus_tag="b0840"
                     /label=deoR
     CDS             complement(877432..878190)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="deoR"
                     /locus_tag="b0840"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional repressor DeoR"
                     /label=deoR
                     /note="The transcriptional repressor DeoR, for 
                     ""<b>Deo</b>xyribose <b>R</b>egulator,"" is involved in the
                     negative expression of genes related to transport and 
                     catabolism of deoxyribonucleoside nucleotides |CITS:[17155 
                     3][6098525][2998936][3019678][2826388][3019678][2265760][26
                     67765][1791752][2115441]|. A transposon insertion mutation 
                     in the <i>deoR</i> gene produces cellular susceptibility to
                     the antibiotic trimethoprim |CITS: [34874820]|. On the 
                     other hand, inhibition of <i>deoR</i> expression by CRISPRi
                     reduced cellular fitness under treatment with the 
                     antibiotic carbonyl cyanide 3-chlorophenylhydrazone (CCCP) 
                     |CITS:[40352728]|.
                     DeoR belongs to the DeoR family of transcriptional 
                     regulators |CITS:[2994018][8955387]|. This protein consists
                     of two domains, an amino-terminal domain that contains a 
                     potential helix-turn-helix DNA-binding motif and a 
                     carboxy-terminal domain involved in the oligomerization and
                     the recognition of a possible co-inducer 
                     |CITS:[2994018][8955387][18844374]|.
                     DeoR is an octamer in solution |CITS:[2653814]| and it 
                     forms multiple complexes (oligomers) in its target 
                     promoters; the cooperative binding of this regulator to 
                     different  tandem inverted repeat sequences generates a 
                     repression DNA loop |CITS:[8001800][1809188][2667765][3019 
                     678][3004952][16453417][2653814][1437558][33511418]|. The 
                     binding targets for DeoR consist of 16-nucleotide inverted 
                     repeat sequences that possess conserved motifs 
                     |CITS:[8455551][3019678][1437558]|.
                     By using synthetic gene circuits, it was demonstrated that 
                     DeoR modulates RNA polymerase (RNAP) recruitment or 
                     transcription initiation in a position-dependent manner 
                     |CITS:[39921566]|.
                     It has been observed that the deletion of <i>deoR</i> 
                     favors the transformation of competent cells with large 
                     plasmids |CITS: [36317996]|.
                     The DeoR binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
