     gene            879129..880361
                     /gene="mdfA"
                     /locus_tag="b0842"
                     /label=mdfA
     CDS             879129..880361
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mdfA"
                     /locus_tag="b0842"
                     /product="multifunctional multidrug efflux pump MdfA /
                     Na(+):H(+) antiporter / K(+):H(+) antiporter / K(+):H(+) 
                     antiporter / Na(+):H(+) antiporter / multidrug efflux pump 
                     MdfA"
                     /label=mdfA
                     /note="The MdfA protein, also known as Cmr, is a multidrug 
                     efflux protein belonging to the major facilitator 
                     superfamily (MFS) |CITS:[8987357]|. Overexpression of MdfA 
                     confers resistance to a broad range of antibiotics and 
                     toxic molecules including tetracycline, chloramphenicol, 
                     erythromycin, daunomycin, puromycin, benzalkonium, 
                     rifampin, some aminoglycosides and fluoroquinolones and 
                     organic cations such as ethidium bromide and 
                     tetraphenylphosphonium |CITS:[9079913]|. Deletion of 
                     <I>mdfA</I> results in increased susceptibility to ethidium
                     bromide and benzalkonium chloride |CITS:[11257026]|.  
                     Transport experiments suggest that MdfA confers resistance 
                     via an efflux mechanism dependent on the proton motive 
                     force |CITS:[9079913]|. Consistent with this, 
                     chloramphenicol/proton antiport is observed in membrane 
                     vesicles prepared from cells overexpressing <I>mdfA</I> 
                     |CITS:[9644262]|.
                     Overexpression of <I>mdfA</I> results in spectinomycin 
                     sensitivity and isopropyl-&Beta;-D-thiogalactopyranoside 
                     (IPTG) exclusion via an unknown mechanism |CITS:[9811673]| 
                     (see also |CITS: [29973719]|). Mutations in MdfA (and also 
                     in other PMF-related transporters) confer an adaptive 
                     advantage against spectinomycin by altering proton 
                     homeostasis |CITS: [40205803]|.
                     Sequence analysis and experimental topology analysis 
                     indicate that MdfA contains12 transmembrane domains with 
                     the N and C-termini located in the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [9079913][12029048]|.
                     MdfA catalyses drug:proton antiport with a stoichiometry of
                     1; MdfA-catalyzed transport of neutral substrates is 
                     electrogenic, resulting in a net movement of electric 
                     charge, whereas transport of cationic substrates is 
                     electroneutral with no net movement of charge, driven 
                     primarily by proton chemical gradient |CITS:[12578981]|. 
                     The negatively charged, membrane embedded, amino acid 
                     residues glutamic acid 26 (E26) and aspartic acid 34 (D34) 
                     and the positively charged arginine residue at position 112
                     (R112) have been implicated in transport function |CITS: 
                     [10022825][14717607],[15557318][16274234]|. R112 is located
                     within a  predicted drug binding pocket and a positive 
                     charge at this position is essential for transport function
                     |CITS: [16274234]|. No single replacement of any acidic 
                     residue with a neutral residue abolishes activity of the 
                     transporter, however an E26C/D34C double mutation disrupts 
                     MdfA-mediated resistance to a variety of test drugs 
                     |CITS:[16855255][19129186]|. Aspartic acid at position 34 
                     (D34) is the proton binding carboxylic residue |CITS: 
                     [22841484]|. Substrate and protons compete for binding to 
                     MdfA but they bind at different sites within the protein 
                     |CITS: [22841484]|.
                     Insertion of an additional acidic residue into the 
                     predicted substrate binding pocket of MdfA broadens the 
                     substrate specificity of the transporter to include 
                     divalent cationic compounds |CITS: [22802625]|. Wild-type 
                     MdfA can transport symmetric divalent cationic compounds 
                     when the two positively charged moieties are separated by a
                     long linker (eg.dequalinium); transport of these compounds 
                     may proceed in a processive manner whereby each cationic 
                     moiety is treated as a separate substrate |CITS: 
                     [25105370]|.
                     Crystal structures of MdfA in alternative conformational 
                     states - inward facing with chloramphenicol bound |CITS: 
                     [26238402]| and outward open |CITS: [30275448]| - have been
                     reported. MdfA consists of 12 transmembrane (TM) helices 
                     (helices 1-6 constitute the N-terminal domain or lobe and 
                     helices 7-12 constitute the C-terminal domain or lobe) and 
                     a 12-residue amphipathic linking helix (&alpha;6-7); the 12
                     TM helices form four pseudo-symmetrical 3 helix repeats 
                     with helices 1, 4, 7 and 10 contributing to the formation 
                     of a central substrate binding cavity which is lined with 
                     hydrophobic residues and contains the two proton-titratable
                     residues, E26 and D34. D34 is directly involved in 
                     chloramphenicol binding and is likely to be the residue 
                     that gets deprotonated upon chloramphenical binding |CITS: 
                     [26238402]|. Elements of the MdfA-mediated transport cycle 
                     vary from those predicted by the canonical alternating 
                     access mechanism; MdfA may have a lateral gate exposed to 
                     the inner membrane for the entry of lipophilic substrates 
                     |CITS: [32860775]|.
                     In addition to its role in multidrug resistance, MdfA also 
                     plays a physiological role in alkaline pH tolerance. 
                     <I>mdfA</I> null mutants are sensitive to mild alkaline 
                     conditions; overexpression of <i>mdfA</i> confers extreme 
                     alkaline pH resistance, allowing growth of cells under 
                     conditions that are close to those used normally by 
                     alkaliphiles (up to pH 10). Purified MdfA catalyzes 
                     Na<sup>+</sup> or K<sup>+</sup>-dependent proton transport 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS:[15371593]|. <i>mdfA</i> has been 
                     implicated in arabinose efflux |CITS: [22952739]|. 
                     <i>mdfA</i> is one of 35 efflux-encoding genes that have 
                     been deleted or inactivated in 'Efflux KnockOut-35' 
                     (EKO-35) -  a K-12 BW25133-derived strain that lacks 35 
                     efflux pumps and can be used as a platform to study their 
                     function |CITS: [36065018][36318669]|.
                     <i>mdfA</i>: <U>m</U>ulti<U>d</U>rug <U>f</U>acilitator 
                     <U>A</U> |CITS: [9079913]|<BR><i>cmr</i>: 
                     <U>c</U>hlora<U>m</U>phenicol <U>r</U>esistance |CITS: 
                     [8655497]|
                     Reviews: |CITS:[11321570][17114895][15953929][28951231]|"
