     gene            complement(885952..886209)
                     /gene="grxA"
                     /locus_tag="b0849"
                     /label=grxA
     CDS             complement(885952..886209)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="grxA"
                     /locus_tag="b0849"
                     /product="trifunctional reduced glutaredoxin 1 /
                     protein-disulfide reductase (glutathione) / 
                     S-sulfo-L-cysteine reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.8.4.2"
                     /label=grxA
                     /note="<b>General Information</b><br>Glutaredoxins are 
                     ubiquitous proteins that catalyze the reduction of 
                     disulfides.  Glutaredoxins are similar to thioredoxins; 
                     both are usually small (9-12 kDa), and both are capable of 
                     catalyzing thiol-disulfide exchange reactions. 
                     Representatives of at least one of these two protein 
                     families have been found in all organisms studied, 
                     indicating that proteins of this type are essential for 
                     cellular functions.  A main difference between the two is 
                     the mechanism for their reduction.  Glutaredoxins are 
                     generally reduced by the compound |FRAME: GLUTATHIONE| 
                     (GSH), while thioredoxins are reduced by the specific 
                     flavoenzyme |FRAME: THIOREDOXIN-REDUCT-NADPH-CPLX|.
                     While thioredoxins are found to be general reductants of 
                     protein disulfides, glutaredoxins are better at reducing 
                     mixed disulfides between proteins (or low molecular weight 
                     thiol-containing compounds) and GSH.  The substrate 
                     specificities of glutaredoxins and thioredoxins appear to 
                     depend on geometric and electrostatic complementarity and 
                     long-distance electrostatic interactions between the 
                     redoxin and its target |CITS: [29861944]|.
                     <b>About This Protein</b>
                     Grx1 is the first of four glutaredoxins discovered in <i>E.
                     coli</i> |CITS: [7783][6352262]|.  Grx1, |FRAME: 
                     GRXB-MONOMER Grx2| and |FRAME: GRXC-MONOMER Grx3| all carry
                     the classic dithiol active site CPYC, while |FRAME: 
                     EG12181-MONOMER Grx4| has a monothiol (CGFS) active site 
                     |CITS: [7937896][8636094][15833738]|.  The most effective 
                     disulfide-reducing proteins in the cytoplasm have been 
                     reported to be Grx1 and thioredoxins 1 and 2 |CITS: 
                     [9755155]|; ribonucleotide reductase (RNR) appears to be 
                     the only essential enzyme that requires at least one of 
                     these proteins |CITS: [15123823]|.  Grx1 is the primary 
                     electron carrier involved in ribonucleotide reduction 
                     |CITS: [11893749]|.
                     An experiment using the fluorescent disulfide probe |FRAME:
                     CPD-26606| to measure thioredoxin and glutaredoxin reducing
                     activity <i>in vivo</i> concluded that the thioredoxin 
                     system was the predominant disulfide reductase for fast 
                     disulfide reduction in |FRAME: TAX-562| |CITS: [35204259]|.
                     Contrary to these findings, an experiment utilizing a 
                     different probe, |FRAME: CPD-26605|, concluded that Grx2 
                     and Grx3 exhibited higher activity in reducing the 
                     disulfide probe than the other redoxins (Grx1 and 
                     thioredoxins 1 and 2) |CITS: [35107099]|.
                     The catalytic mechanisms of the reduction of protein 
                     disulfide model substrates by Grx1 with GSH as the electron
                     donor, as well as of the reverse (oxidation) reaction, have
                     been studied |CITS: [29675162]|.  Grx1 reduces glutathione 
                     (GSH)-mixed disulfides such as glutathionylated peptides; a
                     glutaredoxin-GSH mixed disulfide is formed as an 
                     intermediate, which is then reduced by GSH |CITS: 
                     [16956877][19361272]|.
                     The standard redox potential <i>E<sup>o'</sup></i> of Grx1 
                     was determined to be -233 mV |CITS: [9388218]|.  The 
                     oxidized and reduced forms of glutaredoxin show similar 
                     thermal stability |CITS: [2036416]|; the backbone dynamics 
                     of both forms were characterized by NMR |CITS: [9125525]|. 
                     The Tyr13 residue within the dithiol active site of Grx1 is
                     AMPylated by |FRAME: G6924-MONOMER SelO| |CITS: 
                     [30270044]|.
                     An N-terminally extended form of glutaredoxin is expressed 
                     from a translation initiation codon located five codons 
                     upstream of the regular translation initiation site |CITS: 
                     [2036416][1821800]|.  NMR assignments and solution 
                     structures of reduced and oxidized Grx1 and the mixed 
                     disulfide of Grx1(C14S) with glutathione or an RNR peptide 
                     have been solved |CITS: [1889405][1942053][1304339][139071 
                     5][8269921][8107093][10493864]|. The binding site of the 
                     |FRAME: NRDA-MONOMER B1 subunit| of ribonucleotide 
                     reductase on Grx1 has been mapped |CITS: [9558318]|.  
                     Reduced Grx1 was analyzed by molecular dynamics simulations
                     and electrostatic calculations, providing a revised view of
                     the active site |CITS: [14962389]|.
                     In the presence of glutathione, Grx1 is able to reduce the 
                     disulfide bond within OxyR <i>in vitro</i> and thereby 
                     deactivate the transcription factor |CITS: [9497290]|. 
                     Cysteine disulfides within apo-FNR appear to be reduced by 
                     Grx1, 2 and 3 |CITS: [15135079]|.  Reduced glutaredoxin can
                     act as a hydrogen donor for |FRAME: PAPSSULFOTRANS-MONOMER 
                     PAPS reductase| <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [7016827][10075658][17305371]|.  <i>In vivo</i>, Grx1 can 
                     partially replace the dedicated NrdH protein to reduce the 
                     class Ib ribonucleotide reductase NrdEF |CITS: [16771665]|.
                     A Cys14Ser point mutant of Grx1 retains 38% of the 
                     glutathione disulfide oxidoreductase activity using the 
                     artificial acceptor hydroxyethyl disulfide, but has lost 
                     protein disulfide reductase activity |CITS: [1390715]|. 
                     Grx1 catalyzes the reductive cleavage of |FRAME: 
                     SULFO-CYSTEINE| to |FRAME: CYS| and |FRAME: SO3| <i>in 
                     vitro</i>.  <i>In vivo</i>, overexpression of Grx1 in 
                     &Delta;cysK cells grown with thiosulfate and sulfate as the
                     sulfur sources significantly enhanced L-cysteine 
                     production, presumably by the action of Grx1 on |FRAME: 
                     SULFO-CYSTEINE|, which is produced from thiosulfate by 
                     |FRAME: ACSERLYB-CPLX| |CITS: [22607201]|.
                     <i>In vitro</i>, Grx1 is able to act as a protein oxidase 
                     (forming disulfide bonds) via formation of a reactive mixed
                     disulfide between one active site cysteine and glutathione 
                     |CITS: [15814611]|.
                     Initial data on mutant phenotypes was ambiguous; |CITS: 
                     [3275617]| noted that <i>grx</i> mutants were deficient in 
                     sulfate and ribonucleotide reduction, while |CITS: 
                     [3277191]| noted no obvious growth defect.  A <i>grxA 
                     trxA</i> double mutant is unable to grow in minimal medium 
                     |CITS: [8702583][9188456]|.
                     Levels of Grx1 are lower in stationary phase than 
                     exponential phase |CITS: [11893749]|.  Expression of 
                     <i>grxA</i> is induced by OxyR in response to hydrogen 
                     peroxide |CITS: [9294462][10788450]| and paraquat |CITS: 
                     [10788450]|; in a regulatory loop, OxyR activity is 
                     downregulated by Grx1 |CITS: [9497290]|.  <i>grxA</i> 
                     transcription increases significantly in a <i>trxA gshA</i>
                     double mutant compared to wild type |CITS: [9660805]|; 
                     induction is dependent on OxyR |CITS: [10788450]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [3152490][2668278][8430514][11018134][1096 
                     3327][11544348][14713336][19691428]|<br>Comment: |CITS: 
                     [9754321]|"
