                     /label=ybjC
     CDS             886369..886656
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ybjC"
                     /locus_tag="b0850"
                     /product="DUF1418 domain-containing protein YbjC"
                     /label=ybjC
                     /note="<i>ybjC</i> expression is down regulated in an
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strain engineered for increased 
                     tolerance to <i>n</i>-butanol; a &Delta;<i>ybjC</i> strain 
                     has increased <i>n</i>-butanol tolerance |CITS: 
                     [27252779]|.
                     A YbjC-GFP fusion protein localizes to the membrane region 
                     |CITS: [27252779]|.
                     The <i>nfsA</i> and <i>ybjC</i> genes are co-transcribed; 
                     transcription is induced by paraquat and appears to be 
                     directly regulated by SoxS |CITS: [11741843]|."
                     /translation="MRAIGKLPKGVLILEFIGMMLLAVALLSVSDSLSLPEPFSRPEVQ
                     ILMIFLGVLLMLPAAVVVILQVAKRLAPQLMNRPPQYSRSEREKDNDANH"
                     /go_component="membrane [goid 0016020] [evidence IDA]
                     [PMID 27252779]"
                     /go_process="response to paraquat [goid 1901562] [evidence
                     IEP] [PMID 11741843]"
                     /go_process="response to butan-1-ol [goid 1901422]
                     [evidence IEP] [PMID 27252779]"
     gene            886640..887362
                     /gene="nfsA"
                     /locus_tag="b0851"
                     /label=nfsA
     CDS             886640..887362
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nfsA"
                     /locus_tag="b0851"
                     /product="trifunctional NADPH-dependent nitro/quinone
                     reductase NfsA / NADPH-dependent nitroreductase / 
                     NADPH-dependent quinone reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.7.1.16; 1.6.5.10"
                     /label=nfsA
                     /note="The <i>nfsA</i>-encoded nitroreductase is the major 
                     oxygen-insensitive nitroreductase present in <i>E. 
                     coli</i>. NfsA uses only NADPH and has broad electron 
                     acceptor specificity |CITS: [8755878]|. The physiologically
                     relevant substrates may be quinones |CITS: [10097071]|. 
                     Nitroreductases can be used as prodrug activators in cancer
                     therapy |CITS: [19455141][19852945]|. NfsA catalyzes the 
                     reduction of nitrocompounds using a ping-pong Bi-Bi 
                     mechanism, transferring two electrons |CITS: 
                     [8755878][10531489][27986535]|. The mechanism for 
                     two-electron reduction of quinones is most consistent with 
                     a single-step hydride transfer |CITS: [27986535]|. Turnover
                     of the enzyme is limited by the oxidative half-reaction 
                     |CITS: [27986535]|. The reduction of nitrocompounds to 
                     hydroxylamines occurs via a nitroso intermediate; ascorbate
                     inhibition patterns suggest that the reduction of the 
                     nitroso intermediate occurs directly by NADPH rather than 
                     enzymatically |CITS: [29987261]|.
                     The R203 residue plays a key role in binding the NADPH 
                     substrate |CITS: [11034992]|, and F42 is important for 
                     activity |CITS: [23801116]|.  A single amino acid 
                     substitution, E99G, transforms NfsA into an active flavin 
                     reductase |CITS: [9440535]|. Evolution of NfsA for 
                     increased catalytic activity towards chloramphenicol 
                     eliminates the enzyme's ability to reduce 1,4-benzoquinone 
                     |CITS: [33185191]|.
                     NfsA has chromate [Cr(VI)] reductase activity |CITS: 
                     [12902220][15250887]| which involves a single-electron 
                     transfer mechanism and generates a flavin semiquinone form 
                     of the enzyme. This leads to the generation of reactive 
                     oxygen species |CITS: [15250887]|.
                     A crystal structure of NfsA was solved at 1.7 &Aring; 
                     resolution |CITS: [10531489][11034992]|. Structures of NfsA
                     with ligands also have been determined and modeled 
                     indicating that prior to reduction of the substrate, NADPH 
                     may be necessary at one active site of the dimer for 
                     NADP<sup>+</sup> to be released from the other site |CITS: 
                     [34142705][35648111]|.
                     Expression of NfsA is induced by paraquat and 
                     glycolaldehyde; induction is dependent on the SoxRS system 
                     |CITS: [10097071][11741843][12392714]|.
                     Mutations in <i>nfsA</i> cause resistance to nitrofurazone 
                     and furazolidone |CITS: [338576][9791100]|. Resistance to 
                     nitrofurantoin via mutation of <i>nfsA</i> reduces the 
                     fitness of clinical isolates of <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [18544599]|. Overproduction of NfsA causes resistance to 
                     adriamycin |CITS: [7568050]| and increases tolerance to 
                     chromate |CITS: [15250887]|. NfsA has been implicated in 
                     the ability to utilize 2,4,6-trinitrotoluene (TNT) as a 
                     source of nitrogen |CITS: [17504490]|. An <i>nfsA nfsB</i> 
                     double mutant retains 30% of wt nitroreductase activity 
                     under aerobic conditions |CITS: [23795903]|.
                     The reduction of nitroaromatic compounds in the gut to 
                     nitroso- or hydroxylamino-compounds, most of which are 
                     carcinogenic and mutagenic agents, is considered harmful. 
                     The anti-mutagenic activity of cloves (the flower buds of 
                     the tree |FRAME: TAX-219868|) has been attributed to the 
                     presence of compounds such as |FRAME: CPD-14493| and 
                     |FRAME: CPD-17769|, which were found to be potent 
                     competitive inhibitors of NfsA |CITS: [35907649]|.
                     MdaA: ""<b>m</b>odulator of <b>d</b>rug <b>a</b>ctivity 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [7568050]|
                     NfsA: ""<b>n</b>itro<b>f</b>uran <b>s</b>ensitivity 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [338576]|
                     Review: |CITS: [18355273]|
                     Commentary: |CITS: [34554213]|"
