     gene            complement(904787..906505)
                     /gene="poxB"
                     /locus_tag="b0871"
                     /label=poxB
     CDS             complement(904787..906505)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="poxB"
                     /locus_tag="b0871"
                     /product="bifunctional pyruvate oxidase / acetoin
                     synthesis"
                     /EC_number="1.2.5.1"
                     /label=poxB
                     /note="Pyruvate oxidase is a peripheral membrane enzyme
                     that catalyzes the oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate to
                     form acetate and CO<sub>2</sub>. The reaction is coupled to
                     the electron transport chain via ubiquinone. Metabolism of 
                     pyruvate by pyruvate oxidase is less efficient than the 
                     route via |FRAME: PYRUVATEDEH-CPLX| (PDH); however, the 
                     pyruvate oxidase route is important for wild-type growth 
                     efficiency and responsible for a significant amount of 
                     pyruvate metabolism under aerobic conditions |CITS: 
                     [11390679][15736164][19861135]|. PoxB is the main pathway 
                     for acetate production in stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [16080684]|. Under aerobic phosphate starvation conditions,
                     pyruvate flux is diverted from PDH to PoxB, which may 
                     decrease oxidative stress |CITS: [15489448][17209032]|. 
                     Modern systems biology approaches have been used to enable 
                     a more thorough understanding of the transition to overflow
                     metabolism (excretion of acetate) |CITS: 
                     [19861135][21122111]| and growth on glycerol as the carbon 
                     source |CITS: [22513097]|.
                     The enzyme is a homotetramer, although multimerization does
                     not appear to be necessary for enzyme activity <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [2040613]|. The enzyme must be activated 
                     by lipidation or limited proteolysis, both involving the 
                     protein's C-terminal 23 residues, a membrane-anchoring 
                     domain known as the '&alpha;-peptide' |CITS: 
                     [16593486][3527254][3902830][3545288]|. A mutant protein 
                     lacking the &alpha;-peptide is active <i>in vitro</i>, but 
                     can not be activated by lipids and is not active <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [3527254]|. The domain that interacts with 
                     lipid appears to be formed by contribution from two 
                     subunits |CITS: [2040613][7713884][9305946]|. The presence 
                     of the substrate pyruvate and the TPP cofactor causes a 
                     conformational change in the C terminus, which goes from a 
                     solvent-inaccessible state in which the C termini within 
                     the tetramer do not interact with each other to a 
                     solvent-accessible state in which C termini are proximal 
                     |CITS: [7713884][9305946]|. The active site residue V380 is
                     an important determinant of substrate specificity |CITS: 
                     [11104678]|.
                     Mutations of <i>poxB</i> that abolish pyruvate oxidase 
                     enzymatic activity were isolated by selecting for lack of 
                     growth in the absence of acetate in an <i>aceEF</i> mutant 
                     background |CITS: [6341362]|. The <i>poxB3</i> (P536S) and 
                     <i>poxB4</i> (A467T) mutations localize to the C-terminal 
                     domain |CITS: [3522547]|. The <i>poxB3</i> mutant is 
                     altered in lipid binding and has 15% of wild type activity 
                     |CITS: [16593486]|. The study of additional mutants has 
                     contributed to a detailed understanding of PoxB interaction
                     with and activation by lipids |CITS: [2663858][9305946]|.
                     Crystal structures of full-length PoxB and the 
                     proteolytically activated &Delta;23 protein have been 
                     solved at 2.9 and 2.5 &Aring; resolution, showing that 
                     activation is the result of a conformational rearrangement 
                     that uncovers the active site |CITS: [18323602][18988747]|.
                     When the FAD cofactor is oxidized, the &alpha;-peptide is 
                     tightly clamped over the active site funnel. Reduction of 
                     the cofactor makes the &alpha;-peptide flexible and 
                     accessible to lipidation (or proteolytic removal <i>in 
                     vitro</i>), which prevents the peptide from covering the 
                     active site |CITS: [36727297]|. PoxB is evolutionarily 
                     related to the large subunits of <i>E. coli</i> 
                     acetohydroxy acid synthases (AHAS) |FRAME: EG10494 IlvB|, 
                     |FRAME: G8221 IlvG|, and |FRAME: EG10500 IlvI| |CITS: 
                     [3016647][3045082][2651151]| and |FRAME: GLYOCARBOLIG-CPLX|
                     |CITS: [8440684]|.
                     Gene expression and/or protein abundance increases at 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [8022274]|, in response to the 
                     herbicide and acetolactate synthase inhibitor, sulfometuron
                     methyl |CITS: [9473030]|, in response to osmotic stress 
                     |CITS: [17015655]|, and during growth on glycerol compared 
                     to growth on glucose as the carbon source |CITS: 
                     [22513097]|. <i>poxB</i> is part of the &sigma;<sup>S</sup>
                     regulon |CITS: [8022274][9473030][19838295]|.  Gene 
                     expression decreases under anaerobic conditions |CITS: 
                     [8022274]|. <i>poxB</i> transcript levels are higher in a 
                     K-12 strain than in <i>E. coli</i> B, contributing to 
                     higher acetate production in K-12 strains |CITS: 
                     [15063611][15806547]|. Manipulation of metabolic flux 
                     through pyruvate has been successfully used as a strategy 
                     in metabolic engineering; e.g. |CITS: 
                     [15543598][15781417][15801810][17715942][18806 
                     005][22144366]|. Knock outs of both poxB and |FRAME: 
                     EG20173| reduces acetic acid production and significant 
                     increases cytidine biosynthesis yield with reduced glucose 
                     consumption |CITS: [39890743]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [19476487]|"
