     gene            complement(910808..911503)
                     /gene="aqpZ"
                     /locus_tag="b0875"
                     /label=aqpZ
     CDS             complement(910808..911503)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aqpZ"
                     /locus_tag="b0875"
                     /product="water channel AqpZ"
                     /label=aqpZ
                     /note="AqpZ is a water channel, or aquaporin, that allows 
                     bi-directional passive diffusion of water across the inner 
                     membrane. It is a member of the Major Intrinsic Protein 
                     (MIP) family, a large and diverse family of transmembrane 
                     channels that transport a range of substrates by energy 
                     independent mechanisms |CITS: [24225317]|. AqpZ mediates 
                     the rapid entry or exit of water in response to changes in 
                     extracellular osmolarity |CITS: [10400575]|.
                     AqpZ structure has been analysed by electron microscopy 
                     |CITS: [10518953]|, atomic force microscopy |CITS: 
                     [10487750]| and x-ray crystallography |CITS: 
                     [14691544][14993693][17376483][16239219]|. AqpZ contains 6 
                     transmembrane helices and two half membrane helices with 
                     the N and C-termini located in the cytoplasm. AqpZ forms 
                     homotetramers of 4 monomer channels. The monomer channel 
                     adopts an 'hour-glass' structure with periplasmic and 
                     cytoplasmic conical shaped vestibules connected by a narrow
                     central amphipathic pore which constitutes a water 
                     conduction pathway. A selectivity filter, formed by the 
                     side chains of Phe43, His174, Arg189 and Thr183 is located 
                     at the narrowest point in the channel. Two distinct 
                     conformations of the channel have been obtained, 
                     differentiated by the side chain displacement of Arg189. 
                     The conformations may represent open and closed forms of 
                     the channel or they could be dynamic conformations 
                     occurring during water permeation (see also |CITS: 
                     [29799200]|).
                     Purified AqpZ, reconstituted into proteoliposomes, displays
                     high osmotic water permeability |CITS:[10518952]|. 
                     Expression of <I>aqpZ</I> in <I>Xenopus</I> oocytes, 
                     produces a marked increase in osmotic water permeability 
                     |CITS: [7493926]|. Expression of <i>aqpZ</i> from a plasmid
                     increases osmotic water permeability |CITS: [15859950]|. An
                     <i>aqpZ::lacZ-kan</i> knockout mutant is viable but forms 
                     small colonies in rich and minimal media and has reduced 
                     viability in hypotonic media |CITS:[9520416]|. No 
                     phenotypic effect could be detected in an <i>aqpZ::cam</i> 
                     knockout strain under a range of conditions 
                     |CITS:[12107150]|.
                     Cardiolipin stabilises and modulates the function of AqpZ 
                     |CITS: [24899312]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [30414848][33581256][40047061]|).
                     <I>aqpZ</I> mRNA is monocistronic. Expression of 
                     <I>aqpZ-lacZ</I> from a low copy plasmid peaks during mid 
                     logarithmic growth and is dependent upon the extracellular 
                     osmolality |CITS:[9520416]|. Expression of a chromosomal 
                     <i>aqpZ-lacZ</i> fusion is independent of osmolality and 
                     increases in a &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent manner as cell
                     growth enters the stationary phase |CITS:[12107150]|. AqpZ 
                     has been used in the development of biomimetic aquaporin 
                     membranes |CITS: [18077364][32872107][32810188]|.
                     Reviews:  |CITS:[10931322][16837191][17047223]|"
