     gene            914691..915806
                     /gene="macA"
                     /locus_tag="b0878"
                     /label=macA
     CDS             914691..915806
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="macA"
                     /locus_tag="b0878"
                     /product="multifunctional ABC-type tripartite efflux pump
                     membrane fusion protein / ABC-type tripartite efflux pump /
                     macrolide antibiotic efflux pump / bacitracin efflux pump /
                     colisitin efflux pump"
                     /label=macA
                     /note="MacA is the membrane fusion protein (MFP) component
                     of an ABC-type tripartite efflux transporter - MacAB-TolC -
                     which contributes to antibiotic resistance in 
                     drug-hypersensitive strains and is also implicated in 
                     protoporphyrin IX and glycolipid export.
                     Purified MacA (residues 32-371) forms a hexamer; the MacA 
                     protomer consists of an extended &alpha;-helical hairpin 
                     domain, a lipoyl domain and a &beta;-barrel domain; the 6 
                     protomers pack together in a side-by-side arrangement to 
                     form a structure resembling an 'upside-down funnel' |CITS: 
                     [19254725]|. A fourth domain, termed the membrane-proximal,
                     or MP domain is described in |CITS: [28504659]|. A 
                     non-cleavable signal-like sequence anchors MacA to the 
                     inner membrane |CITS: [11544226], [17214741]|. MacA  
                     interacts with MacB and with TolC. MacA stimulates the 
                     ATPase activity of MacB when reconstituted into 
                     proteoliposomes and in <i>E. coli</i> membrane vesicles 
                     |CITS: [17214741]|. Binding of MacA to MacB stabilises the 
                     ATP bound conformation of MacB |CITS: [21696464]|. In a 
                     cryo-EM structure of the tripartite complex, the helical 
                     hairpin domains pack into a cylinder to form a helical 
                     barrel which interacts with TolC in the open state to form 
                     a long continuous channel and the &beta;-barrel and MP 
                     domains interact with MacB. A conserved glutamine residue 
                     (Q209) located in the lipoyl domain of the MacA protomer 
                     forms a hydrogen-bonded network and constriction point in 
                     the hexamer which may serve as a gate favouring outward 
                     movement of substrates |CITS: [28504659]|.
                     Purified MacA contains a tightly bound rough-type 
                     lipopolysaccharide (R-LPS) molecule; one molecule of R-LPS 
                     appears to bind one MacA molecule; MacAB-TolC may be 
                     involved in the transport of outer membrane glycolipids 
                     |CITS: [23974027]|.
                     "
