                     /note="MacB is the ATPase component of a tripartite efflux 
                     complex - MacAB-TolC - which contributes to antibiotic 
                     resistance in drug-hypersensitive strains and is also 
                     implicated in protoporphyrin IX and glycolipid export. MacB
                     contains 4 transmembrane (TM) segments and an N-terminal 
                     nucleotide binding domain that is located in the cytoplasm;
                     a large periplasmic loop is located between between TM1 and
                     2 |CITS: [11544226][12832048]|. MacB is dimeric; MacB 
                     interacts with MacA |CITS: [18955484]|; the periplasmic 
                     domain of MacB interacts with the &beta;-barrel and 
                     membrane-proximal domains of MacA |CITS: [28504659]|. MacB 
                     is designated as the type specimen for the type VII ABC 
                     transporter superfamily; salient features of the MacB fold 
                     include its four-transmembrane helix topology and large 
                     periplasmic domain which is elevated above the inner 
                     membrane by a four helix bundle or 'stalk' |CITS: 
                     [29109272]|. MacB couples cytoplasmic ATP hydrolysis to 
                     transmembrane and periplasmic conformational change in a 
                     process termed 'mechanotransmission' |CITS: [29109272]|.
                     Purified MacB, in detergents and reconstituted into 
                     proteoliposomes, displays a basal ATPase activity which is 
                     stimulated by addition of MacA |CITS: [17214741][18955484]|
                     (see also |CITS: [36442691]|). Purified MacB binds 
                     erythromycin; MacA enhances MacB's capacity to bind 
                     erythromycin |CITS: [18955484]|. MacB lacking the 
                     periplasmic loop, retains nucleotide binding capacity and 
                     ATPase activity but is uncoupled from macrolide efflux; the
                     periplasmic loop is required for MacA dependent stimulation
                     of ATP hydrolysis and also contributes to the recruitment 
                     of TolC into the trans-envelope complex |CITS: [23057817]|.
                     In a cryo-EM structure of the tripartite complex, MacB is 
                     dimeric and has an opening to the periplasm which may 
                     represent the access point for periplasmic substrates 
                     |CITS: [28504659]|.
                     <i>macB</i> is one of 35 efflux-encoding genes that have 
                     been deleted or inactivated in 'Efflux KnockOut-35' 
                     (EKO-35) -  a K-12 BW25133-derived strain that lacks 35 
                     efflux pumps and can be used as a platform to study their 
                     function |CITS: [36065018][36318669]|. Swimming motility is
                     increased in the <i>macB</i> Keio collection mutant 
                     (BW25113 &Delta;<i>macB::kan</i>) |CITS: [39529380]|. 
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [29892271]|"
