     gene            complement(917822..918046)
                     /gene="cspD"
                     /locus_tag="b0880"
                     /label=cspD
     CDS             complement(917822..918046)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cspD"
                     /locus_tag="b0880"
                     /product="DNA replication inhibitor CspD"
                     /label=cspD
                     /note="CspD is a toxin that appears to act by inhibiting 
                     replication |CITS: [11260474][20105222]|, and which 
                     influences persister cell formation |CITS: [19909729]|. 
                     CspD forms a homodimer in solution, is able to bind 
                     single-stranded DNA and RNA, and packs ssDNA into 
                     structures that are distinguishable from SSB-coated DNA. 
                     Binding does not show sequence specificity. <i>In 
                     vitro</i>, CspD inhibits initiation and elongation of 
                     minichromosome replication |CITS: [11260474]|. The CspD 
                     protein localizes to the nucleoid in stationary-phase cells
                     |CITS: [11520610]|.
                     Expression of <i>cspD</i> is not inducible by cold shock 
                     |CITS: [8022261]| and is inversely correlated with growth 
                     rate; it is induced at stationary phase and upon glucose 
                     starvation, but is not dependent on &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, 
                     the stationary phase sigma factor |CITS: 
                     [9260955][24952137]|. Expression is upregulated by the 
                     golbal metabolic regulator CRP |CITS: [24509317]|. |CITS: 
                     [32753505]| showed <i>cspD</i> to be highly expressed at 
                     all time points including long-term stationary phase. 
                     Diverse expression responses of the cold shock proteins, 
                     CspA-I, were found under different stress conditions, 
                     specifically cold, heat, rich vs minimal media and 
                     oxidative stress caused by H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. 
                     Expression of <i>cspD</i> is down upon exposure to 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> stress and throughout cold-shock
                     conditions but initially up under heat stress. This gene 
                     was up in mid, late and stationary phases of the growth 
                     cycle for both rich media and only showed small initial 
                     response in minimal media |CITS: [40312551]|.
                     RNA polymerase stalls at a site near the <i>cspD</i> 
                     promoter |CITS: [18333883]|. The <i>cspD</i> mRNA region 
                     was identified in a screen for double-stranded RNAs and 
                     belongs to a set of coding genes that substantially overlap
                     a convergent RNA transcript. In an RNase III mutant, 
                     <i>cspD</i> mRNA levels and half life and CspD protein 
                     levels are increased, suggesting that CspD expression is 
                     regulated by antisense RNA |CITS: [32518184]|.
                     Reports regarding the regulation of <i>cspD</i> expression 
                     by the MqsR/MqsA toxin/antitoxin system differ. |CITS: 
                     [20105222]| showed that <i>cspD</i> expression is repressed
                     by the MqsR/MqsA complex and MqsA alone, while MqsR 
                     overproduction induced <i>cspD</i> expression. In contrast,
                     |CITS: [31848281]| showed no effect of MqsRA on the 
                     expression of <i>cspD</i>.
                     CspD protein levels are regulated by proteolysis. CspD is 
                     an unstable protein whose half life depends on the growth 
                     phase and growth rate; its degradation is due to the 
                     |FRAME: EG10542-MONOMER| |CITS: [21435040]|.
                     <i>cspD</i> null mutants are viable, but overproduction of 
                     the CspD protein is lethal |CITS: [11260474]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9484881][12959196]|"
