     gene            918369..918689
                     /gene="clpS"
                     /locus_tag="b0881"
                     /label=clpS
     CDS             918369..918689
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="clpS"
                     /locus_tag="b0881"
                     /product="specificity factor for ClpA-ClpP
                     chaperone-protease complex"
                     /label=clpS
                     /note="ClpS is a specificity adapter for the |FRAME:
                     CPLX0-3104| protease complex, targeting it to aggregated 
                     proteins, N-end rule substrates, and others.
                     ClpS binds to the ClpA amino-terminus and affects the 
                     specificity of protein degradation by the ClpAP 
                     chaperone-protease complex, possibly by interfering with 
                     interactions between substrate and ClpA |CITS: [11931773]|.
                      ClpS stimulates ClpAP recognition and degradation of 
                     aggregated protein substrates while it inhibits degradation
                     of non-aggregated substrates, including ClpA |CITS: 
                     [11931773]|.  ClpS also links ClpAP to the N-end rule 
                     degradation pathway, binding to the amino-terminal 
                     destabilizing residues in N-end substrates and targeting 
                     those substrates for degradation by ClpAP |CITS: 
                     [16467841]|.
                     A mutant protein lacking the flexible 17 amino-terminal 
                     residues shows a defect in ClpA inhibition but does not 
                     show a defect in interaction with ClpA |CITS: [12235156]|. 
                     Binding of ClpA and ClpS requires ClpS residues E79 and 
                     K84, as does proteolytic activity by the ClpAPS complex 
                     |CITS: [12426582]|.
                     ClpS has structural similarity to the ribosomal protein 
                     L7/L12 |CITS: [12235156][12576022]|.  ClpS has similarity 
                     to the eukaryotic N-recognin E3 ubiquitin ligase and to 
                     proteins of plant chloroplast, actinobacteria, and 
                     cyanobacteria |CITS: [12576022]|.
                     Crystal structures of ClpS bound the the amino-terminal 
                     region of ClpA are presented at 2.3 |CITS: [12235156]|, 2.5
                     |CITS: [12426582]|, and 3.3 |CITS: [12235156]| &Aring; 
                     resolution, and crystallization has been described |CITS: 
                     [12077445]|.
                     Crystal structures of the ClpS-ClpA N-domain complex, and 
                     the complex with bound Zn<sup>2+</sup> have been solved at 
                     2.25 and 2.35 &Aring; resolution, respectively |CITS: 
                     [15037248]|. Structures have also been solved for ClpS in 
                     complex with each of three peptides containing the 
                     destabilizing N-terminal residues Leu, Phe or Trp at 1.70, 
                     2.15 and 2.90 &Aring; resolution, respectively |CITS: 
                     [19373253]|. Structures of apo-ClpS, ClpS complexed with 
                     peptides containing N-terminal Phe or Leu, and the ClpS 
                     ring complex have been solved at 1.40, 2.05, 1.70 and 2.90 
                     &Aring; resolution, respectively |CITS: [21777811]|.
                     In the absence of ClpS, ClpAP can recognize N-end motifs 
                     but with significantly reduced substrate affinity. In 
                     addition to enhancing the degradation of N-end-rule 
                     proteins, ClpS inhibits ClpAP degradation of |FRAME: 
                     EG30100 ""SsrA""|-tagged substrates |CITS: [17322400]|. The
                     ClpS-ClpAP complex specifically recognizes an N-end motif 
                     using both a free alpha-amino group and the identity of the
                     N-terminal residue. The identity of adjacent residues can 
                     also affect recognition, and the distance between the N-end
                     residue and the folded portion of a protein is critical 
                     |CITS: [18550545]|. Studies of how ClpS regulates substrate
                     choice by ClpAP suggested that it may function as an 
                     allosteric effector of ClpAP |CITS: [18297088]|.
                     ClpS alone is responsible for sensing the nature of 
                     N-terminal residues in N-degron model substrates, making it
                     essential for the N-end rule pathway |CITS: [19317833]|. 
                     Data suggest that a single ClpS monomer is necessary for 
                     optimal delivery of substrate to the ClpA hexamer |CITS: 
                     [20068042]|.
                     Following the formation of a ClpS-substrate-ClpA complex, 
                     an N-degron is transferred from ClpS to ClpA to initiate 
                     degradation. Mutant studies showed that an essential step 
                     in substrate delivery involves entrance of the N-terminal 
                     extension of ClpS into the ClpA translocation pore, 
                     suggesting that ClpA activity may remodel ClpS to promote 
                     substrate transfer |CITS: [25187555]|.
                     A ClpS affinity column has been used to isolate and 
                     characterize N-degron-bearing proteins in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     |CITS:[23960079]|.
                     ClpS shows OxyR-dependent induction of expression by 
                     hydrogen peroxide |CITS: [11443091]|.
                     <i>clpS</i> shows differential codon adaptation, resulting 
                     in differential translation efficiency signatures, in 
                     thermophilic microbes.  It was therefore predicted to play 
                     a role in the heat shock response.  A <i>clpS</i> deletion 
                     mutant was shown to be more sensitive than wild-type 
                     specifically to heat shock, but not other stresses |CITS: 
                     [24580753]|.
                     Reviews:  |CITS: 
                     [23479435][21781991][19781640][19050717][17306546]|
                     "
