     gene            928680..932669
                     /gene="ftsK"
                     /locus_tag="b0890"
                     /label=ftsK
     CDS             928680..932669
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftsK"
                     /locus_tag="b0890"
                     /product="cell division DNA translocase FtsK"
                     /label=ftsK
                     /note="FtsK is an essential cell division protein linking
                     cell division and chromosome segregation |CITS: 
                     [18363794]|.  FtsK segregates the terminus region of sister
                     chromosomes |CITS: [23781109]|.  It acts as a DNA 
                     translocase at the division site and is required for 
                     unlinking chromosome dimers.
                     FtsK colocalizes with FtsZ to the septal ring structure; 
                     localization is dependent on FtsZ, FtsA and ZipA, but not 
                     FtsI and FtsQ |CITS: [9495771][9723913][11847116]|. 
                     Conversely, FtsQ, FtsL and FtsI require FtsK for 
                     localization to the Z ring |CITS: [11703663]|.  The FtsK 
                     protein domains involved in the interactions with other 
                     cell division proteins have been mapped |CITS: [18759781]|.
                      When FtsK is overexpressed, Z ring formation and cell 
                     division are inhibited |CITS: [9721304]|. Although the 
                     hierarchy of dependency in the assembly of cell division 
                     proteins is largely linear, recent results showed that 
                     assembly of the cell division machinery is complex |CITS: 
                     [15630023][16824093]|.  The requirement for FtsK for 
                     localization of FtsI is indirect |CITS: [16194242]|.  
                     Overexpression of FtsN partially restores localization of 
                     FtsL and FtsQ in the absence of FtsK |CITS: [17071748]|.
                     The FtsK protein is very large, and its membrane and 
                     cytoplasmic domains appear to have separable functions 
                     during cell division |CITS: [15522074]|.  The N-terminal 
                     domain of FtsK, spanning ~200 amino acids, is sufficient 
                     for targeting FtsK to the septum |CITS: [9495771]| and for 
                     its function in cell division |CITS: [9721304][9723913]|. 
                     This domain shares some functional overlap with FtsQ, FtsB,
                     FtsA, ZipA and FtsN and may be involved in the stability of
                     the division protein machinery |CITS: [16194242]|.  It 
                     contains four transmembrane helices linking two periplasmic
                     loops, one of which contains a zinc metalloprotease 
                     consensus sequence that may |CITS: [10922461]| or may not 
                     |CITS: [25002583]| be required for function of FtsK.  
                     Refinement of FtsK membrane topology has revealed the 
                     presence of a periplasmic loop between TM3 and TM4 that is 
                     required for FtsK function |CITS: [25002583]|.  The 
                     N-terminal domain is sufficient for hexamerization of FtsN 
                     at midcell |CITS: [24302254]|.  As long as it is targeted 
                     to the cell division septum, a truncated form of FtsK that 
                     lacks all transmembrane segments still functions in the 
                     resolution of chromosome dimers, indicating that DNA does 
                     not need to be transported through a pore formed by the 
                     transmembrane helices |CITS: [20033058]|.
                     Following the N-terminal membrane domain is a ~500 residue 
                     cytoplasmic linker region which may function in stabilizing
                     the interactions of FtsK with other cell division proteins 
                     |CITS: [15522074]|.  Two regions within this domain, 
                     179-331 and 332-641, independently interact with FtsZ, 
                     FtsQ, FtsL and FtsI and were found to be required for 
                     normal septation |CITS: [21091498]|.
                     The C-terminal domain of ~500 amino acids is cytoplasmic 
                     and involved in septation and chromosome partitioning 
                     |CITS: [9723927][9829960][10027974]|.  It can be separated 
                     into three domains, &alpha;, &beta; and &gamma; |CITS: 
                     [16916635]|.  The &alpha;&beta; domain contains a 
                     nucleotide binding motif belonging to the AAA family of 
                     ATPases |CITS: [7592387]| and has ATP-dependent DNA 
                     translocase activity |CITS: [11832210][15167891]|, which 
                     has been observed in single molecule assays |CITS: 
                     [15681387][15821742]|.  The translocation step size is ~2 
                     bp per ATP |CITS: [21048089]|.  The chromosomal domain 
                     within which FtsK acts has been identified |CITS: 
                     [15916604][21799784]|.  DNA translocation by FtsK is 
                     directional and is guided by octameric sequences (known as 
                     KOPS - Fts<b>K</b> <b>O</b>rienting <b>P</b>olar 
                     <b>S</b>equences - in <i>E. coli</i>) |CITS: 
                     [16211009][16301526][17041597]|.  The &gamma; domain is a 
                     DNA-binding winged-helix domain that recognizes KOPS |CITS:
                     [17057717]|.  KOPS appear to act only as FtsK loading sites
                     and are not read during DNA translocation |CITS: 
                     [21048089]|.  Real-time imaging of FtsK activity showed 
                     that KOPS binding does not involve scanning along the DNA, 
                     and is enhanced in the presence of ADP and inhibited by ATP
                     |CITS: [22493241]|.  Failure to recognize KOPS has little 
                     effect in wild type <i>E. coli</i>, but more serious 
                     consequences in cell populations where chromosome dimers 
                     occur more frequently |CITS: [19170870]|.
                     Although FtsK can displace proteins from DNA, DNA 
                     translocation by FtsK stops at XerCD-<i>dif</i> sites 
                     |CITS: [19854947]|.  FtsK's DNA translocation activity and 
                     its ability to displace roadblocks on DNA can be separated 
                     |CITS: [20379135]|.  Single-molecule real time imaging 
                     showed that, depending on their relative binding affinity, 
                     FtsK can push, displace, or bypass many other DNA-bound 
                     proteins.  In contrast, an orientation-specific interaction
                     between FtsK and XerD causes reversal of FtsK and prevents 
                     XerCD removal.  Collision with RecBCD leads to reversal or 
                     displacement of FtsK, indicating that RecBCD is a more 
                     powerful motor protein than FtsK |CITS: [24768536]|.
                     Recombination between sister chromosomes causes formation 
                     of chromosome dimers, which must be resolved by 
                     XerCD-mediated recombination between <i>dif</i> sites.
                     The C-terminal domain of FtsK is required for this 
                     activity, activating the recombinase and actively 
                     positioning the <i>dif</i> sites |CITS: 
                     [11832210][12034757][15031713][16553881]|.  In particular, 
                     the &gamma; regulatory subdomain activates the formation of
                     a Holliday junction intermediate by XerD; it is proposed 
                     that this activation of unlinking is normally coupled to 
                     the translocation function of FtsK |CITS: [21371996]|.  
                     Single-molecule techniques have allowed observation of the 
                     activation of XerCD-<i>dif</i> recombination by FtsK |CITS:
                     [24101525][24214995]|.  A topological mechanism for the 
                     unlinking reaction has been proposed |CITS: [24218579]|.
                     Newly replicated chromosomes are topologically linked and 
                     must be decatenated by Topo IV before they can be 
                     physically separated.  FtsK and Topo IV interact physically
                     via the ParC subunit of Topo IV.  Functionally, the 
                     C-terminal domain of FtsK stimulates the decatenation 
                     activity of Topo IV on positively supercoiled DNA |CITS: 
                     [12939258][20081205]|, but not the activity of DNA gyrase 
                     |CITS: [12939258]|.  Unlinking of chromosomes can also be 
                     accomplished by step-wise XerCD-<i>dif</i> recombination in
                     the presence of chromosomal translocation by FtsK |CITS: 
                     [17805344]|.  Unexpectedly, FtsK antagonizes 
                     <i>Ter</i>-induced recombination |CITS: [17376083]|.
                     The crystal structure of a monomeric point mutant of the 
                     motor domain has been solved at 2.7 &Aring; resolution 
                     |CITS: [16916635]|, and a solution structure of the &gamma;
                     winged-helix domain was obtained |CITS: [17057717]|.
                     The cell division defect of an <i>ftsK</i> ts mutant, but 
                     not a deletion mutant, can be suppressed specifically by 
                     deletion of <i>dacA</i>, which encodes PBP5 
                     (peptidoglycan-modifying D-alanine:D-alanine 
                     carboxypeptidase) |CITS: [7592387][9721304]|.  A deletion 
                     of <i>ftsK</i> can be partially suppressed by 
                     overproduction of FtsN |CITS: [9721304]|.
                     <i>ftsK</i> expression is increased as part of the SOS 
                     response, conferring increased resistance to DNA damage 
                     |CITS: [9723913]|.
                     Selected reviews: |CITS: [28373867][25460806][23161015][23 
                     514160][22934648][22047950][20922738][20298190][19243443][ 
                     17511809][17082786][15554958][15491352][15065657][12626683 
                     ][11864592]|"
