     gene            932828..933439
                     /gene="lolA"
                     /locus_tag="b0891"
                     /label=lolA
     CDS             932828..933439
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lolA"
                     /locus_tag="b0891"
                     /product="outer membrane lipoprotein carrier protein"
                     /label=lolA
                     /note="LolA is a periplasmic chaperone which transports 
                     lipoproteins from the inner membrane (IM) to the outer 
                     membrane (OM); LolA is part of the 5-protein LolABCDE 
                     lipoprotein trafficking pathway; LolA receives lipoproteins
                     destined for the outer membrane* from the IM |FRAME: 
                     ABC-62-CPLX ""LolCDE release complex""| and delivers them 
                     to the OM lipoprotein |FRAME: EG11293-MONOMER ""LolB""| for
                     insertion into the OM.
                     LolA is essential for translocation of the major OM 
                     lipoprotein |FRAME: EG10544-MONOMER ""Lpp""|, from the 
                     inner to the outer membrane; LolA is required for the 
                     release of labeled Lpp from spheroplasts; LolA forms a 
                     soluble complex with Lpp <i>in vitro</i>; Lpp is 
                     incorporated into the outer membrane when the LolA-Lpp 
                     complex is incubated with a total membrane preparation 
                     |CITS: [7628437]|. Lpp is transferred from Lpp-LolA to a 
                     water-soluble LolB (mLolB) <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [9384574]|. LolA dependent release of an Lpp derivative 
                     (L10P) from membrane vesicles requires nucleoside 
                     triphosphate hydrolysis and an outer membrane-specific 
                     sorting signal (eg. Ser at position 2 of L10P); L10P 
                     released <i>in vitro</i> in the presence of LolA, localizes
                     to the outer membrane in a LolB-dependent manner |CITS: 
                     [9829994]|. LolA functions as a general chaperone for 
                     lipoproteins that are destined for the outer membrane 
                     |CITS: [10521496]|. Aminoacylation of apolipoprotein is 
                     essential for release of OM specific lipoproteins from the 
                     membrane |CITS: [12198129]| (see also |CITS: [15513925]|). 
                     Depletion of LolA results in a severe growth defect |CITS: 
                     [9849875]|. Depletion of LolA is toxic due to the 
                     accumulation of two lipoproteins - Lpp and OsmB - in the 
                     inner membrane; LolA (and LolB) are dispensable in a 
                     &Delta;<i>lpp</i> &Delta;<i>rcsB cpxA24</i> background (ie 
                     the essentiality of LolA and LolB can be overcome by 
                     activating the Cpx stress response and removing LolA 
                     substrates that are toxic when mislocalized) |CITS: 
                     [28416660]|. Overexpression of <i>lolA</i> activates the 
                     |FRAME: PWY0-1493| inducing the capsular polysaccharide 
                     synthesis (<i>cps</i>) operon-<i>lacZ</i> reporter 
                     consrtuct |CITS: [11758943]|. Mutations which cause the 
                     accumulation of outer membrane specific lipoproteins in the
                     inner membrane induce expression of <i>lolA</i> via the Rcs
                     phosphorelay system |CITS: [22563052]|.
                     LolA crystal structures have been obtained; LolA consists 
                     of an incomplete &beta;-barrel and an &alpha;-helical lid 
                     which enclose a hydrophobic cavity that is likely the 
                     binding site of a lipoprotein's acyl chain; the helical lid
                     is proposed to open and close upon accomodation and release
                     of substrate; LolA and LolB have high structural similarity
                     |CITS: [12839983] [18617521]|. A 'mouth-to-mouth' model for
                     the transfer of lipoproteins from LolA to LolB has been 
                     proposed |CITS:[19307584]| (see also |CITS: [19546215]|). 
                     LolA interacts physically with LolC and this interaction 
                     results in LolA conformational change; the interaction 
                     between LolA and LolCDE occurs exclusively through LolC;  
                     LolA binding to  LolCDE occurs through the 'hook' and 'pad'
                     of LolC and is not regulated by nucleotide binding or 
                     hydrolysis |CITS: [30012603]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [33782615]|). LolA has higher binding affinity to 
                     lipoprotein-bound LolCDE than to apo-LolCDE |CITS: 
                     [33782615]|. Purified LolA accomodates the three acyl 
                     chains of a modified Pal lipoprotein within a hydrophobic 
                     cavity; the lipoprotein amino acids do not interact with 
                     LolA which may explain how it is able to transport diverse 
                     lipoprotein substrates; structural analysis suggests that 
                     insertion of the acyl chains dislodges the LolA-lipoprotein
                     complex from LolCDE |CITS: [36037338]|.
                     The small molecule MAC13243 inhibits LolA function 
                     resulting in increased permeability of the outer membrane 
                     |CITS: [19783991][29247166]| (see also |CITS: [23473681]|. 
                     * Lipoproteins destined for retention in the inner membrane
                     possess a Lol avoidance signal - initially characterized as
                     an Asp residue at position 2 (relative to the N-terminal 
                     Cys residue of a mature lipoprotein) (see |CITS: 
                     [10564520][11592971]| and |CITS: [15276320]| for a review).
                     <i>lol</i>: localization of lipoproteins |CITS: [9384574]| 
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [15221203][15276320][26443779][204 
                     19407][30900542]|<BR>Comments: |CITS: [28461688]|"
