                     /product="pyruvate formate-lyase activating enzyme"
                     /EC_number="1.97.1.4"
                     /label=pflA
                     /note="Under anaerobic conditions, pyruvate formate-lyase 
                     activating enzyme (PflA, PFL-AE) activates |FRAME: 
                     PYRUVFORMLY-MONOMER| by generating an essential glycyl 
                     radical within the enzyme |CITS: [4902610][6364987]|. 
                     PFL-AE belongs to the radical SAM superfamily of proteins 
                     |CITS: [11222759][15289575]|.  The enzyme is also capable 
                     of activating the <i>tdcE</i>-encoded |FRAME: 
                     KETOBUTFORMLY-INACT-MONOMER| |CITS: [9484901]| and |FRAME: 
                     EG11784-MONOMER| |CITS: [11444864]|.
                     PFL-AE uses S-adenosylmethionine (SAM) and reduced 
                     flavodoxin as co-substrates to produce the glycyl radical, 
                     generating 5'-deoxyadenosine and methionine as side 
                     products.  The pro-<i>S</i> hydrogen from Gly734 in 
                     pyruvate formate-lyase is abstracted by the 
                     5'-deoxyadenosyl radical in the active site of PFL-AE and 
                     incorporated into 5'-deoxyadenosine |CITS: 
                     [8175649][9918833]|.  The interaction of the enzyme with 
                     SAM and the mechanism of radical generation has been 
                     investigated |CITS: [HENSHAW00][12824504][15859242]|. 
                     Formation of an organometallic intermediate, &Omega;, which
                     consists of an Fe-C5' bond between the 5'-deoxyadenosyl 
                     radical and the [4Fe4S] cluster, is central to catalysis 
                     |CITS: [27174986][29954180]|.  A monovalent cation 
                     (M<sup>+</sup>), likely K<sup>+</sup>, binds within the 
                     active site, where it interacts with SAM and controls 
                     reactivity |CITS: [28768413]|.
                     A redox-interconvertible [4Fe-4S] cluster is essential for 
                     interaction with SAM |CITS: [9478932][10708574]|.  PFL-AE 
                     undergoes cluster interconversions <i>in vivo</i> in 
                     response to the redox state of the cell |CITS: [19711960]|.
                     Crystal structures of the substrate-free and 7-mer 
                     substrate peptide-bound forms have been solved.  PFL-AE 
                     undergoes a conformational change upon substrate binding 
                     |CITS: [18852451]|.  The conformational change is rate 
                     limiting for the interaction between PFL-AE and PFL |CITS: 
                     [24338017]|.
                     Site-directed mutagenesis showed that the iron-sulfur 
                     cluster-coordinating cysteine residues C30, 34 and 37 are 
                     required for catalytic activity |CITS: [9478932]|.  The 
                     aspartate residues D105 and D130 coordinate M<sup>+</sup> 
                     in the crystal structure; site-directed mutagenesis showed 
                     that only D105 is required for cation binding and catalytic
                     activity |CITS: [28768413]|.
                     PflA expression is not significantly induced by 
                     anaerobiosis |CITS: [3053170][2192229]|.  The fermentation 
                     products of a <i>pflA</i> mutant have been measured with 
                     different carbon sources and under aerobic and microaerobic
                     conditions |CITS: [14673546]|.
                     Act: ""pyruvate formate-lyase <b>act</b>ivating enzyme"" 
                     PflA: ""<b>p</b>yruvate <b>f</b>ormate-<b>l</b>yase 
                     <b>a</b>ctivating enzyme""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [31494759][30346729][25178795][24486374][1 
                     7291766][15581584][11315557][9767563][2248795]|
                     Curator's Note: Several of the publications cited above 
                     lack information on the source of the protein the authors 
                     were studying, most notably |CITS: [27174986]|.  Paper 
                     archaeology suggests that the original source of the 
                     <i>pflA</i> gene used by the Broderick lab was a plasmid 
                     obtained from Dr. John Kozarich."
