     gene            complement(946728..949010)
                     /gene="pflB"
                     /locus_tag="b0903"
                     /label=pflB
     CDS             complement(946728..949010)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pflB"
                     /locus_tag="b0903"
                     /product="bifunctional pyruvate formate-lyase / pyruvate 
                     formate-lyase (inactive)"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.54"
                     /label=pflB
                     /note="<i>Escherichia coli</i> pyruvate-formate lyase
                     (PflB) is a central enzyme of anaerobic metabolism. It 
                     catalyzes the coenzyme A-dependent, non-oxidative cleavage 
                     of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA and formate in anaerobically 
                     growing cells |CITS: [4615902][15516572]| (see pathway 
                     |FRAME: FERMENTATION-PWY|). Under anaerobic conditions, 
                     induction of transcription of <i>pflB</i> involves |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7797| and the |FRAME: PHOSPHO-ARCA| and |FRAME: 
                     ARCB-CPLX| regulatory proteins |CITS: [1592804]|.
                     At the post-translational level the enzyme is regulated by 
                     activation |CITS: [6364987]|. Activation of the inactive 
                     enzyme is catalyzed by the same |FRAME: PFLACTENZ-MONOMER| 
                     that also activates |FRAME: KETOBUTFORMLY-INACT-MONOMER| 
                     |CITS: [9484901]|. This activation process requires reduced
                     flavodoxin that is generated via a coenzyme A-acylating 
                     |FRAME: PYRUFLAVREDUCT-MONOMER|, or |FRAME: 
                     FLAVONADPREDUCT-MONOMER| |CITS: [7042345]|. Activation 
                     introduces a free radical into the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [6369325][9478932]| (see below).
                     PflB utilizes a ""ping-pong"" mechanism that proceeds via 
                     two half-reactions. The enzyme is also active with 
                     2-oxobutanoate as substrate, but pyruvate is the preferred 
                     substrate |CITS: [2248795][4615902][9484901]|. An active 
                     site Gly734 radical is used to reversibly cleave the C1-C2 
                     bond of pyruvate. This radical is also thought to generate 
                     thiyl radicals at active site residues Cys418 and Cys419 
                     for homolytic substrate cleavage |CITS: 
                     [10504733][10648809]|. The relatively stable glycyl radical
                     is sensitive to |FRAME: OXYGEN-MOLECULE| and essential for 
                     catalysis |CITS: [6369325][1310545][11300793]|. Destruction
                     of the glycyl radical by oxygen results in cleavage of the 
                     85 kDa polypeptide to 83 kDa and a 3 kDa fragment and 
                     consequent inactivation of the enzyme |CITS: [1310545]|. 
                     Using PflB G734A and C418A/C419A variants, it was 
                     determined that the glycyl radical formation on G734 is 
                     independent of the intramolecular radical transfer to C419 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [40388635]|.
                     The damaged PFL enzyme may be reactivated by the ""spare 
                     part"" protein |FRAME: EG11784-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [11444864][17013945]|. This small (14-kDa) protein forms a 
                     complex with the damaged PFL dimer and displaces the 
                     C-terminal glycyl radical domain, restoring the catalytic 
                     function of PflB |CITS: [35377165]|.
                     The enzyme can also be deactivated enzymatically. It has 
                     been suggested that deactivation is catalyzed by |FRAME: 
                     ADHE-CPLX| encoded by <i>adhE</i> |CITS: 
                     [2015910][1325457]|. However, subsequent work questioned 
                     this finding noting that small molecule thiols can effect 
                     deactivation |CITS: [17280641]|.
                     Crystal structures have been determined for PflB in several
                     different forms including: non-radical form, in complex 
                     with the substrate analog oxamate, and for a double mutant 
                     |CITS: [10504733]|; in complex with pyruvate and coenzyme 
                     A, oxamate and coenzyme A, and pyruvate |CITS: [12163496]|;
                     inactive enzyme in complex with pyruvate |CITS: 
                     [12454503]|; and a proteolytic fragment of PflB |CITS: 
                     [10089368][10425676]|.
                     PflB has also been the subject of enzyme engineering 
                     studies |CITS: [17341838]| and metabolic engineering 
                     studies. <i>E. coli</i> strains containing a <i>pflB</i> 
                     mutation are used to engineer lactate production |CITS: 
                     [19471981]|.
                     The mechanism of inhibition by methacrylate has been 
                     studied |CITS: [37797332]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17954980][9767563][2248795]|
                     Review: Kessler D. and J. Knappe (1996) ""Anaerobic 
                     Dissimilation of Pyruvate"". EcoSal Print Edition Chapter 
                     15 |CITS: [ecosal]|"
