     gene            953109..954197
                     /gene="serC"
                     /locus_tag="b0907"
                     /label=serC
     CDS             953109..954197
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="serC"
                     /locus_tag="b0907"
                     /product="multifunctional
                     phosphoserine/phosphohydroxythreonine aminotransferase / 
                     3-phosphoserine aminotransferase / phosphohydroxythreonine 
                     aminotransferase / N-succinyldiaminopimelate 
                     aminotransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.6.1.52; 2.6.1.17"
                     /label=serC
                     /note="The <i>serC</i>-encoded enzyme, 
                     phosphoserine/phosphohydroxythreonine aminotransferase, 
                     functions in the biosythesis of both serine and pyridoxine,
                     by using different substrates |CITS: [2121717]|.  Pyridoxal
                     5'-phosphate is a cofactor for both enzyme activities, 
                     suggesting that it can act in an autocatalytic fashion, 
                     stimulating its own biosynthesis |CITS: [8706854]|.
                     The redundancy and promiscuity among aminotransferase 
                     enzymes has been investigated.  |CITS: [8706854]| stated 
                     that no activity could be observed with non-phosphorylated 
                     substrates; however, |CITS: [3518706]| was able to use 
                     3-hydroxypyruvate as the substrate for an assay of SerC 
                     enzymatic activity.  In addition, genetic experiments 
                     showed that SerC is a minor alanine transaminase |CITS: 
                     [20729367][21597182]|.
                     The normal activities of two enzymes, ArgD and SerC, are 
                     sufficient for succinyldiaminopimelate (SDAP) and lysine 
                     biosynthesis; a third enzyme, AstC, is sufficient for SDAP 
                     biosynthesis, but alone can not fulfill the cell's 
                     requirement for lysine.  Additional enzymes, including GabT
                     and PuuE, may be able to contribute to SDAP biosynthesis.  
                     Expression of <i>argD</i>, <i>astC</i>, <i>serC</i>, 
                     <i>aspC</i>, <i>gabT</i>, <i>hisC</i>, <i>ilvE</i>, 
                     <i>patA</i>, <i>puuE</i>, or <i>tyrB</i> from a plasmid 
                     allow growth of the triple &Delta;<i>argD serC astC</i> 
                     mutant on minimal medium |CITS: [25243376]|. Crystal 
                     structures of the enzyme in the unligated form and in 
                     complex with the substrate analog 
                     &alpha;-methyl-L-glutamate have been solved, and a 
                     molecular reaction mechanism was proposed |CITS: 
                     [10024454]|.
                     In |FRAME: SERSYN-PWY-1|, &alpha;-ketoglutarate (&alpha;KG)
                     is the side product of the SerC-catalyzed reaction that 
                     follows SerA in the pathway.  Unlike enzymes from some 
                     other organisms, <i>E. coli</i> SerA utilizes an intrinsic 
                     interconversion cycle between NAD<sup>+</sup> and NADH that
                     is coupled to utilization of &alpha;KG |CITS: [29494135]|.
                     <i>serC</i> is essential for growth on glycerol minimal 
                     medium; the growth defect can be rescued by addition of 
                     serine and pyridoxol/pyridoxine |CITS: 
                     [17012394][16233211][3108237][4570768]|.
                     The binding of the transcription factor |FRAME: CPLX0-226 
                     CRP| to a predicted CRP DNA binding site around the start 
                     of the <i>serC</i> gene has been demonstrated; however an 
                     effect of this binding on gene expression has not been 
                     analyzed |CITS: [37114659]|.
                     Loss of function of |FRAME: EG10946| significantly 
                     upregulates |FRAME: EG11544| expression, acid resistance, 
                     and heat cross-resistance, though the involved mechanism is
                     currently not understood |CITS: [36453903]|."
