     gene            956657..957340
                     /gene="cmk"
                     /locus_tag="b0910"
                     /label=cmk
     CDS             956657..957340
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cmk"
                     /locus_tag="b0910"
                     /product="trifunctional cytidylate kinase / CMP kinase /
                     dCMP kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.4.25"
                     /label=cmk
                     /note="Cytidylate kinase (Cmk) rephosphorylates CMP and
                     dCMP that is produced by the turnover of nucleic acids and 
                     CDP diglycerides.  Cmk is not an essential enzyme, because 
                     CMP is not an intermediate in the <i>de novo</i> pathway 
                     for the synthesis of CTP.  Unlike the eukaryotic UMP/CMP 
                     kinases, this enzyme is specific for CMP or dCMP. 
                     Cytidylate kinase affects the rate of 5'-end-dependent mRNA
                     degradation by acting together with |FRAME: 
                     DIAMINOPIMEPIM-MONOMER DapF| to stimulate the activity of 
                     |FRAME: G7459-MONOMER RppH|; in addition, by reducing the 
                     concentration of CMP in the cell, the synthesis of 
                     transcripts beginning with monophosphorylated cytidine is 
                     reduced |CITS: [34643703]|.
                     Cmk appears to have some peptidyl-prolyl <i>cis-trans</i> 
                     isomerase activity |CITS: [32823955]|.
                     Crystal strutures of Cmk have been solved |CITS: 
                     [8576266][9862805][11827479]|.  They dynamic properties of 
                     the ATP binding site were probed with a fluorescent dATP 
                     analog, and molecular modeling suggested that ATP-mediated 
                     induced fit is modulated by CMP binding, which leads to a 
                     closed conformation of the active site |CITS: [11123913]|. 
                     Residues involved in the interaction with the pentose 
                     moiety |CITS: [11827479]| and the pyrimidine ring of CMP 
                     |CITS: [17542990]| have been identified in the crystal 
                     structure and functionally verified by site-directed 
                     mutagenesis.
                     The folding/unfolding kinetics of the enzyme have been 
                     investigated |CITS: [24205218]|.  Kinetic modeling of 
                     co-translational folding suggested the occurrence of 
                     kinetic traps and may explain the prevalence of rare codons
                     at the 3' end of the <i>cmk</i> ORF |CITS: [31911473]|.
                     <i>cmk</i> was isolated as a multicopy suppressor of the 
                     cold-sensitive growth phenotype of the <i>smbA2</i> allele 
                     of <i>pyrH</i>, which encodes the UMP kinase |CITS: 
                     [8190075]|.  <i>cmk</i> is also a multicopy suppressor of 
                     the growth defect of a &Delta;6<i>ppi</i> strain that lacks
                     six cytoplasmic peptidyl-prolyl <i>cis-trans</i> 
                     isomerases, as well as of a &Delta;(<i>dnaK dnaJ tig</i>) 
                     strain |CITS: [32823955]|.  <i>cmk</i> was identified as a 
                     mutational hotspot in papillae of ageing colonies |CITS: 
                     [28442761]|.  The <i>cmk</i>A216E mutant in combination 
                     with <i>crp</i>A144T improves maltose fermentation |CITS: 
                     [34620864]|.
                     A <i>cmk</i> deletion strain is cold sensitive; its CMP and
                     dCMP pools are elevated 30-fold, and its rate of 
                     replication elongation is decreased |CITS: [7836281]|. 
                     While <i>cmk</i> is not essential for gowth of <i>E. 
                     coli</i>, it is required for growth of bacteriophage T7 in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [17135349]|.
                     MssA: ""<b>m</b>ulticopy <b>s</b>uppressor of <b>s</b>mbA2 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [8190075]|
                     Cmk: ""<b>CM</b>P <b>k</b>inase"" |CITS: [7836281]|"
