     gene            957451..959124
                     /gene="rpsA"
                     /locus_tag="b0911"
                     /label=rpsA
     CDS             957451..959124
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpsA"
                     /locus_tag="b0911"
                     /product="trifunctional 30S ribosomal subunit protein S1 /
                     30S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rpsA
                     /note="The S1 protein is essential in <i>E. coli</i>; it
                     is a component of the ribosome and is likely required for 
                     translation of most mRNAs |CITS: [9677288]|. S1 plays roles
                     in translation initiation, trans-translation, 70S 
                     dimerization, ribosome inactivation, and phage Q&beta; 
                     replication.
                     During translation initiation, mRNAs are selected and bound
                     to the ribosome with the help of two components: the 
                     conserved 3' end of 16S rRNA is complementary to the 
                     Shine-Dalgarno region of the typical mRNA, while the S1 
                     protein binds to the leader sequence of mRNAs, upstream of 
                     the Shine-Dalgarno region |CITS: [2011495][12358433]|. S1 
                     promotes RNA strand displacement <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [17901153]|. The S1 protein is not required for the 
                     translation of leaderless mRNAs |CITS: 
                     [11902720][12068815][22412910]|. Association of S1 with the
                     ribosome is unstable |CITS: [342903]|, and the S2 protein 
                     is required for binding of S1 to the 30S subunit of the 
                     ribosome |CITS: [12068815]|. The N-terminal domain of S1 is
                     necessary and sufficient for interaction with S2 and for 
                     ribosome binding |CITS: [22412910]|. Protein S1 is 
                     acetylated at K411 and K464 residues either 
                     nonenzymatically by |FRAME: ACETYL-P| (AcP) or 
                     enzymatically by |FRAME: G7350-MONOMER|; carbon/nitrogen 
                     availability affecting AcP is involved in regulating the 
                     acetylation |CITS: [33128612][36050442]|. |FRAME: 
                     G6577-MONOMER CobB| can deacetylate S1 at the same lysine 
                     residues |CITS: [36050442]|.
                     The S1 protein is the largest of the ribosomal proteins and
                     is composed of six related oligosaccharide-oligonucleotide 
                     binding (OB-fold) domains. S1 assumes a complex elongated 
                     shape and is located at the junction of the head, platform,
                     and main body of the 30S subunit |CITS: [11593008]|. RNA 
                     binding is associated with structural modification of the 
                     S1 protein |CITS: [18211890]|. NMR structures of domains 4 
                     and 6 have been determined |CITS: [19605565]|. In a cyro-EM
                     structure of the 70S ribosome, S1 shows conformational 
                     heterogeneity |CITS: [29247757]|. In cyro-EM structures of 
                     the hibernating ribosome, RMF stabilizes a compact 
                     structure of S1, sequestering the anti-SD sequence of 16S 
                     rRNA |CITS: [30177741]|. Cryo-EM structures of a 
                     transcription-translation complex between the RNA 
                     polymerase core enzyme (RNAP) and the 30S ribosomal subunit
                     show that S1 forms a wall of the tunnel between RNAP and 
                     30S |CITS: [29027901]|. Crystal structures of the 
                     N-terminal half of S1 in the context of the phage Q&beta; 
                     replicase have been solved |CITS: [25122749][26578560]|. 
                     The translation initiation region of <i>rpsA</i> mRNA, 
                     which encodes the S1 protein, lacks a canonical 
                     Shine-Dalgarno region, but nevertheless supports high 
                     levels of translation |CITS: [16923895]|. Expression of S1 
                     is autoregulated; the S1 protein acts as a specific 
                     repressor of translation of its own mRNA |CITS: 
                     [6267426][2120211]|. The N-terminus of S1 is sufficient for
                     repressing <i>rpsA</i> expression |CITS: [2120211]|. 
                     Supported by various types of evidence including 
                     site-directed mutagenesis, footprinting and phylogenetic 
                     studies, a model was proposed in which the <i>rpsA</i> 
                     leader region folds into three stem-loop structures which 
                     enable efficient translation; binding of free S1 protein 
                     disturbs the conformation of the <i>rpsA</i> mRNA and thus 
                     specifically represses translation |CITS: 
                     [11483525][14627832]|.
                     S1 binds to single-stranded RNA, thereby preventing 
                     reannealing of secondary structures, and thus promotes the 
                     unwound RNA state |CITS: [22908248]|. S1 appears to provide
                     an RNA chaperone-like activity to the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [24339747]|. Unfolding of RNA secondary structures by S1 
                     has been investigated |CITS: [31832686]|. The mRNA binding 
                     properties of S1 enable it to regulate translation of 
                     various proteins. Competition with a negative regulator for
                     mRNA binding has been suggested or shown for |FRAME: 
                     TU0-6654 <i>pnp</i>| |CITS: [25825432]|, |FRAME: TU0-8474 
                     <i>rpsO</i>| |CITS: [24339747]|, and |FRAME: EG10568 
                     <i>manY</i>| |CITS: [32291821]|.
                     The S1 protein has been suggested to play a role in 
                     trans-translation by binding transfer-messenger RNA (tmRNA)
                     and delivering it to stalled ribosomes |CITS: 
                     [12457560][11248028][11101533][15494393][15564671]|. An 
                     alternative model suggests that S1 binds to both mRNA and 
                     tmRNA molecules indiscriminately and may not play a direct 
                     role in tmRNA-mediated tagging of incompletely translated 
                     polypeptides |CITS: [15340139]|. Later studies disagree on 
                     whether S1 is |CITS: [17392345]| or is not |CITS: 
                     [17376482]| required for trans-translation <i>in vitro</i>.
                     S1 is an essential component of the phage Q&beta; replicase
                     |CITS: [4208476][25122749]|.
                     The S1 protein has been reported to bind to RNA polymerase 
                     and stimulate transcription of a number of promoters |CITS:
                     [12834354]|. S1 activates transcriptional cycling <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [16775305]|. S1 may also inhibit RNase 
                     E-dependent mRNA decay |CITS: [21685451]|.
                     S1 proteins carrying a C-terminal truncation due to 
                     IS<i>10</i>R insertion in the <i>ssyF29</i> mutant lack the
                     R4 RNA binding domain and cause a growth defect |CITS: 
                     [11004188]|. The growth defect was found to be due to 
                     destabilization of the <i>rpsA</i> mRNA |CITS: [17616604]|.
                     The mutation increases the translation efficiency of 
                     leaderless mRNA |CITS: [23104805]|. The OB-fold domains 1-3
                     of S1 are essential for viability |CITS: [24339747]|.
                     ssyF: ""<b>s</b>uppressor of 
                     <i><b>s</b>ec<b>Y</b>24</i>(Ts)"" |CITS: [3011749]| Review:
                     |CITS: [25184952]|"
