     gene            complement(968078..968857)
                     /gene="elyC"
                     /locus_tag="b0920"
                     /label=elyC
     CDS             complement(968078..968857)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="elyC"
                     /locus_tag="b0920"
                     /product="envelope biogenesis factor"
                     /label=elyC
                     /note="Cells lacking ElyC have a defect in peptidoglycan 
                     synthesis and genetic analysis suggests a role for ElyC in 
                     |FRAME: CPD-9646| metabolism |CITS: [24391520]|. 
                     &Delta;<i>elyC</i> cells show an excess of protein 
                     aggregation; overproduction of the periplasmic chaperones, 
                     Spy or DsbG, reduces this to a wild-type level |CITS: 
                     [30012727]|. ElyC and |FRAME: CPD0-2646| 
                     (ECA<sub>CYC</sub>) are implicated in a pathway that 
                     regulates synthesis of |FRAME: CPD-21605 
                     ""phosphoglyceride-linked ECA""| (ECA<sup>PG</sup>); ElyC 
                     inhibits production of ECA<sub>PG</sub> most likely at a 
                     posttranscriptional level; the full function of ElyC 
                     requires ECA<sub>CYC</sub> |CITS: [34809459]| (consider 
                     also |CITS: [39992125]|).
                     A &Delta;<i>elyC</i> mutant grows poorly and forms small 
                     colonies at room temperature. In a screen developed to 
                     identify genes associated with envelope biogenesis 
                     disorders, a &Delta;<i>elyC</i> mutant has a positive 
                     phenotype (chlorophenyl red-&beta;-D-galactopyranoside+ or 
                     CPRG+). The growth defect and CPRG+ phenotype can be 
                     complemented by expressing <i>elyC</i> in <i>trans</i>. 
                     Overproduction of |FRAME: UPPSYN-CPLX ""UppS""| also 
                     specifically suppresses the phenotype associated with loss 
                     of ElyC function. <i>&Delta;elyC&Delta;mrcB</i> cells are 
                     not viable at room temperature |CITS: [24391520]|.
                     ElyC contains two predicted trans-membrane regions plus a 
                     large domain of unknown function (DUF218) that is probably 
                     located in the periplasm |CITS: [24391520]|.
                     <i>elyC</i>: <u>el</u>evated frequency of l<u>y</u>sis C 
                     |CITS: [24391520]|"
