     gene            969775..971097
                     /gene="mukF"
                     /locus_tag="b0922"
                     /label=mukF
     CDS             969775..971097
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mukF"
                     /locus_tag="b0922"
                     /product="multifunctional chromosome partitioning protein
                     MukF / MukF dimer / MukEF complex / bacterial condensin 
                     MukBEF"
                     /label=mukF
                     /note="MukF plays a role in chromosome partitioning during
                     cell division, DNA topology and chromosome condensation 
                     |CITS: [8602138][10660686]| as part of the |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-2561|. MukF is the kleisin subunit (from the Greek 
                     word <i>kleisimo</i> for ""closure"" |CITS: [12667442]|) 
                     within MukBEF, consistent with the N- and C-termini sharing
                     homology to kleisin domains |CITS: [15902272]|; unlike 
                     other kleisins, MukF is present as a dimer |CITS: 
                     [29323635]|. MukF and MukE initially were proposed to be a 
                     toxin-antitoxin pair, respectively |CITS: [7513784]|.
                     MukF has a leucine zipper region and an acidic region; both
                     regions are functionally important |CITS: [8602138]|. MukF 
                     bound to MukE is required for binding to MukB, whereas MukF
                     can bind to MukB without being bound to MukE |CITS: 
                     [10545099]|. MukBEF complex which is stimulated by 
                     Ca<sup>2+</sup> or Mg<sup>2+</sup>; MukF was found to bind 
                     to Ca<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [10545099]|. A crystal structure 
                     of a 33 kDa N-terminal fragment of MukF (nucleotides 1-287)
                     was purified to 2.9&Aring; resolution in presence of 
                     Ca<sup>2+</sup>, however no divalent metals were observed 
                     within the crystal structure |CITS: [15902272]|. The 
                     leucine-zipper region of MukF is part of the four-helix 
                     coiled-coil segment adjacent to the MukF dimerization 
                     domain, which interacts with the MukB coiled-coiled 
                     ""neck"" adjacent to the ATPase head |CITS: 
                     [15902272][29323635]|. The C-terminal winged-helix domains 
                     bind to the ATPase domain of MukB, whereas the acidic 
                     region between the N- and C-terminal domains is needed for 
                     MukE |CITS: [15902272][19135891][29323635]|. These 
                     structural interactions form an asymmetric tripartite 
                     complex |CITS: [29323635]|.
                     A <i>mukF</i> mutant is viable and suppresses the viability
                     defect of a <i>mukE</i> mutant |CITS: [7513784]|. A 
                     <i>mukF</i> mutant exhibit heat sensitivity, forms 
                     anucleate cells and increased sensitivity to novobiocin, 
                     compared to wild type cells |CITS: [8602138][11004192]|. 
                     The heat sensitivity and division defects are suppressed by
                     two <i>topA</i> mutations that are DNA gyrase-dependent, 
                     <i>dam</i> and <i>seqA</i> mutations |CITS: [10660686]|. 
                     Loss of mukF exhibits a defect in wild-type localization of
                     MukB to nucleoid-associated foci, resulting in dispersed 
                     distribution of MukB and loss of the MukBEF complex locus 
                     positioning, the latter of which is independent of DNA 
                     replication |CITS: [11401691][19674109][22753058]|.
                     MukF dimer interacting with MukB neck and head regions 
                     activate MukB ATPase; whereas MukE inhibits these ATPase 
                     activities |CITS: [29323635]|. Using protein-protein 
                     crosslinking and site-specific mutagenesis of contact 
                     points, MukF interaction with the MukB neck region is 
                     required for MukB ATPase activity and for cell viability 
                     |CITS: [35452680]|. The presence of MukF C-terminus is 
                     associated with DNA becoming trapped in the MukB head 
                     |CITS: [35770038]|.
                     KicB: ""killing of cell"" |CITS: [7513784]|.
                     Regulation has been described |CITS: [7513784][8566713]|."
