     gene            971078..971782
                     /gene="mukE"
                     /locus_tag="b0923"
                     /label=mukE
     CDS             971078..971782
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mukE"
                     /locus_tag="b0923"
                     /product="trifunctional chromosome partitioning protein
                     MukE / MukEF complex / bacterial condensin MukBEF"
                     /label=mukE
                     /note="MukE plays a role in chromosome partitioning during
                     cell division |CITS: [8602138]|. The effect of MukE on 
                     chromosome partitioning may be due to a role in DNA 
                     topology or condensation |CITS: [10660686]|. MukF (the 
                     kleisin) and MukE (the kleisin-interacting winged-helix 
                     tandem element, ""KITE"") are proposed to be a 
                     toxin-antitoxin pair, respectively |CITS: [7513784]|. 
                     Published reports disagree about whether |CITS: [7513784]| 
                     or not |CITS: [8602138]| the MukE protein is essential for 
                     viability. A <i>mukE</i> mutant is reported to exhibit heat
                     sensitivity and formation of anucleate products of cell 
                     division |CITS: [8602138]|. A <i>mukE</i> mutant also 
                     exhibits increased sensitivity to novobiocin, compared to 
                     wild type |CITS: [11004192]|. A <i>mukE</i> mutant exhibits
                     a defect in wild-type localization of MukB to 
                     nucleoid-associated foci |CITS: [11401691]|. A <i>mukF</i> 
                     mutation suppresses the observed inviability of a 
                     <i>mukE</i> mutant |CITS: [7513784]|. The heat sensitivity 
                     and division defects of a <i>mukE</i> mutant are suppressed
                     by a <i>topA10</i> or <i>topA66</i> mutation, and this 
                     suppression is DNA gyrase dependent |CITS: [10660686]|. The
                     heat sensitivity of a <i>mukE</i> mutant is partly 
                     suppressed by a <i>dam</i> or <i>seqA</i> mutation, whereas
                     the novobiocin and anucleate cell phenotypes are not |CITS:
                     [11004192]|.
                     Loss of MukE affects focal localization of the MukBEF 
                     complex <i>in vivo</i>, and this is independent of DNA 
                     replication |CITS: [21855551][22753058][23171168]|.
                     MukB, MukE and MukF form a complex; MukE binding to MukB 
                     requires MukF, whereas MukF binding to MukB does not 
                     require MukE, and complex formation is stimulated by 
                     Ca<sup>2+</sup> or Mg<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [10545099]|. 
                     KicA: <b>ki</b>lling of <b>c</b>ell <b>A</b> |CITS: 
                     [7513784]|
                     Regulation has been described |CITS: [7513784][8566713]|."
