     gene            971782..976242
                     /gene="mukB"
                     /locus_tag="b0924"
                     /label=mukB
     CDS             971782..976242
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mukB"
                     /locus_tag="b0924"
                     /product="bifunctional chromosome partitioning protein
                     MukB / bacterial condensin MukBEF"
                     /label=mukB
                     /note="The MukB protein belongs to the family of SMC
                     (Structural Maintenance of Chromosomes) proteins found in 
                     |FRAME: TAX-543| |CITS: [9744887][11707343]|. It is the 
                     ATPase subunit of the |FRAME: CPLX0-2561| complex that is 
                     involved in chromosome condensation and partitioning (e.g. 
                     segregation) through its interactions with |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-2424| (Topo IV) and |FRAME: CPLX0-7789| MatP |CITS: 
                     [1464330][10545099][28842485][26818444]|. MukB acts as a 
                     macromolecular clamp that condenses DNA in a cooperative 
                     manner; ATP stimulates initiation of condensation but is 
                     not required for binding to DNA, bridging between two DNA 
                     strands, nor translocating along the chromosome |CITS: 
                     [16368697][18376412][20075860]|.
                     GFP-marked MukB protein is observed to occupy the same 
                     location as the nucleoid in the presence of MukE and MukF 
                     |CITS: [11401691]| but appears to not co-localize with the 
                     FtsZ ring based on fluorescent antibody labelling |CITS: 
                     [11886550]|. <i>In vivo</i>, cellular localization of MukB 
                     with the nucleoid requires MukEF and is affected by 
                     mutations in the MukF linker region |CITS: 
                     [17644586][19674109]|. MukB generally colocalizes with the 
                     origin of replication (<i>oriC</i>) throughout the cell 
                     cycle |CITS: [17824928]|, although the number of MukB foci 
                     is larger than the number of <i>oriC</i> foci |CITS: 
                     [18233960]|. The <i>oriC</i> region is aberrantly 
                     positioned at the cell pole in a <i>mukB</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [17824928]|. Interactions with sister chromatid are not 
                     significantly affected in a <i>mukB</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [22820946]|.
                     The MukB protein forms a homodimer which binds to the 
                     chromosome partitioning proteins |FRAME: EG11252-MONOMER 
                     MukE| and |FRAME: EG12165-MONOMER MukF| in different 
                     combinations to form the |FRAME: CPLX0-2561| complex |CITS:
                     [1464330][16982609][23112333]|. Like other members of the 
                     SMC protein family, MukB is a large protein made up of five
                     domains: N- and C-terminal globular ""head domains"", a 
                     central linker region or ""hinge domain"", and two 
                     coiled-coil rod or ""neck"" domains that connect the head 
                     domains to the hinge domain, forming a V-shaped molecule. 
                     This structure can be visualized by EM; the coiled-coil 
                     regions are arranged in an antiparallel fashion in the 
                     homodimer |CITS: [1464330][9744887][15979051]|. A crystal 
                     structure of the 227 N-terminal amino acid residues of MukB
                     has been solved at 2.2 &Aring; resolution |CITS: 
                     [10545328]|. Crystal structures of the central hinge domain
                     responsible for dimerization of MukB, including a portion 
                     of the adjacent coiled-coil domain, have been solved at 3.1
                     and 2.3 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [19853611][20034111]|.
                     The head domains associate via a shared ABC type 
                     ATP-binding site |CITS: [19135891]|. The interactions of 
                     the various domains, and between the MukB and the ParC 
                     subunit of TopoIV, have been investigated by site-directed 
                     crosslinking |CITS: [19482037][35452680]|. Interacting 
                     pairs of residues were identified by disulfide 
                     crosslinking, revealing that five coiled-coil segments are 
                     interrupted by a novel structural motif, called a 
                     coiled-coil knuckle |CITS: [22041452]|. Interactions 
                     between the hinge and neck regions of MukB, as well as 
                     between MukF and the neck region of MukB, are essential for
                     the ATPase activity of MukB; specific amino acid residues 
                     determined through site-specific mutagenesis have been 
                     identified in these interactions and ATPase activity |CITS:
                     [35452680]|. The C-terminal region of MukB is essential for
                     interactions with MukEF and its DNA binding activity |CITS:
                     [1464330][8837474][10545099][19135891]|, while the hinge 
                     domain does not appear to interact with DNA |CITS: 
                     [20034111]|. ATP is required for the the MukB dimer to form
                     |CITS: [19135891]|. A purified N-terminal domain of MukB 
                     binds to FtsZ <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [9688555]|.
                     The hinge domain interacts directly with the C-terminal 
                     domain of TopoIVs subunit A, |FRAME: EG10686-MONOMER ParC|,
                     and stimulates the DNA relaxation and knotting activities 
                     of Topo IV <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [20696938][20921377][23349462][24520061]|, but not its 
                     decatenation activity |CITS: [23349462]|. The crystal 
                     structure of a minimal MukB-Topo IV complex has been 
                     solved; the MukB hinge domain is shown to stimulate Topo IV
                     by competing for a site on its C-terminal domain that 
                     normally represses activity on negatively supercoiled DNA 
                     |CITS: [24097060]|. The binding site for ParC and MatP 
                     (when bound to MatS) overlap in the MukB hinge region and 
                     are mutually exclusive |CITS: [34585666]|. Interactions 
                     between MukB and TopoIV results in mutual inhibition of 
                     each protein's activity; <i>in vitro</i> studies indicate 
                     that the ParC subunit inhibits the ATPase activity of MukB 
                     |CITS: [34747485][35452680]|, and MukB inhibits TopoIV's 
                     capture of the transport (T) segment of the DNA. MukB also 
                     inhibits the DNA cleavage activity of the MukB-TopoIV 
                     complex <i>in vivo</i>, suggesting that this complex 
                     provides a stable scaffold for DNA condensation |CITS: 
                     [34747485]|.
                     Gel-shift analyses of purified MukB found it could bind to 
                     linear, circular and supercoiled DNA; MukB also binds to 
                     phage single-stranded DNA better than double-stranded DNA. 
                     MukB promoted right-handed knotting in circular DNA nicked 
                     with phage T2 topoisomerase; left-handed (negative) 
                     supercoiling resulted in relaxed DNA treated with a type-1 
                     topoisomerase. These topological changes to DNA occurred 
                     with high salt not low salt fractionated MukB |CITS: 
                     [16368697]|. Cryo-EM analysis revealed two segments of a 
                     DNA loop are bound to MukBEF (when its bound to MatP) in 
                     separate 'compartments' of the MukBEF tripartite ring: one 
                     in the large circumference of the ring and the other in the
                     small clamp at the ATPase heads |CITS: [34739874]|. In 
                     single-molecule studies with fluorescently-labelled MukB 
                     and Flag-tagged MukE and MukF, DNA was observed to move 
                     unidirectionally through MukB and become compacted by MukB 
                     dimers alone under low salt concentration; ATP hydrolysis 
                     may influence the time that DNA and MukB interact through 
                     regulation of opening and closing of the MukB head region 
                     when MukE and MukF are absent. When MukF C-terminus is 
                     present, DNA becomes trapped in the MukB head |CITS: 
                     [35770038]|.
                     A <i>mukB</i> null mutant has a cell division defect, not 
                     due to inhibition of DNA replication, and cannot form 
                     colonies |CITS: [1989883]|. <i>mukB</i> mutants are 
                     temperature sensitive and produce anucleate cells. A 
                     <i>mukB</i> mutation causes unfolding of the nucleoid; this
                     phenotype is supressed by a mutation in <i>seqA</i> |CITS: 
                     [10540294][11004192]|. Additional supressors of the 
                     <i>mukB</i> null mutant phenotype have been isolated in 
                     <i>smbA</i> |CITS: [1447125]|, <i>msmA</i>, <i>msmB</i>, 
                     <i>msmC</i> |CITS: [7984109]|, <i>smbB</i> |CITS: 
                     [8682798]|, <i>crcA</i>, <i>cspE</i>, <i>crcB</i> |CITS: 
                     [8844142]|, <i>topA</i> |CITS: [10660686]|, and <i>gyrB</i>
                     |CITS: [12813060]|. In a <i>mukB</i> mutant, the chromosome
                     appears to be less supercoiled |CITS: [11263493]|, 
                     particularly when the mutant MukB does not interact with 
                     |FRAME: EG11013-MONOMER| |CITS: [10660686]| or |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-2424| |CITS: [28842485]|. In another <i>mukB</i> 
                     deletion mutant resulting in the absence of condensin, a 
                     high-resolution contact map of <i>E. coli</i> chromosome 
                     revealed that long-range (> 280 kb) chromosomal contacts 
                     are reduced relative to wild-type cells |CITS: [29358050]|.
                     Overproduction of MukB leads to condensation of 
                     chromosomes, even in the absence of MukE and MukF |CITS: 
                     [16740950]|.
                     In single-molecule observations and mutant analysis, both 
                     MukB alone and the MukBEF complex promote shrinkage of 
                     large DNA molecules (T4 phage DNA), and condensation as 
                     well as translocation is dependent on the ATPase activity 
                     of MukB |CITS: [18326568][36937278]|.
                     <i>muk</i> comes from the Japanese word 'mukaku' for 
                     'anucleate', originally used for <i>mukA</i> (now |FRAME: 
                     EG11009|) [Hiraga89].
                     Reviews: |CITS: [1497312][15952899][16879983][19037758][19 
                     308706][20962275][25732339][26585514][27075410]|"
