     gene            976503..978350
                     /gene="ldtD"
                     /locus_tag="b0925"
                     /label=ldtD
     CDS             976503..978350
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ldtD"
                     /locus_tag="b0925"
                     /product="trifunctional L,D-transpeptidase LdtD / 
                     L,D-transpeptidase / L,D-carboxypeptidase"
                     /label=ldtD
                     /note="LdtD is a periplasmic L,D-transpeptidase that
                     catalyses the formation of <i>meso</i>-diaminopimelyl 
                     &rarr; <i>meso</i>-diaminopimelyl cross links (also called 
                     DAP<sup>3</sup>&rarr;DAP<sup>3</sup> or 3-3 cross-links) in
                     peptidoglycan. LdtD cleaves the 
                     <i>m</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup>&rarr;D-ala<sup>4</sup> peptide 
                     bond of an acyl donor stem tetrapeptide and links the 
                     carboxyl group at the L-center of <i>m</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup> 
                     to the side chain amine of <i>m</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup> of an 
                     acceptor stem peptide; LdtD can also use glycine as an acyl
                     acceptor resulting in the formation of tetrapeptide stems 
                     ending in Gly and it can hydrolyse D-Ala<sup>4</sup> in 
                     stem tetrapeptides with resultant formation of tripeptide 
                     stems.
                     LdtD is a stress reponse L,D-transpeptidase with a role in 
                     the protective remodeling of peptidoglycan during cell 
                     envelope stress |CITS: [30723128]|.
                     Muropeptides obtained from the peptidoglycan of an |FRAME: 
                     G7073 ldtA| |FRAME: G6422 ldtB| |FRAME: G6571 ldtC| ldtD 
                     |FRAME: G6904 ldtE| quintuple mutant (BW25113&Delta;5) do 
                     not contain 
                     <i>meso</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup>-<i>meso</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup> 
                     cross-links, tripeptide stems substituted by a fragment of 
                     the Braun lipoprotein, free tripeptide stems nor 
                     tetrapeptide stems with Gly as the fourth amino acid |CITS:
                     [18456808]| (see also |CITS: [30723128]|). Expression of 
                     <i>ldtD</i> in the BW25113&Delta;5 mutant restores 
                     production of 
                     <i>meso</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup>-<i>meso</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup> 
                     cross-links, hydrolysis of D-Ala<sup>4</sup>, and exchange 
                     of D-Ala<sup>4</sup> by Gly |CITS: [18456808]|. <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 constains a second enzyme (LdtE) that forms 
                     DAP<sup>3</sup>&rarr;DAP<sup>3</sup> cross-links in 
                     peptidoglycan; growth of a strain lacking both LdtD and 
                     LdtE (&Delta;<i>ldt2</i>) is not affected under a wide 
                     variety of conditions, but their absence contributes to the
                     growth defect of an &Delta;<i>ldt5</i> strain 
                     (&Delta;<i>ldtABCDE</I>) grown on minimal DAP medium |CITS:
                     [23832002]|.
                     Purified, soluble LdtD, incubated with a peptidoglycan 
                     disaccharide-tetrapeptide catalyses the formation of 
                     DAP<sup>3</sup>&rarr;DAP<sup>3</sup> cross links and also 
                     displays L,D-carboxypeptidase activity; purified, soluble 
                     LdtD is irreversibly acylated by &beta;-lactams of the the 
                     carbapenem class while adducts resulting from acylation by 
                     &beta;-lactams of the penam and cephem classes are prone to
                     hydrolysis |CITS: [27767957]|. LdtD over-production and 
                     increased synthesis of the alarmone (p)ppGpp confers 
                     broad-spectrum resistance to &beta;-lactams (with the 
                     exception of carbapenems) in <i>E. coli</i> mutant strain 
                     M1; in the presence of ampicillin, peptidoglycan 
                     polymerization requires the L,D-transpeptidase activity of 
                     LdtD, the glycosyltransferase activity of PBP1b, and the 
                     D,D-carboxypeptidase activity of PBP5 |CITS: [27767957]| 
                     (see also |CITS: [38443580]|). Copper inhibits purified 
                     LdtD at submillimolar concentration and in a 
                     concentration-dependent manner; copper prevents the 
                     LdtD-mediated &beta;-lactam resistance of <i>E. coli</i> M1
                     |CITS: [30275297]|.
                     LdtD is activated in response to outer membrane damage; 
                     LdtD transpeptidase activity increases the level of 
                     <i>meso</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup>-<i>meso</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup> 
                     cross-links to fortify the peptidoglycan during cell 
                     envelope stress |CITS: [30723128]|. LdtD physically 
                     interacts with PBP1b when cells are experiencing OM stress;
                     LdtD, cooperates with (trans-peptidase inactive) |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3951 PBP1B|, LpoB and |FRAME: CPLX0-8252 PBP5| or 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-8254 PBP6a| to produce a highly 3-3 
                     cross-linked nascent PG <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [30723128]|.
                     The |FRAME: CPLX0-8557 DeaD RNA helicase| upregulates 
                     translation of LdtD under cold shock conditions (5 hrs at 
                     15 &deg;C) resulting in increased frequency of 3-3 PG 
                     cross-links |CITS: [36507682]|.
                     The structure of LdtD with bound meropenem has been 
                     reported; LdtD consists of a catalytic domain (residues 
                     375-576 with canonical L,D-transpeptidase fold plus a 
                     unique a substrate capping subdomain), a scaffolding domain
                     which may be involved in protein interactions, a putative 
                     peptidoglycan binding domain (residues 234 -351), and a 
                     linker region |CITS: [31015395]|
                     LdtD is the main L,D-transpeptidase incorporating the 
                     D-alanine fluorescent probe NADA into peptidoglycan, at 
                     neutral pH during exponential growth; PBP1B, LpoB and CpoB 
                     are required for efficient NADA incorporation by LdtD 
                     |CITS: [30233559]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [6389516][1512190][23055266]|). <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     incorporates exogenously supplied D-methionine into 
                     peptidoglycan stem tetrapeptides; the percentage of 
                     muropeptides that contain D-methionine is reduced to 
                     varying extents in all single <i>ldt</i> insertion mutants*
                     and is reduced by ~75% in the double <i>ldtD ldtE</i> 
                     insertion mutant |CITS: [21792174]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [23832002]|). Deletion of <i>ldtD</i> together with 
                     <i>ldtE</i> also reduces detection of D-cysteine in 
                     peptidoglycan |CITS: [21792174]|.
                     <I>ldtD</I> shows delayed upregulation in response to an 
                     acid shift |CITS: [18302792]|. Constitutive activation of 
                     the Cpx two component pathway results in upregulation of 
                     <i>ldtD</i> and a resultant increase in the level of 
                     DAP<sup>3</sup>&rarr;DAP<sup>3</sup> crosslinks; 
                     <i>ldtD</i> is a member of the CpxR regulon |CITS: 
                     [25422305]|.
                     LdtD is predicted to be a bitopic inner membrane protein 
                     |CITS: [27510400]|. LdtA, B, C and E are all of similar 
                     size and share 42-62% sequence similarity; LdtD is a larger
                     protein and shares only 11-17% sequence similarity with the
                     others; the putative active site cysteine is located in a 
                     conserved motif towards the C-terminus |CITS: [23832002]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>ldtD</i> from an ASKA plasmid leads to
                     a more than 3-fold increase in the MIC of hypochlorous acid
                     |CITS: [34072091]|.
                     *|FRAME: G6108-MONOMER <i>ldtF</i>| was not included in 
                     this analysis"
