     gene            complement(981350..982438)
                     /gene="ompF"
                     /locus_tag="b0929"
                     /label=ompF
     CDS             complement(981350..982438)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ompF"
                     /locus_tag="b0929"
                     /product="multifunctional outer membrane porin F /
                     transport of hydrophilic solute or ion < 600 Da / transport
                     of glycine betaine / transport of an organophosphorus 
                     compound / transport of a glycoside / transport of a sugar 
                     / transport of a sugar phosphate / transport of an aromatic
                     carboxylate / transport of a trisaccharide / transport of a
                     disaccharide / transport of an oligopeptide / transport of 
                     a nucleoside / transport of a nucleotide sugar / transport 
                     of a nucleotide / transport of an aldehyde / transport of a
                     lactone / transport of an amide / transport of an amine / 
                     transport of a hydroxy carboxylate / transport of an 
                     aldonate / transport of a medium chain fatty acid / 
                     transport of a monocarboxylate / transport of a 
                     dicarboxylate / transport of S-methyl-5-thio-D-ribose / 
                     transport of an inositol / transport of an amino acid / 
                     transport of a nucleobase / transport of a sugar alcohol / 
                     transport of a short-chain alcohol / transport of a 
                     short-chain carboxylate / transport of an organosulfur 
                     compound / transport of water / transport of an ion / 
                     transport of glycerol phosphate / transport of a 
                     &beta;-lactam antibiotic"
                     /label=ompF
                     /note="OmpF is a general outer membrane (OM) porin which 
                     mediates the non-specific diffusion of small solutes such 
                     as sugars, ions and amino acids. The major non-specific OM 
                     porins of <i>E. coli</I> K-12 (OmpF and |FRAME: CPLX0-7533 
                     ""OmpC""|) are typically defined by an exclusion limit 
                     based on substrate mass (~600 daltons) but there are large 
                     differences in penetration rates within solutes due to 
                     factors such as size, hydrophobicity and charge (discussed 
                     in |CITS: [1373213]|). OmpF acts as a phage receptor (see 
                     for example |CITS: [330500][2820945][10564794]|) and is 
                     required for the activity of various colicins (see for 
                     example |CITS: 
                     [7047502][2193912][1706457][7524100][14977546]|).
                     Purified OmpF reconstituted into artificial lipid bilayer 
                     membranes forms an ion permeable channel with an estimated 
                     pore diameter of 1.1nm; OmpF exhibits a preference for 
                     cations |CITS: [2580824]|. OmpF (protein <i>b</I>) can be 
                     isolated associated with the pepidoglycan |CITS: [787762]|.
                     OmpF and OmpC are considered to be the two major 
                     non-specific trimeric OM porins under standard laboratory 
                     growth (see |CITS: [1696133]| and references within); in 
                     the Transporter Classification Database both are members of
                     the General Bacterial Porin (GBP) family |CITS: [26546518]|
                     which also includes the related porins |FRAME: CPLX0-7530 
                     ""PhoE""|, |FRAME: EG10659-MONOMER ""NmpC""| and |FRAME: 
                     G6700-MONOMER ""OmpN""|. Imaging of the OM by atomic force 
                     microscopy shows that OmpF foms part of a dense, crowded 
                     network of low mobility trimeric porins; the porin network 
                     is interupted by nanoscale patches enriched in LPS which 
                     may facilitate the insertion of new membrane components as 
                     the cell grows |CITS: [34716276]|.
                     Purified OmpF is trimeric; the OmpF monomer consists of a 
                     16-stranded anti-parallel &beta;-barrel containing an 
                     aqueous pore; short &beta;-hairpin turns define the 
                     'smooth' end of the barrel while long, irregular loops are 
                     found at the other 'rough' end which faces the 
                     extracellular space; one of the long loops (L3) folds into 
                     the barrel and forms a constriction zone at about half the 
                     height of the barrel |CITS: [1850001][1380671][7692068]|. 
                     Voltage gating of OmpF and the role of the L3 loop in 
                     gating has been widely studied (see reviews by |CITS: 
                     [9714531][12440693]| and references within; also |CITS: 
                     [19932117][24106986][35193963]|). Trimeric OmpF binds 
                     specifically to lipopolysaccharide |CITS: [27493217]| (see 
                     also |CITS: [3003028][2442135][8157594][26910429][22484237 
                     ][36322653]|. OmpF-LPS interactions may serve to modulate 
                     OmpF activity <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [29891712]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [30764475]|).
                     OmpF mediates the entry of various antibiotics including 
                     &beta;-lactams (|CITS: 
                     [7044293][6760806][6344786][6294048][2580479][12119404]| 
                     quinolones |CITS: [1516804]| and others |CITS: [8226422]| 
                     (see also |CITS: [3532944][2848006][15240444][16339889][20 
                     012725][20604536][20590090][23798411][23201272][29540483][2
                     0349984][38104323]|). OmpF has been implicated in the 
                     secretion of the extracellular protein |FRAME: 
                     EG11807-MONOMER ""YebF""| |CITS: [22658749]|.
                     OmpF imports the cytotoxic domains of group A colicins E1 
                     and E3 after the colicins bind to the BtuB receptor |CITS: 
                     [15465872][16922495]|. The Colicin E9 (ColE9) translocon is
                     a heptameric complex consisting of single copies of the 
                     ColE9-immunity protein complex, BtuB, the OmpF trimer and 
                     TolB |CITS: [23812713]|. ColE9's unstructured N-terminal 
                     domain occupies two OmpF subunits and captures TolB on the 
                     other side of the membrane |CITS: 
                     [21098297][23812713][29949342]|. Colicin N uses only OmpF 
                     for entry |CITS: [8319674]| - it does not require a 
                     separate receptor protein, rather it binds to 
                     lipopolysaccharide molecules adjacent to OmpF |CITS: 
                     [18334212][24589252]|.
                     Porin genes are subject to complex transcriptional and 
                     post-transcriptional regulation influenced by factors such 
                     as medium osmolarity, temperature and pH (see selected 
                     reviews |CITS: [8809744][20068355]|). Polyamines inhibit 
                     OmpF activity <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [9228026]| and reduce 
                     outer membrane permeability (as measured through effects on
                     chemotaxis and permeation of &beta;-lactams) <i>in vivo</i>
                     |CITS: [8682771]| (see also |CITS: [8846798]|). Cadavarine 
                     may function as an endogenous modulator of OM permeability 
                     via inhibition of porin activity or via a reduction in 
                     porin expression |CITS: [9922241]|.
                     OmpF has been used as a scaffold for the design and 
                     engineering of proteins with varying pore size and specific
                     solute selectivity |CITS: [30202038]|. <i>ompF</i> exhibits
                     mosaic evolution patterns in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [30150258][35285711][40444982]|.
                     A description of outer membrane protein nomenclature, 
                     including early and recommended names can be found in 
                     |CITS: [6254441]|.
                     <i>tolF</i> <U>tol</U>erant (to bacteriocin JF246) |CITS: 
                     [4583254][789358][400794]|<BR>Early studies characterizing 
                     <i>tolF</i> mutants and outer membrane protein 1a (also 
                     called outer membrane protein b) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     include: |CITS: [773686][787762][328491][358984][330500][3 
                     60042][370544][378974][337109][204859][350864][374368]| 
                     Selected related reviews: |CITS: [7039497][6337630][137321 
                     3][1696133][8809744][9615433][18997824][21132209]| 
                     Extensive characterisation of OmpF has occurred in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> B strains. A series of <i>E. coli</i> B strains 
                     (the B<sup>E</sup>strains) lacking the major outer membrane
                     proteins has been constructed and used as an expression 
                     host for purification and characterization of mutant porins
                     including OmpF |CITS: [9631547]|. The K-12 and B proteins 
                     differ by only three amino acid residues (see |CITS: 
                     [7049161][6294623]|)."
