                     /locus_tag="b0930"
                     /product="asparagine--tRNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.1.1.22"
                     /label=asnS
                     /note="Asparagine&mdash;tRNA ligase (AsnRS) is a member of
                     the family of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, which interpret 
                     the genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their
                     specific tRNA molecules.  The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis.  AsnRS belongs to the Class IIb aminoacyl-tRNA 
                     synthetases |CITS: [2203971][1852601]|. AsnRS is a dimer in
                     solution |CITS: [2693216][1425658]|. Analysis of mutants 
                     suggests that the Y426 residue is involved in ATP binding 
                     |CITS: [2009959]|; a P231L mutation, which is located in 
                     the conserved motif 2 of class II aminoacyl-tRNA 
                     synthetases, leads to increases in the K<sub>m</sub> for 
                     asparagine and ATP |CITS: [1544480]|.
                     There is a general discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [37224536]|.
                     Tss: ""<b>t</b>emperature-<b>s</b>en<b>s</b>itive"" |CITS: 
                     [783133]|<br>
                     AsnS: ""<b>as</b>paragi<b>n</b>yl-tRNA <b>s</b>ynthetase"" 
                     |CITS: [334719]|
                     Review: |CITS: [10966471]|"
