     gene            986078..988690
                     /gene="pepN"
                     /locus_tag="b0932"
                     /label=pepN
     CDS             986078..988690
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pepN"
                     /locus_tag="b0932"
                     /product="trifunctional aminopeptidase N / alanyl
                     aminopeptidase / cysteinylglycine dipeptidase"
                     /EC_number="3.4.11.2"
                     /label=pepN
                     /note="Aminopeptidase N is an aminoendopeptidase, capable
                     of breaking down small peptides as well as whole, native 
                     proteins |CITS: [1271][12482750]|.
                     Aminopeptidase N catalyzes the hydrolysis of a peptide bond
                     either at the end of a small peptide or in the middle of a 
                     protein. In its exopeptidase activity, it shows a cleavage 
                     preference for basic and small amino acids, in the order 
                     arginine > alanine > lysine > glycine |CITS: [14663077]|. 
                     Other substrates include cysteinylglycine |CITS: 
                     [11157967]|. Phosphorylation of peptide side chains near 
                     the cut site can prevent hydrolysis of the peptide bond 
                     |CITS: [9026356]|.
                     Some results suggest that aminopeptidase N synthesis is 
                     independent of growth conditions, though other experiments 
                     have demonstrated increased synthesis of this protein 
                     during phosphate starvation, anaerobiosis and growth on 
                     glycerol and succinate |CITS: [6752120][6129564][2863254]|.
                     Crystal structures of solitary aminopeptidase N and the 
                     enzyme complexed with bestatin have been determined to 1.5 
                     and 1.6 &Aring; resolution, respectively |CITS: [16885166]|
                     and at 1.65 and 2.30 &Aring; resolution, respectively 
                     |CITS: [16938892]|. Crystal structures of the enzyme in 
                     complex with the following ligands have also been 
                     determined: L-arginine, L-lysine, L-phenylalanine, 
                     L-tyrosine and L-tryptophan at 2.00, 1.50, 1.30, 1.70 and 
                     1.85 &Aring; resolution, respectively |CITS: [18416562]|; 
                     the aminophosphinic inhibitor PL250 at 1.55 &Aring; 
                     resolution |CITS: [19622865]|; 2,4-diaminobutyric acid and 
                     L-serine at 2.30 and 1.45 &Aring; resolution, respectively 
                     |CITS: [22025387]|; and actinonin and amastatin at 1.90 and
                     2.31 &Aring; resolution, respectively |CITS: [25644575]|. 
                     The structure of a Glu121Gln mutant has also been 
                     determined at 2.15 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [22025387]|.
                     There is considerable disagreement in the earlier 
                     literature regarding the properties of PepN including its 
                     native apparent molecular mass, substrate specificity, 
                     active site mechanism, metal ion binding, and <i>in 
                     vivo</i> localization |CITS: 
                     [1271][340221][7041825][12482750][14663077][16380266]|. 
                     Subsequent structural work on PepN showed that active site 
                     residues His297, Glu298, His301 and Glu320 form the zinc 
                     binding motif HEXXHX<sub>18</sub>E. These four atoms 
                     provide a tetrahedral coordination with a central zinc ion 
                     |CITS: [16885166]|. The enzyme also contains the GXMEN 
                     exopeptidase motif at the active site. It cleaves bonds 
                     adjacent to both the large, nonpolar amino acids Phe and 
                     Tyr, and polar amino acids Lys and Arg |CITS: 
                     [18416562][16938892]|. PepN is an M1 class aminopeptidase 
                     |CITS: [18416562][19527767]|.
                     Mutagenesis studies have helped to define the catalytic 
                     mechanism of PepN |CITS: 
                     [19356140][19527767][22411732][23897806]|. Residues E121 
                     and K319 form a conserved salt bridge that is essential for
                     enzymatic activity |CITS: [22411732]|. Mutagenesis 
                     experiments also showed that the catalytic activity of PepN
                     is important for <i>E. coli</i> growth adaptation to 
                     nutritional downshift and high temperature stress |CITS: 
                     [22766257]|.
                     PepN can process the antibacterial agent microcin C to a 
                     compound that inhibits translation |CITS: [18223070]|. 
                     Phosphinate, propenylphosphinate, decylphosphinate analogs 
                     of the dipeptide alanyl-alanine were shown to inhibit PepN 
                     |CITS: [16168644]|. Several potent and selective inhibitors
                     of recombinant <i>E. coli</i> PepN were identified by 
                     screening a library of combinatorially generated 
                     peptidomimetics |CITS: [24989844]|.
                     Using paired termini antisense RNA expression vectors, 
                     silencing of <i>pepN</i>, and parallel silencing of 
                     <i>pepN</i> with several functionally unrelated genes, was 
                     demonstrated |CITS: [19515932]|."
