                     /note="<i>ycbQRSTUVF</i> is a putative chaperone-usher
                     fimbrial operon in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 |CITS: 
                     [12192075][20345943]|. The operon is cryptic under normal 
                     laboratory conditions but when constitutive expression is 
                     induced in a strain that lacks the type 1 fimbrial complex 
                     the <i>ycb</i> operon promotes biofilm formation in minimal
                     media on a variety of abiotic surfaces and produces 
                     numerous surface fimbrial structures that can be observed 
                     microscopically |CITS: [20345943]|. Expression of the 
                     <i>ycb</i> operon is regulated by the global regulators 
                     |FRAME: PD00288 ""H-NS""| and |FRAME: CPLX0-226 ""CRP 
                     cAMP""| |CITS: [20345943]|.
                     Deletion of each gene (with the exception of <i>ycbF</i>) 
                     in the 7 gene <i>ycbQRSTUVF</i> operon (in a K-12 BW25113 
                     background) decreases invasion into a human ileocecal 
                     epithelial cell line compared to wild type |CITS: 
                     [27323865]|.
                     The <i>ycbQRST</i> gene cluster has been renamed 
                     <i>elfADCG</i> (<i><u>E</u>. coli</i> YcbQ 
                     <u>l</u>aminin-binding <u>f</u>imbriae) based on 
                     characterization in Shiga-toxigenic <i>E. coli</i> (STEC) 
                     O157:H7 where the first gene of the operon (<i>ycbQ</i>) is
                     shown to encode the major subunit of laminin-binding 
                     fimbriae; the N-terminal sequence of purified ElfA matches 
                     that of the predicted protein encoded by <i>ycbQ</i> in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655 |CITS: [19508558]|. ElfA cannot 
                     be detected in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 HB101 |CITS: 
                     [19508558]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains 12 chaperone-usher fimbrial 
                     operons, including <i>ycbQRSTUVF</i>; a fimbria-lacking 
                     mutant - strain WQM026 - created by deleting all 12 operons
                     has been characterised |CITS: [33863704]|.
                     "
