     gene            1000224..1001234
                     /gene="pyrD"
                     /locus_tag="b0945"
                     /label=pyrD
     CDS             1000224..1001234
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pyrD"
                     /locus_tag="b0945"
                     /product="trifunctional dihydroorotate dehydrogenase, type
                     2 / dihydroorotate dehydrogenase / dihydroorotate 
                     dehydrogenase (MQ)"
                     /EC_number="1.3.5.2"
                     /label=pyrD
                     /note="Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase (PyrD) catalyzes the 
                     oxidation of dihydroorotate to orotate in the pathway for 
                     <i>de novo</i> biosynthesis of pyrimidine nucleotides. PyrD
                     is a member of the membrane-associated family 2 
                     dihydroorotate dehydrogenases and is linked with the 
                     electron transport system of the cell.  When purified away 
                     from the membrane, the enzyme can be assayed as a 
                     dehydrogenase, using various quinones as well as 
                     ferricyanide or 2,6-dichlorophenolindolphenol (DCIP) as 
                     electron acceptors |CITS: [10074342]|.  There is evidence 
                     that the physiological electron acceptor is ubiquinone 
                     under aerobic conditions and menaquinone under anaerobic 
                     conditions |CITS: [199252][211391]|.
                     The enzyme works by a two-site ping-pong mechanism |CITS: 
                     [10074342]|.  The catalytically competent intermediate is 
                     the reduced enzyme - orotate complex |CITS: [11284694]|.
                     A reaction model for the first half reaction, flavin 
                     reduction, has been proposed |CITS: [12220493]|.  The 
                     available evidence is consistent with a stepwise mechanism 
                     of flavin reduction |CITS: [17154530]|.  The role of 
                     conserved residues near the active site has been 
                     investigated by site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [19530672][19694481]|.
                     A crystal structure of PyrD has been solved at 2.5 &Aring; 
                     resolution.  The structure shows the enzyme in monomeric 
                     form |CITS: [12220493]|; it is thought that the soluble 
                     purified enzyme is a multimer due to the lack of its normal
                     hydrophobic interactions with the membrane.  The 
                     interaction of PyrD with phospholipid vesicles has been 
                     studied |CITS: [17993483]|.
                     "
