     gene            1001408..1001950
                     /gene="zapC"
                     /locus_tag="b0946"
                     /label=zapC
     CDS             1001408..1001950
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="zapC"
                     /locus_tag="b0946"
                     /product="cell division protein ZapC"
                     /label=zapC
                     /note="ZapC is a cell division protein that binds FtsZ,
                     promotes lateral interactions/bundling between FtsZ 
                     protofilaments |CITS: [21216995][21216997]| and suppresses 
                     the GTPase activity of FtsZ |CITS: [21216997]|.  ZapC 
                     interacts with the globular core of FtsZ |CITS: 
                     [26655719]|; reports disagree on whether |CITS: [26619764]|
                     or not |CITS: [26282928]| ZapC also interacts with the 
                     C-terminal tail of FtsZ.  A structural model suggests that 
                     ZapC bridges two FtsZ protofilaments, thereby enhancing 
                     bundling |CITS: [26655719]|. Localization of ZapC to the Z 
                     ring is dependent on FtsZ and independent of FtsA, ZipA, 
                     ZapA or ZapB |CITS: [21216995][21216997]|.
                     Crystal structures of ZapC have been solved |CITS: 
                     [26619764][26655719]|, showing an N-terminal &alpha;/&beta;
                     region and a C-terminal twisted &beta; barrel-like domain. 
                     Residues within a basic pocket in the C-terminal domain are
                     involved in the interaction with FtsZ |CITS: [26655719]|.  
                     Reports disagree on whether ZapC is a monomer |CITS: 
                     [26655719]| or a weak dimer |CITS: [26619764]| in solution.
                     ZapC is a substrate for the ClpXP and ClpAP proteases; 
                     degradation is SspB-independent.  A ZapC mutant containing 
                     the A179D, V180D changes in the <i>ssrA</i>-like tag 
                     sequence is more stable than wild-type and suppresses the 
                     filamentation phenotype of the <i>ftsZ84</i> (Ts) mutation 
                     |CITS: [26978224]|.
                     Cell division in a <i>zapC</i> mutant is almost normal 
                     |CITS: [21216995][21216997]|.  Reports disagree on whether 
                     |CITS: [21216997]| or not |CITS: [21216995]| <i>zapC</i> 
                     mutants in combination with &Delta;<i>minCDE</i> or 
                     <i>zapA</i> mutants show synthetic cell division 
                     phenotypes.  A &Delta;<i>zapC</i> &Delta;<i>zapA</i> double
                     mutant shows increased cell filamentation compared to the 
                     single mutants |CITS: [22505682]|.  Overexpression of 
                     <i>zapC</i> also results in varying phenotypes: |CITS: 
                     [21216995]| report that overexpression of a ZapC-GFP fusion
                     protein results in aberrant organization of the FtsZ ring, 
                     but no growth defect, while |CITS: [21216997][29921670]| 
                     report that ZapC overexpression is lethal due to defective 
                     FtsZ assembly.  The effect of ZapC overexpression can be 
                     suppressed by increased expression of the hypermorphic 
                     FtsA* mutant, but not wild-type FtsA |CITS: [28877250]|.  
                     Overexpression of ZapC can suppress the thermosensitive 
                     phenotype of the <i>zipA1</i> allele at 37&deg;C, but not 
                     42&deg;C |CITS: [29921670]|.
                     ZapC: ""<b>Z</b>-ring-<b>a</b>ssociated <b>p</b>rotein 
                     <b>C</b>"" |CITS: [21216995][21216997]|"
