     gene            1003300..1005408
                     /gene="rlmL"
                     /locus_tag="b0948"
                     /label=rlmL
     CDS             1003300..1005408
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rlmL"
                     /locus_tag="b0948"
                     /product="trifunctional fused 23S rRNA m(2)G2445
                     methyltransferase and 23S rRNA m(7)G2069 methyltransferase 
                     / 23S rRNA m(7)G2069 methyltransferase / 23S rRNA m(2)G2445
                     methyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.264; 2.1.1.173"
                     /label=rlmL
                     /note="RlmL is a fused dual methyltransferase responsible
                     for methylation of 23S rRNA at the N2 position of the G2445
                     nucleotide |CITS: [17010378]| as well as at the N7 position
                     of the G2069 nucleotide |CITS: [22210896]|. Both 
                     nucleotides are part of helix 54, and RlmL has RNA helicase
                     activity that is able to unwind this region during 
                     substrate recognition and methylation |CITS: [22210896]|. 
                     <i>In vitro</i>, recombinant RlmL is able to methylate 
                     naked 23S rRNA, but not 23S rRNA assembled into the 50S 
                     ribosomal subunit |CITS: [17010378][22210896]|. Both 
                     m<sup>2</sup>G and m<sup>7</sup>G were shown to be formed 
                     during early steps of 50S ribosomal subunit assembly |CITS:
                     [20719918]|.
                     The RlmL protein contains an N-terminal THUMP domain 
                     followed by two methyltransferase domains. Analysis of 
                     point mutants in the predicted active sites allowed 
                     separation of the RlmK (G2069 methyltransferase, C-terminal
                     domain) and RlmL (G2445 methyltransferase, N-terminal 
                     domain) functions. RlmK activity enhances the efficiency of
                     RlmL activity |CITS: [22210896]|. The N- and C-terminal 
                     halves can be expressed separately and restore methylation 
                     at their respective target nucleotides |CITS: [22362734]|.
                     Crystal structures of RlmL have been solved, showing that 
                     the N- and C-terminal halves contain separate catalytic 
                     sites and are connected by a flexible loop |CITS: 
                     [21045301][22362734]|.
                     An <i>rlmL</i> null mutation leads to loss of methylation 
                     at G2445 |CITS: [17010378]| and G2069 |CITS: [22210896]| of
                     the 23S rRNA. A strain lacking <i>rlmL</i> shows a slow 
                     growth phenotype and reduced fitness in growth competition 
                     assays |CITS: [17010378]|. Mutations in <i>rlmL</i> cause 
                     phenotypes due to polar effects on the downstream 
                     <i>uup</i> gene, mutations in which affect transposon 
                     excision |CITS: [10715006]|. An <i>rlmL</i> null mutant 
                     shows slightly increased susceptibility to hygromycin A 
                     |CITS: [18554609]|. An <i>rlmL deaD</i> double mutant has a
                     more severe growth defect due to a defect in the assembly 
                     of the 50S ribosomal subunit |CITS: [22210896]|. A knockout
                     of <i>rlmL</i> in combination with <i>rluE</i> was viable 
                     at both 37 and 20&deg;C but resulted in large defects on 
                     ribosome assembly and peptidyl transferase activity at 
                     20&deg;C |CITS: [35260421]|. Ribosomal RNA in a 
                     &Delta;<i>rlmL</i> mutant was more susceptible to reactive 
                     oxygen species (ROS) and UVA damage than wild-type |CITS: 
                     [34277707]|.
                     A study that used a &Delta;10 ribosomal modification 
                     deletion strain lacking nucleoside modifications around the
                     peptidyl-transferase center (PTC) due to deletions of 
                     |FRAME: EG11118|, |FRAME: G6488|, |FRAME: EG12401|, |FRAME:
                     EG11794|, |FRAME: G6581|, |FRAME: EG12433|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11921|, |FRAME: G7845|, |FRAME: G6746| and |FRAME: 
                     EG11507| found that RlmE is the most effective at 
                     alleviating the severe growth defect phenotype of the 
                     &Delta;10 strain under different growth temperatures from 
                     37 &deg;C down to 25 &deg;C and that RlmL can partially 
                     complement the RlmE-containing mutant with respect to large
                     ribosomal subunit assembly in late exponential phase 
                     cultures as determined by sucrose gradient ribosome profile
                     assay. An N309A mutation in the RlmL domain that doesn't 
                     have methylation activity can also alleviate some of the 
                     growth defect of the &Delta;10 strain at 30 &deg;C but not 
                     37 &deg;C, pointing to a possible additional function of 
                     RlmL |CITS: [38554109]|.
                     RlmL: ""<b>r</b>RNA <b>l</b>arge subunit 
                     <b>m</b>ethyltransferase <b>L</b>"" |CITS: [17010378]| 
                     Review: |CITS: [17389639]|"
