     gene            1005420..1007327
                     /gene="uup"
                     /locus_tag="b0949"
                     /label=uup
     CDS             1005420..1007327
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="uup"
                     /locus_tag="b0949"
                     /product="ATP-binding protein Uup"
                     /label=uup
                     /note="Uup is a cytosolic ATP-binding cassette (ABC)
                     protein |CITS: [9139905][15013441][16407313]| that belongs 
                     to the Uup subfamily of the ABCF family |CITS: [30597160]|.
                     Although it was initially suggested that Uup plays a role 
                     in replication fork progression |CITS: [16407313]|, recent 
                     genetic and physical interaction data point to a role in 
                     ribosome assembly or function |CITS: 
                     [COCHRANE15][30597160]|. Uup is implicated in the 
                     resolution of branched DNA intermediates which can arise 
                     from template switching (see |CITS: [28641943]|) at 
                     postreplication gaps |CITS: [31665437]|. Uup compensates 
                     for the function of |FRAME: EG12099-MONOMER| to release 
                     poly-proline-dependent stalled nascent peptides from 
                     translational arrest |CITS: [38661232]|.
                     The purified Uup protein binds and hydrolyzes ATP, leading 
                     to a large conformational change in its C-terminal DNA 
                     binding domain. The ATPase activity of Uup is strongly 
                     inhibited by 0.5 mM N-ethyl maleimide, but is insensitive 
                     to vanadate |CITS: [16407313]|. ATPase function is required
                     for the function of Uup <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [16407313][18848624]|.
                     The DNA-binding activity of Uup does not appear to be 
                     sequence specific |CITS: [16407313]|. The C-terminal domain
                     of Uup, which has similarity to the ""little finger"" 
                     domain of the Y family of DNA polymerases, contributes to 
                     DNA binding activity and is essential for the effect of Uup
                     on the frequency of transposon excision |CITS: [19948254]|.
                     A solution structure of the C-terminal domain indicates the
                     presence of three &alpha;-helices and suggests that the 
                     domain forms an intramolecular antiparallel two-stranded 
                     coiled coil |CITS: [22287065][22995754]|.
                     Mutations in the <i>uup</i> gene cause an increase in 
                     RecA-independent precise excision of transposons |CITS: 
                     [6294054][9139905][10715006]| and decreased growth of 
                     bacteriophage Mu |CITS: [6294054][10715006]|. Deletion of 
                     <i>uup</i> leads to cell contact-dependent decrease in 
                     viability when the mutant is co-cultured with the wild type
                     parent |CITS: [18848624]|. <i>uup</i> insertion mutants 
                     were identified in a genetic screen for genes that are 
                     important for survival of exposure to ionizing radiation 
                     (IR). A <i>uup</i> deletion mutant has a moderate decrease 
                     in IR survival |CITS: [25049088]|. Overexpression of Uup 
                     suppresses the cold sensitivity of a &Delta;<i>bipA</i> 
                     mutant |CITS: [COCHRANE15]|.
                     <i>uup</i> is expressed as the downstream gene in an operon
                     together with <i>ycbY</i>; expression is constitutive at a 
                     moderate level |CITS: [10715006]|.
                     Uup shows increased translation in combination with EF-P 
                     compared to several cytosolic ATP-binding cassette family-F
                     proteins, (|FRAME: YJJK-MONOMER|, |FRAME: G6423-MONOMER|, 
                     |FRAME: YHES-MONOMER|) and |FRAME: YHIH-MONOMER| which were
                     examined for possibly increasing translation efficiency 
                     when nonproteinogenic amino acids incorporation into 
                     proteins cause them to stall within the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [40401556]|.
                     Tex: ""<b>t</b>ransposon <b>ex</b>cision"" |CITS: 
                     [9139905]|
                     Uup: ""<b>U</b>V <b>up</b>"" |CITS: [6294054]|
                     Review: |CITS: [31563533]|"
