     gene            1007457..1008710
                     /gene="pqiA"
                     /locus_tag="b0950"
                     /label=pqiA
     CDS             1007457..1008710
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pqiA"
                     /locus_tag="b0950"
                     /product="intermembrane transport protein PqiA"
                     /label=pqiA
                     /note="PqiA is an inner membrane protein with six
                     transmembrane regions |CITS: [27795327]|.
                     <i>pqiA, pqiB</i> and <i>pqiC</i> form an operon which is a
                     member of the <i>soxRS</i> regulon |CITS: 
                     [7751275][27795327]|; expression is induced during 
                     exponential growth, by paraquat and other agents known to 
                     produce superoxide radicals within cells, such as 
                     menadione, phenazine methosulfate, and plumbagin, but not 
                     by hydrogen peroxide, ethanol nor heat shock |CITS: 
                     [7751275]|. Expression increases under carbon or phosphate 
                     starvation or during stationary phase in an RpoS dependent 
                     manner; expression is regulated independently by SoxS and 
                     RpoS |CITS: [8899708]|.
                     A <i>pqiA</i> mutant has no obvious phenotype, including no
                     change in resistance to oxidants |CITS: [7751275]|. 
                     <i>pqiABC</i>, <i>letAB</I> and |FRAME: ABC-45-CPLX 
                     ""<i>mlaFEDCB""|</i> are related loci; <i>pqiABC</i> (and 
                     <i>letAB</I>) are predicted to encode inter-membrane 
                     transport pathways that contribute to membrane integrity; 
                     simultaneous deletion of <i>pqiABC</i> and <i>letAB</I> in 
                     a &Delta;<i>mlaD</i> background increases sensitivity to 
                     EDTA and SDS; expression of <i>pqiABC</i> from a plasmid 
                     complements the SDS-EDTA hypersensitivity of a 
                     &Delta;(<i>mlaD pqiB letB</i>) strain; the substrate for 
                     transport is not known |CITS: [27795327]|."
