     gene            1011171..1011338
                     /gene="rmf"
                     /locus_tag="b0953"
                     /label=rmf
     CDS             1011171..1011338
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rmf"
                     /locus_tag="b0953"
                     /product="ribosome modulation factor"
                     /label=rmf
                     /note="The hibernation factors RMF, |FRAME:
                     EG11681-MONOMER HPF|, and |FRAME: EG11151-MONOMER RaiA| 
                     protect ribosomes, likely by protecting ribonuclease target
                     sites within the ribosome |CITS: [33503254]|. RMF 
                     reversibly converts active 70S ribosomes to a dimeric form,
                     which appears during the transition from exponential growth
                     to stationary phase and is associated with a decrease in 
                     overall translation activity |CITS: [2181444][7677746]|.
                     In cyro-EM structures of the hibernating ribosome, RMF 
                     together with S1 sequesters the anti-Shine-Dalgarno 
                     sequence of the 16S rRNA |CITS: [30177741]|. Earlier work 
                     using <i>Thermus thermophilus</i> ribosomes showed that RMF
                     binds to a site that overlaps with the binding site for the
                     Shine-Dalgarno region of mRNA, thereby preventing mRNA 
                     interaction with the 16S subunit |CITS: [22605777]|. 
                     Residues in RMF that are involved in ribosome binding and 
                     dimerization were identified by site-directed mutagenesis 
                     of conserved amino acids |CITS: [34012979]|.
                     Purified RMF can cause dimerization of 70S ribosomes 
                     (referred to as 100S ribosomes) <i>in vitro</i>, inhibiting
                     protein synthesis and binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to 
                     ribosomes. RMF specifically associates with 100S ribosomes 
                     in a 1:1 ratio and does not associate with 70S, 50S, and 
                     30S ribosomal particles |CITS: [7677746]|. RMF alone 
                     promotes dimerization to 90S ribosome particles; binding of
                     HPF then promotes formation of stable 100S ribosome dimers 
                     |CITS: [18174192][19170772]|. RMF was reported to bind near
                     the ribosomal proteins S13, L13, and L2, close to the 
                     peptidyl-tRNA binding site |CITS: [12473202]|, and to 
                     protect certain bases in the 23S rRNA, including A2451, 
                     which is thought to be involved in the peptidyl transferase
                     activity |CITS: [15066119]|. However, these results are 
                     contradicted by the results from crystallography |CITS: 
                     [22605777]|. In late stationary phase, the ribosome dimers 
                     dissociate, which is followed by disassembly of the 70S 
                     ribosomes and loss of viability |CITS: [10781560]|.
                     Both RMF and ppGpp contribute to increased levels of noise 
                     in gene expression during stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [17890398]|.
                     An <i>rmf</i> mutant shows reduced viability during 
                     stationary phase and does not contain ribosome dimers 
                     |CITS: [8440252][10781560]|. A null <i>rmf</i> mutant had 
                     longer lag phase, fewer ribosomes and reduced competitive 
                     fitness than wildtype cells in coculture |CITS: 
                     [35377189]|. An <i>rmf ompC</i> double mutant and an <i>rmf
                     ompC rpoS</i> triple mutant show even further reduced cell 
                     viability; this synthetic phenotype may be due to decreased
                     levels of Mg<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [9792800][12437978]|. An 
                     <i>rmf</i> mutant is heat sensitive in stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [15278243]| and shows increased sensitivity to 
                     gentamicin |CITS: [26324267]|. A &Delta;<i>rmf</i> mutant 
                     shows reduced persister cell formation and increased 
                     persister cell resuscitation |CITS: [32007277]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>rmf hpf raiA</i> mutant is defective in regrowth 
                     from starvation; mutants accumulate increased amounts of 
                     fragmented rRNA |CITS: [33503254]|. Genes whose expression 
                     is increased in an <i>rmf</i> mutant compared to wild type 
                     have been identified in a macroarray experiment |CITS: 
                     [16636463]|.
                     Expression of <i>rmf</i> is induced at the transition from 
                     exponential growth to stationary phase or during slow 
                     growth; <i>rmf</i> expression appears to be inversely 
                     proportional to the growth rate |CITS: [8440252]|. 
                     <i>rmf</i> expression is positively regulated by the 
                     stringent starvation factor ppGpp |CITS: [11532026]| and by
                     cAMP-CRP |CITS: [23475967]|. Additional transcription 
                     factors involved in regulation of <i>rmf</i> expression 
                     have been identified |CITS: [30225374]|. <i>rmf</i> 
                     expression is increased by acid stress |CITS: [16088400]|, 
                     and <i>rmf</i> influences survival of acid stress during 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [17185553]|. <i>rmf</i> is one of 
                     only three genes whose expression changed under all stress 
                     conditions tested by |CITS: [19767843]|.
                     The <i>rmf</i> mRNA is extremely stable during stationary 
                     phase, with a half life of approximately 24 minutes in 
                     early and 120 minutes in late stationary phase; transfer of
                     the cells into fresh medium leads to an immediate drop in 
                     half life to approximately 5 minutes. <i>rmf</i> mRNA 
                     degradation requires RNA polymerase activity, RNase E, and 
                     PcnB. Transfer of stationary phase cells also causes Rmf 
                     protein levels to drop rapidly, and inactive ribosome 
                     dimers dissociate into active 70S ribosomes |CITS: 
                     [15743942]|. It was reported that synthesis of RMF is 
                     inhibited by high concentrations of spermidine inside the 
                     cell arising from spermidine supplementation of a 
                     <i>speG</i> mutant |CITS: [7642535][32244348]|. NMR 
                     analysis of the <i>rmf</i> 5' UTR suggested that binding of
                     excess spermidine causes the mRNA to become more highly 
                     structured and makes the initiation codon inaccessible 
                     |CITS: [32244348]|. Another report showed that translation 
                     of <i>rmf</i> is stimulated by polyamines via a structural 
                     change in the ribosome binding site |CITS: [20628056]|. 
                     Depletion of the signal recognition particle receptor, 
                     FtsY, leads to upregulation of RMF levels, thus inhibiting 
                     translation |CITS: [19749044]|. RMF levels are increased by
                     nitrogen starvation |CITS: [27939605]|.
                     The <i>rmf</i> mRNA seems to be regulated by the protein 
                     YhgF, although an inactivating mutation in <i>yhgF</i> does
                     not affect <i>rmf</i> expression, while overexpression of 
                     YhgF does |CITS: [36987847]|.
                     Rmf: ""<b>r</b>ibosome <b>m</b>odulation <b>f</b>actor"" 
                     |CITS: [2181444]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9663655][10320479][15884656][16240630][26 
                     443727][24944100][30476446][31230411][31867037]|"
