     gene            1013941..1014393
                     /gene="matP"
                     /locus_tag="b0956"
                     /label=matP
     CDS             1013941..1014393
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="matP"
                     /locus_tag="b0956"
                     /product="macrodomain Ter protein"
                     /label=matP
                     /note="The MatP protein colocalizes with the chromosomal 
                     terminus region in a discrete focus in the cell. MatP binds
                     specifically to <i>matS</i> sites within the Ter 
                     macrodomain (MD) both <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i>, 
                     preventing early segregation of that domain during cell 
                     division |CITS: [18984159]|. Segregation of the <i>dif</i> 
                     region depends on MatP and |FRAME: G6464-MONOMER FtsK| 
                     |CITS: [23781109]|. After cell division, the Ter MD 
                     migrates from the cell pole to mid-cell, where it is 
                     anchored via the interaction of MatP with the Z 
                     ring-associated protein |FRAME: CPLX0-7686 ZapB| |CITS: 
                     [22580828]|.
                     MatP interacts with the |FRAME: CPLX0-2561| complex and 
                     displaces it from <i>ter</i> |CITS: [26818444][32097603]|. 
                     A contact map of the <i>E. coli</i> chromosome and a 
                     quantitative fluorescence microscopy study show that MatP 
                     is responsible for the formation of a specific chromosomal 
                     domain through restricting the long-range contact-promoting
                     activity of MukBEF in <i>ter</i> and, thus directs MukBEF 
                     localization along the chromosome |CITS: 
                     [29358050][36937278]|.
                     MatP works together with ZapA and ZapB to localize the 
                     divisome |CITS: [25101671][28249098]|; |FRAME: 
                     EG10347-MONOMER|, ZapA, ZapB and MatP form a multilayered 
                     protein network |CITS: [25848771]|. The presence of MatP at
                     the <i>dif</i> site enhances binding and cleavage by 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-2424 Topo IV| |CITS: [27171414]|. MatP is 
                     required for the formation of domain boundaries at the 
                     <i>dif</i> and <i>oriC</i> regions |CITS: [31097704]|. 
                     Using quantitative fluorescence imaging in combination with
                     deletion mutant analysis, MatP and MukBEF have been found 
                     to be essential to chromosome decatenation and, in 
                     combination with other protein partners, contribute to 
                     sister chromosome segregation |CITS: [36937278]|.
                     MatP is a dimer of 35.9 kDa in solution |CITS: [22684059]|,
                     but functions as a homotetramer in DNA binding |CITS: 
                     [23084832][32732900]|. A crystal structure of MatP in 
                     complex with <i>matS</i> DNA has been solved at 3.55 
                     &Aring; resolution. The protein consists of an N-terminal 
                     four-helix bundle followed by a central &beta; 
                     strand-helix-helix that functions as the main dimerization 
                     domain, and a C-terminal coiled-coil. The coiled-coil 
                     domains are able to interact, forming tetramers and thereby
                     enabling Ter domain condensation |CITS: [23084832]|. The 
                     tetrameric form of MatP enables formation of ""foci of 
                     high-intensity""; however, the tetramer does not appear to 
                     function in bridging <i>matS</i> sites |CITS: [32732900]|. 
                     MatP alone and with FtsZ can form condensates in solutions 
                     that contain crowding agents, such as dextran or Ficoll, 
                     but not without them; MatS reduces the condensate formation
                     of MatP and can cause MatP to dissociate from FtsZ bundles 
                     |CITS: [40174834]|. MatP has been shown to associate with 
                     phospholipids. There appears to be a competition between 
                     lipids and <i>matS</i> for binding to MatP, which may 
                     regulate its function |CITS: [31138739]|.
                     MatP was identified in a phylogenetic screen that found 
                     proteins containing domains that only occur in organisms 
                     that also contain a |FRAME: EG10204-MONOMER Dam| 
                     methyltransferase |CITS: [16787973]|.
                     A <i>matP</i> deletion mutant grown in rich medium shows 
                     severe chromosome segregation and cell division defects; 
                     when grown in minimal medium, the cells show no such 
                     defects |CITS: [18984159]|.
                     Under heat stress (40 &Deg;C), the long range distance and 
                     local compactness of the Ter MD chromosome interaction 
                     boundaries increased; this was associated with upregulation
                     of MatP expression |CITS: [38930611]|.
                     MatP: ""<b>ma</b>crodomain <b>T</b>er <b>p</b>rotein"" 
                     |CITS: [18984159]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [20951079][21698131][22201788][23178388][2 
                     8500529][32300862][36549664]|"
