     gene            complement(1014469..1015509)
                     /gene="ompA"
                     /locus_tag="b0957"
                     /label=ompA
     CDS             complement(1014469..1015509)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ompA"
                     /locus_tag="b0957"
                     /product="outer membrane protein A"
                     /label=ompA
                     /note="Early studies (eg. |CITS: [4604263][22003579][11039 
                     76][806476][1107024][340698][328056][783128][328491][323051
                     ][340230][330500][361695][340280][367817]|) identifed OmpA 
                     as one of the major outer membrane (OM) proteins in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> and suggested it had a significant role in 
                     maintaining the integrity of the outer membrane; it was 
                     also implicated in F-pilus mediated conjugation, colicin 
                     sensitivity, and shown to be a bacteriophage receptor (see 
                     reviews by |CITS: [6254441][7039497][6337630]| and further 
                     references within).
                     Purified monomeric OmpA, reconstituted into 
                     proteoliposomes, produces non-specific diffusion channels 
                     of low permeability |CITS: [1370823]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [7678500][7517935][10636850][14644420][15850404]|).
                     The structure of the full length protein has been 
                     controversial; two conformations have been proposed: a 
                     two-domain structure consisting of a narrow, 8-stranded 
                     &beta;-barrel pore in the outer membrane with flexible 
                     extracellular loops and a periplasmic domain that attaches 
                     to the peptidoglycan, and a single domain, large pore 
                     structure (see review by |CITS: [22251410]|; also |CITS: [ 
                     8057857][8577259][9808047][10764596][11276254][15850404][21
                     069910]|). Gating of the transmembrane domain 
                     m39630873|CITS: []|ay involve the switching of an 
                     obstructing Glu52-Arg138 salt bridge within the channel 
                     |CITS: [17041590]|. The surface exposed loops of OmpA are 
                     phage receptor sites and mutations in these regions reduce 
                     phage sensitivity |CITS: [3902787][10368142][16980421]|. 
                     OmpA is found as a dimer in the outer membrane |CITS: 
                     [16079137]|. <i>In vivo</i> homomultimeric OmpA interaction
                     is detected in cross-linking experiments; all the 
                     cross-link sites detected are in the C-terminal domain of 
                     OmpA |CITS: [21697552]|. OmpA forms both monomers and 
                     dimers in detergent micelles; the amino acid region from 
                     residues 188-276 is required for dimerisation |CITS: 
                     [24746938]|. Current models, supported by experimental 
                     evidence, depict full-length monomeric or dimeric OmpA with
                     an N-terminal transmembrane domain attached through a 
                     flexible linker to a C-terminal, globular periplasmic 
                     domain  |CITS: [24746938][25138961][25135663][27760356]|. 
                     OmpA and |FRAME: CPLX0-8279| may function cooperatively to 
                     position the cell wall |CITS: [27866852][28978443]| (see 
                     further |CITS: [30713026]|). Removal of OmpA 
                     (&Delta;<i>ompA</i>) decreases outer membrane integrity and
                     abolishes the outer membrane porin ""lattice"" - a 
                     structure that is usually tolerant to changes in OM 
                     composition; OmpA functions to order the OM lattice by 
                     remaining immobile and interacting with surrounding OM 
                     proteins and lipids |CITS: [39630873]|.
                     OmpA has served as a model for studying the folding and 
                     membrane insertion of outer membrane proteins |CITS: [2229 
                     053][1502158][7849052][7623380][7499313][8670856][8855933][
                     8652568][10548052][9917413][10213603][10213602][12441110][2
                     0356666][21256134][22992178][21925139][27973779][28001375][
                     35257747][37710048]| (and see reviews by |CITS: 
                     [14513830][16581049][25983306]|). OmpA has also been used 
                     to study protein export and sorting (see for example |CITS:
                     [3912172][3025188][3047121][3047120][2689448][21671 
                     76][1825804][1826054][8089132][1830665]|). The periplasmic 
                     chaperone |FRAME: CPLX0-7711 ""Skp""|, and 
                     lipopolysaccharide (LPS) are both required for efficient 
                     insertion and folding of OmpA into phospholipid bilayers 
                     <i>in vitro</i>; binding to Skp maintains OmpA in an 
                     unfolded state in solution |CITS: [12509434]| (see further 
                     |CITS: 
                     [17928002][19780589][19382746][19181847][24077225]|). OmpA 
                     is inserted into the outer membrane by the |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3933 ""Bam outer membrane protein assembly 
                     complex""|.
                     OmpA forms complexes with the outer membrane lipoprotein 
                     |FRAME: RCSF-MONOMER RcsF|; the Bam complex is implicated 
                     in assembly of RcsF-OmpA |CITS: 
                     [25267629][25525882][32985973]|. OmpA interacts with the 
                     sensor lipoprotein |FRAME: EG12137-MONOMER NlpE|; both OmpA
                     and NlpE are required to activate the Cpx response in 
                     surface-adhered cells |CITS: [37022159]|. OmpA modulates 
                     the enzymatic activity of |FRAME:EG10673-MONOMER OmpT| and 
                     |FRAME: EG12180-MONOMER PagP| <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [40669592]|.
                     <i>micA</i> and <i>rseX</i> are small RNAs that bind as 
                     antisense RNAs to the translation initiation region of 
                     <i>ompA</i> mRNA, blocking ribosome binding and 
                     downregulating OmpA expression |CITS: 
                     [16204185][16513633]|. <i>micA</i> binding also results in 
                     <i>ompA</i> mRNA destabilization by RNase E |CITS: 
                     [16204185][16556233]|. This destabilization occurs when 
                     quickly growing cells enter stationary phase, and requires 
                     the RNA-binding protein Hfq |CITS: [16204185][16313626]|. 
                     <i>micA</i> transcription is controlled by 
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup> |CITS: [16336047][17007876]|.  In 
                     response to the &sigma;<sup>E</sup> envelope stress 
                     response, <i>ompA</i> transcription decreases |CITS: 
                     [16336047]|. RNase R, Poly(A) Polymerase I, and PNPase are 
                     also involved in destabilization of the <i>ompA</i> mRNA 
                     transcript |CITS: [16556233]|. <i>ompA</i> expression 
                     increases as part of the Ntr response to help alleviate 
                     nitrogen limitation |CITS: [16575570]|.
                     OmpA is overexpressed during biofilm formation (<i>E. 
                     coli</i> laboratory strain XL1-blue, grown on hydrogel) 
                     |CITS: [16888722]|. Deletion of <i>ompA</i> in strain 
                     MG1655 decreases biofilm formation |CITS: [16317765]|. OmpA
                     influences cellulose production and biofilm formation 
                     through the CpxRA stress response system |CITS: 
                     [19601955]|. OmpA is down-regulated when <i>E. coli</i> is 
                     exposed to ethanol; when cultured in the presence of 
                     ethanol, &Delta;<i>ompA</i> mutants have significantly 
                     decreased levels of intracellular ethanol compared to 
                     wild-type |CITS: [29518576]|.
                     &Delta;<i>ompA</i> cells have reduced outer membrane 
                     stiffness and decreased survival time during mechanical 
                     perturbation; outer membrane proteins (including OmpA) 
                     contribute to the load bearing capacity of the outer 
                     membrane in <i>E. coli</i> cells |CITS: [30022160]|.
                     OmpA is the primary receptor for the uncharacterized 
                     environmental phage isolate R3 |CITS: [32675236]| (see 
                     further |CITS: [34784345]| .
                     A description of outer membrane protein nomenclature, 
                     including early and recommended names can be found in 
                     |CITS: [6254441]|.
                     In the Transporter Classification Database, OmpA is a 
                     member of the large OmpA-OmpF Porin (OOP) family |CITS: 
                     [26546518]|.
                     <i>ompA</i> exhibits mosaic evolution patterns in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [35285711][40444982]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [2202726][14513830][17559395][30713026][22251410]|"
