     gene            complement(1015866..1016375)
                     /gene="sulA"
                     /locus_tag="b0958"
                     /label=sulA
     CDS             complement(1015866..1016375)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sulA"
                     /locus_tag="b0958"
                     /product="cell division inhibitor SulA"
                     /label=sulA
                     /note="SulA inhibits cell division as part of the SOS
                     response |CITS: [6998947][7012641][6087326]|; of the 
                     proteins induced during the SOS response, SulA is the most 
                     responsible for causing cell death |CITS: [20221259]|. 
                     |FRAME: EG10347-MONOMER| is the cellular target of SulA 
                     |CITS: [6087326][2994059][3011740][3011743][2145263][77268 
                     36][8752322]|. SulA blocks Z ring formation |CITS: 
                     [8432706]| by inhibiting polymerization of FtsZ |CITS: 
                     [9501185][9683493 
                     ][10931335][12202250][18245292][22432817]|. The SulA-FtsZ 
                     dissociation constant is 0.78 &micro;M, suggesting 
                     competition between binding of free FtsZ subunits either to
                     protofilament ends or to SulA |CITS: [22432817]|. The 
                     |FRAME: G7084-MONOMER CbeA| antitoxin enhances filament 
                     bundling of |FRAME: EG10608-MONOMER MreB| and FtsZ, and is 
                     able to neutralize the effects of inhibitors of MreB and 
                     FtsZ, including SulA |CITS: [22515815]|.
                     Inhibition of cell division by SulA is reversible even in 
                     the absence of protein synthesis |CITS: [3011740]|. The 
                     central domain of SulA is required for activity |CITS: 
                     [9180687]|. The <i>in vivo</i> half life of SulA in a 
                     <i>lon</i><sup>+</sup> strain is approxmiately 10 minutes 
                     |CITS: [3011740]|; SulA is also a thermolabile protein 
                     |CITS: [17644648]|.
                     A <i>sulA</i> mutant was initially isolated as a suppressor
                     of UV sensitivity of a <i>lon</i> mutant |CITS: [783136]|. 
                     Degradation of SulA by |FRAME: EG10542-MONOMER Lon| is 
                     responsible for the short half life of SulA protein |CITS: 
                     [6300834][6327610][7612249]|. The C-terminal 8 amino acids 
                     of SulA are required for degradation by Lon |CITS: 
                     [9180687][10788793]|; specifically, the final histidine 
                     residue plays a role in recognition by Lon |CITS: 
                     [11513747]|. <i>In vivo</i> inactivation of SulA by Lon 
                     requires binding to Lon's N domain and ATP hydrolysis, but 
                     not degradation |CITS: [24205897]|. Overexpression of 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-1163| |CITS: [9315728]| or its increased 
                     expression at elevated temperatures |CITS: [10368141]| can 
                     substitute for Lon function, and purified HslVU can degrade
                     SulA |CITS: [10368140][10452560]|. Conversely, |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-1161 HslU| alone can act as a chaperone, preventing 
                     aggregation of SulA |CITS: [10908725]|. Cleavage sites 
                     within SulA for Lon |CITS: [11856303]| and HslVU |CITS: 
                     [14572649]| have been determined. SulA mutants can rescue 
                     |FRAME: EG30100|-defective cells in the presence of various
                     DNA-damaging agents, such as UV light and mitomycin C 
                     |CITS: [29027471][37690591]|.
                     Mutations that make the cell resistant to SulA map to FtsZ 
                     |CITS: [2145263]|; in the absence of FtsZ, SulA is 
                     destabilized |CITS: [2994059]|. SulA interacts directly 
                     with FtsZ |CITS: [7726836][8752322]|.
                     The <i>sulA</i> promoter is directly repressed by the SOS 
                     regulator, |FRAME: PC00010 LexA| |CITS: 
                     [6306396][6317868]|. <i>sulA</i> expression is induced by 
                     thymine starvation or UV light |CITS: [7012641]|. 
                     Expression varies under different growth conditions |CITS: 
                     [8332062]| and at different pH |CITS: [7934886]|. |CITS: 
                     [2473377]| discovered a constitutively expressed 353 nt 
                     antisense transcript, <i>isf</i>, in the intergenic region 
                     between <i>sulA</i> and <i>ompA</i> that is complementary 
                     to the 3' end of the <i>sulA</i> mRNA. The increase in 
                     <i>sulA</i> expression during phosphate starvation is 
                     dependent on H-NS |CITS: [8332062]|. <i>sulA</i> mRNA 
                     stability during glucose starvation is diminished in the 
                     absence of |FRAME: CPLX0-3281| function |CITS: [16309817]|.
                     <i>sulA</i> is involved in high-pressure-induced cell 
                     filamentation |CITS: [15748989]|
                     <i>sulA</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     SulA expression and cell filamentation is associated with 
                     UPEC pathogenesis. Virulence and establishment of the 
                     quiescent intracellular reservoir is attenuated in a 
                     &Delta;<i>sulA</i> mutant of UTI89 |CITS: [17172451]|. 
                     SfiA: ""<b>s</b>uppress <b>fi</b>lamentation <b>A</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [1107802]|
                     SulA: ""<b>su</b>ppressor of <i><b>l</b>on</i>"" |CITS: 
                     [783136]|
                     Review: |CITS: [19747971]|"
