     gene            1025520..1025798
                     /gene="yccX"
                     /locus_tag="b0968"
                     /label=yccX
     CDS             1025520..1025798
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yccX"
                     /locus_tag="b0968"
                     /product="acylphosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.1.7"
                     /label=yccX
                     /note="<i>yccX</i> encodes an acylphosphatase with
                     relatively low catalytic efficiency, but very high 
                     structural stability |CITS: [17134700]|. A C5-C49 disulfide
                     bond is not required for catalytic activity |CITS: 
                     [17134700]|, but accelerates protein folding |CITS: 
                     [18495159]|. The functional role of the enzyme <i>in 
                     vivo</i> is unknown |CITS: [17134700]|.
                     An NMR solution structure of YccX has been determined 
                     |CITS: [17021943]|. A crystal structure of YccX was 
                     obtained to 2.55 &Aring; resolution. A 3D model of this 
                     structure differs from the NMR structure in multiple ways, 
                     including the presence of an additional short alpha helix 
                     (residues 81-86) and an intertwined dimer in the C-terminal
                     edge where the C-terminal &beta;-strand was swapped between
                     two protomers. This structure differs from the 
                     <i>Pyrococcus horikoshii</i> AcP dimer |CITS: 
                     [15779887][37804591]|.
                     <i>yccX</i> shows differential codon adaptation, resulting 
                     in differential translation efficiency signatures, in 
                     thermophilic microbes. It was therefore predicted to play a
                     role in the heat shock response. A <i>yccX</i> deletion 
                     mutant was shown to be more sensitive than wild-type 
                     specifically to heat shock, but not other stresses |CITS: 
                     [24580753]|.
                     A protein-enzyme interactions (PEIs) study that examined 
                     the connection between protein-protein interactions (PPIs) 
                     and metabolomics networks studied in 22 different 
                     environmental conditions found that the PEI |FRAME: 
                     EG10598-MONOMER MinE|-YccX may have a previously unknown 
                     role in regulating metabolism, in part because the PEI is 
                     conserved across many other bacterial taxa |CITS: 
                     [39903745]|."
                     /translation="MSKVCIIAWVYGRVQGVGFRYTTQYEAKRLGLTGYAKNLDDGSVE
