     gene            1035901..1035993
                     /gene="appX"
                     /locus_tag="b4592"
                     /label=appX
     CDS             1035901..1035993
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="appX"
                     /locus_tag="b4592"
                     /product="trifunctional cytochrome bd-II accessory subunit
                     AppX / cytochrome bd-II ubiquinol:oxygen oxidoreductase / 
                     cytochrome bd-II terminal oxidase"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.7"
                     /label=appX
                     /note="AppX is a small, single transmembrane, accessory
                     subunit of cytochrome <i>bd</i>-II.
                     AppX and |FRAME: MONOMER0-2663 ""CydX""| are paralogs and 
                     may have overlapping functions in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23749980]|. Analysis of intact protein complexes 
                     released directly from native membranes indicates that AppX
                     and CydX are able to interact simultaneously with CydAB to 
                     form a heterotetramer |CITS: [30442809]|. In the cryo-EM 
                     structure of AppBCX reported by |CITS: [34764272]| (a 
                     homodimer of two AppBCX heterotrimers with inhibitor bound)
                     AppX interacts with AppC and forms the major contact 
                     between the two heterotrimers.
                     AppX is expressed during stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [19121005]|.  Expression of AppX is higher in minimal 
                     glucose medium than in rich medium, increases upon extended
                     heat shock, and is dramatically increased during 
                     oxygen-limited growth |CITS: [19734316]|.
                     AppX is predicted to contain a single transmembrane region;
                     the majority of AppX partitions into the outer membrane 
                     fraction using a sucrose fractionation protocol |CITS: 
                     [21778229]|. AppX is predicted to be a bitopic inner 
                     membrane protein |CITS: [27510400]|."
