     gene            1036073..1037371
                     /gene="appA"
                     /locus_tag="b0980"
                     /label=appA
     CDS             1036073..1037371
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="appA"
                     /locus_tag="b0980"
                     /product="multifunctional periplasmic phosphoanhydride 
                     phosphatase/multiple inositol-polyphosphate phosphatase / 
                     GTP phosphohydrolase / D-myo-inositol hexakisphosphate 
                     phosphohydrolase / D-myo-inositol (2,3,4,5) 
                     pentakisphosphate phosphohydrolase / D-myo-inositol 
                     (2,3,4,5) tetrakisphosphate phosphohydrolase / 
                     D-myo-inositol (2,4,5) triphosphate phosphohydrolase / 
                     D-myo-inositol (2,5) bisphosphate phosphohydrolase"
                     /label=appA
                     /note="<i>appA</i> encodes a periplasmic protein with 
                     phosphoanhydride phosphatase and multiple 
                     inositol-polyphosphate phosphatase activity.
                     The purified protein (initially isolated from <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-10) has phosphoanhydride phosphatase activity 
                     that is optimal at pH2-4; the enzyme is not active on most 
                     phosphomonoesters and preferentially hydrolyses the distal 
                     phosphoryl residues of GTP and of the regulatory nucleotide
                     guanosine 5',3'-bisphosphate  |CITS: [6282821]|.  The 
                     expression of the enzyme is subject to complex regulation; 
                     it is induced by anoxia, influenced by the phase of growth,
                     and the concentration of phosphate |CITS: 
                     [6282821][3031017][8071219][8626281][9079897]|.
                     The biological significance of AppA is not clear; the 
                     enzyme is apparently not able to supply the cell with 
                     inorganic phosphate split from exogenous phosphorylated 
                     compounds or polyphosphates.  Its complex regulatory 
                     characteristics, and in particular its synthesis in 
                     response to oxygen deprivation have suggested a possible 
                     relationship with fermentation or respiration |CITS: 
                     [1658595]|.
                     Purified AppA has multiple inositol-polyphosphate 
                     phosphatase activity; it dephosphorylates myo-inositol 
                     hexakisphosphate (phytate) in a sequential manner to 
                     produce |FRAME: CPD-6746 ""myo-inositol monophosphate""| 
                     (myo-inositol 2-monophosphate). The hydrolysis rate 
                     decreases markedly during the later steps possibly due to 
                     inhibition by the phosphate product |CITS: [8387749], 
                     [9925555], [11035187]|. Inositol-polyphosphate phosphatase 
                     activity is expressed under anaerobic conditions and in 
                     late stationary phase growth |CITS: 
                     [GreinerBiolChemHoppe-Seyler372,664]|. The enzyme may exist
                     as a dimer or higher order oligomer <i>in vivo</i>; two 
                     isoforms can be distinguished on the basis of isolelectric 
                     point; the two isoforms have similar kinetic and 
                     biochemical properties |CITS: [10696472]|.
                     Purified, crystallized AppA is monomeric and contains an 
                     &alpha; domain and an &alpha;&beta; domain; the active site
                     is located at the interface of the two domains; phytate 
                     binding induces conformational change near the active site 
                     |CITS: [10655611]|.
                     AppA is a Group III histidine acid phosphatase (HAP); group
                     III HAPs are capable of hydrolysing metal free phytate plus
                     various other phosphate esters at acidic pH |CITS: 
                     [14586576]|
                     "
