     gene            complement(1046419..1046631)
                     /gene="cspH"
                     /locus_tag="b0989"
                     /label=cspH
     CDS             complement(1046419..1046631)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cspH"
                     /locus_tag="b0989"
                     /product="CspA family protein CspH"
                     /label=cspH
                     /note="CspH was identified as a member of the CspA cold
                     shock protein family; it is most closely related to CspF, 
                     and its function is unknown |CITS: [9484881]|, though 
                     recent studies are finding more conditions under which CspF
                     and CspH respond |CITS: [40312551]|.
                     <i>cspH</i> is most highly expressed in mid-log phase; 
                     <i>cspH</i> expression is not cold-induced |CITS: 
                     [24952137]|. Diverse expression responses of the cold shock
                     proteins, CspA-I, were found under different stress 
                     conditions, specifically cold, heat, rich vs minimal media 
                     and oxidative stress caused by H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. 
                     Expression of <i>cspH</i> is significantly up in response 
                     to H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> stress conditions and to heat
                     stress but is down-regulated throughout the growth cycle in
                     both minimal and rich media and Contrary to previous 
                     reports, it does show significant up-regulation under 
                     conditions of cold shock |CITS: [40312551]|.
                     An <i>in-silico</i> comparative study of evolution, 
                     structure and function was carried out for the 9 CspA 
                     family of proteins in <i>E. coli K-12</i>. Despite high 
                     homology (>70% amino acid sequence similarity), it was 
                     determined that 4 paralogous pairs could be identified and 
                     served as the focus for the comparative analyses: 
                     CspA/CspB, CspC/CspE, CspG/CspI and CspF/CspH. CspH 
                     exhibited high binding affinity to single stranded RNA, 
                     high hydrophobicity in the surface area where the 
                     protein-ssRNA interaction would occur, and higher binding 
                     free energy than its paralogous partner, CspF |CITS: 
                     [37333716]|. A follow-up study conducted mutational 
                     analysis and molecular modeling to determine selection 
                     pressure on the paralogous pairs |CITS: [39779584]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17437059]|"
                     /translation="MSRKMTGIVKTFDRKSGKGFIIPSDGRKEVQVHISAFTPRDAEVL
                     IPGLRVEFCRVNGLRGPTAANVYLS"
