     gene            complement(1048890..1051634)
                     /gene="torS"
                     /locus_tag="b0993"
                     /label=torS
     CDS             complement(1048890..1051634)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="torS"
                     /locus_tag="b0993"
                     /product="trifunctional sensor histidine kinase TorS / 
                     protein-histidine autokinase / TorTS signaling complex"
                     /EC_number="2.7.13.3"
                     /label=torS
                     /note="TorS is the histidine kinase of a multi component 
                     regulatory system which induces expression of the 
                     <i>torCAD</i> operon encoding the trimethylamine-N-oxide 
                     (TMAO) respiratory system of <i>E. coli</i>.
                     TorS is required for <i>torCAD</i> operon expression |CITS:
                     [8809780]|. TorS contains three phosphorylation sites and 
                     is an unorthodox sensor kinase; the protein contains two 
                     predicted transmembrane regions, an N-terminal detector or 
                     sensor region, a transmitter domain with a histidine 
                     phosphorylation site (H443) and an ATP binding motif, a 
                     receiver domain with an aspartate phosphorylation site 
                     (D723), and a C-terminal alternative transmitter domain 
                     with a phosphorylation site (H850) |CITS: 
                     [8809780][9135110]|. All three phosphorylation sites are 
                     required for transphosphorylation of TorR <i>in vitro</i>; 
                     H443 is required for autophosphorylation of TorS and is 
                     likely the initial site of phosphorylation; D723 and H850 
                     are required for transphosphorylation of TorR; TorR is 
                     thought to be transphosphorylated through a four-step 
                     phosphorelay TorS(H850) &rarr; TorS(D723) &rarr; TorS(H850)
                     &rarr; TorR(D53) |CITS: [9135110]|.
                     The periplasmic sensor domain of TorS interacts with the 
                     periplasmic TMAO binding protein |FRAME: TORT-MONOMER 
                     ""TorT""|; TorS interacts with both TorT alone and 
                     TorT-TMAO; TMAO binding may induce a cascade of 
                     conformational change from TorT to TorS |CITS: [17040909]|.
                     TorS-TorT complexes, in both apo and TMAO bound states, 
                     have been characterized from <i>Vibrio 
                     parahaemolyticus</i>; in both this species and <i>E. 
                     coli</i> TorS-TorT complexes are likely the physiologically
                     relevant form of signaling complex whether TMAO is present 
                     or not |CITS: [22483119]|.
                     TorS is able to dephosphorylate TorR via a reverse 
                     phosphorelay when TMAO is no longer available |CITS: 
                     [11274133]|.  The C-terminal domain of apocytochrome TorC 
                     interacts with the periplasmic detector region of TorS and 
                     downregulates expression of the <i>torCAD</i> operon |CITS:
                     [10411745][11562502]|.
                     Pairs of TorS phosphorylation mutants are functional for 
                     TorR transphosphorylation <i>in vitro</i> which suggests 
                     that phosphotransfer reactions can proceed through an 
                     intermolecular (<i>trans</i>) mode |CITS: [9135110]|.
                     When oxygen is present the transcription factor IscR 
                     represses expression of <i>torS</i> and <i>torT</i>; this 
                     regulation results in cell-to-cell variability in aerobic 
                     <i>torCAD</i> expression which permits cells to respond 
                     rapidly to a loss of oxygen in environments that may 
                     contain both TMAO and oxygen |CITS: [25825431][29502970]| 
                     Comment: |CITS: [19748334]|"
