     gene            1054712..1057258
                     /gene="torA"
                     /locus_tag="b0997"
                     /label=torA
     CDS             1054712..1057258
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="torA"
                     /locus_tag="b0997"
                     /product="multifunctional trimethylamine N-oxide reductase
                     1 / trimethylamine N-oxide reductase / 
                     menaquinol:trimethylamine N-oxide oxidoreductase I / 
                     menaquinol:trimethylamine N-oxide oxidoreductase"
                     /label=torA
                     /note="<i>torA</i> encodes an inducible trimethylamine 
                     <i>N</i>-oxide reductase.
                     The enzyme functions as a terminal reductase during 
                     anaerobic respiration on TMAO |CITS: [7000002], [COX81]| - 
                     early work was done using <i>E. coli</i> K-10 |CITS: 
                     [358620][7031034][393699]|. TorA contains the site of TMAO 
                     reduction and the |FRAME: CPD-15873| |CITS: 
                     [8022286][18163615][18522945]|. The presence of the GMP 
                     nucleotides is crucial for the insertion of the cofactor 
                     into the <i>apo</i> enzyme |CITS: [35566344]|. Purified 
                     TorA cannot reduce sulfoxides (including dimethylsulfoxide 
                     - DMSO) and has high specificity for the two 
                     <i>N</i>-oxides - TMAO and 4 
                     methylmorpholine-<i>N</i>-oxide |CITS: [8639717]|. There is
                     some evidence that TorA can exist as both a dimer and a 
                     monomer |CITS: [3282544][2512991]|. A 39 amino acid signal 
                     peptide is cleaved |CITS: [8022286]|.
                     Tor A receives electrons from the membrane bound cytochrome
                     <i>c</i> menaquinol dehydrogenase TorC |CITS: [11056172]|
                     |FRAME: EG12195-MONOMER ""TorD""| is the dedicated 
                     chaperone of TorA; TorD is involved in the protection and 
                     maturation of TorA |CITS: [18522945]|.
                     <i>torC, torA</i> and <i>torD</i> form an operon |CITS: 
                     [8022286]| which is regulated by the |FRAME: PWY0-1506 
                     ""TorTSR two component system""| |CITS: 
                     [8083154][8809780]|. Expression is induced by 
                     trimethylamine <i>N</i>-oxide (TMAO) and repressed by 
                     oxygen; expression is not directly regulated by |FRAME: 
                     EG10325 ""Fnr""| |CITS: [6387391]|. The sulfoxides DMSO and
                     TMSO (tetramethylenesulfoxide) induce expression of 
                     <i>torCA</i> but are not substrates of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [8639717]|. <i>torCAD</i> is expressed during exponential 
                     growth with TMAO under both anaerobic and aerobic 
                     conditions and TMAO can be reduced during aerobiosis |CITS:
                     [17850256]|. In the presence of TMAO, the cellular level of
                     <i>torCAD</i> operon transcription is relatively uniform 
                     within a population of anaerobically growing <i>E. coli</i>
                     but is highly heterogeneous in a population of aerobically 
                     growing cells |CITS:[25825431]|. The cell-to-cell 
                     variability in aerobic <i>torCAD</i> expression results 
                     from IscR mediated repression of <i>torT</i> and 
                     <i>torS</i> and permits cells to respond rapidly to a loss 
                     of oxygen during aerobic growth in the presence of TMAO 
                     |CITS: [29502970]|. TorCA abundance is reduced under 
                     iron-limiting conditions; the |FRAME: CPD-15873| (bis-MGD) 
                     is required for induction of <i>torCAD</i> expression, 
                     likely acting at a transcriptional level; a potential 
                     bis-MGD-dependent riboswitch is located upstream of 
                     <i>torC</i> |CITS: [38193660]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains a second anaerobic respiratory
                     system - TorZY - that can use TMAO as an electron acceptor;
                     unlike the TorCA system, <i>torZY</i> has a low level of 
                     constitutive expression and is not induced by TMAO or 
                     dimethyl sulfoxide |CITS: [11004177]|. Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [3904597], [16221580]|"
