     gene            complement(1058006..1058311)
                     /gene="cbpM"
                     /locus_tag="b0999"
                     /label=cbpM
     CDS             complement(1058006..1058311)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cbpM"
                     /locus_tag="b0999"
                     /product="chaperone modulator CbpM"
                     /label=cbpM
                     /note="CbpM specifically inhibits both the DNA binding and 
                     co-chaperone activity of CbpA <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [15184371]| and <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [17337578]|.
                     The CbpM and CbpA proteins interact |CITS: 
                     [15184371][17337578]|.  The amino acid determinants within 
                     CbpM that are required for this interaction have been 
                     mapped |CITS: [21683710]|.  When CbpM is not bound to CbpA,
                     it is a target for the Lon and ClpAP proteases |CITS: 
                     [18502857]|.
                     The <i>cbpAM</i> operon is maximally transcribed during the
                     transition from exponential growth to stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [18502857]|.  CbpM accumulates in late stationary 
                     phase |CITS: [15184371]|.
                     CbpM: ""<b>Cbp</b>A <b>m</b>odulator"" |CITS: [15184371]|"
                     /translation="MANVTVTFTITEFCLHTGISEEELNEIVGLGVVEPREIQETTWVF
                     DDHAAIVVQRAVRLRHELALDWPGIAVALTLMDDIAHLKQENRLLRQRLSRFVAHP"
                     /go_component="cytosol [goid 0005829] [evidence IEA] [PMID
                     19054114]"
                     /go_function="enzyme inhibitor activity [goid 0004857]
                     [evidence IDA] [PMID 15184371]"
                     /go_function="protein binding [goid 0005515] [evidence
                     IPI] [PMID 15184371]"
     gene            complement(1058311..1059231)
                     /gene="cbpA"
                     /locus_tag="b1000"
                     /label=cbpA
     CDS             complement(1058311..1059231)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cbpA"
                     /locus_tag="b1000"
                     /product="curved DNA-binding protein"
                     /label=cbpA
                     /note="CbpA has similarity to DnaJ and functions as a 
                     co-chaperone with DnaK <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [8302830][15184371]|. CbpA binds to curved DNA <i>in 
                     vitro</i>; binding is not sequence-specific |CITS: 
                     [8302830][10551881]|. CbpA is an irregularly distributed 
                     nucleoid associated protein |CITS: [10947847]|. Binding of 
                     CbpA to DNA inhibits co-chaperone activity <i>in vitro</i>;
                     DNA binding and co-chaperone activity is inhibited by 
                     |FRAME: EG12194-MONOMER ""CbpM""| <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [15184371][17337578]|. CbpA forms either a 
                     monomer or dimer in solution |CITS: [15184371][20633229]|; 
                     CbpA forms large aggregates when bound to plasmid DNA and 
                     protects against degradation by nucleases |CITS: 
                     [20633229]|. CbpM disrupts CbpA-DNA aggregates <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [21683710]|.
                     CbpA consists of an N-terminal J-domain joined to a 
                     C-terminal domain (CTD I and CTD II) by a linker region; 
                     CbpA interacts with CbpM through the J-domain; DNA binding 
                     activity resides C-terminal to the J-domain |CITS: 
                     [16973605]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [20226195][24945826][25670677]|). Dimerization activity 
                     depends on an &alpha;-helix located towards the C-terminus 
                     of the protein; CbpA derivatives that are unable to 
                     dimerize have a significantly reduced affinity for DNA but 
                     can interact with CbpM |CITS: [20633229]|. CbpA interacts 
                     with the minor groove of the DNA helix |CITS: [25670677]|. 
                     <i>cbpA</i> is a multicopy suppressor of <i>dnaJ</i> 
                     mutations |CITS: [8302830]|. A null mutant in <i>cbpA</i> 
                     alone shows no obvious phenotype, but in combination with a
                     <i>dnaJ</i> mull mutation, it shows synthetic phenotypes 
                     including a growth defect at both high and low 
                     temperatures, a cell division defect, and a lambda plasmid 
                     replication defect, indicating that the functions of CbpA 
                     and DnaJ are closely related |CITS: 
                     [8302830][7601860][8824642]|. In a <i>dnaJ</i> mutant, both
                     CbpA and DjlA are required for solubilization of protein 
                     aggregates |CITS: [15489435]|. <i>rpoD</i> is a multicopy 
                     suppressor of the low temperature growth defect of the 
                     <i>cbpA dnaJ</i> double mutant |CITS: [9026454]|. 
                     <i>cbpA</i> expression does not respond to heat shock but 
                     is induced at stationary growth phase and during phosphate 
                     starvation; this conditional expression is regulated in a 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent manner |CITS: [7997164]| (see
                     further |CITS: [18502857]|). CbpA accumulates during late 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [10515926]|. During rapid growth, 
                     <i>cbpA</i> expression is repressed by Fis |CITS: 
                     [23341772]|.
                     CbpA: ""<b>c</b>urved DNA-<b>b</b>inding <b>p</b>rotein"" 
                     |CITS: [8302830]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [17919282]|"
