     gene            complement(1062568..1063164)
                     /gene="wrbA"
                     /locus_tag="b1004"
                     /label=wrbA
     CDS             complement(1062568..1063164)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="wrbA"
                     /locus_tag="b1004"
                     /product="NAD(P)H:quinone oxidoreductase"
                     /label=wrbA
                     /note="The purified WrbA protein has NAD(P)H:quinone 
                     oxidoreductase activity |CITS: [16672604]|. WrbA is related
                     to the flavodoxin family of proteins |CITS: [7756978]|. 
                     Unlike the flavodoxins, WrbA does not have a stabilized 
                     semiquinone state. It rapidly takes up two electrons, 
                     generating the fully reduced form |CITS: [16489832]|. 
                     Purified WrbA protein binds one FMN per monomer with a 
                     binding constant of 2 &micro;M at room temperature, which 
                     is weaker than that of typical flavodoxins |CITS: 
                     [9694845][16672604]|. Binding of FMN appears to be 
                     pH-dependent |CITS: [16672604]|, and it increases the 
                     thermal stability and promotes tetramerization of WrbA 
                     |CITS: [17209564]|.
                     Despite the extensive work on this enzyme, its natural 
                     substrate and physiological role remain opaque. A new 
                     crystal structure combined with a novel application of 
                     quantum mechanical calculations can now constrain 
                     computational docking attempts with potential substrates 
                     |CITS: [27183467]|.
                     WrbA is a multimer in solution, existing in an equilibrium 
                     between the dimeric and tetrameric form |CITS: 
                     [9694845][16672604]|. Crystal structures show WrbA to be a 
                     dimer of dimers. Structural comparisons to flavodoxin and 
                     the mammalian NAD(P)H:quinone oxidoreductase Nqo1 and 
                     between the apo- and holoenzyme forms allow interpretation 
                     of the differences in the cofactor requirements and the 
                     catalytic functions of these proteins |CITS: 
                     [17620713][17893367][19665595]|. Additional crystal 
                     structures of WrbA in complex with benzoquinone or NADH 
                     suggest that binding of quinones and NADH to the FMN 
                     cofactor is mutually exclusive |CITS: [17951395]|; however,
                     a new structure shows benzoquinone bound at a different 
                     site |CITS: [27183467]|. A very high resolution structure 
                     shows the FMN cofactor with a twisted isoalloxazine ring 
                     and Met10 as a sulfoxide |CITS: [23999298]|. Molecular 
                     dynamics simulations did not suggest a function for the 
                     methionine sulfoxide residue |CITS: [25152065]|.
                     Steady-state kinetic analysis suggests a ping-pong reaction
                     mechanism and show two-plateau Michaelis-Menten plots that 
                     are dependent on the temperature at which the enzyme had 
                     been held. This result implies allosteric regulation of the
                     enzyme |CITS: [22952804]|.
                     The ""active scaffold"" of the biofilm inhibitor zosteric 
                     acid, <i>p</i>-aminocinnamate, binds to WrbA |CITS: 
                     [26132116]|. Other antibiofilm compounds, purpurogallin and
                     NZ034, have been identified as active against WrbA at 
                     non-lethal concentrations |CITS: [37627607]|.
                     It was initially reported that WrbA copurifies with the Trp
                     repressor protein TrpR and enhances the formation or 
                     stability of TrpR binding to its operator target sites 
                     |CITS: [8516330]|. However, a later report showed that WrbA
                     does not specifically affect the DNA binding affinity or 
                     mode of binding of TrpR |CITS: [9694845]|. WrbA alone does 
                     not bind to the <i>trp</i> operator DNA |CITS: [8516330]|. 
                     The association between WrbA and TrpR observed by |CITS: 
                     [8516330]| may therefore be due to structural rather than 
                     functional reasons |CITS: [9694845]|. Expression of 
                     <i>wrbA</i> is increased during stationary phase and is 
                     RpoS-dependent |CITS: [8516330][15489429]| and 
                     Crl-dependent |CITS: [17224607]|. WrbA accumulates in 
                     response to exposure to uranium |CITS: [24587082]|. In a 
                     strain lacking the |FRAME: CPLX0-3101|, the level of WrbA 
                     protein is decreased during exponential growth and late 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [12486047]|. Like other members of 
                     the RpoS regulon, the steady-state level of WrbA is 
                     increased by growth on acetate |CITS: [11591692]|. 
                     <i>wrbA</i> was predicted to be a target of the small RNA 
                     OxyS, and overexpression of OxyS decreases the expression 
                     of <i>wrbA</i> |CITS: [16717284]|. Expression of 
                     <i>wrbA</i> is also likely directly repressed by ArcA-P 
                     |CITS: [14711822]|.
                     A <i>wrbA</i> null mutant has no growth defect when assayed
                     with the Biolog system. Growth of the mutant strain is 
                     inhibited by <i>N</i>-trichloromethyl-mercapto-4 
                     -cyclohexene-1,2-dicarboximide and 8-hydroxyquinoline 
                     |CITS: [16672604]|. WrbA: ""tryptophan (<b>W</b>) 
                     <b>r</b>epressor-<b>b</b>inding protein"" |CITS: 
                     [8516330]|"
