     gene            complement(1067627..1068319)
                     /gene="rutB"
                     /locus_tag="b1011"
                     /label=rutB
     CDS             complement(1067627..1068319)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rutB"
                     /locus_tag="b1011"
                     /product="bifunctional ureidoacrylate amidohydrolase / 
                     (Z)-2-methylureidoacrylate peracid amidohydrolase"
                     /label=rutB
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains a pathway for
                     pyrimidine degradation (|FRAME: PWY0-1471|).  RutB, which 
                     belongs to the isochorismatase family, hydrolyzes 
                     ureidoacrylate, the product of the first enzymatic step of 
                     the pathway, to generate the unstable intermediates 
                     carbamate and aminoacrylate |CITS: [20400551]|.
                     RutB from <i>E. coli</i> W was purified and appears to be 
                     homooctameric in solution |CITS: [25511822]|.  (Note: RutB 
                     from <i>E. coli</i> W is 99% identical to RutB from K-12 
                     MG1655.)
                     The enzyme from the K12 strain JM109 was shown to act as a 
                     (+)-&gamma;-lactamase, hydrolyzing |FRAME: CPD-26603| 
                     efficiently, making it a promising candidate for industrial
                     processes (see |FRAME: CPLX-85455 ""enzyme in MetaCyc""|). 
                     Active site residues were identified and confirmed by 
                     alanine scanning mutagenesis |CITS: [25511822]|.
                     Expression of the <i>rutABCDEFG</i> operon is under the 
                     control of nitrogen regulatory protein C (NtrC) |CITS: 
                     [11121068]|.  A <i>rutB</i> insertion mutant loses the 
                     ability to utilize pyrimidine nucleosides and bases as the 
                     sole source of nitrogen at room temperature |CITS: 
                     [16540542]|.
                     A crystal structure of RutB was solved at a resolution of 
                     1.9 &Aring;, showing that it iwith six-stranded parallel 
                     &beta;-sheet surrounded by five &alpha;-helices. Mutation 
                     of residues constituting the catalytic triad (D24, K133, 
                     and C166) resulted in complete inactivation of the enzyme 
                     |CITS: [36599150]|.
                     RutB: ""py<b>r</b>imidine <b>ut</b>ilization <b>B</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [16540542]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16595627][17555842]|."
