     gene            1074761..1076269
                     /gene="putP"
                     /locus_tag="b1015"
                     /label=putP
     CDS             1074761..1076269
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="putP"
                     /locus_tag="b1015"
                     /product="bifunctional proline:Na(+) symporter /
                     propionate transporter"
                     /label=putP
                     /note="PutP is a member of the SSS family of sodium/solute 
                     transporters |CITS:[8031825]|. PutP is a sodium/proline 
                     symporter responsible for the uptake of proline.
                     Mutations in <I>putP</I> decrease proline transport and can
                     be complemented by the cloned <I>putP</I> gene 
                     |CITS:[3007935]|.  The PutP protein has been purified, 
                     reconstituted into proteoliposomes, and shown to mediate 
                     proline transport in the presence of a sodium or lithium 
                     ion gradient |CITS:[9693004],[3512540]|.  Mutant phenotype 
                     and expression analysis also suggest that PutP may be 
                     responsible for the transport of the short chain fatty acid
                     propionate |CITS:[17088549]|.
                     Kinetic analysis has shown that PutP binds proline with a 
                     Km of approximately 2 &mu;M and sodium with a Km of 
                     approximately 700 &mu;M |CITS:[9693004]|. Sodium and 
                     proline are transported by PutP with a stoichiometry of 1:1
                     |CITS:[3512540]|. The transport activity of PutP is 
                     associated with charge translocation 
                     |CITS:[15476811],[19366604]|.
                     Sodium ions and/or proline induce a charge displacement and
                     conformational alteration through the binding process at 
                     the cytoplasmic face of the protein |CITS:[10769140]|. 
                     Genetic and spectroscopic analyses indicate that PutP 
                     consists of 13 transmembrane helices with the N-terminus on
                     the periplasmic side of the membrane and the C-terminus 
                     facing the cytoplasm |CITS:[10769140],[9756872]|.  PutP has
                     been found as a dimer in the inner membrane 
                     |CITS:[16079137]|. The structure of transmembrane domain IX
                     of PutP, thought to form part of the ion and/or proline 
                     translocation pathway |CITS: [18156179], [18692508]| has 
                     been modeled using pulsed electron paramagnetic resonance 
                     (EPR) measurements on spin-labeled mutants 
                     |CITS:[19134477]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS:[9693722],[11248195],[12354616]|.
                     "
