     gene            1076812..1077641
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="efeU"
                     /locus_tag="b4490"
                     /label=efeU
                     /note="<i>efeU_1</i> and <i>efeU_2</i> are two fragments
                     of a pseudogene (<i>efeU*</i>). A functional <i>efeU</i> 
                     (<i>ycdN</i>) gene in <i>E. coli</i> Nissle 1917 encodes a 
                     ferrous iron permease |CITS: [16987175]|; a functional 
                     <i>efeUOB</i> operon in enterohaemorrhagic <i>E. coli</i> 
                     O157:H7 encodes a tripartite, acid-induced Fe(II) transport
                     system |CITS: [17627767]| (and see |CITS: [35033753]|).
                     Analysis of transcriptional <i>efe-lacZ</i> fusions suggest
                     that <i>efeU*OB</i> forms an operon in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     which is transcribed from a promoter located upstream of 
                     <i>efeU*</i> |CITS: [17627767]|. <I>efeU*</I> in <I>E. 
                     coli</I> K-12 is disrupted by a frameshift mutation leaving
                     the <I>efeU_1</I> and <I>efeU_2</I> fragments 
                     nonfunctional; EfeO and EfeB are produced despite the 
                     frameshift mutation |CITS:[16987175]|. <i>efeU*OB</i> 
                     transcription in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 is subject to 
                     Fe(II)-Fur control in response to iron |CITS: 
                     [12746439][16987175][17627767]|. The <i>efeU*OB</i> operon 
                     is induced at acid pH and subject to CpxAR dependent 
                     repression at alkaline pH |CITS: [17627767]|.
                     Deletion of <I>efeU*, efeO,</I> or <I>efeB</I> does not 
                     result in any growth defect under iron limitation 
                     |CITS:[17627767]|. Low copy-number expression of a 
                     'corrected' <i>efeUOB</i> operon in an <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     strain lacking lacking all known iron-transport pathways 
                     confers a growth advantage under iron restricted conditions
                     |CITS: [17627767]|.
                     Expression of EfeU decreases in early osmotic upshift (0.4 
                     M NaCl) |CITS:[12218039]|.  <I>efeU_2</I> expression 
                     decreases under anaerobic conditions in both a wild-type 
                     and a &Delta;<i>fur</i> mutant strain |CITS:[15659690]|. 
                     <i>efe</i>: <U>e</U>lemental <U>fe</U>rrous iron uptake 
                     |CITS: [16987175]|."
     CDS             join(1076812..1076916,1076916..1077641)
                     /pseudo
