                     /product="inactive ferrous iron permease EfeU"
                     /label=efeU
     gene            1077699..1078826
                     /gene="efeO"
                     /locus_tag="b1018"
                     /label=efeO
     CDS             1077699..1078826
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="efeO"
                     /locus_tag="b1018"
                     /product="Fe(2+) transport system protein EfeO"
                     /label=efeO
                     /note="EfeO is predicted to be the periplasmic,
                     metal-binding component of a ferrous ion transport system 
                     (see |CITS: [35033753]|) that is cryptic in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     K-12.
                     Analysis of transcriptional <i>efe-lacZ</i> fusions suggest
                     that <i>efeO</i> does not possess a proximal promoter and 
                     is co-transcribed with <i>efeU</i> |CITS: [17627767]|. 
                     <I>efeU</I> in <I>E. coli</I> K-12 is disrupted by a 
                     frameshift mutation leaving the <I>efeU_1</I> and 
                     <I>efeU_2</I> fragments nonfunctional; EfeO (and EfeB) are 
                     produced despite the frameshift mutation |CITS:[16987175]|.
                     The functional <I>efeU</I> (<I>ycdN</I>) gene in <I>E. 
                     coli</I> Nissle 1917 encodes an oxidase-dependent, OFeT 
                     family ferrous iron permease |CITS:[16987175]|. The 
                     <i>efeUOB</i> operon of enterohaemorrhagic <i>E. coli</i> 
                     O157:H7, lacks any frameshift and is functional |CITS: 
                     [17627767]|. <i>efeUOB</i> transcription in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     K-12 is subject to Fe(II)-Fur control in response to iron 
                     |CITS: [12746439][16987175][17627767]|. EfeO levels 
                     increase under iron restriction and when Fur is 
                     inactivated; EfeO levels are independent of iron in a 
                     <i>fur</i> mutant |CITS: [17627767]|. The <i>efeUOB</i> 
                     operon is induced at acid pH and subject to CpxAR dependent
                     repression at alkaline pH |CITS: [17627767]|.
                     Low copy-number expression of a 'corrected' <i>efeUOB</i> 
                     operon in an <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strain lacking lacking all
                     known iron-transport pathways confers a growth advantage 
                     under iron restricted conditions |CITS: [17627767]|.
                     EfeO levels increase when <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strain W3110 
                     is grown at acid pH |CITS: [12107143]|. EfeO protein levels
                     increase in response to Zn(II) stress in <i>E. coli</i> B 
                     strain BL21(DE3) |CITS: [17122063]|.
                     EfeO, expressed from a plasmid can be detected in both the 
                     cytoplasm and periplasm; the periplasmic protein is 
                     correctly processed and transport is not reliant on the Tat
                     system |CITS: [16551627]|.
                     Sequence analysis suggests EfeO consists of two domains: an
                     N-terminal cupredoxin-like (Cup) domain and a C-terminal 
                     peptidase M75 domain, separated by a flexible linker; 
                     potential metal binding sites are present in both domains 
                     |CITS: [19701722]|. In the EfeO crystal structure reported 
                     by |CITS: [35081501]|, the peptidase M75 domain contains a 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> ion bound via a novel ExxE-//-D-//-E motif.
                     Inactivation of <i>efeO</i> does not affect growth under 
                     iron restriction |CITS: [17627767]|.
                     <i>efeO</i> is associated with stress protection in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12;  an <i>efeO</i> insertion mutant exhibits 
                     hyperlethality to nalidixic acid and is killed more readily
                     than wild-type cells by mitomycin C, hydrogen peroxide, UV 
                     irradiation and sodium dodecyl sulfate |CITS: [20128927]|.
                     <i>efe</i>: <U>e</U>lemental <U>fe</U>rrous ion uptake 
                     |CITS: [16987175]|."
