     gene            1078832..1080103
                     /gene="efeB"
                     /locus_tag="b1019"
                     /label=efeB
     CDS             1078832..1080103
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="efeB"
                     /locus_tag="b1019"
                     /product="bifunctional heme-containing
                     peroxidase/deferrochelatase / deferrochelatase"
                     /EC_number="4.98.1.1"
                     /label=efeB
                     /note="<i>efeB</i> is predicted to encode a periplasmic 
                     component of a ferrous iron transport system (see |CITS: 
                     [35033753]|) that is cryptic in <i>E. coli</i> K-12; EfeB 
                     is implicated in exogenous heme iron acquisition.
                     Analysis of transcriptional <i>efe-lacZ</i> fusions suggest
                     that <i>efeB</i> does not possess a proximal promoter and 
                     is co-transcribed with <i>efeUO</i> |CITS: [17627767]|. 
                     <i>efeU</i> in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 is disrupted by a 
                     frameshift mutation leaving the <i>efeU_1</i> and 
                     <i>efeU_2</i> fragments nonfunctional; EfeB (and EfeO) are 
                     produced despite the frameshift mutation |CITS: 
                     [16987175]|. The functional <i>efeU</i> (<i>ycdN</i>) gene 
                     in <i>E. coli</i> Nissle 1917 encodes an oxidase-dependent,
                     OFeT family ferrous iron permease |CITS: [16987175]|. The 
                     <i>efeUOB</i> operon of enterohaemorrhagic <i>E. coli</i> 
                     O157:H7 lacks any frameshift and is functional |CITS: 
                     [17627767]|.
                     EfeB has been implicated in exogenous heme iron 
                     acquisition. Due to the lack of an outer membrane receptor 
                     for heme, <i>E. coli</i> K-12 is unable to utilize heme as 
                     a source of iron |CITS: [4877132][387910]|. However, 
                     expression of the foreign heme receptor protein HasR from 
                     <i>Serratia marcescens</i> enables utilization of heme as a
                     source of iron. Subsequent heme uptake through the <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 inner membrane also requires the dipeptide 
                     inner membrane transporter or a functional EfeUOB system, 
                     such as occurs in enterohemorrhagic <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7 
                     |CITS: [16905647][19564607]|. EfeB and |FRAME: 
                     G7266-MONOMER ""YfeX""| release iron from heme without 
                     destroying the tetrapyrrol ring and are the sole <i>E. 
                     coli</i> proteins able to provide iron from exogenous heme 
                     sources; a <i>yfeX efeB</i> double mutant in a 
                     HasR-expressing strain is unable to use heme as an external
                     source of iron |CITS: [19564607]|. The function of YfeX and
                     EfeB as heme dechelatases has been disputed |CITS: 
                     [22068980]| (but see also |CITS: [25257218]|).
                     EfeB is a DyP (dye decolorizing peroxidase)-like protein 
                     with a predicted Tat signal sequence; EfeB requires the Tat
                     system for translocation into the periplasm; recombinant 
                     EfeB, purified from the periplasm is dimeric and contains 
                     noncovalently bound heme, identified as 
                     Fe(III)-protoporphyrin IX; the heme iron has a high-spin 
                     electron configuration; both holo (heme-containing) and apo
                     (lacking heme) forms of the EfeB precursor protein are 
                     detected in the cytoplasm |CITS: [16551627]|. It is not 
                     clear if  Fe(III)-protoporphyrin IX is a cofactor or 
                     substrate of EfeB (see |CITS: [25778630]|).
                     EfeB may act to reduce Fe<sup>3+</sup> to Fe<sup>2+</sup> 
                     for transport or to oxidize Fe<sup>2+</sup> during 
                     transport |CITS: [17627767]|.  EfeB exhibits guaiacol 
                     peroxidase activity at an optimum pH of about 4.0 and may 
                     function under acid-stress conditions |CITS: [16551627]|. 
                     The crystal structure of EfeB has been determined in the 
                     apo form to a resolution of 2.0 &Aring;; crystals with 
                     bound haem were obtained and diffract to 2.9 &Aring; |CITS:
                     [17183171]|.
                     <i>efe</i>: <u>e</u>lemental <u>fe</u>rrous iron uptake 
                     |CITS: [16987175]|
                     "
