     gene            complement(1096307..1097140)
                     /gene="csgG"
                     /locus_tag="b1037"
                     /label=csgG
     CDS             complement(1096307..1097140)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="csgG"
                     /locus_tag="b1037"
                     /product="bifunctional curli secretion channel / curli
                     secretion and assembly complex"
                     /label=csgG
                     /note="CsgG is an outer membrane lipoprotein which forms
                     the secretion channel for curli subunits.
                     A transposon insertion in <i>csgG</i> abolishes curli 
                     production without affecting production of the |FRAME: 
                     EG11489-MONOMER ""CsgA""| curlin subunit |CITS: [8817489]|.
                     CsgG is a periplasmic facing outer membrane lipoprotein 
                     which folds into a protease-resistant conformation |CITS: 
                     [9383186]|. Purified CsgG forms pore containing oligomers; 
                     CsgG interacts with |FRAME: G6545-MONOMER ""CsgE""| and 
                     |FRAME: G6544-MONOMER ""CsgF""| at the outer membrane 
                     |CITS: [16420357]|| (see further |CITS: 
                     [25219853][31932609]|). CsgG clusters into foci in 
                     curli-producing cells |CITS:[19011034]|.
                     CsgG forms a nonameric transmembrane channel in the outer 
                     membrane with a large solvent accessible periplasmic 
                     cavity; the cavity is open to the periplasm but is 
                     constricted to form a pore at the membrane interface; the 
                     size of the pore eyelet suggests that curli subunits are 
                     secreted in unfolded form; three aromatic residues (Phe63, 
                     Tyr66 and Phe71) located at the pore eyelet may have a role
                     in recognising curli subunits |CITS: [25453093][25219853]| 
                     (see further |CITS: [31932609]|). Overexpression of CsgG 
                     increases sensitivity to the hydrophobic antibiotic, 
                     erythromycin |CITS:[16420357]|. Integration and assembly of
                     CsgG into the outer membrane does not require the |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3933 ""Bam""| or the |FRAME: CPLX0-7976 ""Tam""| 
                     complex; functional CsgG multimers are present in the outer
                     membrane of a BamA depletion strain |CITS: [25976323]|
                     "
                     /translation="MQRLFLLVAVMLLSGCLTAPPKEAARPTLMPRAQSYKDLTHLPAP
