     gene            complement(1097608..1097997)
                     /gene="csgE"
                     /locus_tag="b1039"
                     /label=csgE
     CDS             complement(1097608..1097997)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="csgE"
                     /locus_tag="b1039"
                     /product="curli assembly component CsgE"
                     /label=csgE
                     /note="The <U>c</U>urli <U>s</U>pecific <U>g</U>enes are
                     present in two divergently transcribed operons, 
                     <i>csgDEFG</i> and <i>csgBAC</i>, which encode the 
                     structural, accessory and regulatory proteins of the curli 
                     biosynthetic pathway (reviewed in |CITS: [16704339]|). 
                     |FRAME: G6545-MONOMER ""CsgE""| forms a nonameric structure
                     which binds to the periplasmic face of the CsgGF outer 
                     membrane |FRAME: CPLX-3945| |CITS: [25219853]|; CsgE also 
                     binds to the major curlin subunit |FRAME: EG11489-MONOMER 
                     ""CsgA""| |CITS: [30018113][31932609][32398666]| and it is 
                     proposed that CsgE may act to capture and confine CsgA into
                     the secretion complex for outward translocation |CITS: 
                     [31932609]| (see also |CITS: [25219853]|).
                     A transposon insertion in <i>csgE</i> abolishes curli 
                     production without affecting production of the |FRAME: 
                     EG11489-MONOMER ""CsgA""| curlin subunit |CITS: [8817489]|.
                     Deletion of <i>csgE</i> results in a significant decrease 
                     in the level of detectable CsgA, CsgF and CsgB |CITS: 
                     [11823641][19131513][21645131]|. CsgE interacts with CsgG 
                     and CsgF at the outer membrane |CITS:[16420357]|. CsgE is 
                     able to restrict CsgG dependent secretion of non-curli 
                     substrates but does not restrict secretion of substrates 
                     with a putative curli secretion signal |CITS: [21645131]|. 
                     CsgE reverses the erythromycin sensitive phenotype of 
                     <i>csgG</i> overexpression |CITS: [21645131]|. CsgE 
                     prevents the self-assembly of soluble CsgA into amyloid 
                     fibres <i>in vitro</i>  |CITS: [21645131]|. CsgG remains 
                     localised to the outer membrane in <i>csgE</i> deleted 
                     strains but does not form distinct clusters |CITS: 
                     [19011034]|.
                     Monomeric CsgE (described as a 'goldfish-shaped' molecule) 
                     contains regions of concentrated surface charge that are 
                     implicated in the interaction with curli structural subunit
                     CsgA |CITS: [27298344][30018113]|."
                     /translation="MKRYLRWIVAAEFLFAAGNLHAVEVEVPGLLTDHTVSSIGHDFYR
