     gene            1099407..1099862
                     /gene="csgB"
                     /locus_tag="b1041"
                     /label=csgB
     CDS             1099407..1099862
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="csgB"
                     /locus_tag="b1041"
                     /product="curlin, minor subunit"
                     /label=csgB
                     /note="CsgB is the minor curlin subunit, which functions
                     as a nucleator, triggering the polymerization of CsgA to 
                     form curli fibers on the cell surface 
                     |CITS:[8692856][9312041]|. CsgB is located on the cell 
                     surface and is a minor component of wild-type curli |CITS: 
                     [9312041][9383186]|. The mature CsgB protein contains an 
                     unstructured 22 amino acid N-terminal region plus a second 
                     domain consisting of 5 repeating units (r1 to r5) of 
                     imperfect homology. Each repeating unit is predicted to 
                     form 2 &beta; strands |CITS: [22684146]|.
                     A non-polar <i>csgB</i> mutation abolishes curli production
                     without affecting production of |FRAME: EG11489-MONOMER 
                     ""CsgA""| - the major curlin subunit |CITS: 
                     [8817489]|.Purified CsgB protein forms amyloid fibrils with
                     a cross-&beta; structure <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [19574225]|. Purified CsgB rapidly polymerises into an 
                     amyloid structure <i>in vitro</i> and can function as a 
                     template for |FRAME: EG11489-MONOMER ""CsgA""| 
                     polymerisation both <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [22684146]|. A CsgB protein lacking the C-terminal 
                     19 amino acids is secreted away from the cell in a |FRAME: 
                     G6543-MONOMER ""CsgG""| dependent manner, forms 
                     amyloid-like aggregates and can nucleate purified CsgA 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [17636121]|. The two C-terminal 
                     repeating units, r4 and r5, facilitate anchoring of the 
                     nucleator to the outer membrane and are essential for CsgB 
                     nucleator function <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [22684146]|. 
                     |FRAME: G6544-MONOMER ""CsgF""| accelerates the 
                     polymerization of CsgB <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [29427517]|. 
                     CsgB directly interacts with both CsgF and CsgA; CsgB 
                     connects CsgF and CsgA in the curli biogenesis system 
                     |CITS: [31932609]|."
