     gene            1099903..1100358
                     /gene="csgA"
                     /locus_tag="b1042"
                     /label=csgA
     CDS             1099903..1100358
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="csgA"
                     /locus_tag="b1042"
                     /product="curlin"
                     /label=csgA
                     /note="<i>csgA</i> encodes curlin - the major subunit
                     protein of curli which are thin, coiled surface structures 
                     involved in inert surface colonization, biofilm formation, 
                     and bacterial binding to a variety of extracellular matrix 
                     and serum proteins |CITS: [8459772][2649795][9573197]|. 
                     Curli production requires CsgA and the nucleator protein 
                     |FRAME: G6547-MONOMER ""CsgB""|; soluble monomeric curlin 
                     is secreted from a <i>csgB</i><sup>-</sup> strain and can 
                     be polymerized into curli on the surface of a 
                     <i>csgA</i><sup>-</sup> strain grown in close proximity 
                     |CITS: [8692856]| (see also |CITS: [9312041]|). Secretion 
                     of CsgA is mediated by the |FRAME: CPLX-3945 ""curli 
                     secretion and assembly complex""| |CITS: 
                     [9383186][16420357]| and requires the curli assembly 
                     component |FRAME: G6545-MONOMER ""CsgE""| |CITS: 
                     [25219853][30018113][31932609][32398666]|. The periplasmic 
                     chaperone, |FRAME: G6548-MONOMER ""CsgC""| maintains CsgA 
                     in a solule monomeric state |CITS: [25620560]|. Purified 
                     CsgA polymerizes into amyloid fibrils <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [11823641]| (see also |CITS: [17164238][19574225]|).
                     CsgA fibrillation can be modulated <i>in vitro</i> using 
                     CsgA-derived peptides |CITS: [37330291]|.
                     Mature CsgA contains an N-terminal domain followed by a 
                     repeat domain that contains five, functionally important, 
                     imperfect repeats (R1 - R5) |CITS: [17164238]|. The 
                     mechanism of curli nucleation and growth is the subject of 
                     ongoing investigation |CITS: [18565345][18508760][19966296 
                     ][21877724][22493266][22684146][28628096]|.
                     The <U>c</U>urli <U>s</U>pecific <U>g</U>enes are present 
                     in two divergently transcribed operons, <i>csgBAC</i> and 
                     <i>csgDEFG</i> |CITS: [8817489]| which encode the 
                     structural, accessory and regulatory proteins of the curli 
                     biosynthetic pathway (reviewed in |CITS: [16704339]|). 
                     Curli are produced at low temperatures, at low osmolarity 
                     and in stationary phase and expression is controlled by a 
                     complex regulatory network (see |CITS: [29088115]|). <i>E. 
                     coli</i> strains vary in their capacity to produce curli 
                     |CITS: [8459772]|; <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655 is proficient
                     in curli production (see |CITS: [17873038][18599830]|). The
                     <i>ompR234</i> regulatory mutation activates curli 
                     expression in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655 |CITS: [9573197]| 
                     (see also |CITS: [11234933][11717281]|).
                     Curli contribute to a multicellular life-cycle that 
                     generates <i>E. coli</i> biofilms in a static liquid growth
                     environment; curli help maintain the unique structure of 
                     clonal cell chains during multicellular morphogenesis 
                     |CITS: [36594031]|.
                     Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [19098444][24080089][26439293][31195143][40643233]|"
