     gene            1100417..1100749
                     /gene="csgC"
                     /locus_tag="b1043"
                     /label=csgC
     CDS             1100417..1100749
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="csgC"
                     /locus_tag="b1043"
                     /product="curlin chaperone"
                     /label=csgC
                     /note="CsgC is a periplasmic chaperone that maintains the
                     curlin subunit, |FRAME: EG11489-MONOMER ""CsgA""|, in a 
                     soluble monomeric state.
                     Genetic and biochemical analyses suggest that CsgC has 
                     anti-amyloid activity. Purified CsgC inhibits CsgA 
                     polymerization <i>in vitro</i>; in the presence of CsgC, 
                     CsgA remains unfolded and non-amyloidogenic; CsgC prevents 
                     CsgA from adopting &beta; sheet containing structures 
                     |CITS: [25620560]|.
                     CsgC (from an unspecified <i>E. coli</i> strain) has been 
                     purified and crystallised. It consists of 7 &beta; strands 
                     forming 2 &beta; sheets; it exhibits structural similarity 
                     with the N-terminal domain of |FRAME: DSBD-MONOMER ""DsbD 
                     disulfide oxidoreductase""| |CITS: [21206057][21893289]| An
                     insertion in <i>csgC</i> does not affect production of 
                     curli, but abolishes the autoagglutinating ability which 
                     curliated cells normally possess |CITS: [8817489]|. 
                     Deletion of <i>csgC</i> in a &Delta;<i>csgG</i> strain 
                     results in the accumulation of intracellular (probably 
                     periplasmic) CsgA aggregates |CITS: [25620560]|.
                     CsgC inhibits amyloid formation by the human 
                     &alpha;-synuclein protein (associated with amyloid 
                     formation in Parkinson's disease) <i>in vitro</i> but not 
                     by the Alzheimer's associated protein A&beta;<sub>42</sub> 
                     |CITS: [25620560]|.
                     Comment: |CITS: [25658202]|
                     "
                     /translation="MNTLLLLAALSSQITFNTTQQGDVYTIIPEVTLTQSCLCRVQILS
                     LREGSSGQSQTKQEKTLSLPANQPIALTKLSLNISPDDRVKIVVTVSDGQSLHLSQQWP
                     PSSEKS"
