     gene            1101811..1103232
                     /gene="clsC"
                     /locus_tag="b1046"
                     /label=clsC
     CDS             1101811..1103232
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="clsC"
                     /locus_tag="b1046"
                     /product="bifunctional cardiolipin synthase C /
                     cardiolipin synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.8.41"
                     /label=clsC
                     /note="ClsC is a third cardiolipin synthase in <i>E.
                     coli</i>. Synthesis of cardiolipin during exponential 
                     growth is due to the activity of |FRAME: 
                     CARDIOLIPSYN-MONOMER ClsA|, while all three cardiolipin 
                     synthases, ClsA, |FRAME: G6406-MONOMER ClsB| and ClsC, 
                     contribute to cardiolipin synthesis in stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [22988102]|.
                     Full complementation of a <i>clsC</i> mutant requires 
                     co-expression with <i>|FRAME: G6550|</i>, the gene directly
                     upstream of <i>clsC</i>. However, more recent studies using
                     plasmids with various lengths of nucleotides upstream of 
                     the currently annotated TTG start site found that the ATG 
                     +9 nucleotides upstream is most likely the true start site 
                     for transcription of <i>clsC</i>; the <i>ymdB</i> gene was 
                     not required. Additionally, using a thin layer 
                     chromatography-based assay with P<sup>32</sup> found the 
                     ClsC (+9 bp ATG) produced the P<sup>32</sup>-labelled 
                     cardiolipin, and <i>ymdB</i> was not required for 
                     phosphatidylethanolamine substrate specificity |CITS: 
                     [38527666]|.
                     Cells with deletions of all three of the genes encoding 
                     cardiolipin synthases, &Delta;<i>clsABC</i>, completely 
                     lack cardiolipin |CITS: [22988102]|. Upon overexpression of
                     AtpF (as well as other membrane proteins), formation of 
                     intracellular membranes (ICMs) is observed; the inverted 
                     hexagonal phase of ICMs is absent in a &Delta;<i>clsABC</i>
                     mutant, and thus requires cardiolipin |CITS: [28284722]|. 
                     The effect of deletion and overexpression of <i>clsC</i> on
                     lipid class distribution has been measured |CITS: 
                     [29933046]|.
                     A <i>ymdAB-clsC</i> triple deletion mutant showed greater 
                     biofilm formation than each single mutant; overexpression 
                     of <i>clsC</i> inhibits biofilm formation |CITS: 
                     [28582517]|.
                     Expression of <i>clsC</i> is upregulated at the end of 
                     exponential growth and by cold shock |CITS: [28582517]|. 
                     ClsC counters the inhibition of |FRAME: CPLX0-3281| 
                     cleavage by YmdB <i>in vivo</i>; purified ClsC enhances 
                     RNase III cleavage of RNA into expected cleavage products 
                     <i>in vitro</i>. Rather than interacting with RNA as YmdB 
                     does, ClsC appears to interact with RNase III directly, 
                     albeit weakly, based on AlphaFold predictions when RNase 
                     III is in dimeric form bound with dsRNA, specifically 
                     (RNase III)<sub>2</sub>-R1.1 RNA complex. These structural 
                     predictions also indicated that the I466 residue on ClsC 
                     interacts with A208 found within the C-terminal dsRNA 
                     binding domain of RNase III. Overexpression of a ClsC I466A
                     mutant resulted in loss-of-function for enhancement of 
                     RNase III cleavage. ClsC controlled gene expression of 
                     RNase III-targeted RNA associated with various 
                     stress-responsive pathways based on both overexpression and
                     knockout experiments compared to wild-type |CITS: 
                     [39842605]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [23012456]|"
