     gene            1106319..1108862
                     /gene="opgH"
                     /locus_tag="b1049"
                     /label=opgH
     CDS             1106319..1108862
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="opgH"
                     /locus_tag="b1049"
                     /product="osmoregulated periplasmic glucans biosynthesis
                     protein H"
                     /label=opgH
                     /note="OpgH is a multifunctional inner membrane protein.
                     OpgH, along with the periplasmic protein OpgG, is required 
                     for synthesis of the periplasmic oligosaccharides known as 
                     |FRAME: CPD-16398 osmoregulated periplasmic glucans| 
                     (OPGs), formerly called membrane-derived oligosaccharides 
                     (MDOs); although exact details remain unclear, an OpgGH 
                     complex is suggested to function for synthesis and 
                     translocation of the polyglucose backbone structure of OPG 
                     in a process requiring |FRAME: CPD-12575 UDP-glucose| and 
                     |FRAME: EG50003-MONOMER acyl carrier protein| |CITS: 
                     [6238027][6365896][2945202]| (and see |CITS: [28593831]|). 
                     OpgH also functions separately and individually as a 
                     UDP-glucose-activated inhibitor of cell division; under 
                     rich nutrient conditions, OpgH localizes to the division 
                     site by an unknown mechanism; OpgH interacts physically 
                     with |FRAME: EG10347-MONOMER FtsZ| to inhibit cell division
                     |CITS: [23935518]|.
                     OpgH is an inner membrane protein with 8 transmembrane 
                     domains; the N and C-termini are located in the cytoplasm, 
                     as is a third domain between the second and third 
                     transmembrane regions |CITS: [9352918]|; this latter 
                     cytoplasmic domain contains sequence features 
                     characteristic of inverting glycosyltransferases.
                     In the Transporter Classification Database |CITS: 
                     [26546518]|, OpgH is a member of the Glycosyl 
                     Transferase/Transporter (GTT) superfamily.
                     Early studies characterized <i>mdoA</i> mutants which were 
                     defective in OPG synthesis |CITS: [6238027][6365896]| and 
                     exhibited pleiotropic phenotypes |CITS: [3049584][3279025]|
                     (also |CITS: [1370954][1320618][12798996]|). The 
                     <i>mdoA</i> locus was later shown to consist of two genes -
                     <i>mdoG</i> and <i>mdoH</i>, now called <i>opgG</i> and 
                     <i>opgH</i>; <i>mdoGH</i> RNA, transcribed from an 
                     expression plasmid, is more abundant in cells grown at low 
                     osmolarity (no NaCl) compared to high osmolarity (0.3M 
                     NaCl) |CITS: [1834913]|. For more information on the 
                     osmotic regulation of OPG synthesis see |CITS: 
                     [7041113][1534803]|).
                     Review: |CITS: [10779706]|"
