                     /label=bssS
     CDS             complement(1116157..1116411)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="bssS"
                     /locus_tag="b1060"
                     /product="regulator of biofilm formation"
                     /label=bssS
                     /note="There have been contradictory reports on the effect
                     of a <i>bssS</i> mutation on biofilm formation.  |CITS: 
                     [14731270]| reported that a <i>bssS</i> mutant is impaired 
                     in biofilm formation, while |CITS: [16597943]| reported 
                     that deletion of <i>bssS</i> leads to increased biofilm 
                     formation in LB+glucose and MC9C+glucose media and 
                     increased motility in LB medium.
                     A <i>bssS</i> deletion mutant does not have a significant 
                     growth defect.  Deletion of <i>bssS</i> affects the 
                     expression of more than 400 genes significantly.  Among the
                     affected pathways are indole transport and AI-2 uptake and 
                     processing.  A regulatory model has been proposed |CITS: 
                     [16597943]|.
                     Transcription of <i>bssS</i> is induced upon biofilm 
                     formation compared to planktonic growth in both exponential
                     and stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [12657059][14731270][14727089]|.  Induction of expression 
                     was found to be dependent on the presence of the F plasmid 
                     |CITS: [14731270]|.  Increased transcription of <i>bssS</i>
                     at 23&deg;C is dependent on RpoS and DsrA |CITS: 
                     [18174134]|.  Expression of <i>bssS</i> is induced by 
                     exposure to hydroquinone; this induction is significantly 
                     lower in a <i>yhaJ</i> mutant |CITS: [27713734]|.
                     In an <i>in vitro</i> assay that involves colonization of a
                     monospecies biofilm with the pathogenic <i>E. coli</i> 
                     strain 55989, a <i>bssS</i> mutant strain does not allow a 
                     significantly higher level of colonization by the pathogen 
                     than does the wild type; however, a <i>bssS</i> mutant 
                     allows significantly increased colonization by pathogens in
                     the mouse gut environment |CITS: [23667443]|.
                     BssS: ""regulator of <b>b</b>iofilm through <b>s</b>ignal 
                     <b>s</b>ecretion"" |CITS: [16597943]|"
