     gene            complement(1116698..1116943)
                     /gene="dinI"
                     /locus_tag="b1061"
                     /label=dinI
     CDS             complement(1116698..1116943)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dinI"
                     /locus_tag="b1061"
                     /product="DNA damage-inducible protein I"
                     /label=dinI
                     /note="The <i>dinI</i> gene was first identified as a 
                     chromosomal locus containing a high affinity operator for 
                     the
                     LexA repressor |CITS: [8057377]|. It encodes a small 
                     protein with 80 amino acids and a molecular weight
                     of 8.818 KDa |CITS: [8682790]|.
                     DinI is not detectable during normal growth of the 
                     bacteria, but is induced by DNA damage
                     and is dependent on both LexA and RecA |CITS: [9606202] 
                     [11230150]|. Its function depends on the
                     concentration of the protein.  At concentrations that are 
                     stoichimetric with those of RecA, DinI protein
                     acts mainly as a positive modulator of RecA function |CITS:
                     [15489505]|. DinI stabilizes the RecA
                     filament, reducing or preventing disassembly |CITS: 
                     [15138263]|, which does not affect RecA-mediated
                     ATP hydrolysis and LexA co-protease activities. DinI and 
                     RecX (a negative modulator of RecA, which
                     blocks RecA filament extension and leads to net filament 
                     disassembly) constitute a regulatory network
                     of RecA |CITS: [15489505]|.
                     When DinI is present at very high concentrations that are 
                     superstoichiometric relative to RecA
                     <i>in vivo</i>, it has contradictory effects on RecA. 
                     Overexpression of <i>dinI</i> in <i>E. coli</i>
                     mutant leads to increased UV sensitivity |CITS: [9606202]|.
                     DinI inhibits both the ability of RecA to induce cleavage 
                     of UmuD as well as its recombinase activity |CITS: 
                     [9606202]|. DinI binds to active
                     RecA filament and inhibits its coprotease activity, but not
                     its ATPase activity |CITS: [11230142]|. Nuclear magnetic 
                     resonance (NMR) spectroscopy show that DinI tightly binds 
                     to the central region
                     of RecA (between the N- and C-terminal domains) and that 
                     this interaction is enhanced upon the
                     oligomerisation of RecA |CITS: [12626715]|.
                     A solution structure of DinI has been determined |CITS: 
                     [11152126]|.
                     "
