     gene            complement(1118863..1119510)
                     /gene="grxB"
                     /locus_tag="b1064"
                     /label=grxB
     CDS             complement(1118863..1119510)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="grxB"
                     /locus_tag="b1064"
                     /product="bifunctional reduced glutaredoxin 2 /
                     glutathione:protein disulfide reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.8.4.2"
                     /label=grxB
                     /note="<b>General Information</b><br>Glutaredoxins are 
                     ubiquitous proteins that catalyze the reduction of 
                     disulfides.  Glutaredoxins are similar to thioredoxins; 
                     both are usually small (9-12 kDa), and both are capable of 
                     catalyzing thiol-disulfide exchange reactions. 
                     Representatives of at least one of these two protein 
                     families have been found in all organisms studied, 
                     indicating that proteins of this type are essential for 
                     cellular functions.  A main difference between the two is 
                     the mechanism for their reduction.  Glutaredoxins are 
                     generally reduced by the compound |FRAME: GLUTATHIONE| 
                     (GSH), while thioredoxins are reduced by the specific 
                     flavoenzyme |FRAME: THIOREDOXIN-REDUCT-NADPH-CPLX|.
                     While thioredoxins are found to be general reductants of 
                     protein disulfides, glutaredoxins are better at reducing 
                     mixed disulfides between proteins (or low molecular weight 
                     thiol-containing compounds) and GSH.  The substrate 
                     specificities of glutaredoxins and thioredoxins appear to 
                     depend on geometric and electrostatic complementarity and 
                     long-distance electrostatic interactions between the 
                     redoxin and its target |CITS: [29861944]|.
                     <b>About This Protein</b>
                     Grx2 is one of several glutaredoxins in <i>E. coli</i>. 
                     |FRAME: RED-GLUTAREDOXIN Grx1|, Grx2 and |FRAME: 
                     GRXC-MONOMER Grx3| all carry the classic dithiol active 
                     site CPYC, while |FRAME: EG12181-MONOMER Grx4| has a 
                     monothiol (CGFS) active site |CITS: 
                     [7937896][8636094][15833738]|.
                     Aside from the active site, Grx2 has no sequence similarity
                     to Grx1 and Grx3, which are much smaller proteins |CITS: 
                     [9111025]|.  Only the N-terminal Cys9 is required for 
                     activity, consistent with a monothiol reaction involving 
                     formation of a mixed protein-SG disulfide during the 
                     reaction cycle |CITS: [10593884]|. Grx2 is the most 
                     abundant glutaredoxin in the cell |CITS: [11893749]|.  
                     Unlike Grx1 and Grx3, Grx2 is not a hydrogen donor for 
                     ribonucleotide reductase |CITS: [7937896]|.
                     Grx2 is the primary hydrogen donor to ArsC-catalyzed 
                     arsenate reduction (|FRAME: RXN-982|) |CITS: [10593884]| 
                     and contributes to the reduction of cytosolic protein 
                     disulfides |CITS: [11741965]|.  Grx2 catalyzes the 
                     reduction of oxidized glutathione by dihydrolipoamide 
                     |CITS: [12135599]|, the reactivation of glutathionylated 
                     PAPS reductase |CITS: [12682041]|, and stimulates the 
                     reconstitution of the [4Fe-4S] cluster of FNR |CITS: 
                     [15135079]|.  Grx2 was also shown to catalyze the reduction
                     of |FRAME: SULFO-CYSTEINE| to |FRAME: CYS| and |FRAME: SO3|
                     |CITS: [22607201]|.
                     An experiment utilizing the fluorescent disulfide probe 
                     |FRAME: CPD-26605| to measure thioredoxin and glutaredoxin 
                     reducing activity <i>in vivo</i> concluded that Grx2 and 
                     Grx3 exhibited higher activity in reducing the disulfide 
                     probe than the other redoxins (Grx1 and thioredoxins 1 and 
                     2) |CITS: [35107099]|. On the other hand, an experiment 
                     using a different probe, |FRAME: CPD-26606|, concluded that
                     the thioredoxin system, rather than the Grx system, was the
                     predominant disulfide reductase for fast disulfide 
                     reduction |CITS: [35204259]|.
                     A solution structure of reduced Grx2 has been solved, 
                     showing an N-terminal thioredoxin-fold domain and a helical
                     C-terminal domain.  Grx2 has structural similarity to 
                     glutathione-<i>S</i>-transferases |CITS: 
                     [10427750][11453697]|.  The two domains behave as a single 
                     cooperative folding unit |CITS: [18788752]|.  A 
                     topologically conserved lock-and-key interaction at the 
                     interface between the N- and C-terminal domains via a 
                     conserved methionine residue (Met17) is important for 
                     cooperative folding |CITS: [21736346]|.
                     Single <i>grxB</i> null mutants have no growth defect in 
                     rich or minimal media |CITS: [11741965]|, but lyse under 
                     starvation conditions |CITS: [11889138]|.  Expression of 
                     <i>grxB</i> is regulated by ppGpp and &sigma;<sup>S</sup> 
                     |CITS: [11889138]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [14713336][17659286][19691428]|"
