     gene            1123295..1124830
                     /gene="murJ"
                     /locus_tag="b1069"
                     /label=murJ
     CDS             1123295..1124830
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="murJ"
                     /locus_tag="b1069"
                     /product="bifunctional lipid II flippase MurJ / lipid II
                     flippase"
                     /label=murJ
                     /note="MurJ is the inner membrane protein responsible for 
                     flipping lipid II from the inner face of the inner membrane
                     to the outer face prior to peptidoglycan (PG) 
                     polymerization; MurJ is essential for PG synthesis 
                     |CITS:[25013077], [18832143],[18708495]|. There are 
                     conflicting reports on MurJ binding to lipid II: MurJ does 
                     not interact directly with lipid II <i>in vitro</i> and 
                     does not affect MrcB mediated lipid II polymerization <i>in
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [28233869]|; purified MurJ binds lipid II 
                     with high affinity (<i>K</i><sub>d</sub> of 2.9 &micro;M 
                     for binding of the first lipid II) |CITS: [29461535]|.
                     MurJ has 14 transmembrane (TM) domains with the N and 
                     C-termini located in the cytoplasm |CITS:[18708495], 
                     [23935042]|. MurJ contains a solvent exposed central cavity
                     located within the membrane region which is consistent with
                     a role in transport; the central cavity is lined by TM 
                     domains 1, 2, 7 and 8 and charged residues within the 
                     cavity are critical for function |CITS: [23935042], 
                     [25225268]|. The crystal structure of substrate-free MurJ 
                     in an inward-open conformation has been reported: the 
                     protein consists of two six-helix TM bundles which form a 
                     bilobed structure with a central hydrophobic cavity that 
                     extends almost completely across the membrane; a pair of 
                     carboxy-terminal helices (TM13 and 14) lie outside the 
                     bundles and form a deep groove which is believed to be the 
                     binding site for the isoprenoid tail of lipid II |CITS: 
                     [29891673]|. High-throughput mutagenesis has identified 
                     functionally important regions of the protein |CITS: 
                     [29891673]|. The pyrophosphate, MurNAc and GlcNAc moieties 
                     of lipid II are implicated in recognition/binding by MurJ 
                     |CITS: [29461535]|. MurJ co-purifies with cardiolipins and 
                     other low molecular mass phospholipids; MurJ binds to 
                     cardiolipin with &micro;M affinity; cardiolipin interferes 
                     with lipid II binding to MurJ <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [29461535]|. Binding of lipid II to purified MurJ is 
                     sensitive to pH but is not affected by changes in 
                     Na<sup>+</sup> concentration |CITS: [29461535]|. MurJ 
                     mediated lipid flipping requires membrane potential 
                     (&Delta;&psi;); dissipating the membrane potential alters 
                     MurJ conformation and inhibits transport function <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [29558128]|. MurJ can adopt both inward 
                     and outward facing conformations <i>in vivo</i>, consistent
                     with an alternating access mechanism of lipid translocation
                     |CITS: [30482840]| (see further |CITS: [32129990]|).
                     Labeled MurJ localizes in the lateral wall and at midcell; 
                     midcell localization is dependent on the assembly of the 
                     divisome, the activity of |FRAME: EG10341-MONOMER ""PBP3""|
                     and the synthesis of lipid II; midcell localization 
                     requires a functional |FRAME: EG10344-MONOMER ""FtsW""|; 
                     MurJ function is not required for lateral wall and midcell 
                     localization |CITS: [30112777]|.
                     Depletion of MurJ causes cells to swell and burst 
                     |CITS:[18832143],[18708495]|.  Expression of |FRAME: 
                     UPPSYN-CPLX ""<I>ispU</I>""| (which encodes an undecaprenyl
                     diphosphate synthase involved in PG synthesis) from a 
                     multicopy plasmid suppresses the growth defect of a 
                     temperature-sensitive <I>murJ</I> mutant |CITS:[18708495]|.
                      Incorporation of radiolabeled diaminopimelic acid into PG 
                     intermediates is increased in a <I>murJ</I> mutant, but 
                     incorporation into the PG layer is reduced 
                     |CITS:[18832143],[18708495]|.
                     <I>murJ</I> has a potential &sigma;<sup>54</sup>-dependent 
                     promoter |CITS:[11528004]|.  <I>murJ</I> is one of a number
                     of genes whose expression increases when |FRAME: EG10935 
                     ""<I>sdiA</I>""| is expressed from a high copy number 
                     plasmid |CITS:[11244066]|.
                     MurJ is a member of the 
                     Multidrug/Oligosaccharidyl-lipid/Polysaccharide (MOP) 
                     Flippase Superfamily |CITS:[24225317]|.
                     The inner membrane protein |FRAME: EG10344-MONOMER 
                     ""FtsW""| has also been shown to act as a lipid II flippase
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [21386816]|. The inner membrane 
                     protein |FRAME: EG10607-MONOMER ""RodA""| has been 
                     implicated in lipid II translocation |CITS: [8955294]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [25043163]|<BR>Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [26792999][28975672]|<BR>Comments: |CITS: 
                     [21494274][25013047]|
                     "
