     gene            1145184..1146113
                     /gene="fabD"
                     /locus_tag="b1092"
                     /label=fabD
     CDS             1145184..1146113
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fabD"
                     /locus_tag="b1092"
                     /product="[acyl-carrier-protein] S-malonyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.39"
                     /label=fabD
                     /note="Malonyl-CoA-acyl carrier protein transacylase
                     (FabD) catalyzes the first committed step in the initiation
                     of fatty acid biosynthesis, the transfer of the malonyl 
                     group from malonyl-CoA to acyl carrier protein (ACP) to 
                     form malonyl-ACP.
                     Initial biochemical studies were performed on the enzyme 
                     purified from <i>E. coli</i> B |CITS: 
                     [5330116][4934182][4584822][4584823]|.  These studies 
                     showed that a malonyl-enzyme intermediate is formed during 
                     the reaction |CITS: [4934182][4584823]|.  The reaction 
                     utilizes a ping-pong bi bi mechanism |CITS: [4584822]|. 
                     Crystal structures of FabD alone and in various binary 
                     complexes have been solved |CITS: 
                     [8078074][7768883][16699188]|.  In a crystal structure 
                     obtained after soaking in the substrate malonyl-CoA, its 
                     thioester bond has been cleaved, and the malonyl moiety is 
                     covalently bound to the active-site Ser92 residue via an 
                     oxo-ester bond.  A detailed reaction mechanism has been 
                     described |CITS: [16699188]|.
                     Arg117 is an active site residue that interacts with the 
                     carboxylate of the malonyl-FabD reaction intermediate 
                     |CITS: [16699188]|.  An R117A variant of FabD has reduced 
                     substrate specificity and is able to synthesize a variety 
                     of ACP-linked products including acetyl-ACP, succinyl-ACP, 
                     isobutyryl-ACP, 2-butenoyl-ACP, and 
                     &beta;-hydroxybutyryl-ACP |CITS: [29075826]|.
                     A temperature-sensitive mutant has been characterized 
                     |CITS: [4612036]|.  The <i>fabD89</i> allele was found to 
                     produce an inactive enzyme that is truncated at codon 257 
                     due to an amber mutation.  The strain contains the amber 
                     suppressor allele <i>supE44</i>, which allows production of
                     a small amount of temperature-labile enzyme |CITS: 
                     [8034061]|.  <i>fabD</i> is part of a cluster of fatty acid
                     biosynthesis genes |CITS: [1556094][1314802][9642179]|.  
                     Transcription from the <i>fabH</i> promoter is under the 
                     control of the stringent response |CITS: [8649995]|.
                     <i>fabD</i> is an essential gene |CITS: [16738554]| and is 
                     therefore a possible antibiotic target.  Potential 
                     inhibitors were identified by virtual screening and were 
                     tested <i>in vitro</i> and with disc inhibition assays 
                     |CITS: [20080587]|.
                     TfpA: ""<b>t</b>ri<b>f</b>luoro<b>p</b>erazine resistance 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [7700236]|
                     Review: |CITS: [15952903]|
                     "
