     gene            1147071..1147307
                     /gene="acpP"
                     /locus_tag="b1094"
                     /label=acpP
     CDS             1147071..1147307
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="acpP"
                     /locus_tag="b1094"
                     /product="acyl carrier protein"
                     /label=acpP
                     /note="Acyl carrier protein (ACP) plays an important role
                     in fatty acid biosynthesis. It carries fatty acid chains 
                     via a thioester linkage to a |FRAME: PANTETHEINE-P| 
                     prosthetic group as the chains are elongated. There is also
                     evidence that it has a function in the biosynthesis of 
                     membrane-derived oligosaccharides. ACP and its acylated 
                     forms interact with at least 12 different |FRAME: TAX-562| 
                     enzymes |CITS: [1556094][3056520][3402450]|.
                     ACP is the most abundant protein in |FRAME: TAX-562|, with 
                     about 1.5 million molecules per cell |CITS: 
                     [MathewsvanHolde]|. ACP contains a |FRAME: PANTETHEINE-P| 
                     moiety (as does Coenzyme A) as the reactive unit, attached 
                     to the ACP protein through a serine side chain. The |FRAME:
                     HOLO-ACP-SYNTH-CPLX| enzyme (encoded by |FRAME: EG10247|) 
                     transfers the |FRAME: PANTETHEINE-P| moiety of |FRAME:CO-A|
                     to the |FRAME:EG50003-MONOMER| to form |FRAME:ACP-MONOMER|,
                     which is the active form of the carrier in lipid synthesis 
                     |CITS: [4872726][7016860]|. Using a technique to shift the 
                     equilibrium to stable <i>E. coli</i> acyl-AcpP proteins, 
                     which involves a recombinant acyl acyl-carrier protein 
                     synthetase (AasS) from |FRAME: TAX-669| during 
                     solution-phase NMR spectroscopy |CITS: [31397556]|, the 
                     interactions of ACP with its various partner enzymes is 
                     regulated through allostery that is dependent on the length
                     of the fatty acid chain attached to the ACP |CITS: 
                     [33846262]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [37345808]|"
