     gene            1149568..1150590
                     /gene="mltG"
                     /locus_tag="b1097"
                     /label=mltG
     CDS             1149568..1150590
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mltG"
                     /locus_tag="b1097"
                     /product="endolytic murein transglycosylase"
                     /label=mltG
                     /note="MltG is an inner membrane enzyme with endolytic
                     murein transglycosylase activity; it may function to 
                     terminate nascent peptidoglycan (PG) synthesis |CITS: 
                     [26507882]|. Evidence for an antagonistic relationship 
                     between MltG function and PG synthases supports this 
                     contention |CITS: [33278861]|.
                     Purified, soluble MltG degrades purified murein sacculi and
                     releases monomeric GlcNAc-anhydroMurNAc tetrapeptide plus 
                     linear tetra- and hexasaccharide oligomers terminating with
                     an anhydroMurNAc end |CITS: [26507882]|. MltG is predicted 
                     to contain a single transmembrane region with a small 
                     N-terminal domain located in the cytoplasm and a large 
                     C-terminal domain in the periplasm |CITS: [26507882]|. MltG
                     interacts with |FRAME: CPLX0-3951 ""MrcB""| (PBP1B) and 
                     with itself |CITS: [26507882]|. Over-expression of 
                     <i>mltG</i> is lethal in cells lacking <i>mrcB</i> |CITS: 
                     [26507882]|. An <i>mltG</i> deletion mutant shows impaired 
                     membrane invagination and cytokinesis and reduced 
                     sensitivity to ampicillin |CITS: [22125496]|. Peptidoglycan
                     isolated from &Delta;<i>mltG</i> cells shows a reduction in
                     anhydromuropeptide content and an increase  in glycan 
                     strand length |CITS: [26507882]|. The Keio collection 
                     <i>mltG</i> mutant exhibits a 'low level' of resistance to 
                     lithium exposure |CITS: [39983623]|. Deletion of 
                     <i>mltG</i> increases sensitivity to ampicillin and 
                     increases resistance to vancomycin (the so-called ""seesaw 
                     effect"") |CITS: [40418177]|.
                     <i>mltG</i> is part of a putative 5-gene operon 
                     (<i>pabC-mltG-tmk-holB-ycfH</i>) |CITS: [16083981]|."
                     /translation="MKKVLLIILLLLVVLGIAAGVGVWKVRHLADSKLLIKEETIFTLK
