     gene            1153325..1154758
                     /gene="ptsG"
                     /locus_tag="b1101"
                     /label=ptsG
     CDS             1153325..1154758
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ptsG"
                     /locus_tag="b1101"
                     /product="trifunctional glucose-specific PTS enzyme IIBC
                     component / Mlc-PtsG / glucose-specific PTS enzyme II"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.199"
                     /label=ptsG
                     /note="<i>ptsG</i> encodes the enzyme IIBC component of
                     the glucose-specific phosphotransferase system (PTS) which 
                     mediates the uptake and concomitant phosphorylation of 
                     glucose. PtsG consists of a large hydrophobic domain (IIC 
                     domain) that spans the membrane 8 - 10 times and contains 
                     the sugar binding site plus a smaller hydrophilic domain 
                     (IIB domain) that is exposed on the cytoplasmic face of the
                     membrane and is the site of phosphotransfer from 
                     EIIA<sup>glc</sup> (Crr) to O-6' of glucose. Purified PtsG 
                     is a homodimeric protein |CITS: [3047116], [8505291], 
                     [10562420]|. Phosphorylated PtsG is an intermediate during 
                     transport and phosphorylation of glucose |CITS: 
                     [BEGLEY82]|. Purified EIIBC<sup>glc</sup> is phosphorylated
                     in the presence of phosphoenolpyruvate, Enzyme I (PtsI), 
                     HPr (PtsH) and EIIA<sup>glc</sup> (Crr) |CITS: [8505292]|. 
                     Cysteine residue 421 is required for transport and 
                     phosphorylation of glucose |CITS: [3129430], [8505292]|.
                     PtsG may exist in two forms. It is oligomeric within the 
                     membrane and monomeric in its soluble form in the 
                     cytoplasm. Both forms exhibit PEP-dependent sugar 
                     phosphorylation and sugar phosphate-dependent 
                     transphosphorylation activity, though the kinetic 
                     properties of these reactions differ between the two forms 
                     of the protein |CITS: [12657050] [15576795],[21935376]|. 
                     Soluble and membrane integrated forms of PtsG 
                     <sup>glc</sup> can be interconverted <i>in vitro</i> |CITS:
                     [17587874]|.
                     The IIB domain of PtsG forms a split &alpha;/&beta; 
                     sandwich containing 4 &beta; strands and 3 &alpha; helices.
                     The active cysteine residue is located on a convex surface 
                     surrounded by hydrophobic residues |CITS: [8784182], 
                     [8112346],[8089109]|. A mutant form of the IIC domain of 
                     PtsG has been overexpressed, purified and crystallised 
                     |CITS: [20516600], [21996078]|.
                     PtsG has been implicated in the import of specific toxins 
                     of contact-dependent growth inhibition (CDI) systems in 
                     gram-negative bacteria; <i>ptsG</i> transposon insertion 
                     mutants and <i>ptsG</i> in-frame deletion mutants are 
                     resistant to the toxins CdiA-CT<sup>EC3006</sup> and 
                     CdiA-CT<sup>NC101</sup>; inner membrane proteins such as 
                     PtsG are thought to act as receptors which bring the 
                     nuclease domain of CDI toxins into close proximity with the
                     inner membrane of target cells |CITS: [26305955]|.
                     The monocistronic <i>ptsG</i> operon is subject to multiple
                     layers of transcriptional and post-transcriptional control 
                     (see reviews by |CITS: [11934616][17158705][21621292]| and 
                     references within). In the absence of glucose, expression 
                     of <i>ptsG</i> is repressed by the transcription factor 
                     |FRAME: PD01896 ""Mlc""|; in the presence of glucose Mlc is
                     sequestered to the inner membrane by the dephosphorylated 
                     form of the PTS glucose transporter |FRAME: PTSG-MONOMER 
                     ""EIIBC<sub>glc</sub>""| and repression is relieved; 
                     <i>ptsG</i> expression is regulated under varying growth 
                     conditions by other transcription factors including the 
                     cAMP receptor protein (|FRAME: CPLX0-226 ""CRP""|), ArcA, 
                     Fis and SoxS; <i>ptsG</i> expression is regulated 
                     post-transcriptionally by the small RNA SgrS and PtsG 
                     activity is regulated post-translationally by the small 
                     protein |FRAME: MONOMER0-2842 ""SgrT""|.
                     A <i>pfkAB</i> knockout strain (with the 
                     Embden-Meyerhof-Parnas pathway [EMPP] inactivated for 
                     glucose metabolism) subjected to adaptive laboratory 
                     evolution recovered growth to wild-type levels and showed 
                     an improved the Entner-Doudoroff pathway (EDP) for glucose 
                     breakdown due to mutations in <i>ugd-|FRAME: 
                     EG10411|-wbbL-2</i>, <i>|FRAME: EG10787|</i>, <i>|FRAME: 
                     EG11556|</i>, <i>|FRAME: EG12630|</i>, <i>|FRAME: 
                     EG10364|</i>, and <i>|FRAME: EG10164|</i> 
                     |CITS:[36352474]|."
