     gene            1158094..1158735
                     /gene="lpoB"
                     /locus_tag="b1105"
                     /label=lpoB
     CDS             1158094..1158735
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lpoB"
                     /locus_tag="b1105"
                     /product="outer membrane lipoprotein - activator of MrcB
                     activity"
                     /label=lpoB
                     /note="LpoB is an outer membrane lipoprotein that forms a 
                     complex with |FRAME: CPLX0-3951 ""penicillin binding 
                     protein 1B (PBP1B)""| and is essential for its 
                     peptidoglycan glycosyl transferase activity |CITS: 
                     [21183074]| and/or transpeptidase activity |CITS: 
                     [21183073]|. Purified LpoB interacts with PBPIB and 
                     stimulates both its glycosyltransferase and transpeptidase 
                     activitities <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [24821816]|. LpoB binds
                     and allosterically activates both the transpeptidase and 
                     transglycosylase activities of PBP1B |CITS: [30044025]| 
                     (see further |CITS: [40691462]|).
                     An <i>lpoB<sup>-</sup></i> mutant is synthetically lethal 
                     in a PBP1A defective strain and this phenotype can be 
                     corrected by the expression of <i>lpoB</i> from a plasmid 
                     |CITS: [21183074]|. Depletion of LpoB in the absence of 
                     PBP1A results in the same terminal phenotype as 
                     PBP1A<sup>-</sup> PBP1B<sup>-</sup> cells and 
                     <i>lpoB<sup>-</sup></i> mutants show a similar &beta;lactam
                     hypersensitivity phenotype to PBP1B<sup>-</sup> mutants 
                     |CITS: [21183074]|.
                     Purified LpoB activates PBP1B activity <i>in vitro</i> and 
                     affects the length of glycan chains with shorter polymers 
                     produced in the presence of LpoB |CITS: [21183074]|. 
                     Purified LpoB increased the percentage of cross-linked 
                     peptides from 53% to 73% in an <i>in vitro</i> 
                     peptidoglycan synthesis assay |CITS: [21183073]|. 
                     Peptidoglycan isolated from an 
                     <i>lpoA<sup>-</sup>lpoB<sup>-</sup></i> mutant prior to 
                     lysis shows a substantial decrease in peptidoglycan 
                     cross-linking and and an increase in pentapeptide 
                     containing  muropeptides - a similar biochemistry is 
                     observed in the peptidoglycan of PBP1A<sup>-</sup> 
                     PBP1B<sup>-</sup> cells |CITS: [21183074]|. Pull-down 
                     assays using tagged LpoB indicate that it specifically 
                     associates with PBP1B; an <i>in vivo</i> LpoB:PBP1B 
                     stoichiometry of 2:1 has been estimated |CITS: [21183074]|.
                     Mis-localized LpoB (artificially tethered to the inner 
                     membrane) is impaired in regulating peptidoglycan 
                     synthesis; mis-localized LpoB is still able to bind and 
                     activate PBP1B but is unable to downregulate peptidoglycan 
                     synthesis when osmolarity increases |CITS: [36304570]|. 
                     LpoB localises independently of PBP1B but, like PBP1B, it 
                     is recruited to the divisome during cell division |CITS: 
                     [21183073]|.
                     LpoB consists of an N-terminal flexible domain (54 amino 
                     acids) followed by a globular domain which comprises a 
                     three-stranded antiparallel &beta;-sheet flanked by a short
                     two-stranded parallel &beta;-sheet and four 
                     &alpha;-helices. LpoB lacking its N-terminal region 
                     (&Delta;1-73) can interact with PBP1B and stimulate its 
                     activity <i>in vitro</i> but is non-functional <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [24821816]|.
                     Comment: |CITS: [21183069][30808703]|"
