     gene            complement(1163656..1164288)
                     /gene="comR"
                     /locus_tag="b1111"
                     /label=comR
     CDS             complement(1163656..1164288)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="comR"
                     /locus_tag="b1111"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA-binding transcriptional
                     repressor ComR / ComR-Cu+"
                     /label=comR
                     /note="ComR is a member of the TetR family of
                     transcriptional repressors and has been implicated in the 
                     the response of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 to copper. ComR 
                     contains a helix-turn-helix motif to bind DNA in its 
                     N-terminal domain |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     In <i>in vitro</i> experiments, ComR binds to a 60 bp 
                     promotor region upstream of the <i>comC</i> gene |CITS: 
                     [22089859]| but is released in the presence of copper, 
                     silver or gold. Expression of <i>comC</i> is dramatically 
                     increased (approx 270 fold) in a <i>comR</i> negative 
                     strain upon exposure to 3mM copper. <i>comR</i> knockout 
                     mutants have lower cytoplasmic copper levels compared to 
                     the wild type when exposed to copper |CITS: [22089859]|. 
                     Expression of <i>comR</i> is not affected by copper |CITS: 
                     [22089859]|, but its expression is affected by cadmium 
                     |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     Genome-wide ComR binding sites were determined <i>in 
                     vivo</i> in M9 glucose minimal medium by chromatin 
                     immunoprecipitation assays (ChIP-exo) |CITS: [34428301]|. 
                     Various target genes of ComR were found upregulated after 
                     10 min of exposure to 2.5 mM H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, 
                     despite the <i>comR</i> gene being slightly upregulated 
                     |CITS:[35453340]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [22918892]|."
                     /translation="MATDSTQCVKKSRGRPKVFDRDAALDKAMKLFWQHGYEATSLADL
