     misc_RNA        1164759..1164858
                     /gene="bhsB"
                     /locus_tag="b4832"
                     /product="small RNA BhsB"
                     /note="The Hfq-dependent small RNA BhsB and two potential 
                     targets, <i>ompC</i> and <i>ytfK</i>, were identified by 
                     applying a small RNA prediction algorithm to RIL-seq data 
                     |CITS: [34093460]|.
                     BhsB is most highly expressed during stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [34093460]|.
                     "
     gene            complement(1164868..1165830)
                     /gene="ldtC"
                     /locus_tag="b1113"
                     /label=ldtC
     CDS             complement(1164868..1165830)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ldtC"
                     /locus_tag="b1113"
                     /product="bifunctional L,D-transpeptidase LdtC / 
                     L,D-transpeptidase"
                     /label=ldtC
                     /note="LdtC is an L,D-transpeptidase which cleaves the 
                     <i>meso</i>-diaminopimeloyl<sup>3</sup>&rarr; 
                     D-alanine<sup>4</sup> peptide bond in a peptidoglycan 
                     tetrapeptide stem and links the carboxyl group at the 
                     L-center of <i>m</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup> to the side chain 
                     amine of the lysine residue located at the C terminus of 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-8279 ""Braun lipoprotein""| |CITS: 
                     [4918558][17369299]|. This activity anchors Braun 
                     lipoprotein to the peptidoglycan and contributes to the 
                     stability of the cell envelope.
                     Muropeptides obtained from the peptidoglycan of an <i>E. 
                     coli</i> BW25113 &Delta;(<i>|FRAME: G7073 ""ldtA""| |FRAME:
                     G6422 ""ldtB""| ldtC |FRAME: G6904 ""ldtE""|</i>) quadruple
                     mutant (BW25113&Delta;4) do not contain tripeptide stems 
                     substituted by a fragment of the Braun lipoprotein |CITS: 
                     [17369299]| (see also |CITS: [18456808]|). Expression of 
                     <i>ldtC</i> in BW25113&Delta;4 restores the appearance of 
                     this muropeptide suggesting that LdtC catalyzes the 
                     covalent anchoring of Braun lipoprotein to the 
                     peptidoglycan |CITS: [17369299]|. Complementation analysis 
                     suggests that <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains three L,D 
                     transpeptidases (LdtA, LdtB and LdtC) that can catalyse the
                     covalent anchoring of Braun lipoprotein to the 
                     peptidoglycan however the analysis of single deletion 
                     mutants suggests LdtB may be of primary importance 
                     (|CITS:[17369299]| ('results not shown'). An <i>E. coli</i>
                     BW25113 &Delta;(<i>ldtA ldtB ldtC</i>) triple mutant 
                     (BW25113&Delta;3) has increased outer membrane 
                     permeability, is hypersensitive to EDTA, and is resistant 
                     to the toxic effects of 25mM D-methionine |CITS: 
                     [23832002]|.
                     LdtA, B, C and E are all of similar size and share 42-62% 
                     sequence similarity including a putative active site 
                     cysteine located in a conserved motif; <i>ldtC</i> and 
                     <i>ldtE</i> also contain a LysM domain (see |CITS: 
                     [18430080]|) which is associated with peptidoglycan binding
                     |CITS: [23832002]|. Sequence analysis suggests that LdtC 
                     may contain &beta;-barrel structure(s) |CITS:[12192075]|.
                     Complementation analysis (|CITS:[18456808]| results not 
                     shown) also suggests LdtC can exchange D-ala<sup>4</sup> in
                     peptidoglycan tetrapeptide stems with glycine (|FRAME: 
                     RXN-16665 ""RXN-16665""|) and hydrolyse D-ala<sup>4</sup> 
                     to produce tripeptide stems (|FRAME: RXN-16664 
                     ""RXN-16664""|). <i>E. coli</i> K-12 incorporates 
                     exogenously supplied D-methionine into peptidoglycan stem 
                     tetrapeptides; the percentage of muropeptides that contain 
                     D-methionine is reduced to varying extents in all single 
                     <i>ldt</i> insertion mutants* and is reduced by ~75% in the
                     double <i>ldtD ldtE</i> insertion mutant |CITS: [21792174]|
                     (see also |CITS: [23832002]|).
                     Expression of <i>ldtC</i> (formerly <I>ycfS</I>) increases 
                     upon amplification of <I>sdiA</I> |CITS:[11244066]|. 
                     Expression is also pH dependent (base upregulated) 
                     |CITS:[15601715],[17026754]|.  <I>ycfS</I> expression is 
                     upregulated in response to zinc stress (0.2mM 
                     ZnSO<sub>4</sub>) |CITS: [15659689]|.
                     *<i>ldtF</i> was not included in this analysis"
                     /translation="MMIKTRFSRWLTFFTFAAAVALALPAKANTWPLPPAGSRLVGENK
