     gene            complement(1165974..1169420)
                     /gene="mfd"
                     /locus_tag="b1114"
                     /label=mfd
     CDS             complement(1165974..1169420)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mfd"
                     /locus_tag="b1114"
                     /product="transcription-repair coupling factor"
                     /label=mfd
                     /note="The Mfd protein, also known as transcription-repair 
                     coupling factor (TRCF) |CITS: [1763073]|, is responsible 
                     for ATP-dependent displacement of stalled RNA polymerase 
                     (RNAP) from DNA lesions with concomitant recruitment of 
                     |FRAME: UVRABC-CPLX ""nucleotide excision repair""| 
                     machinery to the site of damage |CITS: [8465200]|. Mfd 
                     facilitates the repair of template strand lesions |CITS: 
                     [1763073]|. Purified Mfd rescues a back-tracked arrested 
                     transcription complex into productive elongation if NTPs 
                     are present implicating Mfd activity in restoration of the 
                     forward position; Mfd rescues a backtracked complex by 
                     causing the stalled RNAP to move forward; when 
                     transcription is blocked by a DNA lesion, the DNA 
                     translocase activity of Mfd results in dissociation of RNAP
                     |CITS: [12086674]|. Mfd is an autonomous DNA translocase; 
                     Mfd uses ATP dependent translocation to patrol DNA for 
                     stalled RNAPs |CITS: [29224782]|. Labeled Mfd associates 
                     with stalled RNAPs in the absence of exogenously induced 
                     DNA damage; Mfd rescues stalled RNAPs during normal growth 
                     |CITS: [29679003]|.
                     Mfd discriminates the stalled RNAP-DNA elongation complex 
                     and after ATP hydrolysis it disrupts the complex releasing 
                     RNAP from the site. Further ATP hydrolysis is required for 
                     dissociation of Mfd |CITS: [22960746]|. Mfd remains bound 
                     to DNA in a relatively long-lived complex that may act as a
                     marker for sites of damage |CITS: [22960746]|. The 
                     Mfd-dependent transcription coupled repair (TCR) pathway is
                     able to repair template strand lesions downstream of a 
                     stalled RNAP complex. This repair is dependent on the 
                     presence of the Mfd protein. Once activated by binding to 
                     stalled RNAP, Mfd is able to translocate more than 100 bp 
                     along DNA suggesting that repair of downstream lesions is 
                     facilitated by the translocation activity of Mfd |CITS: 
                     [24554077]|. RNAP remains associated with the DNA-bound Mfd
                     protein; RNAP and Mfd form a complex that remains on DNA 
                     after the release of stalled RNAP  |CITS: [25961799]|. 
                     Recruitment of UvrA or UvrAB to DNA bound Mfd RNAP results 
                     in dissolution of the complex with loss of both RNAP and 
                     Mfd |CITS: [27487215]|.
                     Mfd does not impact the repair rate of (6-4) 
                     pyrimidine-pyrimidone photoproducts [(6-4)PPs] which are 
                     efficiently repaired by UvrABC excision nuclease; Mfd 
                     stimulates the repair of cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers 
                     (CPDs) in the transcribed strand as an early response to 
                     DNA damage and the magnitude of TCR increases as a function
                     of transcription rate; TCR is responsible for repairing a 
                     minority of UV damage in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [39441633]|
                     Mfd is implicated in R-loop mediated mutagenesis and 
                     adaption to stress; the interaction of Mfd with elongating 
                     RNAP and DNA results in the formation of a negatively 
                     supercoiled region of DNA between Mfd and RNAP which is a 
                     substrate for R-loop formation |CITS: 
                     [33827922][23828459]|. Mfd-mediated TCR is responsible for 
                     the increased mutation frequency observed in persister 
                     cells following fluoroquinolone treatment |CITS: 
                     [35235801]|.
                     TCR incision rates (measured <i>in vitro</i> on a DNA 
                     substrate with cyclobutane pyrimidine dimer) are 
                     significantly faster than incision rates of the same 
                     substrate in the presence of UvrABC only |CITS: 
                     [27487215]|.
                     Mfd contains helicase motifs with a region similar to RecG 
                     involved in binding DNA and ATP hydrolysis, a leucine 
                     zipper motif believed to allow binding to RNA polymerase, 
                     and a region similar to UvrB which contains a binding 
                     domain for UvrA<sub>2</sub> |CITS: [8465200][7876261]|. The
                     crystal structure of Mfd has been determined to a 
                     resolution of 3.2 &Aring;; eight structural domains (D1a - 
                     D7) connected by flexible linkers have been identified 
                     |CITS: [16469698][16511235]|. The crystal structure of the 
                     N-terminal region of Mfd has been solved to a resolution of
                     2.1 &Aring; |CITS: [16309703]|. The interaction between D2 
                     and D7 maintains Mfd in a ""repressed"" conformation in 
                     which ATP hydrolysis and DNA translocation are inhibited; 
                     derepression is associated with substantial conformational 
                     change |CITS: [21185303]|. UvrA binds at the interface of 
                     D2 and D7; in the apo enzyme the UvrA binding surface is 
                     occluded by the D7 domain; nucleotide binding induces 
                     structural rearrangement |CITS: [22331906]|.
                     <i>In vitro</i> repair assays |CITS: [1763073]| and 
                     UV-induced mutations <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [1438310]| show 
                     that <i>mfd</i> mutants do not exhibit template 
                     strand-specific repair.
                     <i>mfd</i>: <u>m</u>utation <u>f</u>requency <u>d</u>ecline
                     |CITS: [5327888]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [7968917][15063847][17239578][17572090][23 
                     084398][24685770][23756341][27864884][27859304][30978344][3
                     4095223][34674614][37001137][37558547]|<br>Comments: |CITS:
                     [24599593]|"
