     gene            complement(1188123..1189229)
                     /gene="mnmA"
                     /locus_tag="b1133"
                     /label=mnmA
     CDS             complement(1188123..1189229)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mnmA"
                     /locus_tag="b1133"
                     /product="tRNA-specific 2-thiouridylase"
                     /EC_number="2.8.1.13"
                     /label=mnmA
                     /note="MnmA catalyzes formation of the 2-thiouridine 
                     modification of the modified nucleoside 
                     5-methylamino-methyl-2-thiouridine 
                     (mnm<sup>5</sup>s<sup>2</sup>U34) in the wobble position of
                     tRNA<sup>Gln</sup>, tRNA<sup>Lys</sup> and 
                     tRNA<sup>Glu</sup> |CITS: [12549933][16387657]|.
                     A sulfur relay system consisting of IscS and the TusABCDE 
                     proteins is required for delivery of the sulfur atom |CITS:
                     [16387657]|. <i>In vitro</i>, a low level of MnmA activity 
                     on tRNA<sup>Glu</sup> could be achieved in the presence of 
                     Mg-ATP, cysteine, and the IscS cysteine desulfurase. The 
                     enzyme also shows activity toward anticodon stem-loop (tRNA
                     fragment) substrates. MnmA binds to tRNA<sup>Lys</sup> and 
                     appears to bind to ATP |CITS: [12549933]|.
                     Crystal structures of MnmA together with tRNA<sup>Glu</sup>
                     have been solved |CITS: [16582487][16871210]|, revealing an
                     adenylated tRNA intermediate and the basis for recognition 
                     of the specific tRNA substrates. Possible reaction 
                     mechanisms were proposed |CITS: [16871210]|.
                     While initially it was thought that MnmA activity does not 
                     depend on an iron-sulfur cluster, it was later shown that 
                     MnmA from |FRAME: TAX-562| can bind a [4Fe-4S] cluster, and
                     that the binding is essential for sulfuration of 
                     U<sup>34</sup>-tRNA |CITS: [33885822]|. Interestingly, the 
                     enzyme was active in a &Delta;<i>iscUA</i> 
                     &Delta;<i>sufABCDSE</i> strain, which lacks functional ISC 
                     and SUF iron-sulfur cluster assembly machineries, 
                     suggesting an unknown way of maturation of MnmA |CITS: 
                     [33885822]|.
                     One published report claims that <i>mnmA</i> is essential 
                     |CITS: [9743119]|, while later studies report that it is 
                     not |CITS: [10572302][12446633][12549933]|. <i>mnmA</i> 
                     mutants show a slow growth phenotype |CITS: 
                     [10572302][12446633][12549933]|.
                     An <i>mnmA</i> mutation decreases <i>supL</i> nonsense 
                     suppression by causing a defect in 
                     5-methylaminomethyl-2-thiouridine modification (probably a 
                     defect in 2-thiolation) of the suppressor 
                     tRNA<sup>Lys</sup> |CITS: [3881393]|. An <i>mnmA</i> mutant
                     exhibits increased frameshifting, compared to wild type, 
                     indicating that modified nucleosides in tRNAs act to ensure
                     consistent translation in the proper reading frame |CITS: 
                     [11532950]|, but has only a small effect on ribosomal 
                     frameshifting at an exogenous frameshifting ""slippery"" 
                     sequence |CITS: [9237903]|. In contrast to +1 
                     frameshifting, a <i>mnmA</i> mutation has no effect on -1 
                     frameshifting |CITS: [12756333]|. Contrary to expectations,
                     an <i>mnmA</i> mutant shows decreased mis-recognition by 
                     tRNA<sup>Lys</sup> of Asp codons, and does not appear to 
                     cause a defect in lysine charging of the tRNA<sup>Lys</sup>
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [9811540]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>mnmA</i> mutant exposed to UVA (370 nm) had 
                     tRNAs with higher levels of 8-oxo-rG and oxidative damage 
                     products compared to tRNAs in wild-type cultures |CITS: 
                     [29400945]|. tRNA in a &Delta;<i>rlmL</i> mutant was more 
                     susceptible to UVA and Fenton-reaction induced oxidative 
                     stress conditions, resulting in higher 8-oxo-G levels than 
                     wild-type |CITS: [34277707]|.
                     A knockout mutant of this gene was sensitive to boric acid 
                     exposure compared to most other gene knockouts; a 
                     complementation assay restored its intrinsic boric acid 
                     resistance |CITS: [38466471]|.
                     AsuE: ""<b>a</b>nti<b>su</b>ppressor <b>E</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [3881393]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [7541255][17580114][28098827][39660893][40210524]|"
