     gene            complement(1189283..1189744)
                     /gene="nudJ"
                     /locus_tag="b1134"
                     /label=nudJ
     CDS             complement(1189283..1189744)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nudJ"
                     /locus_tag="b1134"
                     /product="multifunctional phosphatase NudJ / dimethylallyl 
                     diphosphate pyrophosphatase / nucleoside di- and 
                     triphosphate hydrolase / HMP-PP hydrolase / thiamin 
                     pyrophosphate hydrolase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.1.76; 3.6.1.42"
                     /label=nudJ
                     /note="The <i>nudJ</i> gene product is a member of the
                     Nudix hydrolase superfamily.  Unlike the other Nudix 
                     nucleoside triphosphatases in <i>E. coli</i>, the product 
                     of the reaction is phosphate instead of pyrophosphate. NudJ
                     is a promiscuous enzyme with no preference for deoxyribose 
                     or ribose, and nucleoside di- and tri-phosphates serve as 
                     substrates equally.  The enzyme is a monomer in solution 
                     |CITS: [16766526]|.
                     NudJ also contributes to the production of dimethylallyl 
                     phosphate, which is the substrate for |FRAME: UBIX-MONOMER 
                     UbiX|-catalyzed generation of the prFMN cofactor in 
                     ubiquinone biosynthesis |CITS: [29551348]|.
                     Crystal structures of NudJ have been solved.  Together with
                     kinetic data for active site mutants, they provide insight 
                     into the catalytic mechanism of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [24816099]|.
                     Overexpression of NudJ from a multicopy plasmid leads to 
                     resistance to the HMP 
                     (4-amino-2-methyl-5-hydroxymethylpyrimidine) analog 
                     bacimethrin (MeO-HMP).  The enzyme is involved in the 
                     hydrolysis of HMP-PP and TPP, intermediates in thiamin 
                     biosynthesis |CITS: [15292217]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>nudJ</i> mutant has aggravating genetic 
                     interactions with genes involved in DNA recombination 
                     |CITS: [26774489]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [16378245]|
                     "
                     /translation="MFKPHVTVACVVHAEGKFLVVEETINGKALWNQPAGHLEADETLV
