     gene            1194151..1195044
                     /gene="lit"
                     /locus_tag="b1139"
                     /label=lit
     CDS             1194151..1195044
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lit"
                     /locus_tag="b1139"
                     /product="e14 prophage; cell death peptidase Lit"
                     /label=lit
                     /note="The lit gene is part of the defective prophage
                     element e14 |CITS: [6998954][14733619]|.
                     A mutation in the putative promoter region of the lit gene 
                     (termed litCon) causes constitutive expression of Lit and 
                     blocks late gene expression during T4 infection, and thus 
                     causes phage exclusion |CITS: [3546267]|. The inhibitory 
                     effect of Lit on T4 replication is dependent on the gol 
                     site located within gene 23, which encodes the major capsid
                     protein of T4 |CITS: [6323755]|. Translation of the gol 
                     region in the presence of Lit protein causes global 
                     inhibition of translation |CITS: [2191141]|. Both <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [8290603]| and in an <i>in vitro</i> system
                     consisting of
                     purified Lit protein, translation elongation factor TU 
                     (EF-Tu), and a short synthetic peptide derived from the gol
                     region, interaction between the Lit protein and the gol 
                     product leads to the cleavage of EF-Tu |CITS: [9501186]|. 
                     The substrate for Lit appears to be a specific complex 
                     between the Gol peptide and domains II and III of EF-Tu
                     |CITS: [10801848]|. Site-specific mutagenesis of residues 
                     within the metallopeptidase motif has defined essential 
                     elements for the catalytic activity of Lit |CITS: 
                     [15196039]|.
                     The litCon phenotype can be supressed by elevated levels of
                     Rho or GroEL proteins |CITS: [7916307]|. Binding of the 
                     emerging Gol peptide to EF-Tu may cause translation to 
                     pause and enable the GroEL chaperonin to bind to the 
                     polypeptide, regulating synthesis of functional capsid 
                     protein |CITS: [14623179]|.
                     Lit - ""late inhibitor of T4"" |CITS: [227837]|."
