     gene            1205802..1206635
                     /gene="mcrA"
                     /locus_tag="b1159"
                     /label=mcrA
     CDS             1205802..1206635
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mcrA"
                     /locus_tag="b1159"
                     /product="bifunctional e14 prophage; Type IV
                     methyl-directed restriction enzyme EcoKMcrA / type IV 
                     deoxyribonuclease"
                     /label=mcrA
                     /note="The McrA protein is a type IV site-specific 
                     deoxyribonuclease and is one of three restriction systems 
                     defending the cell against foreign DNA, such as 
                     bacteriophages.  It specifically recognizes 
                     5-methylcytosine residues in DNA |CITS: [3024165]|, but the
                     precise recognition sequence has not yet been identified.  
                     McrA was initially identified as a cellular activity that 
                     degraded T-even phage DNA containing nonglucosylated DNA 
                     |CITS: [767337]|.  The rglA and mcrA phenotypes are 
                     separable; see the discussion in |CITS: [15317774]| and 
                     references therein.
                     Structural modelling of McrA has identified similarity to 
                     the &beta;&beta;&alpha;Me superfamily of endonucleases 
                     |CITS: [10972843]|.  Linker insertion scanning mutagenesis 
                     has been used to study the functional domain architecture 
                     |CITS: [15317774]|.
                     mcrA is part of the defective lambdoid prophage element e14
                     |CITS: [14733619]|.  After induction of the SOS response by
                     UV irradiation, the prophage is abortively excised from the
                     E. coli chromosome, leading to decreased levels of McrA 
                     restriction within a cell population |CITS: 
                     [1655051][1938927]|.
                     Restriction of methylated DNA is responsible for 
                     difficulties with cloning methylcytosine-containing DNA 
                     from various sources in E. coli |CITS: 
                     [2831502][2841642][2548161]|.
                     RglA = ""<b>r</b>estricts <b>g</b>lucose<b>l</b>ess 
                     DNA""<br>
                     McrA = ""<b>m</b>odified <b>c</b>ytosine 
                     <b>r</b>estriction"" |CITS: [3024165]|
                     "
