     gene            complement(1219735..1220001)
                     /gene="minE"
                     /locus_tag="b1174"
                     /label=minE
     CDS             complement(1219735..1220001)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="minE"
                     /locus_tag="b1174"
                     /product="Z-ring positioning protein MinE"
                     /label=minE
                     /note="The |FRAME: EG10596-MONOMER ""MinC""|-|FRAME: 
                     EG10597-MONOMER ""MinD""|-MinE system acts to direct 
                     septation to the proper (central) site in the dividing 
                     <i>E. coli</i> cell; the Min proteins are involved in a 
                     pole-to-pole oscillating system that restricts Z-ring 
                     formation to midcell. MinE is considered to be the 
                     'topological specificity factor' |CITS: [2645057]| that 
                     relieves MinCD inhibition of cell division specifically at 
                     mid-cell.
                     MinDE is also implicated in a generic mechanism which 
                     regulates the positioning of membrane proteins in cells; 
                     MinDE form a propagating diffusion barrier which regulates 
                     the positioning of functionally unrelated membrane-bound 
                     molecules <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [30258191]|. MinDE 
                     constitutes the minimal set of proteins to drive the 
                     formation of FtsA-FtsZ cytoskeletal patterns on supported 
                     lipid bilayers |CITS: [35798943]|. The Min proteins play a 
                     role in determining cell size; increasing the 
                     <i>minE/minD</i> expression ratio delays FtsZ binding to 
                     the membrane and subsequent Z-ring formation leading to an 
                     increase in average cell size |CITS: [ 37714867]|.
                     MinD recruits MinC to the membrane to form the MinC-MinD 
                     complex, which inhibits septum formation at any site within
                     the cell, and MinE acts to localize and restrict inhibition
                     activity to inappropriate septation sites |CITS: 
                     [2645057][7753804][8709851][9393861][10096083][1209 
                     3736][12486056]|. MinC-MinD has been reported to interfere 
                     with FtsZ ring assembly |CITS: [11673433]| and with FtsA 
                     assembly into the FtsZ ring |CITS: [10931335]|. The 
                     location of the MinC-MinD complex oscillates from pole to 
                     pole within the cell; MinE causes dissociation of the 
                     membrane-bound complex, which is followed rapidly by 
                     complex formation at the opposite pole |CITS: 
                     [10220403][10515933][10540287][11 
                     285221][11734639][11800919][11806906][11953319][11983867][1
                     2533449][12486056][12675792][12766229]|. MinD forms 
                     ATP-dependent helical structures within phospholipid 
                     vesicles, and these structures dissociate upon 
                     MinE-stimulated ATP hydrolysis |CITS: 
                     [11983867][12482939]|. MinE causes alterations in the 
                     membrane localization of MinD |CITS: [10633093]|, and 
                     MinE-mediated inhibition of MinC activity requires MinD 
                     |CITS: [1729224]|. MinE and phospholipid positively 
                     regulate MinD ATPase activity |CITS: [11430835][12675792]|,
                     which is required for the MinC-mediated inhibition pathway 
                     |CITS: [1836760]|.
                     MinE forms a ring-shaped structure that is observed at the 
                     cell midregion |CITS: [9393861]|. This ring restricts the 
                     oscillating zone of MinCD from extending past the center of
                     the cell |CITS: [12093736]|. The localization of the MinE 
                     ring exhibits pole-to-pole oscillation |CITS: 
                     [11158581][11285221]|. MinD is necessary for formation of 
                     the MinE ring |CITS: [9393861]|. MinE localization is not 
                     sensitive to the localization of the nucleoid |CITS: 
                     [11157955]|. Large coiled structures containing MinC, MinD,
                     and MinE are observed <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [12766229]|. 
                     Purified MinE forms fibrillar structures on lipid bilayers 
                     <i>in vitro</i> suggesting that self-assembly may underlie 
                     the ring-like structure formed by MinE <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [24914211]|
                     MinC and MinD interact, and MinE disrupts the interaction 
                     between MinC and MinD and the membrane association of the 
                     complex |CITS: [8752322][12486056][12675792][12533449]|. 
                     The MinE N-terminal domain interacts with MinD |CITS: 
                     [12897015]|. The MinE N-terminal domain interacts with the 
                     membrane |CITS: [20025670]|. MinE contains two domains - an
                     N-terminal domain that counteracts MinCD activity and a 
                     C-terminal domain (MinE<sup>TSD</sup>) that imparts 
                     topological specificity (ie. that restricts MinE effects to
                     mid-cell) |CITS: [7753804][8709851]|. MinE may exist as a 
                     mixture of monomers and dimers <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [9791172]|. MinE<sup>TSD</sup> is dimeric and forms a novel
                     &alpha;&beta; sandwich fold |CITS: [10096083][11062554]|.  
                     MinE senses MinD and undergoes conformational change that 
                     releases sequestered MinD binding and membrane targeting 
                     regions |CITS: [21816275]| (see also |CITS: [28652337]|).
                     The minicell locus (<i>minB</i>) contains <i>minC</i>, 
                     <i>minD</i> and <i>minE</i> |CITS: [2645057]|.  Mutation of
                     the <i>min</i> locus results in a minicell phenotype |CITS:
                     [6327636][2834323]|, leads to septation at polar and 
                     non-polar sites |CITS: [2838458]|, disturbances in the cell
                     cycle |CITS: [1406249]|, a DNA supercoiling defect |CITS: 
                     [2183010]|, and defects in DNA segregation |CITS: 
                     [2838458][2183010][1406249]|.  Overexpression of the 
                     <i>minB</i> locus causes a minicell phenotype |CITS: 
                     [2834323]| and a DNA supercoiling defect |CITS: [2183010]|.
                     MinE overproduction is associated with a defect in nucleoid
                     segregation |CITS: [12368455]|.
                     Cells lacking functional Min and |FRAME: CPLX0-7922 
                     ""SlmA""| systems, both of which contribute to septum 
                     positioning, still divide preferentially at mid-cell |CITS:
                     [25101671]|.
                     A protein-enzyme interactions (PEIs) study that examined 
                     the connection between protein-protein interactions (PPIs) 
                     and metabolomics networks studied in 22 different 
                     environmental conditions found that the PEI MinE-|FRAME: 
                     G6502-MONOMER YccX| may have a previously unknown role in 
                     regulating metabolism, in part because the PEI is conserved
                     across many other bacterial taxa |CITS: [39903745]|.
                     <i>min</i>:<U>min</U>icells |CITS: [16591472]|
                     Min system reviews: |CITS: [10744970][11378404][11461697][ 
                     11703919][11731315][12457696][17328675][18783171][23574354]
                     [23845239][25513760][26029202][29188788][29632263][31317204
                     ][30664868][34399108][37491940]|<BR>Comments: |CITS: 
                     [11062548][26944078]|<BR>Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [37524757]|"
