     gene            complement(1220005..1220817)
                     /gene="minD"
                     /locus_tag="b1175"
                     /label=minD
     CDS             complement(1220005..1220817)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="minD"
                     /locus_tag="b1175"
                     /product="bifunctional Z-ring positioning protein MinD /
                     Z-ring inhibitor complex MinCD"
                     /label=minD
                     /note="The |FRAME: EG10596-MONOMER ""MinC""|-MinD-|FRAME: 
                     EG10598-MONOMER ""MinE""| system acts to direct septation 
                     to the proper (central) site in the dividing <i>E. coli</i>
                     cell; the Min proteins are involved in a pole-to-pole 
                     oscillating system that restricts Z-ring formation to 
                     midcell.
                     MinD recruits MinC to the membrane to form the MinC-MinD 
                     complex, which inhibits septum formation, and MinE acts to 
                     localize and restrict inhibition to the inappropriate 
                     septation sites |CITS: [2645057][7753804][8709851][9393861 
                     ][10096083][12093736][12486056]|. MinDE is also implicated 
                     in a generic mechanism which regulates the positioning of 
                     membrane proteins in cells; MinDE form a propagating 
                     diffusion barrier which regulates the positioning of 
                     functionally unrelated membrane-bound molecules <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [30258191]|. MinDE constitutes the minimal
                     set of proteins to drive the formation of FtsA-FtsZ 
                     cytoskeletal patterns on supported lipid bilayers |CITS: 
                     [35798943]|. The Min proteins play a role in determining 
                     cell size; increasing the <i>minE/minD</i> expression ratio
                     delays FtsZ binding to the membrane and subsequent Z-ring 
                     formation leading to an increase in average cell size 
                     |CITS: [ 37714867]|. Although the mean cellular 
                     concentration of MinD is independent of cell length, the 
                     MinD concentration gradient steepens as cells elongate 
                     |CITS: [ 39621132]|. The Min system may link cell division 
                     and motility; &Delta;<i>min</i> cells are hypermotile and 
                     hyperflagellated compared to wild-type; MinD interacts 
                     directly with histidine kinase |FRAME: CPLX0-7870 AtoS| of 
                     the |FRAME: PWY0-1474 AtoSC two-component system| and this 
                     interaction may impact flagellar gene expression |CITS: 
                     [38403244]|
                     MinD is implicated in localization of polar mRNAs; MinD 
                     interacts physically with |FRAME: CPLX0-3461| and affects 
                     the membrane localization and activity of RNase E; in the 
                     absence of MinD, RNase E accumulates at the cell poles; the
                     polar CheA protein predominantly forms lateral clusters in 
                     &Delta;<i>minCDE</i> cells as a result of mRNA 
                     mislocalization |CITS: [38243117]|
                     MinD exhibits ATPase activity |CITS: [1836760]|. MinD 
                     ATPase activity is required for the MinC-mediated 
                     inhibition pathway |CITS: [1836760]|, and MinD ATPase 
                     activity is positively regulated by MinE and phospholipid 
                     |CITS: [11430835][12675792]|. MinD mediates MinC activity 
                     as well as the sensitivity of MinC to MinE 
                     localization/regulation |CITS: [1729224]|. MinD affects the
                     affinity of MinC for the septal ring |CITS: [12003935]|. 
                     MinD is necessary for formation of the MinE ring |CITS: 
                     [9393861]|.
                     MinC-MinD has been reported to interfere with FtsZ ring 
                     assembly |CITS: [11673433]| and with FtsA assembly into the
                     FtsZ ring |CITS: [10931335]|. The location of the MinC-MinD
                     complex oscillates from pole to pole within the cell; MinE 
                     causes complex dissociation that is followed rapidly by 
                     complex formation at the opposite pole |CITS: [ 
                     10220403][10515933][10540287][11285221][11734639][11800919]
                     [11806906][11953319][11983867][12533449][12486056][12675792
                     ][12766229]|. Min protein wave propagation that is observed
                     <i>in vitro</i> results from the interplay between 
                     cooperative MinD binding to the membrane and accelerated 
                     MinD detachment due to persistent MinE binding |CITS: 
                     [21516096]|.
                     MinD localizes to the inner membrane |CITS: 
                     [1836760][10633093]|. Membrane localization is mediated by 
                     a C-terminal membrane targeting sequence (MTS) |CITS: 
                     [12424340]|. Membrane interaction is discussed in detail 
                     |CITS: [12519187][12533449][12676941][12867440][12882967]|.
                     MinD exhibits self-interaction |CITS: [11591668]| that is 
                     regulated by ATP |CITS: [12486056]|. MinD forms 
                     ATP-dependent helical structures within phospholipid 
                     vesicles, and these structures dissociate upon 
                     MinE-stimulated ATP hydrolysis |CITS: 
                     [11983867][12482939]|. Large coiled structures containing 
                     MinC, MinD, and MinE are observed <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [12766229]|. MinC and MinD interact, and MinE disrupts the 
                     interaction between MinC and MinD and the membrane 
                     association of the complex |CITS: 
                     [8752322][12486056][12533449]|. The MinC C terminus 
                     mediates interaction with MinD |CITS: [10869074]|.
                     MinD and MinE interact |CITS: [12897015]|. MinD forms 
                     dimers <i>in vitro</i>; the binding sites for MinC and MinE
                     are located at the dimer interface and overlap |CITS: 
                     [21231967]|.
                     MinD has been implicated in chromosome segregation; MinD 
                     binds to DNA and tethers it to liposomes in an ATP 
                     dependent manner <i>in vitro</i>; &Delta;<i>minD</i> 
                     strains have an increased number of anucleate cells 
                     compared to &Delta;<i>minC</i> strains (excluding minicells
                     from the analysis) |CITS: [24022004]|.
                     Mutations in the ATP binding region affect interaction with
                     MinC |CITS: [11296216]|. Overexpression of <i>minCD</i> 
                     causes inviability, and this phenotype is suppressed by 
                     overexpression of <i>ftsZ</i> or by certain <i>ftsZ</i> 
                     mutations |CITS: [2211499]|. Overproduction of MinD causes 
                     a cell division defect that is suppressed by mutations in 
                     MinC |CITS: [1729222]|.
                     The minicell locus (<i>minB</i>) contains <i>minC</i>, 
                     <i>minD</i> and <i>minE</i> |CITS: [2645057]|.  Mutation of
                     the <i>min</i> locus results in a minicell phenotype |CITS:
                     [6327636][2834323]|, leads to septation at polar and 
                     non-polar sites |CITS: [2838458]|, disturbances in the cell
                     cycle |CITS: [1406249]|, a DNA supercoiling defect |CITS: 
                     [2183010]|, and defects in DNA segregation |CITS: 
                     [2838458][2183010][1406249]|. Overexpression of the 
                     <i>minB</i> locus causes a minicell phenotype |CITS: 
                     [2834323]| and a DNA supercoiling defect |CITS: [2183010]|.
                     &Delta;<i>minCDE::kan</i> mutants produce cells of normal 
                     size, minicells and short filaments when growing in rich 
                     media at 37&deg;C; FtsZ rings assemble promiscuously in 
                     nucleoid free regions in cells lacking MinCDE |CITS: 
                     [10231488]|
                     MinD is a member of a diverse subgroup of ATPases that are 
                     characterised by the presence of two lysine residues in the
                     Walker motif (reviewed in |CITS: [12675792]|).
                     Cells lacking functional Min and |FRAME: CPLX0-7922 
                     ""SlmA""| systems, both of which contribute to septum 
                     positioning, still divide preferentially at mid-cell |CITS:
                     [25101671]|
                     <i>min</i>:<U>min</U>icells |CITS: [16591472]|
                     Min system reviews: |CITS: [10744970][11378404][11461697][ 
                     11703919][11731315][12457696][17328675][18783171][23574354]
                     [23845239][25513760][26029202][29188788][29632263][31317204
                     ][30664868][34399108][37491940]|.<BR>Comments: |CITS: 
                     [26944078]|<BR>Related reviews: |CITS: [37524757]|"
