     CDS             complement(1224939..1225850)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hlyE"
                     /locus_tag="b1182"
                     /product="hemolysin E"
                     /label=hlyE
                     /note="<i>hlyE</i> (also known as <i>clyA</i> or
                     <i>sheA</i>) encodes a secreted, pore-forming protein 
                     (hemolysin E/cytolysin A) that is active against mammalian 
                     erythrocytes and other cells; <i>E. coli</i> K-12 does not 
                     express <i>hlyE</i> under normal laboratory conditions 
                     however a hemolytic phenotype can be induced by 
                     experimentally manipulating expression levels |CITS: [8861 
                     216][11902713][9724723][10383763][10027972][10858262]| (see
                     also |CITS: [8544813]|). Secretion of hemolysin E into the 
                     medium is independent of its cytolytic activity and is 
                     paralleled by transient leakage of periplasmic contents 
                     |CITS: [11731136]|. Hemolysin E may be secreted via a 
                     vesicle-mediated transport mechanism; over-expressed 
                     hemolysin E is found on the cell surface and in 
                     outer-membrane-derived vesicles (OMVs) released from the 
                     bacterial cell; these vesicles have higher cytotoxic 
                     activity than the purified protein; hemolysin E, isolated 
                     from the periplasmic fraction, is largely monomeric but 
                     forms an oligomeric pore complex in OMVs |CITS: [14532000]|
                     (see also |CITS: [12949101]|).
                     In the x-ray crystal structure reported by |CITS: 
                     [10660049]| the HlyE monomer forms a long rod-shaped 
                     molecule consisting of a bundle of four helices with head 
                     and tail sub-domains; when reconstituted in lipid vesicles,
                     HlyE oligomerizes to form transmembrane pores (also |CITS: 
                     [11006277]|). Assembled HlyE pores form a hollow cylinder 
                     with protomers arranged in head-to-head orientation; 
                     extensive conformational differences are noted between the 
                     soluble monomer and the assembled protomer |CITS: 
                     [19421192]| and mechanisms for membrane insertion and pore 
                     assembly have been widely investigated |CITS: 
                     [16754675][16688219][18227266][24019520][25222267][ 
                     25652783][27797514][27682503][26757820][28930630][28630151]
                     [30012608][33291882][36542809][37740492][40484915]|. 
                     <i>clyA</i>: <U>c</U>yto<U>ly</U>sin A |CITS: 
                     [8861216]|<BR><i>sheA</i>: ""<b>s</b>ilent 
                     <b>he</b>molysin, locus <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [11902713]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [20687485][30142951]|"
                     /translation="MTEIVADKTVEVVKNAIETADGALDLYNKYLDQVIPWQTFDETIK
