     gene            1226223..1226642
                     /gene="umuD"
                     /locus_tag="b1183"
                     /label=umuD
     CDS             1226223..1226642
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="umuD"
                     /locus_tag="b1183"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA polymerase V protein UmuD / DNA 
                     polymerase V"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.7"
                     /label=umuD
                     /note="Following induction of the SOS response, UmuD is 
                     proteolytically processed into UmuD' in a cleavage reaction
                     that depends on activated RecA |CITS: 
                     [2183775][3126496][1654503]|. UmuD has sequence similarity 
                     with LexA, which is also cleaved in a RecA-mediated fashion
                     |CITS: [3279417]|. This cleavage to produce UmuD' is 
                     required for UV mutagenesis and inclusion in the final 
                     Polymerase V (Pol V) protein complex |CITS: 
                     [1911947][10542196]|. RecA does not actually cleave UmuD, 
                     instead somehow promoting cleavage of one member of an UmuD
                     pair by its dimerization partner |CITS: 
                     [8955414][3279418]|. The UmuD amino-terminus controls the 
                     rate of this cleavage, which occurs only after
                     a lag time following UV irradiation |CITS: 
                     [9743621][10430923]|.
                     The full-length UmuD and truncated UmuD' proteins are both 
                     subject to proteolytic regulation. Along with its 
                     functional partner UmuC, UmuD is degraded by the Lon 
                     protease in an ATP-dependent manner |CITS: 
                     [9869642][8816793]|. The signal for this degradation 
                     resides in the UmuD amino-terminus, which is lost when it 
                     is processed into UmuD' |CITS: [9869642]|. UmuD' is rapidly
                     degraded by ClpXP following formation of a heterodimer 
                     between UmuD and UmuD' |CITS: [11013227]|. Full-length UmuD
                     operates in a manner similar to the ClpXP protease 
                     specificity factor SspB, binding via a particular peptide 
                     motif to the amino-terminus of ClpX, thus delivering its 
                     partnered UmuD' to the ClpXP protease |CITS: [14595014]|. 
                     In strains lacking ClpXP, heterodimerized UmuD' has a long 
                     half life and UV mutagenesis is diminished, the latter 
                     probably due to sequestration of UmuD' away from UmuC 
                     |CITS: [8816793]|.
                     A number of structural studies of UmuD and UmuD' have been 
                     carried out. Based on EPR, crosslinking and solvent 
                     accessibility, the amino-terminal arms (amino acids 1-39) 
                     of dimeric UmuD fold over the body of its binding partner; 
                     UmuD' dimers lack these arms and thus expose different 
                     surfaces |CITS: [12509298]|. Based on NMR evaluation, the 
                     dimerization interface of UmuD' is similar to a 
                     filamentation interface |CITS: [9406544]|. Crystals of both
                     native and mutant UmuD' have been developed following minor
                     structural modification |CITS: [8865345]|. The UmuD' 
                     structure has been resolved to 2.5 &Aring; resolution 
                     |CITS: [8614470]|. One solution structure of paired UmuD' 
                     shows that the cleaving and cleavage sites of the two 
                     proteins are not oriented toward each other, suggesting an 
                     outside factor may be required to direct cleavage |CITS: 
                     [11483531]|.
                     UmuD and UmuD' interact with RecA and with components of 
                     the DNA polymerase III holoenzyme. Both proteins interact 
                     with RecA and require the presence of RecA to bind to DNA 
                     |CITS: [8367479]|. The amino-terminus of UmuD' is required 
                     for this interaction with RecA |CITS: [8994967]|. UmuD and 
                     UmuD' also interact with the core polymerase subunits DnaE 
                     and DnaQ and the beta processivity clamp |CITS: 
                     [10535929]|. UmuD binds better to the beta clamp than UmuD'
                     by dint of its intact amino-terminus |CITS: 
                     [11959982][10535929]|. UmuD and UmuD' interact with the 
                     DnaQ carboxy terminus, disrupting DNA pol III function 
                     |CITS: [11208808]|.
                     Following SOS induction, UmuD is about twelve times
                     as abundant as its partner protein UmuC |CITS: [1654503]|."
                     /translation="MLFIKPADLREIVTFPLFSDLVQCGFPSPAADYVEQRIDLNQLLI
                     QHPSATYFVKASGDSMIDGGISDGDLLIVDSAITASHGDIVIAAVDGEFTVKKLQLRPT
