     gene            1238636..1239247
                     /gene="emtA"
                     /locus_tag="b1193"
                     /label=emtA
     CDS             1238636..1239247
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="emtA"
                     /locus_tag="b1193"
                     /product="trifunctional lytic murein transglycosylase E / 
                     peptidoglycan lytic endotransglycosylase / peptidoglycan 
                     lytic exotransglycosylase"
                     /label=emtA
                     /note="<i>emtA</i> encodes a membrane bound lytic murein 
                     transglycosylase (EmtA or MltE) which cleaves the &beta;-1 
                     &rarr; 4 glycosidic bond between <I>N</I>-acetylglucosamine
                     (GlcNAc) and <I>N</I>-acetylmuramic acid (MurNAc) residues 
                     of peptidoglycan with the concomitant formation of a 
                     1,6-anhydro ring at the MurNAc residue.
                     EmtA is a lipoprotein, predicted to reside in the outer 
                     membrane |CITS: [9642199]|. EmtA degrades isolated murein 
                     glycan strands to release muropeptides with a 
                     1,6-anhydroMurNAc terminus; EmtA has endotransglycosylase 
                     activity - it hydrolyses glycan strands <i>in vitro</i> to 
                     produce fragments with lengths of two and three 
                     disaccharide units but does not produce monomeric 
                     anhydrodisaccharide fragments; EmtA displays a preference 
                     for cross-linked murein; EmtA is unable to degrade purified
                     murein sacculi <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [9642199]|. Purified 
                     EmtA (residues 17-203) cleaves <i>Micrococcus luteus</i> 
                     murein sacculi <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [23075328]|. Purified
                     MltE (residues 18-203) degrades the purified cell wall 
                     sacculus to produce the following major products (in order 
                     of abundance): tetra-A1 (GlcNAc-1,6-anhydro-MurNAc monomer 
                     with tetrapeptide stem); tri-A1 (GlcNAc-1,6-anhydro-MurNAc 
                     monomer with tripeptide stem); tetra2-A1 
                     (GlcNAc-1,6-anhydro-MurNAc monomer with cross-linked 
                     tetrapeptide stems) plus other variations in smaller 
                     amounts. The reaction generates cross-linked and 
                     non-crosslinked products (the former in relatively small 
                     amounts); it also generates products that are indicative of
                     an endolytic transglycosylase reaction and some products 
                     containing a reducing end (ie containing a terminal MurNAc 
                     residue rather than a 1,6-anhydro-MurNAc residue) |CITS: 
                     [23421439]|. MltE is the primary endolytic murein 
                     transglycosylase - oligomeric saccharides containing a 
                     1,6-anhydroMurNAc terminus comprise 33% of its reaction 
                     products |CITS: [23421439]|
                     Like the other lytic transglycosylases, EmtA is believed to
                     have a general role in murein metabolism but as an 
                     endolytic enzyme it may also be important for allowing 
                     bulky compounds to pass through the murein barrier |CITS: 
                     [8824596], [9642199]|.
                     Purified, crystallised EmtA (residues 18-203) has a bilobal
                     globular structure containing 10 &alpha; helices; the 
                     protein has an extended groove that accommodates a 
                     peptidolglycan strand of at least 8 disaccharides |CITS: 
                     [21341761][23075328]|. Crystal structures of  EmtA in 
                     complex with chitopentaose and in a ternary complex with a 
                     murodipeptide and an inhibitor (bulgecin A), have also been
                     reported; EmtA appears adapted to optimally bind a MurNAc 
                     residue over a terminal 1,6-anhydroMurNAc residue |CITS: 
                     [23075328]|.
                     <I>E. coli</I> K-12 contains other lytic murein 
                     transglycosylases including |FRAME: G7457-MONOMER 
                     ""MltA""|, |FRAME: G7410-MONOMER ""MltB""|, |FRAME: 
                     G7533-MONOMER ""MltC""|, |FRAME: EG10246-MONOMER ""MltD""|,
                     |FRAME: EG11373-MONOMER ""MltF""| and |FRAME: 
                     EG10950-MONOMER ""Slt70""|. |FRAME: EG11494 ""MltG""|, an 
                     inner membrane protein and potential PG terminase, also has
                     lytic murein transglycosylase activity.
                     <i>emtA</i>: endo-type membrane-bound lytic 
                     transglycosylase |CITS: [9642199]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [18266855][22126997]|"
