     gene            complement(1239249..1239983)
                     /gene="ycgR"
                     /locus_tag="b1194"
                     /label=ycgR
     CDS             complement(1239249..1239983)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ycgR"
                     /locus_tag="b1194"
                     /product="flagellar brake protein YcgR"
                     /label=ycgR
                     /note="YcgR regulates flagellar motility in a |FRAME:
                     C-DI-GMP| (c-di-GMP)-dependent manner <i>in vivo</i> |CITS:
                     [16920715]|.  Entry into stationary phase initiates 
                     production of increased levels of c-di-GMP, which binds to 
                     YcgR and thereby leads to slower flagellar motor speeds 
                     |CITS: [20303158][20346719]| and increased 
                     counter-clockwise flagellar rotation bias |CITS: 
                     [20346719][20444091]|.  YcgR first biases the flagellar 
                     motor towards counter-clockwise rotation and subsequently 
                     decreases motor speed |CITS: [31611290]|.
                     c-di-GMP-bound YcgR acts as a molecular brake that 
                     interacts directly with the |FRAME: 
                     MOTA-FLAGELLAR-MOTOR-STATOR-PROTEIN MotA| flagellar stator 
                     element at a 1:4 ratio |CITS: 
                     [20303158][31836667][37125196]|.  The c-di-GMP-bound 
                     C-terminal PilZ domain of YcgR is sufficient for 
                     interaction with MotA; however, full-length YcgR is 
                     required for regulation of motility |CITS: [31836667]|. 
                     YcgR interacts with the flagellar switch complex proteins 
                     |FRAME: FLIG-FLAGELLAR-SWITCH-PROTEIN FliG| |CITS: 
                     [20346719][20444091][37125196]| and |FRAME: 
                     FLIM-FLAGELLAR-C-RING-SWITCH FliM| |CITS: [20346719]|. 
                     Published evidence is contradictory regarding whether 
                     binding the flagellar switch complex proteins is 
                     c-di-GMP-dependent |CITS: [20346719]| or only strengthened 
                     by c-di-GMP |CITS: [20444091]|. However, data from 2023 
                     suggested that YcgR can interact with both MotA and FliG 
                     without c-di-GMP, but the affinities are enhanced upon 
                     c-di-GMP binding |CITS: [37125196]|. Residues  F117 and 
                     E232 are required for binding to MotA, and residue D54 is 
                     needed for FliG binding |CITS: [37125196]|.  A dynamic 
                     fluorescence experiment suggested that YcgR is retained at 
                     the flagellar motor after dissociation of stator elements 
                     |CITS: [30447993]|.
                     Purified YcgR specifically binds one c-di-GMP dimer per 
                     YcgR monomer with a K<sub>d</sub> of 0.84 &micro;M
                     <i>in vitro</i>; the PilZ domain is sufficient for binding 
                     activity |CITS: [16249258][16920715]|.  An R118D mutant is 
                     unable to bind c-di-GMP |CITS: [16920715]|.  A crystal 
                     structure of YcgR in a complex with c-di-GMP has been 
                     solved at 2.3&Aring; resolution |CITS: 
                     [23722848][31836667]|; crystal structures have also been 
                     solved for both the N- and c-di-GMP-bound C-terminal 
                     domains of YcgR |CITS: [31836667]|.  Amino acids involved 
                     in c-di-GMP binding and interaction with MotA were 
                     investigated by site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [31836667]|.
                     The structural conservation, important amino acid sequence 
                     motifs, and evolutionary diversification of PilZ 
                     domain-containing proteins has been investigated |CITS: 
                     [31740493]|.
                     <i>ycgR</i> mutations suppress the flagellar motility 
                     defect of an |FRAME: EG10457| mutant |CITS: [11031114]| and
                     a |FRAME: EG12252| mutant |CITS: [17941710][20444091]| and 
                     the swarming defect of a |FRAME: G7354| mutant |CITS: 
                     [17941710]|.  Point mutations in <i>ycgR</i> that weaken 
                     the interaction between YcgR and FliG allow improved 
                     motility of an <i>hns</i> deletion strain |CITS: 
                     [21890701]|.  <i>ycgR</i> null mutants swim faster than the
                     wild type organism at entry into stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [20303158]| and show enhanced surface attachment |CITS: 
                     [31793092]|.  Point mutants that lost the ability to 
                     interact with c-di-GMP do not inhibit motility and do not 
                     interact with MotA |CITS: [31836667]|.
                     In a random transposon mutagenesis screen, <i>ycgR</i> was 
                     indentified as a gene whose inactivation is beneficial for 
                     anaerobic hydrogen production from glycerol.  A 
                     &Delta;<i>ycgR</i> mutant strain grows slightly faster than
                     the parent strain under anaerobic conditions |CITS: 
                     [25567513]|.
                     Expression of <i>ycgR</i> requires FlhDC |CITS: 
                     [11031114]|.
                     YcgR has been utilized as the sensor domain for a c-di-GMP 
                     biosensor |CITS: [29466657]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17993515][19756011][20371342][23480406][3 
                     0710060][31136265]|"
