                     /locus_tag="b1197"
                     /product="bifunctional periplasmic trehalase / trehalase"
                     /EC_number="3.2.1.28"
                     /label=treA
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> contains two trehalases: the
                     periplasmic TreA (discussed here) and the cytoplasmic 
                     |FRAME: TREHALACYTO-MONOMER ""TreF""|.  Both enzymes 
                     catalyze the hydrolysis of trehalose into two molecules of 
                     D-glucose.
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme alone and complexed with 
                     inhibitors have been solved |CITS: 
                     [17455176][19123216][20461849]|.
                     Expression of periplasmic trehalase is increased under 
                     conditions of high osmolarity |CITS: [2820965][1710760]| 
                     and during the transition to stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [1744047]|. TreA-mediated periplasmic trehalose recycling 
                     during nutrient stress is proposed to have regulatory 
                     implications via glucose-flux sensing |CITS: [37916804]|. 
                     <i>treA</i> mutants accumulate extracellular trehalose 
                     under osmotic stress conditions |CITS: [1825082]|. Reviews:
                     |CITS: [8391102][18822375][23302511]|"
