     gene            complement(1245224..1246294)
                     /gene="dhaK"
                     /locus_tag="b1200"
                     /label=dhaK
     CDS             complement(1245224..1246294)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dhaK"
                     /locus_tag="b1200"
                     /product="bifunctional dihydroxyacetone kinase subunit K / 
                     dihydroxyacetone kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.121"
                     /label=dhaK
                     /note="DhaK is similar to the N-terminal half of the 
                     ATP-dependent dihydroxyacetone kinase of <i>Citrobacter 
                     freundii</i> and eukaryotes |CITS: 
                     [11021910][11158340][11350937]|.
                     Crystal structures of apo-DhaK, DhaK in complex with 
                     dihydroxyacetone (DHA) |CITS: [12813127]| as well as 
                     glyceraldehyde or DHA-phosphate |CITS: [15476397]|, and the
                     DhaK-DhaL complex |CITS: [21209328]| have been solved. DhaK
                     is a homodimer in the crystal structure; DHA is bound in a 
                     hemiaminal linkage to the N&epsilon;2 of His218 in the 
                     active site |CITS: [12813127][15476397]|.  A H218A mutant 
                     lacks kinase activity |CITS: [12813127]|; the His56 residue
                     is involved in formation of the DHA-His218 hemiaminal bond 
                     |CITS: [21209328]|.  Covalent binding of DHA to His218 
                     orients DHA for subsequent phosphoryl transfer.  The Asp109
                     residue may act as a general base to activate the 
                     &gamma;-hydroxyl group of DHA |CITS: [21209328]|.
                     DhaK is a corepressor of |FRAME: G6628-MONOMER DhaR|, the 
                     transcription factor that controls expression of the 
                     <i>dhaKLM</i> operon |CITS: [15616579]|.  Crystal 
                     structures of DhaK bound to DhaR have been solved.  DhaK 
                     interacts with the GAF-like domain of a DhaR subunit via a 
                     surface that overlaps with the region that interacts with 
                     DhaL |CITS: [24440518]|.
                     The levels of DhaK and DhaL are increased in a <i>ptsI</i> 
                     mutant |CITS: [11158340]|.
                     A <i>dhaK</i> transposon insertion mutant is defective for 
                     growth on and phosphorylation of dihydroxyacetone |CITS: 
                     [11021910]|.
                     DhaK: ""<b>d</b>i<b>h</b>ydroxy<b>a</b>cetone"" |CITS: 
                     [11350937]|
                     Review: |CITS: [16505971]|"
