     gene            complement(1252177..1253268)
                     /gene="ychF"
                     /locus_tag="b1203"
                     /label=ychF
     CDS             complement(1252177..1253268)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ychF"
                     /locus_tag="b1203"
                     /product="bifunctional ATPase / redox-responsive ATPase
                     YchF"
                     /label=ychF
                     /note="|CITS: []|The highly conserved YchF protein is a 
                     HAS-ATPase, the founding member of a family of GTP-binding 
                     proteins |CITS: [11200227]| with ATPase activity |CITS: 
                     [17430889]|. The conservation and essentiality of this 
                     protein in many bacteria points to an ancient origin 
                     associated with cell survival in a variety of environments 
                     |CITS: [40128973]|. The bacterial GTPase superfamily is 
                     associated with RNA- and translation-related roles in the 
                     cell |CITS: [11489118]|. The ATPase activity of YchF 
                     appears to be redox-regulated |CITS: [26160547]|. YchF 
                     regulates adaptation of the cell to various stress 
                     conditions by interacting with |FRAME: EG10506-MONOMER|, 
                     inhibiting leaderless mRNA translation and its association 
                     with the |FRAME: CPLX0-3953| |CITS: [34026826]|. 
                     Additionally it inhibits production of the major stress 
                     response |FRAME: RPOS-MONOMER| as well as polyphosphate 
                     levels via regulation of |FRAME: PPK-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [38993675]|.
                     YchF has K<sup>+</sup>-activated ATPase activity and binds 
                     to both the 70S ribosome and the 50S ribosomal subunit in a
                     nucleotide-independent manner |CITS: [21527254]|. 
                     Nucleotide binding to YchF induces different conformational
                     states. The K<sub>D</sub> of the YchF&middot;ADPNP complex 
                     for 70S ribosomes is 3 &micro;M, and the 70S ribosome 
                     stimulates the ATPase activity of YchF |CITS: [22995830]|. 
                     The highly conserved His114 residue is located in a 
                     flexible loop of the G-domain and is critical for the 
                     ATPase activity of the protein |CITS: [26018081]|.
                     YchF was initially reported to be a monomer in solution 
                     |CITS: [21527254]|. In the presence of hydrogen peroxide 
                     (H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>), overexpressed YchF interacts 
                     directly with the major catalase |FRAME: HYDROPEROXIDI-CPLX
                     ""KatG""| and inhibits its activity. The function of YchF 
                     may be controlled by phosphorylation and perhaps additional
                     modifications |CITS: [23139412]|. It was later shown that 
                     oxidative stress promotes homodimerization of YchF via an 
                     intramolecular Cys35-Cys35 disulfide bridge. ATPase 
                     activity of the dimer form of YchF is lower than that of 
                     the momoner; thioredoxin 1 is able to dissociate the YchF 
                     dimers, stimulating the ATPase activity |CITS: [26160547]|.
                     A <i>ychF</i> null mutant strain has a slow growth 
                     phenotype |CITS: [9743119]|, which is due to delay in 
                     regrowth <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [40128973]|, and is more 
                     sensitive to kasugamycin than wild type |CITS: [23308060]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>ychF</i> results in hypersensitivity 
                     to hydrogen peroxide; this phenotype is dependent on the 
                     ATPase activity of YchF. Transcription of <i>ychF</i> is 
                     down-regulated by OxyR in response to oxidative stress 
                     |CITS: [23139412]|. Expression of <i>ychF</i> is growth 
                     phase dependent and reduced during stationary phase and 
                     other stress conditions. In a &Delta;<i>ychF</i> mutant the
                     growth defect is observed in late exponential/early 
                     stationary phase under oxidative stress conditions |CITS: 
                     [40128973]|. YchF interacts preferentially with the 30S 
                     ribosomal subunit via its N-terminal ATPase-domain |CITS: 
                     [34026826]|. Deletion of <i>ychF</i> increases translation 
                     of the RpoS and the steady-state levels of PPK leading to 
                     higher polyphosphate production |CITS: [38993675]|. The 
                     growth inhibition is also observed in &Delta;<i>ychF</i> 
                     mutants when |FRAME: EG10917|, which codes for the 
                     ribosomal protein S18, is overexpressed |CITS: [40128973]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11200227][11489118][16333325][21885683][3 
                     0742486][35056463][35456773]|"
