     gene            complement(1253385..1253969)
                     /gene="pth"
                     /locus_tag="b1204"
                     /label=pth
     CDS             complement(1253385..1253969)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pth"
                     /locus_tag="b1204"
                     /product="peptidyl-tRNA hydrolase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.1.29"
                     /label=pth
                     /note="During the early stage of bacterial translation 
                     peptidyl-tRNAs frequently dissociate from the ribosome and 
                     are recycled by peptidyl-tRNA hydrolase. An analysis of 
                     peptidyl-tRNAs in a |FRAME: EG10785|<sup>ts</sup> strain 
                     found that about 20% of them contained a single amino-acid 
                     substitution compared with the translated ORF. Most of the 
                     substitutions occur at the C-terminal drop-off site. The 
                     miscoded peptides do not participate in the next round of 
                     elongation; instead, they dissociate immediately from the 
                     ribosome and are recylced by peptidyl-tRNA hydrolase (Pth),
                     which catalyzes the removal of peptides from peptidyl-tRNAs
                     that have been prematurely released from the ribosome 
                     |CITS: [37198183]|.
                     The enzyme is essential for growth in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS:
                     [368041][15547251]|. |FRAME: EG30056|, encoding a 
                     tRNA<sup>Lys</sup>, was found to be a multicopy suppressor 
                     of a a |FRAME: EG10785|<sup>ts</sup> mutant, indicating 
                     that the <i>pth</i> mutant phenotype is due to starvation 
                     for tRNA<sup>Lys</sup> |CITS: [8654380][16540595]|.  Pth 
                     relieves the growth inhibition by freeing tRNAs from 
                     aborted translation products |CITS: [10049395][10452886]|. 
                     Pth is also required for growth of bacteriophage lambda due
                     to translation of minigene ORFs in the lambda bar region 
                     |CITS: [779235][9191062][11136457][15292158]|.
                     The crystal structure of Pth has been solved at 1.2 &Aring;
                     resolution |CITS: [9144799][9303320]|.  Pth is monomeric.  
                     Predicted active site residues were confirmed by 
                     site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: [9303320]|.  The His20 
                     residue is essential for catalytic activity |CITS: 
                     [15078105]|.  The catalytic center has been mapped by NMR 
                     |CITS: [21718701]|.
                     The integrity of <i>ychF</i>, the downstream open reading 
                     frame in the <i>pth</i> operon, influences the stability of
                     the transcript.  Lower RNase E activity results in 
                     overproduction of Pth, and thus it is proposed that RNase E
                     processing within <i>ychF</i> causes mRNA degradation 
                     |CITS: [12427937]|.
                     A temperature-sensitive <i>pth</i> mutant was isolated 
                     |CITS: [4566050][4571804]|; the mutant protein is unstable 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [10692356]|.  The mutant allele can 
                     revert rapidly by gene duplication |CITS: [11390689]|. 
                     Overexpression of |FRAME: SSRA-RNA| can rescue the defect 
                     of the <i>pth</i><sup>ts</sup> allele |CITS: [15547251]|. 
                     The <i>rap</i> mutant of Pth |CITS: [779235]| is highly 
                     sensitive to the length of the peptidyl-tRNA |CITS: 
                     [10835367]|.
                     A laboratory experimental evolution study identified 
                     several mutations in <i>pth</i> that conferred 
                     hyper-persister phenotypes when exposed to ampicillin and 
                     ciprofloxacin |CITS: [31964772]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9451455][16849786]|."
