                     IEA] [PMID 26602691]"
                     /go_process="single-species biofilm formation on inanimate
                     substrate [goid 0044011] [evidence IMP] [PMID 19919618]"
                     /go_process="cellular response to hydrogen peroxide [goid
                     0070301] [evidence IMP] [PMID 19919618]"
                     /go_process="cellular response to cadmium ion [goid
                     0071276] [evidence IMP] [PMID 19919618]"
     gene            complement(1254580..1256259)
                     /gene="dauA"
                     /locus_tag="b1206"
                     /label=dauA
     CDS             complement(1254580..1256259)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dauA"
                     /locus_tag="b1206"
                     /product="multifunctional aerobic C4-dicarboxylate
                     transporter DauA / succinate transporter / aspartate 
                     transport / fumarate transporter"
                     /label=dauA
                     /note="DauA is an inner membrane protein that transports
                     C4 dicarboxylates in an aerobic, acidic (pH 5) environment.
                     DauA transports the monoanion and dianion form of succinate
                     and fumarate, and the dicarboxylic amino acid aspartate. At
                     pH 5, DauA is the main succinate transporter and |FRAME: 
                     DCTA-MONOMER ""DctA""|, which is active for dicarboxylate 
                     transport at pH 7, is not produced |CITS: [23278959]|. DauA
                     is required for the optimal expression of DctA at pH 7 
                     |CITS: [23278959]|.
                     In liquid culture (pH 5) with succinate as the sole carbon 
                     source a <i>dauA</i> deletion mutant and a <i>dauA dctA</i>
                     double deletion mutant are unable to grow. In liquid 
                     culture (pH 7) with succinate as sole carbon source a 
                     <i>dctA</i> deletion mutant grows poorly when compared to a
                     <i>dauA</i> deletion mutant and both grow less than the 
                     wild type |CITS: [23278959]|. DauA can be detected during 
                     growth with succinate at pH 5 or pH 7 |CITS: [23278959]|. 
                     Measurement of kinetic parameters for succinate transport 
                     indicates that DauA has a weaker affinity for succinate 
                     than DctA and a lower maximal transport rate |CITS: 
                     [23278959]|.
                     DauA is present at consistent levels in both wild type and 
                     <i>dcuS</i> deleted strains when cultures are grown with 
                     succinate as sole carbon source at pH 5 or pH 7 suggesting 
                     that, unlike DctA, DauA is not induced by the DcuSR 
                     two-component system |CITS: [23278959]|.
                     DauA is a member of the SLC26 (solute carrier 26)/SulP 
                     (sulphate permease) family of anion transporters 
                     responsible for the transport of sulphate, chloride and 
                     bicarbonate across the plasma membrane |CITS: [16381841]|. 
                     <i>dauA</i> and related sequences from bacteria form a 
                     cluster within the SLC 26 family that is independent from 
                     the ""BicA-like"" transporters predicted to be involved in 
                     bicarbonate transport |CITS: [23278959]|. Topology 
                     predictions suggest the presence of 12 transmembrane 
                     regions and a C-terminal cytoplasmic domain containing a 
                     STAS (<U>s</U>ulphate <u>t</u>ransporter <u>a</u>ntagonist 
                     of anti-<u>s</u>igma factor |CITS: [10662676]|) domain 
                     |CITS: [21070944], [23278959]|.
                     The DauA STAS domain co-purifies in a 1:1 complex with 
                     |FRAME: EG50003-MONOMER ""acyl carrier protein (ACP)""| 
                     |CITS: [21070944]|. DauA consistently co-purifies with 
                     proteins involved in fatty acid metabolism - FabA, FabR, 
                     FadE and FadJ |CITS: [21070944]|. <i>dauA</i> and 
                     <i>dauA</i> STAS region deletion mutants show reduced 
                     growth compared to the wild type at alkaline pH and a 
                     <i>dauA</i> deletion shows altered bicarbonate 
                     incorporation |CITS: [21070944]|. DauA is thus predicted to
                     form part of a 'membrane metabolon' which functions to 
                     channel external bicarbonate/carbonate directly to enzymes 
                     of fatty acid metabolism sequestered by its STAS domain 
                     |CITS: [21070944]| (see also |CITS: [22705263]|). 
                     Bicarbonate transport by DauA was not detected by |CITS: 
                     [23278959]|.
                     Transcription is induced upon biofilm formation |CITS: 
                     [14727089]|.
                     <i>dauA</i>: <U>d</U>icarboxylic <U>a</U>cid <U>u</U>ptake 
                     system A
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [27415771]|"
