     gene            complement(1258333..1258956)
                     /gene="lolB"
                     /locus_tag="b1209"
                     /label=lolB
     CDS             complement(1258333..1258956)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lolB"
                     /locus_tag="b1209"
                     /product="bifunctional outer membrane lipoprotein LolB / 
                     lipoprotein chaperone LolB"
                     /label=lolB
                     /note="LolB is an outer membrane lipoprotein which is part
                     of the 5 protein LolABCDE lipoprotein trafficking pathway; 
                     LolB interacts with |FRAME: G6465-MONOMER 
                     ""LolA""|-lipoprotein complexes and catalyses lipoprotein 
                     insertion into the outer membrane.
                     The major outer membrane lipoprotein Lpp, is incorporated 
                     into proteoliposomes reconstituted from purified LolB and 
                     phospholipids <i>in vitro</i>; Lpp is transferred from 
                     Lpp-LolA to a water-soluble LolB (mLolB) <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [9384574]|. Lipoproteins (Lpp, Pal and NlpB) 
                     released from spheroplasts in the presence of LolA are 
                     subsequently incorporated into LolB-containing membranes 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [10521496]|. LolB specifically 
                     interacts with liganded LolA and the interaction between 
                     free LolA and LolB is negligible |CITS: [18029423]|.
                     LolB (itself a lipoprotein) released from spheroplasts 
                     forms LolA-LolB and larger heterogeneous complexes; <i>in 
                     vitro</i> incorporation of LolB into outer membranes 
                     containing normal levels of LolB is inefficient but is 
                     enhanced in outer membranes prepared from 
                     LolB-overproducing cells; LolB localization <i>in vivo</i> 
                     probably takes place in a LolB-dependent manner |CITS: 
                     [10521496]|.
                     LolB is essential for growth |CITS: [9384574]|. Depletion 
                     of LolB causes the accumulation of LolA-lipoprotein 
                     complexes in the periplasm and of mature lipoproteins in 
                     the inner membrane |CITS: [11673422]|. LolB lacking the N 
                     terminal acyl chain (mLolB) localizes in the periplasm of 
                     <i>E. coli</i> and is able to support cell growth; mLolB 
                     cannot compensate for LolA function; mLolB incorporates Lpp
                     into both outer and inner membranes <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [19678842]|. LolB is expressed constitutively |CITS: 
                     [8997718]|. Depletion of LolB is toxic due to the 
                     accumulation of two lipoproteins - Lpp and OsmB - in the 
                     inner membrane; LolB (and LolA) are dispensable in a 
                     &Delta;<i>lpp</i> &Delta;<i>rcsB cpxA24</i> background 
                     (i.e. the essentiality of LolB and LolA can be overcome by 
                     activating the Cpx stress response and removing Lol 
                     substrates that are toxic when mislocalized); the Bam 
                     complex (which contains 4 OM lipoproteins) remains fully 
                     formed and functional in &Delta;<i>lpp</i> &Delta;<i>rcsB 
                     cpxA24</i> cells that lack LolB |CITS: [28416660]|. 
                     Purified, soluble mLolB has been crystallized and a 
                     structure determined; LolB comprises an incomplete 
                     &beta;-barrel and an &alpha;-helical lid which enclose a 
                     hydrophobic cavity proposed to bind an acyl chain; LolA and
                     LolB have high structural similarity |CITS: [12839983]|.  A
                     'mouth-to-mouth' model for the transfer of lipoproteins 
                     from LolA to LolB has been proposed |CITS: [19307584]| (see
                     also |CITS: [19546215]|).
                     <i>lol</i>: localization of lipoproteins |CITS: 
                     [9384574]|<BR><i>hemM</i>: heme biosynthesis |CITS: 
                     [1427085]|.
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [15221203][15276320][26443779][204 
                     19407][30900542]|<BR>Comments: |CITS: [28461688]|"
