     gene            1260468..1261550
                     /gene="prfA"
                     /locus_tag="b1211"
                     /label=prfA
     CDS             1260468..1261550
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="prfA"
                     /locus_tag="b1211"
                     /product="peptide chain release factor RF1"
                     /label=prfA
                     /note="Release factor 1 (RF1) is one of two class 1 
                     codon-specific factors in <i>E. coli</i> that facilitate 
                     the release of the growing polypeptide chain at stop 
                     codons.  RF1 recognizes the termination codons UAG and UAA 
                     |CITS: [5233840][4879404][4911223][5422622][3049546]|; 
                     |FRAME: EG10762-MONOMER RF2| recognizes UGA and UAA.  In 
                     <i>E. coli</i>, the UAG stop codon is relatively rare, 
                     terminating only ~7% of all protein-coding genes including 
                     only seven essential genes |CITS: [22662873]|.
                     Termination is highly precise; RF1 is able to discriminate 
                     against related sense codons by 3 to 6 orders of magnitude 
                     and without requiring energy-driven error correction |CITS:
                     [10681447]|.  The discriminator site consists of a 
                     tripeptide which appears to be functionally equivalent to 
                     the anticodon of tRNA |CITS: [10688208]|.  The I196 and 
                     L126 amino acids contribute to discrimination between the 
                     different stop codons |CITS: [28743745]|.  An <i>E. 
                     coli</i> cell is variously estimated to contain between 500
                     and 4900 molecules of RF1 and reaches the highest RF1 
                     content at a high growth rate |CITS: [5543670][8176726]|. 
                     RF1 is less abundant than RF2 under most growth conditions 
                     |CITS: [25217634]|.
                     RF1-mediated termination is sensitive to the 
                     codon-anticodon interaction of the wobble base at the last 
                     amino acid residue of the peptide chain and the tRNA at the
                     ribosomal P-site |CITS: [8757279]|.  Interactions between 
                     RF1 and the ribosome have been mapped |CITS: 
                     [1742355][12818198]|.  The universally conserved tripeptide
                     sequence GGQ is thought to interact with the 
                     peptidyl-transferase center of the ribosome and 
                     hypothesized to be functionally equivalent to the CCA end 
                     of tRNA |CITS: [12419223][12492870]|.  Molecular dynamics 
                     free-energy simulations have revealed specific amino acids 
                     within RF1 that contribute to stop codon recognition and 
                     also showed that it can not be described as tRNA mimicry 
                     with a tripeptide anticodon |CITS: [20512119]|. 
                     Site-directed mutagenesis of the predicted key residues 
                     Gln185, Arg186, Thr190, and Thr198 showed that they 
                     residues are critical for RF1 binding to the ribosome, 
                     while the rate of peptide release was similar to wild type 
                     |CITS: [24699820]|.  The conserved His197 residue is 
                     required for ribosomal A site binding and peptide release 
                     |CITS: [20873815]|, and Glu18 is required for RF3-mediated 
                     recycling of RF1 |CITS: [23755360]|.
                     A small-angle X-ray scattering solution structure of RF1 
                     alone initially showed an open structure, similar to its 
                     structure when RF1 is associated with the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [16364917]|.  tmFRET experiments later showed that RF1 
                     changes from a closed to an open conformation upon binding 
                     to an A site stop codon-containing ribosome |CITS: 
                     [26827724]|.  A cryo-EM structure of the post-termination 
                     ribosome with both RF1 and apo-RF3 shows a conformational 
                     change in RF1 upon RF3 binding |CITS: [23755360]|. 
                     GTP-bound RF3 enhances dissociation of RF1 from the 
                     ribosome 500-fold |CITS: [24667215]|; because GTP 
                     hydrolysis and dissociation of RF3 occur before RF1 
                     dissociation, it was proposed that RF3 stabilizes the 
                     ratcheted state of the ribosome, thereby accelerateing the 
                     dissociation of RF1 and RF2 |CITS: [27779391]|.
                     Uridine residues in mRNA may be modified to pseudouridine 
                     (&Psi;).  A crystal structure of RF1 bound to the <i>T. 
                     thermophilus</i> ribosome containing a &Psi;AA codon in the
                     A site shows no difference in stop codon recognition. The 
                     presence of the &Psi; nucleotide also had no effect on the 
                     rate of peptide release <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [27107638]|.
                     RF1 is post-translationally modified by 
                     <i>N<sup>5</sup></i> methylation by PrmC <i>in vitro</i>, 
                     most likely at position Gln235 within the conserved GGQ 
                     sequence |CITS: [11805295][11847124]|.  Methylation is 
                     required for normal translation termination activity of RF1
                     |CITS: [17932046]|.  A crystal structure of the complex 
                     between RF1, PrmC, and S-adenosyl-homocysteine, the product
                     of the methyl transfer reaction, has been solved at 3.1 
                     &Aring; resolution |CITS: [16364916]|. Phylogenetic 
                     profiling shows an unexplained link between |FRAME: 
                     EG11085-MONOMER RsmH| and RF1 |CITS: [22411911]|. Certain 
                     mutations in <i>prfA</i> lead to suppression of the UAG and
                     UAA stop codons and are conditionally lethal |CITS: 
                     [6419024]|.  An allele of <i>rpsL</i> suppresses this 
                     phenotype, indicating that RF1 and L7/L12 interact |CITS: 
                     [7932718]|.  The N-terminal domain of RF1 is not required 
                     for its activity |CITS: [14651631]|.  Temperature sensitive
                     growth of the <i>prfA1</i> allele can be suppressed by a 
                     lack of the 5-methylaminomethyl-2-thiouridine modification 
                     of the wobble base of certain tRNAs |CITS: [19103926]|.  
                     RF1 and the toxin RelE functionally interact; certain 
                     <i>prfA</i> mutants display increased sensitivity to RelE 
                     |CITS: [19019162]|.
                     While <i>prfA</i> is essential for growth in K-12 strains 
                     |CITS: [22662873]|, strains with null mutations in 
                     <i>prfA</i> were constructed in certain genetic backgrounds
                     |CITS: [20702426][21764749][21926996]|.  The essentiality 
                     of <i>prfA</i> in K-12 strains is due to the Ala246Thr 
                     mutation in RF2, which lowers termination efficiency of RF2
                     at UAA stop codons, making them dependent on termination by
                     RF1.  The small number of essential genes terminated with a
                     UAG stop codon also contain other in-frame stop codons 
                     within a short distance downstream of the UAG.  Termination
                     at the downstream stop codons apparently is sufficent for 
                     enabling growth of a <i>prfA</i> null mutant if RF2 is 
                     fully active |CITS: [22662873]|.
                     RF1 appears to contribute to the rescue of stalled 
                     ribosomes |CITS: [22326347]| and was shown to promote the 
                     release of peptides from certain mismatched P site-tRNA 
                     complexes |CITS: [24798339]|.  Overexpression of RF1 
                     accelerates peptidyl-tRNA turnover from termination-paused 
                     ribosomes |CITS: [19761774]|.
                     Expression of RF1 increases with the growth rate; the 
                     regulation is controlled at the <i>hemA1</i> promoter 
                     |CITS: [15308332]|.
                     Translation termination, and specifically the dissociation 
                     of RF1 or RF2 from the ribosome, is the target of the 
                     antimicrobial peptide apidaecin.  An Asp241Gly mutation in 
                     RF1 leads to resistance to Api137, a derivative of the 
                     natural apidaecin 1b |CITS: [28741611]|.
                     PrfA: ""<b>p</b>rotein <b>r</b>elease <b>f</b>actor 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [3782033]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [3126822][12514123]|
                     "
