     gene            complement(1266205..1267305)
                     /gene="chaA"
                     /locus_tag="b1216"
                     /label=chaA
     CDS             complement(1266205..1267305)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="chaA"
                     /locus_tag="b1216"
                     /product="Na(+)/K(+):H(+) antiporter ChaA"
                     /label=chaA
                     /note="ChaA is an Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>:proton
                     antiporter which contributes to growth at alkaline 
                     pH.<BR><i>E. coli</i> appears to contain a number of 
                     transporters which mediate active proton uptake for 
                     alkaline pH homeostasis including |FRAME: CPLX0-7629 
                     ""NhaA""| (the prominent Na<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> 
                     antiporter), |FRAME: NHAB-MONOMER ""NhaB""|, |FRAME: 
                     CMR-MONOMER ""MdfA""| and |FRAME:  YJIO-MONOMER ""MdtM""|, 
                     each of which may function under varying conditions of 
                     external pH and cation composition (see |CITS: 
                     [16277975][23701827]|)
                     Overexpression of <i>chaA</i> complements the 
                     Na<sup>+</sup> sensitivity of <i>E. coli</i> strain EP432 
                     (&Delta;|FRAME: EG10652 ""<i>nhaA</i>""| &Delta;|FRAME: 
                     EG11392 ""<i>nhaB</i>|) which lacks 
                     Na<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> antiporter activity 
                     |CITS:[8496184]|. The <i>E. coli</i> K-12 mutant strain 
                     KNabc (&Delta;<i>nhaA</i> &Delta;<i>nhaB</i> 
                     &Delta;<i>chaA</i>) cannot export Na<sup>+</sup> and is 
                     unable to grow in the presence of of 0.2 M NaCl (or 10 mM 
                     LiCl); membrane vesicles prepared from KNabc cells do not 
                     show any Na<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> antiporter activity 
                     |CITS: [8651921]|. Characterization of mutant strains 
                     containing varying combinations of NhaA, NhaB and ChaA is 
                     also reported in |CITS: [8021217][9518629]|) (and further 
                     |CITS: [9845336][10652103][11779554][23701827]|). ChaA is 
                     implicated in proton uptake at alkaline pH >8 |CITS: 
                     [9518629]|. Overexpression of <i>chaA</i> in the <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 derived mutant strain TO114 (<i>nhaA</i>::Km 
                     <i>nhaB</i>::Em <i>chaA</i>::Cmr) supports growth in LBK 
                     medium at pH 10; Na<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> exchange 
                     activity (in strain TO114 overexpressing <i>chaA</i>) is 
                     not detected at pH 7, is low at pH 8 and is significant at 
                     pH 9 |CITS: [20460696]|.
                     ChaA is implicated in the adaption to high KCl 
                     environments; cells lacking <i>chaA</i> are sensitive to 
                     high external KCl concentrations (>500mM) particularly 
                     under alkaline conditions (pH 8.5); ChaA mediates 
                     physiologically significant K<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> 
                     antiporter activity |CITS:[16687400]|.
                     ChaA was initially suggested to be a 
                     Ca<sup>2+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> antiporter with a role in 
                     calcium ion circulation at alkaline pH; everted membrane 
                     vesicles prepared from <i>E. coli</i> strain EP432 
                     overexpressing <i>chaA</i> display increased cation:proton 
                     antiport activity with both Na<sup>+</sup> and 
                     Ca<sup>2+</sup>, but not with Li<sup>+</sup> or 
                     K<sup>+</sup> |CITS:[8496184]|. However, direct measurement
                     of cytosolic calcium levels using the Ca<sup>2+</sup> 
                     activated photoprotein aequorin, suggests that ChaA does 
                     not play a major role in regulating cytosolic 
                     Ca<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [18342619]|.
                     ChaA contains 11 predicted transmembrane segments plus a 
                     central hydrophilic motif rich in acidic residues 
                     |CITS:[8496184][8710949]| (see also |CITS: [20460696]|). In
                     the Transporter Classification Database |CITS: [26546518]| 
                     ChaA is a member of the Ca<sup>2+</sup>:Cation Antiporter 
                     (CaCA) family (see also |CITS: [15163769][17086450]|).
                     A <i>chaA-lacZ</i> transcriptional fusion derived from 
                     <I>E. coli</I> O157:H7 and expressed in a K-12 derived 
                     strain is induced under hyperosmotic stress at alkaline pH 
                     |CITS:[12460671]|.
                     "
