     gene            1273413..1274804
                     /gene="narK"
                     /locus_tag="b1223"
                     /label=narK
     CDS             1273413..1274804
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="narK"
                     /locus_tag="b1223"
                     /product="bifunctional nitrate:nitrite antiporter NarK / 
                     nitrate:nitrite antiporter"
                     /label=narK
                     /note="NarK is a nitrate/nitrite transporter with a 
                     physiological role in nitrate-dependent respiration. NarK 
                     mediates the uptake of nitrate and the extrusion of nitrite
                     - the exact mechanism of transport has been debated but 
                     structural constraints suggest that NarK functions as a 
                     nitrate:nitrite exchanger |CITS: [23665960]|. Purified 
                     NarK, reconstituted in liposomes functions as a 
                     nitrate:nitrite antiporter |CITS: [25959928]|. The location
                     of nitrate and nitrite ions in crystal structures of NarK 
                     suggest that the two ions bind to the same site 
                     competitively |CITS: [25959928][23665960]| (see also |CITS:
                     [33836147]|).
                     Early studies characterizing <i>narK</i> mutant alleles in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 include |CITS: [7047497][3035558]| and 
                     |CITS: [3049531]|. Physiological studies of <I>narK</I> 
                     deletion mutants implicated the protein in nitrate and 
                     nitrite transport and proposed that NarK functions as a 
                     nitrate:nitrite antiporter |CITS: 
                     [2045360][2668029][11967075]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [15667293][18691156]|). The suggestion that NarK functions 
                     primarily as a nitrite extrusion protein |CITS: [7934881]| 
                     is now thought to be incorrect.
                     NarK consists of an amino terminal domain and a carboxy 
                     terminal domain each containing 6 transmembrane (TM) 
                     helices. NarK contains two nitrogen signature (NS) motifs 
                     located in TM5 and TM11. The substrate translocation 
                     pathway lies at the domain interface and polar residues 
                     lining the central pore form a substrate binding pocket. 
                     The substrate pathway is positively charged and does not 
                     contain protonatable residues suggesting that NarK 
                     functions as a nitrate:nitrite exchanger |CITS: 
                     [23665960]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains a second nitrate transport 
                     protein, |FRAME: NARU-MONOMER NarU|, whose function confers
                     selective advantage during nutrient starvation or slow 
                     growth |CITS: [16804183]|.
                     In the Transporter Classification Database |CITS: 
                     [33170213]| NarK is a member of the Nitrate/Nitrite Porter 
                     (NNP) family within the Major Facilitator Superfamily 
                     (MFS).
                     Related reviews: |CITS:[8412692][11289303][15667293]|"
