     gene            1285698..1286711
                     /gene="rssB"
                     /locus_tag="b1235"
                     /label=rssB
     CDS             1285698..1286711
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rssB"
                     /locus_tag="b1235"
                     /product="regulator of RpoS"
                     /label=rssB
                     /note="RssB is an adaptor protein that facilitates
                     degradation of the alternative sigma factor |FRAME: 
                     RPOS-MONOMER &sigma;<sup>S</sup>| by the protease ClpXP 
                     |CITS: [8635466][8637901]|.  RssB is specific to 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> and has no effect on the stability of 
                     other ClpXP substrates |CITS: [9495753][11238382]|.  In the
                     absence of ClpXP and upon overexpression of RssB, a direct 
                     anti-sigma factor effect of RssB on &sigma;<sup>S</sup> 
                     activity can also be observed |CITS: [9495753][10672187]|. 
                     RssB was also found to modulate polyadenylation and thereby
                     mRNA stability by affecting the activity and intracellular 
                     localization of |FRAME: EG10690-MONOMER| (PAP I) |CITS: 
                     [19767441]|.  During stationary phase, RssB maintains the 
                     association of PAP I with the degradosome |CITS: 
                     [20472786]|.
                     RssB is composed of an N-terminal two-component system 
                     receiver domain and a C-terminal output domain.  Both 
                     domains are required for RssB function |CITS: [11442836]|. 
                     RssB can be phosphorylated at the Asp58 residue within the 
                     receiver domain |CITS: [9515704]|.  The Asp58 
                     phosphorylation site is located at the binding interface 
                     between RssB and &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: [33346011]|. 
                     Acetyl phosphate is an efficient phospho donor for RssB 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and promotes &sigma;<sup>S</sup> 
                     proteolysis <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [9515704]|, but an 
                     additional phosphate donor contributes to RssB 
                     phosphorylation <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [15489452]|.  The 
                     sensor kinase |FRAME: ARCB-MONOMER ArcB| was later shown to
                     phosphorylate RssB <i>in vivo</i>, providing a link between
                     energy starvation conditions and increased levels of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: [16291649]|.
                     While it was shown that phosphorylation stimulates RssB 
                     binding of &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: 
                     [10339606][11238382][11442836]|, surface plasmon resonance 
                     data showed that the acetyl phosphate-phosphorylated 
                     N-terminal domain of RssB interacts more weakly with the R3
                     domain of &sigma;<sup>S</sup> than the unphosphorylated 
                     form <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [33346011]|.  RssB 
                     phosphorylation is not required for regulated cessation of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> degradation following carbon starvation
                     |CITS: [15489452]|.  Absence of |FRAME: EG10690-MONOMER| 
                     appears to enhance RssB-mediated degradation of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> in starved cells |CITS: [16556229]|.
                     Acetyl phosphate-phosphorylated RssB (RssB-P) binds 
                     directly to region 2.5 of &sigma;<sup>S</sup> near Lys173 
                     and to the amino-terminus of ClpX, thus connecting 
                     substrate and protease and allowing degradation |CITS: 
                     [10339606][12912910]|.  &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, RssB-P and 
                     ClpXP form a quarternary complex that degrades 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> and releases RssB |CITS: 
                     [11238382][11442836]|.  The concentration of RssB is 
                     rate-limiting for &sigma;<sup>S</sup> proteolysis |CITS: 
                     [12354235]|.  |FRAME: EG11092-MONOMER Crl| enhances 
                     association of &sigma;<sup>S</sup> with RNA polymerase, 
                     thereby rescuing &sigma;<sup>S</sup> from proteolysis 
                     |CITS: [17332743]|.
                     Solution-state NMR resonance assignments for the N-terminal
                     receiver domain of RssB have been published |CITS: 
                     [31228091]|.  Crystal structures of the N- and C-terminal 
                     domains of RssB have been solved separately |CITS: 
                     [32316259][33599047]|.  The C-terminal effector domain has 
                     a &beta;-sandwich structure and is responsible for 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> binding as well as interaction with the
                     zinc-binding domain of ClpX |CITS: [32316259]|. 
                     Anti-adaptor proteins interact with RssB and interfere with
                     RssB-mediated degradation of &sigma;<sup>S</sup> in 
                     response to different environmental signals: phosphate 
                     starvation is mediated by |FRAME: EG11256-MONOMER IraP| 
                     |CITS: [16600914]|, magnesium starvation by |FRAME: 
                     G6600-MONOMER IraM|, and DNA damage by |FRAME: 
                     G7923-MONOMER IraD| |CITS: [18383615]|.  Each anti-adaptor 
                     protein interacts with RssB in a unique way |CITS: 
                     [24352426][25988182]|.  A crystal structure of the 
                     RssB-IraD complex has been solved at 2 &Aring; resolution; 
                     the data suggests that structural plasticity conferred by 
                     the flexible linker region between the N- and C-terminal 
                     domains of RssB is critical for its regulatory function. 
                     When bound to IraD, RssB assumes a compact conformation. 
                     The D104 residue of RssB is critical for interaction with 
                     IraD and for <i>in vivo</i> sensitivity of RssB to both 
                     IraD and IraP |CITS: [30975721]|.  The binding interface 
                     between IraD and RssB overlaps with the binding interface 
                     between &sigma;<sup>S</sup> and RssB, and IraD outcompetes 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> for RssB binding |CITS: [33346011]|. 
                     Under standard lab conditions, RssB levels are 20-fold 
                     lower than &sigma;<sup>S</sup> levels |CITS: [10672187]|. 
                     RssB levels increase upon entry into stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [10869095]|.  The absolute level of RssB is 
                     extremely low, at only 7-8 molecules per cell |CITS: 
                     [27189321]|.  <i>rssB</i> transcription is 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> dependent; thus, RssB levels depend on 
                     the relative abundance of &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, and the 
                     abundance of &sigma;<sup>S</sup> depends on the amount of 
                     RssB available to facilitate its degradation |CITS: 
                     [11566997][12354235]|.  The transcription factor LrhA 
                     modulates RssB activity, but does not affect RssB synthesis
                     |CITS: [9882671]|.  An extracellular signal that may act 
                     through RssB appears to stabilize &sigma;<sup>S</sup> 
                     |CITS: [18616600]|.
                     <i>rssB</i> mutants show 10-fold increased levels of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> even during exponential growth, and 
                     induction of &sigma;<sup>S</sup> expression by osmotic 
                     stress is impaired |CITS: [8635466]|.  An <i>rssB</i> 
                     mutant has a lower frequency of cell death and displays 
                     enhanced resistance to multiple external stresses, but is 
                     outcompeted by wild type cells in M9 and LB medium |CITS: 
                     [18036141]|.  Anti-adaptor-resistant mutants of <i>rssB</i>
                     have been isolated; a subset of these mutations partially 
                     mimics the effect of RssB phosphorylation |CITS: 
                     [24352426]|.  Deletion of <i>rssB</i> restores the motility
                     of a <i>ycgR</i> mutant |CITS: [31611290]|.  Overexpression
                     of <i>rssB</i> is toxic; deletions of <i>pcnB</i> or 
                     <i>hfq</i> suppress the toxic phenotype |CITS: [19767441]|.
                     Phenotype microarray analysis of an <i>rssB</i> mutant 
                     showed a highly pleiotropic phenotype |CITS: [12897016]|. 
                     SprE: ""<b>s</b>tationary-<b>p</b>hase <b>r</b>egulator 
                     <b>E</b>"" |CITS: [8637901]|<br>RssB: ""<b>r</b>egulator of
                     <b>s</b>igma <b>S</b>"" |CITS: [8635466]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8885260][9830102][12208995][15530384][162 
                     67278][18792681][19765651][19781640][24396271][31197038]|"
