     gene            complement(1290902..1293577)
                     /gene="adhE"
                     /locus_tag="b1241"
                     /label=adhE
     CDS             complement(1290902..1293577)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="adhE"
                     /locus_tag="b1241"
                     /product="multifunctional fused acetaldehyde-CoA
                     dehydrogenase and Fe-dependent alcohol 
                     dehydrogenasealdehyde/alcohol dehydrogenase AdhE / fused 
                     acetaldehyde-CoA dehydrogenase and Fe-dependent alcohol 
                     dehydrogenase / ethanol dehydrogenase / acetaldehyde 
                     dehydrogenase / alcohol dehydrogenase / pyruvate 
                     formate-lyase deactivase"
                     /EC_number="1.2.1.10; 1.1.1.1"
                     /label=adhE
                     /note="Under anaerobic conditions, the multifunctional
                     enzyme AdhE catalyzes the sequential reduction of 
                     acetyl-CoA to acetaldehyde and then to ethanol (see the 
                     |FRAME: FERMENTATION-PWY| pathway). The two catalytic 
                     functions are conferred by its N-terminal aldehyde 
                     dehydrogenase (ALDH) domain and C-terminal iron-dependent 
                     alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) domain.
                     The unusual quarternaty structure of AdhE was noticed very 
                     early; 20-60 subunits are helically arranged to form 
                     rod-like ultrastructures that appear to change conformation
                     in the presence of Fe<sup>2+</sup> and NADH |CITS: 
                     [1325457]|. More recently, cryo-EM structures confirmed the
                     higher-order helical architecture and conformational 
                     changes upon cofactor addition |CITS: 
                     [31586059][32188856][32523125]|. The two active sites are 
                     topologically separated; ALDH active sites face the outer 
                     surface, while ADH active sites face the inner surface 
                     |CITS: [31586059]|. Helical filament formation is essential
                     for AdhE function <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i>; the 
                     extended conformation allows substrate channeling between 
                     the two active sites |CITS: [32188856][32523125]|. Crystal 
                     structures of the apo- and cofactor-bound alcohol 
                     dehydrogenase domain of AdhE have been solved at 1.95 and 
                     1.65 &Aring; resolution, respectively |CITS: [32880589]|. 
                     Using a method that distinguishes N-phosphorylation from 
                     O-phosphorylation, N-phosphorylation was detected <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [33277485]|.
                     AdhE is one of the major proteins damaged by oxidative 
                     stress, and its expression is low under aerobic conditions 
                     |CITS: [9446617]|. However, AdhE mutants that confer the 
                     ability to grow aerobically on ethanol can be selected 
                     |CITS: [10922373]|; their <i>in vivo</i> activity depends 
                     on protection against metal-catalyzed oxidation by the 
                     chaperone DnaK |CITS: [11917132]|. A Glu568Lys substitution
                     identified in the <i>adhC</i> mutant also confers high 
                     activity of the enzyme under aerobic assay conditions 
                     |CITS: [11029424]|. AdhE's aerobic role is uncertain, but 
                     it may protect against oxidative stress, acting as a 
                     hydrogen peroxide scavenger |CITS: [12783863]|. Under 
                     aerobic conditions, AdhE may be protected against 
                     metal-catalyzed oxidation in the presence of copper |CITS: 
                     [18524928]| and heat inactivation |CITS: [19238259]| by the
                     heat shock proteins IbpA and IbpB.
                     AdhE appears to interact directly with the ribosome at the 
                     head of the small subunit, where it may unwind RNA helices 
                     |CITS: [26822933]|. Ribosomes containing the |FRAME: 
                     EG30090 <i>rrsH</i>| variant of the 16S rRNA have enhanced 
                     interactions with AdhE, leading to phenotypic changes 
                     |CITS: [30282032]|.
                     In the pathogenic <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7, AdhE activity 
                     affects the regulation of virulence genes in various ways; 
                     an <i>adhE</i> mutant is significantly less virulent than 
                     wild type |CITS: [24846743]|.
                     The pyruvate formate-lyase deactivase activity of AdhE 
                     described by |CITS: [2015910][1325457]| has been 
                     questioned. |CITS: [17280641]| were unable to reproduce the
                     PFL deactivase activity; they noted that small molecule 
                     thiols can effect deactivation.
                     Expression of <i>adhE</i> is induced under anaerobic |CITS:
                     [2015910][1744060][8423158][8763968]| and microaerobic 
                     |CITS: [12655459]| growth conditions and repressed in the 
                     presence of nitrate |CITS: [1744060]|. The half-maximal 
                     increase (pO<sub>0.5</sub>) for <i>adhE</i> expression is 
                     at 0.8 mbar oxygen |CITS: [9297466]|. <i>adhE</i> 
                     expression is correlated with the NADH:NAD<sup>+</sup> 
                     ratio in the cell |CITS: [8423158][8830700]|. 
                     Transcriptional regulation is complex; expression involves 
                     two promoter regions, is repressed by |FRAME: PD00521 Cra| 
                     |CITS: [9371462]| and NarL in the presence of nitrate 
                     |CITS: [10601216]|, and induced by |FRAME: PD00196 Fis| 
                     |CITS: [10572146][10601216]| and |FRAME: PD00197 FNR| 
                     |CITS: [10601216]|.
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3281| cleavage of the <i>adhE</i> mRNA is 
                     required for translation, perhaps eliminating a secondary 
                     structure that occludes the ribosome binding site |CITS: 
                     [8763968]|. <i>adhE</i> mRNA is a target for degradation by
                     |FRAME: EG11299-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [11380618][12083772][18078441][24317071]|.
                     Mutants overproducing alcohol dehydrogenase are able to use
                     ethanol as the sole source of carbon |CITS: [6986356]|. 
                     Temperature-sensitive mutants have been isolated |CITS: 
                     [6752127][3550385]|. Null mutants are unable to grow 
                     anaerobically with glucose unless an external electron 
                     acceptor such as nitrate is supplied |CITS: 
                     [796666][7033467][2015910]|. An F670E mutant disrupts the 
                     helical superstructure and affects ALDH catalytic activity 
                     more significantly in the direction of acetyl-CoA reduction
                     |CITS: [31586059]|. A &Delta;446-449 mutant that shortens 
                     the linker between the ALDH and ADH domains also prevents 
                     helical filament formation and affects ALDH catalytic 
                     activity, and phenocopies a &Delta;<i>adhE</i> mutant <i>in
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [32188856]|.
                     In the metabolic engineering field, deletion of <i>adhE</i>
                     is a determinant in the production of compounds such as 
                     succinate |CITS: [21040799]|, D-lactate |CITS: [21086129]|,
                     and polyhydroxyalkanoates |CITS: [20535465]|.
                     A protein-enzyme interactions (PEIs) study that examined 
                     the connection between protein-protein interactions (PPIs) 
                     and metabolomics networks studied in 22 different 
                     environmental conditions found that the PEI |FRAME: 
                     EG10901-MONOMER RpsB|-AdhE may have a previously unknown 
                     role in regulating metabolism, in part because the PEI is 
                     conserved across many other bacterial taxa |CITS: 
                     [39903745]|.
                     <i>ana</i>: |CITS: [796666]|<br><i>adhC</i>: 
                     ""<b>a</b>lcohol <b>d</b>e<b>h</b>ydrogenase 
                     <b>c</b>onstitutive mutant"" |CITS: [6986356]|<br>AdhE: 
                     ""<b>a</b>lcohol <b>d</b>e<b>h</b>ydrogenase 
                     <b>e</b>nzyme"" |CITS: [6752127]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [2698228][26443350][26443368]|"
