     gene            1295439..1297070
                     /gene="oppA"
                     /locus_tag="b1243"
                     /label=oppA
     CDS             1295439..1297070
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="oppA"
                     /locus_tag="b1243"
                     /product="bifunctional oligopeptide ABC transporter
                     periplasmic binding protein / oligopeptide ABC transporter"
                     /EC_number="7.4.2.6; 7.4.2.10"
                     /label=oppA
                     /note="OppA is the periplasmic binding protein of an ABC 
                     transporter that mediates the high affinity uptake of 
                     oligopeptides.
                     The crystal structure of OppA complexed with peptide ligand
                     (modeled using a tripeptide) is highly similar to that of 
                     the well characterized <i>S. typhimurium</i> OppA (see 
                     |CITS: [8946852][9245406]|); the ligand is bound in a 
                     closed cavity deep inside the protein; the binding cavity 
                     contains large hydrated pockets believed to allow the 
                     presence of chemically diverse peptide side chains |CITS: 
                     [21983341]|. OppA has a preference for positively charged 
                     tripeptides and tetrapeptides |CITS: [21983341]|. A 
                     periplasmic binding protein (suggested to be OppA) purified
                     from <i>E. coli</i> W binds the tripeptide Ala-Phe-Gly with
                     a K<sub>d</sub> of 0.1 &micro;M and a stoichiometry of 1:1;
                     it does not bind amino acids or dipeptides and tolerates a 
                     wide variety of amino acyl side chains |CITS: 
                     [3897225][3536860]|. OppA from <i>E. coli</i> has 85% 
                     sequence identity with OppA from <i>S. typhimurium</i> and 
                     is a class II, cluster C type substrate binding protein 
                     |CITS: [21983341][20412802]|. <i>oppA</i> shows 
                     differential codon adaptation, resulting in differential 
                     translation efficiency signatures, in thermophilic 
                     microbes. It is therefore predicted to play a role in the 
                     heat shock response. An <i>oppA</i> deletion mutant is more
                     sensitive than wild-type specifically to heat shock, but 
                     not other stresses |CITS: [24580753]|. OppA has 
                     chaperone-like properties; the purified protein increases 
                     the refolding of denatured substrate, protects against 
                     thermal denaturation, and interacts with a model unfolded 
                     protein (reduced carboxymethyl a-lactalbumin) |CITS: 
                     [9188448]|. Overproduction of OppA stabilises a highly 
                     unstable MBP mutant; purified OppA inhibits the aggregation
                     of lactate dehydrogenase and &alpha;-lactalbumin |CITS: 
                     [26003922]|.
                     Resequencing of multiple isolates of the MG1655 strain has 
                     identified several genetic variations compared to the 
                     reference sequence, including an IS5 insertion in the 
                     region upstream of <i>oppA</i> which may alter its 
                     regulation |CITS: [22081388]|.  <a href=""http://ecoliwiki 
                     .net/colipedia/index.php/Category:Strain:MG1655#Resequencin
                     g_of_MG1655_and_comparison_to_the_reference_strain"">This 
                     EcoliWiki page</a> summarizes these sequence differences. 
                     The <i>oppA</i> mutation known as <i>oppA462</i>, initially
                     reported to be a non-polar mutation |CITS: [3301822]| was 
                     later shown to be a polar mutation |CITS: [9495761]|."
