     gene            complement(1301442..1302902)
                     /gene="clsA"
                     /locus_tag="b1249"
                     /label=clsA
     CDS             complement(1301442..1302902)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="clsA"
                     /locus_tag="b1249"
                     /product="bifunctional cardiolipin synthase A /
                     cardiolipin synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.8.41"
                     /label=clsA
                     /note="ClsA is the major cardiolipin synthase in <i>E.
                     coli</i>. Synthesis of cardiolipin during exponential 
                     growth is due to the activity of ClsA, while all three 
                     cardiolipin synthases, ClsA, |FRAME: G6406-MONOMER ClsB| 
                     and |FRAME: G6551-MONOMER ClsC|, contribute to cardiolipin 
                     synthesis in stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [353047][2982784][2828323][22988102]|.
                     The ClsA protein appears to undergo posttranslational 
                     processing at the N terminus |CITS: 
                     [10634942][19341704][29280215]|. A &Delta;2-60 deletion 
                     mutant retains activity and membrane association |CITS: 
                     [10634942][19341704]|. The catalytic domain of ClsA is 
                     located in the cytoplasm; ClsA mainly localizes to the cell
                     poles; localization is cardiolipin-dependent |CITS: 
                     [29280215]|. ClsA is predicted to contain two N-terminal 
                     transmembrane domains followed by a soluble C-terminal 
                     globular domain; ClsA is a dual-topology protein which 
                     retains or flips its catalytic domain to supply cardiolipin
                     on the desired leaflet of the inner membrane; ClsA 
                     temporarily adopts an ""inverted"" topology (the catalytic 
                     domain is on the periplasmic side of the inner membrane) in
                     cells subject to osmotic down-shock |CITS: [39752486]|.
                     A <i>clsA</i> mutant is resistant to 
                     <i>rac</i>-3,4-dihydroxybutyl 1-phosphonate (DBP) |CITS: 
                     [6321438]|. A <i>clsA</i> mutant has increased sensitivity 
                     to novobiocin |CITS: [7665497]|, but is highly resistant to
                     killing by sphingosine |CITS: [32327486]|. <i>clsA</i> 
                     mutations are recovered in strains adapted to 
                     freeze-thaw-growth cycles |CITS: [18757947]|. Cells with 
                     deletions of all three of the genes encoding cardiolipin 
                     synthases, &Delta;<i>clsABC</i>, completely lack 
                     cardiolipin |CITS: [22988102]|. Upon overexpression of AtpF
                     (as well as other membrane proteins), formation of 
                     intracellular membranes (ICMs) is observed; the inverted 
                     hexagonal phase of ICMs is absent in a &Delta;<i>clsABC</i>
                     mutant, and thus requires cardiolipin |CITS: [28284722]|. 
                     The effect of deletion and overexpression of <i>clsA</i> on
                     lipid class distribution has been measured |CITS: 
                     [29933046]|. The mutant combinations <i>clsA</i> and 
                     <i>lpxM</i> |CITS: [33833055]| as well as <i>clsA</i> and 
                     <i>waaA</i> and <i>clsA</i> and <i>lpxL</i> show synthetic 
                     lethality; <i>clsA</i> is also essential for growth of a 
                     &Delta;<i>lpxLMP</i> strain in minimal media at 30&deg;C 
                     |CITS: [34065855]|.
                     Expression of <i>clsA</i> is osmoregulated |CITS: 
                     [17504273]|.
                     ClsA: ""<b>c</b>ardio<b>l</b>ipin <b>s</b>ynthase"" |CITS: 
                     [353047]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9370333][19539601]|"
