     gene            1305346..1306065
                     /gene="tonB"
                     /locus_tag="b1252"
                     /label=tonB
     CDS             1305346..1306065
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tonB"
                     /locus_tag="b1252"
                     /product="multifunctional Ton complex subunit TonB /
                     energy transducing Ton complex / pyrroloquinoline quinone 
                     outer membrane transport complex / ferric catecholate outer
                     membrane transport complex II / ferric catecholate outer 
                     membrane transport complex I / ferric catecholate outer 
                     membrane transporter / ferric coprogen outer membrane 
                     transport complex / transport of ferric coprogen / ferric 
                     citrate outer membrane transport complex / transport of 
                     ferric citrate / ferrichrome outer membrane transport 
                     complex / transport of ferrichrome / ferric enterobactin 
                     outer membrane transport complex / ferric enterobactin 
                     outer membrane transporter / cobalamin outer membrane 
                     transport complex"
                     /label=tonB
                     /note="TonB is a component of the energy transducing Ton
                     system which functions to harness the energy of the proton 
                     motive force (pmf) at the cytoplasmic membrane to support 
                     active transport of iron-siderophore complexes and vitamin 
                     B12 across the outer membrane.
                     Early studies characterized <i>tonB</i> mutants which were 
                     defective in iron and Vitamin B12 transport, resistant to 
                     various phages and colicins and resistant to the 
                     sideromycin, albomycin |CITS: 
                     [4892368][126991][135755][124727][803957]| and suggested 
                     that TonB function couples the energized state of the inner
                     membrane to the binding and translocation processes of 
                     outer membrane proteins |CITS: [128553][353030][6768753]|.
                     The TonB protein is predicted to anchor to the inner 
                     membrane by its amino terminus while the bulk of the 
                     protein extends into the periplasmic space (|CITS: 
                     [6310567]|, see |CITS: [2150975]|). TonB contains an 
                     N-terminal, membrane anchoring domain (residues 1-33), an 
                     intermediate periplasmic domain (residues 34-154) and a 
                     C-terminal domain that interacts with TonB dependent outer 
                     membrane transporters |CITS: [16741125][18390658]|. The 
                     intermediate domain contains multiple Glu-Pro and Lys-Pro 
                     dipeptide repeats and adopts a rigid, elongated 
                     conformation |CITS: [2266560]|. TonB lacking the repeat 
                     motif (TonB&Delta;66-100) can interact with ExbB and FepA 
                     and appears to be functional in energy transduction |CITS: 
                     [7934870]|. TonB localizes with both the inner membrane and
                     outer membranes in approximately equal amounts; the 
                     C-terminus is required for association with the outer 
                     membrane |CITS: [9159515]|
                     TonB forms a complex with the inner membrane proteins, ExbB
                     and ExbD |CITS: [8631671][25802296][27654919]|. TonB 
                     interacts with ExbD; the detection of cross-links between 
                     TonB and ExbD requires PMF; TonB and ExbD interact via 
                     their periplasmic domains |CITS: [19627500][21984795]|. 
                     TonB contains a ""D-box"" motif which binds to the ExbD 
                     periplasmic domain; this interaction is suggested to be 
                     critical for force transduction between the membranes 
                     |CITS: [38311172]|.
                     TonB binds to a conserved region in its partner outer 
                     membrane proteins called the Ton box |CITS: 
                     [1729219][10485884][12618451][15644214][16741124]|. TonB 
                     interacts with BtuB |CITS: [3294107][2193917]|. Purified 
                     TonB (residues 147-239) binds to approximately one-half of 
                     the periplasmic surface of BtuB in a 1:1 stoichiometry 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [16741124]|. Purified TonB (lacking 
                     its transmembrane domain) binds to the outer membrane 
                     transporters BtuB and FhuA; mutations in the BtuB Ton box 
                     disrupt binding and impair transport |CITS: 
                     [11029413][23517233]|.
                     TonB interacts with FhuA |CITS: [2253788][2549378]|. 
                     Purified TonB (residues 33-239) binds to FhuA in a 1:1 
                     stoichiometry; an interprotein &beta; sheet forms between 
                     the FhuA Ton box and a central &beta; sheet of the TonB 
                     C-terminal domain |CITS: [16741125]|. Purified TonB and 
                     FhuA form stable 2:1 complexes; FhuA-TonB interactions are 
                     multiple and TonB may contain two distinct binding regions 
                     for FhuA |CITS: [14668326][15736954][16414071]|. TonB 
                     binding induces conformational changes in the 
                     extraccellular loops of the outer-membrane receptor FhuA 
                     |CITS: [18653801]|.
                     TonB interacts with FecA; this interaction does not require
                     the PMF and is enhanced in the presence of substrate |CITS:
                     [12618451]|.
                     Various constructs of TonB have been detected as monomers 
                     and dimers |CITS: 
                     [11328822][13129945][14668326][21179522][23517233]|. The 
                     energy transduction cycle may involve transition of TonB 
                     through distinct conformations |CITS: [10209752]|. TonB 
                     activity is unaffected when it's amino terminus is trapped 
                     as a homodimer (through disulfide cross-linking) but when 
                     the carboxy terminus is trapped as a homodimer TonB is 
                     inactive and fractionates almost solely with the inner 
                     membrane |CITS: [26283773]|. GFP-TonB fusions localize to 
                     the central region of the cell envelope and are largely 
                     absent from the cell poles; TonB is a dynamic protein; PMF 
                     drives GFP-TonB motion (likely rotational); deletion of 
                     <i>exbBD</i> compromises the connection between PMF and 
                     TonB motion |CITS: [23798405]|.
                     TonB interacts with the ferrichrome periplasmic binding 
                     protein FhuD; this interaction has a 1:1 stoichiometry 
                     <i>in vitro</i>; addition of FhuD to a TonB-FhuA complex 
                     results in formation of a trimeric complex <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [16928679]|. TonB interacts with the cyanocobalamin 
                     periplasmic binding protein, BtuF <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [19708689]|.
                     <i>ton</i>: <U>T-on</U>e |CITS: [17247100]| (as cited in 
                     |CITS: [2150975]|)
                     Reviews: |CITS: [6210573]|"
