     gene            complement(1310113..1310292)
                     /gene="yciG"
                     /locus_tag="b1259"
                     /label=yciG
     CDS             complement(1310113..1310292)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yciG"
                     /locus_tag="b1259"
                     /product="stress-induced bacterial acidophilic repeat 
                     motifs-containing protein YciG"
                     /label=yciG
                     /note="This |FRAME: EG11127| gene and its paralog, |FRAME: 
                     G0-10443|, is homologous to gene STM14_1829 in |FRAME: 
                     TAX-90371| 14028s that belongs to a family of proteins 
                     involved in motility |CITS: [30373755]|. These two genes 
                     also show homology to gene PA2146 in |FRAME: TAX-208964|, 
                     shown to contribute to both biofilm formation and 
                     susceptibility to a variety of antimicrobial agents |CITS: 
                     [35798749]|.
                     Both of these genes are small, intrinsically disordered 
                     proteins and have KGG-containing domains (called 
                     stress-induced bacterial acidophilic repeat motif) 
                     associated with proteins expressed under stressful 
                     conditions. <i>yciF</i> contains a Walker nucleotide 
                     binding motif that is absent in <i>ymdF</i>; the functional
                     significance of this difference was not examined |CITS: 
                     [30373755][35798749]|.
                     Deletion of both <i>yciG</i> and <i>ymdF</i> together 
                     exhibited a slight swimming motility defect |CITS: 
                     [30373755]|.
                     Transcription of <i>yciG</i> is induced by sulfometuron 
                     methyl, an herbicide that inhibits acetolactate synthase, 
                     by salicylate |CITS: [9473030]|, and by high salt 
                     conditions |CITS: [15716429]|.  The <i>yciG</i> gene is 
                     part of the &sigma;<sup>S</sup> regulon |CITS: 
                     [9473030][15716429]|. The <i>yciGFE</i> locus is expressed 
                     poorly |CITS: [20713450]|.
                     Transcription of <i>yciG</i> has been used as a reporter 
                     for analyzing metabolic shifts in response to substrate 
                     perturbations |CITS: 
                     [22209969][22370947][23124973][23376621]|.
                     "
                     /translation="MAEHRGGSGNFAEDREKASDAGRKGGQHSGGNFKNDPQRASEAGK
                     KGGQQSGGNKSGKS"
                     /go_component="cytosol [goid 0005829] [evidence IEA] [PMID
                     19054114]"
                     /go_process="bacterial-type flagellum-dependent swarming
                     motility [goid 0071978] [evidence IMP] [PMID 17122336]"
     gene            complement(1310673..1311479)
                     /gene="trpA"
                     /locus_tag="b1260"
                     /label=trpA
     CDS             complement(1310673..1311479)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="trpA"
                     /locus_tag="b1260"
                     /product="trifunctional tryptophan synthase subunit alpha
                     / indoleglycerol phosphate aldolase / tryptophan synthase"
                     /EC_number="4.2.1.20; 4.1.2.8"
                     /label=trpA
                     /note="The TrpA polypeptide (TSase &alpha;) functions as
                     the &alpha; subunit of the tetrameric 
                     (&alpha;<sub>2</sub>-&beta;<sub>2</sub>) tryptophan 
                     synthase complex |CITS: [330534]|. As a purified protein, 
                     the &alpha; subunit is a monomer. TSase &alpha; contains 
                     the binding site for indole-3-glycerol-phosphate (InGP) and
                     can carry out the cleavage reaction of InGP to indole and 
                     glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, also termed the &alpha; 
                     reaction. Within the physiological complex with the &beta; 
                     subunit, the reaction rate is increased by 1-2 orders of 
                     magnitude (in |CITS: [1899027]|).
                     TrpA has been shown to lack tryptophan residues |CITS: 
                     [13906502]|. Numerous TrpA mutant studies have examined 
                     structure-function relationships in this protein. Mutations
                     that affect catalytic activity |CITS: [8795043] [7777488] 
                     [1939081] [3023357] [2888759] [8662916] [8463331] 
                     [2001993]|, subunit interactions |CITS: [1603055] 
                     [2001993]|, conformational stability |CITS: [8795042] 
                     [1727648]| and folding |CITS: [11716512] [2001993]| have 
                     been identified.
                     The crystal structure of the wild-type TrpA protein has 
                     been reported at 2.8 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [14684907] 
                     [15451433]|, 2.5 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [15879705]| and 
                     2.3 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [15667212]|. The crystal 
                     structure of a double mutant TrpA protein has been reported
                     at 1.8 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [15451433] [15879705]|.
                     The TrpA protein has been structurally classified as a 
                     (beta alpha)(8) TIM barrel protein, a member of the common 
                     TIM barrel superfamily.  Nuclear magnetic resonance 
                     spectroscopic techniques have been used to investigate its 
                     equilibrium folding mechanism in order to obtain insights 
                     into the development of structure and stability |CITS: 
                     [18234216]|.  Many previous studies of the folding 
                     mechanism of recombinant wild-type and mutant TrpA proteins
                     using various biophysical techniques identified 
                     intermediates in the folding pathway, for example  |CITS: 
                     [2872918] [7499309][10452606] [12480516] [15784252] 
                     [16023136] [17222865]|."
                     /translation="MERYESLFAQLKERKEGAFVPFVTLGDPGIEQSLKIIDTLIEAGA
                     DALELGIPFSDPLADGPTIQNATLRAFAAGVTPAQCFEMLALIRQKHPTIPIGLLMYAN
