     gene            1317477..1318358
                     /gene="rnm"
                     /locus_tag="b1266"
                     /label=rnm
     CDS             1317477..1318358
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rnm"
                     /locus_tag="b1266"
                     /product="multifunctional RNase AM / 5' -> 3'
                     exoribonuclease / 3',5'-nucleoside bisphosphate phosphatase
                     / 5'-FAD-capped RNA FAD-hydrolase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.3.97"
                     /label=rnm
                     /note="RNase AM catalyzes the final maturation of the 5'
                     ends of 5S, 16S and 23S rRNA. The enzyme exonucleolytically
                     cleaves the final three nucleotides from the 5' ends of all
                     three rRNAs; maturation takes place more accurately and 
                     efficiently in the context of the ribosome. 5' end 
                     processing of 23S rRNA is dependent on prior 3' end 
                     processing |CITS: [32343306]|. This enzyme also is capable 
                     of removing FAD cap from 5' end of RNA molecules, referred 
                     to as ""deFADding"", at comparable rates <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [35904778]|.
                     RNase AM is a metal-dependent 5' &rarr; 3' exonuclease with
                     a preference for short RNA oligonucleotides. Substrates 
                     must be phosphorylated at the 5'-end of the 
                     oligonucleotide; the enzyme does not hydrolyze 
                     oligonucleotides that are only phosphorylated at the 3' 
                     terminus. The enzyme hydrolyzes ssDNA substrates, but rates
                     of catalysis are generally lower; dsDNA is a poor 
                     substrate. Purified RNase AM also hydrolyzes the 3' 
                     phosphate from 3'-5' bisphosphonucleotides |CITS: 
                     [25871919]|.
                     Crystal structure has been obtained 3 manganese ions and a 
                     sulfate molecule in the active site, providing insight into
                     the hydrolysis reaction. Mutations of the several active 
                     site residues, including Mn-associated residues, disrupted 
                     the deFADding and 5'P hydrolysis activities |CITS: 
                     [35904778]|.
                     A down mutation in the <i>yciV</i> ribosome binding site 
                     increases fitness for growth on 18-20% cellulosic 
                     hydrolysate |CITS: [22689973]|.
                     Rnm: ""<b>RN</b>ase A<b>M</b>""
                     Review: |CITS: [30149858][35787606]|"
