     gene            1325305..1327902
                     /gene="topA"
                     /locus_tag="b1274"
                     /label=topA
     CDS             1325305..1327902
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="topA"
                     /locus_tag="b1274"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA topoisomerase I /
                     topoisomerase"
                     /EC_number="5.6.2.1"
                     /label=topA
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> possess multiple DNA topoisomerases
                     which coordinate, as well as compete, in order to maintain 
                     chromosomes in the appropriate topological state 
                     commensurate with their need for replication and 
                     transcription. They comprise the type IA enzymes DNA 
                     topoisomerase I (Topo I) and DNA topoisomerase III, which 
                     are encoded by the |FRAME: EG11013| and |FRAME: EG11014| 
                     genes, respectively, and the type II enzymes |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-2424| and |FRAME: CPLX0-2425|.
                     Type I topoisomerases function by causing single-strand DNA
                     breaks, while type II topoisomerases cause double-strand 
                     DNA breaks. DNA topoisomerase I, referred to initially as 
                     protein omega (&omega;), was the first known topoisomerase 
                     to be purified from <i>E. coli</i> (strain 1100) |CITS: 
                     [4927945]|.
                     Topoisomerase I interacts with RNA polymerase |CITS: 
                     [12788950][35927234]| and relaxes negative supercoiling 
                     generated behind moving RNA polymerases during 
                     transcription |CITS: [10347233]|. Deletion of topoisomerase
                     I causes excess negative supercoiling of DNA and the 
                     formation of R-loops (three-stranded DNA-RNA hybrids that 
                     consist of two antiparallel DNA strands plus one RNA 
                     strand) that can inhibit gene expression and activates 
                     R-loop-dependent replication |CITS: 
                     [7536935][8294458][10347234]|. The mutant phenotype can be 
                     suppressed by mutations in <i>gyrA</i> or <i>gyrB</i>, 
                     causing a reduction in DNA gyrase activity |CITS: 
                     [6297751][6297752][2987184]|, and by overexpression of 
                     RNase H |CITS: [7536935]|. Topo I has a slight preference 
                     for binding negatively supercoiled DNA |CITS: [35420137]| 
                     and also relaxes local transcription-induced negative 
                     supercoiling |CITS: [10347233]|. It interacts directly with
                     the beta' subunit of RNA polymerase via its C-terminal zinc
                     ribbon domain |CITS: [12788950]|. Upon inhibition by 
                     rifampicin, Topo I relocates to promoter regions along with
                     RNA polymerase; uncoupling of the RNA polymerase:Topo I 
                     interaction results in accumulation of R-loops |CITS: 
                     [35927234]|.
                     Topoisomerase I was thought to be essential for growth in 
                     <i>E. coli</i>; excess negative supercoiling that results 
                     from mutations in the <i>topA</i> gene is thought to 
                     promote the formation of R-loops during transcription, 
                     inhibiting RNA synthesis and causing the growth defect 
                     |CITS: [15458416]|. However, a study showed that strains 
                     containing temperature-sensitive <i>topA</i> mutant alleles
                     are viable at the restrictive conditions. These strains, as
                     well as a <i>topA</i> deletion strain, are not viable at 30
                     degrees C |CITS: [15522872]|. An <i>E. coli</i> strain with
                     mutations in both <i>topA</i> and <i>topB</i>, lacking both
                     type IA topoisomerases, is not viable |CITS: 
                     [11493711][15522872]|. Even though topoisomerase I shows 
                     preference for relaxation of negative supercoiling and 
                     topoisomerase III for decatenation, the two enzyme can act 
                     as backups for each other. Mutated topoisomerase I that is 
                     able to cleave but not subsequently separate from DNA is 
                     lethal when overexpressed |CITS: [16159875]|.
                     Type IA topoisomerases play major roles at the termination 
                     step of replication when convergent replication forks 
                     merge. In the absence of both type IA topoisomerases 
                     unregulated replication involving RNA polymerase 
                     backtracking occurs from R-loops, leading to DNA 
                     amplification in the chromosome terminus region because the
                     replication forks are blocked at termination barriers and 
                     leading to overproduction of topo IV due to amplifying 
                     chromosomal region with <i>parC</i> and <i>parE</i> genes 
                     |CITS: [30222737]|. Inhibition of R-loop-dependent 
                     replication by topoisomerase I does not require its 
                     C-terminal-mediated interaction with RNA polymerase |CITS: 
                     [37141391]|. A quantitative model shows that the activities
                     of Topo I and DNA gyrase best explains <i>in vivo</i> 
                     experimental results of the impact of gene context on 
                     regulation of gene expression |CITS: [37667073]|.
                     Crystal and solution structures of the amino terminal 
                     domain of topoisomerase I have been presented |CITS: 
                     [8114910][7779808][14604525][10504732][10504724]|. 
                     Transcription of the <i>topA</i> gene is regulated by the 
                     level of DNA supercoiling |CITS: [2845101]| and growth 
                     conditions |CITS: [9126832]|. <i>topA</i> transcription is 
                     increased specifically in response to |FRAME: CPD-9138| and
                     its indicator compounds |FRAME: 13-DINITROBENZENE|, |FRAME:
                     CPD-9132|, and |FRAME: CPD-9133|; the response was utilized
                     for construction of an <i>E. coli</i> biosensor strain 
                     |CITS: [25561288]|.
                     RstA interacts with TopA weakly <i>in vivo</i> and 
                     increases its activity related to relaxing the negative 
                     supercoiled plasmid carrying the <i>rstA</i> gene in the 
                     study |CITS:[30011323]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [9263411][32120891][39520813]|"
