     gene            1330088..1332763
                     /gene="acnA"
                     /locus_tag="b1276"
                     /label=acnA
     CDS             1330088..1332763
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="acnA"
                     /locus_tag="b1276"
                     /product="multifunctional aconitate hydratase 1 /
                     aconitate hydratase A / D-threo-isocitrate hydro-lyase 
                     (cis-aconitate-forming) / citrate hydro-lyase 
                     (cis-aconitate-forming)"
                     /label=acnA
                     /note="There are two aconitases in <i>E. coli</i>, both of
                     which catalyze the reversible isomerization of citrate and 
                     iso-citrate via <I>cis</I>-aconitate.   The apo form of 
                     AcnA is able to bind mRNA and enhances translation
                     of AcnA |CITS: [10589714]|.
                     The AcnA enzyme is more stable, has a higher affinity for 
                     citrate and is active over a wider pH range than
                     the AcnB enzyme |CITS: [10585860][12486059]|.  Unlike AcnB,
                     AcnA is resistant to oxidation <i>in vivo</i>
                     |CITS: [12486059]|.  The main role of the AcnA enzyme 
                     appears to be as a maintenance or survival enzyme
                     during nutritional or oxidative stress, while the AcnB 
                     enzyme appears to function as the main catabolic
                     enzyme |CITS: [9421904]|.
                     The catalytically inactive AcnA apo-protein, lacking its 
                     iron-sulfur cluster, is able to bind mRNA and
                     enhances translation of AcnA |CITS: [10589714]| and has a 
                     positive effect on SodA synthesis
                     |CITS: [11932448]|.
                     Cells grown in the presence of nitrate contain lower levels
                     of aconitase and fumarase |CITS: [1838390]|. Synthesis of 
                     AcnA is subject to catabolite and anaerobic repression 
                     |CITS: [8000525]| and is induced by
                     salt stress under aerobic conditions |CITS: [17015655]|. 
                     <i>acnA</i> expression is induced under oxidative
                     stress |CITS: [15575693][15672380]|.  Expression of AcnA is
                     posttranscriptionally downregulated in
                     response to low iron levels by the small RNA RyhB |CITS: 
                     [11917098]|.
                     An <i>acnA</i> mutant is more susceptible to pyrazinoic 
                     acid than wild type |CITS: [12076823]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9020582][12732309]|
                     "
