     gene            complement(1339014..1340999)
                     /gene="pdeR"
                     /locus_tag="b1285"
                     /label=pdeR
     CDS             complement(1339014..1340999)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pdeR"
                     /locus_tag="b1285"
                     /product="bifunctional cyclic di-GMP phosphodiesterase
                     PdeR / c-di-GMP phosphodiesterase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.4.52"
                     /label=pdeR
                     /note="Curli amyloid fibers are thin, coiled surface
                     structures composed of a polymer of a single type of 
                     subunit, |FRAME: EG11489-MONOMER|, encoded by the |FRAME: 
                     EG11489| gene |CITS: [2649795][8459772]|. Curli are the key
                     component of the extracellular matrix produced during 
                     biofilm formation by <i>Escherichia coli</i> and related 
                     <i>Enterobacteriaceae</i> and are involved in inert surface
                     colonization, biofilm formation, and bacterial binding to a
                     variety of extracellular matrix and serum proteins |CITS: 
                     [1357528][9457880][11823641][12819107][30305312]|.
                     Curli production is controlled by a complex signaling 
                     cascade, with the |FRAME: EG12396 DgcE|/|FRAME: EG12252 
                     PdeH| diguanylate cyclase (DGC)/phosphodiesterase (PDE) 
                     pair acting upstream of |FRAME: G6639 PdeR|, which itself 
                     is upstream of the DGC |FRAME: G6673 DgcM|, and all acting 
                     upstream of the |FRAME: EG12008 ""MlrA transcription 
                     factor""|. MlrA controls expression of |FRAME: G6546 ""CsgD
                     transcriptional regulator""|, which controls the 
                     transcription of the |FRAME: TU00390| operon, encoding 
                     curli production |CITS: [23708798]|. The |FRAME: 
                     G6639-MONOMER PdeR| phosphodiesterase inhibits DgcM; this 
                     inhibition is relieved when PdeR binds and hydrolyzes 
                     |FRAME: C-DI-GMP| (c-di-GMP), allowing DgcM to activate the
                     transcription factor |FRAME: EG12008-MONOMER MlrA|. 
                     Production of c-di-GMP by DgcM contributes to, but is not 
                     essential for MlrA activation by DgcM |CITS: 
                     [23708798][30954572]|.
                     Curli expression is heterogeneous - it does not occur 
                     within all cells that occupy the same position within a 
                     biofilm |CITS: [23359678][23512962][27761132][30954572]|. 
                     This bimodal expression was also observed in a well-stirred
                     planktonic culture, indicating that it is not due to 
                     microenvironmental gradients |CITS: [37186613]|. Removal of
                     the regulatory network does not abolish the bimodality of 
                     curli gene expression |CITS: [37186613]|. PdeR does not 
                     play a global regulatory role; <i>csgD</i> transcription 
                     may be its only target. PdeR contains PAS, GGDEF, and EAL 
                     domains and displays c-di-GMP phosphodiesterase activity 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [17010156]|. PdeR interacts directly
                     with DgcM and MlrA, primarily via its C-terminal EAL 
                     domain. The EAL domain is also required for transmitting 
                     information from the DgcE/PdeH system to DgcM/MlrA. The 
                     protein binds GTP via its GGDEF domain |CITS: [23708798]|.
                     A <i>pdeR</i> mutant shows enhanced Congo red binding and 
                     enhanced sedimentation of stationary phase cells, 
                     indicating a change in various outer membrane and surface 
                     properties. Curli-shaped fibers are overproduced. 
                     Transcription of <i>csgD</i>, which controls production of 
                     curli, is elevated in a <i>pdeR</i> mutant at 28&deg;C 
                     |CITS: [17010156]|. In contrast, an EAL domain point mutant
                     of <i>pdeR</i> reduces <i>csgB</i> (and thus curli) 
                     expression in a wild type background and loses sensitivity 
                     to mutations in <i>dgcE</i> and <i>pdeH</i> |CITS: 
                     [23708798]|.
                     A <i>pdeR</i> deletion mutant contains slightly higher 
                     levels of cytoplasmic c-di-GMP |CITS: [22519888]|. 
                     Expression of <i>pdeR</i> is increased during the 
                     transition to stationary phase and is dependent on 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> under a number of stress conditions 
                     |CITS: [17010156][19332833]|. H-NS plays a role in 
                     regulating <i>pdeR</i> expression |CITS: [17010156]|. At 
                     28&deg;C, <i>pdeR</i> expression is higher on solid medium 
                     than in liquid medium |CITS: [19332833]|.
                     A <i>pdeR</i> mutant exhibits 3-fold elevated RNase II 
                     abundance as a result of decreased protein turnover, 
                     compared to wild type, and thus lacks the regulation of 
                     RNase II abundance that is observed in wild type in 
                     response to changes in growth environment |CITS: 
                     [11260472]|.
                     During swarming, expression of <i>|pdeR</i> and <i>pdeO</i>
                     (<i>|FRAME: G6783|</i>) were significantly upregulated, 
                     however deletion of either did not effect either swimming 
                     or swarming |CITS: [40326769]|.
                     Gmr: ""<b>g</b>ene <b>m</b>odulating <b>R</b>Nase II"" 
                     |CITS: [11260472]|
                     PdeR: ""<b>p</b>hospho<b>d</b>i<b>e</b>sterase <b>R</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [26148715]|
                     Comment: |CITS: [23749210]|
                     Review: |CITS: [27672149]|"
