     gene            complement(1341235..1343169)
                     /gene="rnb"
                     /locus_tag="b1286"
                     /label=rnb
     CDS             complement(1341235..1343169)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rnb"
                     /locus_tag="b1286"
                     /product="bifunctional RNase II / exoribonuclease II"
                     /EC_number="3.1.13.4; 3.1.13.1"
                     /label=rnb
                     /note="Ribonuclease II (RNase II) is a
                     single-strand-specific exoribonuclease that cleaves RNA 
                     processively from the 3' end to produce ribonucleoside 
                     5'-monophosphates. It has roles in the maturation, turnover
                     and quality control of different RNA types.
                     RNase II is one of six exonucleases (RNase II, RNase D, 
                     RNase BN, RNase T, RNase PH and PNPase) that can process 
                     tRNA and is responsible for the first step in the 
                     conversion of precursor tyrosine tRNA to its final form 
                     |CITS: [8422961][320007]|. It has been shown <i>in 
                     vitro</i> to cleave long 3' tRNA sequences to yield 2-4 
                     nucleotide intermediates for subsequent processing |CITS: 
                     [7509797]|. Mutant analyses found RNase II plays only a 
                     minor exoribonuclease role in removing 3-4 nucleotides of 
                     the 3' Rho-independent transcription terminator for |FRAME:
                     leuX-tRNA|, with PNPase being the primary enzyme for with 
                     role in |FRAME: PWY0-1614| |CITS: [19906695]|. RNase II, 
                     along with RNase PH and PNPase, exonucleolytically remove 
                     3' tRNA sequences with and without poly(A) tails in the 
                     |FRAME: PWY0-1623| following initial cleavage by |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3281| and in |FRAME: PWY0-1627| following initial 
                     cleavage by |FRAME: CPLX0-1382| |CITS: 
                     [17981836][35652235]|. See |FRAME: PWY0-1628| and |FRAME: 
                     PWY0-1625| as well as individual tRNA processing pathways.
                     It also degrades the polycistronic tryptophan operon mRNA 
                     from its primary transcription termination site (trp t') to
                     a secondary termination hairpin (trp t) to yield the mature
                     3' end of tryptophan operon mRNA |CITS: [2992951]|. RNase 
                     II is responsible for a significant portion of the 
                     degradation of artificial hammerhead ribozymes <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [8626292]|. RNase II also contributes to 
                     the degradation of smaller RNA during carbon starvation, 
                     though it has no role in 23 S or 16 S RNA breakdown |CITS: 
                     [4358625][1091648]|. In concert with PNPase, RNase II 
                     degrades the major 3' cleavage product of the antisense RNA
                     CopA, though it can also protect the 3' end from PNPase 
                     degradation |CITS: [9695924]|. Temperature-sensitive double
                     mutants in RNase II and PNPase are inviable at the 
                     nonpermissive temperature and accumulate mRNA fragments of 
                     100 to 1,500 nucleotides in length |CITS: [2417233]|. 
                     Triple mutants in RNase II, PNPase and <i>ams</i> (RNase E)
                     show a three- to four-fold increase in the half life of 
                     pulse-labeled RNA including mRNAs |CITS: [2459106]|. RNase 
                     II degrades <i>rps</i>O mRNA following removal of the 
                     transcript's 3' stem-loop structure by RNase E, but 
                     protects it from degradation by other factors if the 3' 
                     stem-loop is present |CITS: [7519147]|.
                     RNase II also plays a role in bacteriophage T4 ribonucleic 
                     acid metabolism |CITS: [6989808]|.
                     RNase II has a major role in degradation of Rep mRNA from 
                     the ColE2 plasmid |CITS: [18191205]|. Plasmid-borne RNase 
                     II can complement the cold-sensitive phenotype of a 
                     &Delta;<i>pnp</i> mutant |CITS: [18606734]|. RNase II has a
                     role in the A-site mRNA cleavage process during ribosome 
                     pausing, degrading ribosome-bound mRNA downstream of the 
                     ribosomal A-site |CITS: [19627501]|. However, RNase II 
                     activity does not accelerate ribosome recycling |CITS: 
                     [24260569]|. Overexpression of RNase II or RNase R can 
                     suppress the growth defects of cells lacking the 
                     phosphorolytic RNases PNPase and RNase PH |CITS: 
                     [19617368]|. RNase II has a role in processing the 
                     downstream 3' sequences of <i>leuX</i> precursor tRNA after
                     the action of PNPase or RNase P |CITS: 
                     [19906695][22287637]|.
                     RNase II degrades the poly(A) tails of mRNA. In triple 
                     mutants lacking RNase II, PNPase and RNase E, the length 
                     and abundance of mRNA poly(A) tails increases dramatically 
                     |CITS: [7534403]|. Double mutants in RNase II and PNPase 
                     show a twenty- to sixty-fold increase in appearance of 
                     poly(A) RNA and a three- to four-fold increase in the 
                     length of poly(A) tails relative to wild type, but less 
                     than a two-fold change in non-poly(A) RNA levels. poly(A) 
                     synthesis is also higher in mutant cells, indicating that 
                     the exonucleases tested both degrade polyadenylated mRNA 
                     and reduce its rate of synthesis |CITS: [9242986]|. RNase 
                     II removes poly(A) tails from <i>rps</i>O mRNA. The 
                     resulting deadenylated <i>rps</i>O mRNA is more stable than
                     oligoadenylated <i>rps</i>O mRNA |CITS: [10943897]|. RNase 
                     II is responsible for most degradation of poly(A) tails 
                     associated with 23 S rRNA and can be overexpressed to 
                     prevent toxicity due to excessive polyadenylation, which 
                     decreases the half-life of mRNA |CITS: 
                     [10844684][10594833]|. Hfq blocks the degradation of 
                     poly(A) tails by RNase II |CITS: [14654705]|. 
                     Polyadenylation of the mRNA for the ribosomal protein S20 
                     allows RNase II and PNPase to degrade it |CITS: [8663115]|.
                     RNase II catalyzes the direct removal of ribonucleoside 
                     5'-monophosphates from the 3' end of oligoribonucleotides 
                     |CITS: [13782415][14004272][13992679][13978492][14257598]|.
                     It degrades substrates with 3'-hydroxyl or 2',3'-cyclic 
                     phosphate ends somewhat more readily than those with 
                     3'-phosphate or 2'-phosphate ends, and degrades substrate 
                     at a rate of seventy nucleotides per second at 37 degrees 
                     C. Its enzymatic activity has been extensively 
                     characterized with regards to substrate and reaction 
                     conditions |CITS: [7966309]|. RNase II preferentially 
                     degrades longer ribonucleotides and does so processively, 
                     leaving a short oligonucleotide and little evidence of 
                     intermediates |CITS: [4867942]|. It has been shown that the
                     RNase II catalytic site binds the 3' end of substrate RNA 
                     and its anchor site binds 15-25 nucleotides from the 3' 
                     end, with both bindings needed to maintain the enzyme-RNA 
                     complex. The enzyme remains fixed at the anchor point on 
                     the RNA, processively cleaving twelve nucleotides from the 
                     3' end and then dissociating |CITS: [10446370]|. An 
                     Asp209Asn mutant of RNase II binds but does not hydrolyze 
                     substrate |CITS: [15654875]|. Site-directed mutagenesis of 
                     conserved aromatic residues that position RNA in the active
                     site identified Tyr253 as critical for setting the length 
                     of the smallest product |CITS: [18337246]|. Active site 
                     residues Tyr313 and Glu390 are crucial for the RNA (rather 
                     than DNA) specificity of the enzyme. Active site Arg500 is 
                     important for activity, but not for binding of RNA. A 
                     Glu542Ala mutant results in a large decrease in 
                     <i>K<sub>m</sub></i> and large increase in 
                     <i>K<sub>cat</sub></i> for a poly(A) substrate |CITS: 
                     [19458082]|. Although RNase II and RNase R both belong to 
                     the RNR family, differences in their nuclease domains 
                     |CITS: [19004832]| and the RNase R S1 domain and C-terminal
                     lysine-rich region |CITS: [21465561]| may account for the 
                     lack of activity of RNase II on double-stranded RNA.
                     Stable stem-loops block RNase II, forcing it to dissociate 
                     from the substrate RNA |CITS: [10762241][8557629]|. Stable 
                     stem-loops and other secondary structure elements have been
                     shown to block RNase II degradation of the Tn10/IS10 
                     antisense RNA RNA-OUT, <i>rps</i>O mRNA and <i>gly</i>A 
                     mRNA, though stem-loop structures alone do not appear to 
                     stall RNase II long enough <i>in vitro</i> to account for 
                     their effectiveness in stabilizing mRNA <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [7531807][7519147][1691434][1920421]|.
                     In addition to its exonuclease activity, one study suggests
                     a significant endonuclease function for RNase II |CITS: 
                     [14257598]|. A study based on a thermolabile RNase II 
                     appears to show that RNase II is not responsible for the 
                     majority of ribonucleoside 5'-monophosphate formation in 
                     carbon-starved cells |CITS: [320188]|.
                     ATP has been reported to be a competitive inhibitor of 
                     RNase II |CITS: [4941574]|. This was subsequently 
                     demonstrated to be an experimental artifact caused by the 
                     presence of adenylate kinase, which works in concert with 
                     nucleoside monophosphate kinases to convert 
                     5'-mononucleosides to insoluble nucleoside diphosphates, 
                     thus resulting in an apparent diminishing of enzymatic 
                     output |CITS: [4573984][4570468]|.
                     Methods for the purification and characterization of RNase 
                     II have been summarized |CITS: [11586905][19161842]|. The 
                     properties of purified RNase II have been compared with 
                     those of its homolog RNase R |CITS: [11948193]|, and with 
                     RNase D |CITS: [6153805]|. RNase II appears to function as 
                     a monomer |CITS: [6153805]|. Surface plasmon resonance and 
                     electrophoretic mobility shift assay techniques have been 
                     used to characterize RNA-RNase II complexes |CITS: 
                     [20589527]|.
                     Crystal structures of RNase II in the bound and unbound 
                     states have been determined to 2.74 and 2.44 &Aring; 
                     resolution, respectively |CITS: 
                     [16957732][16820694][16996291]|. Based on these structures,
                     RNase II appears to
                     bind RNAs at two locations, and to constrain catalytic 
                     access to single-stranded RNA via a narrow, basic channel. 
                     Based on subsequent truncation mutants, the RNase II S1 
                     domain is important for stable protein-RNA complexes,
                     the amino-terminal domain prevents overbinding to poly(A) 
                     RNA, and there is a third RNA-binding domain in the 
                     amino-terminal portion of the catalytic domain |CITS: 
                     [16806266]|. Domain replacement studies show that the S1 
                     domain is functionally similar in RNase II, PNPase and 
                     RNase R |CITS: [17242308]|.
                     Cell fractionation data suggest that the amino-terminal 
                     amphipathic helix of RNase II mediates an association with 
                     the cytoplasmic membrane |CITS: [23344958]|. 
                     Immunoprecipitation studies using cell extracts suggest 
                     that RNase II may be a component of the degradosome |CITS: 
                     [25299745]|. More generally, double-label localization 
                     studies indicate that RNase II and other components of the 
                     <i>E. coli</i> RNA degradation and processing network are 
                     sequestered within extended cellular structures coiled 
                     around the periphery of the cell |CITS: [24266791]|. 
                     However, work on RNase E using epifluorescence microscopy 
                     under live cell conditions has questioned the formation of 
                     such cytoskeletal-like structures |CITS: [25647427]|. 
                     PNPase degrades <i>rnb</i> mRNA, limiting RNase II 
                     expression. PNP expression is increased in an RNase II null
                     strain, suggesting that this mRNA-based regulation is 
                     reciprocal |CITS: [8809756]|. RNase E and RNase III also 
                     affect RNase II expression, the latter indirectly and the 
                     former directly by cleaving <i>rnb</i> mRNA |CITS: 
                     [7649446]|. Gmr regulates RNase II by modulating its 
                     degradation In a <i>gmr</i> deletion mutant, RNase II is 
                     three times more abundant than in a wild type background 
                     |CITS: [11260472]|.
                     Strains deleted in five exoribonucleases (RNase II, RNase 
                     D, RNase BN, RNase T, RNase PH) are inviable, but any one 
                     of them can support growth with no accumulation of 
                     precursor RNA or disruption of RNA synthesis |CITS: 
                     [1400219][6207170]|. Mutants in RNase II do show reduced 
                     DNA degradation, possibly due to competitive inhibition of 
                     endonuclease I by RNA |CITS: [4105037]|.
                     RNA-Seq analysis of wild-type and deletion mutants of RNase
                     II, RNase R and PNPase shows that deletion of RNase II 
                     significantly affected 187 transcripts related to flagellum
                     assembly, motility and biofilm formation. RNase II and 
                     RNase R mutants produce more biofilm than wild-type |CITS: 
                     [25757888]|. Regulation of RNase II at the protein level 
                     has been shown to occur via the acetylation of Lys501 by 
                     Pka and its deacetylation by CobB. Acetylation affects 
                     substrate binding, decreasing catalytic activity. When the 
                     growth of cells is slowed, acetylation levels are higher 
                     and RNase II activity is decreased |CITS: [26847092]|.
                     An <i>in vivo</i> genome-wide targetome investigation that 
                     the stability of the stem-loops formed at the 3' end of 
                     processed mRNAs from polycistronic operons affected the 
                     termination of the 3' to 5' exonuclease activity of PNPase,
                     RNase II and RNase R |CITS: [37845007]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [19215773][20484980][20659169][35787606]|"
