     gene            complement(1349724..1351367)
                     /gene="sapA"
                     /locus_tag="b1294"
                     /label=sapA
     CDS             complement(1349724..1351367)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sapA"
                     /locus_tag="b1294"
                     /product="trifunctional ABC transporter periplasmic
                     binding protein SapA / putative antimicrobial peptide ABC 
                     transporter / antimicrobial peptide ABC transporter"
                     /label=sapA
                     /note="SapA is the periplasmic binding protein of an
                     ATP-binding cassette (ABC)-family transporter complex, 
                     SapABCDF, that may be involved in the uptake of a range of 
                     molecules, such as AMPs, dipeptides and heme |CITS: 
                     [40084591][40387417][40618440]|. 'Sap family' transporters 
                     are widely present in Gram-negative pathogens where they 
                     contribute to resistance against antimicrobial peptides 
                     (AMPs) (see |CITS: [35749576]|). <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     contains a 'consensus' <i>sap</i> operon (<i>sapABCDF</i>) 
                     encoding all five Sap components (a substrate binding 
                     protein, two membrane proteins and two ATP-binding 
                     proteins) in tandem |CITS: [38972435]|.
                     SapA contains two intramolecular disulfide bonds that 
                     contribute to the structural integrity of the protein; 
                     purified SapA interacts with |FRAME: Heme-b| and with the 
                     cationic AMP, |FRAME: Protamines protamine| |CITS: 
                     [40387417]|.
                     Separately, in <i>E. coli</i> K-12, |FRAME: CPLX0-13226 
                     SapBCDF| (but not SapA) has been implicated in putrescine 
                     export |CITS: [27803167]|. The concentration of putrescine 
                     in the culture supernatant of a &Delta;<i>sapA</i> strain 
                     is not significantly different compared to wild type |CITS:
                     [27803167]|. SapA is implicated in uptake of the natural 
                     product antibiotic |FRAME: CPD-28147| |CITS: [33468467]|."
                     /translation="MRQVLSSLLVIAGLVSGQAIAAPESPPHADIRDSGFVYCVSGQVN
