     gene            1357000..1358487
                     /gene="puuC"
                     /locus_tag="b1300"
                     /label=puuC
     CDS             1357000..1358487
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="puuC"
                     /locus_tag="b1300"
                     /product="multifunctional x dehydrogenase /
                     aminobutyraldehyde dehydrogenase / succinate-semialdehyde 
                     dehydrogenase (NAD(+)) / 3-hydroxypropionaldehyde 
                     dehydrogenase / gamma-glutamyl-gamma-aminobutyraldehyde 
                     dehydrogenase"
                     /EC_number="1.2.1.3; 1.2.1.19; 1.2.1.24; 1.2.1.99"
                     /label=puuC
                     /note="The puuC gene was first reported as a gene encoding
                     a putative aldehyde dehydrogenase of unknown function. The 
                     product of the gene shares 40% identity and over 60% 
                     similarity, with both the cytosolic and the mitochondrial 
                     forms of mammalian aldehyde dehydrogenases |CITS: 
                     [1840553]|.
                     It was later inferred to be the 
                     &gamma;-glutamyl-&gamma;-aminobutyraldehyde dehydrogenase 
                     in a putrescine utilization pathway; together with PuuB, 
                     &gamma;-glutamyl-&gamma;-aminobutyrate is produced from 
                     &gamma;-glutamylputrescine |CITS: [15590624]|.
                     The purified enzyme was shown to have somewhat promiscuous 
                     aldehyde dehydrogenase activity, acting on several 
                     aldehydes including |FRAME: HYDROXYPROPANAL|; 
                     &gamma;-glutamyl-&gamma;-aminobutyraldehyde was not tested 
                     as a substrate in that study |CITS: [18668238]|.
                     A later study has shown that the enzyme was able to 
                     catalyze three separate reactions involved in putrescine 
                     catabolism: |FRAME: EC-1.2.1.99|, |FRAME: EC-1.2.1.19|, and
                     |FRAME: EC-1.2.1.24| |CITS: [22636776]|. In a 2019 study 
                     aiming to produce the common plant auxin |FRAME: 
                     INDOLE_ACETATE_AUXIN| in engineered |FRAME: TAX-562|, the 
                     enzyme was shown to act on |FRAME: INDOLE_ACETALDEHYDE|, 
                     forming |FRAME: INDOLE_ACETATE_AUXIN| |CITS: [31272146]|. 
                     Inactivation of six genes encoding aldehyde dehydrogenases 
                     (|FRAME: EG12292|, |FRAME: EG10036|, |FRAME: EG10110|, 
                     |FRAME: G6755|, |FRAME: G7961|, and |FRAME: EG11329|) 
                     resulted in a strain with greatly reduced aromatic aldehyde
                     oxidation activity (the strain was named ROAR for reduced 
                     oxidation and reduction of aromatic aldehydes) |CITS: [ 
                     37100193]|.
                     The <i>puuC</i>, <i>puuB</i>, and <i>puuE</i> genes may 
                     form an operon |CITS: [9150200][1840553]|.  The function of
                     genes in the <i>puu</i> gene cluster was initially inferred
                     by similarity with the <i>ipuABCDEGFH</i> operon in 
                     <i>Pseudomonas</i> sp. |CITS: [15590624]|."
