     gene            1359807..1361072
                     /gene="puuE"
                     /locus_tag="b1302"
                     /label=puuE
     CDS             1359807..1361072
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="puuE"
                     /locus_tag="b1302"
                     /product="trifunctional 4-aminobutyrate aminotransferase
                     PuuE / 4-aminobutyrate aminotransferase / 5-aminovalerate 
                     aminotransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.6.1.19; 2.6.1.48"
                     /label=puuE
                     /note="The 4-aminobutyrate aminotransferase PuuE is the
                     initial enzyme of one of two 4-aminobutyrate (GABA) 
                     degradation pathways.  PuuE was identified as a 
                     putrescine-inducible GABA aminotransferase, which allows 
                     cells to grow on putrescine as the sole source of nitrogen 
                     even in the absence of GabT |CITS: [15590624]|.  PuuE also 
                     functions as a 5-aminovalerate aminotransferase during 
                     degradation of L-lysine |CITS: [30498244]|.
                     The function of genes in the <i>puu</i> gene cluster was 
                     initially inferred by similarity with the 
                     <i>ipuABCDEGFH</i>
                     operon in <i>Pseudomonas</i> sp. |CITS: [15590624]|. 
                     Site-directed mutagenesis confirmed that the K267 residue 
                     is essential for catalytic activity |CITS: [20639325]|. 
                     PuuE activity is induced by growth on putrescine and 
                     repressed by addition of succinate and under conditions of 
                     limited oxygen availability |CITS: [20639325]|.
                     The three major transaminases in alanine biosynthesis are 
                     AvtA, AlaA and AlaC.  Expression of <i>aspC</i>, 
                     <i>gabT</i>, <i>puuE</i>, <i>tyrB</i>, or <i>ygjG</i> from 
                     a plasmid partially suppresses the growth defect of a 
                     &Delta;<i>avtA alaA alaC</i> mutant |CITS: [20729367]|. The
                     redundancy and promiscuity among aminotransferase enzymes 
                     was further investigated.  Four enzymes, ArgD, AstC, GabT, 
                     and PuuE, show some level of <i>N</i>-acetylornithine 
                     aminotransferase activity involved in arginine 
                     biosynthesis.  An <i>argD astC gabT</i> triple mutant does 
                     not grow on medium lacking arginine.  Accumulation of 
                     suppressor mutations eventually allows growth of the triple
                     mutant; an additional deletion of <i>puuE</i> eliminates 
                     this effect |CITS: [25243376]|. In the hands of |CITS: 
                     [25564669]|, the triple deletion mutant 
                     &Delta;<i>argD</i>&Delta;<i>astC</i>&Delta;<i>gabT</i> 
                     shows an extensive lag phase on glucose minimal medium; a 
                     quadruple deletion mutant that includes <i>puuE</i> does 
                     not grow.
                     The normal activities of two enzymes, ArgD and SerC, are 
                     sufficient for succinyldiaminopimelate (SDAP) and lysine 
                     biosynthesis; a third enzyme, AstC, is sufficient for SDAP 
                     biosynthesis, but alone can not fulfill the cell's 
                     requirement for lysine.  Additional enzymes, including GabT
                     and PuuE, may be able to contribute to SDAP biosynthesis. 
                     Overexpression of <i>puuE</i> from a plasmid allows growth 
                     of the triple &Delta;<i>argD serC astC</i> mutant on 
                     minimal medium |CITS: [25243376]|.
                     A <i>puuE</i> deletion mutant accumulates GABA |CITS: 
                     [20639325]|.  A &Delta;<i>puuE</i> &Delta;<i>gabT</i> 
                     double deletion mutant grows more slowly than wild type 
                     with putrescine as the sole source of nitrogen, because 
                     only one of its two nitrogens can be utilized |CITS: 
                     [22636776]|.
                     GoaG: ""<b>G</b>ABA or <b>o</b>rnithine 
                     <b>a</b>minotransferase"" |CITS: [9150200]|
                     PuuE: ""<b>pu</b>trescine <b>u</b>tilization"" |CITS: 
                     [15590624]|"
