     gene            1362336..1363004
                     /gene="pspA"
                     /locus_tag="b1304"
                     /label=pspA
     CDS             1362336..1363004
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pspA"
                     /locus_tag="b1304"
                     /product="bifunctional phage shock protein A / PspABC
                     complex"
                     /label=pspA
                     /note="PspA is a bifunctional protein, protecting membrane 
                     integrity under stress conditions and regulating |FRAME: 
                     EG12344-MONOMER PspF| transcription factor activity.  PspA 
                     was first identified as a highly expressed protein upon 
                     infection of <i>E. coli</i> with the filamentous phage f1. 
                     PspA is a bacterial member of the conserved ESCRT-III 
                     family of proteins which are involved in membrane 
                     remodelling and repair across all domains of life |CITS: 
                     [34166615]|.
                     Based on its heptad repeat motif, PspA was predicted to 
                     form a coiled-coil structure |CITS: [1712397]|. 
                     Three-dimensional reconstruction of a homomultimeric PspA 
                     complex revealed a 9-fold rotationally symmetric ring; the 
                     nine domains are predicted to be composed of four 
                     individual PspA subunits |CITS: [14688274]|.  Native PspA 
                     forms large oligomeric scaffold-like structures that are 
                     detergent-sensitive |CITS: [18789328]|.
                     PspA is a negative regulator of <i>psp</i> operon 
                     expression |CITS: [1717346]|.  PspA does not interact 
                     directly with DNA |CITS: [10629175]|; rather, it regulates 
                     the activity of the catalytic AAA domain of the |FRAME: 
                     EG12344-MONOMER PspF| transcription factor via a direct 
                     interaction that affects nucleotide binding |CITS: 
                     [12079332][19804784]|.  To be able to inhibit the activity 
                     of a PspF hexamer, more than three PspA subunits must bind,
                     with at least two of them binding to adjacent PspF subunits
                     |CITS: [23659791]|. The N-terminal domain of PspA is 
                     implicated in regulatory function and membrane binding 
                     |CITS: [15838051][24361331][26330516][27806910]|.  A 
                     crystal structure of the minimal PspF-interacting domain of
                     PspA, comprising the N-terminal region of residues 1-144, 
                     has been solved at 1.8 &Aring; resolution, and residues 
                     important for interacting with PspF have been identified 
                     |CITS: [26235546]|.
                     A large oligomeric PspA protein complex is able to suppress
                     proton leakage through damaged membranes |CITS: 
                     [17725563]|.  PspA involvement in protein export |CITS: 
                     [8387148]| is due to its role in maintenance of the 
                     protonmotive force under certain conditions of cellular 
                     stress |CITS: [8598199]|.
                     PspA is a peripheral |CITS: [8387148]| inner membrane 
                     protein |CITS: [1712397][7921245]|.  PspA localizes to the 
                     cell poles and interacts with the |FRAME: EG10608-MONOMER|,
                     forming dynamic rafts in the lateral membrane |CITS: 
                     [19555453]|.  PspA localization and dynamics change under 
                     stress conditions |CITS: [23764692]|; inner membrane stress
                     leads to increased PspA expression and assembly of 
                     higher-order oligomers at the cell poles |CITS: 
                     [25118250]|.  PspA interacts with |FRAME: TATA TatA|, 
                     potentially counteracting membrane instability caused by 
                     Tat transport |CITS: [22689583]|. PspA, PspB, and PspC form
                     a complex, and PspC is required for PspA to bind to PspB 
                     |CITS: [12562786]|.
                     A <i>pspABC</i> mutant exhibits a defect in long-term 
                     survival of stationary phase in high pH media and a defect 
                     in competition with wild-type strains under these 
                     conditions |CITS: [8134371]|, and overexpression of the 
                     <i>psp</i> operon increases survival of stress caused by 
                     n-hexane treatment |CITS: [9493373]|.  A mutant shows 
                     defects in protein translocation |CITS: 
                     [8387148][8598199]|, and overexpression of <i>pspA</i> on a
                     multicopy plasmid improves the efficiency of protein export
                     by the Tat system |CITS: [14702305]|.  PspA was found to be
                     involved in survival of the uropathogenic <i>E. coli</i> 
                     strain UTI89 within mouse bone marrow-derived macrophages 
                     |CITS: [25410299]|. High expressions of the phage shock 
                     proteins was found in long-term stationary phase cells 
                     |CITS: [37024648]|.
                     The phenotypes of the <i>cog-192</i> mutation initially 
                     suggested that PspA was involved in OmpG regulation |CITS: 
                     [2473977]|.  It was later found that the <i>cog-192</i> 
                     mutation causes deletion of several genes and causes 
                     <i>ompG</i> transcription to be directed by the <i>pspA</i>
                     promoter |CITS: [9721282]|.
                     Regulation of the <i>psp</i> operon has been described 
                     |CITS: [9344746][9512522][9878422]|.  The <i>psp</i> operon
                     is induced upon phage infection, temperature increase, 
                     osmotic shock, or exposure to ethanol |CITS: [1712397]| or 
                     the organic solvents n-hexane or cyclooctane |CITS: 
                     [9493373]|, and overexpression of IbsC |CITS: [18710431]|. 
                     Induction is mediated by &sigma;<sup>54</sup>, PspB, PspC 
                     |CITS: [1717346]|, PspF |CITS: [8606168]|, and IHF |CITS: 
                     [7596833]|. Transcription is induced by conditions that 
                     cause stress related to energy depletion |CITS: [8134371]|.
                      Treatment with the drugs diazaborine or cerulenin, which 
                     inhibit synthesis of fatty acids and phospholipids, or 
                     treatment with globomycin, which disrupts lipoprotein 
                     processing, causes transcriptional induction of <i>pspA</i>
                     |CITS: [7921245]|.  PspA production is also increased when 
                     protein export is compromised |CITS: 
                     [8387148][8605893][14594846][20061485]| and by osmotic 
                     stress under aerobic conditions |CITS: [17015655]|. 
                     Transcription is repressed by PspA and by the heat shock 
                     (&sigma;<sup>32</sup>-dependent) system |CITS: [1717346]|. 
                     DksA stimulates abortive synthesis of the <i>pspA</i> 
                     promoter |CITS: [26365052]|.
                     Microarray experiments comparing gene expression in a 
                     non-polar &Delta;<i>pspA</i> strain to wild type have been 
                     performed; the authors also proposed a signal transduction 
                     pathway for inducing the Psp regulon that involves ArcB 
                     |CITS: [16709570]|.  ArcB appears to be involved only under
                     microaerobic growth conditions |CITS: [19682256]|.
                     A mechanistic model of the Psp response has been developed 
                     |CITS: [21569396]|, and its evolutionary history has been 
                     studied |CITS: [21059793]|.
                     PspA: ""<b>p</b>hage <b>s</b>hock <b>p</b>rotein <b>A</b>""
                     |CITS: [2105503][1712397]|
                     Cog: ""<b>c</b>ontrol of <b>o</b>mp<b>G</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [2473977]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9159513][16045608][20636484]|"
