     gene            1363058..1363282
                     /gene="pspB"
                     /locus_tag="b1305"
                     /label=pspB
     CDS             1363058..1363282
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pspB"
                     /locus_tag="b1305"
                     /product="bifunctional phage shock protein B / PspABC
                     complex"
                     /label=pspB
                     /note="PspB and |FRAME: EG10778-MONOMER PspC| together act
                     to activate transcription of the |FRAME: TU00326| operon 
                     induced by infection with bacteriophage, exposure to 
                     ethanol, osmotic shock, or heat shock; PspC is essential 
                     for this activation, whereas PspB is not strictly required 
                     |CITS: [1717346]|.  PspC and PspB are also reported to be a
                     toxin-antitoxin pair |CITS: [14594833]|.
                     |FRAME: EG10776-MONOMER PspA|, PspB, and |FRAME: 
                     EG10778-MONOMER PspC| form a complex, and PspC is required 
                     for PspA to bind to PspB |CITS: [12562786]|.  PspA, PspB, 
                     and PspC are not observed to cross-link with PspD |CITS: 
                     [12562786]|.
                     A pspB mutant exhibits a defect in protein translocation 
                     that is suppressed by production of PspA, which plays a 
                     role in maintenance of the protonmotive force under certain
                     conditions of cellular stress |CITS: [8598199]|. Multi-copy
                     overexpression of the |FRAME: TU00326| operon increases 
                     survival upon stress caused by n-hexane treatment |CITS: 
                     [9493373]|.
                     The corresponding <i>psp</i> locus of <i>Yersinia 
                     enterocolitica</i> is virulence-related |CITS: [11136463]|.
                     Regulation has been described |CITS: [1712397], [1717346], 
                     [9344746], [9512522], [9878422], [9987127], [9493373], 
                     [1717346], [8606168], [7596833], [8134371], [7921245]|. The
                     <i>psp</i> operon shows induction upon phage infection, 
                     temperature increase, or exposure to ethanol, osmotic shock
                     |CITS: [1712397]|, or the organic solvents n-hexane or 
                     cyclooctane |CITS: [9493373]|.  Induction is mediated by 
                     sigma54, PspB, PspC |CITS: [1717346]|, PspF |CITS: 
                     [8606168]|, and IHF |CITS: [7596833]|. Transcription is 
                     induced by conditions that cause stress related to energy 
                     depletion |CITS: [8134371]|.  Treatment with the drugs 
                     diazaborine or cerulenin, which inhibit synthesis of fatty 
                     acids and phospholipids, or treatment with globomycin, 
                     which disrupts lipoprotein processing, causes 
                     transcriptional induction of pspA |CITS: [7921245]|. 
                     Transcription is repressed by PspA and by the heat shock 
                     (sigma32-dependent) system |CITS: [1717346]|. High 
                     expressions of the phage shock proteins was found in 
                     long-term stationary phase cells, and a mutant lacking the 
                     <i>psp</i> operon showed lower survival and growth rate in 
                     a long-term stationary phase culture |CITS: [37024648]|. 
                     PspB:  phage shock protein |CITS: [1712397]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [9159513][16045608]|."
                     /translation="MSALFLAIPLTIFVLFVLPIWLWLHYSNRSGRSELSQSEQQRLAQ
                     LADEAKRMRERIQALESILDAEHPNWRDR"
