     gene            1363282..1363641
                     /gene="pspC"
                     /locus_tag="b1306"
                     /label=pspC
     CDS             1363282..1363641
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pspC"
                     /locus_tag="b1306"
                     /product="bifunctional phage shock protein C / PspABC
                     complex"
                     /label=pspC
                     /note="PspC is a positive regulator of the |FRAME:
                     TU00326| operon expression |CITS: [1712397]|.  |FRAME: 
                     EG10777-MONOMER PspB| and PspC together act to activate 
                     expression of the <i>psp</i> operon in response to 
                     infection with bacteriophage, exposure to ethanol, osmotic 
                     shock, and heat shock; PspC is essential for this 
                     activation, whereas PspB is not strictly required |CITS: 
                     [1717346][20595257]|.  PspC appears to be required for PspB
                     to interact with PspA |CITS: [12562786]|.
                     PspC is also reported as the toxin of a potential PspC-PspB
                     toxin-antitoxin pair |CITS: [14594833]|.  The intramembrane
                     protease FtsH destabilizes PspC |CITS: [21965563]|.
                     PspC is an inner membrane protein |CITS: [8598199]| that is
                     proposed to consist of an N-terminal cytoplasmic domain, a 
                     central transmembrane domain (residues 40-68), and a 
                     C-terminal periplasmic domain with a leucine zipper motif 
                     |CITS: [8598199][20595257]|.  A conserved glycine residue 
                     at position 48 is required for PspC function.  It has been 
                     proposed that stress induces a topology switch whereby the 
                     C-terminal domain becomes cytoplasmic, thus enabling its 
                     interaction with |FRAME: EG10776-MONOMER PspA| |CITS: 
                     [20595257]|.
                     Early results suggested the existence of a PspC dimer that 
                     resists dissociation in the presence of SDS |CITS: 
                     [12562786]|.  However, PspC was not seen to interact with 
                     itself in bacterial two-hybrid assays and does not form a 
                     dimer under physiological conditions |CITS: [19682256]|. 
                     The protein may be post-translationally modified |CITS: 
                     [1712397]|.
                     The corresponding <i>psp</i> locus of <i>Yersinia 
                     enterocolitica</i> (and the PspC homolog in particular 
                     |CITS: [10216859]|) is virulence-related |CITS: 
                     [11136463]|.  <i>pspC</i> is upregulated in a number of 
                     clinical isolates of fluoroquinolone-resistant <i>E. 
                     coli</i> and appears to contribute to fluoroquinolone 
                     resistance |CITS: [22211478]|.
                     A <i>pspABC</i> mutant exhibits a defect in long-term 
                     survival of stationary phase in high pH media and a defect 
                     in competition with wild-type strains under these 
                     conditions |CITS: [8134371]|.  Multi-copy overexpression of
                     the <i>psp</i> operon increases survival of stress caused 
                     by n-hexane treatment |CITS: [9493373]|. Overexpression of 
                     <i>pspC</i> alone causes inhibition of cell growth, 
                     compared to wild type, after 4 hours of induction |CITS: 
                     [14594833]| and decreases the proton motive force |CITS: 
                     [20595257]|.  In a &Delta;<i>pspBC</i> mutant, 
                     indole-induced persistence is reduced compared to wild-type
                     |CITS: [22426114]|. High expressions of the phage shock 
                     proteins was found in long-term stationary phase cells 
                     |CITS: [37024648]|.
                     Regulation of the <i>psp</i> operon has been described 
                     |CITS: [9344746][9512522][9878422]|.  The Psp response 
                     integrates multiple signals |CITS: [21259006]|.  The 
                     <i>psp</i> operon is induced upon phage infection, 
                     temperature increase, osmotic shock, exposure to ethanol 
                     |CITS: [1712397]| or the organic solvents n-hexane or 
                     cyclooctane |CITS: [9493373]|, or indole |CITS: 
                     [22426114]|, and overexpression of IbsC |CITS: [18710431]|.
                      Induction is mediated by &sigma;<sup>54</sup>, PspB, PspC 
                     |CITS: [1717346]|, PspF |CITS: [8606168]|, and IHF |CITS: 
                     [7596833]|. Transcription is induced by conditions that 
                     cause stress related to energy depletion |CITS: [8134371]|.
                      Treatment with the drugs diazaborine or cerulenin, which 
                     inhibit synthesis of fatty acids and phospholipids, or 
                     treatment with globomycin, which disrupts lipoprotein 
                     processing, causes transcriptional induction of <i>pspA</i>
                     |CITS: [7921245]|.   Transcription is repressed by PspA and
                     by the heat shock (&sigma;<sup>32</sup>-dependent) system 
                     |CITS: [1717346]|.  DksA stimulates abortive synthesis of 
                     the <i>pspA</i> promoter |CITS: [26365052]|.
                     PspC: <b>p</b>hage <b>s</b>hock <b>p</b>rotein <b>C</b> 
                     |CITS: [1712397]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9159513][16045608][20636484][25849912]|"
