     gene            1363946..1364260
                     /gene="pspE"
                     /locus_tag="b1308"
                     /label=pspE
     CDS             1363946..1364260
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pspE"
                     /locus_tag="b1308"
                     /product="trifunctional thiosulfate sulfurtransferase PspE
                     / thiosulfate sulfurtransferase / thiosulfate--thiol 
                     sulfurtransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.8.1.1"
                     /label=pspE
                     /note="PspE is a periplasmic sulfurtransferase of the
                     rhodanese family that catalyzes the transfer of sulfur from
                     |FRAME: S2O3| to a recipient thiol compound |CITS: 
                     [11997041]|.
                     Based on analysis of deletion mutants, PspE appears to 
                     carry out about 85% of the thiosulfate sulfurtransferase 
                     activity, with |FRAME: CPLX0-242 ""GlpE""| accounting for 
                     approximately 10% |CITS: [19088907]|. The main receipient 
                     of the sulfur is |FRAME: GLUTATHIONE| (GSH), the dominant 
                     cellular thiol in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [37237993]|.  The 
                     product, |FRAME: CPD-11281| (GSSH), was shown to be the 
                     dominant species of cellular sulfane sulfur. It should be 
                     noted that the reaction is strongly inhibited by GSSH, and 
                     thus the reaction normally favors the reverse direction. 
                     The forward reaction is enhanced by the removal of GSSH via
                     translocation from the periplasm to the cytoplasm.  In the 
                     yeast <i>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</i> GSSH was shown to 
                     react with a second GSH molecule, forming |FRAME: 
                     OXIDIZED-GLUTATHIONE|  and |FRAME: HS| |CITS: [30217845]|. 
                     However, rapid reduction of GSSH to H<sub>2</sub>S was not 
                     observed in <i>E. coli</i>. The authors suggested that 
                     conversion of GSSH to other forms of reactive sulfane 
                     sulfur species such as GSSSH, H<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>, 
                     and Cys-SSH, is likely to occur inside the cytoplasm, 
                     though GSSH is still the dominant species there |CITS: 
                     [37237993]|.
                     Solution structures of the sulfur-free and persulfide 
                     intermediate forms of PspE have been solved, indicating 
                     that there are no significant conformational changes during
                     the catalytic cycle |CITS: [18355042]|.  The 
                     persulfide-modified form of PspE is present when the native
                     protein is purified |CITS: [19088907]|. Overexpression of 
                     PspE does not confer increased resistance to cyanide |CITS:
                     [11997041]|, and deletion of <i>pspE</i> does not increase 
                     sensitivity to cyanide |CITS: [19088907]|.
                     <i>pspE</i> does not appear to be co-regulated with the 
                     other members of the <i>psp</i> operon; rather, PspE 
                     activity is carbon source regulated |CITS: [19088907]|.
                     It was previously shown that the <i>pspE</i> gene may also 
                     be transcribed from its own promoter |CITS: [1712397]|. 
                     <i>pspE</i> may be an evolutionarily recent addition to the
                     Psp response |CITS: [21059793]|.
                     PspE:  <b>p</b>hage <b>s</b>hock <b>p</b>rotein <b>E</b> 
                     |CITS: [1712397]|
                     Review: |CITS: [9159513]|"
