     gene            1376204..1377109
                     /gene="ompG"
                     /locus_tag="b1319"
                     /label=ompG
     CDS             1376204..1377109
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ompG"
                     /locus_tag="b1319"
                     /product="bifunctional outer membrane porin G / mono-,
                     di-, or trisaccharide transport"
                     /label=ompG
                     /note="<i>ompG</i> encodes an outer membrane protein (OMP)
                     in <i>E. coli</i> K-12. <i>ompG</i> is not expressed under 
                     normal laboratory conditions in many K-12 strains; 
                     deletions upstream of <i>ompG</i> which bring it under the 
                     control of an unrelated promoter induce expression |CITS: 
                     [2473977][9721282]|.
                     When expressed, OmpG functions as a nonspecific outer 
                     membrane channel; OmpG produces a larger channel than the 
                     classical outer membrane porins (OmpF and OmpC) - in 
                     addition to the efficient transport of monosaccharides, 
                     OmpG  facilitates the diffusion of dissacharides (sucrose) 
                     and trisaccharides (raffinose); OmpG appears to function as
                     a monomer; OmpG is predicted to contain 16 transmembrane 
                     &beta; strands |CITS:[9721282]|.
                     OmpG is a monomeric porin |CITS: 
                     [11009596][11114248][12899633][16797588]|. OmpG is a 
                     14-stranded &beta;-barrel porin with short periplasmic 
                     turns and seven extracellular loops; crystal structures in 
                     two different conformations suggest that extracellular loop
                     6 may be involved in pH dependent gating of the OmpG 
                     channel |CITS: [16888630]|. When reconstituted into native 
                     <i>E. coli</i> lipids, OmpG assembles into both monomers 
                     and dimers - both forms exhibit open pore entrances at 
                     neutral pH and a closed pore conformation at pH5.0 |CITS: 
                     [20036258]|. OmpG undergoes pH induced conformational 
                     change |CITS: [20171227][20561532][22402479][24020969]|. 
                     When expressed, OmpG is able to rescue the growth of 
                     porin-deficient bacteria (OmpC<sup>-</sup>, 
                     OmpF<sup>-</sup>, LamB<sup>-</sup>) on media containing 
                     maltodextrins as large as maltopentose |CITS:[2473977]|. 
                     <i>ompG</i> exhibits mosaic evolution patterns in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [40444982]|.
                     An OmpG-based nanopore sensing sytem has been developed 
                     |CITS: [25575121][26451707][27500277][30990011][32918733][ 
                     32918733][37379512]|."
