     gene            1383187..1384152
                     /gene="ycjG"
                     /locus_tag="b1325"
                     /label=ycjG
     CDS             1383187..1384152
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ycjG"
                     /locus_tag="b1325"
                     /product="L-Ala-D/L-Glu epimerase"
                     /EC_number="5.1.1.20"
                     /label=ycjG
                     /note="YcjG is an L-Ala-D/L-Glu epimerase that may play a
                     role in the metabolism of murein peptide.  The substrate 
                     specificity of the enzyme is not strict.  YcjG belongs to 
                     the MLE subfamily of the enolase family of enzymes |CITS: 
                     [11747447]|.
                     The crystal structure of YcjG has been solved at 2.6 
                     &Aring; resolution.  Although YcjG behaves like a monomer 
                     in solution, the crystal structure shows extensive surface 
                     contacts between two monomers |CITS: [11747448]|.
                     A D297G mutant can catalyze both the wild-type reaction as 
                     well as the <i>o</i>-succinylbenzoate synthase reaction; 
                     thus this substitution relaxes the substrate specificity of
                     the enzyme.  <i>O</i>-succinylbenzoate synthase activity of
                     the mutant is sufficient to partially rescue the growth 
                     defect of the <i>o</i>-succinylbenzoate synthase-deficient 
                     <i>menC</i> mutant under anaerobic growth conditions |CITS:
                     [12859183]|.  The additional substitution I19F enhances the
                     kinetic constants of the <i>o</i>-succinylbenzoate synthase
                     reaction and growth of the <i>menC</i> mutant strain |CITS:
                     [16128573]|.
                     YcjG is able to catalyze fluoride elimination in a 
                     dipeptide that contains difluorinated alanine at the C 
                     terminus, resulting in a 3-fluorodehydroalanine-containing 
                     peptide |CITS: [14613113]|.
                     An electrophoretic mobility shift assay (EMSA) showed that 
                     PgrR is capable of interacting with the <i>ycjG</i> 
                     promoter region; in addition, in a <i>pgrR</i> mutant 
                     strain the expression of <i>ycjG</i> was increased |CITS:[ 
                     27231238]|.
                     <i>ycjG</i> expression was increased during growth under 
                     nitrogen starvation conditions |CITS:[ 27231238]|. Reviews:
                     |CITS: [15581566][18535144]|
                     "
                     /translation="MRTVKVFEEAWPLHTPFVIARGSRSEARVVVVELEEEGIKGTGEC
