     gene            complement(1393978..1394493)
                     /gene="ogt"
                     /locus_tag="b1335"
                     /label=ogt
     CDS             complement(1393978..1394493)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ogt"
                     /locus_tag="b1335"
                     /product="trifunctional methylated-DNA--[protein]-cysteine 
                     S-methyltransferase / O(4)-methylthymine-DNA 
                     methyltransferase / O(6)-alkylguanine-DNA-alkyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.63"
                     /label=ogt
                     /note="Ogt is the second (after |FRAME: PD00230 ""Ada""|) 
                     methyltransferase enzyme for the direct repair of alkylated
                     DNA in <I>Escherichia coli</I>.
                     The enzyme (characterized initially in <i>E. coli</i> B) 
                     repairs alkylated DNA (O<sup>6</sup>-methylguanine, 
                     O<sup>4</sup>-methylthymine, and O<sup>6</sup>-ethylguanine
                     lesions) by irreversibly transferring the alkyl group to 
                     its own cysteine residue (Cys-139); it does not repair 
                     methylphosphotriesters, is not inducible and and does not 
                     trigger the adaptive response to alkylating agents |CITS: 
                     [2825131][3283737][3290202][2670886][2685744][1520330]|. 
                     Ogt repairs O<sup>4</sup>-methylthymine lesions more 
                     effectively than O<sup>6</sup>-methylguanine <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [1993655]|. Cell extracts from a strain 
                     lacking both Ada and Ogt (&Delta;<i>ada</i> 
                     <i>ogt-1</i>::Kan) show no methyltransferase activity; Ogt 
                     is the only constitutively expressed 
                     O<sup>6</sup>-methylguanine DNA methyltransferase in <i>E. 
                     coli</i>; Ogt provides resistance to DNA mutation caused by
                     low doses of alkylating agents and may protect cells prior 
                     to the induction of Ada; Ogt also protects cells from 
                     spontaneous mutations which may arise from endogenous 
                     alkylating agents present in non-dividing cells |CITS: 
                     [2002008][8195077]|.
                     The efficiency of Ogt and Ada catalysed methyltransferase 
                     repair <i>in vivo</i> is affected by the very-short-patch 
                     (VSP) repair pathway; the VSP pathway repairs the G:T 
                     mismatch that results from methyltransferase repair of a 
                     pro-mutagenic O<sup>6</sup>-methylguanine:T mismatch; 
                     strains lacking the VSP pathway proteins MutS, MutL, or Vsr
                     show a decrease in O<sup>6</sup>-methylguanine repair by 
                     Ada and Ogt |CITS: [17951114]|. The role of 
                     O<sup>6</sup>-alkylguanine alkyltransferases (Ada and Ogt) 
                     in O<sup>6</sup>-ethylguanine repair is small compared with
                     that of O<sup>6</sup>-methylguanine |CITS: [23446177]|
                     <i>ogt</i>: <U>O</U><sup>6</sup>-alkyl<U>g</U>uanine-DNA-a 
                     lkyl<U>t</U>ransferase |CITS: [2825131]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [2233796]|"
                     /translation="MLRLLEEKIATPLGPLWVICDEQFRLRAVEWEEYSERMVQLLDIH
