     gene            complement(1394504..1396030)
                     /gene="abgT"
                     /locus_tag="b1336"
                     /label=abgT
     CDS             complement(1394504..1396030)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="abgT"
                     /locus_tag="b1336"
                     /product="p-aminobenzoyl glutamate:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=abgT
                     /note="Subject to experimental manipulation, AbgT mediates
                     the transport of <i>p</i>-aminobenzoyl-glutamate - a folate
                     catabolite; the physiological role of the protein has not 
                     been confirmed - it may participate in a folate salvage or 
                     recycling pathway or it may function to transport an as-yet
                     undefined molecule.
                     Overexpression of <i>abgT</i> supports the growth of 
                     <i>p</i>-aminobenzoate auxotrophs on exogenous 
                     p-aminobenzoyl glutamate |CITS: [9829935]|. Overexpression 
                     of <i>abgT</i> from a high-copy-number plasmid mediates 
                     significant uptake of labelled 
                     <i>p</i>-aminobenzoyl-glutamate (K<i><sub>T</sub></i> = 123
                     &micro;M) in a wild-type background; labelled 
                     <i>p</i>-aminobenzoyl-glutamate accumulates in the cell - 
                     it does not appear to be metabolized (although 
                     overexpression of <i>abgAB</i>, encoding |FRAME: CPLX0-7844
                     ""p-aminobenzoyl glutamate hydrolase""|, may promote its 
                     cleavage to <i>p</i>-aminobenzoate and glutamate) |CITS: 
                     [17307853]|. Uptake of <i>p</i>-aminobenzoyl glutamate is 
                     inhibited by sodium azide, potassium fluoride and the 
                     protonophore uncoupler, carbonyl cyanide 
                     3-chlorophenylhydrazone |CITS: [17307853]|, suggesting a 
                     dependence on the H<sup>+</sup> gradient.
                     Overexpression of <i>abgT</i> results in increased 
                     sensitivity to the anti-folate compounds, methotrexate and 
                     aminopterin |CITS: [GREEN02]|.
                     <I>abgT</I> is located in a probable operon with <i>abgA, 
                     abgB </i> and <i>ogt</i>; <i>abgT</i> may be cryptic in 
                     wild-type <i>E. coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [9829935][17307853]| 
                     AbgT has twelve predicted transmembrane regions and is the 
                     prototype member of the <i>p</i>-Aminobenzoyl-glutamate 
                     Transporter (AbgT) Family within the IT superfamily |CITS: 
                     [26546518]| (see also |CITS: [26443496]|).
                     <i>abg</i>: 
                     ""<i>p</i>-<b>a</b>mino<b>b</b>enzoyl-<b>g</b>lutamate 
                     utilization"" |CITS: [9829935]|
                     "
