     gene            complement(1399915..1400008)
                     /gene="mcaS"
                     /locus_tag="b4426"
                     /label=mcaS
     misc_RNA        complement(1399915..1400008)
                     /gene="mcaS"
                     /locus_tag="b4426"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA McaS"
                     /note="McaS was initially identified as a Hfq-binding
                     small regulatory RNA.  <i>In vivo</i>, it is part of a 
                     ribonucleoprotein complex together with |FRAME: 
                     EG10900-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10438-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG12866-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10743-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11447-MONOMER| and |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX| |CITS: 
                     [26609136]|.
                     McaS regulates the expression of curli by inhibiting 
                     translation and enhancing degradation of <i>csgD</i> mRNA. 
                     Conversely, it positively regulates flagellar motility and 
                     biofilm formation |CITS: [22250746][22289118][22925049]|. 
                     McaS stimulates cleavage and subsequent degradation of the 
                     <i>csgD</i> mRNA by RNase E |CITS: [29905843]|.  McaS not 
                     only acts as a direct regulator of translation by 
                     base-pairing with the 5' UTRs of mRNAs, but also indirectly
                     by binding and sequestering the global post-transcriptional
                     regulator CsrA.  In addition to <i>pgaABCD</i>, other 
                     regulatory targets of CsrA, including <i>dgcZ</i>, 
                     <i>ycdT</i> and <i>glgC</i>, are thereby indirectly 
                     regulated by McaS |CITS: [23666921]|. McaS also impacts the
                     function of RyhB by competing for Hfq |CITS: [34210798]|.
                     McaS interacts specifically with RNA polymerase (RNAP) 
                     |CITS: [26609136]|.  The secondary structure of McaS 
                     contains three stem-loops |CITS: [25582129]|; stem 2 is 
                     required for the interaction with RNAP |CITS: [26609136]|. 
                     McaS was classified as a Class II small RNA, which 
                     accumulate to higher levels in the rim mutants and to 
                     reduced levels in distal face mutants of Hfq |CITS: 
                     [23318956][26373314]|.  Regulation of <i>csgD</i> by McaS 
                     is defective in rim mutants of Hfq |CITS: [26373314]|. McaS
                     has at least two GGA sequence elements that function as 
                     CsrA-binding sequences; McaS binds CsrA with an apparent 
                     K<sub>d</sub> of 0.71 nM |CITS: [23666921]|.
                     The half life of McaS is strongly reduced in the absence of
                     Hfq.  <i>mcaS</i> expression in rich medium is highest 
                     during the transition from log to stationary phase; in 
                     minimal medium with glycerol, expression is highest during 
                     logarithmic growth |CITS: [22250746][22289118]|. 
                     Overexpression of <i>mcaS</i> inhibits production of curli 
                     and lowers the expression of <i>csgD</i> |CITS: 
                     [22250746]|.  Conversely, it positively regulates 
                     expression of <i>flhD</i> and <i>pgaA</i>, leading to 
                     increased motility and biofilm formation |CITS: 
                     [22289118][22925049][23666921]|.
                     McaS was first identified as IS061, a potential small RNA 
                     of approximately 85-90 nt in length |CITS: [12069726]|. 
                     Using a network biology approach, both GlmZ and McaS (IsrA)
                     were predicted to be involved in the DNA damage response.  
                     A <i>glmZ mcaS</i> double mutant is less sensitive to DNA 
                     damaging agents and has a lower basal mutation rate than 
                     wild type |CITS: [21876160]|.  McaS shows genetic 
                     interactions with its predicted target <i>ssb</i> |CITS: 
                     [26774489]|.  Overexpression of McaS increases swarming 
                     motility and decreases curli expression and biofilm 
                     formation |CITS: [26469694][29905843]|, but does not affect
                     growth |CITS: [26609136]|. Single mutation of <i>mcaS</i> 
                     slightly enhances cell growth when glucosamine or ribose is
                     used as a sole carbon source |CITS: [35920556]|.
                     Significant differences in McaS expression between 
                     different <i>E. coli</i> strains may be due to a 
                     polymorphism in the -10 promoter region |CITS: [26609136]|.
                     The RNA of <i>mcaS</i> appears to interact with the small 
                     regulatory RNA GlnZ, but it is not known how it regulates 
                     McaS |CITS: [36062564]|.
                     McaS: ""<b>m</b>ulti-<b>c</b>ellular <b>a</b>dhesive 
                     <b>s</b>RNA"" |CITS: [22250746][22289118]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12654996][23699406][25028968][32213244]|"
