     gene            1402307..1403290
                     /gene="zntB"
                     /locus_tag="b1342"
                     /label=zntB
     CDS             1402307..1403290
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="zntB"
                     /locus_tag="b1342"
                     /product="bifunctional Zn(2+):H(+) symporter / Zn(2+):H(+)
                     symport"
                     /label=zntB
                     /note="ZntB is a zinc ion transporter; initial
                     investigations conducted concurrently in <i>E. coli</i> and
                     <i>Salmonella enterica</i> serovar Typhimurium implicated 
                     ZntB (formerly YdaN) in zinc efflux while later work 
                     characterized it as a Zn<sup>2+</sup>:proton symporter 
                     likely mediating zinc ion uptake.
                     An <i>E. coli</i> strain (GR480) which lacks the 5 known 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> transporters ZntA, ZitB, ZupT, ZnuABC and 
                     FieF, but retains ZntB (YdaN), exhibits a greater 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> efflux rate than a strain (RS1220) which 
                     lacks all 6 transporters suggesting that ZntB is able to 
                     facilitate Zn<sup>2+</sup> efflux; expression of the cloned
                     homologous gene from <i>Salmonella enterica</i> increases 
                     the rate of Zn<sup>2+</sup> efflux in <i>E.coli</i> strain 
                     RS1220 |CITS: [12142406]|. Deletion of <i>zntB</i> in 
                     <i>Salmonella enterica</i> increases sensitivity to 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [12142406]|.
                     ZntB consists of a large N-terminal cytoplasmic domain and 
                     two transmembrane helices joined by a periplasmic loop; 
                     ZntB forms a symmetrical pentamer with a central 
                     transmembrane pore |CITS: [29101379]|. Purified ZntB binds 
                     (in order of decreasing affinity) Zn<sup>2+</sup>, 
                     Cd<sup>2+</sup>, Ni<sup>2+</sup> and Co<sup>2+</sup> in a 
                     1:1 stoichiometry; purified ZntA reconstituted in liposomes
                     also transports the aforementioned cations; ZntB mediated 
                     transport of Zn<sup>2+</sup> is driven by the pH gradient; 
                     ZntB mediates Zn<sup>2+</sup>:proton symport <i>in 
                     vitro</i> and likely functions as a Zn<sup>2+</sup> 
                     importer <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [29101379]|.
                     YdaN is predicted to be an inner membrane protein with two 
                     transmembrane domains; experimental topology analysis 
                     suggests the C terminus is located in the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [15919996]|.
                     "
