     gene            1403768..1405141
                     /gene="dbpA"
                     /locus_tag="b1343"
                     /label=dbpA
     CDS             1403768..1405141
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dbpA"
                     /locus_tag="b1343"
                     /product="bifunctional ATP-dependent RNA helicase DbpA /
                     RNA helicase"
                     /EC_number="5.6.2.6"
                     /label=dbpA
                     /note="DbpA is a 3'->5' RNA helicase |CITS: [15910005]|
                     that plays a role in the late stage of biogenesis of the 
                     50S subunit of the ribosome |CITS: [19734347]|.
                     DbpA interacts with 23S rRNA |CITS: 
                     [8253085][9836593][10525403]| and exhibits RNA-dependent 
                     ATPase activity that shows specificity for 23S rRNA 
                     molecules that are not incorporated within the ribosome 
                     |CITS: [9836593][19393667]|. Footprinting of DbpA protein 
                     on a fragment of 23S rRNA indicates binding at multiple 
                     sites |CITS: [15173385]|, but the primary binding site 
                     appears to be hairpin 92 of the 23S rRNA, which is part of 
                     the peptidyltransferase center of the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [8524828][11350034]|. A dominant negative R331A active site
                     mutant accumulates an incompletely assembled 45S particle 
                     which can stimulate the ATPase activity of wild-type DbpA 
                     |CITS: [19734347]|. Overexpression of the R331A mutant in 
                     the absence of wild-type protein leads to accumulation of 
                     various sizes of ribosomal particles (27S, 35S, and 45S) at
                     an early stage of the large ribosomal subunit assembly 
                     process; these 3 particles may follow distinct assembly 
                     pathways |CITS: [27194011]|. The ribosome maturation defect
                     caused by the R331A mutation is likely due to the delayed 
                     dissociation of mutant DbpA from the nascent ribosome 
                     |CITS: [34453003]|. The timing of 12 modifications carried 
                     out on 35S and 45S large subunit intermediates using the 
                     inactive R331A mutant compared to wild-type 50S large 
                     subunit has been studied |CITS: [35481783]|. Further 
                     experiments using the same R331A DbpA mutant indicates the 
                     majority of 23S rRNA post-transcriptional modifications 
                     occur in the 27S large subunit intermediate |CITS: 
                     [37751522]|.
                     The binding of ATP and 23S rRNA shows cooperativity |CITS: 
                     [11888286]|. Kinetics and equilibria of ATP and ADP binding
                     have been measured |CITS: [15654752]|, and the 
                     rRNA-activated ATPase cycle mechanism has been investigated
                     |CITS: [18237742]|. Measurements of the kinetics of rRNA 
                     unwinding showed that hydrolysis of a single ATP molecule 
                     is sufficient for unwinding of 8 bp of duplex RNA; strand 
                     displacement occurs after ATP hydrolysis. The release of 
                     phosphate weakens binding of DbpA to rRNA, thus 
                     facilitating its release from the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [20160110]|.  DbpA loads directly onto double helix 
                     substrates |CITS: [26755693]|. The active form of DbpA is a
                     monomer, binding to a single RNA molecule |CITS: 
                     [16325852]|. The residence time of the C-terminal domain of
                     DbpA on a hairpin 92-containing fragment of 23S rRNA have 
                     been measured; the results suggest that multiple helices 
                     can be unwound by DbpA for each binding event |CITS: 
                     [29214235]|.
                     DbpA has a DEAD box motif, which is characteristic of 
                     RNA-dependent ATPases/helicases |CITS: [2216714]|. The 
                     ATPase activity |CITS: 
                     [9836593][11350034][9016593][8524828][8253085]|, helicase 
                     activity |CITS: [11574482][11296244][10525403][9016593]|, 
                     and interactions of DbpA with the substrates and products 
                     |CITS: [12324462][11888286][11350034][11296244][10525403][ 
                     9836593][8524828][8253085]| have been characterized in 
                     detail. Conformational changes within the ATPase site were 
                     analyzed by ENDOR spectroscopy |CITS: [21819147]|. Binding 
                     of hairpin 92 containing an additional 14 nt 5' ssRNA 
                     overhang ((N<sub>14</sub>)HP92) in the presence of ATP 
                     stabilizes the closed (active) state of the helicase core. 
                     A solution structure of the C-terminal domains of DbpA (aa 
                     209-457) has been obtained, and the full length structure 
                     with (N<sub>14</sub>)HP92 was modeled |CITS: [34453003]|.
                     Translation of DbpA is unusual in that it initiates at a 
                     GUG codon |CITS: [8253085]|.
                     Site-directed mutants were designed to generate a dominant 
                     negative <i>dbpA</i> mutant. A mutation in the proposed 
                     ""arginine finger"" of motif VI, R331A, leads to loss of 
                     helicase activity and low ATPase activity; expression of 
                     this mutant confers a dominant slow growth and cold 
                     sensitive phenotype |CITS: [17986459]|. Site-directed 
                     mutations that replace the positively charged R92, R96 or 
                     K102 residues within the N-terminal domain abolish helicase
                     activity and strongly decrease the ATPase activity |CITS: 
                     [34453003]|.
                     Deletion of <i>dbpA</i> does not alter growth or the 
                     ribosome profile of the cell |CITS: [16935881][18565105]|. 
                     A mutant strain with deletions of all five DEAD-box 
                     proteins (&Delta;<i>deaD dbpA rhlB rhlE srmB</i>) is 
                     viable. Alternative growth conditions such as growth in 
                     minimal media or elevated temperatures can alleviate the 
                     growth defects of this strain |CITS: [20484467]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [16935881][19376708][21529161][23415794][23416748]|"
