     gene            complement(1408241..1409050)
                     /gene="recT"
                     /locus_tag="b1349"
                     /label=recT
     CDS             complement(1408241..1409050)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="recT"
                     /locus_tag="b1349"
                     /product="Rac prophage; recombinase RecT"
                     /label=recT
                     /note="The Rac prophage of <i>E. coli</i> encodes two
                     proteins of the ATP-independent RecE recombination pathway 
                     involved in homologous pairing and strand exchange.  Under 
                     certain conditions RecE and RecT can act in place of RecA 
                     as a homologous pairing, strand-exchange recombination 
                     system. The overlapping <i>recE</i> and <i>recT</i> genes 
                     encode proteins involved in RecE-mediated double strand 
                     break (DSB) repair, and are activated in <i>recBC sbcA</i> 
                     mutants.
                     RecE and RecT were shown to interact directly |CITS: 
                     [10921910]|.
                     Both RecE and RecT were required for double-strand break 
                     repair in a <i>recBC sbcA</i> background |CITS: [8125297]|.
                     The RecE protein creates 3' overhangs from double-strand 
                     breaks and RecT anneals them with homologous DNA and 
                     promotes strand exchange |CITS: [8159725]|.  RecT is able 
                     to substitute for &lambda; <i>red</i> &beta; in &lambda; 
                     recombination |CITS: [8244937]|. RecT was shown to promote 
                     the renaturation of homologous ssDNA |CITS: [8416902]|. 
                     RecT promotes the formation of joint molecules of 
                     supercoiled DNA with
                     exonuclease-resected dsDNA having 5' homologous 
                     single-stranded ends |CITS: [12514138]|. Binding of RecT to
                     ssDNA results in the unstacking of bases, the aggregation 
                     of ssDNA, and the formation of complexes of 
                     ssDNA-RecT-dsDNA |CITS: [12514138][9575178]|. Binding of 
                     RecT to dsDNA results in unwinding of dsDNA |CITS: 
                     [12514138]|. It was also shown that binding of RecT to 
                     dsDNA to form D-loops requires low Mg<sup>2+</sup> 
                     concentrations, and inhibits the formation of complexes of 
                     ssDNA-RecT-dsDNA |CITS: [9575178]|.
                     The structures of RecT alone and in complexes with its DNA 
                     substrates have been analyzed by electron microscopy |CITS:
                     [7490755]|.
                     In a <i>recBC sbcA</i> mutant, <i>recA</i>, <i>recE</i>, 
                     <i>recT</i>, <i>recJ</i>, <i>recQ</i>, <i>recF</i>, 
                     <i>recO</i>, and <i>recR</i> mutations resulted in the 
                     formation of large linear fragments of DNA |CITS: 
                     [12718760]|.
                     Plaque formation assays show RecT is involved in 
                     restriction alleviation of type III 
                     restriction-modification systems EcoP1 and EcoP15 |CITS: 
                     [16237019]|. <i>recT</i> was necessary for restriction 
                     alleviation in a <i>recA</i> mutant, but dispensible when 
                     RecA was present, while RecE was necessary in both cases 
                     |CITS: [16237019]|. RecE and RecT are also involved in type
                     I restriction alleviation |CITS: [16237019]|.
                     RecE and RecT have been shown to enhance UV-induced 
                     illegitimate recombination, involving RecJ, RecF, RecO, and
                     RecR |CITS: [15476890][12169595]|. RecQ suppresses the 
                     RecET-mediated illegitimate recombination |CITS: 
                     [15476890][12169595]|.
                     <i>recT</i> gene expression can be induced in a direct or 
                     indirect way by OmpR. Using a gene array system, an 
                     induction of this gene was observed as a result of an up 
                     mutation in the <i>ompR</i> gene |CITS: [14523117]|. 
                     <i>recT</i> is preferentially transcribed by 
                     E&sigma;<sup>S</sup> <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [21398637]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8145642][8080625][7968921]|"
                     /translation="MTKQPPIAKADLQKTQGNRAPAAVKNSDVISFINQPSMKEQLAAA
                     LPRHMTAERMIRIATTEIRKVPALGNCDTMSFVSAIVQCSQLGLEPGSALGHAYLLPFG
