     gene            1414622..1414918
                     /gene="ydaS"
                     /locus_tag="b1357"
                     /label=ydaS
     CDS             1414622..1414918
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ydaS"
                     /locus_tag="b1357"
                     /product="Rac prophage; putative DNA-binding
                     transcriptional regulator YdaS"
                     /label=ydaS
                     /note="This Rac prophage gene was first identified in
                     |FRAME: TAX-562| K-12 as a putative homolog of the &lambda;
                     phage <i>cro</i> gene that codes for a lytic regulator, and
                     thus was indicated as a putative DNA-binding 
                     transcriptional regulator in the Rac prophage. |FRAME: 
                     G6681| is immediately upstream of |FRAME: G6682|, coding 
                     for a putative lysogenic regulator and downstream of the 
                     divergently transcribed |FRAME: G6680|, coding a 
                     transcriptional repressor |CITS: [12886937]|. YdaS is 
                     involved in the excision of the Rac prophage, in which it 
                     is contained |CITS:[38153127]|.
                     The <i>ydaST</i> locus was computationally predicted to 
                     encode a toxin/antitoxin (TA) pair |CITS: [17678530]|. 
                     However, no evidence for this function was found in a study
                     aimed at validating and characterizing new TA loci in <i>E.
                     coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [19943910]|. The homologous YdaS 
                     protein from <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7 was found to have 
                     sequence similarity to HigA antitoxin but when analyzed by 
                     NMR was found to behave as a monomer and contained an 
                     intrinsically disordered C-tail, unlike other HigA 
                     antitoxin proteins |CITS: [31625047]|.
                     YdaS is not expressed in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 under normal 
                     physiological growth conditions in LB broth. 
                     Over-expression of <i>ydaS</i> and <i>ydaST</i> was 
                     associated with reduced growth and loss of cell viability, 
                     whereas deletion or interruption of <i>ydaS</i> or 
                     <i>ydaST</i> shows no growth defect |CITS: 
                     [29205228][29205229]|.
                     <i>ydaS</i> is repressed by the upstream RacR transcription
                     regulator. RacR binds to the intergenic region (IGR) 
                     between <i>racR</i> and <i>ydaS</i> and was shown to 
                     repress transcription of genes immediately downstream of 
                     the IGR in multiple mutants. Silencing of <i>racR</i> 
                     expression resulted in increased expression of both 
                     <i>ydaS</i> and <i>ydaT</i> as well as YdaS levels |CITS: 
                     [29205228][29205229]|.
                     Studies with silencing <i>racR</i> in deletion mutant 
                     strains of &Delta;<i>ydaS</i>, &Delta;<i>ydaT</i>, and 
                     &Delta;<i>ydaS</i> &Delta;<i>ydaT</i> resulted in 
                     differential effects on cell viability and morphology; YdaS
                     was predominantly contributing to cell toxicity whereas 
                     YdaT contributed to both toxicity and increased 
                     filamentation |CITS: [29205229]|. Derepression of 
                     <i>ydaST</i> and subsequent, extensive cell filamentation 
                     was observed when <i>racR</i> expression was reduced by a C
                     protein regulator of the R-M system resulting from 
                     transcription factor cross-talk when the |FRAME: TAX-544| 
                     sp. Csp231I restriction-modification (R-M) system was 
                     introduced into <i>E. coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [31372643]|. 
                     Whether or not YdaS/T regulatory elements also have a role 
                     in <i>E. coli</i> as a toxin/antitoxin system or as a 
                     by-product of their likely lysis/lysogeny regulatory 
                     activity in the context of the Rac prophage has been 
                     discussed in two Letters to the Editor (|CITS: [29695625]| 
                     and response by |CITS: [29695627]|).
                     The YdaS binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
                     /translation="MKKENYSFKQACAVVGGQSAMARLLGVSPPSVNQWIKGVRQLPAE
                     RCPAIERATRGEVLCEELRPDIDWSYLRRSACCSQNMSVKQLNDSNKSSFDHT"
                     /go_function="DNA binding [goid 0003677] [evidence IEA]
                     [GO_REF 0000002]"
     gene            1414941..1415363
                     /gene="ydaT"
                     /locus_tag="b1358"
                     /label=ydaT
     CDS             1414941..1415363
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ydaT"
                     /locus_tag="b1358"
                     /product="Rac prophage; DNA binding transcriptional
                     activator YdaT"
                     /label=ydaT
                     /note="This Rac prophage |FRAME: G6682| gene was first 
                     identified in |FRAME: TAX-562| K-12 as a putative homolog 
                     of the &lambda; phage <i>cII</i> lysogenic regulatory gene 
                     and is next to |FRAME: G6681|, possibly as part of the same
                     operon |CITS: [12886937]|. YdaT appears to regulate 
                     lipopolysaccharide biosynthesis and cell division genes 
                     |CITS:[38153127]|.
                     The <i>ydaST</i> locus was computationally predicted to 
                     encode a toxin/antitoxin (TA) pair |CITS: [17678530]|. 
                     However, no evidence for this function was found in a study
                     aimed at validating and characterizing new TA loci in <i>E.
                     coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [19943910]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>ydaT</i> mutant has aggravating genetic 
                     interactions with genes involved in DNA recombination 
                     |CITS: [26774489]|. Over-expression of <i>ydaS</i> and 
                     <i>ydaST</i> was associated with reduced growth and loss of
                     cell viability, whereas deletion or interruption of 
                     <i>ydaS</i> or <i>ydaST</i> shows no growth defect. When 
                     <i>ydaT</i> is expressed alone, it does not lead to a large
                     loss of cell viability. However, YdaT also does not act as 
                     an antitoxin to the toxic effects of YdaS; YdaT may not be 
                     functional as it was found to accumulate in the insoluble 
                     fraction when overexpressed |CITS: [29205228][29205229]|.
                     YdaT reduces cell viability as long as RcsA is present 
                     |CITS:[38153127]|. YdaT increases the expression of RcsA 
                     |CITS:[38153127]|.
                     <i>ydaT</i> is repressed by the upstream RacR transcription
                     regulator when RacR binds to the intergenic region (IGR) 
                     between <i>racR</i> and <i>ydaS</i>. Silencing of 
                     <i>racR</i> expression resulted in increased expression of 
                     both <i>ydaS</i> and <i>ydaT</i> |CITS: 
                     [29205228][29205229]|.
                     Studies with silencing <i>racR</i> in deletion mutant 
                     strains of &Delta;<i>ydaS</i>, &Delta;<i>ydaT</i>, and 
                     &Delta;<i>ydaS</i> &Delta;<i>ydaT</i> resulted in 
                     differential effects on cell viability and morphology; YdaS
                     was predominantly contributing to cell toxicity whereas 
                     YdaT contributed to both toxicity and increased 
                     filamentation.  Expression of YdaT alone does cause cell 
                     filamentation |CITS: [29205229]|. Derepression of 
                     <i>ydaST</i> and subsequent, extensive cell filamentation 
                     was observed when <i>racR</i> expression was reduced by a C
                     protein regulator of the R-M system resulting from 
                     transcription factor cross-talk when the |FRAME: TAX-544| 
                     sp. Csp231I restriction-modification (R-M) system was 
                     introduced into <i>E. coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [31372643]|. 
                     Whether or not YdaS/T regulatory elements also have a role 
                     in <i>E. coli</i> as a toxin/antitoxin system or as a 
                     by-product of their likely lysis/lysogeny regulatory 
                     activity in the context of the Rac prophage has been 
                     discussed in two Letters to the Editor (|CITS: [29695625]| 
                     and response by |CITS: [29695627]|)."
