     gene            1418039..1419496
                     /gene="trkG"
                     /locus_tag="b1363"
                     /label=trkG
     CDS             1418039..1419496
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="trkG"
                     /locus_tag="b1363"
                     /product="bifunctional Rac prophage; Na(+)-dependent K(+) 
                     transporter TrkG / Na(+)-dependent K(+) transporter TrkG"
                     /label=trkG
                     /note="TrkG is a Na<sup>+</sup>-dependent potassium ion 
                     transporter that makes a minor contribution to 
                     K<sup>+</sup> uptake; TrkG-mediated uptake contributes to 
                     potassium replenishment when Na<sup>+</sup> is present in 
                     the growth environment |CITS: [36586436]|.
                     Early studies in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 identified four 
                     separate K<sup>+</sup> uptake systems: two constitutive 
                     systems [TrkA (also called Trk) and TrkD (also called 
                     Kup)], a high affinity system (Kdp) and a non-saturable 
                     system TrkF |CITS: [4578]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [320207][359759][6405784][3896791]|. Although initially 
                     thought to be a single system TrkA/Trk activity was later 
                     shown to result from the activity of two K<sup>+</sup> 
                     transporters which were named |FRAME: TRKG-MONOMER 
                     ""TrkG""| and |FRAME: TRKH-MONOMER ""TrkH""| |CITS: 
                     [1987159][7896723]|.
                     <i>trkG trkH</i> double null mutations abolish saturable 
                     K<sup>+</sup> uptake in a strain lacking |FRAME: 
                     ATPASE-1-CPLX ""Kdp""| and |FRAME: KUP-MONOMER ""Kup""| 
                     mediated potassium transport |CITS: [1987159]|. TrkG and 
                     TrkH show 41% amino acid identity; inactivation of either 
                     <I>trkG</I> or <I>trkH</I> (in a &Delta;<i>kdpFABC 
                     trkD1</i> background) affects the kinetics of potassium 
                     uptake |CITS: [7896723]|. TrkG and TrkH are both active as 
                     low affinity, high rate potassium ion transporters |CITS: 
                     [1987159][7896723]| and function in conjunction with 
                     |FRAME: TRKA-MONOMER ""TrkA""|, a peripheral membrane 
                     binding protein that binds NAD+ and is essential for TrkG/H
                     activity |CITS: [2674131] [8412700]|. |FRAME: EG12304 
                     ""TrkE""| (also known as SapD), an ATP binding protein, is 
                     required for TrkH activity and affects the kinetics of TrkG
                     activity |CITS: [1987159], [11700350]|. In the Transporter 
                     Classification Database TrkG and TrkH are members of the 
                     K<sup>+</sup> transporter (Trk) family within the VIC 
                     superfamily |CITS: [26546518]|.
                     <i>trkG</i> expression is regulated by |FRAME: PD00288 
                     H-NS| |CITS: [36586436]|
                     TrkG is an inner membrane protein with 10 - 12 predicted 
                     transmembrane regions |CITS: [2022616]|. Similar to TrkH, 
                     TrkG is predicted to contain 4 sequential MPM 
                     (membrane-pore-membrane) regions plus two additional TM 
                     regions |CITS: [    10423425]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [40662853]|).
                     <i>trkG</i> displays atypical codon usage and has a G+C 
                     content of only 37%. The <I>trkG</I> gene is probably a 
                     foreign gene which was inserted with a <I>rac</I> prophage 
                     |CITS: [2022616]|.
                     Trk mediated K<sup>+</sup> transport in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     requires both ATP and the proton motive force |CITS: 
                     [320207]| however Trk systems are not energized directly by
                     ATP |CITS: [3886836]|.
                     A strain lacking all 4 major K<sup>+</sup> uptake systems 
                     (|FRAME: ATPASE-1-CPLX ""Kdp""|, |FRAME: KUP-MONOMER 
                     ""Kup""|, |FRAME: TRKG-MONOMER ""TrkG""|, and |FRAME: 
                     TRKH-MONOMER ""TrkH""|) can only grow in medium 
                     supplemented with 30mM K<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [36586436]|. 
                     <i>trk</i>: <U>tr</U>ansport of <U>K</U> |CITS: [4942756]|"
                     /translation="MNTSHVRVVTHMCGFLVWLYSLSMLPPMVVALFYKEKSLFVFFIT
