                     oxidoreductase / pyruvate-flavodoxin oxidoreductase"
                     /EC_number="1.2.8.1"
                     /label=ydbK
                     /note="Based on sequence similarity, YdbK is predicted to 
                     function as a pyruvate:flavodoxin oxidoreductase and/or 
                     pyruvate synthase |CITS: [11574054][12952533]|. The 
                     activity has been assayed in crude extracts with methyl 
                     viologen as the electron acceptor |CITS: [24025246]|. 
                     Coexpression of YdbK with a heterologous hydrogenase enzyme
                     enhances the production of hydrogen; the effect depends on 
                     the coexpression of an <i>E. coli</i> flavodoxin or a 
                     heterologous ferredoxin |CITS: [19558967]|. Overexpression 
                     of YdbK in an engineered strain that utilizes ethanol 
                     aerobically as the sole source of carbon and secretes 
                     valine enables better growth and higher valine production, 
                     suggesting enhanced availability of pyruvate |CITS: 
                     [EREMINA10]|.
                     The authors of |CITS: [19558967]| suggest that YdbK is most
                     likely the enzyme characterized by |CITS: [7042345]|, 
                     |FRAME: PYRUFLAVREDUCT-MONOMER|. Under the conditions 
                     employed by |CITS: [EREMINA10]|, the enzyme may function in
                     the direction of pyruvate synthesis.
                     A <i>ydbK</i> mutant is unable to grow on glucose under 
                     oxidative stress conditions |CITS: [EREMINA10]|. 
                     <i>ydbK</i> is needed for superoxide resistance during 
                     growth on minimal medium |CITS: [22515487]|, and a <i>ydbK 
                     fpr</i> double mutant is sensitive to methyl viologen 
                     |CITS: [24025246]|.
                     Expression of <i>ydbK</i> is induced by superoxide and is 
                     SoxS-dependent |CITS: [24025246]|."
