                     /EC_number="1.7.1.17"
                     /label=azoR
                     /note="AzoR is an NADH:quinone oxidoreductase with a role
                     in protection against thiol-specific stress. Purified AzoR 
                     is active on a variety of electrophilic quinones |CITS: 
                     [19666717]|.
                     Purified AzoR also has azoreductase activity |CITS: 
                     [11583992][23795903]|; it is the major azoreductase under 
                     aerobic conditions |CITS: [23795903]|. Compounds containing
                     azo bonds (N=N) have environmental and clinical 
                     significance and thus proteins with azoreductase activity 
                     are subjects of experimental interest. AzoR was originally 
                     thought to be an acyl carrier protein phosphodiesterase 
                     |CITS: [2168383]|; however, the overexpressed and purified 
                     protein has azoreductase activity, but not acyl carrier 
                     protein phosphodiesterase activity |CITS: [11583992]|. AzoR
                     activity is FMN-dependent and requires 2 mol of NADH for 
                     the reduction of 1 mol of methyl red |CITS: [11583992]|.
                     AzoR is also able to act as a nitroreductase, activating 
                     the prodrug CB1954 |CITS: [19852945]| and reducing 
                     7-nitrocoumarin-3-carboxylic acid (7NCCA) |CITS: 
                     [23795903]|.
                     Crystal structures of AzoR in the oxidized and reduced 
                     forms have been solved |CITS: [16684776][18337254]|. The 
                     enzyme has structural similarity to flavodoxins; the FMN 
                     cofactors of each catalytic site are located at the dimer 
                     interface |CITS: [16684776]|. The structure of the active 
                     site and the FMN cofactor differs between the oxidized and 
                     reduced forms |CITS: [18337254]|. The Arg59 residue may be 
                     involved in determining substrate specificity |CITS: 
                     [18337254]|.
                     An <i>E. coli azoR::kan</i> mutant has impaired growth in 
                     the presence of 2-methylhydroquinone (2-MHQ), catechol and 
                     menadione and shows decreased levels of glutathione. 
                     Externally added glutathione can partially restore impaired
                     growth of the <i>azoR</i> mutant caused by 2-MHQ |CITS: 
                     [19666717]|.
                     Transcription of <i>azoR</i> was induced by the presence of
                     0.5 mM 2-MHQ, 1 mM diamide, 6 mM catechol, 0.3 mM 
                     menadione, and 10 mM H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> |CITS: 
                     [19666717]|.
                     Characterization of the azoreduction and subsequent release
                     of aryl amines from azo-conjugates has been examined |CITS:
                     [39707960]|."
