     gene            1477318..1481220
                     /gene="hrpA"
                     /locus_tag="b1413"
                     /label=hrpA
     CDS             1477318..1481220
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hrpA"
                     /locus_tag="b1413"
                     /product="bifunctional ATP-dependent 3'->5' RNA helicase
                     HrpA / ATP-dependent 3'->5' RNA helicase"
                     /EC_number="5.6.2.6"
                     /label=hrpA
                     /note="HrpA belongs to the family of DEAH-box RNA
                     helicases |CITS: [7899078]| and has ATP-dependent 
                     3'&rarr;5' RNA helicase activity |CITS: [34290142]|. The 
                     helicase activity resides within the N-terminal 783 amino 
                     acids of HrpA, while the C-terminal region directly 
                     contacts the N-terminal region and appears to modulate its 
                     helicase activity |CITS: [34290142]|.
                     Crystal structures of apo-HrpA<sup>1-178</sup>, 
                     HrpA<sup>1-178</sup> in complex with a ssRNA, and 
                     apo-HrpA<sup>1-178, D305A</sup> have been solved, showing 
                     that he enzyme undergoes large conformational changes upon 
                     RNA binding that are facilitated by alternative interdomain
                     contacts involving the D305 residue. A K106A mutant within 
                     a conserved sequence motif of the RecA1-like domain of HrpA
                     shows reduced ATPase activity. The ""stacking triad"" 
                     consisting of F655, P656, and F661 within the OB domain of 
                     HrpA is required for helicase activity |CITS: [34290142]|. 
                     Further crystallization studies found functional N-terminal
                     and C-terminal extensions. The N-terminal has an 
                     antiparallel helix-bundle domain (APHB) near the RecA-like 
                     domains that functions in ssDNA and ssRNA recognition and 
                     binding, and functions in RNA/RNA and RNA/DNA duplex 
                     unwinding. Co-crystals of the full Hrpa protein with 
                     various substrates also revealed its ability to bind 
                     DNA/RNA G-quadruplex (G4) and intercalated motif (i-motif) 
                     structures |CITS: [38412313]|.
                     HrpA is involved in the post-transcriptional processing of 
                     the <i>daa</i> operon mRNA, which encodes proteins involved
                     in fimbrial biogenesis of an enteropathogenic <i>E. 
                     coli</i> strain. Activity requires the predicted nucleotide
                     triphosphate binding and hydrolysis functions |CITS: 
                     [15186427]|. The ATPase activity of His-tagged partially 
                     purified HrpA has been measured |CITS: [16139517]|.
                     An <i>hrpA</i> mutant has no obvious growth defect |CITS: 
                     [7899078][34290142]|, but shows increased tolerance to 
                     rifampicin and impaired survival in the presence of 
                     chloramphenicol, tetracycline, or ampicillin |CITS: 
                     [34290142]|. Both the ATPase activity and the C-terminal 
                     domain of HrpA are required for enhanced chloramphenicol 
                     tolerance of wild-type <i>E. coli</i> compared to the 
                     deletion mutant |CITS: [34290142]|.
                     HrpA: ""DEA<b>H</b>-family <b>R</b>NA helicase-like 
                     <b>p</b>rotein"" |CITS: [7899078]|"
