     gene            1482489..1483928
                     /gene="aldA"
                     /locus_tag="b1415"
                     /label=aldA
     CDS             1482489..1483928
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aldA"
                     /locus_tag="b1415"
                     /product="multifunctional aldehyde dehydrogenase A /
                     aldehyde dehydrogenase A, NAD-linked / lactaldehyde 
                     dehydrogenase / glycolaldehyde dehydrogenase"
                     /EC_number="1.2.1.22; 1.2.1.21"
                     /label=aldA
                     /note="Aldehyde dehydrogenase A (AldA) is an 
                     NAD<sup>+</sup>-dependent dehydrogenase of relatively broad
                     specificity for small &alpha;-hydroxyaldehyde substrates. 
                     It is thus utilized in several metabolic pathways |CITS: 
                     [3308886]|. The glycolaldehyde dehydrogenase activity of 
                     AldA may serve an essential function in removing the 
                     glycolaldehyde side product of |FRAME: 
                     H2NEOPTERINALDOL-MONOMER FolB| within the folate 
                     biosynthesis pathway. The function can be bypassed by 
                     |FRAME: G6198-MONOMER PrpC| |CITS: [26441892]|.
                     L-fucose and L-rhamnose are metabolized through parallel 
                     pathways which converge after their corresponding aldolase 
                     reactions yielding the same products: dihydoxy-acetone 
                     phosphate and L-lactaldehyde. Aerobically, aldehyde 
                     dehydrogenase A oxidizes L-lactaldehyde to L-lactate |CITS:
                     [3298215][3308886]|.
                     In parallel pathways utilizing the same enzymes, 
                     D-arabinose and L-lyxose can be metabolized to 
                     dihydoxy-acetone phosphate and glycolaldehyde, which is 
                     oxidized to glycolate by aldehyde dehydrogenase A |CITS: 
                     [4928018][1650346]|.
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme alone and in ternary and 
                     binary complexes have been solved, suggesting a structural 
                     basis for AldA's specificity for NADH |CITS: [17173928]|. 
                     Site-directed mutagenesis of the F180 residue and kinetic 
                     characterization of the mutant enzyme showed that this 
                     residue is involved in cosubstrate specificity of AldA 
                     |CITS: [18218709]|.
                     Aldehyde dehydrogenase A is only present under aerobic 
                     conditions and is most highly induced by the presence of 
                     fucose, rhamnose or glutamate |CITS: 
                     [181364][3308886][3275622]|. The enzyme is inhibited by 
                     NADH, which may act as a switch to shift from oxidation of 
                     lactaldehyde to its reduction by propanediol oxidoreductase
                     |CITS: [3275622]|.
                     Cysteine sites in peptides of this AldA protein were 
                     significantly oxidized in a redox proteomics study in a 
                     &Delta;|FRAME: CPLX0-245 Ahp| mutant |CITS: [37725887]|. 
                     Based on sequence similarity, AldA was predicted to be a 
                     succinate-semialdehyde dehydrogenase |CITS: [12952533]|. 
                     Regulation of <i>aldA</i> expression has been investigated.
                     The gene is regulated by catabolite repression |CITS: 
                     [1684935][9202484][12003946]|, repression under anaerobic 
                     conditions via ArcA |CITS: [9202484][10071223]|, and 
                     induction by the carbon source |CITS: [10071223]|. AldA 
                     expression is upregulated during short-term adaptation to 
                     glucose limitation |CITS: [11683869][16804185]| and by the 
                     presence of oleate |CITS: [18317523]|.
                     An <i>aldA</i> mutant shows slower growth on fucose and 
                     rhamnose than wild type |CITS: [3298215]|. <i>aldA</i> and 
                     |FRAME: G6198 <i>prpC</i>| are synthetically lethal.
                     Based on the lack of sequence and structural similarity 
                     between AldA and PrpC, it seems unlikely that PrpC also 
                     catalyzes the glycolaldehyde dehydrogenase reaction. 
                     Potential explanations for the synthetic lethality of 
                     <i>aldA</i> and <i>prpC</i> were discussed |CITS: 
                     [26441892]|.
                     In order to understand the role of natural selection in 
                     bacterial regulation, the <i>aldA</i> gene, among other 
                     genes, has undergone an analysis that compared the level of
                     expression, plasticity, and noise of random versus 
                     segregating variants of many environmental isolates of 
                     <i>E. coli</i>, including <i>E. coli</i> MG1655 |CITS: 
                     [35726087]|.
                     AldA: ""lact<b>al</b>dehyde <b>d</b>ehydrogenase"""
