     gene            complement(1486179..1486328)
                     /gene="hokB"
                     /locus_tag="b4428"
                     /label=hokB
     CDS             complement(1486179..1486328)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hokB"
                     /locus_tag="b4428"
                     /product="toxin HokB"
                     /label=hokB
                     /note="HokB is the toxin of the HokB-SokB type I
                     toxin-antitoxin system. Increased expression of HokB 
                     mediates the effect of |FRAME: G7656-MONOMER ObgE| on 
                     persister cell formation. Moderately increased expression 
                     of HokB leads to a decrease in the membrane potential. It 
                     may thus lower metabolic activity, inducing some cells to 
                     enter the persistent state that enables survival of 
                     exposure to antibiotics |CITS: [26051177]|. A regulatory 
                     mechanism has been proposed |CITS: [27672159]|.
                     HokB is able to insert in the cytoplasmic membrane and form
                     pores that result in ATP leakage, reducing the cellular 
                     energy ratio (ATP/ADP) and increasing persister cell 
                     formation. Addition of the channel blocker polyethylene 
                     glycol reduces HokB-induced persistence |CITS: [30108166]|.
                     The <i>hokB</i> mRNA is a target for RNA editing by |FRAME:
                     EG11372-MONOMER TadA|, changing a tyrosine (TAC) codon into
                     a cysteine (TGC) codon at position 29. The level of editing
                     increases from 28% at early logarithmic phase to 93% at 
                     entry to stationary phase. Expression of a HokB variant 
                     with a genetically encoded Cys29 residue results in 
                     increased toxicity |CITS: [28864459]|. The |FRAME: 
                     EG12436-MONOMER| cleaves HokB suppressing its toxicity 
                     |CITS: [37409810]|.
                     Sequence analysis indicates that the <i>hokB</i> gene is a 
                     homologue of the <i>hok</i> (host killing) gene which is 
                     responsible for mediating plasmid stabilization by 
                     post-segregational killing (PSK) in plasmid R1. <i>hok</i> 
                     encodes a stable mRNA whose translation is inhibited by a 
                     less stable small RNA encoded by <i>sok</i>. The stable 
                     mRNA from <i>hokB</i> encodes a polypeptide that induction 
                     experiments have shown to be toxic to <i>E. coli</i> host 
                     cells, resulting in cell growth arrest, morphological 
                     changes, and rapid cell killing. Sequence analysis shows 
                     that the <i>hokB</i> system contains the regulatory 
                     elements described for the <i>hok/sok</i> system from 
                     plasmid R1 including a fold-back inhibition element 
                     (<i>fbi</i>), a translational activation element 
                     (<i>tac</i>), promoter sequences, and an overlapping open 
                     reading frame named <i>mokB</i>. Northern analysis has 
                     shown that the <i>fbi</i> sequence is exonucleolytically 
                     removed during post-transcriptional processing, and that 
                     the locus also encodes a small, less stable SokB RNA 
                     complementary to the <i>hokB</i> mRNA leader in the 
                     <i>mokB</i> open reading frame. SokB is homologous to the 
                     Sok RNA from plasmid R1. This antisense RNA is believed to 
                     regulate <i>hokB</i> translation by inhibiting translation 
                     of the overlapping <i>mokB</i> |CITS: [10361310]|.
                     Ectopic overexpression of HokB leads to growth arrest and 
                     cell killing |CITS: [10361310]|.
                     HokB: ""<b>ho</b>st <b>k</b>illing <b>B</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [10361310]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [26119572][26140365][36960293]| Hypothesis:
                     |CITS: [27672159]|"
                     /translation="MKHNPLVVCLLIICITILTFTLLTRQTLYELRFRDGDKEVAALMA
                     CTSR"
