     gene            1503543..1503719
                     /gene="hicA"
                     /locus_tag="b4532"
                     /label=hicA
     CDS             1503543..1503719
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hicA"
                     /locus_tag="b4532"
                     /product="bifunctional mRNA interferase toxin HicA / 
                     endoribonuclease"
                     /label=hicA
                     /note="HicA is an mRNA interferase acting as the toxin of
                     the HicA-HicB toxin-antitoxin system.  Overexpression of 
                     HicA induces mRNA cleavage and growth inhibition, but not 
                     cell death.  Expression of HicB neutralizes the effect of 
                     HicA |CITS: [19060138]|.  HicA contributes to 
                     autoregulation of HicAB expression |CITS: [28266056]|, 
                     likely by affecting the kinetics of DNA binding by HicB 
                     |CITS: [31495532]|.
                     An analysis of the targets and site specificity of HicA 
                     showed some cleavage site specificity, favoring an A 
                     immediately downstream of the cleavage site.  Similar to 
                     ribosome-dependent endonuclease toxins, HicA shows a bias 
                     towards cleaving before the first position in a codon. Like
                     other <i>E. coli</i> endoribonuclease toxins, HicA activity
                     does not affect mature ribosomes, but inhibits ribosome 
                     biogenesis, likely by affecting the translation of a 
                     specific set of ribosomal proteins |CITS: [34544284]|.
                     HicA belongs to a family of proteins with a predicted 
                     double-stranded RNA binding fold |CITS: [16895922]|.  A 
                     crystal structure of the HicAB complex has been solved 
                     |CITS: [31495532]|.
                     <i>hicAB</i> expression is induced by nutrient starvation 
                     |CITS: [19060138]|.  The <i>hicBA</i> operon is transcribed
                     from two promoters, hicAp1 (upstream) and hicAp2 
                     (downstream).  The hicAp2 promoter is autorepressed by HicB
                     and produces an mRNA that enables only HicB translation 
                     |CITS: [28266056]|.
                     Ectopic expression of HicA cross-activates expression of 
                     the RelBE toxin-antitoxin system; conversely, ectopic 
                     expression of RelE also induces expression of <i>hicA</i> 
                     |CITS: [23432955]|.  Overexpression of <i>hicA</i> 
                     suppresses the essentiality of <i>lapB</i>, which encodes a
                     protein involved in lipopolysaccharide (LPS) assembly 
                     |CITS: [24722986]|.  Overexpression of any one of three RNA
                     interferase toxins HicA, HigB or YafQ suppresses the 
                     essentiality of |FRAME: RPOE-MONOMER &sigma;<sup>E</sup>| 
                     |CITS: [25917909]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [22060041][31932311]|"
                     /translation="MKQSEFRRWLESQGVDVANGSNHLKLRFHGRRSVMPRHPCDEIKE
                     PLRKAILKQLGLS"
