     gene            1509835..1511259
                     /gene="patD"
                     /locus_tag="b1444"
                     /label=patD
     CDS             1509835..1511259
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="patD"
                     /locus_tag="b1444"
                     /product="bifunctional gamma-aminobutyraldehyde
                     dehydrogenase / 5-aminopentanal dehydrogenase"
                     /EC_number="1.2.1.19"
                     /label=patD
                     /note="&gamma;-aminobutyraldehyde dehydrogenase (PatD) is
                     the second enzyme in one of two pathways for putrescine 
                     degradation (|FRAME: PUTDEG-PWY|) |CITS: 
                     [3510672][16023116][22636776]|. PatD along with |FRAME: 
                     ALDHDEHYDROG-MONOMER ""PuuC""| are two enzymes  in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> known to carry out &gamma;-aminobutyraldehyde 
                     dehydrogenase activity |CITS: [22636776]|. PatD also 
                     functions as a 5-aminopentanal dehydrogenase during 
                     degradation of L-lysine |CITS: [30498244]|.
                     It was initially reported that purified 
                     &gamma;-aminobutyraldehyde dehydrogenase consists of a 
                     dimer of 95 kDa subunits |CITS: [3129020]|. Later 
                     purification showed the enzyme to be a homotetramer of 51 
                     kDa subunits |CITS: [16023116]|. Crystal structures of PatD
                     have been solved. Analysis of the active site configuration
                     as well as enzyme kinetics data suggested that PatD is a 
                     medium-chain aldehyde dehydrogenase with a preference for 
                     linear <i>n</i>-alkyl medium-chain aldehydes, using NAD as 
                     the cofactor |CITS: [15381418]|. Expression of 
                     &gamma;-aminobutyraldehyde dehydrogenase activity is 
                     regulated by catabolite repression and by nitrogen source 
                     |CITS: [3897202]|. <i>patD</i> is part of the 
                     <i>ydcSTUV-patD</i> operon; transcription is controlled by 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> and Nac |CITS: [23531166]|.
                     A <i>patD</i> mutant lost more than half of its of 
                     &gamma;-aminobutyraldehyde dehydrogenase activity; a mutant
                     lacking both <i>patD</i> and |FRAME: EG10036| lost 85% of 
                     this activity and did not utilize putrescine as the sole 
                     source of nitrogen |CITS: [22636776]|.
                     Inactivation of six genes encoding aldehyde dehydrogenases 
                     (|FRAME: EG12292|, |FRAME: EG10036|, |FRAME: EG10110|, 
                     |FRAME: G6755|, |FRAME: G7961|, and |FRAME: EG11329|) 
                     resulted in a strain with greatly reduced aromatic aldehyde
                     oxidation activity (the strain was named ROAR for reduced 
                     oxidation and reduction of aromatic aldehydes) |CITS: [ 
                     37100193]|.
                     PatD: ""<b>p</b>utrescine <b>a</b>mino<b>t</b>ransferase 
                     pathway, <b>d</b>ehydrogenase"" |CITS: [22636776]|"
