     gene            complement(1536929..1538317)
                     /gene="narU"
                     /locus_tag="b1469"
                     /label=narU
     CDS             complement(1536929..1538317)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="narU"
                     /locus_tag="b1469"
                     /product="bifunctional nitrate/nitrite transporter NarU / 
                     nitrate:nitrite antiporter"
                     /label=narU
                     /note="NarU catalyses nitrate uptake and nitrite export by
                     an unknown mechanism in. NarU is a member of the major 
                     facilitator superfamily (MFS) of transporters 
                     |CITS:[1419050]|, and is highly similar to the 
                     nitrate:nitrite antiporter |FRAME: NARK-MONOMER ""NarK""| 
                     |CITS:[7934881]|. <I>narU</I> forms an operon with 
                     <I>narZYWV</I>, encoding |FRAME: NITRATREDUCTZ-CPLX| 
                     |CITS:[16804183]|. An |FRAME: RPOS-MONOMER 
                     ""RpoS""|-dependent promoter is located upstream of the 
                     <I>narU</I> transcription start site |CITS:[16804183]|. 
                     <I>narU</I> expressed from a plasmid restores the ability 
                     of a strain lacking <i>narK</i> to export nitrite from the 
                     cytoplasm |CITS: [9037773]|. <i>&Delta;(narUnarK)</I> 
                     strains are defective in nitrate dependent growth; single 
                     <i>&Delta;narU</I> or <i>&Delta;narK</I> strains can import
                     nitrate at rates similar to wild-type. A multicopy plasmid 
                     expressing <i>narU</i> complements a <i>narK</i> mutation 
                     for nitrite excretion but not uptake |CITS: [11967075]|. 
                     Under anaerobic conditions NarU confers a selective 
                     advantage during nitrate starvation or very slow growth 
                     |CITS: [16804183]|. NarU catalysed nitrate uptake is 
                     mechanistically linked to nitrite expulsion - NarU may 
                     function as a nitrate:nitrite antiporter or as a 
                     H<sup>+</sup> symporter for nitrate import and nitrite 
                     export |CITS: [18691156]|. If NarU functions as a symporter
                     the co-transported ion is unlikely to be a proton but could
                     be a sodium or potassium ion |CITS: [23523348]|.
                     NarU has 12 transmembrane (TM) helices in two domains - TM 
                     1-6 form the N-terminal domain and TM 7-12 form the 
                     C-terminal domain |CITS:[18691156], [23523348]|. 4 
                     conserved residues (Arg87, Arg303, Asn173 and Tyr261) form 
                     a putative substrate binding site. NarU binds nitrate with 
                     high affinity (K<sub>D</sub> = 33&micro;M); the binding 
                     affinity for nitrite is approximately 10 times lower |CITS:
                     [23523348]|.
                     Review: |CITS:[7747940], [23934766]|."
