     gene            complement(1548229..1549926)
                     /gene="maeA"
                     /locus_tag="b1479"
                     /label=maeA
     CDS             complement(1548229..1549926)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="maeA"
                     /locus_tag="b1479"
                     /product="malate dehydrogenase
                     (oxaloacetate-decarboxylating)"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.38"
                     /label=maeA
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> encodes two ""malic enzymes"" that 
                     catalyze the decarboxylation of malate to form pyruvate 
                     with concomitant reduction of NAD(P)<sup>+</sup>. One 
                     enzyme, MaeA (described here) requires NAD<sup>+</sup>, 
                     while the other, |FRAME: MALIC-NADP-MONOMER MaeB|, requires
                     NADP<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [4390688][17557829]|. Metabolic 
                     flux through the malic enzymes during aerobic growth on 
                     glycerol as the sole source of carbon appears to be 
                     essentially zero |CITS: [29107627]|. In a strain blocked in
                     the fermentative metabolism of pyruvate, overexpressed 
                     NAD<sup>+</sup>-dependent malate dehydrogenase can support 
                     growth by catalyzing the normally non-physiological 
                     reductive carboxylation of pyruvate to form malate |CITS: 
                     [9212416]|.
                     MaeA is present as a mixture of monomer, homotetramer and 
                     homooctamer in solution |CITS: [17215140]|. Mg<sup>2+</sup>
                     and Mn<sup>2+</sup> appear to stabilize two different 
                     conformational states of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [224913][6360216]|. MaeA has been engineered to utilize the
                     bioorthogonal co-substrates nicotinamide flucytosine 
                     dinucleotide (NFCD) and nicotinamide cytosine dinucleotide 
                     (NCD) in place of NAD |CITS: [JI11]|. A crystal structure 
                     of the engineered variant, L310R/Q401C, has been solved 
                     |CITS: [33523590]|.
                     Expression of <i>maeA</i> is induced by growth on acetate 
                     |CITS: [11815613]|. A <i>maeA</i> mutant does not show a 
                     defect when grown on acetate; however, a <i>maeB maeA</i> 
                     double mutant in a <i>pckA</i> mutant background abolishes 
                     growth on acetate |CITS: [11815613]|. A <i>maeB maeA</i> 
                     double mutant alone shows a severe lag phase during the 
                     transition from growth on glucose to growth on acetate 
                     |CITS: [16141204]|. MaeA, but not MaeB, can reduce NAD(P) 
                     in the presence of fumarate though not as efficiently as in
                     the presence of malate. Fumarase activity of MaeA can be 
                     inhibited by oxaloacetate and aspartate, and fumarate can 
                     inhibit MaeA malic enzyme activity in a dose-dependent 
                     manner |CITS: [37634412]|.
                     Overexpression or deletion of <i>maeA</i> has been utilized
                     in metabolic engineering for production of several 
                     compounds, e.g. by |CITS: [9212416][21097588][2711 
                     6993][27342774][29019075][29800611][33668723]|. SfcA: 
                     |CITS: [2199308]|"
