     gene            complement(1564902..1566302)
                     /gene="gadB"
                     /locus_tag="b1493"
                     /label=gadB
     CDS             complement(1564902..1566302)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gadB"
                     /locus_tag="b1493"
                     /product="glutamate decarboxylase B"
                     /EC_number="4.1.1.15"
                     /label=gadB
                     /note="GadB, a glutamate decarboxylase enzyme, is part of
                     the glutamate-dependent acid resistance system 2 (AR2) 
                     which confers resistance to extreme acid conditions. There 
                     are two distinct <i>E. coli</i> GAD polypeptides which are 
                     highly similar to one another. AR2 also protects the cell 
                     during anaerobic phosphate starvation when glutamate is 
                     available by preventing damage from weak acids produced 
                     from carbohydrate fermentation. <i>gadABC</i> mutants have 
                     reduced viability after anaerobic phosphate starvation 
                     compared to wild-type |CITS: [17209032]|.
                     The crystal structure of GadB has been determined at acidic
                     and neutral pH to resolutions of 2.0 and 2.3 &Aring;, 
                     respectively |CITS: [12912902]|. GadB is hexameric |CITS: 
                     [12912902]|. At neutral pH GadB is inactive and is found in
                     the cytosol, but when it becomes active at acidic pH, GadB 
                     associates with the membrane |CITS: [12912902]|. Crystal 
                     structures of the autoinhibited, bromide-bound, and 
                     iodide-bound forms have been determined to 1.90, 3.15, and 
                     1.95 &Aring;, respectively |CITS: [16675957]|. These 
                     structures reveal how Cl<sup>-</sup> ions bind and 
                     allosterically stabilize the active form of GadB |CITS: 
                     [16675957]|.
                     GadB is involved in the increased organic solvent tolerance
                     (OTS) mechanism in the <i>crp</i> and <i>cyaA</i> mutants 
                     |CITS: [18499056]|. A &Delta;<i>gadB</i> mutant shows 
                     increased persister cell formation under antibiotic stress 
                     |CITS: [22221537]|. Regulation has been described |CITS: 
                     [12940989]|.
                     Directed evolution of GadB resulted in a mutants that had 
                     significantly higher substrate affinities and higher 
                     enzymatic activities at more neutral pHs |CITS: 
                     [39736285]|.
                     This enzyme in <i>E. coli</i> was shown to interact with 
                     the minimally characterized protein |FRAME: YADG-MONOMER| 
                     in a protein-enzyme interactions (PEIs) study that examined
                     the connection between protein-protein interactions (PPIs) 
                     and metabolomics networks studied in 22 different 
                     environmental conditions. This PEI may have a metabolic 
                     regulatory role in the |FRAME: PWY0-1305| pathway, in part 
                     because the PEI is conserved across many other bacterial 
                     taxa |CITS: [39903745]|."
