                     /product="putative HTH-type transcriptional regulator
                     YneL"
                     /label=yneL
                     /note="YneL contains a helix-turn-helix motif to bind DNA,
                     and it appears to belong to the AraC family of 
                     transcription factors |CITS: [35880217]|. Upstream of the 
                     <i>yneL</i> gene, a sequence was found that could work as a
                     transcriptional promoter, and the transcription of the 
                     <i>yneL</i> gene is affected by temperature stress and 
                     oxidative stress |CITS: [35880217]|."
     misc_recomb     1585007..1585034
                     /locus_tag="DIF"
                     /note="Site of action for XerCD chromosome resolvase.
                     During chromosomal replication XerC and XerD bind 
                     cooperatively to different halves of dif in the replication
                     terminus and catalyze the recombination reactions required 
                     to separate the two chromosomes."
                     /recombination_class="other"
     gene            complement(1585111..1586433)
                     /gene="hipA"
                     /locus_tag="b1507"
                     /label=hipA
     CDS             complement(1585111..1586433)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hipA"
                     /locus_tag="b1507"
                     /product="trifunctional serine/threonine-protein kinase
                     toxin HipA / serine/threonine kinase / HipAB 
                     toxin/antitoxin complex / DNA-binding transcriptional 
                     repressor"
                     /label=hipA
                     /note="The HipAB system is a type II toxin/antitoxin
                     module.  HipA is a serine/threonine kinase that was the 
                     first protein found to mediate the phenomenon of 
                     persistence in <i>E. coli</i>.  A small fraction of cells 
                     within a population are dormant persister cells; these 
                     cells are phenotypic variants that are not killed by 
                     antibiotics, leading to multidrug tolerance (MDT). 
                     Persistent cells have also been linked with resistance to 
                     UV exposure |CITS: [35490545]|.  Persistence may be 
                     ultimately due to global remodeling of the persister cell's
                     ribosomes |CITS: [25425348]|.
                     HipA expression was shown to activate ppGpp synthesis by 
                     |FRAME: RELA-MONOMER RelA|, which then leads to inhibition 
                     of macromolecular synthesis, triggering growth arrest, and 
                     enabling resistance to &beta;-lactam antibiotics |CITS: 
                     [23667235]|.  ppGpp synthesis is triggered by an increased 
                     concentration of uncharged tRNA<sup>Glu</sup> that is 
                     caused by HipA inhibition of |FRAME: GLURS-MONOMER GluRS| 
                     activity |CITS: [24095282][24343429]|.
                     Autophosphorylation of HipA, which inactivates it, may be 
                     the crucial initial event that allows persister cells to 
                     revert to a growth phenotype |CITS: [22999936]|.
                     HipA is toxic in the absence of its binding partner HipB, a
                     transcriptional repressor |CITS: 
                     [1715862][8021189][20616060]|.  Expression of HipA leads to
                     growth arrest due to a shutdown of macromolecular 
                     synthesis, but does not cause cell death |CITS: 
                     [16707675]|.  The HipA-mediated phenotypic switch has been 
                     investigated at the single cell level |CITS: [15308767]|, 
                     and mathematical models have been developed |CITS: 
                     [18721814][22607777]|, suggesting that phenotypic variation
                     in persister levels within a population can be due to 
                     different levels of noise in the regulation of the gene 
                     circuit |CITS: [22607777]|.  The bistability of the HipBA 
                     system has been successfully simulated with a stochastic 
                     model that includes reciprocal coupling of free HipA to the
                     cellular growth rate |CITS: [24344277]|.
                     Above a certain threshold, the level of HipA in the cell 
                     determines the typical duration of growth arrest.  The 
                     threshold level is determined by the level of HipB |CITS: 
                     [20616060]|.
                     HipA is a member of the PI 3/4-kinase superfamily, capable 
                     of autophosphorylation at Ser150.  This activity depends on
                     two aspartates, Asp309, which is located in the active 
                     site, and Asp332, which binds the Mg<sup>2+</sup> cofactor.
                      This phosphorylation activity is required both for HipA 
                     toxicity and for antibiotic resistance conferred by 
                     overexpression of HipA |CITS: [17041039]|.  Unlike in other
                     kinases, the autophosphorylated Ser150 residue is not 
                     located in a solvent-accessible loop, but in the catalytic 
                     core P-loop of the protein (""in-state""). Phosphorylation 
                     of Ser150 results in a shift to an ""out-state"" in which 
                     the serine residue is solvent exposed |CITS: [22999936]|. A
                     molten-globule-like intermediate form of unphosphorylated 
                     HipA that is aggregation-prone has been described, in which
                     the serine residue and its two flanking hydrophobic 
                     neighbors (Val/Ile) are solvent exposed, enabling 
                     phosphorylation of the serine residue |CITS: [36802529]|.
                     One target of the kinase activity was suggested to be 
                     EF-Tu, which is inactivated by phosphorylation at Thr382 
                     |CITS: [19150849]|.  However, it was later shown that the 
                     <i>in vivo</i> target of HipA is GluRS.  HipA 
                     phosphorylates the conserved Ser239 residue near the active
                     site of GluRS and inhibits its aminoacylation activity 
                     |CITS: [24095282][24343429]|.  Additional substrates of 
                     HipA and the HipA7 mutant were investigated by quantitative
                     phosphoproteomics, confirming GluRS as the primary target 
                     of both the wild-type and variant kinase |CITS: 
                     [30206139]|.
                     Although HipA does not itself bind the <i>hip</i> 
                     regulatory region, it plays an indirect role in the 
                     regulation of <i>hipBA</i> transcription via its binding to
                     HipB |CITS: [1715862][8021189]|.  Binding to HipB may lock 
                     HipA into a catalytically inactive conformation |CITS: 
                     [19150849]|.  The crystal structure of an extended promoter
                     region that contained two operator sites revealed that the 
                     interaction with HipB bound to both sites leads to 
                     dimerizarion of HipA, which blocks the active sites of both
                     HipA molecules |CITS: [26222023]|.
                     A variety of crystal structures of HipA, including in the 
                     apo form, in complex with ATP or ADP, and in a complex with
                     DNA and HipB have been solved |CITS: 
                     [19150849][19622872][22999936][26222023]|.  Inhibitors of 
                     HipA have been identified by structure-based virtual 
                     screening |CITS: [27190591]|.
                     The <i>hipA7</i> allele that was initially isolated |CITS: 
                     [6348026]| is a gain-of-function mutant that contains two 
                     amino acid changes |CITS: [14622409]|, one of which likely 
                     results in weakened interaction between HipA and HipB and 
                     thus resulting in higher HipA kinase activity |CITS: 
                     [19150849]|.  <i>hipA7</i>-like mutations have been shown 
                     to be clinically relevant and are responsible for drug 
                     tolerance in recurrent UTIs caused by <i>E. coli</i> 
                     strains |CITS: [26222023]|.
                     A <i>hipBA</i> deletion strain shows a decrease in 
                     stationary phase persister cells after treatment with 
                     antibiotics |CITS: [15576765]| and a decrease in biofilm 
                     formation even in the absence of antibiotics |CITS: 
                     [23329678]|.  Expression of wild-type <i>hipA</i> in excess
                     of <i>hipB</i> results in the shutdown of macromolecular 
                     synthesis and a high frequency of persister formation 
                     |CITS: [16707675]|.  Overexpression of <i>gltX</i> 
                     suppresses HipA toxicity |CITS: [24095282][24343429]|.  
                     Ectopic expression of HipA leads to expression of the 
                     <i>relBEF</i> toxin/antitoxin module |CITS: [23432955]|.  
                     HipA enhances repression of the <i>relA</i> promoter by 
                     HipB |CITS: [24089053]|. <i>hipA</i> is most highly 
                     expressed during stationary phase |CITS: [19129642]|.
                     The HipA binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     HipA: ""<b>hi</b>gh <b>p</b>ersistence factor <b>A</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [6348026]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [24766804][24056929][22060041][18453274][1 
                     9179767][17143318][15807669]|
                     Note that |CITS: [23993101][25848049]| have been retracted 
                     due to phage contamination |CITS: [29474913][31110010]|."
