     CDS             complement(join(1586922..1588382,1588384..1592343))
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="yneO"
                     /locus_tag="b4696"
                     /product="AIDA-I family autotransporter YneO"
                     /label=yneO
                     /note="<i>ydeK</i> is a putative autotransporter-encoding 
                     sequence; <i>ydeK</i> is a pseudogene in strain MG1655 
                     |CITS: [20447993]|.
                     YdeK has C-terminal similarity to <i>Saccharomyces 
                     cerevisiae</i> Isp42p and to <i>Neurospora crassa</i> 
                     MOM38, which localize to the mitochondrial outer membrane 
                     |CITS: [8274505]|.
                     In a random transposon mutagenesis study, <i>ydeK</i> was 
                     found to be required for growth under optimum (rich medium 
                     at 37&deg;C) growth conditions but not under cold 
                     conditions (15&deg;C) or in minimal medium |CITS: 
                     [15380559]|.  However, other studies have not found the 
                     gene to be essential for growth in rich medium.
                     A &Delta;<i>ydeK</i> mutant has aggravating genetic 
                     interactions with genes involved in mismatch repair |CITS: 
                     [26774489]|.
                     "
     gene            complement(1592874..1594466)
                     /gene="lsrK"
                     /locus_tag="b1511"
                     /label=lsrK
     CDS             complement(1592874..1594466)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lsrK"
                     /locus_tag="b1511"
                     /product="autoinducer-2 kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.189"
                     /label=lsrK
                     /note="LsrK is a kinase that is able to phosphorylate the 
                     quorum-sensing autoinducer molecule AI-2 |CITS: 
                     [20025244]|.  Based on its similarity to LsrK from 
                     <i>Salmonella typhimurium</i>, LsrK was already predicted 
                     to have AI-2 kinase activity |CITS: [15601708]|. 
                     Phosphorylation of AI-2 by LsrK is essential for 
                     internalization and sequestration of AI-2 |CITS: 
                     [22384939]|.
                     Kinetic analysis of LsrK from <i>Salmonella</i> was most 
                     consistent with a rapid equilibrium ordered reaction 
                     mechanism with ATP binding first |CITS: [23672516]|.
                     LsrK has been overexpressed and crystallized |CITS: 
                     [28471356]|.  LsrK copurifies with |FRAME: PTSH-MONOMER|, 
                     and co-crystal structures have been solved; LsrK domain I 
                     binds HPr, and domain II binds ATP |CITS: [29868643]|.
                     HPr inhibits LsrK activity <i>in vitro</i>; phosphorylated 
                     HPr has a reduced ability to inhibit LsrK activity. 
                     HPr-mediated regulation of LsrK may link sugar metabolism 
                     to quorum sensing activity |CITS: [29868643]|.
                     An <i>lsrK</i> mutant accumulates AI-2 in the culture fluid
                     |CITS: [15601708][15743955]|.  In the absence of glucose, 
                     <i>lsrK</i> expression is significantly decreased in a 
                     <i>luxS</i> mutant compared to wild type |CITS: 
                     [16321939]|.
                     The biofilm architecture of an <i>lsrK</i> mutant is 
                     different from wild type |CITS: [17557827]|.  Expression of
                     <i>lsrK</i> is higher in biofilms compared to planktonic 
                     growth |CITS: [12657059]|, and LsrK itself regulates the 
                     expression of biofilm-related genes |CITS: [17557827]|.  
                     Overexpression of LsrK induces transcription of <i>mcbR</i>
                     |CITS: [18309357]|.
                     LsrK is being investigated as a potential drug target to 
                     address antibiotic resistance |CITS: [33152241]|.
                     LsrK: ""<i><b>l</b>ux<b>S</b></i>-<b>r</b>egulated 
                     <b>k</b>inase"" |CITS: [15601708]|"
