     gene            complement(1607572..1608960)
                     /gene="sad"
                     /locus_tag="b1525"
                     /label=sad
     CDS             complement(1607572..1608960)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sad"
                     /locus_tag="b1525"
                     /product="trifunctional succinate semialdehyde
                     dehydrogenase (NAD(P)(+)) Sad / succinate semialdehyde 
                     dehydrogenase (NAD(+)) / glutarate-semialdehyde 
                     dehydrogenase"
                     /EC_number="1.2.1.24; 1.2.1.20"
                     /label=sad
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> K-12 encodes two succinate
                     semialdehyde dehydrogenases, Sad and |FRAME: CPLX0-7688 
                     ""GabD""|, which differ in their ability to utilize 
                     NAD<sup>+</sup> and NADP<sup>+</sup>. Sad is the smaller 
                     enzyme and preferentially utilizes NAD<sup>+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [7004491]|; the gene encoding it was long unknown. Sad also
                     functions as a glutarate semialdehyde dehydrogenase during 
                     degradation of L-lysine |CITS: [30498244]|.
                     YneI was found to be identical to the orphan enzymatic 
                     activity of NAD<sup>+</sup>-dependent succinate 
                     semialdehyde dehydrogenase (Sad). Its activity was first 
                     predicted using phylogenetic profiles |CITS: [16507154]| 
                     and later confirmed biochemically |CITS: [17873044]|.
                     Sad has an important role during growth on arginine and is 
                     likely required for growth on putrescine as the sole source
                     of nitrogen. Its main function may be to prevent 
                     accumulation of the toxic compound succinate semialdehyde 
                     |CITS: [17873044]|. Sad is more important than GabD for 
                     growth on putrescine as the sole source of carbon |CITS: 
                     [20639325]|.
                     Growth of a <i>sad</i> deletion mutant is strongly 
                     inhibited by succinate semialdehyde. Expression of 
                     <i>sad</i> is induced by succinate semialdehyde |CITS: 
                     [17873044]|; Sad activity is induced by growth on GABA as a
                     nitrogen source |CITS: [7004491][7009588]| and by growth on
                     putrescine |CITS: [20639325]|. <i>sad</i> expression is 
                     increased by external acidification |CITS: [18302792]|.
                     A variety of mutations found in an adaptive laboratory 
                     evolution (ALE) experiment revealed <i>sad</i> has the 
                     ability to substitute for |FRAME: ERYTH4PDEHYDROG-MONOMER| 
                     in |FRAME: PYRIDOXSYN-PWY| and for |FRAME: G8205-MONOMER| 
                     in glycolytic pathways. A Q262R mutation significantly 
                     improved Sad's catalytic efficiency on two sugar phosphates
                     (|FRAME: ERYTHROSE-4P| and |FRAME: GAP|) by lowering the 
                     K<sub>m</sub>. Crystal structures of the Q262R mutant are 
                     available. Gene expression of <i>sad</i> was also increased
                     as a result of a mutation in |FRAME: G6812| causing 
                     consistently up-regulation of expression and an indirect 
                     effect of a mutation in |FRAME: EG10998| that caused lysine
                     accumulation |CITS: [39406738]|.
                     Sad: ""<b>s</b>uccinate semi<b>a</b>ldehyde 
                     <b>d</b>ehydrogenase"" |CITS: [6756331]|"
