     gene            1609061..1609942
                     /gene="ptrR"
                     /locus_tag="b1526"
                     /label=ptrR
     CDS             1609061..1609942
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ptrR"
                     /locus_tag="b1526"
                     /product="multifunctional putrescine utilization regulator
                     PtrR / DNA-binding transcriptional regulator 
                     PtrR-L-glutamine / PtrR-glt / PtrR-4-aminobutanoate"
                     /label=ptrR
                     /note="The LysR-type transcriptional regulator PtrR,
                     putrescine utilization regulator, is involved in 
                     L-glutamate and putrescine metabolism and in resistance to 
                     the tetracycline group of antibiotics 
                     |CITS:[35508583][34428301][35835781]|. Additionally, was 
                     predicted to regulate genes related to iron and succinate 
                     oxidation |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     PtrR contains a helix-turn-helix motif for DNA binding in 
                     its N-terminal domain |CITS: [35880217]|. In systematic 
                     studies of oligomerization, it was shown that some members 
                     of the LysR family, like PtrR, interact with other members 
                     of the family to form heterodimers, but the physiological 
                     significance of this is unknown |CITS: [21187915]|. The 
                     efficiency of PtrR binding to DNA was found to be more 
                     robust in the presence of Gln than Glu or putrescine 
                     |CITS:[35835781]|.
                     Genome-wide PtrR binding sites were determined <i>in 
                     vivo</i> by the chromatin immunoprecipitation method 
                     combined with lambda exonuclease digestion (ChIP-exo) in 
                     glucose M9 minimal medium |CITS: [34428301]| and by 
                     ChIP-seq in M9 minimal medium with putrescine and with 
                     tryptone |CITS:[35835781]|. The transcriptional response to
                     the deletion of PtrR was characterized by RNA-seq in M9 
                     minimal medium with Ptr and/or Glu as nitrogen source 
                     |CITS:[35508583]|. By the integrative analysis of ChIP-exo 
                     and RNA-seq data, it was determined that PtrR directly 
                     represses the transcription of the <i>sad</i> gene 
                     (encoding the succinate-semialdehyde dehydrogenase) and 
                     probably <i>fnrS</i>]|. In presence of &gamma;-aminobutyric
                     acid (GABA), PtrR unbinds, derepressing <i>sad</i> and 
                     <i>fnrS</i> genes |CITS:[35508583]|.
                     A knockout mutant for the <i>ptrR</i> gene showed 
                     deficiency in growth with L-glutamate and putrescine as 
                     nitrogen and energy sources; it was concluded that PtrR has
                     an important role in regulation for putrescine utilization 
                     as an energy source |CITS:[35508583]|.
                     The <i>ptrR</i> mutant showed defects in growth with 
                     glycine, l-ornithine, or gamma-hydroxybutyrate in M9 medium
                     with l-glutamate as the sole nitrogen source 
                     |CITS:[35508583]|. The <i>E. coli</i> WT strain had a 
                     longer lag phase than the <i>ptrR</i> mutant during aerobic
                     growth with putrescine and Glu as the nitrogen source in M9
                     medium with glycerol as the primary carbon source 
                     |CITS:[35508583]|.
                     A PtrR knockout strain showed increased resistance to 
                     chlorotetracycline and to demeclocycline antibiotics 
                     |CITS:[35508583]|. A <i>ptrR</i> mutant showed growth 
                     defects in M9 medium with putrescine, glutamine, or 
                     glutamic acid, while in M9 with tryptone, growth was not 
                     affected relative to the wild type |CITS:[35835781]|. The 
                     deletion of <i>ptrR</i> decreased the resistance to SDS 
                     (sodium dodecyl sulfate) of an <i>acrR</i> knockout strain 
                     |CITS:[35835781]|.
                     An E97A mutation in <i>ptrR</i> resulted in the 
                     <i>sad-glsB-yneG</i> operon being consistently up-regulated
                     |CITS: [39406738]|.
                     Upstream of the <i>ptrR</i> gene, a sequence was found that
                     could work as a transcriptional promoter, and the 
                     transcription of the <i>ptrR</i> gene is affected by 
                     temperature and conditions that lead to biofilm formation 
                     |CITS: [35880217]|."
