     gene            complement(1614495..1615394)
                     /gene="eamA"
                     /locus_tag="b1533"
                     /label=eamA
     CDS             complement(1614495..1615394)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="eamA"
                     /locus_tag="b1533"
                     /product="trifunctional cysteine/O-acetylserine exporter
                     EamA / cysteine exporter / O-acetyl-L-serine exporter"
                     /label=eamA
                     /note="EamA (formerly YdeD) is an integral membrane
                     protein |CITS: [10844694]|. EamA is implicated in exporting
                     metabolites of the cysteine pathway; strains overexpressing
                     <i>eamA</i> do not grow on minimal medium with sulfate as 
                     the sole sulfur source but do grow when supplemented with 
                     L-cystine or with L-methionine plus thiosulfate; strains 
                     overexpressing <i>eamA</i> (and grown with L-methionine 
                     plus thiosulfate) excrete cysteine, O-acetyl-L-serine and 
                     other unidentified compounds* into the culture medium 
                     |CITS: [10844694]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>eamA</i> counteracts the toxicity 
                     associated with increased levels of O-acetylserine and 
                     increases resistance to the glutamine antagonist, |FRAME: 
                     L-AZASERINE|; EamA appears to act independently of |FRAME: 
                     EG12445-MONOMER ""EamB""|, an alternate 
                     <i>O</i>-acetylserine and cysteine efflux permease; EamA 
                     may function to modulate O-acetyl-L-serine concentration 
                     during exponential growth |CITS: [12562784]|.
                     EamA, |FRAME: CPLX0-8152 ""TcyJLN""| and |FRAME: 
                     G6934-MONOMER ""TcyP""| participate in an 
                     L-cystine/L-cysteine shuttle system which is suggested to 
                     protect the cell membrane under oxidative stress 
                     conditions; L-cystine importers (TcyJLN and TcyP) cooperate
                     with EamA to supply reducing equivalents to the periplasm; 
                     operation of the shuttle is advantageous because it allows 
                     cells to scavenge periplasmic H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     without <i>de novo</i> L-cysteine synthesis |CITS: 
                     [20351115][25837721]| (see also |CITS: [32900959]|).
                     Transcription of <i>eamA, tcyJ, tcyL, tcyN, dcyD</i> and to
                     a lesser extent <i>tcyP</i> is induced by 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> |CITS: [20351115][25837721]|. 
                     Overexpression of <i>eamA</i> confers resistance to the 
                     toxic chemical, bromoacetate |CITS: [20921376]|. Deletion 
                     of <i>eamA</i> in <i>E. coli</i> strain BL21 is associated 
                     with a decline in extracellular |FRAME: 5-AMINO-LEVULINATE|
                     concentration |CITS: [34826004]|.
                     In the Transporter Classifcation Database |CITS: 
                     [26546518]|, EamA is a member of the Drug/Metabolite 
                     Exporter (DME) Family within the Drug/Metabolite 
                     Transporter (DMT) Superfamily.
                     * glutamine and asparagine |CITS: [DASSLER01]| as 
                     referenced in |CITS: [12562784]|."
