     gene            complement(1619592..1621637)
                     /gene="dcp"
                     /locus_tag="b1538"
                     /label=dcp
     CDS             complement(1619592..1621637)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dcp"
                     /locus_tag="b1538"
                     /product="bifunctional peptidyl-dipeptidase Dcp / 
                     peptidyl-dipeptidase"
                     /EC_number="3.4.15.5"
                     /label=dcp
                     /note="Dipeptidyl carboxypeptidase is a peptidase capable
                     of cleaving peptide bonds in amino-blocked
                     small peptide substrates |CITS: 
                     [4554640][216006][8226676]|. It is
                     required for growth when only amino-blocked peptides such 
                     as N-acetyl-alanylalanylalanine and 
                     N-benzoyl-glycylhistidylleucine are available as carbon 
                     sources |CITS: [216006][8226676]|.
                     A fraction of the total pool of dipeptidyl carboxypeptidase
                     is in the periplasm |CITS: [216006]|. Dcp<sup>-</sup> 
                     mutants are unable to grow with 
                     <i>N</i>-acetylalanylalanylalanine as the sole nitrogen 
                     source |CITS: [216006]|.
                     Recombinant enzyme purified from cell extracts appeared to 
                     be a monomer based on an apparent molecular mass of 80 kDa 
                     by gel filtration chromatography and 78 kDa by SDS-PAGE 
                     |CITS: [8226676]|.
                     Protease inhibitors and divalent cations had varying 
                     effects on enzymatic activity. PMSF showed little 
                     inhibition; the cysteine protease inhibitor E64, trypsin 
                     inhibitors, pepstatin, EDTA, and NaCl showed some 
                     inhibition; chymostatin was a relatively stronger 
                     inhibitor; and 1,10-phenanthroline completely inhibited. 
                     Among divalent cations, some activation was seen with 
                     Mn<sup>2+</sup>, Mg<sup>2+</sup>, Ca<sup>2+</sup> and 
                     Co<sup>2+</sup>. Strong inhibitors included 
                     Cu<sup>2+</sup>, Zn<sup>2+</sup> and Ni<sup>2+</sup> |CITS:
                     [8226676]|. In contrast, a His<sub>6</sub>-tagged 
                     recombinant enzyme had somewhat different properties 
                     including activation rather than inhibition by 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> and NaCl |CITS: 
                     [CHEN09WORDJMICRO][LO10BIOLOGIA]|. Captopril, an inhibitor 
                     of the functionally similar mammalian angiotensin 
                     I-converting enzyme (EC 3.4.15.1) also strongly inhibits 
                     Dcp activity |CITS: [19558329]|. Fluorogenic and 
                     chromogenic peptide substrates have been described |CITS: 
                     [8226676][208842][4554640]|.
                     Dcp has been described as a C-terminal exopeptidase and is 
                     a member of the M3 family of metallopeptidases |CITS: 
                     [8226676][CHEN09WORDJMICRO]|. The active site of DCP 
                     contains an HEXXH motif that is found in other zinc 
                     metalloenzymes. His470, His474 and putatively Glu499 
                     coordinate the zinc ion at the active site. Site-directed 
                     mutagenesis, biophysical measurements and computer modeling
                     suggested that His470, Glu471, His474, Glu499 and Phe500 
                     are important to catalytic activity |CITS: [LO10BIOLOGIA]|.
                     The crystal structure of <i>E. coli</i> Dcp has been 
                     determined in the presence of an inhibitory octapeptide at 
                     2.0 &Aring; resolution. The structure showed close 
                     similarity with mammalian neurolysin/thimet oligopeptidase,
                     and a similar topology with structures of the mammalian 
                     angiotensin I-converting enzyme |CITS: [15876371]|."
