     gene            1621774..1622520
                     /gene="ydfG"
                     /locus_tag="b1539"
                     /label=ydfG
     CDS             1621774..1622520
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ydfG"
                     /locus_tag="b1539"
                     /product="multifunctional 3-hydroxy acid dehydrogenase
                     YdfG / 3-hydroxy acid dehydrogenase / L-allo-threonine 
                     dehydrogenase / malonic semialdehyde reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.298"
                     /label=ydfG
                     /note="The <i>ydfG</i> gene belongs to the short-chain 
                     dehydrogenase/reductase (SDR) family and encodes a 
                     homotetrameric NADP<sup>+</sup>-dependent 3-hydroxy acid 
                     dehydrogenase, also called a serine dehydrogenase (SerDH). 
                     The high K<sub>m</sub> for NADP<sup>+</sup> indicates that 
                     the enzyme acts in the reductive direction <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [12535615]|.
                     A <i>ydfG</i> deletion mutant only grows poorly with 
                     uridine as the sole source of nitrogen, likely due to 
                     toxicity of the pathway intermediate, malonic semialdehyde 
                     |CITS: [20400551]|.
                     The crystal structure of the ligand-free homotetrameric 
                     SerDH complex and the NADP<sup>+</sup>-phosphate bound 
                     SerDH complex were solved to resolutions of 1.9 and 2.7 
                     &Aring;, respectively. The catalytic residues, Asn106, 
                     Ser134, Tyr147 and Lys151, conserved in most 
                     oxidoreductases within the SDR family, were observed. 
                     Direct mutagenesis studies revealed the C-terminal region 
                     has a role in substrate binding through stabilization of 
                     the substrate-binding loop in the closed form complex 
                     |CITS: [21349860]|.
                     In an experiment using directed cell evolution of a 
                     &Delta;<i>panD</i> mutant (containing a damaged CoA 
                     biosynthesis pathway), a pathway for &beta;-alanine 
                     biosynthesis via uracil degradation emerged. One of the 
                     required mutations is a K108E change in YdfG, which 
                     decreases, but does not abolish its enzymatic activity. The
                     mutation decreases reduction of 3-oxopropanoate, an 
                     intermediate of the rerouted &beta;-alanine biosynthesis 
                     pathway, to malonic semialdehyde (MSA), thus increasing the
                     availability of MSA to the pathway while not allowing MSA 
                     to build up to toxic levels |CITS: [30327558]|.
                     The half life of <i>ydfG</i> mRNA is substantially longer 
                     in cells lacking |FRAME: G7459-MONOMER RppH| than in wild 
                     type cells |CITS: [22984254]|."
