     gene            complement(1635596..1635811)
                     /gene="cspB"
                     /locus_tag="b1557"
                     /label=cspB
     CDS             complement(1635596..1635811)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cspB"
                     /locus_tag="b1557"
                     /product="Qin prophage; cold shock-like protein CspB"
                     /label=cspB
                     /note="CspB is a member of the CspA family of cold-shock 
                     proteins and is induced on transition to cold temperatures 
                     |CITS: [8022261][9140061]|. <i>cspB</i> belongs to a small 
                     set of genes whose expression is upregulated and whose 
                     absence is a competitive disadvantage in long-term 
                     stationary phase (LTSP) |CITS: [32753505]|.
                     CspB was initially identified based on its similarity with 
                     CspA |CITS: [8022261]|. Cold-shock proteins are typically 
                     involved in binding RNA to prevent formation of deleterious
                     secondary structure at low temperatures. Based on an <i>in 
                     vitro</i> selection method, CspB preferentially binds to 
                     U/T stretches in target RNAs |CITS: [10476034]|.
                     Like CspA and CsdA, expression of CspB is regulated by a 
                     ""cold box"" in its 5' UTR |CITS: [9422597]|. Full 
                     expression also depends on an internal sequence downstream 
                     from the initiation codon, termed the Downstream Box (DB). 
                     The DB may increase the affinity of <i>cspB</i> mRNA for 
                     16S rRNA |CITS: [10482531]|.
                     Transcription of <i>cspB</i> is induced by a temperature 
                     shift to below 20&deg;C, with maximal expression at 
                     15&deg;C |CITS: [9140061]|. Cold induction of CspB 
                     increases in the absence of functional CspA and can occur 
                     even in the presence of antibiotics that block protein 
                     synthesis |CITS: [9371456][10074075]|. <i>cspB</i> 
                     transcription levels change in response to temperature 
                     cycling |CITS: [23815967]|. In defined rich medium at 
                     37&deg;C, expression of <i>cspB</i> is highest in mid-log 
                     phase. Transcription of <i>cspB</i> is induced by cold 
                     shock in defined minimal medium, but not in defined rich 
                     medium |CITS: [24952137]|. Expression of <i>cspB</i> may be
                     indirectly regulated by CRP |CITS: [25637299]|. Expression 
                     of <i>cspB</i> is upregulated in LTSP, and it is expressed 
                     at higher levels in LTSP than in any other growth phase. A 
                     <i>cspB</i> mutant has a competitive disadvantage compared 
                     to wild-type in LTSP |CITS: [32753505]|.
                     A <i>cspABEG</i> quadruple null mutant is cold-sensitive, 
                     forming filamentous cells |CITS: [11298285]|. The 
                     transcriptional response to cold shock in wild-type and the
                     quadruple mutant has been analyzed |CITS: [15466053]|. An 
                     <i>in-silico</i> comparative study of evolution, structure 
                     and function was carried out for the 9 CspA family of 
                     proteins in <i>E. coli K-12</i>. Despite high homology 
                     (>70% amino acid sequence similarity), it was determined 
                     that 4 paralogous pairs could be identified and served as 
                     the focus for the comparative analyses: CspA/CspB, 
                     CspC/CspE, CspG/CspI and CspF/CspH |CITS: [37333716]|. A 
                     follow-up study conducted mutational analysis and molecular
                     modeling to determine selection pressure on the paralogous 
                     pairs |CITS: [39779584]|. Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [8022259][9484881][17437059]|"
                     /translation="MSNKMTGLVKWFNADKGFGFISPVDGSKDVFVHFSAIQNDNYRTL
                     FEGQKVTFSIESGAKGPAAANVIITD"
