     gene            1636112..1636324
                     /gene="cspF"
                     /locus_tag="b1558"
                     /label=cspF
     CDS             1636112..1636324
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cspF"
                     /locus_tag="b1558"
                     /product="Qin prophage; cold shock-like protein CspF"
                     /label=cspF
                     /note="CspF belongs to the family of cold shock proteins
                     |CITS: [9484881]|.
                     Expression of <i>cspF</i> is highest during the mid-log 
                     growth phase in both defined rich and minimal medium at 
                     37&deg;C and is not cold-inducible |CITS: [24952137]|. 
                     <i>cspF</i> belongs to a small set of genes whose 
                     expression is upregulated in long-term stationary phase 
                     (LTSP). Loss of <i>cspF</i> does not cause a competitive 
                     disadvantage in LTSP |CITS: [32753505]|. The <i>cspF</i> 
                     gene is highly expressed in long-term stationary phase 
                     (LTSP)  |CITS:[32753505]|. Diverse expression responses of 
                     the cold shock proteins, CspA-I, were found under different
                     stress conditions, specifically cold, heat, rich vs minimal
                     media and oxidative stress caused by 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. Expression of <i>cspF</i> is 
                     significantly up in response to H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     stress conditions and to heat stress. Contrary to previous 
                     reports, the expression is down-regulated throughout the 
                     growth cycle in both minimal and rich media and does show 
                     significant up-regulation under conditions of cold shock 
                     |CITS: [40312551]|.
                     An <i>in-silico</i> comparative study of evolution, 
                     structure and function was carried out for the 9 CspA 
                     family of proteins in <i>E. coli K-12</i>. Despite high 
                     homology (>70% amino acid sequence similarity), it was 
                     determined that 4 paralogous pairs could be identified and 
                     served as the focus for the comparative analyses: 
                     CspA/CspB, CspC/CspE, CspG/CspI and CspF/CspH. CspF 
                     exhibited lower binding affinity to single stranded RNA, 
                     less hydrophobicity in the surface area where the 
                     protein-ssRNA interaction would occur, and lower binding 
                     free energy compared to its paralogous partner, CspH |CITS:
                     [37333716]|. A follow-up study conducted mutational 
                     analysis and molecular modeling to determine selection 
                     pressure on the paralogous pairs |CITS: [39779584]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17437059]|"
