                     [PMID 3019679]"
     gene            complement(1639603..1639890)
                     /gene="relE"
                     /locus_tag="b1563"
                     /label=relE
     CDS             complement(1639603..1639890)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="relE"
                     /locus_tag="b1563"
                     /product="trifunctional Qin prophage; mRNA interferase
                     toxin RelE / RNase / RelB-RelE antitoxin/toxin complex / 
                     DNA-binding transcriptional repressor"
                     /label=relE
                     /note="RelE which its antitoxin, |FRAME: EG10836-MONOMER|,
                     is a BECR-fold toxin in the RelE/ParE superfamily of type 
                     II toxin-antitoxin systems |CITS: [9767574][39998207]|. 
                     RelE inhibits protein translation by catalyzing cleavage of
                     mRNA in the A site of the ribosome in response to amino 
                     acid starvation |CITS: 
                     [12526800][12787364][20005802][21324908]|.
                     RelE is involved in regulation of cellular protein 
                     translation under nutritional stress conditions |CITS: 
                     [11717402][12526800][12787364]|. RelE-mediated translation 
                     inhibition is reported to cause reversible inhibition of 
                     cell growth |CITS: [12123459]|. The activity of |FRAME: 
                     SSRA-RNA| counteracts RelE translation inhibition |CITS: 
                     [12526800][12787364]|. RelE and RF1 functionally interact 
                     |CITS: [19019162]|.
                     An analysis of the targets and site specificity of RelE 
                     showed some cleavage site specificity, favoring a G 
                     immediately downstream of the cleavage site, a strong bias 
                     towards cleavage in the 5' end of mRNAs, and high codon 
                     position preference. Like other <i>E. coli</i> 
                     endoribonuclease toxins, RelE activity does not affect 
                     mature ribosomes, but inhibits ribosome biogenesis, likely 
                     by affecting the translation of a specific set of ribosomal
                     proteins |CITS: [34544284]|.
                     RelE and RelB physically interact |CITS: [11274135]|. When 
                     cells are starved of amino acids, Lon protease degrades 
                     RelB; RelB degradation frees RelE and derepresses 
                     transcription of <i>relBE</i>. RelE accumulates in excess 
                     compared with its RelB antitoxin, and this free RelE causes
                     translation inhibition |CITS: [11717402]|. Conversely, RelB
                     binding to RelE protects RelB from proteolytic degradation 
                     |CITS: [17924660][19747491]|. The structure of the 
                     RelE-RelB complex and cooperative binding to the 
                     <i>relBE</i> promoter region has been studied; at low 
                     levels, RelE enhances the interaction between RelB and the 
                     promoter, while excess RelE destabilizes promoter binding 
                     of the complex |CITS: [18501926][18532983]|. Solution 
                     structures of a RelE mutant together with a RelB peptide 
                     have been determined; RelB appears to induce conformational
                     changes in the RelE active site, inactivating it |CITS: 
                     [19297318]|. Crystal structures of RelE alone and bound to 
                     a heterologous ribosome have elucidated the reaction 
                     mechanism of RelE. RelE binds to the ribosomal A site and 
                     cleaves mRNA after the second nucleotide of the codon; 
                     cleavage is ribosome-dependent |CITS: [20005802]|. The 
                     <i>in vivo</i> frequency and codon specificity of RelE 
                     cleavage has been mapped; contrary to earlier <i>in 
                     vitro</i> results, no sequence preference can be seen <i>in
                     vivo</i>. RelE appears to cleave within the first 100 
                     codons in the coding region of mRNAs |CITS: [21324908]|.
                     Mutations in the <i>relB</i> locus were initially 
                     identified by a ""delayed relaxed"" phenotype, 
                     characterized by a ~10 minute lag period followed by 
                     continued stable RNA synthesis in response to amino acid 
                     starvation |CITS: [5321960][326765]|. This phenotype is due
                     to destabilization of RelB, resulting in hyperactivation of
                     RelE |CITS: [15228536]|.
                     <i>relBE</i> is overexpressed in persister cells. Ectopic 
                     expression of RelE increases the number of persister cells 
                     that survive normally lethal antibiotic treatment |CITS: 
                     [15576765]|. Conversely, deletion of <i>relBE</i> was 
                     reported to increase survival after antibiotic treatment 
                     |CITS: [19707553]| (see also |CITS: [37703274]|).
                     Based on RT-qPCR, it was shown that stress resistance genes
                     (<i>rpoS</i>, <i>marA</i>, <i>ygfA</i>, and <i>relE</i>) 
                     and antibiotic resistance genes (ARGs), especially efflux 
                     genes, are upregulated in the viable but nonculturable 
                     (VBNC) state induced in pathogenic <i>Escherichia coli</i> 
                     strains by low-level chlorination exposure 
                     |CITS:[28662489]|.
                     Phylogenetic and evolutionary analyses of toxin-antitoxin 
                     systems has been performed |CITS: 
                     [14659018][15718296][23543711][39998207]|.
                     The RelE binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [31932311][22060041][19215780][18453274][1 
                     6803593][15807669][12526786][10633087]|"
