     gene            complement(1641877..1642107)
                     /gene="dicC"
                     /locus_tag="b1569"
                     /label=dicC
     CDS             complement(1641877..1642107)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dicC"
                     /locus_tag="b1569"
                     /product="Qin prophage; putative DNA-binding
                     transcriptional regulator DicC"
                     /label=dicC
                     /note="DicC has similarity to the bacteriophage P22 Cro 
                     transcriptional regulator.  The organization of the 
                     <i>dicAC</i> locus is similar to the P22 immunity region. 
                     In the <i>dicA1</i> mutant, expression of the <i>dicAC</i> 
                     locus appears to be under the control of DicC |CITS: 
                     [2836697]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>dicC</i> from a multicopy plasmid can 
                     complement the cell division phenotype of the <i>dicA1</i> 
                     mutant |CITS: [3532030]|.  Expression of <i>dicC</i> is 
                     repressed by DicA |CITS: [2836697]|.
                     The DicC binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     DicC: ""<b>di</b>vision <b>c</b>ontrol <b>C</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [3532030]|"
                     /translation="MLKTDALLYFGSKTKLAQAAGIRLASLYSWKGDLVPEGRAMRLQE
                     ASGGELQYDPKVYDEYRKTKRAGRLNNENHS"
                     /go_function="DNA binding [goid 0003677] [evidence IEA]
                     [GO_REF 0000002]"
                     /go_function="DNA binding [goid 0003677] [evidence IEA]
                     [GO_REF 0000043]"
                     /go_process="regulation of DNA-templated transcription
                     [goid 0006355] [evidence IGI] [PMID 2836697]"
                     /go_process="cell division [goid 0051301] [evidence IEA]
                     [GO_REF 0000043]"
     gene            1642191..1642598
                     /gene="dicA"
                     /locus_tag="b1570"
                     /label=dicA
     CDS             1642191..1642598
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dicA"
                     /locus_tag="b1570"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional dual regulator DicA"
                     /label=dicA
                     /note="The transcription factor DicA, ""<b>Di</b>vision 
                     <b>c</b>ontrol,"" is a temperature-sensitive repressor that
                     controls the transcription of genes involved in the cell 
                     division process |CITS: 
                     [3911021][3532030][3041373][2666395][37439671]| and 
                     activation of its own expression |CITS: [23028867]|. 
                     Nevertheless, the signal to which DicA responds is still 
                     unknown. The family to which DicA belongs has not been 
                     established; it is a protein of 15 kDa with a hypothetical 
                     DNA-binding domain in the N-terminal domain |CITS: 
                     [3532030]|. DicA is homologous in structure and function to
                     RovA (<i>Yersinia</i>) and SlyA (<i>Salmonella</i>), both 
                     of which are activators for pathogenicity-related genes 
                     |CITS: [23028867]|.
                     Rem has been identified as a putative antirepressor of 
                     DicA, encoded in the cryptic prophage Qin, as it induces 
                     the derepression of dicBp, a promoter repressed by DicA 
                     |CITS: [37439671]|.
                     A dicA hypomorphic allele mutant strain had growth defects 
                     and long filamentation, and this phenotype was rescued to 
                     near-wild-type when the gene encoding the TF YjdC (DicD) 
                     was deleted. In a dicA hypomorphic allele mutant strain, 
                     the TF YjdC is transcriptionally downregulated 
                     |CITS:[35835781]|.
                     The DicA binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
