     gene            1643639..1643691
                     /gene="dicF"
                     /locus_tag="b1574"
                     /label=dicF
     misc_RNA        1643639..1643691
                     /gene="dicF"
                     /locus_tag="b1574"
                     /product="Qin prophage; small regulatory RNA DicF"
                     /note="The DicF small regulatory RNA acts to inhibit cell 
                     division |CITS: [2477663]|.  DicF causes inhibition of the 
                     formation of the constriction and the separation of the 
                     replicated chromosomes into the daughter nucleoids |CITS: 
                     [1552861]|.
                     DicF interferes with translation of <i>ftsZ</i> |CITS: 
                     [1372677]| by base-pairing with the <i>ftsZ</i> mRNA |CITS:
                     [27822514]|.  Additional direct targets include <i>xylR</i>
                     and <i>pykA</i>; different residues within DicF are used 
                     for base-pairing with the different targets |CITS: 
                     [27822514]|.  Like SgrS, DicF was found to regulate the 
                     translation of <i>manX</i> indirectly, by facilitating the 
                     interaction of Hfq with a site near the ribosome binding 
                     site, which then inhibits initiation of translation |CITS: 
                     [29294046]|.
                     Under anaerobic conditions, the enolase component of the 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-2381| together with Hfq stabilizes DicF, 
                     leading to a predominantly filamentous morphology of the 
                     cells |CITS: [28874523]|.
                     The DicF-mediated delay in nucleoid segregation is 
                     suppressed by FtsZ overproduction |CITS: [1552861]|.  The 
                     DicF-mediated decrease in FtsZ abundance and activity is 
                     suppressed by an <i>rpoB</i> mutation, and this effect is 
                     partially counteracted by an <i>rpoS</i> mutation |CITS: 
                     [7651342]|.
                     DicF was shown to bind to Hfq |CITS: [14622403][36328526]| 
                     with a K<sub>d</sub> of about 16 nM |CITS: [21510661]|. The
                     DicF RNA is generated via processing of a larger transcript
                     by RNase III and RNase E |CITS: 
                     [1691299][28874523][37439671]|.  DicF predominantly 
                     accumulates under oxygen-limited conditions |CITS: 
                     [28874523]|. It has been observed that DicF production 
                     occurs when <i>E. coli</i> is cultivated aerobically during
                     the stationary phase |CITS: [37439671]|. Additionally, its 
                     expression is induced by urea and high temperatures |CITS: 
                     [37439671]|.
                     Genes similar to <i>dicF</i> occur in multiple 
                     prophage-like elements in a variety of genomes |CITS: 
                     [7508908]|.
                     Overexpression of DicF leads to decreased expression of 
                     RpoS |CITS: [20683441]|, slower cell growth and decreased 
                     swimming and swarming motility and biofilm formation |CITS:
                     [26469694]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [7540245][10068996][15063850][15487940][30 
                     006994][32213244]|"
     gene            1643866..1644054
                     /gene="dicB"
                     /locus_tag="b1575"
                     /label=dicB
     CDS             1643866..1644054
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dicB"
                     /locus_tag="b1575"
                     /product="Qin prophage; cell division inhibition protein
                     DicB"
                     /label=dicB
                     /note="DicB is a small protein encoded on the Qin prophage
                     and is involved in the regulation of cell division |CITS: 
                     [3911021]|.  The N terminus of DicB is essential for its 
                     function as an inhibitor of cell division |CITS: 
                     [27466443]|.
                     |FRAME: EG10596-MONOMER MinC| is involved in both the DicB 
                     pathway of inhibition of cell division and in the MinC-MinD
                     inhibition pathway |CITS: 
                     [2666395][2137246][2211516][1729222]|.  DicB interacts with
                     the C-terminal domain of MinC; the complex then interacts 
                     with the septal ring, where it inhibits stable FtsZ ring 
                     formation |CITS: [12003935]|.  Targeting of DicB-MinC to 
                     the septal ring requires ZipA |CITS: [15060045]|.
                     Inducing expression of the ""<i>dicBF</i> operon"" using a 
                     P<sub><i>lac</i></sub> promoter in place of the native 
                     promoter upstream of <i>ydfA</i> confers substantial 
                     resistance to infection by &lambda;<i>vir</i>.  This 
                     phenotype is mostly due to expression of DicB, which 
                     inhibits injection of phage DNA through the inner membrane 
                     proteins ManYZ.  The DicB expression phenotypes require the
                     interaction of DicB with MinC |CITS: [31527115]|.
                     A <i>dicB</i> mutation suppresses the heat sensitivity of a
                     <i>dicA1 recA</i> double mutant |CITS: [3911021]|. 
                     Overproduction of DicB causes cell filamentation |CITS: 
                     [3041373]|; the phenotype is suppressed by <i>minC</i> 
                     mutations |CITS: [1729222]| and by co-expression of 
                     <i>cbeA</i> |CITS: [22515815]|.  Deletion of <i>dicB</i> 
                     reduces cell viability after exposure to azlocillin or 
                     nalidixic acid |CITS: [21266997]|.  Co-expression of 
                     <i>dicB</i> abolishes the cell lysis phenotype of 
                     overexpression of <i>ydfD</i>, the gene encoded downstream 
                     of <i>dicB</i> |CITS: [26887840]|.
                     Expression of the complex <i>dicB</i> operon is repressed 
                     by DicA |CITS: [3041373]|.  Expression is induced in cells 
                     treated with nalidixic acid or azlocillin |CITS: 
                     [21266997]|.
                     DicB: ""<b>di</b>vision <b>c</b>ontrol <b>B</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [3911021]|"
                     /translation="MKTLLPNVNTSEGCFEIGVTISNPVFTEDAINKRKQERELLNKIC
                     IVSMLARLRLMPKGCAQ"
                     /go_component="cytosol [goid 0005829] [evidence IEA] [PMID
                     19054114]"
                     /go_function="protein binding [goid 0005515] [evidence
                     IPI] [PMID 12003935]"
                     /go_function="protein binding [goid 0005515] [evidence
                     IPI] [PMID 27466443]"
                     /go_process="cell division [goid 0051301] [evidence IEA]
                     [GO_REF 0000043]"
                     /go_process="negative regulation of cell division [goid
                     0051782] [evidence EXP] [PMID 2137246]"
                     /go_process="negative regulation of cell division [goid
                     0051782] [evidence IMP] [PMID 3041373]"
                     /go_process="negative regulation of cell division [goid
                     0051782] [evidence IDA] [PMID 27466443]"
                     /go_process="regulation of cell division [goid 0051302]
                     [evidence IMP] [PMID 3041373]"
     gene            1644051..1644242
                     /gene="ydfD"
                     /locus_tag="b1576"
                     /label=ydfD
     CDS             1644051..1644242
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ydfD"
                     /locus_tag="b1576"
                     /product="Qin prophage; lysis protein"
                     /label=ydfD
                     /note="YdfD is a lysis protein encoded by the Qin
                     prophage. The protein contains a predicted N-terminal 
                     hydrophobic domain, but lacks a recognizable signal peptide
                     |CITS: [26887840]|. YdfD has been shown to interact 
                     directly both <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i> with 
                     |FRAME: EG10370| and may be a specific inhibitor of IspG; 
                     through this interaction YdfD compromises cell wall 
                     biosynthesis leading to cell lysis |CITS: [35163484]|. 
                     Inducing expression of YdfD leads to lysis of 99.9% of 
                     cells within 2 hours. The predicted cytoplasmic domain, 
                     residues 23-63, retains this activity. Both the 
                     co-expression of <i>dicB</i> or of SulA, another protein 
                     that inhibits cell division, abolishes this phenotype, 
                     suggesting that the lytic effect of YdfD is dependent on 
                     cell division |CITS: [26887840]|.
                     A <i>ydfD</i> deletion exhibits aggravating genetic 
                     interactions with genes involved in mismatch repair |CITS: 
                     [26774489]|. Deletions of specific N- and C-terminal 
                     residues reveals that the N-terminal domain of YdfD is 
                     responsible for binding to the N-terminal domain of IspG 
                     and may also recruit its C-terminal domain to IspG |CITS: 
                     [35163484]|.
                     Gene expression levels are influenced by several 
                     environmental stressors, increasing in response to higher 
                     pH, increased oxidative stress (H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>)
                     and high salinity (% NaCl) |CITS: [35163484]|."
                     /translation="MNSAFVLVLTVFLVSGEPVDIAVSVHRTMQECMTAATEQKIPGNC
                     YPVDKVIHQDNIEIPAGL"
                     /go_component="membrane [goid 0016020] [evidence IEA]
                     [PMID 10868275]"
                     /go_process="viral release from host cell by cytolysis
                     [goid 0044659] [evidence IMP] [PMID 26887840]"
