     gene            complement(1662956..1663849)
                     /gene="ynfL"
                     /locus_tag="b1595"
                     /label=ynfL
     CDS             complement(1662956..1663849)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ynfL"
                     /locus_tag="b1595"
                     /product="putative DNA-binding transcriptional regulator
                     YnfL"
                     /label=ynfL
                     /note="Expression of cloned <i>ynfLM</i> in a K-12 strain
                     which lacks the major efflux pump permease AcrB (<i>E. 
                     coli</i> KAM3; see |CITS: [12618449]|) confers 
                     hypersensitivity to acriflavine (minimum inhibitory 
                     concentration, 12.5 &micro;g/mL for KAM3 versus 3.13 
                     &micro;g/mL for KAM3/pUCynfLM) but overexpression of 
                     <i>ynfM</i> alone (KAM3/pTrcHynfM) does not, suggesting 
                     that the sensitivity phenotype results from the expression 
                     of <i>ynfL</i> |CITS: [11566977]|.
                     YnfL was predicted to be a LysR-type transcription factor 
                     |CITS: [34428301] [35880217]|. This protein contains a 
                     helix-turn-helix motif to bind DNA in its N-terminal domain
                     |CITS: [35880217]|. YnfL DNA-binding activity was probed in
                     vivo in glucose M9 minimal medium through chromatin 
                     immunoprecipitation assays (ChIP-exo) |CITS: [34428301]|. 
                     The functional analysis of YnfL-binding targets suggests 
                     that it is involved in regulation of arabinose efflux 
                     |CITS: [34428301]|. However, the effect of YnfL on the 
                     transcription of any gene has not yet been demonstrated 
                     |CITS: [34428301]|.
                     Upstream of the <i>ynfL</i> gene, a sequence was found that
                     could work as a transcriptional promoter |CITS: 
                     [35880217]|.
                     "
