     gene            1665633..1665941
                     /gene="asr"
                     /locus_tag="b1597"
                     /label=asr
     CDS             1665633..1665941
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="asr"
                     /locus_tag="b1597"
                     /product="periplasmic chaperone Asr"
                     /label=asr
                     /note="Asr is a periplasmic, 'context-specific' chaperone
                     that inhibits the aggregation of similarly charged clients 
                     and promotes the aggregation of oppositely charged clients.
                     Asr is a highly charged, stress-induced, intrinsically 
                     disordered protein that contributes to the maintenance of 
                     outer membrane integrity under moderate acid stress |CITS: 
                     [34914967]|.
                     Asr plays a role in survival under acid conditions; it is 
                     involved in an acid-induced protective response that allows
                     wild-type cells to survive incubation at very low pH |CITS:
                     [12670971]|.  Asr also increases fitness in long-term 
                     stationary phase cultures in rich media and is important 
                     for colonization of the mouse intestine |CITS: [18625305]|.
                     Asr is post-translationally processed: the signal sequence 
                     is removed and the polypeptide is then cleaved into two 
                     pieces; only the C-terminal 8 kDa cleavage product can be 
                     detected.  The cleavage sites have been characterized.
                     The 8 kDa species appears to be the active species |CITS: 
                     [12670971]|. Asr contains a large number of hydrophilic and
                     basic amino acids; the protein has some membrane-binding 
                     capacity <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [34914967]|.
                     Transcription of <i>asr</i> is induced under acidic 
                     conditions and during growth in minimal media, compared to 
                     growth in rich media, and is dependent on PhoB |CITS: 
                     [10094685]| and RstA |CITS: [17468243]|.  The low level of 
                     <i>asr</i> expression during stationary phase is dependent 
                     on RpoS |CITS: [15313253]|.  The <i>asr</i> mRNA is stable 
                     and abundant upon acid induction |CITS: [10094685]|. 
                     <i>asr</i> expression is also induced by excess of zinc 
                     |CITS: [15659689]|.
                     An <i>asr</i> insertion mutant has no obvious defect for 
                     growth in rich or minimal media and at pH 5 |CITS: 
                     [10094685]|.  However, growth at pH 4.8 or lower is 
                     reduced.  Overexpression of <i>asr</i> under acidic growth 
                     conditions eventually leads to cell lysis |CITS: 
                     [12670971]|.
                     Asr: ""<b>a</b>cid <b>s</b>hock <b>R</b>NA"" |CITS: 
                     [10094685]|
                     "
