     gene            1678516..1679445
                     /gene="tus"
                     /locus_tag="b1610"
                     /label=tus
     CDS             1678516..1679445
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tus"
                     /locus_tag="b1610"
                     /product="DNA replication terminus site-binding protein"
                     /label=tus
                     /note="Tus, also known as <i>ter</i>-binding protein
                     (TBP), binds to <i>ter</i> sites, blocking
                     the progress of DNA replication in a polar fashion, thus 
                     terminating replication |CITS: 
                     [7929435][2646296][2646639]|.
                     Leading-strand synthesis arrests immediately
                     on contact with Tus, whereas the lagging strand arrests 
                     50-70 nucleotides upstream
                     from the site of Tus binding |CITS: [2181438]|. Tus halts 
                     advancing replication
                     forks by blocking the progress of the replicative helicase 
                     DnaB |CITS: [2684415]|. <i>ter</i>-bound Tus has also 
                     demonstrated directional blocking of the helicases
                     PriA, Rep and UvrD |CITS: [2556700][1533620]|. Conversely, 
                     UvrD in combination
                     with RecA appears to be able to restore viability in 
                     strains that have ectopic <i>ter</i>
                     sites |CITS: [17020578]|. It has been suggested that
                     the effectivenes of Tus in blocking depends on the amount 
                     of adjacent duplex DNA
                     present, though there are conflicting results in this area 
                     |CITS: [1317865][7493939]|. In any case, <i>in vitro</i> 
                     assays of helicase blocking may
                     not reflect <i>in vivo</i> effectiveness, as a mutant Tus 
                     that does not work
                     <i>in vitro</i> is still 75% effective in the cell |CITS: 
                     [8051142]|. Tus has also been shown to block transcription 
                     through <i>ter</i> sites |CITS: [10373601]|.
                     There are two primary <i>ter</i> sites on the chromosome 
                     that are aligned
                     to form a 350 kb ""replication fork trap"" such that active
                     replication forks
                     can pass through either one from the outside but are 
                     blocked from exiting
                     the region |CITS: [2842310][3276664][16148308]|. Whereas 
                     strand separation
                     by replicative DNA helicase at the ""permissive"" end of 
                     the trap site frees Tus,
                     strand separation at the ""nonpermissive"" end of the trap 
                     locks Tus tightly to
                     the DNA, preventing replicative progression |CITS: 
                     [16814717]|. The <i>tus</i> gene overlaps one of these 
                     sites |CITS: [2842310][3276664]|. Mutations in these sites 
                     that disrupt Tus binding lead to diminished
                     replication arrest |CITS: [9334221]|. The termination 
                     signal is a
                     23-base-pair, non-palindromic sequence that has been 
                     extensively
                     evaluated |CITS: [2846183][7499290]|. In addition, the 
                     effects
                     of flanking sequences on termination have been tested. 
                     GC-rich sequence
                     is required on both sides of the <i>terB</i> site for 
                     maximum termination
                     efficiency |CITS: [8021197]|. Tus-<i>ter</i> binding has 
                     been examined
                     in a number of studies |CITS: 
                     [1313800][11009613][11742131]|.
                     Other <i>ter</i> sites have been identified as well |CITS: 
                     [1447156]|.
                     Blocking of DnaB helicase function by Tus depends on 
                     interaction between
                     DnaB and the L1 loop of Tus. This provides a mechanism for 
                     the polarity
                     of Tus function, as the L1 loop is on the ""blocking"" end 
                     of <i>ter</i>-bound
                     Tus |CITS: [11493686][11523786]|. The specific ""locking"" 
                     mechanism of the
                     <i>ter</i> site appears to be a conserved cytosine that 
                     binds Tus when the
                     <i>ter</i> region is unwound |CITS: [16950091]|.
                     A crystal structure of Tus has been determined to 2.7 
                     &Aring; resolution |CITS: [8778788]|.
                     Based on this structure, Tus appears to have two domains 
                     with a central cleft that binds the major groove of 
                     <i>ter</i> DNA |CITS: [8857533]|. This DNA binding site has
                     been evaluated via circular
                     dichroism as well |CITS: [7800495]|.
                     The <i>tus</i> gene overlaps with <i>terB</i>. As a 
                     consequence,
                     transcription from the weak <i>tus</i> promoter is 
                     repressed by
                     binding of Tus to <i>terB</i> |CITS: 
                     [1495392][2023933][1925016]|.
                     In the absence of Tus, plasmid R1 is unstable, tending to 
                     multimerize |CITS: [9194700]|.
                     Review of the properties of the Tus-<i>Ter</i> complex 
                     useful for biotechnology: |CITS: [35641065]|."
