     gene            complement(1679442..1680845)
                     /gene="fumC"
                     /locus_tag="b1611"
                     /label=fumC
     CDS             complement(1679442..1680845)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fumC"
                     /locus_tag="b1611"
                     /product="fumarase C"
                     /EC_number="4.2.1.2"
                     /label=fumC
                     /note="Fumarase C (FumC) is one of three characterized
                     fumarase isozymes in <i>E. coli</i> (encoded by |FRAME: 
                     EG10356|, |FRAME: EG10357|, and |FRAME: EG10358|), all of 
                     which participate in the TCA cycle.  FumC is a tetrameric 
                     protein and belongs to the class II, iron-independent 
                     fumarases |CITS: [3282546]|.
                     The cell adapts to changing environmental oxygen conditions
                     by utilizing different isozymes.  Both FumA and FumB 
                     contain iron-sulfur centers; exposure to oxidative agents 
                     such as superoxide results in damage to the metal cofactor 
                     and loss of enzyme activity |CITS: [8226748][8460946]|.  In
                     contrast, FumC is an iron-independent enzyme and is 
                     insensitive to oxidative damage |CITS: [8203917]|.  FumC is
                     made by the cells primarily as a backup enzyme if the FumA 
                     or FumB enzymes are damaged by reactive oxygen species.
                     Crystal structures of fumarase C have been solved, allowing
                     identification of the active site |CITS: [7552727 
                     ][8909293][9098893][12021453][16204892][31514245]|. What 
                     was thought to be an activator binding site was later 
                     determined to be part of the active site, providing a path 
                     for transfer of substrate and product between the active 
                     site and the bulk solvent |CITS: [14990798]|.  Each active 
                     site is composed of residues from three subunits.  The 
                     function of the SS loop was probed by structural and 
                     kinetic characterization of site-directed mutants |CITS: 
                     [31514245]|.
                     The <i>E. coli</i> enzyme has been used to study the effect
                     of disease-associated mutations found in the human 
                     ortholog, FH |CITS: [12021453]|.
                     Fumarase C abundance is more than 38-fold increased during 
                     growth with vanillin; a <i>fumC</i> deletion mutant shows a
                     decreased final cell yield when grown in the presence of 
                     vanillin |CITS: [31186336]|.
                     Expression of all three fumarase genes is controlled by 
                     oxygen levels.  Expression of <i>fumB</i> is four fold 
                     elevated under anaerobic cell growth conditions versus 
                     aerobic conditions |CITS: [9418241]|.  In contrast, 
                     <i>fumA</i> expression is highest only during aerobic 
                     growth and where <i>fumC</i> is only weakly expressed. When
                     cells encounter oxidative stress conditions, <i>fumC</i> 
                     gene expression is significantly induced by the SoxRS 
                     regulatory system |CITS: [7592392]|.
                     The regulation of <i>fumC</i> expression has been 
                     investigated |CITS: 
                     [7592392][11133938][15672380][18175070][18213409]|. 
                     <i>fumC</i> is a member of the SoxRS regulon; expression 
                     can be induced by paraquat |CITS: 
                     [1631070][8000525][7592392]|.  <i>fumA</i> and <i>fumC</i> 
                     are under catabolite repression; the transcriptional 
                     regulators ArcA and Fnr function to repress <i>fumAC</i> 
                     expression under anaerobic conditions |CITS: [22752906]|. A
                     <i>fumC</i> deletion mutant shows increased biofilm 
                     formation |CITS: [17222132]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [22551392]|"
