     CDS             complement(1680988..1682634)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fumA"
                     /locus_tag="b1612"
                     /product="bifunctional fumarase A / oxaloacetate 
                     keto-enol-isomerase"
                     /EC_number="4.2.1.2; 5.3.2.2"
                     /label=fumA
                     /note="Fumarase A (FumA) is one of three characterized
                     fumarase isozymes in <i>E. coli</i> (encoded by |FRAME: 
                     EG10356|, |FRAME: EG10357|, and |FRAME: EG10358|), all of 
                     which participate in the TCA cycle. FumA is a dimeric 
                     [4Fe-4S] cluster-containing protein and belongs to the 
                     class I fumarases |CITS: [3282546][1329945]|. Fumarase A 
                     can also catalyze the isomerization of <i>enol</i>- to 
                     <i>keto</i>-oxalacetate |CITS: [8422384]|.
                     The cell adapts to changing environmental oxygen conditions
                     by utilizing different isozymes. Both FumA and FumB contain
                     iron-sulfur centers; exposure to oxidative agents such as 
                     superoxide results in damage to the metal cofactor and loss
                     of enzyme activity |CITS: [8226748]|. In contrast, FumC is 
                     an iron-independent enzyme and is insensitive to oxidative 
                     damage |CITS: [8203917]|. FumC is made by the cells 
                     primarily as a backup enzyme if the FumA or FumB enzymes 
                     are damaged by reactive oxygen species |CITS: 
                     [8460946][11133938]|
                     Expression of all three fumarase genes is anaerobically 
                     controlled. Expression of <i>fumB</i> is four fold elevated
                     under anaerobic cell growth conditions versus aerobic 
                     conditions |CITS: [9418241]|. In contrast, <i>fumA</i> 
                     expression is highest only during aerobic growth and where 
                     <i>fumC</i> is only weakly expressed. When cells encounter 
                     oxidative stress conditions, <i>fumC</i> gene expression is
                     then significantly induced by the SoxRS regulatory system 
                     |CITS: [7592392]|. The aerobic/anaerobic transcriptional 
                     regulators ArcA and Fnr function to repress <i>fumA</i> as 
                     well as <i>fumC</i> gene expression under anaerobic 
                     conditions. <i>fumA</i>, among other genes involved in 
                     carbon source transport and metabolism, was downregulated 
                     in two MG1655 lysogens carrying closely related Stx2a 
                     phages O104 and PA8 |CITS: 31208335|.
                     The complex regulation of <i>fumA</i> expression has been 
                     investigated |CITS: [WOODS87][8000525][7592392][16187334][ 
                     18338221][22752906]|. Regulation of <i>fumA</i> expression 
                     by Fur may be indirect, via the small RNA RyhB |CITS: 
                     [11917098][21289064]|. Steady-state levels of <i>fumA</i> 
                     mRNA decrease with increasing growth rate; conversely, the 
                     half life of <i>fumA</i> mRNA increases, leading to 
                     unchanged levels of FumA protein |CITS: [22302452]|. 
                     Cysteine sites in peptides of FumA were significantly 
                     reduced in a redox proteomics study in a &Delta;|FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-245 Ahp| mutant |CITS: [37725887]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [32149453]|"
