     gene            1682833..1684008
                     /gene="manA"
                     /locus_tag="b1613"
                     /label=manA
     CDS             1682833..1684008
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="manA"
                     /locus_tag="b1613"
                     /product="mannose-6-phosphate isomerase"
                     /EC_number="5.3.1.8"
                     /label=manA
                     /note="Mannose-6-phosphate isomerase (phosphomannose
                     isomerase, PMI) catalyzes the reversible isomerization of 
                     mannose-6-phosphate and fructose-6-phosphate. This reaction
                     is the first committed step in the synthesis of 
                     GDP-&alpha;-D-mannose (see pathway |FRAME: PWY-5659|). The 
                     enzyme is required for growth on mannose as the sole source
                     of carbon and energy. It is also required for the formation
                     of GDP-L-fucose, a building block of capsular 
                     polysaccharide |CITS: [4862193]| (see pathways |FRAME: 
                     MANNCAT-PWY| and |FRAME: COLANSYN-PWY|).
                     PMI activity is increased by growth on 
                     <i>p</i>-fluorophenylalanine (FPA), which leads to 
                     formation of mucoid colonies |CITS: [5335965]|. A 
                     <i>manA</i> mutant is unable to grow on mannose as the sole
                     source of carbon and energy |CITS: [4862193]| and has a 
                     defect in the maintenance of colonization of the mouse 
                     intestine |CITS: [15123798]|.
                     The <i>manA</i> gene can be used as a selectable marker 
                     |CITS: [8900006]|.
                     Pmi: ""<b>p</b>hospho<b>m</b>annose <b>i</b>somerase""
                     The <i>E. coli</i> enzyme is a type I PMI, a 
                     monofunctional, Zn<sup>2+</sup>-dependent metalloenzyme 
                     found in most bacteria and mammals |CITS: [16131071]|. 
                     Crystal structures have been determined for the PMI from 
                     <i>Salmonella typhimurium</i> bound to metal atoms and 
                     substrate |CITS: [19564693]|.
                     A novel tandem mass spectrometry based assay for the 
                     quantification of phosphorylated aldose-ketose sugar 
                     isomers has been described which differentiates between the
                     isomeric aldose and ketose 6-phosphates. This enabled 
                     measurement of the equilibrium constant of the reversible 
                     reaction catalyzed by <i>E. coli</i> PMI, as well as its 
                     kinetic parameters in both directions |CITS: 
                     [GaoIntJMassSpec05]|.
                     Electrospray ionization Fourier transform ion cyclotron 
                     resonance mass spectrometry was used to probe the 
                     noncovalent complexes of <i>E. coli</i> PMI with its 
                     ligands including the Zn<sup>2+</sup> cofactor, 
                     mannose-6-phosphate, and fructose-6-phosphate, as well as 
                     the two inhibitors erythrose 4-phosphate and mannitol 
                     1-phosphate |CITS: [16131071]|.
                     The <i>E. coli</i> enzyme has also been included in several
                     inhibitor studies because PMI is of interest as a drug 
                     target. Its activity is essential for the survival and 
                     pathogenesis of several microorganisms |CITS: 
                     [16131071][19783448][21058398][22269276]|.
                     Deletion of <i>man</i>A was part of a strategy for the 
                     metabolic engineering of <i>E. coli</i> to produce 
                     mannosylglycerate from externally added mannose |CITS: 
                     [12514000]|. Finely regulating the expression of 
                     <i>manA</i> by altering the ribosomal binding site to 
                     adjust the translation initiation rate resulted in 
                     increased production of |FRAME: CPD-21504| in an engineered
                     strain of <i>E. coli</i> W3110 |CITS: [37688786]|."
