     gene            complement(1691530..1692297)
                     /gene="hdhA"
                     /locus_tag="b1619"
                     /label=hdhA
     CDS             complement(1691530..1692297)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hdhA"
                     /locus_tag="b1619"
                     /product="7-alpha-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.159"
                     /label=hdhA
                     /note="7-&alpha;-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase catalyzes
                     the dehydroxylation of cholic and chenodeoxycholic acids, 
                     major human bile acids, yielding 7-oxocholic and 
                     7-oxochenodeoxycholic acids, respectively.  The enzyme 
                     belongs to the short-chain nonmetalloenzyme-alcohol 
                     dehydrogenase protein family.  The enzyme shows activity 
                     with several substrates having a hydroxyl group at position
                     7 of the steroid skeleton.  NAD<sup>+</sup> is required for
                     enzyme activity |CITS: [2007545]|.
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme in binary and ternary 
                     complexes have been solved.  Substrate binding induces a 
                     large conformational change at the substrate binding loop 
                     and in the C-terminal amino acids.  A catalytic mechanism 
                     has been proposed |CITS: [8672472]|.  Site-directed 
                     mutagenesis of proposed catalytic residues confirmed their 
                     roles |CITS: [9722677]|.
                     Protein levels of HdhA are increased by osmotic stress 
                     under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions |CITS: 
                     [17015655]|.  <i>hdhA</i> belongs to the 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> regulon |CITS: [15716429]|.
                     HdhA: ""7-&alpha;-<b>h</b>ydroxysteroid 
                     <b>d</b>e<b>h</b>ydrogenase <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [8366061]|"
