     gene            1696490..1697491
                     /gene="add"
                     /locus_tag="b1623"
                     /label=add
     CDS             1696490..1697491
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="add"
                     /locus_tag="b1623"
                     /product="bifunctional deoxyadenosine deaminase /
                     adenosine deaminase"
                     /EC_number="3.5.4.4"
                     /label=add
                     /note="Adenosine deaminase participates in pathways for
                     the degradation and salvage of purine ribonucleotides and 
                     deoxyribonucleotides.  The salvage pathways can convert 
                     adenine, adenosine and deoxyadenosine to guanine 
                     nucleotides |CITS: [765747] [357905]| (see pathways |FRAME:
                     PWY0-1296|, |FRAME: PWY0-1297|, |FRAME: PWY-6605|, |FRAME: 
                     PWY-6609| and |FRAME: PWY-6611|).  This enzyme is one of 
                     two adenosine deaminases in <i>E. coli</i>, the other is 
                     TadA, |FRAME: CPLX0-901|.
                     The enzyme was shown to be induced in <i>E. coli</i> B by 
                     the presence of adenine or hypoxanthine in the culture 
                     medium |CITS: [13654350][4874315]|.  The native apparent 
                     molecular mass of the enzyme from <i>E. coli</i> K-12 was 
                     determined to be 29 kDa by gel filtration chromatography, 
                     using either purified enzyme, or enzyme from crude extracts
                     |CITS: [357905]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> adenosine deaminase shares approximately 33%
                     amino acid sequence identity and 50% overall homology with 
                     mammalian adenosine deaminases, suggesting structural and 
                     functional similarity |CITS: [1998686]|.  Murine adenosine 
                     deaminase is a monomeric enzyme that requires 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> as a cofactor.  Its crystal structure has 
                     been determined |CITS: [9622483]|."
