     CDS             1705780..1706415
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nth"
                     /locus_tag="b1633"
                     /product="multifunctional endonuclease III / bifunctional
                     DNA glycosylase / bifunctional thymine glycol DNA 
                     glycosylase / bifunctional uracil DNA glycosylase (G:U 
                     specific)"
                     /EC_number="4.2.99.18"
                     /label=nth
                     /note="Endonuclease III (Endo III or Nth) is a combined
                     DNA glycosylase and apurinic/apyrimidinic (AP) lyase; Endo 
                     III cleaves the N-glycosidic bond of damaged pyrimidines to
                     create AP sites and incises the phosphodiester backbone 3' 
                     to the abasic site to yield single strand breaks with 
                     3'-unsaturated aldehydic and deoxyribonucleoside 
                     5'-phosphoryl ends. The AP lyase activity of Endo III 
                     generates blocked 3' termini that are then processed by one
                     of two AP endonucleases (|FRAME: EG11073-MONOMER 
                     ""Exonuclease III""| and |FRAME: EG10651-MONOMER 
                     ""Endonuclease IV""|) to generate the 3' OH ends that are 
                     required for DNA polymerase repair synthesis (see |CITS: 
                     [10946230]|).
                     Endonuclease III recognizes pyrimidine residues damaged by 
                     ring saturation, ring fragmentation or ring contraction. 
                     Examples of these types of damage include: |FRAME: 
                     CPD-18012|, |FRAME: DIHYDRO-THYMINE|, |FRAME: DI-H-URACIL|,
                     |FRAME: CPD-3684|, |FRAME: CPD-18013|, |FRAME: CPD-18014|, 
                     |FRAME: CPD-18015|, |CITS: [8218289][9545349]| |FRAME: 
                     CPD-18016|, |FRAME: CPD-18017| |CITS: [8034633]|, |FRAME: 
                     CPD-18018|, |FRAME: CPD-18019| |CITS: [10820032]| and 
                     others |CITS: [6371006][10079077][10985784]|. See also 
                     |CITS: [27865982]| for a comprehensive list of Nth 
                     substrates. Endo III is a G:U specific uracil DNA 
                     glycosylase and AP lyase which functions in the repair of 
                     cytosine deamination |CITS: [30590231]|.
                     Endo III generates single strand breaks (nicks) in UV 
                     damaged duplex DNA |CITS: [3498][16001]|. Endo III is a DNA
                     glycosylase that excises thymine oxidation products |CITS: 
                     [6253795]|. Endo III has 3' AP endonuclease activity; nicks
                     generated by Endo III have low priming activity with DNA 
                     polymerase I |CITS: [6254032]|. Endo III is an AP lyase; 
                     Endo III cleaves 3' to AP sites by a &beta;-elimination 
                     mechanism with formation of a trans &alpha;,-&beta; 
                     unsaturated aldose |CITS: [2439070][2449657][1846560]|. 
                     Endo III cleaves 5-fluorouracil and 5-formyluracil 
                     containing oligonucleotides with formation of a 5' fragment
                     containing a 3'-deoxyribose phosphate (not an 
                     &alpha;-&beta; unsaturated aldose) |CITS: [19607800]|. Endo
                     III contains a [4Fe-4S] cluster |CITS: [2548577]|. The Fe-S
                     cluster is not directly involved in catalysis, it may have 
                     a structural or regulatory role |CITS: [1644800]|. The Fe-S
                     cluster is redox active when bound to DNA <i>in vitro</i> 
                     and a model for detecting DNA damage based on DNA mediated 
                     charge transport (CT) between repair enzymes has been 
                     proposed and further elucidated |CITS: 
                     [15938629][16505354][16967954][17599416]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [22308447][22377696][28715891][32029762][30693326][3990458 
                     5]|). DNA-mediated CT is sensitive to disturbances in base 
                     pair stacking and the [4Fe-4S] cluster-containing DNA 
                     repair proteins Endo III and |FRAME: EG10627-MONOMER  MutY|
                     use DNA-mediated CT cooperatively to search for DNA damage 
                     |CITS: [19720997][21651304]|. Endo III and |FRAME: 
                     EG11357-MONOMER| use DNA-mediated CT cooperatively to 
                     localize to regions of damage; Endo III signals to DinG via
                     CT to help DinG locate and process R-loops |CITS: 
                     [24738733][28817778]|.
                     The protein is primarily &alpha; helical in structure and 
                     can be divided into two domains - a 6 helix barrel domain 
                     (residues 22-132) and a [4Fe-4S] cluster domain (residues 
                     1-21 and 133-211) - which are separated by a deep cleft or 
                     groove |CITS: [1411536]|. An active site pocket exists 
                     within the interdomain groove and is bracketed by two DNA 
                     binding regions: a helix-hairpin-helix (HhH) domain 
                     (residues108-127) and a [4Fe-4S] cluster loop (FCL) 
                     (residues 186-194) |CITS: [7664751]|.
                     Endo III searches for damage by diffusing along DNA and 
                     probing for damaged bases;  Endo III contains a key 'wedge'
                     residue (Leu81) believed to scan for DNA damage by 
                     inserting into the duplex and testing for strength and 
                     flexibility of base pairs. Mutation of this residue (L81A) 
                     results in faster diffusion of the enzyme along a DNA 
                     substrate <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [21666255][24799677]| 
                     Endonuclease III crosslinks to 2-deoxyribonolactone DNA 
                     lesions; such lesions interfere with endonuclease III 
                     excision repair of abasic lesions |CITS: [12600212]|.
                     Endo III is the representative member of the 
                     helix-hairpin-helix (HhH) superfamily of DNA glycosylases. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [8092678][15811798][24560296][31220976]|<BR>Comment: |CITS:
                     [22377696]|"
