     gene            1722094..1722501
                     /gene="gloA"
                     /locus_tag="b1651"
                     /label=gloA
     CDS             1722094..1722501
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gloA"
                     /locus_tag="b1651"
                     /product="lactoylglutathione lyase"
                     /EC_number="4.4.1.5"
                     /label=gloA
                     /note="Glyoxalase I catalyzes the first of two sequential
                     steps in the conversion of methylgloxal to D-lactate. The 
                     enzyme tightly binds one ion of nickel per enzyme dimer and
                     is the first example of a nickel-containing glyoxalase 
                     |CITS: [9628737]|. Glyoxalase I shows lower levels of 
                     activity with a number of other metal ion cofactors 
                     including Co<sup>2+</sup>, but not including 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [14556652]|.
                     Crystal structures of glyoxalase I in the apo form and in 
                     complex with various metal ions have been determined |CITS:
                     [10913283]|. The nickel active site structure has been 
                     probed by X-ray absorption studies |CITS: 
                     [11196887][11294624]|. The two active sites of the 
                     homodimer are initially asymmetric; only one site binds 
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [19053281]|.
                     A <i>gloA</i> null mutant shows normal growth and 
                     viability, but sensitizes cells to both exogenously added 
                     and endogenously produced methylglyoxal, while a strain 
                     overexpressing <i>gloA</i> shows enhanced tolerance to 
                     methylglyoxal |CITS: [9489668]| and glyoxal |CITS: 
                     [23506073]|. In a <i>gloA</i> mutant, higher levels of 
                     carnosine are required to protect the cells against 
                     methylglyoxal toxicity |CITS: [20952637]|. <i>gloA</i> 
                     appears to be co-transcribed with <i>nemRA</i> |CITS: 
                     [23506073][23646895]|; its expression is induced in 
                     response to growth-inhibitory concentrations of 
                     methylglyoxal |CITS: [23646895]|. <i>gloA</i> expression 
                     appears to be negatively regulated by CRP |CITS: 
                     [27535647]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [2198020][10226043][18533363][21310258][28 
                     358304][37269676]|"
