     gene            complement(1728011..1728358)
                     /gene="grxD"
                     /locus_tag="b1654"
                     /label=grxD
     CDS             complement(1728011..1728358)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="grxD"
                     /locus_tag="b1654"
                     /product="trifunctional Grx4-IbaG complex / Grx4-BolA
                     complex / glutaredoxin 4"
                     /label=grxD
                     /note="GrxD belongs to the family of monothiol
                     glutaredoxins. The dimeric GrxD can act as a scaffold 
                     protein, transferring an intact [2Fe-2S] cluster to the 
                     model acceptor protein ferredoxin |CITS: [21899261]|. 
                     Oxidized GrxD can be reduced by the glutaredoxin (|FRAME: 
                     RED-GLUTAREDOXIN|) or thioredoxin (|FRAME: 
                     THIOREDOXIN-REDUCT-NADPH-MONOMER|) system. GrxD is not 
                     active in the standard glutaredoxin (|FRAME: 
                     GLUTATHIONE-SYN-CPLX|) assay |CITS: [15833738]|.
                     A solution structure of the reduced form of GrxD has been 
                     determined |CITS: [15840565]|. A crystal structure of the 
                     GrxD dimer coordinating a [2Fe-2S] cluster at the dimer 
                     interface has been solved. This arrangement suggests that 
                     GrxD has to undergo a conformational change to release the 
                     [2Fe-2S] cluster |CITS: [19505088]|.
                     Physical |CITS: [20734996][20936830]| and genetic |CITS: 
                     [18677321]| interactions with iron-sulfur cluster assembly 
                     proteins suggest that GrxD acts as a scaffold and/or 
                     transfer protein for iron-sulfur clusters to the |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-246 ""cysteine desulfurase""| (SUF) and |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7838| (CSD) sulfur transfer systems. Both GrxD and 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-7682 ""NfuA""| interact with |FRAME: 
                     G6364-MONOMER ""MiaB""|. NfuA, but not GrxD, can transfer a
                     4Fe-4S cluster to apo-MiaB <i>in vitro</i>, and both 
                     proteins affect MiaB activity <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [23543739]|.
                     GrxD is an abundant protein that is upregulated upon iron 
                     depletion and during stationary phase; the increased 
                     expression is dependent on ppGpp |CITS: [15833738]|. A 
                     <i>grxD</i> null mutant could not be obtained by |CITS: 
                     [15833738]|. <i>grxD</i> has previously been reported to be
                     essential for aerobic growth in rich media |CITS: 
                     [13129938]|. However, a <i>grxD</i> mutant, available as 
                     part of the Keio collection |CITS: [16738554]|, is 
                     sensitive to iron depletion |CITS: [21899261]|. The Keio 
                     collection <i>grxD</i> mutant is sensitive to lithium (<90%
                     growth at 300mM Li) |CITS: [39983623]|.
                     A combination of deletion mutants, complementation assays, 
                     a global chemoproteomics analysis and enzymatic assays 
                     indicates that GrxD is functionally distinct from and 
                     cannot be replaced by other A-type carrier (ATC) proteins, 
                     specifically |FRAME: CPLX0-7908|, |FRAME: CPLX0-7824|, NufA
                     and |FRAME: CPLX0-7617|. Its Fe-S cluster binding adapts to
                     iron limitation conditions. Under iron-depleted conditions,
                     GrxD is required for maturation of only a subset of Fe-S 
                     proteins <i>in vivo</i> and is also required for Fe-S 
                     cluster transfer to apo-ErpA under iron starvation stress 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [38944118]|. Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [17659286][19691428][22583368][23298813]|"
