     gene            1728692..1729507
                     /gene="mepH"
                     /locus_tag="b1655"
                     /label=mepH
     CDS             1728692..1729507
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mepH"
                     /locus_tag="b1655"
                     /product="multifunctional peptidoglycan
                     endopeptidase/peptidoglycan L,D-carboxypeptidase MepH / 
                     peptidoglycan DD-endopeptidase / peptidoglycan 
                     L,D-endopeptidase / peptidoglycan L,D-carboxypeptidase"
                     /label=mepH
                     /note="Purified MepH is a murein DD-endopeptidase with 
                     specificity for D-ala-m-DAP (4&rarr;3) cross-links; the 
                     endopeptidase activity of MepH is associated with murein 
                     incorporation during cell growth; purified MepH has no LD 
                     carboxypeptidase activity |CITS: [23062283]|. Purified MepH
                     has weak hydrolytic activity on m-DAP-m-DAP (3&rarr;3) 
                     cross-links and, in contradiction of earlier results, also 
                     displays L,D-carboxypeptidase activity |CITS: [34382698]|.
                     An <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strain lacking all three murein 
                     endopeptidases MepH, |FRAME: G7147-MONOMER ""MepS""| and 
                     |FRAME: EG10013-MONOMER ""MepM""| exhibits extensive cell 
                     lysis and a terminal phenotype. Incorporation of labelled 
                     mDAP into the peptidoglycan sacculi is decreased by up to 
                     80% in the triple deletion strain. Over-expression of any 
                     of the three genes from a plasmid allows the triple 
                     deletion mutant to grow normally. This suggests that MepH, 
                     MepS and MepM perform essential and redundant functions 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [23062283]|. MepH is a substrate of 
                     the |FRAME: EG10760-MONOMER Prc|-|FRAME: EG12371-MONOMER 
                     Nlp| proteolytic system |CITS: [35418955]|."
