     gene            1729635..1730216
                     /gene="sodB"
                     /locus_tag="b1656"
                     /label=sodB
     CDS             1729635..1730216
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sodB"
                     /locus_tag="b1656"
                     /product="bifunctional superoxide dismutase (Fe) /
                     superoxide dismutase"
                     /EC_number="1.15.1.1"
                     /label=sodB
                     /note="SodB, or FeSOD, is one of three superoxide
                     dismutases in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [330499]|. The three 
                     enzymes differ in their metal cofactor requirement; FeSOD 
                     contains iron. Superoxide dismutase is implicated in the 
                     response to a large number of environmental changes that 
                     lead to the generation of superoxide radicals.
                     FeSOD and the manganese-containing enzyme MnSOD (SodA) are 
                     not functionally equivalent; MnSOD is more effective than 
                     FeSOD in preventing damage to DNA, while FeSOD seems more 
                     effective in protecting a cytoplasmic superoxide-sensitive 
                     enzyme |CITS: [1447175]|. However, both enzymes were shown 
                     to protect dihydroxy acid dehydratase from inactivation by 
                     oxidative stress |CITS: [7771772]|.
                     The reaction mechanism involves cycling of the metal ion 
                     between the +3 and +2 oxidation states; the midpoint 
                     potential of the metal ion determines what redox reactions 
                     are possible, and is thus precisely tuned |CITS: 
                     [18376853]|.
                     Crystal structures of FeSOD have been solved |CITS: 
                     [7849024][16430211][17628062]|. Tyr34 is required for 
                     maximal activity |CITS: [9125514]|; its contribution to 
                     substrate binding has been evaluated |CITS: [15835886]|. 
                     The enzyme is highly specific for the iron cofactor; a 
                     manganese-substituted FeSOD is catalytically inactive 
                     |CITS: [11669646]|. Mutations in Q69 and A141 alter metal 
                     selectivity and catalytic activity |CITS: [12392545]|. The 
                     activity of FeSOD correlates with the chemical properties 
                     of glutamine at position 69 |CITS: [16430211][17628062]|; 
                     hydrogen bonding interactions of Glu affect the the 
                     p<i>K</i> of the Fe-bound solvent ligand |CITS: 
                     [18433120]|.
                     Under anaerobic conditions, FeSOD is the only superoxide 
                     dismutase enzyme present in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [321433][8002590]|. A <i>sodB</i> mutant shows a lag period
                     when an anaerobic culture is shifted to aerobic conditions;
                     this is due to damage to enzymes in the branched chain 
                     amino acid biosynthesis pathway |CITS: [8002590]|. 
                     Expression of FeSOD is dependent on Fur |CITS: 
                     [2180912][10850997]| and is induced by high temperatures 
                     |CITS: [2663632]|. Positive regulation by Fur is indirect 
                     via the small RNA RyhB |CITS: 
                     [11782507][11917098][14617150]|. SodB expression is induced
                     by acidic pH |CITS: [12107143]|.
                     In minimal medium, a <i>sodB</i> mutant is more sensitive 
                     to paraquat than wild type |CITS: [3011417]|. Under aerobic
                     conditions, a <i>sodA sodB</i> double mutant is able to 
                     grow slowly on rich medium, but not on minimal glucose 
                     medium unless it is supplemented with a full complement of 
                     amino acids |CITS: [3011417]|. The <i>ssa</i> suppressor 
                     mutation in <i>dapD</i> enables growth under these 
                     conditions |CITS: [1896012]|. The <i>sodA sodB</i> double 
                     mutant is able to grow under anaerobic conditions |CITS: 
                     [3011417]|. The <i>sodA sodB</i> double mutant has an 
                     increased mutation rate in the presence of oxygen |CITS: 
                     [3022287]| and is more sensitive to aerobic heat stress 
                     than wild type |CITS: [7768839]|.
                     Persistant, sublethally injured <i>E. coli</i> induced by 
                     lactic acid stress exhibited slower resuscitation rates in 
                     <i>&Delta;sodB</i>, <i>&Delta;gshA</i> and 
                     <i>&Delta;gshB</i> mutants, and even more slowly in a 
                     <i>&Delta;sodA</i> mutant, relative to wild-type |CITS: 
                     [35395105]|.
                     A single exposure to the ribosome-binding antibiotic 
                     doxycycline can alleviate stress (slow growth), such as 
                     that due to reactive oxygen species; a &Delta;<i>sodB</i> 
                     knockout strain was shown to only grow in the presence of 
                     doxycycline indicating this gene may mediate growth and 
                     longevity even without antibiotics |CITS: [37751555]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [1779751][7574505][18376853][22079668]| 
                     Commentary: |CITS: [35477006]|"
                     /translation="MSFELPALPYAKDALAPHISAETIEYHYGKHHQTYVTNLNNLIKG
