     gene            complement(1731713..1731802)
                     /gene="cydH"
                     /locus_tag="b4602"
                     /label=cydH
     CDS             complement(1731713..1731802)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cydH"
                     /locus_tag="b4602"
                     /product="bifunctional cytochrome bd-I accessory subunit
                     CydH / cytochrome bd-I ubiquinol:oxygen oxidoreductase"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.7"
                     /label=cydH
                     /note="CydH (also called CydY) is a small, noncatalytic,
                     single transmembrane accessory subunit of |FRAME: 
                     CYT-D-UBIOX-CPLX| |CITS: 
                     [23749980][24681096][31604309][31723136]|. In the cryo-EM 
                     structure of cytochrome <i>bd</i>-I, CydH binds to a 
                     hydrophobic cleft in CydA |CITS: [31604309]|. CydH appears 
                     to block dioxygen access to heme <i>b</i><sub>595</sub> 
                     |CITS: [31723136]|.
                     CydH is a chloroform-soluble protein that extracts with the
                     lipid fraction of <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [21050835]|. CydH 
                     contains a predicted transmembrane segment, and the protein
                     can be found in the membrane fraction |CITS: [19121005]|. 
                     Experimental topology analysis suggests that CydH may be 
                     inserted into the membrane with dual orientation |CITS: 
                     [21778229]|. CydH is expressed during both exponential 
                     growth and stationary phase |CITS: [19121005]|. The amount 
                     of CydH protein is approximately 4-fold greater when cells 
                     are grown under oxygen limited conditions compared to 
                     aerobic growth in rich media |CITS: [19734316]|. Tandem 
                     mass spectrometry indicates that the formyl group attached 
                     to the N-terminal methionine is retained |CITS: 
                     [21050835]|."
