     gene            complement(1733123..1734055)
                     /gene="punR"
                     /locus_tag="b1659"
                     /label=punR
     CDS             complement(1733123..1734055)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="punR"
                     /locus_tag="b1659"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional activator PunR"
                     /label=punR
                     /note="PunR, formerly named YdhB, regulates a gene
                     involved in the purine transport process in the presence of
                     adenosine |CITS: [34413462]|. This protein belongs to the 
                     LysR family of HTH-type transcriptional regulators |CITS: 
                     [34413462][9022701] [35880217]|. This protein contains a 
                     helix-turn-helix motif to bind DNA in its N-terminal domain
                     |CITS: [35880217]|. PunR appears to recognize and bind to a
                     palindromic DNA binding motif of 23 bp |CITS: [34413462]|.
                     Genome-wide PunR-binding sites were determined <i>in 
                     vivo</i> by the chromatin immunoprecipitation method 
                     combined with lambda exonuclease digestion (ChIP-exo) in 
                     glucose M9 minimal medium |CITS: [34428301]|.
                     The <i>punR</i> gene is located in the genome in an 
                     inverted position from its target gene |CITS: [34413462]|. 
                     This arrangement in the genome appears to be conserved in 
                     other proteobacteria |CITS: [34413462]|. Upstream of the 
                     <i>punR</i> gene, a sequence was found that could work as a
                     transcriptional promoter, and the transcription of the 
                     <i>punR</i> gene is affected by norfloxacin and 
                     glucose-acetate shift |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     A <i>punR</i> knockout mutant reduces cellular growth under
                     nitrogen-limiting conditions |CITS: [34413462]|."
