     gene            1735670..1736818
                     /gene="cfa"
                     /locus_tag="b1661"
                     /label=cfa
     CDS             1735670..1736818
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cfa"
                     /locus_tag="b1661"
                     /product="cyclopropane fatty acyl phospholipid synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.79"
                     /label=cfa
                     /note="Cyclopropane fatty acyl phospholipid synthase (CFA) 
                     catalyzes a modification of the acyl chains of phospholipid
                     bilayers through methylenation of unsaturated fatty acyl 
                     chains to their cyclopropane derivatives |CITS: [380648]|. 
                     It is one of the few enzymes known to act on the nonpolar 
                     portion of phospholipids dispersed in a vesicle. The enzyme
                     acts on the double bond of a phospholipid unsaturated fatty
                     acid residue, which must be 9-11 carbon atoms removed from 
                     the glycerol backbone of the molecule. |CITS: 
                     [380648][7024727][1445840][9409147][9882672]|
                     Studies using analogs of S-adenosyl-L-methionine suggest 
                     that the reaction proceeds via methyl transfer followed by 
                     proton loss |CITS: [15491158]|. CFA synthase is thought to 
                     operate via a carbocation mechanism |CITS: [15606764]|. The
                     presence of a bicarbonate ion in the active site was 
                     predicted by similarity to the <i>M. tuberculosis</i> 
                     cyclopropane synthases. The presence of bicarbonate 
                     enhances the V<sub>max</sub> of CFA synthase 3-fold |CITS: 
                     [16104732]|. Mutants in residues predicted to interact with
                     bicarbonate show reduced catalytic efficiency, and some 
                     mutants can be rescued by the addition of free bicarbonate.
                      A reaction mechanism has been proposed |CITS: [16216082]|.
                     It has since been shown that the reaction does not involve 
                     proton exchange, and that both steps of the reaction, the 
                     methyl addition to the double bond and the deprotonation, 
                     are rate determining |CITS: [20538038]|. A G236E mutant in 
                     the active site catalyzes the formation of novel fatty 
                     acids, providing evidence for a carbocation reaction 
                     intermediate |CITS: [23954860]|. CFA synthase shows some 
                     association with the cytoplasmic membrane and shows 
                     specific binding to unsaturated fatty acid- or cyclopropane
                     fatty acid-containing phospholipid vesicles |CITS: 
                     [380648]|. A crystal structure of CFA synthase has been 
                     solved at 2.07 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [30057024]|. The 
                     C-terminal domain of each monomer provides the dimerization
                     interface. An E308Q mutation converts the dimeric wild-type
                     enzyme into a monomeric species and causes loss of 
                     enzymatic activity. A surface area of the N-terminal domain
                     carries a positive charge and may be involved in an 
                     interaction with the negatively charged head groups of the 
                     lipid bilayer. A model for the enzymatic function of CFA 
                     synthase at the membrane surface was proposed |CITS: 
                     [30057024]|.
                     Mutants show a reduced resistance to acid shock |CITS: 
                     [10411742][26828814]| and extreme acid |CITS: [23520457]|, 
                     freeze-thaw cycles |CITS: [3519583]|, increased pressure 
                     |CITS: [21705547][26828814]|, and heat |CITS: [26828814]| 
                     compared to wild type.  Mutants do not exhibit obvious 
                     phenotypic defects under a range of other conditions |CITS:
                     [1107324]|. A &Delta;<i>cfa</i> mutant is less susceptible 
                     to the proline-rich antimicrobial peptide apidaecin 1b 
                     |CITS: [29329756]| and shows a slightly increased growth 
                     rate compared to wild type |CITS: [30806872]|. A <i>cfa 
                     rpoS</i> double mutant shows enhanced acid sensitivity 
                     compared to either single mutant |CITS: [10411742]|. 
                     Overproduction of <i>cfa</i> does not cause obvious growth 
                     defects |CITS: [6325391]|.
                     An RpoS-dependent promoter directs stationary-phase 
                     transcription of <i>cfa</i>, while a 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup>-type promoter directs constitutive 
                     transcription |CITS: [8022273]|. Phospholipid-mediated 
                     enzyme stabilization |CITS: [8022273]| and degradation by a
                     heat-shock-dependent mechanism |CITS: [10894739]| also have
                     regulatory effects. Moderate aciditity (pH 5) induces gene 
                     expression, resulting in an acid-acclimation effect |CITS: 
                     [10411742]|. Stringent control regulation has been 
                     observed, with increases in enzyme activity upon entry into
                     stationary phase and during isoleucine starvation |CITS: 
                     [7007364]|. The nucleotide guanosine-3',5'-bisdiphosphate 
                     (ppGpp) has an indirect regulatory effect via induction of 
                     RpoS |CITS: [9882672]|. Crp, Fur |CITS: [15659676]| and FNR
                     |CITS: [16377617][23520457]| were identified as repressors 
                     of <i>cfa</i> expression, while neutral acetate stimulates 
                     transcription directly |CITS: [18331469]|. Regulation of 
                     <i>cfa</i> by |FRAME: RNA0-328| and |FRAME: RNA0-389|occurs
                     at the distal &sigma;<sup>70</sup>-dependent promoter, 
                     which are influenced indirectly by |FRAME: EG11733-MONOMER|
                     and |FRAME: CPLX0-1461|, thus affecting transcription of 
                     the long isoform and lipid membrane composition |CITS: 
                     [24141880][31308070][38980083]|.
                     Cyclopropane fatty acyl phospholipid synthase dimers are 
                     recognized via an internal degron and degraded by the 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-2982| |CITS: [36456794]|.
                     Cyclopropane fatty acid levels under a variety of 
                     conditions have been measured |CITS: 
                     [6325391][26828814][29450618]|."
