     gene            1737714..1739087
                     /gene="mdtK"
                     /locus_tag="b1663"
                     /label=mdtK
     CDS             1737714..1739087
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mdtK"
                     /locus_tag="b1663"
                     /product="bifunctional multidrug efflux pump MdtK / 
                     deoxycholate:Na(+) antiport"
                     /label=mdtK
                     /note="MdtK (also called NorM) is a member of the Multi 
                     Antimicrobial Extrusion (MATE) family of  transporters 
                     (formerly the multidrug and toxic compound extrusion 
                     family) within the 
                     Multidrug/Oligosaccharidyl-lipid/Polysaccharide (MOP) 
                     flippase superfamily |CITS: [9987140][26546518]|. Increased
                     expression of <I>mdtK</I> in an <i>E. coli</i> strain 
                     lacking the major drug efflux pump AcrAB (<i>E. coli</i> 
                     KAM3) results in increased resistance to norfloxacin, 
                     ciprofloxacin, acriflavine and tetraphenylphosphonium 
                     (TPP<sup>+</sup>) |CITS:[9661020]|. Increased expression of
                     <I>mdtK</I> in <i>E. coli</i> KAM3 results in a 32-fold 
                     increase in resistance to TPP<sup>+</sup> and deoxycholate,
                     an 8-fold increase in resistance to norfloxacin, enoxacin 
                     and doxorubicin, a 4-fold increase in resistance to 
                     trimethoprim and a 2-fold increase in resistance to 
                     chloramphenicol, fosfomycin, ethidium bromide and 
                     benzalkonium |CITS:[11566977]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [12615854]|). Norfloxacin efflux is disrupted upon the 
                     addition of <i>m</i>-chlorophenylhydrazone; MdtK is a 
                     putative Na<sup>+</sup>-driven multidrug efflux pump |CITS:
                     [12615854]| (see also |CITS: [19100867]|). Increased 
                     expression of <i>mdtK</i> in an <i>E. coli</i> strain 
                     lacking AcrAB (AG100AX) results in increased resistance to 
                     a range of antibiotics (norfloxacin, doxorubicin, 
                     novobiocin, enoxacin, and ciprofloxacin), dyes (rhodamine 
                     6G, ethidium bromide, acriflavine, and crystal violet) and 
                     quaternary ammonium compounds (berberine, benzalkonium and 
                     TPP<sup>+</sup>); increased expression of <i>mdtK</i> in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> AG100AX reduces the accumulation of 
                     norflaxacin and ethidium bromide; purified MdtK binds 
                     rhodamine 6G with micromolar affinity and with a 1:1 
                     stoichiometry of monomeric MdtK to ligand; purified MdtK 
                     binds ethidium bromide, proflavine, ciprofloxacin, and 
                     norfloxacin with affinities in the micromolar range |CITS: 
                     [18591276]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>mdtK</i> rescues the hypermutable 
                     phenotype of an 8-oxoguanine repair deficient <i>E. 
                     coli</i> strain possibly by extrusion of molecules that 
                     oxidise deoxy-guanine |CITS: [20463878]|.
                     MdtK is able to transport dipeptides (Ala-Gln and 
                     L-alanyl-L-branched chain amino acids) in a dipeptide 
                     overproducing strain |CITS: [20067529]|.
                     <i>mdtK</i> is one of 35 efflux-encoding genes that have 
                     been deleted or inactivated in 'Efflux KnockOut-35' 
                     (EKO-35) -  a K-12 BW25133-derived strain that lacks 35 
                     efflux pumps and can be used as a platform to study their 
                     function |CITS: [36065018][36318669]|."
