     gene            complement(1751978..1752982)
                     /gene="ldtE"
                     /locus_tag="b1678"
                     /label=ldtE
     CDS             complement(1751978..1752982)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ldtE"
                     /locus_tag="b1678"
                     /product="bifunctional L,D-transpeptidase LdtE / 
                     L,D-transpeptidase"
                     /label=ldtE
                     /note="LdtE (formerly YnhG) is a membrane anchored,
                     periplasmic L,D-transpeptidase that catalyses the formation
                     of <i>meso</i>-diaminopimelate&rarr;<i>meso</i>- 
                     diaminopimelate cross links (also called 
                     DAP<sup>3</sup>&rarr;DAP<sup>3</sup> or 3-3 cross-links) in
                     peptidoglycan; LdtE cleaves the 
                     <i>m</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup>&rarr;D-ala<sup>4</sup> peptide 
                     bond of an acyl donor stem tetrapeptide and links the 
                     carboxyl group at the L-center of <i>m</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup> 
                     to the side chain amine of <i>m</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup> of an 
                     acceptor stem peptide.
                     Muropeptides obtained from the peptidoglycan of a 
                     BW25113&Delta;(|FRAME: G7073 ""ldtA""| |FRAME: G6422 
                     ""ldtB""| |FRAME: G6571 ""ldtC""| ldtE) quadruple mutant 
                     (BW25113&Delta;4) do not contain tripeptide stems 
                     substituted by a fragment of the Braun lipoprotein |CITS: 
                     [17369299]|; muropeptides obtained from the peptidoglycan 
                     of a BW25113&Delta;(ldtA ldtB ldtC |FRAME: EG11253 
                     ""ldtD""| ldtE) quintuple mutant (BW25113&Delta;5) do not 
                     contain tripeptide stems substituted by a fragment of the 
                     Braun lipoprotein, nor do they contain 
                     <i>meso</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup>-<i>meso</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup> 
                     cross-links, free tripeptide stems or tetrapeptide stems 
                     with Gly as the fourth amino acid |CITS:[18456808]|. 
                     Expression of <i>ldtE</i> in the BW25113&Delta;4 mutant 
                     increases the amount of muropeptides with 
                     DAP<sup>3</sup>-DAP<sup>3</sup> cross links |CITS: 
                     [17369299]|. Growth of a strain lacking both LdtE and LdtD 
                     (&Delta;<i>ldt2</i>) is not affected under a wide variety 
                     of conditions, but their absence contributes to the growth 
                     defect of an &Delta;<i>ldt5</i> strain 
                     (&Delta;<i>ldtABCDE</I>) which is also a DAP auxotroph 
                     (<i>dapA</i>::cm) |CITS: [23832002]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [30723128]|).
                     Complementation analysis (|CITS:[18456808]| results not 
                     shown) also suggests LdtE can exchange D-ala<sup>4</sup> in
                     peptidoglycan tetrapeptide stems with glycine (|FRAME: 
                     RXN-16665 ""RXN-16665""|) and hydrolyse D-ala<sup>4</sup> 
                     to produce tripeptide stems (|FRAME: RXN-16664 
                     ""RXN-16664""|). <i>E. coli</i> K-12 incorporates 
                     exogenously supplied D-methionine into peptidoglycan stem 
                     tetrapeptides; the percentage of muropeptides that contain 
                     D-methionine is reduced to varying extents in all single 
                     <i>ldt</i> insertion mutants* and is reduced by ~75% in the
                     double <i>ldtD ldtE</i> insertion mutant |CITS: [21792174]|
                     (see also |CITS: [23832002]|). Deletion of <i>ldtD</i> 
                     together with <i>ldtE</i> also reduces detection of 
                     D-cysteine in peptidoglycan |CITS: [21792174]|. LdtE and 
                     LdtF are implicated in incorporating the fluorescent 
                     D-amino acid NADA, into peptidoglycan at acid pH |CITS: 
                     [30233559]|.
                     LdtA, B, C and E are all of similar size and share 42-62% 
                     sequence similarity including a putative active site 
                     cysteine located in a conserved motif; <i>ldtE</i> and 
                     <i>ldtC</i> also contain a LysM domain (see |CITS: 
                     [18430080]|) which is associated with peptidoglycan 
                     binding; LtdE shares 17% sequence similarity with LtdD 
                     |CITS: [23832002]|.
                     <I>ldtE</I> is &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent and is 
                     upregulated in response to the stationary phase transition,
                     osmotic upshift, and acid stress |CITS:[15716429]|.  
                     Expression of <I>ldtE</I> is induced by acivicin treatment 
                     |CITS:[11344143]| and is supercoiling-dependent 
                     |CITS:[12566398],[15535863]|. Expression of <I>ldtE</I> 
                     increases in biofilms upon deletion of <I>bhsA</I> 
                     |CITS:[17293424]|. Overexpression of LdtE increases 
                     resistance to phage-encoded lytic transglycosylase (LT) 
                     activity; LD-crosslink mediated inhibition of LT activity 
                     (endogenous and predatory) is conserved across diverse 
                     bacterial species |CITS: [39261529]|.
                     *|FRAME: G6108-MONOMER ""<i>ldtF</i>""| was not included in
                     this analysis"
