     gene            complement(1753131..1753547)
                     /gene="sufE"
                     /locus_tag="b1679"
                     /label=sufE
     CDS             complement(1753131..1753547)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sufE"
                     /locus_tag="b1679"
                     /product="bifunctional sulfur carrier protein SufE /
                     SufS-SufE complex"
                     /label=sufE
                     /note="SufE is part of the Suf iron-sulfur cluster
                     assembly system. SufE acts as a sulfur acceptor for the 
                     cysteine persulfide generated by the |FRAME: CPLX0-246 
                     SufS| cysteine desulfurase |CITS: [12941942][14644425]| and
                     transfers the sulfur atoms to the Cys254 residue of |FRAME:
                     G6909-MONOMER SufB| |CITS: [17350958][28839209]|. The Isc 
                     system is the ""housekeeping"" pathway for iron-sulfur 
                     cluster assembly in the cell; the Suf system comprises a 
                     secondary pathway of iron-sulfur cluster assembly |CITS: 
                     [12089140]| that is required under oxidative stress |CITS: 
                     [15101990][21143317]| or iron starvation conditions |CITS: 
                     [15101990]|. IscS-IscU is more susceptible to oxidative 
                     modification than |FRAME: CPLX0-12491| |CITS: [23068614]|.
                     SufE is both a cosubstrate of SufS, accepting sulfur at its
                     C51 residue from the persulfide at Cys364 of SufS, and an 
                     activator for the cysteine desulfurase activity of SufS. 
                     SufE binds near the active site of SufS and modulates the 
                     site's backbone dynamics |CITS: [24196966]|. The kinetic 
                     mechanism of sulfur transfer between SufS and SufE has been
                     investigated, and a model for a protected sulfur transfer 
                     pathway of the Suf system has been proposed |CITS: 
                     [23672190]|. Enhanced cysteine desulfurase activity of 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-12491| |CITS: [12876288][12941942]| was 
                     reported to be dependent on the SufBC components |CITS: 
                     [17350958]| of the |FRAME: CPLX0-1341 SufB-SufC-SufD| 
                     complex |CITS: [12941942]|. The interaction of SufE with 
                     the SufB sulfur acceptor requires the presence of SufC 
                     |CITS: [17350958]|. Additional <i>in vivo</i> interaction 
                     partners for SufE have been found |CITS: [20936830]|. 
                     Residues within the SufS that are critical to forming a 
                     high-affinity close interaction between SufS and SufE in 
                     the presence of cysteine have been identified |CITS: 
                     [36736428]|.
                     Mutation of the conserved cysteine residue C51 eliminates 
                     the ability of SufE to enhance SufS activity; this residue 
                     may function as the acceptor site for sulfur transfer by 
                     SufS |CITS: [12941942][14644425]|. The C51 sulfhydryl group
                     has a p<i>K<sub>a</sub></i> of 6.3 |CITS: [20936830]|. A 
                     D74R mutant located in the active site Cys loop increases 
                     flexibility and solvent accessibility of the loop and leads
                     to stronger affinity of the mutant protein for SufS |CITS: 
                     [26171726]|.
                     A crystal structure of SufE has been solved at 2 &Aring; 
                     resolution, showing structural similarity to |FRAME: 
                     G7324-MONOMER IscU| |CITS: [15522304]|. Although dimeric 
                     forms can be detected |CITS: [12876288][12941942]|, the 
                     protein is predominantly monomeric in solution |CITS: 
                     [15522304]|. A crystal structure of the |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-12491| was obtained and showed that each monomeric 
                     unit of the SufS dimer bound to a single SufE subunit in a 
                     1:1 stoichiometry; four critical amino acids were 
                     identified within four regions of SufE where it contacts 
                     SufA |CITS: [39122000]|.
                     SufE was also shown to transfer sulfur to a cysteine 
                     residue of |FRAME: CPLX0-7824 SufA| |CITS: [17350000]|; 
                     however, label transfer experiments don't show interactions
                     between SufA and |FRAME: CPLX0-12491| |CITS: [19810706]|.
                     A mutant deficient in expression of the <i>sufABCDSE</i> 
                     operon does not exhibit severe phenotypes, whereas a 
                     <i>sufABCDSE isc</i> double mutant exhibits synthetic 
                     lethality, indicating that these systems are redundant and 
                     that iron-sulfur cluster assembly is essential for 
                     viability. Overproduction of the products of the 
                     <i>sufABCDSE</i> operon, or regulatory mutations in the 
                     <i>sufABCDSE</i> operon, suppress defects of a 
                     &Delta;<i>isc</i> strain |CITS: [12089140]|. A <i>sufE</i> 
                     mutant is more sensitive to oxidative stress, acid stress 
                     and cadmium than wild type |CITS: [19919618]|.
                     SufE: ""<b>su</b>l<b>f</b>ur""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16211402][20467446][23298813][25153801][2 
                     5447545][32822728][38838856]|"
