     gene            complement(1753560..1754780)
                     /gene="sufS"
                     /locus_tag="b1680"
                     /label=sufS
     CDS             complement(1753560..1754780)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sufS"
                     /locus_tag="b1680"
                     /product="multifunctional SufS-SufE complex / L-cysteine 
                     desulfurase SufS / L-cysteine desulfurase / selenocysteine 
                     lyase"
                     /label=sufS
                     /note="SufS is one of three members of the NifS protein
                     family in <i>E. coli</i>, the other two being |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7838| (|FRAME: G7454|) and |FRAME: CPLX0-248| 
                     (|FRAME: G7325|) |CITS: [10322040]|. The protein was 
                     initially thought to function mainly as a 
                     <u>p</u>yridoxa<u>l</u> 5'-<u>p</u>hosphate (PLP)-dependent
                     selenocysteine lyase with high specificity for 
                     L-selenocysteine |CITS: [10329673]|. It was later 
                     determined that SufS functions as a PLP-dependent cysteine 
                     desulfurase in a secondary pathway of iron-sulfur cluster 
                     assembly |CITS: [12941942]|; the <i>isc</i> operon encodes 
                     the major assembly pathway |CITS: [12089140]|. The Suf 
                     system appears to be responsible for Fe-S cluster synthesis
                     when iron or sulfur metabolism is disrupted by iron 
                     starvation or oxidative stress |CITS: [15101990]|. |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-12491| is less susceptible to oxidative modification 
                     than IscS-IscU |CITS: [23068614]|.
                     The overall cysteine desulfurase reaction involves two half
                     reactions: the desulfurase reaction with release of 
                     L-alanine and concomitant formation of a persulfide 
                     intermediate on an active site cysteine residue, and a 
                     transpersulfurase reaction by which the persulfide is 
                     transferred to a cysteine residue in an acceptor protein. 
                     In the case of SufS, the conserved C364 residue is modified
                     to the persulfide intermediate from which the sulfur can be
                     transferred to Cys51 of |FRAME: G6905-MONOMER SufE| |CITS: 
                     [10739946][14644425]|. A ""half-sites"" mechanism, where 
                     catalytic activity alternates between the monomers of the 
                     homodimeric SufS, was proposed. Such a mechanism requires 
                     communication between the monomers. The dimer interface of 
                     SufS indeed undergoes conformational changes upon formation
                     of the C364 persulfide intermediate, which provides a 
                     mechanism for communication between its two active sites 
                     |CITS: [29589903]|. Crystal structures of wild-type and 
                     mutant SufS confirm that the dimer interface plays a 
                     functional role in mediating conformational changes that 
                     result in reorientation of the persulfide intermediate, as 
                     well as affecting interactions with SufE |CITS: 
                     [30571100][32133993]|. The conserved N99 residue within the
                     &alpha;<sub>6</sub> helix and the G251 residue in the 
                     glycine-rich loop link the two monomers forming a 
                     high-affinity close interaction between SufS and SufE in 
                     the presence of cysteine |CITS: [36736428]|.
                     SufE, together with the SufB-SufC-SufD complex, was 
                     initially found to stimulate the cysteine desulfurase 
                     activity of SufS |CITS: [12941942]|. SufE is both a 
                     cosubstrate, accepting sulfur at its C51 residue from the 
                     Cys364 persulfide of SufS, and an activator. SufE binds 
                     near the active site of SufS and modulates the site's 
                     backbone dynamics |CITS: [24196966]|. The kinetic mechanism
                     of sulfur transfer between SufS and SufE has been 
                     investigated, and a model for a protected sulfur transfer 
                     pathway of the Suf system has been proposed |CITS: 
                     [23672190]|. Further kinetics analyses indicate that SufE 
                     activates the production of alanine and the 
                     rate-determining downstream step, thus regulating the 
                     half-sites mechanism; SufE does not activate the PLP-based 
                     catalytic steps |CITS: [38813769]|.
                     SufS facilitates the formation of the iron-sulfur cluster 
                     of ferredoxin <i>in vitro</i>, but does not interact with 
                     the IscU scaffold, which acts with IscS in iron-sulfur 
                     cluster assembly |CITS: [12686149]|. Like CsdA and IscS, 
                     SufS can transfer sulfur from L-cysteine to activate 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-2502 ""MPT synthase""| <i>in vitro</i>. Of 
                     the three enzymes, CsdA is the most effective, while SufS 
                     shows the lowest activity |CITS: [11290749]|. SufS does not
                     play a role in formation of the dithiolene group of 
                     molybdopterin |CITS: [28323419]| or in iron-sulfur 
                     cluster-independent tRNA thionucleoside modification |CITS:
                     [12446632][11997471][28323419]|; SufS does not interact 
                     with either TusA or ThiI |CITS: [28323419]|. However, the 
                     SUF system is able to provide iron-sulfur clusters to the 
                     thiomodification enzymes TtcA and MiaB |CITS: [28323419]|.
                     Non-specific incorporation of selenium into proteins 
                     appears to occur via synthesis of free selenocysteine by 
                     the cysteine biosynthesis pathway |CITS: [9325431]|. Free 
                     selenocysteine is likely to be toxic to the cell; SufS may 
                     be responsible for its degradation |CITS: [9850565]| and 
                     thereby also play a role in the provision of selenium to 
                     the selenophosphate synthesis protein |FRAME: 
                     EG10943-MONOMER ""SelD""| |CITS: 
                     [10829016][11889101][15653770]|.
                     SufS crystal structures have been solved in various 
                     conformations |CITS: 
                     [10684605][11827487][11983074][30571100]|. Pre-steady-state
                     kinetics and X-ray crystallography enabled identification 
                     of the amino acid residues critical to cysteine binding to 
                     cleaving the C-S bond |CITS: [31248989]|. A new 
                     crystallization method provided a structure of the 
                     homodimer with 1.5 &Aring; resolution that shows an open 
                     &beta;-hairpin structure in the active site and likely 
                     requires adjacent structural elements to regulate active 
                     site access |CITS: [32133993]|. Crystal structures of the 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-12491| and mutational variants were obtained 
                     and showed that each monomeric unit of the SufS dimer bound
                     to a single SufE subunit in a 1:1 stoichiometry; four 
                     critical amino acids were identified within four regions of
                     SufE where it contacts SufA |CITS: [39122000]|. Additional 
                     mutational studies of two arginine residues in combination 
                     with biochemical and structural characterization found that
                     R56 and R359 are involved in conformational changes in the 
                     active site, and R359 specifically affects movement of the 
                     &alpha;3-&alpha;4 loop that is also involved with SufE 
                     interactions |CITS: [40396880]|.
                     A C364A mutation at the predicted catalytic cysteine 
                     results in a severe defect in L-cysteine desulfurization, 
                     but a more mild defect in activity toward L-selenocysteine 
                     |CITS: [10739946]|. <i>sufD</i>, <i>sufS</i> or <i>fhuF</i>
                     mutants exhibit a defect in utilization of ferrioxamine B 
                     as a source of iron |CITS: [10322040]|. A <i>sufABCDSE</i> 
                     deletion mutant grows normally, whereas a <i>sufABCDSE 
                     isc</i> double mutant exhibits synthetic lethality, 
                     indicating that these systems are redundant and that 
                     iron-sulfur cluster assembly is essential for viability. 
                     Overproduction of the <i>sufABCDSE</i> operon suppresses 
                     the growth defects of a &Delta;<i>isc</i> strain |CITS: 
                     [12089140]|.
                     Expression of the <i>suf</i> operon is activated in 
                     response to oxidative stress by the DNA-binding 
                     transcriptional dual regulator RNAs |FRAME: PD00214 OxyR| 
                     |CITS: [11443091]| and |FRAME: PC00027 IHF| |CITS: 
                     [15101990]| and is regulated in response to iron starvation
                     by |FRAME: PD00260| |CITS: [10322040][15101990]|.
                     SufS: ""<b>su</b>l<b>f</b>ur""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11555280][11568442][16211402][20467446][2 
                     1603932][23298813][23660107][25153801][25447545][25447671] 
                     [32822728][34201508][38838856][39033933]|"
