     gene            complement(1756023..1756769)
                     /gene="sufC"
                     /locus_tag="b1682"
                     /label=sufC
     CDS             complement(1756023..1756769)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sufC"
                     /locus_tag="b1682"
                     /product="bifunctional Fe-S cluster scaffold complex
                     subunit SufC / SufBC2D Fe-S cluster scaffold complex"
                     /label=sufC
                     /note="The assembly of iron-sulfur clusters requires
                     complex biosynthetic machinery. <i>E. coli</i> encodes two 
                     sets of proteins, the Isc and the Suf system, to achieve 
                     this task. SufC is a component of the SufBC<sub>2</sub>D 
                     Fe-S cluster assembly scaffold complex. SufC is a member of
                     the ABC ATPase superfamily; its ATPase activity is required
                     for iron acquisition <i>in vivo</i>, and both SufC and SufD
                     are required for Fe-S cluster formation on SufB <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [20857974]|.
                     A crystal structure of SufC has been determined at 2.5 
                     &Aring; resolution |CITS: [16364320]|, and a crystal 
                     structure of the SufC<sub>2</sub>-SufD<sub>2</sub> complex 
                     has been determined at 2.2 &Aring; resolution |CITS: 
                     [19361433]|. Upon associating with SufD, the structure of 
                     SufC changes to become competent for ATP binding and 
                     hydrolysis |CITS: [19361433]|. Crystal structures of the 
                     SufBC<sub>2</sub>D complex have been solved. The SufB and 
                     SufD subunits form the center of the complex, and each is 
                     associated with one SufC subunit. In the presence of ATP, 
                     SufC appears to form a transient head-to-tail dimer which 
                     results in large structural changes in the SufBCD complex 
                     |CITS: [26472926]|.
                     SufB and SufC were reported to be associated with membranes
                     by immuno-EM |CITS: [11943156]|, but studies conducted in 
                     <i>Erwinia chrysanthemi</i> indicate that the homologs are 
                     cytoplasmic |CITS: [12876288]|.
                     A <i>sufABCDSE</i> operon deletion mutation leads to 
                     increased sensitivity to superoxide-generating agents 
                     |CITS: [15009899]|, whereas a <i>sufABCDSE isc</i> double 
                     mutant exhibits synthetic lethality, indicating that these 
                     systems are redundant and that iron-sulfur cluster assembly
                     is essential for viability |CITS: [12089140][15496591]|. 
                     Overproduction of the products of the <i>sufABCDSE</i> 
                     operon, or regulatory mutations in the <i>sufABCDSE</i> 
                     operon, suppress defects of a strain deleted of the 
                     <i>isc</i> operon |CITS: [12089140]|. A non-polar 
                     <i>sufC</i> mutant shows lower induction of <i>soxS</i> 
                     expression in response to paraquat, indicating a possible 
                     effect on the [2Fe-2S] cluster of SoxR |CITS: [11251816]|. 
                     The <i>sufC</i> deletion mutant of the Keio collection was 
                     identified in a screen for mutants with increased 
                     susceptibility to X-rays or UV-radiation |CITS: 
                     [27718375]|.
                     Site-directed mutagenesis of residues conserved in the ABC 
                     ATPases showed that the ATPase activity of SufC is required
                     for Fe-S cluster assembly <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [26472926]|.
                     Expression of <i>sufA</i>, the first gene in the 
                     <i>sufABCDSE</i> operon, is induced by superoxide 
                     generators and hydrogen peroxide |CITS: [15009899]|, and 
                     the amount of SufC was increased in a proteomics study in a
                     &Delta;|FRAME: CPLX0-245 Ahp| mutant |CITS: [37725887]|. 
                     Regulation occurs via the iron-dependent Fur repressor 
                     |CITS: [10322040]|, OxyR, IHF, and IscR |CITS: 
                     [15009899][16824106][18849427]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12935308][15952888][16211402][16221578][2 
                     0467446][25153801][31419582]|"
