     gene            complement(1756779..1758266)
                     /gene="sufB"
                     /locus_tag="b1683"
                     /label=sufB
     CDS             complement(1756779..1758266)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sufB"
                     /locus_tag="b1683"
                     /product="bifunctional Fe-S cluster scaffold complex
                     subunit SufB / SufBC2D Fe-S cluster scaffold complex"
                     /label=sufB
                     /note="The assembly of iron-sulfur clusters requires
                     complex biosynthetic machinery. <i>E. coli</i> encodes two 
                     sets of proteins, the Isc and the Suf system, to achieve 
                     this task. The SufE protein transfers sulfur to SufB |CITS:
                     [17350958]|, which is a component of the SufBC<sub>2</sub>D
                     Fe-S cluster assembly scaffold complex. The SufBCD complex 
                     activates the cysteine desulfurase activity of SufSE, 
                     likely by acting as the sulfur acceptor |CITS: 
                     [12941942][17350958]|.
                     The Cys405 residue of SufB and the His360 residue of SufD, 
                     located next to each other at the SufB-SufD interface, are 
                     required for Fe-S cluster assembly |CITS: [26472926]|. The 
                     Cys254 residue of SufB is required for transfer of sulfur 
                     from SufE. C405 and E434 of SufB, both located at the 
                     SufB-SufD interface, appear to be part of the site of Fe-S 
                     cluster formation; a potential tunnel in the &beta;-helix 
                     core domain may enable sulfur transfer from C254 to C405. 
                     In contrast, the CxxCxxxC motif comprised of C96, C99, and 
                     C103, is not required for <i>in vivo</i> function of SufB 
                     |CITS: [28839209]|.
                     <i>In vitro</i> reconstituted SufB stabilizes a 2Fe-2S 
                     cluster that is more resistant to hydrogen peroxide, oxygen
                     and EDTA than the corresponding 2Fe-2S cluster of IscU. The
                     2Fe-2S cluster can be converted to a 4Fe-4S cluster by 
                     reduction |CITS: [25485887]|.
                     SufB and SufC were reported to be associated with membranes
                     by immuno-EM |CITS: [11943156]|, but studies conducted in 
                     <i>Erwinia chrysanthemi</i> indicate that the homologs are 
                     cytoplasmic |CITS: [12876288]|.
                     A <i>sufABCDSE</i> operon deletion mutation leads to 
                     increased sensitivity to superoxide-generating agents 
                     |CITS: [15009899]|, whereas a <i>sufABCDSE isc</i> double 
                     mutant exhibits synthetic lethality, indicating that these 
                     systems are redundant and that iron-sulfur cluster assembly
                     is essential for viability |CITS: [12089140][15496591]|. 
                     Overproduction of the products of the <i>sufABCDSE</i> 
                     operon, or regulatory mutations in the <i>sufABCDSE</i> 
                     operon, suppress defects of a strain deleted of the 
                     <i>isc</i> operon |CITS: [12089140]|. Expression of 
                     <i>sufA</i>, the first gene in the <i>sufABCDSE</i> operon,
                     is induced by superoxide generators and hydrogen peroxide 
                     |CITS: [15009899]|. Regulation occurs via the 
                     iron-dependent Fur repressor |CITS: [10322040]|, OxyR, IHF,
                     and IscR |CITS: [15009899][16824106][18849427]|.
                     Cysteine sites in peptides of SufB were significantly 
                     reduced in a redox proteomics study in a &Delta;|FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-245 Ahp| mutant |CITS: [37725887]|.
                     Deletion mutants, such as <i>iscS</i> and <i>|FRAME: 
                     G6909|</i>, along with 87 other identified mutants, many of
                     which contain [Fe-S] clusters or are involved in [Fe-S] 
                     cluster synthesis and maintenance, were hypersentitive to 
                     non-thermal atmospheric pressure plasma jets. Examination 
                     of Fe supplementation and overexpression were carried out 
                     |CITS: [30913981][40023980]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [15952888][16211402][16221578][20467446][2 
                     5153801][25447545]|"
