     gene            1764629..1764734
                     /gene="rprA"
                     /locus_tag="b4431"
                     /label=rprA
     misc_RNA        1764629..1764734
                     /gene="rprA"
                     /locus_tag="b4431"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA RprA"
                     /note="RprA is a small regulatory RNA that is required for 
                     wild-type production of |FRAME: RPOS-MONOMER| in response 
                     to osmotic shock |CITS: [11251852]|. Both the |FRAME: 
                     DSRA-RNA| and RprA small RNAs alter secondary structure in 
                     the leader sequence of the <i>rpoS</i> mRNA to facilitate 
                     RpoS translation |CITS: [12410838]| and increase both the 
                     accumulation and half life of <i>rpoS</i> mRNA |CITS: 
                     [20802038]|. RprA inhibits Rho-dependent transcription 
                     termination within the <i>rpoS</i> 5' UTR upon transition 
                     to the stationary phase |CITS: [27662085]|. RprA negatively
                     regulates the expression of |FRAME: PD01379| |CITS: 
                     [22250746][22356413]|, |FRAME: G6673-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [22356413]| and |FRAME: NARP-MONOMER| |CITS: [ 35748881]|. 
                     Additional targets of RprA have been identified by an 
                     affinity purification method |CITS: [29294085]|.
                     Hfq is required for stimulation of RpoS translation by RprA
                     |CITS: [12410838]|. RprA binds |FRAME: CPLX0-1461|, with 
                     dissociation constants variously measured to be between 1.2
                     and 96 nM |CITS: 
                     [11445539][18826256][21205841][21510661][21889623]|. Hfq 
                     enhances binding of RprA to the <i>rpoS</i> |CITS: 
                     [18826256]| and <i>csgD</i> |CITS: [22250746]| leader 
                     sequence, but is not essential for regulation of 
                     <i>csgD</i> expression |CITS: [22356413]|. Hfq and an Hfq 
                     binding site upstream of the base pairing region within 
                     <i>rpoS</i> mRNA are required for RprA activity <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [20457943]|. Interaction with Hfq alters 
                     the secondary structure of RprA |CITS: [23804244]|.
                     A co-crystal structure of a catalytically inactive |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3461| with what may be the RprA RNA from 
                     <i>Salmonella</i> shows a stem-loop structure, potentially 
                     the stem-loop closest to the 5' end of RprA, interacting 
                     with the catalytic domain of RNase E. Mutagenesis of RNase 
                     E residues that potentially interact with the RprA 
                     stem-loop structure shows that these residues are not 
                     involved in sensing the 5' end of RNAs |CITS: [30270108]|. 
                     Measurements of the half-life of RprA have given varied 
                     results: |CITS: [11251852]| report 7-8 min, while |CITS: 
                     [20717695]| report 32 min at 37&deg;C. The differences may 
                     be due to strain differences or different growth 
                     conditions. RprA RNA is subject to specific 
                     endoribonuclease cleavage |CITS: [11448770][20717695]|. The
                     stability of RprA RNA is strongly influenced by osmolarity 
                     and depends on the presence of Hfq |CITS: [20717695]|.
                     RprA appears to have some genetic interactions with other 
                     sRNAs under different growth conditions |CITS: [35920556]|.
                     RprA is expressed during stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [11445539][11448770]|; expression is activated by the 
                     |FRAME: RCSC-MONOMER RcsC|-|FRAME: RCSB-MONOMER RcsB| 
                     phosphorelay system |CITS: [12410838][23852870]| and 
                     repressed by |FRAME: EG12123-MONOMER| |CITS: [16621809]|. 
                     Expression of <i>rprA</i> is increased at lower 
                     concentrations of Mg<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [21665928]| and by
                     growth in low pH medium |CITS: [24319011]|. RprA is 
                     expressed at higher levels when cells are grown in pyruvate
                     compared to glucose |CITS: [31363904]|. Overexpression of 
                     RprA increases acid tolerance |CITS: [24319011]|; 
                     simultaneous overexpression of all three small RNA 
                     translational regulators of RpoS - DsrA, RprA and |FRAME: 
                     SRAH-RNA ArcZ| - increases acid tolerance during active 
                     cell growth supra-additively |CITS: [23892399]|. 
                     Overexpression of RprA inhibits the Cpx response |CITS: 
                     [25246476]|, decreases swarming motility and curli 
                     expression |CITS: [26469694]|, and rescues the cell shape 
                     defect of &Delta;<i>qseC</i> mutants |CITS: [32979869]|. An
                     <i>rprA</i> mutant exhibits a defect in production of RpoS 
                     in response to osmotic shock |CITS: [11251852]|. Deletion 
                     of <i>rprA</i> supresses the defect in biofilm formation of
                     an <i>rcsC</i> mutation. This effect of <i>rprA</i> is due 
                     to its action on <i>rpoS</i> mRNA |CITS: [19696107]|.
                     PsrA: ""<b>p</b>redicted <b>s</b>mall <b>R</b>NA"" |CITS: 
                     [11448770]|
                     RprA: ""<b>Rp</b>oS <b>r</b>egulator RNA"" |CITS: 
                     [11251852]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12753181][15487940][16153174][25028968][2 
                     9897834][29992897][31197038][32213244]|"
