     gene            1768046..1768912
                     /gene="ydiB"
                     /locus_tag="b1692"
                     /label=ydiB
     CDS             1768046..1768912
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ydiB"
                     /locus_tag="b1692"
                     /product="trifunctional quinate/shikimate dehydrogenase /
                     shikimate dehydrogenase / quinate dehydrogenase"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.282"
                     /label=ydiB
                     /note="YdiB was identified as a quinate/shikimate
                     dehydrogenase.  The low catalytic efficiency of YdiB with 
                     both quinate and shikimate suggests that neither may be the
                     physiological substrate |CITS: [12637497]|.  Wild-type 
                     <i>E. coli</i> is not known to synthesize quinate or to use
                     it as a source of carbon.
                     Phylogenomic analysis of the shikimate dehydrogenase family
                     showed that the two enzymes encoded in <i>E. coli</i>, 
                     |FRAME: AROE-MONOMER AroE| and YdiB, belong to different 
                     subclasses; the authors suggested that YdiB was acquired by
                     horizontal gene transfer.  Common and subclass-specific 
                     catalytic residues and substrate binding motifs were 
                     identified |CITS: [18669580]|.
                     YdiB can use both NAD<sup>+</sup> or NADP<sup>+</sup> as a 
                     cosubstrate for catalysis |CITS: [12637497]|.  Because the 
                     intracellular concentration of NAD<sup>+</sup> is 40-fold 
                     higher than that of NADH<sup>+</sup>, the dehydrogenase 
                     direction is expected to be favored by YdiB <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [16828913]|.  Conversely, AroE is NADP<sup>+</sup>- 
                     and substrate-specific and has much higher catalytic 
                     efficiency than YdiB |CITS: [12637497]|.  <i>aroE</i> 
                     mutant data and the results from metabolic engineering 
                     experiments strongly suggest that YdiB is unable to replace
                     AroE under normal physiological conditions. Expression of 
                     YdiB for engineering of L-tyrosine production led to 
                     accumulation of the pathway intermediates dehydroquinate 
                     and dehydroshikimate and the side product quinate |CITS: 
                     [22020510]|.
                     Crystal structures of YdiB have been solved at 2.3 &Aring; 
                     and 2.5 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [12624088][12637497]|. 
                     Although AroE and YdiB have low sequence similarity, they 
                     have high structural similarity with two &alpha;/&beta; 
                     domains separated by a wide cleft |CITS: [12637497]|. 
                     Results from site-directed mutagenesis of predicted 
                     active-site residues do not support a general acid-base 
                     mechanism for catalysis |CITS: [15596430]|.
                     Quinate is produced as a by-product in a genetically 
                     modified shikimate-producing <i>E. coli</i> strain |CITS: 
                     [DRATHS99][11603966]| and can be attributed to YdiB 
                     activity |CITS: [27855390]|.  Expression of <i>ydiB</i> in 
                     a shikimate-producing strain is only induced under 
                     carbon-limiting growth conditions |CITS: [16828913]|. 
                     Review: |CITS: [25524738]|
                     "
