     CDS             1768943..1769701
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aroD"
                     /locus_tag="b1693"
                     /product="3-dehydroquinate dehydratase"
                     /EC_number="4.2.1.10"
                     /label=aroD
                     /note="3-Dehydroquinate dehydratase (DHQ dehydratase) is 
                     involved in the 3rd step of the chorismate pathway, which 
                     leads to the biosynthesis of aromatic amino acids. DHQ 
                     dehydratase catalyzes the conversion of DHQ to 
                     3-dehydroshikimate and introduces the first double bond of 
                     the aromatic ring |CITS: [13198937]|.
                     The first step of the 3-dehydroquinase reaction involves 
                     the formation of an imine intermediate between the keto 
                     group of 3-dehydroquinate and the e-amino group of a lysine
                     at the active site of the enzyme. Chemical modification and
                     mutagenesis experiments have identified this lysine to be 
                     Lys-170 |CITS: [1826831]|. In addition to the lysine side 
                     chain involved in imine formation, the enzyme also requires
                     a basic group for proton abstraction. His-143 has been 
                     shown to be essential for catalysis in this enzyme and that
                     this residue may be acting as the general base in the 
                     catalytic mechanism. His-143 may also be involved in Schiff
                     base formation/breakdown. |CITS: [7592767]|
                     The pH activity studies demonstrate that 3-dehydroquinase 
                     requires groups with pKa values near 6 in a deprotonated 
                     form for maximal activity. Experiments with 
                     diethylpyrocarbonate provide evidence that this group is 
                     the imidazole side chain of a histidine residue. 
                     |CITS:[2950851]| The reaction exhibits cis stereochemistry 
                     in both directions |CITS: [13198937]|.
                     The structural gene, <i>aroD</i>, has been cloned and 
                     overexpressed |CITS: [3541912]|. Crystals of DHQ 
                     dehydratase in <i>E. coli</i> and <i>Salmonella typhi</i> 
                     were grown. However, only the <i>S. typhi</i> DHQ 
                     dehydratase structure has been resolved to 2.3 &Aring; 
                     resolution. The <i>E. coli</i> and <i>S. typhi</i> DHQ 
                     dehydratase is 69% identical at the amino acid level |CITS:
                     [2045778]|."
