     gene            1771453..1772604
                     /gene="ydiO"
                     /locus_tag="b1695"
                     /label=ydiO
     CDS             1771453..1772604
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ydiO"
                     /locus_tag="b1695"
                     /product="putative acyl-CoA dehydrogenase YdiO"
                     /label=ydiO
                     /note="YdiO has sequence similarity to |FRAME: 
                     CROBETREDUCT-MONOMER| and is much less similar to |FRAME: 
                     ACYLCOADEHYDROG-MONOMER ""FadE""|, the acyl-CoA 
                     dehydrogenase involved in fatty acid oxidation.  The nearby
                     genes <i>ydiQR</i> encode a putative electron transfer 
                     flavoprotein-like heterodimer, while <i>ydiST</i> may form 
                     an ETF-quinone oxidoreductase and may be involved in an 
                     electron transfer pathway with YdiO, analogous to 
                     <i>fixABCX</i> and CaiA |CITS: [12535077][21832992]|.
                     An assay of unpurified YdiO showed a small amount of 
                     dehydrogenase activity with butyryl-CoA; no activity was 
                     detected with the purified protein, indicating that the 
                     enzyme may require auxiliary flavoproteins and an electron 
                     donor to function |CITS: [23656231]|.  Overexpression of 
                     YdiO did not result in a significant increase in 
                     butyryl-CoA dehydrogenase activity in crude extracts |CITS:
                     [25527535]|.
                     YdiO was hypothesized to catalyze the reduction of 
                     enoyl-CoA to acyl-CoA, enabling the engineered one-turn 
                     reversal of the &beta;-oxidation cycle to yield butanol.
                     A &Delta;<i>ydiO</i> mutation in the engineered strain led 
                     to increased yield of trans-2-butenoate and reduced butanol
                     production |CITS: [21832992]|.  In contrast to these 
                     initial results, it was later shown that neither deletion 
                     nor overexpression of YdiO affected butyrate yield in the 
                     engineered strain.  The <i>trans</i>-enoyl-CoA reductase 
                     activity was ascribed primarily to |FRAME: 
                     ENOYL-ACP-REDUCT-NADH-MONOMER FabI| |CITS: [25527535]|.
                     YdiO was predicted to be an acyl-CoA dehydrogenase |CITS: 
                     [12535077][12952533]|."
