     gene            1777234..1778934
                     /gene="fadK"
                     /locus_tag="b1701"
                     /label=fadK
     CDS             1777234..1778934
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fadK"
                     /locus_tag="b1701"
                     /product="multifunctional medium-chain-fatty-acid--CoA
                     ligase / medium-chain acyl-CoA synthetase (transporting) / 
                     cis-vaccenoyl-CoA synthetase (transporting) / oleoyl-CoA 
                     synthetase (transporting) / palmitoleoyl-CoA synthetase 
                     (transporting)"
                     /EC_number="6.2.1.2; 6.2.1.3"
                     /label=fadK
                     /note="FadK is an acyl-CoA synthetase that is primarily
                     active on medium-chain (C6:0 and C8:0) fatty acids and acts
                     in anaerobic &beta;-oxidation of fatty acids |CITS: 
                     [12535077][15213221]|.  During anaerobic &beta;-oxidation 
                     of fatty acids, |FRAME: G7213 FadI|, |FRAME: G7212 FadJ|, 
                     and |FRAME: EG12357 FadK| serve functions parallel to those
                     of |FRAME: EG10278 FadA|, |FRAME: EG10279 FadB|, and 
                     |FRAME: EG11530 FadD| in the aerobic pathway |CITS: 
                     [12535077]|.
                     FadK has a FACS (fatty acyl-CoA-binding) motif and an 
                     ATP/AMP binding motif |CITS: [12535077]|.  Based on 
                     sequence similarity, FadK has been predicted to be a 
                     hydroxycinnamate-CoA ligase |CITS: [12952533]|.
                     A <i>fadD fadK</i> double mutant exhibits a complete defect
                     in anaerobic growth on fatty acids, indicating that FadD 
                     acyl-CoA synthetase activity partially complements FadK 
                     activity under anaerobic growth conditions |CITS: 
                     [12535077]|.  The failure of FadK to complement a 
                     <i>fadD</i> mutant under aerobic conditions is likely due 
                     to poor expression of FadK under those conditions |CITS: 
                     [15213221]|.
                     FadK is not expressed under aerobic conditions, and the 
                     level of expression under anaerobic conditions depends on 
                     the terminal electron acceptor: in the presence of nitrate,
                     expression is low, while in the presence of fumarate,
                     expression is high |CITS: [15213221]|."
                     /translation="MHPTGPHLGPDVLFRESNMKVTLTFNEQRRAAYRQQGLWGDASLA
