     gene            1782692..1783738
                     /gene="aroH"
                     /locus_tag="b1704"
                     /label=aroH
     CDS             1782692..1783738
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aroH"
                     /locus_tag="b1704"
                     /product="bifunctional 3-deoxy-7-phosphoheptulonate
                     synthase, Trp-sensitive / 3-deoxy-7-phosphoheptulonate 
                     synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.5.1.54"
                     /label=aroH
                     /note="2-Dehydro-3-deoxyphosphoheptonate aldolase (DAHP 
                     synthase) is involved in the first committed step of the 
                     chorismate pathway, which leads to the biosynthesis of 
                     aromatic amino acids. DAHP synthase catalyzes an aldol 
                     reaction between phosphoenolpyruvate and D-erythrose 
                     4-phosphate to generate 3-deoxy-D-arabino-heptulosonate 
                     7-phosphate (DAHP). |CITS: [13654249]|
                     There are three isozymes of DAHP synthase, each 
                     specifically feedback regulated by tyrosine, phenylalanine 
                     or tryptophan |CITS: [13989507][6167722]|. DAHP synthase 
                     which is sensitive to tryptophan (DAHP synthase (Trp), 
                     AroH) is encoded by <i>aroH</i>. All three isozymes are 
                     metalloenzymes, and DAHP synthase (Trp) is activated by 
                     Fe<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [1672127]|. There is a high degree 
                     of sequence identity (41%) between the three isozymes and 
                     the polypeptides are nearly identical in size |CITS: 
                     [2903857]|. In wild-type cells grown in minimal media, DAHP
                     synthase (Phe) makes up about 80% of the total DAHP 
                     synthase activity, DAHP synthase (Tyr) makes up about 20%, 
                     and DAHP synthase (Trp) makes up about 1% |CITS: [8426]|. 
                     Mutations in <i>aroH</i> lead to tryptophan-insensitive 
                     DAHP synthase |CITS: [4887505][2903857]|. Essential regions
                     for catalytic activity and feedback regulation have been 
                     described |CITS: [2903857]|.
                     Transcriptional control of <i>aroH</i> is mediated by the 
                     <i>trpR</i> repressor with the end products of the pathway 
                     acting as co-repressors |CITS: [4887504][6125934]|."
