     gene            complement(1785564..1786277)
                     /gene="rflP"
                     /locus_tag="b1707"
                     /label=rflP
     CDS             complement(1785564..1786277)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rflP"
                     /locus_tag="b1707"
                     /product="anti-FlhDC factor RflP"
                     /label=rflP
                     /note="RflP is involved in the control of motility |CITS: 
                     [22461489]| and may be involved in quorum sensing |CITS: 
                     [18560382]|.  However, impaired translation leads to low 
                     levels of RflP protein <i>in vivo</i> and makes flagellar 
                     synthesis insensitive to RflP-mediated inhibition in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [22461489]|.  RflP contains a 
                     degenerate EAL phosphodiesterase domain |CITS: 
                     [22461489][26148715]|.
                     Both <i>E. coli</i> and <i>Salmonella</i> regulate 
                     expression of flagella and motility in response to nutrient
                     availability, but in opposite directions.  <i>E. coli</i> 
                     shows higher motility under nutrient-poor conditions, while
                     <i>Salmonella</i> is more motile under nutrient-rich 
                     conditions.  The function of RflP was first studied in 
                     <i>Salmonella</i>, where it was shown to regulate flagellar
                     biosynthesis by acting as an anti-FlhDC factor.  It has now
                     been shown that RflP functions as an anti-|FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3930 ""FlhDC""| factor in <i>E. coli</i> as well.  
                     <i>In vitro</i>, RflP interacts with both FlhD and FlhC and
                     inhibits <i>fliA</i> promoter binding of FlhDC in a gel 
                     shift assay |CITS: [22461489]|. The effect of RflP on the 
                     DNA binding ability of FlhD<sub>4</sub>C<sub>2</sub> is 
                     concentration-dependent. Binding of three or more RflP 
                     molecules to FlhD<sub>4</sub>C<sub>2</sub> may disrupt the 
                     ring-like structure and thereby the DNA binding surfaces of
                     FlhD<sub>4</sub>C<sub>2</sub> |CITS: [23002140]|. 
                     Anti-FlhDC factor RflP (YdiV) creates a monotonic 
                     ultrasensitive switch that serves as a digital filter to 
                     eliminate small-amplitude input fluctuations. The 
                     high-frequency (fast) fluctuations of FlhDC are filtered by
                     integration over time. The filter-and-integrate design can 
                     generate stochastic pulses without feedback, and  this 
                     strategy allows cells to turn on gene expression only for 
                     persistently strong input signals |CITS:[33046652]|. Unlike
                     other EAL family proteins, RflP does not form dimers in 
                     solution; however, RflP can form stable ternary complexes 
                     with |FRAME: CPLX0-3930 FlhD<sub>4</sub>C<sub>2</sub>| 
                     |CITS: [23002140]|.
                     Crystal structures of RflP and a 
                     RflP<sub>2</sub>-FlhD<sub>2</sub> complex have been solved 
                     |CITS: [23002140]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>rflP</i> inhibits motility and 
                     flagellin production |CITS: [22461489][23002140][32209689]|
                     and enables mechanosensing of a soft agar growth substrate 
                     |CITS: [32209689]|. Expression of <i>rflP</i> is inhibited 
                     by glucose |CITS: [18560382]|.  Expression of <i>rflP</i> 
                     is induced by extracellular |FRAME: 
                     Acyl-homoserine-lactones ""autoinducer-1""| (via |FRAME: 
                     PD02198|) that is produced by other organisms |CITS: 
                     [18560382]| and by iron deficiency (this induction is 
                     independent of Fur) |CITS: [32813023]|.  <i>rflP</i> 
                     expression may be dependent on &sigma;<sup>S</sup> under 
                     low pH, but not other stress conditions |CITS: [17010156]|,
                     and displayed significantly lower variability across 
                     populations adapted to different toxins |CITS: [28217741]|.
                     RflP, in cooperation with the |FRAME: EG11663 
                     ""peptidyl-prolyl <i>cis-trans</I> isomerase SlyD""|, is 
                     also involved in regulation of the |FRAME: PD00260|. During
                     iron starvation, Fur is inactivated by RflP and SlyD in a 
                     cooperative manner.  According to the current model, RflP 
                     binds to Fur, and this binding enhances the ability of SlyD
                     to modify Pro18, which converts Fur into a conformer that 
                     cannot bind to DNA. In a strain overexpressing RflP, in the
                     presence of SlyD, the DNA-binding activity of Fur decreases
                     300-fold |CITS:[32813023][37010421]|.
                     RflP: ""<b>r</b>egulator of <b>Fl</b>hDC 
                     <b>p</b>roteolysis"" |CITS: [26148715]|
                     Review: |CITS: [16045609]|"
