     gene            complement(1787815..1788366)
                     /gene="btuE"
                     /locus_tag="b1710"
                     /label=btuE
     CDS             complement(1787815..1788366)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="btuE"
                     /locus_tag="b1710"
                     /product="trifunctional thioredoxin/glutathione peroxidase
                     BtuE / thioredoxin peroxidase / glutathione peroxidase"
                     /EC_number="1.11.1.9"
                     /label=btuE
                     /note="BtuE is a relatively non-specific peroxidase that
                     is able to utilize both thioredoxins as well as 
                     glutathione.  Using thioredoxin as a reducing agent, the 
                     enzyme is able to decompose a variety of organic 
                     hydroperoxides |CITS: [20621065]|.
                     BtuE is encoded as part of the <i>btuCED</i> operon, which 
                     also encodes proteins that are part of a vitamin 
                     B<sub>12</sub> transport system |CITS: [8789466]|. 
                     Transposon insertions in the <i>btuC</i> and <i>btuD</i> 
                     regions conferred a deficiency in vitamin B<sub>12</sub> 
                     utilization and transport |CITS: [3528128]|.  However, 
                     there are indications that BtuE (residing within the 
                     operon) is not required for transport.  Transposon 
                     insertions in <i>btuE</i> were not complemented by plasmids
                     carrying <i>btuE</i> alone, whereas insertions in 
                     <i>btuC</i> and <i>btuD</i> were effectively complemented 
                     by plasmids carrying the corresponding functional gene 
                     |CITS: [3528128][2671656]|.  <i>btuE</i> mutants were also 
                     shown to have little effect on vitamin B<sub>12</sub> 
                     binding and transport and did not affect the utilization of
                     vitamin B<sub>12</sub> or other cobalamins for methionine 
                     biosynthesis |CITS: [2671656]|.
                     Production of BtuE in maxicells leads to partial 
                     periplasmic localization of the protein; however, the 
                     protein does not behave like other periplasmic proteins in 
                     fractionation studies |CITS: [3528128]| and lacks an 
                     obvious signal sequence |CITS: [3528129]|.
                     <i>btuE</i> expression is increased in stationary phase and
                     induced by oxidative stress |CITS: [20621065]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>btuE</i> mutant is more sensitive to oxidative 
                     stress than wild type |CITS: [21264338]|.
                     A Tn<i>10</i> insertion in the <i>btuCED</i> promoter 
                     region restores colony-forming ability to an <i>rne</i> 
                     mutant.  The suppression phenotype is reversed by 
                     overexpression of <i>btuC, E</i> or <i>D</i> |CITS: 
                     [22328678]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [22609271]|
                     "
