     gene            complement(1793483..1793527)
                     /gene="pheM"
                     /locus_tag="b1715"
                     /label=pheM
     CDS             complement(1793483..1793527)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pheM"
                     /locus_tag="b1715"
                     /product="pheST-ihfA operon leader peptide"
                     /label=pheM
                     /note="The PheM leader peptide controls by attenuation the 
                     expression of the <i>pheMST</i> operon
                     (which codes for phenylalanyl-tRNA synthetase) in response 
                     to phenylalanine availability
                     |CITS: [3158742][6317866]|. Specifically, decreased levels 
                     of charged tRNA<sup>Phe</sup> lead to derepression of 
                     <i>pheMST</i> |CITS: [6426518]|. Although other ORFs have 
                     been detected upstream from <i>pheS</i>,
                     PheM appears to be the only functional one |CITS: 
                     [3162032][3126825]|.
                     PheM is a 14-amino acid peptide, with five phenylalanine 
                     residues serving as regulatory points
                     during attenuation |CITS: [3158742]|.
                     One of the first tests of <i>pheM</i> as an attenuator was 
                     done by |CITS: [6096210]|."
                     /translation="MNAAIFRFFFYFST"
                     /go_component="membrane [goid 0016020] [evidence IEA]
                     [PMID 10868275]"
                     /go_process="regulation of DNA-templated transcription
                     [goid 0006355] [evidence IMP] [PMID 3158742]"
     gene            complement(1793650..1794006)
                     /gene="rplT"
                     /locus_tag="b1716"
                     /label=rplT
     CDS             complement(1793650..1794006)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rplT"
                     /locus_tag="b1716"
                     /product="trifunctional 50S ribosomal subunit protein L20
                     / 50S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rplT
                     /note="The L20 protein is a component of the 50S subunit
                     of the ribosome and autoregulates its own expression and 
                     that of L35 at the posttranscriptional level.  The 
                     N-terminal domain of L20 is required for ribosome assembly,
                     and the C-terminal domain is required for its regulatory 
                     function |CITS: [15840820]|.
                     L20 binds to the 5' terminal third of 23S rRNA |CITS: 
                     [800986][1093869][414209][386275][8308887]|.  The 
                     C-terminal domain of L20 interacts with both 23S rRNA 
                     (H40-41) and the translational regulatory region.  Proper 
                     folding of the L20 binding sites within RNA is required for
                     interaction with L20, and binding of L20 stabilizes their 
                     structure |CITS: [22025688]|.  L20 is required for early 
                     assembly of the 4.3c core particle, but is not required for
                     function of the mature 50S ribosomal subunit |CITS: 
                     [7021848]|.  L20 can replace L24 for the initiation of 
                     assembly of the 50S subunit at permissive temperatures in 
                     an L24 mutant |CITS: [2461735]|.  L20 can be crosslinked to
                     L13 and L21 |CITS: [2645289][2653827][2665813]|.
                     L20 might be required for maintaining the 50S subunit in 
                     the correct conformation for binding of aminoacyl-tRNAs 
                     |CITS: [786630]|.  Decreasing the amount of L20 in the cell
                     leads to a decrease in growth rate and the appearance of an
                     aberrant ribosome peak at 41-43S |CITS: [8628231]|. 
                     Expression of <i>rplT</i> is autoregulated at the 
                     posttranscriptional level.  Two mRNA regions, one located 
                     within <i>infC</i> |CITS: [2191140]| and a second located 
                     close to the translation start site of <i>rpmI</i> |CITS: 
                     [8861967]|, form a long-range RNA pseudoknot structure 
                     |CITS: [8861967]|.  Both the pseudoknot structure and a 
                     second stem-loop binding site are required for repression 
                     |CITS: [12166643]|, and both sites share secondary 
                     structure similarity with the L20 binding site in rRNA 
                     |CITS: [15916597]|.  The two binding sites interact, and 
                     only one molecule of L20 binds to the regulatory region 
                     |CITS: [17439971]|.  Structural analysis of RNA binding by 
                     the L20 C-terminal domain by NMR show that binding of L20 
                     to rRNA and mRNA is similar |CITS: [12840018]|.  Unlike the
                     regulation of the ribosomal alpha operon and of 
                     <i>rpsO</i>, translational autoregulation by L20 appears to
                     be based on competition with the ribosome for binding to 
                     its mRNA |CITS: [18037435]|.
                     Decreased translation of <i>infC</i> leads to increased 
                     L20-mediated repression of L35 expression; translating 
                     ribosomes may disrupt the RNA pseudoknot structure, thereby
                     attenuating repression |CITS: [9256461]|.  The pseudoknot 
                     structure appears to enhance binding of the 30S ribosomal 
                     subunit to this mRNA |CITS: [18037435]|.
                     L20 directly represses the expression of <i>rpmI</i>, but 
                     only indirectly that of <i>rplT</i>, via translational 
                     coupling with <i>rpmI</i> |CITS: [1453449]|.  A secondary 
                     structure forms between the translation initiation site of 
                     <i>rplT</i> and a site internal to <i>rpmI</i> |CITS: 
                     [1453449]|; this structure forms <i>in vivo</i> and is 
                     required for translational coupling |CITS: [8955899]|.  It 
                     is thought that the secondary structure can form when 
                     <i>rpmI</i> is not translated, and it can be shown that it 
                     blocks binding of the ribosome to the ribosome binding site
                     of <i>rplT</i> |CITS: [8955899]|.
                     Transcription of <i>rplT</i> is sensitive to changes in 
                     growth rate |CITS: [2185470]|.  L20 appears to be part of 
                     the polyamine modulon |CITS: [16476727]|."
