     gene            complement(1794899..1796827)
                     /gene="thrS"
                     /locus_tag="b1719"
                     /label=thrS
     CDS             complement(1794899..1796827)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="thrS"
                     /locus_tag="b1719"
                     /product="trifunctional L-serine--tRNA(Thr) ligase / 
                     L-seryl-[tRNA(Thr)] deacylase / threonine--tRNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.1.1.3"
                     /label=thrS
                     /note="Threonine-tRNA ligase (ThrRS) is a member of the
                     family of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, which interpret the 
                     genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their 
                     specific tRNA molecules.  The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis.  ThrRS belongs to the Class IIA aminoacyl-tRNA 
                     synthetases |CITS: [2203971][1852601][23075299]|.
                     Kinetic analysis of ThrRS supports a two-step reaction 
                     mechanism with an induced fit conformational change for the
                     adenylation reaction |CITS: [14690420]|.  A proton relay 
                     model involving an active site histidine residue has been 
                     proposed |CITS: [18997014]|.  The tightly binding 
                     noncompetitive inhibitor borrelidin acts at the threonine 
                     activation step |CITS: [15507440]|.  Mutations in the zinc 
                     binding site inactivate the enzymatic function of ThrRS, 
                     but not its regulatory function, leading to a dominant 
                     negative phenotype when overexpressed in wild-type |CITS: 
                     [17644600]|.  Acetylation of several lysine residues within
                     ThrRS reduces the tRNA charging activity of the enzyme 
                     |CITS: [30642164]|.
                     ThrRS has both pre- and posttransfer editing activities 
                     |CITS: [20504770]|.  A zinc ion at the active site provides
                     the ability to discriminate against the isosteric amino 
                     acid valine at the activation step |CITS: [10881191]|.  In 
                     the absence of tRNA, amino acid activation is asymmetric; 
                     serine is activated at rates similar to threonine, but the 
                     seryl-adenylate is hydrolyzed.  When tRNA is present, seryl
                     transfer to tRNA is only 2-fold slower than threonyl 
                     transfer |CITS: [20504770]|.  The N-terminal domain of 
                     ThrRS contains a post-transfer editing function that is 
                     able to deacylate mischarged Ser-tRNA<sup>Thr</sup> |CITS: 
                     [11136973][15525511]|.  Oxidative stress leads to oxidation
                     of the Cys182 residue to cysteine sulfenic acid |CITS: 
                     [24744241]|, which impairs the editing activity and leads 
                     to formation of Ser-tRNA<sup>Thr</sup>, protein 
                     mistranslation, and impaired growth |CITS: [20160114]|. 
                     ThrRS can not effectively discriminate against the 
                     unnatural amino acid &beta;-hydroxynorvaline |CITS: 
                     [21222438]|.  Editing of mischarged Ser-tRNA<sup>Thr</sup> 
                     is negatively affected by acetylation of the Lys169 residue
                     in ThrRS |CITS: [30642164]|.  ThrRS is able to cross-edit 
                     mischarged human Ala-tRNA<sup>Thr</sup>, which is not known
                     to occur in bacteria.  This activity is proposed to enable 
                     the evolution of the mischargeable G4:U69-containing 
                     tRNA<sup>Thr</sup> in vertebrates |CITS: [32484512]|.
                     Threonyl-tRNA synthetase represses its own expression at 
                     the translational level |CITS: 
                     [6325425][3930755][3510186]|.  Mutations that affect 
                     autoregulation map to a region between the transcription 
                     start site and the start of the <i>thrS</i> open reading 
                     frame; the site has sequence and structural similarity to 
                     parts of tRNA<sup>Thr</sup> |CITS: 
                     [3086882][3054873][3072027]|.  ThrRS interacts with two 
                     stem-loop structures in the mRNA leader region |CITS: 
                     [1701663][2207165][1383551][8199252][7683056][8918475]|. 
                     Defects in the regulatory and aminoacylation functions in 
                     ThrRS are correlated, suggesting that the enzyme recognizes
                     its mRNA and tRNA ligands in analogous ways |CITS: 
                     [2676521][1701663]|; however, one ThrRS dimer binds only 
                     one operator region, but each of the two ThrRS subunits 
                     binds one tRNA<sup>Thr</sup> |CITS: [8918475]|.
                     A change in the leader region according to tRNA identity 
                     rules to resemble the tRNA<sup>Met</sup> anticodon leads to
                     translational regulation of ThrRS by MetRS |CITS: 
                     [1372129]|.  tRNA<sup>Thr</sup> acts as an antirepressor 
                     |CITS: [2254931]|, and the operator site of the mRNA acts 
                     as a competitive inhibitor of tRNA<sup>Thr</sup> 
                     aminoacylation |CITS: [1280807]|.
                     Binding of ThrRS to its mRNA target sites prevents the 
                     ribosome from binding and initiating translation |CITS: 
                     [2207165]|.  Mutations in the <i>thrS</i> UTR that increase
                     repression cause additional base pairing and sequestration 
                     of the ribosome binding site |CITS: [7563089]|.  
                     Competition between ThrRS and the ribosome for binding to 
                     the mRNA can be explained by steric hindrance |CITS: 
                     [9767575]|.  The catalytic and C-terminal domains of ThrRS 
                     are responsible for operator binding, while the N-terminal 
                     domain is responsible for competition with the ribosome 
                     |CITS: [12581352]|.  Untranslated <i>thrS</i> mRNA appears 
                     to be immediately degraded and is therefore not detected 
                     |CITS: [11453682]|.
                     ThrRS is a dimer in solution |CITS: [330505]|.  Crystal 
                     structures of ThrRS in various conformations have been 
                     determined, elucidating the role of the zinc ion in the 
                     active site, the nature of the editing domain, the 
                     structural basis for translational control, and the nature 
                     of inhibition by borrelidin |CITS: [10319817][10881191][11 
                     136973][11953757][15525511][25824639]|.
                     Specificity determinants within tRNA<sup>Thr</sup> that are
                     important for recognition by ThrRS have been identified 
                     |CITS: [2457500][2109304][1567450][18586541]|. The 2'-OH of
                     A76 plays a role in aminoacyl transfer |CITS: [18997014]|.
                     Inhibitors of ThrRS activity with selectivity for the 
                     bacterial enzyme have been designed and tested |CITS: 
                     [23362938][31675206]|.
                     ThrRS is under growth rate-dependent control which acts at 
                     the level of translation and requires feedback regulation 
                     by ThrRS |CITS: [8757280]|.  Certain mutations leading to 
                     borrelidin resistance cause increased expression of ThrRS 
                     |CITS: [6997265]|.  A selection for borrelidin-resistant 
                     ThrRS mutants identified a potential binding site |CITS: 
                     [15507440]|; the binding site has been further defined by 
                     molecular docking and site-directed mutagenesis of 
                     predicted binding site residues |CITS: [25128830]| and in 
                     crystal structures |CITS: [25824639]|.  A mutation in 
                     <i>thrS</i> slows protein production and the cellular 
                     growth rate, and thereby confers increased resistance to 
                     inhibitors of |FRAME: UDPACYLGLCNACDEACETYL-MONOMER LpxC| 
                     |CITS: [23316051]|.
                     <i>thrS</i> is essential for growth |CITS: [16738554]|. 
                     There is a general discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [37224536]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10966471][8419283][10881182][12615010][12 
                     787346][23075299][23536244]|."
                     /translation="MPVITLPDGSQRHYDHAVSPMDVALDIGPGLAKACIAGRVNGELV
