     CDS             1805191..1806582
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tcyP"
                     /locus_tag="b1729"
                     /product="multifunctional cystine/sulfocysteine:cation
                     symporter / L-cystine:H(+) symporter / S-sulfocysteine:H(+)
                     symporter / D-cystine:H(+) symporter / homocystine:H(+) 
                     symporter"
                     /label=tcyP
                     /note="<i>tcyP</i> encodes an inner membrane L-cystine 
                     transporter in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [15262924] 
                     [25139244][26350134]|. Two cystine uptake systems (now 
                     known to be the ABC transporter |FRAME: CPLX0-8152 
                     ""TcyJLN""| and TcyP) were first identified in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> W |CITS: [4564569]|. TcyP is responsible for the 
                     majority (approx. 80%) of L-cystine uptake during growth on
                     minimal salts media; transport of labeled L-cystine is 
                     inhibited by L-cysteine and to a lesser extent D-cystine 
                     |CITS: [25139244]|. TcyP also mediates the import of 
                     S-sulfocysteine for use as a sulfur source |CITS: 
                     [27481704]|. Under aerobic growth conditions, <i>E. 
                     coli</i> imports cystine as an economical alternative to 
                     sulfate assimilation; <i>E. coli</i> lacks a dedicated 
                     cysteine importer and under anaerobic conditions when 
                     cysteine remains in the reduced form cells may assimilate 
                     sulfur from hydrogen sulfide |CITS: [32518189]|.
                     TcyP is also able to transport the toxic compounds 
                     L-selenaproline and L-selenocystine |CITS: [25139244]|. 
                     TcyP imports L-homocystine as a methionine precursor |CITS:
                     [26350134]|. Both TcyP and TcyJLN function to replenish 
                     cysteine pools in sulfur starved cells; TcyP is the primary
                     transporter when cystine is abundant whereas the high 
                     affinity ABC transporter, TcyJLN, functions when cystine is
                     scarce |CITS: [26350134]|. TcyP may have specificity for 
                     compounds containing disulfide bonds that can be 
                     reductively cleaved |CITS: [26350134]|.
                     Disruption of <i>tcyP</i> (&Delta;<i>cysE ydjN::kan</i>) 
                     results in the inability to grow with S-sulfocysteine as 
                     the sole source of sulfur; sulfocysteine uptake accelerates
                     when <i>tcyP</i> is overexpressed and is eliminated when 
                     <i>tcyP</i> is deleted |CITS: [27481704]|. 
                     &Delta;<i>tcyP</i> cells have a significantly reduced rate 
                     of <sup>14</sup>C-cystine import compared to wild type; 
                     rapid cystine import by TcyP results in a 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> supersensitive phenotype when 
                     cells are pregrown in sulfate media and then grown in 
                     cystine media and exposed to H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     |CITS: [26350134]|. When cystine is abundant, TcyP imports 
                     more than the cell requires; excess cysteine is exported 
                     thus setting up a futile import/reduction/export cycle 
                     |CITS: [26350134]|.
                     TcyP, TcyJLN and the L-cysteine exporter |FRAME: 
                     EG11639-MONOMER ""EamA""| participate in an 
                     L-cystine/L-cysteine shuttle system which is suggested to 
                     protect the cell membrane under oxidative stress 
                     conditions; L-cystine importers (TcyJLN and TcyP) cooperate
                     with an L-cysteine exporter (EamA) to supply reducing 
                     equivalents to the periplasm; operation of the shuttle is 
                     implicated in the protection against periplasmic 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>  |CITS: [20351115][25837721]| 
                     (see also |CITS: [32900959]|). <i>tcyP</i> is expressed as 
                     a monocistronic transcript; expression is regulated by CysB
                     and is induced when cells are grown with poor sulfur 
                     sources such as sulfate and djenkolate and repressed during
                     growth with cystine |CITS: [26350134]|.
                     TcyP belongs to the Dicarboxylate/Amino Acid:Cation 
                     Symporter (DAACS) family of transporters |CITS: 
                     [24225317]|. TcyP (and other proteins of the CysB regulon) 
                     contain very few cysteine residues which is probably the 
                     result of selection against cysteine due to the strict 
                     requirement for TcyP (and other CysB regulon proteins) when
                     intracellular cysteine levels fall |CITS: [26350134]|."
