     gene            complement(1807678..1807920)
                     /gene="cedA"
                     /locus_tag="b1731"
                     /label=cedA
     CDS             complement(1807678..1807920)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cedA"
                     /locus_tag="b1731"
                     /product="cell division modulator"
                     /label=cedA
                     /note="CedA appears to be involved in the regulation of
                     cell division in a wild-type or <i>dnaA</i> mutant strain 
                     |CITS: [9427399]|.
                     Multicopy overproduction of CedA suppresses the cold 
                     sensitivity of a <i>dnaAcos</i> mutant |CITS: [9427399]|. 
                     Multicopy overproduction causes increased cell division in 
                     a <i>dnaAcos</i> mutant background, but does not suppress 
                     the DNA over-replication phenotype of this strain, 
                     suggesting that excessive DNA replication may trigger a 
                     cell division checkpoint and that overproduction of CedA 
                     bypasses the arrest |CITS: [9427399]|.  The N-terminal 
                     domain of CedA is not required for suppression; however, an
                     E32A mutation eliminates the suppressor activity |CITS: 
                     [26400504]|.
                     The solution structure of CedA has been determined by NMR 
                     spectroscopy, showing similarity of CedA to DNA binding 
                     proteins.  CedA has four anti-parallel &beta;-strands, an 
                     &alpha;-helix, and a large N-terminal tail |CITS: 
                     [15865419]|.
                     CedA has been shown through gel-shift experiments and NMR 
                     chemical shift perturbations to bind double-stranded DNA. 
                     The &beta;-sheet mediates this interaction |CITS: 
                     [15865419]|.  A consensus binding site sequence was 
                     identified by SELEX |CITS: [26400504]|.  CedA also appears 
                     to interact with |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX|; both the 
                     interaction with DNA and RNA polymerase require the 
                     presence of the N-terminal domain |CITS: [26400504]|. CedA:
                     ""<b>ce</b>ll <b>d</b>ivision"" |CITS: [9427399]|"
                     /translation="MKKPLRQQNRQIISYVPRTEPAPPEHAIKMDSFRDVWMLRGKYVA
                     FVLMGESFLRSPAFTVPESAQRWANQIRQEGEVTE"
