     gene            1808124..1810385
                     /gene="katE"
                     /locus_tag="b1732"
                     /label=katE
     CDS             1808124..1810385
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="katE"
                     /locus_tag="b1732"
                     /product="trifunctional catalase HPII / catalase / heme d
                     synthase"
                     /EC_number="1.11.1.6"
                     /label=katE
                     /note="There are two distinct catalases in <i>E. coli</i>.
                     The KatE enzyme is the monofunctional catalase HPII. A 
                     bifunctional catalase, HPI, is encoded by <i>katG</i> 
                     |CITS: [3019370]|. Aerobically grown <i>E. coli</i> produce
                     sufficient endogenous H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> to cause 
                     toxic levels of DNA damage via the Fenton reaction |CITS: 
                     [15967999]|; endogenously produced 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> is primarily scavenged by a 
                     third enzyme, |FRAME: CPLX0-245|, while catalase is the 
                     primary scavenger at high H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     concentrations |CITS: [11717276][11717277]|.
                     While most heme proteins contain |FRAME: PROTOHEME| as the 
                     prosthetic group, HPII contains a unique cofactor, |FRAME: 
                     HEME_D|. It has been suggested that heme <i>d</i> is formed
                     in a reaction catalyzed by HPII itself. Based on this 
                     model, HPII first binds protoheme, which is subsequently 
                     hydroxylated by HPII utilizing one of the first 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> substrate molecules |CITS: 
                     [Timkovich90]|. Later work has supported this theory. While
                     HPII from aerobically grown <i>E. coli</i> contains heme 
                     <i>d</i>, HPII purified from microaerobic or anaerobic 
                     cultures contains a mixture of heme <i>d</i> and |FRAME: 
                     PROTOHEME|. The protoheme of HPII obtained from such cells 
                     can be converted into heme <i>d</i> by treatment of the 
                     purified enzyme with hydrogen peroxide. The His128 residue 
                     of HPII is required for this conversion |CITS: [8399141]|.
                     A number of crystal structures of wild type and mutant 
                     forms of KatE have been reported |CITS: [2187997][7663946] 
                     [8621527][9144772][10022351][10091651][11455600][12777389][
                     15823018][21332158][22172685]|. The apparent hexameric 
                     structure of HPII was noted to be unusual |CITS: 
                     [3019370]|; however, crystal structures show a 
                     homotetrameric enzyme, each subunit containing a 
                     <i>cis</i>-heme <i>d</i> group |CITS: [7663946]|. The heme 
                     <i>d</i> group is rotated 180&deg; with the respect to the 
                     orientation found for protoheme IX in beef liver catalase 
                     |CITS: [8621527][10022351]|. The Ile274 residue influences 
                     heme orientation |CITS: [21332158]|. The proximal ligand 
                     for the heme group is Tyr415 |CITS: [1662642][10022351]|. 
                     His392 and Tyr415 are linked by a bond between the 
                     N<sub>&delta;</sub> of His and the C<sub>&beta;</sub> of 
                     Tyr. Although the bond was initially only apparent in 
                     subunits that contained a heme <i>d</i> group |CITS: 
                     [9144772]|, it was later found that His-Tyr bond formation 
                     and heme oxidation can be uncoupled |CITS: [11455600]|. The
                     catalytic activity of various site-directed mutants in 
                     active site residues N201 and H128 has been analyzed. 
                     Formation of heme <i>d</i> from protoheme may occur via a 
                     protoheme compound I |CITS: [9185614]|. The S234 and E530 
                     residues also appear to have a role in the oxidation of the
                     heme <i>b</i> to heme <i>d</i> |CITS: [22820098]|.
                     The active site heme groups are deeply buried within the 
                     enzyme, requiring substrates and products to move through 
                     channels within the enzyme. Various mutants have been 
                     constructed, and activity measurements and crystal 
                     structures of the resulting enzymes have elucidated the 
                     structure and function of those channels |CITS: [10091651] 
                     [10488114][11455600][12777389][15823018][21332158][22820098
                     ]|. Compared to some other catalases, HPII contains both N-
                     and C-terminal extensions. Both domains are required for 
                     accumulation of active enzyme |CITS: [9740372]|. 
                     Approximately 90 residues of the N-terminus of each subunit
                     are inserted into a loop on the adjacent subunit. This 
                     interwoven structure was thought to contribute to the 
                     exceptional thermal stability of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [10194300]|, but a later study showed that C-terminal 
                     truncation had a larger effect on stability |CITS: 
                     [12741830]|. The C-terminal domain is a divergent member of
                     the type I glutamine amidotransferase (GAT) superfamily; it
                     lacks the catalytic triad and thus also enzymatic activity 
                     |CITS: [11119647]|. A proteolytically truncated form of 
                     HPII that lacks 36% of its sequence has been analyzed 
                     |CITS: [15823018]|.
                     KatE production is increased in stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [3904630][3045091]| and by hyperosmotic stress |CITS: 
                     [17015655]| and is four-fold higher under aerobic compared 
                     to anaerobic growth conditions |CITS: [3045091]|. 
                     Expression is dependent on &sigma;<sup>S</sup> (KatF) 
                     |CITS: [3045091][2541439][9199247]|. Overexpression of 
                     <i>katE</i> suppresses H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     sensitivity of an <i>oxyR</i> mutant strain |CITS: 
                     [2847922]|. A <i>katE</i> mutant is more sensitive to 
                     chromate than wild type |CITS: [16621832]|. Overexpression 
                     of <i>katE</i> from an ASKA plasmid leads to a more than 
                     3-fold increase in the MIC of hydrogen peroxide |CITS: 
                     [34072091]|.
                     Persistant, sublethally injured <i>E. coli</i> induced by 
                     lactic acid stress exhibited slower resuscitation rates in 
                     <i>&Delta;katG</i> and <i>&Delta;katE</i> mutants, though 
                     not as slow as <i>&Delta;sodC</i> and <i>&Delta;rpoS</i> 
                     mutants, relative to wild-type |CITS: [31487417]|. Reviews:
                     |CITS: [7557318][8955627][22609271]|"
