     gene            1826685..1827491
                     /gene="xthA"
                     /locus_tag="b1749"
                     /label=xthA
     CDS             1826685..1827491
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="xthA"
                     /locus_tag="b1749"
                     /product="multifunctional exodeoxyribonuclease III / 
                     apurinic/apyrimidinic endonuclease / 3' -> 5' exonuclease /
                     3'-phosphomonoesterase / 3'-phosphodiesterase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.21.2; 3.1.11.2"
                     /label=xthA
                     /note="Exonuclease III (XthA) is the major
                     apurinic/apyrimidinic (AP) endonuclease under normal growth
                     conditions; Exonuclease III is a multifunctional enzyme, it
                     displays physiologically important 3' phosphomonoesterase 
                     and 3' repair diesterase activity as well as 3' &rarr; 5' 
                     exonuclease and ribonuclease H activity.
                     Exonuclease III (XthA) was first purified from <i>E. 
                     coli</i> strain B and characterised as a 'DNA 
                     phosphatase-exonuclease'; active on dsDNA, the enzyme 
                     displayed both exonuclease activity (releasing 5' 
                     mononucleotides sequentially from the 3'-hydroxyl end of 
                     DNA) and 3'-phosphatase activity (releasing inorganic 
                     phosphate from 3'-phosphoryl terminated DNA) |CITS: 
                     [14114850][14114851]|. The enzyme (purified from a K-12 
                     strain) requires Mg<sup>2+</sup> or Mn<sup>2+</sup> for 
                     optimal activity and is inhibited by Zn<sup>2+</sup>; it 
                     displays AP endonuclease activity, processive 3' &rarr; to 
                     5' exodeoxyribonuclease activity which is specific for 
                     dsDNA, 3'-phosphatase activity, and it degrades the RNA 
                     strand of a synthetic RNA-DNA hybrid (RNase H activity) 
                     |CITS: [6246343]|.
                     Exonuclease III repairs the blocked 3'-termini of hydrogen 
                     peroxide induced DNA breaks; exonuclease III removes 3' 
                     terminal phosphoglycolaldehyde esters from model DNA 
                     substrates |CITS: [2429316]|. Exonuclease III hydrolyses 
                     &gamma;-ray induced 3'-phosphate and 3'-phosphoglycolate 
                     termini |CITS: [6361028]|. Exonuclease III removes the 3' 
                     termini left by class II AP endonucleases (AP lyases). 
                     Exonuclease III has endonuclease activity on 
                     apurinic/apyrimidinic (AP) DNA; Exonuclease III is the 
                     major endonuclease for apurinic/apyrimidinic sites in DNA 
                     |CITS: [1091930][773929][333397][6282808]|. Exonuclease III
                     incises the phosphodiester backbone 5' to AP sites via 
                     hydrolysis to yield single strand breaks with 5' 
                     deoxyribose-5-phosphate (dRP) and nucleotide-3'-hydroxyl 
                     ends (see |CITS: [10946230]|).
                     Exonuclease III has endonuclease activity on urea residues 
                     in oxidized DNA |CITS: [3001698][2663068]|.
                     Exonuclease III is a globular &alpha;-&beta; protein with 
                     single divalent metal and nucleotide binding sites |CITS: 
                     [7885481]|
                     XthA recognizes AP sites based on the intercalation of 
                     Trp-212 into the DNA pocket formed at the AP site |CITS: 
                     [16540594]|.
                     Mutants lacking Exonuclease III are extremely sensitive to 
                     hydrogen peroxide |CITS: [6337115]|
                     <i>xth</i>: e<U>x</U>onuclease III (<U>th</U>ree) |CITS: 
                     [4341749][4570597]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [7979257][26442508]|"
