     gene            1833724..1835031
                     /gene="ynjE"
                     /locus_tag="b1757"
                     /label=ynjE
     CDS             1833724..1835031
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ynjE"
                     /locus_tag="b1757"
                     /product="bifunctional rhodanese-like sulfurtransferase / 
                     molybdopterin synthase sulfurtransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.8.1.11"
                     /label=ynjE
                     /note="YnjE is a monomeric sulfurtransferase containing
                     three rhodanese-like domains. <i>In vitro</i>, the 
                     sulfurtransferase |FRAME: G7325 IscS| is able to transfer 
                     sulfur to the active site cysteine of YnjE; both 
                     thiosulfate and 3-mercaptopyruvate are poor sulfate donors.
                     The physiological substrate of YnjE was unknown for a while
                     |CITS: [19798741]|. However, a later study showed a 
                     potential role of the enzyme in molybdopterin biosynthesis 
                     |CITS: [21856748]|.
                     Similarly to the eukaryotic rhodanese MOCS3, YnjE was shown
                     to enhance the rate of |FRAME: PRECURSOR-Z| conversion to 
                     |FRAME: CPD-4| <i>in vitro</i>, yet it was not an enhancer 
                     of the cysteine desulfurase activity of |FRAME: G7325 
                     IscS|. The protein was also shown to interact with |FRAME: 
                     EG10154 MoeB| <i>in vivo</i> under anaerobic conditions, 
                     supporting a role in transferring a sulfur from IscS to the
                     |FRAME: Carboxyadenylated-MPT-synthases 
                     ""carboxy-adenylated MoaD""|, forming the thiocarboxylated 
                     form that is required for the |FRAME: CPLX0-2502| activity 
                     |CITS: [21856748]|.
                     Crystal structures of YnjE in its sulfur-free and 
                     persulfurated state (2.4 &Aring; resolution) have been 
                     solved. The active site is located in a cleft at the 
                     interface between the central and C-terminal rhodanese 
                     domains. Site-directed mutagenesis of the active site loop 
                     showed that it is not the only determinant for substrate 
                     specificity |CITS: [19798741]|.
                     Transcription of <i>ynjE</i> is increased under anaerobic 
                     conditions |CITS: [23281480]|."
                     /translation="MKRVSQMTALAMALGLACASSWAAELAKPLTLDQLQQQNGKAIDT
