     gene            complement(1839256..1841217)
                     /gene="topB"
                     /locus_tag="b1763"
                     /label=topB
     CDS             complement(1839256..1841217)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="topB"
                     /locus_tag="b1763"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA topoisomerase III /
                     topoisomerase"
                     /EC_number="5.6.2.1"
                     /label=topB
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> possess multiple DNA topoisomerases
                     which coordinate, as well as compete, in order to maintain 
                     chromosomes in the appropriate topological state 
                     commensurate with their need for replication and 
                     transcription. They comprise the type IA enzymes DNA 
                     topoisomerase I and DNA topoisomerase III, which are 
                     encoded by the |FRAME: EG11013| and |FRAME: EG11014| genes,
                     respectively, and the type II enzymes |FRAME: CPLX0-2424| 
                     and |FRAME: CPLX0-2425|.
                     Type I topoisomerases function by causing single-strand DNA
                     breaks, while type II topoisomerases cause double-strand 
                     DNA breaks. Topoisomerase III transiently cleaves 
                     single-stranded DNA via formation of a covalent 
                     5'-phosphotyrosine intermediate. Topo III is associated 
                     with the replication fork and plays a role in chromosome 
                     decatenation |CITS: [30617245]|.
                     Topo III can decatenate and relax negative supercoils in 
                     DNA |CITS: [6326814][2843517]|, supporting decatenation 
                     during <i>oriC</i> DNA replication |CITS: 
                     [8294462][7798272]|. Genetic/functional interactions also 
                     suggest a role for Topo III in chromosome segregation 
                     |CITS: [23066834][23396913][25102178][30617245]|. Topo III 
                     is also able to relax transcription-induced negative 
                     supercoiling and inhibits R-loop formation |CITS: 
                     [10632877]|, preventing unregulated replication originating
                     in the Ter region of the chromosome |CITS: [30222737]|.
                     The DNA binding domain was located in the carboxy terminus 
                     of Topo III |CITS: [7510701][8621552]|; a specific DNA 
                     binding site was identified, and a model for DNA cleavage 
                     was proposed |CITS: [7559540]|. A Y328F point mutant in the
                     predicted active site tyrosine is able to bind DNA, but has
                     no strand cleavage activity |CITS: [7510701][7559540]|. A 
                     17 amino acid loop that is present in Topo III, but not 
                     Topo I, is required for Topo III's decatenation activity 
                     |CITS: [10692165]|. A proposed reaction model for 
                     decatenation was tested by generating trapped DNA-enzyme 
                     intermediates |CITS: [11239459]|. The catalytic cycle was 
                     investigated using computational simulation methods |CITS: 
                     [18186484]|. In a single-molecule DNA unlinking assay, Topo
                     III's rate of unlinking was linearly related to the DNA 
                     strand collision frequency |CITS: [23460205]|; the 
                     decatenase activity of Topo III is higher than that of Topo
                     I and shows dependence on the DNA strand crossover angle 
                     |CITS: [25232096]|.
                     Crystal structures of Topo III have been solved |CITS: 
                     [10574789][11429611]|. A 2.05 &Aring; crystal structure of 
                     inactive Topo III in complex with an 8-base single-stranded
                     oligonucleotide provides insight into the roles of 
                     conserved active site residues |CITS: [11429611]|. A series
                     of crystal structures of catalytically active DNA-bound 
                     Topo III at three different pHs allowed the examination of 
                     different conformational states of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [17331537]|.
                     The |FRAME: EG10833-MONOMER RecQ| helicase together with 
                     SSB can stimulate Topo III, enhancing supercoiling and 
                     catenation of covalently closed DNA |CITS: 
                     [10360177][12909639]|. Topo III together with RecQ and SSB 
                     is able to resolve converging replication forks |CITS: 
                     [18570879]|. DnaX complex stimulates the ability of Topo 
                     III to unlink bothcatenated and precatenated DNA rings 
                     |CITS: [30617245]|. Topo III is also able to bind and act 
                     on RNA |CITS: [1383203][8790355]|.
                     Mutations in <i>topB</i> were first isolated as frameshift 
                     mutator alleles |CITS: [1092652][1102526]|. Cells lacking 
                     topoisomerase III show an increased frequency of 
                     spontaneous deletions |CITS: [2016043][9164483]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>topB</i> mutant shows delayed and irregular 
                     nucleoid separation |CITS: [30617245]|. <i>topB</i> can act
                     as a multicopy supressor of a null mutation in <i>topA</i> 
                     |CITS: [10632877][25102178]|. A <i>topA topB</i> double 
                     mutant filaments extensively; this phenotype is suppressed 
                     by deletion of <i>recA</i> |CITS: [11493711]|. <i>topB</i> 
                     is also a multicopy supressor of a temperature-sensitive 
                     <i>parC</i> and <i>parE</i> alleles |CITS: [12509418]|. 
                     Contrary to the results reported by |CITS: [16164551]|, 
                     &Delta;<i>topB parE</i><sup>ts</sup> and &Delta;<i>topB 
                     parC</i><sup>ts</sup> double mutants are synthetically 
                     sick; <i>recQ</i> or <i>recA</i> null mutations did not 
                     rescue the synthetic phenotype |CITS: [23066834]|. In the 
                     absence of both type IA topoisomerases unregulated 
                     replication involving RNA polymerase backtracking occurs 
                     from R-loops, leading to DNA amplification in the 
                     chromosome terminus region because the replication forks 
                     are blocked at termination barriers in addition to 
                     overproduction of topo IV due to amplifying chromosomal 
                     region with <i>parC</i> and <i>parE</i> genes |CITS: 
                     [30222737]|. Lack of decatenation activity by Topo III 
                     during replication elongation and termination results in 
                     over-replication at the terminus region |CITS: [37141391]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>topB</i> mutant is sensitive to novobiocin, an 
                     antibiotic that targets type II topoisomerases; the 
                     increased sensitivity is mediated by Topo IV |CITS: 
                     [23066834]|.
                     MutR: |CITS: [1092652][2016043][1321123]|
                     TopB: ""<b>top</b>oisomerase"" |CITS: [2553698][1321123]| 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8397515][7783632][9748482][10421567][1142 
                     7885][12007989][17141394][23514156][28510215][32120891][39 
                     520813]|"
