                     /locus_tag="b1768"
                     /product="bifunctional nicotinamidase / pyrazinamidase"
                     /EC_number="3.5.1.19; 3.5.1.4"
                     /label=pncA
                     /note="<i>pncA</i> encodes a nicotinamidase (a member of
                     the isochorismatase superfamily) that is active in the 
                     cyclical salvage pathway for production of NAD<sup>+</sup> 
                     from nicotinamide |CITS: [4399474]| (see pathway |FRAME: 
                     PYRIDNUCSAL-PWY|).  Homologs of PncA are found in other 
                     bacteria, archaea, and some eukaryotes, but not in 
                     vertebrates (reviewed in |CITS: [19721089]|).  Early work 
                     established this salvage pathway in <i>E. coli</i> and a 
                     role for nicotinamidase |CITS: 
                     [4292720][4313131][4312778][19420]|.  Exogenous 
                     nicotinamide appears to be passively taken up by <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [4146188]|.
                     The <i>pncA</i>-encoded nicotinamidase also has 
                     pyrazinamidase activity.  |FRAME: PYRAZINAMIDE 
                     Pyrazinamide| is an antibiotic with a structure similar to 
                     nicotinamide |CITS: [8726014]|; it is an antituberculosis 
                     prodrug that is hydrolytically converted to its active 
                     form, pyrazine-2-carboxylate (pyrazinoic acid), by the 
                     pyrazinamidase activity of PncA.  However, this drug is not
                     a relevant antibiotic for <i>E. coli</i>, which is 
                     naturally resistant |CITS: [8726014][12076823][17373362]|. 
                     The pyrazinamidase activity was shown to be dependent on 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup>; metal-depleted enzyme can be reactivated 
                     <i>in vitro</i> by addition of ZnuA |CITS: [31636108]|.
                     A comparison of the <i>pncA</i> sequence with other members
                     of the isochrismatase superfamily identified a potential 
                     catalytic triad (Asp10-Lys111-Cys156). It also identified 
                     two residues (Gln12 and Asp52) putatively involved in 
                     stabilizing the substrate. The sequence comparison also 
                     identified six amino acid residues (GENPLV) not found in 
                     the other enzymes that acts as a lid that limits substrate 
                     entrance into the tunnel leading to the active site. 
                     Deletion of these six amino acids resulted in a protein 
                     with a wider substrate range that was able to hydrolyze the
                     &gamma;-lactam |FRAME: CPD-26603| |CITS: [35171012]|.
                     Nicotinamidase is constitutively expressed |CITS: 
                     [4399474][4146188]|.  Overexpression of <i>pncA</i> enables
                     <i>E. coli</i> to utilize nicotinamide as a sole source of 
                     nitrogen |CITS: [8726014]|.  A <i>pncA</i><sup>-</sup> 
                     mutant is unable to recycle nicotinamide, which is excreted
                     into the medium |CITS: [4146189]| and grows more slowly 
                     than wild type |CITS: [29253849]|.
                     PncA: ""<b>p</b>yridine <b>n</b>ucleotide <b>c</b>ycle"" 
                     |CITS: [4315893]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [26443758][19721089]|"
