     gene            complement(1849248..1850228)
                     /gene="ydjG"
                     /locus_tag="b1771"
                     /label=ydjG
     CDS             complement(1849248..1850228)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ydjG"
                     /locus_tag="b1771"
                     /product="bifunctional NADH-dependent methylglyoxal
                     reductase / methylglyoxal reductase"
                     /label=ydjG
                     /note="The YdjG protein belongs to subfamily 11 of the
                     aldo-keto reductase superfamily.  Members of this family 
                     generally use NADPH as the cosubstrate, although some are 
                     able to use both NADH and NADPH.  YdjG is the first member 
                     of this family that is only able to use NADH.   The crystal
                     structure of the dual-specificity xylose reductase from 
                     <i>Candida tenuis</i> identified a conserved glutamic acid 
                     residue which appears to allow binding of both 
                     NAD<sup>+</sup> and NADP<sup>+</sup>.  Mutagenesis of the 
                     analogous residue in YdjG, D232A or D232E, converts YdjG 
                     into a dual-specificity enzyme |CITS: [16813561]|.
                     The purified enzyme was found to possess a relatively high 
                     activity with methylglyoxal as the substrate 
                     (K<sub>cat</sub> = 15.7 s<sup>-1</sup>) |CITS: [16813561]|.
                      The physiological significance of this activity has not 
                     been investigated.
                     YdjG is important for survival under competitive planktonic
                     growth conditions |CITS: [16849790]|.
                     YdjG protein levels appear to be higher in gnotobiotic mice
                     colonized with <i>E. coli</i> than in comparable growth 
                     medium; in an assay for intestinal colonization of 
                     gnotobiotic mice, a <i>ydjLKJIHG</i> deletion mutant 
                     reached a 1.2-log-lower cecal concentration than the wild 
                     type at certain time points during the experiment |CITS: 
                     [20562286]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>ydjG</i> was utilized to increase 
                     production of 1,2-propanediol in a metabolically engineered
                     strain |CITS: [22074179]|."
