     gene            complement(1856273..1856686)
                     /gene="msrB"
                     /locus_tag="b1778"
                     /label=msrB
     CDS             complement(1856273..1856686)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="msrB"
                     /locus_tag="b1778"
                     /product="trifunctional methionine sulfoxide reductase B / 
                     protein-methionine-S-oxide reductase / methionine sulfoxide
                     reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.8.4.12; 1.8.4.14"
                     /label=msrB
                     /note="MsrB is one of several methionine sulfoxide
                     reductases in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [11677230][12504094][15601707]|.  Methionine residues in 
                     proteins can be damaged by oxidation and can be repaired by
                     methionine sulfoxide reductases |CITS: [15057280]|. 
                     Modification of chemically oxidized N-terminal methionines 
                     by formylation or acetylation significantly increases the 
                     catalytic efficiency of reduction by MsrB and |FRAME: 
                     EG11433-MONOMER MsrA| in redox kinetic studies <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [39499632]|. MsrA and MsrB are also 
                     important for keeping |FRAME: EG10823-MONOMER RecA| active 
                     under oxidative stress conditions |CITS: [33605213]|. 
                     Conversely, the transcription factor |FRAME: G7924 HypT| is
                     activated by methionine oxidation and inactivated by 
                     MsrA/MsrB activity |CITS: [23690622]|.
                     The CXXC motifs in MsrB are essential for metal binding and
                     catalytic activity |CITS: [16251365]|.
                     Some organisms produce a methionine sulfoxide reductase 
                     composed of two domains with similarity to MsrA and MsrB, 
                     respectively, whereas <i>E. coli</i> produces these two 
                     distinct polypeptides. MsrB exhibits 1000-fold lower 
                     catalytic efficiency than MsrA toward free methionine 
                     sulfoxide |CITS: [11677230]|. MsrA and MsrB exhibit similar
                     activity (quantitatively) toward an oxidized peptide 
                     substrate, though they appear to exhibit some differences 
                     in specificity for substrate sites within the peptide 
                     |CITS: [11677230][15057280]|. The <i>E. coli</i> MsrA and 
                     MsrB polypeptides do not interact with each other <i>in 
                     vitro</i> or in a yeast two-hybrid test |CITS: [11677230]|.
                     Additional methionine sulfoxide reductase activities have 
                     been observed in an <i>msrA msrB</i> double mutant, 
                     including activities toward both the met-<i>R</i>-(o) and 
                     met-<i>S</i>-(o) enantiomers of free (non-peptide) 
                     methionine sulfoxide (fRMsr and fSMsr activities) |CITS: 
                     [12504094]|, a soluble NADPH-dependent activity toward the 
                     met-<i>S</i>-(o) enantiomer of peptide-linked methionine 
                     sulfoxide (MsrA1 activity) |CITS: [12504094][12604343]|, 
                     and a membrane-associated NADPH-dependent activity toward 
                     both met-<i>S</i>-(o) and met-<i>R</i>-(o) enantiomers of 
                     peptide-linked and free methionine sulfoxide |CITS: 
                     [12504094][12604343]|.
                     A methionine auxotroph can utilize methionine sulfoxide for
                     growth |CITS: [37234][3533930]|.
                     An <i>msrB</i> mutant shows increased cadmium sensitivity 
                     compared to wild type, but does not exhibit an apparent 
                     growth defect on rich medium, consistent with a role for 
                     MsrB in resistance to oxidative stress |CITS: [11677230]|. 
                     Expression of MsrB is repressed at the translational level 
                     by the iron-responsive small RNA RyhB |CITS: [23671689]|. 
                     MsrB: ""<b>m</b>ethionine <b>s</b>ulfoxide <b>r</b>eductase
                     <b>B</b>"" |CITS: [11677230]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [15680228][15680230][18302927][25963551]|"
