     gene            complement(1869012..1869833)
                     /gene="nimR"
                     /locus_tag="b1790"
                     /label=nimR
     CDS             complement(1869012..1869833)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nimR"
                     /locus_tag="b1790"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional repressor NimR"
                     /label=nimR
                     /note="NimR, formerly called YeaM, belongs to the
                     AraC/XylS superfamily |CITS:[11282467]|. It confers 
                     resistance to 2-nitroimidazole, an antibacterial and 
                     antifungal agent |CITS:[25790494]|.
                     NimR plays a regulatory role in divergent transcription of 
                     the <i>nimT</i> and <i>nimR</i> genes |CITS:[25790494]|. 
                     Genome-wide NimR binding sites were determined <i> in 
                     vivo</i> by chromatin immunoprecipitation method combined 
                     with lambda exonuclease digestion (ChIP-exo) in glucose M9 
                     minimal medium |CITS: [34428301]|.
                     A negative correlation between cellular growth and the copy
                     number of the proteins NimR has been reported |CITS: 
                     [35694815]|.
                     NimR: <b>r</b>egulator of <b>nim</b>T |CITS:[25790494]|. 
                     The NimR binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
