     gene            complement(1875143..1876066)
                     /gene="dmlR"
                     /locus_tag="b1799"
                     /label=dmlR
     CDS             complement(1875143..1876066)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dmlR"
                     /locus_tag="b1799"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional regulator DmlR"
                     /label=dmlR
                     /note="DmlR (<b>D</b>-<b>m</b>a<b>l</b>ate degradation 
                     <b>r</b>egulator), formerly known as YeaT |CITS: 
                     [20233924]|, is required for growth on D-malate as the sole
                     carbon source under high-throughput growth conditions with 
                     limited aeration |CITS: [17088549]|. DmlR is a LysR-type 
                     transcriptional regulator and has 49% sequence identity and
                     67% similarity with TtdR |CITS: [20233924]|. Induction of 
                     <i>dmlA</i> by DmlR requires the presence of D-malate or L-
                     or meso-tartate, but only D-malate supports aerobic growth 
                     |CITS: [17088549][20233924]|. The expression of <i>dmlR</i>
                     is subject to negative autoregulation independently of 
                     DcuS-DcuS and TtdR |CITS: [20233924]|.
                     DmlR was transcriptionally upregulated in iron excess over 
                     iron limitation at 63% dissolved oxygen as determined by 
                     RNA-seq |CITS:[ 35777694]|.
                     The DmlR binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
