     gene            complement(1881121..1882248)
                     /gene="rnd"
                     /locus_tag="b1804"
                     /label=rnd
     CDS             complement(1881121..1882248)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rnd"
                     /locus_tag="b1804"
                     /product="bifunctional RNase D / RNase D exonuclease"
                     /EC_number="3.1.13.5"
                     /label=rnd
                     /note="Ribonuclease D (RNase D) is an exonuclease involved
                     in the 3' ribonucleolytic processing of precursor tRNA. 
                     Though RNase D appears to be a minor player in this task 
                     and is not required for viability or proper tRNA 
                     processing, it can support such processing in the absence 
                     of other exonucleases (RNase II, RNase BN, RNase T and 
                     RNase PH) |CITS: [8422961][1400219][6337139][6207170]|. 
                     <i>In vitro</i>, RNase D cleaves tRNA nonprocessively 
                     (randomly) from the 3' end to yield 5""-mononucleotides and
                     active tRNA. Cleavage of tRNA slows at the CCA nucleotide 
                     sequence, allowing aminoacylation of the tRNA that prevents
                     additional cleavage |CITS: [6153805]|. RNase D activity is 
                     very dependent on the structure of the 3' end of the target
                     tRNA, with cleavage of altered tRNA proceeding much faster 
                     than cleavage of wild type, and no cleavage of tRNA bearing
                     a 3' phosphate |CITS: [342522][364419][6263886]|. When 
                     overexpressed <i>in vivo</i>, RNase D cleaves the 3' end of
                     tRNA, eventually cleaving past the CCA sequence to yield 
                     damaged tRNA that cannot be fixed by tRNA 
                     nucleotidyltransferase |CITS: [3056931]|. In the case of 
                     proline tRNAs, the endoribonuclease RNase E, rather than 
                     RNase D and the other 3' to 5' exoribonucleases, was shown 
                     to have a role in maturation of the <i>proK</i>, 
                     <i>proL</i> and <i>proM</i> tRNA 3' ends |CITS: 
                     [27288443]|. See |FRAME: PWY0-1628| and |FRAME: PWY0-1625| 
                     as well as individual tRNA processing pathways.
                     The purified enzyme has an apparent molecular mass of 40 
                     kDa by SDS-PAGE and gel filtration chromatography, 
                     suggesting a monomeric structure |CITS: [6263885]|. Assay 
                     methods for RNase D have been described |CITS: [2166215]|. 
                     Bioinformatics analysis shows that RNase D contains a 
                     domain that is similar to the proofreading 3' to 5' 
                     exodeoxyribonuclease domain of DNA polymerases |CITS: 
                     [9241229]|. The crystal structure of <i>E. coli</i> RNase D
                     has been determined at 1.6 &Aring; resolution. It has a 
                     ring shaped architecture formed by the catalytic domain 
                     which contains conserved DEDD residues, and two 
                     &alpha;-helical domains. The ring shape may have a role in 
                     substrate specificity |CITS: [16004870]|.
                     RNase D may interact with a T4 bacteriophage encoded 
                     protein during infection |CITS: [6260104]|.
                     Translation of RNase D mRNA depends on a stem-loop 
                     structure followed by eight uridines upstream of the 
                     Shine-Delgarno sequence |CITS: [2681190][1372983]|. 
                     Overexpression of RNase D leads to slow growth |CITS: 
                     [2828310]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [29676246][35787606]|"
