     gene            complement(1884829..1885524)
                     /gene="tsaB"
                     /locus_tag="b1807"
                     /label=tsaB
     CDS             complement(1884829..1885524)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tsaB"
                     /locus_tag="b1807"
                     /product="multifunctional N(6)-L-threonylcarbamoyladenine
                     synthase, TsaB subunit / metallopeptidase / 
                     N(6)-L-threonylcarbamoyladenine synthase, TsaB-TsaD dimer 
                     subunit / N(6)-L-threonylcarbamoyladenine synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.234"
                     /label=tsaB
                     /note="TsaB is involved in the biosynthesis of the 
                     threonylcarbamoyladenosine (t<sup>6</sup>A) residue at 
                     position 37 of ANN-decoding tRNAs.  A mixture of purified 
                     |FRAME: G7698-MONOMER TsaC|, |FRAME: EG11171-MONOMER TsaD|,
                     TsaB, and |FRAME: EG11757-MONOMER TsaE| proteins catalyzes 
                     formation of t<sup>6</sup>A in the presence of a tRNA 
                     substrate, ATP, threonine and bicarbonate <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [22378793][25578970]|.
                     The three essential proteins TsaE, TsaB and TsaD form an 
                     interaction network.  TsaB interacts with itself; it can 
                     interact with both TsaE and TsaD directly, and the 
                     interactions appear to be mutually exclusive |CITS: 
                     [19376873]|.  The specific interaction between TsaD and 
                     TsaB is required for the essential function of these 
                     proteins |CITS: [21285948]|.  TsaB and TsaD form a 
                     heterodimer; crystal structures of this protein complex 
                     have been solved.  The structure shows ADP bound at the 
                     dimer interface.  Site-directed mutants in the TsaB-TsaD 
                     interface were generated and biochemically characterized. 
                     The C-terminal tail of TsaB is not required for formation 
                     of the ternary |FRAME: CPLX0-8182| complex |CITS: 
                     [25578970]|.  A preliminary analysis of the crystal 
                     structure of TsaB alone has been published |CITS: 
                     [16511176]|; the structure was further analyzed and 
                     compared to the structure of the <i>Vibrio 
                     parahaemolyticus</i> protein |CITS: [21858042]|.  This 
                     analysis suggests two different homodimer architectures 
                     corresponding to a free and a nucleotide-bound form |CITS: 
                     [21858042]|.
                     TsaB appears to act a protease that specifically degrades 
                     the TsaD protein |CITS: [19376873]|; this activity may not 
                     be reproducible.
                     TsaB is essential for growth in complex media under aerobic
                     conditions |CITS: [16738554][19376873]|.  The protein is 
                     broadly conserved in bacteria |CITS: [19376873]|.  
                     Depletion of TsaB results in the formation of highly 
                     condensed nucleoids |CITS: [19376873]|.  As for <i>tsaE</i>
                     and <i>tsaD</i>, |FRAME: G6864 <i>rstA</i>| was identified 
                     as a multicopy suppressor of the lethal phenotype of a 
                     strain with a conditional <i>tsaB</i> mutation |CITS: 
                     [19376873]|.  Other genetic interactions were found |CITS: 
                     [21873492]|.
                     TsaB: ""<b>t</b>hreonylcarbamoyl-6(<b>s</b>ix)-<b>a</b>den 
                     osine"" |CITS: [22378793]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [19465656][22905870]|
                     "
