     gene            1889062..1890423
                     /gene="pabB"
                     /locus_tag="b1812"
                     /label=pabB
     CDS             1889062..1890423
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pabB"
                     /locus_tag="b1812"
                     /product="bifunctional aminodeoxychorismate synthase
                     subunit 1 / 4-amino-4-deoxychorismate synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.6.1.85"
                     /label=pabB
                     /note="PabB (component 1) catalyzes the formation of 
                     4-amino-4-deoxychorismate (ADC) from chorismate and ammonia
                     generated by PabA within the amiodeoxychorismate synthase 
                     complex |CITS: [3311153][2251281][7592344]|. Kinetic 
                     studies support an ordered bi-bi catalytic mechanism with 
                     chorismate binding first |CITS: [7592344]|. In the absence 
                     of PabA, PabB can utilize ammonia (at a reduced rate) to 
                     aminate chorismate and produce 4-amino-4-deoxychorismate 
                     |CITS: [3311153][2251281]|.  The reaction catalyzed by PabB
                     <i>in vitro</i> in the presence of ammonia is reversible in
                     the absence of PabC |CITS: [2251281]|.
                     Crystal structures of PabB have been determined at 2.0 
                     &Aring; resolution; a potential active site has been 
                     identified.  A deeply embedded tryptophan molecule may play
                     a structural role |CITS: [11841211]|.  Random and 
                     site-directed mutagenesis has identified residues important
                     for the interaction with PabA and for catalytic activity 
                     |CITS: [8679677]|.  Site-directed mutagenesis supports the 
                     function of K274 as the active-site nucleophile |CITS: 
                     [14982443]|, forming a covalent intermediate |CITS: 
                     [15770625][16605270]|.  The E258 residue acts as a general 
                     acid ctalyst for loss of the C4 OH group of chorismate and 
                     is required for formation of the covalent intermediate 
                     |CITS: [16605270]|.
                     A homology model for the PabA-PabB complex was constructed.
                      Alanine scanning mutagenesis revealed residues within an 
                     interdomain hydrogen bonding network that are important for
                     PabA-PabB complex formation, residues involved in 
                     allosteric signaling between the subunits, as well as 
                     residues involved in ammonia channeling from the PabA- to 
                     the PabB active site |CITS: [31121180]|.  The conserved 
                     PabB D308 residue appears to be essential for both complex 
                     formation and allosteric signaling |CITS: 
                     [31121180][31117390]|.
                     PabB shows sequence |CITS: [6330050]| and structural |CITS:
                     [11841211]| similarity TrpE (anthranilate synthase), 
                     suggesting common ancestry.  Mutations in PabB that enable 
                     the additional ability to eliminate pyruvate, i.e. catalyze
                     the anthranilate synthase reaction, were isolated, tested 
                     and confirmed |CITS: [25710100]|.
                     A <i>pabB</i> mutant requires supplementation with 
                     <i>p</i>-aminobenzoate for growth |CITS: [5337773]|.
                     PabB: ""<b><i>p</i>-a</b>mino<b>b</b>enzoate"" |CITS: 
                     [5337773]|"
