     gene            complement(1901483..1901692)
                     /gene="cspC"
                     /locus_tag="b1823"
                     /label=cspC
     CDS             complement(1901483..1901692)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cspC"
                     /locus_tag="b1823"
                     /product="transcription antiterminator and regulator of
                     mRNA stability CspC"
                     /label=cspC
                     /note="CspC belongs to the cold shock family of proteins
                     and functions as a transcription antiterminator at 
                     &rho;-independent terminators both <i>in vitro</i> and 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [10884409]|. CspC appears to be 
                     involved in downregulating the heat shock response |CITS: 
                     [19624711]|.
                     CspC interacts with both RNA and single-stranded DNA; the 
                     consensus sequence AGGGAGGGA has been determined using an 
                     <i>in vitro</i> selection approach |CITS: [10476034]|. CspE
                     appears to interact specifically with U-rich mRNAs |CITS: 
                     [26225847]|. Proteomic and microarray analysis identified 
                     genes whose expression is responsive to CspC levels, 
                     including <i>rpoS</i> |CITS: [11157932][16547039]|. The 
                     stabilizing effect of CspC on the <i>rpoS</i> mRNA requires
                     the 5' UTR of <i>rpoS</i> |CITS: [20633642]|.
                     Expression of <i>cspC</i> is not cold-shock inducible 
                     |CITS: [8022261][24952137]|; <i>cspC</i> is expressed 
                     constitutively during growth at 37&deg;C |CITS: 
                     [11157932]|. Expression of <i>cspC</i> and eight other 
                     <i>csp</i> genes during growth and under different media 
                     conditions has been measured |CITS: [24952137]|. CspC 
                     protein levels decrease upon heat shock |CITS: [19624711]| 
                     due to proteolysis possibly promoted by interacting with 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3934 GroES-GroEL| |CITS: [24148697]|. CspC 
                     protein levels increase during the transition to stationary
                     phase |CITS: [23760898]|. The expression of the 
                     <i>yobF-cspC</i> operon is predicted to be regulated 
                     posttranscriptionally by the small regulatory RNAs CyaR, 
                     OmrA, OmrB, and OxyS; regulation by CyaR was shown 
                     experimentally |CITS: [23980183]|. Diverse expression 
                     responses of the cold shock proteins, CspA-I, were found 
                     under different stress conditions, specifically cold, heat,
                     rich vs minimal media and oxidative stress caused by 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. CspC expression is down 
                     initially after exposure to H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     stress and throughout cold-shock conditions in addition to 
                     heat stress, but up throughout the growth cycle for both 
                     minimal and rich media |CITS: [40312551]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>cspC</i> leads to higher levels and 
                     greater stability of <i>rpoS</i> mRNA |CITS: [11157932]| 
                     and to increased mRNA levels for 11 heat shock genes |CITS:
                     [19624711]|. CspC overexpression during heat shock 
                     (42&deg;C) leads to a lower growth rate |CITS: [19624711]|.
                     <i>cspC</i> is a multicopy suppressor of the 
                     temperature-sensitive colony formation phenotype of the 
                     <i>mukB106</i> mutation |CITS: [7984109]|. Overexpression 
                     of CspC suppresses the acid stress survival defect of an 
                     <i>hfq</i> deletion mutant |CITS: [20633642]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>cspC</i>&Delta;<i>cspE</i> double mutant is 
                     more sensitive to acid and oxidative stress in stationary 
                     phase than wild type |CITS: [23760898]|. Deletion of 
                     <i>cspC</i> increases benzoate tolerance |CITS: 
                     [31175192]|.
                     Laboratory strains of <i>E. coli</i> accumulate 
                     loss-of-function mutations in <i>cspC</i>, apparently 
                     providing a competitive advantage for growth in broth 
                     culture at 37&deg;C |CITS: [16980479]|. <i>cspC</i> belongs
                     to a network of genes which facilitate stress-induced 
                     mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 |CITS: 
                     [23224554]|.
                     An <i>in-silico</i> comparative study of evolution, 
                     structure and function was carried out for the 9 CspA 
                     family of proteins in <i>E. coli K-12</i>. Despite high 
                     homology (>70% amino acid sequence similarity), it was 
                     determined that 4 paralogous pairs could be identified and 
                     served as the focus for the comparative analyses: 
                     CspA/CspB, CspC/CspE, CspG/CspI and CspF/CspH |CITS: 
                     [37333716]|. A follow-up study conducted mutational 
                     analysis and molecular modeling to determine selection 
                     pressure on the paralogous pairs |CITS: [39779584]|.
                     MsmB: ""<b>m</b>ulticopy <b>s</b>uppressor of 
                     <i><b>m</b>ukB106</i>"" |CITS: [7984109]|<br>CspC: 
                     ""<b>c</b>old <b>s</b>hock <b>p</b>rotein <b>C</b>"" 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8022259][17437059][34260797]|"
                     /translation="MAKIKGQVKWFNESKGFGFITPADGSKDVFVHFSAIQGNGFKTLA
                     EGQNVEFEIQDGQKGPAAVNVTAI"
