     gene            complement(1907025..1909073)
                     /gene="prc"
                     /locus_tag="b1830"
                     /label=prc
     CDS             complement(1907025..1909073)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="prc"
                     /locus_tag="b1830"
                     /product="tail-specific protease"
                     /EC_number="3.4.21.102"
                     /label=prc
                     /note="Early studies in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 identified
                     various soluble serine proteases, including protease Re 
                     |CITS: [7043205][7019728][7037737][2892828]|, later 
                     suggested to be identical with the periplasmic protease 
                     known as tail-specific protease (Tsp) encoded by <i>prc</i>
                     (see |CITS: [1856173][8520476][8982462]|).
                     Prc cleaves the C-terminal 11 residue peptide of |FRAME: 
                     EG10341-MONOMER ""penicillin binding protein 3""| to 
                     generate the mature protein |CITS: 
                     [1856173][2681145][2681146][31387902]|. Prc binds to and 
                     degrades proteins that have been tagged with the quality 
                     control peptide sequence coded for by |FRAME: EG30100 
                     ""<i>ssrA</i>""|, indicative of a role in removing 
                     improperly translated proteins |CITS: [12177052][8584937]|.
                     Prc together with its adaptor protein |FRAME: CPLX0-8198 
                     ""NlpI""|, forms a proteolytic system that regulates 
                     peptidoglycan synthesis by degrading |FRAME: G7147-MONOMER 
                     ""MepS""|, a murein endopeptidase that participates in 
                     expansion of the peptidoglycan sacculus during bacterial 
                     growth and morphogenesis |CITS: [26283368]| (and further 
                     |CITS: [35418955][37276399][38003545][38937433]|). NlpI-Prc
                     degrades |FRAME: EG10246-MONOMER| |CITS: [38422114]|. Prc 
                     binds NlpI with high affinity |CITS: [2913848 8]|. Prc does
                     not cleave soluble, recombinant FtsI in the presence of 
                     NlpI |CITS: [31387902]|. NlpI-Prc mediated proteolysis of 
                     peptidoglycan-cleaving enzymes is negatively regulated by 
                     the periplasmic protein |FRAME: EG12254-MONOMER BipP| in 
                     response to reduced DD-endopeptidase activity |CITS: 
                     [39841140]|.
                     Prc degrades proteins with non-polar C-termini <i>in 
                     vitro</i>; Prc cleaves substrates that are not stably 
                     folded |CITS:[1729701][8159175][8520476]|. C-terminal amino
                     acid sequence determinants influencing Prc-mediated 
                     cleavage have been studied |CITS: [8576225]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [22267294]|. Prc contains a PDZ domain that binds to
                     nonpolar residues, which may help set its substrate 
                     specificity for proteins with nonpolar carboxy-terminal 
                     tags |CITS: [10715137]|. The PDZ domain is required for 
                     binding to the SsrA degradation tag |CITS: [12177052]|. The
                     PDZ domain is required for Prc-NlpI mediated degradation of
                     MepS |CITS: [29138488]| and for Prc mediated processing of 
                     FtsI |CITS: [31387902]|. In the absence of substrate, Prc 
                     adopts an inactive, resting conformation; Prc activation 
                     results from substrate binding to the PDZ domain which 
                     undergoes extensive repositioning to facilitate a cleavage 
                     reaction |CITS: [31387902]|.
                     Prc is a 'non-classical' serine protease which uses a 
                     Ser-Lys catalytic dyad mechanism |CITS: [7499412]|.
                     Prc is implicated in potentiating the rate of uptake of 
                     long chain fatty acids; <i>prc</i> null mutants 
                     (<i>prc-phoA</i>) have a reduced growth rate and long lag 
                     period when grown in oleate minimal medium; lactate fails 
                     to stimulate oleic acid transport in &Delta;<i>fadR 
                     prc-phoA</i> fusion strains |CITS: [7961418]|. A <i>prc</i>
                     insertion mutation (<i>prc</i>::Tn5) is associated with 
                     increased susceptibility to a number of antibiotics |CITS: 
                     [1447154]|.
                     <i>prc</i> is essential under high temperature growth in 
                     hypotonic media |CITS: [1856173]|; multicopy expression of 
                     |FRAME: G7682 <i>degQ</i>|-|FRAME: EG11652 <i>degS</i>| or 
                     |FRAME: EG10230 <i>dksA</i>| suppresses this conditionally 
                     lethal phenotype |CITS: [8576052]|. When grown under high 
                     salinity (250mM NaCl), <i>prc</i> null mutants form 
                     spherical cells with an enlarged periplasm and eccentric 
                     nucleoids - a phenotype that is suppressed by deletion of 
                     |FRAME: G7147 <i>mepS</i>| |CITS: [24443528]|. A phenotypic
                     analysis of single and double mutants involving 15 genes 
                     implicated in protein quality control (including 
                     <i>prc</i>), has been reported; 5 negative synthetic 
                     combinations involving <i>prc</i> were detected |CITS: 
                     [22505681]|.
                     <i>prc</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|. <i>prc</i> insertion mutants were 
                     identified in a genetic screen for genes that are important
                     for survival of exposure to ionizing radiation (IR); 
                     <i>prc</i> deletion mutant has a moderate decrease in IR 
                     survival |CITS: [25049088]|.
                     A fluorimetric assay for Prc activity is described in 
                     |CITS: [9750142]|. Prc is a cooperative enzyme; peptidic 
                     inhibitors, candidate substrates and potential activators 
                     are identified in |CITS: [22267294]|.
                     Prc contributes to virulence in pathogenic <i>E. coli</i> 
                     |CITS: [22825444][31900139]|.
                     <i>prc</i>: 'processing involving the C-terminal cleavage' 
                     |CITS: [1856173]|"
                     /translation="MNMFFRLTALAGLLAIAGQTFAVEDITRADQIPVLKEETQHATVS
                     ERVTSRFTRSHYRQFDLDQAFSAKIFDRYLNLLDYSHNVLLASDVEQFAKKKTELGDEL
                     RSGKLDVFYDLYNLAQKRRFERYQYALSVLEKPMDFTGNDTYNLDRSKAPWPKNEAELN
                     ALWDSKVKFDELSLKLTGKTDKEIRETLTRRYKFAIRRLAQTNSEDVFSLAMTAFAREI
