     gene            complement(1909093..1909791)
                     /gene="proQ"
                     /locus_tag="b1831"
                     /label=proQ
     CDS             complement(1909093..1909791)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="proQ"
                     /locus_tag="b1831"
                     /product="RNA chaperone ProQ"
                     /label=proQ
                     /note="ProQ is an RNA-binding protein involved in global
                     small RNA-dependent regulation. It binds double-stranded 
                     RNA and has both strand exchange and RNA duplexing 
                     activities.
                     ProQ is a soluble protein that occurs at approximately 
                     2,000 copies per cell |CITS: [24205389]|. It belongs to the
                     FinO family of proteins, whose members contain a conserved 
                     RNA-binding domain (named after the founding member of this
                     family, the F-like plasmid (F') FinO protein) |CITS: 
                     [1092654]|. The protein also contains a C-terminal Tudor 
                     domain |CITS: [28193673]|, and the two domains are 
                     connected by a long, flexible, protease-sensitive linker 
                     region |CITS: [17319698]|. All three domains contain 
                     potential RNA-binding surfaces.
                     ProQ was initially characterized as a positive regulator of
                     the osmosensing transporter |FRAME: PROP-MONOMER ""ProP""|,
                     which mediates the uptake of zwitterionic osmolytes such as
                     proline and glycine betaine (hence its name) |CITS: 
                     [10049386]|. <i>proQ</i>-deficient mutants have 
                     significantly reduced ProP activity |CITS: [15476391]|. A 
                     transposon insertion in <i>proQ</i> increases resistance to
                     |FRAME: CPD0-1103| by impairing the osmotic activation ProP
                     at a post-transcriptional level |CITS: 
                     [2536686][10049386]|. In reconstituted proteoliposomes, 
                     activity of His<sub>6</sub>-tagged ProP corresponds to ProP
                     activity in ProQ-deficient bacteria and is determined by 
                     medium osmolality |CITS: [12525168]|. A <i>proQ</i> defect 
                     leads to reduced levels of ProP also when the cells are 
                     grown at lower osmolality |CITS: [21381725]|. ProQ likely 
                     controls the translation of ProP via interaction with a 
                     small regulatory RNA, though the identity of that sRNA is 
                     currently unknown |CITS: [21381725]|.
                     It is now known that ProQ associates with ~400 transcripts,
                     including >50 sRNAs, and affects the expression of hundreds
                     of genes involved in energy production, osmoregulation, 
                     amino acid metabolism, translation, biofilm formation, 
                     motility and virulence |CITS: [29804828]|. Some of the 
                     targets of ProQ identified in a pulldown assay include 
                     |FRAME: IS061-RNA| |CITS: [26609136]|, |FRAME: RNA0-161|, 
                     |FRAME: RDLD-RNA|, |FRAME: SRAL-RNA|, |FRAME: RNA0-333|, 
                     |FRAME: RYEC-RNA|, |FRAME: RYED-RNA|, |FRAME: RYGC-RNA|, 
                     |FRAME:  C0730-RNA|, |FRAME: RNA0-332|, |FRAME: RNA0-165|, 
                     and |FRAME: RNA0-141| |CITS: [36328526]|. ProQ was also 
                     reported to interact with mRNAs that are being translated 
                     |CITS: [24205389]|, though it is likely that the 
                     interaction was between an sRNA and the mRNA, which is 
                     facilitated by ProQ |CITS: [28336682]|. ProQ is extensively
                     involved in sRNA-mediated regulation, acting as an RNA 
                     chaperone that protects sRNAs from cellular RNases and 
                     stabilizing the sRNA-mRNA duplex against the unwinding 
                     activity of the initiating ribosome |CITS: [28336682]|. 
                     Using the <u>R</u>NA <u>i</u>nteraction by <u>l</u>igation 
                     and <u>seq</u>uencing (RIL-seq) method found that the 
                     post-transcriptional Hfq-dependent RNA-RNA interactome was 
                     much more extensive than the ProQ-dependent RNA-RNA 
                     interactome, though there was overlap between the two 
                     interactomes |CITS: [31761494]|.
                     The FinO N-terminal domain (NTD) was found to recognize and
                     tightly bind to 3′-intrinsic terminators with a terminator 
                     hairpin based on studies with mutations in the 3' 
                     untranslated region (3'-UTR) of |FRAME: EG10559| and 
                     |FRAME: EG12179| |CITS: [32542384]|. The NTD has a concave 
                     face and crosslinking experiments have identified several 
                     amino acid residues witihin the NTD region that interact 
                     with the RNA substrates. An assay that analyzed ProQ 
                     binding to seven different RNAs has identified three 
                     residues (R58, Y70, and R80) that are essential for binding
                     of all seven RNAs, suggesting that these residues form the 
                     main RNA-binding site |CITS: [37607742]|.
                     A <i>proQ</i> deletion mutant is more sensitive to the DNA 
                     damaging agent nalidixic acid than wild type |CITS: 
                     [23308060]| and shows a defect in biofilm formation that is
                     independent of ProP |CITS: [24205389]|. <i>proQ</i> mutants
                     grow more slowly at high salinity than wild type and show 
                     cell morphology defects |CITS: [24443528]|. <i>proQ</i>: 
                     <u>pro</u>line uptake regulator <u>Q</u> Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [10066837][25102058][28189381][28370625]|"
