     CDS             complement(1916570..1917226)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pphA"
                     /locus_tag="b1838"
                     /product="trifunctional phosphoprotein phosphatase 1 / 
                     protein-tyrosine phosphatase / protein-serine/threonine 
                     phosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.3.48; 3.1.3.16"
                     /label=pphA
                     /note="PphA and PphB are phosphoprotein phosphatases that
                     act in transduction of the misfolded protein stress signal 
                     in a pathway that includes the |FRAME: PWY0-1485| and which
                     activates transcription of <i>htrA</i>.  PphA is involved 
                     in induction of the heat shock response.  PphA exhibits 
                     phosphatase activity toward phosphorylated serine/threonine
                     and phosphorylated tyrosine in protein substrates and 
                     |FRAME: PHOSPHO-CPXR phosphorylated CpxR|. Additional 
                     phosphoproteins accumulate in a <i>pphA</i> mutant, 
                     suggesting that they are <i>in vivo</i> targets of PphA 
                     |CITS: [9130712]|.
                     PphA and PphB have similarity to the phosphoprotein 
                     phosphatase of bacteriophage lambda (&lambda;-PP) |CITS: 
                     [11467953]| and to <i>Salmonella enterica</i> serovar 
                     Typhimurium PrpA and PrpB proteins |CITS: [11717262]|.
                     A <i>pphA</i> null mutant has a slow growth phenotype. 
                     Overexpression of <i>pphA</i> induces the heat shock 
                     response |CITS: [9130712]|.  Transcription of the |FRAME: 
                     RYEA-RNA RyeA| RNA, which is encoded upstream of 
                     <i>pphA</i>, interferes with read-through transcription of 
                     <i>pphA</i> from the <i>sdsR</i> promoter |CITS: 
                     [31270363]|.
                     PrpA: ""<b>pr</b>otein <b>p</b>hosphatase <b>A</b>"" |CITS:
                     [9130712]|
                     Review: |CITS: [9383148]|"
