     gene            1917323..1917594
                     /gene="ryeA"
                     /locus_tag="b4432"
                     /label=ryeA
     misc_RNA        1917323..1917594
                     /gene="ryeA"
                     /locus_tag="b4432"
                     /product="small antisense RNA RyeA"
                     /note="RyeA (also named SraC the same year) and |FRAME: 
                     RYEB-RNA| are two small RNAs encoded in opposite directions
                     within the same inter-ORF region of the genome |CITS: 
                     [11445539][14602901]|. RyeA was initially found to be 
                     approximately 234-249 nt |CITS: [11448770]| or 275 nt 
                     |CITS: [11445539]| in length. The 5' and 3' ends were later
                     mapped by RACE |CITS: [31270363]|.
                     SdsR is complementary to an internal segment of RyeA |CITS:
                     [14602901]|. RyeA may act as an antitoxin to SdsR; 
                     coexpression of RyeA suppresses the toxic effect of ectopic
                     SdsR expression |CITS: [30293519]|. Transcription of the 
                     <i>ryeA</i> RNA interferes with read-through transcription 
                     of the <i>pphA</i> gene from the <i>sdsR</i> promoter 
                     |CITS: [31270363]|.
                     Minimal binding of RyeA to Hfq protein has been reported 
                     |CITS: [11445539]|.
                     RyeA is most strongly expressed in minimal medium and 
                     during exponential growth |CITS: 
                     [11445539][11448770][31270363][31248592]|, is upregulated 
                     upon acid exposure |CITS: [17026754][32087965]|, and is 
                     more strongly expressed at 37&deg;C than at 28&deg;C |CITS:
                     [26020590]|. RyeA is expressed at higher levels when cells 
                     are grown in pyruvate compared to glucose |CITS: 
                     [31363904]|. The half life of RyeA in stationary phase is 2
                     minutes |CITS: [14602901]|.
                     RyeA RNA is subject to RNase III cleavage, which coincides 
                     with the appearance of SdsR at the onset of stationary 
                     phase |CITS: [11448770][14602901]|. The expression patterns
                     of RyeA and SdsR appear to be reciprocal |CITS: 
                     [30293519]|; mutation of the <i>sdsR</i> promoter leads to 
                     continued expression of RyeA in stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [31270363]|. RyeA abundance during exponential phase is 
                     significantly increased in an <i>|FRAME: G7175|</i> mutant 
                     |CITS: [31248592]|. Subsequent studies found |FRAME: 
                     GCVB-RNA| directly regulates the abundance of RyeA RNA in 
                     exponential phase and at low pH; it also divergently 
                     regulates SdsR |CITS: [39278090]|.
                     Sra: ""small RNA"" |CITS: [11448770]|
                     PsrA: ""predicted small RNA"" |CITS: [11448770]|
                     Ryx: ""RNA of unknown function at X position (10 minute) in
                     the genome"" |CITS: [11445539]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12762038][12654996][32213244]|"
     gene            complement(1917361..1917464)
                     /gene="sdsR"
                     /locus_tag="b4433"
                     /label=sdsR
     misc_RNA        complement(1917361..1917464)
                     /gene="sdsR"
                     /locus_tag="b4433"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA SdsR"
                     /note="SdsR is a small regulatory RNA that was shown to 
                     base-pair with a part of the <i>|FRAME: EG10625|</i> coding
                     region. Deletion of <i>sdsR</i> decreases 
                     ampicillin-induced mutagenesis |CITS: [23511474]|. 
                     <i>|FRAME: EG11009|</i> |CITS: [26811318]| and <i>|FRAME: 
                     G7681|</i> |CITS: [30293519]| were found to be direct 
                     targets of SdsR. RNA-seq analysis of an <i>sdsR</i> 
                     promoter mutant identified many potential targets for 
                     regulation by SdsR |CITS: [31270363]|.
                     Overexpression of SdsR decreases biofilm development and 
                     swarming motility |CITS: [26469694][26811318]| and 
                     translation of |FRAME: EG11009-MONOMER|, and increases 
                     sensitivity to crystal violet and novobiocin |CITS: 
                     [26811318]|. Ectopic expression of SdsR during exponential 
                     phase leads to cell filamentation and death. The effect 
                     appears to be mediated by downregulation of |FRAME: 
                     G7681-MONOMER| expression; coexpression of |FRAME: 
                     RYEA-RNA| suppresses the effect of ectopic SdsR expression 
                     |CITS: [30293519]|.
                     RyeA and SdsR are two small RNAs encoded in opposite 
                     directions within the same inter-ORF region of the genome. 
                     SdsR is approximately 104 nt in length, and a minor SdsR 
                     species of 74 nt has also been detected |CITS: 
                     [11445539][14602901]|. SdsR is complementary to an internal
                     segment of RyeA |CITS: [14602901]|.
                     SdsR interacts with |FRAME: EG10438-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [11445539][30293519][36328526][38142457]|; SdsR levels are 
                     decreased in an <i>hfq</i> mutant |CITS: [31248592]|.
                     SdsR is most highly expressed in stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [11445539][31270363][31248592]|, with a coincident 
                     appearance of the shorter ~150 nt form of RyeA, which may 
                     be due to interaction with SdsR and subsequent processing 
                     by RNase III |CITS: [14602901][22180532]|. The expression 
                     patterns of SdsR and RyeA appear to be reciprocal |CITS: 
                     [30293519]|; mutation of the <i>ryeA</i> promoter shifts 
                     expression of SdsR to an earlier growth phase |CITS: 
                     [31270363]|. |FRAME: GCVB-RNA| directly regulates the 
                     abundance of RyeA RNA and divergently regulates SdsR |CITS:
                     [39278090]|.
                     A study using the <u>R</u>NA <u>i</u>nteraction by 
                     <u>l</u>igation and <u>seq</u>uencing (RIL-seq) method with
                     cells grown in various nitrogen (N) growth conditions found
                     that the Hfq-dependent SdsR-RNA interactions were most 
                     extensive during long-term growth arrest due to N 
                     starvation. Further examination with <i>&Delta;sdsR</i> 
                     found that both direct and indirect regulatory targets of 
                     SdsR are involved, and both positive and negative 
                     translational regulation occurred, including downregulation
                     of the |FRAME: TU0-14632| operon whose products are also 
                     involved in the adaptive response to N starvation. SdsR 
                     binding sites on mRNA targets include ribosome binding 
                     sites (RBS), 5'-UTR not overlapping with the RBS, and 
                     internal to the coding sequence or in the 3'-UTR region 
                     |CITS: [38142457]|.
                     The homologous sRNA in <i>S. enterica</i> was shown to 
                     regulate synthesis of the major porin OmpD |CITS: 
                     [22180532]|.
                     Deletion of <i>sdsR</i> has no significant effect on growth
                     |CITS: [26811318]|. However, faster recovery of growth from
                     N starvation occurs when <i>sdsR</i> is not deleted |CITS: 
                     [38142457]|.
                     Ryx: ""RNA of unknown function at X position (10 minute) in
                     the genome"" |CITS: [11445539]|
                     SdsR (in <i>S. enterica</i>): sigma 
                     <b>S</b>-<b>d</b>ependent <b>sR</b>NA |CITS: 
                     [22180532][23511474]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12762038][12654996][32213244]|"
     gene            complement(1917622..1917963)
                     /gene="yebY"
                     /locus_tag="b1839"
                     /label=yebY
     CDS             complement(1917622..1917963)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yebY"
                     /locus_tag="b1839"
                     /product="DUF2511 domain-containing protein YebY"
                     /label=yebY
                     /note="YebY is a periplasmic protein; the purified 
                     selenomethionine-labeled protein is monomeric and contains 
                     an intramolecular disulfide bond; YebY binds several metals
                     with low affinity but this is not thought to be a 
                     physiologically relevant property |CITS: [34822841]|. YebY 
                     is encoded in an operon with the |FRAME: G7013-MONOMER 
                     copper importer YebZ| and a periplasmic |FRAME: 
                     G7014-MONOMER Cu<sup>2+</sup>-binding protein YobA|; 
                     YobA-YebZ-YebY (the AZY proteins) constitute a copper 
                     uptake system that is essential for activation of the 
                     |FRAME: TU00190 <i>mar</i> operon| and for resistance to 
                     the antibiotics norfloxacin, ciprofloxacin, and ampicillin 
                     |CITS: [34822841][40108346]|. YebZ functions as an 
                     energy-dependent copper importer; YebZ activity is enhanced
                     by YobA and diminished by YebY |CITS: [40108346]|.
                     The AZY proteins are implicated in delivering copper to 
                     membrane targets such as copper-dependent |FRAME: 
                     NADH-DHII-MONOMER| (NDH-2) |CITS: [34822841]|. Membranes 
                     prepared from a strain that lacks all three proteins (<i>E.
                     coli</i> BW25113 &Delta;<i>yobA</i> &Delta;<i>yebZ</i> 
                     &Delta;<i>yebY</i>) have significantly reduced NADH 
                     oxidation levels despite containing wild-type levels of 
                     NDH-2; membranes prepared from a &Delta;<i>yebZ</i> strain 
                     show no significant difference to wild-type |CITS: 
                     [34822841]|. When grown in the presence of 
                     CuSO<sub>4</sub>, cells lacking the AZY proteins have 
                     significantly reduced intracellular copper levels and show 
                     reduced activation of the <i>mar</i> operon, compared to 
                     wild-type |CITS: [40108346]|."
                     /translation="MMKKSILAFLLLTSSAAALAAPQVITVSRFEVGKDKWAFNREEVM
                     LTCRPGNALYVINPSTLVQYPLNDIAQKEVASGKTNAQPISVIQIDDPNNPGEKMSLAP
                     FIERAEKLC"
                     /go_component="outer membrane-bounded periplasmic space
                     [goid 0030288] [evidence IDA] [PMID 34822841]"
                     /go_process="copper ion homeostasis [goid 0055070]
                     [evidence IDA] [PMID 34822841]"
     gene            complement(1917976..1918848)
                     /gene="yebZ"
                     /locus_tag="b1840"
                     /label=yebZ
     CDS             complement(1917976..1918848)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yebZ"
                     /locus_tag="b1840"
                     /product="bifunctional copper transporter YebZ / copper 
                     transporter"
                     /label=yebZ
                     /note="YebZ is a member of the CopD family of 
                     copper-sequestering proteins; YebZ is encoded in an operon 
                     with the |FRAME: G7014-MONOMER copper-binding protein YobA|
                     and a periplasmic protein |FRAME: G7012-MONOMER YebY| (the 
                     <i>AZY</i> operon); YobA-YebZ-YebY (the AZY proteins) 
                     constitute a copper uptake system that is essential for 
                     activation of the |FRAME: TU00190 <i>mar</i> operon| and 
                     for resistance to the antibiotics norfloxacin, 
                     ciprofloxacin, and ampicillin |CITS: [34822841][40108346]|.
                     YebZ functions as an energy-dependent copper importer; YebZ
                     activity is enhanced by YobA and diminished by YebY |CITS: 
                     [40108346]|.
                     The AZY proteins are implicated in delivering copper to 
                     membrane targets such as copper-dependent |FRAME: 
                     NADH-DHII-MONOMER| (NDH-2) |CITS: [34822841]|. Membranes 
                     prepared from a strain that lacks all three proteins (<i>E.
                     coli</i> BW25113 &Delta;<i>yobA</i> &Delta;<i>yebZ</i> 
                     &Delta;<i>yebY</i>) have significantly reduced NADH 
                     oxidation levels despite containing wild-type levels of 
                     NDH-2; membranes prepared from a &Delta;<i>yebZ</i> strain 
                     show no significant difference to wild-type |CITS: 
                     [34822841]|. When grown in the presence of 
                     CuSO<sub>4</sub>, cells lacking the AZY proteins have 
                     significantly reduced intracellular copper levels and show 
                     reduced activation of the <i>mar</i> operon, compared to 
                     wild-type |CITS: [40108346]|.
                     YebZ is predicted to be an inner membrane protein with 8 
                     transmembrane domains; experimental topology analysis 
                     suggests the C terminus is located in the periplasm |CITS: 
                     [15919996]|."
