     gene            complement(1924291..1924647)
                     /gene="yebF"
                     /locus_tag="b1847"
                     /label=yebF
     CDS             complement(1924291..1924647)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yebF"
                     /locus_tag="b1847"
                     /product="secreted protein YebF"
                     /label=yebF
                     /note="YebF is a secreted protein of unknown function
                     |CITS: [16369539]|.
                     The precise mechanism by which YebF is secreted to the 
                     extracellular medium is not clear however the outer 
                     membrane porins |FRAME: EG10671-MONOMER ""OmpF""|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10670-MONOMER ""OmpC""| and |FRAME: EG12117-MONOMER 
                     ""OmpX""| contribute to the process. Deletion of 
                     <i>ompF</i> or <i>ompX</i> severely impairs YebF secretion 
                     whilst deletion of <i>ompC</i> impairs secretion to a 
                     lesser extent. Overexpression of <i>ompF</i> from a plasmid
                     restores secretion of YebF in an <i>ompF</i> deletion 
                     strain. YebF blocks conductance of OmpF channels in planar 
                     lipid bilayer experiments. Pull-down experiments using 
                     his-tagged YebF suggest that YebF interacts with OmpX and 
                     OmpF/C. YebF interacts with OmpF/C at the periplasmic face 
                     of the outer membrane |CITS: [22658749]|. An NMR derived 
                     solution structure of YebF indicates that the protein is 
                     composed of an ordered core surrounded by a dynamic surface
                     including two extended loop structures |CITS: [22658749]|.
                     YebF has been used as a carrier protein to direct secretion
                     of fusion proteins (eg.YebF-&alpha;-amylase and YebF-human 
                     interleukin-2) to the culture medium |CITS: 
                     [16369539][28182400]|"
                     /translation="MKKRGAFLGLLLVSACASVFAANNETSKSVTFPKCEDLDAAGIAA
                     SVKRDYQQNRVARWADDQKIVGQADPVAWVSLQDIQGKDDKWSVPLTVRGKSADIHYQV
                     SVDCKAGMAEYQRR"
                     /go_component="outer membrane-bounded periplasmic space
                     [goid 0030288] [evidence IEA] [PMID 19054114]"
                     /go_component="extracellular region [goid 0005576]
                     [evidence IEA] [GO_REF 0000044]"
                     /go_component="extracellular region [goid 0005576]
                     [evidence ] [PMID 16369539]"
     gene            complement(1924714..1925004)
                     /gene="yebG"
                     /locus_tag="b1848"
                     /label=yebG
     CDS             complement(1924714..1925004)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yebG"
                     /locus_tag="b1848"
                     /product="DNA damage-inducible protein YebG"
                     /label=yebG
                     /note="The <I>yebG</I> gene is a DNA damage-inducible gene
                     of the SOS regulon |CITS: [9368369]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>yebG</i> increases the MIC of 
                     polyhexamethylene biguanide to between 5.25 and 6.75 mg/L 
                     |CITS: [16549663]|.
                     <i>yebG</i> is regulated by the LexA transcriptional 
                     repressor |CITS: [16264194][15454462]|.  Transcription of 
                     <i>yebG</i> is induced by mitomycin C |CITS: 
                     [9368369][11226277]|, is induced upon entry into stationary
                     phase, and requires cAMP and H-NS protein |CITS: 
                     [10474193]|.  Expression of <i>yebG</i> is induced 14-fold 
                     upon exposure of cells to the biocide polyhexamethylene 
                     biguanide |CITS: [16549663]|.
                     Expression of <i>yebG</i> also increases in <i>dam</i> and 
                     <i>dam mutS</i> mutants |CITS: [16199573]|.  Expression of 
                     <i>yebG</i> is induced 3.9-fold in the presence of 
                     autoinducer-2 (AI-2) |CITS: [11514505]|.  A <i>yebG</i> 
                     deletion mutant is 6-fold more sensitive to X-radiation 
                     than wild type |CITS: [27718375]|.
                     "
                     /translation="MAVEVKYVVIREGEEKMSFTSKKEADAYDKMLDTADLLDTWLTNS
                     PVQMEDEQREALSLWLAEQKDVLSTILKTGKLPSPQVVGAESEEEDASHAA"
                     /go_component="cytosol [goid 0005829] [evidence IDA] [PMID
                     18304323]"
                     /go_process="response to X-ray [goid 0010165] [evidence
                     IMP] [PMID 27718375]"
                     /go_process="DNA repair [goid 0006281] [evidence IEP]
                     [PMID 16199573]"
                     /go_process="SOS response [goid 0009432] [evidence IEP]
                     [PMID 9368369]"
     gene            1925138..1926316
                     /gene="purT"
                     /locus_tag="b1849"
                     /label=purT
     CDS             1925138..1926316
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="purT"
                     /locus_tag="b1849"
                     /product="bifunctional phosphoribosylglycinamide
                     formyltransferase 2 / acetate kinase"
                     /EC_number="6.3.1.21; 2.7.2.1"
                     /label=purT
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> contains two different 
                     phosphoribosylglycinamide (GAR) transformylases, both of 
                     which can catalyze the third step in <i>de novo</i> purine 
                     biosynthesis.  The transformylase encoded by <i>purN</i> 
                     utilizes 10-formyl-tetrahydrofolate as the formyl donor. 
                     The second transformylase encoded by <i>purT</i> utilizes 
                     formate, which is provided by PurU-catalyzed hydrolysis of 
                     10-formyl-tetrahydrofolate |CITS: [8226647]|.  The 
                     existence of these two transformylase enzymes was 
                     determined by mutant studies.  A strain containing a 
                     knockout insertion in <i>purN</i> did not result in purine 
                     auxotrophy |CITS: [3301838]|.  Only mutants defective in 
                     both <i>purN</i> and <i>purT</i> required exogenously added
                     purine for growth |CITS: [8501063]|.  There is no 
                     significant homology between the two transformylases |CITS:
                     [8117714]|.
                     Kinetic studies of wild-type and mutant PurT, as well as 
                     positional isotope exchange studies, demonstrated a formyl 
                     phosphate intermediate in the catalytic mechanism of the 
                     GAR transformylase reaction |CITS: [9184151]|.  GAR 
                     transformylase 2 can also catalyze a second, side reaction 
                     that is different from its transformylase activity.  The 
                     second activity is acetate kinase, which cleaves ATP in the
                     presence of acetate to produce acetylphosphate and ADP 
                     |CITS: [8117714]|.
                     The crystal structure of phosphoribosylglycinamide 
                     transformylase 2 has been determined in complex with 
                     substrate and/or nucleotide analogs |CITS: 
                     [10913290][11953435]|.  The enzyme was determined to be a 
                     monomer in solution |CITS: [8117714]|, but a homodimer in 
                     the crystal |CITS: [10913290]|.  It belongs to the 
                     ATP-grasp superfamily |CITS: [10913290]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> naturally lacks the <i>fhs</i> gene encoding
                     formyltetrahydrofolate synthetase, but possesses 
                     <i>purT</i>. In strains engineered to contain either 
                     <i>fhs</i>, <i>purT</i>, or both genes, growth competition 
                     assays in mixed cultures showed that strains with only a 
                     single gene had a fitness advantage |CITS: [26100103]|. 
                     Review: Jensen, K.F., G. Dandanell, B. Hove-Jensen, and M. 
                     Willemoes (2008) ""Nucleotides, Nucleosides and 
                     Nucleobases"" EcoSal 3.6.2 |CITS: [ecosal]|"
                     /translation="MTLLGTALRPAATRVMLLGSGELGKEVAIECQRLGVEVIAVDRYA
                     DAPAMHVAHRSHVINMLDGDALRRVVELEKPHYIVPEIEAIATDMLIQLEEEGLNVVPC
