     gene            complement(1927050..1928861)
                     /gene="edd"
                     /locus_tag="b1851"
                     /label=edd
     CDS             complement(1927050..1928861)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="edd"
                     /locus_tag="b1851"
                     /product="phosphogluconate dehydratase"
                     /EC_number="4.2.1.12"
                     /label=edd
                     /note="Phosphogluconate dehydratase catalyzes the
                     dehydration of D-gluconate 6-phosphate to 
                     2-dehydro-3-deoxy-D-gluconate 6-phosphate. This is the 
                     first reaction of the |FRAME: ENTNER-DOUDOROFF-PWY "" 
                     Entner-Doudoroff pathway""| which constitutes the primary 
                     route of gluconate metabolism in <i>E. coli</i>. 
                     Phosphogluconate dehydratase is induced by growth of cells 
                     on gluconate |CITS: [5337840][5337844][16742809][1624451]|.
                     The enzyme is sensitive to oxidants such as superoxide and 
                     hydrogen peroxide due to its [4Fe-4S] iron-sulfur cluster 
                     content. It is also inactivated by iron chelators and 
                     thiol-reactive compounds. Fluoride ions protect the enzyme 
                     from the effects of oxidants |CITS: 
                     [1846355][17102132][15308657]|. Under continuous culture 
                     conditions induction of phosphogluconate dehydratase is 
                     favored at low oxygen concentrations |CITS: [1336799]|. Edd
                     from <i>E. coli</i> K-12, a laboratory strain, has not been
                     well characterized biochemically. However, using <i>E. 
                     coli</i> strains from the ECOR collection of natural 
                     isolates, the <i>K<sub>m</sub></i> and 
                     <i>V<sub>max</sub></i> of phosphogluconate dehydratase were
                     determined along with the growth rate in gluconate minimal 
                     salts medium |CITS: [11430650]|.
                     Reduced expression levels of the GroEL-GroES molecular 
                     chaperonin complex during steady-state growth increases the
                     synthesis of Edd and Eda, suggesting a regulatory function 
                     |CITS: [7912695]|. Plasmid-encoded Edd overproduction 
                     suppresses the stationary phase toxicity of DnaK 
                     overproduction |CITS: [8544815]|. The product of 
                     <i>gntR</i> regulates <i>edd</i> inducibility |CITS: 
                     [8655507]|.
                     |FRAME: IDNCAT-PWY| proceeds through the Entner Doudoroff 
                     pathway and an <i>edd</i> mutant grows more slowly than 
                     wild-type on L-idonate |CITS: [9658018]|. It also grows 
                     more slowly on gluconate which can be metabolized via the 
                     |FRAME: PENTOSE-P-PWY| |CITS: [9658018][5337844]|. Analysis
                     of the ability of <i>eda</i> mutants and <i>edd-eda</i> 
                     double mutants to colonize the large intestine of 
                     streptomycin-treated mice suggests that the ability to 
                     utilize the Entner-Doudoroff pathway promotes colonization 
                     |CITS: [8751891]|. A &Delta;<i>edd</i> mutant shows 
                     defective colonization |CITS: [15123798]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>aer</i> mutant has a defect in colonization of 
                     the streptomycin-treated mouse large intestine which can be
                     complemented by a plasmid expressing <i>edd</i> and 
                     <i>eda</i> |CITS: [19130287]|. In an <i>eda</i> mutant, 
                     intracellular accumulation of the Edd product 
                     2-dehydro-3-deoxy-D-gluconate 6-phosphate results in 
                     bacteriostatic growth inhibition |CITS: [9503620]|. In 
                     yeast cells, expression of <i>edd</i> results in very low 
                     activity due to its iron-sulfur cluster content |CITS: 
                     [24333129]|.
                     Mutations in <i>iscS</i> involved in iron-sulfur cluster 
                     biosynthesis result in decreased activity of Edd |CITS: 
                     [10908675][14978044]|. Edd is a nitric oxide-sensitive 
                     dehydratase that is more susceptible to inactivation in 
                     <i>ygaK</i> or <i>ygaA</i> mutants that affect nitric oxide
                     metabolism |CITS: [11751865]|. A <i>sufABCDSE</i> deletion 
                     mutant shows reduced Edd activity during iron starvation 
                     |CITS: [15101990]|. <i>In vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i> 
                     studies suggest that Fpr has a role in repair of 
                     oxidatively damaged iron-sulfur clusters in Edd |CITS: 
                     [16549675]|.
                     In an <i>E. coli</i> JM101 PTS<sup>-</sup>Glc<sup>+</sup> 
                     strain, knockout of <i>edd</i> and batch cultivation 
                     studies on gluconate or glucose suggested a broader role 
                     than carbon metabolism for the Entner Doudroff pathway 
                     |CITS: [16333337]|.
                     An <i>edd</i> disruption mutant has been used in metabolic 
                     engineering studies to overproduce inosine |CITS: 
                     [16041126]|. Overexpression of <i>edd</i> can be used in 
                     the engineered production of alcohols |CITS: [20426856]|. 
                     <i>edd</i> is one of four genes that are part of an 
                     engineering strategy to control carbon flux through three 
                     glucose utilization pathways in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [22454498]|. Disruption of <i>edd</i> has been used to 
                     increase riboflavin production in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [27071937]|. Overexpression of <i>edd</i> and <i>eda</i> 
                     and knockout of <i>pfkA</i> has been used to alter native 
                     glycolytic flux |CITS: [27766116]|.
                     A deletion mutant in <i>edd</i> leading to inactivation of 
                     the Entner-Doudoroff shunt resulted in increased biofilm 
                     formation under planktonic growth conditions relative to 
                     microtiter plates and to the wild type strain |CITS: 
                     [37728257]|.
                     edd: ""<b>E</b>ntner-<b>D</b>oudoroff <b>d</b>ehydrase"" 
                     |CITS: [5337844]|"
