     gene            complement(1929096..1930571)
                     /gene="zwf"
                     /locus_tag="b1852"
                     /label=zwf
     CDS             complement(1929096..1930571)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="zwf"
                     /locus_tag="b1852"
                     /product="NADP(+)-dependent glucose-6-phosphate
                     dehydrogenase"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.49"
                     /label=zwf
                     /note="Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PDH) is the
                     first enzyme of the |FRAME: PENTOSE-P-PWY| and provides a 
                     large fraction of the NADPH needed for anabolism.
                     The <i>E. coli</i> G6PDH shows a strong preference for 
                     NADP<sup>+</sup> over NAD<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [22519976]|. 
                     The structural basis for this preference was studied using 
                     molecular simulations, kinetic characterization of 
                     site-directed mutants and phylogenetic analyses |CITS: 
                     [27010804]|.
                     Metabolic flux through the pathways of central carbon 
                     metabolism was measured using GC-MS |CITS: [12603321]| and 
                     <sup>13</sup>C labeling and 2D NMR spectroscopy |CITS: 
                     [14645264][25178796]|. Regulation of these pathways under 
                     different growth conditions was measured at the level of 
                     enzyme expression and activity |CITS: 
                     [12655459][24482123]|.
                     Substitution of an NADH-producing glucose-6-phosphate 
                     dehydrogenase for the native NADPH-producing enzyme reduced
                     the growth rate of otherwise wild-type cells, while 
                     increasing the growth rate of a &Delta;<i>pgi</i> mutant. 
                     This suggests that whether production of NADH by G6PDH is 
                     beneficial or detrimental <i>in vivo</i> depends on the 
                     operation of the upper |FRAME: GLYCOLYSIS| |CITS: 
                     [25246670]|.
                     In addition to its role in central carbon metabolism, G6PDH
                     was found to be the source of a linear peptide with the 
                     amino acid sequence Asn-Asn-Trp-Asn-Asn (NNWNN) that acts 
                     as an ""extracellular death factor"" (EDF) for 
                     MazEF-mediated cell death |CITS: [17962566][18310334]|. The
                     peptide acts by increasing the endoribonuclease activity of
                     the toxins MazF and ChpBK |CITS: [21419338]|. EDF 
                     production under stress conditions is due to cleavage of 
                     the <i>zwf</i> mRNA at specific ACA sites by MazF, 
                     generating a leaderless truncated mRNA. The location of the
                     EDF-coding region with respect to the MazF cleavage sites 
                     is important, and the trans-translation system is required 
                     |CITS: [26814184]|.
                     <i>zwf</i> is one of the most consistently flux-coupled 
                     genes, which are genes whose expression transition patterns
                     upon perturbations are correlated with their corresponding 
                     flux values |CITS: [23420780]|. Expression of <i>zwf</i> is
                     growth rate-regulated |CITS: [383688][1729252]| at the 
                     transcriptional level |CITS: [1906868]|. G6PDH activity is 
                     greater in rapidly growing cells, and is greater under 
                     nitrogen-limited compared to carbon-limited growth 
                     conditions |CITS: [14276099]|. <i>zwf</i> is a part of the 
                     SoxRS regulon which responds to superoxide stress |CITS: 
                     [1696718][1653416][1317841][80 
                     34583][7891555][7896696][8825776][11401718]|. Additional 
                     regulators have been shown to activate transcription of 
                     <i>zwf</i> |CITS: [7896685][8522515][8626315]|. Exposure to
                     tellurite activates transcription of <i>zwf</i> and thereby
                     increases the synthesis of NADPH |CITS: [21984934]|.
                     A <i>zwf</i> null mutation does not affect the growth rate 
                     significantly |CITS: [4869212]|. However, central carbon 
                     metabolism and metabolic flux is changed |CITS: 
                     [12603321][14645264][14661115][15113569][17631881]|. A 
                     <i>pgi zwf</i> double mutant does not grow on glucose as 
                     the sole source of carbon |CITS: [4869212]|. In the 
                     presence of glucose, it accumulates high levels of 
                     glucose-6-phosphate, which inhibits |FRAME: F16B-CPLX| 
                     activity |CITS: [4302393]|. Deletion of <i>zwf</i> reduces 
                     the organic solvent tolerance of <i>E. coli</i> JM109 
                     |CITS: [24492952]|.
                     Exposure to oxidation by peroxyl radicals results in 
                     partial inactivation of G6PDH due to oxidation of Trp and 
                     Met residues that are dispersed throughout the enzyme 
                     |CITS: [36476946]|. When all three |FRAME: 
                     OXIDATIVEPENT-PWY| enzymes (G6PDH, |FRAME: 
                     6PGLUCONOLACT-MONOMER| and |FRAME: 
                     6PGLUCONDEHYDROG-MONOMER|) are exposed to a peroxyl radical
                     oxidation together, the production of NADPH was lower than 
                     what is generated by non-oxidized enzymes but not during 
                     the initial stages of oxidation |CITS: [38848786]|.
                     Manipulation of G6PDH levels plays a role in many metabolic
                     engineering strategies, e.g. |CITS: [21538115][2 
                     1732330][22454498][24062132][24885133][25258165][26581029]|
                     . A strategy for the production of D-glucose from pentoses 
                     such as xylose and arabinose included a &Delta;<i>pfkA 
                     zwf</i> double knockout strain |CITS: [25746993]|. G6PDH 
                     can produce NADPH due to a newly identified ability to 
                     substitute erythrose-4-phosphate from the |FRAME: 
                     NONOXIPENT-PWY| for glucose-6-phosphate under xylose growth
                     conditions |CITS: [39387572]|.
                     Zwf: ""<b>Zw</b>ischen<b>f</b>erment"" |CITS: [4869212]| 
                     Review/Comment: |CITS: 
                     [18042446][21419335][24688678][24228917]|"
