     gene            complement(1934570..1935892)
                     /gene="mepM"
                     /locus_tag="b1856"
                     /label=mepM
     CDS             complement(1934570..1935892)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mepM"
                     /locus_tag="b1856"
                     /product="trifunctional peptidoglycan endopeptidase MepM / 
                     peptidoglycan D,D-endopeptidase / peptidoglycan 
                     L,D-endopeptidase"
                     /label=mepM
                     /note="Purified MepM is a dual specificity murein
                     endopeptidase which cleaves both D-ala-m-DAP (4 &rarr; 3) 
                     |CITS: [23062283] | and m-DAP-mDAP (3 &rarr; 3) |CITS: 
                     [34382698]| cross-links with similar efficacy; the 
                     endopeptidase activity of MepM is associated with murein 
                     incorporation during cell growth.
                     MepM is a metalloendopeptidase - activity is dependent on 
                     the presence of a divalent metal ion |CITS: [23062283]|. In
                     addition to promoting cell elongation, MepM influences the 
                     cell division process; elevated activity of the 
                     endopeptidases which promote cell elongation interferes 
                     with the activation of septal PG biogenesis by the divisome
                     |CITS: [32866331]|.
                     An <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strain lacking all three murein 
                     endopeptidases MepM, |FRAME: G7147-MONOMER ""MepS""| and 
                     |FRAME: G6892-MONOMER ""MepH""| exhibits extensive cell 
                     lysis and a terminal phenotype. Incorporation of labelled 
                     mDAP into the peptidoglycan sacculi is decreased by up to 
                     80% in the triple deletion strain. Over-expression of any 
                     of the three genes from a plasmid allows the triple 
                     deletion mutant to grow normally. This suggests that MepM, 
                     MepS and MepH perform essential and redundant functions 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [23062283]|. MepM alone is essential 
                     and sufficient for the insertion of new glycan strands into
                     the wild-type peptidoglycan net; the MepM and |FRAME: 
                     G6474-MONOMER MepK| endopeptidases are minimally required 
                     for bacterial growth in strains engineered to produce 
                     |FRAME: EG11253-MONOMER LdtD|-mediated 3&rarr;3 
                     cross-linked peptidoglycan |CITS: [34382698]|.
                     MepM contains a predicted transmembrane domain, a LysM 
                     (lysin motif) domain (associated with peptidoglycan binding
                     |CITS: [18430080][24978025]|) and a LytM (lysostaphin/M23 
                     peptidase) domain. LytM domains are also present in |FRAME:
                     EG12111-MONOMER NlpD|, |FRAME: EG12287-MONOMER EnvC| and 
                     |FRAME: G7484-MONOMER YgeR| and a strain lacking all 4 LytM
                     domain-containing proteins shows severe defects in septal 
                     peptidoglycan (PG) splitting and forms long cell chains 
                     |CITS: [19525345]|. MepM is predicted to be a membrane 
                     protein with N<sub>in</sub>:C<sub>out</sub> topology |CITS:
                     [19525345]|. A <i>mepM</i> deletion mutant shows impaired 
                     membrane invagination and cytokinesis and reduced 
                     sensitivity to ampicillin |CITS: [22125496]|."
