     gene            complement(1942437..1942889)
                     /gene="nudB"
                     /locus_tag="b1865"
                     /label=nudB
     CDS             complement(1942437..1942889)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nudB"
                     /locus_tag="b1865"
                     /product="bifunctional dihydroneopterin triphosphate
                     diphosphatase / pyrophosphohydrolase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.1.67"
                     /label=nudB
                     /note="Dihydroneopterin triphosphate diphosphatase
                     (DHNTPase) catalyzes the hydrolytic removal of 
                     pyrophosphate from dihydroneopterin triphosphate (DHNTP) to
                     yield dihydroneopterin phosphate.  This is the committed 
                     step in the biosynthesis of folate |CITS: 
                     [4362677][17698004]|.
                     DHNTPase was also shown to have nucleotide 
                     pyrophosphohydrolase activity with various substrates 
                     |CITS: [8798731][15533835]|, but the catalytic efficiency 
                     of the enzyme for DHNTP is over 7 times that for dATP.
                     The severe reduction of folate levels in a <i>nudB</i> null
                     mutant similarly argues that DHNTP is the physiological 
                     substrate of the enzyme |CITS: [17698004]|. Crystal 
                     structures of NudB have been solved |CITS: 
                     [17698004][28742822]|.  The amino acid residues involved in
                     coordinating four metal ions were identified, substrate 
                     binding was modeled |CITS: [28742822]|, and a catalytic 
                     mechanism was proposed |CITS: [17698004][28742822]|.
                     In an effort to engineer novel biosynthetic pathways for 
                     5-carbon alcohols, it was shown that NudB has a low level 
                     of isopentenyl pyrophosphate phosphatase activity <i>in 
                     vitro</i>, producing the desired intermediate 
                     3-methyl-3-butenol |CITS: [22941086]|.  The promisouous 
                     activity of NudB towards geranyl diphosphate and farnesyl 
                     diphosphate has also been utilized for metabolic 
                     engineering purposes |CITS: [30061932]|.  NudB can 
                     dephosphorylate dimethylallyl pyrophosphate <i>in vitro</i>
                     |CITS: [29551348]|.
                     Although |CITS: [17698004]| stated that the growth rate of 
                     a <i>nudB</i> null mutant is wild-type in both M9 minimal 
                     medium and LB, it was later shown that a &Delta;<i>nudB</i>
                     mutant shows a prolonged lag phase in E minimal medium 
                     |CITS: [28223385]|.  <i>nudB</i> null mutant cells contain 
                     significantly reduced levels of folic acid |CITS: 
                     [17698004]|.  A &Delta;<i>nudB</i> mutant is hypersensitive
                     to the antifolate antibiotics sulfamethoxazole (SMX) and 
                     trimethoprim (TMP) |CITS: [28223385]|.
                     Deletion of <i>nudB</i> causes a ""glycogen-excess"" 
                     phenotype during entrance to stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [17543954]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16378245][17697994]|"
                     /translation="MKDKVYKRPVSILVVIYAQDTKRVLMLQRRDDPDFWQSVTGSVEE
