     gene            complement(1943007..1944779)
                     /gene="aspS"
                     /locus_tag="b1866"
                     /label=aspS
     CDS             complement(1943007..1944779)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aspS"
                     /locus_tag="b1866"
                     /product="trifunctional aspartate--tRNA ligase /
                     D-aspartate--tRNA ligase / L-aspartate--tRNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.1.1.12"
                     /label=aspS
                     /note="Aspartate&mdash;tRNA ligase (AspRS) is a member of
                     the family of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, which interpret 
                     the genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their
                     specific tRNA molecules.  The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis.  AspRS belongs to the Class IIb aminoacyl-tRNA 
                     synthetases |CITS: [2203971][1852601]|.
                     The enzyme is a dimer in solution |CITS: [2129559]|. 
                     Crystal structures of AspRS have been determined and allow 
                     modelling of specific interactions with the tRNA and the 
                     reaction mechanism |CITS: [10562565][10873442][11566892]|. 
                     AspRS activity appears to be the target of processed 
                     |FRAME: CPD0-1129|, which is an aspartyl adenylate analog 
                     |CITS: [16574659]|.
                     The <i>tls-1</i> allele of <i>aspS</i> consists of a P555S 
                     mutation in the highly conserved proline residue of motif 
                     3.  It has no significant effect on substrate binding, but 
                     may affect the active site |CITS: [9171418]|.  Specific 
                     interactions of AspRS with tRNA(Asp) were deduced from the 
                     crystal structures and by mutagenesis of the tRNA substrate
                     |CITS: [12649491]|.  The L<sub>45</sub> loop within the 
                     OB-fold domain of AspRS appears to be responsible for 
                     anticodon recognition |CITS: [12766171]|. Mutations that 
                     allow charging of an amber tRNA<sup>Asp</sup> with 
                     aspartate mostly localize to the anticodon binding domain 
                     of AspRS, although some are far from the anticodon binding 
                     domain |CITS: [15289581]|. Amino acid substrate specificity
                     and discrimination by AspRS is complex.  AspRS can 
                     misacylate tRNA<sup>Asp</sup> with D-Asp |CITS: 
                     [10918062][17690095]|.  The misacylated 
                     D-Asp-tRNA<sup>Asp</sup> can be hydrolyzed by |FRAME: 
                     EC-3.1.1.96| |CITS: [10918062]|.  Molecular-dynamics 
                     free-energy simulations have been used to study substrate 
                     specificity |CITS: [9480772][11686711][11237602]|.  They 
                     suggest that a labile proton at His448 is mostly 
                     responsible for discrimination between Asp and Asn, that 
                     ATP acts as a mobile discriminator |CITS: [16408313]|, and 
                     that strongly bound ATP-associated Mg<sup>2+</sup> aids 
                     AspAMP recognition |CITS: [16774919]|.  Mutations in 
                     binding pocket residues have similar effects in 
                     experimental and simulation tests: discrimination between 
                     Asp and Asn is reduced, but not eliminated |CITS: 
                     [18076053]|.
                     There is a general discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [37224536]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8955904][10966471]|."
