                     /note="The periplasmic oxidoreductase TorZ and pentaheme 
                     <I>c</I>-type cytochrome |FRAME: G7023-MONOMER ""TorY""| 
                     constitute an anaerobic respiratory system that can use 
                     several <i>N</i> and <i>S</i>-oxides, including 
                     trimethylamine <i>n-</i>-oxide (TMAO) and biotin sulfoxide 
                     (BSO) as terminal electron acceptors |CITS: [11004177]|. 
                     TorZ (formerly BisZ) is responsible for the small amount of
                     biotin-<i>d</i>-sulfoxide (BDS) reductase activity that is 
                     present in a strain lacking |FRAME: EG10124-MONOMER|; 
                     <i>torZ</i> encodes a putative molybdoenzyme |CITS: 
                     [8919859]|. Overexpression of <i>torYZ</i> allows anaerobic
                     TMAO respiration in a strain 
                     (<i>tor</i>C2::&Omega;Spc<sup>r</sup>&Delta;<i>dms</i> 
                     Km<sup>r</sup>) that otherwise grows extremely slowly with 
                     TMAO as terminal oxidant |CITS: [11004177]|. TMAO and 
                     4-methylmorpholine are good substrates <i>in vitro</i>; 
                     BSO, tetramethylene sulfoxide and DL-methionine sulfoxide 
                     are reduced less efficiently and DMSO acts as a competitive
                     inhibitor |CITS: [11004177]|. TorZ is exported to the 
                     periplasm via the Tat pathway |CITS: [11004177]|. TorZ has 
                     62% identity with the cytoplasmic |FRAME: EG10124-MONOMER 
                     ""BSO reductase""| and 42% identity with |FRAME: 
                     TORA-MONOMER ""TorA""| |CITS: [11004177]|. <i>torYZ</i> 
                     expression is very low and is not induced by TMAO or DMSO 
                     |CITS: [11004177]|."
