     gene            1954319..1956052
                     /gene="argS"
                     /locus_tag="b1876"
                     /label=argS
     CDS             1954319..1956052
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="argS"
                     /locus_tag="b1876"
                     /product="arginine--tRNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.1.1.19"
                     /label=argS
                     /note="Arginine-tRNA ligase (ArgRS) is a member of the
                     family of aminoacyl tRNA synthetases, which interpret the 
                     genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their 
                     specific tRNA molecules.  The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis.  ArgRS belongs to the Class IA aminoacyl tRNA 
                     synthetases; apart from sequence motifs within the active 
                     site, the different enzymes show little similarity in their
                     primary amino acid sequences |CITS: [2203971][7647112]|.
                     The kinetic mechanism of ArgRS has been investigated |CITS:
                     [16427818]|.
                     ArgRS has been crystallized |CITS: [9416614][17061034]|, 
                     and the structure of a complex with L-arginine has been 
                     solved at 2.9 &Aring; resolution, elucidating the 
                     structural and fuctional role of active site residues 
                     |CITS: [24474195]|.  Conformational changes induced by 
                     interactions with its tRNA and arginine substrates have 
                     been studied |CITS: [14672708][12860413][12232610]|, and 
                     specific interations with nucleotide residues in some tRNA 
                     species have been determined |CITS: [11733016]|. 
                     Specificity determinants within tRNA<sup>Arg</sup> that are
                     important for recognition by ArgRS have been identified 
                     |CITS: 
                     [6776488][2688091][2251270][1375736][11733016][22831759]|. 
                     The <i>argS</i> open reading frame uses the unusual 
                     initiation codon GUG and has a codon usage pattern which is
                     typical for highly expressed genes |CITS: [2668891]|. 
                     Several mutations in <i>argS</i> have been described.  The 
                     MA5002 mutant carries an Arg134Ser mutation located close 
                     to the active site of ArgRS, leading to defects in 
                     enzymatic activity and K<sub>m</sub> value for ATP |CITS: 
                     [2183195]|.  The <i>lov-1</i> mutation confers a slow 
                     growth phenotype as well as mecillinam resistance, which 
                     appears to be dependent on the RelA-mediated stringent 
                     response |CITS: [1563353]|.  An <i>argS</i> mutant 
                     conferring novobiocin resistance has been isolated |CITS: 
                     [10217798]|.  Site-directed and random mutagenesis has 
                     enabled identification of a variety of residues that are 
                     important for enzymatic activity |CITS: 
                     [9748544][10460155][12167998][11733016][24474195]|.
                     ArgRS levels are unaffected by the level of arginine in the
                     growth medium |CITS: [770426]|.
                     There is a general discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [37224536]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [GIEGE12][23075299]|"
