     gene            complement(1961305..1961694)
                     /gene="cheY"
                     /locus_tag="b1882"
                     /label=cheY
     CDS             complement(1961305..1961694)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cheY"
                     /locus_tag="b1882"
                     /product="chemotaxis protein CheY"
                     /label=cheY
                     /note="CheY is one of two response regulators in
                     chemotactic two component signaling pathways. CheY 
                     undergoes two covalent modifications - phosphorylation and 
                     acetylation - both modifications influence CheY's 
                     interaction with the cognate sensor kinase |FRAME: 
                     CHEA-CPLX ""CheA""|, the switch protein and target at the 
                     flagellar complex, |FRAME: FLIM-FLAGELLAR-C-RING-SWITCH 
                     ""FliM""| and the phosphatase |FRAME: CHEZ-CPLX ""CheZ""|. 
                     CheY is reversibly phosphorylated (at aspartate residue 57)
                     by the sensor histidine kinase CheA |CITS: 
                     [3313398][3280143][9047301]|. CheY-phosphate is inherently 
                     unstable; CheZ accelerates its dephosphorylation |CITS: 
                     [3280143][2689446]| (see also |CITS:  [14636076]|). CheY 
                     phosphorylation is associated with conformational change 
                     |CITS: [8514749][7929354]|. CheY binds Mg<sup>2+</sup> 
                     which is required for both the phosphorylation and 
                     dephosphorylation reactions |CITS: [2689446][8176739]|. A 
                     number of small molecules including acetyl phosphate, 
                     phosphoramidate, monophosphoimidazole, diphosphoimidazole 
                     and others can serve as phosphodonors for CheY <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [9398221][10029518]|.
                     CheY binds to CheA in ternary signaling complexes 
                     (MCP-CheW-CheA); CheY dissociates from signaling complexes 
                     under conditions which favour its phosporylation; 
                     CheY-phosphate diffuses to the flagellar motor where it 
                     docks and increases the probability of clockwise motor 
                     rotation leading to cell tumbling; phosphorylated CheY has 
                     been described as a 'diffusible tumble signal' |CITS: 
                     [6089173][3546269][3553150][2068106][1737035][8377825]|. 
                     CheY competes with CheB for binding to CheA; 
                     phosphorylation reduces CheY's affinity for CheA |CITS: 
                     [7578071]|. CheY binds to CheZ; phosphorylation increases 
                     the binding of CheY to CheZ and the binding of both forms 
                     of CheY to CheZ is Mg<sup>2+</sup> dependent |CITS: 
                     [8305438][10373376]|. Phosphorylation enhances the 
                     diffusion of CheY and alters its spatial distribution 
                     |CITS: [39710331]|.
                     Genetic studies suggest CheY interacts with proteins of the
                     flagellar switch system |CITS: [1400175]|; CheY binds 
                     directly to FliM; phosphorylation increases the binding of 
                     CheY to FliM |CITS: [8415608][8068685]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [9727015][12232047][12736245]|). FliM-bound CheY-P 
                     interacts with FliN to trigger the switch to clockwise 
                     rotation |CITS: [20439729]| (see also |CITS: [10959841]|). 
                     CheY is reversibly acetylated <i>in vitro</i> by the enzyme
                     acetyl-CoA synthetase (ACS) using various acetyl donors; 
                     CheY autoacetylates <i>in vitro</i> with acetyl-CoA as 
                     donor; ACS-mediated acetylation and autoacetylation of CheY
                     are enhanced by CheZ and inhibited by acetyl phosphate and 
                     CheA; CheY acetylation appears to influence chemotaxis 
                     |CITS: [1390767][9560203][11359578][1 
                     5327942][15327941][16630631]|. CheY is acetylated at 
                     multiple lysine residues (Lys91, 92, 109, 1119, 122 and 
                     126) |CITS: [15327942]|. CheY is highly acetylated in vivo 
                     and evidence suggests that this high level is mainly due to
                     autoacetylation |CITS: [18234227]|. CheY acetylation 
                     reduces its binding affinity to the purified flagellar 
                     motor switch protein FliM |CITS: [20345663]|. The protein 
                     deacetylase CobB deacetylates CheY <i>in vitro</i> and 
                     <i>in vivo</i> and CobB null mutants show reduced response 
                     to chemotactic stimuli; the sites of acetylation and 
                     deacetylation in CheY are lysine 91 and 109 |CITS: 
                     [20345663]| (see also |CITS: [25388160]|.
                     The mechanism of allosteric transition in CheY remains an 
                     open question (see |CITS: 
                     [11353835][17050923][17050920][16475196] 
                     [22727815][23648838]| and also |CITS: 
                     [10698740][11327786][20133571][20133562] 
                     [22498629][22498621][23454041]|). A molecular mechanism for
                     the control of flagellar motor switching comprising three 
                     steps of CheY binding to the switch complex has been 
                     proposed |CITS: [33620739]|.
                     Mutants defective in cheY function are non-chemotactic 
                     'smooth swimmers' |CITS: [353036][6305913]|.
                     CheY has been extensively mutagenized |CITS: 
                     [2404281][8514750][7623663][1390767] [7592655][7578071][76 
                     15544][7552716][8763950][9295320][9030562][10594818][110237
                     87][11278903][12591865][21642453]|. The antigen 43 
                     dependent aggregating phenotype seen in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     strain W3110 (RpoS<sup>+</sup>) grown at 37&deg;C to 
                     mid-exponential phase is severely restricted when 
                     <i>cheY</i> is deleted; the Tsr chemoreceptor is also 
                     important in the aggregation process |CITS: [27687245]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [8381790][7953516][8809743][10068993]|<BR>Related reviews: 
                     |CITS: [8218244]|"
