     gene            complement(1961709..1962758)
                     /gene="cheB"
                     /locus_tag="b1883"
                     /label=cheB
     CDS             complement(1961709..1962758)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cheB"
                     /locus_tag="b1883"
                     /product="protein-glutamate methylesterase/protein
                     glutamine deamidase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.1.61; 3.5.1.44"
                     /label=cheB
                     /note="CheB is one of two response regulators in
                     chemotactic two component signaling pathways; CheB receives
                     a phosphoryl group from the histidine kinase CheA; 
                     CheB-phosphate catalyses the irreversible deamidation of 
                     specific glutamine residues and the hydrolysis of specific 
                     methylated glutamates in the chemoreceptor proteins |FRAME:
                     CPLX0-8103 ""Tsr""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-8102 ""Tar""|, |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-8105 ""Trg""| and |FRAME: CPLX0-8104 ""Tap""|. 
                     Together, the CheB deamidase/methylesterase and the |FRAME:
                     CHER-MONOMER ""CheR""| methyltransferase modulate the level
                     of chemoreceptor methylation in a feedback loop that 
                     enables the cell to adapt to chemotactic stimuli (see 
                     reviews |CITS: [9442881][25834953]|). In the absence of 
                     chemoeffector gradients the adaption enzymes CheR and CheB 
                     promote synchronous rotational switching of flagellar 
                     motors which supports the characteristic run-and-tumble 
                     swimming behaviour |CITS: [36448786]|.
                     <i>cheB</i> mutants exhibit excessive tumbling |CITS: 
                     [353036]| (see also |CITS: [4895809][770453][7045071]|). A 
                     <i>cheB</i> deletion mutant is unable to adapt to 
                     chemotactic stimuli |CITS: [6358193]|.
                     CheB catalyses the demethylation of methylated 
                     chemoreceptor proteins; <i>cheB<sup>-</sup></i> mutants 
                     lack methylesterase activity (ie. they are deficient in 
                     hydrolysing glutamyl-methyl ester residues from 'membrane 
                     bound proteins essential for chemotaxis') |CITS: [358191]|.
                     Methylesterase activity is modulated in response to 
                     chemotactic stimuli; methylesterase activity decreases 
                     following exposure to an attractant stimuli (serine) and 
                     increases when the stimulus is withdrawn |CITS: [6384215]|.
                     The interaction between CheA-phosphate and CheB results in 
                     rapid formation of phosphorylated CheB <i>in vitro</i>; 
                     subsequently the phosphoryl group on CheB is slowly 
                     hydrolysed |CITS: [3280143]|. Phosphotransfer from 
                     CheA-phosphate to CheB activates the methylesterase 
                     activity of CheB in response to negative stimuli (repellent
                     increase or attractant decrease) |CITS: [2188960]|.
                     CheB dependent modification permits additional methylations
                     of chemoreceptor proteins; CheB mediates the enzymatic 
                     deamidation of specific glutamine residues to 
                     (methyl-accepting) glutamic acid in methyl-accepting 
                     chemotaxis proteins |CITS: 
                     [6758950][6304723][6300110][3032955][3041407][2033064]|. 
                     CheB consists of an N-terminal regulatory domain which 
                     contains the site of phosphorylation (Asp56), joined by a 
                     linker region to a C-terminal domain containing the 
                     methylesterase active site; the structural basis for 
                     regulation of methylesterase activity by phosphorylation 
                     has been studied in the <i>Salmonella typhimurium</I> 
                     homolog (see for example |CITS: 
                     [9465023][10850799][11286548][12022879]|).
                     CheB binds to CheA with &micro;M affinity; CheB competes 
                     with CheY for binding to CheA<sub>1-233</sub> |CITS: 
                     [7578071]|. CheB binds to a carboxy-terminal pentapeptide 
                     docking site in the high abundance receptors Tar and Tsr; 
                     presence of the site enhances rates of demethylation by 
                     both activated (phosphorylated) and unactivated CheB; low 
                     abundance receptors (Tap and Trg) lack this sequence and 
                     are less efficiently deamidated and demethylated than high 
                     abundance receptors |CITS: [10485883]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [11435446]|); CheB binds to the isolated pentapeptide with 
                     low affinity (<i>K</i><sub>d</sub> = 130 &micro;M); the 
                     interaction of CheB or phospho-CheB with the receptor 
                     pentapeptide enhances demethylation allosterically; the 
                     interaction may alter the receptor protein (rather than the
                     enzyme) to enhance demethylation |CITS: [12196531]| (see 
                     also |CITS: [16030204][16573695]|). The receptor protein 
                     pentapeptide serves as a high-affinity binding site for the
                     open, enzymatically active form of CheB; the differences in
                     pentapeptide binding kinetics between a (<i>Salmonella</i> 
                     typhmurium) CheB-phospho mimic and low activity 
                     unphosphorylated CheB observed <i>in vitro</i> reflect the 
                     absence of a pentapeptide binding site in the closed 
                     conformation but its presence in the open conformation 
                     |CITS: [34809457]|.
                     High abundance receptors assist low abundance receptors in 
                     adaptional modification (methylation, demethylation and 
                     deamidation) |CITS: [15916610]|
                     "
