     CDS             complement(1962761..1963621)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cheR"
                     /locus_tag="b1884"
                     /product="bifunctional chemotaxis protein
                     methyltransferase / S-adenosyl-L-methionine:glutathione 
                     methyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.80"
                     /label=cheR
                     /note="<i>cheR</i> encodes an
                     S-adenosylmethionine-dependent methyltransferase; CheR 
                     methylates specific glutamate residues in the cytoplasmic 
                     domains of the methyl-accepting chemotaxis proteins (MCPs) 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-8103 ""Tsr""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-8102 ""Tar""|, 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-8105 ""Trg""| and |FRAME: CPLX0-8104 
                     ""Tap""|. Together, the CheR methyltransferase and the 
                     |FRAME: CHEB-MONOMER ""CheB""| deamidase/methylesterase 
                     modulate the level of chemoreceptor methylation in a 
                     feedback loop that enables the cell to adapt to chemotactic
                     stimuli (see reviews |CITS: [9442881][25834953]|). In the 
                     absence of chemoeffector gradients the adaption enzymes 
                     CheR and CheB promote synchronous rotational switching of 
                     flagellar motors which supports the characteristic 
                     run-and-tumble swimming behaviour |CITS: [36448786]|.
                     CheR, from <i>Salmonella typhimurium</i> has been well 
                     characterized and used in experiments examining methylation
                     of <i>E. coli</i> chemoreceptors (see for example |CITS: 
                     [3298235][2061337][1918019][1349019][911544 
                     3][9628482][8664291]|); the <i>S. typhimurium</i> CheR 
                     methyltransferase is expected to be functionally identical 
                     to the <i>E. coli</i> homolog |CITS: [378950]|.
                     Mutants defective in <i>cheR</i> (formerly <i>cheX</i>) 
                     function are non-chemotactic and lack MCP methyltransferase
                     activity |CITS: [322131][365357][365356] 
                     [353036][6782079][6458812][7045071]|."
