     gene            complement(1967093..1967596)
                     /gene="cheW"
                     /locus_tag="b1887"
                     /label=cheW
     CDS             complement(1967093..1967596)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cheW"
                     /locus_tag="b1887"
                     /product="multifunctional chemotaxis protein CheW /
                     chemotaxis signaling complex core unit - 
                     ribose/galactose/glucose sensing / chemotaxis signaling 
                     complex core unit - dipeptide sensing / chemotaxis 
                     signaling complex core unit - serine sensing / chemotaxis 
                     signaling complex core unit - aspartate sensing"
                     /label=cheW
                     /note="CheW is the coupling protein in the ternary
                     receptor complexes of two-component signaling pathways 
                     (chemotaxis, thermotaxis and energy taxis) which respond to
                     environmental cues (both external and internal) and signal 
                     change to the flagellar switch complex to regulate swimming
                     behavior.
                     CheW forms ternary signaling complexes with chemoreceptor 
                     proteins (|FRAME: CPLX0-8103 ""Tsr""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-8102 
                     ""Tar""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-8105 ""Trg""| and |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-8104 ""Tap""|) and the protein histidine kinase 
                     |FRAME: CHEA-CPLX ""CheA""|; ligand binding to the receptor
                     proteins generates a signal that is transmitted across the 
                     inner membrane to regulate CheA histidine kinase activity 
                     and the subsequent flow of phosphoryl groups to the 
                     response regulators |FRAME: CHEY-MONOMER ""CheY""| - whose 
                     phosphorylation status regulates the direction of flagellar
                     motor rotation - and |FRAME: CHEB-MONOMER ""CheB""| - a 
                     protein glutamate methylesterase which modulates the 
                     sensory adaption system (see reviews |CITS: 
                     [9442881][25834953]|). CheW also assembles into 
                     higher-order complexes with the receptor |FRAME: 
                     G7595-MONOMER ""Aer""| and CheA to mediate the aerotaxis 
                     (oxygen-sensing) response |CITS: [27803157]|. CheW is 
                     required for the chemotactic response to PTS sugars |CITS: 
                     [7592359][10198062]| (see also |CITS: [22778402]|).
                     Purified CheW and CheA form a reversible complex <i>in 
                     vitro</i> with a stoichiometry of two CheW monomers per 
                     CheA dimer |CITS: [1992467]| (see also |CITS: [1326408]|). 
                     CheW forms a stable ternary complex with CheA and receptor 
                     protein (Tsr or Tar); autophosphorylation of CheA is 
                     stimulated in the presence of wild-type (unliganded) 
                     receptor (Tar/Tsr) and CheW protein; addition of ligand 
                     (aspartate/serine) inhibits formation of CheA-phosphate; 
                     addition of CheY and CheB results in the disappearance of 
                     CheA-phosphate |CITS: [2645576][2261645][1326408]|. |FRAME:
                     CHEA-CPLX ""CheAL""|/|FRAME: CHEA-SMALL ""CheAS""|/CheW 
                     complexes can be isolated from wild-type cells; CheW 
                     enhances CheA binding to CheY and increases the 
                     autophosphorylation rate of CheA by increasing its affinity
                     for ATP |CITS: [2068106]|. CheW enhances Tsr methylation 
                     <i>in vitro</i>; serine and CheW promote synergistic 
                     increases in Tsr methylation <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [15766389]|.
                     Excess levels of CheW disrupt CheA activation and 
                     chemotactic response |CITS: [2681160][11923283]| possibly 
                     by disrupting the normal formation of receptor complexes 
                     |CITS: [16230637][20487303]|.
                     CheW interacts with chemoreceptors in a manner similar to 
                     CheA; CheW and CheA bind to the same region of 
                     chemoreceptors due to structural similarity between CheW 
                     and the regulatory or P5 domain of CheA |CITS: 
                     [22659323][25060668]|. CheW adopts an overall fold similar 
                     to <i>Thermotoga maritima</i> CheW; the two proteins have 
                     29% sequence identity |CITS: [17631272]|.
                     Early work in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 characterized <i>cheW</i>
                     mutants, all of which were motile, nonchemotactic, smooth 
                     swimmers |CITS: [353036][770453][324984][336614]| (see also
                     |CITS: [3553150]|.
                     <i>cheW</i> was found upregulated in two MG1655 lysogens 
                     carrying closely related Stx2a phages O104 and PA8 |CITS: 
                     31208335|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [30642465]|"
