                     /label=cheA
     CDS             complement(1967617..1969581)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cheA"
                     /locus_tag="b1888"
                     /product="multifunctional chemotaxis protein CheA /
                     chemotaxis protein CheA(L) / chemotaxis signaling complex 
                     core unit - ribose/galactose/glucose sensing / chemotaxis 
                     signaling complex core unit - dipeptide sensing / 
                     chemotaxis signaling complex core unit - serine sensing / 
                     chemotaxis signaling complex core unit - aspartate sensing"
                     /label=cheA
                     /note="CheA is the histidine protein kinase of
                     two-component signaling pathways (chemotaxis, thermotaxis 
                     and energy taxis) which respond to environmental cues and 
                     signal change to the flagellar switch complex to regulate 
                     swimming behavior.
                     CheA forms ternary signaling complexes with chemoreceptor 
                     proteins (|FRAME: CPLX0-8103 ""Tsr""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-8102 
                     ""Tar""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-8105 ""Trg""| and |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-8104 ""Tap""|) and the coupling protein |FRAME: 
                     CHEW-MONOMER ""CheW""|; ligand binding to the receptor 
                     proteins generates a signal that is transmitted across the 
                     inner membrane to regulate CheA histidine kinase activity 
                     and the subsequent flow of phosphoryl groups to the 
                     response regulators |FRAME: CHEY-MONOMER ""CheY""| - whose 
                     phosphorylation status regulates the direction of flagellar
                     motor rotation - and |FRAME: CHEB-MONOMER ""CheB""| - a 
                     protein glutamate methylesterase which modulates the 
                     sensory adaption system (see reviews |CITS: 
                     [9442881][25834953]|). CheA also assembles into 
                     higher-order complexes with the receptor |FRAME: 
                     G7595-MONOMER ""Aer""| and CheW; Aer reversibly regulates 
                     CheA activity based on FAD redox state |CITS: [27803157]|. 
                     The interaction of CheA with |FRAME: CPLX0-7938 ""PtsI""| 
                     is also implicated in the chemotactic response to PTS* 
                     sugars |CITS: [8524808][10198062]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [22778402]|)
                     Purified CheA autophosphorylates on His48 in the presence 
                     of ATP and MgCl<sub>2</sub> and the phosphate group can be 
                     transferred to CheB or to CheY <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [3313398][3185734][3280143]| (see also |CITS: [9020767]|). 
                     CheA is a dimer in solution; purified CheA and CheW form a 
                     reversible complex <i>in vitro</i> with a stoichiometry of 
                     two CheW monomers per CheA dimer |CITS: [1992467]| (see 
                     also |CITS: [1326408]|). CheA catalyses a 
                     <i>trans</i>-phosphorylation reaction, that is one subunit 
                     within the dimer binds ATP and transfers a phosphate group 
                     to the other subunit |CITS: [8326858]|. CheA forms a stable
                     ternary complex with CheW and receptor protein (Tsr or 
                     Tar); autophosphorylation of CheA is stimulated in the 
                     presence of wild-type (unliganded) receptor (Tar/Tsr) and 
                     CheW protein; addition of ligand (aspartate/serine) 
                     inhibits formation of CheA-phosphate; addition of CheY and 
                     CheB results in the disappearance of CheA-phosphate |CITS: 
                     [2645576][2261645][1326408]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> expresses CheA as both a full length 
                     molecule (CheAL) and as a shorter version translated from 
                     an alternative start codon - |FRAME: CHEA-SMALL ""CheAS""|;
                     CheAS lacks the N-terminal 97 amino acids of CheAL 
                     including the site of autophosphorylation |CITS: 
                     [6449010][2002011]|. CheAL/CheAS/CheW complexes can be 
                     isolated from wild-type cells and may represent a minimal 
                     functional unit <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [2068106]|.
                     CheA is a modular protein and a variety of studies have 
                     delineated and characterized functional domains associated 
                     with phosphotransfer (P1), CheY binding (P2), dimerization 
                     (P3), ATP binding/kinase (P4) and regulation/receptor and 
                     CheW binding (P5) |CITS: [8347572][8384620][8202513][75779 
                     80][8555213][11052668][15090507][14741223][21642453][224943
                     39][22824284][24444349][29483161]|. CheA interacts with 
                     chemoreceptors in a manner similar to CheW; CheA and CheW 
                     bind to the same region of chemoreceptors due to structural
                     similarity between CheW and the regulatory or P5 domain of 
                     CheA |CITS: [22659323][25060668]|.
                     CheA and CheY exist in equilibrium between multiple states:
                     the free state; the P1-CheY unbound state, the P1-CheY 
                     bound state and the doubly bound state (ie a CheY molecule 
                     bound to each of the P1 and P2 domains of CheA). The 
                     dynamic domain arrangement of the CheA-CheY complex 
                     exhibits temperature dependent population shift which 
                     induces changes in the concentration of CheY-phosphate 
                     suggesting that the complex functions as the primary 
                     temperature sensor for thermotaxis |CITS: [29184123]|. 
                     Early work in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 characterized <i>cheA</i>
                     mutants, all of which were motile, nonchemotactic, smooth 
                     swimmers |CITS: [770453][324984][336614][353036]|.
                     * phosphoenolpyruvate dependent carbohydrate 
                     phosphotransferase system"
