     gene            complement(1969586..1970512)
                     /gene="motB"
                     /locus_tag="b1889"
                     /label=motB
     CDS             complement(1969586..1970512)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="motB"
                     /locus_tag="b1889"
                     /product="trifunctional motility protein B / flagellar
                     basal body / flagellum"
                     /label=motB
                     /note="MotB and |FRAME:
                     MOTA-FLAGELLAR-MOTOR-STATOR-PROTEIN MotA| comprise the 
                     stator element of the flagellar motor complex. MotAB 
                     complexes are anchored in the peptidoglycan and form a 
                     proton conducting channel across the inner membrane; 
                     movement of protons down their concentration gradient via 
                     the stator provides the energy for torque generation in the
                     flagellar motor.
                     Early studies in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 derived strains 
                     characterized <i>mot</i> mutants which are paralysed (cells
                     possess flagella but are non-motile) and defined the 
                     'Mocha' operon which includes the <i>motA, motB</i> and 
                     <i>cheA</i> cistrons |CITS: [4861166][4567134][4583202 
                     ][4587615][794740][794876][787985][324984][336614]|. 
                     Inducing expression of cloned, wild-type <i>motB</i> 
                     restores torque to paralysed <i>motB</i> mutants |CITS: 
                     [6374467][2849208]|
                     <i>motA</i> and <i>motB</i> gene products are integral 
                     inner membrane proteins |CITS:[334749]| which can be 
                     visualized, using freeze-fracture electron micrscopy, as 
                     intramembrane particle ring structures arrayed around the 
                     flagellar base |CITS:[3050128]|. MotB is predicted to have 
                     a single transmembrane domain with the majority of the 
                     molecule located in the periplasm |CITS: 
                     [3007435][2447650]|. The C-terminal region of MotB may be 
                     important for interaction with the peptidoglycan |CITS: 
                     [8057857][8577259]|. MotB interacts with MotA |CITS: 
                     [7892209][8627625][8892808][7619816][8757288]| to form a 
                     complex with probable stoichiometry 
                     MotA<sub>4</sub>MotB<sub>2</sub>* |CITS: [14705928]| (see 
                     also |CITS: [14705929][18834143]|). Individual flagella 
                     motors contain a maximum of 11 stator units |CITS: 
                     [16698936]|; stator units are not fixed in place but 
                     undergo exchange between motors and a membrane bound pool 
                     |CITS: [16971952][23216828][23818629]| (and see |CITS: 
                     [26457925][30906857][33876769]|).  A short segment in the 
                     periplasmic domain of MotB may act as a plug to block 
                     proton movement through MotAB channels not associated with 
                     flagellar basal bodies |CITS: [17052729]|.  An atomic model
                     structure of the transmembrane region of the stator has 
                     been constructed |CITS: [26056313][28389950]|. 
                     Overexpression of <i>motB</i> from an ASKA plasmid leads to
                     a more than 3-fold increase in the MIC of hypochlorous acid
                     |CITS: [34072091]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [30069936]|
                     * later work suggests a conserved arrangement of 
                     MotA<sub>5</sub>MotB<sub>2</sub> is present across all 
                     types of stator units (see |CITS: [34294545]|)"
