     gene            complement(1970509..1971396)
                     /gene="motA"
                     /locus_tag="b1890"
                     /label=motA
     CDS             complement(1970509..1971396)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="motA"
                     /locus_tag="b1890"
                     /product="trifunctional motility protein A / flagellar
                     basal body / flagellum"
                     /label=motA
                     /note="MotA and |FRAME:
                     MOTB-FLAGELLAR-MOTOR-STATOR-PROTEIN MotB| comprise the 
                     stator unit of the flagellar motor complex; MotAB complexes
                     are anchored in the peptidoglycan and form a proton 
                     conducting channel across the inner membrane; movement of 
                     protons down their concentration gradient via the stator 
                     provides the energy for torque generation in the flagellar 
                     motor.
                     Early studies in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 derived strains 
                     characterized <i>mot</i> mutants which are paralysed (cells
                     possess flagella but are non-motile) and defined the 
                     'Mocha' operon which includes the <i>motA, motB</i> and 
                     <i>cheA</i> cistrons |CITS: [4861166][4567134][4583202 
                     ][4587615][794740][794876][787985][324984][336614]|. 
                     Inducing expression of cloned, wild-type <i>motA</i> 
                     restores torque to paralysed <i>motA</i> mutants |CITS: 
                     [2849208]|.
                     <i>motA</i> and <i>motB</i> gene products are integral 
                     inner membrane proteins |CITS:[334749]| which can be 
                     visualized, using freeze-fracture electron micrscopy, as 
                     intramembrane particle ring structures arrayed around the 
                     flagellar base |CITS:[3050128]|. MotA contains four 
                     membrane-spanning alpha-helical segments and two small 
                     periplasmic loops with the bulk of the protein located in 
                     the cytoplasm |CITS: [6090403][1719217][7643400]|. MotA 
                     interacts with MotB |CITS: 
                     [7892209][8627625][8892808][7619816][8757288]| to form a 
                     complex with probable stoichiometry 
                     MotA<sub>4</sub>MotB<sub>2</sub>* |CITS: [14705928]| (see 
                     also |CITS: [14705929][18834143]|). Individual flagella 
                     motors contain a maximum of 11 stator units |CITS: 
                     [16698936]|; stator units are not fixed in place but 
                     undergo exchange between motors and a membrane bound pool 
                     |CITS: [16971952][23216828][23818629]| (and see |CITS: 
                     [26457925][30906857][33876769]|). An atomic model structure
                     of the transmembrane region of the stator has been 
                     constructed |CITS: [26056313][28389950]|.
                     Interaction between MotA and FliG is important for motor 
                     function |CITS: [9344750][9600984]|; MotA interacts with 
                     the |FRAME: G6623-MONOMER| |CITS: [20303158]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [20346719][31836667][37125196]|).
                     The 'pole-localizer' protein |FRAME: G7087-MONOMER TmaR| 
                     protects and stabilizes <i>motA</i> transcripts |CITS: 
                     [37934665]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [30069936]|
                     * later work suggests a conserved arrangement of 
                     MotA<sub>5</sub>MotB<sub>2</sub> is present across all 
                     types of stator units (see |CITS: [34294545][31836667]|). 
                     MotAB is a heteroheptameric 5:2 rotary motor (see |CITS: 
                     [35992645]|)."
