     gene            complement(1981324..1981482)
                     /gene="azuR"
                     /locus_tag="b4434"
                     /label=azuR
     misc_RNA        complement(1981324..1981482)
                     /gene="azuR"
                     /locus_tag="b4434"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA AzuR"
                     /note="AzuR is a small base-pairing regulatory RNA that 
                     functions to repress expression of |FRAME: EG10131 
                     <i>cadA</i>|, encoding lysine decarboxylase, and |FRAME: 
                     EG10362 <i>galE</i>|, the first gene in the <i>galETKM</i> 
                     galactose operon. The AzuR base-pairing region (coordinates
                     1,987,920 - 1,987,890) is predicted to bind near the 
                     ribosome binding site of <i>cadA</i> mRNA and to bind 
                     internal to the <i>galE</i> coding sequence |CITS: 
                     [35239434]|.
                     AzuR is found on a transcription unit (TU) referred to as 
                     AzuCR, as the TU also contains the protein coding region 
                     for |FRAME: MONOMER0-2880 AzuC| |CITS: 
                     [19121005][19734316]|. AzuCR transcript is a dual-function 
                     RNA which has both functional sRNA activity and mRNA 
                     activity. The AzuR base-pair binding region and AzuC coding
                     sequence physically overlap and compete; that is, the mRNA 
                     and base-pairing activities of the AzuCR RNA interfere with
                     each other. The competing activities of AzuC mRNA and AzuR 
                     impact the utilization of different carbon sources |CITS: 
                     [35239434]|.
                     AzuR binds the RNA chaperones |FRAME: EG12866-MONOMER ProQ|
                     and |FRAME: CPLX0-1461 Hfq|; the stability and activity of 
                     AzuR sRNA appears to depend on ProQ and to a lesser extent 
                     on Hfq |CITS: [35239434]|.
                     The AzuCR transcript (candidate name IS092) was identified 
                     using a bioinformatics-based approach by |CITS: 
                     [12069726]|.
                     Single mutation of <i>azuR</i> slightly enhances cell 
                     growth when glucosamine or ribose is used as a sole carbon 
                     source  |CITS: [35920556]|."
     gene            complement(1981354..1981440)
                     /gene="azuC"
                     /locus_tag="b4663"
                     /label=azuC
     CDS             complement(1981354..1981440)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="azuC"
                     /locus_tag="b4663"
                     /product="small regulatory membrane protein AzuC"
                     /label=azuC
                     /note="The AzuC coding sequence lies within an RNA
                     initially called |FRAME: G0-17112 IsrB| |CITS: [12069726]| 
                     and later AzuCR |CITS: [35239434]|. AzuCR is a 
                     dual-function RNA which can also act as the |FRAME: 
                     RNA0-430 AzuR| regulatory small RNA. The AzuC coding 
                     sequence overlaps the AzuR base-pairing region; AzuC and 
                     AzuR activities compete, that is, the mRNA and base-pairing
                     activities of the AzuCR RNA interfere with each other 
                     |CITS: [35239434]|.
                     AzuC interacts with |FRAME: AERGLYC3PDEHYDROG-MONOMER| and 
                     increases dehydrogenase activity; the physiological 
                     relevance of this is uncertain but it may serve to modulate
                     membrane composition |CITS: [35239434]|.
                     AzuC is predicted to form an amphipathic helix; the protein
                     localizes to the inner membrane |CITS: 
                     [19121005][35239434]|.
                     AzuC levels (detected as the sequential peptide affinity 
                     (SPA)-tagged protein) are higher in cells grown with 
                     glucose compared to those grown with glycerol or galactose 
                     due to cAMP receptor protein (CRP)-mediated repression 
                     |CITS: [19734316][35239434]|; AzuC-SPA levels are also 
                     repressed under low-oxygen conditions, are moderately 
                     induced during heat shock, oxidative stress, and thiol 
                     stress, and are substantially increased under acidic (pH 
                     5.5) conditions |CITS: [19734316]|. A discordance between 
                     AzuC-SPA protein level (high) and AzuCR RNA level (low) is 
                     observed when cells are grown to stationary phase in 
                     glycerol at pH 5.5 |CITS: [35239434]|.
                     AzuC repression under anaerobic growth is mediated by the 
                     Hfq-dependent, |FRAME: RNA0-349|, which base pairs near the
                     AzuC ribosome binding site |CITS: [35239434]|."
                     /translation="MKLRKILKSMFNNYCKTFKDVPPGNMFR"
                     /go_component="plasma membrane [goid 0005886] [evidence
                     IEA] [GO_REF 0000044]"
                     /go_component="plasma membrane [goid 0005886] [evidence
                     IDA] [PMID 21778229]"
                     /go_function="protein binding [goid 0005515] [evidence
                     IPI] [PMID 35239434]"
                     /go_function="enzyme activator activity [goid 0008047]
                     [evidence IDA] [PMID 35239434]"
     gene            1981703..1982026
                     /gene="yecR"
                     /locus_tag="b1904"
                     /label=yecR
     CDS             1981703..1982026
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yecR"
                     /locus_tag="b1904"
                     /product="lipoprotein YecR"
                     /label=yecR
                     /note="YecR is a verified lipoprotein according to
                     unpublished data by S. Matsuyama et al. (cited in |CITS: 
                     [12876315]|).  Expression of <i>yecR</i> was shown to be 
                     regulated by the FlhDC flagellar transcriptional regulator 
                     |CITS: [15941987]|.
                     "
                     /translation="MRLLILTLSLITLAGCTVTRQAHVSEVDAATGIVRLVYDQAFLQH
                     AHTDRYVSRGIADRACQQEGYTHAVPFGQPVGNCSLFAGSLCLNTEFTLSYQCHHSAFP
                     VFL"
                     /go_component="cytosol [goid 0005829] [evidence IEA] [PMID
                     19054114]"
     gene            1982197..1982694
                     /gene="ftnA"
                     /locus_tag="b1905"
                     /label=ftnA
     CDS             1982197..1982694
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftnA"
                     /locus_tag="b1905"
                     /product="trifunctional ferritin iron storage protein /
                     ferritin iron-storage complex / non-heme bacterial 
                     ferritin"
                     /EC_number="1.16.3.2"
                     /label=ftnA
                     /note="<i>ftnA</i> encodes the iron-storage protein
                     ferritin, which is similar to human ferritin H |CITS: 
                     [2017145][8281950]|. Ferritin forms a multi-subunit, hollow
                     spherical shell of 24 individual ferritin polypeptides that
                     can sequester more than 2000 iron atoms in the center 
                     |CITS: [8281950]|. Ferritin and |FRAME: CPLX0-1421| are 
                     distantly related |CITS: [1959654][1431878]|.
                     The crystal structure of ferritin has been determined, 
                     identifying three iron-binding sites per subunit |CITS: 
                     [8070575][11254384][12730190]|. Two of these sites form a 
                     dinuclear iron center where oxidation of Fe(II) occurs, 
                     allowing iron to be stored as ferric phosphate |CITS: 
                     [9720927][16229472]|. The third site appears to modulate 
                     Fe<sup>2+</sup> binding to the dinuclear iron center |CITS:
                     [16229472]|. No protons are released upon initial binding 
                     of Fe<sup>2+</sup>. Subsequent oxidation of Fe<sup>2+</sup>
                     leads to production of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, which is
                     itself utilized for oxidation of Fe<sup>2+</sup> bound at 
                     other ferroxidase centers |CITS: [24380371]|. The iron core
                     forms a hollow sphere in the presence of phosphate |CITS: 
                     [35986810]|. <i>In vitro</i>, FtnA was shown to sequester 
                     iron released from damaged [2Fe-2S] clusters of IscU. Iron 
                     sequestered by FtnA can be transferred to IscA, but not 
                     IscU |CITS: [18618270]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>ftnA</i> rescues the oxygen 
                     sensitivity of a &Delta;<i>fur</i> &Delta;<i>recA</i> 
                     mutant, presumably by preventing toxic iron accumulation 
                     |CITS: [7730258]|. An <i>ftnA</i> mutant shows impaired 
                     growth in iron-deficient media |CITS:[10049371]|. 
                     Expression of <i>ftnA</i> is higher in stationary phase 
                     than log phase cells and is dependent on iron and Fur 
                     |CITS: [20015147]|. Although <i>ftnA</i> expression was 
                     thought to be regulated by RyhB |CITS: [11917098]|, 
                     microarray experiments indicated that it is not dependent 
                     on RyhB |CITS: [16199566]|. It was later shown that 
                     <i>ftnA</i> expression is directly regulated by Fur, which 
                     reverses silencing by H-NS |CITS: [20015147]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12829269][31069627]|"
                     /translation="MLKPEMIEKLNEQMNLELYSSLLYQQMSAWCSYHTFEGAAAFLRR
                     HAQEEMTHMQRLFDYLTDTGNLPRINTVESPFAEYSSLDELFQETYKHEQLITQKINEL
                     AHAAMTNQDYPTFNFLQWYVSEQHEEEKLFKSIIDKLSLAGKSGEGLYFIDKELSTLDT
                     QN"
