     gene            complement(1988184..1988840)
                     /gene="uvrY"
                     /locus_tag="b1914"
                     /label=uvrY
     CDS             complement(1988184..1988840)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="uvrY"
                     /locus_tag="b1914"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional activator UvrY"
                     /label=uvrY
                     /note="UvrY, which belongs to the LuxR/UhpA family, is the 
                     transcriptional regulator of the |FRAME: EG11367 
                     BarA|/|FRAME: EG11140 UvrY| two-component signal 
                     transduction system which plays a role in central carbon 
                     metabolism via its regulation of the small non-coding RNAs 
                     |FRAME: CSRC-RNA ""CsrC| and |FRAME: CSRB-RNA ""CsrB""|. 
                     BarA senses and responds to the presence of the protonated 
                     form of short-chain carboxylic acids such as |FRAME: 
                     FORMATE ""formic acid""| and |FRAME: ACET ""acetic acid""|,
                     which accumulate at the late exponential phase of growth on
                     acetogenic substrates such as glucose. Upon sensing of 
                     these products, BarA autophosphorylates, followed by 
                     transphosphorylation of UvrY at the conserved Asp54 residue
                     |CITS: [33318202]|. Phosphorylated UvrY activates 
                     transcription of the |FRAME: G0-8785| and |FRAME: G0-8874| 
                     noncoding regulatory RNAs |CITS: [34220787]|. These RNAs 
                     bind to the RNA-binding protein |FRAME: CPLX0-7956 CsrA|, 
                     preventing it from interacting with its mRNA targets. CsrA 
                     is a regulator of carbohydrate metabolism that affects 
                     glycogen biosynthesis |CITS: [7493933]|, glycogen 
                     degradation |CITS: [8576033]|, gluconeogenesis |CITS: 
                     [7493933]|, and glycolysis |CITS: [7493933]|, and is also 
                     involved in biofilm dynamics |CITS: [11741870]| and 
                     motility |CITS: [11298291]|.
                     UvrY requires the presence of IHF for binding to |FRAME: 
                     G0-8785| but not |FRAME: G0-8874| |CITS:[ 26673755]|. Z 
                     nucleotides (|FRAME: AICAR| and/or |FRAME: CPD-26637|) can 
                     act as direct phosphodonors to UvrY <i>in vivo</i>, 
                     activating it in a BarA-independent manner |CITS: 
                     [33318202]|.
                     Inhibition of <i>uvrY</i> expression by CRISPRi enhanced 
                     cellular fitness under treatment with the antibiotic 
                     polymyxin B |CITS:[40352728]|.
                     The UvrY binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
