     gene            complement(1994551..1995270)
                     /gene="fliA"
                     /locus_tag="b1922"
                     /label=fliA
     CDS             complement(1994551..1995270)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fliA"
                     /locus_tag="b1922"
                     /product="bifunctional RNA polymerase sigma factor FliA /
                     RNA polymerase sigma 28"
                     /label=fliA
                     /note="&sigma;<sup>28</sup> is a minor sigma factor that
                     is responsible for initiation of transcription of a number 
                     of genes involved in motility and flagellar synthesis 
                     |CITS: [3536871][2644646][7590326][8866483]|. 
                     &sigma;<sup>28</sup> competes with &sigma;<sup>70</sup> for
                     |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX|, binding to it more tightly than 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> |CITS: [9209042]|.
                     A distinguishing feature of &sigma;<sup>28</sup> promoters 
                     is a long -10 region (GCCGATAA); in addition, its upstream 
                     GC is highly conserved |CITS: [19400790]|. Thus, 
                     &sigma;<sup>28</sup> not only uses extended -10 recognition
                     but also requires three recognition regions, -35, extended 
                     -10, and -10, for successful utilization of the core 
                     &sigma;<sup>28</sup> promoter |CITS: [19400790]|. Both GC 
                     and the CG motifs are functionally important in the -10 
                     region of &sigma;<sup>28</sup>, and this is a composite 
                     element. Based on mutational and biochemical analyses, it 
                     was shown that the upstream GC constitutes an extended -10 
                     motif and is recognized by R91 (which recognizes both 14G 
                     and 13C), a residue in domain 3 of &sigma;<sup>28</sup>. 
                     The downstream CG is the upstream edge of the -10 region of
                     the promoter; two residues in region 2.4 of domain 2, D81 
                     (which recognizes 11G) and R84 (which recognizes 12C), 
                     participate in its recognition |CITS: [19400790]|.
                     R91, D81, and R84 are universally conserved in 
                     &sigma;<sup>28</sup> orthologues |CITS: [19400790]|.
                     The dependence of &sigma;<sup>28</sup> on promoter sequence
                     has been tested using the <i>Chlamydia trachomatis</i> 
                     <i>hctB</i> promoter |CITS: [16855242]|. 
                     &sigma;<sup>28</sup> is required for flagellin production 
                     |CITS: [6993282][6374380]|. The structural consequences of 
                     <i>fliA</i> mutation have been examined via electron 
                     microscopy |CITS: [7007337]|.
                     &sigma;<sup>28</sup> is half as abundant as 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> under most growth conditions, but 
                     drops in abundance following heat shock |CITS: [8808934]|. 
                     Biofilm formation depends on the level of <i>fliA</i> 
                     expression |CITS: [16317765][16397770]|.
                     A global antagonistic effect on gene expression was 
                     perceived between &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, 
                     &sigma;<sup>54</sup>, and &sigma;<sup>28</sup>, based on 
                     transcriptome expression analysis, and this resulted in 
                     many physiological traits, including flagellum-mediated 
                     motility and utilization of nitrogen sources; as many as 
                     60% of genes in the RpoN regulon are under reciprocal RpoS 
                     control |CITS: [21219458]|. In addition, there is a complex
                     regulatory interaction of the three &sigma; factors: 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, &sigma;<sup>54</sup>, and 
                     &sigma;<sup>28</sup> |CITS: [21219458]|.
                     The FliA regulon was comprehensively mapped using ChIP-seq 
                     and RNA-seq |CITS: [25275371][26379184]|.
                     Based on transcriptional fusion of intragenic binding sites
                     of FliA (&sigma;<sup>28</sup>) previously identified by 
                     ChIP-seq |CITS: [25275371]|, it was determined that the 
                     most intragenic FliA binding sites are active promoters 
                     that transcribe highly unstable RNAs, and the genetic 
                     locations of intragenic FliA promoters are generally not 
                     conserved. FliA promoters are not conserved across species 
                     nor across <i>Escherichia coli</i> strains |CITS: 
                     [29476659]|.
                     FliA appears not be able to induce pauses during 
                     transcription elongation in vitro |CITS: [31228934]|. 
                     Deletion of the &sigma; finger of FliA, formed by region 
                     3.2, decreased the overall RNAP holoenzyme activity <i>in 
                     vitro</i>, increasing the stability of the promoter complex
                     |CITS: [33573428]|. However, this deletion makes the 
                     promoter complex less sensitive to the stringent response 
                     factor DksA in presence of ppGpp |CITS: [33573428]|.
                     It was reported that RNAP holoenzyme containing FliA 
                     &sigma; factor can incorporate NADH during transcription 
                     initiation |CITS: [33573428]|.
                     <i>fliA</i> gene expression was upregulated in a mutant 
                     deficient for the |FRAME:EG11495-MONOMER| HdeD 
                     |CITS:[34694904]|.
                     The 'pole-localizer' protein |FRAME: G7087-MONOMER TmaR| 
                     protects and stabilizes <i>fliA</i> transcripts |CITS: 
                     [37934665]|.
                     Under oscillating glucose conditions in high cell-density 
                     culture experiments, the transcriptional responses of 
                     knockout strains of three transcriptional factors sensitive
                     to glucose starvation, |FRAME: EG10338|, |FRAME: EG10164|, 
                     and |FRAME: EG10510|, were more sensitive and severe than 
                     knockout strains of two other glucose-sensitive 
                     transcriptional factors, |FRAME: EG11355| and |FRAME: 
                     G6195|, compared to a control strain; all strains had a 
                     &Delta;<i>|FRAME: EG10302|</i> background |CITS: 
                     [39285935]|. A systematic study of &Delta;<i>fliA</i> 
                     strain for biomanufacturing found it produced best in a 
                     mineral media whereas &Delta;<i>|FRAME: EG10896|</i> strain
                     performed better under a variety of other conditions |CITS:
                     [39994955]|.
                     The antimicrobial plantaricin BM-1 caused significant 
                     reduction in growth in a &Delta;<i>fliA</i> strain |CITS: 
                     [40106189]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [19400790][28904250]|"
