     gene            1999637..2001124
                     /gene="amyA"
                     /locus_tag="b1927"
                     /label=amyA
     CDS             1999637..2001124
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="amyA"
                     /locus_tag="b1927"
                     /product="alpha-amylase"
                     /EC_number="3.2.1.1"
                     /label=amyA
                     /note="AmyA is a cytoplasmic &alpha;-amylase.  |FRAME: 
                     Alpha-Amyloses &alpha;-Amylose|, a linear &alpha;-glucan, 
                     is the most effective substrate <i>in vitro</i>; |FRAME: 
                     Starch starch| and |FRAME: CPD-7043 amylopectin|, a lightly
                     branched &alpha;-glucan, can serve as good substrates, 
                     while the highly branched &alpha;-glucan |FRAME: Glycogens
                     glycogen| is a poor substrate.  Linear oligomeric 
                     &alpha;-glucans of six or more glucose moieties are good 
                     substrates of AmyA |CITS: [1400215]|.
                     The physiological role of AmyA is uncertain.  Although 
                     under experimental conditions glycogen is a poor substrate 
                     for the enzyme, it is the most likely natural substrate 
                     since it is the only polysaccharide present in appreciable 
                     amounts in the cytoplasm.  It has been hypothesized that in
                     the absence of exogenous oligosaccharides that could act as
                     primers for glycogen synthesis, the cytoplasmic amylase 
                     might provide oligosaccharides through catabolism of 
                     existing cellular glycogen, which would then act as the 
                     source of primers for the synthesis of more molecules of 
                     glycogen |CITS: [1400215]|.
                     AmyA is one of two &alpha;-amylases present in <i>E. 
                     coli</i>.  The second enzyme, |FRAME: 
                     ALPHA-AMYL-PERI-MONOMER ""MalS""|, is periplasmic."
