     gene            2006710..2008368
                     /gene="fliF"
                     /locus_tag="b1938"
                     /label=fliF
     CDS             2006710..2008368
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fliF"
                     /locus_tag="b1938"
                     /product="trifunctional flagellar basal-body MS-ring and
                     collar protein / flagellar basal body / flagellum"
                     /label=fliF
                     /note="<i>fliF</i> encodes a flagellar protein that
                     polymerizes to form the MS (membrane/supramembrane) ring of
                     the flagellar basal body; together with the C (cytoplasmic 
                     ring) consisting of FliG, FliM and FliN proteins, the MS-C 
                     ring complex functions as the reversible rotor of the 
                     flagella motor. FliF is widely studied in <i>Salmonella</i>
                     typhimurium however the characterization is considered to 
                     apply to the homologous protein in <i>E. coli</i>.<BR>The 
                     two proteins are 85% identitical over their entire length 
                     |CITS: [26925337]|. In <i>Salmonella</i> typhimurium, the 
                     MS ring is an inner membrane structure consisting of 
                     approximately 26 subunits of FliF; the MS ring is the base 
                     of the flagella structure and serves as the 'housing' for 
                     the protein export apparatus that is required for 
                     construction of the flagella; at its cytoplasmic face the 
                     MS ring interacts with the upper C ring protein FliG and 
                     this interface is an essential component of flagella motor 
                     function |CITS: [ 
                     2982790][3313394][2544561][1404383][2181149][1594581][16404
                     58][8107139][8568890][9006037][10049798][11591685][11160096
                     ][15001355][24450479]|. An <i>E. coli</i> K-12, FliF-yellow
                     fluorescent protein fusion displays 'motor-like 
                     localization' with up to 10 discrete foci distributed 
                     fairly evenly along the cell periphery; FliF-YFP can 
                     self-assemble into oligomers but does so poorly and only at
                     high expression levels or with the assistance of other 
                     flagella proteins; MS ring formation may be a second step 
                     (after FlhA) in motor assembly; FRET mapping suggests FliF 
                     interacts with itself and with FlhA and FliG |CITS: 
                     [21244534]| (see also |CITS: [8604139][28089452]|).
                     Sequence analysis suggests FliF consists of two 
                     transmembrane helices, flanking a periplasmic region which 
                     contains an SctJ homology domain and a FliF-specific 
                     domain, and a cytoplasmic region which interacts with FliG;
                     a predicted N-terminal signal peptide may target FliF for 
                     secretion and inner-membrane localization |CITS: 
                     [26925337]|.
                     Early studies in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 identified the 
                     <i>flaBI</i> gene within the <i>flaB</i> locus of region 
                     III <i>fla</i> (flagella) genes (see |CITS: 
                     [6452447][6094477]|). For a description of flagellar gene 
                     nomenclature, including old (<i>fla, flb</I>) and new 
                     (<i>flg, flh</i> and <i>fli</i>) symbols, see |CITS: 
                     [3070322]|.
                     <i>fliF</i> exhibits mosaic evolution patterns in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [40444982]|."
