     gene            2013099..2013563
                     /gene="fliL"
                     /locus_tag="b1944"
                     /label=fliL
     CDS             2013099..2013563
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fliL"
                     /locus_tag="b1944"
                     /product="trifunctional flagellar protein FliL / flagellar
                     basal body / flagellum"
                     /label=fliL
                     /note="<i>fliL</i> is the first of seven genes within the 
                     flagellar associated <i>flaA</i> locus |CITS: [2651416]| 
                     (see also |CITS: [3519573][2984180]|). The specific 
                     function of FliL remains uncertain - recent studies |CITS: 
                     [25714720][28717192]| in <i>E. coli</i> and 
                     <i>Salmonella</i> typhimurium, report conflicting evidence 
                     implicating FliL in motor torque generation. FliL is 
                     required for the 'motor remodeling' that is associated with
                     swarming; FliL may act as a hub to connect and stabilize 
                     the stator (MotAB) and core basal body complexes |CITS: 
                     [37675594]|.
                     Single flagella motors from &Delta;<i>fliL</i> strains 
                     rotate at lower speeds and switch directions less 
                     frequently when compared to wild type and this defect is 
                     exacebated under conditions of high load; a 
                     &Delta;<i>fliL</i> strain is defective in swarming and 
                     mutant cells appear to have fractured or released filaments
                     on swarm agar |CITS: [25714720]|. Loss of <i>fliL</i> does 
                     not affect motor torque generation under a range of loads; 
                     loss of <i>fliL</i> does not affect rotor-stator 
                     associations (the number of stator units engaged by motors 
                     at high loads is similar in wild-type and <i>fliL</i> 
                     mutants); loss of <i>fliL</i> does not affect 
                     motor-reversal (switching) and a <i>fliL</i> deletion 
                     mutant is capable of swarming |CITS: [28717192]|. Varying 
                     phenotypes have been reported for <i>fliL</i> null mutants.
                     The non-flagellate phenotype of a <i>fliL</i> insertion 
                     mutant |CITS: [2651416]| is the result of polarity; 
                     in-frame <i>fliL</i> deletion mutants exhibit wild-type 
                     chemotactic swarming |CITS: [8157599]|. In-frame 
                     <i>fliL</i> deletion mutants (in both <i>E. coli</i> and 
                     <i>Salmonella</i> typhimurium) are completely defective in 
                     swarming and slightly impaired in swimming; loss of FliL in
                     <i>Salmonella</i> typhimurium is associated with flagellar 
                     rod breakage on swarm agar |CITS: [18284590]|. FliL is 
                     found in the insoluble (membrane) fraction of disrupted 
                     cells |CITS: [2651416]|. FRET mapping indicates that FliL 
                     oligomerizes in the membrane and locates in close proximity
                     to FliG in assembled motors |CITS: [21244534]|. FliL is 
                     predicted to be a single-spanning transmembrane protein 
                     |CITS: [29126305]|.
                     FliL from <i>E. coli</i> K-12 and <i>Salmonella</i> 
                     typhimurium have sequence identity of 77% |CITS: 
                     [2656645]|.
                     For a description of flagellar gene nomenclature, including
                     old (<i>fla, flb</I>) and new (<i>flg, flh</i> and 
                     <i>fli</i>) symbols, see |CITS: [3070322]|<BR><i>cheC</i>: 
                     see |CITS: [770453]|"
                     /translation="MTDYAISKKSKRSLWIPILVFITLAACASAGYSYWHSHQVAADDK
                     AQQRVVPSPVFYALDTFTVNLGDADRVLYIGITLRLKDEATRSRLSEYLPEVRSRLLLL
                     FSRQDAAVLATEEGKKNLIAEIKTTLSTPLVAGQPKQDVTDVLYTAFILR"
