     gene            complement(2018704..2018794)
                     /gene="dsrA"
                     /locus_tag="b1954"
                     /label=dsrA
     misc_RNA        complement(2018704..2018794)
                     /gene="dsrA"
                     /locus_tag="b1954"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA DsrA"
                     /note="DsrA RNA activates the production of RpoS (the 
                     stationary-phase sigma factor), stimulating this process 
                     during log-phase growth at low temperature |CITS: 
                     [8670904]|.  DsrA acts to inhibit H-NS translation |CITS: 
                     [9770507]|.  DsrA RNA affects biosynthesis of capsular 
                     polysaccharide via increased production of the activator 
                     RcsA, due to inhibition of H-NS mediated transcriptional 
                     silencing |CITS: [7534408]|, and it similarly affects 
                     biofilm formation via its effect on H-NS expression |CITS: 
                     [28470798]|.  Additional genes induced by DsrA have been 
                     identified by microarray analysis and include acid 
                     resistance genes |CITS: [15342588]|. DsrA also appears to 
                     regulate the replication initiation process in a way that 
                     is dependent on Hfq, Dps, and OxyR proteins |CITS: 
                     [37482259]|.
                     Overexpression of DsrA decreases swimming and swarming 
                     motility, biofilm formation and type I fimbriae 
                     biosynthesis |CITS: [26469694]|. Overexpression of DsrA 
                     increases biofilm formation in both LB and YESCA medium; 
                     deletion of <i>dsrA</i> significantly decreases biofilm 
                     formation only in YESCA medium |CITS: [28470798]|. 
                     Structural and functional components of DsrA were 
                     identified using a library of single nucleotide mutants and
                     evaluating their effects on known regulatory targets |CITS:
                     [25262352]|.
                     DsrA regulatory effects are mediated by specific RNA-to-RNA
                     pairing interactions that can either enhance or inhibit 
                     translation |CITS: [9770507]| or mRNA stability |CITS: 
                     [10954740]|.  The mechanism of action and modularity of 
                     DsrA RNA is discussed |CITS: 
                     [9770507][9770508][10954740][12600997]|.  The structure of 
                     DsrA RNA has been examined |CITS: [9770508][10954740]|. 
                     DsrA RNA interacts with the 30S subunit of the ribosome 
                     |CITS: [12600997]|.  DsrA RNA binds to the Hfq protein 
                     |CITS: [11222598][12554874][37116495]| and Hfq is required 
                     for wild-type stability and activity of DsrA |CITS: 
                     [11222598][15381101]| by protecting it from RNase E 
                     cleavage |CITS: [14561880]|. During regulation of 
                     <i>rpoS</i>, the Hfq-DsrA complex is stable until DsrA is 
                     bound to the target site in <i>rpoS</i> mRNA, which appears
                     to happen immediately after the target site is transcribed 
                     |CITS: [37116495]|. DsrA interacts with HU protein |CITS: 
                     [12006568]|.  H-NS specifically binds to DsrA and enhances 
                     its cleavage |CITS: [15147838]|.  DsrA RNA is more stable 
                     at low temperatures than at higher temperature |CITS: 
                     [11395466]|.  The crystal structure of the DsrA-Hfq complex
                     has been solved |CITS: [21979921]|. DsrA appears to have 
                     many genetic interactions with other sRNAs under different 
                     growth conditions |CITS: [35920556]|.
                     A <i>dsrA</i> mutant does not exhibit a growth defect or a 
                     generalized H-NS-related transcription regulatory defect 
                     |CITS: [8670904]|. However, the double mutation 
                     <i>arcZ</i>-<i>dsrA</i> increases growth on saccharate, 
                     pyruvate, lactate, fucose, and gluconate, and it was 
                     suggested that the increase is due to the lack of 
                     expression activation of RpoS by ArcZ and DsrA  |CITS: 
                     [35920556]|. Overexpression of DsrA RNA inhibits H-NS 
                     mediated transcriptional silencing of <i>rcsA</i> 
                     expression, and derepression of <i>rcsA</i> stimulates 
                     biosynthesis of capsular polysaccharide |CITS: [7534408]|. 
                     Overexpression of DsrA RNA in a <i>recA</i> mutant 
                     background causes increased lambda phage induction |CITS: 
                     [9829941]|.  Published reports disagree about whether 
                     |CITS: [8670904]| or not |CITS: [12193624]| a <i>dsrA</i> 
                     mutant exhibits a defect in RpoS production at stationary 
                     phase.  The RpoS translation defect of a <i>dsrA</i> mutant
                     is suppressed by high-copy expression of RprA RNA |CITS: 
                     [11251852]|.
                     Regulation has been described |CITS: 
                     [9302018][11395466][12975324]|.  DsrA RNA accumulates at 
                     low temperature |CITS: [11395466]|, and promoter 
                     determinants for temperature regulation have been studied 
                     |CITS: [14594834]|.  Degradation of OxyS or DsrA RNA is 
                     probably stimulated by RNA duplex formation between the 
                     sRNA and its target RNA |CITS: [12975324]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9830102][10068996][11115103][12753181][12 
                     732300][15063850][15487940][19366629][26607444][29992897][ 
                     31197038][40043836]|"
