     gene            complement(2019804..2021498)
                     /gene="dgcQ"
                     /locus_tag="b1956"
                     /label=dgcQ
     CDS             complement(2019804..2021498)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dgcQ"
                     /locus_tag="b1956"
                     /product="bifunctional diguanylate cyclase DgcQ /
                     diguanylate cyclase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.65"
                     /label=dgcQ
                     /note="DgcQ is a probable inner membrane protein with two 
                     predicted transmembrane domains and a C-terminal GGDEF 
                     domain with diguanylate cyclase activity |CITS: 
                     [26148715][28892259]|.  The N-terminal CHASE 
                     (Cyclases/Histidine kinases Associated Sensory) domain is 
                     predicted to be periplasmic |CITS: [26148715]|.  The C 
                     terminus is predicted to localize to the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [15919996]|. DgcQ is implicated in phage N4 infection 
                     |CITS: [34903045]|.
                     The diguanylate cyclase domain of DgcQ physically interacts
                     with PyrB, the catalytic subunit of |FRAME: 
                     ASPCARBTRANS-CPLX| (PyrBI), an enzyme involved in <i>de 
                     novo</i>UMP biosynthesis.  The diguanylate cyclase activity
                     of this domain is activated by UTP and inhibited by |FRAME:
                     CARBAMYUL-L-ASPARTATE|, the product of PyrBI |CITS: 
                     [28892259]|.
                     In the commensal <i>E. coli</i> strain 1094, DgcQ is a 
                     regulator that constitutively activates cellulose 
                     production
                     under all tested environmental conditions |CITS: 
                     [16585767]|.
                     Expression of <i>dgcQ</i> is dependent on 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> under a number of stress conditions 
                     |CITS: [17010156]| and increases during transition into 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [18765794]|.  DgcQ does not appear 
                     to act as a global regulator of gene expression |CITS: 
                     [17010156]|.  Deletion of <i>dgcQ</i> partially restores 
                     motility of a <i>pdeH</i> mutant, suggesting that DgcQ is 
                     one of four diguanylate cyclases that adjust c-di-GMP 
                     concentration for motility control |CITS: 
                     [18765794][20303158]|. Deletion of <i>dgcQ</i>, |FRAME: 
                     EG12396 <i>dgcE</i>|, and |FRAME: G6972 <i>dgcJ</i>| 
                     together, fully restores the motility of a 
                     &Delta;<i>pdeH</i> mutant |CITS: [34903052]|.  A 
                     <i>dgcQ</i> deletion mutant shows 12-fold increased early 
                     biofilm formation and 6-fold increased swimming motility. 
                     Deletion of <i>dgcQ</i> does not alter the concentration of
                     c-di-GMP inside the cell |CITS: [21181144]|.  Deletion of 
                     <i>dgcQ</i> in a &Delta;<i>carB</i> background appears to 
                     inhibit cellulose overproduction when the strain is grown 
                     in the presence of uracil |CITS: [22359582]|.
                     An A361E mutation in DgcQ, which causes increased 
                     intracellular c-di-GMP levels, leads to improved 
                     phototrophic growth in a chemostat evolution experiment 
                     with an engineered rhodopsin-expressing <i>E. coli</i> 
                     strain grown under illumination with limited glucose |CITS:
                     [28619035]|.  Overexpression of <i>dgcQ</i> from an ASKA 
                     plasmid leads to a more than 3-fold increase in the MIC of 
                     hypochlorous acid |CITS: [34072091]|.
                     DgcQ: <b>d</b>i<b>g</b>uanylate <b>c</b>yclase <b>Q</b> 
                     |CITS: [26148715]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [27498163][33640237]|"
