     gene            2029316..2030167
                     /gene="hchA"
                     /locus_tag="b1967"
                     /label=hchA
     CDS             2029316..2030167
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hchA"
                     /locus_tag="b1967"
                     /product="trifunctional protein/nucleic acid deglycase 1 /
                     protein deglycase / glyoxalase"
                     /EC_number="3.5.1.124; 4.2.1.130"
                     /label=hchA
                     /note="HchA (also known as Hsp31 and glyoxalase III) has
                     been identified as a protein deglycase that repairs 
                     glyoxal- and methylglyoxal-glycated proteins |CITS: 
                     [26102038]| as well as a nucleotide deglycase that repairs 
                     glycated GTP as well as DNA and RNA |CITS: [28596309]|. The
                     enzyme was first identified as a chaperone that interacts 
                     with partially and fully denatured proteins and named Hsp31
                     for heat shock protein 31 |CITS: [12235139][12565879]|. At 
                     elevated temperatures, Hsp31 homodimers bind to substrates 
                     undergoing denaturation, and upon temperature decrease the 
                     chaperone dissociates from the renatured substrates |CITS: 
                     [12235139]|. Hsp31 appears to undergo thermal activation 
                     that results in formation of a high molecular weight 
                     oligomer with enhanced chaperone activity |CITS: 
                     [23202248]|. Hsp31 also protects proteins during acid 
                     stress |CITS: [14731284][37093306]|.
                     The enzyme also converts methylglyoxal to D-lactate in a 
                     single glutathione (GSH)-independent step, giving it the 
                     name glyoxylase III (the other known route for this 
                     conversion is the two-step GSH-dependent pathway catalyzed 
                     by |FRAME: G6891 glyoxalase I| and |FRAME: G6099 glyoxalase
                     II|, see |FRAME: PWY-5386|). Glyoxalase III was partially 
                     characterized by |CITS: [7848303]|, although the gene 
                     encoding the enzyme was not identified at that time. It was
                     later shown that glyoxalase III is identical to Hsp31 in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [21696459]|, and that the glyoxalase 
                     activity in fact reflects the amino acid deglycase activity
                     of Hsp31 |CITS: [26102038]|.
                     Active site residues have been predicted |CITS: 
                     [17257049]|. Mutations in predicted residues of the 
                     catalytic triad, E77A, C185A and H186A, strongly reduce 
                     catalytic activity of the enzyme |CITS: [21696459]|.
                     Each Hsp31 monomer comprises two domains separated by a 
                     flexible linker region |CITS: [12621151][14691241]|. 
                     Crystal structures are presented at 1.6 &Aring; |CITS: 
                     [12621151]|, 2.2 &Aring; |CITS: [14500888]|, 2.7 &Aring; 
                     |CITS: [14691241]| and 2.8 &Aring; |CITS: [12939276]| 
                     resolution. NMR backbone resonance assignments of Hsp31 in 
                     solution have been published |CITS: [28258548]|. Analysis 
                     of a variety of mutants identified the linker-loop region 
                     of Hsp31 as a gate which may control access of nonnative 
                     proteins to the high-affinity substrate binding site and 
                     which is thermally activated |CITS: [15173574]|. Extensions
                     of Hsp31 with histidine tags at either its N- or C-terminus
                     abolish chaperone activity |CITS: [19517531]|.
                     Hsp31 may bind ATP, but does not hydrolyze it |CITS: 
                     [12235139][12565879]|. ATP induces conformational changes 
                     within Hsp31 and is inhibitory with respect to chaperone 
                     activity at high temperatures |CITS: [12235139]|.
                     Hsp31 appears to have weak proteolytic activity using BSA 
                     as a substrate |CITS: [12939276]|; others have found no 
                     endo- or exopeptidase activity |CITS: 
                     [12565879][14500888]|. Later, Hsp31 was shown to have an 
                     aminopeptidase activity with short peptide substrates that 
                     is abolished in a C185A mutant |CITS: [15550391]|.
                     An <i>hchA</i> mutant has a growth defect at 48&deg;C and 
                     is strongly affected in its ability to recover from 
                     transient exposure to 50&deg;C |CITS: [14731284]|. In 
                     stationary phase, an <i>hchA</i> mutant is more sensitive 
                     to methylglyoxal |CITS: [21696459]| and acid stress |CITS: 
                     [17158627]| than wild type. An <i>hchA</i> mutant contains 
                     increased levels of glycated proteins compared to wild type
                     |CITS: [26102038]|. An <i>hchA</i> mutant as well as an 
                     <i>hchA yhbO yajL</i> triple mutant accumulates glycated 
                     DNA and RNA and shows increased mutation frequency, with 
                     increased levels of GC&rarr;AT and AT&rarr;GC transitions 
                     |CITS: [28596309]|.
                     Unlike glyoxalases I and II, glyoxalase III activity 
                     increases at the transition to stationary phase; the 
                     increase is dependent on RpoS |CITS: [15448747]|. 
                     Glyoxalase III activity is not induced by growth in the 
                     presence of methylglyoxal |CITS: [10931845]|. Expression of
                     <i>hchA</i> is induced by heat shock |CITS: [10481021]|. 
                     <i>hchA</i> is a member of the &sigma;<sup>S</sup> stress 
                     regulon |CITS: [16796689]|, and Hsp31 expression is also 
                     induced by osmotic stress |CITS: [17015655]|. The stability
                     of the <i>hchA</i> transcript is higher at increased 
                     temperature |CITS: [17526696]|.
                     The HchA H186A mutant exhibits esterase activity, pointing 
                     to the evolutionary origin of this activity in the human 
                     homolog DJ-1 that is the causative agent for hereditary 
                     recessive Parkinson's disease |CITS: [38879013]|.
                     Comment: |CITS: [28706026]|, Reviews: |CITS: 
