     gene            2032959..2033963
                     /gene="msrP"
                     /locus_tag="b1971"
                     /label=msrP
     CDS             2032959..2033963
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="msrP"
                     /locus_tag="b1971"
                     /product="multifunctional protein-L-methionine sulfoxide
                     reductase catalytic subunit MsrP / periplasmic 
                     protein-L-methionine sulfoxide reducing system / 
                     periplasmic protein-L-methionine-(R)-S-oxide reductase / 
                     periplasmic protein-L-methionine-(S)-S-oxide reductase"
                     /label=msrP
                     /note="MsrP (formerly YedY) is a periplasmic methionine 
                     sulfoxide reductase; MsrP and MsrQ, an inner membrane, 
                     heme-binding, quinol dehydrogenase, function together to 
                     protect periplasmic proteins from oxidative damage <i>in 
                     vivo</i>. MrsPQ activity relies on electrons provided by 
                     the membrane quinone pool |CITS: [26641313]|. MsrPQ may 
                     also rely on electrons from cytoplasmic NADH via a pathway 
                     involving the cytosolic NAD(P)H:flavin reductase |FRAME: 
                     FMNREDUCT-MONOMER Fre| |CITS: [28028176][34550690]|. 
                     Purified MsrP has no detectable sulfite oxidase activity, 
                     but does show reductase activity with substrates including 
                     TMAO, DMSO, phenylmethyl sulfoxide, methionine sulfoxide, 
                     and tetramethylene sulfoxide |CITS: [15355966]|. Purified 
                     MsrP reduces hypochlorous acid oxidized methionine residues
                     in a variety of periplasmic proteins, including SurA and 
                     Pal; MsrP activity does not exhibit stereospecificity - it 
                     reduces both diastereoisomers of methionine sulfoxide 
                     |CITS: [26641313]|. MsrP is induced during copper stress 
                     and may be required to protect the |FRAME: CPLX0-1721 
                     CusCFBA| copper efflux pump from oxidative damage |CITS: 
                     [35816552]|.
                     Purified, crystallised MsrP contains a single molybdopterin
                     cofactor that is not conjugated by an additional 
                     nucleotide; the structure is not similar to other 
                     molybdoenzymes of the bacterial DMSO reductase family or 
                     xanthine oxidase family |CITS: [15355966]|. MsrP 
                     crystallises as a pentamer but is monomeric in solution 
                     |CITS: [15355966]|. Mo(IV) and Mo(V) oxidation states have 
                     been identified but a Mo(V) to Mo(VI) transition has not 
                     been observed |CITS: [16042411]|. X-ray absorption and EPR 
                     spectra were suggestive of an Mo(V) oxidation state with a 
                     six-coordinate active site (see |FRAME: CPD-18906|) |CITS: 
                     [21190337], [21190336]|. MsrP catalysis involves redox 
                     processes that are both metal based and pyranopterin based;
                     formation of the catalytically active state involves two 
                     redox transitions: a 1H<sup>+</sup>/1e<sup>-</sup> 
                     transfer, Mo(V) &rarr; Mo(IV) plus a 
                     2H<sup>+</sup>/2e<sup>-</sup> dihydro to tetrahydro 
                     transition of the pyranopterin ligand |CITS: [26561582]|.
                     Exposure of wild type cells to hypochlorous acid induces 
                     synthesis of MsrP and this induction is dependent on the 
                     presence of a functional |FRAME: PWY0-1588| |CITS: 
                     [26641313]| (see further |CITS: [34898261]|). An 
                     <i>msrPQ</i> deletion strain does not have an obvious 
                     growth defect under any conditions measured |CITS: 
                     [16042411]|.
                     Orthologues of MsrP are found in a variety of Gram-negative
                     bacteria |CITS: [15355966]|.
                     <i>msrP</i>: <U>m</U>ethionine <U>s</U>ulfoxide 
                     <U>r</U>eductase - <U>p</U>eriplasmic |CITS: [26641313]| 
