     gene            2033964..2034599
                     /gene="msrQ"
                     /locus_tag="b1972"
                     /label=msrQ
     CDS             2033964..2034599
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="msrQ"
                     /locus_tag="b1972"
                     /product="multifunctional membrane-bound flavocytochrome
                     MsrQ / periplasmic protein-L-methionine sulfoxide reducing 
                     system / periplasmic protein-L-methionine-(R)-S-oxide 
                     reductase / periplasmic protein-L-methionine-(S)-S-oxide 
                     reductase"
                     /label=msrQ
                     /note="MsrQ (formerly YedZ) is a heme-binding, inner
                     membrane quinol dehydrogenase that provides reducing 
                     equivalents to MsrP which in turn, reduces oxidized 
                     methionine residues of functionally diverse periplasmic 
                     proteins. The MsrPQ system protects periplasmic proteins, 
                     including SurA and Pal, from oxidative damage |CITS: 
                     [26641313]|. MsrQ is a member of the ferric reductase 
                     domain (FRD) superfamily |CITS: [28028176][23505460]|; MsrQ
                     may also receive electrons from the cytosolic 
                     NAD(P)H:flavin reductase |FRAME: FMNREDUCT-MONOMER Fre|; 
                     purified MsrQ can be efficiently reduced by Fre in the 
                     presence of free FMN and NADPH |CITS: [28028176]|. MsrQ can
                     be reduced by Fre in the absence of free flavins; the MsrQ 
                     FMN cofactor is used by Fre as a substrate to catalyse the 
                     reduction of the MsrQ heme in the presence of NADH |CITS: 
                     [34550690]|.
                     Sequence analysis indicates that MsrQ is the 
                     heme-containing transmembrane subunit of the oxidoreductase
                     heterodimer MsrPQ. MsrQ is thought to anchor MsrP, the 
                     catalytic subunit, to the membrane |CITS: [15355966]|. MsrQ
                     contains a single &beta;-type heme located on the 
                     periplasmic side of the membrane and an FMN cofactor 
                     located on the cytosolic side of the membrane |CITS: 
                     [34550690][28028176][15987891]|. Under anaerobic 
                     conditions, the heme of purified MsrQ can be reduced by 
                     menadiol and other menaquinol and ubiquinol analogues 
                     |CITS: [16042411]|.
                     When the MsrP active site residue Cys102 was changed to 
                     Ser, interaction with MsrQ and association of MsrP with the
                     membrane could be shown |CITS: [16042411]|. MsrQ interacts 
                     with the cytosolic NAD(P)H:flavin reductase Fre |CITS: 
                     [28028176]|.
                     Protein topology in the inner membrane has been determined 
                     |CITS: [11867724]|.
                     Homologs of MsrQ have been found in bacteria and animals, 
                     but not in the archaea or other eukaryotic kingdoms |CITS: 
                     [16088215]|. The homologous vertebrate 6TM domain family of
                     proteins is involved in apoptosis and cancer |CITS: 
                     [15623366]|.
                     A <i>msrPQ</i> deletion strain does not have an obvious 
                     growth defect under any conditions measured |CITS: 
                     [16042411]|.  The <i>msrQ</i> mRNA belongs to a group of 
                     transcripts whose levels at different growth rates are 
                     under predominantly degradational control |CITS: 
                     [24243845]|.
                     <i>msrQ</i>: <u>m</u>ethionine <u>s</u>ulfoxide 
                     <u>r</u>eductase - <u>q</u>uinone |CITS: [26641313]|."
