     gene            2034856..2035506
                     /gene="zinT"
                     /locus_tag="b1973"
                     /label=zinT
     CDS             2034856..2035506
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="zinT"
                     /locus_tag="b1973"
                     /product="metal-binding protein ZinT"
                     /label=zinT
                     /note="Best characterised in |FRAME: TAX-90371|, the
                     periplasmic metal-binding protein ZinT functions as an 
                     accessory component of the |FRAME: ABC-63-CPLX ZnuABC 
                     transporter| by delivering Zn<sup>2+</sup> to ZnuA under 
                     conditions of severe Zn<sup>2+</sup> shortage |CITS: 
                     [20097857][24128931]|. ZinT contributes to ZnuA-mediated 
                     recruitment of zinc in the periplasmic space in |FRAME: 
                     TAX-83334| |CITS: [21338480]|.
                     ZinT consists of a major lipocalin-like domain and a 
                     smaller helical domain; purified ZinT binds metals such as 
                     cadmium and zinc and crystal structures have been 
                     determined |CITS: [12909634]|. Purified ZinT binds zinc, 
                     cadmium, mercury and possibly nickel |CITS: [17931600]|. 
                     Purified ZinT preferentially binds Zn<sup>2+</sup> over 
                     Cd<sup>2+</sup> |CITS:[19377097]|. Purified ZinT binds a 
                     range of divalent metals - zinc, nickel, cadmium, cobalt, 
                     mercury and copper; a zinc-loaded crystal structure 
                     suggests a physiological dimer with multiple zinc binding 
                     sites |CITS: [26758285]|. ZinT exists as a monomer in 
                     solution in the presence and absence of zinc ions; 
                     purified, crystallized ZinT contains one zinc ion located 
                     at the center of the lipocalin-like domain |CITS: 
                     [29596824]|. A detailed study on the coordination chemistry
                     of ZinT complexes with Zn<sup>2+</sup> and Cu<sup>2+</sup> 
                     is reported by |CITS: [32588863]|.
                     ZinT (identified as F023.3) is produced in response to 
                     cadmium exposure (273 &micro;M final concentration) and 
                     levels remain elevated after recovery and regrowth of the 
                     cells in the presence of cadmium; ZinT contains a 24 amino 
                     acid signal sequence; the mature product is exported from 
                     the cytoplasm |CITS: [9579078]|. ZinT is primarily detected
                     in the cytoplasm (45 minutes after cadmium addition); ZinT 
                     is also detected in the periplasm (150 min after cadmium 
                     addition) |CITS: [12480884]|. ZinT is a substrate of DsbA 
                     and may contain one or more disulphide bonds |CITS: 
                     [14739460]|.
                     A <i>zinT::km</i> mutant appears unable to bind labeled 
                     cadmium and shows a distinguishble growth pattern from that
                     of the isogenic parent upon exposure to cadmium |CITS: 
                     [17632755]|. A <i>zinT::cam</i> mutant has decreased levels
                     of cellular Zn<sup>2+</sup> and grows more poorly than the 
                     wild type in zinc depleted medium |CITS:[19377097]|. Growth
                     experiments using a &Delta;<i>zinT</i> mutant implicate 
                     ZinT in cobalt, mercury and cadmium resistance |CITS: [    
                     26758285]|. Expression of a <i>PzinT-lacZ</I> 
                     transcriptional fusion increases in response to cadmium 
                     (136.5 &micro;M) and hydrogen peroxide (10 mM) but does not
                     vary from basal levels in response to zinc (0.7mM), copper 
                     (3.1 mM), cobalt (1 mM), nickel (2 mM) or paraquat (2 mM); 
                     cadmium-induced transcription is dependent on <i>soxS, 
                     fur</i> and <i>relA/spoT</i> but not on <i>oxyR</i> |CITS: 
                     [12480884]|. A candidate Zur (zinc repressor) binding site 
                     occurs upstream of <i>zinT</i> |CITS: [12904577]|. ZinT 
                     abundance is increased in bacteria grown at pH 5.8 compared
                     to pH 7 |CITS: [12748954]|. <i>zinT</i> expression 
                     decreases in response to increasing external copper; growth
                     of a &Delta;<i>zinT</i> mutant is not affected by cadmium 
                     (growth in LB supplemented with 0.25, 0.5 and 1 mM 
                     cadmium); growth of a &Delta;<i>zinT</i> mutant is copper 
                     and zinc dependent |CITS: [17931600]|. <i>zinT</i> is 
                     upregulated 8-fold in response to Zn<sup>2+</sup> 
                     deprivation; Zur is a negative regulator of <i>zinT</i> 
                     transcription |CITS:[19377097]|.
                     "
