     gene            complement(2054497..2055414)
                     /gene="nac"
                     /locus_tag="b1988"
                     /label=nac
     CDS             complement(2054497..2055414)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nac"
                     /locus_tag="b1988"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional dual regulator Nac"
                     /label=nac
                     /note="Nac, ""<b>N</b>itrogen <b>a</b>ssimilation 
                     <b>c</b>ontrol,"" regulates, without a coeffector, genes 
                     involved in nitrogen metabolism under nitrogen-limiting 
                     conditions |CITS: [9495755]|.
                     The genes regulated by Nac are transcribed by RNA 
                     polymerase &sigma;<sup>70</sup>. By using synthetic gene 
                     circuits, it was suggested that Nac shows stabilization of 
                     RNA polymerase (RNAP) at a downstream repressing position, 
                     while activation at upstream positions occurs without 
                     stabilization |CITS:[39921566]|.
                     The genes regulated by Nac are coupled to the nitrogen 
                     regulatory (Ntr) system, which is &sigma;<sup>54</sup> 
                     dependent, through Nac, whose transcription is activated by
                     NtrC |CITS: [9495755]|. Using DNA microarray analyses, it 
                     was shown that Nac could affect the expression of 25 genes 
                     |CITS: [11121068]|. This transcriptional regulator is 
                     negatively autoregulated |CITS: [9495755]| and is expressed
                     under nitrogen-limiting conditions |CITS: [9785451]|.
                     Inhibition of <i>nac</i> expression by CRISPRi reduced 
                     cellular fitness under treatment with the antibiotic 
                     pyocyanin |CITS:[40352728]|.
                     In a gene expression study during the transition from 
                     aerobic to anaerobic conditions, part of the regulatory 
                     cascade involving the protein Nac was analyzed 
                     |CITS:[38891842]|.
                     RIL-seq experiments with cells grown in various nitrogen 
                     (N) growth conditions found that <i>nac</i> mRNA is 
                     targeted by the |FRAME: CPLX0-1461|-dependent |FRAME: 
                     RNA0-434 GlnZ| interactions under N-starvation conditions, 
                     indicating that <i>nac</i> is post-transcriptionally 
                     regulated by GlnZ with the assistance of Hfq |CITS: 
                     [38142457]|.
                     Nac belongs to the LysR family |CITS: [9495755]| and 
                     features a helix-turn-helix motif close to the N terminus 
                     |CITS: [9922258]|. In contrast with most LysR family 
                     members, which are transcribed divergently from the gene 
                     they regulate, <i>nac</i> is distant on the genome from its
                     regulated genes |CITS: [9495755]|.
                     In <i>Klebsiella pneumoniae</i>, Nac functions as a dimer, 
                     and for some sites it can bind as a tetramer; it recognizes
                     long and short sites in the absence of a coeffector |CITS: 
                     [20363946]|.
                     High concentrations of Nac appear to stop cellular growth 
                     |CITS: [35694815]|.
                     The Nac binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [30514772][38061078]|."
