                     /locus_tag="b1989"
                     /product="tRNA-Asn(GUU)"
                     /label=asnV
                     /note="tRNA(asnV) is one of four asparagine tRNAs.
                     tRNAs are the adapters that allow synthesis of proteins 
                     from mRNAs.
                     Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome for
                     protein synthesis.
                     There, the tRNA recognizes an RNA codon with its own 
                     three-nucleotide
                     anticodon, thus allowing synthesis of a specific peptide 
                     based on an mRNA
                     template.
                     tRNAs are processed to their active, mature forms by RNA 
                     cleavage
                     and by modification of their bases. RNA cleavage consists 
                     of
                     removal of both 5' and 3' extensions in a multistep process
                     involving
                     many RNases |CITS: [11252717]|. RNases taking part in tRNA 
                     processing include |FRAME: CPLX0-3461|,
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3601|, |FRAME: EG10858-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: EG11620-MONOMER|, and |FRAME: CPLX0-3602|. tRNAs 
                     are also subject to a
                     wide variety of base modifications catalyzed by proteins 
                     such as
                     |FRAME: EG11932-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10595-MONOMER|, |FRAME:
                     G6364-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11344-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7199-MONOMER|, |FRAME: CPLX0-1101|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11779-MONOMER|, |FRAME: G7422-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7449-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11177-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10454-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10967-MONOMER|,
                     and |FRAME: EG11022-MONOMER|.
                     Mature tRNAs are linked via a 3' CCA sequence to their 
                     cognate
                     amino acid in an ATP-dependent fashion by the appropriate 
                     amino-acid-tRNA synthetase, as shown in the |FRAME: 
                     TRNA-CHARGING-PWY|.
                     Subsequently, these charged tRNAs interact with the 
                     ribosome
                     and template mRNA to generate polypeptides. The discovery 
                     of the
                     role of tRNA in protein synthesis is reviewed in detail in 
                     |CITS: [7033244]|."
     gene            complement(2055872..2056804)
                     /gene="ldtA"
                     /locus_tag="b1990"
                     /label=ldtA
     CDS             complement(2055872..2056804)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ldtA"
                     /locus_tag="b1990"
                     /product="bifunctional L,D-transpeptidase LdtA / 
                     L,D-transpeptidase"
                     /label=ldtA
                     /note="LdtA is an L,D-transpeptidase which cleaves the 
                     <i>meso</i>-diaminopimeloyl<sup>3</sup>&rarr; 
                     D-alanine<sup>4</sup> peptide bond in a peptidoglycan 
                     tetrapeptide stem and links the carboxyl group at the 
                     L-center of <i>m</i>-DAP<sup>3</sup> to the side chain 
                     amine of the lysine residue located at the C terminus of 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-8279 ""Braun lipoprotein""| |CITS: 
                     [4918558][17369299]|. This activity anchors Braun 
                     lipoprotein to the peptidoglycan and contributes to the 
                     stability of the cell envelope.
                     Muropeptides obtained from the peptidoglycan of an <i>E. 
                     coli</i> BW25113 &Delta;(<i>ldtA |FRAME: G6422 ""ldtB""| 
                     |FRAME: G6571 ""ldtC""| |FRAME: G6904 ""ldtE""|</i>) 
                     quadruple mutant (BW25113&Delta;4) do not contain 
                     tripeptide stems substituted by a fragment of the Braun 
                     lipoprotein |CITS: [17369299]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [18456808]|). Expression of <i>ldtA</i> in BW25113&Delta;4 
                     restores the appearance of this muropeptide suggesting that
                     LdtA catalyzes the covalent anchoring of Braun lipoprotein 
                     to the peptidoglycan |CITS: [17369299]|. Complementation 
                     analysis suggests that <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains three 
                     L,D transpeptidases (LdtA, LdtB and LdtC) that can catalyse
                     the covalent anchoring of Braun lipoprotein to the 
                     peptidoglycan however the analysis of single deletion 
                     mutants suggests LdtB may be of primary importance 
                     (|CITS:[17369299]| 'results not shown'). An <i>E. coli</i> 
                     BW25113 &Delta;(<i>ldtA ldtB ldtC</i>) triple mutant 
                     (BW25113&Delta;3) has increased outer membrane 
                     permeability, is hypersensitive to EDTA, and is resistant 
                     to the toxic effects of 25mM D-methionine |CITS: 
                     [23832002]|.
                     LdtA, B, C and E are all of similar size and share 42-62% 
                     sequence similarity including a putative active site 
                     cysteine located in a conserved motif |CITS: [23832002]|. 
                     Complementation analysis (|CITS:[18456808]| results not 
                     shown) also suggests LdtA can exchange D-ala<sup>4</sup> in
                     peptidoglycan tetrapeptide stems with glycine (|FRAME: 
                     RXN-16665 ""RXN-16665""|) and hydrolyse D-ala<sup>4</sup> 
                     to produce tripeptide stems (|FRAME: RXN-16664 
                     ""RXN-16664""|). <i>E. coli</i> K-12 incorporates 
                     exogenously supplied D-methionine into peptidoglycan stem 
                     tetrapeptides; the percentage of muropeptides that contain 
                     D-methionine is reduced to varying extents in all single 
                     <i>ldt</i> insertion mutants* and is reduced by ~75% in the
                     double <i>ldtD ldtE</i> insertion mutant |CITS: [21792174]|
                     (see also |CITS: [23832002]|).
                     <i>ldtA</i> is &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent and is 
                     upregulated in response to the stationary phase transition,
                     osmotic upshift, and acid stress |CITS:[15716429]|. 
                     <i>ldtA</i> is not expressed during growth in rich medium 
                     |CITS:[11133948]|.
                     *<i>ldtF</i> was not included in this analysis
                     "
