     gene            2064798..2064992
                     /gene="isrC"
                     /locus_tag="b4435"
                     /label=isrC
     misc_RNA        2064798..2064992
                     /gene="isrC"
                     /locus_tag="b4435"
                     /product="CP4-44 prophage; small RNA IsrC"
                     /note="<i>isrC</i> was first identified in a screen for 
                     potential small RNA genes |CITS: [12069726]|. It is not 
                     clear whether IsrC simply represents the attenuated UTR of 
                     |FRAME: G7080| or whether it has an independent function 
                     |CITS: [24837285]|.
                     IsrC is transcribed from the region between <i>yeeP</i> and
                     <i>flu</i> |CITS: [12069726]|. Expression of <i>isrC</i> is
                     repressed in the presence of PhoP and independent of the 
                     concentration of Mg<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [21665928]|.
                     A predicted secondary structure at the 3' end of 
                     <i>isrC</i> functions as a rho-independent terminator <i>in
                     vitro</i>. Antitermination is regulated <i>in vivo</i>; 
                     initiation of translation at the in-frame ATG codon within 
                     <i>isrC</i> enhances expression of |FRAME: G7080-MONOMER 
                     Ag43| |CITS: [24837285]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12654996][32213244]|"
     gene            2065020..2068139
                     /gene="flu"
                     /locus_tag="b2000"
                     /label=flu
     CDS             2065020..2068139
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="flu"
                     /locus_tag="b2000"
                     /product="CP4-44 prophage; self recognizing antigen 43
                     (Ag43) autotransporter"
                     /label=flu
                     /note="<i>agn43</i> encodes a 1,039 amino acid product
                     with a 52 amino acid N-terminal signal sequence. The 
                     mature, peptidase-processed protein is then 
                     autocatalytically cleaved to yield two subunits |CITS: 
                     [10094691]|. One is a 60 kDa &alpha;<sup>43</sup> |CITS: 
                     [3301815]| subunit found peripheral to the outer membrane 
                     |CITS: [2661530]|, extending beyond O-side chains of smooth
                     lipopolysaccharide |CITS: [9192040]|. The other is a heat 
                     modifiable 53 kDa &beta;<sup>43</sup> |CITS: [3301815]| 
                     integral membrane protein |CITS: [2661530]|. Ag43 is an 
                     autotransporter as &beta;<sup>43</sup> forms an 18 stranded
                     &beta;-barrel through which &alpha;<sup>43</sup> is 
                     translocated |CITS: [10094691]|. The Ag43 &alpha; subunit 
                     forms a &beta;-helix structure consisting of 20 rungs, each
                     containing 3 &beta;-strands and 3 turns, which stack in 
                     parallel |CITS: [14651628]|. Both polypeptides are 
                     associated with the outer membrane in equal stoichiometry 
                     |CITS: [3301815]|. Immunofluorescence studies of 
                     Ag43-producing <i>E. coli</i> showed that the protein is 
                     seen evenly distributed over the surface of the entire cell
                     |CITS: [9103983]|.
                     Based on conserved features, Ag43 can be placed in a group 
                     of similar autotransporters that includes AIDA-I and TibA, 
                     an adhesin of enterotoxigenic <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [16765057]|.
                     Ag43 expression is subject to phase variation with 
                     switching frequencies of 2.2 x 10(-3) for Ag43+ to -, and 1
                     x 10(-3) for Ag43- to + |CITS: [9192040]|. <i>agn43</i> 
                     expressing variants usually autoaggregate in liquid |CITS: 
                     [6102553]| due to Ag43&alpha;- Ag43&alpha; interactions, 
                     but this is prevented by fimbriation |CITS: [10438752]|. 
                     They are non-piliated with frizzy colony morphology |CITS: 
                     [6102553]|. The frizzy colony phenotype, found in all 
                     strains expressing Ag43, and the self-aggregating 
                     phenotype, absent in some strains, are independent 
                     properties. Ag43-expressing strains, whether they are 
                     capable of autoaggregation or not, are able to form 
                     biofilms, though it is most efficient in non-aggregating 
                     strains |CITS: [14651628]|. Non-expressing variants remain 
                     suspended, are piliated, and have smooth colonies |CITS: 
                     [6102553]|.
                     The balance between Dam-dependent methylation of, and 
                     binding of OxyR to the regulatory region of <i>agn43</i> is
                     responsible for the phase-variation of Ag43 expression. 
                     Methylation of GATC sites by Dam in the regulatory region 
                     of <i>agn43</i> is required for transcription of the gene, 
                     and binding of OxyR to this region when they are 
                     unmethylated prevents transcription |CITS: [10094691]|. 
                     Regulation has been described further |CITS: [10094691][12 
                     644490][12644494][10692164][11972787][11972788][12029051][1
                     2015987][9497290][15543158][11406584]|. The effect of 
                     glycosylation on the extracellular domain of Ag43 has been 
                     investigated. Glycosylation increases the stability of 
                     Ag43, reduces cellular aggregation of <i>E. coli</i> |CITS:
                     [18341480]| and may affect Ag43's ability to adhere to 
                     eukaryotic cells |CITS: [16484190][19230760]|.
                     Ag43 abolishes the inhibition of adsorption of phages 
                     &lambda;, T4, and P1 by bile salts coupled with some 
                     carbohydrates. Membrane fractions containing Ag43 sequester
                     phage &lambda; |CITS: [11988528]|.
                     Ag43 is important for biofilm formation in W3110 cells 
                     grown in glucose-minimal medium, but not LB |CITS: 
                     [10931336]|. Ag43 (and flagella-driven motility) is 
                     required for the aggregating phenotype seen in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> strain W3110 (RpoS<sup>+</sup>) grown at 37&deg;C 
                     to mid-exponential phase; Ag43-dependent aggregation 
                     promotes resistance to oxidative stress and biofilm 
                     formation |CITS: [27687245]|.
                     Ag43 has a role in a 'rosette'-initiated, multicellular 
                     life-cycle that generates <i>E. coli</i> biofilms in a 
                     static liquid growth environment; Ag43 is required for 
                     rosette formation and clonal chain structure during 
                     multicellular morphogenesis |CITS: [36594031]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [38814871]|).
                     Ag43 has been used in the design of multispecies biofilms 
                     |CITS: [11214802]|, and for expressing foreign protein 
                     segments in a number of gram negative bacteria |CITS: 
                     [12107137]|.
                     Membrane vesicles (MVs), released naturally from an 
                     Ag43-overexpressing <i>E. coli</i> strain contain 
                     functional Ag43; these Ag43<sup>+ve</sup> MVs bind to 
                     Ag43<sup>+ve</sup> bacteria to promote aggregation |CITS: 
                     [39840972]|.
