     gene            2071050..2071424
                     /gene="cbtA"
                     /locus_tag="b2005"
                     /label=cbtA
     CDS             2071050..2071424
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cbtA"
                     /locus_tag="b2005"
                     /product="CP4-44 prophage; cytoskeleton-binding toxin
                     CbtA"
                     /label=cbtA
                     /note="CbtA is the toxin of the CbtA-CbeA type IV 
                     toxin-antitoxin system.  CbtA directly interacts with the 
                     cell division proteins FtsZ and MreB and inhibits the 
                     GTPase activity and the GTP-dependent polymerization of 
                     FtsZ, as well as the ATP-dependent polymerization of MreB 
                     |CITS: [21166897]|.  The effects of CbtA on cell division 
                     (via FtsZ) and cell elongation (via MreB) are independent 
                     and separable.  The functionally relevant target surfaces 
                     for the inhibitory action of CbtA on FtsZ and MreB have 
                     been identified |CITS: [28931012]|.
                     Ectopic expression of <i>cbtA</i> causes inhibition of cell
                     growth and decreased viability, compared to wild type 
                     |CITS: [14594833]|, and leads to changes in cell morphology
                     |CITS: [21166897][28931012]|.  The growth defect is 
                     suppressed by coexpression of <i>cbeA</i> |CITS: 
                     [14594833]|.  Antitoxin production appeared to reduce toxin
                     abundance |CITS: [14594833]|.  Unlike other antitoxins, 
                     CbeA does not interact directly with the toxin CbtA.  
                     Instead, it antagonizes action of CbtA by interacting 
                     directly with its targets MreB and FtsZ, and enhances 
                     filament bundling |CITS: [22515815]|.  CbtA directly 
                     interacts with the H6/H7 loop of FtsZ and with the 
                     inter-protofilament interface of MreB |CITS: [28931012]|.
                     An F65S mutant is only slightly less toxic than wild type, 
                     but specifically disrupts the interaction of CbtA with 
                     FtsZ, while an R15C mutant specifically disrupts the 
                     interaction with MreB.  Overexpression of a double F65S 
                     R15C mutant is no longer toxic |CITS: [28931012]|.
                     CbtA, YkfI, and YpjF have similarity to each other and 
                     define a protein family of cellular toxins.  The YafW, CbeA
                     |CITS: [14594833]|, and YfjZ |CITS: [28257056]| antitoxins 
                     are partially interchangeable with respect to inhibition of
                     toxicity caused by CbtA production.
                     CbtA: ""<b>c</b>ytoskeleton <b>b</b>inding <b>t</b>oxin 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [21166897]|"
                     /translation="MKTLPVLPGQAASSRPSPVEIWQILLSRLLDQHYGLTLNDTPFAD
                     ERVIEQHIEAGISLCDAVNFLVEKYALVRTDQPGFSACTRSQLINSIDILRARRATGLM
                     TRDNYRTVNNITLGKYPEAK"
