     gene            2076237..2077664
                     /gene="sbcB"
                     /locus_tag="b2011"
                     /label=sbcB
     CDS             2076237..2077664
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sbcB"
                     /locus_tag="b2011"
                     /product="exodeoxyribonuclease I"
                     /EC_number="3.1.11.1"
                     /label=sbcB
                     /note="Exonuclease I (ExoI) hydrolyzes DNA through its
                     3'-5' exonuclease acitivity. ExoI is specific for 
                     single-stranded DNA requiring a 3'-hydroxyl group. ExoI 
                     removes 5'-mononucleotides one at a time leaving the 5' 
                     dinucleotide intact |CITS: [14235546]|.
                     ExoI exhibits DNA deoxyribophosphodiesterase activity 
                     |CITS: [1329027]|. ExoI releases deoxyribose-5-phosphate 
                     from DNA after endonuclease IV incision at 
                     apurinic/apyrimidinic (AP) sites, and 
                     4-hydroxy-2-pentenal-5-phosphate after incision by 
                     endonuclease III at AP sites |CITS: [1329027]|. ExoI 
                     activity has been shown to suppress illegitimate 
                     recombination by RecE |CITS: [10672041]|.
                     Exonuclease I activity is stimulated by |FRAME: 
                     EG10976-MONOMER|. SSB binds to mutliple sites on 
                     exonuclease I, recruiting the enzyme to substrate and thus 
                     enhancing its activity |CITS: [18591666]|.
                     The nucleases, ExoI and |FRAME: CPLX0-3957 ""SbcCD""| are 
                     required for the completion of chromosome replication in 
                     <i>E. coli</i>; mutants lacking ExoI and SbcCD show an 
                     increased copy number of sequences in the terminus region; 
                     Exo I and SbcCD process the palindrome-like structure where
                     replication forks converge at termination; converging 
                     replication forks must be processed by SbcCD-Exo I before 
                     RecBCD facilitates resection and joining to complete the 
                     process |CITS: [29208713]|.
                     There are two types of ExoI deficiencies. <i>sbcB/cpeA</i> 
                     mutations suppress the recombination-deficiency phenotype, 
                     the UV and visible light sensitivities, the mitomycin C 
                     sensitivity of exonuclease V-deficient strains |CITS: 
                     [6343375][783113][3911022]|, and result in RecA-dependent 
                     increased expression of plasmid genes |CITS: [7700238]|. 
                     <i>xonA</i> mutations only suppress UV and mitomycin C 
                     sensitivities, suggesting the mutants retain an activity 
                     that prevents suppression of the recombination-deficiency 
                     phenotype |CITS: [6343375][2834321]|. <i>xonA</i> mutations
                     have also been shown to enhance illegitimate recombination 
                     |CITS:[2029968]|.
                     The structure of exonuclease I has been resolved to 2.4 and
                     1.7 &Aring; in its apo form, and to 2.7 &Aring; while bound
                     to the carboxy-terminal portion of SSB |CITS: 
                     [18591666][11101894]|.
                     <i>sbcB</i> insertion mutants were identified in a genetic 
                     screen for genes that are important for survival of 
                     exposure to ionizing radiation (IR).  A <i>sbcB</i> 
                     deletion mutant has a substantial decrease in IR survival 
                     |CITS: [25049088]|.
                     Comments: |CITS: [29259108]|"
