     gene            complement(2077707..2077934)
                     /gene="tsuB"
                     /locus_tag="b2012"
                     /label=tsuB
     CDS             complement(2077707..2077934)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tsuB"
                     /locus_tag="b2012"
                     /product="putative thiosulfate sulfurtransferase"
                     /label=tsuB
                     /note="TsuB shows sequence similarity to the sulfur
                     transfer protein |FRAME: EG12216-MONOMER TusA|; however, 
                     unlike TusA, TsuB does not appear to function in molybdenum
                     cofactor assembly |CITS: [23281480]|. TsuB is implicated in
                     |FRAME: S2O3| metabolism; deletion of <i>tsuB</i> inhibits 
                     the ability of a &Delta;|FRAME: EG10183 <i>cysA</i>| mutant
                     to grow with thiosulfate as sole sulfur source |CITS: 
                     [32779574]|. <i>tsuB</i> is a member of the |FRAME: PC00040
                     CysB| regulon; TsuB is subject to post-transcriptional 
                     control by |FRAME: G7459-MONOMER RppH|-dependent mRNA 
                     degradation; TsuB supports the preferential use of 
                     thiosulfate as a sulfur source in &Delta;<i>rppH</i> cells 
                     |CITS: [32779574]|. TsuB is required for TsuA-mediated 
                     thiosulfate uptake <i>in vivo</i> and the two proteins may 
                     physically interact |CITS: [38656967]|.
                     Several genes described to be responsive to sulfur 
                     availability, including <i>tsuB</i> |CITS: [15659685]|, 
                     were among the most strongly upregulated genes in two 
                     MG1655 lysogens carrying closely related Stx2a phages O104 
                     and PA8 |CITS: [31208335]|.
                     This protein in <i>E. coli</i> was shown to interact with 
                     the enzyme |FRAME: UDPNACETYLGLUCOSAMENOLPYRTRANS-MONOMER| 
                     (MurA) in a protein-enzyme interactions (PEIs) study that 
                     examined the connection between protein-protein 
                     interactions (PPIs) and metabolomics networks studied in 22
                     different environmental conditions. This PEI may have a 
                     metabolic regulatory role in the |FRAME: PWY-6387| pathway,
                     in part because the PEI is conserved across many other 
                     bacterial taxa |CITS: [39903745]|.
                     TsuB: ""<b>t</b>hio<b>s</b>ulfate <b>u</b>ptake"" |CITS: 
                     [32779574]|"
                     /translation="MVIKKLDVVTQVCPFPLIEAKAALAEMVSGDELVIEFDCTQATEA
                     IPQWAAEEGHAITDYQQIGDAAWSITVQKA"
                     /go_component="cytosol [goid 0005829] [evidence IEA] [PMID
                     19054114]"
                     /go_function="protein binding [goid 0005515] [evidence
                     IPI] [PMID 24561554]"
     gene            complement(2077948..2079006)
                     /gene="tsuA"
                     /locus_tag="b2013"
                     /label=tsuA
     CDS             complement(2077948..2079006)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tsuA"
                     /locus_tag="b2013"
                     /product="thiosulfate transporter"
                     /label=tsuA
                     /note="TsuA is an inner membrane protein that imports
                     |FRAME: S2O3| as a sulfur source. Deletion of <i>tsuA</i> 
                     inhibits the ability of strains (&Delta;|FRAME: ABC-7-CPLX 
                     <i>cysPUWA</i>| or &Delta;|FRAME: EG10183 <i>cysA</i>|) 
                     lacking the sulfate/thiosulfate ABC-type transporters to 
                     grow with thiosulfate as sole sulfur source |CITS: 
                     [32923628][32779574]|. |FRAME: EG11894-MONOMER TsuB| is 
                     required for TsuA-mediated thiosulfate uptake <i>in 
                     vivo</i> and the two proteins may physically interact 
                     |CITS: [38656967]|.
                     TsuA is predicted to be an inner membrane protein with nine
                     transmembrane domains; experimental topology analysis 
                     suggests the C terminus is located in the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [15919996]|.
                     <i>tsuA</i> is a member of the |FRAME: PC00040 CysB| 
                     regulon; TsuA may be subject to post-transcriptional 
                     control by |FRAME: G7459-MONOMER RppH|-dependent mRNA 
                     degradation; TsuA supports the preferential use of 
                     thiosulfate as a sulfur source in &Delta;<i>rppH</i> cells 
                     |CITS: [32779574]|.
                     Expression of <i>tsuA</i> is downregulated in response to 
                     cobalt |CITS: [20545747]| and upregulated in response to 
                     hydroquinone |CITS: [27713734]|. Several genes described to
                     be responsive to sulfur availability, including <i>tsuA</i>
                     |CITS: [15659685]|, were among the most strongly 
                     upregulated genes in two MG1655 lysogens carrying closely 
                     related Stx2a phages O104 and PA8 |CITS: [31208335]|.
                     TsuA: ""<b>t</b>hio<b>s</b>ulfate <b>u</b>ptake"" |CITS: 
                     [32779574]|"
