     gene            2088016..2088606
                     /gene="hisH"
                     /locus_tag="b2023"
                     /label=hisH
     CDS             2088016..2088606
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hisH"
                     /locus_tag="b2023"
                     /product="bifunctional imidazole glycerol phosphate
                     synthase subunit HisH / imidazole glycerol phosphate 
                     synthase"
                     /EC_number="4.3.2.10"
                     /label=hisH
                     /note="HisH is the glutamine amidotransferase subunit of 
                     imidazole glycerol phosphate synthase (HisFH). HisFH 
                     catalyzes the fifth step of histidine biosynthesis, as well
                     as generates aminoimidazole carboxamide ribonucleotide, an 
                     intermediate in purine nucleotide biosynthesis.
                     The two components of the HisFH dimer work together to 
                     catalyze the addition of nitrogen from glutamine to the 
                     imidazole ring of phosphoribulosylformimino-AICAR-phosphate
                     to generate aminoimidazole carboxamide ribonucleotide, 
                     D-erythro-imidazole-glycerol-phosphate, and glutamate 
                     |CITS: [8494895]|. Although isolated HisH has no activity, 
                     isolated HisF catalyzes an ammonia-dependent reaction that 
                     uses ammonia as a nitrogen donor in the place of the 
                     physiological donor glutamine |CITS: [8494895]|. The 
                     kinetics of HisFH have been modeled |CITS: [15627387]|. 
                     HisFH is a 1:1 dimer of HisF and HisH |CITS: [8494895]|. 
                     The critical residues involved in subunit interaction in 
                     general and in the specific case of cooperation of the two 
                     active sites have been examined |CITS: 
                     [11208798][16142895]|.
                     The predicted structure of HisFH, which shows a conserved 
                     core region and variable domain has been described |CITS: 
                     [11551184]|.
                     Multiple mutations in <i>hisF</i> and <i>hisH</i>, in 
                     addition to mutations in other genes involved in amino acid
                     biosynthesis, resulted in increased resistance to the 
                     &Beta;-lactam antibiotic carbenicillin |CITS: [39748127]|."
