     gene            complement(2093343..2094749)
                     /gene="gnd"
                     /locus_tag="b2029"
                     /label=gnd
     CDS             complement(2093343..2094749)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gnd"
                     /locus_tag="b2029"
                     /product="6-phosphogluconate dehydrogenase,
                     decarboxylating"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.44"
                     /label=gnd
                     /note="6-phosphogluconate dehydrogenase (6PGDH) is an
                     enzyme of the oxidative branch of the pentose phosphate 
                     pathway.
                     Three crystal structures of the enzyme in complex with 
                     substrate and cosubstrate compounds have been solved. 
                     Binding of NADP<sup>+</sup> may induce a conformational 
                     change in the enzyme. A catalytic mechanism has been 
                     proposed |CITS: [19686854]|.
                     <i>gnd</i> is a highly polymorphic gene within <i>E. 
                     coli</i> populations, likely due to interstrain transfer 
                     and recombination. This may be a result of its proximity to
                     the <i>rfb</i> region, which determines O antigen structure
                     |CITS: [7937867]|.
                     Expression of 6PGDH is growth rate-regulated |CITS: 
                     [383688][6182137][10753455]|. Most of the growth 
                     rate-dependent increase in 6PGDH levels is due to increased
                     transcription, leading to higher mRNA levels |CITS: 
                     [8282686]|. Post-transcriptional regulation |CITS: 
                     [6337127][6329905]| involves a secondary structure element 
                     between codons 67 and 78 of the <i>gnd</i> mRNA |CITS: 
                     [6440141][2465546]|. This region may function by 
                     sequestration of the translation initiation region into an 
                     mRNA secondary structure, thus reducing the efficiency of 
                     translation initiation |CITS: [7592434]|. However, the 
                     effector of this regulatory mechanism has apparently not 
                     yet been identified. <i>truA</i> (<i>hisT</i>) mutants 
                     reduce the growth rate-dependent increase of 6PGDH 
                     expression by post-transcriptional regulation |CITS: 
                     [2407715]|. Growth under acidic conditions upregulates 
                     expression of <i>gnd</i> |CITS: [17026754]|. <i>gnd</i> is 
                     one of the most consistently flux-coupled genes (FCGs), 
                     which are genes whose expression transition patterns upon 
                     perturbations are correlated with their corresponding flux 
                     values |CITS: [23420780]|.
                     Certain growth conditions selected for a deletion mutation 
                     in the promoter region that results in increased 
                     transcription of <i>gnd</i> and increased enzyme activity 
                     |CITS: [2464736]|. An <i>edd gnd</i> double mutant is 
                     unable to grow on gluconate |CITS: [4869212]|. A null 
                     mutation in <i>gnd</i> does not significantly alter the 
                     growth rate. However, cellular metabolism and metabolic 
                     flux is changed |CITS: [12670695][14661115]|; succinate 
                     production is increased during growth on glucose or 
                     glycerol |CITS: [26878126]|. A <i>gnd</i> deletion mutant 
                     shows enhanced ethanol and H<sub>2</sub> production 
                     compared to wild type during anaerobic growth on glycerol 
                     |CITS: [26122736]|, whereas in a different, heavily 
                     engineered strain, overexpression of <i>gnd</i> increases 
                     ethanol and H<sub>2</sub> production |CITS: [26581029]|. 
                     Stasis-induced carbonylation of 6-phosphogluconate 
                     dehydrogenase is increased in <i>lon</i> and <i>hslVU</i> 
                     mutants |CITS: [15937182]|.
                     Exposure to oxidation by peroxyl radicals results in 
                     inactivation of 6PGDH due to oxidations of Trp and Met 
                     residues close to the active site |CITS: [36476946]|. When 
                     all three |FRAME: OXIDATIVEPENT-PWY| enzymes (|FRAME: 
                     GLU6PDEHYDROG-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 6PGLUCONOLACT-MONOMER| and 
                     6PGDH) are exposed to a peroxyl radical oxidation together,
                     the production of NADPH was lower than what is generated by
                     non-oxidized enzymes but not during the initial stages of 
                     oxidation |CITS: [38848786]|.
                     A deletion mutant in <i>gnd</i> leading to inactivation of 
                     the pentose phosphate pathway resulted in increased biofilm
                     formation under planktonic growth conditions relative to 
                     microtiter plates and to the wild type strain |CITS: 
                     [37728257]|.
                     A <i>pfkAB</i> knockout strain (with the 
                     Embden-Meyerhof-Parnas pathway [EMPP] inactivated for 
                     glucose metabolism) subjected to adaptive laboratory 
                     evolution recovered growth to wild-type levels and showed 
                     an improved the Entner-Doudoroff pathway (EDP) for glucose 
                     breakdown due to mutations in <i>ugd-gnd-wbbL-2</i>, 
                     <i>|FRAME: EG10787|</i>, <i>|FRAME: EG11556|</i>, 
                     <i>|FRAME: EG12630|</i>, <i>|FRAME: EG10364|</i>, and 
                     <i>|FRAME: EG10164|</i> |CITS:[36352474]|.
                     Gnd: ""<b>g</b>luco<b>n</b>ate-6-phosphate 
                     <b>d</b>ehydrogenase"" |CITS: [4869212]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8572885][24688678]|"
