     gene            complement(2101818..2103065)
                     /gene="rfbX"
                     /locus_tag="b2037"
                     /label=rfbX
     CDS             complement(2101818..2103065)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rfbX"
                     /locus_tag="b2037"
                     /product="polyisoprenol-linked O16-antigen repeat unit
                     flippase"
                     /label=rfbX
                     /note="RfbX, also known as Wzx, WzxB, and Wzx(O16), is the 
                     flippase that translocates undecaprenylpyrophosphate 
                     (Und-PP) linked O-antigen subunits across the inner 
                     membrane.
                     The genes encoding the enzymes involved in the biosynthesis
                     of O-specific polysaccharides are clustered in the 
                     <i>rfb</i> region. <i>E. coli</i> K-12 is phenotypically 
                     rough and does not express O-antigen due to mutations in 
                     the <i>rfb</i> region; <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655 contains 
                     the <i>rfb-50</i> mutation - an IS5 element which disrupts 
                     |FRAME: EG11986 <i>wbbL</i>| encoding rhamnose transferase;
                     an engineered strain of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 with all 
                     <i>rfb</i> genes intact synthesizes |FRAME: CPD0-2249 O16 
                     antigen| |CITS: [7517391][7512872][7517390]|. An engineered
                     strain of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655 which contains an 
                     intact <i>wbbL</i> (MG1655 <i>wbbL</I><sup>+</sup>) 
                     displays increased resistance to phage P1 and 
                     complement-mediated killing and and an enhanced ability to 
                     colonize and kill <i>Caenorhabditis elegans</i> |CITS: 
                     [23350972]|.
                     RfbX is a member of the Polysaccharide Transporter (PST) 
                     Family |CITS: [9274022][26546518]|.
                     <i>rfbX</i> encodes a protein with 12 predicted 
                     transmembrane segements; an <i>rfbX</i> mutant (more 
                     specifically a mutation in <i>wzx</i> within the cloned 
                     <i>E. coli</i> Dysenteriae 1 O-antigen gene cluster 
                     expressed in a <i>Salmonella enterica</i> strain which 
                     lacks the O-antigen gene cluster) accumulates UndPP-linked 
                     single O antigen units on the inner side of the cytoplasmic
                     membrane |CITS: [8606190]|. Wzx has been widely studied in 
                     bacteria which express O antigens including pathogenic 
                     <i>E. coli</i> (see |CITS: [10574995][20870764]|), 
                     <i>Salmonella enterica</i> (see |CITS: 
                     [1708761][18707624][22497246][25837817]|) and others; for 
                     reviews see |CITS: [23016929][25358682][28696276]|. 
                     Disruption of <i>wzxB</i> in MG1655 <i>wbbL</I><sup>+</sup>
                     results in swollen, filamentous or chained cells that lyse 
                     frequently; these cell shape defects are likely due to 
                     accumulation of Und-PP-linked O-antigen intermediates which
                     in turn limits the availability of Und-P and interferes 
                     with peptidoglycan synthesis |CITS: [27573014]|.
                     <i>rfbX</i> was identified in a screen for genes that 
                     reduce the lethal effects of stress.  An <i>rfbX</i> 
                     insertion mutant is more sensitive than wild type to 
                     mitomycin C and other stresses |CITS: [20128927]|. 
                     <i>rfbX</i>: the acronym rfb was assigned in <i>Salmonella 
                     enterica</i> to designate the gene cluster representing 
                     rough B mutants (see |CITS: [5231414]|. For further 
                     information on bacterial polysaccharide gene nomenclature 
                     see |CITS: [9004408]|."
                     /translation="MNTNKLSLRRNVIYLAVVQGSNYLLPLLTFPYLVRTLGPENFGIF
