     gene            complement(2113641..2115035)
                     /gene="wcaJ"
                     /locus_tag="b2047"
                     /label=wcaJ
     CDS             complement(2113641..2115035)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="wcaJ"
                     /locus_tag="b2047"
                     /product="UDP-glucose:undecaprenyl-phosphate
                     glucose-1-phosphate transferase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.8.31"
                     /label=wcaJ
                     /note="WcaJ belongs to the polyisoprenyl-phosphate 
                     hexose-1-phosphate transferase (PHPT) family and catalyzes 
                     the transfer of glucose-1-phosphate from UDP-glucose to 
                     undecaprenyl-phosphate |CITS: [22408159]|, which is the 
                     initial step of colanic acid biosynthesis in <i>E. coli</i>
                     |CITS: [318640]|.  WcaJ was predicted to catalyze this 
                     reaction based on sequence similarity and its presence in a
                     colanic acid biosynthesis operon |CITS: [8759852]|.
                     Experimental validation led to a revision of the predicted 
                     membrane topology of WcaJ and the PHPT family of enzymes. 
                     The enzyme comprises four canonical transmembrane helices 
                     followed by a cytoplasmic loop and an additional membrane 
                     domain that adopts a helix-break-helix structure that does 
                     not span the membrane, thus leading to cytoplasmic 
                     localization of the C terminus |CITS: [25776537]|. 
                     Inactivation of <i>wcaJ</i> was used to increase the 
                     cellular pool of the colanic acid (CA) synthesis building 
                     block GDP-fucose |CITS: [12084982]|.  A <i>wcaJ</i> mutant 
                     produces nonmucoid colonies in an RcsA-overexpressing 
                     strain background |CITS: [22408159]|.  The C-terminal half 
                     of WcaJ (residues 252-464) is sufficient for CA production 
                     |CITS: [25776537]|.
                     Nomenclature for genes involved in bacterial polysaccharide
                     synthesis is discussed in |CITS: [9004408]|."
