                     /label=wzc
     CDS             complement(2126971..2129133)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="wzc"
                     /locus_tag="b2060"
                     /product="trifunctional protein-tyrosine kinase Wzc /
                     tyrosine kinase / polysaccharide export complex"
                     /label=wzc
                     /note="The |FRAME: G7105| gene encodes a bacterial
                     tyrosine (BY) kinase that regulates production of the 
                     extracellular polysaccharide |FRAME: CPD-21504 ""colanic 
                     acid""| (CA).
                     Purified Wzc, incubated in the presence of ATP, 
                     autophosphorylates on a tyrosine residue; phosphorylated 
                     Wzc is dephosphorylated by |FRAME: G7106-MONOMER Wzb| 
                     |CITS: [10348860]|. Wzc-autophosphorylation negatively 
                     regulates colanic acid biosynthesis, and Wzb phosphatase 
                     activity counteracts this negative regulation |CITS: 
                     [11090276]|. Both the phosphorylated and nonphosphorylated 
                     forms of Wzc are required for wild-type synthesis of CA in 
                     a CA-producing K-12 strain; Wzc phosphorylation influences 
                     the size distribution of CA; Wzc has an ATPase activity in 
                     addition to its kinase activity |CITS: 
                     [17254603][17627778]|. Wzc also catalyzes tyrosine 
                     phosphorylation of |FRAME: UGD-MONOMER| (Ugd) and this 
                     phosphorylation stimulates Ugd activity |CITS: [12851388]|.
                     Wzc consists of an N-terminal domain containing two 
                     transmembrane &alpha;-helices separated by a periplasmic 
                     region, and a C-terminal cytoplasmic domain containing a 
                     Walker A and B ATP binding motif; Wzc contains 18 tyrosine 
                     residues, 7 located in the N-terminal domain and 11 in the 
                     C-terminal domain (6 of which are located in a cluster 
                     between Y705 and Y715); the C-terminal domain harbours the 
                     phosphorylation sites |CITS: [11751920][12138098]|. Wzc can
                     oligomerize <i>in vivo</i> and the C-terminal domain is 
                     implicated in this process |CITS: [12138098]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [20633230]|).
                     The crystal structure of the non-phosphorylated cytoplasmic
                     domain of Wzc (Wzc<sub>cyt</sub>K540M) has been reported: 
                     Wzc<sub>cyt</sub>K540M forms a ring shaped octamer; each 
                     subunit binds ADP and a cation; the &alpha;-phosphate of 
                     ADP interacts with Tyr569 and in each subunit Tyr715 is 
                     bound in the active site of the neighbouring subunit; the 
                     structure supports a mechanism involving intermolecular 
                     autophosphorylation on the C-terminal tyrosine cluster but 
                     does not support an intramolecular step involving Tyr569 
                     |CITS: [20633230]|.
                     A two-step model for phosphorylation involving both intra- 
                     and intermolecular phosphorylation has been proposed |CITS:
                     [11751920]|, as has a single-step intermolecular mechanism 
                     |CITS: [20633230]|.
                     Colanic acid is not produced when <i>wzc</i> is disrupted 
                     in a K-12 CA-producing strain |CITS: [11090276]|. 
                     Involvement of Wzc in production of the capsule was shown 
                     in other <i>E. coli</i> strains, including serotype K30 
                     (the prototype for Wzy-dependent capsule biosynthesis 
                     |CITS: [10094716][10200954][11053445][12426330]|. Cryo-EM 
                     experiments show that Wzc from <i>E coli</i> serotype K30 
                     forms homotetramers |CITS: [16172129]|, and Wza and Wzc 
                     form a complex |CITS: [17283336]|. <i>E. coli</i> K-12 Wzc 
                     can rescue K30 capsule expression in a mutant O9a:K30 
                     strain only in combination with the cognate K-12 Wza 
                     protein |CITS: [16030241]|.
                     Nomenclature for genes involved in bacterial polysaccharide
                     synthesis is discussed in |CITS: [9004408]|.
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [16756484][19525115][19189200]|"
