     gene            complement(2129585..2130724)
                     /gene="wza"
                     /locus_tag="b2062"
                     /label=wza
     CDS             complement(2129585..2130724)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="wza"
                     /locus_tag="b2062"
                     /product="bifunctional outer membrane polysaccharide
                     export protein Wza / polysaccharide export complex"
                     /label=wza
                     /note="<i>wza</i> is located within a cluster of genes
                     that are responsible for production of the extracellular 
                     polysaccharide, |FRAME: CPD-21504 ""colanic acid""| (CA) 
                     and based on sequence similarity it may be involved in a CA
                     export system |CITS: [8759852]|. Wza is a member of the 
                     outer membrane auxiliary (OMA) protein family |CITS: 
                     [9274022]|. Much of the information known about Wza comes 
                     from experiments characterizing Wza from the group 1 
                     capsule producing strain <i>Escherichia coli</i> serotype 
                     K30 (Wza<sub>K30</sub>); <i>wza</i> from <i>E. coli</i> 
                     K-12 can complement a <i>wza</i> defect in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     K30 |CITS: [16030241]|. The &Delta;<i>wza</i> mutant of an 
                     |FRAME: EG10820| overexpressing, CA producing strain of 
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655 grows as misshapen rods and 
                     spheres |CITS: [34159784]|.
                     Wza<sub>K30</sub> is a surface exposed outer membrane 
                     lipoprotein that forms a multimeric 'secretin-like' 
                     structure |CITS: [10200954], [10619844]|. Insertional 
                     inactivation of <i>wza</i> in <i>E. coli</i> strain E69 
                     serotype O9a:K30 results in loss of the K30 capsular layer 
                     although K30 polysaccharide is still produced |CITS: 
                     [10200954]|. Wza<sub>K30</sub> forms ring-like octameric 
                     structures arranged as tetramers of dimers |CITS: 
                     [14522970], [15090537], [14993692]|. Wza<sub>K30</sub> 
                     contains a transmembrane domain that is a novel 
                     &alpha;-helical barrel structure and a large periplasmic 
                     domain that forms the bulk of the structure. The protein 
                     has a large central cavity that is open to the 
                     extracellular region |CITS: [17086202]|.
                     Wza plays a role in protecting <i>E. coli</i> K-12 against 
                     the <i>Vibrio cholerae</i> type 6 secretion system 
                     effector, TseH |CITS: [32094588]|.
                     Wza<sub>K30</sub> is believed to form the outer membrane 
                     translocation channel for K30 capsular antigen. K30 
                     polysaccharide intermediates have been trapped and 
                     identified in the interior of multimeric Wza structures 
                     |CITS: [24843147]|.
                     Wza<sub>K30</sub> forms a complex with Wzc<sub>K30</sub>; 
                     the Wza-Wzc complex spans the periplasm, linking the inner 
                     membrane to the outer membrane |CITS: 
                     [14522970][17283336]|. The periplasmic domain of 
                     Wza<sub>K30</sub> interacts with the periplasmic domain of 
                     Wzc<sub>K30</sub> |CITS: [17283336]|.
                     Integration and assembly of Wza into the outer membrane 
                     does not require the |FRAME: CPLX0-3933 ""Bam""| or the 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-7976 ""Tam""| complex; Wza octamers can be 
                     identified in the outer membrane of a BamA depletion strain
                     |CITS: [25976323]|
                     Nomenclature for genes involved in bacterial polysaccharide
                     synthesis is discussed in |CITS: [9004408]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17275308]|"
