     gene            complement(2135115..2135696)
                     /gene="dcd"
                     /locus_tag="b2065"
                     /label=dcd
     CDS             complement(2135115..2135696)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dcd"
                     /locus_tag="b2065"
                     /product="dCTP deaminase"
                     /EC_number="3.5.4.13"
                     /label=dcd
                     /note="dCTP deaminase (Dcd) catalyzes the deamination of
                     dCTP to dUTP in the main pathway for dTTP biosynthesis in 
                     <i>E. coli</i>, |FRAME: PWY-7187| |CITS: [5541540]|.
                     dCTP deaminase is unusual among nucleoside and nucleotide 
                     deaminases in that it does not require a metal ion for 
                     catalysis.  Crystal structures of wild-type and mutant 
                     forms of the enzyme in complexes with the substrate, 
                     product or inhibitor have been determined |CITS: 
                     [15539408][17651436][17996716]|.  Site-directed mutagenesis
                     of predicted active site residues produced catalytically 
                     inactive enzymes |CITS: [15539408]|.  The true substrate of
                     dCTP deaminase appears to be the 
                     dCTP&middot;Mg<sup>2+</sup> complex; a catalytic mechanism 
                     was proposed |CITS: [15539408]|.  Mutations in the S111 and
                     E138 residues alter the enzyme's inhibition by dTTP |CITS: 
                     [17996716]|.
                     <i>dcd</i> mutants require thymidine supplementation for 
                     optimal growth.  <i>deoA</i> mutations bypass the 
                     requirement for thymidine supplementation by enabling 
                     diversion of an intermediate of the |FRAME: PWY0-181| 
                     |CITS: [8157589]|.  Mutations in <i>dcd</i> suppress the 
                     temperature-sensitive lethal phenotype of a <i>dut-1</i> 
                     &Delta;<i>xthA</i> double mutant |CITS: [6282808]|, the 
                     temperature-sensitive <i>dut-22</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [1624437]|, and the synthetic lethality of a 
                     <i>dut-1/dut-11 rec</i> double mutant |CITS: [14982624]|. 
                     However, various <i>dcd</i> mutations are unable to 
                     suppress the lethal phenotype of a <i>dut</i> insertion 
                     mutant |CITS: [2830228]|.
                     A <i>dcd</i> transposon insertion mutant showed increased 
                     expression of <i>dinD</i> |CITS: [15489426]|.  Conversely, 
                     expression of <i>umuC</i> (as well as <i>dinD</i>) after 
                     DNA damage is decreased in a <i>dcd</i> mutant background, 
                     suggesting a decreased SOS response in the mutant |CITS: 
                     [25824947]|.  A <i>dcd</i> insertion mutant grown in rich 
                     medium shows altered dNTP pools - increased levels of dCTP 
                     and reduced levels of dGTP - and has a mutator phenotype 
                     during replication |CITS: [23218950][24269257]|.  A 
                     <i>dcd</i> deletion mutant is hypersensitive to killing by 
                     exogenous cytidine, adenosine, or guanosine; this can be 
                     reversed by exogenous addition of thymidine |CITS: 
                     [24633874]|.  A <i>dcd ndk</i> double mutant shows a more 
                     extreme dNTP pool imbalance and a significantly increased 
                     mutator phenotype compared to the <i>dcd</i> single mutant 
                     |CITS: [26789486]|.
                     PaxA: |CITS: [5541540]|
                     Dus: ""<i><b>du</b>t</i> <b>s</b>uppressor"" |CITS: 
                     [1624437][1324907]|
                     Dcd: ""<b>d</b>eoxy<b>c</b>ytidine <b>d</b>eaminase"" 
                     |CITS: [177407]|"
