     gene            complement(2140173..2141021)
                     /gene="alkA"
                     /locus_tag="b2068"
                     /label=alkA
     CDS             complement(2140173..2141021)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="alkA"
                     /locus_tag="b2068"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA-3-methyladenine glycosylase 2 / 
                     DNA-3-methyladenine glycosylase"
                     /EC_number="3.2.2.21"
                     /label=alkA
                     /note="The <i>alkA</i> gene product is known as 
                     3-methyl-adenine-DNA glycosylase II and is induced by 
                     exposure to alkylating agents as part of the cell's 
                     adaptive response to damage caused by alkylation.  The 
                     purified enzyme acts preferentially on dsDNA to remove 
                     3-methyl-adenine and 3-methyl-guanine with equal efficiency
                     and also removes, somewhat less efficiently, 
                     7-methyl-adenine, 7-methyl-guanine, 2-methyl-thymine and 
                     their ethylated derivatives |CITS: [6287922]|.  AlkA is one
                     of two (along with 3-methyl-adenine DNA glycosylase I, the 
                     <i>tag</i> gene product) glycosylases that remove 
                     3-methyl-adenines.  Glycosylase II differs in two ways: it 
                     can remove several other methylated bases besides 
                     3-methyl-adenine and it is inducible to a 20-fold higher 
                     level in cells exposed to alkylating agents |CITS: 
                     [3052269]|.
                     Additional roles for AlkA were found through studies on 
                     removal of deaminated adenine residues and studies on 
                     repair of oxidative damage.  AlkA was found to be the 
                     hypoxanthine DNA glycosylase |CITS: [98523]| in <i>E. 
                     coli</i>.  Hypoxanthine is potentially mutagenic as it can 
                     result in A-T to G-C transition mutations upon replication.
                      Studies on oxidative damage repair found that AlkA can 
                     also remove thymine residues oxidized in the methyl group 
                     such as 5-formyluracil (5-foU) and 5-hydroxymethyl uracil 
                     (5-hmU).  5-foU and 5-hmU are major products resulting from
                     exposure of DNA to gamma irradiation and they also occur 
                     spontaneously during normal aerobic metabolism |CITS: 
                     [7982966]|.
                     AlkA was also found to be the DNA glycosylase involved in 
                     the removal of xanthine and oxanine which are deamination 
                     products formed as a result of exposure of guanine residues
                     to nitrogen oxides |CITS: [12434002]|.
                     Purified AlkA excises the mutagenic, |FRAME: 
                     ACROLEIN|-derived, exocyclic adduct 
                     1,<i>N</i><sup>6</sup>-&alpha;-hydroxypropanoadenine (HPA);
                     AlkA repairs HPA <i>in vivo</i> without prior Ada induction
                     |CITS: [39667663]|."
