     gene            2156357..2157760
                     /gene="baeS"
                     /locus_tag="b2078"
                     /label=baeS
     CDS             2156357..2157760
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="baeS"
                     /locus_tag="b2078"
                     /product="bifunctional sensor histidine kinase BaeS / 
                     protein-histidine autokinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.13.3"
                     /label=baeS
                     /note="BaeS is the sensor histidine kinase of the BaeSR 
                     two-component system.
                     BaeS is a predicted integral membrane protein with sequence
                     similarity to the sensor kinase family of proteins 
                     including a conserved histidine (His-250) which is presumed
                     to be the site of autophosphorylation; overproduced BaeS 
                     autophosphorylates in the presence of ATP and transfers a 
                     phosphate to its cognate response regulator, BaeR <i>in 
                     vitro</i>; it is also able to phosphorylate purified OmpR 
                     with low efficiency |CITS: [8282725]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [15522865]|).
                     BaeSR is implicated in the adaption to envelope stress 
                     |CITS: [12354228]|. The <i>baeS1::</i>Tn10<i>cam</i> 
                     mutation is a gain-of-function mutation which induces 
                     expression of a <i>spy-lacZ</i> transcriptional fusion in a
                     Cpx-independent manner; inactivation of <i>baeS</I> 
                     prevents induction of <i>spy</i> expression in response to 
                     spheroplasting |CITS: [12354228]|. The <i>baeS51</i> 
                     missense mutation (R104C) is a gain of function mutation 
                     which induces expression of the TolC-dependent |FRAME: 
                     TRANS-CPLX-202 MdtABC efflux pump| and contributes to 
                     antibiotic resistance in a strain lacking the major efflux 
                     pumps |FRAME: TRANS-CPLX-201 AcrAB| and |FRAME: CPLX0-2141 
                     AcrEF| |CITS: [33972351]|.
                     <i>baeS</i>: <U>b</U>acterial <U>a</U>daptive 
                     r<U>e</U>sponse, <U>s</U>ensor kinase |CITS: [8282725]|. 
                     <i>Note</i>: bacterial histidine kinases function as 
                     homodimers; both <i>cis</i>- and 
                     <i>trans</i>-autophosphorylation mechanisms have been 
                     documented (see |CITS: [19575571][25982528]|). In the 
                     absence of experimental evidence BaeS is represented as a 
                     monomer in the EcoCyc database."
