     gene            complement(2166402..2167757)
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="gatC"
                     /locus_tag="b2092"
                     /label=gatC
     CDS             complement(join(2166402..2166843,2166844..2167757))
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="gatC"
                     /locus_tag="b2092"
                     /product="galactitol-specific PTS enzyme IIC component"
                     /label=gatC
                     /note="<i>gatC</i> sequenced from a wild-type isolate of
                     <i>E. coli</i>, strain EC3132, encodes a very hydrophobic 
                     protein with similarity to Enzyme IIC of the fructose PTS 
                     |CITS: [8955298]|.
                     Resequencing of multiple isolates of the MG1655 strain has 
                     identified several genetic variations compared to the 
                     reference sequence, including a 2 bp insertion in 
                     <i>gatC</i> in strain ATCC700926 and CGSC7740 |CITS: 
                     [22081388]|. The resulting frameshift in <i>gatC</i> 
                     results in truncation after 311 residues and is predicted 
                     to disrupt the function of the galactitol permease |CITS: 
                     [22081388]|. Galactitol (also known as dulcitol) is a 
                     substrate in the |FRAME: Phenotype-Microarray-Media 
                     ""Biolog phenotype microarray system""| (PM1 well A12) and 
                     it is likely that differences in the growth phenotype of 
                     <i>E. coli</i> MG1655 on this particular carbon source are 
                     due to the genetic variation described above.
                     <a href=""http://ecoliwiki.net/colipedia/index.php/Categor 
                     y:Strain:MG1655#Resequencing_of_MG1655_and_comparison_to_th
                     e_reference_strain"">This EcoliWiki page</a> summarizes the
                     sequence differences that have been reported."
