     gene            complement(2168538..2169800)
                     /gene="gatZ"
                     /locus_tag="b2095"
                     /label=gatZ
     CDS             complement(2168538..2169800)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gatZ"
                     /locus_tag="b2095"
                     /product="bifunctional D-tagatose 6-phosphate 4-epimerase
                     2 / D-tagatose 6-phosphate 4-epimerase"
                     /EC_number="5.1.3.40"
                     /label=gatZ
                     /note="The GatZ protein has no catalytic activity, but may 
                     stabilize the tagatose-1,6-bisphosphate aldolase |FRAME: 
                     TAGAALDOL2-MONOMER GatY| as a heterodimer. GatZ appears to 
                     be required for full activity of GatY, and thus may have a 
                     chaperone-like function |CITS: [11976750]|. However, <i>E. 
                     coli's</i> <i>gatZ</i> gene shows high sequence similarity 
                     for genes with tagatose-6-phosphate epimerase activity in 
                     other Gram negative bacteria: the <i>gatZ</i> of |FRAME: 
                     TAX-571|, the <i>tagE</i> gene of |FRAME: TAX-382| and the 
                     <i>agaZ</i> of |FRAME: TAX-358| |CITS: 
                     [15257457][30879203]|.
                     Structural analysis with AlphaFold and Rosetta predicted a 
                     structure with a Zn<sup>2+</sup> cofactor and conserved 
                     amino acid residues in an active site that is structurally 
                     similar and mechanistically consistent with known C4 
                     epimerases; another putative tagatose-1,6-bisphosphate 
                     aldolase chaperone |FRAME: G7631-MONOMER KbaZ| also had 
                     similar predicted structure and mechanisms |CITS: 
                     [39999377]|.
                     A double deletion mutant of 
                     &Delta;<i>pfkA</i>&Delta;<i>zwf</i>, which removed 
                     competing pathways from D-glucose-6-P to the pentose 
                     phosphate pathway and from D-fructose-6-P to glycolysis, 
                     resulted in the production of D-tagatose, indicating a 
                     natural D-tagatose pathway in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655 
                     |CITS: [39999377]|. However, the triple mutant 
                     &Delta;<i>pfkA</i>&Delta;<i>zwf</i>&Delta;<i>gatZ</i> was 
                     not able to produce D-tagatose. Additional mutational 
                     analyses with these and other enzymes, including under high
                     cell densities, indicated that GatZ as well as KbaZ, could 
                     produce D-tagatose when |FRAME: G7187-MONOMER| is present 
                     to dephosphorylate D-tagatose-6-P |CITS: [39999377]|.
                     In a random transposon mutagenesis screen, <i>gatZ</i> was 
                     identified as a gene whose inactivation is beneficial for 
                     anaerobic hydrogen production from glycerol. A 
                     &Delta;<i>gatZ</i> mutant strain grows slightly faster than
                     the parent strain under anaerobic conditions |CITS: 
                     [25567513]|."
