                     /label=thiD
     CDS             complement(2177195..2177995)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="thiD"
                     /locus_tag="b2103"
                     /product="trifunctional bifunctional
                     hydroxymethylpyrimidine kinase/phosphomethylpyrimidine 
                     kinase / hydroxymethylpyrimidine kinase / 
                     phosphomethylpyrimidine kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.49; 2.7.4.7"
                     /label=thiD
                     /note="The bifunctional hydroxymethylpyrimidine (HMP) 
                     kinase/phosphomethylpyrimidine (HMP-P) kinase (ThiD) is 
                     involved in the biosynthesis of the essential cofactor 
                     thiamine pyrophosphate.  The two phosphorylation reactions 
                     appear to be successive, with transient formation of HMP-P 
                     |CITS: [Tetra54-15983][10075431]|.
                     Modeling of the phosphorylation reactions based on the 
                     crystal structure of the <i>Salmonella</i> Typhimurium 
                     enzyme suggest an inline replacement mechanism |CITS: 
                     [11839308]|.
                     While |CITS: [10075431]| reported that ThiD is a monomer 
                     (31 kDa) in solution, |CITS: [Tetra54-15983]| suggested 
                     that ThiD as well as PdxK (which is in fact a homodimer) 
                     are homotetramers.  The <i>Salmonella</i> homolog is 91% 
                     identical to <i>E. coli</i> ThiD and forms a homodimer.
                     A <i>thiD</i> mutant requires thiamine supplementation 
                     |CITS: [6276219]|.  <i>E. coli</i> encodes two enzymes with
                     HMP kinase activity: ThiD (described here) and |FRAME: 
                     PDXK-CPLX PdxK|.  A <i>thiD pdxK</i> double mutant lacks 
                     all HMP kinase activity |CITS: [8885414][10075431]|. Under 
                     anaerobiosis, FNR represses <i>thiD</i> expression, but it 
                     is not known if this regulation is direct or indirect  
                     |CITS: [12754220]|.
                     ThiD: |CITS: [6276219]|<br>ThiJ: |CITS: [8885414]| Reviews:
                     |CITS: [10382260][26443755]|"
