     gene            2187779..2189812
                     /gene="metG"
                     /locus_tag="b2114"
                     /label=metG
     CDS             2187779..2189812
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="metG"
                     /locus_tag="b2114"
                     /product="bifunctional L-homocysteine cyclase /
                     methionine--tRNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.1.1.10"
                     /label=metG
                     /note="Methionine-tRNA ligase (MetRS) is a member of the
                     family of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, which interpret the 
                     genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their 
                     specific tRNA molecules. The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis. MetRS belongs to the Class I aminoacyl-tRNA 
                     synthetases; apart from sequence motifs whithin the active 
                     site, the different enzymes show little similarity in their
                     primary amino acid sequences |CITS: [2203971][7647112]|.
                     The biochemical properties and reaction mechanism of MetRS 
                     have been studied extensively; e.g. |CITS: [4297674][46217 
                     06][4581817][4581818][4581819][4592651][4361287][4600363][4
                     365501][168070][182214][323013][336037][193563][348471][699
                     7291][7044416][6756919][6337846][3061464][2658972][1647199]
                     |. MetRS acylates both initiator tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> and 
                     elongator tRNA<sup>Met</sup>; acylation of 
                     tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> appears to be more efficient |CITS: 
                     [216545]|. MetRS contains one zinc atom per active site 
                     that is coordinated by four cysteine residues which are 
                     essential for activity |CITS: 
                     [8460131][8515465][8515466][8344912]|.
                     Crystal structures of MetRS have been determined |CITS: [7 
                     019723][7042987][6363712][2254937][10600385][11243794][1294
                     6347][19837083]|. The structural domains can be expressed 
                     separately and can reconstitute active MetRS enzyme |CITS: 
                     [1988033][9473497]|. The C-terminal domain can function 
                     independently for specific binding of the tRNA anticodon 
                     stem-loop |CITS: [7612631]|. The ATP binding site was 
                     identified by affinity labeling |CITS: [2271710]|. 
                     Catalysis is proposed to involve an induced fit mechanism 
                     |CITS: [10587461]|. The evaluation of enzymes containing 
                     point mutations in residues that may link the anticodon 
                     binding motif to the catalytic domain |CITS: 
                     [10587461][19772352]| as well as comparisons between 
                     crystal structures of the apo- and methionine-bound enzyme 
                     |CITS: [11243794]| support this hypothesis. Molecular 
                     dynamics simulations confirm significant motility of the 
                     enzyme, suggesting that Trp461 mediates the distribution of
                     structural ensembles |CITS: [17510965]| and confirming the 
                     pathways of communication between distant sites |CITS: 
                     [17898174][18842003]|. The methionine binding site was 
                     predicted |CITS: [15388861]|, and non-hydrolyzable ATP 
                     analogues and methionine and methionyl adenylate analogues 
                     have been used to study conformational changes during 
                     catalysis |CITS: [1989972][12946347][16787318]|.
                     Regions within MetRS that interact with tRNA<sup>fMet</sup>
                     |CITS: 
                     [3913464][3094575][3118944][3122828][3320968][1702021]| and
                     tRNA<sup>Met</sup> |CITS: [3109475]| have been determined 
                     |CITS: [7522052]|. The Trp461 residue may interact directly
                     with the tRNA anticodon loop |CITS: [2186810]|; Asp391 may 
                     interact directly with U36 |CITS: [8399196]|. Suppressor 
                     enzymes that are able to aminoacylate methionine amber tRNA
                     carry mutations in the vicinity of Trp461 |CITS: 
                     [1986377][8411169]|. Site-directed mutagenesis and analysis
                     of the mutant enzymes has been used to study the domain 
                     structure, catalytic properties and the roles of active 
                     site residues of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [2477552][2690943][21175197]|. Lys335 is required for 
                     methionine activation |CITS: [1847216]|; Tyr358 |CITS: 
                     [1654323]| contributes to the ATP-PP<sub>i</sub> exchange 
                     reaction. All residues within the 
                     <sub>332</sub>KMSKS<sub>336</sub> motif |CITS: [7932711]| 
                     and His21 and His24 within the N-terminal HLGH motif |CITS:
                     [8532520]| are important for methionyl adenylate formation.
                     Asp55 and Arg233 appear to play a role in stabilizing the 
                     transition state for formation of the methionine adenylate 
                     |CITS: [1911742]|. His301 and Trp305 are required for the 
                     interaction with methionine |CITS: [1911742][1959615]|, and
                     Trp305 and Tyr15 serve to discriminate against homocysteine
                     |CITS: [8265588]|. The conserved Pro14 within the 
                     nucleotide binding fold may open an amino acid binding 
                     cleft |CITS: [1304356]|, and a Leu13Gly |CITS: [16801548]| 
                     and other selected mutations |CITS: [19706454]| allow use 
                     of azidonorleucine as a substrate |CITS: [19837083]|. 
                     Arg395 |CITS: [1751493]|, Asp391 |CITS: [8399196]|, Asp449 
                     and Asp456 |CITS: [8411169]| play roles in tRNA 
                     recognition. Mutations in the C-terminal domain peptide 
                     appendix affect interactions with the acceptor stem of 
                     tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> and enzyme dimerization |CITS: 
                     [8241156][12390027]|. Ile29 appears to be involved in 
                     discrimination against tRNA<sup>Thr</sup>GGU |CITS: 
                     [9856352]|. Residues within the CP1 domain that contains 
                     the zinc-binding peptide contribute to the stabilization of
                     the transition state |CITS: [7495798]|.
                     Specificity determinants within tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> that 
                     are important for recognition by MetRS have been 
                     investigated |CITS: [4568813][4575301][4607244][331263][14 
                     133][336359][6338482][6346266][6359155][6395901][6389181][3
                     892480][2412892][3910101][3513829][3092857][2480897][211240
                     6][1856854][1408786][8421312][8555234][21482813]|. The 
                     acceptor stem |CITS: [8421312]| and the anticodon loop 
                     including the wobble base play an essential role. Unlike 
                     CysRS and other Class I tRNA synthethases, MetRS does not 
                     discriminate against an unpaired C-A that is unique to the 
                     1-72 position of tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> |CITS: [22184460]|. 
                     MetRS can reject misactivated homocysteine by 
                     tRNA-independent pre-transfer editing |CITS: [427110][7024 
                     910][2191291][8422964][8454625][8265588][8679580]|. The 
                     homocysteine-AMP intermediate is destroyed in an 
                     intramolecular cyclization reaction in which the thiolate 
                     side chain of homocysteine displaces the AMP group, forming
                     homocysteine-thiolactone |CITS: [7024910]|. A C-terminal 
                     region of MetRS is suggested to play a role in editing 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [7918366]|.
                     MetRS expression is upregulated under methionine-limited 
                     growth conditions |CITS: [4551738]| and by reduced 
                     acylation levels of tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> |CITS: [1097419]|. 
                     MetRS levels are coupled to the growth rate |CITS: 
                     [1097418]|.
                     The <i>metG</i> gene encoding MetRS has been cloned |CITS: 
                     [6756915][6094501]|. Overproduction of MetRS leads to 
                     increased levels of adenylylated bis(5'-nucleosidyl) 
                     tetraphosphates (Ap<sub>4</sub>N) in the cell |CITS: 
                     [2554306]|.
                     A transposon insertion into the the C-terminal coding 
                     region of <i>metG</i> results in a 10,000-fold higher 
                     frequency of persister formation than wild type |CITS: 
                     [22802628]|. A laboratory experimental evolution study 
                     identified mutations in MetG, including one with a 12-bp 
                     deletion (positions 1726 to 1737) referred to as 
                     <i>metG*</i>, that conferred hyper-persister phenotypes 
                     |CITS: [31964772]|. MetG* cells exposed to antibiotics 
                     become more persistent as they enter stationary phase 
                     compared to wild-type cells with |FRAME: CPLX0-2881| and 
                     |FRAME: G7524-MONOMER uncharacterized yqgE| playing major 
                     roles in this persistence phenotype |CITS: [39506104]|. 
                     Inhibitors of MetRS activity |CITS: [9934462][10360737][11 
                     327600][12643918][14642576][15951176][16824759]|, 
                     L-methionine analogs that can be activated by MetRS |CITS: 
                     [11478942][11752401]|, and tRNAs that can be mischarged by 
                     MetRS |CITS: [21482813]| have been identified.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [2126467][1935928][8199241][7530434][89558 
                     98][11860363][21643559][23075299]|"
