     gene            2208345..2209076
                     /gene="mlrA"
                     /locus_tag="b2127"
                     /label=mlrA
     CDS             2208345..2209076
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mlrA"
                     /locus_tag="b2127"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional activator MlrA"
                     /label=mlrA
                     /note="MlrA is a regulator of curli production in an avian 
                     pathogenic <i>Escherichia coli</i> strain and in 
                     <i>Salmonella enterica</i> serovar Typhimurium |CITS: 
                     [11489123]|.  Shiga toxin-encoding prophages have been 
                     observed to integrate at the <i>mlrA</i> gene of 
                     enterohemorrhagic <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [11111050]|.
                     MlrA is a member of the MerR family, containing the 
                     conserved N-terminal DNA-binding domain present in members 
                     of this family |CITS: [11489123][20874755]|.  On the other 
                     hand, its C-terminal domain, probably used by a 
                     yet-to-be-identified effector, showed no similarity to any 
                     of the hitherto-characterized MerR family members |CITS: 
                     [20874755]|.
                     MlrA binds an AAAGTTGTACA(12N)TGCACAATTTT palindromic 
                     sequence and probably induces DNA underwinding, as in the 
                     case of MerR |CITS: [20874755]|.  Based on the site 
                     identified on the <i>csgD</i>p1 promoter, sites for another
                     four genes were identified, two of which are 
                     transcriptional regulators |CITS: [20874755]|.  MlrA is 
                     found at a higher level in the hierarchy of the 
                     transcription factor network |CITS: [20874755][20491932]|. 
                     MlrA is part of a signaling cascade that controls curli 
                     biosynthesis, in which |FRAME: C-DI-GMP| (c-di-GMP) 
                     regulates through the diguanylate cyclase/phosphodiesterase
                     (DGC/PDE) pair |FRAME: EG12396 DgcE|/|FRAME: EG12252 PdeH| 
                     (module I) and the activity of the |FRAME: G6673 
                     DgcM|/|FRAME: G6639 PdeR| pair (module II), which forms a 
                     signaling complex with MlrA. PdeR functions as a connector 
                     and a trigger enzyme in local c-di-GMP signaling; its 
                     direct inhibition of DgcM is relieved when it binds and 
                     degrades c-di-GMP, allowing DgcM to activate the 
                     transcription factor MlrA |CITS: [23708798]|.
                     A positive correlation between cellular growth and the copy
                     number of the protein MlrA has been reported |CITS: 
                     [35694815]|.
                     Expression of the <i>mlrA</i> gene is RpoS-regulated in an 
                     avian pathogenic <i>E. coli</i> strain |CITS: [11489123]|. 
                     MlrA: ""<b>M</b>erR-<b>l</b>ike <b>r</b>egulator <b>A</b>""
                     |CITS: [11489123]|
                     The MlrA binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [37251514]|"
                     /translation="MALYTIGEVALLCDINPVTLRAWQRRYGLLKPQRTDGGHRLFNDA
