     gene            2215664..2217379
                     /gene="dld"
                     /locus_tag="b2133"
                     /label=dld
     CDS             2215664..2217379
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dld"
                     /locus_tag="b2133"
                     /product="bifunctional quinone-dependent D-lactate
                     dehydrogenase / D-lactate dehydrogenase (quinone)"
                     /EC_number="1.1.5.12"
                     /label=dld
                     /note="D-lactate dehydrogenase (Dld) is a FAD-dependent 
                     peripheral membrane dehydrogenase catalysing the oxidation 
                     of D-lactate to pyruvate. Dld is a respiratory enzyme; 
                     electrons derived from D-lactate oxidation are transferred 
                     to the membrane soluble quinone pool |CITS: 
                     [4582730][4575624][3013300]|.
                     D-lactate dehydrogenase functions as a primary 
                     dehydrogenase in aerobic and anaerobic respiratory chains 
                     <i>in vitro</i>. Electron transfer from D-lactate to oxygen
                     under aerobic conditions and electron transfer from 
                     D-lactate to nitrate under anaerobic conditions depends on 
                     the presence of a quinone |CITS: [195602]|. Anaerobic 
                     &beta;-galactoside transport in whole cells and membrane 
                     vesicles can be coupled to the oxidation of D-lactate with 
                     fumarate as a terminal acceptor |CITS: [4587250]|. 
                     Proteoliposomes reconstituted with purified cytochrome 
                     <i>bo</i> oxidase, ubiquinone-8 and D-lactate dehydrogenase
                     generate an H<sup>+</sup> electrochemical gradient 
                     (negative inside) in the presence of D-lactate. Studies 
                     using strains with defined respiratory enzyme composition 
                     suggest that the electron flow from D-lactate dehydrogenase
                     to ubiquinone is not electrogenic |CITS: [3013300]|. 
                     Membrane vesicles prepared from Dld negative mutants do not
                     catalyse D-lactate oxidation |CITS: [4343963]|.
                     The crystal structure has been solved at 1.9 &Aring; 
                     resolution. The protein contains 3 discontinuous domains - 
                     a FAD binding domain, a cap domain and a membrane binding 
                     domain. The membrane domain of Dld has an electropositive 
                     surface; Dld binds to the membrane through electrostatic 
                     force |CITS: [10944213][2185834][8268803]|.
                     D-lactate dehydrogenase was widely used in experiments 
                     investigating the mechanism for energy coupling between 
                     respiration and active transport (see articles by |CITS: 
                     [4363926]|, |CITS: [4275121]| and |CITS: [4620043]|). <i>E.
                     coli</i> K-12 also contains a membrane bound |FRAME: 
                     L-LACTDEHYDROGFMN-MONOMER ""L-lactate dehydrogenase""| and 
                     a soluble NAD-linked fermentative |FRAME: 
                     DLACTDEHYDROGNAD-MONOMER ""D-lactate dehydrogenase""|. 
                     Review: |CITS: [2655708]|"
