                     /product="putative multidrug resistance outer membrane
                     protein MdtQ"
                     /label=mdtQ
                     /note="Sequence analysis suggests <i>mdtQ</i> encodes the
                     outer membrane factor of a multidrug efflux system; 
                     deletion of <i>mdtQ</i> from strain W3110 results in 
                     increased susceptibility to acriflavin, puromycin, and 
                     tetraphenylarsonium chloride |CITS: [11257026]|.
                     Note: EcoGene indicates that <i>mdtQ</i> is a pseudogene in
                     strain MG1655 and that intact alleles are present in many 
                     <i>E. coli</i> strains. The mutant phenotype observed by 
                     |CITS: [11257026]| may be due to the presence of a 
                     functional gene in the strain used by these authors. 
                     Sequence differences between W3110 and MG1655 have been 
                     documented in |CITS: [16738553]|; <i>mdtQ</i> was not one 
                     of the 8 confirmed sequence differences noted."
     gene            2222461..2222544
                     /gene="yohP"
                     /locus_tag="b4679"
                     /label=yohP
     CDS             2222461..2222544
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yohP"
                     /locus_tag="b4679"
                     /product="uncharacterized membrane protein YohP"
                     /label=yohP
                     /note="The <i>yohP</i> open reading frame was predicted
                     based on the presence of a ribosome binding site in an 
                     intergenic region. YohP contains a predicted transmembrane 
                     segment, and the protein can be found in the membrane 
                     fraction |CITS: [19121005][21778229]|. YohP is highly 
                     expressed during stationary phase |CITS: [19121005]|. 
                     Topology assays using GFP and PhoA fusion proteins suggest 
                     that YohP may have a dual orientation within the membrane 
                     |CITS: [21778229]|.
                     <i>yohP</i> mRNA preferentially localizes to the inner 
                     membrane; membrane localization is dependent on nucleotide 
                     sequence (in particular uracil content) and secondary 
                     structure; |FRAME: SECE-G-Y-CPLX SecYEG| and |FRAME: YIDC 
                     YidC| are implicated as mRNA receptors at the inner 
                     membrane and mRNA binding to the membrane is 
                     translation-independent; membrane-bound <i>yohP</i> mRNA is
                     translated and YohP is inserted into the membrane. This 
                     alternative strategy for membrane protein insertion may be 
                     important under stress conditions |CITS: [36842086]|"
                     /translation="MKIILWAVLIIFLIGLLVVTGVFKMIF"
