     gene            complement(2222917..2223864)
                     /gene="dusC"
                     /locus_tag="b2140"
                     /label=dusC
     CDS             complement(2222917..2223864)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dusC"
                     /locus_tag="b2140"
                     /product="tRNA-dihydrouridine(16) synthase"
                     /label=dusC
                     /note="|FRAME: EG11311-MONOMER| (DusB) and DusC together
                     account for about half of the 5,6-dihydrouridine 
                     modification observed in wild-type cellular tRNA, and 
                     |FRAME: EG11932-MONOMER| (DusA) accounts for the other half
                     |CITS: [11983710]|. DusC specifically modifies U16 of the D
                     loop in tRNAs. The site selectivity of the enzyme is 
                     conveyed by binding the tRNA substrate in a different 
                     orientation when compared to an enzyme that modifies U20, 
                     on the opposite side of the D loop |CITS: [25902496]|. 
                     Crystal structures of DusC have been solved, showing an 
                     N-terminal catalytic TIM-barrel domain and a C-terminal 
                     tRNA binding domain |CITS: [23908023][25902496]|. Crystal 
                     structures of DusC in complex with tRNAs elucidated the 
                     basis for modification site specificity of the enzyme 
                     |CITS: [25902496]|.
                     A <i>dusA dusB dusC</i> triple mutant exhibits a complete 
                     lack of 5,6-dihydrouridine modification in cellular tRNA, 
                     whereas each single mutant exhibits a partial reduction, 
                     compared to wild type |CITS: [11983710]|. A |FRAME: G7297 
                     <i>tmcA</i>| <i>dusC</i> double deletion strain has a 
                     severe cold-sensitive growth defect |CITS: [18668122]|. 
                     Using a <i>&Delta;dusC</i> mutant, the specificity of DusC 
                     for dihydrouridylation of nucleotide 16 was confirmed for 
                     |FRAME: LEU-tRNAs| (CAG) |CITS: [29294097]|. Follow-up 
                     experiments with the &Delta;3 mutant found that mRNAs are 
                     not dihydrouridylated and that the dihydrouridine 
                     nucleotide in 23S rRNA is dihydrouridylated by some other 
                     dihydrouridine synthase than DusA, DusB or DusC |CITS: 
                     [34798057]|. Using a new method that blocks reverse 
                     transcriptase by using rhodamine-labelled dihydrouridine 
                     (called RhoRT-PCR), the <i>dusA dusB dusC</i> triple mutant
                     was found to not prevent the dihydrouridylation of 23S 
                     rRNA, consistent with the previous evidence; instead 
                     dihydrouridylation of 23S rRNA was found to be carried out 
                     with |FRAME: EG12229-MONOMER| |CITS: [39078675]|.
                     Analysis of each single mutant compared to wild type found 
                     reduced 5,6-dihydrouridine modification activity under 
                     paraquat-induced anaerobic conditions, with DusA being the 
                     least sensitive and DusC being the most sensitive to 
                     changes in redox best represented by a decrease in NADPH 
                     availability. Position specificity differed between the 
                     three tRNA-dihydrouridine synthases; kinetics were tested 
                     but undetectable under aerobic conditions |CITS: 
                     [39460624]|.
                     DusC: ""<b>d</b>ihydro<b>u</b>ridine <b>s</b>ynthase 
                     <b>C</b>""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [35638108][35737906]|"
