     gene            complement(2240542..2242011)
                     /gene="lysP"
                     /locus_tag="b2156"
                     /label=lysP
     CDS             complement(2240542..2242011)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lysP"
                     /locus_tag="b2156"
                     /product="lysine:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=lysP
                     /note="LysP is a lysine-specific transporter which
                     mediates the uptake of lysine for biosynthetic purposes and
                     functions as a co-sensor for lysine in the lysine dependent
                     acid-resistance (LDAR) system*.
                     <i>lysP</i> encodes a lysine-specific transporter which 
                     contributes to lysine uptake when cells are grown 
                     aerobically at neutral pH; LysP mediated lysine uptake 
                     increases under anaerobic conditions in media of low pH 
                     with added lysine |CITS: [1315732]|. <i>lysP</i> insertion 
                     mutants are resistant to the lysine analog, thialysine and 
                     lack lysine specific transport when cells are grown 
                     aerobically in nutrient rich YT medium |CITS: [1315732]|. 
                     LysP and CadC are implicated in responding to the lysine 
                     signal which induces the LDAR system (also called the Cad 
                     system); in the absence of external lysine, LysP negatively
                     regulates |FRAME: PD00436 ""<i>CadC</i>""| activity; 
                     overexpression of <i>lysP</i> inhibits expression of 
                     <i>cadBA</i> under inducing conditions |CITS: [8195083]|. 
                     Lysine dependent regulation of the Cad system is mediated 
                     by a LysP-CadC interaction; LysP physically interacts with 
                     CadC <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [18086202]|. LysP and CadC 
                     interact most strongly at pH 7.6 and at lysine 
                     concentrations of 1 &micro;M or less, that is, under 
                     non-Cad-inducing conditions; this interaction is attenuated
                     under Cad-inducing conditions (low pH, increased lysine 
                     levels); the LysP-CadC interaction is mediated via 
                     trans-membrane and periplasmic contacts |CITS: [24056175]|.
                     Oligomerization of LysP is induced at low pH |CITS: 
                     [24056175]|. LysP is a 'trigger transporter' |CITS: 
                     [19708919]| - a protein that combines regulation and 
                     transport function within the one polypeptide.
                     Experimental topology analysis suggests LysP contains 12 
                     transmembrane segments with the N and C-termini located in 
                     the cytosol |CITS: [7551055]|.
                     LysP is a member of the Amino Acid Transporter (AAT) family
                     within the Amino Acid-Polyamine-Organocation (APC) 
                     superfamily |CITS: [26546518]|.
                     <i>lysP</i> expression is repressed in the presence of 
                     lysine but is not affected by pH |CITS: [8808945]| (see 
                     further |CITS: [21441513]|).
                     <i>lysP</i>: lysine specific permease |CITS: [1315732]| 
                     *LDAR is one of a number of amino acid dependent acid 
                     resistance mechanisms in <i>E. coli</i> (reviewed in |CITS:
                     [23701194]|)"
