     gene            2244319..2245176
                     /gene="nfo"
                     /locus_tag="b2159"
                     /label=nfo
     CDS             2244319..2245176
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nfo"
                     /locus_tag="b2159"
                     /product="trifunctional 3'-phosphomonoesterase / 
                     3'-phosphodiesterase / endonuclease IV"
                     /EC_number="3.1.21.2"
                     /label=nfo
                     /note="Endonuclease IV is an inducible
                     apurinic/apyrimidinic (AP) site endonuclease.
                     Early studies purified and characterized Endonuclease IV 
                     (Endo IV) from K-12 strains |CITS: 
                     [177402][16002][6246344]|.
                     Endo IV is induced when cells are grown in the presence of 
                     the DNA damaging agents, methyl viologen, and to a lesser 
                     extent, plumbagin, phenazine methosulfate and menadione 
                     |CITS: [2437576]|. The purified enzyme catalyses AP 
                     endonuclease activity on plasmid DNA containing AP sites; 
                     it also exhibits 3'-phosphomonesterase and 
                     3'-phosphodiesterase activity - releasing a variety of 
                     fragments from DNA 3'-termini, including phosphate, 
                     phosphoglycoaldehyde and deoxyribose 5-phosphate |CITS: 
                     [2453510]|. The enzyme may |CITS: 
                     [12444080][35905924][36230958]| or may not |CITS: 
                     [2453510]| have 3'-5' exonuclease activity. Purified Endo 
                     IV is a metal-dependent endonuclease |CITS: [1720775]|. 
                     Endo IV can incise the DNA backbone 5' of the oxidative 
                     lesion &alpha;-deoxyadenosine |CITS: [7516707]| (see 
                     further |CITS: [15568813][15050824]|). Endo IV is involved 
                     in the (glycosylase independent) nucleotide incision repair
                     (NIR) pathway which is able to process a number of lesions 
                     generated by oxidative stress including 6-dihydrouracil, 
                     5,6-dihydrothymidine, &alpha;-deoxyadenosine, and 
                     5-hydroxyuridine |CITS: 
                     [15568813][16978929][16473948][19751747]|. Endo IV cleaves 
                     spermine- and spermidine-conjugated AP sites <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [39763436]|.
                     Endo IV adopts an 8-stranded TIM barrel fold; its active 
                     site, located at the bottom of a distinct groove, contains 
                     a trinuclear mixed-metal cluster; Endo IV recognizes AP 
                     sites by flipping both the abasic site and its nucleotide 
                     partner out of the DNA duplex; DNA binding is mediated by 5
                     recognition loops (R loops) which contact nucleotides on 
                     both strands of the duplex |CITS: [10458614]| (see further 
                     |CITS: 
                     [18408731][23355472][35905924][34445579][39655415]|). 
                     <i>nfo</i>: e<U>n</U>donuclease <U>fo</U>ur |CITS: 
                     [2430946]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [1697933][7522423][9493955][10946230][20667510]|"
