     gene            2258929..2259501
                     /gene="efpL"
                     /locus_tag="b2171"
                     /label=efpL
     CDS             2258929..2259501
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="efpL"
                     /locus_tag="b2171"
                     /product="elongation factor protein EfpL"
                     /label=efpL
                     /note="EfpL, a paralog of |FRAME: EG12099-MONOMER|, is
                     also involved in translational stress response through 
                     rescuing ribosome stalling at polyproline 
                     (XP(P)X)-containing proteins and acts as a sensor for the 
                     metabolic state of the cell via lysine acylation |CITS: 
                     [39622818]|. A third protein, |FRAME: UUP-MONOMER|, is also
                     involved in rescuing polyproline-dependent ribosome 
                     stalling in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [38661232]|.
                     Single, double and triple deletion mutants and 
                     complementation analyses with <i>efpL, EF-P</i> and 
                     <i>uup</i> reveals a similar overlapping function and 
                     phenotypic patterns. EfpL, like EF-P, alleviates ribosome 
                     stalling at diproline motifs. However, overexpression of 
                     EfpL revealed a distinct suite of XP(P)X motifs in the 
                     genes that it could rescue from translational arrest but at
                     a lower efficiency than EF-P with just a few exceptions. A 
                     guanosine in the first position of the E-site codon 
                     promotes both the binding of EF-P and EfpL to the ribosome 
                     |CITS: [39622818]|.
                     Sequence analysis suggests that YeiP may contain 
                     &beta;-barrel structure(s) |CITS: [12192075]|.
                     A high-resolution crystal structure was solved and 
                     characterized. EfpL contains many highly conserved features
                     present in EF-P-like proteins but also some key differences
                     related to its similar functional role to EF-P, 
                     particularly the elongation of the KOW domain 
                     &beta;3&Omega;&beta;4 loop and an arginine R33 at its 
                     central tip. The R33 interacts directly with RNA based on 
                     both molecular docking and R33K variant analyses |CITS: 
                     [39622818]|.
                     The <i>yeiP</i> mRNA has been used as a test substrate for 
                     RppH-dependent mRNA degradation |CITS: 
                     [18202662][22989003]|.
                     EfpL is deactivated by acylation at lysine K51, implicating
                     that EfpL can detect the metabolic status of the cell and 
                     regulate translation of specific XP(P)X proteins in 
                     response |CITS: [39622818]|.
                     EfpL for <u>EF-P</u> <u>l</u>ike"
