     gene            2263458..2264024
                     /gene="mepS"
                     /locus_tag="b2175"
                     /label=mepS
     CDS             2263458..2264024
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mepS"
                     /locus_tag="b2175"
                     /product="multifunctional peptidoglycan
                     endopeptidase/peptidoglycan L,D-carboxypeptidase / 
                     peptidoglycan DD-endopeptidase / peptidoglycan LD 
                     endopeptidase / peptidoglycan L,D-carboxypeptidase"
                     /label=mepS
                     /note="Purified MepS (formerly Spr) is a murein
                     DD-endopeptidase with specificity for D-ala-m-DAP 
                     (4&rarr;3) cross links; the endopeptidase activity of MepS 
                     is associated with murein incorporation during cell growth;
                     purified MepS also has weak LD-carboxypeptidase activity; 
                     purifed MepS has murein hydrolase activity on intact 
                     peptidoglycan (PG) sacculi |CITS: [23062283]|. Purified 
                     MepS hydrolyses both 4&rarr;3  and 3&rarr;3 (m-DAP-mDAP) 
                     cross-links with similar efficacy |CITS: [34382698]|. In 
                     addition to promoting cell elongation, MepS influences the 
                     cell division process; elevated MepS activity interferes 
                     with the activation of septal PG biogenesis by the divisome
                     |CITS: [32866331]|.
                     <i>mepS</i> deletion mutants are unable to grow on nutrient
                     agar at high temperatures - over-expression of <i>mepH</i> 
                     or <i>mepM</i> from a plasmid suppresses this phenotype 
                     |CITS: [23062283]|. A strain lacking all three murein 
                     endopeptidases MepS, |FRAME: EG10013-MONOMER ""MepM""|, and
                     |FRAME: G6892-MONOMER ""MepH""| exhibits extensive cell 
                     lysis and a terminal phenotype. Incorporation of labelled 
                     mDAP into the peptidoglycan sacculi is decreased by up to 
                     80% in the triple deletion strain. Over-expression of any 
                     of the three genes from a plasmid allows the triple 
                     deletion mutant to grow normally. This suggests that MepS, 
                     MepM and MepH perform essential and redundant functions 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [23062283]|.
                     Overproduction of MepS promotes resistance to |FRAME: 
                     CPD0-2599|; overproduction specifically enhances 
                     PBP1b-mediated peptidoglycan synthesis in mecillinam 
                     treated cells |CITS: [28749938]|
                     <i>mepS</i> encodes a predicted periplasmic surface outer 
                     membrane lipoprotein. MepS is monomeric in solution; the 
                     C-terminal 126 residues of MepS adopt an &alpha;+&beta; 
                     fold consisting of four &alpha; helices and a sheet of six 
                     antiparallel &beta; strands; a Cys-His-His catalytic triad 
                     is present in the active site of the protein |CITS: 
                     [18715016]|.
                     <i>mepS</i> is expressed under aerobic and anaerobic growth
                     conditions |CITS: [15126489]|. Levels of MepS are high 
                     throughout the exponential phase of growth and fall steeply
                     at the onset of stationary phase (aerobic growth in LB); 
                     MepS is degraded by a proteolytic system comprising the 
                     outer membrane lipoprotein, |FRAME: EG12371-MONOMER 
                     ""NlpI""| and the periplasmic protease, |FRAME: 
                     EG10760-MONOMER Prc| (also known as tail-specific protease)
                     |CITS: [26283368][38937433]|. Cellular phospholipid and 
                     fatty acid abundance affects MepS levels via the NlpI-Prc 
                     proteolytic system |CITS: [37276399]| (consider also |CITS:
                     [38109534][38109530]|).
                     A MepS(C68A) substitution mutant has no endopeptidase 
                     activity <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [23062283]|. The 
                     <i>spr-1</i> mutation results in thermosensitive growth at 
                     low osmolarity; the <i>spr-1</i> mutation suppresses the 
                     thermosensitivity of a <i>&Delta;prc</i> mutation; 
                     <i>spr</i> and <i>prc</i> are mutual suppressors |CITS: 
                     [9158724]|. <i>nlpI</i> mutations (frame-shift, nonsense 
                     and missense) suppress the thermosensitivity of 
                     <i>spr-1</i> |CITS: [15047720]|. Overexpression of |FRAME: 
                     EG12015 | corrects the thermosensitivity associated with 
                     <i>spr-1</i> |CITS: [9158724]|.
                     <i>spr</i>: <u>s</u>uppressor of <i><u>pr</u>c</i> |CITS: 
                     [9158724]|<BR><i>mep</i>: <u>m</u>urein 
                     <u>e</u>ndo<u>p</u>eptidase |CITS: [23062283]|"
