     gene            complement(2273267..2273962)
                     /gene="rsuA"
                     /locus_tag="b2183"
                     /label=rsuA
     CDS             complement(2273267..2273962)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rsuA"
                     /locus_tag="b2183"
                     /product="16S rRNA pseudouridine(516) synthase"
                     /EC_number="5.4.99.19"
                     /label=rsuA
                     /note="RsuA is the pseudouridine synthase that is
                     responsible for pseudouridylation of 16S rRNA at position 
                     516 |CITS: [7612632][10376875]|.  <i>In vitro</i>, the 
                     enzyme does not modify free 16S rRNA.  The preferred 
                     substrate is a 5'-terminal fragment of 16S rRNA complexed 
                     with 30S ribosomal proteins, suggesting that 
                     pseudouridylation occurs at an intermediate stage of 30S 
                     assembly <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [7612632]|.  RsuA has the 
                     highest affinity to the 5'-terminal 16S fragment complexed 
                     with ribosomal protein S17 alone |CITS: [32486254]|. 
                     Crystal structures of RsuA in complex with uracil or UMP 
                     have been solved.  RsuA consists of an N-terminal domain 
                     with structural similarity to ribosomal protein S4, an 
                     extended linker region, and a C-terminal catalytic domain. 
                     Despite limited sequence similarity, the structure of RsuA 
                     is similar to that of TruA, a tRNA pseudouridine synthase 
                     |CITS: [11953756]|.  The N-terminal S4-like domain may be 
                     important for substrate recognition |CITS: [32486254]|.
                     An <i>rsuA</i> mutation causes a defect in 
                     pseudouridylation at position 516 in the 16S rRNA, and a 
                     D102N or D102T mutation at a conserved aspartate residue 
                     was shown to cause a defect in catalytic activity |CITS: 
                     [10376875]|.  An <i>rsuA</i> mutant does not exhibit any 
                     obvious growth defect |CITS: [10376875]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>rsuA</i> from an ASKA plasmid leads to
                     a more than 3-fold increase in the MIC of hypochlorous acid
                     |CITS: [34072091]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8722007][17113994][17085441][25616362]|"
