     gene            complement(2284837..2285889)
                     /gene="ccmH"
                     /locus_tag="b2194"
                     /label=ccmH
     CDS             complement(2284837..2285889)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ccmH"
                     /locus_tag="b2194"
                     /product="trifunctional holocytochrome c synthase CcmH
                     component / thiol:disulfide oxidoreductase / holocytochrome
                     c synthase complex"
                     /label=ccmH
                     /note="CcmH and |FRAME: EG12054-MONOMER CcmF| are the
                     components of holocytochrome <i>c</i> synthase in the 
                     System I pathway of cytochome <i>c</i> maturation; CcmFH 
                     releases heme from holo|FRAME: CCME-MONOMER CcmE| and 
                     transfers it to apocytochromes in the periplasm. 
                     <i>ccmH</i> is part of an 8 gene cluster 
                     (<i>ccmABCDEFGH</i>) which bears sequence similarity to 
                     <i>Bradyrhizobium japonicum</i> genes that are required for
                     the biogenesis of |FRAME: Cytochromes-c ""<i>c</i>-type 
                     cytochromes""|; an <i>E. coli</i> &Delta;<i>ccmA-H</i> 
                     mutant strain (EC06) is not able to produce mature 
                     <i>c</i>-type cytochromes under anaerobic, nonfermentative 
                     growth conditions |CITS: [7635817]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [8830238][8842153][9716493]|).
                     CcmH is anchored to the membrane by two transmembrane 
                     regions; the protein contains an (essential) N-terminal 
                     periplasmic oxidoreductase domain containing the CXXC 
                     active site motif and a second (non-essential) C-terminal 
                     periplasmic domain containing tetratricopeptide repeat 
                     (TPR) motifs |CITS: [9914305][18625227][22789558]|. The 
                     oxidized form of |FRAME: EG12053-MONOMER CcmG| accumulates 
                     in mutants defective in CcmF |CITS: [11256948]|.
                     CcmH copurifies with CcmF |CITS: 
                     [19629033][11744735][37929956]|; the purified CcmFH complex
                     also contains &beta;-heme which is a stable component of 
                     CcmF; the CcmFH complex functions as a cytochrome <i>c</i> 
                     synthetase and as a quinol:heme oxidoreductase |CITS: 
                     [19629033]| (and see |CITS: 
                     [22066495][20599545][24397552][37827288]|).
                     In an engineered and optimized system, CcmFH together wth 
                     |FRAME: EG12053-MONOMER|, can attach heme to (plasmid 
                     expressed) apocyctochrome <i>c</i> in the absence of 
                     CcmABCDE |CITS: [24397552]|.
                     The System I pathway of cytochrome <i>c</i> biogenesis has 
                     also been studied in other gram-negative organisms 
                     including |FRAME: TAX-1061| and a comprehensive model for 
                     apocytochrome <i>c</i> thioreduction and stereo-specific 
                     heme ligation described (see for example |CITS: [18753134] 
                     [18786143][21956106][24631867][25979338][28634234]|). 
                     <i>ccm</i>: <u>c</u>ytochrome <u><i>c</i></u> 
                     <u>m</u>aturation |CITS: [7635817]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [12196152][21958041]|."
