     gene            complement(2285886..2286443)
                     /gene="ccmG"
                     /locus_tag="b2195"
                     /label=ccmG
     CDS             complement(2285886..2286443)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ccmG"
                     /locus_tag="b2195"
                     /product="bifunctional thiol:disulfide oxidoreductase / 
                     thiol:disulfide oxidoreductase CcmG"
                     /label=ccmG
                     /note="CcmG is a membrane-anchored thiol-disulfide
                     reductase implicated in apocytochrome <i>c</i> 
                     thioreduction prior to heme ligation in the System I 
                     pathway of cytochome <i>c</i> maturation.
                     <i>ccmG</i> is part of an 8 gene cluster 
                     (<i>ccmABCDEFGH</i>) which bears sequence similarity to 
                     <i>Bradyrhizobium japonicum</i> genes that are required for
                     the biogenesis of |FRAME: Cytochromes-c ""<i>c</i>-type 
                     cytochromes""|; an <i>E. coli</i> &Delta;<i>ccmA-H</i> 
                     mutant strain (EC06) is not able to produce mature 
                     <i>c</i>-type cytochromes under anaerobic, nonfermentative 
                     growth conditions |CITS: [7635817]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [8830238][9716493]|). An in-frame <i>ccmG</i> deletion 
                     mutant is able to produce (plasmid-encoded) apocytochrome 
                     <i>c</i>-550 but not holocytochrome <i>c</i>-550 |CITS: 
                     [9237661]|.
                     CcmG is a small protein with a thioredoxin-like motif 
                     facing the periplasm, but tethered to the cytoplasmic 
                     membrane by a hydrophobic N-terminal domain; CcmG has a 
                     reducing role <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [9537397][11843181][11350062]|. CcmG catalyses a late 
                     reductive step in holocytochrome <i>c</i> biosynthesis 
                     |CITS: [11256948]|. CcmG is reduced by the |FRAME: 
                     DSBD-MONOMER| |CITS: [11114333][16004871]|.
                     In an engineered and optimized system, CcmG together wth 
                     |FRAME: CPLX-9494 CcmFH|, can attach heme to (plasmid 
                     expressed) apocyctochrome <i>c</i> in the absence of 
                     CcmABCDE |CITS: [24397552]|.
                     The System I pathway of cytochrome <i>c</i> biogenesis has 
                     also been studied in other gram-negative organisms 
                     including |FRAME: TAX-1061| and a model for apocytochrome 
                     <i>c</i> thioreduction and stereo-specific heme ligation 
                     described (see for example |CITS: [18753134][18786143][219 
                     56106][24631867][25979338][28634234]|). <i>ccm</i>: 
                     <u>c</u>ytochrome <u><i>c</i></u> <u>m</u>aturation |CITS: 
                     [7635817]|"
