     gene            complement(2286440..2288383)
                     /gene="ccmF"
                     /locus_tag="b2196"
                     /label=ccmF
     CDS             complement(2286440..2288383)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ccmF"
                     /locus_tag="b2196"
                     /product="multifunctional holocytochrome c synthase CcmF
                     component / holocytochrome c synthase / heme reductase CcmF
                     / holocytochrome c synthase complex"
                     /label=ccmF
                     /note="CcmF and |FRAME: EG12052 CcmH| are the components
                     of holocytochrome <i>c</i> synthase in the System I pathway
                     of cytochome <i>c</i> maturation; CcmFH releases heme from 
                     holo|FRAME: CCME-MONOMER CcmE| and transfers it to 
                     apocytochromes in the periplasm.
                     <i>ccmF</i> is part of an 8 gene cluster 
                     (<i>ccmABCDEFGH</i>) which bears sequence similarity to 
                     <i>Bradyrhizobium japonicum</i> genes that are required for
                     the biogenesis of |FRAME: Cytochromes-c ""<i>c</i>-type 
                     cytochromes""|; an <i>E. coli</i> &Delta;<i>ccmA-H</i> 
                     mutant strain (EC06) is not able to produce mature 
                     <i>c</i>-type cytochromes under anaerobic, nonfermentative 
                     growth conditions |CITS: [7635817]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [8830238][8842153][9716493]|). An in-frame <i>ccmF</i> 
                     deletion mutant is able to produce (plasmid-encoded) mature
                     |FRAME: Cytochromes-B <i>b</i>-type cytochrome| and 
                     apocytochrome <i>c</i>-550 but not holocytochrome 
                     <i>c</i>-550 |CITS: [9237661]|. Heme-containing CcmE 
                     accumulates in a mutant that is unable to synthesize CcmF 
                     |CITS: [9770295]|. Release of heme from CcmE is blocked in 
                     the absence of CcmFGH proteins |CITS: [9712585]|. CcmF 
                     interacts with CcmE and with CcmH but not with 
                     apocytochrome <i>c</i> |CITS: [11744735]| (and see |CITS: 
                     [14532274]|).
                     Purified CcmF is itself a cytochrome <i>b</i> (it contains 
                     &beta;-heme in a 1:1 stoichiometry); purified CcmF 
                     functions as a quinol:heme oxidoreductase using its bound 
                     heme (sometimes called the stable or accessory heme) to 
                     reduce the incoming heme of holoCcmE; the CcmFH complex 
                     functions as a cytochrome <i>c</i> synthetase and as a 
                     quinol:heme oxidoreductase |CITS: [19629033]| (and see 
                     |CITS: [22066495][20599545][24397552][37827288]|). The 
                     source of <i>in vivo</i> reductant for the CcmF &beta;-heme
                     remains uncertain (see |CITS: [27198710]|). CcmF copurifies
                     with holoCcmE in the absence of CcmGH; the interaction of 
                     CcmF with holoCcmE is dependent on the release of holoCcmE 
                     from CcmABCD; apoCcmE is not able to interact with CcmF 
                     |CITS: [24513106]|. In an engineered and optimized system, 
                     CcmFH together wth |FRAME: EG12053-MONOMER|, can attach 
                     heme to (plasmid expressed) apocyctochrome <i>c</i> in the 
                     absence of CcmABCDE |CITS: [24397552]|.
                     CcmF contains a periplasmically-oriented tryptophan 
                     (W)-rich sequence motif (the WWD domain) that functions to 
                     bind heme from CcmE |CITS: [37929956][11004446]|. CcmF 
                     contains 4 conserved histidine residues, 2 of which (P-His 
                     1 and P-His 2) are located in periplasmic domains flanking 
                     the WWD domain and 2 (TM-His1 and TM-His2) which are 
                     ligands to the stable &beta;-heme; mechanisms for 
                     trafficking heme from holoCcmE to apocytochromes continue 
                     to be investigated (see |CITS: 
                     [33958791][34103722][37827288]|).
                     The System I pathway of cytochrome <i>c</i> biogenesis has 
                     also been studied in other gram-negative organisms 
                     including |FRAME: TAX-1061| and a model for apocytochrome 
                     <i>c</i> thioreduction and stereo-specific heme ligation 
                     described (see for example |CITS: [18753134][18786143][219 
                     56106][24631867][25979338][28634234]|). <i>ccm</i>: 
                     <u>c</u>ytochrome <u><i>c</i></u> <u>m</u>aturation |CITS: 
                     [7635817]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [12196152][21958041]|"
