     gene            complement(2288380..2288859)
                     /gene="ccmE"
                     /locus_tag="b2197"
                     /label=ccmE
     CDS             complement(2288380..2288859)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ccmE"
                     /locus_tag="b2197"
                     /product="trifunctional periplasmic heme chaperone /
                     CcmCDE complex / heme binding and reduction"
                     /label=ccmE
                     /note="CcmE is a membrane anchored, periplasmic heme
                     chaperone that is required for |FRAME: PWY-8147 
                     ""cytochrome <i>c</i> biogenesis""|.
                     <i>ccmE</i> is part of an 8 gene cluster 
                     (<i>ccmABCDEFGH</i>) which bears sequence similarity to 
                     <i>Bradyrhizobium japonicum</i> genes that are required for
                     the biogenesis of |FRAME: Cytochromes-c ""<i>c</i>-type 
                     cytochromes""|; an <i>E. coli</i> &Delta;<i>ccmA-H</i> 
                     mutant strain (EC06) is not able to produce mature 
                     <i>c</i>-type cytochromes under anaerobic, nonfermentative 
                     growth conditions |CITS: [7635817]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [8830238][9716493][9237661]|). An in-frame <i>ccmE</i> 
                     deletion mutant cannot produce mature <i>c</i>-type 
                     cytochromes |CITS: [9712585]|; CcmE binds heme covalently 
                     via a conserved histidine residue (His-30) |CITS: 
                     [9712585]|. Heme ligation in CcmE exhibits flexibility and 
                     it has been extensively studied (see |CITS: 
                     [12119398][12429096][12657624][15465823][15771504][ 
                     16373344][ 17142277]|). The presence of apocytochrome 
                     <i>c</i> triggers release of heme from CcmE; heme release 
                     is blocked in the absence of CcmFGH |CITS: [9712585]| (see 
                     also |CITS: [9770295][11744735]|).
                     During cyctochrome <i>c</i> biosynthesis, heme is moved 
                     across the inner membrane concomitant with the formation of
                     a CcmC-heme-CcmE-CcmD complex; subsequent release of 
                     holo-CcmE (for eventual heme transfer to apocytochromes 
                     <i>c</i>) requires |FRAME: CcmAB-Complex CcmAB|-mediated 
                     ATP hydolysis |CITS: [10339610][11384983][14532274][155139 
                     13][16824107][17419738][18326572]|. A stable (intermediate)
                     holo-CcmCDE complex (isolated in the absence of CcmAB) 
                     contains oxidized heme (Fe<sup>3+</sup>) bound to CcmC via 
                     His-60 and His-184 axial ligands and to CcmE via a His130 
                     adduct; a purified holo-CcmCDE(H130A) 'pre-adduct' complex 
                     contains reduced heme suggesting that the adduct formation 
                     in holoCcmE involves oxidation to the Fe<sup>3+</sup> state
                     |CITS: [19629033]|; the identity of the electron acceptor 
                     is not known although membrane quinols and other oxidants 
                     such as oxygen and nitrate all possess potentials capable 
                     of oxidizing 'pre-adduct' CcmCDE heme |CITS: [27198710]| 
                     (see also |CITS: [20599545][29518410][30206118]|).
                     Transfer of heme from holo-CcmE in the periplasm to 
                     apocytochromes c is catalysed by the |FRAME: CPLX-9494|, 
                     CcmFH |CITS: [11744735]|. Synthesis of holocytochome 
                     <i>c</i> requires both reduction of the heme molecule 
                     (mediated by CcmF) and reduction of the cysteine residues 
                     of the apocytochrome c. CcmF contains a stably associated 
                     <i>b</i> heme which may function to reduce the heme in 
                     holoCcmE; the source of reductant for the <i>b</i> heme in 
                     CcmF remains unknown although it can be reduced by various 
                     quinones <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [19629033]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [22066495][24513106][27198710]|).
                     <i>ccm</i>: <u>c</u>ytochrome <u><i>c</i></u> 
                     <u>m</u>aturation |CITS: [7635817]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [11004446][16042600][19721088][21958041][24631867]|"
