     gene            complement(2288856..2289065)
                     /gene="ccmD"
                     /locus_tag="b2198"
                     /label=ccmD
     CDS             complement(2288856..2289065)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ccmD"
                     /locus_tag="b2198"
                     /product="multifunctional cytochrome c maturation protein
                     D / CcmABCD release complex / CcmCDE complex / heme binding
                     and reduction"
                     /label=ccmD
                     /note="CcmD is a small inner membrane protein that is
                     required for the release of holo-CcmE during |FRAME: 
                     PWY-8147 ""type I cytochrome <i>c</i> biogenesis""|. 
                     <i>ccmD</i> is part of an 8 gene cluster 
                     (<i>ccmABCDEFGH</i>) which bears sequence similarity to 
                     <i>Bradyrhizobium japonicum</i> genes that are required for
                     the biogenesis of |FRAME: Cytochromes-c ""<i>c</i>-type 
                     cytochromes""|; an <i>E. coli</i> &Delta;<i>ccmA-H</i> 
                     mutant strain (EC06) is not able to produce mature 
                     <i>c</i>-type cytochromes under anaerobic, nonfermentative 
                     growth conditions |CITS: [7635817]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [8830238][9716493][9237661]|). An in-frame <i>ccmD</i> 
                     mutant produces very low levels of holo-|FRAME: 
                     CCME-MONOMER CcmE| |CITS: [10339610]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [10998170]|).
                     CcmD is a small membrane protein that contains a single 
                     hydrophobic region. The topology of CcmD has been debated; 
                     an earlier study proposed that both the N-terminal domain 
                     (NTD) and C-terminal domain (CTD) are located in the 
                     cytoplasm with the hydrophobic region in the inner leaflet 
                     of the inner membrane |CITS: [15513913]|. However, a later 
                     study suggested that the hydrophobic region constitutes a 
                     transmembrane domain with the NTD located in the periplasm 
                     and the CTD in the cytoplasm |CITS: [18326572]|. The short 
                     N terminus contains a conserved tyrosine (Tyr-17) of 
                     unknown function |CITS: [19629033]|.
                     CcmD participates in what is sometimes referred to as 'step
                     1' of cyctochrome <i>c</i> biosynthesis which refers to the
                     movement of heme across the membrane concomitant with the 
                     formation of a CcmC-heme-CcmE-CcmD complex and subsequent 
                     release of holoCcmE. Holo-CcmE release (for subsequent heme
                     transfer to apocytochromes <i>c</i>) requires |FRAME: 
                     CcmAB-Complex CcmAB|-mediated ATP hydolysis and the 
                     presence CcmD |CITS: [10339610][14532274 
                     ][15513913][16824107][17419738][18326572]|. CcmD is not 
                     necessary for formation of a CcmC-heme-CcmE complex but is 
                     necessary for the release of holo-CcmE; CcmD is a component
                     of the CcmABCD 'release complex' |CITS: [18326572]|. A 
                     CcmCD complex has been modeled; Tyr-17 (in the conserved 
                     GXY motif) is predicted to interact with the heme-binding 
                     WWD domain of |FRAME: CCMC-MONOMER CcmC| |CITS: 
                     [29518410]|."
