     gene            complement(2289062..2289799)
                     /gene="ccmC"
                     /locus_tag="b2199"
                     /label=ccmC
     CDS             complement(2289062..2289799)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ccmC"
                     /locus_tag="b2199"
                     /product="multifunctional cytochrome c maturation protein
                     C / CcmABCD release complex / CcmCDE complex / heme binding
                     and reduction"
                     /label=ccmC
                     /note="CcmC is an inner membrane protein required for for
                     the transfer of heme to the periplasmic heme chaperone 
                     |FRAME: CCME-MONOMER CcmE| during |FRAME: PWY-8147 ""type I
                     cytochrome <i>c</i> biogenesis""|.
                     <i>ccmC</i> is part of an 8 gene cluster 
                     (<i>ccmABCDEFGH</i>) which bears sequence similarity to 
                     <i>Bradyrhizobium japonicum</i> genes that are required for
                     the biogenesis of |FRAME: Cytochromes-c ""<i>c</i>-type 
                     cytochromes""|; an <i>E. coli</i> &Delta;<i>ccmA-H</i> 
                     mutant strain (EC06) is not able to produce mature 
                     <i>c</i>-type cytochromes under anaerobic, nonfermentative 
                     growth conditions |CITS: [7635817]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [8830238][9716493][9237661]|). An in-frame <i>ccmC</i> 
                     deletion mutant cannot synthesize holocytochrome <i>c</i>; 
                     CcmC is required for covalent incorporation of heme into 
                     the periplasmic heme chaperone |FRAME: CCME-MONOMER CcmE| 
                     |CITS: [10339610]|.
                     CcmC is predicted to contain six transmembrane helices plus
                     a periplasmic domain that contains the functionally 
                     important tryptophan-rich motif (the WWD domain) |CITS: 
                     [10998170]| and two strictly conserved histidines 
                     implicated in heme binding |CITS: [10339610]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [11384983]|).
                     CcmC participates in what is sometimes referred to as 'step
                     1' of cyctochrome <i>c</i> biosynthesis which refers to the
                     movement of heme across the membrane concomitant with the 
                     formation of a CcmC-heme-CcmE-CcmD complex and subsequent 
                     release of holoCcmE. Holo-CcmE release (for subsequent heme
                     transfer to apocytochromes <i>c</i>) requires |FRAME: 
                     CcmAB-Complex CcmAB|-mediated ATP hydolysis and the 
                     presence the presence of the small membrane protein, 
                     |FRAME: EG12169-MONOMER CcmD| |CITS: [10 
                     339610][14532274][15513913][16824107][17419738][18326572]|.
                      A stable (intermediate) holo-CcmCDE complex (isolated in 
                     the absence of CcmAB) contains oxidized heme 
                     (Fe<sup>3+</sup>) bound to CcmC via His-60 and His-184 
                     axial ligands and to CcmE via a His130 adduct; heme binding
                     to CcmC is only detected when CcmE is present |CITS: 
                     [19629033]| (see also |CITS: [20599545]|. Heme binds 
                     directly to the periplasmic WWD domain in CcmC and is 
                     liganded by His-60 and His-84; binding positions the heme 
                     vinyl groups for interaction with CcmE; the mechanism for 
                     trafficking heme from the cytoplasm to the WWD domain is 
                     not clear |CITS: [29518410]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [27198710][30206118]|).
                     <i>ccm</i>: <u>c</u>ytochrome <u><i>c</i></u> 
                     <u>m</u>aturation |CITS: [7635817]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [11004446][16042600][19721088][21958041][24631867]|"
