     gene            complement(2289841..2290503)
                     /gene="ccmB"
                     /locus_tag="b2200"
                     /label=ccmB
     CDS             complement(2289841..2290503)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ccmB"
                     /locus_tag="b2200"
                     /product="multifunctional cytochrome c maturation protein
                     B / CcmABCD release complex / CcmAB / release of heme-bound
                     CcmE"
                     /label=ccmB
                     /note="CcmB is an integral membrane protein of the |FRAME: 
                     PWY-8147 ""type I cytochrome <i>c</i> biogenesis system""|.
                     <i>ccmB</i> is part of an 8 gene cluster 
                     (<i>ccmABCDEFGH</i>) which bears sequence similarity to 
                     <i>Bradyrhizobium japonicum</i> genes that are required for
                     the biogenesis of |FRAME: Cytochromes-c ""<i>c</i>-type 
                     cytochromes""|; an <i>E. coli</i> &Delta;<i>ccmA-H</i> 
                     mutant strain (EC06) is not able to produce mature 
                     <i>c</i>-type cytochromes under anaerobic, nonfermentative 
                     growth conditions |CITS: [7635817]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [8830238][9716493][9237661]|). An in-frame <i>ccmB</i> 
                     deletion mutant cannot synthesize holocytochrome <i>c</i> 
                     but does produce heme-bound |FRAME: CCME-MONOMER CcmE| 
                     (when |FRAME: CCMC-MONOMER CcmC| is overproduced); |FRAME: 
                     CCMA-MONOMER CcmA| and CcmB are implicated in heme transfer
                     from holo-CcmE and attachment to apocytochrome <i>c</i>; 
                     CcmAB does not transport heme to the periplasm |CITS: 
                     [10339610]| (and |CITS: [17419738]|). CcmB is predicted to 
                     be an integral membrane protein with six transmembrane 
                     helices; CcmA is detected in the membrane when CcmB is 
                     present and purified CcmAB has ATPase activity; 
                     inactivation of this activity does not abolish formation of
                     holo-CcmE but does prevent production of <i>c</i>-type 
                     cytochromes |CITS: [17419738]|. CcmA, CcmB and ATP 
                     hydrolysis (by CcmA) are required for the release of 
                     holoCcmE from CcmC; in the absence of CcmAB, CcmC binds 
                     holoCcmE tightly |CITS: [16824107]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [19629033]|).
                     <i>ccm</i>: <u>c</u>ytochrome <u><i>c</i></u> 
                     <u>m</u>aturation |CITS: [7635817]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [9293186][11004446][12196152]|"
