     gene            complement(2292194..2293057)
                     /gene="napH"
                     /locus_tag="b2204"
                     /label=napH
     CDS             complement(2292194..2293057)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="napH"
                     /locus_tag="b2204"
                     /product="trifunctional ferredoxin-type protein NapH / 
                     ubiquinol--[NapC cytochrome c] reductase / ubiquinol--[NapC
                     cytochrome] reductase"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.8"
                     /label=napH
                     /note="The |FRAME: EG12062| gene encodes a ferredoxin-type 
                     non-heme iron-sulfur protein. |CITS: [8842153] [8830238] 
                     [8919448]|.
                     NapH is an integral membrane protein with four 
                     membrane-spanning domains; the C-terminal domain with its 
                     two predicted [4Fe-4S] clusters is located in the cytoplasm
                     |CITS: [14674886]|.  Using two-hybrid assays, it has been 
                     shown that NapH interacts strongly with |FRAME: EG12060 
                     NapC| |CITS: [14674886]|.
                     A mutant lacking |FRAME: EG12064| and |FRAME: EG12062| was 
                     totally defective for growth in glycerol/nitrate medium. 
                     When grown in glucose/nitrate medium, it was found that the
                     mutant had almost completely lost the ability to reduce 
                     nitrate. With ubiquinol as the electron donor no 
                     significant nitrate reduction could be observed |CITS: 
                     [11967083]|.
                     In a subsequent study, deletion of either |FRAME: EG12064| 
                     or |FRAME: EG12062| almost abolished Nap-dependent nitrate 
                     reduction by a strain uncapable of producing menaquinone. 
                     Thus it was concluded that NapG and NapH are required for 
                     electron transfer from ubiquinol, but not menaquinol, via 
                     |FRAME: EG12060 NapC|, to the |FRAME: NAP-CPLX NapAB| 
                     complex |CITS: [14674886]|."
