     gene            complement(2293044..2293739)
                     /gene="napG"
                     /locus_tag="b2205"
                     /label=napG
     CDS             complement(2293044..2293739)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="napG"
                     /locus_tag="b2205"
                     /product="trifunctional ferredoxin-type protein NapG / 
                     ubiquinol--[NapC cytochrome c] reductase / ubiquinol--[NapC
                     cytochrome] reductase"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.8"
                     /label=napG
                     /note="The |FRAME: EG12064| gene, which is located in the
                     operon of the periplasmic nitrite reductase, encodes a 
                     ferredoxin-type non-heme iron-sulfur protein |CITS: 
                     [8842153] [8830238] [8919448]|. A mutant lacking |FRAME: 
                     EG12064| and |FRAME: EG12062| was totally defective for 
                     growth in glycerol/nitrate medium. When grown in 
                     glucose/nitrate medium, it was found that the mutant had 
                     almost completely lost the ability to reduce nitrate. With 
                     ubiquinol as the electron donor no significant nitrate 
                     reduction could be observed |CITS: [11967083]|.
                     In a subsequent study, deletion of either |FRAME: EG12064| 
                     or |FRAME: EG12062| almost abolished Nap-dependent nitrate 
                     reduction by a strain incapable of producing menaquinone. 
                     Since experiments with a bacterial two-hybrid system showed
                     that NapH interacts with NapC, it was concluded that NapG 
                     and NapH are required for electron transfer from ubiquinol,
                     but not menaquinol, via |FRAME: EG12060 NapC|, to the 
                     |FRAME: NAP-CPLX NapAB| complex |CITS: [14674886]|. NapG 
                     contains a twin-arginine sequence that is essential for its
                     function, suggesting that it may be secreted into the 
                     periplasm by the twin arginine translocation pathway |CITS:
                     [14674886]|."
