     gene            complement(2302170..2302820)
                     /gene="alkB"
                     /locus_tag="b2212"
                     /label=alkB
     CDS             complement(2302170..2302820)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="alkB"
                     /locus_tag="b2212"
                     /product="multifunctional 1-ethyladenine demethylase / 
                     1,N(6)-ethenoadenine repair / 1-methyl-dATP demethylase / 
                     1-methyl-dAMP demethylase / N1-methyladenine demethylase / 
                     N3-methylcytosine demethylase / 2-methylthiocytosine 
                     dethiomethylase / DNA oxidative demethylase"
                     /EC_number="1.14.11.54"
                     /label=alkB
                     /note="AlkB is an Fe(II)-containing,
                     2-oxoglutarate-dependent dioxygenase which catalyses the 
                     non-mutagenic repair of methyl lesions in DNA and RNA. 
                     Purified AlkB is a 2-oxoglutarate and Fe(II) dependent 
                     dioxygenase which accurately repairs 1-methyladenine and 
                     3-methylcytosine lesions in DNA |CITS: [12226667]| (see 
                     also |CITS: [12226668]|). Purified AlkB accurately repairs 
                     1-methyladenine and 3-methylcytosine in RNA; episomal 
                     expression of <i>alkB</i> reactivates methylated RNA phage 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [12594517]| (and further |CITS: 
                     [15469826][23276627]|). The enzyme is capable of repairing 
                     methylation lesions on various nucleic acid substrates 
                     (ssDNA, dsDNA, ssRNA, dsRNA and hybrids) <i>in vitro</i> 
                     but shows a preference for single-stranded substrate |CITS:
                     [15229293]|; the enzyme may be active at the replication 
                     fork or at sites of transcription <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [10950872]|.
                     AlkB repairs 1-ethyladenine and 3-ethylcytosine lesions in 
                     DNA |CITS: [12486230][15381779]|; AlkB repairs the minor 
                     DNA lesions, 1-methylguanine (1-meG), and to a lesser 
                     extent 3-methylthymine (3-meT) |CITS: 
                     [15381779][15269201][15576352]| (although a later study 
                     observed no detectable repair in trinucleotides containing 
                     1-methyl-dG or 3-methyl-dT |CITS: [19706517]|). AlkB 
                     demethylates 2-methylthiocytidine containing tRNAs which 
                     are generated from exposure to methylating agents |CITS: 
                     [31811240]|. AlkB is able to repair exocyclic base adducts 
                     (eg.3,N<sup>4</sup>-ethenocytosine, 
                     1,N<sup>2</sup>-ethenoguanine and 
                     1,N<sup>6</sup>-ethenoadenine) resulting from oxidative 
                     stress or exposure to industrial carcinogens |CITS: [16231 
                     911][16200073][17658757][19941873][21193516][23148216][2515
                     7679][25797601][28408432]|. Purified AlkB is unable to 
                     demethylate single, alkylated nucelosides |CITS: 
                     [12517755]|; it can demethylate (with varying efficiency) 
                     1-methyl-deoxyadenosine 5'-phosphate, 
                     1-methyldeoxyadenosine 5'-triphosphate, and 1-methyladenine
                     within an oligonucleotide trimer or dimer; a phosphate 
                     residue 5' to the lesion may be important for catalysis 
                     |CITS: [12944387]|. AlkB is not involved in the repair of 
                     apyrimidinic and apurinic DNA site lesions or crosslinks 
                     (DNA-protein, DNA interstrand) that occur at such lesions; 
                     AlkB is not involved in the repair of 3-methyladenine 
                     lesions |CITS: [9637239]|.
                     Overproduction and purification of the protein is described
                     leading to analysis of substrate binding and enzyme assays 
                     |CITS: [3536913][15612731][16793366]|. Crystal structures 
                     of substrate and product complexes of AlkB have been 
                     determined |CITS: [16482161][19706517]| (and further |CITS:
                     [18617893][20084272][28989656]|). The Fe(II) cofactor of 
                     AlkB has been replaced with Cu(II) in a study probing the 
                     active site of the enzyme |CITS: [17553567]|. AlkB-dsDNA 
                     complexes containing 3-methyl cytosine, 3-methyl thymine or
                     a cyclic adduct have been crystallised and the structures 
                     resolved to high resolution; intermediates of the repair 
                     reactions have been characterised after the complexes are 
                     subject to 'oxidation <i>in crystallo</i>' |CITS: 
                     [21068844]|.
                     AlkB interacts directly with RecA <i>in vivo</i>; the 
                     purified proteins form a stable complex <i>in vitro</i>; 
                     RecA stimulates AlkB catalyzed oxidative demethylation 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [27378775]|.
                     <i>alkB</i> deficient cells are susceptible to the toxicity
                     and mutagenicity of alkylation lesions |CITS: 
                     [15381779][16733554]|. An <i>alkB</i> mutant 
                     (<i>alkB22</i>) exhibits increased sensitivity to methyl 
                     methanesulfonate (MMS) and a defect in repair of 
                     MMS-treated phage, compared to wild type, but shows 
                     wild-type sensitivity to UV irradiation and a wild-type 
                     response to N-methyl-N'-nitro-N-nitrosoguanidine (MNNG) 
                     |CITS: [6337994]|. The <i>aidD</i>6::Mu d1 (<i>bla lac</i>)
                     mutation disrupts the <i>alkB</i> gene |CITS: [1921981]|.
                     Regulation has been described |CITS: [3536913][10420268]|. 
                     Gene expression is activated by self-methylation of the Ada
                     protein upon interaction with alkylated DNA |CITS: 
                     [3009022]|. Gene expression is induced by the alkylating 
                     agents MMS |CITS: [3080354]|, MNNG |CITS: 
                     [3080354][2965298]|, streptozotocin (STZ), and 
                     N-methyl-N-nitrosourea (MNU) |CITS: [2965298]|. Methylation
                     is a more effective inducer than some other types of 
                     alkylation |CITS: [2493133]|. Gene expression is not 
                     induced by heat shock |CITS: [1425661]|, pH changes, 
                     thiosalicyclic acid, or N-ethylmaleimide treatment |CITS: 
                     [7504191]|.
                     AlkB has 23% identity to human ABH protein |CITS: 
                     [8600462]| and similarity to human ABH2 and ABH3 |CITS: 
                     [12486230]|. AlkB is a member of the iron-dependent, 
                     2-oxoglutarate-dependent dioxygenase protein family |CITS: 
                     [12226667][12226668]|.  Expression of <i>ada</i> and 
                     <i>alkB</i> genes in human cells increases the cellular 
                     resistance to alkylation |CITS: [3526337]|. Expression of 
                     <i>alkB</i> alone in human cells increases resistance 
                     specifically to S<sub>N</sub>2 alkylation compounds but not
                     to S<sub>N</sub>1 alkylation compounds, the same spectrum 
                     of resistance that AlkB provides in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [7928996]|. Expression of the <i>Saccharomyces 
                     cerevisiae</i> YFW1, YFW12, or YFW16 gene functionally 
                     complements the MMS resistance of an <i>E. coli alkB</i> 
                     mutant |CITS: [7665478]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [3278898][12483506][12498704][12531015][12 
                     594492][12517444][14527653][15040447][15380096][17132930][1
                     7201058][16469386][17175108]|<BR>Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [26152727][26152720][27237585][34586107]| AidD: 
                     ""alkylation-inducible"" |CITS:[3080354]|"
