                     /locus_tag="b2215"
                     /product="multifunctional outer membrane porin C /
                     transport of hydrophilic solute or ion < 600 Da / transport
                     of an organophosphorus compound / transport of a medium 
                     chain fatty acid / transport of a glycoside / transport of 
                     a sugar / transport of a sugar phosphate / transport of a 
                     monocarboxylate / transport of an aromatic carboxylate / 
                     transport of a trisaccharide / transport of a disaccharide 
                     / transport of an oligopeptide / transport of a nucleotide 
                     sugar / transport of a nucleotide / transport of a 
                     nucleoside / transport of an aldehyde / transport of a 
                     lactone / transport of an amide / transport of an amine / 
                     transport of a hydroxy carboxylate / transport of an 
                     aldonate / transport of glycine betaine / transport of 
                     S-methyl-5-thio-D-ribose / transport of an inositol / 
                     transport of an amino acid / transport of a nucleobase / 
                     transport of a dicarboxylate / transport of a sugar alcohol
                     / transport of a short-chain alcohol / transport of a 
                     short-chain carboxylate / transport of an organosulfur 
                     compound / transport of H2O / transport of an ion / 
                     transport of glycerol phosphate"
                     /label=ompC
                     /note="OmpC is a general outer membrane (OM) porin which 
                     mediates the non-specific diffusion of small solutes such 
                     as sugars, ions and amino acids; the major non-specific OM 
                     porins of <i>E. coli</I> K-12 (OmpC and |FRAME: CPLX0-7534 
                     ""OmpF""|) are typically defined by an exclusion limit 
                     based on substrate mass (~600 daltons) but there are large 
                     differences in penetration rates within solutes due to 
                     factors such as size, hydrophobicity and charge (discussed 
                     in |CITS: [1373213]|). OmpC acts as a receptor for 
                     bacteriophages 434, T4 and Bp7 (see |CITS: 
                     [360042][7002910][3894021][27273222][32238583]| and further
                     |CITS: [32675236][33893116][34784345]|).
                     Purified OmpC reconstituted into artificial lipid bilayer 
                     membranes forms an ion permeable channel with a slight 
                     preference for cations |CITS: [2580824]|. OmpC and OmpF are
                     considered to be the two major non-specific trimeric OM 
                     porins under standard laboratory growth (see |CITS: 
                     [1696133]| and references within); in the Transporter 
                     Classification Database both are members of the General 
                     Bacterial Porin (GBP) family |CITS: [26546518]| which also 
                     includes the related porins |FRAME: CPLX0-7530 ""PhoE""|, 
                     |FRAME: EG10659-MONOMER ""NmpC""| and |FRAME: G6700-MONOMER
                     ""OmpN""|. Imaging of the OM by atomic force microscopy 
                     shows that OmpC foms part of a dense, crowded network of 
                     low mobility trimeric porins; the porin network is 
                     interupted by nanoscale patches enriched in LPS which may 
                     facilitate the insertion of new membrane components as the 
                     cell grows |CITS: [34716276]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [36322653]|).
                     Purified OmpC is trimeric; the OmpC monomer consists of a 
                     16-stranded anti-parallel &beta;-barrel containing an 
                     aqueous pore; the &beta;-strands show short connections at 
                     the periplasmic rim and long irregular loops at the 
                     extracellular surface with the exception of loop L3 which 
                     folds into the barrel and forms a constriction zone; the 
                     OmpC monomers are arranged into trimers via hydrophobic 
                     interactions between barrel surfaces and a 'latching loop""
                     L2 which connects one monomer to its neighbour 
                     |CITS:[16949612]|. OmpC is tightly but noncovalently 
                     associated with the peptidoglycan layer 
                     |CITS:[7021763][2903556]|.
                     OmpC is implicated in a retrograde phospholipid trafficking
                     pathway (the Mla pathway) that functions to maintain lipid 
                     asymmetry in the outer membrane; stationary phase 
                     &Delta;<I>ompC</i> cells have disrupted outer membrane 
                     lipid asymmetry; OmpC interacts physically with the outer 
                     membrane lipoprotein |FRAME: G7216-MONOMER ""MlaA""|; 
                     strong genetic interaction between <i>mlaA</i> and 
                     <i>ompC</i> suggests that the proteins function together 
                     within the Mla system |CITS: [26314242]| (see further 
                     |CITS: [29848551][30284446 ]| and also |CITS: 
                     [31420510][31420446]|).
                     OmpC mediates the entry of various antibiotics including 
                     &beta;-lactams, tetracyclines and others |CITS: [6760806][ 
                     2580479][8226422][23011778][25082756][38104323]|. OmpC is 
                     implicated in the secretion of the extracellular protein 
                     |FRAME: EG11807-MONOMER ""YebF""| |CITS: [22658749]|. OmpC 
                     is important for adaption to the mammalian intestine; a 
                     strain lacking OmpC (MG1655 Str<sup>r</sup> 
                     &Delta;<i>ompC::cam</i>) is sensitive to physiological 
                     concentrations of bile salts and is outcompeted by the 
                     wild-type <i>in vivo</i> due to the presence of bile salts 
                     in the intestine |CITS: [37014216]|.
                     Porin genes are subject to complex transcriptional and 
                     post-transcriptional regulation influenced by factors such 
                     as medium osmolarity, temperature and pH (see selected 
                     reviews |CITS: [8809744][20068355]|).
                     <i>ompC</i> exhibits mosaic evolution patterns in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [35285711][40444982]|.
                     A description of outer membrane protein nomenclature, 
                     including early and recommended names can be found in 
                     |CITS: [6254441]|.
                     Selected reviews: |CITS: [6337630][2580220][14665678]|"
