     CDS             complement(2310506..2313355)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rcsC"
                     /locus_tag="b2218"
                     /product="bifunctional histidine kinase RcsC / protein
                     histidine autokinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.13.3"
                     /label=rcsC
                     /note="RcsC is the histidine kinase of the complex Rcs 
                     phosphorelay system; RcsC autophosphorylates and transfers 
                     a phosphate to the RcsD phosphotransfer protein; RcsC is 
                     not thought to act as a direct sensor rather its kinase 
                     activity is regulated by the inner membrane protein |FRAME:
                     G7741-MONOMER ""IgaA""| in response to signals sensed by 
                     the outer membrane lipoprotein |FRAME: RCSF-MONOMER 
                     ""RcsF""|; RcsC may be a bifunctional histidine 
                     kinase/phosphatase (see |CITS: [29897834]|). <i>rcsC</i> 
                     was initially identified as a <U>r</U>egulator of 
                     <U>c</U>olanic acid <U>s</U>ynthesis; strains carrying the 
                     <i>rcsC137</i> recessive mutation express 
                     &beta;-galactosidase from <i>cps::lac</i> fusions and are 
                     mucoid |CITS: [3888955]| (further |CITS: [2836365]|). RcsC 
                     is a hybrid histidine kinase which autophosphorylates on a 
                     conserved histidine (His-463) in its His-kinase domain and 
                     transfers this phosphate to a conserved aspartate (Asp-859)
                     in its receiver domain; RcsC lacks a histidine-containing 
                     phosphotransmitter (HPt) domain and instead donates its 
                     phosphate to the phosphotransfer transfer protein |FRAME: 
                     EG12385-MONOMER ""RcsD""| which in turn phosphorylates the 
                     transcription regulator |FRAME: RCSB-MONOMER ""RcsB""| 
                     |CITS: [2404948][11807084][11309126]|. A solution structure
                     of the C-terminal region of RcsC containing two separate 
                     domains (an &alpha;-&beta;-loop domain and a 
                     phosphoreceiver domain) has been reported and interaction 
                     between this region and RcsD has been modeled |CITS: 
                     [17005198]| (further |CITS: [19021569]|).
                     RcsC interacts physically with RcsF and this interaction 
                     appears necessary to activate the Rcs phosphorelay |CITS: 
                     [28691662]|.
                     Null mutations in <i>rcsC</i> abolish the induction of 
                     <i>cps::lacZ</I> that occurs in response to osmotic upshift
                     |CITS: [8576059]| (see also |CITS: [9352941]|). 
                     Over-production of the membrane protein, |FRAME: 
                     EG11570-MONOMER ""DjlA""|, induces expression of the 
                     <i>cps</i> operon in an <i>rcsC</i> dependent manner |CITS:
                     [9364917]|. RcsC is required for normal biofilm development
                     in a biofilm forming strain of <i> E. coli</i> K-12; 
                     constitutive activation of RcsC (via over-production of 
                     DjlA) affects the expression of over 150 genes, many of 
                     which have functions related to cell surface composition; 
                     the RcsC sensor responds to growth on a solid surface 
                     |CITS: [14651646]|.
                     A DNA replication arrest site, <i>TerF</i> is located 
                     within the coding sequence of <i>rcsC</i> |CITS: 
                     [1447156]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [20722597]|.
                     <i>Note</i>: bacterial histidine kinases function as 
                     homodimers; both <i>cis</i>- and 
                     <i>trans</i>-autophosphorylation mechanisms have been 
                     documented (see |CITS: [19575571][25982528]|). In the 
                     absence of experimental evidence RcsC is represented as a 
                     monomer in the EcoCyc database.
                     "
