     gene            complement(2344495..2345853)
                     /gene="glpT"
                     /locus_tag="b2240"
                     /label=glpT
     CDS             complement(2344495..2345853)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glpT"
                     /locus_tag="b2240"
                     /product="sn-glycerol 3-phosphate:phosphate antiporter"
                     /label=glpT
                     /note="GlpT is the major <I>E. coli</I> uptake system for 
                     <i>sn</i>-glycerol 3-phosphate, and this transporter 
                     belongs to the Major Facilitator Superfamily (MFS) of 
                     transporters |CITS:[8438231]|.  Uptake of 
                     <i>sn</i>-glycerol 3-phosphate via the GlpT transporter 
                     leads to the simultaneous counterflow of inorganic 
                     phosphate from the cell 
                     |CITS:[8416912][8282692][7836304][9711545]|. GlpT also 
                     catalyzes a reversible phosphate:phosphate exchange 
                     |CITS:[3522583]|. Both <i>sn</i>-glycerol 
                     3-phosphate:phosphate and phosphate:phosphate exchange 
                     activities were observed in GlpT-reconstituted 
                     proteoliposomes |CITS:[3522583]|. Using phosphate-loaded 
                     proteoliposomes, the K<sub>m</sub> for the transport of 
                     <i>sn</i>-glycerol 3-phosphate via GlpT was estimated to be
                     near 20 &mu;M and the V<sub>max</sub> was 130 nmol 
                     min<sup>-1</sup> mg<sup>-1</sup> |CITS:[3522583]|.
                     GlpT has been crystallized and the structure has been 
                     determined by X-ray crystallography to a resolution of 3.3 
                     &Aring; |CITS:[12893936],[14627735]|. The functional form 
                     of GlpT is the monomer |CITS:[11380257]|. GlpT exhibits a 
                     rocker-switch mechanism of transport 
                     |CITS:[17915951][19037818]| although transport via a 
                     'tilted' mechanism has also been proposed 
                     |CITS:[18942157]|. Experiments using PhoA/LacZ fusions 
                     revealed GlpT has twelve transmembrane segments 
                     |CITS:[3141744]|. The residues involved in binding the 
                     substrate have been identified as well as the substrate 
                     binding mechanism which involves protonation of a histidine
                     residue at the binding site. It has been suggested that 
                     translocation of the substrate occurs as a result of salt 
                     bridge formation and disruption 
                     |CITS:[18395745][19720022]|. GlpT contains a putative 
                     charge-relay system located on the intracellular side of 
                     the protein; disruption of this network (through targeted 
                     mutation) triggers a conformational shift towards the 
                     inward facing state <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [30297844]| (see
                     also |CITS: [26234418]|).
                     Mutants that lack the GlpT system fail to exchange internal
                     phosphate for either external phosphate or 
                     <i>sn</i>-glycerol 3-phosphate |CITS:[3882662]|. <I>E. coli
                     glpT</I> mutants or mutants that result in down-regulation 
                     of <I>glpT</I> have been identified as being able to confer
                     fosfomycin and fosmidomycin resistance due to the mutants' 
                     inability to take up the antibiotics 
                     |CITS:[7044304][14519998]|. Expression of <I>glpT</I> from 
                     a multicopy plasmid confers the ability to utilize 
                     diisopropyl phosphate as a sole P source in K-12 strain 
                     JA221 |CITS:[9647836]|.
                     Aerobic expression of <I>glpT</I> is induced by 
                     <i>sn</i>-glycerol 3-phosphate, while anaerobic expression 
                     of <I>glpT</I> is repressed by nitrate, glucose, and 
                     mutation of <I>fnr</I> |CITS:[1521763]|. Mutation in the 
                     gene <I>glpR</I> derepressed the transcription of 
                     <I>glpT</I>, indicating the regulatory role of the GlpR in 
                     <I>glpT</I> transcription |CITS:[9179845]|. GlpT activity 
                     is stimulated by internal phosphate levels 
                     |CITS:[7836304]|.
                     GlpT and |FRAME: UHPT-MONOMER UhpT| are homologous in <i>E.
                     coli</i> |CITS: [15260472]|. GlpT and UhpT are responsible 
                     for uptake of |FRAME: CPD0-1113| (see |CITS: 
                     [27029300][36009997][37926273]|).
                     Reviews: 
                     |CITS:[15802247][15380549][15313234][15313233][12893929]|"
