                     19054114]"
                     /go_process="response to antibiotic [goid 0046677]
                     [evidence IEA] [GO_REF 0000043]"
     gene            complement(2367127..2368482)
                     /gene="menE"
                     /locus_tag="b2260"
                     /label=menE
     CDS             complement(2367127..2368482)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="menE"
                     /locus_tag="b2260"
                     /product="o-succinylbenzoate--CoA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.2.1.26"
                     /label=menE
                     /note="<i>O</i>-succinylbenzoate-CoA ligase (MenE)
                     catalyzes the formation of an intermediate in menaquinone 
                     biosynthesis, <i>o</i>-succinylbenzoyl-CoA |CITS: 
                     [8955296][9324253]|.
                     Inhibition of the enzymatic activity by 
                     diethylpyrocarbonate suggested that a histidine residue 
                     located near the active site is essential for catalysis; 
                     mutagenesis of the His341 residue located in the predicted 
                     ATP binding region leads to loss of 65% of enzymatic 
                     activity |CITS: [9324253]|.  Mutagenesis of the conserved 
                     R195 residue results in reduced catalytic activity due to 
                     an increase in K<sub>M</sub> for <i>O</i>-succinylbenzoate 
                     |CITS: [26394156]|.
                     A crystal structure of the R195K mutant in a complex with 
                     the inhibitor OSB-AMS has been solved |CITS: [26394156]|. 
                     Menaquinone biosynthesis provides an attractive 
                     antibacterial drug target.  Inhibitors of MenE activity 
                     have been identified and tested |CITS: 
                     [18762421][22109989][26394156]|.
                     A <i>menE</i> mutant lacks <i>o</i>-succinylbenzoate-CoA 
                     ligase activity |CITS: [6754698]|.  Deletion of 
                     <i>menC</i>, <i>menD</i> or <i>menE</i> leads to loss of 
                     the ability to reduce selenate |CITS: [18811645]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [11153266]|"
