     gene            complement(2383527..2384984)
                     /gene="nuoN"
                     /locus_tag="b2276"
                     /label=nuoN
     CDS             complement(2383527..2384984)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nuoN"
                     /locus_tag="b2276"
                     /product="multifunctional NADH:quinone oxidoreductase
                     subunit N / NADH:quinone oxidoreductase I / NADH:ubiquinone
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting) / NADH:menaquinone 
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting)"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.2"
                     /label=nuoN
                     /note="NuoN is part of the inner membrane component of
                     NADH dehydrogenase I |CITS: [7607227]|.  The protein has 14
                     transmembrane helices |CITS: [20505720]|.  The 
                     transmembrane topology of NuoN has been investigated, 
                     supporting data from the crystal structure and indicating 
                     that both the N and C terminus are located in the periplasm
                     |CITS: [21120593]|.  Transmembrane helices were assigned to
                     locations in the crystal structure using Fourier transform 
                     analysis |CITS: [21420404]|.  A crystal structure of the 
                     membrane component at higher resolution has allowed better 
                     identification of the unusual arrangement of the 
                     transmembrane helices |CITS: [21822288]|.
                     This subunit may function in proton translocation |CITS: 
                     [9593861]|.  Site-directed mutagenesis has identified 
                     residues important for dNADH oxidase activity, proton 
                     translocation, and interaction with quinones |CITS: 
                     [12718520]|.  NuoM and NuoN share a pattern of conserved 
                     residues; predictions of membrane topology indicate that 
                     conserved Lys residues are located in a transmembrane helix
                     and suggest a proton translocation mechanism |CITS: 
                     [18590697]|.  Further site-directed mutagenesis of the 
                     three antiporter-like subunits NuoL, M and N indicate that 
                     these subunits have a common role in NDH-1 |CITS: 
                     [21387012]| and likely pump protons by a similar mechanism 
                     |CITS: [23864658]|.  The specific functional 
                     complementation by NuoN of a <i>B. subtilis</i> 
                     &Delta;<i>mrpD</i> H<sup>+</sup> channel mutant indicates 
                     that NuoN may transport H<sup>+</sup> as well |CITS: 
                     [21236240]|.  Mutational analysis of the conserved K395 
                     residue suggests that it is involved in H<sup>+</sup> 
                     transport; the K158 residue is required for interaction 
                     with NuoL |CITS: [23864658]|.  The role of conformational 
                     changes in proton translocation was also investigated by 
                     introducing intra-subunit crosslinks; crosslinks near K365 
                     severely impaired NDH-1 activity |CITS: [27632419]|. 
                     Molecular dymanics and hybrid quantum mechanics/classical 
                     mechanics simulations show hydration of a putative proton 
                     entry channel at the NuoN/K interface between NuoN and NuoK
                     as well as changes in internal hydration, suggesting a 
                     conduit for proton translocation |CITS: [24778264]|. Null 
                     mutants of all individual <i>nuo</i> genes have a growth 
                     defect under aerobic conditions in rich medium |CITS: 
                     [22063474]|.  Constraining the movement of the lateral 
                     helix of NuoL by crosslinking did not affect NDH-1 
                     activity, although crosslinking between the NuoN and NuoM 
                     subunits did result in some loss of NDH-1 activity |CITS: 
                     [26134569]|.
                     NuoN: ""<b>N</b>ADH:<b>u</b>biquinone 
                     <b>o</b>xidoreductase"" |CITS: [8387992]|"
