     gene            complement(2384991..2386520)
                     /gene="nuoM"
                     /locus_tag="b2277"
                     /label=nuoM
     CDS             complement(2384991..2386520)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nuoM"
                     /locus_tag="b2277"
                     /product="multifunctional NADH:quinone oxidoreductase
                     subunit M / NADH:quinone oxidoreductase I / NADH:ubiquinone
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting) / NADH:menaquinone 
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting)"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.2"
                     /label=nuoM
                     /note="NuoM is part of the inner membrane component of
                     NADH dehydrogenase I |CITS: [7607227][17360196]|.  The 
                     protein has 14 transmembrane helices |CITS: [20505720]|. 
                     Transmembrane helices were assigned to locations in the 
                     crystal structure using Fourier transform analysis |CITS: 
                     [21420404]|.  A crystal structure of the membrane component
                     at higher resolution has allowed better identification of 
                     the unusual arrangement of the transmembrane helices |CITS:
                     [21822288]|.
                     This subunit may function in proton translocation |CITS: 
                     [9593861]| and may contain the ubiquinone binding site 
                     |CITS: [12730198]|.  Site-directed mutagenesis of various 
                     conserved amino acid residues suggest that E144 and K234 
                     are essential for energy transduction and may thus 
                     participate in proton translocation |CITS: 
                     [17977822][18590697]|.  The E144 residue was systematically
                     shifted to other positions within helix V and adjacent 
                     helices by site-directed mutagenesis.  When the glutamic 
                     acid residue was shifted by one turn of the helix upstream 
                     or downstream, a moderate level of energy-transducing 
                     activity of NDH-1 was restored |CITS: [19815558]|.  NuoM 
                     and NuoN share a pattern of conserved residues; predictions
                     of membrane topology indicate that conserved Lys residues 
                     are located in a transmembrane helix and suggest a proton 
                     translocation mechanism |CITS: [18590697]|.  Further 
                     site-directed mutagenesis of the three antiporter-like 
                     subunits NuoL, M and N indicate that these subunits have a 
                     common role in NDH-1 |CITS: [21387012][23864658]|.  Results
                     with C-terminally truncated forms of NuoM indicated that 
                     intact NuoM is required for correct assembly of NDH-1 
                     |CITS: [21835926]|. The membrane topology of NuoM was 
                     investigated using His<sub>6</sub> tags; helices X and XI 
                     are probably located outside of the membrane, on the 
                     cytoplasmic side. The N- and C-terminus of NuoM are located
                     in the periplasm.  An overall membrane topology was 
                     proposed |CITS: [19815558]|.  NuoM was proposed to be 
                     located at the distal end of the membrane arm of NDH-1 
                     |CITS: [12923180][17360196]|.
                     Null mutants of all individual <i>nuo</i> genes have a 
                     growth defect under aerobic conditions in rich medium 
                     |CITS: [22063474]|.
                     NuoM: ""<b>N</b>ADH:<b>u</b>biquinone 
                     <b>o</b>xidoreductase"" |CITS: [8387992]|"
