     gene            complement(2386684..2388525)
                     /gene="nuoL"
                     /locus_tag="b2278"
                     /label=nuoL
     CDS             complement(2386684..2388525)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nuoL"
                     /locus_tag="b2278"
                     /product="multifunctional NADH:quinone oxidoreductase
                     subunit L / NADH:quinone oxidoreductase I / NADH:ubiquinone
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting) / NADH:menaquinone 
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting)"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.2"
                     /label=nuoL
                     /note="NuoL is part of the inner membrane component of
                     NADH dehydrogenase I |CITS: [7607227][17360196]|.  The 
                     protein
                     has 14 transmembrane helices and a 110 &Aring; long 
                     amphipathic &alpha;-helix that spans almost the entire 
                     length of the membrane domain |CITS: [20505720]|. 
                     Transmembrane helices were assigned to locations in the 
                     crystal structure using Fourier transform analysis |CITS: 
                     [21420404]|.  A crystal structure of the membrane component
                     at higher resolution has allowed better identification of 
                     the unusual arrangement of the transmembrane helices |CITS:
                     [21822288]|.
                     NuoL was proposed to be located at the distal end of the 
                     membrane arm of NDH-1 |CITS: [12923180][17360196]| and to 
                     function in proton translocation |CITS: [9593861]|.  The 
                     striking placement within the NDH-1 complex of NuoL's long 
                     amphipathic &alpha;-helix suggested that proton 
                     translocation may be facilitated by a piston-like movement 
                     of this helix |CITS: [20505720]|.  A modified indirect 
                     coupling mechanism was later proposed by |CITS: 
                     [20816962]|.  However, analysis of an extensive set of NuoL
                     mutants now indicates that the amphipathic helix of NuoL 
                     does not function as a mechanical piston and may instead 
                     function like a clamp for the NuoLMN subunits during 
                     catalytic turnover |CITS: [22060017]|.  An analysis of 
                     movement of the amphipathic helix utilizing 
                     cysteine-scanning and labeling of individual sites revealed
                     only minor conformational rearrangements |CITS: 
                     [26115017]|, and constraining its movement by crosslinking 
                     did not affect NDH-1 activity |CITS: [26134569]|. 
                     Introduction of small infrared labels at the K551 and Y590 
                     residues allowed enhanced visualization of movement and 
                     showed that the labeled residues relocate to a more 
                     hydrophobic environment upon NADH reduction |CITS: 
                     [31556114]|.
                     Site-directed mutagenesis of residues that are conserved 
                     between NuoL and Na<sup>+</sup>/H<sup>+</sup> antiporters 
                     as well as studies using specific inhibitors indicate that 
                     NuoL is involved in the indirect coupling mechanism for 
                     proton pumping |CITS: [20826797]|.  Site-directed 
                     mutagenesis of the three antiporter-like subunits NuoL, M 
                     and N indicate that these subunits have a common role in 
                     NDH-1 |CITS: [21387012][23864658]|.  When the NDH-1 complex
                     is reconstituted with NuoL subunits that are truncated at 
                     various points in the amphipathic helix, proton 
                     translocation is diminished, but not abolished, arguing for
                     at least two coupling sites for proton translocation, with 
                     NuoL being essential for the translocation of 
                     2H<sup>+</sup>/2e<sup>-</sup> |CITS: [21417432]|.  However,
                     additional reports using C-terminally truncated forms of 
                     NuoL indicated that intact NuoL is required for correct 
                     assembly of NDH-1 |CITS: [21835926][22060017]|.  Yet, 
                     overexpression of all NDH-1 subunits except NuoL is 
                     reported to result in formation of a &Delta;NuoL NDH-1 
                     complex that is capable of the electron transfer reaction, 
                     and whose proton translocation activity is approximately 
                     half that of wild type |CITS: [22063474]|.  A &Delta;NuoL 
                     complex I also lacked NuoM, but not NuoN, and only 
                     contained ~1 molecule of ubiquinone |CITS: [25868873]|.  A 
                     point mutation in the conserved D563 residue within the 
                     amphipathic helix of NuoL also results in significantly 
                     reduced proton translocation |CITS: [22326235]|.  Several 
                     additional NuoL point mutants in reconstituted NDH-1 were 
                     tested <i>in vitro</i> for proton pumping activity |CITS: 
                     [27118783]|.  The V259A mutation (analogous to the V253A 
                     mutation in human mitochondrial ND5 of a patient) abolishes
                     NDH-1 activity due to instability of the complex |CITS: 
                     [34135385]|.
                     The specific functional complementation by NuoL of a <i>B. 
                     subtilis</i> &Delta;<i>mrpA</i> Na<sup>+</sup> channel 
                     mutant indicates that NuoL may transport Na<sup>+</sup> as 
                     well |CITS: [21236240]|.  A C-terminally truncated fragment
                     of NuoL was previously shown to function as a 
                     Na<sup>+</sup> pump |CITS: [12740360][17583799]|.  NDH-1 
                     lacking the NuoL subunit does not appear to transport 
                     Na<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [25447539]|.
                     Null mutants of all individual <i>nuo</i> genes have a 
                     growth defect under aerobic conditions in rich medium 
                     |CITS: [22063474]|.  A <i>nuoL</i> mutant contains two 
                     populations of partially assembled complex I, one with 
                     electron transfer activity and about half of the proton 
                     transfer activity of wild type, and one inactive complex 
                     that is associated with CadA |CITS: [22063474]|. 
                     <i>nuoL</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     NuoL: ""<b>N</b>ADH:<b>u</b>biquinone 
                     <b>o</b>xidoreductase"" |CITS: [8387992]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11695827][24059520][24973951][25077450]|"
