     CDS             complement(2388522..2388824)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nuoK"
                     /locus_tag="b2279"
                     /product="multifunctional NADH:quinone oxidoreductase
                     subunit K / NADH:quinone oxidoreductase I / NADH:ubiquinone
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting) / NADH:menaquinone 
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting)"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.2"
                     /label=nuoK
                     /note="NuoK is part of the inner membrane component of
                     NADH dehydrogenase I |CITS: [7607227]|. The protein has 
                     three predicted transmembrane domains; the C terminus is 
                     located in the cytoplasm |CITS: [15919996]|. A crystal 
                     structure of the membrane component at higher resolution 
                     has allowed better identification of the interactions 
                     between the subunits |CITS: [21822288]|. NuoK participates 
                     in proton translocation |CITS: [23105119]|.
                     The SecYEG protein secretion machinery and its YidC 
                     component are required for insertion of NuoK in the 
                     cytoplasmic membrane. The YidC requirement is due to the 
                     presence of two conserved glutamate residues (Glu36, Glu72)
                     in transmembrane segments of NuoK |CITS: [19959836]|. Both 
                     of these membrane-embedded acidic residues are required for
                     high rates of ubiquinone reduction |CITS: [14730982]|. 
                     Glu36 mutants appear to lack electron transfer activity and
                     thus the ability to generate an electrochemical gradient 
                     |CITS: [15996109]|. The importance of the specific location
                     of Glu36 and Glu72 and their role in proton translocation 
                     was investigated |CITS: [23105119]|. Analysis of 
                     site-directed mutants in other conserved residues of NuoK 
                     has been performed |CITS: [15996109]|. Charged residues in 
                     cytoplasmic loop 1, between the transmembrane helices TM1 
                     and TM2, are important for activity |CITS: [23105119]|. 
                     Human mitochondrial Complex I mutations were modeled by 
                     studying the analogous mutations in NuoK; the E72Q mutation
                     lowered the enzyme activity, but introduction of a second 
                     mutation, I39D, rescues proton pumping |CITS: [25747201]|. 
                     The cytoplasmic C terminus of NuoK has no dynamic role in 
                     energy transduction, but is essential for assembly of 
                     NDH-1. Deleting two or more C-terminal amino acids results 
                     in loss of NDH-1 activity |CITS: [26931547]|.
                     Null mutants of all individual <i>nuo</i> genes have a 
                     growth defect under aerobic conditions in rich medium 
                     |CITS: [22063474]|.
                     <i>nuoK</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     NuoK: ""<b>N</b>ADH:<b>u</b>biquinone 
                     <b>o</b>xidoreductase"" |CITS: [8387992]|"
