     gene            complement(2390918..2393644)
                     /gene="nuoG"
                     /locus_tag="b2283"
                     /label=nuoG
     CDS             complement(2390918..2393644)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nuoG"
                     /locus_tag="b2283"
                     /product="multifunctional NADH:quinone oxidoreductase
                     subunit G / NADH:quinone oxidoreductase I, peripheral arm /
                     NADH:quinone oxidoreductase I / NADH:ubiquinone 
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting) / NADH:menaquinone 
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting)"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.2"
                     /label=nuoG
                     /note="NuoG is part of the soluble fragment of NADH 
                     dehydrogenase I, which represents the electron input part 
                     of the enzyme |CITS: [7607227][9485311]|.  NuoG is 
                     essential for NDH-1 function |CITS: [9495756]|.
                     This subunit contains the 2Fe-2S cluster N1b |CITS: 
                     [17640900]| and three 4Fe-4S clusters.  N7 was formerly 
                     misidentified as ""N1c"", whose EPR signal is in fact 
                     derived from the 2Fe-2S cluster N1a on the NuoE subunit 
                     |CITS: [15683249][17640900]|.  N7 is non-conserved and not 
                     thought to be involved in electron transfer; however, it is
                     essential for the stability of NDH-1 |CITS: [17489563]|.  
                     The cysteine residues responsible for ligation of the 
                     4Fe-4S clusters were identified by site-directed 
                     mutagenesis |CITS: [15520003][17489563]|. However, the 
                     location and identity of EPR spectra for the N4 and N5 Fe-S
                     clusters were subject of some controversy |CITS: 
                     [17640900]|.  Recent reevaluation of the data |CITS: 
                     [18486592]| and mutational analysis of the N5 
                     His(Cys)<sub>3</sub> ligands confirmed the location of both
                     N4 and N5 in the NuoG subunit |CITS: [18603533]|. NuoG is 
                     involved in the increased organic solvent tolerance (OTS) 
                     mechanism in the <i>crp</i> and <i>cyaA</i> mutants |CITS: 
                     [18499056]|.
                     Null mutants of all individual <i>nuo</i> genes have a 
                     growth defect under aerobic conditions in rich medium 
                     |CITS: [22063474]|.
                     <i>nuoG</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     NuoG: ""<b>N</b>ADH:<b>u</b>biquinone 
                     <b>o</b>xidoreductase"" |CITS: [8387992]|"
