     gene            complement(2393697..2395034)
                     /gene="nuoF"
                     /locus_tag="b2284"
                     /label=nuoF
     CDS             complement(2393697..2395034)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nuoF"
                     /locus_tag="b2284"
                     /product="multifunctional NADH:quinone oxidoreductase
                     subunit F / NADH:quinone oxidoreductase I, peripheral arm /
                     NADH:quinone oxidoreductase I / NADH:ubiquinone 
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting) / NADH:menaquinone 
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting)"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.2"
                     /label=nuoF
                     /note="NuoF is part of the soluble fragment of NADH 
                     dehydrogenase I, which represents the electron input part 
                     of the enzyme |CITS: [7607227][9485311]|.
                     Based on sequence similarity, NuoF contains the FMN and 
                     NADH binding sites |CITS: [7690854][9593861]|. 
                     Site-directed mutagenesis of the E95 residue in the 
                     predicted NADH binding site led to altered binding of NADH 
                     and inhibition by NAD<sup>+</sup>, and a change in the 
                     midpoint potential of the FMN cofactor in Complex I |CITS: 
                     [19061856]|.  The E95Q mutation may distort the catalytic 
                     site and thereby retard the hydride transfer from NADH to 
                     FMN |CITS: [25283488]|.  NADH-dependent production of 
                     hydrogen peroxide is increased in the E95Q mutant |CITS: 
                     [24325249]|.  NuoF was shown to contain the N3 4Fe-4S 
                     cluster; the cysteine residues responsible for ligation of 
                     the 4Fe-4S cluster were identified by site-directed 
                     mutagenesis |CITS: [15922336]|.
                     Evolved <i>E. coli</i> strains that are adapted to higher 
                     production of NADPH contain an E183A mutation in NuoF; 
                     NDH-1 containing this subunit is able to oxidize both NADH 
                     and NADPH |CITS: [21205901]|.  Although other changes to 
                     E183 also result in the ability to oxidize NADPH |CITS: 
                     [21832062]|, only the E183A and E183G mutants that were 
                     also obtained from the evolved <i>E. coli</i> strains 
                     generate lower levels of reactive oxygen species than wild 
                     type with the NADPH substrate |CITS: [25897800]|.
                     Null mutants of all individual <i>nuo</i> genes have a 
                     growth defect under aerobic conditions in rich medium 
                     |CITS: [22063474]|.
                     NuoF: ""<b>N</b>ADH:<b>u</b>biquinone 
                     <b>o</b>xidoreductase"" |CITS: [8387992]|"
