     gene            complement(2395534..2397324)
                     /gene="nuoC"
                     /locus_tag="b2286"
                     /label=nuoC
     CDS             complement(2395534..2397324)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nuoC"
                     /locus_tag="b2286"
                     /product="multifunctional NADH:quinone oxidoreductase
                     subunit CD / NADH:quinone oxidoreductase I, peripheral arm 
                     / NADH:quinone oxidoreductase I / NADH:ubiquinone 
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting) / NADH:menaquinone 
                     oxidoreductase (H(+)-transporting)"
                     /EC_number="7.1.1.2"
                     /label=nuoC
                     /note="NuoCD is part of the connecting fragment within the 
                     peripheral arm of NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase I (NDH-1).
                     Unlike in other bacteria, which contain two separate genes 
                     encoding the NuoC and NuoD subunits, the <i>nuoC</i> gene 
                     of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 encodes a fused version of these 
                     subunits |CITS: [9485311]|.  NuoCD is the only subunit of 
                     the peripheral arm that does not contain a cofactor.  This 
                     subunit was predicted to function as the proton channel 
                     |CITS: [9593861]|.
                     NuoCD interacts with FliG and FliM, components of the 
                     flagellar switch-motor complex |CITS: [22210351]|. 
                     Mutagenesis of two conserved histidine residues, H224 and 
                     H228, only has a modest effect on ubiquinone reductase 
                     activity of NDH-1.  An R274A mutant leads to a significant 
                     loss of signal from the N2 4Fe-4S cluster as well as from a
                     second fast-relaxing 4Fe-4S cluster |CITS: [17209562]|. 
                     Mutagenesis of several highly conserved amino acid residues
                     in the NuoC domain showed that certain Glu and Asp residues
                     within the third &alpha; helix are required for energy 
                     transduction of NDH-1, as well as for assembly of the 
                     enzyme |CITS: [20979355]|.  Deletion of the NuoC domain 
                     results in loss of NDH-1 activity; the activity can not be 
                     restored by expressing the NuoC domain separately |CITS: 
                     [31925988]|.  The NuoD segment of NuoCD is required for the
                     assembly of NDH-1 |CITS: [25545070]|.  NuoD was proposed to
                     contribute to a quinone binding cavity; mutagenesis of 
                     conserved residues elucidated their central roles, 
                     suggesting that NuoD is at the core of NDH-1, where the 
                     quinone as well as many inhibitors bind |CITS: [25545070]|.
                     Null mutants of all individual <i>nuo</i> genes have a 
                     growth defect under aerobic conditions in rich medium 
                     |CITS: [22063474]|.
                     <i>nuoC</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     NuoC: ""<b>N</b>ADH:<b>u</b>biquinone 
                     <b>o</b>xidoreductase"" |CITS: [8387992]|"
