     gene            2406949..2408151
                     /gene="ackA"
                     /locus_tag="b2296"
                     /label=ackA
     CDS             2406949..2408151
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ackA"
                     /locus_tag="b2296"
                     /product="bifunctional acetate kinase / propionate kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.2.1; 2.7.2.15"
                     /label=ackA
                     /note="AckA has propionate kinase activity as well as
                     acetate kinase activity. The <i>ackA</i>-encoded propionate
                     kinase 2 has an important role in propionyl-CoA metabolism 
                     |CITS: [9484901]|. Acetate kinase can also catalyze 
                     acetylation of CheY, increasing signal strength for 
                     flagellar rotation |CITS: [9560203][9473056]|.
                     A second gene encoding acetate kinase, <i>|FRAME: G14|</i>,
                     was thought to exist |CITS: [7033467]|. Transcription of 
                     <i>ackA</i> is induced by the CreBC two-component system by
                     minimal media growth conditions |CITS: [11350954]|. Several
                     metabolic genes, including <i>ackA</i>, were found 
                     upregulated in two MG1655 lysogens carrying closely related
                     Stx2a phages O104 and PA8 |CITS: 31208335|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> double mutants deficient in AckA and Pta 
                     activity, or AckA and TdcD activity, were unable to 
                     metabolize threonine to propionate. This suggested the 
                     participation of this enzyme in pathway |FRAME: PWY-5437|. 
                     An <i>ackA</i> mutant showed a significant reduction in the
                     conversion of threonine to propionate, as well as a 75% 
                     reduction in acetate production |CITS: [9484901]|.
                     In <i>E. coli</i> acetylphosphate formation via the 
                     products of <i>pta</i> and <i>ackA</i> (as in pathway 
                     |FRAME: PWY0-1312|) is the only source of this key 
                     metabolite. Acetylphosphate is involved in many cellular 
                     processes including the protein quality control system 
                     |CITS: [16987178][19026742]| and it acts as a global signal
                     regulator |CITS: [17545286]|. The 
                     <i>&Delta;pta</i><i>&Delta;ackA</i> double mutant produced 
                     more inclusion bodies and decreased protein aggregation, 
                     whereas increased protein acetylation in a <i>cobB</i> 
                     mutant lead to increased levels of protein aggregation 
                     |CITS: [27832787]|. Mutant analyses found that increased 
                     lysine acetylation correlated with increased protein 
                     aggregation, cell viability and formation of persister 
                     cells under late stationary phase conditions |CITS: 
                     [38257296]|.
                     An <i>ackA</i>-negative mutant was unable to utilize xylose
                     in minimal medium under anaerobic conditions. This 
                     suggested that ATP generated from the conversion of 
                     acetylphosphate to acetate is necessary for growth under 
                     these conditions |CITS: [15516572]|. In other metabolic 
                     engineering studies, <i>pta</i> and <i>ackA</i> negative 
                     mutants were used to conserve acetyl-CoA for succinate 
                     production in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [15885621]|. 
                     Differences in acetate production among <i>E. coli</i> 
                     strains have been described |CITS: [20703804]|. 
                     <i>&Delta;ackA</i> and <i>&Delta;|FRAME: EG20173|</i> 
                     mutant analyses found that acetyl phosphate metabolism 
                     affects lysine acetylation globally in cells, whereas 
                     <i>&Delta;|FRAME: G7350|</i> did not affect global 
                     acetylation levels |CITS: [23830618]|. Studies of ATP 
                     fluctuations in single cells using <i>&Delta;pta</i> and 
                     <i>&Delta;ackA</i> mutants found that acetate fermentation 
                     and overflow metabolism was associated with increased 
                     fluctuations of ATP levels and coordination of peaks in ATP
                     with the timing of cell division when grown in nutrient 
                     rich media and aerobic conditions |CITS: [35961315]|.
                     AckA has a role in post-translational modification in <i>E.
                     coli</i>; polysome profiling of a &Delta;<i>ackA</i> mutant
                     grown in a rich medium results in high lysine-acetylation 
                     of ribosomes and dissociation of 70S ribosomes into the 30S
                     and 50S subunits with pronounced acetylated proteins 
                     leading to inhibition of translation. Complementation 
                     analysis resulted in polysome profiling identical to that 
                     in wild-type cells |CITS: [35604121]|. Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [15755952][17668256]|"
