     gene            2408226..2410370
                     /gene="pta"
                     /locus_tag="b2297"
                     /label=pta
     CDS             2408226..2410370
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pta"
                     /locus_tag="b2297"
                     /product="bifunctional phosphate acetyltransferase /
                     phosphate propionyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.8; 2.3.1.222"
                     /label=pta
                     /note="Phosphate acetyltransferase (Pta) catalyzes the 
                     reversible conversion between acetyl-CoA and 
                     acetylphosphate, a step in the metabolism of acetate. Both 
                     pyruvate and phosphoenolpyruvate activate the enzyme in the
                     direction of acetylphosphate synthesis and inhibit the 
                     enzyme in the direction of acetyl-CoA synthesis |CITS: 
                     [20236319]|.
                     Pta is composed of three domains; only the C-terminal 
                     domain is required for phosphate acetyltransferase 
                     activity. The N-terminal domain is involved in 
                     stabilization of the native quarternary structure and 
                     metabolic regulation |CITS: [20236319]|.
                     Pta may be able to utilize both acetyl-CoA and 
                     propionyl-CoA. An <i>|FRAME: EG10027|pta</i> double mutant 
                     has reduced levels of propionate from L-threonine, 
                     suggesting that the enzyme is part of the anaerobic pathway
                     metabolizing L-threonine to propionate |CITS: [9484901]| 
                     (see |FRAME: PWY-5437|).
                     A <i>pta</i> mutant does not grow on acetate as the sole 
                     source of carbon |CITS: [21941]|. Both <i>pta</i> and 
                     <i>ackA pta</i> mutants are impaired in their ability to 
                     survive glucose starvation |CITS: [8052134]|. The growth 
                     defect of a <i>pta</i> mutant appears to be due to 
                     perturbation of acetyl-CoA flux |CITS: [10542166]|. 
                     <i>pta</i> mutants produce large amounts of lactate when 
                     grown on glucose as the carbon source under microaerophilic
                     conditions |CITS: [15781419]|. The effect of a <i>pta</i> 
                     mutation on metabolism, enzyme activity and gene expression
                     has been thoroughly studied recently |CITS: [19852855]|. 
                     <i>pta</i> and <i>|FRAME: EG10824||FRAME: EG10825|</i> 
                     mutants are synthetically growth inhibited |CITS: 
                     [15687190]|. <i>&Delta;pta</i> and <i>&Delta;|FRAME: 
                     EG10027|</i> mutant analyses found that acetyl phosphate 
                     metabolism affects lysine acetylation globally in cells, 
                     whereas <i>&Delta;|FRAME: G7350|</i> did not affect global 
                     acetylation levels |CITS: [23830618]|. The 
                     <i>&Delta;pta</i><i>&Delta;ackA</i> double mutant produced 
                     more inclusion bodies and decreased protein aggregation, 
                     whereas increased protein acetylation in a <i>cobB</i> 
                     mutant lead to increased levels of protein aggregation 
                     |CITS: [27832787]|. Studies of ATP fluctuations in single 
                     cells using <i>&Delta;pta</i> and <i>&Delta;ackA</i> 
                     mutants found that acetate fermentation and overflow 
                     metabolism was associated with increased fluctuations of 
                     ATP levels and coordination of peaks in ATP with the timing
                     of cell division when grown in nutrient rich media and 
                     aerobic conditions |CITS: [35961315]|. Mutant analyses 
                     found that increased lysine acetylation correlated with 
                     increased protein aggregation, cell viability and formation
                     of persister cells under late stationary phase conditions 
                     |CITS: [38257296]|.
                     Levels of Pta are decreased by growth on acetate |CITS: 
                     [11591692]| and under low pH conditions |CITS: [12107143]|.
                     <i>pta</i> belongs to the CreBC regulon |CITS: [11350954]|.
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-7797| has a slightly positive effect on 
                     <i>pta</i> expression |CITS: [15988767]|. The growth-rate 
                     dependent expression pattern of <i>pta-ackA</i> was 
                     measured |CITS: [19861135]|.
                     The |FRAME: PWY0-1312| pathway has been the target of 
                     metabolic engineering to reduce the flux to acetate and 
                     increase the production of commercially desired end 
                     products; see, for example, |CITS: 
                     [16080684][17715942][18840539][21040799]|. It has also been
                     studied using systems biology approaches such as metabolic 
                     modeling and flux balance analysis; see, for example, 
                     |CITS: [16319065][18438875][21122111]|.
                     Knock outs of both |FRAME: EG10754| and pta reduces acetic 
                     acid production and significant increases cytidine 
                     biosynthesis yield with reduced glucose consumption |CITS: 
                     [39890743]|.
                     Pta: ""<b>p</b>hospho<b>t</b>rans<b>a</b>cetylase"" |CITS: 
                     [21941]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16530464][15755952]|"
