     gene            complement(2421536..2422105)
                     /gene="ubiX"
                     /locus_tag="b2311"
                     /label=ubiX
     CDS             complement(2421536..2422105)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ubiX"
                     /locus_tag="b2311"
                     /product="flavin prenyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.5.1.129"
                     /label=ubiX
                     /note="Evidence gathered for orthologous enzymes and
                     genetic interactions indicate that UbiX is a flavin 
                     prenyltransferase that produces a novel cofactor for 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-301 UbiD|, |FRAME: CPD-18260| |CITS: 
                     [25647642][26083754]|.  <i>ubiX</i> was first thought to 
                     encode a second 3-octaprenyl-4-hydroxybenzoate 
                     decarboxylase in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [9658014]|; however,
                     no biochemical evidence for the enzymatic function of UbiX 
                     was available at that time.
                     The fungal genes <i>pad1</i> and <i>fdc1</i> are homologs 
                     of <i>E. coli ubiX</i> and <i>ubiD</i>, respectively. 
                     Coexpression of Fdc1 from both <i>Saccharomyces 
                     cerevisiae</i> |CITS: [25647642]| and <i>Aspergillus 
                     niger</i> |CITS: [26083754][27418477]| with either the 
                     respective Pad1 enzymes or <i>E. coli</i> UbiX allows 
                     isolation of active Fdc1, suggesting that UbiX is 
                     isofunctional with Pad1.
                     The enzymatic function of <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> 
                     UbiX and the structure of its product, |FRAME: CPD-18260|, 
                     were fully characterized.  UbiX is a flavin 
                     prenyltransferase that catalyzes the addition of the 
                     dimethylallyl moiety of dimethylallyl phosphate to the N5 
                     and N6 atoms of FMNH<sub>2</sub>.  This adds a fourth 
                     non-aromatic ring to the flavin isoalloxazine group, 
                     producing prenylated FMNH<sub>2</sub>.  Crystallographic, 
                     biochemical, and biophysical studies characterized this new
                     cofactor and suggested a UbiX reaction mechanism |CITS: 
                     [26083743]|.  The cofactor role of prenylated 
                     FMNH<sub>2</sub> in UbiD-catalyzed reactions was also 
                     investigated |CITS: [26083754]|.
                     UbiX is found in both the cytosolic and membrane fractions.
                      UbiD and UbiX co-migrate and may form a 700 kDa complex in
                     the cytosol |CITS: [30686758]|.
                     Based on genetic interactions between <i>ubiX</i> and 
                     <i>ubiD</i> as well as their mutant phenotypes, it was 
                     initially proposed that UbiX and UbiD interact to be able 
                     to carry out the 3-octaprenyl-4-hydroxybenzoate 
                     decarboxylase step in ubiquinone biosynthesis during 
                     logarithmic growth |CITS: [17889824]|.
                     A genetic interaction between <i>ubiX</i> and <i>ubiG</i> 
                     was shown.  Expression of UbiX can restore the final 
                     <i>O</i>-methylation reaction of the ubiquinone 
                     biosynthesis pathway in the IS16 mutant strain with the 
                     UbiG L132Q mutation |CITS: [16923914]|.
                     Expression of <i>ubiX</i> shows dependence on carbon source
                     and regulation via Fnr, ArcA and HemA |CITS: [12799002]|.
                     A <i>ubiX</i> null mutant grows more slowly |CITS: 
                     [16030245][17889824]| and is more resistant to heat shock 
                     |CITS: [16030245]| than wild type.  Ubiquinone levels in a 
                     <i>ubiX</i> null mutant are lower than in wild type during 
                     logarithmic growth, but increase to near-wild type levels 
                     in stationary phase.  The <i>ubiX</i> null mutant 
                     accumulates |FRAME: 3-OCTAPRENYL-4-HYDROXYBENZOATE| |CITS: 
                     [17889824]|.  <i>ubiX, ubiF, ubiG, ubiH</i>, and 
                     <i>ubiE</i> mutants from the Keio K-12 mutant collection 
                     showed elevated resistance to the broad-spectrum antibiotic
                     D-cycloserine (DCS) when grown in complex media, suggesting
                     that they may be involved in DCS sensitivity |CITS: 
                     [23316042]|.
                     <i>ubiX</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     Some <i>E. coli</i> strains such as O157:H7 contain a 
                     <i>pad1</i> paralog of <i>ubiX</i> of unknown function. 
                     Pad1 from strain O157:H7 shows 51% amino acid sequence 
                     identity to UbiX and 54% identity with <i>S. cerevisiae</i>
                     PAD1 protein |CITS: [15459342]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [30326424][28754323][26465651][26183239][2 
                     4480387][11583838][11153266]|; Comment: |CITS: [26083748]|"
