     gene            complement(2424501..2425163)
                     /gene="dedD"
                     /locus_tag="b2314"
                     /label=dedD
     CDS             complement(2424501..2425163)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dedD"
                     /locus_tag="b2314"
                     /product="cell division protein DedD"
                     /label=dedD
                     /note="DedD is a cell division protein; it contains a
                     C-terminal SPOR domain which targets the protein to the 
                     septal ring |CITS: [19684127][19880599]|.  SPOR domains 
                     localize to the division site by preferentially binding to 
                     |FRAME: a-denuded-peptidoglycan denuded glycans|, which are
                     enriched in septal peptidoglycan |CITS: 
                     [19880599][26305949]|.  A model for the function of DedD, 
                     where both FtsN and DedD act in parallel to stimulate cell 
                     constriction, has been proposed |CITS: [30692172]|.
                     DedD interacts with both |FRAME: EG10748-MONOMER PBP1A| and
                     |FRAME: EG10605-MONOMER PBP1B| and stimulates the 
                     glycosyltransferase activity of both enzymes |CITS: 
                     [33144379]|.
                     DedD is predicted to be a bitopic inner membrane protein 
                     |CITS: [27510400]|; residues within the transmembrane 
                     domain that are required for DedD function have been 
                     identified |CITS: [30692172]|.  A solution structure of the
                     periplasmic portion of DedD shows a disordered region 
                     followed by a canonical SPOR domain |CITS: [33144379]|. 
                     <i>dedD</i> mutant cells have a mild cell division defect, 
                     showing a chaining phenotype |CITS: [19684127]|.  The 
                     N-terminal 54 amino acids of DedD, which include the 
                     transmembrane helix, are required and sufficient for 
                     suppression of this division defect |CITS: [30692172]|.  A 
                     <i>dedD</i> deletion mutant shows increased susceptibility 
                     to the &beta;-lactam antibiotic cefsulodin |CITS: 
                     [33144379]|.  <i>dedD</i> is essential in 
                     <i>ftsN<sup>slm117</sup></i> mutant cells |CITS: 
                     [19684127]|; <i>dedD damX</i> |CITS: [19880599]|, <i>dedD 
                     ytfB</i> |CITS: [29686141]|, <i>dedD mrcB</i> |CITS: 
                     [30692172]|, and <i>dedD drpB</i> |CITS: [32900831]| double
                     mutants have synthetic defects in cell division.
                     At low temperatures (10&deg;C), the Keio collection mutant 
                     shows reduced growth and becomes filamentous; the defect 
                     can be partially complemented by expressing <i>ftsN</i> 
                     from a plasmid |CITS: [27477251]|. The Keio collection 
                     <i>dedD</i> mutant is sensitive to lithium (<90% growth at 
                     100mM Li) |CITS: [39983623]|. <i>dedD</i> is one of a small
                     number of genes required for growth at high hydrostatic 
                     pressure |CITS: [24040140]|.
                     For unknown reasons that may be related to the specific 
                     strain background, |CITS: [27601680]| were unable to 
                     replace the rare arginine codons AGA and AGG within the 
                     essential gene <i>folC</i> (encoded upstream of 
                     <i>dedD</i>) that may constitute the ribosome binding site 
                     for <i>dedD</i>, or to delete an internal segment of 
                     <i>dedD</i> that does not overlap with <i>folC</i>.
                     DedD: ""<b>d</b>ownstream (from <i>hisT</i>, now named 
                     <i>truA</i>) <i><b>E</b>. coli</i> <b>D</b>NA <b>D</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [3040734]|
                     Review: |CITS: [22203377]|"
