     gene            complement(2425153..2426421)
                     /gene="folC"
                     /locus_tag="b2315"
                     /label=folC
     CDS             complement(2425153..2426421)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="folC"
                     /locus_tag="b2315"
                     /product="multifunctional bifunctional folylpolyglutamate 
                     synthetase / dihydrofolate synthetase / 
                     methylene-tetrahydrofolylpolyglutamate synthetase / 
                     10-formyl-tetrahydrofolylpolyglutamate synthetase / 
                     folylpoly-gamma-glutamate synthetase / dihydrofolate 
                     synthetase"
                     /EC_number="6.3.2.17; 6.3.2.12"
                     /label=folC
                     /note="During <i>de novo</i> tetrahydrofolate
                     biosynthesis, dihydrofolate synthetase encoded by 
                     <i>folC</i> catalyzes the addition of the glutamyl residue 
                     to dihydropteroate (7,8-dihydropteroate) to form 
                     dihydrofolate (7,8-dihydrofolate monoglutamate) in an 
                     ATP-dependent reaction |CITS: [14114858]| (see pathway 
                     |FRAME: PWY-6614|). In <i>E. coli</i> FolC is a 
                     bifunctional enzyme that also catalyzes the subsequent 
                     additions of L-glutamate to tetrahydrofolate 
                     (folylpoly-&gamma;-glutamate synthetase activity).  This 
                     latter activity can also utilize the 10-formyl and 
                     methylene derivatives of tetrahydrofolate as substrate 
                     |CITS: [2985605]| (see pathway |FRAME: PWY-2161|).
                     The <i>folC</i> gene has been shown to be essential in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> because this organism lacks specific folate 
                     transporters |CITS: [1548226]|. Recombinant FolC has been 
                     overexpressed as a soluble protein, purified and 
                     characterized |CITS: [2985605]|.
                     The enzyme is a member of the Mur synthetase superfamily. 
                     The crystal structure of FolC has been determined at 2.1 
                     &Aring; resolution and analysis of crystallographic data 
                     suggested the existence of different binding sites for 
                     dihydropteridine and tetrahydrofolate on FolC. Its 
                     dihydrofolate binding site was found to be distinct from 
                     that of <i>Lactobacillus casei</i>, which is a model for 
                     the human enzyme, making the <i>E. coli</i> enzyme a 
                     possible drug target |CITS: [15705579]|.
                     However, mutant studies have shown that dihydrofolate 
                     synthase and folylpolyglutamate synthetase activities could
                     not be separated |CITS: [1989505][1939056]|.  A later 
                     mutant study showed that the amino terminal domain of FolC 
                     (residues 1-287) binds tetrahydrofolate and dihydroptroate 
                     with the same affinity as the intact enzyme |CITS: 
                     [18232714]|.
                     The antifolate drug trimethoprim is an inhibitor of |FRAME:
                     DIHYDROFOLATEREDUCT-MONOMER| (DHFR) encoded by <i>folA</i>.
                      Trimethoprim also indirectly blocks the folylpolyglutamate
                     synthetase activity of FolC due to the accumulation of 
                     inhibitory dihydrofolate species that results from DHFR 
                     inhibition |CITS: [18724364]|.
                     Reviews:  Green, J.M. and R.G. Matthews (2007) ""Folate 
                     Biosynthesis, Reduction, and Polyglutamylation and the 
                     Interconversion of Folate Derivatives"" EcoSal 3.6.3.6 
                     |CITS: [ecosal]|; and reviewed from a structural 
                     perspective in |CITS: [12111724]|."
