     gene            2435243..2437249
                     /gene="mnmC"
                     /locus_tag="b2324"
                     /label=mnmC
     CDS             2435243..2437249
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mnmC"
                     /locus_tag="b2324"
                     /product="trifunctional fused tRNA
                     5-aminomethyl-2-thiouridylate methyltransferase and tRNA 
                     5-aminomethyl-2-thiouridylate synthase / tRNA 
                     5-aminomethyl-2-thiouridine synthase / tRNA 
                     5-aminomethyl-2-thiouridine methyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.61"
                     /label=mnmC
                     /note="The tRNAs specific for glutamate, lysine, and
                     possibly glutamine contain the hypermodified nucleoside 
                     5-methylaminomethyl-2-thiouridine 
                     (mnm<sup>5</sup>s<sup>2</sup>U) in position 34, the wobble 
                     position. The MnmC protein was shown to catalyze the 
                     formation of mnm<sup>5</sup>s<sup>2</sup>U from 
                     5-carboxymethylaminomethyl-2-thiouridine 
                     (cmnm<sup>5</sup>s<sup>2</sup>U) in tRNA |CITS: 
                     [3298234][15247431]|. Both the tRNA species and growth 
                     conditions modulate synthesis of the wobble base 
                     modifications |CITS: [24293650]|.
                     A tentative reaction mechanism has been proposed. The 
                     enzyme catalyzes two steps, an initial FAD-dependent 
                     demodification of cmnm<sup>5</sup>s<sup>2</sup>U to 
                     nm<sup>5</sup>s<sup>2</sup>U followed by the transfer of a 
                     methyl group from AdoMet to nm<sup>5</sup>s<sup>2</sup>U to
                     produce mnm<sup>5</sup>s<sup>2</sup>U |CITS: 
                     [3298234][15247431]|. The second reaction occurs faster 
                     than the first reaction, indicating that the enzyme is 
                     tuned to produce fully 
                     mnm<sup>5</sup>s<sup>2</sup>U-modified tRNA without 
                     accumulating the nm<sup>5</sup>s<sup>2</sup>U-modified tRNA
                     intermediate |CITS: [21306992]|. 
                     tRNA<sup>Gln</sup><sub>cmnm5s2UUG</sub> and 
                     tRNA<sup>Leu</sup><sub>cmnm5UmAA</sub> are not substrates 
                     for the demodification activity of MnmC |CITS: [24293650]|.
                     The MnmC protein consists of two conserved domains, an 
                     N-terminal methyltransferase domain and a C-terminal 
                     FAD-dependent oxidoreductase domain. The purified 
                     recombinant protein contains a flavin derivative |CITS: 
                     [15247431]|. As already indicated by the mutant phenotypes 
                     of the <i>trmC1</i> and <i>trmC2</i> mutant alleles |CITS: 
                     [6387394]|, a structural model of MnmC suggests that the 
                     initial demodification reaction is catalyzed by the 
                     C-terminal domain of the protein, while the methylation 
                     reaction is catalyzed by the N-terminal domain. 
                     Site-directed mutagenesis of predicted active site residues
                     and separate expression of the two domains confirmed this 
                     hypothesis |CITS: [18186482][24293650]|. The oxidoreductase
                     activity is able to utilize the advanced glycation 
                     endproduct (AGE) carboxyethyl-lysine <i>in vitro</i>, 
                     restoring it to lysine. A &Delta;<i>lysA</i> lysine 
                     auxotroph was found to be able to utilize 
                     carboxyethyl-lysine as the sole source of lysine. 
                     Structure-based engineering enabled selection of mutants 
                     with increased catalytic activity |CITS: [31013547]|.
                     A crystal structure of MnmC containing the FAD cofactor has
                     been solved at 3 &Aring; resolution. The catalytic sites of
                     the two domains face opposite sides of the protein, arguing
                     against substrate channeling |CITS: [21574198]|. A crystal 
                     structure of the <i>E. coli</i> B enzyme (which is 99% 
                     identical to the K-12 enzyme) containing both FAD and SAM 
                     has also been solved |CITS: [23617613]|.
                     |CITS: [21306992]| report that an <i>mnmC</i> null mutant 
                     has a lower growth rate than wild type, while |CITS: 
                     [24293650]| found no effect of any tested <i>mnmC</i> 
                     mutation on growth rate, although growth competition assays
                     showed that the wild type does outcompete certain 
                     <i>mnmC</i> mutants.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [22386868][22905870][25607529][40210524]|"
