     gene            complement(2449806..2450291)
                     /gene="sixA"
                     /locus_tag="b2340"
                     /label=sixA
     CDS             complement(2449806..2450291)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sixA"
                     /locus_tag="b2340"
                     /product="bifunctional phosphohistidine phosphatase SixA / 
                     phosphohistidine phosphatase"
                     /label=sixA
                     /note="SixA is a phosphohistidine phosphatase. Genetic
                     evidence suggested that |FRAME: MONOMER0-4292 NPr-P| is a 
                     target for dephosphorylation by SixA |CITS: [30482831]|; 
                     the activity has now been confirmed <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [33199374]|.  Dephosphorylation of NPr-P by SixA involves 
                     formation of a transient phosphohistidine intermediate 
                     |CITS: [33199374]|.
                     SixA was shown to remove the phosphoryl group from the 
                     His717 residue of |FRAME: PHOSPHO-ARCB717 ArcB| <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [9489669][10734219]|. Initial results 
                     showed that under anaerobic growth conditions, 
                     dephosphorylation of ArcB accelerates the derepression of 
                     the <i>sdh</i> operon via the ArcB/ArcA phosphorelay in the
                     presence of anaerobic electron acceptors |CITS: 
                     [10734219]|. However, a later report shows no evidence that
                     SixA affects ArcB/ArcA signaling to <i>icdA</i> expression 
                     under anaerobic conditions <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [30482831]|. SixA dephosphorylates the inactive, 
                     phosphorylated |FRAME: 6PFK-1-MONOMER| reversing its 
                     inhibition |CITS: [40201162]|.
                     Crystal structures of the free and tungstate-bound forms of
                     SixA have been solved at 2.06 and 1.9 &Aring; resolution 
                     |CITS: [10089421][15670209][17628145]|.
                     A His8Arg mutant of SixA lacks phosphohistidine phosphatase
                     activity both <i>in vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [9489669]|; a His8Ala mutant is unable to dephosphorylate 
                     NPr-P <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [33199374]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>sixA</i> mutant grows more slowly than wild type 
                     under aerobic conditions on minimal medium with glycerol, 
                     acetate, maltose, or glucose as the carbon source; this 
                     growth defect is independent of ArcA/ArcB |CITS: 
                     [30482831]|. Both a &Delta;|FRAME: G6620 <i>ycgO</i>| 
                     mutant and overexpression of |FRAME: EG11682 <i>ptsN</i>| 
                     suppress the slow growth phenotype of &Delta;<i>sixA</i> 
                     |CITS: [30482831]|. In a competition experiment, a 
                     &Delta;<i>sixA</i> mutant is defective in colonizing the 
                     mouse intestine; this defect appears to be due to aberrant 
                     dephosphorylation of NPr-P |CITS: [33199374]|.
                     Expression of <i>sixA</i> is dependent on the alternative 
                     sigma factor &sigma;<sup>E</sup> |CITS: [12900013]|.
                     SixA: ""<b>s</b>ignal <b>i</b>nhibitory factor <b>X</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [9489669]|
                     Review: |CITS: [31707654]|"
