     gene            complement(2450494..2452638)
                     /gene="fadJ"
                     /locus_tag="b2341"
                     /label=fadJ
     CDS             complement(2450494..2452638)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fadJ"
                     /locus_tag="b2341"
                     /product="multifunctional anaerobic multifunctional fatty
                     acid oxidation complex subunit alpha / 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA 
                     dehydrogenase / enoyl-CoA hydratase / 3-hydroxybutyryl-CoA 
                     epimerase / dodecenoyl-CoA D-isomerase / anaerobic fatty 
                     acid beta-oxidation complex"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.35; 4.2.1.17; 4.2.1.74; 5.1.2.3; 5.3.3.8"
                     /label=fadJ
                     /note="During anaerobic &beta;-oxidation of fatty acids,
                     |FRAME: G7213 FadI|, |FRAME: G7212 FadJ|, and |FRAME: 
                     EG12357 FadK| serve functions parallel to those of |FRAME: 
                     EG10278 FadA|, |FRAME: EG10279 FadB|, and |FRAME: EG11530 
                     FadD| in the aerobic pathway |CITS: [12535077]|.
                     FadJ shows sequence similarity to FadB; the enoyl-CoA 
                     hydratase, 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA epimerase, and 
                     3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase active site residues appear
                     to be conserved. In a strain engineered for 
                     polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) production, FadJ is essential 
                     for processing of long-chain fatty acids to medium-chain 
                     PHA if <i>fadB</i> is absent. Crude cell extracts from a 
                     strain that overproduced FadJ contain increased enoyl-CoA 
                     hydratase and 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase activity 
                     |CITS: [12270828]|.
                     FadJ and FadI exhibit partial functional redundancy with 
                     FadA and FadB under aerobic conditions; the two complexes 
                     are proposed to increase efficiency of &beta;-oxidation by 
                     favoring substrates of different chain length |CITS: 
                     [12535077]|. Comparison of the structures of the two 
                     enzymes showed that the fatty acyl tail binding tunnel of 
                     the |FRAME: G7212 FadJ| hydratase domain is wider than that
                     of |FRAME: EG10279 FadB|, accommodating longer fatty acyl 
                     tails |CITS: [37192613]|.
                     FadR represses transcription of <i>fadIJ</i> in the 
                     presence of oxygen |CITS: [12535077]|. Transcription of 
                     <i>fadJ</i> is induced upon biofilm formation compared to 
                     planktonic growth in both exponential and stationary phase.
                     Induction of expression was found to be independent of the 
                     presence of the F plasmid |CITS: [14731270]|.
                     A <i>fadJ</i> mutant is impaired in biofilm formation 
                     |CITS: [14731270]|."
