                     /locus_tag="b2342"
                     /label=fadI
     CDS             complement(2452638..2453948)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fadI"
                     /locus_tag="b2342"
                     /product="trifunctional 3-ketoacyl-CoA thiolase FadI /
                     acetyl-CoA C-acyltransferase / anaerobic fatty acid 
                     beta-oxidation complex"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.16"
                     /label=fadI
                     /note="During anaerobic &beta;-oxidation of fatty acids,
                     |FRAME: G7213 FadI|, |FRAME: G7212 FadJ|, and |FRAME: 
                     EG12357 FadK| serve functions parallel to those of |FRAME: 
                     EG10278 FadA|, |FRAME: EG10279 FadB|, and |FRAME: EG11530 
                     FadD| in the aerobic pathway |CITS: [12535077]|.
                     FadI and FadJ also exhibit partial functional redundancy 
                     with FadA and FadB under aerobic conditions; the two 
                     complexes are proposed to increase efficiency of 
                     &beta;-oxidation by favoring substrates of different chain 
                     length |CITS: [12535077]|.
                     A strain producing FadI and FadJ from a plasmid exhibits 
                     thiolase activity with &beta;-ketoacyl-CoAs of 6 to 12 
                     carbon units, but not with acetoacetyl-CoA |CITS: 
                     [12270828]|.
                     It has been shown that the &beta;-oxidation enzymes can be 
                     induced to work in the reverse direction, a phenomenon that
                     is being utilized for metabolic engineering |CITS: 
                     [21832992][23656231]|. In the reverse direction FadI acts 
                     on an acyl-CoA and acetyl-CoA, catalyzing a 
                     non-decarboxylative Claisen condensation reaction that 
                     forms a 3-oxo-acyl-CoA elongated by two carbons. The enzyme
                     has a broad substrate range when working in the reverse 
                     reaction, and was shown to act on |FRAME: CINNAMOYL-COA| 
                     and |FRAME: CPD-503|, catalyzing two rounds of extension 
                     and resulting in formation of the styrylpyrones |FRAME: 
                     CPD-22601| and |FRAME: CPD-26560| |CITS: [35726672]|.
                     |FRAME: G7213 FadI| and |FRAME: G7212 FadJ| are transcribed
                     together. FadR represses transcription of <i>fadIJ</i> in 
                     the presence of oxygen |CITS: [12535077]|. FadI is a 
                     putative C-tail-anchored (TA) membrane protein |CITS: 
                     [20406421]|."
