     gene            2454785..2456125
                     /gene="fadL"
                     /locus_tag="b2344"
                     /label=fadL
     CDS             2454785..2456125
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fadL"
                     /locus_tag="b2344"
                     /product="bifunctional long-chain fatty acid outer
                     membrane channel / bacteriophage T2 receptor / long-chain 
                     fatty acid outer membrane porin; bacteriophage T2 binding"
                     /label=fadL
                     /note="FadL is an outer membrane, ligand gated channel
                     that functions in the uptake of long chain (C12-C18) fatty 
                     acids (LCFAs) |CITS: [356053][21593406]|. Exogenous 
                     long-chain fatty acids are first recognized at the surface 
                     of FadL by a low-affinity binding site, followed by 
                     diffusion to a high-affinity binding site within a barrel. 
                     This translocation displaces an N-terminus plug, opening 
                     the barrel and resulting in release of the substrate in the
                     outer membrane.
                     Mutations in FadL can be generated that distinguish regions
                     required for binding from those required for transport 
                     |CITS: [1985953][8340375]|. FadL binding affinity is 
                     highest for |FRAME: OLEATE-CPD| and |FRAME: PALMITATE|; no 
                     binding of the medium-chain acid |FRAME: CPD-3617| is 
                     detected |CITS: [2204431]|.
                     FadL crystal structures at 2.6 and 2.8 &Aring; indicate 
                     that the protein forms a 14-stranded antiparallel &beta; 
                     barrel. The amino terminus forms a small ""hatch"" domain 
                     which plugs the barrel on the extracellular side. A 
                     prominent hydrophobic pocket is located inside the barrel 
                     towards the extracellular side of the membrane and forms a 
                     high affinity substrate binding site; a hydrophobic groove 
                     formed between two extracellular loops may constitute a 
                     low-affinity site for initial interaction with LCFAs. 
                     &beta;-strand S3 adopts an unusual conformation which 
                     disrupts interstrand H bonding and results in a gap in the 
                     barrel wall. There is no channel connecting the 
                     extracellular space with the periplasm |CITS: [15178802]|. 
                     The transport of long chain fatty acids through FadL is 
                     thought to involve diffusion of the substrate coupled with 
                     conformational change of FadL |CITS: [15178802][18811191]|.
                     A FadL mutant (A77E S100R) in which the lateral opening in 
                     the barrel wall is constricted does not transport 
                     substrate. This result supports a lateral diffusion model 
                     for transport whereby long-chain fatty acids exit into the 
                     outer membrane through an opening in the FadL barrel wall 
                     and then diffuse into the periplasm |CITS:[19182779]|. High
                     affinity binding of LCFAs results in conformational change 
                     that drives the formation of a membrane facing exit channel
                     and release of substrate |CITS: [21593406]|.
                     Once the acids exit FadL, they crossg the inner membrane 
                     coupled to activation by the cytosolic |FRAME: 
                     ACYLCOASYN-CPLX| (FadD).
                     FadL also serves as the receptor site for bacteriophage T2 
                     |CITS: [3536849][3286621]|. Bacteriophage T2 binding 
                     specificity resides in the amino terminal region of FadL 
                     |CITS: [10845710]|.
                     FadL is reported to exist as a monomer |CITS: [8340375]| or
                     a dimer |CITS: [16079137]| in the outer membrane. 
                     <i>fadL</i> exhibits mosaic evolution patterns in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [40444982]|.
                     <i>ttr</i>: bacteriophage <U>T2 r</U>esistance |CITS: 
                     [3536849]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16005205]|<BR>Comments: |CITS: 
                     [21693645]|"
