     gene            complement(2457731..2458486)
                     /gene="mlaA"
                     /locus_tag="b2346"
                     /label=mlaA
     CDS             complement(2457731..2458486)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mlaA"
                     /locus_tag="b2346"
                     /product="intermembrane phospholipid transport system -
                     outer membrane lipoprotein MlaA"
                     /label=mlaA
                     /note="MlaA is an outer membrane lipoprotein |CITS:
                     [12876315]| implicated in a retrograde phospholipid 
                     trafficking pathway that is thought to maintain outer 
                     membrane lipid asymmetry by removing mislocalized outer 
                     leaflet phospholipids and transporting them back to the 
                     inner membrane; the Mla system comprises MlaA, the |FRAME: 
                     ABC-45-CPLX ""MlaFEDB""| complex in the inner membrane and 
                     a periplasmic protein, |FRAME: G7659-MONOMER ""MlaC""| 
                     |CITS: [19383799][27529189]|. Evidence that the Mla system 
                     functions for anterograde phospholipid movement has also 
                     been reported |CITS: [31235958]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>mlaA</i> mutant has permeability defects of the
                     outer membrane; the mutant strain shows no defects in LPS 
                     or outer membrane protein levels but does have increased 
                     sensitivity to SDS-EDTA; a &Delta;<i>mlaA</i> mutation in 
                     combination with any of &Delta;<i>mlaF/E/D</i> or 
                     &Delta;<i>mlaC</i> exhibits the same phenotype |CITS: 
                     [19383799]|. &Delta;<i>mlaA</i> cells accumulate 
                     phospholipid in the outer leaflet of the outer membrane; 
                     MlaA interacts specifically with the outer membrane 
                     proteins |FRAME: CPLX0-7533 ""OmpC""| and |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7534 ""OmpF""|; strong genetic interaction between 
                     <i>mlaA</i> and <i>ompC</i> suggests that the proteins 
                     function together within the Mla system |CITS: [26314242]|.
                     The interaction with trimeric porins may be required for 
                     MlaA to adopt a functional conformation |CITS: [38092770]|.
                     Mechanistic insight into retrograde phospholipid transfer 
                     from OmpC-MlaA to MlaC is provided by |CITS: [38092770]|. 
                     MlaA recruits lipids to OmpF<sub>3</sub>-MlaA complexes; 
                     the OmpF<sub>3</sub>-MlaA-MlaC lipid shuttle has been 
                     reconstituted and dissected |CITS: [40168124]|.
                     The gain-of-function <i>mlaA*</i> mutation (loss of amino 
                     acid residues N41 and F42) results in increased outer 
                     membrane permeability and an increase in surface exposed 
                     phospholipids; this phenotype is independent of the other 
                     Mla system proteins; <i>mlaA*</i> results in outer membrane
                     vesiculation and causes cell death in stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [26929379]|. <i>mlaA*</i> cells undergo a lengthy 
                     death trajectory which is described in |CITS: [26929379]|. 
                     <i>mlaA*</i>  causes high flux passive phospholipid 
                     movement to the outer membrane |CITS: [33046656]|.
                     An <i>mlaA</i> knockout mutant displays increased 
                     resistance to vancomycin and plays a role in virulence 
                     against silkworms |CITS: [35830444]|.
                     <i>mla</i>: <U>m</U>aintenance of outer membrane 
                     <U>l</U>ipid <U>a</U>symmetry |CITS: 
                     [19383799]|<BR><i>vacJ</i>: 
                     <U>v</U>irulence-<U>a</U>ssociated <U>c</U>hromosome locus 
                     J |CITS: [8145644]|"
