     gene            2492150..2493847
                     /gene="pyrS"
                     /locus_tag="b2380"
                     /label=pyrS
     CDS             2492150..2493847
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pyrS"
                     /locus_tag="b2380"
                     /product="bifunctional sensor histidine kinase PyrS / 
                     protein-histidine autokinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.13.3"
                     /label=pyrS
                     /note="PyrS is the sensory kinase of a two-component
                     signal transduction system which senses extracellular 
                     pyruvate and induces expression of the putative pyruvate 
                     transporter |FRAME: YHJX-MONOMER ""YhjX""| |CITS: 
                     [23222720][30624637]|.
                     PyrS is predicted to be an inner membrane protein with 6 
                     trans-membrane domains |CITS: [23222720]|. Phosphorylation 
                     of PyrS has not been detected <i>in vitro</i>, however 
                     substitution of the conserved histidine residue H371 with 
                     glutamine prevents <i>yhjX</i> induction <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [23222720]|. <i>yhjX</i> is not induced in a 
                     <i>pyrSRypdC</i> deletion mutant; complementation with 
                     <i>pyrSRypdC</i> expressed <i>in trans</i> restores 
                     induction |CITS: [23222720]|. <i>yhjX</i> and <i>yhcC</i> 
                     promoter activity decreases, and <i>gltB</i> promoter 
                     activity increases, when a <i>pyrS</i> deletion mutant is 
                     grown in pyruvate minimal media |CITS: [30624637]|.
                     PyrS interacts with |FRAME: YHJX-MONOMER ""YhjX""| and with
                     the pyruvate:H+ symporter |FRAME: G7942-MONOMER ""BtsT""|; 
                     PyrS is capable of homo-oligomerisation |CITS: [24659770]|.
                     A <i>pyrSR</i> deletion mutant (&Delta;<i>ypdAB1334</i>) 
                     does not show a significant phenotype when measured by 
                     phenotype microarray |CITS: [12897016]|.
                     <i>Note</i>: bacterial histidine kinases function as 
                     homodimers; both <i>cis</i>- and 
                     <i>trans</i>-autophosphorylation mechanisms have been 
                     documented (see |CITS: [19575571][25982528]|). In the 
                     absence of experimental evidence PyrS is represented as a 
                     monomer in the EcoCyc database."
