     gene            complement(2501940..2502905)
                     /gene="glk"
                     /locus_tag="b2388"
                     /label=glk
     CDS             complement(2501940..2502905)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glk"
                     /locus_tag="b2388"
                     /product="bifunctional glucokinase / glucosamine kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.2; 2.7.1.8"
                     /label=glk
                     /note="The cytoplasmic glucokinase is not required for
                     growth on glucose as the carbon source, because glucose is 
                     phosphorylated during transport by the PTS system. 
                     Glucokinase phosphorylates glucose which is produced by 
                     amylomaltase.
                     Growth on glucose reduces the expression of <i>glk</i> by 
                     50%. Growth on other carbon sources does not appear to 
                     affect <i>glk</i> expression |CITS: [9023215]|. 
                     Overexpression of foreign proteins appears to induce 
                     expression of
                     <i>glk</i> as well |CITS: [11056062]|.
                     A crystal structure of the enzyme from <i>E. coli</i> 
                     O157:H7 has been reported |CITS: [15466045]|. Evolutionary 
                     relationships between glucose kinases based on their 
                     tertiary structures have been discussed |CITS: [16233797]|.
                     A periplasmic glucokinase whose expression is induced 
                     during overexpression of foreign proteins has been 
                     reported, but it does not appear to be the product of the 
                     <i>glk</i> gene |CITS: [7786044]|.
                     A role for cytoplasmic glucose and Glk in the complex 
                     regulation of maltose and maltodextrin utilization by <i>E.
                     coli</i> has been proposed. The maltose utilization system 
                     is controlled by MalT, the central transcriptional 
                     activator of <i>mal</i> genes. Glk was suggested to exert 
                     regulatory effects on MalT. A model for MalT activity 
                     inhibition via binding of Glk to MalT monomers was 
                     proposed. In addition, a model for the effect of 
                     competition between Mlc (the transcriptional repressor of 
                     MalT) and Glk for binding to PtsG (the glucose transporter)
                     on repression of MalT expression was proposed |CITS: 
                     [19028900]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> Glk has been used as a model system for the 
                     study of active site amino acid residues that are important
                     for catalysis. Site-directed mutagenesis identified 
                     residues important in non-hydrogen bonding (van der Waals) 
                     contacts with bound substrate. This study is in contrast to
                     many site-directed mutagenesis studies which focus on polar
                     residues that form hydrogen bonds with bound substrate 
                     |CITS: [17766388]|.
                     Lysine acetylation of Glk chemically by acetyl phosphate is
                     a major mechanism of acetylation compared to enzymatic 
                     acetylation by |FRAME: G7350-MONOMER| (YfiQ); acetylation 
                     was observed at K3, K57, K113, K204, and K284. |FRAME: 
                     G6577-MONOMER| (CobB) was also able to deacetylate Glk 
                     whereas YfiQ was not. Two other acetylated sites, K214 and 
                     K216 (generated using the genetic code expansion approach) 
                     resulted in major impairment of Glk activity, presumably 
                     because their location at the opening of the active site 
                     prevented substrates from entering; CobB can deacetylate 
                     these two sites |CITS: [37996965]|.
                     Based on work with engineered host strains, the important 
                     role of <i>glk</i> in glucose uptake has been determined 
                     |CITS:[31868221]|."
