     gene            complement(2512736..2514151)
                     /gene="gltX"
                     /locus_tag="b2400"
                     /label=gltX
     CDS             complement(2512736..2514151)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gltX"
                     /locus_tag="b2400"
                     /product="glutamate--tRNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.1.1.17"
                     /label=gltX
                     /note="Glutamate-tRNA ligase (GluRS) is a member of the
                     family of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, which interpret the 
                     genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their 
                     specific tRNA molecules. The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis.  GluRS belongs to the Class IB aminoacyl-tRNA 
                     synthetases |CITS: [2203971][7647112]|. GluRS charges 
                     tRNA<sup>Glu</sup> for both protein and 
                     &delta;-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) synthesis.  ALA is the 
                     first committed tetrapyrrole precursor |CITS: [ColiSalII]|.
                     The enzyme only aminoacylates the 2'-OH group of tRNA 
                     substrates with a terminal 3'-deoxyadenosine base |CITS: 
                     [1108023]|. GluRS can bind both ATP and its tRNA substrate 
                     independently, but binding of glutamate requires the 
                     presence of tRNA<sup>Glu</sup> |CITS: 
                     [386286][229901][7014220]|. A two-step catalytic mechanism 
                     has been proposed |CITS: [6986385][6280993][6249345]|. The 
                     contributions of the five domains of GluRS to substrate 
                     recognition and catalytic activity have been studied |CITS:
                     [19496540]|.
                     A tightly bound Zn<sup>2+</sup> atom is required for native
                     conformation and catalytic activity |CITS: [8218204]|; the 
                     binding site has been identified by site-directed 
                     mutagenesis |CITS: [7797500]| and belongs to the SWIM 
                     domain family |CITS: [14764088]|.
                     The |FRAME: EG10443-MONOMER| phosphorylates the conserved 
                     Ser239 residue near the active site of GluRS and inhibits 
                     its aminoacylation activity, leading to an increased 
                     concentration of uncharged tRNA<sup>Glu</sup> and 
                     triggering (p)ppGpp synthesis |CITS: [24095282][24343429]|.
                     Using chemoproteomics analysis with a stable analogue of 
                     3-pHis, this protein was identified as a putative 
                     phosphohistidine acceptor at 3 amino acids |CITS: 
                     [40201162]|.
                     Specificity determinants within tRNA<sup>Glu</sup> that are
                     important for recognition by GluRS have been identified 
                     |CITS: 
                     [1726806][1474577][8385989][7505112][8642591][10215844]|. 
                     The 2-thio element of the hypermodified nucleotide 
                     5-methylaminomethyl-2-thiouridine at position 34 in 
                     tRNA<sup>Glu</sup> is an important recognition element for 
                     aminoacylation by GluRS |CITS: 
                     [8385989][9826502][10583410]|. GluRS competes effectively 
                     with TyrRS for aminoacylation of tRNA<sup>Glu</sup> <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [1377381]|. Residues within GluRS that 
                     interact with tRNA<sup>Glu</sup> have been identified 
                     |CITS: [10841758]|. Both the anticodon-binding domain and 
                     the catalytic domain of GluRS may participate in 
                     tRNA<sup>Gln</sup> discrimination |CITS: [19481543]|. The 
                     Arg266 residue may be uniquely involved in phylum-specific 
                     tRNA<sup>Glu</sup> recognition |CITS: [22584057]|. 
                     Temperature-sensitive alleles of <i>gltX</i> have been 
                     isolated |CITS: [4942764][4577134][8479423]|.
                     Expression of GluRS may be regulated post-transcriptionally
                     |CITS: [2201777]|.
                     In general, there is a discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i>. 
                     However, GluRS was the exception, with measured values 
                     matching the predicted ones |CITS: [37224536]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [9426192]|."
