     gene            complement(2523606..2524592)
                     /gene="zipA"
                     /locus_tag="b2412"
                     /label=zipA
     CDS             complement(2523606..2524592)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="zipA"
                     /locus_tag="b2412"
                     /product="cell division protein ZipA"
                     /label=zipA
                     /note="The ZipA protein is an essential component of the
                     cell division machinery |CITS: [9008158]|. ZipA localizes 
                     to the septal ring structure; this localization requires 
                     the presence of the FtsZ ring, but not FtsA |CITS: 
                     [9864327]|, FtsQ |CITS: [9882666]|, FtsL |CITS: 
                     [10027987]|, or FtsI |CITS: [10209756]|, indicating that 
                     FtsZ recruits ZipA to the cell division site. Both ZipA and
                     FtsA are required for recruitment of FtsK |CITS: 
                     [11847116]| and FtsEX |CITS: [14729705]| to the cell 
                     division site; ZipA is required for localization of FtsQ, 
                     FtsL, and FtsN |CITS: [11948172]|. Both FtsN and FtsZ 
                     deprivation lead to disappearance of ZipA rings; upon 
                     restoration of expression, the divisome assembly is 
                     restored |CITS: [20345660]|. Most bacteria lack a 
                     recognizable ZipA homolog.
                     Requirement for ZipA can be bypassed by a single mutation 
                     in FtsA (FtsA*), indicating that FtsA* can stabilize the 
                     FtsZ ring structure and can otherwise substitute for all 
                     ZipA functions |CITS: [12634424]|. Recruitment of the 
                     MinC/DicB complex, but not the MinC/MinD complex, to the 
                     septal ring requires ZipA |CITS: [15060045]|.
                     ZipA has an unusual topology, with its N terminus in the 
                     cytoplasmic membrane and its C terminus in the cytoplasm. 
                     Between these two domains is an unstructured proline and 
                     glutamine-rich linker domain |CITS: [12107152]|. Both the 
                     N-terminal transmembrane domain and the C-terminal 
                     FtsZ-binding domain are required for ZipA function |CITS: 
                     [10960100]|. Although the cytoplasmic domain of ZipA 
                     appears to be monomeric in solution |CITS: [21082789]|, 
                     full-length ZipA is able to homodimerize independently of 
                     interactions with FtsZ |CITS: [22304478]|.
                     ZipA stabilizes and organizes FtsZ protofilaments |CITS: 
                     [10228152]|. FtsZ and ZipA interact through their 
                     C-terminal domains |CITS: [10960100]|; a crystal structure 
                     of the interacting domains has been solved |CITS: 
                     [10880432]|, and the binding interface residues were 
                     analyzed by computational simulation of alanine-scanning 
                     mutagenesis |CITS: [16538616]|. The C-terminal 143 amino 
                     acid residues of ZipA are necessary and sufficient for 
                     binding to FtsZ and induce bundling of FtsZ filaments <i>in
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [10960100]|. The interaction between FtsZ 
                     and a truncated soluble form of ZipA has been studied <i>in
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [21082789]|. Studies focused on the 
                     constriction forces required for the FtsZ protofilaments 
                     and ZipA to start the membrane to curve have been carried 
                     out |CITS: [37806464]|.
                     The requirement for interaction between FtsZ and ZipA is 
                     being exploited for the development of potential 
                     antibacterial compounds; see e.g. |CITS: 
                     [15006376][15351395]|.
                     A solution structure of the C-terminal domain of ZipA 
                     |CITS: [10924108]| and crystal structures of ZipA in 
                     complexes with inhibitors |CITS: [15006376]| have been 
                     determined.
                     ZipA has been found in a complex with YjdB and an 
                     unidentified 24-kDa protein in the inner membrane |CITS: 
                     [16079137]|.
                     ZipA: ""<b>Z</b>-<b>i</b>nteracting <b>p</b>rotein 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [9008158]|
                     Selected reviews: |CITS: [16005287][15491352][12626683][11 
                     163134][37503768][37524757]|"
