     gene            2527123..2527380
                     /gene="ptsH"
                     /locus_tag="b2415"
                     /label=ptsH
     CDS             2527123..2527380
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ptsH"
                     /locus_tag="b2415"
                     /product="phosphocarrier protein HPr"
                     /label=ptsH
                     /note="HPr (histidine containing protein |CITS:
                     [5545082]|) is one of two sugar-non-specific protein 
                     constituents of the phosphoenolpyruvate:sugar 
                     phosphotransferase system (PTS<sup>sugar</sup>). It accepts
                     a phosphoryl group from phosphorylated Enzyme I (PtsI-P) 
                     and then transfers it to the EIIA domain of any one of the 
                     many sugar-specific enzymes (collectively known as Enzymes 
                     II) of the PTS<sup>sugar</sup> (reviewed in |CITS: 
                     [8246840]|).
                     HPr is a small monomeric, single domain, protein that is 
                     relatively heat stable |CITS: [4942330]|. HPr is 
                     phosphorylated at the N-1 position of histidine residue 15 
                     (His-15) |CITS: [4942330][7803390]|. Phosphorylated HPr 
                     transfers its phosphate to the active histidine of the 
                     sugar transporters of the PTS<sup>sugar</sup>. Solution 
                     structures of the complex between HPr and the N-terminal 
                     domain of Enzyme I |CITS: [10048929]|, and between HPr and 
                     the EIIA domain/protein of the glucose-specific PTS |CITS: 
                     [11060015]|, the mannitol PTS |CITS: [12202490]| and the 
                     mannose PTS |CITS: [15788390]| have been obtained. HPr uses
                     a consensus surface to interact with the varying proteins 
                     of the PTS<sup>sugar</sup> |CITS: [10764730]|. Preferential
                     utilization of glucose over mannitol is dependent upon HPr;
                     dephosphorylated HPr, which accumulates during glucose 
                     transport, interacts specifically with |FRAME: PD00369 
                     MtlR| and enhances its repressor activity resulting in the 
                     inhibition of mannitol utilization |CITS: [28225088]| 
                     (further |CITS: [31558743]|).
                     Purified HPr binds to, and enhances the oligomeric 
                     associations of |FRAME: GLYCOPHOSPHORYL-CPLX glycogen 
                     phosphorylase (GlgP)|; HPr stimulates GlgP activity <i>in 
                     vitro</i> (decreased <i>K</i><sub>m</sub> for glycogen; 
                     increased <i>V</i><sub>max</sub> for phosphate); 
                     phosphorylated HPr has 4-fold higher affinity for GlgP than
                     the unphosphorylated form, but does not appear to stimulate
                     GlgP activity |CITS: [9334229]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [11361069]|). The unphosphorylated form of HPr binds to the
                     anti-sigma factor, |FRAME: EG11738-MONOMER Rsd| with high 
                     affinity; dephosphorylated HPr interacts with and 
                     sequesters Rsd from &sigma;<sup>70</sup> <i>in vitro</i>; 
                     dephosphorylated HPr antagonizes Rsd activity resulting in 
                     increased &sigma;<sup>70</sup> dependent transcription and 
                     decreased &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent transcription <i>in
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [24324139]| (see also |CITS: [26457424]|). 
                     HPr copurifies with the AI-2 kinase |FRAME: G6798-MONOMER 
                     LsrK|; HPr inhibits LsrK activity <i>in vitro</i>; 
                     phosphorylated HPr has a reduced ability to inhibit LsrK 
                     activity; HPr mediated regulation of LsrK may link sugar 
                     metabolism to quorum sensing activity |CITS: [29868643]|. 
                     HPr is an allosteric activator of |FRAME: PKI-COMPLEX|, 
                     |FRAME: 6PFK-2-CPX| and |FRAME: 
                     GLUCOSAMINE-6-P-DEAMIN-CPLX|; phosphorylated HPr is an 
                     allosteric inhibitor of |FRAME: ADENYL-KIN-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [28634232][29229699]|.
                     Both of the general PTS proteins - PtsI and PtsH - localize
                     mainly near the cell poles |CITS: [20924357]|. Phosphoryl 
                     flux through PTS systems (via Enzyme I and HPr) plays a 
                     regulatory role in coordinating carbon utilization strategy
                     and more specifically in switching between the two 
                     alternative metabolic strategies of hierarchical sugar 
                     utilization versus co-utilization |CITS: [26784570]|. HPr 
                     is responsible for the phosphorylation of |FRAME: 
                     6PFK-1-MONOMER| creating an inactive isozyme which is 
                     dephosphorylated by |FRAME: G7211-MONOMER| reversing the 
                     inhibition |CITS: [40201162]|.
                     The Keio collection <i>ptsH</i> mutant exhibits a 'low 
                     level' of resistance to lithium exposure |CITS: 
                     [39983623]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9923705]|<BR>Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [11361064][21621292][22573269]|"
