     gene            2529193..2529702
                     /gene="crr"
                     /locus_tag="b2417"
                     /label=crr
     CDS             2529193..2529702
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="crr"
                     /locus_tag="b2417"
                     /product="multifunctional Enzyme IIA(Glc) /
                     glucose-specific PTS enzyme II / trehalose-specific PTS 
                     enzyme II / N-acetylmuramic acid-specific PTS enzyme II"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.192; 2.7.1.201; 2.7.1.199"
                     /label=crr
                     /note="EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> is an intermediate
                     phosphotransfer protein in the uptake and phosphorylation 
                     of glucose |CITS: [8089109]|, N-acetylmuramic acid |CITS: 
                     [15060041]| and trehalose |CITS: [2160944]| and also 
                     probably interacts with MalX - a PTS permease whose 
                     physiological substrate is unclear |CITS: [1856179]|. 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> accepts a phosphoryl group from |FRAME: 
                     PTSH-MONOMER ""PtsH""| and transfers it to the the EIIB 
                     domain of the sugar PTS permeases.
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> is phosphorylated by phosphoenolpyruvate
                     (PEP) in a reaction requiring PtsH and |FRAME: PTSI-MONOMER
                     ""PtsI""|. EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> is phosphorylated at the N3 
                     position of His90 |CITS: [6383826][2657735]|. Solution 
                     structures of the complex between PtsH and 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> and between EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> and 
                     |FRAME: PTSG-MONOMER ""EIIB<sup>Glc</sup>""| have been 
                     determined. The binding surface on EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> for 
                     EIIB<sup>Glc</sup> and PtsH overlap. There are both 
                     similarities and differences between the binding 
                     interaction of EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> with PtsH and 
                     EIIB<sup>Glc</sup> |CITS: [11060015][12716891]|. Two forms 
                     of EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> have been purified from <i>E.coli</i>
                     K-12 and identified on the basis of electrophoretic 
                     mobility as EIIA<sup>Glc</sup><sub>fast</sub> and 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup><sub>slow</sub>. 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup><sub>fast</sub> lacks an N-terminal 
                     heptapeptide region due to proteolytic activity which 
                     appears to reside in the outer membrane. The two forms are 
                     equally able to act as phosphoacceptors but 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup><sub>fast</sub> is an inefficient 
                     phosphodonor |CITS: [3531206][6754734]|.
                     Crr or Enzyme IIA<sup>Glc</sup> also plays an important 
                     role in the regulation of carbon metabolism in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 (|CITS: [789369]| and reviewed in |CITS: 
                     [8246840]| and |CITS: [17158705]|). EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> is a
                     negative regulator of several enzymes involved in the 
                     transport and metabolism of non-PTS carbohydrates such as 
                     glycerol, lactose, melibiose and maltose. Purified, 
                     unphosphorylated EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> binds to, and inhibits 
                     galactoside transport by the lactose permease <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [16453452][7041121]|. Purified, 
                     unphosphorylated EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> binds to, and inhibits 
                     glycerol kinase <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [2985549][3013838]|.
                     Purified EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> binds to the melibiose 
                     symporter, MelB, in both the presence and absence of 
                     melibiose; binding inhibits the conformation dynamics and 
                     melibiose affinity of the transport protein. 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> binds to LacY and MelB in a 1:1 
                     stoichiometry; binding constrains conformational dynamics 
                     and prevents both permeases from interacting with their 
                     sugar substrates; different thermodynamic mechanisms are 
                     responsible for  EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> binding to LacY and 
                     MelB |CITS: [25296751][25675534]|.
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> inhibits the activity of the |FRAME: 
                     ABC-16-CPLX| reconstituted in proteopliposomes |CITS: 
                     [2250006]|. EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> interacts with the 
                     nucleotide binding domain and the C-terminal regulatory 
                     domain of the MalK dimer and inhibits the ATPase activity 
                     of the maltose transporter  reconstituted in 
                     proteoliposomes. The stoichiometry of interaction between 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> and MalFGK<sub>2</sub> is 2:1 |CITS: 
                     [23821551]|.  A crystal structure of EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> in 
                     complex with the maltose ABC transporter has been obtained.
                     Two molecules of EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> interact with the MalK 
                     subunits and stabilise an inward facing conformation of the
                     transporter. This interaction would prevent the structural 
                     rearrangements required for ATP hydrolysis and maltose 
                     transport. The phosphorylation site, His90, is located at 
                     the EIIA<sup>Glc</sup>-MalK interface, and is in its 
                     unphosphorylated form. Phosphorylation of the active 
                     histidine is predicted to disrupt the interface |CITS: 
                     [23770568]|.
                     The N-terminal domain of EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> (residues 1-18)
                     adopts a helical conformation in detergent micelles and in 
                     the presence of <i>E. coli</i> lipids. The N-terminal 
                     domain probably acts as a membrane anchor and allows 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> to shuttle between the cytoplasm and the
                     inner membrane |CITS: [11044440][12717030]|. Full-length 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> is concentrated near the inner surface 
                     of the cytoplasmic membrane via its N-terminal region 
                     |CITS: [23770568]|.
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> is also involved in the regulation of 
                     |FRAME: ADENYLATECYC-MONOMER| activity although the precise
                     mechanism remains unclear |CITS: [765335][6245052] 
                     [6321447][3545197][2844115][2155859][9749675]|. A direct 
                     interaction between membrane tethered EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> 
                     (both phosphorylated and unphosphorylated) and adenylate 
                     cyclase has been demonstrated <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [16407219]|. Adenylate cyclase activity is not affected by 
                     either phosphorylated or dephosphorylated 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> however phosphorylated 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> activates adenylate cyclase in the 
                     presence of a crude <i>E. coli</i> extract implying that 
                     regulation depends upon an additional unidentified factor 
                     |CITS: [16407219]|. Phosphorylated EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> is a 
                     positive effector of adenylate cyclase activity while 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> does not affect basal activity |CITS: 
                     [9457881]|.
                     <i>crr</i> insertion-deletion mutants were identified in a 
                     genetic screen for genes that are important for survival of
                     exposure to ionizing radiation (IR).  A <i>crr</i> deletion
                     mutant has a moderate decrease in IR survival |CITS: 
                     [25049088]|. <i>crr</i> insertion-deletion mutants have 
                     growth and morphology defects under nutrient rich 
                     conditions and are unable to grow under gluconeogenic 
                     conditions (AB minimal media + 0.2% succinate); the growth 
                     defects of <i>crr</i> mutants may result from defects in 
                     cAMP synthesis and signaling, rather than changes in 
                     glucose import |CITS: [29432413]|. The Keio collection 
                     <i>crr</i> mutant exhibits a 'low level' of resistance to 
                     lithium exposure |CITS: [39983623]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8432747][2699242]|
                     <i>crr</I>: <U>c</U>arbohydrate <U>r</U>epression 
                     <U>r</U>esistant"
