     gene            complement(2529745..2530596)
                     /gene="pdxK"
                     /locus_tag="b2418"
                     /label=pdxK
     CDS             complement(2529745..2530596)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pdxK"
                     /locus_tag="b2418"
                     /product="multifunctional
                     pyridoxine/pyridoxal/pyridoxamine kinase / pyridoxal kinase
                     1 / hydroxymethylpyrimidine kinase / pyridoxal kinase / 
                     pyridoxine kinase / pyridoxamine kinase / 
                     hydroxymethylpyrimidine kinase / 4-deoxypyridoxine kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.49; 2.7.1.35"
                     /label=pdxK
                     /note="Pyridoxal kinase 1 is able to phosphorylate
                     pyridoxal (PL), pyridoxine (PN), pyridoxamine (PM), and 
                     hydroxymethylpyrimidine (HMP), forming their respective 
                     5'-phosphoesters |CITS: [2540841][8885414][15249053]|. 
                     There are two pyridoxal kinases in <i>E. coli</i>: the 
                     enzyme described here (PdxK), and a second enzyme, |FRAME: 
                     PDXY-MONOMER PdxY| |CITS: [9537380]|. PdxK is the major PL 
                     kinase activity in the cell |CITS: [15249053]|. Both 
                     enzymes function in the scavenger/salvage pathway of 
                     pyridoxal 5'-phosphate (PLP) |CITS: [8764513]|. The HMP 
                     kinase activity of PdxK contributes to thiamine salvage 
                     |CITS: [8885414]|.
                     Under physiological conditions, MgATP is the favored 
                     cosubstrate over ZnATP |CITS: [15249053]|. Potassium is 
                     required for catalytic activity; kinetic and inhibitor 
                     studies suggest a sequential random addition of the 
                     substrates |CITS: [16740960]|. After approximately 25 
                     catalytic turnovers, the pyridoxal kinase activity is 
                     inhibited by covalent binding of the PLP product, likely to
                     the Lys229 residue within the active site |CITS: 
                     [22848564]|. This complex may be formed by initial 
                     formation of a Schiff base bond between pyridoxal and 
                     Lys229, followed by a single catalytic cycle that converts 
                     pyridoxal to PLP |CITS: [25655354]|. PLP-bound PdxK can 
                     transfer PLP to apo-serine hydroxymethyltransferase, 
                     regaining some catalytic activity |CITS: [22848564]|.
                     PdxK was found to be very sensitive to |FRAME: CPD0-1605|, 
                     which acts as a competitive inhibitor with a Ki of 0.5 
                     &mu;M |CITS: [37040165]|.
                     Crystal structures of PdxK alone and in complexes with 
                     pyridoxal or MgATP have been determined |CITS: [16740960]|.
                     The K229 residue is involved in substrate binding and 
                     catalysis |CITS: [22848564]|.
                     A <i>pdxK</i> mutant is able to grow on medium lacking PL 
                     or PN; <i>pdxK</i> is thus not required for <i>de novo</i> 
                     PLP biosynthesis |CITS: [8764513]|. Immobilizing PdxK in an
                     epoxy resin resulted in increased production of pyridoxal 
                     5'-phosphate and could be potentially used for industrial 
                     production |CITS: [39901467]| .
                     PdxK: ""<b>p</b>yri<b>d</b>o<b>x</b>al <b>k</b>inase"" 
                     |CITS: [8764513]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10382260][11200221]|"
