     gene            complement(2532031..2532942)
                     /gene="cysM"
                     /locus_tag="b2421"
                     /label=cysM
     CDS             complement(2532031..2532942)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cysM"
                     /locus_tag="b2421"
                     /product="multifunctional cysteine synthase B /
                     O-acetylserine sulfhydrylase / S-sulfo-L-cysteine synthase 
                     / L-cysteine desulfhydrase"
                     /EC_number="2.5.1.47; 2.5.1.144; 4.4.1.1"
                     /label=cysM
                     /note="Cysteine synthase B (CysM) is one of two isozymes
                     in <i>E. coli</i> that catalyze the formation of L-cysteine
                     from O-acetyl-L-serine and sulfide |CITS: [3329675]|.  
                     Unlike cysteine synthase A, cysteine synthase B does not 
                     form a complex with serine acetyltransferase |CITS: 
                     [16546401]|.
                     Cysteine synthase B can also use thiosulfate in place of 
                     sulfide, producing S-sulfocysteine |CITS: 
                     [3329675][12640465]|.  The broad substrate specificity of 
                     cysteine synthase was exploited for the synthesis of 
                     unnatural L-&alpha;-amino acids |CITS: [12640465]|. Initial
                     synthesis rates of nonproteinaceous amino acids have been 
                     determined |CITS: [16546401]|.
                     CysM provides one of the activities that is able to provide
                     sulfur for Fe-S cluster biosynthesis <i>in vitro</i> |CITS:
                     [8663055]|.  CysM is one of at least five enzymes that 
                     exhibit cysteine desulfhydrase activity (CD) in <i>E. 
                     coli</i>.  Deletion of each of the <i>cysM</i>, 
                     <i>metC</i>, <i>tnaA</i>, <i>cysK</i> and <i>malY</i> genes
                     individually decreases CD activity |CITS: 
                     [12883870][16000837]|
                     Crystal structures of CysM have been solved at 2.1, 2.7 and
                     1.33 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [15952768][17894825]|. The 
                     differing substrate specificities of the two isozymes CysK 
                     and CysM could be explained by comparison of the CysM 
                     structure to that of CysK from <i>Salmonella</i> 
                     Typhimurium |CITS: [15952768]|.  Mutants in predicted 
                     active site residues were investigated |CITS: [17894825]|. 
                     A CysM mutant in which all cysteine residues were replaced 
                     by serine (CysM-C) has higher cysteine synthase activity 
                     than wild-type and rescues growth of a &Delta;<i>cysM 
                     cysK</i> mutant.  In addition, the CysM-C-containing strain
                     has a shorter lag phase on minimal medium |CITS: 
                     [29379050]|.
                     <i>cysM</i> mutants can not grow on thiosulfate as the sole
                     source of sulfur |CITS: [3329675]|.  More recent 
                     experiments showed that a &Delta;<i>cysM</i> mutant can 
                     grow very slowly on thiosulfate as a sulfur source. 
                     Overexpression of |FRAME: EG10395-MONOMER GlpE| restores 
                     growth to wild-type levels |CITS: [28756590]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [30046857]|"
