     gene            complement(2549769..2550656)
                     /gene="eutC"
                     /locus_tag="b2440"
                     /label=eutC
     CDS             complement(2549769..2550656)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="eutC"
                     /locus_tag="b2440"
                     /product="bifunctional ethanolamine ammonia-lyase subunit
                     beta / ethanolamine ammonia-lyase"
                     /EC_number="4.3.1.7"
                     /label=eutC
                     /note="An <i>E. coli</i> BW25113 mutant, which had the 
                     interrupting prophage between <i>eutA</i> and <i>eutB</i> 
                     removed, grows similarly to the <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strain 
                     W3110, which lacks the prophage in its <i>eut</i> 
                     transcription unit, when provided with ethanolamine (EA) as
                     the sole nitrogen source |CITS: [39023255]|.
                     Further experiments to analyze ethanolamine utilization and
                     the microcompartments were carried out in the <i>E. 
                     coli</i> W3110 strain. A GFP-tagged <i>eutC</i> strain of 
                     W3110 formed distinct puncta of irregular polyhedrals 
                     surrounded by a shell layer with sharp edges and vertices 
                     observed by fluorescent microscopy and cryo-electron 
                     tomography, likely representing the ethanolamine 
                     utilization microcompartments. A <sup>13</sup>C-fluxome 
                     study with carbon supplied by <sup>13</sup>C<sub>2</sub>-EA
                     and <sup>12</sup>C<sub>3</sub>-glycerol found ~90% of the 
                     ethanol product had <sup>13</sup>C directly indicating EA 
                     utilization in <i>E. coli</i>. The study also showed that 
                     EA and glycerol contributed equally to acetyl-P synthesis; 
                     a dynamic isotopic model predicted that the EA produced 
                     acetyl-P primarily ended up in central metabolism through 
                     the TCA cycle |CITS: [39023255]|.
                     <i>eut</i> = <u>e</u>thanolamine <u>u</u>tilization"
