     gene            complement(2569457..2571736)
                     /gene="maeB"
                     /locus_tag="b2463"
                     /label=maeB
     CDS             complement(2569457..2571736)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="maeB"
                     /locus_tag="b2463"
                     /product="trifunctional malate dehydrogenase 
                     (oxaloacetate-decarboxylating) (NADP(+)) / malate 
                     dehydrogenase / malolactic enzyme"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.40; 4.1.1.101"
                     /label=maeB
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> encodes two ""malic enzymes"" that 
                     catalyze the decarboxylation of malate to form pyruvate 
                     with concomitant reduction of NAD(P)<sup>+</sup>. One 
                     enzyme, MaeB (described here) reduces NADP<sup>+</sup>, 
                     while the other, |FRAME: MALIC-NAD-MONOMER MaeA|, reduces 
                     NAD<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [4390688]|.
                     MaeB activity is highly regulated by key metabolites |CITS:
                     [17557829]|. Inhibition by acetyl-CoA may direct carbon 
                     distribution at the PEP-pyruvate-oxaloacetate metabolic 
                     node |CITS: [32485238]|. MaeB appears to be important for 
                     supplying NADPH during growth on two-carbon compounds 
                     |CITS: [22068497]|; flux-balance analysis predicts flux to 
                     pyruvate through MaeB during growth on acetate |CITS: 
                     [29677222]|. Metabolic flux through the malic enzymes 
                     during growth on glycerol as the sole source of carbon 
                     appears to be essentially zero |CITS: [29107627]|.
                     A malolactic enzyme activity - decarboxylation of malate to
                     lactate without concomitant reduction of NAD(P)<sup>+</sup>
                     - was recently discovered for MaeB; this activity may 
                     contribute to maintaining the NADPH/NADH balance of the 
                     cell |CITS: [33824210]|.
                     Purification of the enzyme from <i>E. coli</i> K10 and K-12
                     indicated that it was composed of 8 subunits |CITS: 
                     [SpinaBiochem193794][17557829]|. The C-terminal domain of 
                     MaeB is similar to phosphotransacetylases; it is not 
                     required for catalytic activity, but appears to be required
                     for metabolic regulation and oligomerization |CITS: 
                     [17557829]|.
                     A study using a thiol-based redox proteomic approach showed
                     that the Cys-642 residue of MaeB is modified by 
                     peroxynitrite, an antibacterial compound. Reversible 
                     modification of specific thiols in AsnB, FrmA, MaeB, and 
                     RidA may be involved in adaptation to nitrosative stress 
                     |CITS: [23696645]|. Using a method that distinguishes 
                     N-phosphorylation from O-phosphorylation, N-phosphorylation
                     was detected <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [33277485]|.
                     Deletion of <i>maeB</i> abolishes 
                     NADP<sup>+</sup>-dependent malic enzyme activity |CITS: 
                     [11092847]|. A <i>maeB</i> mutant does not show a defect 
                     when grown on acetate; however, a <i>maeB maeA</i> double 
                     mutant in a <i>pckA</i> mutant background abolishes growth 
                     on acetate |CITS: [11815613]|. A <i>maeB maeA</i> double 
                     mutant alone shows a severe lag phase during the transition
                     from growth on glucose to growth on acetate |CITS: 
                     [16141204]|. A &Delta;<i>maeB</i> mutant is more sensitive 
                     to peroxynitrite stress than wild type |CITS: [23696645]|.
                     Expression of <i>maeB</i> is induced by growth on acetate 
                     |CITS: [11815613]| and is upregulated in a <i>pykF</i> 
                     (pyruvate kinase) null mutant |CITS: [15158258]|. 
                     Overexpression of MaeB increases the production of C4 
                     metabolites under anaerobic conditions with supplemental 
                     bicarbonate |CITS: [18045419]|. <i>maeB</i> is among a 
                     number of metabolic genes whose expression was 
                     downregulated in two MG1655 lysogens carrying closely 
                     related Stx2a phages O104 and PA8 |CITS: [31208335]|. 
                     Overexpression or deletion of <i>maeB</i> has been utilized
                     in metabolic engineering for production of several 
                     compounds, e.g. by |CITS: [18062242][21097588][270 
                     88218][27342774][29663332][29800611][33668723]|."
