     gene            2572995..2574998
                     /gene="tktB"
                     /locus_tag="b2465"
                     /label=tktB
     CDS             2572995..2574998
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tktB"
                     /locus_tag="b2465"
                     /product="bifunctional transketolase 2 / transketolase"
                     /EC_number="2.2.1.1"
                     /label=tktB
                     /note="Transketolase catalyzes the reversible transfer of
                     a ketol group between several donor and acceptor 
                     substrates.  This key enzyme is a reversible link between 
                     glycolysis and the pentose phosphate pathway.  The enzyme 
                     is involved in the catabolism of pentose sugars, the 
                     formation of D-ribose 5-phosphate, and the provision of 
                     D-erythrose 4-phosphate, a precursor of aromatic amino 
                     acids and PLP |CITS: [4597996][7607225]|.  <i>E. coli</i> 
                     contains two transketolase isozymes, TktA and TktB.  TktB 
                     is responsible for the minor transketolase activity |CITS: 
                     [8396116]|.
                     The subunit structure of transketolase 2 (TktB) has not 
                     been explicitly determined.  However, transketolase 1 
                     (TktA) is homodimeric |CITS: [7607225]|.
                     Overproduction of TktB suppresses the <i>tktA</i> mutant 
                     phenotype |CITS: [8396116]|.  A <i>tktA tktB</i> double 
                     mutant requires pyridoxine (or 4-hydroxy-L-threonine or 
                     glycolaldehyde), aromatic amino acids and vitamins for 
                     growth |CITS: [7928977]|.  Mutations that suppress the 
                     growth defect of a <i>tktA tktB</i> double mutant in LB 
                     were found to constitutively express <i>glpK</i> or 
                     inactivate <i>deoB</i>, suggesting that disrupted 
                     ribose-5-P metabolism results in glycerol-3-P limitation 
                     |CITS: [26691989]|.
                     Expression of <i>tktB</i> is increased in a <i>tyrR</i> 
                     mutant in the presence of phenylalanine |CITS: [15639085]|.
                      <i>tktB</i> expression is increased in stationary phase 
                     and positively regulated by RpoS |CITS: 
                     [15968503][18175070]| and ppGpp |CITS: [18532980]|. 
                     Expression of <i>tktA</i> and <i>tktB</i> is complementary,
                     resulting in approximately constant levels of transketolase
                     expression throughout growth |CITS: [18175070]|.  A 
                     <i>tktB</i> mutant has lower acetate yield, but increased 
                     biomass yield under aerobic growth conditions |CITS: 
                     [18213409]|.  Levels of TktB protein increase during 
                     osmotic stress under aerobic, but not anaerobic growth 
                     conditions |CITS: [17015655]|.
                     TktB appears to be associated with the degradosome |CITS: 
                     [16139413]| and may connect carbon metabolism to 
                     replication |CITS: [21453533]|.  Filament formation of a 
                     <i>dnaE486</i> mutant is partially suppressed by deletion 
                     of <i>tktB</i> |CITS: [22565187]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [8572885]|
                     "
