     gene            2580954..2584067
                     /gene="acrD"
                     /locus_tag="b2470"
                     /label=acrD
     CDS             2580954..2584067
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="acrD"
                     /locus_tag="b2470"
                     /product="bifunctional multidrug efflux pump RND permease
                     AcrD / multidrug efflux pump AcrAD-TolC"
                     /label=acrD
                     /note="AcrD is a member of the
                     resistance-nodulation-division (RND) superfamily |CITS: 
                     [24225317]| and a component of the AcrAD-TolC efflux pump 
                     complex in <i>E. coli</i> K-12.
                     AcrD is homologous to the major efflux pump permease AcrB 
                     |CITS: [7889326][11566977]|. Disruption of <i>acrD</i> in 
                     the K-12 strain JC7623 increases susceptibility to a range 
                     of aminoglycoside antibiotics (amikacin, gentamicin, 
                     tobramycin, kanamycin and neomycin) and cells accumulate 
                     higher levels of labeled dihydrostreptomycin and gentamicin
                     than the parent strain; disruption of <i>acrD</i> in the 
                     K-12 strain JC7623 slightly increases susceptibility to 
                     erythromycin and puromycin but does not affect 
                     susceptibility to a range of other compounds including 
                     crystal violet, novobiocin, rifampin, chloramphenicol, 
                     carbenicillin, cefoxitin, nalidixic acid, norfloxacin and 
                     ampicillin |CITS: [10692383]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [11257026]|).
                     Expression of cloned <i>acrD</i> in the K-12 strain KAM3 
                     which lacks AcrB (see |CITS: [12618449]|) confers increased
                     resistance to deoxycholate, SDS, novobiocin and kanamycin 
                     (compared to the mutant parent); overexpression of 
                     <i>acrD</i> in the KAM3 strain confers a slightly increased
                     resistance to tetracycline, nalidixic acid, norfloxacin and
                     fosfomycin (compared to the mutant parent) |CITS: 
                     [11566977]| (see also |CITS: [12426336]|). Overproduction 
                     of AcrD in the K-12 strain KAM3 confers increased levels of
                     resistance to the antibiotics novobiocin, oxacillin, 
                     cloxacillin and nafcillin (compared to the mutant parent), 
                     restores wild-type levels of resistance to deoxycholate and
                     SDS and confers resistance above the wild-type level to 
                     carbenicillin, sulbenicillin and aztreonam; AcrD requires 
                     TolC for &beta;-lactam export |CITS: [12937021]|. AcrD 
                     requires AcrA for efflux of novobiocin and bile acids; AcrD
                     interacts physically with AcrA |CITS: [12426336]|. The two 
                     large periplasmic loops determine the substrate specificity
                     of the AcrD and AcrB transporters |CITS: [12426336]|; the 
                     difference in &beta;-lactam selectivity between AcrB and 
                     AcrD |CITS: [12937021]| resides in the so-called 'proximal'
                     binding pocket |CITS: [24558035]|.
                     AcrD can likely capture aminoglycosides from both the 
                     cytoplasm and the periplasm. AcrD proteoliposomes with an 
                     interior-acid &Delta;pH and entrapped AcrA, mediate efflux 
                     of H<sup>+</sup> when aminoglycosides (gentamicin, 
                     amikacin, tobramycin or kanamycin) are present in the 
                     reaction buffer; AcrD proteoliposomes with entrapped AcrA 
                     accumulate labeled gentamicin; AcrD proteoliposomes with an
                     interior-acid &Delta;pH, entrapped AcrA and entrapped 
                     aminoglycosides (gentamycin or amikacin) show enhanced 
                     proton efflux compared to AcrD proteoliposomes containing 
                     only AcrA |CITS: [15743938]| (and see |CITS: [15743933]|). 
                     Cryo-EM structures of trimeric and dimeric AcrD, and 
                     AcrD-gentamicin complexes have been reported and three 
                     possible drug extrusion pathways have been identified 
                     |CITS: [36625574]|
                     Typical substrates of the AcrB and AcrD systems are 
                     hydrophilic and hydrophobic compounds respectively while 
                     amphiphilic compounds are transported by both; putative 
                     substrate binding sites of AcrB and AcrD have been compared
                     with a view to explaining substrate specificity |CITS: 
                     [28808284]|.
                     <i>acrD</i> is one of 35 efflux-encoding genes that have 
                     been deleted or inactivated in 'Efflux KnockOut-35' 
                     (EKO-35) -  a K-12 BW25133-derived strain that lacks 35 
                     efflux pumps and can be used as a platform to study their 
                     function |CITS: [36065018][36318669]|. Swimming motility is
                     increased in the <i>acrD</i> Keio collection mutant 
                     (BW25113 &Delta;<i>acrD::kan</i>) |CITS: [39529380]|.
                     By analogy with the AcrB RND permease, AcrD is shown as a 
                     homotrimer in the complex.
                     Comments: |CITS: [15743933]|"
