     gene            complement(2587203..2589218)
                     /gene="tmcA"
                     /locus_tag="b2474"
                     /label=tmcA
     CDS             complement(2587203..2589218)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tmcA"
                     /locus_tag="b2474"
                     /product="tRNA(Met) cytidine acetyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.193"
                     /label=tmcA
                     /note="tRNA<sup>Met</sup> cytidine acetyltransferase
                     acetylates the wobble base C34 of the |FRAME: 
                     Elongation-tRNAMet|. TmcA specifically recognizes the 
                     anticodon stem of tRNA<sup>Met</sup>, thus distinguishing 
                     between tRNA<sup>Met</sup> and tRNA<sup>Ile2</sup>, which 
                     is structurally similar and has the same anticodon loop 
                     |CITS: [18668122][19322199]|.
                     TmcA has also been shown to be a protein-lysine 
                     2-hydroxyisobutyryltransferase but not participate in 
                     lysine acetylation. 467 potential protein modification 
                     sites were identified, including the K121 residue of H-NS 
                     |CITS: [34903851]|.
                     A crystal structure of the enzyme complexed with acetyl-CoA
                     and ADP has been solved at 2.35 &Aring; resolution. A 
                     modified DEAD-box RNA helicase module is formed by the 
                     peripheral N-terminal and the DUF699 PFAM domains. The 
                     authors propose that the RNA helicase motor delivers the 
                     wobble base substrate of the tRNA to the enzyme active site
                     |CITS: [19322199]|.
                     The presence of acetyl-CoA is essential for the formation 
                     of a stable complex between TmcA and tRNA<sup>Met</sup> and
                     also activates the intrinsic ATPase activity of TmcA. The 
                     substrate tRNA may be transiently rearranged during ATP 
                     hydrolysis. Residues essential for either the ATPase or 
                     tRNA binding activities were identified by site-directed 
                     mutagenesis |CITS: [19322199]|.
                     <i>In vitro</i>, it was observed that the 
                     N<sup>4</sup>-acetylcytidine (ac<sup>4</sup>C) modification
                     at wobble position of tRNA<sup>Met</sup> is important for 
                     preventing misreading of the AUA codon |CITS: [355249]|. 
                     RNA isolated from a <i>tmcA</i> deletion strain lacks the 
                     ac<sup>4</sup>C modification; however, the strain does not 
                     show a growth defect. Thus, this modification does not 
                     appear to be required for faithful translation in an 
                     otherwise wild-type strain. A <i>tmcA</i> |FRAME: EG12022 
                     <i>dusC</i>| double deletion strain has a severe 
                     cold-sensitive growth defect |CITS: [18668122]|.
                     Overexpression of TmcA increases survival under acid stress
                     conditions |CITS: [34903851]|.
                     TmcA: ""<b>t</b>RNA<sup><b>M</b>et</sup> <b>c</b>ytidine 
                     <b>a</b>cetyltransferase"" |CITS: [18668122]|"
