     gene            complement(2591190..2592224)
                     /gene="bamC"
                     /locus_tag="b2477"
                     /label=bamC
     CDS             complement(2591190..2592224)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="bamC"
                     /locus_tag="b2477"
                     /product="bifunctional outer membrane protein assembly
                     factor BamC / outer membrane protein assembly complex"
                     /label=bamC
                     /note="The BamC (formerly NlpB) lipoprotein is part of the
                     large multi-protein BAM complex responsible for the 
                     assembly and insertion of outer membrane &beta;-barrel 
                     proteins in <i>E.coli</i>.
                     BamC is a non-essential outer membrane lipoprotein 
                     |CITS:[1885529]|. BamC is exposed on the cell surface 
                     |CITS: [22683355]|. BamC forms a complex with BamA, BamB 
                     and BamD |CITS: [15851030], [16079137]|. BamC interacts 
                     directly with BamD <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [16824102]|. Lack
                     of BamC results in slight defects in outer membrane 
                     permeability; no clear decrease in levels of OmpA or LamB 
                     are evident |CITS: [15851030]|. <I>bamC</I> deletion 
                     results in sensitivity to rifampin. A <I>bamC surA</I> 
                     double mutant exhibits a synthetic phenotype. BamC may form
                     part of the DegP/Skp folding pathway for outer membrane 
                     proteins |CITS:[15968066]|. Levels of BamC decrease when 
                     resistance to ampicillin and tetracycline is acquired by 
                     laboratory cultures of <i>E. coli</i> |CITS:[16372265]|.
                     BamC consists of an N-terminal unstructured region of 
                     approximately 70 residues followed by two structurally 
                     related helix-grip domains connected by an &alpha;-helical 
                     linker. BamC has been crystallized after cleavage into two 
                     domains |CITS: [21139201], [21586578]|. The N-terminal 
                     unstructured region of BamC interacts extensively with BamD
                     through hydrogen bonds, salt bridges and van der Waals 
                     forces |CITS: [21937441], [22683355]|. The helix-grip 
                     domains of BamC are surface exposed |CITS: [22683355]|
                     <i>bamC</i> is part of the |FRAME: RPOE-MONOMER ""Sigma 
                     E""| regulon |CITS: [11274153]|."
