     gene            complement(2592241..2593119)
                     /gene="dapA"
                     /locus_tag="b2478"
                     /label=dapA
     CDS             complement(2592241..2593119)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dapA"
                     /locus_tag="b2478"
                     /product="4-hydroxy-tetrahydrodipicolinate synthase"
                     /EC_number="4.3.3.7"
                     /label=dapA
                     /note="4-hydroxy-tetrahydrodipicolinate synthase,
                     historically called dihydrodipicolinate synthase (DHDPS, 
                     DapA) is the first enzyme unique to lysine biosynthesis, 
                     catalyzing the condensation of pyruvate and 
                     (<i>S</i>)-aspartate &beta;-semialdehyde. This is thought 
                     to be the rate-limiting step in lysine biosynthesis after 
                     aspartate kinase III |CITS: [1463470]|. The product of the 
                     reaction catalyzed by DapA was identified as (4<i>S</i>)-4 
                     -hydroxy-2,3,4,5-tetrahydro-(2<i>S</i>)-dipicolinate (HTPA)
                     |CITS: [8993314]|. The reaction proceeds via a ping-pong 
                     bi-bi mechanism; pyruvate initially binds to the enzyme via
                     a Schiff base to the &epsilon;-amino group of the active 
                     site Lys161 residue |CITS: [1463470]|. This is followed by 
                     addition of L-aspartate semialdehyde and transimination 
                     leading to cyclization and dissociation of HTPA |CITS: 
                     [8993314]|. The kinetic mechanism was refined using initial
                     velocity and dead-end inhibition studies at both high and 
                     low pH, confirming the ping-pong reaction mechanism of the 
                     enzyme |CITS: [9048556]|. Surprisingly, Lys161 is not 
                     absolutely essential for catalysis |CITS: [20353808]|.
                     Crystal structures of the apo-enzyme and in complexes with 
                     substrates, substrate analogs and inhibitors, as well as of
                     mutant enzymes have been solved |CITS: [7853400][899331 
                     4][15066435][16041077][16185069][18556019][18787203][189374
                     97][19052357][19505526][20353808][22552955][35775984]|. 
                     DapA is a homotetramer that can be characterized as a dimer
                     of dimers; each monomer consists of an N-terminal 
                     (&beta;/&alpha;)<sub>8</sub>-barrel domain and three 
                     C-terminal &alpha;-helices. Site-directed mutants in the 
                     catalytic triad residues Tyr133, Thr44 and Tyr107 provided 
                     evidence for their functional importance |CITS: 
                     [15066435][19505526]|. Arg138 plays a role in substrate 
                     binding |CITS: [16185069]|, and Ile203 may play a role in 
                     catalysis |CITS: [18787203]|. Although the active site of a
                     dimeric variant of DapA is not disturbed, it shows reduced 
                     activity |CITS: [18556019]|. A Y107W mutant exists as a 
                     mixture of monomers and tetramers in solution and has 
                     reduced catalytic activity |CITS: [18937497]|, and a 
                     L197D/Y107W double mutant is monomeric, has reduced 
                     catalytic efficiency and is not inhibited by lysine |CITS: 
                     [20709017]|. Molecular dynamics simulations indicate that 
                     the tetrameric form is relatively rigid, while the dimeric 
                     form is more flexible and shows disorder in the active site
                     |CITS: [22685390]|. The C-terminal domain is required for 
                     maintenance of quarternary structure of the enzyme and thus
                     for catalysis |CITS: [19338756]|. The chaperone GroE 
                     appears to be required for folding of DapA |CITS: 
                     [9515958]|.
                     Inhibition of enzyme activity by lysine was investigated by
                     site-directed mutagenesis of predicted lysine binding 
                     residues |CITS: [22644522]|.
                     The pyruvate analog |FRAME: CPD-26607| is a competitive 
                     inhibitor and was found to inactivate the enzyme in a 
                     pseudo-first order process in a study to examine the DAP 
                     pathway as a potential target for the development of new 
                     antibiotics |CITS: [35775984]|.
                     Transcription of <i>dapA</i> increases in response to 
                     diaminopimelic acid limitation |CITS: [15158272]|.
                     DapA: ""<b>d</b>i<b>a</b>mino<b>p</b>imelate biosynthesis 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [4926684]|"
