     gene            2609453..2610916
                     /gene="bepA"
                     /locus_tag="b2494"
                     /label=bepA
     CDS             2609453..2610916
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="bepA"
                     /locus_tag="b2494"
                     /product="beta-barrel assembly-enhancing protease"
                     /EC_number="3.4.21.107"
                     /label=bepA
                     /note="BepA (formerly YfgC) is a periplasmic protein and a 
                     member of the M48 metalloprotease family |CITS: 
                     [22491786]|; BepA functions as a protease/chaperone with a 
                     role in maintaining outer membrane integrity |CITS: 
                     [24003122]|.
                     Purified BepA has low proteolytic activity <i>in vitro</i> 
                     which is absent in the presence of metal chelating 
                     reagents. BepA promotes the formation of correct disulfide 
                     bonds in |FRAME: EG11569-MONOMER ""LptD""|. This activity 
                     is independent of its protease activity. The protease 
                     function of BepA serves to degrade LptD that fails to form 
                     an outer membrane translocon. BepA also cleaves BamA in a 
                     <i>&Delta;surA</i> background |CITS: [24003122]|. BepA 
                     degrades mutant LptD (LptD4213) that is stalled at later 
                     stages of the Bam-mediated assembly process; the 
                     periplasmic proteases BepA, |FRAME: G6470-MONOMER ""YcaL""|
                     and |FRAME: CPLX0-2921 ""DegP""| degrade stalled LptD 
                     substrate at different stages in the OMP assembly process 
                     |CITS: [28784813]|. The &beta;2 edge-strand of BepA 
                     interacts directly with LptD and this interaction is 
                     important for both the proteolytic and chaperone-like 
                     activity of BepA; a ternary complex between BepA, LptD and 
                     the BAM complex has been demonstrated experimentally |CITS:
                     [34463613]|.
                     Upon cell fractionation the majority of BepA is located in 
                     the soluble fraction but a small amount is identified in 
                     the membrane fraction |CITS: [24003122]|. Mature BepA 
                     contains an N-terminal protease domain and a C-terminal 
                     tetratricopeptide repeat (TPR) domain; in the crystal 
                     structure reported by |CITS: [30521812]|, the active site 
                     of the protease domain binds one zinc atom coordinated by 
                     His-136, His-140, Glu-201, and His-246 |CITS: 
                     [30521812][28960545]|. BepA protease activity may be 
                     regulated by an autoinhibitory mechanism (or histidine 
                     switch) involving the His-246 zinc ligand; deletion of the 
                     loop region containing His-246 or a H246A mutation 
                     increases the protease activity of BepA <i>in vivo</i> and 
                     <i>in vitro</i>; immobilization of this region by disulfide
                     cross-linking hinders the protease activity of BepA 
                     suggesting that mobility of the loop containing His-246 is 
                     important for regulation |CITS: [33093205]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [33106348]|).
                     BepA interacts with the outer membrane metallopeptidase 
                     |FRAME: EG11291-MONOMER ""LoiP""| and LoiP is not detected 
                     in the outer membrane fraction of a <i>bepA</i> null mutant
                     |CITS: [22491786]|. Purified BepA does not digest LoiP 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [22491786]|.
                     <i>bepA</i> mutants show increased susceptibility to 
                     multiple antibiotics |CITS: [18621901], [19462005]|. Cells 
                     lacking BepA show an increase in hepta-acylated Lipid A in 
                     relation to hexa-acylated Lipid A; loss of BepA leads to an
                     increase in surface-exposed phospholipid and subsequent 
                     |FRAME: EG12180-MONOMER PagP|-mediated modification of 
                     Lipid A |CITS: [33106348]|.
                     <i>bepA</i> is a member of the |FRAME: RPOE-MONOMER ""Sigma
                     E""| regulon; <i>bepA</i> is induced in a 
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup> over-expressing strain compared to 
                     wild-type |CITS: [16336047]|.
                     <i>bepA</i>: <U>&beta;</U>-barrel assembly <U>e</U>nhancing
                     <U>p</U>rotease
                     Related review: |CITS: [39008906]|"
                     /translation="MFRQLKKNLVATLIAAMTIGQVAPAFADSADTLPDMGTSAGSTLS
