     gene            complement(2611434..2612135)
                     /gene="hda"
                     /locus_tag="b2496"
                     /label=hda
     CDS             complement(2611434..2612135)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hda"
                     /locus_tag="b2496"
                     /product="trifunctional inibitor of reinitiation of DNA
                     replication / Hda-beta-clamp complex / DnaA-ATP 
                     phosphohydrolase"
                     /label=hda
                     /note="Hda is a member of the AAA<sup>+</sup>
                     chaperone-like family of ATPases |CITS: [11483528]| that 
                     inhibits the reinitiation of DNA replication in concert 
                     with the |FRAME: CPLX0-3761| in a process termed RIDA 
                     (regulatory inactivation of DnaA) |CITS: 
                     [9674428][11483528]|.  Hda and the sliding clamp stimulate 
                     ATP hydrolysis within the |FRAME: MONOMER0-160 DnaA-ATP 
                     complex|, which renders it inactive for the initiation of 
                     DNA replication |CITS: [11483528]|. The RIDA mechanism was 
                     reported to be the most important factor that controls the 
                     replication initiation frequency, which maintains the 
                     chromosome-to-cell size ratio within a narrow range |CITS: 
                     [37220276]|.
                     Hda has been shown to interact with the &beta; subunit of 
                     DNA polymerase III <i>in vitro</i>; the interaction is 
                     mediated by a hexapeptide sequence located in the amino 
                     terminus of Hda |CITS: [15150238]|.  Apo-Hda is homodimeric
                     in solution |CITS: [15611053]|; however, the active protein
                     is monomeric and bound to ADP |CITS: [18977760]|.  Two 
                     molecules of Hda associate with a &beta;-clamp dimer |CITS:
                     [16603382]|.  The Arg168 residue within the AAA<sup>+</sup>
                     Box VII motif is involved in DnaA-ATP hydrolysis |CITS: 
                     [15611053]|.
                     The level of Hda within the cell correlates with expression
                     of <i>dnaA</i> |CITS: [16882985]|.
                     Hda also binds to and antagonizes the activity of TrfA, an 
                     episomal factor that regulates replication of the RK2 
                     plasmid |CITS: [12618445]|.
                     Hda is localized to membranes, predominantly to the inner 
                     membrane |CITS: [12618445]|.
                     <i>hda</i> was identified as a multicopy suppressor of the 
                     temperature-sensitive <i>dnaN36</i> allele |CITS: 
                     [11483528]|.  Hda was initially reported to be essential 
                     for viability |CITS: [11483528]|, although later studies 
                     determined that it is not |CITS: [12730188]|. 
                     &Delta;<i>hda</i> mutants have increased doubling time and 
                     increased DNA content |CITS: [15939703]| due to two-fold 
                     over-initiation of replication |CITS: [16041320]|.  A 
                     &Delta;<i>hda</i>::Tet<sup>R</sup> allele caused a strong 
                     growth defect in an otherwise wild type strain, but not in 
                     strains that contained the constitutively inactivated 
                     DnaA<sub>A345S</sub> or DnaA<sub>T174P</sub> mutants |CITS:
                     [16482221]|.  It was later shown that &Delta;<i>hda</i> 
                     mutants accumulate suppressor mutations that allow 
                     wild-type growth |CITS: [16882985]|.
                     A <i>ygfZ</i> null mutant supresses the growth defect of 
                     two <i>hda</i><sup>ts</sup> alleles |CITS: [16359333]|. 
                     Mutations in <i>diaA</i> or overexpression of 
                     ribonucleotide reductases suppress the defects of the 
                     cold-sensitive <i>hda</i>-185 mutant allele |CITS: 
                     [18502852]|, and mutations that reduce the frequency of 
                     replication initiation from <i>oriC</i> suppress the 
                     Hda<sup>-</sup> growth defect |CITS: [19007419]|. 
                     Overexpression of <i>hda</i> leads to increased cell 
                     filamentation |CITS: [16321957]|.
                     Dp: ""<b>D</b>naA <b>p</b>aralog"" |CITS: [12618445]|
                     Hda: ""<b>h</b>omologous to <b>D</b>na<b>A</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [11483528]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16753031][16670681][17459114][29357054]|"
