     gene            2615593..2616231
                     /gene="purN"
                     /locus_tag="b2500"
                     /label=purN
     CDS             2615593..2616231
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="purN"
                     /locus_tag="b2500"
                     /product="phosphoribosylglycinamide formyltransferase 1"
                     /EC_number="2.1.2.2"
                     /label=purN
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> contains two different 
                     phosphoribosylglycinamide (GAR) transformylases, both of 
                     which can catalyze the third step in <i>de novo</i> purine 
                     biosynthesis.  The transformylase encoded by <i>purN</i> 
                     utilizes 10-formyl-tetrahydrofolate as the formyl donor. 
                     The second transformylase encoded by <i>purT</i> utilizes 
                     formate, which is provided by PurU-catalyzed hydrolysis of 
                     10-formyl-tetrahydrofolate |CITS: [8226647]|.  The 
                     existence of these two transformylase enzymes was 
                     determined by mutant studies.  A strain containing a 
                     knockout insertion in <i>purN</i> did not result in purine 
                     auxotrophy |CITS: [3301838]|.  Only mutants defective in 
                     both <i>purN</i> and <i>purT</i> required exogenously added
                     purine for growth |CITS: [8501063]|.  There is no 
                     significant homology between the two transformylases |CITS:
                     [8117714]|.
                     In early work using <i>E. coli</i> B, 
                     10-formyltetrahydrofolate was identified as the formyl 
                     donor for this enzyme |CITS: [350869]|.  Later work using 
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 showed that the enzyme is 
                     monofunctional and catalyzes a reaction involving the 
                     transfer of a formyl group from 10-formyl-tetrahydrofolate 
                     to the amino nitrogen of 5-phospho-ribosyl-glycineamide 
                     (GAR), producing 
                     5'-phosphoribosyl-<i>N</i>-formylglycineamide (FGAR) and 
                     tetrahydrofolate |CITS: [350869][2656702][2185839]|.  The 
                     PurN catalyzed reaction is one of two reactions in the 
                     <i>de novo</i> purine biosynthesis pathway that require 
                     reduced folate cofactors.  The other is the |FRAME: 
                     AICARTRANSFORM-ENZRXN| activity of the bifunctional product
                     of <i>purH</i>.
                     A random sequential kinetic mechanism for PurN was proposed
                     |CITS: [9628739]|.  Asp144 was identified as a functional 
                     active site residue |CITS: [2204419]| and catalytically 
                     important residues Asn106, His108, Ser135 and Asp144, were 
                     also identified by site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [8688421][10433709]|.  The crystal structure of the enzyme 
                     has been solved |CITS: 
                     [2656702][1631098][1522592][9698564][10606510]|.  Although 
                     <i>E. coli</i> PurN was shown to be a monomer in solution 
                     |CITS: [2185839]|, it was a dimer in the crystal structure 
                     |CITS: [1522592]|.  The important role of <i>E. coli</i> 
                     PurN in purine <i>de novo</i> biosynthesis has made it a 
                     model for inhibitor development |CITS: 
                     [1631098][1522592][10606510]|.  The human enzyme is a 
                     target for the development of antineoplastic drugs |CITS: 
                     [18686942]|.
                     A novel, engineered PurN heterodimer has been produced with
                     activity and kinetics similar to wild-type |CITS: 
                     [11352469]|. A novel, engineered hybrid PurN/PurU enzyme 
                     was also constructed and its phosphoribosylglycinamide 
                     formyltransferase (GAR transformylase) efficiency was 
                     improved by combinatorial mutagenesis |CITS: [10835105]|. 
                     The subcellular location of <i>E. coli</i> PurN was studied
                     using GFP-labeled protein.  It was shown to be diffused 
                     throughout the cytoplasm, thus eliminating the possibility 
                     of complex formation with other pathway enzymes mediated by
                     cytoskeletal or membrane elements |CITS: [11381136]|.
                     Review: Jensen, K.F., G. Dandanell, B. Hove-Jensen, and M. 
                     Willemoes (2008) ""Nucleotides, Nucleosides and 
                     Nucleobases"" EcoSal 3.6.2 |CITS: [ecosal]|"
