     gene            2618474..2620015
                     /gene="ppx"
                     /locus_tag="b2502"
                     /label=ppx
     CDS             2618474..2620015
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ppx"
                     /locus_tag="b2502"
                     /product="exopolyphosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.1.11"
                     /label=ppx
                     /note="Exopolyphosphatase (PPX) degrades inorganic 
                     polyphosphates (polyP).  It has a strong preference for 
                     long-chain polyphosphates; activity decreases with smaller 
                     size polyP of about 15 residues.  PPX acts on the ends of 
                     the polyP chain, removing orthophosphate processively 
                     |CITS: [8380170]|.  Steady-state levels of polyP are 
                     dependent on the level of PPX in the cell |CITS: 
                     [9143103]|.
                     pppGpp and ppGpp strongly inhibit PPX activity while also 
                     acting as a substrate; however, GppA is the major source of
                     pppGppase activity in the cell |CITS: [9261133]|.  PPX 
                     activity is redox-regulated; the enzyme is highly sensitive
                     to inactivation by oxidation.  Thus, oxidative stress 
                     conditions lead to accumulation of poly(P), which acts as a
                     global chaperone |CITS: [24560923]|.
                     The N terminus of PPX contains the active site and belongs 
                     to the ASKHA (acetate and sugar kinases, Hsp70, actin) 
                     phosphotranferase superfamily, while the C terminus binds 
                     polyphosphate.  The halves can be expressed separately and 
                     combined to form a functional enzyme |CITS: [10922361]|. 
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme have been solved at 1.9 
                     &Aring; |CITS: [16905100]| and 2.2 &Aring; |CITS: 
                     [16678853]| (using <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7, with 100% 
                     identical sequence) resolution.  The enzyme consists of 
                     four domains, with the active site located at the interface
                     between domains I and II.  The location of the active site 
                     was confirmed by deletions and site-directed mutagenesis 
                     |CITS: [16905100]|.  The active site region opens to a 
                     ""canyon"" that extends along the dimer interface, which 
                     may represent the polyP binding site |CITS: 
                     [16678853][16905100]|.
                     A strain overexpressing <i>ppx</i> together with <i>ppk</i>
                     has decreased levels of intracellular polyphosphate and 
                     increased tolerance to heavy metals such as cadmium 
                     compared to a strain that only expresses <i>ppk</i> |CITS: 
                     [8593078][10191394]|.  Both <i>ppx</i> and <i>ppk ppx</i> 
                     mutants are deficient in biofilm formation |CITS: 
                     [23226268]|.  A &Delta;<i>ppx</i> mutant accumulates polyP 
                     that is dispersed throughout the cell; under wild type 
                     conditions, polyP forms foci primarily at the cell poles 
                     |CITS: [18593213]|.
                     Expression of Ppx is induced by growth under low phosphate 
                     conditions |CITS: [166880][7832534]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [7836277][10448677][10872445][10739472][10 
                     739473][10739474][22994490][22337166][24606917]|
                     Note that |CITS: [23993101]| has been retracted due to 
                     phage contamination |CITS: [29474913]|.
                     "
