     gene            complement(2629243..2630715)
                     /gene="der"
                     /locus_tag="b2511"
                     /label=der
     CDS             complement(2629243..2630715)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="der"
                     /locus_tag="b2511"
                     /product="bifunctional 50S ribosomal subunit stability
                     factor / GTPase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.5.3"
                     /label=der
                     /note="The Der protein is a GTPase that is ubiquitously 
                     conserved in eubacteria |CITS: [16963571]|.  It is 
                     associated with the large ribosomal subunit and is required
                     for its stability |CITS: [16930151][16963571]|. In <i>E. 
                     coli</i>, Der is essential for growth |CITS: 
                     [11387344][16738554][16930151][16963571]|.
                     GTP hydrolysis appears to regulate the specificity of 
                     interactions of Der with ribosomal subunits |CITS: 
                     [19246542]|.  The GAP-like protein |FRAME: CPLX0-7853 
                     ""YihI""| interacts with Der and activates its GTPase 
                     activity |CITS: [20434458]|.  The GTPase activity of Der is
                     also stimulated by the 50S ribosomal subunit |CITS: 
                     [25389271]|.  The KH-like C-terminal domain of Der plays a 
                     role in recognition of the ribosome |CITS: [20172997]|. Der
                     also interacts with |FRAME: G6362-MONOMER YbeY| |CITS: 
                     [27834201]| and binds (p)ppGpp |CITS: [29511080]|.
                     The Der protein consists of two GTPase domains (GD1 and 
                     GD2) followed by a KH-like putative RNA-binding domain.  A 
                     cryo-EM structure of Der bound to the 50S ribosomal subunit
                     has been solved, showing that Der interacts with the 
                     peptidyl transferase center and induces significant 
                     structural changes |CITS: [25389271]|.
                     Mutants containing substitutions in either of the two 
                     predicted GTP binding domains of Der are not able to 
                     complement the lethal phenotype of the <i>der</i> null 
                     mutant at low temperature and are defective in ribosome 
                     biogenesis |CITS: [16930151][16963571]|.  Depletion of Der 
                     results in the accumulation of 23S and 16S rRNA precursors,
                     accumulation of aberrant 50S ribosomal subunits at low 
                     concentrations of Mg<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [16930151]| and 
                     cold sensitivity |CITS: [24822275]|. Overexpression of RelA
                     suppresses the growth defect of a <i>der</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [18296517]|.
                     In a synthetic lethality screen, two mutations that alter 
                     highly conserved residues in Der, T57I and E271K, lead to 
                     dependence on the presence of |FRAME: EG10870-MONOMER L9| 
                     for survival.  Both mutations are recessive and impair the 
                     GTPase activity of Der.  The N-terminal domain of L9 is 
                     both necessary and sufficient for complementation of the 
                     <i>derT57I</i> mutant allele.  Moderate overexpression of 
                     <i>yihI</i> can partially complement the colony and liquid 
                     culture growth defects of the <i>derT57I</i> mutant, but 
                     does not restore the GTPase activity of Der |CITS: 
                     [23772068]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>der</i> suppresses the growth and 
                     ribosome-related defects of a mutant lacking the RrmJ rRNA 
                     methyltransferase |CITS: [11976298]|.  Both the GTP binding
                     domains and the C-terminal KH-like domain of Der are 
                     required for suppression |CITS: [20172997]|.
                     Der homologs from <i>Klebsiella pneumoniae</i> and 
                     <i>Salmonella enterica</i> serovar Typhimurium can 
                     complement an <i>E. coli der</i> mutant |CITS: [27297882]|.
                     A high-throughput screen for inhibitors of Der has been 
                     performed |CITS: [23606650]|.
                     The Der protein appears to be evenly distributed throughout
                     the cytoplasm |CITS: [17272300]|, but was also reported to 
                     associate with membrane proteins |CITS: [21354313]|.
                     Der: ""<b>d</b>ouble-<b>Er</b>a-like domains"" |CITS: 
                     [11387344]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11200227][11489118][12732302][21529161][2 
                     1675921][21885683]|"
