     gene            complement(2630833..2632011)
                     /gene="bamB"
                     /locus_tag="b2512"
                     /label=bamB
     CDS             complement(2630833..2632011)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="bamB"
                     /locus_tag="b2512"
                     /product="bifunctional outer membrane protein assembly
                     factor BamB / outer membrane protein assembly complex"
                     /label=bamB
                     /note="The BamB (formerly YfgL) lipoprotein is part of the
                     large multi-protein BAM complex responsible for the 
                     assembly and insertion of outer membrane &beta;-barrel 
                     proteins in <i>E.coli</i>.
                     BamB is an outer membrane lipoprotein that is attached via 
                     its N-terminal palmitate lipid anchor to the periplasmic 
                     side of the outer membrane |CITS:[15126489], [15851030]|. 
                     BamB forms a complex with BamA, BamC and BamD |CITS: 
                     [15851030], [16079137]|. BamB interacts directly with BamA 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [16824102]|.
                     <i>bamB</i> is not essential; deletion of <i>bamB</i> 
                     results in increased outer membrane permeability and 
                     decreased levels of outer membrane proteins such as LamB 
                     and OmpA |CITS: [15851030], [15851036]|. Loss of function 
                     mutations in <i>bamB</i> (yfgL::IS1E insertions) suppress 
                     the antibiotic and bile sensitive phenotype of an |FRAME: 
                     EG11569-MONOMER ""LptD""| mutant (<i>imp4213</i>) which has
                     defects in OM permeability. <i>bamB::kan</i> and 
                     <i>imp4213</i> mutations show a conditional 
                     synthetic-lethality phenotype unless cell growth is slowed 
                     down |CITS: [15851036]|. <i>bamB</i> and <i>surA</i> 
                     mutants show similar defects in proLamB processing 
                     |CITS:[17071751]|.
                     Deletion of <I>bamB</I> results in sensitivity to rifampin,
                     SDS, and novobiocin, as well as increased 
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup> activity. Deletion of <i>bamB</i> 
                     results in decreased levels of the outer membrane proteins 
                     OmpF, OmpC, OmpA and LamB. <i>bamB</i> deletions produce 
                     synthetic phenotypes when the periplasmic chaperones DegP 
                     or FkpA are absent |CITS:[15968066]|. <I>bamB degP</I> 
                     double mutations are lethal at 37<sup>o</sup> due to the 
                     accumulation of misfolded OMPs compromising the assembly 
                     apparatus |CITS:[17015657]|. A differential effect on OMPs 
                     is observed in response to <i>bamB</i> mutation: TolC 
                     levels are significantly increased, OmpF, LamB and OmpA 
                     levels are reduced and OmpC levels remain stable. The major
                     OMPS may thus be in competition with each other for 
                     BamA-BamD mediated insertion into the outer membrane 
                     |CITS:[17015657]|.
                     Crystallized BamB adopts an eight bladed &beta;-propellar 
                     structure with a central pore. Each blade contains four 
                     &beta;-sheets connected by four loops. BamB is very 
                     electronegative particularly near the central pore. The 
                     overall structure of BamB resembles a flat cylinder with a 
                     shallow groove on one side (the top of the cylinder) and a 
                     deep opening on the opposite, bottom side. The palmitate 
                     lipid attachment is located at the top of the cylinder 
                     |CITS: [21168416], [21277859], [21236263], [22948914]|. 
                     BamB interacts with BamA POTRA regions 1-3 |CITS: 
                     [22948914]|.
                     Purified BamB is individually able to facilitate assembly 
                     of unfolded BamA into proteoliposomes |CITS: [23919461]|. 
                     BamB binds the antibacterial adjuvant, |FRAME: CPD-28148 
                     nitazoxanide|, with nanomolar-level affinity |CITS: 
                     [39984113]|."
