     gene            complement(2633954..2634045)
                     /gene="sroE"
                     /locus_tag="b4761"
                     /label=sroE
     misc_RNA        complement(2633954..2634045)
                     /gene="sroE"
                     /locus_tag="b4761"
                     /product="small RNA SroE"
                     /note="The SroE small RNA is processed from the 3' UTR of
                     the <i>ispG</i> mRNA and extends into the promoter region 
                     of the downstream <i>hisS</i> gene.  The half life of SroE 
                     is 32 minutes in stationary phase |CITS: [14602901]|.
                     The sequence of SroE is conserved between <i>E. coli</i> 
                     and <i>Salmonella</i> species |CITS: [14602901]|.
                     Likely functional regions of SroE were identified; testing 
                     by electrophoretic mobility shift assay of predicted mRNA 
                     targets confirmed a weak interaction with the <i>ligB</i> 
                     mRNA |CITS: [30287822]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [32213244]|"
     gene            complement(2634045..2635163)
                     /gene="ispG"
                     /locus_tag="b2515"
                     /label=ispG
     CDS             complement(2634045..2635163)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ispG"
                     /locus_tag="b2515"
                     /product="bifunctional
                     (E)-4-hydroxy-3-methylbut-2-enyl-diphosphate synthase 
                     (flavodoxin) / 1-hydroxy-2-methyl-2-(E)-butenyl 
                     4-diphosphate synthase"
                     /EC_number="1.17.7.3"
                     /label=ispG
                     /note="1-hydroxy-2-methyl-2-(<i>E</i>)-butenyl
                     4-diphosphate synthase (IspG) catalyzes the sixth step of 
                     the mevalonate-independent |FRAME: NONMEVIPP-PWY 
                     methylerythritol phosphate (MEP) pathway|, the conversion 
                     of 2C-methyl-D-erythritol 2,4-cyclodiphosphate into 
                     1-hydroxy-2-methyl-2-(<i>E</i>)-butenyl 4-diphosphate 
                     |CITS: [11163766][11274098][11752431]|.
                     IspG activity depends on additional proteins, most likely 
                     involved in the oxidation portion of the reaction |CITS: 
                     [12571359]|. IspG contains a [4Fe-4S] cluster that is 
                     required for its activity |CITS: 
                     [12434382][15650872][16268586]|. Mutations in cysteine 
                     residues responsible for Fe-S cluster coordination abolish 
                     IspG's enzymatic activity |CITS: [16268586]|. <i>In 
                     vitro</i>, |FRAME: CPLX0-7617 ErpA| can transfer a [4Fe-4S]
                     cluster to apo-IspG |CITS: [17698959]|. Increased IspG 
                     activity was observed under new assay conditions |CITS: 
                     [19821611]|. Flux through the entire MEP pathway was shown 
                     to depend on the presence of |FRAME: FLAVODOXIN1-MONOMER|, 
                     which may supply reducing equivalents necessary to reduce 
                     the Fe-S cluster and yield an active enzyme |CITS: 
                     [15978585]|.
                     A new reaction mechanism was proposed |CITS: [20534554]|. 
                     Further studies on the reaction mechanism and reaction 
                     intermediate have been published |CITS: 
                     [20820543][22238143][21574560]|, and potential reaction 
                     mechanisms are reviewed in |CITS: [24481599]|.
                     An <i>ispG</i> mutant does not activate human 
                     &gamma;&delta; T lymphocytes due to its defect in 
                     production of (<i>E</i>)-4-hydroxy-3-methyl-but-2-enyl 
                     pyrophosphate (HMB-PP). This intermediate in the MEP 
                     pathway accumulates in an <i>ispH</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [11238603][11741609]|.
                     <i>ispG</i> is essential |CITS: 
                     [1521767][11274098][12859972]|. Missense mutants which are 
                     defective in the MEP pathway |CITS: [12859972]|, as well as
                     a conditional amber mutant |CITS: [15090508]| have been 
                     isolated.
                     Since the enzymes of the MEP pathway are not found in 
                     humans, they have attracted interest for their potential as
                     anti-infective drug targets. A MEP pathway inhibitor 
                     (maculosin, a cyclic dipeptide from Bacillus subtilis) has 
                     been identified and found to bind IspG |CITS: [23832336]|. 
                     The lysis protein |FRAME: EG11303| has been shown to 
                     interact directly with IspG, both in vitro and in vivo, and
                     is a likely inhibitor of it's reaction in the MEP pathway, 
                     compromising cell wall biosynthesis that leads to cell 
                     lysis |CITS: [35163484]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [24481599]|"
