     gene            complement(2635190..2636203)
                     /gene="rodZ"
                     /locus_tag="b2516"
                     /label=rodZ
     CDS             complement(2635190..2636203)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rodZ"
                     /locus_tag="b2516"
                     /product="cytoskeleton protein RodZ"
                     /label=rodZ
                     /note="RodZ is a bitopic inner membrane protein that is
                     involved in the maintenance of cell shape through 
                     interaction with the |FRAME: EG10608-MONOMER ""MreB""| 
                     cytoskeleton |CITS: [19008860], [19078962], [19164570]|. 
                     RodZ links MreB to cell wall synthesis proteins (possibly 
                     |FRAME: EG10606-MONOMER ""MrdA""| and /or |FRAME: 
                     EG10607-MONOMER ""RodA""|); this interaction mediates MreB 
                     processive motion as the cell wall is synthesized |CITS: 
                     [26396257]|. Regulation of RodZ modulates MreB localization
                     patterns to drive changes in cell shape; RodZ binding is 
                     predicted to alter the biophysical properties of MreB 
                     filaments |CITS: [29599448]|.
                     RodZ colocalizes to the division site with MreB; RodZ 
                     localizes to the division site in a |FRAME:EG10347-MONOMER 
                     ""FtsZ""|-dependent way; RodZ physically interacts with 
                     FtsZ; RodZ is involved in formation of the Z ring during 
                     the early stage of cell division |CITS: [30742332]|. 
                     GFP-RodZ fusion proteins distribute in a spiral-like or 
                     banded manner along the length of bacterial cells |CITS: 
                     [19008860], [19078962], [19164570]|. RodZ and MreB 
                     fluorescent fusion proteins colocalise in a helical manner 
                     along the inner membrane |CITS: [19078962], [19164570]|. 
                     BACTH (bacterial two-hybrid) assays suggest that RodZ 
                     interacts with MreB and MreC <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [19078962]|. RodZ is required for the proper formation of 
                     MreB spirals |CITS: [19078962]|. RodZ and MreB colocalize 
                     and rotate together around the cell circumference |CITS: 
                     [26396257]|.
                     RodZ consists of an N-terminal domain including a 
                     helix-turn-helix motif (residues 1-84) and a juxta-membrane
                     region (residues 85-110) of highly basic amino acids, a 
                     trans-membrane domain (residues 111-113) and a large 
                     C-terminal region (134-337) |CITS: [19008860], [19078962], 
                     [19164570]|. The C-terminus of RodZ is located in the 
                     periplasm, and the N-terminus is in the cytoplasm 
                     |CITS:[10419954], [19008860]|. Deletion analyses suggest 
                     that the cytoplasmic domain of RodZ is critical for helical
                     organisation and maintenance of cell shape |CITS: 
                     [19008860], [19078962]|. Mutations in the helix-turn-helix 
                     region of RodZ (F60A and Y64A) impair the interaction of 
                     RodZ with MreB <i>in vivo</i>, RodZ localises evenly along 
                     the membrane and cells are unable to maintain rod-shape 
                     |CITS: [20168300]|. Deleting the periplasmic domain of RodZ
                     (residues 112-337) results in round-shaped cells and 
                     eliminates MreB motion; deleting a portion of the 
                     cytoplasmic domain of RodZ (residues 1-83) that includes 
                     the helix-turn-helix motif does not completely eliminate 
                     MreB motion; the juxta-membrane region of RodZ may also be 
                     involved in MreB binding |CITS: [26396257]|.
                     A <I>rodZ</I> deletion mutant grows more slowly than wild 
                     type and with a round or oval shape |CITS:[19008860]|. 
                     Deletion of <i>rodZ</i> results in defects in cell shape 
                     ranging from large and misshapen in log phase growth to 
                     spheroid in stationery phase |CITS: [19164570]|. 
                     <I>rodZ</I> deletion mutants have also been shown to be 
                     non-motile |CITS:[17122336],[18180259]|, to have reduced 
                     biofilm formation, and to have reduced amounts of OmpA, 
                     OmpC, OmpF, and LamB |CITS:[18180259]|.  Deletion of 
                     <I>rodZ</I> with |FRAME: EG10985 ""<I>surA</I>""|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11122 ""<I>yciB</I>""|, |FRAME: EG12691 ""<i>lapB</i>""| 
                     or |FRAME: G7681 ""<I>yhcB</I>""| results in a synthetic 
                     lethal phenotype |CITS:[18180259]|. In &Delta;<i>rodZ</i> 
                     cells, MreB motion is reduced and less processive (ie. less
                     directed) |CITS: [26396257]|.
                     A RodZ-alkaline phosphatase fusion requires the signal 
                     recognition particle (SRP) pathway for efficient insertion 
                     into the membrane |CITS:[10419954]|.
                     Due to the presence of the helix-turn-helix motif, RodZ was
                     initially predicted to be a transcriptional regulator 
                     |CITS:[11274098]|.  <I>rodZ</I> may be cotranscribed with 
                     <I>gcpE</I> which is involved in isoprenoid biosynthesis 
                     |CITS:[11274098]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [19194484][31915748]|
                     <i>rodZ</i> is essential when cells are grown on LB at 
                     30<sup>o</sup>C or below and moderate expression of 
                     <i>ftsZ</i> is sufficient to suppress the lethality. 
                     <BR>Hence <i>rodZ</i>: <U>r</U>equires an 
                     <U>o</U>ver<U>d</U>ose of <i>fts<U>Z</U></i> for 
                     propagation on LB at, or below, 30<sup>o</sup>C |CITS: 
                     [19078962]|<BR><i>rod</i>: determinant of <U>rod</U> shape 
                     |CITS: [19008860]|"
