     gene            complement(2638372..2640684)
                     /gene="pbpC"
                     /locus_tag="b2519"
                     /label=pbpC
     CDS             complement(2638372..2640684)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pbpC"
                     /locus_tag="b2519"
                     /product="bifunctional peptidoglycan glycosyltransferase
                     PbpC / peptidoglycan glycosyltransferase"
                     /label=pbpC
                     /note="<i>pbpC</i> encodes a HMW penicillin binding
                     protein (PBP) known as PBP1C. PBP1C (like |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7717 PBP1A| and |FRAME: CPLX0-3951 PBP1B|) contains 
                     characteristic transglycosylase and transpeptidase amino 
                     acid motifs but only transglycosylase activity has been 
                     assayed |CITS: [10542235]|. PBP1C does not bind most of the
                     &beta;-lactams known to bind other PBPs leading to 
                     speculation that PBP1C does not have transpeptidase 
                     activity (the transpeptidase region is also responsible for
                     penicillin binding) |CITS: [10542235]|.
                     PPB1C is predicted to contain a single transmembrane domain
                     which anchors the cell to the inner membrane with the 
                     remainder of the enzyme located in the periplasm |CITS: 
                     [10542235]|. PBP1C is responsible for 75% of 
                     transglycosylase activity in ether pemeabilized cells, but 
                     only less than 3% in crude membranes; PBP1C-specific murein
                     synthesizing activity is sensitive to moenomycin |CITS: 
                     [10542235]|. PBP1C binds <sup>35</sup>S-labeled penicillin;
                     maximum binding occurs at pH 5 |CITS: [3090016]|. PBP1C 
                     binds moxalactam poorly |CITS: [3918982]|. PBP1C binds 
                     furazlocillin |CITS: [6450748]|. PBP1C binds a 
                     <sup>125</sup>I labelled derivative of ampicillin |CITS: 
                     [SCHWARZ81]|.
                     Cells lacking PBP1C do not exhibit defects in growth rate, 
                     yield or sensitivity to a range of antibiotics; the 
                     muropeptide composition of these cells is unaffected except
                     for an increase in the amount of tetra-penta disaccharides 
                     |CITS: [10542235]|. Overproduction of the PBP1C protein 
                     does not rescue the defect of an <i>mrcA</i><sup>ts</sup> 
                     <i>mrcB</i> double mutant |CITS: [10542235]|.
                     Purified PBP1C interacts with PBP1B, |FRAME: 
                     EG10341-MONOMER PBP3|, |FRAME: EG10202-MONOMER PBP4|, 
                     |FRAME: EG10606-MONOMER PBP2| (through an unknown 
                     periplasmic factor), and |FRAME: G7457-MONOMER MltA| |CITS:
                     [10542235][10037771]|. PBP1C interacts with |FRAME: 
                     EG10950-MONOMER Slt70| and |FRAME: G7410-MONOMER MltB| 
                     |CITS: [9158739]|.
                     There is no apparent fluctuation in PBP1C level (as 
                     measured via the binding of labelled ampicillin) during 
                     different points of the cell cycle |CITS: [6337993]|. 
                     <i>pbpC</i> is often found linked to |FRAME: G7323-MONOMER 
                     <i>yfhM</i>| homologs (encoding &alpha;2-macroglobulins) 
                     leading to the suggestion that the two enzymes may act 
                     together as a defense and repair mechanism to protect the 
                     cell from host defenses during colonization |CITS: 
                     [15186489]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16339737][9841666][9529891]|"
