     gene            complement(2640685..2645646)
                     /gene="yfhM"
                     /locus_tag="b2520"
                     /label=yfhM
     CDS             complement(2640685..2645646)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yfhM"
                     /locus_tag="b2520"
                     /product="alpha2-macroglobulin"
                     /label=yfhM
                     /note="<i>yfhM</i> encodes an
                     &alpha;<sub>2</sub>-macroglobulin which serves to protect 
                     the cell from host peptidases |CITS: [15186489][18697741]|.
                     YfhM functions as an irreversible, monomeric 'snap-trap'. 
                     Cleavage within the 'bait region' of YfhM by attacking host
                     peptidases results in conformational rearrangment and 
                     exposure of an internal reactive thioester bond which 
                     covalently traps the peptidase and restricts its action 
                     |CITS: [26100869]|. <i>yfhM</i> encodes a 1653 residue 
                     prolipoprotein containing a 17 residue signal peptide and 
                     an internal thiol ester |CITS: [18697741]|. YfhM is 
                     anchored to the periplasmic face of the inner membrane 
                     |CITS: [18697741][26100869]|. YfhM, lacking the signal 
                     sequence and the N-terminal cysteine, purifies as a soluble
                     monomeric protein |CITS: [18697741]|. Proteomic analysis of
                     membrane preparations suggests that YfhM forms a 
                     homooligomeric complex |CITS: [21210718]|.
                     YfhM is a pan-proteinase target - induced YfhM is obtained 
                     by reaction with physiologically relevant host proteinases 
                     (trypsin, thermolysin, chymotrypsin, subtilisin, ulilysin, 
                     pancreatic elastase). Native YfhM is a monomer. Cleavage at
                     R946 in the 'bait region' is required to generate induced 
                     YfhM; induced YfhM forms monomers and dimers. Cleavage at 
                     R162 after induction is required for dimerization; 
                     dimerization is restricted to treatment with particular 
                     peptidases; the induced monomer is the most abundant 
                     functional form of YfhM |CITS: [26100869]|. Although bait 
                     region cleavage is efficient, peptidase binding may be 
                     inefficient and relies on the presence of a host peptidase 
                     lysine that is near to the exposed thioester bond; induced 
                     YfhM may be either bound or unbound to host peptidase 
                     |CITS: [26100869]|. On average, one host peptidase is 
                     covalently bound by 3 - 4 induced YfhM molecules |CITS: 
                     [26100869]|.
                     Crystallised, trypsin-induced YfhM is dimeric; the monomer 
                     unit contains 6 structurally different domain types and 
                     includes 6 macroglobulin-type domains (MG0 - MG6) |CITS: 
                     [26100869]|.
                     &alpha;<sub>2</sub>-macroglobulins are broad spectrum 
                     protease binding proteins of the innate immune system in 
                     metazoans (reviewed in |CITS: [10426429]|; bacterial 
                     &alpha;<sub>2</sub>-macroglobulins were probably acquired 
                     by horizontal gene tranfer from a metazoan host |CITS: 
                     [15186489]|. <i>yfhM</i> homologs in bacteria are typically
                     paired with <i>pbpC</i>, encoding a peptidoglycan 
                     glycosyltransferase; YfhM and PBP1C may act as a defense 
                     and repair mechanism to protect the cell from host defenses
                     during colonization |CITS: [15186489]|.
                     A <i>yfhM</i> knockout mutant has diminished survival under
                     anaerobic conditions in the presence of trypsin |CITS: 
                     [26100869]|
                     <i>yfhM</i> is not expressed under aerobic conditions 
                     |CITS: [15126489]|. Expression of <i>yfhM</i> increases 
                     under anaerobic conditions |CITS: [15126489][15659690]|. 
                     YfhM is important for growth in the absence of aromatic 
                     amino acids |CITS: [17519022]|."
