     gene            2645853..2646698
                     /gene="sseA"
                     /locus_tag="b2521"
                     /label=sseA
     CDS             2645853..2646698
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sseA"
                     /locus_tag="b2521"
                     /product="trifunctional 3-mercaptopyruvate
                     sulfurtransferase / 3-mercaptopyruvate:cyanide 
                     sulfurtransferase / thiosulfate sulfurtransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.8.1.2; 2.8.1.1"
                     /label=sseA
                     /note="3-Mercaptopyruvate sulfurtransferase (3MST) is a 
                     rhodanese-like enzyme that, in contrast to rhodanese, uses 
                     3-mercaptopyruvate as the preferred sulfur donor; 
                     thiosulfate can be used as a less efficient donor |CITS: 
                     [11445076]|. 3MST is the major source of hydrogen sulfide 
                     production in <i>E. coli</i> grown in LB medium |CITS: 
                     [22096201][28533366]|; H<sub>2</sub>S in turn protects the 
                     cell against DNA damage by sequestering free 
                     Fe<sup>2+</sup>, which otherwise drives hydroxyl radical 
                     generation via the Fenton reaction |CITS: [28533366]|.
                     A crystal structure of 3MST has been solved at 2.8 &Aring; 
                     resolution; the structure shows differences with the 
                     structure of rhodanese enzymes, including a variation in 
                     the active site loop. The structure provides insight into 
                     the catalytic mechanism of 3MST |CITS: 
                     [12499560][14672665]|. The Ser240 residue is important for 
                     catalytic activity |CITS: [11445076]|.
                     Overexpression enhances the serine sensitivity caused by 
                     serine-mediated inhibition of |FRAME: 
                     ASPKINIHOMOSERDEHYDROGI-CPLX homoserine dehydrogenase I| 
                     activity during growth on some carbon sources and results 
                     in increased cellular rhodanese activity |CITS: [7982894]|.
                     Deletion of <i>sseA</i> leads to increased sensitivity to 
                     antibiotics such as spectinomycin and nalidixic acid; 
                     addition of exogenous H<sub>2</sub>S restores wild-type 
                     antibiotic resistance |CITS: [22096201]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>sseA</i>
                     mutant was reported to generate wild-type levels of sulfide
                     under both aerobic and anaerobic growth conditions in 
                     medium containing cysteine |CITS: [28607157]|. However, a 
                     difference in sulfide levels between a mutant and wild-type
                     was observed in another study on cysteine homeostasis 
                     |CITS: [38674008]|.
                     SseA: ""<b>s</b>erine <b>s</b>ensitivity <b>e</b>nhancing 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [7982894]|
                     MstA: ""3-<b>m</b>ercaptopyruvate 
                     <b>s</b>ulfur<b>t</b>ransferase""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17454295][22096180]|"
