     gene            complement(2650444..2652294)
                     /gene="hscA"
                     /locus_tag="b2526"
                     /label=hscA
     CDS             complement(2650444..2652294)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hscA"
                     /locus_tag="b2526"
                     /product="bifunctional [Fe-S] cluster biosynthesis
                     chaperone HscA / [Fe-S] cluster biosynthesis 
                     chaperone/co-chaperone system"
                     /label=hscA
                     /note="HscA together with HscB comprises a
                     chaperone/cochaperone system similar to DnaK/DnaJ |CITS: 
                     [9144776][9852006]|.  HscA is required for the assembly of 
                     iron-sulfur clusters |CITS: [10544286][11432781]|.  Models 
                     for chaperone-facilitated Fe-S cluster transfer have been 
                     proposed.  One model involves an exchange of Fe-S cluster 
                     ligands in the iron-sulfur cluster scaffold protein |FRAME:
                     G7324-MONOMER IscU| upon chaperone binding that facilitates
                     cluster transfer |CITS: [21977977]|; another involves 
                     chaperone-mediated disordering of IscU that promotes the 
                     transfer of the Fe-S cluster to an acceptor protein |CITS: 
                     [22782893]|.
                     HscA consists of two domains, a nucleotide-binding domain 
                     (NBD) that binds and hydrolyzes ATP |CITS: [9144776]| and a
                     substrate-binding domain that binds IscU |CITS: 
                     [11053447]|.  The inter-domain linker peptide is able to 
                     stimulate the rate of ATP hydrolysis of the isolated NBD 
                     |CITS: [25372495]|.
                     Under steady-state conditions, ATP hydrolysis rather than 
                     ADP/ATP nucleotide exchange is the rate-limiting step in 
                     the HscA reaction cycle |CITS: [10713091]|; the 
                     dissociation rates for ATP and ADP are comparatively fast 
                     |CITS: [11323718]|.  IscU is a substrate for HscA |CITS: 
                     [11053447]|; in the presence of HscB, IscU stimulates the 
                     ATPase activity of HscA up to 480-fold |CITS: 
                     [10869428][12871959]|.  The <i>in vivo</i> turnover rate of
                     the chaperone cycle may be determined by the availability 
                     of the IscU-HscB complex |CITS: [15485839]|. In the 
                     presence of HscA and HscB, conformational changes in the 
                     [2Fe-2S] cluster of IscU appear to be coupled to ATP 
                     hydrolysis by HscA |CITS: [18986169]|.  Interaction of HscA
                     with IscU occurs via the LPPVK sequence motif in IscU, is 
                     highly directional |CITS: [11994302][12871959][15100228]|, 
                     and occurs in both the ATP- and ADP-bound state, while 
                     interaction with HscB occurs primarily in the ATP-bound 
                     state |CITS: [15485839][25080945]|.  HscA alone or in the 
                     ADP-bound state preferentially binds to and stabilizes the 
                     dynamically disordered state of IscU.  Binding of ATP leads
                     to release of IscU |CITS: [22782893]|.
                     HscA interacts with the apo form of |FRAME: 
                     BIOTIN-SYN-CPLX| and improves the efficiency of the 
                     assembly of the [2Fe-2S] cluster on the enzyme, which is 
                     thought to provide the sulfur atom in the biotin synthase 
                     reaction.  The HscA-BioB complex also binds IscU |CITS: 
                     [19821612]|.
                     A crystal structure of the HscA substrate binding domain in
                     complex with the IscU recognition peptide has been reported
                     |CITS: [15351650]|.  The sequence requirements for 
                     recognition of IscU by HscA have been explored by 
                     site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: [16800630]|.  Suppressor 
                     mutations in <i>iscU</i> can bypass the requirement for the
                     HscA-HscB chaperone system |CITS: [26560204]|. Expression 
                     of <i>hscA</i> is induced by cold shock, but not by heat 
                     shock |CITS: [7665466]|.  An <i>hscA</i> mutant does not 
                     have a general protein folding defect, and overproduction 
                     of HscA does not complement a <i>&Delta;dnaK52</i> mutant 
                     |CITS: [9707441]|.  An <i>hscA</i> mutant has increased 
                     doubling time in rich media and has lower activity of the 
                     Fe-S enzymes GltS and SDH than wild type |CITS: 
                     [11432781]|.  A &Delta;<i>hscA</i> mutant shows reduced 
                     selenate reductase activity |CITS: [25307727]|.  Under 
                     anaerobic conditions, a &Delta;<i>hscA</i> mutant lacks 
                     nitrate reductase, formate dehydrogenases FDH-N and FDH-O, 
                     and hydrogenases 1 and 2 activities |CITS: [28640740]|.
                     HscA: ""<b>h</b>eat <b>s</b>hock <b>c</b>ognate protein 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [8300516][8134349]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [30194723][23333622][23660107][17919282][1 
                     7453917][12475170][35611886]|"
