     gene            complement(2653262..2653648)
                     /gene="iscU"
                     /locus_tag="b2529"
                     /label=iscU
     CDS             complement(2653262..2653648)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="iscU"
                     /locus_tag="b2529"
                     /product="bifunctional scaffold protein for [Fe-S] cluster
                     assembly / iron binding"
                     /label=iscU
                     /note="The assembly of iron-sulfur clusters requires
                     complex biosynthetic machinery. <i>E. coli</i> encodes two 
                     sets of proteins, the Isc and the Suf system, to achieve 
                     this task. IscU is the scaffold protein for assembly and 
                     transfer of iron-sulfur clusters of the Isc system. The 
                     cysteine desulfurase |FRAME: CPLX0-248 IscS| transfers the 
                     sulfane sulfur to IscU, |FRAME: EG12132-MONOMER IscA| acts 
                     as the iron chaperone, |FRAME: EG11653-MONOMER CyaY| 
                     appears to regulate cluster assembly, and the |FRAME: 
                     EG12130-MONOMER HscA| and |FRAME: EG12131-MONOMER HscB| 
                     chaperone and cochaperone facilitate Fe-S cluster transfer.
                     An iron-first mechanism for Fe-S cluster assembly has been 
                     proposed |CITS: [32131593]|.
                     The C terminus of IscS interacts with IscU and transfers 
                     sulfur directly to IscU |CITS: 
                     [11577100][11972033][12686149]|. The mechanism of transfer 
                     is discussed |CITS: [11972033][12686149]|. In the presence 
                     of IscS, IscU is able to form [2Fe-2S] clusters that are 
                     converted to [4Fe-4S] clusters; CyaY appears to slow this 
                     process |CITS: [21799759]|. In the presence of IscS and 
                     L-cysteine, the IscA protein is able to deliver iron to 
                     IscU |CITS: 
                     [14720122][15247288][15828873][15985427][16877383]|; 
                     purified IscU does not bind iron directly |CITS: 
                     [15153099][20942799]|. The interaction between IscU and 
                     IscS has been studied by NMR |CITS: [20471481]|. A 
                     co-crystal structure of the IscS-IscU complex from <i>E. 
                     coli</i> O157:H7 EDL933 has been reported |CITS: 
                     [20404999]|. The Y3 residue of IscU is critical for the 
                     interaction with IscS as well as sulfur transfer |CITS: 
                     [31532036]|. Time-dependent native mass spectrometry was 
                     used to elucidate the mechanism of Fe-S cluster 
                     biosynthesis, supporting an iron-first model in which iron 
                     binding to IscU promotes sulfur transfer from IscS |CITS: 
                     [32131593]|. The IscU binding site on IscS is distinct from
                     but overlaps with the binding site of TusA to IscS; IscS 
                     preferentially binds IscU over TusA |CITS: [38980029]|.
                     Site-directed mutagenesis of potential Fe-S cluster ligands
                     in IscU suggests specific roles for individual cysteine 
                     residues; a model for chaperone-facilitated cluster 
                     transfer has been proposed |CITS: [21977977]|. C37, C63, 
                     H105, and C106 |CITS: [24415728][31532036]| as well as D39,
                     K103 and the strictly conserved Y3 |CITS: [31532036]| are 
                     required for <i>in vivo</i> function of IscU. The D39 and 
                     H105 residues play conflicting roles in the cluster 
                     coordination shell, explaining the instability of [2Fe-2S] 
                     cluster bound to IscU |CITS: [25447544]|. Although CyaY can
                     function as an iron donor to IscU in the presence of IscS 
                     and cysteine |CITS: [16603772]|, it was later shown that it
                     inhibits Fe-S cluster formation on IscU |CITS: [19305405]| 
                     by slowing the reaction |CITS: [21799759]|.  Molecular 
                     dynamics simulations and NMR studies of the interactions 
                     between IscS, IscU and CyaY suggested that CyaY hampers 
                     specific movements between IscU and IscS |CITS: 
                     [27474202]|. <i>In vivo</i> under iron-rich growth 
                     conditions, CyaY has a positive effect on Fe-S cluster 
                     biogenesis |CITS: [25430730]|. Changing Ile108 to Met, a 
                     conserved amino acid in eukaryotic IscU proteins, renders 
                     the Isc system dependent on CyaY/frataxin (as it is in 
                     eukaryotes) |CITS: [25996492]|. |FRAME: G7866-MONOMER YtfE|
                     interacts directly with IscS and IscU |CITS: [33995335]| 
                     and is able to donate iron for the assembly of an Fe-S 
                     cluster into apo-IscU |CITS: [24740378]|.
                     IscU has been reported to be either dimeric |CITS: 
                     [11972033]| or monomeric |CITS: [15153099]| in solution. 
                     The 24.3 kDa molecular weight obtained by analytical gel 
                     filtration may result from the partly unstructured nature 
                     of the protein |CITS: [18702525]|. Solution structures 
                     shows that apo-IscU exists in two different conformations, 
                     one disordered, and another largely ordered, which 
                     interconvert quickly |CITS: [19492851][22203963]| and are 
                     pH-dependent |CITS: [25055301]|. Temperature-induced 
                     transitions between the two states are cooperative |CITS: 
                     [26331259]|. In the ordered state, all four prolyl residues
                     of apo-IscU are in the <i>trans</i> configuration, whereas 
                     the conserved P14 and P101 residues become <i>cis</i> in 
                     the disordered state |CITS: [23110687]|. It was initally 
                     reported that IscS preferentially interacts with and 
                     stabilizes the disordered state of IscU |CITS: [22203963]|,
                     whereas a later report showed that the disordered form of 
                     IscU does not bind to IscS |CITS: [25044349]|. Mutants that
                     stabilize the ordered state of IscU show significantly 
                     slower cluster assembly than wild type |CITS: [22203963]|. 
                     HscA exhibits chaperone activity toward IscU, and this 
                     activity is suggested to play a role in iron-sulfur cluster
                     maturation |CITS: [11053447]|. IscU binds to both HscB and 
                     HscA |CITS: 
                     [10869428][11983079][11994302][12871959][15100228]|, 
                     interacting with both the ATP- and ADP-bound HscA; together
                     with the co-chaperone HscB, it stimulates the ATPase 
                     activity of HscA |CITS: [10869428][15485839]|. The surface 
                     region of IscU which interacts with HscB has been 
                     identified |CITS: [19492851]|; it interacts with the 
                     C-terminal domain of HscB |CITS: [21269500]|. HscB binds to
                     and stabilizes the ordered state of IscU |CITS: 
                     [19492851]|, while HscA and HscA-ADP preferentially 
                     interact with the disordered state of IscU |CITS: 
                     [22782893]|. IscU is able to form [2Fe-2S] clusters and 
                     transfer them to apo-ferredoxin, acting catalytically 
                     |CITS: [15964837][18986169]|. HscA and HscB and ATP 
                     hydrolysis by HscA accelerate cluster transfer |CITS: 
                     [18986169]|; a mechanism was proposed whereby cluster 
                     transfer is coupled to hydrolysis of ATP bound to HscA, 
                     conversion of IscU to the disordered state, and release of 
                     HscB |CITS: [22782893]|. HscB appears to modulate the 
                     formation and transfer of Fe-S clusters by IscU |CITS: 
                     [26246371]|. Single amino acid substitutions in IscU that 
                     bypass the requirement for the HscA-HscB chaperone system 
                     have been isolated, revealing the importance of a denatured
                     state of IscU for function <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [26560204][33202070]|.
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> binding stabilizes IscU |CITS: [24917298]|,
                     apparently by increasing secondary structure |CITS: 
                     [29210568]|, and protects the active site cysteine residues
                     from oxidation |CITS: [32131593]|. Zn<sup>2+</sup> binding 
                     also restores the structured state of IscU mutants that 
                     don't require HscA and HscB |CITS: [33202070]|.
                     The Fe<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub> cluster assembled on IscU 
                     is less resistant to oxygen, hydrogen peroxide, and EDTA 
                     than the |FRAME: G6909-MONOMER SufB| 
                     Fe<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub> cluster |CITS: [25485887]|. 
                     Cobalt, excess copper, and excess zinc are toxic to the 
                     cell, which may be due to their ability to replace iron in 
                     the Fe-S cluster of IscU |CITS: 
                     [17642475][28576762][30824435]|.
                     Co-overproduction of IscU with eight polypeptides produced 
                     from neighboring genes 
                     (<i>trmJ-iscR-iscS-iscU-iscA-hscB-hscA-fdx-iscX</i>) causes
                     increased ferredoxin iron-sulfur cluster assembly |CITS: 
                     [10393315]|. An <i>iscU</i> mutant has a growth defect 
                     compared to wild type |CITS: [11432781][15308657]|. It has 
                     very low activity of the iron-sulfur cluster enzymes 
                     succinate dehydrogenase and glutamate synthase |CITS: 
                     [11432781]| and no formate dehydrogenase, nitrate reductase
                     |CITS: [22081393]|, hydrogenase 1, hydrogenase 2 |CITS: 
                     [22363723]|, and formate-hydrogen lyase activity |CITS: 
                     [23558265]|. An <i>iscU</i> mutant also contains no HypD, 
                     the Fe-S cluster-containing hydrogenase maturase |CITS: 
                     [22363723]|. In the absence of NfuA, IscU is able to 
                     provide a replacement [4Fe-4S] cluster to LipA |CITS: 
                     [29051382]|.
                     Transcription of the <i>isc</i> operon is repressed by the 
                     IscR protein, which contains an iron-sulfur cluster that 
                     could act as a sensor of iron-sulfur cluster assembly and 
                     regulate expression of the <i>isc</i> operon accordingly 
                     |CITS: [11742080]|. Protein abundance is increased in the 
                     presence of the antifouling agent zosteric acid |CITS: 
                     [22176949]|.
                     IscU: ""<b>i</b>ron-<b>s</b>ulfur-<b>c</b>luster 
                     formation"" |CITS: [9582371]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12196163][15952888][18322036][19021507][2 
                     0467446][23298813][23333622][25450980][25510311][25996679] 
                     [35611886]|"
