     gene            complement(2655002..2655490)
                     /gene="iscR"
                     /locus_tag="b2531"
                     /label=iscR
     CDS             complement(2655002..2655490)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="iscR"
                     /locus_tag="b2531"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA-binding transcriptional dual
                     regulator IscR / DNA-binding transcriptional repressor 
                     IscR-a [2Fe-2S] iron-sulfur cluster"
                     /label=iscR
                     /note="The transcription factor IscR, for 
                     ""<b>I</b>ron-<b>s</b>ulfur <b>c</b>luster 
                     <b>R</b>egulator,"" is negatively autoregulated, and it 
                     contains an iron-sulfur cluster that could act as a sensor 
                     of iron-sulfur cluster assembly 
                     |CITS:[11742080][16677314]|. This protein regulates the 
                     expression of the operons that encode components of a 
                     secondary pathway of iron-sulfur cluster assembly, 
                     iron-sulfur proteins, anaerobic respiration enzymes, and 
                     biofilm formation |CITS:[11432781][11742080][16677314][188 
                     49427][16824106][19074392]|. In addition, IscR contributes 
                     to the cellular response to stress caused by the 
                     superoxide-producing redox-cycling agent phenazine 
                     methosulfate (PMS) |CITS: [40936345]|. The role of IscR in 
                     iron-sulfur (Fe-S) cluster biogenesis was simulated and 
                     analyzed in a mathematical model in |CITS: [37223744]|.
                     IscR is a member of the Rrf2 family |CITS:[19361432]| and 
                     carries a predicted N-terminal helix-turn-helix DNA-binding
                     motif and four conserved cysteines in its C terminus |CITS:
                     [35793678]|. IscR is a dimer in solution |CITS:[19361432]|,
                     and it contains the [2Fe-2S]<sup>1+</sup> cluster when 
                     purified under anaerobic conditions |CITS:[11742080]|. This
                     [2Fe-2S]<sup>1+</sup> cluster has an unusual 
                     (Cys)<sub>3</sub>(His)<sub>1</sub> ligand scheme and is 
                     essential for cluster ligation |CITS:[22583201]|. Some 
                     proteins, such as SufB, IscU, EprA, and IscA, are involved 
                     in the assembly and transfer of the [2Fe-2S]cluster to IscR
                     |CITS:[23320508]|. Recent work indicates that the oxidation
                     state of the IscR [2Fe-2S] cluster is itself regulatory: 
                     the oxidized [2Fe-2S]<sup>2+</sup> form shows impaired DNA 
                     binding to type 1 sites under aerobic in vitro assay 
                     conditions, while under strictly anaerobic conditions 
                     binding can be restored, consistent with auto-reduction 
                     back to [2Fe-2S]<sup>1+</sup> |CITS: [40936345]|.
                     Two types of DNA-binding sites have been described for 
                     IscR: type 1 and type 2. Both sites resemble 25-bp 
                     imperfect palindromes but differ in their sequence 
                     |CITS:[16677314][19361432]|. IscR requires the iron-sulfur 
                     cluster for its activity when bound to type 1 sites. The 
                     binding of the [2Fe-2S]<sup>1+</sup> cluster allows IscR to
                     induce a conformational change, mainly around the residue 
                     Glu43, that modifies its DNA-binding affinity. This 
                     modification enables IscR to bind to type 1 DNA-binding 
                     sites  |CITS:[ 23644595]|.
                     P<sub>iscR</sub> contains two type 1 sites 
                     |CITS:[16677314]|. Therefore, under conditions when Fe-S 
                     cluster assembly becomes rate limiting, levels of 
                     [2Fe-2S]-containing IscR may decrease due to a lower rate 
                     of its synthesis, thus relieving repression of the 
                     <i>iscRSUA</i> operon for the biogenesis of the iron-sulfur
                     cluster |CITS:[11742080][11752417][23075318]|. In contrast,
                     for the regulation of promoters containing type 2 sites, 
                     such as P<sub><i>sufA</i></sub>, the [2Fe-2S] cluster of 
                     IscR is dispensable |CITS:[16824106][18849427]|.
                     IscR regulates in a coordinated manner the expression of 
                     the <i>iscRSUA</i> to the <i>sufABCDSE</i> operons through 
                     repression by  [2Fe-2S]-IscR and activation by apo-IscR, 
                     respectively. Both the apo- and holoprotein conformations 
                     were able to activate the Suf pathway in order to maintain 
                     differential control for Fe-S cluster biogenesis pathways 
                     to ensure viability under a variety of growth conditions 
                     |CITS:[25266384] |. The superoxide-producing redox-cycling 
                     agent phenazine methosulfate (PMS) derepresses the type 
                     1-controlled <i>isc</i> promoter in vivo and, under aerobic
                     conditions, also increases expression from type 2 
                     promoters, supporting the idea that redox cycling can both 
                     oxidize holo-IscR and increase apo-IscR pools |CITS: 
                     [40936345]|. In vitro, PMS alone does not measurably 
                     destabilize the IscR [2Fe-2S] cluster, but superoxide 
                     generated during aerobic PMS redox cycling promotes cluster
                     loss contributing to apo-IscR accumulation in vivo |CITS: 
                     [40936345]|. IscR binds cooperatively to DNA, and four 
                     protomers bind to one palindromic site |CITS:[19361432]|.
                     Under ampicillin treatment, the activity of IscR appears to
                     be affected |CITS: [33731833]|. On the other hand, 
                     inhibition of <i>iscR</i> expression by CRISPRi reduced 
                     cellular fitness under treatment with the antibiotic 
                     carbonyl cyanide 3-chlorophenylhydrazone (CCCP) 
                     |CITS:[40352728]|.
                     A positive correlation between cellular growth and the copy
                     number of the protein IscR has been reported |CITS: 
                     [35694815]|.
                     The transcription and translation of the <i>iscR</i> gene 
                     increase at pH 4.4  and a <i>mhpR</i> knockout mutant 
                     diminishes cellular survival at pH 3.0 compared to the wild
                     type |CITS: [37909716]|.
                     Cellular growth is diminished in an <i>iscR</i> knockout 
                     mutant under iron starvation |CITS: [37223744]|.
                     The IscR binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [25641558][38763301]|."
