     gene            2656801..2657604
                     /gene="suhB"
                     /locus_tag="b2533"
                     /label=suhB
     CDS             2656801..2657604
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="suhB"
                     /locus_tag="b2533"
                     /product="multifunctional Nus factor SuhB /
                     inositol-phosphate phosphatase / inositol monophosphatase /
                     glycerol-2-phosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.3.25; 3.1.3.19"
                     /label=suhB
                     /note="Together with other Nus factors, SuhB plays a role
                     in rRNA maturation |CITS: [26980831]| and antitermination 
                     |CITS: [31020314]|.  A model suggests that SuhB promotes 
                     the formation of loops between the elongating RNA 
                     polymerase and NusB/E bound to <i>boxA</i> |CITS: 
                     [26980831]|.  In the presence of all Nus factors, SuhB 
                     suppresses &rho;-dependent and intrinsic transcription 
                     termination in a ribosomal RNA primary transcript |CITS: 
                     [31020314]|.
                     SuhB was shown to interact with RNA polymerase |CITS: 
                     [17652087]|; a weak interaction with the &beta; or &beta;' 
                     subunits was shown by solution-state NMR |CITS: 
                     [31127279]|.  <i>In vivo</i>, SuhB association with rRNA 
                     gene-transcribing RNA polymerase is dependent on |FRAME: 
                     EG10665-MONOMER NusB| |CITS: [26980831]|.  SuhB interacts 
                     directly with the C-terminal AR2 domain of |FRAME: 
                     EG10665-MONOMER NusA| and the unprocessed rRNA transcript 
                     |CITS: [31020314][31127279]| and is required for 
                     integration of |FRAME: CPLX0-7879 NusBE| into the Nus 
                     factor complex |CITS: [31020314]|.  SuhB, NusG and the 
                     &alpha;CTD of RNA polymerase all interact with the same 
                     site on NusA and appear to be able to displace the AR2 
                     domain from the SSK domain of NusA |CITS: [31127279]|. SuhB
                     plays a role in BoxA-mediated antitermination in an <i>in 
                     vivo</i> reporter system, but is not required for 
                     N-mediated antitermination in phage &lambda; |CITS: 
                     [26980831]|.
                     Initial experiments to elucidate the physiological function
                     of SuhB showed that the protein has inositol 
                     monophosphatase and glycerol-2-phosphatase activity |CITS: 
                     [8002619]|.  However, inositol monophosphatase activity was
                     neither necessary nor sufficient for rescue of the 
                     cold-sensitive growth phenotype of an <i>suhB</i> mutant 
                     |CITS: [10747806][17652087]|.
                     Crystal structures of SuhB alone, in complex with the 
                     C-terminal domain of NusA, and the SuhB R184A point mutant 
                     have been solved |CITS: [17652087][31020314]|.  SuhB 
                     appears in a homodimeric form in the crystal structures 
                     |CITS: [17652087][31020314]|; reports differ on whether 
                     |CITS: [31020314]| or not |CITS: [31127279]| the wild-type 
                     protein also behaves as a homodimer in solution.  The 
                     binding site for Li<sup>+</sup>, an inhibitor of the 
                     catalytic activity of SuhB, has been determined |CITS: 
                     [22384802]|.
                     <i>suhB</i> was initially identified as a suppressor of a 
                     <i>secY</i> mutant |CITS: [6389495]|, an <i>rpoH</i> mutant
                     |CITS: [2138605]|, and a <i>dnaB</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [1847383]|.  The protein products of these genes are 
                     involved in protein export, the heat shock response, and 
                     DNA replication, respectively.  In all cases, the 
                     <i>suhB</i> suppressor allele causes a cold-sensitive 
                     growth phenotype.  The ability of <i>suhB</i> point mutants
                     to bind to RNA polymerase correlates with their ability to 
                     rescue the cold-sensitive growth defect of an <i>suhB</i> 
                     mutant |CITS: [17652087]|.  Suppressors of the 
                     cold-sensitive phenotype of an <i>suhB</i> suppressor 
                     allele or &Delta;<i>suhB</i> mutant map to <i>rnc</i>, 
                     which encodes |FRAME: EG10857-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [8589060][26980831]|, and to |FRAME: EG10909 <i>nusE</i>| 
                     (<i>rpsJ</i>) |CITS: [26980831]|.  Mutation of <i>nusA</i> 
                     or deletion of <i>nusB</i> were also found to suppress the 
                     growth defect of &Delta;<i>suhB</i> |CITS: [26980831]|. 
                     Expression of <i>suhB</i> appears to be autoregulated 
                     |CITS: [8831954]|.  The 5' UTR of <i>suhB</i> contains a 
                     conserved BoxA site that is involved in regulation of 
                     <i>suhB</i> expression.  Based on the experimental data, 
                     Nus factors at BoxA interact with either the ribosome or 
                     RNA polymerase to prevent translation, with is then 
                     followed by Rho-dependent termination of transcription 
                     |CITS: [29229908]|.
                     <i>suhB</i> expression is increased in the presence of the 
                     Rho inhibitor bicyclomycin |CITS: [23207917]|. 
                     Transcription termination within <i>suhB</i> is decreased 
                     in a Rho mutant with decreased termination activity |CITS: 
                     [29229908]|.
                     SsyA: ""<b>s</b>uppressor of <i><b>s</b>ec<b>Y</b>24</i>"" 
                     |CITS: [6389495]|
                     SuhB: ""<b>su</b>ppressor of <i>rpo<b>H</b>15</i> |CITS: 
                     [2138605]|"
