     gene            complement(2677613..2678866)
                     /gene="glyA"
                     /locus_tag="b2551"
                     /label=glyA
     CDS             complement(2677613..2678866)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glyA"
                     /locus_tag="b2551"
                     /product="multifunctional serine hydroxymethyltransferase
                     / D-serine degradation / 
                     5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate:glycine 
                     hydroxymethyltransferase / 5,10-methenyltetrahydrofolate 
                     hydrolase / D-alanine transaminase / allothreonine 
                     acetaldehyde-lyase (glycine-forming)"
                     /EC_number="4.3.1.18; 2.1.2.1"
                     /label=glyA
                     /note="Serine hydroxymethyltransferase (GlyA) converts
                     L-serine (but not D-serine |CITS: [37700610]|) to glycine, 
                     transferring a methyl group to tetrahydrofolate, thus 
                     forming 5,10-methylene-tetrahydrofolate (5,10-mTHF). 
                     5,10-mTHF is the major source of C1 units in the cell, 
                     making GlyA a key enzyme in the biosynthesis of purines, 
                     thymidine, methionine, choline and lipids.
                     The enzyme catalyzes several side reactions including 
                     hydrolysis of 5,10-methenylTHF to 5-formylTHF |CITS: 
                     [2201683]| and the reversible cleavage of 3-hydroxy amino 
                     acids (L-threonine, allothreonine, 3-phenylserine) to 
                     glycine and an aldehyde |CITS: [3891721][3512553]|. 
                     D-alanine inactivates the enzyme by reacting with the 
                     pyridoxal phosphate prosthetic group to form pyridoxamine 
                     phosphate |CITS: [3891721][3512553]|. GlyA has also been 
                     shown to degrade D-serine, but not L-serine, to pyruvate 
                     and ammonia via dehydratase activity, which also occurs 
                     through the action of |FRAME: DSERDEAM-MONOMER| (DsdA) in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [37700610]|. The D-serine dehydratase
                     activity of GlyA is more than 100-fold higher than its 
                     hydroxymethyltransferase activity, and THF was found to be 
                     required for the dehydratase activity and increased 
                     activity rates with increasing concentrations |CITS: 
                     [37700610]|.
                     The Thr226 residue within a conserved region of the enzyme 
                     appears to be involved in substrate discrimination |CITS: 
                     [1731867]|. The His228 residue plays a role in determining 
                     reaction specificity |CITS: [1517215]|.  Lys229 does not 
                     appear to play a catalytic role |CITS: [8226831]|. Arg363 
                     appears to be the binding site for the carboxyl group of 
                     the amino acid substrate |CITS: [7925461]|. The hydroxyl 
                     group of Tyr65 may be involved in the conversion of the 
                     active site from a closed to an open conformation |CITS: 
                     [10858298]|. Both Tyr55 and Arg235 are required for the 
                     transaldimination reaction |CITS: [19883126]|.
                     Studies on refolding of the enzyme indicate that pyridoxal 
                     5'-phosphate (PLP) only binds to the dimeric apoenzyme at 
                     the end of the folding pathway |CITS: [7642604]|. The 
                     mechanism of PLP addition has been investigated further. At
                     high concentrations of PLP, a second molecule of PLP can 
                     bind at Lys346 |CITS: [17341210]|. A conserved hydrophobic 
                     contact area is involved in stability of the PLP binding 
                     site |CITS: [19019081][19909338]|. Tyr55 is required for 
                     correct positioning of the PLP cofactor |CITS: [19883126]|.
                     Crystal structures of wild type and mutant serine 
                     hydroxymethyltransferase have been solved |CITS: 
                     [10656824][10858298][19883126]|.
                     <i>glyA</i> mutants can not use glycine as the sole source 
                     of nitrogen |CITS: [764875]|. A <i>glyA</i> mutant is 
                     auxotrophic for glycine |CITS: [14276110]|; <i>glyA</i> was
                     later shown to be essential for growth on glycerol minimal 
                     medium |CITS: [17012394]|.
                     Sequences 3' to the structural gene within the <i>glyA</i> 
                     mRNA are required for mRNA stability |CITS: 
                     [3881406][1691434]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [16125438]|"
