     gene            2679194..2680384
                     /gene="hmp"
                     /locus_tag="b2552"
                     /label=hmp
     CDS             2679194..2680384
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hmp"
                     /locus_tag="b2552"
                     /product="bifunctional nitric oxide dioxygenase /
                     dihydropteridine reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.14.12.17; 1.5.1.34"
                     /label=hmp
                     /note="Hmp is a flavohemoglobin |CITS: [1334413]| with
                     multiple enzymatic activities <i>in vitro</i>; however, its
                     nitric oxide dioxygenase (NOD) activity, which is also the 
                     major nitric oxide detoxification system in the cell, 
                     appears to be the physiologically relevant function |CITS: 
                     [9724711][31061166]|. Under aerobic conditions, Hmp 
                     detoxifies |FRAME: NITRIC-OXIDE| (NO) and thus protects the
                     respiratory cytochromes  |FRAME: CYT-O-UBIOX-CPLX 
                     ""cytochrome <i>bo</i>""|, |FRAME: CYT-D-UBIOX-CPLX 
                     ""cytochrome <i>bd</i>-I""|, and |FRAME: APP-UBIOX-CPLX 
                     ""cytochrome <i>bd</i>-II""| |CITS: 
                     [9826660][10915782][11751864]|.  Hmp remains the primary 
                     consumer of NO under microaerobic conditions (O<sub>2</sub>
                     concentrations as low as 35 &mu;M) |CITS: [23658508]|.  
                     However, <i>in vivo</i> measurements of the dynamics of NO 
                     detoxification showed that NO defenses are greatly impaired
                     under microaerobic conditions due to limited activities of 
                     both |FRAME: CPLX0-1 NorV| and Hmp |CITS: [26951670]|.  NO 
                     tolerance is correlated with virulence |CITS: [16407044]| 
                     and survival in macrophages |CITS: [17611046]|.
                     The nitric oxide dioxygenase reaction appears to occur in 
                     two steps.  First, NAD(P)H reduces the bound FAD, which 
                     then reduces the heme iron.  O<sub>2</sub> then binds to 
                     the reduced flavohemoglobin, which dioxygenates NO, forming
                     nitrate |CITS: [10777548]|.  However, heme ligand turnover 
                     and NADH consumption at different O<sub>2</sub>/NO 
                     concentrations suggests that the enzyme binds NO first; the
                     nitroxyl equivalent is then oxidized |CITS: [11517313]|.  
                     The reaction mechanism has been investigated further |CITS:
                     [16439024]|.  There is some debate whether the reaction 
                     follows a dioxygenase or a denitrosylase/NO reductase 
                     mechanism |CITS: [22343413][22796210]|.  Computer 
                     simulations have provided further insights into the 
                     catalytic mechanism |CITS: [23220591]|.
                     The steric and dielectric environment of the heme iron has 
                     been investigated |CITS: [11690654]|.  The active site 
                     structure appears to be similar to that of peroxidases 
                     |CITS: [11092893]|.  A crystal structure of Hmp has been 
                     solved at 2.2 &Aring; resolution, showing that the 
                     N-terminal globin domain, central FAD-binding domain, and 
                     C-terminal NADH-binding domain are arranged in a 
                     heart-shaped structure |CITS: [11964402]|.  All domains are
                     required for Hmp function |CITS: [12826671]|.  Folding of 
                     the globin domain has been studied |CITS: [18187151]|. 
                     Kinetic competition between Hmp and respiratory cytochromes
                     for oxygen appears to be the cause of impaired NO tolerance
                     under microaerobic conditions |CITS: [26951670]|.  During 
                     growth on a limited amount of carbon, Hmp expression is 
                     induced, Hmp activity depletes the NADH pool of the cell, 
                     and NO detoxification subsequently fails |CITS: 
                     [27207837]|. When cels are exposed simultaneously to 
                     |FRAME: HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| (H<SUB>2</SUB>O<SUB>2</SUB>) and
                     NO, removal of NO is impeded until 
                     H<SUB>2</SUB>O<SUB>2</SUB> has been eliminated, apparently 
                     due to diminished transcription and translation of Hmp. 
                     This regulatory response involves |FRAME: CPLX0-7756| 
                     |CITS:[31061166]|.
                     Hmp activity causes oxidative stress in the cell due to 
                     production of superoxide by its NADH oxidase activity 
                     |CITS: [8612736][8022841][8704956][15340787]|.  Hmp can 
                     oxidize both NADH and NADPH with a K<sub>m</sub> of 1.8 and
                     19.6 &micro;M, respectively |CITS: [9770277]|.
                     The ferrisiderophore reductase activity |CITS: [1601132]| 
                     and ferric citrate reductase activity |CITS: [8612736]| of 
                     Hmp is a result of the flavin reductase activity of Hmp 
                     |CITS: [8292013]|.  Hmp was also shown to have 
                     dihydropteridine reductase |CITS: [2034230]|, cytochrome 
                     <i>c</i> reductase and ferric hydroxamate reductase |CITS: 
                     [9168606]| as well as alkylhydroperoxide reductase activity
                     |CITS: [12663656]|.
                     Hmp binds unsaturated or cyclopropanated fatty acids |CITS:
                     [12741837][15189885]|.
                     The enzyme is inhibited by the imidazole class of 
                     antibiotics, including miconazole and econazole |CITS: 
                     [15855504]|.
                     Expression of <i>hmp</i> is regulated in response to 
                     oxygen, nitrogen compounds, and iron availability |CITS: 
                     [8808940][15647275]| and is induced by acid |CITS: 
                     [14679238]|.  NO induction is mediated by Fnr |CITS: 
                     [12093725]| and Fur |CITS: [12475930]|.  An <i>hmp</i> 
                     mutant is more sensitive to nitric oxide than wild type 
                     |CITS: [9724711][9826660][9873011]|, but has a growth 
                     advantage in mixed cultures |CITS: [17611046]|.
                     Expression of <i>hmp</i> under NO stress is decreased in a 
                     |FRAME: EG10158| mutant |CITS: [26112956]|.  The rate of NO
                     reduction in anaerobically grown cells appears to be 
                     unaffected in an <i>hmp</i> mutant |CITS: [21265775]|. When
                     cells are grown in culture media that contain amino acids, 
                     production of Hmp is reduced due to depletion of ATP, which
                     is consumed by ABC-type amino acid transporters, leading to
                     reduced NO detoxification |CITS: [37253411]|.
                     Hmp: ""<b>h</b>ae<b>m</b>o<b>p</b>rotein"" |CITS: 
                     [2034230]|
                     FsrB: ""<b>f</b>erri<b>s</b>iderophore <b>r</b>eductase 
                     <b>B</b>"" |CITS: [1601132]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10844666][15598505][15667299][16417522][1 
                     8237632][18379989][22343413][22796210]|"
