     gene            complement(2684516..2684699)
                     /gene="glmY"
                     /locus_tag="b4441"
                     /label=glmY
     misc_RNA        complement(2684516..2684699)
                     /gene="glmY"
                     /locus_tag="b4441"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA GlmY"
                     /note="GlmY is a small RNA that specifically increases
                     synthesis of GlmS, encoded by the second open reading frame
                     in the <i>glmUS</i> operon, without affecting GlmU 
                     synthesis |CITS: [17854828]|.  GlmY acts indirectly by 
                     antagonizing processing of a second small RNA, |FRAME: 
                     SRAJ-RNA GlmZ|.  Only full-length GlmZ can stabilize 
                     <i>glmS</i> mRNA and enhance its translation |CITS: 
                     [18351803][18334534]|.  Unlike many other small RNAs, GlmY 
                     does not act by Hfq-facilitated base pairing.  Instead, 
                     GlmY binds and sequesters |FRAME: CPLX0-7998 RapZ|, thereby
                     inhibiting RapZ-enabled processing of GlmZ |CITS: 
                     [23475961]|.  RapZ binds glucosamine-6-phosphate (GlcN6P); 
                     in the GlcN6P-free form, RapZ stimulates phosphorylation of
                     the two-component system kinase |FRAME: CPLX0-8275 GlrK|, 
                     which subsequently causes increased expression of 
                     <i>glmY</i>.  Both GlmY and GlmZ can counteract RapZ's 
                     effect on GlrK, resulting in a negative feedback loop 
                     |CITS: [32065419]|.
                     The GlmY small RNA was first reported to be present as two 
                     RNA species of approximately 150 and 180 nt in length 
                     |CITS: [11553332]|.  Later, it was shown that the two GlmY 
                     RNAs have the same 5' end; thus, the shorter species, 
                     GlmY*, may be generated by processing at the 3' end.  The 
                     abundance and ratio of the two species changes with the 
                     growth phase |CITS: [14602901]|.
                     RapZ is required for accumulation of the processed GlmY* in
                     response to depletion of GlcN6P |CITS: [32065419]|; under 
                     GlcN6P-limiting conditions, RapZ binding protects GlmY* 
                     from degradation |CITS: [32065419]|.  GlmY* is 
                     polyadenylated by PcnB, and mutation of <i>pcnB</i> leads 
                     to stabilization of GlmY* RNA |CITS: [18351803][18334534]|.
                      |FRAME: EG10743-MONOMER ""PNPase""| and |FRAME: 
                     EG11620-MONOMER ""RNase II""| are major factors controlling
                     the steady-state level of GlmY RNA |CITS: [22355164]|.
                     The terminator region of the GlmY RNA was shown to interact
                     with the |FRAME: EG10030 <i>add</i>| and |FRAME: G7339 
                     <i>yphC</i>| mRNAs |CITS: [30287822]|. Overexpression of 
                     GlmY decreases swimming motility |CITS: [26469694]|.  In 
                     growth competition experiments, a <i>glmY</i> deletion 
                     mutant is more sensitive to cell envelope stress than the 
                     wild type |CITS: [19734312]|.
                     The GlmY/GlmZ system provides resistance to inhibitors of 
                     GlmS activity, such as bacilysin and Nva-FMDP.
                     <i>glmY</i> or <i>glmZ</i> mutants do not overexpress 
                     <i>glmS</i> in response to GlmS inhibitors, leading to 
                     increased susceptibility to these antibiotics |CITS: 
                     [27379045]|.
                     <i>glmY</i> is expressed from overlapping 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> and &sigma;<sup>54</sup> promoters 
                     |CITS: [19843219]|.  GlmY accumulates upon depletion of 
                     intracellular glucosamine-6-phosphate |CITS: 
                     [18334534][23475961]|.  Expression of <i>glmY</i> is lower 
                     in a <i>phoP</i> mutant than in wild type |CITS: 
                     [21665928]|.  GlmY is expressed at higher levels when cells
                     are grown in pyruvate compared to glucose |CITS: 
                     [31363904]|.
                     SroF: ""<b>s</b>mall <b>R</b>N<b>o</b>mics RNA"" |CITS: 
                     [14602901]|
                     GlmY: ""<i><b>glm</b>S</i> activator RNA <b>Y</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [17854828]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12654996][15487940][18981470][24667238][2 
                     6618164][32213244][32391395]|"
     gene            complement(2685015..2688902)
                     /gene="purL"
                     /locus_tag="b2557"
                     /label=purL
     CDS             complement(2685015..2688902)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="purL"
                     /locus_tag="b2557"
                     /product="phosphoribosylformylglycinamide synthetase"
                     /EC_number="6.3.5.3"
                     /label=purL
                     /note="PurL catalyzes the fourth step in the <i>E.
                     coli</i> purine <i>de novo</i> biosynthesis pathway.  It 
                     converts 5'-phosphoribosyl-<i>N</i>-formylglycineamide 
                     (FGAR) to 5-phosphoribosyl-<i>N</i>-formylglycineamidine 
                     (FGAM) in the presence of glutamine and ATP.  It is the 
                     second glutamine amidotransferase in the pathway, the other
                     being PurF.  PurL is a large, 141 kDa polypeptide that was 
                     shown by genetic complementation tests to contain three 
                     different domains.  The N-terminal Domain I contains a 
                     potential ATP binding motif, the C-terminal Domain III is 
                     similar to several glutamine amidotransferases, and Domain 
                     II contains elements observed in the triosephosphate 
                     isomerase family.  Thus, PurL may have arisen from the 
                     fusion of at least three different gene families |CITS: 
                     [2531746]|.
                     The enzyme mechanism was studied using initial velocity, 
                     dead-end inhibition, and substrate/product exchange 
                     studies.  A sequential mechanism was suggested in which 
                     glutamine binds first, followed by rapid equilibrium 
                     binding of MgATP, followed by binding of FGAR |CITS: 
                     [2659070]|.
                     The apparent molecular mass was determined to be 135 kDa by
                     SDS-PAGE and 125 kDa by gel filtration chromatography, 
                     indicating a single polypeptide |CITS: [2659070]|.
                     The crystal structure of the ortholog from <i>Salmonella 
                     enterica</i> subsp. <i>enterica</i> serovar Typhimurium has
                     been determined.  Four distinct domains were identified and
                     analyzed, providing further insight into the catalytic 
                     mechanism |CITS: [15301531]|.
                     Review: Jensen, K.F., G. Dandanell, B. Hove-Jensen, and M. 
                     Willemoes (2008) ""Nucleotides, Nucleosides and 
                     Nucleobases"" EcoSal 3.6.2 |CITS: [ecosal]|"
