     gene            complement(2691274..2691909)
                     /gene="pgpC"
                     /locus_tag="b2560"
                     /label=pgpC
     CDS             complement(2691274..2691909)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pgpC"
                     /locus_tag="b2560"
                     /product="bifunctional phosphatidylglycerophosphatase C / 
                     phosphatidylglycerophosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.3.27"
                     /label=pgpC
                     /note="<i>E.coli</i> contains three 
                     phosphatidylglycerophosphatases - |FRAME: EG10704 
                     ""PgpA""|, |FRAME: PGPPHOSPHAB-MONOMER ""PgpB""| and PgpC -
                     which catalyse the dephosphorylation of 
                     phosphatidylglycerol phosphate (PGP) to 
                     phosphatidylglycerol (PG), an essential phospholipid of the
                     inner and outer membrane in <i>E. coli</i>. PgpA and PgpC 
                     appear to be specific for PGP |CITS: [6296050], [21148555]|
                     whereas PgpB is a multifunctional enzyme that is also 
                     active on undecaprenyl diphosphate, phosphatidicic acid and
                     lysophosphatidic acid |CITS: [15778224], [8940025]|.
                     <i>pgpC</i> is essential for growth in a <i>pgpApgpB</i> 
                     mutant |CITS: [21148555]| but not in wild type cells |CITS:
                     [16738554]|. A <i>pgpApgpBpgpC</i> triple mutant is not 
                     viable but can be generated with a complementing plasmid 
                     containing any one of the three 
                     phosphatidylglycerophosphatases. A triple mutant 
                     complemented with any one of <i>pgpA, pgpB</i> or 
                     <i>pgpC</i> expressed on a temperature sensitive plasmid is
                     viable at 30&deg;C but not at 42&deg;C |CITS: [21148555]|. 
                     Lipid profiling of these strains showed accumulation of PGP
                     and disappearance of PG when shifted from 30&deg;C to 
                     42&deg;C |CITS: [21148555]|. Overexpression of <i>pgpC</i> 
                     from a plasmid results in increased 
                     phosphatidylglycerophosphatase activity compared to the 
                     wild type |CITS: [21148555]|. The activity of PgpC is 
                     temperature sensitive with significant reduction observed 
                     at 42&deg;C compared to 30&deg;C |CITS: [1309518], 
                     [21148555]|. PgpC activity accounts for up to 50% of the 
                     total phosphatidylglycerophosphatase activity in wild type 
                     cells |CITS: [1309518]|.
                     PgpC contains a single predicted transmembrane segment and 
                     an active site that faces the cytoplasm |CITS: [21148555]|.
                     "
