     gene            complement(2694357..2695088)
                     /gene="pdxJ"
                     /locus_tag="b2564"
                     /label=pdxJ
     CDS             complement(2694357..2695088)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pdxJ"
                     /locus_tag="b2564"
                     /product="pyridoxine 5'-phosphate synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.6.99.2"
                     /label=pdxJ
                     /note="Pyridoxine 5'-phosphate synthase (PdxJ) is one of
                     the key enzymes in <i>de novo</i> pyridoxine 5'-phosphate 
                     synthesis.  It catalyzes a complicated ring-closure 
                     reaction involving the condensation of 1-deoxy-D-xylulose 
                     5-phosphate (DXP) and 3-amino-1-hydroxyacetone 1-phosphate 
                     to form pyridoxine 5'-phosphate (PNP) |CITS: [10225425]|. 
                     Crystal structures of PdxJ have been solved |CITS: 
                     [10944349][11286891][12206776][12269807]|.  The enzyme is 
                     an octamer in solution |CITS: [10944349]|.  The octamer is 
                     organized as a tetramer of active dimers; each monomer 
                     consists of a (&beta;/&alpha;)<sub>8</sub> TIM barrel 
                     domain.  Structures of enzyme/substrate and enzyme/product 
                     complexes allowed localization of the active site within a 
                     deep cavity which is closed in the presence of the product 
                     |CITS: [11286891][12269807]|.  Each subunit of the octamer 
                     has a unique conformation that is correlated with the 
                     occupancy of the active site. The substrate-binding sites 
                     are located at the interface of two subunits |CITS: 
                     [12269807]|.  A reaction mechanism involving Schiff base 
                     formation, water elimination and addition, phosphate 
                     elimination, ring closure, and various proton shifts has 
                     been proposed |CITS: [12206776][12269807]|.  The open state
                     accepts substrates and releases products, while most of the
                     catalytic events occur in the closed state.  Glu72 appears 
                     to be the central acid-base catalyst of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [11286891]|.
                     A Mini-Tn<i>10</i> insertion mutant within <i>pdxJ</i> 
                     requires pyridoxine for growth |CITS: [1537799]|. 
                     <i>acpS</i>, encoded downstream of <i>pdxJ</i> in the same 
                     operon, is essential for viability |CITS: 
                     [1537799][1537800]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17822383][12686115][11200221]|
                     "
