     gene            complement(2695100..2695828)
                     /gene="recO"
                     /locus_tag="b2565"
                     /label=recO
     CDS             complement(2695100..2695828)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="recO"
                     /locus_tag="b2565"
                     /product="recombination mediator protein RecO"
                     /label=recO
                     /note="RecO is a recombination mediator protein |CITS: 
                     [33916151]| in the RecF pathway for the homology-directed 
                     repair of post-replicative single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) 
                     gaps.
                     <i>recO</i> mutations confer UV sensitivity and a 
                     Rec<sup>-</sup> (recombination deficient) phenotype in 
                     strains which lack the major |FRAME: RECBCD RecBC| pathway 
                     of recombination; RecO, |FRAME: CPLX0-8596 RecF|, and 
                     |FRAME: EG10834-MONOMER RecR| belong to the same epistastic
                     group |CITS: [2993230][1883889]|.
                     RecO, |FRAME: CPLX0-8596 RecF|, and |FRAME: EG10834-MONOMER
                     RecR| are implicated in DNA daughter-strand gap repair and 
                     may function together as a complex |CITS: 
                     [6358188][7687008][7517004][8962075]|. RecF, RecO, and RecR
                     function at an early stage of recombinational repair and 
                     affect the interaction of |FRAME: EG10823-MONOMER RecA|, 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-8165 SSB|, and ssDNA |CITS: 
                     [8483906][7962001][8206844][8537414][9048946][9363943]| 
                     (also |CITS: [11743007][12618388][17272275][21504984]|). 
                     The RecF, RecO, and RecR proteins mediate the loading of 
                     RecA protein onto SSB-coated gapped DNA |CITS: [12769856]| 
                     (and further |CITS: [22902627]|).
                     Purified RecO binds non-specifically to both ssDNA and 
                     double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) and promotes the 
                     ATP-independent renaturation of two complementary ssDNA 
                     molecules |CITS: [8107098]|. Purified RecO catalyzes the 
                     assimilation of ss DNA into homologous superhelical 
                     double-stranded DNA in a reaction that requires 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup> and is ATP independent; over-expression of 
                     <i>recO</i> partially suppresses the UV damage repair 
                     deficiency of a <i>recA</i> null mutant |CITS: [7836288]|. 
                     Purified RecO mediates the annealing of complementary ssDNA
                     that is complexed with SSB; RecO may be the functional 
                     counterpart of eukaryotic Rad52 |CITS: [12438681]|.
                     DNA lesions that block replication fork progression induce 
                     a regressed structural intermediate that is maintained by 
                     RecA and RecFOR |CITS: [12543983][14625283]|. RecF, RecR, 
                     and RecO function together to maintain replication forks 
                     arrested by UV-induced DNA damage and limit degradation of 
                     the nascent lagging strand by |FRAME: EG10830-MONOMER RecJ|
                     and |FRAME: EG10833-MONOMER RecQ| |CITS: [14625283]|. 
                     RecFOR and RecOR may define two distinct RecA loading 
                     pathways in |FRAME: TAX-562| |CITS: [18986990]|. RecFOR is 
                     implicated in a mechanism for removal of a blocked leading 
                     strand polymerase; in reconstituted replication assays, 
                     RecF, RecO, and RecR, along with RecA, are able to 
                     dismantle stalled |FRAME: CPLX0-3803 Pol III| from 
                     SSB-bound DNA |CITS: [17609212]|
                     The RecF pathway can promote recombinational repair of 
                     dsDNA breaks, and joint molecule formation between linear 
                     dsDNA and a homologous supercoiled recipient has been 
                     reconstituted <i>in vitro</i> with purified proteins (RecA,
                     RecF, RecO, RecR, |FRAME: EG10830-MONOMER RecJ|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10833-MONOMER RecQ|, and SSB); omission of RecFOR 
                     abolishes joint molecule formation but use of the 
                     gain-of-function mutant RecA730 protein |CITS: [1400384]| 
                     significantly restores formation when RecFOR is absent 
                     |CITS: [19451222]|.
                     RecF and RecO may have distinct functions in reponse to DNA
                     damage; RecF colocalizes with replisomes both before and 
                     after UV treatment while RecO binds at sites that are 
                     spatially distinct from the replisome; the two proteins 
                     rarely colocalize |CITS: [30657965]|.
                     RecFOR plays an important role in |FRAME: EG10747-MONOMER 
                     Pol II|-dependent replication restart and |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3925 Pol V|-dependent translesion synthesis |CITS: 
                     [11929519]| (and further |CITS: [17139245][30482842]|). 
                     Mutations in <i>recF, recO</i> or <i>recR</i> delay 
                     induction of the SOS response following UV irradiation or 
                     treatment with mitomycin C |CITS: [7862097][7862088]|. 
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [2673532][1911953][8080625][796892 
                     1][11459953][17364684][26525148][37212693]|"
