     gene            complement(2697694..2698668)
                     /gene="lepB"
                     /locus_tag="b2568"
                     /label=lepB
     CDS             complement(2697694..2698668)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lepB"
                     /locus_tag="b2568"
                     /product="bifunctional signal peptidase I / leader
                     peptidase"
                     /EC_number="3.4.21.89"
                     /label=lepB
                     /note="Signal peptidase catalyzes the cleavage of the 
                     amino-terminal leader or signal peptide from 
                     membrane-tethered secretory pre-proteins |CITS: [2999144], 
                     [7837264]|. The action of signal peptidase releases the 
                     mature secretory protein into the periplasm and retains the
                     signal peptide in the membrane where it is further degraded
                     by the inner membrane protease |FRAME: EG12436-MONOMER 
                     ""RseP""|. Signal peptidase cleaves pre-proteins 
                     translocated by both the general Sec-system and the 
                     twin-arginine translocation (Tat) system |CITS: 
                     [19809807]|. Signal peptidase also cleaves the signal 
                     peptide from bacteriophage M13 procoat protein |CITS: 
                     [343108], [6995457], [6273848]|. Signal peptidase is 
                     required for import of the bacterial toxin, colicin D 
                     |CITS: [11511369][26499796]|
                     Signal peptidase has two N-terminal transmembrane segments 
                     separated by a small cytoplasmic domain and a large 
                     C-terminal catalytic domain that is located in the 
                     periplasm. The second transmembrane segment is believed to 
                     function as a non-cleavable signal sequence |CITS: 
                     [3036831], [3544218], [3308874], [2551889], [2202591]|. A 
                     soluble form of leader peptidase which lacks residues 2-76 
                     is catalytically active <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [8512314], 
                     [7696258], [11741964]| and has been crystallised on its own
                     and in complex with inhibitors |CITS: [8539246], [9823901],
                     [11741964], [15136583], [19655811], [21999324]|. The 
                     structure reveals a large hydrophobic region that  extends 
                     across the active site and may be embedded in the outer 
                     leaflet of the inner membrane thus creating a 'pocket' 
                     which optimises contact with signal peptides exposed on the
                     periplasmic surface of the inner membrane. LepB catalysis 
                     may utilise a Ser/Lys/Ser triad mechanism (reviewed in 
                     |CITS: [24333859]|).
                     <i>E. coli</i> LepB functions as a monomer and there are 
                     approximately 1000 molecules of LepB in each cell during 
                     exponential growth |CITS: [8747280]|. <i>lepB</i> is 
                     essential |CITS: [6339483], [2999144]|.
                     Purified LepB undergoes autocatalytic cleavage at the A38QA
                     / A41 site which is protected <i>in vivo</i> by its 
                     cytoplasmic location |CITS: [1755848]|.
                     Signal peptides in gram-negative bacteria have an average 
                     length of 25 residues and approximately 10% of genes in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> are thought to contain signal peptides 
                     (|CITS: [24333859]| and references therein). The substrate 
                     sequence requirements of signal peptidase have been 
                     investigated |CITS: [2243078], [1751494], [9545377], 
                     [2393890]| - they include conformation to the '-3,-1 rule' 
                     |CITS: [6852022], [6345794]| which dictates a preference 
                     for small residues at the -3 and -1 amino acid positions 
                     relative to the cleavage site.
                     Signal peptidase is considered to be a valuable target in 
                     the development of novel anti-microbial drugs (reviewed in 
                     |CITS: [22082289]|).
                     <i>lep</i>: <U>le</U>ader <U>p</U>eptidase
                     Reviews: |CITS: [1809829], [7845215], [2202719], 
                     [24333859]|<BR>Comments: |CITS: [9823886]|"
