     gene            complement(2698684..2700483)
                     /gene="lepA"
                     /locus_tag="b2569"
                     /label=lepA
     CDS             complement(2698684..2700483)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lepA"
                     /locus_tag="b2569"
                     /product="bifunctional 30S ribosomal subunit biogenesis
                     factor LepA / GTPase, ribosome-stimulated"
                     /EC_number="3.6.5.3"
                     /label=lepA
                     /note="EF4 (LepA) is a highly conserved protein whose
                     function in translation is still controversial.  The most 
                     recent results indicate that LepA functions in 30S 
                     ribosomal subunit biogenesis |CITS: [28096346]|.
                     Various experimental results that suggested functions in 
                     translation initiation or elongation are summarized below. 
                     EF4 competes with EF-G for binding to the ribosome and 
                     forms a complex that is an intermediate between the PRE and
                     POST complexes, thus sequestering a catalytically active 
                     ribosome |CITS: [21930951]|.  EF4 induces 
                     back-translocation of the ribosome and prevents peptide 
                     bond formation exclusively in the post-translocation (POST)
                     state of the ribosome.  A kinetic model of 
                     back-translocation has been proposed |CITS: 
                     [17110332][20045415]|. Cryo-EM structures of the ribosome 
                     in pre- and post-EF4 states suggest that EF4 enables 
                     back-translocation by disengaging the CCA end of tRNA from 
                     the peptidyl transferase center of the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [26809121]|.  A mechanism for EF4-induced 
                     back-translocation was proposed |CITS: [27472653]|.  The 
                     back-translocation activity of EF4 could not be reproduced 
                     by |CITS: [25378333]|.
                     EF4 increases the rate of protein synthesis under certain 
                     stress conditions |CITS: [21300907]|.  It was recently 
                     shown that even in unstressed cells, loss of EF4 reduces 
                     the average ribosome density of many coding regions |CITS: 
                     [25378333]|.  EF4 is detrimental during severe stress 
                     conditions; this effect requires functional |FRAME: 
                     SSRA-RNA| and |FRAME: CPLX0-3101| |CITS: [25491353]|.
                     EF4 has uncoupled ribosome-dependent GTPase activity, and 
                     GTP hydrolysis appears to be required for dissociation of 
                     EF4 from the ribosome.  Addition of EF4 to a coupled <i>in 
                     vitro</i> transcription-translation system increases the 
                     total protein yield and improves the activity of the 
                     produced proteins.  A model was proposed in which EF4 
                     recognizes defective translocation states of the ribosome 
                     and induces back-translocation, which allows EF-G a second 
                     chance to catalyze the formation of a correct POST state 
                     |CITS: [17110332]|.  While EF4 does not increase the 
                     fidelity of protein synthesis <i>in vivo</i> or <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [19925844][21300907]|, it does increase 
                     the rate of protein synthesis at high salt concentrations 
                     or low temperature |CITS: [21300907]|.  The H81 residue is 
                     required for GTPase activity |CITS: [25712150]|.
                     The macrocyclic thiopeptide antibiotic thiostrepton 
                     inhibits binding of EF4 to the ribosome as well as 
                     ribosome-dependent GTP hydrolysis |CITS: [21908407]|.
                     EF4 is an extremely highly conserved protein in all domains
                     of life.  It also shows high conservation with EF-G, 
                     although a domain similar to domain IV of EF-G is absent, 
                     and an additional C-terminal domain is present |CITS: 
                     [17110332]|.  EF4/EF-G chimeric proteins do not complement 
                     EF-G function <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [7596295]|. A crystal 
                     structure of EF4 has been solved at 2.8 &Aring; resolution.
                      Structural similarity between EF4 and EF-G enabled 
                     modelling of EF4 bound to the ribosome, which places the 
                     unique C-terminal domain of EF4 in the vicinity of the A 
                     site of the 50S subunit |CITS: [18362332]|. Single-particle
                     cryo-EM was used to visualize EF4 in the process of 
                     back-translocation, where it is seen to interact directly 
                     with the back-translocated tRNA |CITS: [19172743]|.  A 
                     crystal structure of EF4 bound to <i>Thermus 
                     thermophilus</i> 70S ribosome has been solved, showing that
                     the C-terminal domain of EF4 interacts with the acceptor 
                     stem of the peptidyl-tRNA in the P site |CITS: [25104389]|.
                      The C-terminal domain is important for ribosome-stimulated
                     GTPase activity of EF4 |CITS: [25712150]|.
                     Association and dissociation rate and equilibrium binding 
                     constants for GDP and GTP have been measured, showing that 
                     EF4 has a 5-fold higher affinity for GDP than for GTP 
                     |CITS: [25712150]|.  Association of EF4 with the ribosome 
                     does not absolutely require the L7/L12 subunit |CITS: 
                     [28342293]|.  EF4 was found to bind (p)ppGpp |CITS: 
                     [29511080]|.
                     Expression analysis has shown that the <i>lepA</i> gene is 
                     a member of the <i>lep</i> operon along with the downstream
                     gene, <i>lepB</i>, which encodes signal peptidase I |CITS: 
                     [2987248]|.  In subcellular fractionation studies, EF4 was 
                     detected in the inner membrane fraction |CITS: 
                     [2987248][2999765][21300907]|. However, under conditions of
                     salt stress, low temperature or low pH, the majority of EF4
                     protein is released to the cytoplasm |CITS: [21300907]|.
                     <i>lepA</i> deletion mutants are viable and show no obvious
                     defect in growth or protein export |CITS: [3514582]| and do
                     not have a significant ribosome assembly defect |CITS: 
                     [25777676]|.  However, overproduction of EF4 is lethal on 
                     solid agar, and cells become filamentous in liquid cultures
                     after induction |CITS: [2987248][17110332]|.  A <i>lepA</i>
                     deletion mutant was found to be hypersensitive to potassium
                     tellurite, but not to other oxidizing agents |CITS: 
                     [19925844]|.  Recently, it was shown that a <i>lepA</i> 
                     null mutant is outcompeted by wild type in growth 
                     competition experiments in minimal media at low 
                     temperature, high salt concentrations or low pH |CITS: 
                     [21300907]|, and that it has a growth defect at pH 4 |CITS:
                     [24706296]|.  A &Delta;<i>lepA</i> mutant has synthetic 
                     growth phenotypes with &Delta;<i>dksA</i>, 
                     &Delta;<i>molR1</i>, &Delta;<i>rsgA</i>, 
                     &Delta;<i>tatB</i>, &Delta;<i>tonB</i>, &Delta;<i>tolR</i>,
                     &Delta;<i>ubiF</i>, &Delta;<i>ubiG</i>, and 
                     &Delta;<i>ubiH</i> |CITS: [25378333]|.  A 
                     &Delta;<i>lepA</i> mutation protects against lethal 
                     antimicrobials under aerobic growth conditions; <i>lepA</i>
                     and <i>mazF</i> are epistatic |CITS: [25491353]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [29235176][25135187][23662805][21885683][2 
                     1675921][17307049][11489118]|
                     |CITS: [29784847]| has been retracted because the effect 
                     was found to be strain-specific |CITS: [LIU2019]|."
