     gene            complement(2702796..2703371)
                     /gene="rpoE"
                     /locus_tag="b2573"
                     /label=rpoE
     CDS             complement(2702796..2703371)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpoE"
                     /locus_tag="b2573"
                     /product="bifunctional RNA polymerase sigma factor RpoE /
                     RNA polymerase sigma 24"
                     /label=rpoE
                     /note="Sigma E is a minor sigma factor, specializing in 
                     responses to the effects of heat shock and other stresses 
                     on membrane and periplasmic proteins. It is regulated by a 
                     multistep protease system that sense disruptions in those 
                     proteins. Its transcriptional initiation targets include 
                     another minor sigma factor, Sigma 32.
                     Sigma E drives transcription of a number of genes whose 
                     functions revolve around heat shock and misfolded proteins.
                     Notably, Sigma E is induced by heat shock and is required 
                     for heat-induced transcription of <i>rpoH</i>, which codes 
                     for the heat-shock factor Sigma 32 |CITS: 
                     [12670975][3315851][2546916]|. Sigma E is also required for
                     transcription of <i>degP</i>, which codes for a protease 
                     that degrades abnormal proteins in the periplasm |CITS: 
                     [3057437][2691330][7751307]|. Both <i>rpoH</i> and 
                     <i>degP</i> are induced at high temperature |CITS: 
                     [2691330]|. Sigma E also initiates transcription of the 
                     peptidyl-prolyl cis-trans isomerase FkpA, which also acts 
                     as a chaperone in the periplasm |CITS: [9159399]|. RpoE is 
                     a positive regulator of the prophage DLP12 lysis genes 
                     (<i>essD</i>p) |CITS: [25998262]|. In addition to these 
                     specific genes, a number of additional targets for Sigma E 
                     have been identified |CITS: 
                     [11274153][12900013][16336047]|.
                     A number of cellular stresses other than heat shock also 
                     induce  &sigma;<sup>E</sup>. &sigma;<sup>E</sup> is induced
                     in response to increased expression of as well as 
                     misfolding of outer membrane proteins (OMPs) |CITS: 
                     [8276244]|. Other inducers include hyperosmotic shock, 
                     metal ion exposure, and changes in the outer membrane 
                     lipopolysaccharide (LPS) structure |CITS: 
                     [10594827][15774872][15720549]|. &sigma;<sup>E</sup> 
                     regulates extracytoplasmic functions including LPS 
                     translocation and some of its nonstoichiometric 
                     modifications |CITS:[27629414]|. &sigma;<sup>E</sup> is 
                     also induced by the starvation signal ppGpp on entry into 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [16788171]|. <i>rpoE</i> is a 
                     positive regulator of polyP synthesis upon nutrient 
                     limitation |CITS: [32341074][35482529]|. A novel pathway in
                     cell lysis has been identified on the &sigma;<sup>E</sup> 
                     regulon;  this pathway proceeds in a cascade that entails 
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup>  --> expression of <i>micA</i> and 
                     <i>rybB</i> small RNAs --> reduction in Omp proteins --> 
                     disintegration of the outer membrane |CITS: [22251246]|.
                     Under ampicillin treatment, the activity of 
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup> appears to increase |CITS: [33731833]|.
                     <i>rpoE</i> expression is regulated by a multistep system 
                     that allows increased transcription in response to 
                     unassembled or misfolded proteins, in addition to 
                     initiating its own transcription at at least one of its 
                     promoters |CITS: [7889934][7889935]|. Binding of 
                     unassembled OMPs to the protease DegS stimulate its 
                     cleavage of the regulatory protein RseA |CITS: 
                     [12679035][11442831]|. The destabilized RseA is then 
                     degraded by YaeL |CITS: [12183368]|. As RseA normally 
                     tethers Sigma E to the membrane with the aid of RseB, its 
                     degradation releases Sigma E to allow it to interact with 
                     |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX| and initiate transcription |CITS: [1 
                     2016219][12670975][11777003][9159522][9159523][11442831]|. 
                     The envelope stress sigma factor &sigma;<sup>E</sup> (RpoE)
                     becomes non-essential when both cardiolipin synthesis and 
                     the major porin OmpC are absent |CITS:[40823832]|. The loss
                     of cardiolipin reduces Sec translocon activity, decreasing 
                     the translocation and misfolding of outer membrane proteins
                     in the periplasm and thus suppressing the need for the  
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup> stress response |CITS:[40823832]|.
                     Under stress, Sigma E induces the mutagenic repair of DNA 
                     breaks through a network genes that function in the 
                     activation of Sigma E. The genes that appear to be involved
                     in the activation of Sigma E are <i>arcA </i>, <i>lrp</i>, 
                     <i>trxB</i>, <i>fre</i>, <i>pstB</i>,<i>ubiX</i>, 
                     <i>znuC</i>, <i>ubiD</i>, <i>phoU</i>, <i>lipB</i>, 
                     <i>sdhD</i>, <i>fruR</i>, <i>pstC</i>, <i>cyoD</i>, 
                     <i>cyoA</i>, <i>apaH</i>, <i>sdhB</i>, <i>mdh</i>, 
                     <i>yigP</i>, <i>ygfA</i>, <i>nuoK</i>, <i>hemL</i>, 
                     <i>nuoG</i>, <i>lon</i>, <i>rppH</i>, <i>nuoH</i>, 
                     <i>ubiH</i>, <i>nuoL</i>, <i>nuoC</i>, <i>nuoJ</i>, 
                     <i>ubiE</i>, and <i>ubiA</i> |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     A structure of Sigme E complexed with RseA to 2 &Aring; 
                     resolution shows that RseA sterically blocks surfaces 
                     required for binding to |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX| |CITS: 
                     [12718891]|.
                     Sigma E is required for resistance to zinc, cadmium and 
                     copper, as well as for growth at high temperature |CITS: 
                     [15774872][7889934][7751307]|. It is also required for 
                     growth at normal temperature, but an unlinked suppressor 
                     mutation almost always occurs to allow such growth when 
                     <i>rpoE</i> function is lost |CITS: [9352942]|. 
                     Overexpression of Sigma E appears to prompt lysis of dead 
                     cells during stationary phase |CITS: [10960110]|. Sigma 
                     E-dependent gene expression during stationary phase has 
                     been studied |CITS: [16079349]|.
                     Based on 60 known &sigma;<sup>E</sup> core promoters, a 
                     modular position weight matrix (PWM) model was developed to
                     predict promoter strength and distinguish between active 
                     and inactive &sigma;<sup>E</sup>, and this model is 
                     applicable to both <i>in vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> 
                     measures of promoter strength. The combined  -35/-10 score 
                     is the most important predictor of activity. Additionally, 
                     a conserved ""AAC"" motif in the -35 region is likely to be
                     a general predictor of function for promoters recognizedby 
                     group 4 &sigma;s |CITS:[20133665]|.
                     The structure of the complex formed by &sigma;<sup>E</sup> 
                     and its cognate -10 promoter has been solved by NMR 
                     spectroscopy and X-ray crystallography |CITS:[24531660]|. 
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup> recognizes the 5-G-T-C-N-N-N7-3 motif 
                     (where C is -10, numbered with respect to the transcription
                     start site). Sequence-specific recognition of C at -10 is 
                     exclusively mediated by a dynamic and variable protein loop
                     that triggers specific recognition of the inverted 
                     nucleotide among group IV &sigma; factors |CITS: 
                     [24531660]|. Deletion of the &sigma; finger of 
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup>, formed by the linker between domains 2
                     and 4, decreases the RNAP holoenzyme activity <i>in 
                     vitro</i>, increasing the stability of the promoter complex
                     |CITS: [33573428]|. However, this deletion makes the 
                     promoter complex less sensitive to the stringent response 
                     factor DksA in presence of ppGpp |CITS: [33573428]|.
                     It was reported that RNAP holoenzyme containing RpoE 
                     &sigma; factor can incorporate NADH during transcription 
                     initiation |CITS: [33573428]|.
                     SigmaE is able to induce pauses during transcription 
                     elongation in vitro when it binds to the -10-like element 
                     |CITS: [31228934]|.
                     The first sequence-based model of a full-length bacterial 
                     promoter was proposed by Rhodius et al. in 2010 |CITS: 
                     [20133665]|. They built the model based on 60 promoters 
                     that are &sigma;<sup>E</sup> dependent and  which include 
                     UP elements and core promoter sequences.  The model 
                     identifies the element determinants of the promoter 
                     sequence that correlate with promoter strength and uses 
                     these features to establish a predictive model for promoter
                     strength. The role of the UP elements in promoter strength 
                     depends both on RNAP levels and properties of the core 
                     promoter |CITS: [22156164]|. The model can also distinguish
                     active from weak-inactive promoters |CITS: [22156164]|.
                     Several growth conditions affect the transcription of the 
                     <i>rpoE</i> promoter, such as defects in LPS, the addition 
                     of ammonium metavanadate, entry into the stationary phase, 
                     shifts to high osmolarity, growth phase-dependent challenge
                     with polymyxin B, a shift to nitrogen-limiting growth 
                     conditions, overexpression of certain genes encoding 
                     lipoproteins, and repression in glucose-supplemented medium
                     (catabolite repression) |CITS:[27629414]|, confirming what 
                     has been reported from other studies, where <i>rpoE</i> 
                     activity was induced by stresses such as exposure to high 
                     osmolarity, cold shock conditions, and entry into the 
                     stationary phase |CITS:[11274153][10594827]|.
                     Multiple transcription factors control the expression of 
                     the <i>rpoE</i> gene, in addition to the RpoS, RpoD, and 
                     RpoN sigma factors, which specifically respond to a variety
                     of stimuli and cell envelope defects, including LPS 
                     alterations, and allow linking of different 
                     stress-responsive pathways  |CITS:[27629414]|.
                     Based on a biophysics <i>in vitro</i> model of 
                     transcription, it was shown that, like RpoD 
                     (&sigma;<sup>70</sup>), RpoE (&sigma;<sup>E</sup>, 
                     &sigma;<sup>24</sup>) exhibits a kinetic mix-and-match 
                     mechanism where the promoter elements complement each 
                     others binding strengths in achieving sufficient 
                     transcription activity |CITS:[28251873]|.
                     More than 300 proteins modulate production of curli, which 
                     is involved in fundamental cellular processes such as 
                     regulation, environmental sensing, respiration, metabolism,
                     cell envelope biogenesis, transport, and protein turnover, 
                     whereas the sigma factors, &sigma;<sup>S</sup> and 
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup>, have opposing roles in curli 
                     production and the related processes |CITS:[29088115]|. 
                     Mutations that induce the &sigma;<sup>E</sup> or Cpx stress
                     response system reduce curli production; on the other hand,
                     mutant strains with increased &sigma;<sup>S</sup> levels 
                     show increased curli production. Curli fiber formation is 
                     tightly linked to key metabolic pathways, including 
                     nucleotide synthesis, cell envelope maintenance, and the 
                     citric acid cycle |CITS:[29088115]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [23479440][27789741][35658528][37010795][36972330]|."
