     gene            2703779..2705401
                     /gene="nadB"
                     /locus_tag="b2574"
                     /label=nadB
     CDS             2703779..2705401
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nadB"
                     /locus_tag="b2574"
                     /product="L-aspartate oxidase"
                     /EC_number="1.4.3.16"
                     /label=nadB
                     /note="L-aspartate oxidase (NadB) is the first enzyme in
                     the <i>de novo</i> |FRAME: PYRIDNUCSYN-PWY| pathway, 
                     catalyzing the FAD-dependent oxidation of L-aspartate to 
                     iminoaspartate |CITS: [8706749]|.  NadB activity is 
                     required for NAD biosynthesis under both aerobic and 
                     anaerobic conditions |CITS: [12200425]|; fumarate is the 
                     more efficient electron acceptor and is used under 
                     anaerobic conditions, while oxygen appears to be the 
                     predominant electron acceptor under aerobic conditions due 
                     to the lower fumarate levels |CITS: [20149100]|.
                     The enzyme can exist in monomeric and dimeric form 
                     depending on pH |CITS: [10103021][10425677]|.  Crystal 
                     structures of the apo- and holoenzyme have been determined,
                     suggesting that an unusual tertiary structure is shared by 
                     L-aspartate oxidase, the SdhA subunit of succinate 
                     dehydrogenase, and the FrdA subunit of fumarate reductase 
                     |CITS: [10425677][11863440]|.  NadB may have evolved from 
                     succinate dehydrogenase/fumarate reductase-type enzymes 
                     |CITS: [28700220]|.
                     NadB is a predominant source of hydrogen peroxide under 
                     aerobic conditions |CITS: [20149100]|.
                     Amino acid residues important for enzymatic activity have 
                     been identified by site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [8706749][11294641]|.  The E121 residue is essential and 
                     thought to be required for orienting the L-aspartate 
                     substrate in a productive binding mode; mutants lose 
                     L-aspartate oxidase activity, but retain fumarate reductase
                     activity |CITS: [20600565]|.  Measurement of various 
                     kinetic isotope effects elucidated the catalytic mechanism 
                     of NadB.  Similar to typical amino acid oxidases, 
                     deprotonation and hydride transfer appear to occur in a 
                     concerted manner, where the hydride transfer from the C2 of
                     L-aspartate to FAD is rate-limiting |CITS: [28700220]|.
                     The fumarate reductase activity of NadB can provide 
                     succinate for growth of a &Delta; <i>lipB</i> 
                     &Delta;<i>sdhB</i> strain |CITS: [25303731]|.
                     NadB: ""<b>NAD</b> biosynthesis"" |CITS: [4315893]| Review:
                     |CITS: [26443758]|"
