     gene            complement(2709425..2709808)
                     /gene="grcA"
                     /locus_tag="b2579"
                     /label=grcA
     CDS             complement(2709425..2709808)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="grcA"
                     /locus_tag="b2579"
                     /product="multifunctional stress-induced alternate
                     pyruvate formate-lyase subunit / PFL-GrcA complex / 
                     2-oxobutanoate formate-lyase / pyruvate formate-lyase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.54"
                     /label=grcA
                     /note="GrcA is a small (14 kDa) glycyl radical enzyme
                     (GRE) that can restore activity of oxygen-damaged |FRAME: 
                     PYRUVFORMLY-CPLX pyruvate formate-lyase| (PFL). The 
                     relatively stable glycyl radical of PFL, which is essential
                     for catalysis, is sensitive to |FRAME: OXYGEN-MOLECULE| 
                     |CITS: [6369325][1310545][11300793]|. Destruction of the 
                     glycyl radical by oxygen results in cleavage of the 85 kDa 
                     polypeptide to 83 kDa and a 3 kDa fragment and consequent 
                     inactivation of the enzyme |CITS: [1310545]|.
                     A solution structure of GrcA shows a flexible N-terminal 
                     domain followed by a C-terminal region with high amino acid
                     sequence similarity to the glycyl radical domain of PFL 
                     |CITS: [31250200]|. GrcA forms a complex with 
                     oxygen-damaged PFL enzyme |CITS: [11444864][34801558]| and 
                     displaces the damaged C-terminal glycyl radical domain 
                     |CITS: [35377165]|. After activation by |FRAME: 
                     PFLACTENZ-MONOMER| (PflA), the PFL-GrcA complex has full 
                     pyruvate-formate lyase activity both <i>in vitro</i> and 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [11444864][35377165]|. In an 
                     <i>arcA</i> mutant under microaerobic conditions, GrcA 
                     contributes 18% of the flux through the pyruvate 
                     formate-lyase reaction |CITS: [17013945]|.
                     Residue Gly102 is predicted to be the glycyl radical site 
                     in GrcA and is necessary for restoring PflB activity |CITS:
                     [11444864][35377165]|. Lower intracellular pH increases 
                     both <i>grcA</i> expression and the extent of glycyl 
                     radical formation by PflA. |FRAME: ADHE-CPLX AdhE| does not
                     appear to deactivate GrcA |CITS: [11932447]|. GrcA is 
                     phosphorylated in L-form (wall-less) <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [9884220]|. Using a method that distinguishes 
                     N-phosphorylation from O-phosphorylation, two 
                     N-phosphorylation sites were identified in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     strain K-12 <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [33277485][35636362]|.
                     Unlike most other cellular proteins, synthesis of GrcA is 
                     slightly increased after induction of the toxin |FRAME: 
                     EG11249-MONOMER MazF| |CITS: [19282968]|. <i>grcA</i> mRNA 
                     is cleaved by the MazF endoribonuclease, generating a 
                     leaderless mRNA that is preferentially translated by the 
                     ""stress-ribosome"". This specialized form of the ribosome 
                     is generated by MazF cleavage of the 16S rRNA, removing its
                     anti-Shine-Dalgarno sequence |CITS: [21944167][26908653]|. 
                     <i>grcA</i> mRNA processing by MazF promotes heterogeneous 
                     expression of GrcA |CITS: [28948108]|.
                     A <i>grcA</i> mutant shows a defect in acid homeostasis 
                     under low-oxygen conditions |CITS: [11932447]|. Under 
                     conditions leading to inhibition of translation or DNA 
                     damage, most of the population of a <i>grcA</i> mutant 
                     survives, phenocopying a &Delta;<i>mazEF</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [19282968]|.
                     Regulation of <i>grcA</i> expression under a variety of 
                     conditions has been described |CITS: [9179852][9767578][10 
                     094700][10726772][11114930][11169114][11591692][11932447][1
                     2107143][12949096][15988767][16223443][20545747][21784905][
                     23274360]|. A proteoform of GrcA was found to be 
                     significantly more abundant when grown in glucose as 
                     compared to acetate based on a new quantitative top-down 
                     proteomics method using iodo-tandem mass tags |CITS: 
                     [39885717]|.
                     GrcA: ""<b>g</b>lycine <b>r</b>adical <b>c</b>ofactor, 
                     <b>a</b>utonomous""
                     Comment: |CITS: [22064559]|"
                     /translation="MITGIQITKAANDDLLNSFWLLDSEKGEARCIVAKAGYAEDEVVA
                     VSKLGDIEYREVPVEVKPEVRVEGGQHLNVNVLRRETLEDAVKHPEKYPQLTIRVSGYA
                     VRFNSLTPEQQRDVIARTFTESL"
