     gene            2710113..2710802
                     /gene="ung"
                     /locus_tag="b2580"
                     /label=ung
     CDS             2710113..2710802
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ung"
                     /locus_tag="b2580"
                     /product="uracil-DNA glycosylase"
                     /EC_number="3.2.2.27"
                     /label=ung
                     /note="<i>ung</i> encoded uracil-DNA glycosylase (Ung/UDG)
                     is a base-excision repair (BER) glycosylase which catalyses
                     the removal of uracil in both single-stranded and 
                     double-stranded DNA |CITS: [4610583][350837]| (also |CITS: 
                     [765833][365859]|); Ung hydrolyses the N-glycosylic bond 
                     between uracil and deoxyribose releasing free uracil and 
                     leaving an apurinic/apyrimidinic (AP) site in the DNA 
                     |CITS: [324994]| which is subject to further processing 
                     (see |CITS: [10946230]|).
                     Ung deficient mutants (<i>ung-1</i> |CITS: [350837]|) have 
                     an increased rate of spontaneous G:C &rarr; A:T mutation 
                     |CITS: [7047496]| (also |CITS: [3312959]|). Purified Ung 
                     excises the pyrimidine analogue and chemotherapy drug, 
                     5-fluorouracil, from DNA |CITS: [8316218]|; purified Ung 
                     excises 5-hydroxyuracil from DNA |CITS: [8034633]|; 
                     purified Ung excises isodialuric acid - a major cytosine 
                     oxidation product - from DNA |CITS: [7758578]|.
                     Ung activity is inhibited after phage T5 infection |CITS: 
                     [6245250]| (and see |CITS: [34074772]|); Ung is inhibited 
                     by the <i>Bacillus subtilis</i> phage PBS-1 and PBS-2 
                     encoded inhibitor protein, Ugi |CITS: 
                     [2492016][1902430][1429601][9261156]|. The crystal stucture
                     of Ung complexed with the inhibitor protein from PBS-2 was 
                     the first bacterial UDG structure analysed |CITS: 
                     [9776748]|; Ugi functions as an imperfect DNA mimic and 
                     entraps UDG |CITS: [10080896]|. Crystal structures of Ung 
                     in its free state (purified from <i>E. coli</i> B), and in 
                     complex with the inhibitors uracil and glycerol are also 
                     available |CITS: [10090282]|.
                     Ung-catalysed uracil excision involves a base-flipping 
                     mechanism |CITS: [9893991][11551943][12220189][12220190]| 
                     (see also |CITS: 
                     [15558051][15065862][15835884][17017766][17704764]|). The 
                     role of highly conserved active site residues Asp-64 and 
                     His-187 in glycosidic bond cleavage has been investigated 
                     |CITS: [10508389][10508390][11009598][18652484]| as has the
                     mechanism by which Ung locates uracil residues within DNA 
                     |CITS: [7687003][7742315][18669665][21971549]| (and see 
                     |CITS: [19909758][20469926]|).
                     Ung physically interacts with |FRAME: 
                     NUCLEOSIDE-DIP-KIN-CPLX| (Ndk) <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in 
                     vivo</i>; Ndk stimulates Ung-mediated uracil excision <i>in
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [16895920]|.
                     <i>ung</i>: <U>u</U>racil-DNA <U>N</U>-<U>g</U>lycosylase: 
                     |CITS: [350837]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [392601][10946228][10946227]|
                     "
