     CDS             2712094..2712513
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="trxC"
                     /locus_tag="b2582"
                     /product="thioredoxin 2"
                     /label=trxC
                     /note="Thioredoxins are small electron transfer proteins
                     that contain a cysteine disulfide/dithiol active site with 
                     the amino acid sequence motif Cys-X-X-Cys (where X is any 
                     amino acid).  They are members of the thioredoxin protein 
                     family.  Members of this family/superfamily contain the 
                     thioredoxin fold, a characteristic and stable protein fold 
                     consisting of four &beta;-sheets surrounded by three 
                     &alpha;-helices (reviewed in |CITS: [17008712]|).  The 
                     proteins function in a wide variety of cellular processes. 
                     Thioredoxin is reduced by NADPH in a reaction catalyzed by 
                     thioredoxin reductase. The conversion between the oxidized 
                     and reduced forms results in a subtle change of 
                     conformation.  The functional properties differ between the
                     two forms of thioredoxin.  The reduced form of thioredoxin 
                     is a protein disulfide reductase, and catalyzes 
                     dithiol-disulfide exchange reactions. <i>Escherichia 
                     coli</i> thioredoxin contains the active site amino acid 
                     sequence Trp-Cys-Gly-Pro-Cys.  In |CITS: 
                     [7812718][2187529][2193685][8098620][2181145][6378624]| and
                     reviewed in |CITS: [3896121] [3152490]|. <i>Escherichia 
                     coli</i> has two thioredoxins: |FRAME: 
                     RED-THIOREDOXIN-MONOMER ""thioredoxin 1""|, encoded by the 
                     |FRAME: EG11031| gene, and |FRAME: RED-THIOREDOXIN2-MONOMER
                     ""thioredoxin 2""|, encoded by the |FRAME: EG11887| gene.
                     Reduced thioredoxin 2 can perform many of the <i>in 
                     vivo</i> roles of |FRAME: RED-THIOREDOXIN-MONOMER 
                     ""thioredoxin 1""| and is able to reduce some essential 
                     cytoplasmic enzymes.  Along with thioredoxin 1 and 
                     glutaredoxin 1, thioredoxin 2 was reported to be one of 
                     <i>E. coli</i>'s most effective cytoplasmic 
                     disulfide-reducing proteins.  Any one of these three was 
                     found to be sufficient to support aerobic growth |CITS: 
                     [9755155] [9388228]|.
                     An experiment using the fluorescent disulfide probe |FRAME:
                     CPD-26606| to measure thioredoxin and glutaredoxin reducing
                     activity <i>in vivo</i> concluded that the thioredoxin 
                     system was the predominant disulfide reductase for fast 
                     disulfide reduction in |FRAME: TAX-562| |CITS: [35204259]|.
                     Contrary to these findings, an experiment utilizing a 
                     different probe, |FRAME: CPD-26605|, concluded that Grx2 
                     and Grx3 exhibited higher activity in reducing the 
                     disulfide probe than the other redoxins (Grx1 and 
                     thioredoxins 1 and 2) |CITS: [35107099]|.
                     Thioredoxin 2 (Trx2) is a member of the Trx2 subfamily of 
                     the larger TrxA protein family.  Thioredoxin 2 differs from
                     thioredoxin 1 in having a non-conserved N-terminal domain 
                     that contains two additional CXXC motifs and zinc bound 
                     with high affinity in a 1:1 stoichiometry.  These four 
                     cysteine residues are involved in coordination of the zinc 
                     atom.  The exact function of the zinc center is unknown, 
                     although it might have a role as a redox switch. However, 
                     thioredoxin 2 is similar to thioredoxin 1 in having a 
                     conserved pair of cysteine residues present in a WCGPC 
                     motif at the active site, capable of reducing disulfide 
                     bonds (|CITS: [12952960]|).
                     Transcriptional regulation of Trx2 is also different.  Its 
                     expression is regulated by binding of the transcriptional 
                     activator OxyR to the <i>trxC</i> promoter, inducing its 
                     expression during oxidative stress |CITS: [10644706]|.  A 
                     study of the unique characteristics of Trx2 suggested a 
                     role for it under oxidative stress conditions.  These 
                     included the finding that Trx2 is more oxidizing that Trx1 
                     (it has a higher redox potential) implying different 
                     substrate specificity, and that the N-terminal zinc center 
                     affects its redox and thermodynamic properties.  In 
                     addition, Trx2 was shown to participate in the formation of
                     as yet undefined high molecular mass complexes during 
                     oxidative stress |CITS: [19073194]|.
                     Protein levels of thioredoxins 1 and 2 and glutaredoxins 1,
                     2 and 3 have been determined using an ELISA assay under 
                     different conditions, helping to define their functional 
                     roles |CITS: [11893749]|."
