     gene            complement(2728390..2729370)
                     /gene="rluD"
                     /locus_tag="b2594"
                     /label=rluD
     CDS             complement(2728390..2729370)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rluD"
                     /locus_tag="b2594"
                     /product="23S rRNA pseudouridine(1911/1915/1917) synthase"
                     /EC_number="5.4.99.23"
                     /label=rluD
                     /note="RluD is a pseudouridine synthase that catalyzes 
                     pseudouridine formation at positions 1911, 1915, and 1917 
                     in helix 69 (H69) of the 23S rRNA |CITS: 
                     [9814761][9843401][11087118][17937767]|.  23S rRNA 
                     modification by RluD occurs in the late assembly phase of 
                     the 50S subunit and 70S ribosome |CITS: 
                     [20719918][24422502]|.  RluD activity is involved in the 
                     resuscitation of persister cells |CITS: [31608580]|. It may
                     also play a role in releasing the |FRAME: EG11178-MONOMER| 
                     from the ribosomal 30S subunit and thus contributing to 
                     fidelity of the Initiator tRNA<sup>Met</sup> selection in 
                     the P-site of the |FRAME: CPLX0-3964| |CITS: [35439479]|.
                     RluD is most active with assembled 50S ribosomal subunits; 
                     pseudouridylation of free 23S rRNA is inefficient |CITS: 
                     [17872507]|.  The A1916 base of 23S rRNA is an important 
                     specificity determinant |CITS: [17937767]|.  There appear 
                     to be different specificity determinants for uridines at 
                     different positions of H69.  The enzyme shows more 
                     stringent specificity <i>in vivo</i> than <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [22586702]|.  Cryo-EM reconstructions of 50S 
                     ribosomal subunit precursors revealed a network of 
                     interacting ribosomal biogenesis factors that consists of 
                     RluD, RsfS, YjgA, and ObgE |CITS: [33639093]|.
                     Crystal structures of the catalytic domain of RluD have 
                     been solved at 2.0 and 1.7 &Aring; resolution |CITS: 
                     [14501142][14730022][15078091][17668295]|.  A crystal 
                     structure of RluD from <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7 has been 
                     solved at 1.8 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [14659742]|.
                     An <i>rluD</i> null mutation is reported to cause lethality
                     |CITS: [9743119]| or a large growth defect |CITS: 
                     [9814761][9843401][15928344]|.  Initial experiments 
                     complementing an <i>rluD</i> mutant with catalytically 
                     inactive <i>rluD</i> alleles seemed to indicate that lack 
                     of pseudouridylation was not responsible for the severe 
                     growth defect of an <i>rluD</i> mutant |CITS: [11453071]|. 
                     However, the interpretation of those results was 
                     complicated by the use of a truncated <i>rluD</i> gene. 
                     Later experiments using an <i>rluD</i> deletion strain 
                     showed that the loss of pseudouridylation at positions 
                     1911, 1915, and 1917 in 23S rRNA leads to a defect in 
                     ribosome assembly, biogenesis and function |CITS: 
                     [15928344]|.  A mutation that suppresses the growth defect 
                     of an <i>rluD</i> mutant localizes to <i>prfB</i>, which 
                     encodes |FRAME: EG10762-MONOMER| |CITS: [18032607]|. Recent
                     experiments show that the severe mutant phenotype resulting
                     from inactivation of <i>rluD</i> is the result of genetic 
                     interactions with particular alleles of RF2 (containing 
                     Thr246 in place of an alanine residue) |CITS: 
                     [21037010][21219455]| and |FRAME: EG10906-MONOMER S7| 
                     |CITS: [21219455]| that are present in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     strains.
                     In the <i>E. coli</i> K-12 background, at least one of the 
                     three pseudouridine bases appears to have a function in 
                     termination of translation |CITS: [18032607]|.  In an <i>in
                     vitro</i> system, lack of the three pseudouridines in 23S 
                     rRNA decreased the activity of RF2 |CITS: [21281690]|.
                     An <i>rluD</i> deletion enhances the slow growth phenotype 
                     of an <i>rsgA</i> mutation |CITS: [18223068]| and reduces 
                     the frequency of single-cell persister resuscitation |CITS:
                     [31608580]|.  Overproduction of RluD increases the 
                     frequency of waking and resuscitation of persister cells by
                     BPOET |CITS: [31608580]|.
                     An E265K point mutation in the C-terminal tail domain 
                     allows for initiation of translation with a 3GC i-tRNA 
                     mutant and increased retention of |FRAME: EG11178-MONOMER|,
                     similar to the role that |FRAME: G7841-MONOMER| plays. This
                     mutation does not affect the pseudouridine synthase 
                     activity of RluD, cause any defects in the ribosomal 
                     assembly, or affect growth of <i>E. coli</i> <i>in vivo</i>
                     |CITS: [35439479]|.
                     SfhB: ""suppressor of ftsH mutation"" |CITS: [7958944]| 
                     RluD: ""<b>r</b>ibosomal <b>l</b>arge subunit 
                     pseudo<b>u</b>ridine synthase <b>D</b>"" |CITS: [9814761]| 
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [12762017][17113994][17085441][21219451][25616362]|"
