     gene            2729505..2730242
                     /gene="bamD"
                     /locus_tag="b2595"
                     /label=bamD
     CDS             2729505..2730242
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="bamD"
                     /locus_tag="b2595"
                     /product="bifunctional outer membrane protein assembly
                     factor BamD / outer membrane protein assembly complex"
                     /label=bamD
                     /note="The BamD lipoprotein is part of the large
                     multi-protein BAM complex responsible for the assembly and 
                     insertion of outer membrane &beta;-barrel proteins.
                     BamD is an essential outer membrane lipoprotein 
                     |CITS:[15968066],[15851030]|. BamD forms a complex with 
                     BamA, BamB and BamC |CITS: [15851030], [16079137]|. BamD 
                     interacts directly with BamA <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [16824102]|. Substrate recruitment by BamD induces 
                     conformational change in BamA which facilitates folding and
                     insertion of outer membrane proteins: the slow folding 
                     mutant, LptD<sub>Y721D</sub>, binds to BamD but is slow to 
                     integrate into the membrane; outer membrane defects 
                     conferred by single copy expression of LptD<sub>Y721D</sub>
                     are suppressed by a BamA<sub>F494L</sub> mutation which 
                     influences the conformation of the BamA extracellular loops
                     |CITS: [29463713]|. BamD functions to regulate the activity
                     of BamA; BamD is non-essential in the presence of a 
                     <i>bamA<sub>E470K</sub></i> gain-of-function mutation 
                     |CITS: [32675245]|. The essentiality of BamD results from 
                     its role in preventing BamA 'jamming' by |FRAME: 
                     RCSF-MONOMER RcsF|; BamA and RcsF variants that prevent 
                     jamming bypass BamD essentiality; BamD kinetically enhances
                     OMP assembly by BamA |CITS: [40823823]|
                     A <i>bamD</i> depleted strain has decreased levels of 
                     OmpF/C, OmpA and TolC and decreased outer membrane density.
                     Depletion of BamD does not affect the level of 
                     lipopolysaccharide in the outer membrane. <i>degP</i> 
                     expression is elevated in a <i>bamD</i> depleted strain 
                     |CITS: [16824102]|. Deletion of <i>bamD</i> (in a 
                     <i>bamA<sup>E470K</sup></i> background) destabilizes the 
                     outer membrane due to a decrease in OMP level and 
                     subsequent decrease in OM stiffness; &Delta;<i>bamD</i> 
                     <i>bamA<sup>E470K</sup></i> cells exhibit defects in growth
                     rate and cell shape, and cells lyse in stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [37714857]| .
                     Levels of BamD increase when resistance to ampicillin and 
                     tetracycline is acquired by laboratory cultures of <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS:[16372265]|.
                     BamD consists of 10 &alpha;-helices and contains 5 
                     tetratricopeptide repeat (TPR) motifs. BamCD interaction is
                     mediated by direct contact between the TPR motifs and the 
                     N-terminal region of BamC |CITS: [21937441], [21586578], 
                     [22281737]|.
                     Purified BamD is individually able to facilitate assembly 
                     of unfolded BamA (itself a &beta;-barrel protein) into 
                     proteoliposomes |CITS: [23919461]|. Depletion of BamD 
                     results in the misfolding of BamA  |CITS: [24792419]| 
                     <i>bamD</i> is part of the |FRAME: RPOE-MONOMER ""Sigma 
                     E""| regulon |CITS: [11274153]|."
