     gene            complement(2733439..2734509)
                     /gene="aroF"
                     /locus_tag="b2601"
                     /label=aroF
     CDS             complement(2733439..2734509)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aroF"
                     /locus_tag="b2601"
                     /product="bifunctional 3-deoxy-7-phosphoheptulonate
                     synthase, Tyr-sensitive / 3-deoxy-7-phosphoheptulonate 
                     synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.5.1.54"
                     /label=aroF
                     /note="2-Dehydro-3-deoxyphosphoheptonate aldolase (DAHP 
                     synthase) is involved in the first committed step of the 
                     chorismate pathway, which leads to the biosynthesis of 
                     aromatic amino acids. DAHP synthase catalyzes an aldol 
                     reaction between phosphoenolpyruvate and D-erythrose 
                     4-phosphate to generate 3-deoxy-D-arabino-heptulosonate 
                     7-phosphate (DAHP). |CITS: [13654249]|
                     There are three isozymes of DAHP synthase, each 
                     specifically feedback regulated by tyrosine, phenylalanine 
                     or tryptophan |CITS: [13989507][6167722]|. DAHP synthase 
                     which is sensitive to tyrosine (DAHP synthase (Tyr), AroF) 
                     is encoded by <i>aroF</i>. All three isozymes are 
                     metalloenzymes and require a divalent metal for catalysis 
                     and/or structural integrity. Different metal ions have been
                     found in DAHP synthase (Tyr) <i>in vivo</i> and in pure 
                     preparations |CITS: [2572582][33383][9056491]|. There is a 
                     high degree of sequence identity (41%) between the three 
                     isozymes and the polypeptides are nearly identical in size 
                     |CITS: [2903857]|. In wild-type cells grown in minimal 
                     media, DAHP synthase (Phe) makes up about 80% of the total 
                     DAHP synthase activity, DAHP synthase (Tyr) makes up about 
                     20%, and DAHP synthase (Trp) makes up about 1% |CITS: 
                     [8426]|.
                     Mutations in <i>aroF</i> lead to tyrosine-insensitive DAHP 
                     synthase. The N-terminal region of <i>aroF</i> seems to be 
                     involved in the feedback-inhibition mechanism since 
                     deletion of this region caused tyrosine-resistance |CITS: 
                     [11506923]|. Transcriptional control of <i>aroF</i> is 
                     mediated by the <i>tyrR</i> repressor with the end products
                     of the pathway acting as co-repressors |CITS: 
                     [4887504][6125934]|.
                     "
