     gene            complement(2737542..2737889)
                     /gene="rplS"
                     /locus_tag="b2606"
                     /label=rplS
     CDS             complement(2737542..2737889)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rplS"
                     /locus_tag="b2606"
                     /product="trifunctional 50S ribosomal subunit protein L19
                     / 50S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rplS
                     /note="The L19 protein is a component of the 50S subunit
                     of the ribosome.  Depending on the method used, L19 does 
                     |CITS: [7559342]| or does not |CITS: [6423642]| appear to 
                     be essential for viability.
                     L19 can be crosslinked to 23S rRNA |CITS: 
                     [350280][6163479][6998892][1702198]|, mRNA |CITS: 
                     [7024914]|, and ribosomal proteins S9 and L18 |CITS: 
                     [3510664]|, L3 |CITS: [2645289][2665813]|, L14 |CITS: 
                     [2645289][2665813]|, L6 |CITS: [2653827]|, and L25 |CITS: 
                     [2645289][2653827][2665813]|.
                     L19 has RNA chaperone-like activity in an <i>in vitro 
                     trans</i> splicing assay |CITS: [15525706]| as well as 
                     protein chaperone activity |CITS: [19071121]|.
                     L19 is photoaffinity labeled by dyhydrorosaramyicin |CITS: 
                     [3905404]|.  L19 appears to be phosphorylated at residues 
                     Ser18 and Ser82 |CITS: [19469554]|.
                     The phenotypes of mutants lacking L19 vary; a slow growth 
                     phenotype |CITS: [6423642]| and accumulation of ribosome 
                     precursor particles during exponential growth and 
                     unbalanced synthesis of RNA |CITS: [2462010]| have been 
                     reported.  A number of L19 mutations that lead to increased
                     stop codon readthrough during translation have been 
                     isolated |CITS: [27477481]|.
                     Protein synthesis rates of the <i>rpsP-rimM-trmD-rplS</i> 
                     operon are growth rate regulated |CITS: [3290194]|; 
                     expression does not appear to be under transcriptional or 
                     translational feedback control |CITS: [2460631]|.  The 
                     lower expression levels of <i>rimM</i> and <i>trmD</i> 
                     appear to be achieved by regulating their level of 
                     translation |CITS: [3290194][2478711][2516239][1569581]|."
