     gene            complement(2737931..2738698)
                     /gene="trmD"
                     /locus_tag="b2607"
                     /label=trmD
     CDS             complement(2737931..2738698)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="trmD"
                     /locus_tag="b2607"
                     /product="tRNA m(1)G37 methyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.228"
                     /label=trmD
                     /note="tRNA m<sup>1</sup>G37 methyltransferase (TrmD)
                     catalyzes the addition of a methyl group to G37 of certain 
                     tRNAs |CITS: [6337136][342495][6298573]|.  Specificity of 
                     this activity is controlled by tRNA structure, specifically
                     including the V, T and D side loops and the presence of 
                     G36pG37 |CITS: [1618846][9220956]|.  TrmD can accomodate 
                     both A and G at position 36 in tRNA<sup>Asp</sup> and 
                     tRNA<sup>Phe</sup> |CITS: [15248782]|.  Analysis of enzyme 
                     activity with truncated tRNAs showed that TrmD minimally 
                     requires the anticodon loop and a 9 bp stem <i>in 
                     vitro</i>, distinguishing the prokaryotic enzymes from the 
                     archaeal and eukaryotic Trm5 |CITS: [17868690]|.  The 
                     catalytic mechanisms of TrmD- and Trm5-type enzymes differ,
                     and kinetic assays that distinguish their activities have 
                     been described |CITS: [26253967]|.
                     The m<sup>1</sup>G37 modification of tRNAs contributes to 
                     the prevention of +1 frameshift errors during translation, 
                     particularly at the second codon of an open reading frame 
                     |CITS: [26009254]|, and is required for translation of the 
                     proline-encoding CC[C/U] codons |CITS: [30981730]|. CC[C/U]
                     codons at codon 2 are overrepresented in ORFs encoding 
                     membrane proteins; accordingly, TrmD deficiency results in 
                     a variety of membrane-related phenotypes |CITS: 
                     [30981730]|.  Distinct from the frameshifting mechanism, 
                     lack of the m<sup>1</sup>G37 modification leads to ribosome
                     stalling at codons that are normally translated by 
                     m<sup>1</sup>G37-containing tRNAs |CITS: [34382933]|. 
                     Aminoacylation of tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> that lacks the 
                     m<sup>1</sup>G37 modification is less efficient than that 
                     of the modified tRNA.  An experimental evolution experiment
                     showed that the growth defect due to TrmD deficiency can be
                     suppressed by point mutations in |FRAME: CPLX0-3950 ProRS| 
                     that enable more efficient aminoacylation of unmodified 
                     tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> |CITS: [34761260]|.
                     The crystal structure of the TrmD homodimer bound to 
                     S-adenosyl homocysteine was determined to 2.5 &Aring; 
                     resolution.  The subunits consist of an N-terminal domain 
                     with AdoHcy bound to a trefoil knot, and a flexible linker 
                     connecting the C-terminal domain.  Predicted active site 
                     residues were mutagenized, and the kinetic parameters of 
                     the resulting enzymes were analyzed |CITS: [14583191]|. The
                     knot appears to remain even in the unfolded state |CITS: 
                     [23512842]|.  Protection of tRNA and conformational changes
                     during methylation have been analyzed |CITS: 
                     [9409623][15850396]|.  Pre-steady state kinetic analysis 
                     showed that, unlike the archaeal Trm5 enzyme, TrmD does not
                     show burst kinetics, and only one of the two active sites 
                     in the dimeric enzyme is functional at any given time 
                     |CITS: [20452364]|.  Molecular dynamics simulations and 
                     kinetic analysis of site-directed mutants suggest that the 
                     knot undergoes conformational changes during the catalytic 
                     cycle that supports signaling by the AdoMet substrate 
                     across the dimer interface |CITS: [27571175]|. Adenosine 
                     and methionine competitively inhibit TrmD, but not Trm5 
                     |CITS: [21602303]|.  Both the exocyclic 2-amino group of 
                     G37 and the adjacent G36 base in the tRNA are important for
                     TrmD activity |CITS: [22847817]|.  TrmD requires a divalent
                     metal ion for catalytic activity; the physiologically 
                     relevant ion is Mg<sup>2+</sup>.  It appears to aid the 
                     abstraction of the N<sup>1</sup> proton and stabilizes the 
                     developing negative charge at O<sup>6</sup> of the G37 base
                     |CITS: [25219964]|.  A model of the catalytic cycle was 
                     proposed based on crystal structures and kinetic analysis 
                     of mutants of the <i>Haemophilus influenzae</i> enzyme 
                     |CITS: [26183229]|. Unlike the <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i>
                     enzyme, <i>E. coli</i> TrmD does not bind 3',5'-cAMP and is
                     thus able to discriminate between its substrate AdoMet and 
                     the signaling molecule; the Tyr86 residue is important for 
                     discrimination |CITS: [27881678]|.
                     The level of TrmD activity is not growth rate-dependent 
                     |CITS: [6154481]|.  The enzyme is present in very low 
                     amounts |CITS: [6337136]|.  TrmD is encoded within an 
                     operon together with the ribosomal proteins S16 and L19, 
                     and its lower abundance appears to be regulated at the 
                     translational level |CITS: [3290194]|.
                     A high-throughput experiment initially identified the 
                     <i>trmD</i> gene as essential for growth |CITS: 
                     [16738554]|.  <i>trmD</i> has been the subject of a pilot 
                     study to screen for genetic interactions with null alleles 
                     in the Keio collection |CITS: [24463526]|.  The originally 
                     isolated <i>trmD</i> mutant is a temperature-sensitive 
                     allele |CITS: [342494][342495]|.  A S88L mutation causes a 
                     temperature-sensitive growth phenotype due to a decrease in
                     TrmD activity at increased temperatures |CITS: [23986443]|.
                      Non-polar <i>trmD</i> mutants grow several fold slower 
                     |CITS: [7559342]| and experience more frameshifting than 
                     wild-type <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [11532950][34330903]|.  The
                     strongest effect of TrmD depletion is on frameshifting at 
                     the second codon of an open reading frame, when that codon 
                     is CCC-C |CITS: [26009254]|.  TrmD depletion, and thus 
                     m<sup>1</sup>G37 deficiency, weakens the cell envelope and 
                     sensitizes the cells to several classes of antibiotics 
                     |CITS: [30981730]|.  TrmD depletion leads to decreased 
                     aminoacylation of substrate tRNAs and a gene expression 
                     phenotype reminiscent of the stringent response |CITS: 
                     [34382933]|.
                     TrmD: ""<b>tR</b>NA <b>m</b>ethylation"" |CITS: [342495]| 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [7599277][28601227][30457841][36986305]|"
