     gene            2748255..2748737
                     /gene="smpB"
                     /locus_tag="b2620"
                     /label=smpB
     CDS             2748255..2748737
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="smpB"
                     /locus_tag="b2620"
                     /product="SsrA-binding protein"
                     /label=smpB
                     /note="Small protein B (SmpB) is a component of the 
                     trans-translation process which marks proteins that have 
                     stalled during translation for degradation.
                     SmpB is required for trans-translation, the process in 
                     which tmRNA attaches to mRNA that is stalled in translation
                     and generates an SsrA tag on the protein |CITS: 
                     [10393194][11917023]|.  It also enhances the aminoacylation
                     of tmRNA, though it inhibits the same process on true tRNA 
                     |CITS: [11724528][11917023][11904185][15494393]|.
                     SmpB is a member of the SsrA tagging complex, along with 
                     |FRAME: EG10900-MONOMER|, |FRAME: PRPPSYN-MONOMER|, |FRAME:
                     EG11259-MONOMER|, |FRAME: G7168-MONOMER| and |FRAME: 
                     SSRA-RNA| |CITS: [11248028]|.  SmpB binds specifically to 
                     tmRNA, and is required for stable association of tmRNA with
                     ribosomes |CITS: [10393194][15069072]|.  Up to three SmpBs 
                     can bind to each tmRNA, the first associating with the 3' 
                     end, the other two with the anticodon-like stem region 
                     |CITS: [11724528][12102625]|.  An interaction between the 
                     D- and T-loops of tmRNA is required for SmpB stimulation of
                     aminoacylation, but not for simple SmpB binding |CITS: 
                     [11904186]|.  In addition to its interaction with tmRNA, 
                     SmpB binds to the 70S ribosome, with one SmpB monomer bound
                     to each of the 30S and 50S subunits |CITS: 
                     [15972795][15069072]|.
                     Cryo-EM structures of tmRNA-SmpB located in the A site, P 
                     site and past the E site of the ribosome elucidate the 
                     specificity of tmRNA and SmpB for stalled ribosomes, the 
                     movement of the circularized complex through the ribosome, 
                     and the switch to translation of the tmRNA |CITS: 
                     [30765567]|.
                     The carboxy-terminus of SmpB is required for activation of 
                     trans-translation, but is not significantly involved in 
                     binding to ribosomes and tmRNA, or in promoting stable 
                     association of tmRNA with ribosomes |CITS: 
                     [15596445][15699355]|.
                     SmpB prevents inhibition of tmRNA aminoacylation by 
                     aminoglycosides |CITS: [12588865]|.  At high concentrations
                     of SmpB, however, the aminoglycoside antibiotic paramycin 
                     can actually induce frameshifted trans-translation |CITS: 
                     [12754221]|.
                     In addition to its other roles in tmRNA function, SmpB 
                     appears to be required to maintain normal tmRNA levels, as 
                     well |CITS: [11917023]|.
                     An <i>smpB</i> mutant has slightly reduced ability to act 
                     as a recipient in cell-to-cell transfer of a plasmid |CITS:
                     [22497891]|."
