                     /label=ssrA
     misc_RNA        2748952..2749314
                     /gene="ssrA"
                     /locus_tag="b2621"
                     /product="tmRNA"
                     /note="tmRNA (for transfer-messenger RNA), also known as
                     SsrA RNA, acts both as a tRNA and as an mRNA to direct the 
                     modification of proteins whose biosynthesis has stalled or 
                     has been interrupted. It functions to release a stalled 
                     ribosome from the end of a ""broken"" mRNA that has no stop
                     codon by acting both as a tRNA and as an mRNA, using a 
                     ""trans-translation"" mechanism |CITS: [9150876]|. SsrA 
                     also mediates release of a ribosome that has stalled due to
                     lack of an appropriate tRNA |CITS: [10449423][11891313]|. 
                     Other translation problems can also lead to SsrA activity 
                     |CITS: [12047352][12081641][12105207][12458795][12787364]|.
                     In addition, SsrA RNA stimulates degradation of defective 
                     mRNAs |CITS: [12649493]|.
                     The SsrA RNA contains a tRNA-like structural region that is
                     processed by |FRAME: CPLX0-1382| and charged by |FRAME: 
                     ALAS-CPLX| |CITS: [7524073][9180372]|. The SsrA RNA also 
                     has a region that acts as a messenger RNA and encodes a 
                     translated tag |CITS: [7536743][9180372]| that is added to 
                     the nascent protein and which targets this protein for 
                     degradation |CITS: [8584937][9573050][9573051][10359771][1 
                     0882100][10922052][11009422][11259663][11344323][11406586][
                     11535833][11779508][12177052][12667450][12717012]|. 
                     SsrA-tagged proteins are degraded with zero-order kinetics 
                     at the single-cell level |CITS: [17667952]|.
                     The structure of the SsrA RNA |CITS: [9150875][8990402][10 
                     024447][10471735][11713316][12888352][12904796]| and the 
                     translational mechanism |CITS: [10508174][12457560]| have 
                     been examined in detail. SsrA RNA interacts with tRNA(Ala) 
                     |CITS: [11387229]|, with elongation factor Tu |CITS: 
                     [11595738][11724528]|, and with |FRAME: EG10900-MONOMER| 
                     |CITS: [12457560]|. SsrA also binds to |FRAME: 
                     EG10978-MONOMER| (SspB), which is a factor that confers 
                     specificity on the activity of ClpXP protease |CITS: 
                     [12445774]|. A crystal structure of SspB bound to an 
                     SsrA-encoded tag peptide is presented |CITS: [12887894]|. 
                     Under some circumstances, SsrA may compete with the 
                     activity of the translational |FRAME: EG10762-MONOMER| 
                     |CITS: [12139620]|. Alanine-aminoacylated |CITS: [8985150]|
                     SsrA binds specifically to 70S ribosomes |CITS: 
                     [8830040][8985150]|. SsrA ribosome association and its 
                     activity require the |FRAME: EG11782-MONOMER| (SmpB) |CITS:
                     [10393194]|, which binds to SsrA RNA |CITS: 
                     [10393194][11724528]|. SsrA and SmpB are found in a larger 
                     complex also containing ribosomal protein S1, |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-8299|, |FRAME: EG11259-MONOMER|, and |FRAME: 
                     G7168-MONOMER YfbG| |CITS: [11248028]|. SmpB and elongation
                     factor Tu exhibit chaperone activity toward SsrA RNA |CITS:
                     [12588865]|. A crystal structure of SsrA RNA bound to SmpB 
                     is presented at 3.2 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [12904796]|.
                     Cryo-EM structures of tmRNA-SmpB located in the A site, P 
                     site and past the E site of the ribosome elucidate the 
                     specificity of tmRNA and SmpB for stalled ribosomes, the 
                     movement of the circularized complex through the ribosome, 
                     and the switch to translation of the tmRNA |CITS: 
                     [30765567]|.
                     The tRNA functionality is required for the mRNA 
                     functionality of SsrA |CITS: [9586061]|. The aminoglycoside
                     antibiotic neomycin B interferes with aminoacylation of 
                     SsrA |CITS: [12588865]|. RelE, which downregulates 
                     translation in response to starvation, cleaves SsrA RNA as 
                     well as mRNA, and SsrA is involved in recovery from a RelE 
                     stress response |CITS: [12787364]|. The <i>lacI</i> mRNA 
                     appears to be a physiologically-relevant SsrA system 
                     substrate |CITS: [10899129][11373298]|; other substrates 
                     detected <i>in vivo</i> include mRNAs encoding lambda cI 
                     repressor, |FRAME: EG12851-MONOMER YbeL|, |FRAME: 
                     UDPGLUCEPIM-MONOMER GalE|, |FRAME: RIBOKIN-MONOMER RbsK|, 
                     and a SlyD-kan(R) fusion protein |CITS: [11373298]|, mRNAs 
                     subject to amber or ochre tRNA suppression |CITS: 
                     [12047352]|, and mRNAs whose translation is affected by 
                     miscoding antibiotic drugs |CITS: [12081641]|.
                     The SsrA RNA is transcribed as a larger precursor RNA that 
                     is processed to form the mature RNA |CITS: [2475757][24824 
                     06][1707682][1375563][1378735][7524073][9501180][10535935]|
                     . An <i>ssrA</i> mutant exhibits a growth defect |CITS: 
                     [1716727][7524073]| and a motility defect |CITS: 
                     [7524073]|. An <i>ssrA</i> mutation causes increased 
                     |FRAME: EG11131-MONOMER RelE|-mediated toxicity and ssrA 
                     overexpression causes decreased toxicity, compared to wild 
                     type |CITS: [12787364]|. A <i>sipB391</i> mutation causes a
                     defect in growth of lambda immP22, which is a hybrid 
                     bacteriophage |CITS: [2941412]|. An <i>ssrA</i> mutation 
                     causes a defect in regulation of Mu lysogeny |CITS: 
                     [11517307]|, and SsrA may exert regulatory effects via 
                     tagging of repressor molecules |CITS: [12217693]|. Single 
                     mutation of <i>ssrA</i> slightly decreases cell growth when
                     D-alanine, glucosamine, mannitol, thymidine, or xylose is 
                     used as a sole carbon source, but the double mutant 
                     <i>arcZ</i>-<i>ssrA</i> enhances growth on pyruvate, 
                     lactate, oxaloacetate, D-alanine, or L-alanine as sole 
                     carbon source  |CITS: [35920556]|.
                     SsrA affects DNA binding by the phage proteins |CITS: 
                     [8144474][7585940]| and has been observed to bind directly 
                     to these proteins |CITS: [7585940]|, although this 
                     interaction is nonspecific and appears not to be 
                     responsible for the phage growth phenotype |CITS: 
                     [10094693]|. The SsrA RNA and the |FRAME: PRPPSYN-MONOMER| 
                     (Prs) gene exhibit a genetic interaction |CITS: [8925474]|.
                     An <i>ssrA</i> deletion mutant is sensitive to cell 
                     envelope stress |CITS: [19734312]|.
                     Multiple studies with <i>ssrA</i> mutants have found that 
                     they are more sensitive to drugs that cause DNA damage have
                     been studied; |FRAME: EG10984| mutants restored viability 
                     through maintenance of genome integrity and possibly 
                     efficiency of cell division |CITS: 
                     [20807197][29027471][37690591]|.
                     The tmRNA genes have been used as probes for identification
                     of bacterial species |CITS: [11560762][12365411]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8584931][9150873][9150876][9478132][10068 
                     996][10583945][10881189][11186008][11694632][11737633][1193
                     4611][12583891][12730326][15102450][17291191][17383929][173
                     97189][18557701][18598387][19914241][20073035 
                     ][21111023][21354362][21558793][21664408][21823225][219570 
                     08][22243584][24600445][24706739][24711807][24778639][2478 
                     2844][24782856][25135187][25228900][25446863][27484476][28 
                     138069][28629612][28707598][30815901][32213244]|"
