     gene            complement(2754710..2756899)
                     /gene="abpB"
                     /locus_tag="b2627"
                     /label=abpB
     CDS             complement(2754710..2756899)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="abpB"
                     /locus_tag="b2627"
                     /product="CP4-57 prophage; anti-bacteriophage protein
                     AbpB"
                     /label=abpB
                     /note="AbpB together with |FRAME: G7363-MONOMER| operate 
                     together as a bacteriophage defense system against several 
                     types of bacteriophage. AbpAB defense system is activated 
                     by phage single-stranded binding (SSB) protein bound to 
                     <i>E. coli</i> ssDNA; it is also activated in the absence 
                     of phage infections by chemically interrupting <i>E. 
                     coli's</i> DNA replication or by deleting DNA repair 
                     factors RecB and RecC |CITS: [37882551]|.
                     Non-synonymous point mutations in <i>abpB</i> (<i>yfjK</i>)
                     were identified in cell populations that were selected for 
                     high resistance to ionizing radiation. The A151D allele was
                     tested and was found to contribute significantly to 
                     resistance. A &Delta;<i>abpB</i> mutant was tested and also
                     shows increased resistance, although to a lesser extent 
                     than the A151D point mutant |CITS: [24596148]|.
                     Overexpression of AbpA and AbpB together from a plasmid 
                     blocks the propagation of T2, T4, T7, and &lambda; family 
                     bacteriophages at 30&deg;C, but not 37 or 42&deg;C. The 
                     AbpAB proteins impair phage DNA synthesis, but not 
                     bacterial DNA synthesis |CITS: [25224971]|.
                     Suppressor mutations that allow growth of phage T4 on cells
                     overexpressing AbpAB localize to T4 gene <i>41</i>, a 
                     replicative DNA helicase that is part of the primosome 
                     |CITS: [25224971]|.
                     Mutants in which specific amino acid in regions with high 
                     structural similarity to ATP-dependent RNA helicases that 
                     are required for ATP binding were substituted did not 
                     inhibit T4 phage growth when coexpressed with AbpA |CITS: 
                     [37882551]|.
                     This enzyme was found to interact with the |FRAME: 
                     GAPDH-A-MONOMER| enzyme in glycolysis in a large assessment
                     of protein-protein interactions (PPIs) |CITS: [33917325]|.
                     AbpB: ""<b>a</b>nti-<b>b</b>acterio<b>p</b>hage protein 
                     <b>B</b>"" |CITS: [25224971]|"
