                     /gene="abpA"
                     /locus_tag="b2628"
                     /product="CP4-57 prophage; anti-bacteriophage protein
                     AbpA"
                     /label=abpA
                     /note="AbpA together with |FRAME: G7362-MONOMER| operate 
                     together as a bacteriophage defense system against several 
                     types of bacteriophage. AbpAB defense system is activated 
                     by phage single-stranded binding (SSB) protein bound to 
                     <i>E. coli</i> ssDNA; it is also activated in the absence 
                     of phage infections by chemically interrupting <i>E. 
                     coli's</i> DNA replication or by deleting DNA repair 
                     factors RecB and RecC |CITS: [37882551]|.
                     Overexpression of AbpA and AbpB together from a plasmid 
                     blocks the propagation of T2, T4, T7, and &lambda; family 
                     bacteriophages at 30&deg;C, but not 37 or 42&deg;C.  The 
                     AbpAB proteins impair phage DNA synthesis, but not 
                     bacterial DNA synthesis |CITS: [25224971]|.
                     Suppressor mutations that allow growth of phage T4 on cells
                     overexpressing AbpAB localize to T4 gene <i>41</i>, a 
                     replicative DNA helicase that is part of the primosome 
                     |CITS: [25224971]|.
                     <i>abpA</i> (formerly yfjL) belongs to a network of genes 
                     which facilitate stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     Point mutations in specific amino acid in the N-terminal 
                     dsDNA endonuclease region did not inhibit T4 phage growth 
                     when coexpressed with AbpA |CITS: [37882551]|.
                     AbpA: ""<b>a</b>nti-<b>b</b>acterio<b>p</b>hage protein 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [25224971]|"
