                     /gene="lhgD"
                     /locus_tag="b2660"
                     /product="L-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase"
                     /EC_number="1.1.5.13"
                     /label=lhgD
                     /note="L-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase (LhgD) is an
                     electron transport chain-coupled dehydrogenase that feeds 
                     electrons from the reaction into the membrane quinone pool 
                     |CITS: [30498244]|. LhgD contains an FAD cofactor which is 
                     not covalently attached, and whose reduction potential is 
                     relatively high at -25 mV |CITS: [18390652]|.
                     LhgD was initially thought to be an oxidase, i.e. using 
                     molecular oxygen for oxidation of L-2-hydroxyglutarate and 
                     producing hydrogen peroxide |CITS: [18390652]|.
                     LhgD is associated with the cytoplasmic membrane |CITS: 
                     [17309111]|, and its activity is only found in the membrane
                     fraction |CITS: [30498244]|.
                     <i>lhgD</i> is part of an operon whose expression is 
                     induced upon carbon starvation and in stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [9512707][11251833][11532138][14731280]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [32149453]|
                     LhgO: ""<b>L</b>-2-<b>h</b>ydroxy<b>g</b>lutarate 
                     <b>o</b>xidase"" |CITS: [18390652]|
                     LhgD: ""<b>L</b>-2-<b>h</b>ydroxy<b>g</b>lutarate 
                     <b>d</b>ehydrogenase |CITS: [30498244]|"
