     gene            2786094..2787374
                     /gene="gabT"
                     /locus_tag="b2662"
                     /label=gabT
     CDS             2786094..2787374
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gabT"
                     /locus_tag="b2662"
                     /product="multifunctional 4-aminobutyrate aminotransferase
                     GabT / 4-aminobutyrate transaminase / 5-aminovalerate 
                     transaminase / N-acetylornithine aminotransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.6.1.19; 2.6.1.48; 2.6.1.11"
                     /label=gabT
                     /note="4-aminobutyrate aminotransferase is the initial
                     enzyme of one of two 4-aminobutyrate (GABA) degradation 
                     pathways |CITS: [4550821][22636776]|.  It belongs to the 
                     aminotransferase subgroup II of pyridoxal 5'-phosphate 
                     (PLP)-dependent enzymes |CITS: [8513804]|.  GabT also 
                     functions as a 5-aminovalerate aminotransferase during 
                     degradation of L-lysine |CITS: [30498244]|.
                     Crystal structures of the free and inhibitor-bound enzyme 
                     and various point mutants have been reported |CITS: 
                     [15323550][15723541]|.  Although a dimer by gel filtration 
                     |CITS: [2254272]|, the enzyme appears as a tetramer in the 
                     crystal structure; tetramer formation is promoted by 
                     increasing concentration of PLP |CITS: [15323550]|.  A 
                     reaction mechanism was proposed based on analysis of 
                     kinetic properties and crystal structures of point mutants 
                     in active site residues |CITS: [15723541]|.
                     The redundancy and promiscuity among aminotransferase 
                     enzymes has been investigated.  Four enzymes, ArgD, AstC, 
                     GabT, and PuuE, show some level of ACOAT activity involved 
                     in arginine biosynthesis.  On minimal medium lacking 
                     arginine, single <i>argD</i> or <i>astC</i> mutants have 
                     subtle growth defects, and an <i>argD astC</i> double 
                     mutant has a slower doubling time than wild type.  An 
                     <i>argD astC gabT</i> triple mutant does not grow on medium
                     lacking arginine.  Accumulation of suppressor mutations 
                     eventually allows growth of the triple mutant; an 
                     additional deletion of <i>puuE</i> eliminates this effect 
                     |CITS: [25243376]|.  Expression of <i>gabT</i> is 
                     upregulated in a &Delta;<i>argD</i> mutant grown on minimal
                     medium |CITS: [25564669]|.  In the hands of |CITS: 
                     [25564669]|, the triple deletion mutant 
                     &Delta;<i>argD</i>&Delta;<i>astC</i>&Delta;<i>gabT</i> 
                     shows an extensive lag phase on glucose minimal medium; a 
                     quadruple deletion mutant that includes &Delta;<i>puuE</i> 
                     does not grow.
                     The normal activities of two enzymes, ArgD and SerC, are 
                     sufficient for succinyldiaminopimelate (SDAP) and lysine 
                     biosynthesis; a third enzyme, AstC, is sufficient for SDAP 
                     biosynthesis, but alone can not fulfill the cell's 
                     requirement for lysine.  Additional enzymes, including GabT
                     and PuuE, may be able to contribute to SDAP biosynthesis.  
                     Expression of <i>argD</i>, <i>astC</i>, <i>serC</i>, 
                     <i>aspC</i>, <i>gabT</i>, <i>hisC</i>, <i>ilvE</i>, 
                     <i>patA</i>, <i>puuE</i>, or <i>tyrB</i> from a plasmid 
                     allows growth of the triple &Delta;<i>argD serC astC</i> 
                     mutant on minimal medium |CITS: [25243376]|.
                     The three major transaminases in alanine biosynthesis are 
                     AvtA, AlaA and AlaC.  Expression of <i>aspC</i>, 
                     <i>gabT</i>, <i>puuE</i>, <i>tyrB</i>, or <i>ygjG</i> from 
                     a plasmid partially suppresses the growth defect of a 
                     &Delta;<i>avtA alaA alaC</i> mutant |CITS: [20729367]|.
                     A <i>gabT</i> mutant can not utilize GABA as the sole 
                     source of nitrogen at 37&deg;C |CITS: [374339]|. Regulation
                     of the operon containing <i>gabT</i> has been studied 
                     extensively |CITS: [2188954][9512707][11251833][11 
                     532138][12446648][14731280][15576807][20639325]|. 
                     Expression can be induced by growth in high pH medium 
                     |CITS: [12107143]|.  <i>gabT</i> was also identified in a 
                     screen for genes which are activated by self-produced 
                     extracellular signals |CITS: 
                     [10200310][11418561][14612251]|.
                     Mutational knockout of <i>gabT</i> has been used as part of
                     a strategy to increase GABA yield in <i>E. coli</i> strains
                     metabolically engineered to produce GABA |CITS: 
                     [21971608]|.
                     GabT: ""<b>g</b>amma-<b>a</b>mino<b>b</b>utyrate 
                     utilization; <b>t</b>ransaminase"" |CITS: [4590473]|"
                     /translation="MNSNKELMQRRSQAIPRGVGQIHPIFADRAENCRVWDVEGREYLD
