     gene            2787612..2789012
                     /gene="gabP"
                     /locus_tag="b2663"
                     /label=gabP
     CDS             2787612..2789012
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gabP"
                     /locus_tag="b2663"
                     /product="bifunctional 4-aminobutanoate:H(+) symporter / 
                     5-aminopentanoate:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=gabP
                     /note="GabP mediates the uptake of 4-aminobutanoate, the 
                     zwitterionic form of &gamma;-aminobutyric acid (GABA). 
                     <i>gabP</i> encodes a predicted integral membrane protein; 
                     moderate overexpression of cloned <i>gabP</i> enhances 
                     uptake of labeled GABA; GabP is a high affinity GABA 
                     transporter that is driven by membrane potential |CITS: 
                     [8297211]|.  Both open chain and cyclic analogs of GABA 
                     (eg. 5-aminopentanoate, piperidine-3-carboxylate) are 
                     effective inhibitors of GABA transport |CITS: [7650003]|; 
                     GabP transports a range of structurally diverse GABA 
                     analogs |CITS: [8557687]|. Experimental topology analysis 
                     suggests the protein contains 12 transmembrane domains with
                     the N and C-termini located in the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [9806886]|.
                     GabP mediated transport of GABA is dependent upon the 
                     presence of phosphatidylethanolamine (PE) within the 
                     membrane; in cells lacking PE, the N-terminal hairpin 
                     domain of GabP is misorganized and transport function is 
                     compromised |CITS:[15890647]|.
                     GabP is a member of the Amino Acid Transporter Family 
                     within the Amino Acid-Polyamine-Organocation (APC) 
                     Superfamily of transporters |CITS: [10931886][26546518]|. 
                     Early studies isolated and characterized mutants in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 CS101 unable to use &gamma;-aminobutyrate as 
                     a source of nitrogen; <i>gabP</i> mutants (<i>gabP5, 
                     gabP9</I>) are specifically impaired in GABA transport 
                     |CITS: [28310][374339]|.
                     <i>gabP</i>: <U>g</U>amma-<U>a</U>mino<U>b</U>utyrate 
                     <U>p</U>ermease
                     "
