     gene            2789033..2789695
                     /gene="glaR"
                     /locus_tag="b2664"
                     /label=glaR
     CDS             2789033..2789695
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glaR"
                     /locus_tag="b2664"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA-binding transcriptional
                     repressor GlaR / GlaR-glutarate"
                     /label=glaR
                     /note="The transcription factor CsiR, for ""<b>C</b>arbon 
                     <b>s</b>tarvation <b>i</b>nduced <b>R</b>egulator,"" was 
                     initially identified as a repressor that controls the 
                     transcription of genes involved in the degradation and 
                     transport of 4-aminobutyrate (GABA) for utilization as a 
                     source of nitrogen |CITS: [374339][12446648][14731280]|. 
                     However, CsiR does not appear to respond directly to the 
                     presence of GABA |CITS: [12446648]|, and later reports 
                     suggested that it does not affect expression of 
                     <i>gabDTP</i> |CITS: [14731280][28061857]|.  CsiR represses
                     transcription from the <i>csiD</i> promoter during 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [14731280]|.
                     The CsiR binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     A negative correlation between cellular growth and the copy
                     number of the proteins CsiR has been reported |CITS: 
                     [35694815]|.
                     <i>csiR</i> expression is induced at stationary phase, but 
                     is not autoregulated and not dependent on 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: [14731280]|.
                     Transcription of the <i>glaR</i> gene is upregulated in 
                     cells cultivated in LB broth in the presence of the 
                     antibiotic erythromycin and downregulated in the presence 
                     of mitomycin C or imipenem |CITS:[36853057]|.
                     A <i>csiR</i> mutant (<i>gabC1</i>) has the ability to 
                     utilize &gamma;-aminobutyrate (GABA) as the sole source of 
                     nitrogen |CITS: [4550821][4552985][4590473][12446648]|.
                     In an experiment using directed cell evolution of a 
                     &Delta;<i>panD</i> mutant (containing a damaged CoA 
                     biosynthesis pathway), a pathway for &beta;-alanine 
                     biosynthesis via uracil degradation emerged.  One of the 
                     mutations is an S144P change in CsiR.  This mutation may 
                     lead to derepression of GabT, which converts malonic 
                     semialdehyde, an intermediate of the |FRAME: PWY0-1471| 
                     pathway, to &beta;-alanine |CITS: [30327558]|.
                     GabC: ""<b>g</b>amma-<b>a</b>mino<b>b</b>utyrate 
                     utilization <b>C</b>"" |CITS: [4590473]|
                     GlaR: |CITS: [30498244]|"
