     gene            2794082..2794327
                     /gene="nrdH"
                     /locus_tag="b2673"
                     /label=nrdH
     CDS             2794082..2794327
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nrdH"
                     /locus_tag="b2673"
                     /product="glutaredoxin-like protein NrdH"
                     /label=nrdH
                     /note="NrdH is an electron donor for |FRAME: 
                     RIBONUCLEOSIDE-DIP-REDUCTII-CPLX| |CITS: [16771665]|. It 
                     has higher specificity for the class Ib (NrdEF) enzyme than
                     for the class Ia (NrdAB) enzyme. NrdH itself is reduced by 
                     thioredoxin reductase, but not by glutathione. The redox 
                     potential of NrdH is 248.5 mV |CITS: [9218434]|. NrdH 
                     behaves functionally like a classical thioredoxin, but has 
                     sequence similarity to glutaredoxins |CITS: [9218434]|.  A 
                     crystal structure of NrdH in the oxidized state has been 
                     determined at 1.7 &Aring; resolution, showing that the 
                     protein is structurally similar to glutaredoxin 3. However,
                     the glutathione-binding cleft is absent, and NrdH instead 
                     contains a thioredoxin-like hydrophobic pocket at the 
                     surface, which may allow it to interact with thioredoxin 
                     reductase |CITS: [11441020]|. Expression of the 
                     <i>nrdHIEF</i> operon is increased by hydroxyurea |CITS: 
                     [8820648]| and oxidative stress |CITS: [11278973]|. 
                     Expression is highest in minimal medium and in early log 
                     phase growth in complex medium; deletion of Trx1 and Grx1 
                     (<i>trxA<sup>-</sup> grxA<sup>-</sup></i>) increases 
                     expression more than 100-fold |CITS: [11278973]|.  
                     <i>nrdHIEF</i> belongs to the Fur regulon |CITS: 
                     [11101675]|."
