     gene            complement(2807577..2808092)
                     /gene="luxS"
                     /locus_tag="b2687"
                     /label=luxS
     CDS             complement(2807577..2808092)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="luxS"
                     /locus_tag="b2687"
                     /product="S-ribosylhomocysteine lyase"
                     /EC_number="4.4.1.21"
                     /label=luxS
                     /note="LuxS is involved in biosynthesis of AI-2, the 
                     hormone-like signal that mediates cell-cell communication 
                     during quorum sensing, the response to increased cell 
                     density |CITS: [9990077]|.  LuxS is the synthase that 
                     catalyzes formation of AI-2 by cleavage of 
                     S-ribosylhomocysteine |CITS: [11489131]|.  LuxS also 
                     participates in recycling of S-adenosylhomocysteine via the
                     |FRAME: PWY-6151| |CITS: [11932438]|.
                     Stochastic modelling has predicted an alternative pathway 
                     for AI-2 formation |CITS: [17170762]|.  It was later found 
                     that AI-2 can form spontaneously from ribulose-5-phosphate,
                     but this reaction may not be relevant in wild type <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [18564424]|.
                     A catalytic mechanism for LuxS has been proposed |CITS: 
                     [17014073]|.
                     The DH5&alpha; strain has a <i>luxS</i> mutation that 
                     prevents autoinducer production, whereas the MG1655 strain 
                     produces autoinducer |CITS: [9990077]|.  The differences 
                     between wild type and <i>luxS</i> mutants have been 
                     examined by gene expression |CITS: 
                     [11514505][15470704][16321939]| and phenotype |CITS: 
                     [12897016]| microarrays and by profiling the metabolites of
                     the |FRAME: PWY-6151| |CITS: [20417170]|.  A <i>luxS</i> 
                     mutant shows increased swimming motility and synthesis of 
                     flagella |CITS: [20875395]|.
                     The expression of <i>luxS</i>, as well as the accumulation 
                     of AI-2, is decreased in <i>uvrY</i> and <i>hfq</i> mutant 
                     strains, and it is marginally decreased in a <i>uvrY 
                     crp</i> double mutant. A <i>csrB</i> mutant strain showed 
                     marginally increased expression of <i>luxS</i>; on the 
                     other hand, <i>cya</i>, <i>crp</i>, and <i>csrA</i> mutant 
                     strains and a <i>uvrY cya</i> double mutant strain showed 
                     constitutive expression of <i>luxS</i>. In the case of the 
                     <i>csrA</i> mutant, there was an increase in AI-2 levels 
                     that was associated with the stability of <i>luxR</i> mRNA 
                     and mediated by CsrA in this strain |CITS: [27362507]|. 
                     Transcription of <i>luxS</i> is induced by acetate |CITS: 
                     [11591692]| or by acidic pH |CITS: [12107143]|.
                     Expression of <i>luxS</i> is posttranscriptionally 
                     regulated by the small RNA CyaR |CITS: [18978044]|.  The 
                     MicA small RNA appears to regulate processing of both 
                     <i>luxS</i> primary transcripts by RNase III |CITS: 
                     [20976191]|.  A <i>luxS</i> mutant does not produce AI-2 
                     and has a different biofilm architecture than wild type 
                     |CITS: [23078586]|.
                     Coexpression of LuxS with recombinant proteins in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> was found to enhance the yield of recombinant 
                     protein.  Coincident upregulation of the chaperone GroEL 
                     (GroL) was also found, demonstrating that this chaperone is
                     positively regulated at the posttranscriptional level by 
                     AI-2 |CITS: [21278275]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11934621][12949525][15864263][15908344][1 
                     6487745][16459080][17869606]|
                     "
