     gene            2808161..2808233
                     /gene="micA"
                     /locus_tag="b4442"
                     /label=micA
     misc_RNA        2808161..2808233
                     /gene="micA"
                     /locus_tag="b4442"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA MicA"
                     /note="MicA is a small RNA that negatively regulates
                     expression of a number of genes.  The first identified 
                     target of MicA was OmpA, whose expression is regulated 
                     during stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [16204185][16313626][17264113]|.  MicA acts as an antisense
                     RNA, binding to the translation initiation region of 
                     <i>ompA</i> mRNA and blocking ribosome binding, thereby 
                     downregulating OmpA expression.  This downregulation 
                     requires the RNA-binding protein Hfq |CITS: 
                     [16204185][16313626]|.  Hfq acts as an RNA chaperone, 
                     bringing MicA and <i>ompA</i> into close proximity and 
                     restructuring MicA to expose the <i>ompA</i>-binding site 
                     for pairing |CITS: [23361466]|.  MicA may either direct 
                     cleavage of <i>ompA</i> mRNA that generates a 5' mRNA 
                     fragment or protect the 3' end of the fragment from 
                     exonucleolytic processing |CITS: [33460557]|.
                     In addition, MicA downregulates expression of a number of 
                     other genes.  The list includes <i>phoPQ</i>, linking the 
                     PhoPQ regulon to the envelope stress response |CITS: 
                     [20345657][23300478]|, and a number of other genes |CITS: 
                     [21768388]|.  However, MicA does not appear to play a role 
                     in regulating temperature-dependent <i>lpxT</i> expression 
                     |CITS: [31299227]|.  MicA can downregulate expression of 
                     OmpX, likely under conditions where its primary regulator, 
                     CyaR, is poorly expressed |CITS: [18619465]|.  MicA 
                     positively regulates motility, but not by regulating the 
                     expression of <i>flhDC</i> |CITS: [22925049]|.
                     MicA is essential for cellular viability in a mutant with a
                     deletion in the gene <i>lapD</i>, a gene that encodes a 
                     protein that regulates lipopolysaccharide assembly |CITS: 
                     [36077106]|.
                     MicA is able to form dimers; self-association is dependent 
                     on Mg<sup>2+</sup> concentration and obstructs the 
                     <i>ompA</i> binding site |CITS: [23361466]|.
                     MicA interacts with both |FRAME: CPLX0-1461| and |FRAME: 
                     EG12866-MONOMER| |CITS: [36328526]|.
                     A <i>micA</i> deletion mutant is sensitive to cell envelope
                     stress |CITS: [19734312]| and appears unable to establish 
                     long-term stationary phase |CITS: [23594456]|. 
                     Overexpression of either MicA or RybB rescues the growth 
                     and viability phenotypes of &sigma;<sup>E</sup> depletion 
                     |CITS: [21768388]| and also leads to increased cell lysis 
                     in stationary phase |CITS: [22251246]|.  Conversely, 
                     deletion of <i>micA</i> partly restores stationary phase 
                     viability of a &Delta;<i>rseA</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [28373273]|.  Overexpression of MicA increases swimming 
                     motility and decreases swarming motility and expression of 
                     type I fimbriae |CITS: [26469694]|.
                     Expression of <i>micA</i> is controlled by the alternative 
                     sigma factor &sigma;<sup>E</sup> |CITS: 
                     [17007876][17267407][31992590]| and is increased upon entry
                     into stationary phase |CITS: [11448770][16204185]|. Proper 
                     regulation of <i>micA</i> expression by &sigma;<sup>E</sup>
                     is critical |CITS: [28373273]|.  The transcripts of many 
                     MicA targets are decreased after &sigma;<sup>E</sup> 
                     induction as expected |CITS: [31992590]|.  MicA levels in 
                     stationary phase are increased in a <i>pnp</i> mutant 
                     |CITS: [18203924]|.  The half life of <i>micA</i> in 
                     stationary phase is 4 minutes |CITS: [14602901]|.  |FRAME: 
                     EG10743-MONOMER PNPase| and |FRAME: EG10863-MONOMER| 
                     together stabilize MicA during exponential growth; 
                     stabilization is dependent on Hfq binding to the 3' tail of
                     MicA |CITS: [27698082]|. Overexpression of the sRNA |FRAME:
                     RNA0-410 MalH| decreases MicA levels |CITS: [33043783]|.
                     SraD: ""<b>s</b>mall <b>R</b>N<b>A</b> <b>D</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [11448770]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17055775][26618164][29992897][32213244]|"
