                     /EC_number="6.3.2.2"
                     /label=gshA
                     /note="<i>gshA</i> was first cloned and sequenced from
                     <i>E. coli</i> B |CITS: [2872655]|; the sequence of the 
                     <i>E. coli</i> B and K-12 genes are identical. 
                     &gamma;-Glutamate-cysteine ligase catalyzes the first of 
                     two steps in the pathway for the biosynthesis of |FRAME: 
                     GLUTATHIONE|, also referred to as GSH for its 
                     &gamma;-Glu-Cys-Gly tripeptide structure. The enzyme is 
                     feedback-inhibited by glutathione |CITS: [238647]|.
                     The enzyme contains two divalent metal ions which play a 
                     role in amino acid binding. Substrate specificity depends 
                     on whether Mg<sup>2+</sup> or Mn<sup>2+</sup> is present; 
                     Mn<sup>2+</sup> broadens the specificity |CITS: 
                     [11675389]|. Transition state analog inhibitors have been 
                     used to study substrate recognition |CITS: [12224634]|. The
                     enzyme can utilize ethylamine as a substrate, thus 
                     producing theanine (&gamma;-glutamylethylamide) |CITS: 
                     [19966457]|.
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme from <i>E. coli</i> B 
                     (whose wild-type sequence is identical to the K-12 enzyme) 
                     have been solved |CITS: [11807262][15477603]|. The 
                     structure shows coordination of an inhibitor by three 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup> ions, and showing that the binding of 
                     cysteine follows an induced-fit model |CITS: [15477603]|. 
                     Although not highly conserved, the cysteine residues C372 
                     and C395 of the intrachain disulfide bond are important for
                     catalytic activity and stability of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [23126248]|.
                     A <i>gshA</i> insertion mutant is more sensitive to 
                     thiol-specific damage |CITS: [3536846]|, mercury and 
                     arsenite |CITS: [9439611]|, and is unable to grow at high 
                     osmolarity |CITS: [1972940]|, but is not affected in its 
                     resistance to oxidative damage |CITS: [3536846]|. However, 
                     a strain overexpressing GshA and GshB is more resistant to 
                     radiation than wild type |CITS: [2564202]|. A <i>gshA 
                     trxB</i> double mutant exhibits a severe growth defect 
                     which can be suppressed by growth in rich medium containing
                     DTT or by certain mutations in <i>aphC</i> |CITS: 
                     [18456836]|. In cells lacking metal efflux systems, 
                     glutathione biosynthesis is required for resistance to 
                     Cd<sup>2+</sup>, Zn<sup>2+</sup> and Cu<sup>2+</sup> |CITS:
                     [18539744]|. The resistance of a <i>gshA</i> mutant to a 
                     variety of metals has been measured |CITS: [19555372]|. As 
                     shown by the effect of a <i>gshA</i> mutation, glutathione 
                     plays a role in formation of the active, [4Fe-4S] 
                     cluster-containing form of FNR |CITS: [10903516]|.
                     Suppressor mutations that allow a &Delta;<i>gshA</i> strain
                     to grow in the presence of arsenate map to <i>proA</i> and 
                     <i>proB</i>; enabling synthesis of glutathione |CITS: 
                     [21186348]|.
                     Transcription of <i>gshA</i> increases with increased 
                     cadmium concentration. A microarray analysis measuring the 
                     transcriptional response of wild-type and <i>gshA</i> 
                     mutant strains to cadmium stress has been performed |CITS: 
                     [18539742]|. Under oxidative stress conditions, translation
                     of <i>gshA</i> is increased by the presence of polyamines. 
                     The increase is dependent on the non-canonical UUG start 
                     codon of the <i>gshA</i> open reading frame |CITS: 
                     [25898225]|.
                     Persistant, sublethally injured <i>E. coli</i> induced by 
                     lactic acid stress exhibited slower resuscitation rates in 
                     <i>&Delta;sodB</i>, <i>&Delta;gshA</i> and 
                     <i>&Delta;gshB</i> mutants, and even more slowly in a 
                     <i>&Delta;sodA</i> mutant, relative to wild-type |CITS: 
                     [35395105]|.
                     Studies with <i>&Delta;gshA</i> mutants have shown that 
                     GshA is involved with cysteine homeostasis, and this can be
                     affected by antibiotic exposure, such as to ciprofloxacin 
                     |CITS: [31617110][38674008][38890214]|. Review: |CITS: 
                     [37371929]|"
