                     CsrA complex with McaS RNA / CsrA complex with CsrB RNA"
                     /label=csrA
                     /note="CsrA is a regulator of carbohydrate metabolism that 
                     affects glycogen biosynthesis |CITS: [7493933]|, glycogen 
                     degradation |CITS: [8576033]|, gluconeogenesis |CITS: 
                     [7493933]|, and glycolysis |CITS: [7493933]|. CsrA has a 
                     regulatory role in biofilm dynamics |CITS: [11741870]| and 
                     also plays a role in motility via posttranscriptional 
                     activation of expression of the <i>|FRAME: EG10320| and 
                     |FRAME: EG10319|</i> flagellar regulator genes |CITS: 
                     [11298291]|. CsrA positively regulates glucose-6-phosphate 
                     isomerase |CITS: [7493933]|, triose-phosphate isomerase 
                     |CITS: [7493933]|, enolase |CITS: [7493933]|, 
                     acetyl-coenzyme A synthetase |CITS: [10692369]|, and 
                     isocitrate lyase |CITS: [10692369]|, and negatively 
                     regulates fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase |CITS: [7493933]|, 
                     phosphoenolpyruvate synthetase |CITS: [7493933]|, GlgS 
                     |CITS: [8576033]|, glycogen synthase |CITS: [8576033]|, and
                     glycogen phosphorylase |CITS: [8576033]|. More recently, 
                     CsrA has been found to play a role the transition into 
                     stationary phase through regulation of expression of 
                     anti-sigma factor protein, such as |FRAME: EG11738|, and 
                     genes involved in ribosome hibernation, such as those in 
                     the |FRAME: TU0-13863| |CITS: [37943032]|. CsrA activates 
                     transcription of its inhibitor <i>|FRAME: G0-8785|</i>, 
                     whereas CsrA does not alter the half-life of <i>csrB</i> 
                     mRNA |CITS: [11567002]|. An autoregulatory loop involving 
                     the |FRAME: BARA-MONOMER BarA|-|FRAME: EG11140-MONOMER 
                     UvrY| two-component regulatory system acts in regulation of
                     <i>csrB</i> gene expression; CsrA activates UvrY, which 
                     activates transcription of <i>csrB</i>, and CsrB RNA 
                     inhibits CsrA activity |CITS: [12193630]|. UvrY-mediated 
                     <i>csrB</i> activation is partially dependent on BarA 
                     |CITS: [12193630]|. A <i>|FRAME: G0-8874|</i> mutation 
                     causes increased transcription of <i>csrB</i> |CITS: 
                     [12694612]|. CsrA destabilizes <i>|FRAME: EG10379|</i> mRNA
                     |CITS: [7751274][9226279]|, probably via a direct 
                     interaction between the KH motif of CsrA and the 
                     <i>glgC</i> RNA |CITS: [7751274]|. CsrA has been proposed 
                     to disrupt binding of the F plasmid LetD protein to the 
                     |FRAME: EG10423-MONOMER|, whereas |FRAME: G7689-MONOMER|, 
                     |FRAME: EG10741-MONOMER| and |FRAME: EG10599-MONOMER| have 
                     been proposed to antagonize CsrA |CITS: [8604133]|. CsrA 
                     modestly and conditionally activates <i>crp</i> directly 
                     and also, nonspecifically, <i>|FRAME: EG10170|</i> 
                     expression in a temperature-dependent fashion |CITS: 
                     [27551019]|.
                     The global CsrA regulon has been studied using an 
                     integrated transcriptomics approach |CITS: [29150605]|. 
                     Translation of one of the identified targets, |FRAME: 
                     G6549-MONOMER|, is activated by CsrA both <i>in vivo</i> 
                     and <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [32934077]|.
                     CsrA binds to <i>glgC</i> mRNA |CITS: [9226279]|. CsrB 
                     binding to CsrA appears to interfere with binding of CsrA 
                     to <i>glgC</i> mRNA, and CsrA acts to decrease <i>glgC</i> 
                     mRNA stability by interfering with translation of the 
                     <i>glgC</i> mRNA |CITS: [12067347]|. The CsrB and CsrC RNAs
                     each inhibit the activity of the CsrA protein |CITS: 
                     [12694612]|. The <i>csrB</i> RNA binds to CsrA protein, 
                     forming an RNA-protein complex with 1:18 stoichiometry, 
                     respectively |CITS: [9211896]|.
                     A <i>csrA</i> mutation exhibits increased expression of the
                     <i>|FRAME: EG10377|</i>, <i>|FRAME: EG10378|</i>, and 
                     <i>|FRAME: EG10688|</i> genes |CITS: [8393005]|, and causes
                     increased <i>glgC</i> mRNA abundance and stability |CITS: 
                     [7751274]|. A <i>csrA</i> mutation affects cell adherence 
                     and cell size |CITS: [8393005]|. A <i>csrA</i> mutation 
                     causes decreased (<i>ldhA</i>) gene expression, probably 
                     due to indirect effects |CITS: [11535784]|. CsrA 
                     overproduction causes decreased synthesis of glycogen 
                     |CITS: [8393005]| and decreased abundance of <i>glgC</i> 
                     mRNA |CITS: [7751274]|. A <i>csrA</i> mutation suppresses 
                     the resistance to the F-plasmid-encoded LetD DNA gyrase 
                     inhibitor exhibited by a <i>|FRAME: G7689|</i>, <i>|FRAME: 
                     EG10741|</i> or <i>|FRAME: EG10600|</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [8604133]|. A <i>csrA |FRAME: EG10510|</i> double mutant 
                     exhibits growth inhibition by acetate, and inhibition is 
                     suppressed by <i>glg</i> mutations or rescued by 
                     supplementation with TCA cycle intermediates or pyruvate. 
                     The double mutant also exhibits a defect in amino acid 
                     utilization |CITS: [10692369]|. In a study using a <i>csrA 
                     |FRAME: EG11005|</i> double mutant, |FRAME: INDOLE| was 
                     found to directly inhibit transcription of csrA and 
                     indirectly regulate post-transcription of <i>flhDC, 
                     glgCAP</i> and <i>|FRAME: EG10167|</i> and their functions 
                     via CsrA |CITS: [37423745]|.
                     Purification of His-tagged CsrA is described |CITS: 
                     [9211896]|. CsrA has been used as a tool to engineer 
                     metabolism alterations |CITS: [11375660]|.
                     CsrA-mediated regulation is relieved during the stringent 
                     response. The Csr system fine-tunes the stringent response 
                     |CITS: [21488981]|.
                     The |FRAME: EG12342-MONOMER| was found to phosphorylate 
                     CsrA at Ser56, which negatively affects its function |CITS:
                     [36537800]|.
                     CsrA:  ""carbon storage regulator"" |CITS: [8393005]|. 
                     Regulation has been described |CITS: 
                     [9211896][12067347][12694612][11567002][12533459]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9781871][9084762][10744991][11768529][204 
                     46015][23699406][29573256][33193284][38488356]|"
