     CDS             complement(2815498..2815998)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="recX"
                     /locus_tag="b2698"
                     /product="RecA inhibitor RecX"
                     /label=recX
                     /note="RecX is an inhibitor of |FRAME: EG10823-MONOMER
                     RecA|. RecX interacts directly with RecA, inhibits its 
                     ssDNA-dependent ATPase, coprotease, and DNA strand exchange
                     activities |CITS: [12427742][15466870]|, and blocks the 
                     extension of RecA filaments |CITS: [15350222]| or promotes 
                     RecA filament disassembly |CITS: [18650935]|. RecX itself 
                     has weak ssDNA binding activity |CITS: [15350222]|; 
                     functional competition between |FRAME: EG10976-MONOMER SSB|
                     and RecX suggest that an interaction of RecX with ssDNA may
                     be part of the mechanism by which RecX inhibits RecA |CITS:
                     [18385131]|. RecX binds from the C-terminal domain of one 
                     RecA subunit to the nucleotide-binding core of another 
                     subunit inhibiting ATPase activity of RecA |CITS: 
                     [14529621]|.
                     The interaction between RecX and RecA itself is modulated 
                     by other proteins.  RecX competes directly with |FRAME: 
                     G6558-MONOMER DinI|, a positive modulator of RecA function,
                     for binding to RecA |CITS: [15489505]|. Both proteins 
                     modulate RecA-DNA structures, with <i>dinI</i> being 
                     epistatic to <i>recX</i>, which is consistent with DinI 
                     acting upstream of RecX |CITS: [17163974]|. |FRAME: 
                     EG10828-MONOMER RecF| protects nucleated RecA filaments 
                     from RecX inhibition |CITS: [16387652]|.
                     A structural model of RecX has been proposed, and its 
                     interaction with RecA |CITS: [14680809]| and ssDNA |CITS: 
                     [24530684]| was modeled. The interaction between RecX and 
                     RecA is modulated by the C-terminal domain of RecA and the 
                     concentration of free Mg<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [15466870][17163974]|. Single-molecule studies revealed 
                     several mechanisms by which RecX regulates RecA-ssDNA 
                     filaments, and a model of the regulation under conditions 
                     of continuous ATP hydrolysis was proposed |CITS: 
                     [35730924]|.
                     A crystal structure of RecX from <i>E. coli</i> E24377A 
                     (99% sequence identity with the <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     protein) has been solved at 1.8 &Aring; resolution, showing
                     a modular protein with repeated three-helix motifs. 
                     Site-directed mutagenesis of conserved basic residues on 
                     the concave side of RecX showed that they are important for
                     repression of RecA activity <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [18650935]|.
                     Transcription of <i>recX</i> is regulated by LexA |CITS: 
                     [12427742]|. Expression is induced by DNA damage |CITS: 
                     [12427742]|, nalidixic acid or mitomycin C |CITS: 
                     [11544210]|. Although <i>recA</i> and <i>recX</i> are 
                     cotranscribed, RecX is approximately 500 times less 
                     abundant than RecA. SOS transcriptional regulation and a 
                     translational regulation process each contribute to 
                     regulation of RecX abundance |CITS: [12547390]|. 
                     Overproduction of RecX causes UV sensitivity and additional
                     phenotypes that are consistent with RecA inhibition, such 
                     as defects in DNA damage induction of transcription and 
                     proteolysis of UmuD and LexA, and less P1 transduction 
                     compared to wild type |CITS: [12427742]|. Published reports
                     differ as to whether there is a small |CITS: 
                     [12427742][17163974]| or no |CITS: [12547390]| difference 
                     in UV sensitivity of a <i>recX</i> null mutant compared to 
                     wild type. A null mutation does not cause increased 
                     mutagenesis |CITS: [12547390]|, defects in DNA damage 
                     induction of transcription and proteolysis of UmuD and LexA
                     |CITS: [12427742]|, or defects in P1 transduction |CITS: 
                     [12427742]|. Overproduction of RecA is not lethal in a 
                     <i>recX</i> null mutant background, in contrast to the 
                     situation observed in other organisms |CITS: [12547390]|.  
                     RecX does not appear to regulate transcription of 
                     <i>recA</i> |CITS: [12547390]|. In wild-type cells, RecX 
                     itself does not appear to play a significant role in 
                     repression of SOS expression |CITS: [22843848]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [17364684]|"
