     CDS             complement(2816067..2817128)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="recA"
                     /locus_tag="b2699"
                     /product="DNA recombination/repair protein RecA"
                     /label=recA
                     /note="RecA is a DNA strand exchange protein that is
                     central to the process of homologous recombination. RecA 
                     assembles on single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) tracts, such as 
                     those formed when replication forks collapse or stall and, 
                     in the presence of ATP, converts to an activated form - the
                     RecA* nucleoprotein filament. RecA* filaments promote DNA 
                     strand exchange reactions (synapsis) during the 
                     recombinational repair of chromosomal damage and during 
                     bacterial conjugation (see current reviews |CITS: 
                     [26442506][27156117]|). RecA* filaments also promote 
                     autocatalytic cleavage of the LexA repressor leading to 
                     induction of the bacterial SOS response and are a required 
                     component for the mutagenic bypass of DNA lesions during 
                     the SOS reponse (see |CITS: 
                     [20441441][27043933][30447030]|).
                     <i>recA</i> mutations are associated with a range of 
                     pleiotropic phenotypes including the inability to form 
                     recombinants by conjugation and high sensitivity to UV 
                     radiation |CITS: [14294081]| (and see |CITS: 
                     [4949037][4205905][8831293]|).
                     RecA* acts as a coprotease to stimulate the autocatalytic 
                     cleavage of the SOS response repressor, |FRAME: PC00010 
                     ""LexA""|, |CITS: [6447873][7027255][2554312]| resulting in
                     the increased expression of numerous genes which constitute
                     the SOS response (see for example reviews by |CITS: 
                     [7049397][3899189][26443738][24923554]|). The ternary 
                     complex between RecA, ssDNA and LexA constitutes the 'SOS 
                     signal complex'; a complete structure of this complex has 
                     been published and provides insight into the mechanism by 
                     which the SOS response orchestrates gene induction in a 
                     chronological fashion |CITS: [38755298]|. RecA* is required
                     to activate the mutagenic DNA polymerase |FRAME: CPLX0-3925
                     ""Pol V""|, induced during the late stages of the SOS 
                     reponse. Acting as a coprotease, RecA* promotes the 
                     autocatalytic cleavage of UmuD to form UmuD', which 
                     dimerizes and binds to UmuC to form Pol V; RecA* activates 
                     Pol V by transferring an ATP-bound RecA monomer from its 3'
                     proximal end to form the active Pol V mutasome (comprising 
                     UmuD'<sub>2</sub>-UmuC-RecA-ATP) |CITS: [19606142]|.
                     Acting as a coprotease, RecA* also promotes self-cleavage 
                     of the cI repressor of bacteriophage &lambda;, which 
                     induces the shift from lysogeny to induction and lytic 
                     growth |CITS: [1090931]|.
                     The binding of RecA to single-stranded DNA in regions of 
                     stalled replication may serve a structural role - 
                     protecting ssDNA from degradation by exonucleases while 
                     awaiting repair (see |CITS: [12543983]|). RecA 
                     filamentation is important for genomic stability when 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3621| is dysregulated |CITS: [38808668]|. 
                     <i>tif</i>: <U>t</U>hermal <U>i</U>nduction of &lambda; and
                     thermal <U>f</U>ilamentation |CITS: 
                     [4565754]|<BR><i>zab</i> |CITS: [4577433]|<BR><i>lexB</i>: 
                     |CITS: [328486]|<BR>and see also |CITS: [331073]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [7023698][3899189][1883888][1911941][18838 
                     77][8240315][7709802][8048163][8954148][8882707][9013652][1
                     0506835][11987178][12045091][14527291][14616075][15078218][
                     16194225][17228330][17364684][27156117][31654073]|<BR>Relat
                     ed reviews: |CITS: 
                     [8080625][7968921][9632377][10585965][1134 
                     0628][11459985][11397077][18081011][21599536][26459995][29 
                     599286][37212693]|"
