     gene            complement(2817850..2818935)
                     /gene="mltB"
                     /locus_tag="b2701"
                     /label=mltB
     CDS             complement(2817850..2818935)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mltB"
                     /locus_tag="b2701"
                     /product="trifunctional membrane-bound lytic murein 
                     transglycosylase B / peptidoglycan lytic 
                     endotransglycosylase / peptidoglycan lytic 
                     exotransglycosylase"
                     /label=mltB
                     /note="<i>mltB</i> encodes a membrane bound lytic murein 
                     transglycosylase which cleaves the &beta;-1,4 glycosidic 
                     bond between <I>N</I>-acetylglucosamine (GlcNAc) and 
                     <I>N</I>-acetylmuramic acid (MurNAc) residues of 
                     peptidoglycan with the concomitant formation of a 
                     1,6-anhydro ring at the MurNAc residue.
                     MltB is an outer membrane lipoprotein |CITS: [7476170]|. 
                     Proteolytic cleavage of MltB releases a soluble, active 
                     form of the protein (known as Slt35) |CITS: 
                     [1356966][7476170]|. Purified Slt35 has peptidoglycan 
                     hydrolase activity, releasing 1,6-anhydromuropeptides from 
                     isolated peptidoglycan polymer |CITS: [7789526]|. Purified,
                     soluble MltB is able to degrade a synthetic peptidoglycan 
                     substrate containing a short chain backbone of only two 
                     repeats of GlcNAc-MurNAc |CITS: [18700763]|. Purified MltB 
                     (residues 21-361) degrades the purified cell wall sacculus 
                     to produce the following major products (in order of 
                     abundance): tetra-A1 (GlcNAc-1,6-anhydro-MurNAc monomer 
                     with tetrapeptide stem); tetra2-A2 (a 
                     GlcNAc-1,6-anhydro-MurNAc dimer with crosslinked 
                     tetrapeptide stems); tri-A1 (GlcNAc-1,6-anhydro-MurNAc 
                     monomer with tripeptide stem) plus other variations in 
                     smaller amounts. The reaction generates both cross-linked 
                     and non-crosslinked products; it also generates products 
                     that are indicative of an endolytic transglycosylase 
                     reaction and some products containing a reducing end (ie 
                     containing a terminal MurNAc residue rather than a 
                     1,6-anhydro-MurNAc residue) |CITS: [23421439]|.
                     Purified, crystallised Slt35 consists of an &alpha; helical
                     rich &alpha; domain, a &beta; domain and a core domain 
                     located between the two which binds peptidoglycan strands 
                     and contains the active site. The core domain also contains
                     a metal binding site that resembles an EF-hand calcium 
                     binding domain |CITS: [9761817][10545329][10684641]|. 
                     Purified Slt35 binds calcium or sodium ions; calcium 
                     binding increases the thermostability of the protein |CITS:
                     [10570954]|
                     Purified MltB interacts with |FRAME: EG10605-MONOMER 
                     ""PBP1B""|, |FRAME: G7322-MONOMER ""PBP1C""| and |FRAME: 
                     EG10341-MONOMER ""PBP3""| <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [9158739]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>mltB</i> results in rapid cell lysis; 
                     controlled overexpression results in a significant increase
                     in murein hydrolase activity in the total cell homogenate 
                     |CITS: [7476170]|. Deletion of <i>mltB</i> gene results in 
                     no obvious growth phenotype and no significant change in 
                     muropeptide composition |CITS: [7476170]|.  A strain 
                     lacking three lytic transglycosylases, MltA, MltB and Slt70
                     shows no change in morphology or growth but significant 
                     changes to peptidoglycan composition including decreased 
                     anhydromuropeptide content and an increase in L-D peptide 
                     cross links |CITS: [9287002]|. A strain lacking MltA, MltB 
                     and Slt70 shows a significant reduction in murein turnover 
                     without affecting growth |CITS:[10572120]|. The Keio 
                     collection <i>mltB</i> mutant exhibits a 'low level' of 
                     resistance to lithium exposure |CITS: [39983623]|.
                     <I>E. coli</I> K-12 contains other lytic murein 
                     transglycosylases including |FRAME: G7457-MONOMER 
                     ""MltA""|, |FRAME: G7533-MONOMER ""MltC""|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10246-MONOMER ""MltD|, |FRAME: EG11373-MONOMER ""MltF""|,
                     |FRAME: G6622-MONOMER ""EmtA""| and |FRAME: EG10950-MONOMER
                     ""Slt70""|. |FRAME: EG11494 ""MltG""|, an inner membrane 
                     protein and potential PG terminase, also has lytic murein 
                     transglycosylase activity.
                     Related reviews:  |CITS: [8765311][18266855][22126997]|"
