     gene            2823172..2824137
                     /gene="gutQ"
                     /locus_tag="b2708"
                     /label=gutQ
     CDS             2823172..2824137
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gutQ"
                     /locus_tag="b2708"
                     /product="bifunctional D-arabinose 5-phosphate isomerase
                     GutQ / D-arabinose 5-phosphate isomerase"
                     /EC_number="5.3.1.13"
                     /label=gutQ
                     /note="D-arabinose-5-phosphate is a precursor for 
                     2-keto-3-deoxy-octulosonate (KDO), a constituent of the 
                     cell envelope lipopolysaccharide.  Arabinose-5-phosphate 
                     isomerase is responsible for the interconversion of 
                     D-ribulose-5-phosphate and D-arabinose-5-phosphate, the 
                     first committed step in the biosynthesis of KDO.  The 
                     enzyme was originally isolated and partially characterized 
                     from <i>E. coli</i> strain B |CITS: [5332197]|. <i>gutQ</i>
                     encodes the second of two D-arabinose 5-phosphate 
                     isomerases (API) in <i>E. coli</i>.  Its biochemical 
                     properties are very similar to those of |FRAME: CPLX0-1262 
                     KdsD|, the first identified D-arabinose 5-phosphate 
                     isomerase.  Strains containing single mutations in either 
                     the <i>kdsD</i> or <i>gutQ</i> genes are viable and have a 
                     functional LPS.  A <i>kdsD gutQ</i> double mutant strain 
                     required the addition of exogenous arabinose 5-phosphate 
                     for viability |CITS: [16199563]|. GutQ has previously been 
                     shown to have a sugar isomerase (SIS) domain |CITS: 
                     [10203754]| and a predicted ATP binding site |CITS: 
                     [2134185]|.  GutQ also has similarity to of <i>E. coli</i> 
                     K1 KpsF, which plays a role in polysialic acid capsule 
                     expression |CITS: [9383150]|. <i>gutQ</i> is the last gene 
                     in the <i>gut</i> operon; its expression is induced by 
                     glucitol |CITS: [2134185][16199563]|.  GutQ appears to be 
                     involved in biofilm formation.  A mutant with a deletion of
                     <i>gutQ</i> shows a significant decrease in biofilm 
                     formation.  Expression of <i>gutQ</i> increases 9-fold upon
                     deletion of <i>tqsA</i>, which increases biofilm formation 
                     |CITS: [16385049]|.  A <i>gutQ kdsD</i> double mutant is 
                     not viable |CITS: [16765569]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [23974348]|"
