     gene            complement(2828532..2830784)
                     /gene="hypF"
                     /locus_tag="b2712"
                     /label=hypF
     CDS             complement(2828532..2830784)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hypF"
                     /locus_tag="b2712"
                     /product="carbamoyl--[HypE] ligase"
                     /label=hypF
                     /note="HypF is one of a number of proteins required for 
                     maturation of |FRAME: FORMHYDROGI-CPLX|, |FRAME: 
                     FORMHYDROG2-CPLX| and |FRAME: HYDROG3-CPLX| |CITS: 
                     [8661925][8756471][20807532]|.
                     In the absence of other substrates, HypF hydrolyzes 
                     carbamoyl phosphate |CITS: [12377778]|.  However, under 
                     physiological conditions in the presence of |FRAME: 
                     EG10487-MONOMER HypE|, HypF functions as a carbamoyl 
                     transferase, generating |FRAME: MONOMER0-4166| at the 
                     C-terminal cysteine residue of HypE |CITS: [12586941]|. 
                     This reaction may involve the formation of a 
                     carbamoyladenylate intermediate |CITS: [22153500]|.
                     During the carbamoyl transfer reaction, HypE and HypF form 
                     a tight stochiometric complex |CITS: [15291820]|; the 
                     interaction has been characterized in detail |CITS: 
                     [18065529]|.
                     Crystal structures of HypF lacking the N-terminal 
                     acylphosphatase domain have been solved.  Mutations in the 
                     N-terminal nucleotide-binding site 1 abolished the 
                     hydrogenase maturation activity of HypF; the C-terminal 
                     nucleotide-binding site 2 is also required for full 
                     activity |CITS: [22153500]|.
                     <i>In vitro</i> maturation of |FRAME: FORMHYDROG2-CPLX| 
                     with protein components including HypF has been achieved 
                     |CITS: [28539366]|.
                     The N-terminal acylphosphatase domain of HypF forms 
                     amyloid-like protein aggregates under certain conditions 
                     |CITS: [11714922]|, and its folding and toxicity have been 
                     studied; e.g. see |CITS: [11932737][12842473][14690417][18 
                     466920][20081829][22326346][23041425][30020284]|. Crystal 
                     structures of this domain have been solved at 1.3 &Aring; 
                     resolution |CITS: [12206761]|.
                     A <i>hypF</i> in-frame deletion mutant lacks significant 
                     hydrogenase activity due to defects in hydrogenase 
                     maturation |CITS: [8661925]|.  A <i>hypF</i> insertion 
                     mutant lacking all hydrogenase activity shows significantly
                     decreased acid resistance during stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [17026754]|.
                     The <i>hydN-hypF</i> operon is maximally transcribed under 
                     anaerobic conditions in the presence of formate and is 
                     dependent on FhlA |CITS: [8661925]|.
                     HypF: ""affect the activity of all three 
                     <b>hy</b>drogenases in a <b>p</b>leiotropic manner"" 
                     Review: |CITS: [17216401]|"
