                     /locus_tag="b2725"
                     /label=hycA
     CDS             complement(2843333..2843794)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hycA"
                     /locus_tag="b2725"
                     /product="regulator of the transcriptional regulator FhlA"
                     /label=hycA
                     /note="This regulator participates in controlling several
                     genes involved in the formate hydrogenlyase system
                     |CITS: [1625581][10848985][12426353]|. The mechanism of 
                     regulation by HycA is unknown. HycA may interact
                     directly with the FhlA protein or prevent the binding of 
                     FhlA to activator sequences.
                     Expression of <i>hycA</i> is activated by formate and 
                     hypophosphite (a formate analog) |CITS: [8022272]|."
                     /translation="MTIWEISEKADYIAQRHRRLQDQWHIYCNSLVQGITLSKARLHHA
                     MSCAPDKELCFVLFEHFRIYVTLADGFNSHTIEYYVETKDGEDKQRIAQAQLSIDGMID
                     GKVNIRDREQVLEHYLEKIAGVYDSLYTAIENNVPVNLSQLVKGQSPAA"
                     /go_component="cytosol [goid 0005829] [evidence IEA] [PMID
                     19054114]"
     gene            2844006..2844356
                     /gene="hypA"
                     /locus_tag="b2726"
                     /label=hypA
     CDS             2844006..2844356
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hypA"
                     /locus_tag="b2726"
                     /product="trifunctional hydrogenase 3 Ni(2+) incorporation
                     protein HypA / Ni(2+) insertion into hydrogenase large 
                     subunit / HypA-HypB heterodimer"
                     /label=hypA
                     /note="HypA and its homolog, HybF, are involved in the 
                     maturation of hydrogenase isozymes. A mutation in 
                     <i>hypA</i> almost completely abolishes hydrogenase 3 
                     activity |CITS: [1482271]|. HypA is specifically involved 
                     in hydrogenase 3 maturation, while HybF is involved in the 
                     maturation of hydrogenase 1 and 2; HypA and HybF can only 
                     partially substitute for each other |CITS: [12081959]|. The
                     presence of high levels of nickel can phenotypically rescue
                     <i>hypA</i> and <i>hybF</i> mutants and a triple <i>hypA 
                     hypB hybF</i> mutant to a low level |CITS: 
                     [12081959][15090500]|.
                     HypA has both a nanomolar-affinity Zn<sup>2+</sup> binding 
                     site and a micromolar-affinity Ni<sup>2+</sup> binding 
                     site. The Zn<sup>2+</sup> binding site appears to play a 
                     structural role, and the Zn<sup>2+</sup> content modulates 
                     oligomeric complex formation |CITS: [15995183]|. The 
                     K<sub>D</sub> of the Ni<sup>2+</sup> binding was later 
                     reevaluated and found to be 75 nM |CITS: [23899293]|. 
                     Interactions of HypA with other proteins have been shown to
                     be important for hydrogenase 3 maturation. HypA and |FRAME:
                     EG10484-MONOMER HypB|, another accessory protein for 
                     hydrogenase maturation, interact directly |CITS: 
                     [15995183][22384275]|, and HypA by itself can interact with
                     the large subunit of hydrogenase 3, |FRAME: 
                     HYCELARGE-MONOMER HycE|. HypA may thus act as a scaffold 
                     for assembly of HypB and SlyD, the proteins responsible for
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup> insertion to [NiFe] hydrogenase 3 |CITS: 
                     [22016389]|. However, in competitive binding experiments, 
                     SlyD is able to block the interaction of HypB with HypA; 
                     thus, it appears that a hierarchy of binary protein-protein
                     interactions supports the nickel delivery process |CITS: 
                     [30758762]|.
                     The GDP-bound form of HypB promotes formation of the 
                     HypA-HypB complex |CITS: [27951644]|. Within the HypA-HypB 
                     complex, the Ni<sup>2+</sup> ion migrates from the GTPase 
                     domain binding site in HypB to HypA |CITS: [23899293]|. 
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup> transfer from HypB to HypA involves 
                     modification of the nickel coordination environment of HypA
                     by HypB |CITS: [31273981]|. When HypA is fully loaded with 
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup>, the complex dissociates |CITS: 
                     [27951644]|.
                     A HypA His2Gln mutant shows reduced nickel affinity (from a
                     K<sub>D</sub> of 0.04 &micro;M to 4 &micro;M), inhibits 
                     migration of Ni<sup>2+</sup> from HypB, and does not 
                     support hydrogenase maturation |CITS: [27951644]|. 
                     Conversely, mutagenesis of the E3, C38 or E40 residues 
                     still enables active hydrogenase production |CITS: 
                     [31273981]|.
                     HypA: <b>hy</b>drogenase, <b>p</b>leiotropic
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17216401][22970729]|"
                     /translation="MHEITLCQRALELIEQQAAKHGAKRVTGVWLKIGAFSCVETSSLA
                     FCFDLVCRGSVAEGCKLHLEEQEAECWCETCQQYVTLLTQRVRRCPQCHGDMLQIVADD
                     GLQIRRIEIDQE"
