     gene            2844360..2845232
                     /gene="hypB"
                     /locus_tag="b2727"
                     /label=hypB
     CDS             2844360..2845232
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hypB"
                     /locus_tag="b2727"
                     /product="multifunctional hydrogenase isoenzymes Ni(2+) 
                     incorporation protein HypB / Ni(2+) transfer to HypA / 
                     GTPase / HypA-HypB heterodimer / hydrogenase isozymes 
                     Ni(2+) incorporation protein HypB"
                     /label=hypB
                     /note="The HypB protein is an accessory protein involved
                     in the maturation of all hydrogenase isoenzymes. It is 
                     required for the incorporation of nickel into the large 
                     subunit of hydrogenases <i>in vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [3015243][1849603][1482271][8756471]|. HypB binds 
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup> with sub-picomolar K<sub>D</sub> in 
                     approximately stoichiometric amounts; the binding site was 
                     localized to the N-terminal CXXCGC motif and characterized 
                     extensively |CITS: [16142921][18834129]|. A short peptide 
                     containing this motif retains Ni<sup>2+</sup> binding 
                     activity |CITS: [22415482]|. A low-affinity metal binding 
                     site with preference for Zn<sup>2+</sup> exists in the 
                     C-terminal GTPase domain |CITS: [16142921][18942856]|. Both
                     sites are required for HypB activity |CITS: [18942856]|.
                     The N-terminal high affinity site can bind both 
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup> and Zn<sup>2+</sup>; however, the 
                     coordination environment of the two metal ions differs. 
                     Based on data from XAS, modeling, and mutagenesis 
                     experiments, the Glu9 residue coordinates Zn<sup>2+</sup>, 
                     but not Ni<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [28352890]|.
                     The properties of the metal binding site within the GTPase 
                     domain are modulated by nucleotide binding. While the 
                     dissociation constant for Zn<sup>2+</sup> is unaffected, 
                     the K<sub>D</sub> for Ni<sup>2+</sup> significantly 
                     decreases in the presence of GDP and further decreases when
                     GppCp is supplied |CITS: [27951644]|.
                     The metal specificity and binding affinity of peptides 
                     comprising the two metal binding sites was measured by 
                     |CITS: [34545874]|.
                     Overproduced HypB appears to form a homodimer in solution 
                     |CITS: [8423137]|. Binding of nucleotide enhances 
                     dimerization, but dimerization itself is not required for 
                     activity of HypB |CITS: [21544686]|. HypB is able to bind 
                     and hydrolyze guanine nucleotides |CITS: [8423137]|, and 
                     the GTP hydrolysis activity of HypB is required for the 
                     incorporation of nickel into hydrogenases |CITS: 
                     [7601092]|.
                     Interactions of HypB with a number of other proteins have 
                     been shown to be important for hydrogenase maturation. 
                     |FRAME: EG11663-MONOMER SlyD|, a peptidyl prolyl cis-trans 
                     isomerase, has been shown to interact directly with HypB 
                     |CITS: [15569666]|. A loop in the SlyD chaperone domain is 
                     required for interaction with HypB, and the interaction is 
                     important for hydrogenase maturation. The C-terminal 
                     metal-binding domain of SlyD is required for stimulating 
                     release of Ni<sup>2+</sup> from the high-affinity 
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup> binding site of HypB and for hydrogenase 
                     activation |CITS: [17426034][22310044]|. Contributing to 
                     the metal specificity of the system, SlyD stimulates 
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup> dissociation, but prevents Zn<sup>2+</sup> 
                     dissociation from HypB |CITS: [28352890]|. SlyD enhances 
                     the GTPase activity of HypB |CITS: [22085337]|, and HypB 
                     and SlyD form a tighter complex in the presence of GDP 
                     |CITS: [30758762]|.
                     |FRAME: EG10483-MONOMER HypA| is another accessory protein 
                     for hydrogenase maturation. HypA and HypB interact directly
                     |CITS: [15995183]|; Leu78 and Val80 of HypB are required 
                     for this interaction and for hydrogenase maturation |CITS: 
                     [22384275]|. HypA may act as a scaffold for assembly of the
                     proteins responsible for Ni<sup>2+</sup> insertion to 
                     [NiFe] hydrogenase |CITS: [22016389]|. The GDP-bound form 
                     of HypB promotes formation of the HypA-HypB complex |CITS: 
                     [27951644]|. Within the HypA-HypB complex, the 
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup> ion migrates from the GTPase domain binding
                     site in HypB to HypA. Ni<sup>2+</sup> transfer is 
                     accelerated when GDP is bound to HypB |CITS: [23899293]|. 
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup> transfer from HypB to HypA involves 
                     modification of the nickel coordination environment of HypA
                     by HypB |CITS: [31273981]|. In competitive binding 
                     experiments, SlyD is able to block the interaction of HypB 
                     with HypA; thus, it appears that a hierarchy of binary 
                     protein-protein interactions supports the nickel delivery 
                     process |CITS: [30758762]|.
                     The GTP-binding pocket of HypB was found to bind the 
                     alarmone (p)ppGpp with a K<sub>d</sub> comparable to that 
                     of GTP; (p)ppGpp may therefore regulate the maturation of 
                     hydrogenases |CITS: [29511080]|.
                     HypB is synthesized under anaerobic conditions; 
                     approximately 25,000 molecules of HypB are present in the 
                     cell under those conditions |CITS: [8423137]|.
                     The phenotype of a mutation in <i>hypB</i> can be partially
                     suppressed by high levels of nickel ions in the medium 
                     |CITS: [1849603]|.
                     Overexpression of the HypB and HypC accessory proteins 
                     improves the survivability of <i>E. coli</i> when grown 
                     under lethal acid stress from |FRAME: D-LACTATE| |CITS: 
                     [35271275]|.
                     HypB: ""<b>hy</b>drogenase, <b>p</b>leiotropic""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17091562][17216401][22970729]|"
