     gene            2845495..2846616
                     /gene="hypD"
                     /locus_tag="b2729"
                     /label=hypD
     CDS             2845495..2846616
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hypD"
                     /locus_tag="b2729"
                     /product="bifunctional Fe-(CN)2CO cofactor assembly
                     scaffold protein HypD / HypCD complex involved in 
                     hydrogenase maturation"
                     /label=hypD
                     /note="HypD is a scaffold protein for assembly of the
                     |FRAME: CPD-24862| of [NiFe] hydrogenases |CITS: 
                     [23597401]| and is the only Hyp protein that is 
                     redox-active |CITS: [17091562]|.  HypD  is essential for 
                     the maturation of all three hydrogenase isoenzymes |CITS: 
                     [1849603][1482271][8756471]|.
                     HypD has been shown to interact directly with HypC and its 
                     paralog HybG |CITS: [12441107][15504408]| and with HypE 
                     |CITS: [16412426]|.  HypD contains a [4Fe-4S]<sup>2+</sup> 
                     cluster |CITS: [15504408]|, and seven conserved cysteine 
                     residues are required for its function |CITS: [16814778]|. 
                     The highly conserved C41 residue is required for 
                     coordination of the three ligands, CO, CN<sup>-</sup>, and 
                     CO<sub>2</sub> |CITS: [23022438]|.
                     Anaerobically purified HypD carries both CO and 
                     CN<sup>-</sup> ligands associated with a 
                     non-[4Fe-4S]<sup>2+</sup> cluster iron ion that is likely 
                     coordinated by C41, C69 and C72 |CITS: [23597401]|. C69 and
                     C72 are contained within a conserved thiol-disulfide 
                     thioredoxin fold that links the [4Fe-4S]<sup>2+</sup> 
                     cluster with C41 |CITS: [17612488][23123111]|. Based on 
                     structural modeling, C69 and C72 can udergo reversible 
                     disulfide bond formation |CITS: [28029041][34195174]|. HypD
                     has low intrinsic ATPase activity; the purified HypC-HypD 
                     and HybG-HypD complexes catalyze hydrolysis of ATP with a 
                     5-fold higher k<sub>cat</sub> of ~ 0.85 s<sup>-1</sup>.  
                     The Cys41 residue of HypD is required for the stimulated 
                     ATPase activity of the heterodimeric complexes |CITS: 
                     [31614069]|. HybG-HypD complexes lacking the [4Fe-4S] 
                     cluster retain partial ATPase activity. However, 
                     substitution of C69 and (particularly) C72 individually 
                     with alanine abolished hydrogenase activity and strongly 
                     reduced the ATPase activity |CITS: [36602404]|.
                     Maturation of Hyd-1 and Hyd-2 can be reconstituted <i>in 
                     vitro</i> with anaerobically purified HybG-HypDE under 
                     reducing conditions in the presence of ATP, 
                     carbamoylphosphate, and nickel |CITS: [25184670]|.  <i>In 
                     vitro</i> maturation of |FRAME: FORMHYDROG2-CPLX| with 
                     protein components including HypD has been achieved |CITS: 
                     [28539366]|.
                     HypD: ""<b>hy</b>drogenase, <b>p</b>leiotropic"" |CITS: 
                     [1849603]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17216401][26919691][27735784]|."
