     gene            2846613..2847623
                     /gene="hypE"
                     /locus_tag="b2730"
                     /label=hypE
     CDS             2846613..2847623
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hypE"
                     /locus_tag="b2730"
                     /product="multifunctional carbamoyl dehydratase HypE / 
                     [HypE]:[HypC/D] cyanotransferase / [HypE]-S-carboxamide 
                     dehydratase / a HypCDE trimer"
                     /label=hypE
                     /note="HypE is one of the hydrogenase maturation proteins 
                     required for the assembly of the CN ligand of the NiFe 
                     metal center of |FRAME: FORMHYDROGI-CPLX|, |FRAME: 
                     FORMHYDROG2-CPLX| and |FRAME: HYDROG3-CPLX|.
                     At least seven gene products are involved in the maturation
                     of hydrogenases, which includes the generation and 
                     incorporation of the CN ligand from carbamoylphosphate 
                     (CP).  First, |FRAME: EG11551-MONOMER HypF| converts CP and
                     ATP to carbamoyladenylate, inorganic phosphate, 
                     pyrophosphate, and AMP |CITS: [12377778]|.  It then 
                     transfers the carbamoyl group to the carboxy terminal 
                     cysteine of HypE |CITS: [12586941]|.  During the carbamoyl 
                     transfer reaction, HypE and HypF form a tight 
                     stoichiometric complex |CITS: [15291820]| where HypE 
                     catalyzes the ATP hydrolysis-dependent dehydration of the 
                     carbamate group to a cyanate group |CITS: 
                     [15291820][12586941]|.  The mechanism of the conversion of 
                     the primary amide (carbamate) to the nitrile (cyanate) has 
                     been structurally investigated in the <i>Thermococcus 
                     kodakarensis</i> protein |CITS: [24297906]|.  IR 
                     spectroscopy suggests that the thiocyanate group is 
                     converted to an isothiocyanate group; this may play a role 
                     in substrate channeling |CITS: [26186649]|.  Cyanate is 
                     then transferred to the HypC-HypD complex |CITS: 
                     [15504408]| and from there to the hydrogenase.
                     <i>In vitro</i> maturation of |FRAME: FORMHYDROG2-CPLX| 
                     with protein components including HypE has been achieved 
                     |CITS: [28539366]|.
                     Purified HypE displays low intrinsic ATPase activity |CITS:
                     [15291820]|.  HypE can form a homodimer via a disulfide 
                     bridge formed by the C-terminal cysteine residue.  It is 
                     unclear whether the disulfide-bridged dimer has biological 
                     significance |CITS: [15291820]|.  A crystal structure of 
                     apo-HypE from <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7 (98% identical to the 
                     K-12 protein) has been solved at 2 &Aring; resolution, and 
                     a reaction mechanism has been proposed |CITS: [18065529]|.
                     A <i>hypE</i> deletion mutant contains no active 
                     hydrogenase 1, 2 or 3 |CITS: [1482271]|.  HypE is required 
                     for maturation of hydrogenase 3 <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [8756471]|.
                     HypE: ""affect the activity of all three 
                     <b>hy</b>drogenases in a <b>p</b>leiotropic manner"" |CITS:
                     [1849603]|
                     Review: |CITS: [17216401]|
                     "
