     gene            complement(2853826..2854593)
                     /gene="ygbI"
                     /locus_tag="b2735"
                     /label=ygbI
     CDS             complement(2853826..2854593)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ygbI"
                     /locus_tag="b2735"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional repressor YgbI"
                     /label=ygbI
                     /note="YgbI was predicted to be a DeoR-type transcription
                     factor |CITS: [34428301] [35880217]|. It contains a 
                     helix-turn-helix motif to bind DNA in its N-terminal domain
                     |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     YgbI DNA-binding activity was probed in vivo in glucose M9 
                     minimal medium through chromatin immunoprecipitation assays
                     (ChIP-exo), which identified a single binding region in 
                     between the <i>ygbI</i> and <i>ygbJK</i> divergent 
                     transcription units |CITS: [34428301]|. By RNA-seq of the 
                     wild-type and <i>ygbI</i> knockout strains, it was 
                     determined that transcription of <i>ygbJ</i> and 
                     <i>ygbK</i> is negatively regulated by YgbI |CITS: 
                     [34428301]|. In conjunction, ChIP-exo and RNA-seq studies 
                     indicated that <i>ygbJK</i> is a direct target of YgbI 
                     |CITS: [34428301]|.
                     Upstream of the <i>ygbI</i> gene, a sequence was found that
                     could work as a transcriptional promoter, and the 
                     transcription of the <i>ygbI</i> gene is affected by 
                     glucose-lactose shift and heat shock |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     A <i>ygbI</i> knockout strain showed growth in M9 minimal 
                     medium with 20 mM L-tartrate, while the wild-type strain 
                     was unable to grow under these culture conditions |CITS: 
                     [34428301]|.
                     The deletion of <i>ygbI</i> substantially decreases 
                     viability in soil |CITS:[39905026]|.
                     The YgbI binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
