     gene            complement(2859918..2860910)
                     /gene="rpoS"
                     /locus_tag="b2741"
                     /label=rpoS
     CDS             complement(2859918..2860910)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpoS"
                     /locus_tag="b2741"
                     /product="bifunctional RNA polymerase sigma factor RpoS /
                     RNA polymerase sigma S"
                     /label=rpoS
                     /note="<i>rpoS</i> encodes the alternative sigma factor 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, a subunit of RNA polymerase that acts 
                     as the master regulator of the general stress response in 
                     <i>E. coli</i>. Genome-wide analyses of RpoS-dependent gene
                     expression showed that up to 10% of the genes in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> are under direct or indirect control of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: [15558318][15716429]|.
                     RNA polymerase bound to &sigma;<sup>S</sup> efficiently 
                     transcribes genes involved in stress responses and 
                     secondary metabolism, as well as RNAs from intergenic 
                     regions with yet-unknown function(s). The effects of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> on <i>E. coli</i> metabolism have been 
                     investigated |CITS: [16511888]|. A lack of <i>rpoS</i> 
                     increases the transcription of genes belonging to 
                     high-affinity uptake systems and central metabolism, under 
                     both glucose excess and glucose-limited conditions |CITS: 
                     [26204448]|. In addition, RpoS regulates transcription of a
                     significant fraction of genes related to sugar metabolism 
                     and polyamine metabolism in response to cellular stresses, 
                     and it also has important roles in nucleic acid synthesis, 
                     modification, and turnover |CITS: [21398637]|.
                     RpoS is also involved in the transcription of genes related
                     to plasmid transformation, membrane and periplasmic 
                     proteins |CITS: [22438941]|, cellular resistance to 
                     antibiotics |CITS: 
                     [34874820][38617560][40352728][40518995]|, control of 
                     hydrostatic pressure |CITS:[21705547][28819154][36737893]| 
                     and inhibition of mazEF-dependent cellular death during 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [19251848]|. Additionally, RpoS 
                     seems to be involved in phage T7 resistance |CITS: 
                     [36978471]|.
                     A <i>rpoS</i> knockout mutant exhibited a fourfold increase
                     in aerobic biofilm formation, whereas no significant effect
                     was observed under microaerobic conditions |CITS: 
                     [36894813]|.
                     In <i>E. coli</i> strains lacking the 
                     phosphoenolpyruvate:carbohydrate phosphotransferase system,
                     RpoS acts as a controller of almost all the glycolytic 
                     genes. Additionally, genes whose products participate in 
                     ppGpp metabolism also are controlled by RpoS, although 
                     <i>relA</i> is not |CITS: [17938565]|. Curli fiber 
                     synthesis, which is tightly linked to key metabolic 
                     pathways, is regulated by RpoS under oxidative stress 
                     conditions |CITS: [24212724][29088115]|. The sigma factors 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> and &sigma;<sup>E</sup> have opposing 
                     roles in curli production and the related processes |CITS: 
                     [29088115]|. Mutations that induce the &sigma;<sup>E</sup> 
                     or Cpx stress response system reduce curli production; on 
                     the other hand, mutant strains with increased 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> levels show increased curli production.
                     Crl, a thermosensor identified in the <i>E. coli</i> RpoS 
                     regulon, interacts directly with &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, 
                     promoting its binding to the <i>csgB</i> promoter |CITS: 
                     [14978043]|.
                     RpoS plays a central role in survival from dehydration, 
                     protecting cells from damage during the drying phase and/or
                     from stresses associated with the early stages of 
                     rehydration before protein expression has restarted |CITS: 
                     [29457688]|.
                     By using ChIP-seq analysis, 63 promoter regions were found 
                     to be bound by &sigma;<sup>S</sup> in response to the 
                     transition into stationary phase, although there were not 
                     good correlations between &sigma;<sup>S</sup>  binding and 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent transcription. Only 38 of the
                     63 promoter regions (60%) recognized by the sigma factor 
                     appear to be controlled by &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: 
                     [26020590]|. Another ChIP-seq data analysis revealed that 
                     RpoS-dependent genes encode small proteins involved in 
                     resistance to oxidative stress and to osmotic stress  
                     |CITS: [26020590]|. Various target genes of RpoS were found
                     upregulated after 10 min of exposure to 2.5 mM 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, despite the <i>rpoS</i> 
                     transcription level not showing 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>-dependent regulation |CITS: 
                     [35453340]|.
                     The genome-wide binding profile of RpoS was determined by 
                     ChIP-exo under mid-exponential phase, and the differential 
                     gene expression between the wild-type and an <i>rpoS</i> 
                     knockout strain was determined by RNA-seq under the same 
                     growth condition |CITS:[39527186]|.
                     Most genes belonging to the &sigma;<sup>S</sup> regulon are
                     also subject to additional forms of gene expression 
                     regulation |CITS: [21398637]|. Several 
                     <i>rpoS</i>-dependent genes are maximally expressed at the 
                     transition between the exponential and stationary phases, 
                     or even in the mid-exponential phase |CITS: [21398637]|. 
                     The activity of the RpoS iModulon decreases in cells 
                     evolved on glycerol and lactate as carbon sources 
                     |CITS:[38636729]|.
                     The protein level of the heat shock sigma factor RpoH is 
                     increased due to protein mistranslation, and such an 
                     increase is dependent on RpoS. This suggests a novel 
                     regulatory role of RpoS in the heat shock response |CITS: 
                     [31419308]|.
                     This sigma factor is a negative regulator in the formation 
                     of viable but nonculturable (VBNC) cells |CITS:[14510852][ 
                     29458560]|.
                     Polymorphisms in <i>rpoS</i> that affect fitness across 
                     different environments are commonly found in <i>E. coli</i>
                     |CITS: [11790751][15207864][16489226]|. Additionally, the 
                     deletion of <i>rpoS</i> drastically reduces the viability 
                     of <i>E. coli</i> in soil after 42 days of incubation, 
                     confirming its key role in survival under prolonged 
                     environmental stress |CITS:[39905026]|. RpoS accumulates 
                     recurrent mutations under glucose limitation within its 
                     DNA-binding domain, reducing competition with RpoD for RNA 
                     polymerase and promoting expression of glucose-scavenging 
                     genes |CITS:[40830465]|. <b>Promoter sequences responding 
                     to &sigma;<sup>S</sup>:</b>  &sigma;<sup>S</sup> is 
                     homologous to the housekeeping sigma factor 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> and recognizes similar consensus 
                     promoter sequences |CITS: [11737638][14704342]|. 
                     Discrimination between &sigma;<sup>S</sup> and 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> promoters may be achieved through 
                     their tolerance of different levels of deviation from the 
                     consensus sequence |CITS: [11737638]| and nonoptimal 
                     spacing between the -35 and -10 elements |CITS: 
                     [16420370]|. The presence and location of UP elements 
                     |CITS: [15612932]| and -35 region elements |CITS: 
                     [16420371]| may also influence usage of a subset of 
                     promoters. Based on promoter sequence comparisons, it was 
                     found that selective promoter recognition by either form of
                     RNA polymerase can be affected by the A/T content in the 
                     -10/+1 region. Indeed, site-directed mutagenesis 
                     experiments confirmed that an A/T bias in the -10/+1 region
                     could improve promoter recognition by &sigma;<sup>S</sup> 
                     |CITS: [21398637]|.
                     Based on various studies, it is known that there are a set 
                     of genes with promoters that are recognized by both 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> and &sigma;<sup>38</sup> (another name
                     for &sigma;<sup>S</sup>), and these genes are referred to 
                     as &sigma;<sup>70+38</sup> genes. Sequence conservation was
                     found in promoters of &sigma;<sup>70+38</sup> genes. It was
                     proposed that the transcription response strength of 
                     &sigma;<sup>70+38</sup> genes to changes in 
                     &sigma;<sup>38</sup> levels can be quantitatively related 
                     to the promoter sequence |CITS:[35338024]|. RNA-seq 
                     analysis indicated that &sigma;<sup>70+38</sup> genes are 
                     upregulated to a similar extent as those with promoters 
                     recognized only by &sigma;<sup>38</sup> when in stationary 
                     phase, compared to exponential phase, whereas those genes 
                     responding only to &sigma;<sup>70</sup> are weakly 
                     downregulated |CITS:[35338024]|. Gene ontology analysis 
                     showed that &sigma;<sup>70+38</sup> genes are involved 
                     mainly in respiration, while genes with promoters 
                     recognized only by &sigma;<sup>38</sup> are related to 
                     metabolic processes |CITS:[35338024]|.
                     A global antagonistic effect on gene expression was 
                     perceived between &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, 
                     &sigma;<sup>54</sup>, and &sigma;<sup>28</sup>, based on 
                     transcriptome expression analysis, and this effect results 
                     in many physiological traits, including flagellum-mediated 
                     motility and utilization of nitrogen sources; as many as 
                     60% of genes in the RpoN regulon are under reciprocal RpoS 
                     control |CITS: [21219458]|. In addition, there is a complex
                     regulatory interaction of the three &sigma; factors 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, &sigma;<sup>54</sup>, and 
                     &sigma;<sup>28</sup> |CITS: [21219458]|. A &sigma; factor 
                     competition model based on comparative kinetic and 
                     thermodynamic properties was developed by Ganguly and 
                     Chatterji in 2012 |CITS: [22995505]|.
                     <b>Regulation</b>: &sigma;<sup>S</sup> is itself subject to
                     complex regulation at all levels (transcription, 
                     translation, activity, and protein degradation), thereby 
                     allowing integration of multiple stress signals in the 
                     cell. The regulatory mechanisms and signals are summarized 
                     below.
                     <b>Regulation of transcription:</b>  Transcription of 
                     <i>rpoS</i> is repressed by the phosphorylated response 
                     regulator ArcA |CITS: [16291649]| and during the early log 
                     phase, by the regulator CRP |CITS: [7525405]|, although a 
                     positive effect by CRP in the mid-log, late-log, and 
                     stationary phases has been reported. These findings suggest
                     that cAMP-CRP plays a biphasic role in <i>rpoS</i> 
                     expression |CITS: [26260461]|. While previous reports have 
                     described a contradictory effect of CRP on <i>rpoS</i> at 
                     two CRP-binding sites, Ruiz Amores et al. determined, based
                     on glucose system and gene expression analyses, that both 
                     sites have a repressive effect |CITS: [29196655]|. The BarA
                     regulator is required for exponential-phase induction of 
                     <i>rpoS</i> transcription |CITS: [10931332]|. The presence 
                     of mitomycin C (MMC) induces RpoS synthesis, and the 
                     presence of BarA is necessary for the full induction of it.
                     The principal factor for this activation is the production 
                     of reactive oxygen species (ROS) in cells exposed to MMC 
                     |CITS:[ 28468910]|. The <i>rpoS</i> gene is upregulated in 
                     response to ampicillin treatment |CITS:[35196813]|.
                     <i>rpoS</i> transcription is directly activated by 
                     TorR_phosporylated under extreme acid conditions (pH 2.0) 
                     |CITS: [35181503]|. RpoS is induced via multiple mechanisms
                     when under prolonged exposure to alkaline stress under 
                     semiaerobic conditions |CITS:[29802191]|. Regulatory 
                     effects of ppGpp and DksA on RpoS have been described at 
                     the levels of transcription initiation, transcription 
                     |CITS: [8253685][12193624]|, and elongation |CITS: 
                     [7642494]|, or indirectly by directly regulating the 
                     <i>iraP</i> and <i>dsrA</i> promoters, which in turn 
                     results in an increase in <i>RpoS</i> synthesis or 
                     stability|CITS:[29061665]|.
                     During entry into stationary phase in complex medium, 
                     transcription of <i>rpoS</i> increases 5- to 10-fold |CITS:
                     [2254248][7525405][8208244]|, but this effect is limited in
                     defined medium |CITS: [15629914]|. On the other hand, it 
                     has been observed that polyamines increase the level of 
                     RpoS to induce glutamate-dependent acid resistance (GDAR) 
                     via <i>gadE</i> |CITS:[26025365]|.
                     Based on RT-qPCR, it was shown that the gene <i>rpoS</i> is
                     upregulated in the viable but nonculturable (VBNC) state 
                     that can be induced in pathogenic <i>Escherichia coli</i> 
                     strains by low-level chlorination exposure 
                     |CITS:[28662489]|. The expression of <i>rpoS</i> is also 
                     induced when the cell is exposed to the carbon-based 
                     nanomaterial, carbon nanotubes |CITS: [37317195]|. On the 
                     other hand, it has been found that <i>rpoS</i> expression 
                     is strongly induced in multicellular chains just before 
                     cellular adhesion to surfaces |CITS:[38814871]|. Moreover, 
                     the formation of circular multicellular communities, called
                     rosettes, also induces the expression of this gene 
                     |CITS:[38814871]|.
                     <b>Regulation of mRNA stability and translation:</b> The 
                     <i>rpoS</i> mRNA contains a long untranslated 5' region 
                     which plays an important role in the regulation of its 
                     stability and translation |CITS: [9721296]|. Many factors 
                     have been shown to influence formation of secondary 
                     structures, translation, and degradation of <i>rpoS</i> 
                     mRNA:  H-NS binds to <i>rpoS</i> mRNA and enhances its 
                     cleavage |CITS: [15147838]|, while the DsrA small RNA 
                     stabilizes it |CITS: [10954740]|. <i>rpoS</i> mRNA 
                     stability during glucose starvation is diminished in the 
                     absence of |FRAME: CPLX0-3281| function |CITS: [16309817]|.
                     Hfq is required for translation of the <i>rpoS</i> mRNA by 
                     promoting annealing of the DsrA small RNA to <i>rpoS</i> 
                     mRNA |CITS: [8654929][34571778][37116495]|. The Hfq-DsrA 
                     complex is stable until DsrA is bound to the target site in
                     <i>rpoS</i> mRNA, which appears to happen immediately after
                     the target site is transcribed |CITS: [37116495]|. On the 
                     other hand, the OxyS small RNA represses translation of 
                     <i>rpoS</i>, possibly by altering Hfq activity |CITS: 
                     [9774349]|. HU |CITS: [11251825]| and the RprA small RNA 
                     |CITS: [12410838]| each stimulate translation of 
                     <i>rpoS</i>. A review by Majdalani et al. (|CITS: 
                     [15814430]|) summarized the knowledge regarding small RNA 
                     regulators of <i>rpoS</i> mRNA.
                     Starvation for phosphorus leads to accumulation of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> due to increased translation of 
                     <i>rpoS</i> mRNA. The upregulation is dependent on Hfq and 
                     may therefore involve an as-yet-unidentified small RNA 
                     |CITS: [14526009]|. LrhA represses translation of 
                     <i>rpoS</i> in an Hfq-dependent manner, which may also 
                     involve an unidentified small RNA |CITS: [16621809]|. 
                     Twenty-four nucleotides of the ribosome-binding site were 
                     shown to be necessary and sufficient for a 10-fold increase
                     in <i>rpoS</i> translation |CITS: [16237004]|. A 
                     <i>miaA</i> insertion mutant showed decreased levels of 
                     RpoS, consistent with an MiaA requirement for efficient 
                     RpoS translation |CITS: [24296670]|.
                     RpoS levels during growth in LB medium have been closely 
                     monitored |CITS:[35338024]|. During exponential phase, low 
                     RpoS levels are detected, and these levels increase during 
                     stationary phase |CITS:[35338024]|.
                     <b>Regulation of protein activity:</b>  6S RNA activates 
                     transcription from several &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent 
                     promoters while not affecting the levels of <i>rpoS</i> 
                     transcription or &sigma;<sup>S</sup> protein |CITS: 
                     [15262935]|. The alarmone ppGpp enhances the ability of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> to compete with &sigma;<sup>70</sup> 
                     for binding to core RNA polymerase |CITS: [12023304]|; the 
                     Rsd protein appears to serve a similar function |CITS: 
                     [10368152]|. Nitrogen starvation induces 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent gene expression |CITS: 
                     [15716358]|, although levels of &sigma;<sup>S</sup> are 
                     only increased approximately 2-fold |CITS: [15629914]|, 
                     suggesting that nitrogen starvation acts at the level of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> activity |CITS: [16267278]|. The 
                     |FRAME: EG11092-MONOMER| enhances &sigma;<sup>S</sup> 
                     activity by increasing its ability to compete for the 
                     |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX| |CITS: [17332743]|. Crl is a cognate 
                     enhancer of RpoS activity under different growth conditions
                     |CITS:[23867204]|. &beta;-Lactam antibiotics induce the 
                     RpoS regulon and increase activity |CITS: 
                     [23511474][33731833]|. Deletion of the &sigma; finger of 
                     RpoS formed by region 3.2 decreases the overall RNAP 
                     holoenzyme activity <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [33573428]|. The
                     protein IraD appears to stabilize RpoS |CITS: [35445706]|.
                     Under stress conditions, &sigma;<sup>S</sup> induces the 
                     mutagenic repair of DNA breaks through a network of genes 
                     that function in the activation of &sigma;<sup>S</sup>. The
                     genes that appear to be involved in the activation of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> are <i>cyoD</i>, <i>mdh</i>, 
                     <i>nuoC</i>, <i>sdhD</i>, <i>ubiD</i>,<i> apaH</i>, 
                     <i>cspC</i>, <i>cyoA</i>, <i>fre</i>, <i>glnK</i>, 
                     <i>hdfR</i>, <i>hemL</i>, <i>hfq</i>, <i>hscB</i>, 
                     <i>metJ</i>, <i>nuoG</i>, <i>nuoH</i>, <i>nuoJ</i>, 
                     <i>nuoK</i>, <i>nuoL</i>, <i>pgi</i>, <i>rbsK</i>, 
                     <i>sdhB</i>, <i>speD</i>, <i>speE</i>, <i>ubiA</i>, 
                     <i>ubiE</i>, <i>ubiH</i>, <i>ubiX</i>, and <i>yifE</i> 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     <b>Protein degradation:</b>  In exponentially growing 
                     cells, the energy-dependent ClpXP protease degrades 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> rapidly |CITS: [8550468]|. The RssB 
                     protein is required for rapid proteolysis of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>; it acts as a direct recognition 
                     factor, facilitating degradation of &sigma;<sup>S</sup> by 
                     the ClpXP protease |CITS: [10339606]|. |FRAME: 
                     EG10690-MONOMER| |CITS: [16556229]| as well as IraP |CITS: 
                     [16600914]|, |FRAME: EG11092-MONOMER| |CITS: [17332743]|, 
                     and H-NS |CITS: [16980505]| modulate the effect of RssB. 
                     <i>rssB</i> transcription is itself controlled by 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: [11566997]|.  Acetyl phosphate 
                     is able to transfer a phosphate group to the Asp-58 residue
                     of RssB and activate it |CITS: [9515704]|, although the 
                     phosphorylation level of RssB does not appear to play a 
                     role in signaling carbon starvation |CITS: [15489452]|.  
                     Carbon starvation leads to accumulation of 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> due to stabilization of the protein 
                     |CITS: [9179856][15629914]|; the molecular signal has not 
                     yet been determined |CITS: [16267278]|. DnaK positively 
                     regulates the amount of RpoS through its chaperone function
                     |CITS:[ 30271935]|, and DksA may directly or indirectly 
                     protect RpoS from degradation by ClpXP |CITS:[ 9473038]|.
                     Under oscillating glucose conditions in high cell-density 
                     culture experiments, the transcriptional responses of 
                     knockout strains of three transcriptional factors sensitive
                     to glucose starvation, |FRAME: EG10338|, |FRAME: EG10164|, 
                     and |FRAME: EG10510|, were more sensitive and severe than 
                     knockout strains of two other glucose-sensitive 
                     transcriptional factors, |FRAME: EG11355| and |FRAME: 
                     G6195|, compared to a control strain; all strains had a 
                     &Delta;<i>|FRAME: EG10302|</i> background |CITS: 
                     [39285935]|.
                     Selected reviews: |CITS: [12208995][12753181][12823806][15 
                     814430][15866041][15948944][16267278][17725229][19765651][2
                     1639793][31197038]| Review about the contribution of 
                     <i>rpoS</i> to biofilm formation |CITS: [20480211]|.
