                     /label=pcm
     CDS             complement(2862252..2862878)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pcm"
                     /locus_tag="b2743"
                     /product="bifunctional protein-L-isoaspartate
                     O-methyltransferase / protein-L-isoaspartate(D-aspartate) 
                     O-methyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.77"
                     /label=pcm
                     /note="L-isoaspartate protein carboxylmethyltransferase
                     (PIMT) transfers a methyl group from 
                     <i>S</i>-adenosylmethionine (SAM) to the free carboxyl 
                     group of D-aspartyl and L-isoaspartyl residues within 
                     polypeptides.  The enzyme may therefore serve to repair 
                     abnormal L-isoaspartyl residues that arise spontaneously 
                     from aspartyl and asparaginyl residues in proteins.  PIMT 
                     enhances survival of <i>E. coli</i> under conditions of 
                     environmental stress in late stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [9573145]| and oxidative stress at alkaline pH |CITS: 
                     [16000706]|.
                     PIMT is monomeric in solution.  A crystal structure of PIMT
                     was solved at 1.8 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [20857228]|.  
                     Enzymes containing mutations in glutamate residues that 
                     were predicted to be involved in SAM binding retained the 
                     ability to bind SAM, but altered the kinetic properties of 
                     the enzyme |CITS: [14527954]|. Overexpression of <i>pcm</i>
                     leads to increased tolerance for thermal stress, likely due
                     to induction of the misfolded protein response |CITS: 
                     [14527954]|.  <i>pcm</i> was also isolated as a multicopy 
                     suppressor of protein aggregation; this effect was not due 
                     to induction of the heat shock response |CITS: [15687202]|.
                      PIMT may work together with SurE; mutations have additive 
                     effects on accumulation of isoaspartyl in stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [9785447]|.  A &Delta;<i>pcm</i> &Delta;<i>glpD</i> 
                     double mutant shows increased persister cell formation at 
                     24 h after the onset of stationary phase; this effect is 
                     dependent on (p)ppGpp |CITS: [27371580]|.
                     PIMT was thought to be required for stationary phase 
                     survival |CITS: [1409717][7928962]|, but this effect was 
                     due to a secondary mutation in <i>rpoS</i> |CITS: 
                     [9209028][9573145]|.
                     Transcription of <i>pcm</i> is highest during late 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [9387229]|.
                     Pcm: ""<b>p</b>rotein <b>c</b>arboxyl 
                     <b>m</b>ethyltransferase"" |CITS: [1860862]|
                     Review: |CITS: [12943218]|"
