     gene            complement(2865868..2866179)
                     /gene="ftsB"
                     /locus_tag="b2748"
                     /label=ftsB
     CDS             complement(2865868..2866179)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftsB"
                     /locus_tag="b2748"
                     /product="bifunctional cell division protein FtsB / FtsLBQ
                     cell division complex"
                     /label=ftsB
                     /note="FtsB is an essential cell division protein which 
                     localizes to the cell division site. Depletion of FtsB 
                     results in abnormal filamentous morphology, but does not 
                     cause a defect in segregation of replicated DNA |CITS: 
                     [11972052]|.
                     Localization of FtsB to the division site is dependent on 
                     FtsQ and FtsL, but does not require FtsW and FtsI. 
                     Conversely, FtsB is required for localization of FtsI and 
                     FtsW to the Z ring |CITS: [11972052]|. Although the 
                     hierarchy of dependency in the assembly of cell division 
                     proteins is largely linear, subsequent sults showed that 
                     assembly of the cell division machinery is complex. 
                     Premature targeting of FtsB to the division site enables 
                     recruitment of FtsL, FtsW and FtsI in the absence of FtsQ 
                     |CITS: [16824093]|.
                     FtsB contains a small N-terminal cytoplasmic domain, a 
                     membrane-spanning region and a periplasmic C-terminal 
                     domain with a leucine zipper motif |CITS: [11972052]|. The 
                     transmembrane domain homo-oligomerizes; the interaction is 
                     mediated by polar amino acids on one face of the helix and 
                     hydrogen bonding with Gln16 |CITS: [23520975]|. A crystal 
                     structure of the periplasmic domain has been solved, 
                     showing a canonical coiled-coil structure |CITS: 
                     [23520975]|.
                     FtsB and FtsL localization to the constriction site is 
                     codependent |CITS: [11972052]|; the periplasmic leucine 
                     zipper motif is required for optimal interaction between 
                     FtsL and FtsB |CITS: [21784946]|. FtsQ, FtsL and FtsB form 
                     a complex <i>in vivo</i> even in the absence of FtsK and 
                     thus independently of their localization to the septal 
                     region |CITS: [15165235]|. The N-terminal half of FtsB 
                     including parts of the periplasmic coiled-coil domain is 
                     required for its interaction with FtsL; in the complex with
                     FtsL, this region is also sufficient for recruitment of 
                     downstream division proteins |CITS: [19233928]|. The 
                     transmembrane helices of FtsB and FtsL appear to be major 
                     contributors to the stability of a 1:1 higher-order 
                     oligomeric complex between FtsB and FtsL |CITS: 
                     [24083359]|. A part of the periplasmic C-terminal domain is
                     required for interaction with FtsQ |CITS: 
                     [19233928][35798142]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>ftsB</i> can partially suppress the 
                     viability and cell division phenotypes of an <i>ftsK</i> 
                     mutant |CITS: [16194242]|.
                     A periplasmic region of FtsB that mediates its binding to 
                     FtsQ was used to design a macrocyclic covalent inhibitor of
                     the |FRAME: CPLX0-7934| |CITS: [PAULUSSEN2022]|. Selected 
                     reviews: |CITS: [20943430][15491352][12626683]|"
