     gene            complement(2877912..2880578)
                     /gene="cas3"
                     /locus_tag="b2761"
                     /label=cas3
     CDS             complement(2877912..2880578)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cas3"
                     /locus_tag="b2761"
                     /product="CRISPR-associated endonuclease/helicase Cas3"
                     /label=cas3
                     /note="Cas3 is considered the 'signature' protein of Type
                     I-E CRISPR systems responsible for degradation, i.e. 
                     ""interference"", of target DNA. The protein contains an 
                     N-terminal histidine-aspartate (HD) domain nuclease fused a
                     C-terminal RecA-like helicase domain |CITS: 
                     [11952905][18703739][21699496]|.
                     Cas3 works with the |FRAME: CPLX0-7725 Cascade| complex to 
                     provide resistance against phages whose genomes have 
                     regions of complementarity to elements in the CRISPR repeat
                     |CITS: [18703739]|. Cas3 is recruited to the Cascade 
                     complex by |FRAME: G7430-MONOMER|, which recognizes DNA 
                     target sites, undergoes conformational change and positions
                     Cas3 near the ""protospacer adjacent motif"" (PAM) and 
                     stabilized R-loop |CITS: [24748111][26863189]|. Cas3 nicks 
                     the target DNA and subsequently unwinds and degrades it via
                     its joint ATP-dependent helicase activity and 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup>-dependent HD-nuclease activity |CITS: 
                     [22521689][23760266]|.
                     Cas3 catalyses ATP-independent annealing of RNA with DNA to
                     form hybrid molecules of RNA base-paired into duplex DNA, 
                     also known as R-loops. The HD motif residues D75 and K78 as
                     well as the presence of Mg<sup>2+</sup> are important for 
                     annealing activity, while mutations in the ATPase motif 
                     have no effect. <i>In vivo</i>, the HD motif is important 
                     for protection against phage &lambda;vir. In the presence 
                     of ATP, Cas3 catalyzes the opposite reaction, unwinding of 
                     the invading RNA strand of a model R-loop substrate |CITS: 
                     [21699496]|.
                     Depending on the DNA target (e.g. spacer) sequence next to 
                     the PAM site, Cas3 can still be recruited to Cascade bound 
                     to the DNA target but doesn't degrade the DNA target; 
                     instead primed space acquisition, i.e. adaptation, results 
                     rather than interference |CITS: [26522594][26586800]|.
                     The high-temperature |FRAME: CPLX0-2641|, is required for 
                     maintaining functional levels of Cas3 |CITS: [22114197]|. 
                     When |FRAME: PD00288|, a transcriptional repressor of 
                     Cascade |CITS: [20659289]|, is deleted, <i>E. coli</i> was 
                     able to resist phage infection at 37&deg;C because the 
                     amount of Cas3 was increased, particularly under stationary
                     growth phase |CITS: [26956996]|. Over-expression of Cas3 
                     stimulates uncontrolled DNA replication of ColE1 plasmids 
                     that depended on its ATPase/translocase activity and 
                     |FRAME: EG10860-MONOMER|, possibly related to the R-loop 
                     formed by the plasmid near its origin of replication |CITS:
                     [23406879]|. This process is temperature-dependent |CITS: 
                     [31095294]|.
                     The Cascade-Cas3 can delete thousands of DNA base pairs 
                     upstream of 5'-ARG PAM sequence in human cells |CITS: 
                     [31811138]|.
                     Cas3: ""<b>C</b>RISPR-<b>as</b>sociated"" |CITS: 
                     [18703739]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [20056882][21552286][22060043][22337052][2 
                     2588490][23145983][28385959][32085454][32570107][36809461] 
                     |"
