     gene            complement(2903253..2904044)
                     /gene="mazG"
                     /locus_tag="b2781"
                     /label=mazG
     CDS             complement(2903253..2904044)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mazG"
                     /locus_tag="b2781"
                     /product="nucleoside triphosphate pyrophosphohydrolase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.1.9"
                     /label=mazG
                     /note="MazG is a nucleoside pyrophosphohydrolase that
                     limits the deleterious effect of the MazF toxin under 
                     nutritional stress conditions |CITS: [12218018][16390452]|.
                     MazF belongs to the superfamily of all-&alpha;
                     NTP pyrophosphatases. Members of this family are predicted 
                     to perform ""house-cleaning"" functions by
                     hydrolyzing non-canonical NTPs |CITS: [15740738]|.
                     MazG exhibits pyrophosphohydrolase activity toward all four
                     dNTPs and also exhibits (somewhat less)
                     activity toward all four rNTPs |CITS: [12218018]|. 
                     Hydrolysis of GTP is more efficient that hydrolysis of ATP 
                     |CITS: [16390452]|. Addition of purified MazEF proteins 
                     causes inhibition of MazG enzymatic activity
                     |CITS: [16390452]|.
                     MazG and Era associate <i>in vitro</i> and by yeast 
                     two-hybrid test. Nucleotide influences the
                     association; binding is weaker with GTP&gamma;S than with 
                     GDP added |CITS: [12218018]|.
                     Crystal structures of MazG alone and in complex with ATP 
                     have been solved. MazG is a dimer in the
                     crystal structure, and each monomer contains two domains 
                     with similar folds. Point
                     mutations in potential active site residues and domain 
                     deletion mutants showed that the NTP
                     pyrophosphohydrolase activity is located in the C-terminal 
                     domain |CITS: [18353782]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>mazG</i> inhibits cell growth and 
                     negatively affects accumulation of (p)ppGpp, while 
                     overexpression of the entire <i>mazEFG</i> operon has no 
                     effect on cell growth or (p)ppGpp accumulation.
                     A <i>mazG</i> deletion mutant is less able to survive under
                     nutritional stress conditions |CITS: [16390452]|. Review: 
                     |CITS: [16359314]|"
