     gene            complement(2904776..2907010)
                     /gene="relA"
                     /locus_tag="b2784"
                     /label=relA
     CDS             complement(2904776..2907010)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="relA"
                     /locus_tag="b2784"
                     /product="trifunctional GDP/GTP pyrophosphokinase / GDP 
                     pyrophosphokinase / GTP pyrophosphokinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.6.5"
                     /label=relA
                     /note="RelA is a key enzyme involved in the stringent
                     response of <i>Escherichia coli</i> to amino acid 
                     starvation. It activates the synthesis of the global 
                     regulatory molecules of the stringent response, ppGpp and 
                     pppGpp (referred to collectively as (p)ppGpp), via a 
                     ribosomal mechanism (see below). The amino acid sequence of
                     RelA has been shown to be extensively related to that of 
                     |FRAME: SPOT-MONOMER| |CITS: [2542299]|. The RelA (p)ppGpp 
                     synthetase activity is referred to as (p)ppGpp synthetase 
                     I, or PSI. RelA also contains an inactive hydrolase domain 
                     (reviewed in |CITS: [18454629]|). Its catalytic activity is
                     located in the N-terminal portion of the molecule, while 
                     the C-terminal portion appears to have a (p)ppGpp-dependent
                     regulatory function |CITS: [11133950][35238978]|. The 
                     bifunctional SpoT protein provides opposing hydrolase 
                     activity for RelA synthetase activity, both of which are 
                     involved in the regulation of (p)ppGpp levels. SpoT also 
                     has a RelA-independent ppGpp synthetase activity, referred 
                     to as PSII, that can be demonstrated in <i>relA</i> null 
                     mutants (reviewed in Cashel et. al. (1996) ""The Stringent 
                     Response"", chapter 92, in |CITS: [ColiSalII]|). A 
                     non-NUDIX hydrolase domain containing protein, |FRAME: 
                     EG10048|, has also been shown to hydrolyze pppGpp |CITS: 
                     [35294098]|.
                     Mutations in the amino acid residues His219, Glu220, 
                     Tyr228, Pro253, Gly318, and Glu351 located in the 
                     synthetase domain of RelA resulted in reduced ppGpp 
                     synthesis |CITS: [25188023]|. Strains with missense 
                     mutations in different positions of the N-terminal coding 
                     regions of <i>relA</i> allowed the synthesis of 
                     metabolically stable RelA proteins. Such strains also have 
                     a <i>spoT</i> gene mutation and are able to increase the 
                     ppGpp levels in the exponential phase when grown on 
                     Kornberg medium (KM) |CITS: [25188023]|.
                     SpoT mutant strains with missense mutations in the 
                     <i>relA</i> gene showed an increase in glycogen content and
                     elevated levels of glycogen gene expression when the 
                     strains were grown in KM. RelA is the major source of 
                     ppGpp, compared with SpoT, in cells growing in KM |CITS: 
                     [25188023]|.
                     Homologs of RelA and SpoT are members of the RelA/SpoT 
                     Homolog (RSH) superfamily. This superfamily has been 
                     divided into 30 subgroups comprising long RSHs, small 
                     alarmone synthetases (SASs) and small alarmone hydrolases 
                     (SAHs). Their phylogenetic relationships were studied 
                     across all domains of life |CITS: [21858139]|.
                     The mechanism of action of RelA has been studied both 
                     genetically and biochemically. It is a ribosome-associated 
                     (p)ppGpp synthetase that is activated by the binding of 
                     uncharged tRNA to its cognate codon on the acceptor site (A
                     site) of a translating ribosome in response to amino acid 
                     starvation |CITS: [4576025][21410133]|. Under these 
                     conditions ppGpp is synthesized and accumulates and stable 
                     RNA synthesis (rRNA, tRNA) is inhibited. This inhibition of
                     stable RNA synthesis during the stringent response is 
                     ""relaxed"" in <i>relA</i> mutants (reviewed in Cashel et. 
                     al. (1996) ""The Stringent Response"", chapter 92, in 
                     |CITS: [ColiSalII]|).
                     Detailed <i>in vitro</i> studies using recombinant RelA 
                     have allowed models of RelA function to be proposed. In one
                     study it was determined that although the presence of a 
                     deacylated tRNA on the ribosomal A site was essential for 
                     RelA-mediated ppGpp synthesis, the binding of RelA to 
                     ribosomes was dependent upon the presence of a protruding 
                     3' extension of mRNA on the ribosome. RelA binding was 
                     independent of ribosomal protein L11, the product of gene 
                     <i>relC</i> (<i>rplK</i>). Synthesis of ppGpp by RelA 
                     reduced its binding to the ribosome. These findings led to 
                     a model in which RelA may hop between blocked ribosomal 
                     complexes during the stringent response, allowing a 
                     relatively low concentration of the enzyme to produce 
                     sufficient ppGpp |CITS: [12419222]|. Another study 
                     implicated ribosomal protein L11 in the regulation of RelA 
                     activity |CITS: [11673421]|. Antibiotics that block 
                     ribosome function at different sites, such as thiostrepton 
                     and tetracycline, inhibit pppGpp synthesis |CITS: 
                     [15670150]|.
                     An <i>in vivo</i>, single molecule tracking methodology was
                     developed to monitor the intracellular RelA catalytic cycle
                     using fluorescence imaging. Results suggested that RelA is 
                     ribosome-associated under non-starvation conditions, but 
                     dissociates for an extended time period after activation to
                     perform its catalytic cycle. Ribosome association was not 
                     necessary to trigger each ppGpp biosynthesis cycle. Heat 
                     stress caused fast activation of RelA with a rapid return 
                     to its inactive state, suggesting an excitable response 
                     mechanism for rapid adaptation to environmental changes 
                     |CITS: [21730169]|. In contrast, the results of a 
                     quantitative and more extensive fluorescent imaging study 
                     were consistant with the hypothesis that ppGpp is 
                     synthesized during starvation while RelA is bound to the 
                     ribosome |CITS: [26480956]|.
                     Allosteric regulation of RelA activity is 'multi-layered'; 
                     controlled through ligands, such as pppGpp, uncharged tRNAs
                     and ""starved"" ribosomal complexes (see above), but also 
                     by intramolecular RelA domain interactions. Two studies 
                     suggested that oligomerization, or dimerization, of RelA 
                     via its C-terminal domain (amino acid residues 456-744) may
                     have a role in regulation of (p)ppGpp synthetase activity. 
                     They supported a model in which oligomeric RelA is 
                     enzymatically inactive but during the stringent response 
                     becomes activated by dissociation to a monomer |CITS: 
                     [11133950][11800013]|. Other studies provide evidence that 
                     autoinhibition of the N-terminal domain by the C-terminal 
                     domain <i>in cis</i> modulates binding of RelA to target 
                     ribosomes and is the predominant mechanism of 
                     intramolecular regulation |CITS: [31507571][35238978]|.
                     In cells with <i>relA</i> deleted, ppGpp synthesis during 
                     steady-state growth but not during transient shifts between
                     different states of the ribosome, i.e. dwelling and 
                     translocating ribosomes, is attributed to SpoT |CITS: 
                     [35544692]|.
                     Apart from its known function, mutant studies have also 
                     suggested that RelA may have other as yet undefined roles 
                     in ribosome function apart from its (p)ppGpp synthetase 
                     activity |CITS: [19168615]|. It has also been shown that 
                     <i>E. coli</i> RelA carries a functionally important EXDD 
                     motif that has been implicated in a preference for GDP as 
                     pyrophosphate acceptor. This is in contrast to earlier 
                     reports that GTP is preferred. The EXDD motif may also 
                     provide this enzyme with the ability to synthesize pGpp at 
                     a low level, the significance of which has yet to be 
                     determined |CITS: [19201753]|. Several <i>relA</i> mutant 
                     alleles were shown to confer temperature-sensitive 
                     phenotypes, suggesting that ppGpp may be required for the 
                     expression of genes involved in thermotolerance |CITS: 
                     [13129947]|.
                     Native RelA has been purified to homogeneity |CITS: 
                     [195816]|. By itself it has little activity and must be 
                     activated <i>in vitro</i> by a complex of 70S ribosomes, 
                     mRNA and uncharged tRNA. In a non-ribosomal based assay it 
                     can be activated by solvents such as ethanol or methanol at
                     concentrations of about 20% |CITS: [3015258]|.
                     A <i>relA spoT</i> mutant was found to form biofilms with 
                     reduced catalase activity and elevated levels of hydroxyl 
                     radicals; the mutant biofilms are more sensitive to the 
                     antibiotics ofloxacin and tobramycin than wild type |CITS: 
                     [22096200]|.
                     A triple <i>&Delta;|FRAME: EG11510| relA |FRAME: 
                     EG10966|</i> mutant in minimal media has a growth defect 
                     phenotype that isn't rescued by addition of casamino acids 
                     alone but is rescued by complementation with PPK or with 
                     SpoT with (p)ppGpp synthesis. Relative to the single and 
                     double mutants, the triple mutant grown in LB media shows a
                     variety of heterogenous cell shapes, division and cell 
                     envelope defects, some of which can be rescued by RNA 
                     polymerase stringent alleles. These results indicate 
                     distinct nutritional and morphological defects, the latter 
                     of which may be linked to (p)ppGpp regulated transcription 
                     |CITS: [39727417]|.
                     A putative alarmone |FRAME: CPD0-1415| from <i>Enterococcus
                     faecalis</i> showed inhibition of rrnB operon transcription
                     by <i>E. coli</i> RNA polymerase, and activation of <i>E. 
                     coli</i> RelA synthetase activity in the presence of both 
                     pGpp and ppGpp |CITS: [26124242]|. pGpp is an inhibitor of 
                     <i>E. coli</i> |FRAME: SPOT-MONOMER ""SpoT""| |CITS: 
                     [340264]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [30980074][33162969]|"
