     gene            complement(2917515..2917883)
                     /gene="csrB"
                     /locus_tag="b4408"
                     /label=csrB
     misc_RNA        complement(2917515..2917883)
                     /gene="csrB"
                     /locus_tag="b4408"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA CsrB"
                     /note="CsrB is one of two small regulatory RNAs that
                     modulate regulation by the |FRAME: EG11447-MONOMER| CsrA 
                     |CITS: [9211896][12694612]|.
                     CsrB binds to the CsrA protein, forming an RNA-protein 
                     complex with 1:18 stoichiometry |CITS: [9211896]|.  18 
                     potential CsrA-binding elements are located in loop regions
                     within the CsrB RNA |CITS: [9211896][16131593]|.
                     A mechanism for formation of the globular ribonucleoprotein
                     complex between CsrA and CsrB has been proposed |CITS: 
                     [19619561]|.  CsrB inhibits CsrA-mediated acceleration of 
                     <i>glg</i> mRNA degradation |CITS: [9211896]| by competing 
                     with binding of CsrA to <i>glg</i> mRNA |CITS: [12067347]|.
                     CsrB also interacts with the protein YfiF, which is a 
                     ncRNA-binding protein |CITS:[38531971]|.
                     Degradation of both CsrB and CsrC is controlled by |FRAME: 
                     EG10018-MONOMER CsrD|.  CsrD itself does not function as a 
                     ribonuclease |CITS: [16980588]|; instead, it enables the 
                     RNase E-mediated degradation of CsrB RNA by counteracting 
                     the protection against degradation that is due to the 
                     CsrB-CsrA interaction |CITS: [27235416]|.  The dynamics and
                     genetic dependencies of the Csr signaling cascade have been
                     investigated both by modeling and <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [23878244][29599472]|.
                     Decay of CsrB and CsrC is stimulated when unphosphorylated 
                     |FRAME: CRR-MONOMER EIIA<sup>Glc</sup>| binds to the EAL 
                     domain of CsrD.  The phosphorylation state of 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> depends on the carbon source; growth on 
                     preferred sources, including glucose, leads to a net 
                     dephosphorylation of EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> and other PTS 
                     proteins.  Thus, growth on a preferred carbon source 
                     promotes decay of CsrB/CsrC |CITS: [26507976]|.  The RNase 
                     E cleavage site required for degradation is located in an 
                     unstructured region near the 3' end of CsrB.  CsrA 
                     stablizes CsrB by binding near the cleavage site, and CsrD 
                     counteracts this protection |CITS: [27235416]|.
                     A <i>csrB</i> mutant exhibits wild-type abundance of CsrA 
                     protein |CITS: [11567002]|.  A <i>csrB</i> mutation leads 
                     to increased transcription of <i>csrC</i>, and a 
                     <i>csrC</i> mutation leads to increased transcription of 
                     <i>csrB</i> |CITS: [12694612]|.
                     CsrA activates <i>csrB</i> transcription indirectly |CITS: 
                     [11567002][25535275]|, potentially via the BarA/UvrY 
                     two-component system |CITS: [25535275][26673755]|; CsrA 
                     does not alter the half-life of CsrB RNA |CITS: 
                     [11567002]|.  &sigma;<sup>E</sup> also indirectly activates
                     <i>csrB</i> transcription |CITS: [28924029]|. Expression of
                     <i>csrB</i> is positively regulated by UvrY |CITS: 
                     [12193630][12694612][21488981][26673755]| and is 
                     significantly higher during growth in minimal medium 
                     compared to rich medium |CITS: [19538511]|.  ppGpp and DksA
                     enhance <i>csrB</i> transcription |CITS: 
                     [21488981][22672726][26673755]|.  Levels of CsrB are 
                     decreased during the SOS response |CITS: [20378712]|. 
                     cAMP-CRP negatively regulates transcription of <i>csrB</i>,
                     potentially indirectly |CITS: [27551019]|. Regulation of 
                     biofilm formation by CsrB occurs via its effect on CsrA, 
                     which in turn regulates <i>pgaABCD</i> expression |CITS: 
                     [15916613]|.  Overexpression of CsrB leads to an increase 
                     in amino acid levels, changes in metabolite and protein 
                     levels in glycolysis and the TCA cycle |CITS: [22694848]|, 
                     and increases curli expression and biofilm formation and 
                     decreases swarming motility |CITS: [26469694]|.
                     CsrB: ""<b>c</b>arbon <b>s</b>torage <b>r</b>egulator 
                     <b>B</b>"" |CITS: [9211896]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9781871][10068996][12732300][15814430][17 
                     383221][20446015][22672726][32213244]|
                     Comment: |CITS: [26593223]|"
     gene            complement(2918094..2918639)
                     /gene="syd"
                     /locus_tag="b2793"
                     /label=syd
     CDS             complement(2918094..2918639)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="syd"
                     /locus_tag="b2793"
                     /product="SecY-interacting protein"
                     /label=syd
                     /note="The Syd protein may serve as a quality control
                     system for assembly of the SecY secretion channel |CITS: 
                     [19139097]|.
                     Syd interacts with the cytosolic C4 and C5 loops of SecY, a
                     membrane-embedded subunit of protein translocase |CITS: 
                     [7890670][9645430][9668058][19139097]|.  Purified Syd 
                     protein inhibits the ATPase activity of SecA and the 
                     protein translocation activity of a <i>secY24</i> mutant 
                     |CITS: [9668058]| and is able to destabilize SecYEG dimers 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [19139097]|.
                     Syd is loosely associated with the cytoplasmic surface of 
                     the inner membrane |CITS: [7890670]|.
                     A crystal structure of Syd has been solved at 2 &Aring; 
                     resolution.  In solution, Syd is monomeric and forms a 1:1 
                     complex with the SecYEG complex |CITS: [19139097]|. 
                     Overexpression of <i>syd</i> suppresses the phenotype of 
                     the <i>secY<sup>-d</sup>1</i> mutation
                     |CITS: [7890670]|.  Deletion of <i>syd</i> causes no 
                     obvious phenotype |CITS: [7890670]|.
                     Syd: ""<b>s</b>uppressor of <i>sec<b>Y</b></i> 
                     <b>d</b>ominance"" |CITS: [7890670]|"
