     CDS             2924414..2925169
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ygdG"
                     /locus_tag="b2798"
                     /product="flap endonuclease"
                     /label=ygdG
                     /note="YgdG (ExoIX) was recently reported to cleave a 
                     double-flap DNA substrate |CITS: [23821668]|, contrary to 
                     an earlier report |CITS: [19000038]|.
                     YdgG was previously identified as a 3'-5' exonuclease named
                     exonuclease IX (ExoIX) with an associated 
                     3'-phosphodiesterase activity specific for apurinic and 
                     apyrimidinic sites |CITS: [9592142][9840179]|. However, 
                     despite these reported activities, deletion of <i>ydgG</i> 
                     had no effect on DNA mismatch repair, UV resistance, or 
                     homologous recombination |CITS: [14599740]|. Further 
                     purification showed that the exonuclease activity actually 
                     belongs to copurifying |FRAME: EG11073-MONOMER|, and 
                     completely pure YdgG has no detectable exonuclease activity
                     |CITS: [17567612][19000038]|.
                     Using a new gene fusion tool, YdgG was shown to have 
                     5'&rarr;3' exoribonuclease cleavage activity but not 
                     3'&rarr;5' exoribonuclease nor deoxyribonuclease activities
                     |CITS: [39675608]|.
                     Crystal structures of the protein both in the absence and 
                     presence of DNA and metal ions have been solved, showing a 
                     typical FEN fold |CITS: [23821668]|.
                     YdgD has been shown to bind to the chaperone proteins Skp 
                     and GroEL, as well as SSB, although the signficance of 
                     these binding interactions is unknown |CITS: [17567612]|. 
                     <i>ydgD</i> and <i>polA</i> show synthetic lethality |CITS:
                     [17905985]|.
                     Xni: ""e<b>x</b>onuclease <b>ni</b>ne"" |CITS: [9592142]|"
