     gene            2936055..2936260
                     /gene="gcvB"
                     /locus_tag="b4443"
                     /label=gcvB
     misc_RNA        2936055..2936260
                     /gene="gcvB"
                     /locus_tag="b4443"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA GcvB"
                     /note="GcvB is a small RNA of approximately 205 nt in
                     length |CITS: [11448770]|. Although its expression appears 
                     to be regulated by the glycine cleavage system regulators 
                     |FRAME: PD00339| and |FRAME: EG11149-MONOMER|, GcvB itself 
                     is not involved in regulating components of the glycine 
                     cleavage system. Instead, GcvB regulates expression of the 
                     periplasmic transporter components |FRAME: DPPA-MONOMER| 
                     and |FRAME: OPPA-MONOMER| |CITS: [10972807]|, the |FRAME: 
                     YGJU-MONOMER| |CITS: [18952787]|, |FRAME: CYCA-MONOMER| 
                     |CITS: [19118351]|, the |FRAME: PD01379| |CITS: [22250746]|
                     and |FRAME: PD00353| |CITS: [21876160][27137887]|, the 
                     |FRAME: PWY0-1458| |CITS: [23300478]|, and the 
                     toxin/antitoxin pair |FRAME: G0-8865| and |FRAME: G0-8883| 
                     |CITS: [39278090]|. Both OppA |CITS: [10972807]| and DppA 
                     |CITS: [17264113]| expression is repressed by GcvB at the 
                     level of translation. Repression of SstT |CITS: 
                     [18952787]|, CycA |CITS: [19118351]| and DppA/OppA |CITS: 
                     [19118352]| expression by GcvB requires |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-1461|. GcvB is unstable in an <i>hfq</i> mutant 
                     |CITS: [17264113][19118352]|. The interaction of GcvB with 
                     Hfq has been demonstrated |CITS: [36328526]|. Using a 
                     network biology approach, GcvB was predicted to be involved
                     in the regulation of amino acid availability |CITS: 
                     [21876160]|. GcvB-target interactions were investigated by 
                     RIL-seq |CITS: [27588604]|. A screen using MS2-affinity 
                     purification coupled with RNA sequencing (MAPS) was used to
                     identify additional targets of GcvB regulation |CITS: 
                     [30447071]|. A candidate GcvB regulon was inferred by 
                     integrating information on experimentally supported targets
                     and sRNA-mRNA interactions with transcription datasets; new
                     targets with experimental support are <i>aroG, aroP, asd, 
                     cysD, dcyD, hcxB, icd, ilvN, leuA, purU, ydiJ</i>, and 
                     <i>yecS</i> |CITS: [32487739]|. 21 new targets have been 
                     validated |CITS: [34543491]|. Additional post-translational
                     mRNA targets of proteins involved with the common and 
                     terminal pathways of |FRAME: COMPLETE-ARO-PWY aromatic 
                     amino acid biosynthesis pathways| (<i>aroC and trpE</i>) 
                     and with transport systems that import exogenous 
                     tryptophan, tyrosine and phenylalanine (<i>aroP, livK and 
                     livJ</i>) were found to be downregulated using 
                     translational GFP reporter system. Regulation occurs 
                     through binding to specific hybridization (""seed"") sites 
                     within the target mRNAs |CITS: [39868872]|.
                     GcvB promotes mutagenic break repair |CITS: [27698081]|. In
                     exponential-phase cells, GcvB together with Hfq promotes 
                     the stability of the <i>|FRAME: G7175|</i> mRNA by directly
                     interacting with it and protecting it from RNase E cleavage
                     |CITS: [31744883]|. Further analyses using double mutants 
                     and genome-wide expression revealed that GcvB interaction 
                     with |FRAME: CPLX0-3461| significantly affects 276 genes to
                     differing degrees with the dominant GcvB-RNase E 
                     interaction being an enhancement of RNase E cleavage |CITS:
                     [39036964]|.
                     Two transcripts, presumably due to two Rho-independent 
                     terminators, can be detected; transcriptional fusions 
                     indicate that the shorter, ~130 nt transcript predominates 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [10972807]|, although the longer 
                     transcript predominated in a Northern blot experiment 
                     |CITS: [27190161]|.
                     Mutagenesis of nucleotides predicted to be involved in 
                     binding to <i>dppA</i> and <i>oppA</i> mRNAs as well as 
                     compensatory mutations in the <i>dppA</i> and <i>oppA</i> 
                     UTRs showed mixed results, indicating that base pairing 
                     between GcvB and its targets is only part of its regulatory
                     mechanism |CITS: [18312576]|. A second predicted base 
                     pairing site within GcvB does not appear to be required for
                     regulation of <i>oppA</i> expression |CITS: [20603180]|.
                     The GcvB homolog of <i>Salmonella enterica</i> was shown to
                     inhibit translation initiation by binding to the 
                     <i>dppA</i> and <i>oppA</i> mRNAs |CITS: [17974919]|. There
                     are more than 20 validated targets of GcvB in <i>Salmonella
                     enterica</i> |CITS: [21696468]|.
                     GcvB appears to have many genetic interactions with other 
                     sRNAs under different growth conditions |CITS: [35920556]|,
                     such as regulating the abundance of RyeA RNA in exponential
                     phase and at low pH|CITS: [39278090]|.
                     A <i>gcvB</i> mutant does not exhibit a defect in glycine 
                     cleavage system activity |CITS: [10972807]|. It 
                     overproduces OppA, DppA |CITS: [10972807]| and Lrp |CITS: 
                     [27137887]|, is more sensitive to acid challenge |CITS: 
                     [19379489]| and to oxidative stress |CITS: [34829644]| than
                     wild type. A &Delta;<i>gcvB</i> mutant also shows an 
                     increased &sigma;<sup>E</sup>-dependent membrane stress 
                     response and reduced &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent general 
                     stress response |CITS: [27698081]|. Overexpression of GcvB 
                     increases biofilm formation and decreases swarming motility
                     |CITS: [26469694]|.
                     <i>gcvB</i> is expressed during early log phase, but its 
                     level decreases during cell growth |CITS: [11448770]|; in 
                     LB, GcvB is produced from exponential through stationary 
                     phase, until ~OD<sub>600nm</sub> 3 |CITS: [30447071]|. 
                     Expression is much higher in rich (LB) medium than in 
                     glucose minimal medium |CITS: [27190161][30447071]|. GcvB 
                     is expressed at higher levels when cells are grown in 
                     pyruvate compared to glucose |CITS: [31363904]|. 
                     Transcription of <i>gcvB</i> is regulated by the GcvA 
                     activator and the GcvR repressor in response to glycine 
                     |CITS: [10972807]|.  Expression of <i>gcvB</i> is lower in 
                     a <i>|FRAME: EG10731|</i> mutant than in wild type |CITS: 
                     [21665928]|. The |FRAME: RNA0-124| destabilizes GcvB and 
                     may act as a ""sponge"" |CITS: [25630703][30447071]|. 
                     Levels of SroC and GcvB during growth are dissimilar |CITS:
                     [30447071]|.
                     A double deletion <i>&Delta;gcvB &Delta;sroC</i> mutant 
                     with an ectopically expressed GcvB grown in minimal media 
                     found that GcvB increased the lag phase in the presence of 
                     each aromatic amino acid, tyrosine, tryptophan and 
                     phenylalanine. Tyrosine was found to cause more growth 
                     inhibition, suggesting that GcvB inhibits the synthesis of 
                     tryptophan and phenylalanine more than tyrosine. The same 
                     double deletion mutant experimental system found that GcvB 
                     limited uptake of the antibiotic azaserine, which normally 
                     occurs through the AroP symporter and the ABC transporters 
                     LivJ and LivK |CITS: [39868872]|.
                     PsrA11: ""<b>p</b>redicted <b>s</b>mall <b>R</b>N<b>A</b>""
                     |CITS: [11448770]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [19366629][25028968][29992897][32213244][3 
                     8061078][38116200]|"
