     gene            complement(2961806..2962336)
                     /gene="rppH"
                     /locus_tag="b2830"
                     /label=rppH
     CDS             complement(2961806..2962336)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rppH"
                     /locus_tag="b2830"
                     /product="bifunctional RNA phosphohydrolase / RNA 
                     pyrophosphohydrolase"
                     /label=rppH
                     /note="RppH is an RNA pyrophosphohydrolase that initiates
                     mRNA degradation by hydrolysis of the 5'-triphosphate end, 
                     preparing mRNAs for further degradation by |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3461| and the |FRAME: CPLX0-2381|. This process is 
                     functionally similar to decapping of eukaryotic mRNAs 
                     |CITS: [18202662]|. Pyrophosphate removal is the 
                     rate-limiting step for degradation of a subset of mRNAs and
                     may be limited by a prior event or another cellular factor 
                     |CITS: [22984254]|. Because RppH prefers diphosphorylated 
                     RNA substrates, an as yet unidentified phosphatase acting 
                     on the triphosphorylated substrates may be involved in the 
                     process |CITS: [28673541]|. mRNAs that are 5'-NAD-modified 
                     are protected from RppH activity |CITS: [25533955]|. Weak 
                     ribosome binding accelerates mRNA decay that is independent
                     of RppH activity |CITS: [22989003]|. RppH decaps RNA 
                     molecules that contain Ap<sub>4</sub> and Gp<sub>4</sub> 
                     caps at the 5' end, generating a monophosphorylated RNA 
                     product |CITS: [31178354]|. It has also been shown that 
                     RppH can cleave unmethylated Np<sub>n</sub>N caps |CITS: 
                     [32103016]|. Epistasis experiments suggest an RNA decay 
                     pathway involving the consecutive actions of |FRAME: 
                     EG10048-MONOMER ApaH| followed by RppH, triggering the 
                     degradation of Np<sub>4</sub>-capped RNAs |CITS: 
                     [31178354]|.
                     The substrate specificity of RppH has been investigated. 
                     The enzyme requires at least two unpaired nucleotides at 
                     the 5' end of the mRNA, but has permissive sequence 
                     preferences |CITS: [25657006][25657011]|. RppH prefers 
                     diphosphorylated over triphosphorylated RNA substrates 
                     |CITS: [28673541]|. Residues within RppH that are involved 
                     in its enzymatic activity and substrate recognition 
                     properties have been identified by mutational analysis 
                     |CITS: [25657006][25657011]|. Crystal structures of RppH in
                     apo- and RNA-bound forms further elucidated the basis for 
                     its enzymatic properties; a catalytic mechanism has been 
                     proposed |CITS: [25657011]|. Additional crystal structures 
                     of RppH in complexes with mono- and dinucleotides have 
                     suggested an explanation for the low activity of RppH with 
                     these substrates |CITS: [31960065]|. A predictive 
                     sequence-to-function model was developed using a 
                     combination of bacterial mRNA kinetic decay measurements, 
                     biophysical modeling and machine-learning to find the RppH 
                     binding sites, secondary mRNA structures and other sequence
                     determinants related to RppH's role in mRNA decay |CITS: 
                     [39505899]|.
                     |FRAME: DIAMINOPIMEPIM-MONOMER DapF| directly interacts 
                     with RppH and stimulates its activity |CITS: [25313159]|. 
                     The mode of RppH stimulation depends on the length of the 
                     substrate RNA |CITS: [29733359]|. Crystal structures of the
                     DapF-RppH complex have been solved |CITS: 
                     [29733359][29931175]|. DapF and |FRAME: CMPKI-MONOMER Cmk| 
                     appear to act together to stimulate RppH |CITS: 
                     [34643703]|.
                     In an <i>rppH</i> deletion strain, the phosphorylation 
                     state of the 5' end of the <i>rpsT</i> P1 transcript 
                     changes, and the stability of both <i>rpsT</i> transcripts 
                     increases 3- to 5-fold, indicating that removal of the 5' 
                     pyrophosphate triggers degradation. Hydrolysis is dependent
                     on the availability of an unpaired 5' end. Other mRNA 
                     targets of RppH have been identified by microarray analysis
                     |CITS: [18202662]|. Mutations disrupting <i>rppH</i> 
                     suppress the temperature sensitivity of <i>degP</i> null 
                     mutants and mirror the effects of the <i>prlF1</i> 
                     mutation. RppH levels appear to decrease during cellular 
                     stress |CITS: [14594825]|. RppH orthologs of various 
                     pathogenic bacteria have been implicated in invasive 
                     behavior and virulence (see for example |CITS: 
                     [10760174]|). Deletion of <i>rppH</i> in a strain that 
                     lacks the PTS system for glucose transport substantially 
                     increased its growth rate |CITS: [22884033]|; 
                     differentially expressed proteins were identified by 
                     proteomics |CITS: [30284012]|. A &Delta;<i>rppH</i> mutant 
                     was analyzed by phenotype microarray and other methods; the
                     mutant is more sensitive to various antibiotics and 
                     stresses including cell envelope stress, but less sensitive
                     to cold stress than wild type |CITS: [28859318]|. The level
                     and stability of <i>csrA</i> RNA is increased in an 
                     <i>rppH</i> mutant |CITS: [30031486]|. While both 
                     <i>rppH</i> null and <i>rne</i>&Delta;CTH mutants are 
                     viable, the double mutant is not. The synthetic lethal 
                     phenotype can be suppressed by <i>rho</i> or <i>nusG</i> 
                     mutations that impair Rho-dependent transcription 
                     termination |CITS: [22026368]| and under conditions that 
                     reduce stable RNA levels |CITS: [28167522]|. In an 
                     <i>rph</i> wild-type strain background, RppH is not 
                     required for maturation of tRNAs; however, strains with the
                     |FRAME: EG10863 <i>rph-1</i>| allele, including K-12 
                     MG1655, require RppH for maturation of certain 
                     monocistronic tRNAs with short leader sequences |CITS: 
                     [28808133]|. Deletion of <i>rppH</i> and <i>yciW</i> 
                     together appears to improve L-cysteine overproduction 
                     |CITS: [32779574]|.
                     The regulation of RppH activity has been investigated. 
                     Overproduction of RppH does not significantly affect either
                     the number of mRNAs targeted or the degree to which they 
                     are downregulated |CITS: [22984254]|. Together with other 
                     components of the RNA processing and degradation machinery,
                     RppH localizes to helical structures at the cell periphery 
                     |CITS: [24266791]|.
                     <i>rppH</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     A genome-wide targetome of RppH was investigated <i>in 
                     vivo</i> enabling identification of its Selective RNA 
                     Processing and Stabilization (SRPS) sites; the effect of 
                     the first three nucleotides of the transcription start 
                     sites on RppH's ability to regulate transcript abundance 
                     was also quantified |CITS: [37845007]|.
                     RppH: ""<b>R</b>NA 
                     <b>p</b>yro<b>p</b>hospho<b>h</b>ydrolase"" |CITS: 
                     [18202662]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [16378245][22074454][24438330][25801053][29619898]|"
