     gene            complement(2966028..2967221)
                     /gene="lplT"
                     /locus_tag="b2835"
                     /label=lplT
     CDS             complement(2966028..2967221)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lplT"
                     /locus_tag="b2835"
                     /product="lysophospholipid transporter"
                     /label=lplT
                     /note="LplT is a phospholipid flippase that catalyses the 
                     facilitated diffusion of lysophospholipid across the inner 
                     membrane bilayer. LplT functions to reutilize 
                     2-acylglycerophosphoethanolamine (2-acyl-GPE) that is 
                     generated in the periplasm during the maturation of the 
                     major outer membrane lipoprotein (Lpp); LplT import 
                     provides substrate for the |FRAME: AAS-MONOMER ""Aas""| 
                     catalysed 2-acyl-GPE acyltransferase reaction |CITS: 
                     [15661733]|. <i>lplT</i> forms an operon with <i>aas</i> 
                     |CITS:[15661733]|. LplT interacts directly with Aas; LplT 
                     and Aas form a functional complex <i>in vivo</i> which 
                     facilitates specific and efficient reacylation of 
                     lysophospholipids |CITS: [39173951]|. LplT/Aas function 
                     plays a role in the resistance of gram-negative bacteria to
                     mammalian secretory phospholipase A<sub>2</sub> 
                     (sPLA<sub>2</sub>) |CITS: [29348168]|.
                     In a &Delta;<i>fadD</i> background, loss of <i>lplT</i> 
                     results in significant accumulation of acyl-GPE |CITS: 
                     [15661733]|. <i>E. coli</i> &Delta;<i>lplT</i> and 
                     &Delta;<i>lplT</i>&Delta;<i>aas</i> strains have perturbed 
                     phospholipid composition resulting in membrane 
                     destabilization; the strains show reduced PE and PG levels 
                     accompanied by an increase in lysophospholipids and in 
                     cardiolipin levels |CITS: [26613781]|. <i>E. coli</i> 
                     &Delta;<i>lplT</i> cells are significantly more sensitive 
                     to treatment with rabbit inflammatory fluid (AF) + porcine 
                     pancreatic sPLA<sub>2</sub> (psPLA<sub>2</sub>) than wild 
                     type; &Delta;<i>lplT</i> cells treated with AF + 
                     psPLA<sub>2</sub> show progressive phospholipid 
                     degradation; psPLA<sub>2</sub> (in the presence of AF) 
                     binds to &Delta;<i>lplT</i> cells but not to wild type 
                     cells |CITS: [29348168]|.
                     LplT is predicted to contain 12 transmembrane segments and 
                     is a member of the Major Facilitator Superfamily (MFS) of 
                     transporters |CITS: [15661733]|.
                     LplT from <i>Klebsiella pneumoniae</i>, which has 81% 
                     sequence identity with <i>E. coli</i> LplT, transports lyso
                     forms of the three main bacterial membrane phospholipids - 
                     phosphatidylethanolamine (PE), phosphatidylglycerol (PG) 
                     and cardiolipin; <i>Klebsiella pneumoniae</i> LplT is 
                     specific for lyso- versus diacyl forms of membrane 
                     phospholipids |CITS: [26613781]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [30373772]|).
                     <i>lplT</i>: <U>l</U>yso<U>p</U>hospho<U>l</U>ipid 
                     <U>t</U>ransporter |CITS: [15661733]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [27956138]|."
