     gene            complement(2969461..2969548)
                     /gene="omrA"
                     /locus_tag="b4444"
                     /label=omrA
     misc_RNA        complement(2969461..2969548)
                     /gene="omrA"
                     /locus_tag="b4444"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA OmrA"
                     /note="OmrA is a small RNA of approximately 88 nt in
                     length |CITS: [11448770][11445539]| that is involved in 
                     regulating the protein composition of the outer membrane 
                     |CITS: [16359331]|. OmrA was shown to negatively regulate 
                     the levels of <i>ompT</i>, <i>cirA</i>, <i>fecA</i> |CITS: 
                     [16359331]|, <i>fepA</i> |CITS: [16359331][28918899]|, 
                     <i>ompR</i> |CITS: [18953042][27439713]|, <i>csgD</i> 
                     |CITS: [20407422]|, <i>flhDC</i> |CITS: [22925049]|, 
                     <i>dgcM</i> |CITS: [30833291]|, and <i>flgM</i> |CITS: 
                     [32133913]| mRNAs. The effect of OmrA on <i>ompT</i> and 
                     <i>cirA</i> abundance is dependent on RNase E and involves 
                     direct base pairing at the 5' end of OmrA |CITS: 
                     [18953042]|. OmrA affects DgcM translation by competing 
                     with the ribosome for binding to the <i>dgcM</i> mRNA that 
                     has been restructured by Hfq |CITS: [30833291]|.
                     A set of targets for regulation by OmrA has been predicted,
                     but only a small percentage of those possible targets was 
                     supported by microarray data |CITS: [16717284]|. A 
                     candidate OmrA regulon was inferred by integrating 
                     information on experimentally supported targets and 
                     sRNA-mRNA interactions with transcription datasets |CITS: 
                     [32487739]|. Binding site features for OmrA were identified
                     by high-throughput <i>in vivo</i> mapping |CITS: 
                     [30287822]|.
                     OmrA represses its own synthesis via feedback regulation of
                     its transcriptional activator OmpR |CITS: [27439713]|. 
                     Interaction between OmrA RNA and Hfq protein has been 
                     detected |CITS: [11445539][36328526]|, and the effect of 
                     OmrA on <i>ompT</i> and <i>cirA</i> expression is dependent
                     on Hfq |CITS: [18953042]|. OmrA and, to a lesser extent 
                     OmrB, regulate translation inititation of btuB by directly 
                     interacting with btuB mRNA via Hfq and the degradosome 
                     |CITS: [38742638]|.
                     OmrA and OmrB were first recognized as an intergenic 
                     duplication (PAIR2) containing sites that resemble 
                     Rho-independent terminators |CITS: [10673004]|. The 5' and 
                     3' ends of OmrA and OmrB are nearly identical, but their 
                     central regions differ.
                     OmrA is highly abundant in stationary phase cells grown in 
                     LB medium |CITS: [11448770][11445539][14622403]|.  The half
                     life of OmrA in stationary phase is 16 minutes |CITS: 
                     [14602901]|. Transcription of <i>omrA</i> is regulated by 
                     OmpR |CITS: [16359331]| and by &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: 
                     [26020590]|.
                     The 5' end of OmrA is required for the regulatory function 
                     of the small RNA |CITS: [18953042]|. An <i>omrAB</i> 
                     deletion mutant shows a change in the protein composition 
                     of the outer membrane |CITS: [16359331]|. Overexpression of
                     <i>omrA</i> inhibits the formation of curli by 
                     downregulating CsgD expression |CITS: [20407422]|. 
                     Overexpression of OmrA decreases biofilm formation, 
                     swimming and swarming motility, and curli expression |CITS:
                     [26469694]|.
                     Transcription of <i>omrA</i> is activated by OmpR |CITS: 
                     [16359331][27439713]|.
                     SraE: ""small RNA"", PsrA12: ""predicted small RNA"" |CITS:
                     [11448770]|
                     Ryx: ""RNA of unknown function at X position (10 minute) in
                     the genome"" |CITS: [11445539]|
                     OmrA: ""<b>Om</b>pR-<b>r</b>egulated sRNA <b>A</b>"" |CITS:
                     [16359331]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17055775][19366629][25028968][32213244]|"
     gene            complement(2969663..2969744)
                     /gene="omrB"
                     /locus_tag="b4445"
                     /label=omrB
     misc_RNA        complement(2969663..2969744)
                     /gene="omrB"
                     /locus_tag="b4445"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA OmrB"
                     /note="OmrB is a small RNA that is involved in regulating
                     the protein composition of the outer membrane |CITS: 
                     [16359331]|. OmrB was shown to negatively regulate the 
                     levels of <i>ompT</i>, <i>cirA</i>, <i>fecA</i> |CITS: 
                     [16359331]|, <i>gntP</i> |CITS: [16717284]|, <i>ompR</i> 
                     |CITS: [18953042][27439713]|, <i>csgD</i> |CITS: 
                     [20407422]|, <i>flhDC</i> |CITS: [22925049]|, <i>dgcM</i> 
                     |CITS: [30833291]|, and <i>flgM</i> |CITS: [32133913]| 
                     mRNAs. The effect of OmrB on <i>ompT</i> and <i>cirA</i> 
                     abundance is dependent on RNase E and involves direct base 
                     pairing at the 5' end of OmrB |CITS: [18953042]|. OmrB 
                     affects DgcM translation by competing with the ribosome for
                     binding to the <i>dgcM</i> mRNA that has been restructured 
                     by Hfq |CITS: [30833291]|.
                     A set of targets for regulation by OmrB has been predicted,
                     but only a small percentage of those possible targets was 
                     supported by microarray data |CITS: [16717284]|. Binding 
                     site features for OmrA were identified by high-throughput 
                     <i>in vivo</i> mapping |CITS: [30287822]|.
                     OmrB represses its own synthesis via feedback regulation of
                     its transcriptional activator OmpR |CITS: [27439713]|. 
                     Interaction between OmrB RNA and Hfq protein has been 
                     detected |CITS: [11445539]|, and the effect of OmrB on 
                     <i>ompT</i> and <i>cirA</i> expression is dependent on Hfq 
                     |CITS: [18953042]|. OmrB regulates translation inititation 
                     of btuB by directly interacting with btuB mRNA via Hfq and 
                     the degradosome, although its action is less effective than
                     |FRAME: SRAE-RNA| |CITS: [38742638]|.
                     OmrA and OmrB were first recognized as an intergenic 
                     duplication (PAIR2) containing sites that resemble 
                     Rho-independent terminators |CITS: [10673004]|. The 5' and 
                     3' ends of OmrA and OmrB are nearly identical, but their 
                     central regions differ.
                     OmrB levels increase at the end of exponential phase and 
                     decrease after the onset of stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [14602901]|. The half life of OmrB in both exponential and 
                     stationary phase is 30 minutes |CITS: [14602901]|. 
                     Transcription of <i>omrB</i> is regulated by OmpR |CITS: 
                     [16359331]|.
                     The 5' end of OmrB is required for the regulatory function 
                     of the small RNA |CITS: [18953042]|. An <i>omrAB</i> 
                     deletion mutant shows a change in the protein composition 
                     of the outer membrane |CITS: [16359331]|. Overexpression of
                     <i>omrB</i> inhibits the formation of curli by 
                     downregulating CsgD expression |CITS: 
                     [20407422][26469694]|.
                     Transcription of <i>omrB</i> is activated by OmpR |CITS: 
                     [16359331][27439713]|.
                     Ryx: ""RNA of unknown function at X position (10 minute) in
                     the genome"" |CITS: [11445539]|
                     OmrB: ""<b>Om</b>pR-<b>r</b>egulated sRNA <b>B</b>"" |CITS:
                     [16359331]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17055775][19366629][25028968][32213244]|"
     gene            2969958..2970989
                     /gene="galR"
                     /locus_tag="b2837"
                     /label=galR
     CDS             2969958..2970989
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="galR"
                     /locus_tag="b2837"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA-binding transcriptional dual
                     regulator GalR / GalR-D-galactose"
                     /label=galR
                     /note="The ""<b>Gal</b>actose <b>r</b>epressor,"" GalR, is
                     a DNA-binding transcription factor that represses 
                     transcription of the operons involved in transport and 
                     catabolism of D-galactose |CITS: 
                     [17630975][7934815][8416900][8982002]|. Synthesis of these 
                     operons is induced when <i>E. coli</i> is grown in the 
                     presence of inducer (D-galactose) and the absence glucose 
                     |CITS:[8982002][7934815][1619663][8416900]|. The expression
                     of <i>galR</i> is increased only in the presence of inducer
                     |CITS:[8416900][8982002]|.
                     In particular, in the absence of D-galactose, GalR 
                     represses the <i>galETKM</i> operon |CITS:[9218807]|. In 
                     this repression system, GalR binds to two operators in the 
                     presence of HU, resulting in the formation of a repressor 
                     loop |CITS:[9218807][12169637][15289461]|. This repressor 
                     binds in tandem to inverted repeat sequences that are 16 
                     nucleotides long and possess conserved motifs; each dimer 
                     binds to one of these conserved sequences 
                     |CITS:[7934815][8416900]|.
                     On the other hand, GalR is highly homologous in its amino 
                     acid sequence to GalS (55% identical and 88% similar); 
                     apparently both act together and are capable of cross-talk 
                     to regulate expression of the <i>gal</i> regulon 
                     |CITS:[1619663][8982002]|. For this reason these regulators
                     bind the same operators, in the <i>cis</i> regulatory 
                     regions, with different affinities |CITS:[8175668]|. Also, 
                     it has been suggested that the DNA-binding consensus 
                     sequences that recognize GalR and GalS are not the same 
                     |CITS: [35694815]|. In the presence of inductor, GalR 
                     undergoes a conformational change that reduces its affinity
                     for the operator. Golding et al. showed that GalR is the 
                     major repressor of the <i>gal</i> operon |CITS:[1655705]|.
                     By using synthetic gene circuits, it was demonstrated that 
                     GalR changes from activation to repression depending on the
                     position of the binding site relative to the promoter 
                     |CITS:[39921566]|.
                     GalR belongs to the GalR/LacI family of transcriptional 
                     regulators. Accordingly, this transcriptional repressor 
                     family protein is composed of two domains: a conserved 
                     N-terminal domain which contains the DNA-binding region, 
                     and the carboxy-terminal domain, which is involved in 
                     effector binding and dimerization 
                     |CITS:[1619663][8982002]|. GalR and GalS have only two 
                     substitutions in the first helix of the N-terminal domain 
                     |CITS:[1619663]|.
                     The dynamical flexibility of the GalR regulator in the 
                     presence of different effectors was analyzed using 
                     ultrafast fluorescence spectroscopy |CITS:[30002268]|. 
                     Based on work with engineered host strains, the role of 
                     <i>galR</i> was shown to be highly dependent on the 
                     strength of the downstream module (succinate production 
                     module) |CITS:[31868221]|.
                     Inhibition of <i>galR</i> expression by CRISPRi reduced 
                     cellular fitness under treatment with the antibiotic 
                     carbonyl cyanide 3-chlorophenylhydrazone (CCCP) 
                     |CITS:[40352728]|.
                     A <i>pfkAB</i> knockout strain (with the 
                     Embden-Meyerhof-Parnas pathway [EMPP] inactivated for 
                     glucose metabolism) subjected to adaptive laboratory 
                     evolution recovered growth to wild-type levels and showed 
                     an improved the Entner-Doudoroff pathway (EDP) for glucose 
                     breakdown due to mutations in <i>gntR</i>, <i>galR</i>, 
                     <i>ugd-gnd-wbbL-2</i>, <i>ptsG</i>, <i>crp</i>, and 
                     <i>talB</i> |CITS:[36352474]|. The single deletion of 
                     <i>galR</i> in the <i>pfkAB</i> knockout strain increased 
                     1.1-fold the specific growth rate relative to that of the 
                     <i>pfkAB</i> knockout parental strain, probably due to 
                     derepression of the <i>galP</i> gene encoding galactose 
                     permease, leading to increased glucose uptake 
                     |CITS:[36352474]|.
                     The GalR binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
