                     /locus_tag="b2842"
                     /product="trifunctional 2-keto-3-deoxy-D-gluconate
                     dehydrogenase / D-gluconate 5-dehydrogenase / 
                     11-deoxycorticosterone reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.127"
                     /label=kduD
                     /note="KduD is predicted to encode a
                     2-keto-3-deoxy-D-gluconate dehydrogenase by sequence 
                     similarity to the enzyme from <i>Erwinia chrysanthemi</i>. 
                     The purified enzyme was tested for activity with other 
                     substrates by |CITS: [24509771]|. The authors of |CITS: 
                     [23437267]| hypothesize that under osmotic stress 
                     conditions, KduD may substitute for the reductase 
                     activities of |FRAME: ALTRO-OXIDOREDUCT-MONOMER ""UxaB""| 
                     and |FRAME: MANNONOXIDOREDUCT-MONOMER ""UxuB""|.
                     In certain <i>E. coli</i> strains including DH5&alpha;, 
                     KduD is able to catalyze a 20-ketosteroid reductase 
                     reaction, converting 11-deoxycorticosterone to 
                     4-pregnen-20,21-diol-3-one |CITS: [18035825][24509771]|. 
                     The protein has been shown to be present in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     extracts |CITS: [19903445]|. The native molecular weight of
                     a partially purified preparation of the 20-ketosteroid 
                     reductase activity is 100 kDa |CITS: [24509771]|; the 
                     enzyme may therefore be homotetrameric.
                     KduD was identified as a protein whose expression is 4-fold
                     lower in <i>E. coli</i> found in the cecum of mice that 
                     were fed a casein diet compared to mice fed a starch diet, 
                     and 2.4-fold higher in mice fed a lactose diet |CITS: 
                     [22427493]|. Expression of <i>kduD</i> is increased in the 
                     presence of galacturonate and glucuronate |CITS: 
                     [23437267]|."
                     /translation="MILSAFSLEGKVAVVTGCDTGLGQGMALGLAQAGCDIVGINIVEP
