     gene            2981861..2982670
                     /gene="yqeI"
                     /locus_tag="b2847"
                     /label=yqeI
     CDS             2981861..2982670
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yqeI"
                     /locus_tag="b2847"
                     /product="putative transcriptional regulator YqeI"
                     /label=yqeI
                     /note="YqeI appears to pertain to the CadC family of 
                     transcription factors, and it was predicted to regulate 
                     genes involved in processes related to phages and LPS 
                     synthesis | CITS: [35880217]|. Upstream of the <i>yqeI</i> 
                     gene, a sequence was found that could work as a 
                     transcriptional promoter, and the transcription of the 
                     <i>yqeI</i> gene is affected by conditions that lead to 
                     biofilm formation and UV exposure |CITS: [35880217]|. 
                     <i>yqeI</i> is located within a remnant of an ETT2 (type 
                     III secretion system) pathogenicity island that is fully 
                     present
                     in pathogenic strains such as <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7 and at
                     least partially present in most sequenced <i>E. coli</i>
                     and <i>Shigella</i> strains |CITS: [15150243]|.  ETT2 has 
                     been shown to contribute to pathogenesis
                     |CITS: [16291689]|.
                     YqeI is predicted to be a bitopic inner membrane protein 
                     |CITS: [27510400]|.
                     YqeI affects the expression of the genes involved in 
                     bacterial localization, locomotion, and biological adhesion
                      in avian pathogenic <i>Escherichia coli</i> (APEC) 
                     |CITS:[32947771]|. It decreases flagella formation, 
                     motility ability, serum resistance, cell adhesion ability, 
                     and bacterial colonization ability, supporting that the 
                     transcriptional regulator YqeI plays an important role in 
                     the pathogenicity of APEC |CITS:[32947771]|.
                     YqeI was transcriptionally upregulated in iron limitation 
                     over iron excess at 63% dissolved oxygen as determined by 
                     RNA-seq |CITS:[ 35777694]|.
                     "
                     /translation="MYWIINDNIEFWPEHRKLISVHNADLNVVLTTPASRCLSLLLEAF
