     gene            3026424..3026972
                     /gene="idi"
                     /locus_tag="b2889"
                     /label=idi
     CDS             3026424..3026972
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="idi"
                     /locus_tag="b2889"
                     /product="isopentenyl-diphosphate Delta-isomerase"
                     /EC_number="5.3.3.2"
                     /label=idi
                     /note="Isopentenyl diphosphate (IPP) isomerase (Idi)
                     catalyzes the reversible isomerization of isopentenyl 
                     diphosphate (IPP) to dimethylallyl diphosphate (DMAPP), a 
                     key step in the biosynthesis of isoprenoids.
                     Idi catalyzes the reversible isomerization of IPP to DMAPP 
                     |CITS: [3519603][10419945][10818234]|. The initial 5-6:1 
                     ratio of IPP to DMAPP generated by |FRAME: CPLX0-7564| is 
                     adjusted to a final ratio of 3:7 by Idi |CITS: [12571359]|.
                     The enzyme requires two divalent cations for activity; a 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> ion is located in a hexacoordinate 
                     His<sub>3</sub>Glu<sub>2</sub> pocket and is part of the 
                     catalytic machinery, while the enzyme-substrate complex 
                     contains an additional Mg<sup>2+</sup> ion that facilitates
                     substrate binding.  A wide variety of divalent metal ions 
                     can provide these functions |CITS: [16939278]|. Crystal 
                     structures of free and metal-bound isopentenyl diphosphate 
                     isomerase enabled identification of the active site |CITS: 
                     [11285217]|.  Crystal structures of complexes with 
                     transition state analogues and irreversible inhibitors 
                     suggested a catalytic mechanism of 
                     protonation/deprotonation involving C67 and E116 |CITS: 
                     [12540835][16332094]|, and crystal structures of 
                     site-directed mutants in C67 were obtained to confirm the 
                     proposed mechanism |CITS: [14696183]|.  Crystal structures 
                     of isopentenyl diphosphate isomerase together with the 
                     bromohydrin of IPP, an inhibitor of the enzyme, show 
                     covalent adducts of C67 and E116 |CITS: [15643873]|. 
                     Crystal structures of mutants in the Y104 residue 
                     demonstrate its role in folding of IPP isomerase |CITS: 
                     [16617181]|.  The structure of isopentenyl diphosphate 
                     isomerase shows similarity to the Nudix hydrolases |CITS: 
                     [11698677]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> isopentenyl diphosphate isomerase is a Type 
                     I enzyme. In contrast, Type II isopentenyl diphosphate 
                     isomerases are structurally unrelated and require reduced 
                     FMN and Mg<sup>2+</sup> for activity |CITS: [20560533]|. 
                     Isotope exchange studies of the reaction mechanism resulted
                     in a proposal that ligand binding to the enzyme is 
                     relatively slow. This is due to a significant kinetic 
                     barrier to reorganization of the initial encounter complex 
                     between enzyme, substrate, and Mg<sup>2+</sup> to form the 
                     Michaelis complex where the metal cation bridges the 
                     protein and the substrate diphosphate group |CITS: 
                     [22471428]|.
                     In metabolic engineering studies, co-expression of the 
                     <i>idi</i> gene was useful in increasing the production of 
                     lycopene |CITS: [17509919]| and prenyl alcohols |CITS: 
                     [19129660]| in <i>E. coli</i>.  Suppressor mutations in 
                     <i>ispH</i> and <i>idi</i> enable growth at low temperature
                     of a strain containing the <i>uppS31</i> missense allele 
                     |CITS: [29986944]|.
                     <i>idi</i> is not essential for growth |CITS: [10419945]|."
                     /translation="MQTEHVILLNAQGVPTGTLEKYAAHTADTRLHLAFSSWLFNAKGQ
