                     /label=lysS
                     /note="The lysine-tRNA ligase LysS is a member of the
                     family of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, which interpret the 
                     genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their 
                     specific tRNA molecules. The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis. LysS belongs to the subclass IIB of 
                     aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases |CITS: [2203971][23075299]|. 
                     <i>E. coli</i> contains both a constitutive and an 
                     inducible lysyl-tRNA synthetase |CITS: [7012120]|; 
                     <i>lysS</i> encodes the constitutively expressed enzyme and
                     <i>lysU</i> encodes the inducible |CITS: [2183178]|. The 
                     |FRAME: LYSU-MONOMER LysU| enzyme appears to be more 
                     error-prone than LysS |CITS: [25918376]|.
                     The ATP binding site was investigated by affinity labeling 
                     |CITS: [7852265][7852266]|. Key residues for recognition of
                     the cognate amino acid and catalytic activity have been 
                     identified |CITS: [14747465][15362869]|, providing insight 
                     into differences between the class I and class II tRNA 
                     synthetases. A model for the recognition specificity of the
                     cognate anticodon is presented |CITS: [9614943]|.
                     A solution structure of the N-terminal anticodon binding 
                     domain has been solved |CITS: [7473706]|. Specificity 
                     determinants within tRNA that are important for recognition
                     by LysS have been identified |CITS: 
                     [8809018][9614943][16772402]|, and residues within LysS 
                     that are important for tRNA<sup>Lys</sup> anticodon 
                     recognition were analyzed by site-directed mutagenesis 
                     |CITS: [9614943][12766171]|.
                     Crystal structures of unliganded and lysine-bound LysS 
                     revealed reorganization of the active site upon lysine 
                     binding |CITS: [11041850]|.
                     A LysS T208M mutant has low activity and exhibits 
                     cooperative kinetics for lysine binding |CITS: [7794932]|. 
                     The Y280F and F426W mutants in the lysine binding pocket of
                     LysS confer resistance to the amino acid analog 
                     <i>S</i>-(2-aminoethyl)-L-cysteine (AEC) |CITS: 
                     [18154269]|.
                     Under aerobic conditions and at growth temperatures below 
                     37&deg;C, LysS is required for normal growth |CITS: 
                     [1744045]|; the growth defect can be suppressed by 
                     overexpression of <i>lysU</i>, the gene encoding the 
                     inducible lysyl-tRNA synthetase |CITS: [1321323]|.
                     Using a method that distinguishes N-phosphorylation from 
                     O-phosphorylation, N-phosphorylation was detected <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [35636362]|.
                     There is a general discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [37224536]|.
                     AsuD: ""<b>a</b>nti<b>su</b>ppressor"" |CITS: [3881393]| 
                     <br>
                     HerC: ""<b>h</b>ost factor affecting Col<b>E</b>1 
                     <b>r</b>eplication"" |CITS: [6320195]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [7934813][7576245][23075299]|"
