                     /label=prfB
     CDS             complement(join(3028543..3029565,3029567..3029641))
                     /codon_start=1
                     /ribosomal_slippage
                     /gene="prfB"
                     /locus_tag="b2891"
                     /product="peptide chain release factor RF2"
                     /label=prfB
                     /note="Release factor 2 (RF2) is one of two class 1 
                     codon-specific factors in <i>E. coli</i> that facilitate 
                     the release of the
                     growing polypeptide chain at stop codons. RF2 recognizes 
                     the termination codons UGA and UAA
                     |CITS: [5233840][4879404][4911223][5422622]|. Termination 
                     is highly precise; RF2 is able to discriminate against 
                     related sense codons by 3 to 6 orders of magnitude and 
                     without requiring energy-driven error correction
                     |CITS: [10681447]|. The discriminator site consists of a 
                     tripeptide which appears to be functionally equivalent to 
                     the
                     anticodon of tRNA |CITS: [10688208]|. An <i>E. coli</i> 
                     cell is variously estimated to contain between 700 and 
                     24,900
                     molecules of RF2 and reaches the highest RF2 content at a 
                     high growth rate |CITS: [5543670][8176726]|.
                     In addition, RF2 is involved in the rescue of stalled 
                     ribosomes by interaction with |FRAME: G0-10465 ArfA|. ArfA 
                     binds to the vacant A-site of the stalled ribosome and then
                     recruits RF2, which releases the polypeptide |CITS: 
                     [22857598][22922063]|.
                     A +1 translational frameshift allows translation of the 
                     <i>prfB</i> mRNA past a premature in-frame stop codon at 
                     position 26; the frameshift was confirmed by both DNA and 
                     peptide sequencing data |CITS: [3889910]| as well as
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [3736654]|. The natural frameshift 
                     efficiency was variously estimated to be between 18% |CITS:
                     [1805966]| and 48% |CITS: [8493101]| and strongly depends 
                     on the identity of the codon preceding the in-frame stop 
                     codon |CITS: [8493101]|. The rate of frame-shifting 
                     increases in a <i>prfB</i> mutant, indicating
                     autoregulation of the process |CITS: [1805966][2251114]|. 
                     Translation termination, frameshifting, and readthrough by 
                     a suppressor tRNA directly compete at the <i>prfB</i> 
                     frameshift site |CITS: [7504811]|. High-level frameshifting
                     is
                     coupled with premature release of the E site tRNA from the 
                     ribosome |CITS: [15242643]|.
                     Interactions between RF2 and the ribosome have been mapped 
                     |CITS: [1742355]|. The universally conserved
                     tripeptide sequence GGQ is thought to interact with the 
                     peptidyl-transferase center of the ribosome and may be 
                     functionally equivalent to the CCA end of tRNA |CITS: 
                     [12419223][12492870]|. RF2 is post-translationally modified
                     by <i>N<sup>5</sup></i> methylation at Gln252 within the 
                     GGQ sequence; methylation increases the termination 
                     efficiency of the protein. Overproduced RF2 lacks this 
                     modification, which may be one cause for the lethality of 
                     overproducing RF2 |CITS: [11118225][11805295]|. 
                     Interactions of the conserved motifs within RF2 with the 
                     ribosome
                     have been determined |CITS: [12458201]|. The N-terminal 
                     domain of RF2 is required for normal activity
                     |CITS: [14651631]|.
                     Uridine residues in mRNA may be modified to pseudouridine 
                     (&Psi;).  The presence of a &Psi; nucleotide in the stop 
                     codon has no effect on the rate of peptide release <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [27107638]|.
                     Certain mutations in <i>prfB</i> lead to suppression of the
                     UGA stop codon and are conditionally lethal
                     |CITS: [3053663]|. The RF2* mutant (E167K) facilitates 
                     termination at all three stop codons |CITS: [9653158]|.
                     A crystal structure of RF2 has been solved at 1.8 &Aring; 
                     resolution; the structure is not similar to that of 
                     eukaryotic
                     peptide release factor |CITS: [11779511]|. A 
                     single-particle cryo-electron microscopy structure of RF2 
                     in a
                     post-termination complex with the ribosome shows that RF2 
                     adopts a different conformation on the ribosome which is 
                     consistent with functional data |CITS: 
                     [12511960][12511961]|.
                     Translation termination, and specifically the dissociation 
                     of RF1 or RF2 from the ribosome, is the target of the 
                     antimicrobial peptide apidaecin.  R262C or Q280L mutations 
                     in the <i>E. coli</i> B-encoded wild type version of RF2 
                     lead to resistance to Api137, a derivative of the natural 
                     apidaecin 1b |CITS: [28741611]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [3126822][12514123][34995554]|"
