     gene            complement(3029732..3031465)
                     /gene="recJ"
                     /locus_tag="b2892"
                     /label=recJ
     CDS             complement(3029732..3031465)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="recJ"
                     /locus_tag="b2892"
                     /product="bifunctional ssDNA-specific exonuclease RecJ / 
                     ssDNA-specific exonuclease"
                     /EC_number="3.1.11.6"
                     /label=recJ
                     /note="RecJ is a ssDNA-specific processive exonuclease
                     |CITS: [2649886][16488881]| that is involved in 
                     recombination |CITS: [6317649]| and RecF-dependent repair 
                     |CITS: [3286613]|.  RecJ belongs to the RecF epistatic 
                     group of DNA recombination/repair genes |CITS: [1883889]|. 
                     The early steps of dsDNA break repair have been 
                     reconstituted <i>in vitro</i> using purified components of 
                     the RecF recombinational repair system |CITS: [19451222]|. 
                     RecJ only acts on ssDNA.  Efficient binding requires a 
                     single-stranded 5' tail of at least 7 nucleotides; a 5' 
                     phosphate group is not required.  Only one RecJ molecule 
                     binds per substrate molecule.  Prebinding of ssDNA by SSB 
                     enhances RecJ activity |CITS: [16488881]|.
                     RecJ and the RecQ helicase functionally interact in 
                     double-strand break repair |CITS: [8302849]|.  At blocked 
                     replication forks, RecJ and RecQ process the nascent 
                     lagging strand DNA, enabling stabilization of the 
                     replication fork until the DNA damage is repaired |CITS: 
                     [10589843]|.  RecJ is required for the recovery of DNA 
                     synthesis after replication arrest |CITS: [16754873]|. 
                     However, in <i>recF</i>, <i>recR</i> |CITS: [12543983]| or 
                     <i>recA</i> |CITS: [17903041]| mutants, the nascent DNA at 
                     the replication fork is degraded due to the RecQ helicase 
                     and RecJ nuclease activities.  Recombinational repair from 
                     all types of dsDNA ends is facilitated by the complementary
                     action of RecJ and RecQ |CITS: [25411316]|. Reports differ 
                     on whether RecQ and RecJ do |CITS: [20221259][21705756]| or
                     do not |CITS: [20465561]| contribute to thymineless death.
                     The three ssDNA-specific exonucleases RecJ, ExoI and ExoVII
                     play partially redundant roles in DNA repair |CITS: 
                     [9584082]|.  RecJ's function in methyl-directed mismatch 
                     repair |CITS: [8389365]| is redundant with other activities
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [9473057][9584082][11381137][11418610]|.  The ssDNA 
                     exonucleases RecJ, (Exo)-I, and ExoX play redundant roles 
                     in stabilizing perfect repeats of ~100 bp |CITS: 
                     [12538867]|.  RecJ was reported to be involved in 
                     controlling CAG&middot;CTG repeat tract expansion |CITS: 
                     [25081568]|.
                     RecJ is involved in prespacer generation in the CRISPR-Cas 
                     primed adaption response |CITS: [36427302]|.
                     RecJ is involved in illegitimate recombination between a 
                     prophage and bacterial DNA |CITS: [8636039][12169595]|, but
                     not in illegitimate recombination of 3'-protruding single 
                     strands |CITS: [9512548]|.  RecJ was also thought to 
                     catalyze the DNA deoxyribophosphodiesterase reaction that 
                     removes a single abasic 5' terminal sugar phosphate residue
                     during base excision repair |CITS: [7512263][7535646]|; 
                     however, the pathway involving removal of only a single 
                     nucleotide is only observed <i>in vitro</i> with particular
                     oligonucleotide substrates |CITS: [9469838]|.  A later 
                     reinvestigation found that RecJ does not have DNA 
                     deoxyribophosphodiesterase activity |CITS: [10677682]|.
                     RecJ stimulates the strand transfer activity of RecA |CITS:
                     [7896836]|.  In the RecA loading-deficient <i>recB1080</i> 
                     mutant, three essential activities for the initiation of 
                     recombination are provided by the helicase activity of 
                     RecB1080CD, the 5' --> 3' exonuclease activity of RecJ, and
                     formation of RecA-single-stranded DNA filaments by RecFOR 
                     |CITS: [12618388]|.  RecA-independent recombination is 
                     redundantly inhibited by RecJ, ExoI, ExoVII, and ExoX 
                     |CITS: [17182742]|.
                     Analysis of randomly generated <i>recJ</i> mutants has 
                     allowed identification of conserved, functionally important
                     sequence motifs |CITS: [10498723]|.  A <i>recJ</i> mutant 
                     is defective in catabolism of DNA as a nutrient and is 
                     outcompeted by wild-type cells during long-term stationary 
                     phase incubation |CITS: [19329645]|. Allele-specific 
                     suppressor mutations for <i>recJ</i> map to <i>infC</i> 
                     |CITS: [8407784]| (restoring translation of RecJ |CITS: 
                     [9352920]|), <i>uvrD</i> and <i>helD</i> |CITS: [7635292]|.
                      The UV damage repair defect of a <i>recJ xonA xneA</i> 
                     triple mutant is suppressed by overexpression of <i>rnt</i>
                     |CITS: [10049912]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [7968921][11459953][15476890][20297719][26442508]|"
