     gene            3034672..3035652
                     /gene="ygfZ"
                     /locus_tag="b2898"
                     /label=ygfZ
     CDS             3034672..3035652
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ygfZ"
                     /locus_tag="b2898"
                     /product="folate-binding protein YgfZ"
                     /label=ygfZ
                     /note="YgfZ is a folate (most likely
                     tetrahydrofolate)-dependent protein |CITS: [20489182]|. 
                     Although various physiological functions for YgfZ have been
                     proposed, its true function is still unknown. YgfZ is 
                     currently thought to play a role in Fe-S cluster synthesis 
                     or repair during oxidative stress |CITS: [20489182]|.
                     The mitochondrial homolog of YgfZ, IBA57, promotes assembly
                     of [4Fe-4S] clusters and their insertion into acceptor 
                     proteins. However, while IBA57 is essential, YgfZ is not.
                     YgfZ is needed for activity of the radical S-adenosyl 
                     enzymes |FRAME: G6364 MiaB| and |FRAME: G6435 RimO|, which 
                     thiomethylate some tRNAs and the S12 ribosomal protein, 
                     respectively. Recent models suggest that these proteins 
                     contain one 4Fe-4S cluster and one 3Fe-4S cluster (the 
                     auxiliary cluster) |CITS: [34526715]|. The reaction they 
                     catalyze is thought to take place in two steps. In the 
                     first step, one molecule of SAM is used to methylate a 
                     bridging &mu;-sulfido ion of the auxiliary cluster, forming
                     a thio-methyl group. In the second step a second SAM 
                     molecule is cleaved to a 5'-deoxyadenosyl 5'-radical, which
                     abstracts the C2 hydrogen of the substrate, and the 
                     thio-methyl group is then transferred from the auxiliary 
                     cluster to the radical-activated carbon in the tRNA 
                     substrate, leaving a 3Fe-3S inactive cluster that must be 
                     repaired or replaced before the enzyme could catalyze the 
                     next round |CITS: [23543739][23991893][34526715]|. It is 
                     thought that YgfZ is involved in the repair/exchange of the
                     spent cluster of MiaB and RimO |CITS: [36902159]|. Genetic 
                     interaction experiments indicate that the activity of the 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-7609 MnmEG complex| damages |FRAME: G6364 
                     MiaB| and this damage can be reversed by YgfZ activity 
                     |CITS: [22081392]|.
                     The crystal structure of YgfZ shows a three-domain 
                     architecture with a central hydrophobic channel, similar to
                     the structure of dimethylglycine oxidase. A potential 
                     folate binding site was identified within the channel, and 
                     binding of folate and THF to YgfZ was confirmed |CITS: 
                     [15489424]|.
                     A <i>ygfZ</i> null mutant grows poorly at low temperature 
                     |CITS: [16359333]| and on minimal media and is more 
                     sensitive to oxidative stress than wild type |CITS: 
                     [20489182]|. The mutant supresses the growth defect of two 
                     <i>hda</i><sup>ts</sup> alleles |CITS: [16359333]|. A 
                     <i>ygfZ</i> null mutant has lower levels of several 
                     modified nucleotides in tRNA |CITS: [16359333]| and has 
                     reduced activity of several Fe-S cluster-dependent enzymes 
                     such as MiaB |CITS: [20489182]|. An <i>mnmE</i> mutation 
                     partially supresses the cold-sensitive growth phenotype 
                     |CITS: [16359333][22081392]| and MiaB activity defect 
                     |CITS: [22081392]| of the <i>ygfZ</i> null mutant. The C228
                     residue is required for activity of YgfZ |CITS: 
                     [21059273][22092591]|.
                     A YgfZ T108P mutation along with a Fur P18T mutation were 
                     found to be most beneficial for acid stress in a 
                     meta-analysis of dozens of adaptive laboratory evolution 
                     (ALE) experiments. The YgfZ T108P mutation was also 
                     associated with decreases in CP4-44 prophage iModulon 
                     activities |CITS: [38934464]|.
                     Expression of YgfZ is induced by plumbagin |CITS: 
                     [16385035]|, and it appears that YgfZ is involved in 
                     detoxification of this compound |CITS: [21059273]|."
                     /translation="MAFTPFPPRQPTASARLPLTLMTLDDWALATITGADSEKYMQGQV
