     gene            complement(3044474..3045676)
                     /gene="ubiI"
                     /locus_tag="b2906"
                     /label=ubiI
     CDS             complement(3044474..3045676)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ubiI"
                     /locus_tag="b2906"
                     /product="bifunctional 2-octaprenylphenol 6-hydroxylase /
                     Ubi complex"
                     /EC_number="1.14.13.240"
                     /label=ubiI
                     /note="UbiI is a component of the Ubi complex metabolon
                     |CITS: [30686758]|.  UbiI functions in the hydroxylation of
                     the C5 position during the aerobic biosynthesis of 
                     ubiquinone-8.
                     UbiI predominantly localizes to the cytosol.  The seven 
                     ubiquinone biosynthesis proteins UbiE, UbiF, UbiG, UbiH, 
                     UbiI, UbiJ, and UbiK form a 1 MDa complex in the cytosol 
                     |CITS: [30686758]|.
                     Purified UbiI is unstable; an inactive truncated form was 
                     isolated and behaves like a tetramer in solution.  The 
                     crystal structure of this protein has been solved at 2.0 
                     &Aring; resolution.  Point mutants in the predicted FAD 
                     binding site do not complement a &Delta;<i>ubiI</i> mutant 
                     |CITS: [23709220]|.
                     Deletion of <i>ubiI</i> (<i>visC</i>) has no effect on 
                     photosensitivity or aerobic respiration |CITS: 
                     [1339425][23709220]|; however, a <i>ubiI</i> mutant strain 
                     contains only 7% of wild type levels of ubiquinone-8 and 
                     accumulates 3-octaprenyl-4-hydroxyphenol (4-HP<sub>8</sub>)
                     |CITS: [23709220]|.  The residual C5 hydroxylase activity 
                     appears to be provided by UbiF |CITS: [23709220]|.  A 
                     &Delta;<i>ubiI</i>&Delta;<i>ubiK</i> double mutant produces
                     no detectable ubiquinone and in contrast to the single 
                     mutants, has a significant growth defect when grown 
                     aerobically on rich medium and does not grow on the 
                     non-fermentable carbon sources oleate, acetate, and 
                     succinate |CITS: [29042439]|.  A <i>ubiI</i> null mutant 
                     shows increased mean peak length duration of mechanically 
                     induced calcium flux across the membrane |CITS: 
                     [28808010]|.  The requirement for <i>ubiI</i> and other 
                     ubiquinone biosynthesis genes for growth in long chain 
                     fatty acids (LCFAs) such as oleate suggests that ubiquinone
                     mitigates oxidative stress generated during LCFA 
                     degradation |CITS: [29042439]|.
                     Radiotracer studies have suggested that UbiA, UbiD, and 
                     UbiI can utilize <i>p</i>-aminobenzoate or intermediates 
                     derived from it as substrates |CITS: [25681964]|.
