     gene            3048971..3049300
                     /gene="zapA"
                     /locus_tag="b2910"
                     /label=zapA
     CDS             3048971..3049300
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="zapA"
                     /locus_tag="b2910"
                     /product="cell division protein ZapA"
                     /label=zapA
                     /note="ZapA binds to FtsZ polymers |CITS: [17428494]| and 
                     promotes the coherence of the Z-ring by cross-linking FtsZ 
                     polymers and stabilizing longitudinal interactions between 
                     FtsZ molecules |CITS: [20969647]|.  This effect is 
                     counteracted by ZapB |CITS: [22056926]|.  In the absence of
                     functional Min and nucleoid exclusion (SlmA) systems, the 
                     MatP-ZapB-ZapA system positions the Z ring over the 
                     nucleoid in mid-cell |CITS: [25101671]|.  The protein 
                     network formed by the divisome proteins FtsZ, ZapA, ZapB 
                     and MatP has been studied using superresolution imaging, 
                     showing that these proteins form a multi-layered protein 
                     network that extends from the cell membrane to the 
                     chromosome and that stabilizes the FtsZ ring |CITS: 
                     [25848771]|.  The ZapA and ZapB proteins are able to form 
                     an FtsZ-independent structure at midcell which appears to 
                     provide positional information to FtsZ |CITS: [28249098]|. 
                     Localization of the chromosomal Ter region at the divisome 
                     is dependent on the MatP-ZapB-ZapA system |CITS: 
                     [26762981]|.
                     Before septum formation, a complex of the early-division 
                     proteins ZipA, ZapA, and ZapB oscillates along the cell 
                     with a periodicity that is opposite that of MinC.  The 
                     oscillation is dependent on the presence of FtsZ filaments 
                     and on the Min system |CITS: [24327341]|.
                     During growth in minimal medium, ZapA and FtsZ are present 
                     at approximately equimolar concentration |CITS: 
                     [19842714]|.  ZapA was reported to be a potent inhibitor of
                     the GTPase activity of FtsZ |CITS: [17428494]|, but later 
                     experiments with ZapA lacking a His tag found little effect
                     of ZapA on FtsZ GTPase activity |CITS: [19842714]|. |CITS: 
                     [22056926]| found that inhibition of the GTPase activity of
                     FtsZ by ZapA was stronger at pH 6.5 than at pH 7.5; at the 
                     higher pH, addition of ZapB further weakened the effect.
                     ZapA localizes to the cell division site, but is not 
                     essential |CITS: [12368265]|.  It is not required for 
                     targeting of the MinC/MinD complex to the Z-ring |CITS: 
                     [15060045]|, but is required for recruiting |FRAME: 
                     EG11878-MONOMER ""ZapB""| to the Z-ring |CITS: [20487275]|.
                      ZapA also interacts with FtsI and FtsN |CITS: [20497333]| 
                     and indirectly, via FtsZ, with ZipA |CITS: [23957637]|.  
                     FtsZ and ZapA disassemble from the cell division site 
                     before the constriction of the inner membrane is complete 
                     and the cytoplasm is separated |CITS: [24506818]|.  The 
                     remaining components of the divisome appear to also follow 
                     a ""first in, first out"" principle, i.e. FtsZ and ZapA are
                     the first proteins to depart the divisome |CITS: 
                     [27096604]|; however, ZapA remains within a diffuse 
                     structure at mid-cell after addition of a cell division 
                     inhibitor.  This structure requires the presence of ZapB 
                     |CITS: [28249098]|.
                     A preliminary analysis of the crystal structure of ZapA has
                     been published |CITS: [16511026]|, and a crystal structure 
                     has been solved at 1.95 &Aring; resolution |CITS: 
                     [25002581]|.  ZapA consists of two domains; the N-terminal 
                     globular domain is required for interaction with FtsZ, 
                     while the C-terminal coiled-coil domain is required for 
                     interaction with ZapB and for homodimerization |CITS: 
                     [22056926]|.  ZapA exists in a dimer/tetramer equilibrium 
                     in solution |CITS: [17428494]|. An I83E mutant is a 
                     constitutive dimer that is able to bind to FtsZ, but is 
                     unable to induce FtsZ bundling |CITS: [23098212]|.  
                     Residues that are involved in the interaction with FtsZ and
                     in filament bundling were identified by site-directed 
                     mutagenesis |CITS: [25002581]|.  An N60Y mutant does not 
                     interact with FtsZ and does not complement the <i>zapA</i> 
                     cell division defect, but appears to still interact with 
                     ZapB |CITS: [28249098]|.
                     A <i>zapA</i> deletion strain has a cytokinesis defect 
                     |CITS: [19842714][20969647][23859153]|.  Reports disagree 
                     on whether |CITS: [20969647]| or not |CITS: [19842714]| 
                     His-tagged ZapA can complement the defect.  A <i>zapA</i> 
                     mutant has an abnormal cell division septum; 
                     single-molecule based superresolution imaging shows a 
                     disordered arrangement of FtsZ clusters |CITS: [23859153]|.
                      Overproduction of ZapA results in aberrant localization of
                     FtsZ and ZapB and the formation of elongated cells |CITS: 
                     [22056926]|.
                     ZapA: ""<b>Z</b>-ring-<b>a</b>ssociated <b>p</b>rotein 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [12368265]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16824090][15491352][12526758]|"
