                     /product="6S RNA"
                     /note="The 6S RNA is involved in stationary phase
                     regulation of transcription by the |FRAME: RNAP70-CPLX| 
                     holoenzyme |CITS: [10892648]|. The 6S RNA binds to both 
                     |FRAME: RPOD-MONOMER| (&sigma;<sup>70</sup>) and the 
                     beta/beta' subunits of |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX| enabling 
                     stable association between the two |CITS: [10892648]|. 
                     During stationary phase, 6S RNA represses expression from a
                     subset of sigma70-dependent promoters containing an 
                     extended -10 promoter element |CITS: 
                     [10892648][15357303][15262935]|. 6S RNA also has an effect 
                     on the transcription of several |FRAME: RPOS-MONOMER 
                     ""sigmaS""|-dependent promoters |CITS: [15262935]|. <i>In 
                     vitro</i>, 6S RNA alone inhibits both stationary-phase and 
                     exponential promoters, suggesting that additional factors 
                     are involved in its effects <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [17332013]|. <i>In vivo</i>, 6S RNA serves as a template 
                     for the synthesis of a short RNA product (pRNA), which 
                     during exponential growth forms the 6S RNA:pRNA complex, 
                     releasing the RNA from RNAP, even when 6S RNA transcripts 
                     are incompletely processed |CITS: [36198425]|. The pRNA 
                     synthesis preferentially uses ATP as an initiator, although
                     NADH, NAD+, and FAD can initiate pRNA synthesis by RNAP 
                     |CITS: [36198425]|.
                     The 6S RNA is not required for growth and is not essential 
                     for export of proteins |CITS: [2579059]|, but cells lacking
                     6S RNA show a stationary phase survival defect and are less
                     competitive during growth |CITS: [15262935]|.
                     6S RNA secondary structure has been discussed |CITS: 
                     [2438656]|.
                     The 6S RNA has been detected in a ribonucleoprotein 
                     particle |CITS: [342486]|.
                     Regulation has been described |CITS: 
                     [6183252][2579060][10892648]|. The 6S RNA is expressed at 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [10892648]|. Overexpression of 
                     |FRAME: SPF-RNA| increases the abundance of 6S RNA |CITS: 
                     [6183252]|.
                     The <i>ssrS</i> gene is one of the genes most highly 
                     dependent on RNA polymerase &sigma;<sup>70</sup> complex, 
                     based on runoff/microarray (ROMA) experiments among genes 
                     whose promoters have been characterized |CITS: [21398637]|.
                     Mutational analysis of 6S RNA and RNA polymerase holoenzyme
                     revealed that 6S RNA binding depends on promoter-like 
                     connections, i.e. in the -10 element-like region and in the
                     core-recognition element (CRE)-like region, of the 
                     non-template strand of the <i>ssrS</i> gene, whereas pRNA 
                     synthesis depends on both the promoter-like connections and
                     the &sigma;-finger of the &sigma;<sup>70</sup>, likely 
                     through stabilizing short RNA products within the 6S 
                     RNA/RNAP holoenzyme complex. |FRAME: EG10230-MONOMER| and 
                     |FRAME: EG11578-MONOMER| inhibit full-length pRNA synthesis
                     and release under stringent conditions |CITS: [38692564]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [10068996][12732300][24742053][29916345][32213244]|"
     gene            3049600..3050148
                     /gene="fau"
                     /locus_tag="b2912"
                     /label=fau
     CDS             3049600..3050148
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fau"
                     /locus_tag="b2912"
                     /product="5-formyltetrahydrofolate cyclo-ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.3.3.2"
                     /label=fau
                     /note="The YgfA protein appears to be a
                     5-formyltetrahydrofolate (5-CHO-THF) cyclo-ligase. 
                     5-CHO-THF is formed in a side reaction of |FRAME: 
                     GLYOHMETRANS-CPLX| and is an inhibitor of various 
                     folate-dependent enzymes |CITS: [20952389]|.
                     <i>ygfA</i> is important for survival under competitive 
                     planktonic growth conditions |CITS: [16849790]| and for 
                     formation of dormant persister cells |CITS: 
                     [18519731][21602347][30247120]|. Overexpression of 
                     <i>ygfA</i> leads to increased tolerance to ofloxacin 
                     |CITS: [18519731]|.
                     Deletion of <i>ygfA</i> does not cause a growth defect in 
                     rich or minimal medium. However, when grown in minimal 
                     medium with added glycine, the strain accumulates 
                     5-CHO-THF.  If the nitrogen source in minimal medium is 
                     glycine, a <i>ygfA</i> mutant has a severe growth defect, 
                     likely due to inhibition of serine hydroxymethyltransferase
                     and the glycine cleavage system by the accumulated 
                     5-CHO-THF |CITS: [20952389]|. |CITS: [30247120]| found that
                     a &Delta;<i>ygfA</i> mutant grows to a lower cell density 
                     in rich media than wild type. Mutants lacking the enzyme 
                     become highly susceptible to antifolate agents |CITS: 
                     [21372133]|. Gene expression during growth of a 
                     &Delta;<i>ygfA</i> mutant has been analyzed in comparison 
                     to wild-type |CITS: [30247120]|. Transcription of 
                     <i>ygfA</i> is induced upon biofilm formation |CITS: 
                     [14727089]| and is upregulated in the viable but 
                     nonculturable state induced in pathogenic <i>E. coli</i> 
                     strains by low-level chlorination exposure |CITS: 
                     [28662489]|. Expression may be regulated by Rho-dependent 
                     termination activity |CITS: [21036909]|.
                     <i>ygfA</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     Fau: ""<b>f</b>olinic <b>a</b>cid <b>u</b>tilization"""
