     gene            3050210..3050350
                     /gene="sibC"
                     /locus_tag="b4446"
                     /label=sibC
     misc_RNA        3050210..3050350
                     /gene="sibC"
                     /locus_tag="b4446"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA antitoxin SibC"
                     /note="The SibC small regulatory RNA is encoded on the
                     opposite strand of a small open reading frame encoding the 
                     toxic peptide IbsC and funtions as an antitoxin |CITS: 
                     [18710431]|.  Thus, IbsC-SibC form a type I toxin-antitoxin
                     system.
                     Two independent target recognition domains of SibC 
                     selectively recognize regions within the <i>ibsC</i> mRNA 
                     |CITS: [20453032]|.  To be effective for gene silencing, 
                     the single-stranded nature of the target recognition 
                     domains appears to be required |CITS: [23393193]|.
                     SibC interacts with the protein |FRAME: EG12866-MONOMER| 
                     |CITS: [36328526]|.
                     SibC was first detected during a comparative genomics 
                     screen for sequence conservation |CITS: [11445539]|.  The 
                     <i>ygfA-serA</i> intergenic region was initially shown to 
                     contain one copy of a repeat sequence element (initially 
                     termed QUAD1 and later renamed SIB) that occurs five times 
                     in the <i>E. coli</i> K-12 genome |CITS: 
                     [10673004][18710431]|.  Many sequence regions conserved 
                     between the SIB elements are predicted to form stem-loop 
                     structures |CITS: [18710431]|.  Two RNA products arising 
                     from <i>sibC</i> have been detected |CITS: 
                     [11445539][18710431]|; the shorter RNA product may be 
                     derived by processing of the longer transcript |CITS: 
                     [18710431]|.
                     SibC is expressed at varying levels throughout growth; the 
                     RNA level is generally higher in minimal medium than in 
                     rich medium |CITS: [11445539][18710431]|.  The relative 
                     amounts of SibC RNA and <i>ibsC</i> mRNA have been measured
                     at different time points during growth |CITS: [32516493]|.
                     Deletion of <i>sibC</i> has no detectable effect on cell 
                     growth.  However, in a <i>sibC</i> deletion strain, 
                     expression of SibC from a plasmid must be highly induced to
                     yield viable cells; this appears to be due to 
                     overexpression of the toxic peptide IbsC.  Expression of 
                     SibC prevents the toxic effect of overexpressing IbsC 
                     |CITS: [18710431]|.  Overexpression of an RNA antisense to 
                     SibC was found to suppress cell growth and can be 
                     attributed to the production of the toxic peptide IbsC 
                     |CITS: [19090519]|.
                     Ryx: ""RNA of unknown function at X position (10 minute) in
                     the genome"" |CITS: [11445539]|
                     SibC: ""<b>s</b>hort, <b>i</b>ntergenic, a<b>b</b>undant 
                     sequence"" |CITS: [18710431]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12762038][12654996][32213244]|"
     gene            complement(3050249..3050308)
                     /gene="ibsC"
                     /locus_tag="b4665"
                     /label=ibsC
     CDS             complement(3050249..3050308)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ibsC"
                     /locus_tag="b4665"
                     /product="toxic peptide IbsC"
                     /label=ibsC
                     /note="The small open reading frame |FRAME: G0-10631| was 
                     detected by similarity to the open reading frames encoded 
                     on the opposite strand of the <i>sib</i> small RNAs |CITS: 
                     [18710431]|.  Overexpression of an antisense-SibC (i.e. 
                     sense-IbsC) RNA suppresses cell growth, which can be 
                     attributed to the production of the toxic peptide IbsC 
                     |CITS: [19090519]|.
                     Overexpression of IbsC is toxic to the cell by disrupting 
                     membrane integrity.  Whole-genome gene expression analysis 
                     after induction of IbsC expression shows changes in 
                     expression of many stress response and membrane proteins 
                     |CITS: [18710431]|.
                     The |FRAME: G0-10631| mRNA was one of over two hundred RNAs
                     that were found to bind the aminoglycoside antibiotic 
                     |FRAME: CPD-4822| |CITS: [36927548]|.
                     IbsC is predicted to be a bitopic inner membrane protein 
                     |CITS: [27510400]|.
                     IbsC: ""<b>i</b>nduction <b>b</b>rings <b>s</b>tasis"" 
                     |CITS: [18710431]|"
