                     /go_component="plasma membrane [goid 0005886] [evidence
                     RCA] [PMID 27510400]"
                     /go_component="plasma membrane [goid 0005886] [evidence
                     IEA] [PMID 26602691]"
                     /go_process="programmed cell death [goid 0012501]
                     [evidence IMP] [PMID 18710431]"
     gene            complement(3050537..3051769)
                     /gene="serA"
                     /locus_tag="b2913"
                     /label=serA
     CDS             complement(3050537..3051769)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="serA"
                     /locus_tag="b2913"
                     /product="multifunctional phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase / 
                     D-3-phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase / 2-oxoglutarate 
                     reductase ((R)-2-hydroxyglutarate-forming) / 2-oxoglutarate
                     reductase ((S)-2-hydroxyglutarate-forming)"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.95; 1.1.1.399"
                     /label=serA
                     /note="D-3-phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase (SerA, PHGDH) 
                     catalyzes the first committed step in the biosynthesis of 
                     L-serine. The enzyme is regulated by allosteric end-product
                     inhibition that shows cooperativity. Inhibition by serine 
                     acts primarily through reduction of catalytic velocity and 
                     has only a small effect on the K<sub>m</sub>s of the 
                     substrates; SerA is thus classified as a type V allosteric 
                     enzyme.
                     SerA also has an &alpha;-ketoglutarate reductase activity, 
                     producing 2-hydroxyglutarate. Kinetically, the best SerA 
                     substrates are 3-phosphohydroxypyruvate (3PHP) and 
                     &alpha;-ketoglutarate (&alpha;KG), i.e. <i>in vitro</i>, 
                     the reaction equilibrium is skewed towards production of 
                     3-phosphoglycerate (3PG) rather than the 3PHP intermediate 
                     required for serine biosynthesis |CITS: [8550422]|. The 
                     hypothesis that the activities and reaction equilibria of 
                     SerA may play a role in recycling NADH back to 
                     NAD<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [8550422][GUSYATINER15]| is 
                     supported by experimental data |CITS: [29494135]|. Unlike 
                     enzymes from some other organisms, <i>E. coli</i> SerA 
                     utilizes an intrinsic interconversion cycle between 
                     NAD<sup>+</sup> and NADH that is coupled to utilization of 
                     &alpha;KG, which is the side product of the reaction 
                     catalyzed by SerC that follows SerA in the serine 
                     biosynthesis pathway |CITS: [29494135]|. This ability to 
                     reduce &alpha;KG to 2-hydroxyglutarate is found in Type II 
                     but not in Type I or Type III PHGDHs. A K141R mutant in a 
                     sequence motif that distinguishes &alpha;KG-utilizing from 
                     non-utilizing enzymes decreases the catalytic efficiency of
                     the <i>E. coli</i> enzyme with the &alpha;KG substrate 
                     50-fold |CITS: [31344342]|. The 2-hydroxyglutarate produced
                     by SerA has been shown to be converted back to &alpha;KG by
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-9749 SerA| |CITS: [36144368]|. The basis for 
                     allosteric and cooperative inhibition of SerA by serine has
                     been studied extensively. Occupation of two of the four 
                     serine binding sites in the homotetramer results in 85% 
                     inhibition of activity |CITS: [8771194]|. Further binding 
                     of serine shows negative cooperativity |CITS: [10595555]|. 
                     Phosphate is able to reduce the site-to-site cooperative 
                     effects on serine binding |CITS: [10600116]|; the effect 
                     was mainly due to the presence of intrinsically bound NADH 
                     |CITS: [12183470]|. A Trp139Gly mutation results in a 
                     homodimeric enzyme that has lost cooperativity in serine 
                     binding and allosteric inhibition |CITS: [10683264]|. 
                     Site-directed mutagenesis of residues within the effector 
                     binding site, the regulatory interface between subunits, 
                     and a flexible hinge region support a model where movement 
                     of adjacent domains is involved in inhibition of the 
                     enzymatic activity |CITS: 
                     [8798520][9712860][11050089][10852732]|. Transient kinetic 
                     analysis showed that the cooperativity of inhibition of 
                     catalytic activity results from a conformational change due
                     to serine binding |CITS: [21391703]|. An enzyme missing the
                     regulatory domain is no longer inhibited by serine, but 
                     other kinetic parameters remain the same |CITS: 
                     [12199695]|. Hybrid tetramers provided further insight into
                     the mechanism of allosteric inhibition |CITS: 
                     [12644455][14718528]|. Novel allosteric sites were 
                     predicted by computational modeling and tested <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [26592133][24733054]|.
                     Site-directed mutagenesis has allowed the identification of
                     residues within the active site that contribute to 
                     substrate binding and catalysis |CITS: [10026144]|. 
                     Mutations in the hinge region between the substrate and 
                     nucleotide binding domains affect the 
                     <i>k</i><sub>cat</sub> of the enzyme; certain mutations 
                     uncouple serine binding and catalytic inhibition |CITS: 
                     [11278587]|.
                     Extensive site-directed mutagenesis and structural studies 
                     have contributed to a detailed view of the interactions 
                     between allosteric regulation, cooperativity and catalytic 
                     activity |CITS: [16363798][17459882]|. Further insight into
                     the catalytic pathway was provided by stopped-flow kinetic 
                     analysis, indicating that the rate-limiting step in both 
                     catalytic directions is a conformational change of the 
                     enzyme |CITS: [18776184]|. Serine binding appears to lead 
                     to the formation of a dead-end quaternary complex between 
                     the enzyme, coenzyme, substrate, and effector that 
                     eliminates the conformational change subsequent to 
                     substrate binding |CITS: [19924905]|.
                     Crystal structures of the wild type enzyme and various 
                     mutants have been solved |CITS: 
                     [7719856][15035616][15823035][17459882]|. The structure 
                     showed that each subunit of the homotetramer consists of 
                     three distinct domains, a nucleotide binding domain, a 
                     substrate binding domain, and a regulatory/serine binding 
                     domain |CITS: [7719856]|.
                     <i>serA</i> is essential for growth on glycerol minimal 
                     medium; the growth defect can be rescued by addition of 
                     serine |CITS: [17012394][3108237][14047241]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [30619878][GUSYATINER15][22023909][1698780 
                     5][15662561][32149453]|
                     PHGDH = D-3-<b>ph</b>ospho<b>g</b>lycerate 
                     <b>d</b>e<b>h</b>ydrogenase"
