     gene            complement(3059636..3060532)
                     /gene="srsR"
                     /locus_tag="b2921"
                     /label=srsR
     CDS             complement(3059636..3060532)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="srsR"
                     /locus_tag="b2921"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional activator YgfI"
                     /label=srsR
                     /note="YgfI is a LysR-type transcription factor involved
                     in regulation of formate, glycerol, dihydroxyacetone, and 
                     fucose utilization, hydrogen peroxide resistance, biofilm 
                     formation, antibiotic resistance, and stationary phase 
                     |CITS:[34428301][35682733][35508583]|.
                     The transcription of the <i>srsR</i> gene increases during 
                     transition to stationary phase for growth in LB medium, 
                     reaching levels in stationary phase 25 times higher than 
                     those of the exponential phase |CITS:[35682733]|. YgfI was 
                     also found transcriptionally up-regulated in the presence 
                     of threonine (Thr) in M9 medium |CITS:[35508583]|. 
                     Genome-wide YgfI binding sites were determined <i>in 
                     vivo</i> by the chromatin immunoprecipitation method 
                     combined with lambda exonuclease digestion (ChIP-exo) in 
                     glucose M9 minimal medium |CITS:[34428301]| and <i>in 
                     vitro</i> by genomic systematic evolution of ligands using 
                     exponential enrichment (gSELEX-chip) |CITS: [35682733]|.
                     A total of 10 YgfI binding regions were identified by 
                     gSELEX-chip, some of them flanking operons, giving rise to 
                     an estimated of 30 putative regulated genes; the 
                     transcriptional regulation <i>in vivo</i> of various genes 
                     was confirmed by RT-qPCR |CITS: [35682733]|. The 11-bp YfgI
                     box TCAGATTTTGC was determined by <i>in vitro</i> gel shift
                     assay and DNase-I footprinting assays in the 
                     <i>yfdONMLK-yfdPQ</i> spacer identified by gSELEX-chip, in 
                     which four boxes were found, after this consensus sequence 
                     was identified in the other binding regions 
                     |CITS:[35682733]|. The binding targets identified by 
                     gSELEX-chip were related to biofilm formation, antibiotic 
                     resistance, oxidative stress response, and fucose 
                     metabolism |CITS:[35682733]|, which are mainly expressed 
                     during stationary phase; however, YgfI also regulates genes
                     in exponential phase |CITS:[35682733]|. YgfI suppressed 
                     biofilm formation, probably through transcriptional 
                     activation of the BluR repressor |CITS: [35682733]|. Given 
                     the role of YgfI in biofilm formation, antibiotic 
                     resistance, oxidative stress response, and fucose 
                     metabolism, it has been proposed that YgfI could be 
                     involved in survival of <i>E. coli</i> in the host gut 
                     |CITS:[35682733]|.
                     The transcriptional response for the deletion of YgfI was 
                     characterized by RNA-seq in M9 minimal medium supplemented 
                     with 7 mM l-threonine, and in some differentially expressed
                     genes a putative YgfI binding site was computationally 
                     predicted |CITS:[35508583]|. The YgfI targets genes 
                     identified by integrative analysis of ChIP-exo and RNA-seq 
                     data are involved in formate, glycerol, and 
                     dihydroxyacetone utilization |CITS:[35508583]|.
                     A knockout mutant for YgfI showed deficiency in aerobic 
                     growth with glycerol as carbon source in minimal medium, 
                     compared to the wild type |CITS:[35508583]|. In minimal 
                     medium with glucose as carbon source, the growth of this 
                     YgfI mutant was only slightly affected compared to the wild
                     type when aerobic conditions were employed; however, under 
                     microaerobic conditions, YgfI mutant growth was deficient, 
                     and supplementation with threonine recovered the growth 
                     |CITS:[35508583]|. The YgfI knockout mutant showed 
                     downregulation of genes involved in formate fermentation 
                     during aerobic growth in minimal medium with threonine 
                     |CITS:[35508583]|.
                     A YgfI knockout mutant strain was reported to have an 
                     increased level of biofilm formation and decreased 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> resistance |CITS:[35682733]|.
                     A strain overexpressing YgfI showed decreased levels of 
                     biofilm formation and increased H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     resistance |CITS: [35682733]|.
                     Upstream of the <i>ygfI</i> gene, a sequence was found that
                     could work as a transcriptional promoter, and the 
                     transcription of the <i>ygfI</i> gene is affected by 
                     aerobic-anaerobic shift |CITS: [35880217]|. 
                     Interconnectivity between the <i>ygfI</i> gene and the 
                     stress regulators <i>cspABE</i> and <i>oxyR</i> has been 
                     reported |CITS:[35835781]|."
                     /translation="MDIFISKKMRNFILLAQTNNIARAAEKIHMTASPFGKSIAALEEQ
