     gene            complement(3062306..3063166)
                     /gene="mscS"
                     /locus_tag="b2924"
                     /label=mscS
     CDS             complement(3062306..3063166)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mscS"
                     /locus_tag="b2924"
                     /product="small conductance mechanosensitive channel MscS"
                     /label=mscS
                     /note="MscS is a mechanosensitive channel of small
                     conductance; MscS conductance is 0.8-1 nanosiemens in <i>E.
                     coli</i> giant protoplasts |CITS: [10202137]|.
                     MscS activity is adaptive, that is, it declines in response
                     to sustained stimulation |CITS: [9691118]|. In patch clamp 
                     experiments MscS activity occurs in short bursts that last 
                     for a few seconds |CITS: [10202137]|. Gating of MscS is 
                     controlled by tension within, or voltage across the lipid 
                     bilayer |CITS: [7690260][12080120]|. Activation of MscS 
                     depends upon tension, while inactivation is both tension 
                     and voltage dependent; channel activation requires a high 
                     rate of increase in membrane tension; slower rates of 
                     increase can be ignored |CITS: [15657299]|.
                     MscS is a homoheptamer |CITS: [12446901][16079137]| with 
                     three transmembrane (TM) domains per subunit and a large 
                     cytoplasmic chamber |CITS: [12446901]|. The N-terminus of 
                     each subunit forms a mixed helical hairpin structure 
                     located at the periplasmic interface of the lipid bilayer 
                     |CITS: [18343404]|. The functional state represented in the
                     crystal structure of |CITS: [12446901]| - conducting |CITS:
                     [16500980]| or non-conducting |CITS: 
                     [15111405][16513774][17114233]| - is unclear. A model for 
                     closed |CITS: [18343404]| and open |CITS: 
                     [18591417][18755978]| conformations of MscS has been 
                     proposed.
                     The inhibition of MscS channel activity due to crosslinking
                     of the C-terminus or adding Ni<sup>2+</sup> to 
                     C-terminally, hexahistidine tagged proteins suggests that 
                     the C-terminus acts as a cytoplasmic gate and moves apart 
                     upon activation of the channel |CITS: [12551944]|. Deletion
                     of the C-terminus of MscS resulted in decreased stability 
                     and activity |CITS: [15304354]|. Crosslinking studies of 
                     cysteine substitutions reveal the very flexible 
                     architecture of the MscS channel and support predictions 
                     that MscS undergoes dramatic displacement of domains upon 
                     activation and deactivation |CITS: [12767977]|.
                     MscS fluorescent fusion proteins, expressed at basal 
                     levels, are evenly distributed and mobile throughout the 
                     membrane |CITS: [27596282]|.
                     A strain lacking MscS, MscK and MscL exhibits defects in 
                     Z-ring placement in the presence of the septation 
                     inhibitor, cephalexin |CITS: [21810996]|.
                     Parabens induce MscS activity which allows leakage of cell 
                     contents, suggesting a mechanism for their antimicrobial 
                     activity |CITS: [15770478]| although later experiments 
                     |CITS: [18795793]| suggest that MscS may not be the primary
                     target for parabens. MscS is slightly anion-selective 
                     |CITS: [7690260]|.
                     MscS mediates physiologically relevant copper influx |CITS:
                     [40563205]|.
                     <i>mscS</i> expression is induced by RpoS upon entry into 
                     stationary phase and by osmotic stress |CITS: [14671322]|. 
                     Seven mechanosensitive ion channels have been characterised
                     in <i>E. coli</i> K-12: |FRAME: CPLX0-7627 ""MscL""|, MscS,
                     |FRAME: G6255-MONOMER ""MscK""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-7985 
                     ""MscM""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-7982 ""YnaI""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-7983
                     ""YbiO""| and |FRAME: CPLX0-7981 ""YbdG""|.  A strain 
                     lacking all seven of these proteins fails to demonstrate 
                     channel opening in patch clamp experiments |CITS: 
                     [22874652]|.
                     <i>mscS</i>: <u>m</u>echano<u>s</u>ensitive <u>c</u>hannel 
                     of <u>s</u>mall conductance
                     Reviews: |CITS: [15063854][17466568][22404681]|<BR>Related 
                     reviews: |CITS: [10322175][11275684][12626684][12629971][1 
                     2948773][14630329][15228520][20825352][21599642][22000509][
                     22915507][23947546][24819593][36499396]|<BR>Comment: |CITS:
                     [12446894]|"
