     gene            complement(3066031..3067050)
                     /gene="epd"
                     /locus_tag="b2927"
                     /label=epd
     CDS             complement(3066031..3067050)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="epd"
                     /locus_tag="b2927"
                     /product="D-erythrose-4-phosphate dehydrogenase"
                     /EC_number="1.2.1.72"
                     /label=epd
                     /note="A tetrameric D-erythrose-4-phosphate dehydrogenase 
                     encoded by the <i>epd</i> gene in <i> E. coli</i> catalyses
                     the first reaction of NAD-dependent oxidation of 
                     D-erythrose-4-phosphate in the <i>de novo</i> pyridoxine 
                     5'-phosphate (vitamin B6) and pyridoxal 5'-phosphate 
                     biosynthesis. This enzyme shows an efficient 
                     non-phosphorylating erythrose 4-phosphate dehydrogenase 
                     activity |CITS: [7751290]| and a low phosphorylating 
                     glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase activity |CITS: 
                     [9182530]|. Most of the amino acids that are essential for 
                     the chemical mechanism and for the binding of substrates 
                     and cofactors of the enzyme encoded by <i>epd</i> is 
                     conserved for its glycolytic enzyme |FRAME: 
                     GAPDH-A-MONOMER| activity |CITS: [9182530]|.
                     There is a common chemical mechanism for the oxidation of 
                     glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate and erythrose 4-phosphate 
                     catalyzed by Epd. The reactions proceed through a two-step 
                     mechanism involving a covalent thioacyl intermediate with 
                     Cys-149. The limiting step is associated with the formation
                     of this thioacyl intermediate. Replacement of Cys-149 
                     abolishes the catalytic efficiency of Epd. The binding of 
                     erythrose 4-phosphate to the enzyme-NAD complex favors the 
                     formation of a ternary complex efficient for the hydride 
                     transfer. The binding of inorganic phosphate to the Pi site
                     of Epd, which should occur as proved by the oxidative 
                     phosphorylation of glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate, is not 
                     productive for attacking efficiently the thioacyl 
                     intermediate formed with erythrose 4-phosphate. This is 
                     responsible for the non-phosphorylating erythrose 
                     4-phosphate dehydrogenase activity |CITS: [9182530]|.
                     In contrast to the previous finding |CITS: [9260967]|, 
                     <i>epd</i> mutants were shown to be impaired for growth 
                     without pyridoxine under some growth conditions and 
                     contained less PLP than the wild type. Also, <i>gapA</i> 
                     <i>epd</i> double mutants were shown to be auxotrophic for 
                     pyridoxine |CITS: [9696782]|."
