     gene            complement(3073003..3074994)
                     /gene="tktA"
                     /locus_tag="b2935"
                     /label=tktA
     CDS             complement(3073003..3074994)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tktA"
                     /locus_tag="b2935"
                     /product="bifunctional transketolase 1 / transketolase"
                     /EC_number="2.2.1.1"
                     /label=tktA
                     /note="Transketolase catalyzes the reversible transfer of
                     a ketol group between several donor and acceptor 
                     substrates.  This key enzyme is a reversible link between 
                     glycolysis and the pentose phosphate pathway.  The enzyme 
                     is involved in the catabolism of pentose sugars, the 
                     formation of D-ribose 5-phosphate, and the provision of 
                     D-erythrose 4-phosphate, a precursor of aromatic amino 
                     acids and PLP |CITS: [4597996][7607225]|.  <i>E. coli</i> 
                     contains two transketolase isozymes, TktA and TktB.  TktA 
                     is responsible for the major transketolase activity |CITS: 
                     [8396116]|.
                     In addition to its function in central carbon metabolism, 
                     transketolase appears to also have an unexpected role in 
                     chromosome structure; a <i>tktA</i> mutant affects 
                     chromosome topology |CITS: [16135230]|.
                     Crystal structures of TktA in complex with donor and 
                     acceptor substrates and inhibitors have been solved, 
                     elucidating the reaction mechanism and mode of action of 
                     transketolase |CITS: [15299777][17914867][32839604]|.  A 
                     computational model of transketolase activity using a 
                     quantum mechanical/molecular mechanical method has been 
                     proposed, defining a new route for thiamine diphosphate 
                     activation |CITS: [26998737]|.  An ultra-high-resolution 
                     crystal structure of apo-TktA supports a catalytic model in
                     which a proton wire synchronizes catalysis at the two 
                     active sites of the dimeric enzyme |CITS: [31534226]|. 
                     Inhibition of TktA by 2'-methoxy-thiamine was investigated 
                     by ultrahigh-resolution X-ray crystallography and molecular
                     dynamics simulations.  The 2'-methoxy group clashes with 
                     the side chain of Glu411, prohibiting activation of the 
                     cofactor by protonation in the N1 position |CITS: 
                     [32839604]|.
                     The thiol of the Cys157 active site residue undergoes 
                     single- or double-oxidation to form a sulfenic acid or 
                     sulfinic acid. Single oxidation of Cys157 to the sulfenic 
                     acid leads to the formation of a high-affinity PLP binding 
                     site in TktA and increased activity of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [31695144]|, while double oxidation may inactivate the 
                     enzyme |CITS: [MITRA97]|.  Oxidative stress during 
                     fermentation appears to lead to formation of the 
                     higher-activity enzyme; TktA thus appears to be a 
                     redox-regulated enzyme |CITS: [31695144]|.
                     The urea denaturation pathways of wild type and active site
                     mutants of TktA has been investigated |CITS: 
                     [17969139][18031275]|, and the effects of temperature and 
                     pH on the structure, stability, aggregation and activity of
                     transketolase have been determined |CITS: [21723889]|. The 
                     acceptor specificity of TktA has been investigated |CITS: 
                     [23001740]|.  TktA is also able to irreversibly convert 
                     &beta;-hydroxypyruvate and ribose-5-phosphate to 
                     sedoheptulose-7-phosphate and CO<sub>2</sub> |CITS: 
                     [28880548]|.
                     Directed evolution methods and site-specific introduction 
                     of noncanonical amino acids have been used to shift the 
                     substrate specificity of TktA |CITS: [17825449][18342970][ 
                     20204200][20835425][23079923][25765309][31647171][32896280]
                     |. A crystal structure of the S385Y/D469T/R520Q variant has
                     been solved |CITS: [27767080]|.  A tradeoff between 
                     stability and activity of this enzyme was identified and 
                     addressed by introducing additional mutations |CITS: 
                     [30530661]|.  Variants with increased thermal stability 
                     have been characterized |CITS: [27233131][28145457]|, and 
                     epistatic interactions between single and double point 
                     mutants were investigated |CITS: [30404916]|.
                     A new high-throughput assay has enabled identification of 
                     effective inhibitors of TktA activity |CITS: [29950093]|. 
                     Transketolase mutants can not grow on pentoses |CITS: 
                     [4597996][4902809]|.  Overproduction of TktB causes 
                     increased transketolase activity and suppresses the 
                     <i>tktA</i> mutant phenotype |CITS: [8396116]|.  A <i>tktA 
                     tktB</i> double mutant requires pyridoxine (or 
                     4-hydroxy-L-threonine or glycolaldehyde), aromatic amino 
                     acids and vitamins for growth |CITS: [7928977]|.
                     Mutations that suppress the growth defect of a <i>tktA 
                     tktB</i> double mutant in LB were found to constitutively 
                     express <i>glpK</i> or inactivate <i>deoB</i>, suggesting 
                     that disrupted ribose-5-P metabolism results in 
                     glycerol-3-P limitation.  Growth of a <i>tktA</i> mutant on
                     casamino acids is inhibited by ribose; this effect is due 
                     to glycerol-3-P limitation |CITS: [26691989]|.
                     Null mutations in <i>tktA</i> lead to metabolic flux 
                     alterations that increase intracellular levels of the 
                     growth inhibitory metabolite methylglyoxal, and thereby 
                     have an increased persistence phenotype |CITS: [22802628]|.
                     Metabolic engineering for production of certain metabolites
                     often involves transketolase; see |CITS: [7993 
                     080][18623323][9630954][8987689][10397840][10514257][113756
                     60][12790643][14524706][25012491]| and more. TktA abundance
                     is affected by the SOS inducer and mutagen 
                     7-methoxy-2-nitronaphtho[2,1-<i>b</i>]furan (R7000) |CITS: 
                     [8600350]|.  <i>tktA</i> is negatively regulated during 
                     entry into stationary phase.  The effect by RpoS is likely 
                     indirect and might be mediated by an intermediate regulator
                     that itself is directly regulated by RpoS |CITS 
                     [15968503]|.  Expression of <i>tktA</i> and <i>tktB</i> is 
                     complementary, resulting in approximately constant levels 
                     of transketolase expression throughout growth |CITS: 
                     [18175070]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [8572885]|
                     Please note that results reported in |CITS: [17850260]| 
                     were later found to be erroneous; see the corrigendum 
                     |CITS: [DOMAIN11]| and the subsequent retraction |CITS: 
                     [32315506]|."
