     gene            complement(3077294..3079270)
                     /gene="speA"
                     /locus_tag="b2938"
                     /label=speA
     CDS             complement(3077294..3079270)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="speA"
                     /locus_tag="b2938"
                     /product="bifunctional biosynthetic arginine decarboxylase
                     / arginine decarboxylase"
                     /EC_number="4.1.1.19"
                     /label=speA
                     /note="Biosynthetic arginine decarboxylase (SpeA) carries
                     out the first step in |FRAME: PWY-40|, the decarboxylation 
                     of L-arginine to yield agmatine |CITS: [5330264][4571773]|.
                      This enzymatic activity is subject to feedback inhibition 
                     by the downstream polyamine products putrescine and 
                     spermidine |CITS: [4571773]|.
                     SpeA is specific to arginine, and can neither decarboxylate
                     nor be inhibited by L-ornithine or L-lysine |CITS: 
                     [4571773]|.
                     The active form of SpeA is a tetramer with one molecule of 
                     pyridoxal 5' phosphate bound per subunit |CITS: [4571773]|.
                      This tetrameric conformation depends on the presence of 
                     the Mg<sup>2+</sup> cofactor.  In its absence, arginine 
                     decarboxylase exists in equilibrium between the monomeric 
                     and dimeric forms, with the exact distribution of forms 
                     depending on pH |CITS: [4571774]|.  A crystal structure of 
                     SpeA has been solved at 3.1 &Aring; resolution and shows a 
                     dimer-of-dimers arrangement |CITS: [21139196]|.
                     In experiments with an <i>E. coli</i> W strain, |CITS: 
                     [4901370]| showed that putrescine biosynthesis 
                     preferentially utilized exogenously added arginine.  Cells 
                     grown with exogenous arginine preferentially utilize the 
                     |FRAME: PWY-40| (SpeA-SpeB) pathway |CITS: [4900508]|.  A 
                     significant portion of SpeA is associated with the cell 
                     envelope, suggesting that it is primarily involved in the 
                     conversion of exogenous rather than endogenous arginine to 
                     putrescine |CITS: [3894328]|.  SpeA appears to localize to 
                     the envelope via a signal sequence.  It was initially 
                     proposed that signal sequence cleavage would convert SpeA 
                     from a 74 kDa form to a 70 kDa form |CITS: [3894328]|. 
                     However, alternative N termini of SpeA were observed by 
                     |CITS: [26017780]|, one of which coincides with an internal
                     translation initiation site for SpeA located at codon 
                     Met27.  This translation initiation site was detected by 
                     retapamulin-enhanced Ribo-seq analysis and may generate the
                     70 kDa form detected in the earlier work by Buch et al. 
                     |CITS: [30904393]|.
                     The ribosomal proteins S20/L26 (RpsT, antizyme 1) and L34 
                     (RpmH, antizyme 2), originally identified as inhibitors of 
                     the biosynthetic ornithine decarboxylase, also inhibit SpeA
                     activity <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [6351053][6392292][8088419]| and <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [8088419][7539334]|.  Interestingly, transcription of 
                     <i>rpsT</i>, <i>rpmH</i>, <i>speC</i> and <i>speA</i> is 
                     sensitive to polyamines |CITS: [2185470]|.
                     cAMP-CRP represses expression of SpeA |CITS: [6290846]|. 
                     Conversely, |CITS: [3897202]| reported that <i>speA</i> 
                     expression is not sensitive to catabolite repression; this 
                     result seems to be strain-specific, and cAMP appears to act
                     indirectly |CITS: [1646785]|.  In addition to its role in 
                     directly inhibiting SpeA activity, putrescine also 
                     represses transcription of <i>speA</i> |CITS: [1646785]|. 
                     Expression of <i>speA</i> is repressed by PurR |CITS: 
                     [8388874]|.
                     A strain lacking the polyamines putrescine, spermidine and 
                     cadaverine due to deletion of <i>speA</i>, <i>speB</i>, 
                     <i>speC</i>, <i>speD</i> |CITS: [159306]| and <i>cadA</i> 
                     |CITS: [7002915]| has a reduced growth rate compared to 
                     wild type.  The presence of the <i>rpsL9</i> (streptomycin 
                     resistance) allele leads to a requirement for polyamines 
                     for growth |CITS: [7021537]|.  A &Delta;<i>speA speB speC 
                     speD speE speF cadA ldcC</i> mutant is completely deficient
                     in polyamine production and is able to grow under aerobic 
                     conditions with a decreased growth rate; however, both 
                     under anaerobic and 95% oxygen conditions, it requires 
                     polyamines for growth |CITS: [19542271]|. Deletion of 
                     <i>speA</i> impairs pili-dependent surface motility (PDSM) 
                     in <i>E. coli</i> strain W3110 |CITS: [40178519]|.
                     SpeA: ""<b>spe</b>rmidine biosynthesis <b>A</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [4564714]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [6206782][3157043][3893559]|"
