                     /product="methionine adenosyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.5.1.6"
                     /label=metK
                     /note="Methionine adenosyltransferase catalyzes the
                     formation of the sulfonium compound S-adenosylmethionine. 
                     The reaction is unusual in that the entire tripolyphosphate
                     chain is cleaved from the ATP molecule, and is further 
                     degraded to pyrophosphate and phosphate before the products
                     are released |CITS: [6251075]|. Due to its importance, the 
                     enzyme is a target for the development of antimicrobial and
                     anticancer compounds.
                     The enzyme is a homotetramer in solution |CITS: [6251075]|;
                     the tetrameric form is required for activity |CITS: 
                     [3288619][7629176]|. Various crystal structures of 
                     methionine adenosyltransferase have been solved. The enzyme
                     is a dimer of dimers with active sites located at the dimer
                     interfaces |CITS: [8550549][8611562][14967023]|. A mobile 
                     loop gates access to the active site |CITS: [8723769]|.
                     Methionine adenosyltransferase requires binding of two 
                     divalent cations and one monovalent cation per subunit for 
                     full activity |CITS: [6251075][6257692]|. The binding sites
                     have been studied |CITS: 
                     [6322838][3511045][8396974][7629147]|. The reaction 
                     proceeds by a sequential mechanism consistent with random 
                     binding of substrates and ordered product release |CITS: 
                     [6251075]|. The catalytic mechanism and active site have 
                     been studied |CITS: [3553181][10029549][10413480][10757994 
                     ][12135357][10551856][10660564]|. The R244 residue plays a 
                     role in interactions with the triphosphate chain |CITS: 
                     [9753435]|. A novel catalytic mechanism for SAM formation 
                     has been proposed |CITS: [14967023]|. Crystallographic 
                     studies revealed the physicochemical determinant of high 
                     hydrophobicity within the small dimeric interface is 
                     responsible for the kinetic stability of the methionine 
                     adenosyltransferase but also results in serious constraints
                     on complex aggregation |CITS: [35762725]|.
                     A directed evolution study has found that the I303V/Q22R 
                     mutant has 2.13-fold improved activity towards SAM in 
                     comparison to the wild type |CITS: [36652094]|.
                     Novel inhibitors of methionine adenosyltransferase have 
                     been discovered by computational docking and were tested 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [19739644]|.
                     <i>metK</i> is essential for growth in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     |CITS: [11952912]|. The <i>metK84</i> mutant has an altered
                     promoter sequence |CITS: [11952912]| and shows a defect in 
                     cell division |CITS: [9658005]|. Further studies of 
                     recruitment of cell division proteins to the septal ring in
                     the <i>metK84</i> mutant indicate that a methylation 
                     reaction is required for complete septal ring assembly 
                     |CITS: [16238627]|. Expression of methionine 
                     adenosyltransferase is regulated by MetJ |CITS: [5277087]|.
                     Transcription of <i>metK</i> is induced upon biofilm 
                     formation |CITS: [14727089]|.
                     |CITS: [8231813]| reported the presence of two adjacent 
                     genes encoding methionine adenosyltransferase, <i>metK</i> 
                     and <i>metX</i>; however, as noted by |CITS: [11952912]|, 
                     there is no evidence for the existence of <i>metX</i> in 
                     the sequenced genome.
                     Review: |CITS: [3293911]|"
