     CDS             3081643..3083037
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="galP"
                     /locus_tag="b2943"
                     /product="trifunctional galactose:H(+) symporter /
                     transport of D-galactose / transport of D-glucose"
                     /label=galP
                     /note="GalP is a member of the Major Facilitator
                     Superfamily (MFS) of transporters |CITS: [9529885]| and is 
                     one of two, along with MglABC, major routes for galactose 
                     transport into <I>E. coli</I> K-12. 2-deoxy-D-galactose is 
                     a specific substrate for GalP but not for MglABC and GalP 
                     operates by a sugar-proton symport mechanism while MglABC 
                     does not |CITS: [15558]|.  Mutation studies |CITS: 
                     [6300086]| demonstrated that insertions in the <I>galP</I> 
                     gene resulted in a mutant strain resistant to 
                     2-deoxygalactose and defective in uptake of radiolabelled 
                     2-deoxyglucose.
                     The GalP protein has been overproduced and purified and 
                     reconstituted as a galactose transporter |CITS: [10830123]|
                     and has been shown to share a high level of sequence 
                     similarity with other proton-linked systems for L-arabinose
                     (AraE,
                     64% identity) and for D-xylose (XylE, 34% identity) in 
                     <I>E.coli</I> |CITS:[3543693]|.  Equilibrium binding 
                     studies |CITS: [1850739]| indicate that cytochalasin B, 
                     which is a potent inhibitor of passive glucose transporters
                     in mammals, is bound with high-affinity by membranes of an 
                     E. coli strain constitutive for GalP.
                     The same studies indicate that this binding is inhibited by
                     the presence of substrates for GalP in order of
                     their effectiveness as substrates and/ or inhibitors of the
                     GalP transporter. Mutational studies and <sup>13</sup>C
                     cross-polarization magic-angle spinning and solid-state 
                     nuclear magnetic resonance (<sup>13</sup>C CP-MAS SSNMR) 
                     have identified residues important in substrate recognition
                     and binding |CITS: [18798051]|."
