     gene            3086859..3087275
                     /gene="yqgF"
                     /locus_tag="b2949"
                     /label=yqgF
     CDS             3086859..3087275
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yqgF"
                     /locus_tag="b2949"
                     /product="ATP-dependent, structure-specific DNA
                     endonuclease"
                     /label=yqgF
                     /note="YqgF is an essential |CITS: [11361082]|, broadly 
                     conserved protein in bacteria |CITS: [13129938]|. The YqgF 
                     family of proteins exhibits similarity to the RuvC family 
                     of RNase H fold-containing proteins and may function as 
                     Holliday junction resolvases (HJRs) |CITS: [10982859]|. 
                     YqgF is monomeric in solution in contrast to all known HJRs
                     which form dimers |CITS: [14512736]|. Genetic interaction 
                     screening suggests that YqgF functions during DNA damage 
                     repair |CITS: [26774489]|. Purified YqgF has 
                     metal-dependent 3' to 5' exonuclease activity on ssDNA, 
                     cleaves ssRNA and RNA/DNA hybrids and binds to ssRNA, 
                     ssDNA, and DNA recombination intermediates (Holliday 
                     junction, replication fork and flap substrates) |CITS: 
                     [26774489]|. Purified YqgF has DNA-independent, 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup>-dependent ATPase activity; the protein 
                     (which may dimerize under some conditions) preferentially 
                     binds and cleaves branched DNAs; YqgF binds restriction 
                     fork DNA with high affinity and cleaves at multiple sites; 
                     stalled replication forks may represent the <i>in vivo</i> 
                     target |CITS: [40398672]|
                     An NMR solution structure of YqgF reveals an RNase H fold 
                     |CITS: [12153044][14512736]|. Affinity-tagged YqgF 
                     co-purifies with |FRAME: CPLX0-3761 ""DnaN""|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10665-MONOMER ""NusA""|, |FRAME: EG10667-MONOMER 
                     ""NusG""|, |FRAME: G7656-MONOMER ""ObgE""|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10746-MONOMER ""PolA""|, |FRAME: EG10823-MONOMER 
                     ""RecA""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-2441 ""Rho""|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11061-MONOMER ""UvrA""| and |FRAME: EG11062-MONOMER 
                     ""UvrB""| |CITS: [26774489]|.
                     YqgF is involved in processing of the 5'-end of pre-16S 
                     rRNA during maturation of the ribosome; a 
                     temperature-sensitive <i>yqgF</i> mutant 
                     (<i>yqgF</i><sup>ts</sup>) grown at the nonpermissive 
                     temperature accumulates pre-16S rRNA |CITS: [25545592]|. 
                     YqgF is also implicated in transcription anti-termination; 
                     overexpression of <i>rho</i> or <i>nusA</i> in a 
                     <i>yqgF</i><sup>ts</sup> mutant leads to a synthetic growth
                     defect; transcription of several genes that use intragenic 
                     Rho-dependent terminators is decreased in a 
                     <i>yqgF</i><sup>ts</sup> mutant grown at the non-permissive
                     temperature |CITS: [22353788]|.
                     <i>yqgF</i> is essential for growth in complex media |CITS:
                     [11361082][13129938]|. A <i>yqgF</i> hypomorph is more 
                     sensitive to the DNA replication inhibitor, nalidixic acid,
                     than wild-type |CITS: [26774489]|
                     <i>yqgF</i> is repressed after cold shock treatment |CITS: 
                     [15466053]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [10982859]|."
