     gene            3088457..3089161
                     /gene="yggS"
                     /locus_tag="b2951"
                     /label=yggS
     CDS             3088457..3089161
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yggS"
                     /locus_tag="b2951"
                     /product="PLP homeostasis protein"
                     /label=yggS
                     /note="YggS is a pyridoxal phosphate (PLP)-binding protein
                     that appears to function in PLP homeostasis |CITS: 
                     [26872910]| and/or regulation of |FRAME: CPD0-1942|/|FRAME:
                     2-OXOBUTANOATE| levels |CITS: [27426274]|. YggS binds PLP 
                     at Lys36 |CITS: [24097949]| with a K<sub>D</sub> of 
                     0.37&plusmn;0.49 &micro;M |CITS: [26872910]|.
                     YggS belongs to a subfamily of the fold-type III family of 
                     PLP-dependent enzymes |CITS: [27787586]|. No enzymatic 
                     function has been identified for YggS or its homologs as of
                     2023 |CITS: [24097949]|.
                     <i>yggS</i> mutants were found to have pleiotropic 
                     phenotypes. A <i>yggS</i> null mutant is sensitive to sulfa
                     drugs, but not trimethoprim |CITS: [21185072]|. A 
                     <i>yggS</i> deletion strain was found to accumulate 
                     2-ketobutyrate during log phase and the amino acids 
                     L-valine, L-leucine, L-histidine, L-phenylalanine, 
                     L-tyrosine, as well as 2-aminobutyrate in stationary phase.
                     L-valine and 2-aminobutyrate are secreted into the culture 
                     medium; L-valine is known to inhibit growth of <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 due to that strain's loss of AHAS II |CITS: 
                     [24097949]|. This phenotype appears to be strain-specific; 
                     a &Delta;<i>yggS</i> mutant in the BW25113 background does 
                     not accumulate these metabolites |CITS: [27426274]|. Both 
                     the MG1655 and BW25113 &Delta;<i>yggS</i> strains 
                     accumulate &gamma;-L-glutamyl-L-2-aminobutyryl-glycine 
                     (|FRAME: CPD-20340|) during exponential growth |CITS: 
                     [27426274][29352090]|. The intracellular and extracellular 
                     metabolite pools of a &Delta;<i>yggS</i> mutant were also 
                     analyzed by |CITS: [26872910]|; the cells accumulated the 
                     PLP precursor |FRAME: PYRIDOXINE-5P| (PNP) as well as 
                     putrescine, <i>N</i>-acetylputrescine, and other 
                     putrescine-related metabolites, but no intra- or 
                     extracellular accumulation of valine was found. High levels
                     of pyridoxine in minimal medium appear to be toxic to the 
                     &Delta;<i>yggS</i> mutant |CITS: [26872910]|. The 
                     accumulation of PNP and subsequent perturbation of valine 
                     and threonine biosynthesis appears to be the root cause for
                     several of the &Delta;<i>yggS</i> mutant phenotypes |CITS: 
                     [30902856]|.
                     A <i>yggS</i> mutant is extremely sensitive to |FRAME: 
                     CPD0-1605| through the formation of |FRAME: CPD0-1221|, 
                     which inhibits PLP-dependent enzymes |CITS: [37040165]|. 
                     <i>yggS</i> and <i>glyA</i> null mutations are 
                     synthetically lethal on LB medium |CITS: [21185072]|. The 
                     synthetic lethality in LB medium appeared to be due to the 
                     presence of amino acids in the medium |CITS: [26872910]|. 
                     The double mutant was rescued by growth on M9 glucose + 
                     glycine |CITS: [26872910]|, although this finding was not 
                     reproduced by |CITS: [31677193]|. A <i>serA yggS</i> double
                     mutant also shows a conditional growth defect. Both 
                     phenotypes are caused by the inhibition of the |FRAME: 
                     GCVP-MONOMER| activity of the glycine cleavage system by 
                     PNP |CITS: [31677193]|.
                     Characterization of YggS variant structures and PLP binding
                     found that the active site Lys36 tightly binds PLP which 
                     causes conformational changes in the enzyme; a cluster of 
                     lysine residues near the active site, and particularly 
                     Lys137, can substitute for the Lys36 binding when it is 
                     mutated |CITS: [36218140]|. Lys137 residue located on a 
                     loop away from the PLP binding site can bind |FRAME: 
                     SSRA-RNA| and |FRAME: RNPB-RNA| RNA molecules, with 
                     conformational changes resulting from PLP binding affecting
                     the strength of the RNA binding |CITS: [39604152]|.
                     Mutations in the human homolog, PLPBP, are linked to 
                     early-onset vitamin B6-dependent epilepsy |CITS: 
                     [27912044]|; see entry for PLPBP in <a href=""https://huma 
                     ncyc.org/gene?orgid=HUMAN&id=HS14207"">HumanCyc</a> and at 
                     <a href=""https://www.omim.org/entry/604436"">OMIM</a>."
