                     /product="tRNA m(7)G46 methyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.33"
                     /label=trmB
                     /note="tRNA m<sup>7</sup>G46 methyltransferase (TrmB)
                     catalyzes methylation of N(7) of the G46 nucleotide in the 
                     variable loop of tRNAs |CITS: [12730187][15789416]|.
                     tRNAs carry multiple modifications, often in nearby 
                     nucleotide residues.  TrmB methyltransferase and tRNA 
                     binding activity is not influenced by prior tRNA 
                     modification in the T loop |CITS: [32385137]|.  Depending 
                     on the specific tRNA, lack of prior m<sup>7</sup>G 
                     modification at position 46 reduces 
                     3-(3-amino-3-carboxypropyl)uridine (acp<sup>3</sup>U) 
                     modification at position 47 by |FRAME: G7349-MONOMER TapT| 
                     |CITS: [31863583]|.
                     Based on a homology model of TrmB, potential catalytic 
                     residues have been mutagenized; kinetic analysis of the 
                     mutant enzymes showed a significant reduction in 
                     methyltransferase activity for most mutants |CITS: 
                     [15789416]|.  Crystal structures of TrmB alone and in 
                     complexes with SAM and SAH have been solved, showing that 
                     the enzyme adopts a modified Rossmann fold |CITS: 
                     [18678947][19373903]|.
                     TrmB has a relatively low affinity for tRNA, with a 
                     K<sub>D</sub> of about 6 &mu;M in the absence of its 
                     cofactor SAM |CITS: [32385137]|, but the affinity is 
                     significantly increased in the presence of SAM ( 
                     (K<sub>D</sub> of ~2 &mu;M) |CITS: [36933808]|.
                     A <i>trmB</i> mutant lacks m<sup>7</sup>G in tRNAs, but has
                     no significant growth defect |CITS: [12730187]|. As was 
                     demonstrated for other bacterial species, an <i>E. coli</i>
                     &Delta;<i>trmB</i> mutant grows slowly in the presence of 
                     hydrogen peroxide, suggesting that TrmB is important for 
                     fitness during oxidative stress |CITS: [36933808]|.
                     Using fluorescent tRNA labeling it was found that three 
                     residues located at different areas on the TrmB surface 
                     (R26, T217, and R155) are critically contributing to tRNA 
                     binding. It was also found that the methylation reaction is
                     the likely rate-limiting step, rather than binding to tRNA 
                     or product release |CITS: [36933808]|.
                     In an earlier study, a mutant allele (named <i>trmB1</i>) 
                     causing the same phenotype was mapped to 6' on the <i>E. 
                     coli</i> chromosome.  The GM18 strain containing 
                     <i>trmB1</i> also showed a significantly increased doubling
                     time |CITS: [1091626]|.  The relationship between the 
                     <i>trmB1</i> mutation and the gene encoding tRNA 
                     m<sup>7</sup>G46 methyltransferase identified here remains 
                     unclear.
                     TrmB: ""<b>tR</b>NA <b>m</b>ethyltransferase <b>B</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [15789416]|
                     Review: |CITS: [30562954]|"
