     gene            3099073..3100329
                     /gene="nupG"
                     /locus_tag="b2964"
                     /label=nupG
     CDS             3099073..3100329
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nupG"
                     /locus_tag="b2964"
                     /product="trifunctional nucleoside:H(+) symporter NupG / 
                     nucleoside:H(+) symporter / ADP-glucose:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=nupG
                     /note="Early studies in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 reported the 
                     presence of two different nucleoside transport systems, one
                     of which (<i>gru/nupG</i>) transports all nucleosides and 
                     is not affected by the nucleoside antibiotic, showdomycin, 
                     and the other (<i>cru/|FRAME: NUPC-MONOMER nupC|</i>) which
                     transports pyrimidine and adenine nucleosides and is 
                     inhibited by showdomycin |CITS: [4354132] [827549]|.
                     NupG is a broad specificity nucleoside transporter; it 
                     mediates uptake of all 6 natural purine/pyrimidine 
                     nucleosides |CITS: [374403][15513740]|. NupG mediated 
                     nucleoside transport is inhibited by proton ionophores; 
                     transport is driven primarily by the electrical component 
                     of the proton motive force |CITS: [15513740]|. Structural 
                     motifs important for ligand binding to NupG and NupC have 
                     been investigated |CITS: [15678184]|. Under conditions of 
                     envelope stress, NupG mediates uptake of ADP-glucose in a 
                     cAMP/CRP-regulated process |CITS: [30341391]|.
                     Purified NupG binds adenosine, guanosine, thymidine, 
                     cytidine, and uridine with &micro;M affinity but does not 
                     bind xanthosine; a crystal structure in the inward-open 
                     conformation has been reported and a potential nucleoside 
                     binding site identified |CITS: [33640454]|.
                     In the Transporter Classification Database |CITS: 
                     [33170213]| NupG is a member of the Nucleoside:H+ Symporter
                     (NHS) family within the Major Facilitator Superfamily.
                     <i>nup</i>: <U>n</U>ucleoside <U>up</U>take
                     "
