                     /product="prepilin peptidase"
                     /label=pppA
                     /note="In <i>E. coli</i> K-12, <i>pppA</i> encodes a
                     functional prepilin peptidase; <i>pppA</i> appears to be 
                     poorly translated but the resulting peptidase activity is 
                     sufficient to allow temperature dependent partial 
                     processing of plasmid encoded pre-pilin substrates, 
                     <i>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</i> prePilE and <i>Klebsiella 
                     pneumoniae</i> prePulG |CITS: [9515702]|. Heterologous 
                     over-expression of <i>pppA</I> is capable of restoring 
                     function to both secretion and piliation dedicated systems 
                     in a <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> strain lacking its 
                     endogenous prepilin peptidase, PilD |CITS: [9515702]|. The 
                     cognate substrate of PppA is not clear - when expressed 
                     from a plasmid, it is capable of processing pre|FRAME: 
                     G7702-MONOMER GspG| (product of the normally cryptic 
                     pseudopilin gene) and pre|FRAME: EG12107-MONOMER PpdD| (a 
                     putative type IV prepilin-like protein) |CITS: [9515702]|. 
                     <i>yghJ-pppA-yghGFED</i> are the vestigial genes of a type 
                     II secretion system (T2SS<sub>&beta;</sub>) present in 
                     pathogenic (and some commensal) <i>E. coli</i> strains. 
                     <i>pppA</i> encodes a putative prepilin peptidase required 
                     for assembly of the pseudopilus in a virulence associated 
                     type II secretion system - known as T2SS<sub>&beta;</sub> 
                     or T2SS <sub>H10407</sub>; in enterotoxigenic <i>E. 
                     coli</i> strains the pathway secretes heat-labile 
                     enterotoxin |CITS: [12011463][17085567][22585966]|; in 
                     enteropathogenic <i>E. coli</i> strain E2348/69 and in 
                     non-pathogenic <i>E. coli</i> strain W the substrate of the
                     pathway is a surface anchored lipoprotein |FRAME: 
                     G7541-MONOMER SslE| |CITS: [22451516][23758679]|. 
                     Examination of T2SS<sub>&beta;</sub> encoding loci in 
                     various <i>E. coli</i> strains indicates that the K-12 
                     strain has experienced an internal deletion which removed 
                     the gspD-K<sub>&beta;</sub> genes, inactivating the 
                     T2SS<sub>&beta;</sub> |CITS: [12011463][17085567]|."
