     gene            complement(3130022..3131881)
                     /gene="gss"
                     /locus_tag="b2988"
                     /label=gss
     CDS             complement(3130022..3131881)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gss"
                     /locus_tag="b2988"
                     /product="trifunctional fused glutathionylspermidine
                     amidase / glutathionylspermidine synthetase / 
                     glutathionylspermidine synthetase / glutathionylspermidine 
                     amidase"
                     /EC_number="6.3.1.8; 3.5.1.78"
                     /label=gss
                     /note="<i>gss</i> encodes a bifunctional protein with two 
                     domains: an N-terminal glutathionylspermidine (GSP) amidase
                     domain and a C-terminal GSP synthetase domain.
                     The enzyme catalyzes both the formation and hydrolysis of 
                     an amide bond between glutathione and spermidine.  The net 
                     reaction of the two component activities is ATP hydrolysis,
                     a potential futile cycle |CITS: [8999955]|. 
                     Glutathionylspermidine levels in <i>E. coli</i> have been 
                     measured; levels increase in stationary phase, especially 
                     under anaerobic conditions |CITS: [8526857]| and after 
                     treatment with hydrogen peroxide |CITS: [20530482]|.  The 
                     function of glutathionylspermidine metabolism in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> has been unclear.  Recently it was proposed that 
                     the GSP amidase activity plays a role in reduction of 
                     Gsp-S-S-Gsp and Gsp-S-protein adducts |CITS: [20530482]|. 
                     Regulation of GSP amidase/synthetase at the enzyme level is
                     required to avoid futile cycling.  The two domains can be 
                     physically separated and fold and function independently.  
                     If expressed independently, the GSP amidase domain has 
                     significantly higher amidase activity than the full-length 
                     protein.  Thus, in the context of the full-length protein, 
                     the amidase domain is negatively autoregulated |CITS: 
                     [8999955]|.  However, amidase activity increases up to 
                     15-fold by addition of synthetase substrates |CITS: 
                     [9398217]|.  Hydrogen peroxide selectively inactivates the 
                     amidase, but not the synthetase activity of Gss |CITS: 
                     [20530482]|; amidase activity recovers significantly after 
                     four hours |CITS: [21887840]|.
                     The catalytic mechanism for the amidase function is thought
                     to involve a nucleophilic attack involving cysteine as the 
                     catalytic nucleophile and an acyl-enzyme intermediate.  
                     Cys59 was found to be essential for amidase activity |CITS:
                     [9398217]|.
                     Various synthetic inhibitors have been designed and tested 
                     |CITS: [9371250][9384533]|.
                     Crystal structures of Gss alone or in complex with 
                     substrate, product or inhibitor have been solved; the 
                     structures are consistent with the proposed reaction 
                     mechanism |CITS: [17124497]|.  Crystal structures of the 
                     amidase domain alone, in complex with its substrate, and 
                     modified by hydrogen peroxide treatment have been solved 
                     |CITS: [20530482][21226054]|.
                     Gsp: ""<b>g</b>lutathionyl<b>sp</b>ermidine"" |CITS: 
                     [7775463]|"
