     gene            3141256..3142296
                     /gene="gpr"
                     /locus_tag="b3001"
                     /label=gpr
     CDS             3141256..3142296
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gpr"
                     /locus_tag="b3001"
                     /product="bifunctional L-glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate
                     reductase / methylglyoxal reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.21"
                     /label=gpr
                     /note="YghZ is an L-glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate (L-GAP)
                     reductase |CITS: [18620424]|; the enzyme is also able
                     to detoxify methylglyoxal at a low rate |CITS: 
                     [12583903][16077126]|. YghZ defines the AKR14 (aldo-keto 
                     reductase 14) protein family |CITS: [12583903]|.
                     L-GAP is not a natural metabolite and is toxic to <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [319747]|. L-GAP is a substrate of both the
                     glycerol-3-phosphate and hexose phosphate transport systems
                     of <i>E.coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [319747], [6995450]|. |CITS: 
                     [18620424]| postulate that the physiological role of YghZ 
                     is the detoxification of L-GAP, which may be formed by
                     non-enzymatic racemization of GAP or by an unknown cellular
                     process.
                     Overexpression of <i>yghZ</i> allows a <i>tpiA</i> mutant 
                     to grow slowly on L-lactate as the source of
                     carbon. <i>tpiA</i> mutants lack triosephosphate isomerase 
                     (TIM), and are thus unable to grow under
                     conditions where gluconeogenesis is required |CITS: 
                     [18620424]|. The YghZ protein does not possess TIM activity
                     |CITS: [18620424]|. YghZ overproduction results in 
                     decreased sensitivity to methylglyoxal,
                     compared to wild type |CITS: [12583903]|. Growth of a 
                     <i>yghZ gloA</i> double mutant is inhibited by 0.3
                     mM methylglyoxal |CITS: [16077126]|.
                     Expression of <i>yghZ</i> is not increased in response to 
                     methylglyoxal |CITS: [16077126]|.
                     The crystal structure of the <i>E. coli</i> enzyme has been
                     determined and was suggested to be a tetramer |CITS: 
                     [22393408]|. However, others have found that the protein 
                     forms an octamer based on gel filtration and electron 
                     micoscopy studies |CITS: [23103600]| (author statement). 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [18928248][23103600]|"
                     /translation="MVWLANPERYGQMQYRYCGKSGLRLPALSLGLWHNFGHVNALESQ
                     RAILRKAFDLGITHFDLANNYGPPPGSAEENFGRLLREDFAAYRDELIISTKAGYDMWP
