     gene            3148714..3149877
                     /gene="yqhD"
                     /locus_tag="b3011"
                     /label=yqhD
     CDS             3148714..3149877
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yqhD"
                     /locus_tag="b3011"
                     /product="multifunctional NADPH-dependent aldehyde
                     reductase YqhD / methylglyoxal reductase / aldehyde 
                     reductase / glycolaldehyde reductase / 1,3-propanediol 
                     dehydrogenase / D-3,4-dihydroxybutanal reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.21; 1.1.1.2"
                     /label=yqhD
                     /note="YqhD is an NADPH-dependent aldehyde reductase that
                     may be involved in glyoxal detoxification |CITS: 
                     [20543070]| and/or be part of a glutathione-independent 
                     response to lipid peroxidation |CITS: [18211903]|.  YqhD 
                     appears to be the major glycolaldehyde reductase in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [26336892]|.
                     |CITS: [15327949]| report that various alcohols, aldehydes,
                     amino acids, sugars and &alpha;-hydroxy acids were tested 
                     as substrates for YqhD.  The purified protein only showed 
                     NADP-dependent alcohol dehydrogenase activity, with a 
                     preference for alcohols longer than C(3), but with 
                     K<sub>m</sub> values in the millimolar range, suggesting 
                     that they are not the physiological substrates |CITS: 
                     [15327949]|.  In contrast, |CITS: [18211903][19609521]| 
                     report that YqhD does exhibit short-chain aldehyde 
                     reductase activity with substrates such as propanaldehyde, 
                     acetaldehyde, and butanaldehyde, as well as acrolein and 
                     malondialdehyde.  In a metabolically engineered strain, 
                     phenylacetaldehyde and 4-hydroxyphenylacetaldehyde are 
                     reduced to 2-phenylethanol and 2-(4-hydroxyphenyl)ethanol 
                     by the endogenous aldehyde reductases YqhD, YjgB, and YahK 
                     |CITS: [22752168]|.
                     Overexpression of YqhD increases 1,3-propanediol 
                     oxidoreductase activity of the cell |CITS: 
                     [16285515][17914171]|.  The use of YqhD in the industrial 
                     production of 1,3-propanediol by engineered <i>E. coli</i> 
                     has been reviewed in |CITS: [14580573]|.  YqhD activity 
                     contributes to the production of isobutanol |CITS: 
                     [19609521]|, 1,2-propanediol |CITS: [21404260]|, 
                     1,2,4-butanetriol |CITS: [26048478][27702485]|, acetol 
                     |CITS: [24113547][26078109]| and ethylene glycol |CITS: 
                     [26336892]| as well.  Mutation of <i>yqhD</i> enables 
                     production of butanol by an engineered one-turn reversal of
                     the &beta;-oxidation cycle |CITS: [21832992]|.
                     YqhD has furfural reductase activity, which appears to 
                     cause growth inhibition due to depletion of NADPH in 
                     metabolically engineered strains that produce alcohol from 
                     lignocellulosic biomass |CITS: [19429550][19684179]|.
                     YqhD abundance is almost 35-fold increased during growth 
                     with vanillin, and the enzyme has NADPH-dependent vanillin 
                     reductase activity |CITS: [31186336]|.
                     The crystal structure of YqhD has been solved at 2 &Aring; 
                     resolution.  YqhD is an asymmetric dimer of dimers, and the
                     active site contains a Zn<sup>2+</sup> ion.  The NADPH 
                     cofactor is modified by hydroxyl groups at positions 5 and 
                     6 in the nicotinamide ring |CITS: [15327949]|.  Molecular 
                     dynamics simulations with both NADP and NADPH found that 
                     the oxidation state of the cosubstrate influences the 
                     structure and dynamics of the enzyme |CITS: [33430436]|. 
                     Overexpression of <i>yqhD</i> leads to increased resistance
                     to reactive oxygen-generating compounds such as hydrogen 
                     peroxide, paraquat, chromate and potassium tellurite.  
                     <i>yqhD</i> mutants shows increased sensitivity to these 
                     compounds |CITS: [18211903]| and to glyoxal |CITS: 
                     [20543070][27535647]|, and contains increased levels of 
                     reactive aldehydes that are generated during lipid 
                     peroxidation |CITS: [18211903][23991420]|. Conversely, 
                     <i>yqhD</i> deletion leads to increased furfural tolerance 
                     |CITS: [23431191]|.
                     Transcription of <i>yqhD</i> is induced by exposure to 
                     conditions of lipid peroxidation |CITS: [18211903]|. 
                     Various microarray experiments showed induction of 
                     <i>yqhD</i> upon cold shock |CITS: [15466053]|, nutrient 
                     limitation |CITS: [15066832]|, n-butanol stress |CITS: 
                     [20118358]|, and isopentenol stress |CITS: [25370492]|. 
                     Transcription is activated by YqhC and enhanced by addition
                     of glyoxal |CITS: [20543070]|.  Exposure of <i>E. coli</i> 
                     BL21 to tellurite leads to increased levels of YqhD |CITS: 
                     [24244285]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [20924577]|"
