     gene            complement(3154616..3156028)
                     /gene="ftsP"
                     /locus_tag="b3017"
                     /label=ftsP
     CDS             complement(3154616..3156028)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftsP"
                     /locus_tag="b3017"
                     /product="cell division protein FtsP"
                     /label=ftsP
                     /note="FtsP (SufI) is a component of the cell division
                     machinery that is required during stress conditions and may
                     play a role in stabilizing the assembly of the divisome 
                     |CITS: [17766410]|.  FtsP localizes to the septal ring; 
                     recruitment is dependent on FtsN |CITS: [19135451]|. 
                     Translation of FtsP proceeds via many transient 
                     intermediates that match translational attenuation sites. 
                     Slowly translating regions are important for proper folding
                     of the protein |CITS: [19198590]|.  Comparative molecular 
                     simulations of different folding schemes have been 
                     performed |CITS: [26158498]|.
                     FtsP is a soluble protein that is transported to the 
                     periplasm via the Tat pathway |CITS: [10593889][10766774]|.
                      Overexpression of FtsP saturates the Tat protein export 
                     system |CITS: [11350936][12634052][15347649]|.  The 
                     twin-arginine signal peptide of FtsP is unstructured in an 
                     aqueous environment, but adopts a helical structure in 
                     membrane-mimetic environments |CITS: [12899691]|.  <i>In 
                     vitro</i> in the absence of TatABC, |FRAME: G7716-MONOMER 
                     FkpA| crosslinks to the signal sequence of FtsP; however, 
                     transport of FtsP into inverted inner membrane vesicles can
                     occur in the absence of cytosolic chaperones |CITS: 
                     [19432418]|.  The hydrophobicity of the amino acid at 
                     position 17 in the h-domain of the signal peptide 
                     influences its ability to interact with the membrane and 
                     promote transport |CITS: [19732346]|.  The signal peptide 
                     is cleaved by |FRAME: EG10530-MONOMER LepB| |CITS: 
                     [19809807]|.
                     FtsP is monomeric in solution.  A crystal structure of FtsP
                     has been solved at 1.9 &Aring; resolution; the protein is 
                     related to the multicopper oxidase family and contains 
                     three cupredoxin-like domains, but does not bind copper 
                     |CITS: [19135451]|.  The structure of the TatBC-FtsP 
                     complex was analyzed by single-particle electron microscopy
                     |CITS: [19666509]|.
                     FtsP is essential for viability under various stress 
                     conditions |CITS: [17766410]|.  An <i>ftsP</i> insertion 
                     mutant shows reduced resistance to ionizing radiation 
                     |CITS: [25049088]|.  Overexpression of FtsP can substitute 
                     for FtsEX function, and the combination of an <i>ftsE</i> 
                     mutant and a <i>ftsP</i> deletion is synthetically lethal 
                     |CITS: [17071757]|.  Factors that stabilize FtsZ ring 
                     assembly, such as FtsN, are multicopy supressors of the 
                     <i>ftsP</i> deletion phenotype, and <i>ftsP</i> itself is a
                     multicopy supressor of the cell division defect of 
                     <i>ftsA</i>, <i>ftsQ</i> and <i>ftsK</i> mutant alleles 
                     |CITS: [17766410]|.  <i>ftsP</i> was first found as a 
                     multicopy supressor of the temperature-sensitive growth 
                     defect of an <i>ftsI</i> mutant |CITS: [2842295]|.
                     The gene was identified by a computational analysis as a 
                     candidate for encoding resistance to the antiobiotic 
                     |FRAME: CPD0-2068|. Resistance was confirmed by wet-lab 
                     experiments that compared the minimum inhibitory 
                     concentration (MIC) of a knock-out strain to that of the 
                     wild-type strain |CITS: [35487919]|.
                     Sui: ""<b>su</b>ppressor of 
                     <i>fts<b>I</b><sup>-</sup></i>"" |CITS: [7025060]|
                     SufI: ""<b>su</b>ppression of <i><b>f</b>ts<b>I</b></i>"" 
                     FtsP: ""<b>f</b>ilamentous 
                     <b>t</b>emperature-<b>s</b>ensitive"" |CITS: [17766410]|"
