     gene            complement(3156103..3156840)
                     /gene="plsC"
                     /locus_tag="b3018"
                     /label=plsC
     CDS             complement(3156103..3156840)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="plsC"
                     /locus_tag="b3018"
                     /product="trifunctional 1-acylglycerol-3-phosphate 
                     O-acyltransferase PlsC / 
                     acyl-CoA:1-acyl-sn-glycerol-3-phosphate 2-O-acyltransferase
                     / acyl-[acp]:1-acyl-sn-glycerol-3-phosphate 
                     2-O-acyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.51"
                     /label=plsC
                     /note="Membrane-bound 1-acylglycerol-3-phosphate 
                     <i>O</i>-acyltransferase (PlsC) catalyzes the second step 
                     in phospholipid biosynthesis and is thought to function in 
                     close proximity to the preceding enzyme, |FRAME: 
                     GLYCEROL-3-P-ACYLTRANSFER-MONOMER| (PlsB) |CITS: 
                     [6351928]|. PlsC is specific for acylation at the 
                     <i>sn</i>-2 position of |FRAME: ACYL-SN-GLYCEROL-3P| and 
                     can utilize either acyl-acyl carrier protein (acyl-ACP) or 
                     acyl-coenzyme A (acyl-CoA) as the fatty acyl donor to form 
                     |FRAME: L-PHOSPHATIDATE| (a phosphatidate, a phosphatidic 
                     acid) |CITS: [7005223]|. PlsB and PlsC activity has been 
                     reconstituted in a cell-free liposome system |CITS: 
                     [27711229]|.
                     Fatty acids that are endogenously synthesized are attached 
                     to ACP, and exogenously added fatty acids are attached to 
                     CoA |CITS: [6355102]|. In <i>E. coli</i> phospholipids, the
                     <i>sn</i>-1 position is occupied mainly by either palmitate
                     or <i>cis</i>-vaccenate, whereas the <i>sn</i>-2 position 
                     is predominantly palmitoleate or <i>cis</i>-vaccenate 
                     |CITS: [4552305][6998967][7005223]|. Purified PlsC prefers 
                     unsaturated fatty acyl-CoA donors such as |FRAME: 
                     CPD-10269| [16:1(&Delta;9)-CoA] and |FRAME: OLEOYL-COA| 
                     [18:1(&Delta;9)-CoA] and uses saturated donors to a lesser 
                     extent or not at all; the enzyme is active with |FRAME: 
                     Lysophosphatidates lysophosphatidates| but not with |FRAME:
                     Lysophosphatidylethanolamines 
                     lysophosphatidylethanolamines| nor with |FRAME: 
                     Lysophosphatidylglycerols lysophosphatidylglyerols| |CITS: 
                     [39727331]|.
                     Overexpression of PlsC increases resistance to vancomycin 
                     |CITS: [24957626]|. In an engineered strain of <i>E. 
                     coli</i>, overexpression of PlsC and GalU resulted in 
                     increased production of glycoglycerolipids |CITS: 
                     [22750670]|.
                     PlsC: ""<b>p</b>hospho<b>l</b>ipid <b>s</b>ynthesis 
                     <b>C</b>"" |CITS: [2211622]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [23699259][22981714][23500459][18369234][M 
                     athewsvanHolde]|"
