     gene            complement(3161210..3161605)
                     /gene="mqsA"
                     /locus_tag="b3021"
                     /label=mqsA
     CDS             complement(3161210..3161605)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mqsA"
                     /locus_tag="b3021"
                     /product="bifunctional antitoxin of the MqsRA TA system / 
                     DNA-binding transcriptional repressor MqsA / MqsA-MqsR 
                     antitoxin/toxin complex"
                     /label=mqsA
                     /note="The antitoxin MqsA belongs to the toxin-antitoxin
                     system MqsR-MqsA, which controls biofilm formation, 
                     triggers programmed cell death in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [19690171]| and facilitates stress response to |FRAME: 
                     HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE|, low pH and high heat |CITS: 
                     [38206055]|. <i>mqsR</i> is co-transcribed with the 
                     downstream gene <i>mqsA</i>, and their respective open 
                     reading frames are separated by 1 bp.
                     Contrary to what was reported by Yamaguchi et al. |CITS: 
                     [19690171]|, Fraikin et al. reported that MqsA does not 
                     regulate biofilm formation and is not a response to stress 
                     |CITS: [31848281]|. However, soon afterwards another study 
                     found significant increases in transcription of 
                     <i>mqsRA</i> when challenged by various stresses, and found
                     the response was due to degradation of the antitoxin MqsA 
                     which could no longer activate <i>mqsRA</i> transcription 
                     |CITS: [32533919]|. Single-cell quantitative measurements 
                     show the existence of two cell populations with high or low
                     levels of the MqsA antitoxin that respond differently to 
                     environmental stresses |CITS: [31670944]|. Single-cell 
                     transcriptome analyses with <i>&Delta;mqsRA</i>, 
                     <i>&Delta;mqsR</i> and <i>&Delta;mqsA</i> mutants under 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> oxidative stress found 
                     heterogeneous response, i.e. only a subset of cells within 
                     the entire population were affected by the MqsR-MqsA TA 
                     system |CITS: [38206055]|.
                     The antitoxin MqsA has two functions. It binds and thereby 
                     inhibits the endoribonuclease activity of MqsR.  In 
                     addition, it is a DNA-binding protein, as is the case for 
                     other bacterial antitoxins. MqsA recognizes two palindromic
                     sites located between the promoter and the start codon of 
                     <i>mqsR</i> |CITS: [19690171]|. Although it was initially 
                     reported that binding to these sites is enhanced when MqsR 
                     is bound to MqsA |CITS: [19690171]|, it was later shown 
                     that binding of MqsR in fact destabilizes the MqsA-DNA 
                     complex |CITS: [23172222]|.
                     MqsA rapidly responds to different environmental stresses, 
                     including degradation upon bile acid stress and increased 
                     production upon heat stress; its degradation is followed by
                     regeneration upon amino acid starvation |CITS: [31670944]|.
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3107| is the protease that degrades MqsA when
                     this protein is unfolded, because protein folding hides a 
                     sequence named degron located in the N terminal of MqsA 
                     that is recognized by the protease |CITS:[34974059]|. Due 
                     to the fact that zinc and toxin MsqR binding stabilize the 
                     MqsA fold, their presence and binding prevent protein 
                     degradation |CITS:[34974059]|. Inhibition of <i>mqsA</i> 
                     expression by CRISPRi reduced cellular fitness under 
                     treatment with the antibiotic carbonyl cyanide 
                     3-chlorophenylhydrazone (CCCP) |CITS:[40352728]|.
                     Solution |CITS: [19636869][22789559]| and crystal 
                     structures |CITS: [20041169][21068382]| of MqsA have been 
                     solved.
                     The |FRAME: CPLX0-2881| is required for activation of the 
                     <i>mqsRA</i> locus |CITS: [19943910]|. Transcription of 
                     <i>mqsRA</i> appears to be indirectly upregulated by 
                     |FRAME: PD00257| |CITS: [26408451]|. A DNA-footprinting 
                     assay found that MqsA binds to a putative palindromic 
                     sequence 5'-ACAT (N2) ACAT upstream of the <i>lfgABCDE</i> 
                     operon repressing it's transcription. However, under 
                     oxidative stress, the operon is expressed and the |FRAME: 
                     G7379-MONOMER| degrades MqsA |CITS: [38206055]|.
                     The MqsA binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17001682][19215780][22060041][31932311]|"
                     /translation="MKCPVCHQGEMVSGIKDIPYTFRGRKTVLKGIHGLYCVHCEESIM
                     NKEESDAFMAQVKAFRASVNAETVAPEFIVKVRKKLSLTQKEASEIFGGGVNAFSRYEK
                     GNAQPHPSTIKLLRVLDKHPELLNEIR"
                     /go_component="toxin-antitoxin complex [goid 0110001]
                     [evidence IPI] [PMID 20041169]"
