     gene            complement(3161607..3161903)
                     /gene="mqsR"
                     /locus_tag="b3022"
                     /label=mqsR
     CDS             complement(3161607..3161903)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mqsR"
                     /locus_tag="b3022"
                     /product="trifunctional mRNA interferase toxin MqsR / mRNA 
                     interferase / MqsA-MqsR antitoxin/toxin complex"
                     /label=mqsR
                     /note="MqsR is a BECR-fold toxin in the RelE/ParE
                     superfamily of type II toxin-antitoxin systems |CITS: 
                     [39998207]|. It was found to be a ribosome-independent mRNA
                     interferase, related to |FRAME: EG11131-MONOMER|, that 
                     inhibits protein synthesis by specifically cleaving mRNAs 
                     at GCU sites, 5' or 3' of the G. Elevated levels of free 
                     MqsR result in programmed cell death due to the degradation
                     of mRNA and concomitant inhibition of protein synthesis 
                     |CITS: [19690171][19943910][30315853]|. The mRNA 
                     interferase activity is inhibited by interaction with 
                     |FRAME: G7571-MONOMER| |CITS: [19690171][23172222]|. 
                     Expression of MqsR causes elongation of cells and 
                     reversible growth inhibition |CITS: [20233923]|.
                     MqsR is specific for single-stranded RNA at GCU sites and 
                     is non-selective with respect to the presence or absence of
                     5' phosphate groups |CITS: [26846703]|. MqsR cleavage sites
                     throughout the transcriptome have been mapped; cleavage is 
                     not position-specific. The preferred sequence at the 
                     cleavage site is GCU, followed by GCC and GCA.  GCG is 
                     cleaved rarely |CITS: [30315853]|. An analysis of the 
                     targets and site specificity of MqsR showed cleavage site 
                     specificity and a bias towards cleavage in the 5' end of 
                     mRNAs, but no codon position preference. Like other <i>E. 
                     coli</i> endoribonuclease toxins, MqsR activity does not 
                     affect mature ribosomes, but inhibits ribosome biogenesis, 
                     likely by affecting the translation of a specific set of 
                     ribosomal proteins |CITS: [34544284]|.
                     MqsR is able to cleave its own mRNA |CITS: [23172222]|. The
                     toxin-encoding |FRAME: G0-10472| is one of a small set of 
                     coding regions that do not contain cleavage sites for MqsR,
                     whereas the |FRAME: G7830| coding region contains three 
                     consensus MqsR cleavage sites. Thus, in the presence of 
                     MqsR, the <i>ghoS</i>-encoding 5' region of the 
                     <i>ghoST</i> transcript is degraded, thereby enriching for 
                     the <i>ghoT</i>-encoding 3' region |CITS: [23289863]|. 
                     Overexpression of MqsR leads to cleavage of ribosomal RNAs 
                     that appear to be rRNA precursors |CITS: [27858580]|.
                     A crystal structure of the MqsR-MqsA complex has been 
                     solved, showing that two molecules of MqsR bind to an MqsA 
                     dimer. MqsR adopts an RNase fold similar to other 
                     interferase toxins |FRAME: MONOMER0-1041 YoeB| and |FRAME: 
                     EG11131-MONOMER RelE|. Key residues required for toxicity 
                     of MqsR have been identified by site-directed mutagenesis 
                     |CITS: [20041169]|. Amino acid changes that increase the 
                     stability of MqsR also increase its toxicity and effect on 
                     persister cell formation |CITS: [22221537]|.
                     Binding of MqsR destabilizes the MqsA-DNA complex; binding 
                     of MqsR and DNA by MqsA appear to be mutually exclusive 
                     |CITS: [23172222]|. <i>E. coli</i> is able to inactivate 
                     MqsR toxin overexpressed from a plasmid by mutations in the
                     promoter driving <i>mqsR</i> expression and by mutations in
                     the chromosomal <i>mphR</i> gene |CITS: [31429177]|.
                     There have been contradictory reports on the effects of 
                     MqsRA on its role in processes such as biofilm formation, 
                     persister cells, and the response to various stresses. MqsR
                     plays a role in controlling biofilm formation by inducing 
                     motility via the |FRAME: PWY0-1470|. MqsR links the |FRAME:
                     DIHYDROXYPENTANEDIONE| quorum sensing signal to biofilm 
                     formation |CITS: [16352847]|. Transcription of <i>mqsR</i> 
                     was found to be induced upon biofilm formation |CITS: 
                     [14727089]|, and deletion of <i>mqsR</i> decreased biofilm 
                     formation and abolished motility |CITS: [16352847]|. 
                     Contrasting with this result, it was also reported that 
                     deletion of <i>mqsR</i> increases biofilm formation |CITS: 
                     [20105222]|. However, it was later found that the strain 
                     background influences MqsR activity |CITS: [20233923]|.
                     Expression of <i>mqsR</i> is higher in persister cells than
                     non-persisters, and overexpression of <i>mqsR</i> leads to 
                     growth arrest and increased tolerance to certain 
                     antibiotics |CITS: [16768798][18309357]|. Co-induction of 
                     <i>mqsA</i> expression rescues cell growth |CITS: 
                     [19690171][19943910]|. However, the opposite effect was 
                     reported by |CITS: [18309357]|. Deletion of <i>mqsRA</i> 
                     decreases persister cell formation |CITS: [19909729]|, 
                     while overexpression of MqsR induces expression of |FRAME: 
                     EG11111-MONOMER| |CITS: [20105222]| and increases persister
                     cell formation via both toxins |FRAME: EG10439-MONOMER| and
                     CspD |CITS: [19909729]|. An <i>mqsR</i> deletion strain 
                     shows decreased levels of persister cells only when the 
                     inoculum is from aged cultures |CITS: [21602347]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>mqsRA</i> mutant shows decreased growth under 
                     stress from bile acid |CITS: [25534751]|.
                     Transcription of <i>mqsRA</i> is induced by chloramphenicol
                     treatment, amino acid starvation induced by serine 
                     hydroxamate, isoleucine starvation, and glucose starvation 
                     |CITS: [19943910]|. A subsequent study, however, questioned
                     the role of the MqsR-MqsA TA system in response to stress 
                     and the regulatory network. They found that transcription 
                     of <i>mqsA</i> and <i>mqsR</i> is uncoupled and suggested 
                     that different results in earlier publications may have 
                     been due to polar effects or the specific strain background
                     |CITS: [31848281]|. However, soon afterwards another study 
                     found significant increases in transcription of 
                     <i>mqsRA</i> when challenged by various stresses, and found
                     the response was due to degradation of the antitoxin MqsA 
                     which could no longer activate <i>mqsRA</i> transcription 
                     |CITS: [32533919]|. Single-cell quantitative measurements 
                     show the existence of two cell populations with high or low
                     levels of the MqsA antitoxin that respond differently to 
                     environmental stresses |CITS: [31670944]|. Single-cell 
                     transcriptome analyses with <i>&Delta;mqsRA</i>, 
                     <i>&Delta;mqsR</i> and <i>&Delta;mqsA</i> mutants under 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> oxidative stress found 
                     heterogeneous response, i.e. only a subset of cells within 
                     the entire population were affected by the MqsR-MqsA TA 
                     system; they were able to identify a |FRAME: G7379-MONOMER|
                     LfgB that degrades MqsA, thus affecting the stress response
                     |CITS: [38206055]|.
                     Expression of the <i>relBEF</i> operon can be activated by 
                     overexpression of MqsR |CITS: [23432955]|. Reinvestigation 
                     of |FRAME: G7080-MONOMER Ag43| expression showed that it is
                     not regulated by MqsR |CITS: [24039985]|.
                     The |FRAME: CPLX0-2881| is required for activation of the 
                     <i>mqsRA</i> locus |CITS: [19943910]|. Transcription of 
                     <i>mqsRA</i> appears to be indirectly upregulated by 
                     |FRAME: PD00257| |CITS: [26408451]|.
                     Based on sequence similarity, MqsR was initially predicted 
                     to be a cyanide hydratase |CITS: [12952533]|.
                     MqsR: ""<b>m</b>otility <b>q</b>uorum-<b>s</b>ensing 
                     <b>r</b>egulator"" |CITS: [16352847]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16803593][21685157][21927020][22060041][2 
                     3396045][24434905][24434949][31932311]|"
                     /translation="MEKRTPHTRLSQVKKLVNAGQVRTTRSALLNADELGLDFDGMCNV
                     IIGLSESDFYKSMTTYSDHTIWQDVYRPRLVTGQVYLKITVIHDVLIVSFKEK"
                     /go_component="toxin-antitoxin complex [goid 0110001]
                     [evidence IPI] [PMID 20041169]"
                     /go_function="nuclease activity [goid 0004518] [evidence
                     IEA] [GO_REF 0000043]"
                     /go_function="hydrolase activity [goid 0016787] [evidence
                     IEA] [GO_REF 0000043]"
