     gene            complement(3169365..3170192)
                     /gene="cpdA"
                     /locus_tag="b3032"
                     /label=cpdA
     CDS             complement(3169365..3170192)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cpdA"
                     /locus_tag="b3032"
                     /product="cAMP phosphodiesterase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.4.53"
                     /label=cpdA
                     /note="cAMP phosphodiesterase hydrolyzes the important 
                     regulatory molecule cAMP and may thus influence the level 
                     of transcription of genes regulated by cAMP-CRP |CITS: 
                     [8810311]|.
                     Usage of the unusual translation start codon UUG has been 
                     confirmed by amino-terminal sequencing of the purified 
                     protein and may be involved in translational regulation of 
                     CpdA expression |CITS: [8810311]|.  CpdA is the founding 
                     member of the Class III family of 3',5'-cyclic nucleotide 
                     phosphodiesterases |CITS: [11835503]|.
                     Expression of CpdA appears to be itself 
                     catabolite-repressed |CITS: [4325494]|.  A <i>cpdA</i> 
                     mutant has slightly increased intracellular levels of cAMP 
                     |CITS: [8810311]|.  Overexpression of <i>cpdA</i> leads to 
                     increased resistance to hypochlorous acid stress due to 
                     derepression of <i>rpoS</i> |CITS: [19372151]| and to 
                     increased persister cell formation due to reduced indole 
                     production |CITS: [25219496]|.
                     CpdA: ""<b>c</b>yclic AMP <b>p</b>hospho<b>d</b>iesterase""
                     |CITS: [8810311]| Reviews: |CITS: [23356492][26424768]|
                     The publication by |CITS: [21183666]| reporting that CpdA's
                     low affinity for cAMP compared to the dissociation constant
                     of cAMP from CRP suggests that the enzyme does not have a 
                     major impact on cAMP concentration in <i>E. coli</i> has 
                     been retracted |CITS: [26581823]|."
