     gene            complement(3170217..3170639)
                     /gene="yqiB"
                     /locus_tag="b3033"
                     /label=yqiB
     CDS             complement(3170217..3170639)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yqiB"
                     /locus_tag="b3033"
                     /product="DUF1249 domain-containing protein YqiB"
                     /label=yqiB
                     /note="A predicted deleterious SNP (Q117K) arose in
                     <i>yqiB</i> during an experiment evaluating evolution of 
                     metabolic auxotrophy.  A reconstructed strain containing 
                     only the YqiB Q117A allele was not an amino acid auxotroph,
                     but had a fitness advantage compared to wild type when 
                     amino acids were present in the medium |CITS: [27814362]|.
                     "
                     /translation="MKRYTPDFPEMMRLCEMNFSQLRRLLPRNDAPGETVSYQVANAQY
                     RLTIVESTRYTTLVTIEQTAPAISYWSLPSMTVRLYHDAMVAEVCSSQQIFRFKARYDY
                     PNKKLHQRDEKHQINQFLADWLRYCLAHGAMAIPVY"
     gene            complement(3170640..3171269)
                     /gene="nudF"
                     /locus_tag="b3034"
                     /label=nudF
     CDS             complement(3170640..3171269)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nudF"
                     /locus_tag="b3034"
                     /product="bifunctional dimethylallyl diphosphate
                     pyrophosphatase / ADP-sugar pyrophosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.1.76; 3.6.1.21"
                     /label=nudF
                     /note="NudF is a member of the family of Nudix hydrolases
                     |CITS: [8810257][10542272]| and has ADP-sugar 
                     pyrophosphatase activity |CITS: [10542272][11416161]|. 
                     ADP-glucose is a precursor molecule for glycogen 
                     biosynthesis; thus, it appears that the activity of NudF 
                     needs to be regulated.  NudF is thought to control the flow
                     of carbon toward glycogen, interconnecting gluconeogenesis 
                     with other metabolic pathways |CITS: [11416161]|.
                     NudF also contributes to the production of dimethylallyl 
                     phosphate, which is the substrate for |FRAME: UBIX-MONOMER 
                     UbiX|-catalyzed generation of the prFMN cofactor in 
                     ubiquinone biosynthesis |CITS: [29551348]|.
                     Gel filtration experiments indicate that the enzyme is a 
                     dimer in solution |CITS: [11416161]|.  Crystal structures 
                     of NudF in various forms have been solved, showing a 
                     domain-swapped dimer where the Nudix motif residues compose
                     a catalytic center |CITS: [11323725][12135348][16021622]|. 
                     Binding of the substrate and Mg<sup>2+</sup> results in a 
                     conformational change from an open to a closed complex.  
                     The reaction mechanism involves a nucleophilic attack by 
                     water at the phosphorus of the adenosyl phosphate |CITS: 
                     [12135348]|.
                     NudF activity is enhanced by macromolecular crowding and is
                     stimulated by glucose-1,6-bisphosphate and nucleotide 
                     sugars |CITS: [17306798]|.  A fraction of the enzyme 
                     becomes associated with the membrane during stationary 
                     phase |CITS: [18778276]|.
                     An <i>E. coli</i> enzyme with a similar catalytic activity 
                     has been purified and was thought to be localized in the 
                     periplasm |CITS: [5330035]|.
                     There is no effect on cell growth of either a <i>nudF</i> 
                     null mutation or <i>nudF</i> overexpression.  However, a 
                     <i>nudF</i> null mutant contains increased amounts of 
                     glycogen, while overexpression of <i>nudF</i> leads to a 
                     reduced amount of glycogen in the cell |CITS: 
                     [11416161][17306798]|.  Transcription of <i>nudF</i> is 
                     induced by the CreBC two-component system by minimal media 
                     growth conditions |CITS: [11350954]|.
                     <i>nudF</i> shows differential codon adaptation, resulting 
                     in differential translation efficiency signatures, in 
                     thermophilic microbes.  It was therefore predicted to play 
                     a role in the heat shock response.  A <i>nudF</i> deletion 
                     mutant was shown to be more sensitive than wild-type 
                     specifically to heat shock, but not other stresses |CITS: 
                     [24580753]|.
                     AspP: ""<b>a</b>denosine diphosphate <b>s</b>ugar 
                     <b>p</b>yro<b>p</b>hosphatase"" |CITS: [11416161]| Reviews:
                     |CITS: [8810257][16378245]|"
