                     /product="bifunctional peptide--spermidine ligase YgiC / 
                     peptide--spermidine ligase"
                     /label=ygiC
                     /note="Based on sequence similarity to the 
                     glutathionylspermidine synthetase domain of |FRAME: 
                     GSP-CPLX ""Gss""|, |FRAME: EG11165| and |FRAME: EG11812| 
                     were predicted to encode additional glutathionylspermidine 
                     synthetases. However, while purified YgiC hydrolyzes ATP, 
                     it does not synthesize glutathionylspermidine, glutathione,
                     or glutathione intermediates |CITS: [23097746]|. In 
                     addition, a triple mutant lacking |FRAME: EG12882|, |FRAME:
                     EG11165| and |FRAME: EG11812| has no growth defect in 
                     minimal medium |CITS: [20545840]|, and deletion of |FRAME: 
                     EG11812| has no effect on |FRAME: GLUTATHIONYLSPERMIDINE| 
                     levels in the cell |CITS: [23097746]|.
                     A 2023 study that reported high-resolution crystal 
                     structures for YgiC and YjfC has shown that while both 
                     proteins possess multiple substitutions in key residues 
                     required for binding  glutathione, they should be able to 
                     catalyze the formation of an alternate 
                     peptide&mdash;spermidine conjugate. The physiological 
                     substrates of either enzyme are still unknown, but both 
                     enzymes were able to ligate |FRAME: SPERMIDINE| to a 
                     |FRAME: CPD-26564| peptide. The enzyme appears to be highly
                     specific for |FRAME: SPERMIDINE|, as no other common 
                     polyamines were recognized |CITS: [36745518]|.
                     YgiC and YjfC were found to share highly similar secondary 
                     and tertiary structures, and both enzymes are predominantly
                     monomeric in solution |CITS: [36745518]|. Deletion of 
                     <i>ygiBC</i> exacerbates the growth defects observed when 
                     <i>E. coli tolC</i> mutants are grown in glucose minimal 
                     media |CITS: [20545840]|."
