     CDS             complement(3177172..3177825)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ribB"
                     /locus_tag="b3041"
                     /product="3,4-dihydroxy-2-butanone-4-phosphate synthase"
                     /EC_number="4.1.99.12"
                     /label=ribB
                     /note="RibB catalyzes the synthesis of the 4-carbon
                     compound |FRAME: DIHYDROXY-BUTANONE-P| 
                     (3,4-dihydroxy-2-butanone 4-phosphate), a precursor of the 
                     terminal intermediate |FRAME: DIMETHYL-D-RIBITYL-LUMAZINE| 
                     in the biosynthesis of |FRAME: RIBOFLAVIN| |CITS: 
                     [1597419]|. <i>ribB</i> is an essential gene |CITS: 
                     [1597419][16738554]|.
                     The enzyme is a homodimer in solution |CITS: [10103033]|. 
                     Crystal structures have been solved at 1.55 and 1.4 &Aring;
                     resolution, and various amino acid residues were assigned 
                     proposed roles in catalysis and enzyme structure |CITS: 
                     [11342130]|. A solution structure of the enzyme allowed 
                     identification of the substrate binding site, residues 
                     involved in catalysis, and identification of the 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup> binding site |CITS: [11687623]|.
                     RibB production is induced by low pH, but not by acetate 
                     |CITS: [12107143]| and is regulated by an FMN-sensitive 
                     riboswitch |CITS: [22431636][25661987]| that operates both 
                     on the transcriptional as well as the translational level 
                     |CITS: [25661987]|. When FMN is abundant, the <i>ribB</i>'s
                     <i>rut</i> (Rho utilization) site is open to allow 
                     efficient binding of the |FRAME: CPLX0-2441|, leading to 
                     transcription attenuaton of the downstream <i>ribB</i> gene
                     and as a result, of riboflavin synthesis. When FMN is 
                     scarce, the <i>rut</i> site is closed, forming a hairpin 
                     structure and impeding Rho binding, resulting in 
                     transcription of the gene. A riboflavin biosynthesis 
                     inhibitor, |FRAME: CPD-26642|, competes with FMN for 
                     binding to the riboswitch element |CITS: 
                     [26416753][27485612]|. The Rho-dependent termination of the
                     ribB riboswitch is controlled mainly by modulation of the 
                     ""stand-by Rho"" route of termination |CITS: [36762473]|. 
                     Additional structural strategies related to FMN sensing 
                     have been identified |CITS: [39366707]|. Using a thiamine 
                     monophosphate (TMP) photoaffinity probe and RNA-Seq to 
                     determine TMP interactions, and confirming with competition
                     experiments with FMN, revealed that TMP also acts as a 
                     riboswitch that enhances gene expression of <i>ribB</i> 
                     |CITS: [39968280]|.
                     Certain mutations in <i>ribB</i> rescue growth of |FRAME: 
                     DXS-MONOMER Dxs|-defective mutants by enabling the 
                     production of sufficient DXP <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [22928031][25326299]|. The RibB G108S mutant enzyme 
                     synthesize DXP from ribulose 5-phosphate |CITS: 
                     [25326299]|. In a mouse model, virulence of a 
                     &Delta;<i>ribB</i> mutant is attenuated |CITS: [26416753]|.
                     HtrP: ""<b>h</b>igh <b>t</b>emperature 
                     <b>r</b>equirements"" |CITS: [1917833]|
                     RibB: ""<b>rib</b>oflavin"""
