     gene            3178199..3178489
                     /gene="ubiK"
                     /locus_tag="b3042"
                     /label=ubiK
     CDS             3178199..3178489
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ubiK"
                     /locus_tag="b3042"
                     /product="bifunctional ubiquinone biosynthesis accessory
                     factor UbiK / Ubi complex"
                     /label=ubiK
                     /note="UbiK is an accessory factor in ubiquinone
                     biosynthesis that is part of the Ubi complex metabolon 
                     |CITS: [30686758]|.  UbiK interacts directly with the C 
                     terminus of UbiJ; the isolated proteins appear to form a 
                     2:1 UbiK:UbiJ heterotrimer that is able to bind 
                     palmitoleate |CITS: [28559279]|, ubiquinone-8, 
                     2-octaprenylphenol, and C6-demethoxy-ubiquinone-8 |CITS: 
                     [30686758]|.
                     Purified UbiK homotrimerizes |CITS: [28559279]|.  UbiK 
                     predominantly localizes to the membrane fraction, but can 
                     also be found in the cytosol.  The seven ubiquinone 
                     biosynthesis proteins UbiE, UbiF, UbiG, UbiH, UbiI, UbiJ, 
                     and UbiK form a 1 MDa complex in the cytosol |CITS: 
                     [30686758]|.
                     A <i>ubiK</i> mutant contains 18% of the wild type level of
                     ubiquinone and accumulates octaprenylphenol |CITS: 
                     [28559279][29042439]|.  A 
                     &Delta;<i>ubiI</i>&Delta;<i>ubiK</i> double mutant produces
                     no detectable ubiquinone and in contrast to the single 
                     mutants, has a significant growth defect when grown 
                     aerobically on rich medium and does not grow on the 
                     non-fermentable carbon sources oleate, acetate, and 
                     succinate |CITS: [29042439]|.  <i>ubiK</i> is not required 
                     for ubiquinone biosynthesis under anaerobic conditions 
                     |CITS: [28559279]|.  The requirement for <i>ubiK</i> and 
                     other ubiquinone biosynthesis genes for growth on long 
                     chain fatty acids (LCFAs) such as oleate suggests that 
                     ubiquinone mitigates oxidative stress generated during LCFA
                     degradation |CITS: [29042439]|.
                     <i>ubiK</i> appears to be expressed constitutively |CITS: 
                     [28559279]|.
                     <i>ubiK</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     UbiK: |CITS: [28559279]|"
