     gene            complement(3188679..3190112)
                     /gene="hldE"
                     /locus_tag="b3052"
                     /label=hldE
     CDS             complement(3188679..3190112)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hldE"
                     /locus_tag="b3052"
                     /product="trifunctional fused heptose 7-phosphate
                     kinase/heptose 1-phosphate adenyltransferase / heptose 
                     7-phosphate kinase / heptose 1-phosphate adenyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.167; 2.7.7.70"
                     /label=hldE
                     /note="HldE catalyzes two reactions in the |FRAME:
                     PWY0-1241| pathway, which provides one of the building 
                     blocks for the inner core region of lipopolysaccharide 
                     (LPS) |CITS: [11751812]|.  The bifunctionality of HldE 
                     results from a fusion of an N-terminal ribokinase 
                     superfamily domain and a C-terminal cytidylyltransferase 
                     superfamily domain; the two domains are genetically and 
                     physically separable |CITS: [10629197][16030223]|. 
                     Structural modelling of the ribokinase domain led to the 
                     identification of amino acid residues potentially essential
                     for catalysis.  Site-directed mutagenesis of potential ATP 
                     binding site residues resulted in a dominant negative 
                     mutant phenotype |CITS: [16030223]|.
                     In a genetic screen for heptoseless mutants, a transposon 
                     insertion in the <i>hldE</i> gene was identified |CITS: 
                     [10629197]|.  Based on increased expression of <i>sulA</i> 
                     as a reporter gene, a &Delta;<i>hldE</i> mutant is more 
                     sensitive to exposure to the genotoxic agents nalidixic 
                     acid and mitomycin C than wild type |CITS: [21533638]|. The
                     LPS biosynthesis pathway has been explored as a target for 
                     development of new antibacterial compounds |CITS: 
                     [16632256]|; inhibitors of the kinase activity of HldE have
                     been identified |CITS: [16632256][19124251][21922563]|.
                     HldE: ""<b>L</b>-&beta;-<b>D</b>-<b>h</b>eptose synthesis 
                     <b>E</b>"" |CITS: [11751812]|"
                     /translation="MKVTLPEFERAGVMVVGDVMLDRYWYGPTSRISPEAPVPVVKVNT
