     gene            3195250..3196488
                     /gene="cca"
                     /locus_tag="b3056"
                     /label=cca
     CDS             3195250..3196488
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cca"
                     /locus_tag="b3056"
                     /product="bifunctional fused CCA tRNA 
                     nucleotidyltransferase/phosphohydrolase / CCA tRNA 
                     nucleotidyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.72"
                     /label=cca
                     /note="CCA tRNA nucleotidyltransferase catalyzes the
                     addition of CCA to the 3' ends of tRNAs in a 
                     template-independent manner |CITS: [3516995]|.  Because all
                     <i>E. coli</i> tRNAs encode the 3' CCA nucleotides as part 
                     of the gene |CITS: [2184240]|, CCA-adding enzyme appears to
                     be involved in tRNA quality control and repair of incorrect
                     or degraded 3' CCA ends |CITS: 
                     [7685099][18226598][29180590]||.
                     The N-terminal domain of the enzyme contains the 
                     nucleotidyltransferase activity.  The C-terminal domain 
                     carries an HD motif found in a family of metal-dpendent 
                     phosphohydrolases and displays a variety of 
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup>-dependent phosphohydrolase activities in 
                     the absence of tRNA; these activities may act together to 
                     repair the 3' end of tRNA |CITS: [15210699][15808744]|.  A 
                     truncated enzyme containing the N-terminal 264 amino acids 
                     has full CCA tRNA nucleotidyltransferase activity.  The C 
                     terminus of the protein contains an ""anchor domain"" that 
                     appears to prevent the addition of multiple CCA triplets 
                     |CITS: [15304219]|.  Mutations in tRNAs that destabilize 
                     the acceptor stem cause addition of CCACCA by the enzyme 
                     |CITS: [22076379]|; deep sequencing has confirmed the 
                     existence of CCACCA-tagged tRNAs in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [29180590]|.  These modified tRNAs appear to be degraded by
                     the less substrate-specific RNase R |CITS: [29180590]|. 
                     Bases within tRNAs that are required for activity have been
                     identified |CITS: [2645279][1700367]|.
                     Polymerization of the CCA sequence does not appear to 
                     involve translocation of the enzyme |CITS: [9606201]|.  In 
                     the absence of ATP, the enzyme catalyzes the addition of 
                     multiple C residues |CITS: [10917598]|.  A model for the 
                     specific addition of CCA involving distinct binding sites 
                     for ATP and CTP has been proposed |CITS: [10971651]|.  The 
                     catalytic mechanism has been investigated.  Nucleotide 
                     binding and catalysis requires the presence of metal ions; 
                     both Mn<sup>2+</sup> and Mg<sup>2+</sup> function in that 
                     role, but Mg<sup>2+</sup> enables inproved specificity for 
                     the third base (A) addition and appears to be the 
                     physiologically relevant metal ion |CITS: [16171400]|. 
                     Binding of Hfq to the tRNA substrate stimulates CCA tRNA 
                     nucleotidyltransferase activity |CITS: [17949481]|. 
                     Single-turnover kinetics show that intact tRNA is required 
                     for rapid, high affinity binding |CITS: [18466919]|, and 
                     that the kinetic parameters of the C and A additions differ
                     |CITS: [19696158]|.  The enzyme is able to catalyze the 
                     reverse (pyrophosphorolysis) reaction when provided with a 
                     substrate containing an incorrect A75 nucleotide, but it 
                     does not remove incorrect nucleotides from the other two 
                     positions |CITS: [22001019]|.
                     Comparison of this Cca enzyme with a reconstructed 
                     ancestral CCA-adding enzyme candidate from 
                     Gammaproteobacteria provided insights into optimization of 
                     the extant catalytic performance (the forward 
                     polymerization reaction) through reduction of the tRNA 
                     substrate affinity, resulting in minimization of substrate 
                     inhibition and the reverse reaction |CITS: [36409584]|.
                     A <i>cca</i> mutant that is not impaired in incorporation 
                     of C, but can not add the A nucleotide at the end of tRNAs 
                     has been isolated |CITS: [6176445]|; the point mutation was
                     shown to lead to substitution of glycine at position 70 by 
                     aspartate |CITS: [3533927]|.  Additional mutations in or 
                     deletion of a flexible loop region at positions 65-74 also 
                     interfere with addition of the terminal A residue |CITS: 
                     [18523015][18682528]|.  D21A/D23A mutants are catalytically
                     inactive, while a D93A mutant is specifically impaired for 
                     addition of the A residue |CITS: [11926813]|.
                     tRNA nucleotidyltransferase is not essential for viability 
                     of <i>E. coli</i>; a <i>cca</i> null mutant has a slow 
                     growth phenotype |CITS: [3311729]|.  A mutation in 
                     <i>relA</i> suppresses the slow growth phenotype of 
                     <i>cca</i> mutants |CITS: [342707]|.  Poly(A) polymerase I 
                     and polynucleotide phosphorylase can partially substitute 
                     for tRNA nucleotidyltransferase in the repair of the CCA 
                     end of tRNAs |CITS: [9407115]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [29374586][20101632][4573492]|"
