     gene            complement(3196669..3197490)
                     /gene="bacA"
                     /locus_tag="b3057"
                     /label=bacA
     CDS             complement(3196669..3197490)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="bacA"
                     /locus_tag="b3057"
                     /product="bifunctional undecaprenyl pyrophosphate
                     phosphatase / farnesyl diphosphate phosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.1.27"
                     /label=bacA
                     /note="The purified BacA protein has undecaprenyl
                     pyrophosphate (Und-PP or C<sub>55</sub>-PP) phosphatase 
                     activity, but not undecaprenol phosphokinase activity 
                     |CITS: [15138271]|. The <i>bacA</i> gene product is not 
                     essential for growth, but the product of the BacA catalysed
                     reaction, undecaprenyl phosphate, is essential for the 
                     synthesis of peptidoglycan and other cell wall components. 
                     At least three additional gene products, |FRAME: 
                     G6439-MONOMER ""YbjG""|, |FRAME: PGPPHOSPHAB-MONOMER 
                     ""PgpB""|, and |FRAME: G7146-MONOMER ""LpxT""|, are thought
                     to have undecaprenyl pyrophosphate phosphatase activity in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [15778224]|. BacA accounts for 
                     approximately three quarters of the total C<sub>55</sub>-PP
                     phosphatase activity |CITS: [15138271]|. BacA catalyses 
                     C<sub>55</sub>-PP dephosphorylation on the periplasmic side
                     of the inner membrane |CITS: [26560897]|.
                     Purified BacA dephosphorylates farnesyl pyrophosphate 
                     (FPP); the optimal pH for this assay is 6.5-7 and the 
                     enzyme requires Mg<sup>2+</sup> or Ca<sup>2+</sup> for 
                     activity; bacitracin inhibits the dephosphorylation of FPP 
                     by BacA |CITS: [24855653]|
                     BacA is predicted to contain 8 transmembrane helices with 
                     the N and C-termini located in the cytoplasm; two conserved
                     motifs - region I and region II - and a conserved histidine
                     residue (His-30) are thought to form the catalytic core of 
                     the enzyme |CITS: [24855653]|. A structural model of BacA 
                     in complex with C<sub>55</sub>-PP and a magnesium ion has 
                     been predicted and a catalytic mechanism with a central 
                     role for His-30 proposed |CITS: [24855653]|. Experimental 
                     analysis of BacA topology indicates that it contains 7 
                     transmembrane segments with the C-terminus located in the 
                     cytoplasm and the two conserved BacA signature motifs 
                     located within periplasmic loops. This suggests that BacA 
                     catalyses C<sub>55</sub>-PP dephosphorylation on the 
                     periplasmic face of the inner membrane |CITS: [26560897]|. 
                     His-30 is not essential for catalysis; a catalytic 
                     mechanism with a central role for Ser-27 is proposed |CITS:
                     [26560897]|.
                     Crystal structures of BacA have been determined: the 
                     protein contains 10 &alpha;-helices, 6 of these span the 
                     membrane completely, the remaining 4 make up two 
                     antiparallel reentrant helix-loop-helix regions that likely
                     form the active site; the protein contains a deep cleft or 
                     pocket that is open to both the periplasm and the 
                     periplasmic leaflet of the membrane and provides substrate 
                     access to the active site |CITS: [29540682][29559664]|. 
                     BacA has structural similarity to a number of transport 
                     proteins raising the possibility that, in addition to its 
                     function as a C55-PP phosphatase, it is involved in 
                     transporting or flipping C55-P across the membrane |CITS: 
                     [29540682][29559664]|.
                     Simultaneous inactivation of <i>bacA</i>, <i>ybjG</i> and 
                     <i>pgpB</i> is lethal.  Depletion of BacA in a 
                     &Delta;<i>ybjG</i> &Delta;<i>pgpB</i> strain results in 
                     accumulation of the peptidoglycan precursor, 
                     UDP-MurNAc-pentapeptide, changes in cell morphology and 
                     eventual lysis. Overexpression of <i>bacA</i>, <i>lpxT</i>,
                     <i>ybjG</i> or <i>pgpB</i> leads to increased 
                     C<sub>55</sub>-PP phosphatase activity in crude membrane 
                     extracts. Expression of C<sub>55</sub>-PP phosphatase 
                     activity from any one of the three genes <i>bacA</i>, 
                     <i>ybjG</i> or <i>pgpB</i> appears to be sufficient for 
                     synthesis of undecaprenyl phosphate and survival |CITS: 
                     [15778224]|. <i>bacA ybjG</i> double and <i>bacA ybjG 
                     lpxT</i> triple deletion mutants are hypersensitive to the 
                     undecaprenyl phosphate biosynthesis inhibitor fosmidomycin 
                     |CITS: [17660416]|.
                     When supplied on a multicopy plasmid, <i>bacA</i> confers 
                     resistance to bacitracin |CITS: [8389741]|.
                     The <i>bacA</i> gene belongs to the &sigma;<sup>E</sup> 
                     regulon, which is responsive to extracytoplasmic stress 
                     |CITS: [12900013]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [24068241][24799078]|"
