     gene            3198053..3198670
                     /gene="plsY"
                     /locus_tag="b3059"
                     /label=plsY
     CDS             3198053..3198670
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="plsY"
                     /locus_tag="b3059"
                     /product="putative glycerol-3-phosphate acyltransferase"
                     /label=plsY
                     /note="PlsY, together with |FRAME: EG11437-MONOMER PlsX|,
                     can substitute for |FRAME: 
                     GLYCEROL-3-P-ACYLTRANSFER-MONOMER| (PlsB) in synthesis of 
                     |FRAME: 2-Acyl-sn-glycerol-3-phosphates| required for 
                     initiation of phospholipid synthesis |CITS: [17645809]| and
                     fine-tune the levels of other lipids involved in the 
                     structure of the cell envelope |CITS: [39475235]|. Most 
                     Gram-negative proteobacteria have both the PlsX/PlsY and 
                     PlsB systems |CITS: [37601446]|.
                     PlsY is an inner membrane protein with five predicted 
                     transmembrane domains. The C terminus is located in the 
                     cytoplasm |CITS: [15919996]|.
                     PlsY is similar to the <i>Bacillus subtilis</i> and 
                     <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i> PlsY proteins, which 
                     function as
                     the glycerol-3-phosphate acyltransferase for phospholipid 
                     biosynthesis, a function that is filled by PlsB in <i>E. 
                     coli</i>. However, a <i>plsX plsY</i> double deletion is 
                     lethal |CITS: [17645809]|. Yoshimura et al. hypothesize 
                     that PlsX and
                     PlsY play a role in regulating the intracellular 
                     concentration of acyl-ACP |CITS: [17645809]|.
                     Using a suppressor screen approach, the <i>plsX plsY</i> 
                     double deletion synthetic lethality was confirmed, and 
                     rescue of this phenotype required increased levels of 
                     |FRAME: G3P| that could be obtained through secondary 
                     mutations, such as in |FRAME: EG10400|. <i>&Delta;plsX</i> 
                     alone results in increased long-chain acyl-ACPs and may 
                     regulate fatty acid synthesis through a |FRAME: 
                     ACYLCOASYN-MONOMER FadD|-dependent mechanism, whereas 
                     <i>&Delta;plsY</i> shows hypersensitivity to the antibiotic
                     cerulenin, which decreases fatty acid synthesis. 
                     Suppressors do not rescue individual mutations of 
                     <i>plsX</i> or <i>plsY</i>, only the double mutation. These
                     results indicate that together PlsX/PlsY play a role in the
                     fine-tuned regulation of lipid production and homeostasis 
                     of the cell envelope |CITS: [39475235]|. Deletion of 
                     <i>plsY</i> enhances tolerance to n-butanol |CITS: 
                     [21408113]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [18369234][22981714][23699259]|"
