     gene            complement(3202889..3203902)
                     /gene="tsaD"
                     /locus_tag="b3064"
                     /label=tsaD
     CDS             complement(3202889..3203902)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tsaD"
                     /locus_tag="b3064"
                     /product="trifunctional N(6)-L-threonylcarbamoyladenine
                     synthase, TsaD subunit / N(6)-L-threonylcarbamoyladenine 
                     synthase, TsaB-TsaD dimer subunit / 
                     N(6)-L-threonylcarbamoyladenine synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.234"
                     /label=tsaD
                     /note="TsaD is involved in the biosynthesis of the 
                     threonylcarbamoyladenosine (t<sup>6</sup>A) residue at 
                     position 37 of ANN-decoding tRNAs.  A mixture of purified 
                     |FRAME: G7698-MONOMER TsaC|, TsaD, |FRAME: G6991-MONOMER 
                     TsaB|, and |FRAME: EG11757-MONOMER TsaE| proteins catalyzes
                     formation of t<sup>6</sup>A in the presence of a tRNA 
                     substrate, ATP, threonine and bicarbonate <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [22378793][25578970]|.
                     The three essential proteins TsaE, TsaB and TsaD form an 
                     interaction network.  TsaD interacts with TsaB, but not 
                     with TsaE.  Purified TsaD can oligomerize, with the major 
                     form being a dimer |CITS: [19376873]|.  The specific 
                     interaction between TsaD and TsaB is required for the 
                     essential function of these proteins |CITS: [21285948]|. 
                     TsaB and TsaD form a heterodimer; crystal structures of 
                     this protein complex have been solved.  The structure shows
                     ADP bound at the dimer interface.  Site-directed mutants in
                     the Mg<sup>2+</sup> binding site and in the TsaB-TsaD 
                     interface were generated and biochemically characterized 
                     |CITS: [25578970]|.
                     TsaD binds glycated proteins and was also proposed to 
                     function as a glycopeptidase that may be directly involved 
                     in the metabolism of glycated proteins |CITS: [20824107]|. 
                     The TsaD protein appears to be specifically degraded by 
                     TsaB |CITS: [19376873]|; this activity may not be 
                     reproducible.
                     TsaD is essential for growth in complex media under aerobic
                     conditions |CITS: [9743119][19376873]|.  The protein is 
                     broadly conserved in bacteria.  Depletion of the TsaD 
                     protein results in the formation of enlarged, elongated 
                     cells with an unusual distribution of DNA and membranes 
                     with a ruffled appearance |CITS: [19376873]| or, 
                     conversely, much shorter cells with dramatically reduced 
                     growth rates |CITS: [20824107]|.  These results may be 
                     explained by the use of different <i>E. coli</i> strains 
                     |CITS: [20824107]|.  Depletion of TsaD was analyzed using 
                     single-cell time-lapse microscopy.  The observed decrease 
                     in cell size is dependent on the ability to produce 
                     (p)ppGpp, while termination of cell division is not |CITS: 
                     [21619589]|.  TsaD-depleted cells accumulate 
                     Amadori-modified proteins (AMPs) and advanced glycated end 
                     products (AGEs) |CITS: [20824107]| and contain much lower 
                     levels of t<sup>6</sup>A-modified tRNAs than wild-type 
                     |CITS: [21183954]|.
                     As for <i>tsaE</i> and <i>tsaB</i>, |FRAME: G6864 
                     <i>rstA</i>| was identified as a multicopy suppressor of 
                     the lethal phenotype of a strain with a conditional 
                     <i>tsaD</i> mutation |CITS: [19376873]|.  A 
                     temperature-sensitive <i>tsaD</i> mutant shows lower levels
                     of transcription attenuation at the <i>thr</i> and 
                     <i>ilv</i> operons.  Other genetic interactions were found 
                     |CITS: [21873492]|.  The <i>tsaD</i><sup>ts</sup> allele 
                     causes increased ribosomal frameshifting, and thereby 
                     likely increased IS<i>1</i> transposition frequency.  A 
                     deletion/insertion mutant containing an extra copy of 
                     <i>tsaE</i> suppresses the growth defects of the 
                     <i>tsaD</i><sup>ts</sup> allele |CITS: [23909935]|.
                     Gcp: ""glycopeptidase"" |CITS: [20824107]|
                     TsaD: ""<b>t</b>hreonylcarbamoyl-6(<b>s</b>ix)-<b>a</b>den 
                     osine"" |CITS: [22378793]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [19465656][22905870]|"
