     gene            3204466..3206211
                     /gene="dnaG"
                     /locus_tag="b3066"
                     /label=dnaG
     CDS             3204466..3206211
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dnaG"
                     /locus_tag="b3066"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA primase / replisome"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.101"
                     /label=dnaG
                     /note="DNA primase catalyzes the synthesis of RNA primers
                     on single-stranded
                     template DNA |CITS: [340457]|. These primers then serve as 
                     the
                     starting point for DNA synthesis by DNA polymerase III 
                     |CITS: [1097446]|.
                     Using single-stranded DNA <i>in vitro</i>, primase and NTPs
                     are
                     sufficient to produce RNA primers |CITS: 
                     [8509429][2408271][8519767]|.
                     In practice, primase also relies on ssDNA-binding protein 
                     (SSB) to stabilize
                     its interaction with the primer. The Chi subunit of DNA 
                     polymerase III
                     interacts with SSB near the primer, displacing DNA primase 
                     and
                     allowing loading of the DNA polymerase III beta sliding 
                     clamp |CITS: [9989506][9500915]|.
                     Primase binds three
                     distinct sites during priming, one of them as far as 115 
                     nucleotides
                     distant from the start of primer synthesis |CITS: 
                     [6244591]|.
                     In the case of lagging strand synthesis, primase 
                     dissociates from DNA each time an Okazaki fragment is 
                     completed and then repeats this binding process to begin 
                     priming of the next fragment |CITS: [1740451]|.
                     During replication, primase follows DNA helicase as the 
                     helicase
                     processively unwinds DNA at the replication fork, putting 
                     down
                     primers on the lagging strand in its wake |CITS: [144914]|.
                     Three DnaG
                     monomers bind per helicase hexamer via the DnaG 
                     carboxy-terminal
                     domain |CITS: [15649896]|. This physical interaction is 
                     required for optimal primer synthesis, stimulating primase 
                     function 5,000 fold |CITS: [8917517][12009693][10651639]|. 
                     In addition, interaction with helicase
                     reduces the specificity of the primer initiation site from 
                     CTG
                     to PyPyPu |CITS: [10651640][1828532]|.
                     The degree of interaction between primase and helicase sets
                     the length of Okazaki fragments |CITS: [8702921]|. More 
                     generally, the availability
                     of primase controls how frequently new fragments are 
                     started
                     and thus how long they are, and therefore the ssDNA-binding
                     capability
                     of helicase is required to direct primase to new template 
                     DNA |CITS: [1740453][1740452][14557266]|.
                     Initiation of bidirectional replication from <i>oriC</i> 
                     also
                     requires the helicase-primase interaction, as this controls
                     proper leading-strand primer placement |CITS: 
                     [10480943][8119951]|.
                     Primer length is constrained by
                     the addition of DnaC, which loads helicase onto DNA |CITS: 
                     [14557266]|.
                     The presence of DNA polymerase
                     III, which follows helicase, also limits primer length to 
                     about 10 nucleotides |CITS: [1531480]|.
                     Two primases bind at the site of primer initiation, 
                     although they
                     do not appear to form a dimer. This differs from the 
                     stoichiometry of interaction with helicase discussed
                     above |CITS: [12475230][6355106]|.
                     Primase has been subject to structural and cofactor 
                     analysis.
                     Its catalytic activity is located in the amino-terminus, 
                     although
                     the helicase-interacting carboxy-terminal domain is also 
                     required
                     for priming at the replication fork |CITS: 
                     [8308039][14711519]|.
                     The catalytic center has been evaluated by
                     crosslinking as well as via crystal structures at 2.9, 1.7 
                     and
                     1.6 &Aring; resolution, which reveal that the core has a 
                     TOPRIM
                     domain similar to DNA topoisomerases |CITS: 
                     [7541046][10873470][10741967]|.
                     The core catalytic domain
                     contains several metal-coordinating residues |CITS: 
                     [10741967][10630993]|.
                     Primase appears to bind two magnesium ions
                     and has been shown to contain about one gram of zinc per 
                     mol of
                     purified protein |CITS: [8527445][1511009]|.
                     Deletion of a putative zinc-binding region in the 
                     amino-terminus
                     inactivates primase |CITS: [1511009]|. Zinc in primase 
                     prevents
                     formation of deleterious disulfide bonds between the 
                     cysteines
                     that coordinate the zinc ions |CITS: [8679581]|. The 
                     binding of
                     zinc and the effect of magnesium abundance on zinc binding 
                     has been examined in detail |CITS: [10360938]|.
                     Primase is required for initiation of DNA replication in 
                     plasmids and phages. It is a component of the primosome, a 
                     complex
                     that initiates DNA replication in phiX174 phage <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [8663105][8663104]|.
                     Other phages that depend on primase
                     include T4 and alpha 3 |CITS: [6758324][6985903]|. Plasmids
                     that require primase
                     to initiate replication include Mu, R1, mini RK2 and pSC101
                     |CITS: [8206956][3523437][2542261][2850370][3900047]|.
                     Sequence and structure comparisons have been made across 
                     phage
                     and plasmid priming sites |CITS: [8206839]|.
                     <i>In vitro</i>, primase can polymerize RNA from the 3' end
                     of
                     a DNA oligomer primer |CITS: [9632699]|.
                     Primase is regulated posttranscriptionally by RNase E, 
                     which cleaves
                     its mRNA, and by its use of rare codons, which may serve to
                     keep
                     its expression lower than other cotranscribed genes |CITS: 
                     [7685758][6338495]|.
                     Recent review: |CITS: [38982189]|"
