     gene            3206406..3208247
                     /gene="rpoD"
                     /locus_tag="b3067"
                     /label=rpoD
     CDS             3206406..3208247
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpoD"
                     /locus_tag="b3067"
                     /product="bifunctional RNA polymerase sigma factor RpoD /
                     RNA polymerase sigma 70"
                     /label=rpoD
                     /note="Sigma 70 is the primary sigma factor during
                     exponential growth, targeting |FRAME: RNAP70-CPLX| to a 
                     wide range of promoters that are essential for normal 
                     growth. E&sigma;<sup>70</sup> transcribes, in the absence 
                     of any additional transcription factor, with higher 
                     efficiency genes encoding ribosomal proteins and other 
                     protein synthesis-related genes, such as rRNA- and 
                     tRNA-encoding genes and <i>prfB</i>, which encodes release 
                     factor 2 |CITS:[21398637]|.
                     Sigma 70 accounts for 60-95% of the total pool of cellular 
                     sigma factors during normal exponential growth |CITS: 
                     [8808934]|. Changes away from typical growth conditions, 
                     such as heat shock or growth into stationary phase, lead to
                     the replacement of sigma 70 with other sigma factors in the
                     RNA polymerase holoenzyme complex |CITS: 
                     [7588636][1745227][15596728]|. When this occurs, the 
                     holoenzyme no longer recognizes the same transcription 
                     targets, leading to a decline in many RNA and protein 
                     levels, including components of the protein synthesis 
                     machinery |CITS: [7035833][3543015][12421813]|. 
                     Alternatively, under osmotic stress sigma 70 seems to be 
                     responsible for RNAP redistribution to transcribe 
                     stress-responsive genes |CITS: [30843055]|.
                     Sigma 70 has the strongest interaction with the RNA 
                     polymerase core complex of all the known sigma factors 
                     |CITS: [10982868]|. This interaction may in fact be 
                     required for sigma 70 to bind DNA, as its amino-terminal 
                     portion normally blocks such binding in the absence of core
                     complex |CITS: [1643661]|. When it does bind promoter DNA, 
                     sigma 70 contacts both the -10 and -35 regions 
                     simultaneously, with a binding affinity depending on the 
                     spacing between these points |CITS: [8799117]|. In 
                     promoters with a -35 site, sigma 70 is more effective when 
                     there is also a proximal half-site or a complete UP 
                     element, whereas the stationary phase sigma, |FRAME: 
                     RPOS-MONOMER|, has opposite selectivity |CITS: [15612932]|.
                     Both sigma 70 and |FRAME: RPOS-MONOMER| compete for 
                     available core complex during stationary phase growth 
                     |CITS: [9767572]|. Sigma 70 also competes with NusA for 
                     binding to |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX|, though sigma 70's 
                     affinity for the core drops off dramatically during the 
                     change from transcriptional initiation to elongation |CITS:
                     [1856862][10625443]|. Sigma 70 even competes with the 
                     nascent RNA strand for binding to |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX| 
                     |CITS: [10411885]|. The majority of early elongation 
                     complexes do appear to retain sigma 70 for a while, with 
                     this retention being promoted by promoter-like sequences in
                     the initial transcribed region |CITS: [16285917]|. These 
                     sequences, bearing similarity to -10 promoters, can induce 
                     sigma 70-mediated pausing of |FRAME: RNAP70-CPLX| |CITS: 
                     [14630944][15122345]|, misincorporation-induced pausing 
                     |CITS:[28442761]|. Notably, promoters recognized by sigma 
                     70 occur in regions rife with such promoter-like sequences 
                     |CITS: [14529615]|. Genes with promoters recognized by 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> and not by other &sigma; factors are 
                     weakly downregulated in stationary phase compared to 
                     exponential-phase cells growing in LB medium 
                     |CITS:[35338024]|. <i>In vivo</i> during stationary phase, 
                     the activity of RNA polymerase:&sigma;<sup>70</sup> is 
                     inhibited when it forms a complex with 6S RNA (6S 
                     RNA:RNAP:&sigma;<sup>70</sup>), and it is released when 
                     levels of nutrients are high by accumulation of a complex 
                     of 6S RNA with a short product of RNA (6S RNA:pRNA) 
                     synthesized from 6S RNA:RNAP:&sigma;<sup>70</sup> |CITS: 
                     [36198425][38692564]|.
                     Based on an XACT-seq method, 
                     cross-link-between-active-center-and-template sequencing, 
                     which directly defines the RNAP active center position 
                     relative to DNA with single-nucleotide resolution, 
                     detection and quantification of pausing in initial 
                     transcription at 4<sup>11</sup> (~4,000,000) promoter 
                     sequences <i>in vivo</i> showed that initial transcription 
                     pausing can occur with each nucleotide addition during 
                     initial transcription, particularly the first 4 to 5 
                     nucleotide additions in sequences that resemble the 
                     consensus sequence element for transcription-elongaton 
                     pausing |CITS:[32750314]|.
                     Based on nascent elongating transcript sequencing (NET-seq)
                     combined with RNase I footprinting (RNET-seq), genome-wide 
                     identification of &sigma;<sup>70</sup>-dependent 
                     transcription pauses in <i> E. coli</i> was performed 
                     |CITS: [33568644]|. In these studies, &sigma;<sup>70</sup> 
                     induced backtracked pauses at a 10- to 20-bp distance from 
                     many promoters |CITS: [33568644]|. Promoters with 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup>-induced pauses at bp 10 to 15 have a 
                     canonical 10 element, 6- to 7-nt spacer, and YR+1Y motif, 
                     where the purine (R) corresponds to the transcription start
                     site |CITS: [33568644]|. By contrast, promoters with 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup>-induced pauses at bp 16 to 20 contain 
                     all these elements and, additionally, a 10-like sequence 
                     |CITS: [33568644]|. <i>In vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> 
                     analyses showed that &sigma;<sup>70</sup>-induced 
                     backtracked pauses are relieved by Gre cleavage elongation 
                     factors |CITS: [33568644]|. The backtracking of RNAP at 
                     these pauses was determined to be of 4 to 5 bp by |CITS: 
                     [33568644]| and 2 to 4 bp by |CITS: [35653571]|. In 
                     addition to the backtracked state, the sigma-dependent 
                     pausing involves DNA scrunching with 2 to 3 bp, both at 
                     transcription initiation and transcription elongation, and 
                     the consensus sequence for sigma-dependent pausing 
                     involving DNA scrunching was determined as 
                     T<sub>P1</sub>N<sub>P</sub>Y<sub>A</sub>G<sub>A+1</sub> 
                     expressed in terms of RNAP active-center A-site and P-site 
                     positions (where N is any nucleotide and Y is a pyrimidine)
                     |CITS: [35653571]|.
                     Sigma 70 function is blocked by the anti-sigma factor, 
                     |FRAME: EG11738-MONOMER|.
                     Physiologically relevant &sigma;<sup>70</sup> 
                     concentrations are required for reinitiation of 
                     transcription when the RNA polymerase remains bound to DNA 
                     after a round of transcription |CITS: [37459525]|.
                     Sigma 70 has undergone significant structural analysis. A 
                     crystal structure of sigma 70 has been determined to 2.6 
                     &Aring; resolution, revealing that residues that bind core 
                     polymerase are on one face, with DNA-binding residues on 
                     the other |CITS: [8858155]|. Most of sigma 70 must be 
                     present to allow promoter melting |CITS: [14988563]|. 
                     Within sigma 70, there are conserved motifs responsible for
                     binding to the -10 and -35 and other promoter regions 
                     |CITS: [2661828][2661827][14621989][8345519][12782316]|. 
                     Region 1.2 of sigma 70 is responsible for recognition of 
                     the -10-like pause regions described above, as well as 
                     recognition of the fork junction and actual 
                     promoter-proximal pausing |CITS: [17268549]|. Conserved 
                     region 3.2 of the primary &sigma; factor (&sigma; finger) 
                     directly contacts the template DNA strand in the open 
                     promoter complex and facilitates initiation of NTP binding 
                     in the active center of RNAP |CITS:[30321408]|. Based on a 
                     molecular beacon assay, it was determined that the &sigma; 
                     region 3.2 decreased the rate of promoter escape and led to
                     accumulation of inactive complexes during transcription 
                     initiation |CITS:[29101286]|. A region of sigma 70 that 
                     partially blocks the RNA exit channel in the holoenzyme is 
                     involved in binding the 3' initiating nucleotide in the 
                     active site and aids in promoter clearance |CITS: 
                     [16690607]|. A crystal structure of |FRAME: RNAP70-CPLX| 
                     has been determined to 9.5 &Aring; resolution |CITS: 
                     [11118218]|. |FRAME: EG10893-MONOMER| interacts with sigma 
                     70 at a subset of UP and activator-dependent promoters 
                     |CITS: [12820974]|. Interaction between |FRAME: 
                     RPOC-MONOMER| and sigma 70 promotes sigma 70 interaction 
                     with the nontemplate strand in the -10 region, while 
                     interaction between |FRAME: RPOB-MONOMER| and sigma 70 acts
                     to block extension of nascent RNA, and thus must be 
                     disrupted to allow elongation to occur |CITS: 
                     [11439189][15761057]|. &sigma;<sup>70</sup> contacts with 
                     the nontemplate strand of the discriminator and -10 regions
                     (&sigma; regions 1.2 and 2) of the <i>&lambda;PR</i> 
                     promoter are disrupted stepwise during mid-initiation (5- 
                     to 9-mer RNA synthesis) |CITS:[40462541]|. Subsequently, 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> regions 3 and 4, which interact with 
                     the extended -10 and -35 elements of the promoter, 
                     dissociate in the final initiation steps (10- to 11-mer 
                     synthesis), coinciding with &sigma;<sup>70</sup> release 
                     from the core enzyme and promoter escape |CITS:[40462541]|.
                     These findings reveal a downstream-to-upstream sequence of 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup>-DNA and &sigma;<sup>70</sup>-core 
                     contact disruption during transcription initiation at 
                     <i>&lambda;PR</i> [|CITS:[40462541]|.
                     The interaction of sigma 70 with activator proteins has 
                     also been addressed in detail |CITS: [8289270]|.
                     Sigma 70 undergoes a conformational change when it binds to
                     the core complex, revealing the sigma factor's DNA-binding 
                     surfaces, then undergoes another change on promoter melting
                     |CITS:[9830052][10493811][11511357]|. When 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> is not bound to RNA polymerase (RNAP),
                     it adopts a compact conformation due to the proximity 
                     between its R2 and R4 regions, in contrast to the extended 
                     conformation it assumes when bound to RNAP 
                     |CITS:[38957792]|. This compact conformation is 
                     predominantly observed during the stationary phase, during 
                     which alternative sigma factors are recruited for 
                     transcription |CITS:[38957792]|. This process is considered
                     a mechanism of self-inhibition of DNA binding 
                     |CITS:[38957792]|.
                     In <i>in vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> transcription 
                     analyses, <i>E. coli</i> RNA polymerase holoenzyme 
                     containing &sigma;<sup>70</sup> was intrinsically 
                     promiscuous, driving spurious transcription that was 
                     suppressed by H-NS. The promiscuity was dictated by the 
                     &sigma; factor |CITS: [35241653]|. The residues R156 and 
                     R486 from &sigma;<sup>70</sup> influence the patterns of 
                     DNA opening and contribute to the transcription promiscuity
                     |CITS: [35241653]|.
                     A point mutation, I48S, in the poorly conserved region 1.1 
                     of &sigma;<sup>70</sup> causes higher activity in protein 
                     synthesis during growth and produces a decrease of dormant 
                     cells and therefore a major cellular susceptibility to 
                     antibiotics |CITS:[32997144]|. This mutation alters the 
                     expression of at least 886 genes during exponential and 
                     stationary phases, including genes like sigma factors and 
                     their sigmulons and other transcription factors, the 
                     repression of  6S RNA (ssrS gene), which is a negative 
                     regulator of &sigma;<sup>70</sup>, and increased rRNAs. The
                     mutant has poor sensitivity to DksA protein 
                     |CITS:[32997144]|.
                     A &sigma;-competition model for RpoD based on comparative 
                     kinetic and thermodynamic properties was developed by 
                     Ganguly and Chatterji in 2012 |CITS:[22995505]|. The 
                     <i>rpoD</i> promoter's sensitivity to indirect regulation 
                     by &sigma; factor competition is determined by the 
                     sequence-dependent kinetics of the rate-limiting steps of 
                     transcription initiation |CITS:[27452766] |.
                     Under ampicillin treatment, the activity of 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> appears to increase |CITS: 
                     [33731833]|. On the other hand, inhibition of <i>rpoD</i> 
                     expression by CRISPRi enhanced cellular fitness under 
                     treatment with the antibiotics polymyxin B, phleomycin, or 
                     mitomycin C, but reduced it under treatment with rifampicin
                     |CITS:[40352728]|. In &Delta;|FRAME: EG10509|, 
                     &Delta;|FRAME: G6115| and wild-type <i>E. coli</i> exposed 
                     to sub-lethal concentrations of amoxicillin, multiple and 
                     consistent mutations were found in <i>|FRAME: EG10040|</i>,
                     its direct upstream gene <i>|FRAME: EG10333|</i> and in 
                     |FRAME: EG10896|, pointing to their roles in amoxicillin 
                     resistance |CITS: [39970152]|.
                     A systematic study of &Delta;<i>|FRAME: EG10896|</i> strain
                     for biomanufacturing performed better under a variety of 
                     growth conditions whereas &Delta;<i>fliA</i> strain found 
                     it produced better in a mineral media |CITS: [39994955]|."
