     gene            3208957..3209032
                     /gene="ileX"
                     /locus_tag="b3069"
                     /label=ileX
     tRNA            3208957..3209032
                     /gene="ileX"
                     /locus_tag="b3069"
                     /product="tRNA-Ile(CAU)"
                     /label=ileX
                     /note="tRNA(ileX) is one of five isoleucine tRNAs.
                     tRNAs are the adapters that allow synthesis of proteins 
                     from mRNAs.
                     Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome for
                     protein synthesis.
                     There, the tRNA recognizes an RNA codon with its own 
                     three-nucleotide
                     anticodon, thus allowing synthesis of a specific peptide 
                     based on an mRNA
                     template.
                     tRNAs are processed to their active, mature forms by RNA 
                     cleavage
                     and by modification of their bases. RNA cleavage consists 
                     of
                     removal of both 5' and 3' extensions in a multistep process
                     involving
                     many RNases |CITS: [11252717]|. RNases taking part in tRNA 
                     processing include |FRAME: CPLX0-3461|,
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3601|, |FRAME: EG10858-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: EG11620-MONOMER|, and |FRAME: CPLX0-3602|. tRNAs 
                     are also subject to a
                     wide variety of base modifications catalyzed by proteins 
                     such as
                     |FRAME: EG11932-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10595-MONOMER|, |FRAME:
                     G6364-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11344-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7199-MONOMER|, |FRAME: CPLX0-1101|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11779-MONOMER|, |FRAME: G7422-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7449-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11177-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10454-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10967-MONOMER|,
                     and |FRAME: EG11022-MONOMER|.
                     The CAU anticodon of tRNA(ileX) is modified to contain a 
                     lysidine
                     at position C34 |CITS: [3054566]|. This modification is 
                     necessary for proper
                     recognition of the isoleucine-specifying AUA codon and for 
                     proper aminoacylation of the tRNA with isoleucine, rather 
                     than with
                     methionine |CITS: [3054566][3054566]|. The AUA codon is 
                     relatively rare in
                     <i>E. coli</i>, and tRNA(ileX) is present at a low 
                     abundance that
                     is limiting with respect to production of some heterologous
                     proteins in
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [8522513][10919765][11319928]|. 
                     Mature tRNAs are linked via a 3' CCA sequence to their 
                     cognate
                     amino acid in an ATP-dependent fashion by the appropriate 
                     amino-acid-tRNA synthetase, as shown in the |FRAME: 
                     TRNA-CHARGING-PWY|.
                     Subsequently, these charged tRNAs interact with the 
                     ribosome
                     and template mRNA to generate polypeptides. The discovery 
                     of the
                     role of tRNA in protein synthesis is reviewed in detail in 
                     |CITS: [7033244]|.
                     NMR has been used to look at tRNA(ile) structure on its own
                     and interacting
                     with |FRAME: ILES-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [6756467][6340996][3322383][8199246][35275364]|."
     gene            complement(3209086..3209850)
                     /gene="nfeF"
                     /locus_tag="b3070"
                     /label=nfeF
     CDS             complement(3209086..3209850)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nfeF"
                     /locus_tag="b3070"
                     /product="bifunctional NADPH-dependent Fe(3+)-chelate
                     reductase / ferric-chelate reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.16.1.9"
                     /label=nfeF
                     /note="NfeF is an NADPH-dependent ferric reductase; the
                     enzyme contains FAD that is used as a cofactor rather than 
                     a substrate. The enzyme is only weakly active with purified
                     ferric enterobactin |CITS: [21097627]|; the hydrolyzed 
                     ferric enterobactin complex is the most efficient of the 
                     tested substrates |CITS: [22098718]|. Reports disagree on 
                     whether the FAD cofactor is covalently bound to a cysteine 
                     sidechain via a thioether bond |CITS: [21097627]| or not 
                     covalently attached |CITS: [22098718]|. A preliminary 
                     report of crystallization and structure analysis of the 
                     NfeF protein has appeared. NfeF is structurally related to 
                     the NAD(P)H:flavin oxidoreductase superfamily |CITS: 
                     [18765906]|. The reaction mechanism of NfeF has been 
                     investigated in detail; a transient flavosemiquinone is 
                     involved in the single-electron transfer in a 
                     double-displacement (ping-pong)-type reaction. The K55 and 
                     R130 residues are required for substrate binding and 
                     enzymatic activity. While the enzyme has high affinity for 
                     the intact ferric enterobactin, the catalytic turnover rate
                     is much slower than for the hydrolyzed ferric 
                     triscatecholate |CITS: [22098718]|.
                     The slow growth phenotype of a <i>fes</i> deletion mutant 
                     is enhanced by deletion of <i>nfeF</i>, supporting a role 
                     for NfeF in adaptation to iron starvation. Deletion of 
                     <i>nfeF</i> increases the toxicity of nickel |CITS: 
                     [21097627]|.
                     <i>nfeF</i> is part of the Fur regulon |CITS: [12746439]|; 
                     a candidate FUR box has been found upstream of the ORF 
                     |CITS: [11812853]|. Expression of <i>nfeF</i> is repressed 
                     by both Fur and NfeR and is induced in response to nickel 
                     |CITS: [21097627][29723740]|.
                     NfeF: ""<b>N</b>i-responsive <b>Fe</b>-uptake 
                     <b>f</b>lavoprotein"" |CITS: [29723740]|
                     Review: |CITS: [23443998]|"
