     gene            3231733..3231920
                     /gene="sraF"
                     /locus_tag="b4448"
                     /label=sraF
                     /note="<i>sraF</i> was initially reported to be a small
                     RNA approximately 189 nt in length subject to RNase III 
                     cleavage |CITS: [11448770]|, and also reported to be found 
                     as two species of 120 and 180 nt in length |CITS: 
                     [11553332]|.
                     Further experiments showed that this region is in fact the 
                     5' UTR of the <i>alx</i> gene and is involved in a novel 
                     type of regulation by pH |CITS: [19933154]|.
                     "
                     /note="/phantom"
     gene            3231939..3232904
                     /gene="alx"
                     /locus_tag="b3088"
                     /label=alx
     CDS             3231939..3232904
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="alx"
                     /locus_tag="b3088"
                     /product="Mn(2+) exporter Alx"
                     /label=alx
                     /note="Alx is a ""low-activity"" Mn<sup>2+</sup> exporter
                     with a role in manganese ion homeostasis at alkaline pH; 
                     Alx-mediated Mn<sup>2+</sup> export prevents a build-up of 
                     intracellular Mn<sup>2+</sup> under alkaline conditions 
                     |CITS: [38869303]|. Previously Alx was predicted to be a 
                     membrane-bound redox modulator |CITS: [12107143]|. 
                     Expression from the <i>alx</i> promoter is highly induced 
                     by growth at an external pH of 8.5 and above |CITS: 
                     [2108134][12107143]| both aerobically and anaerobically; 
                     expression is repressed by low pH only under aerobic 
                     conditions |CITS: [17026754]|. <i>alx</i> expression is 
                     repressed by paraquat |CITS: [11395452]|.
                     pH-responsive regulation of <i>alx</i> expression is 
                     achieved via a novel <U>p</U>H-<U>r</U>esponsive 
                     riboregulator <U>e</U>lement (PRE) that was previously 
                     thought to encode small RNA |FRAME: G0-8869 SraF|. The 
                     <i>alx</i> 5' UTR can form alternative secondary structures
                     depending on the pH; under extreme alkaline conditions, the
                     secondary structure that forms during transcription leaves 
                     the ribosome binding site and start codon accessible to the
                     ribosome, thus enabling translation. The formation of this 
                     secondary structure is kinetically controlled by RNA 
                     polymerase (RNAP) pausing |CITS: [19933154]| (and further 
                     |CITS: [24078087]|). RNAP pausing alone is insufficient to 
                     explain PRE folding and RNA may be involved in sensing and 
                     rearranging its structure in response to cytosolic pH 
                     |CITS: [35934054]|
                     A riboswitch belonging to the Mn<sup>2+</sup>-responsive 
                     <i>yybP-ykoY</i> family makes translation of <i>alx</i> 
                     responsive to the presence of Mn<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [25794618]|. Maximal activation of an <i>alx</i> riboswitch
                     reporter requires both manganese and an alkaline pH |CITS: 
                     [36190429]|. The <i>alx</i> riboswitch is responsive to 
                     both alkaline pH and Mn<sup>2+</sup>; alkaline pH augments 
                     the effect of Mn<sup>2+</sup> on <i>alx</i> expression 
                     |CITS: [38869303][40252020]|.
                     Growth of &Delta;<i>alx</i> cells is not affected by high 
                     levels of Mn<sup>2+</sup> (up to 4 mM) nor by varying pH (7
                     - 8.5); <i>alx</i> expression is associated with an 
                     increase in intracellular Mn<sup>2+</sup> levels |CITS: 
                     [29440394]|."
