     gene            3247678..3248379
                     /gene="yhaK"
                     /locus_tag="b3106"
                     /label=yhaK
     CDS             3247678..3248379
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yhaK"
                     /locus_tag="b3106"
                     /product="bicupin-related protein"
                     /label=yhaK
                     /note="YhaK belongs to a subclass of the bicupin family
                     |CITS: [18561187]|.  The protein may be involved in 
                     chloride binding, sensing of oxidative stress |CITS: 
                     [18561187]|, or in the degradation of 2,4-dinitrotoluene 
                     (DNT) |CITS: [27713734]|.
                     A crystal structure of YhaK has been solved |CITS: 
                     [18561187]|.  YhaK contains two structurally similar 
                     domains that are in a face-to-face arrangement.  In the 
                     crystal structure, the two domains are crosslinked via a 
                     disulfide bond between C10 and C204; a third cysteine, 
                     C122, is modified to a sulfenic acid.  Residues that 
                     coordinate divalent metal ions in pirin-related proteins 
                     are not conserved in YhaK, and no metal ion is observed in 
                     the crystal structure |CITS: [18561187]|.
                     YhaK is sensitive to oxidation, forming an intramolecular 
                     disulfide bond and oligomeric complexes; oxidation can be 
                     attenuated by the thioredoxin and glutaredoxin systems. 
                     Unlike the structurally similar YhhW, YhaK does not have 
                     quercetinase activity |CITS: [18561187]|.
                     Expression of <i>yhaK</i> is induced by reactive nitrogen 
                     species |CITS: [14718666]| and by exposure to hydroquinone 
                     |CITS: [27713734]|.  Induction by hydroquinone is abolished
                     in a <i>yhaJ</i> mutant, and induction by DNT is enhanced 
                     in a <i>yhaK</i> mutant |CITS: [27713734]|.  The 
                     <i>yhaK</i> homolog in uropathogenic <i>E. coli</i> strains
                     shows increased expression during biofilm growth compared 
                     to planktonic growth in urine |CITS: [17145952]|."
