     gene            complement(3248700..3250010)
                     /gene="cyuA"
                     /locus_tag="b4470"
                     /label=cyuA
     CDS             complement(3248700..3250010)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cyuA"
                     /locus_tag="b4470"
                     /product="L-cysteine desulfidase"
                     /EC_number="4.4.1.1; 4.4.1.28"
                     /label=cyuA
                     /note="CyuA is an |FRAME: EC-4.4.1.28| in <i>E. coli</i>
                     K-12. It is the major anaerobic cysteine-catabolizing 
                     enzyme |CITS: [28607157]|. Contrary to earlier reports, the
                     enzyme does not act in L-cysteine detoxification; rather, 
                     together with |FRAME: YHAO-MONOMER| the enzyme allows the 
                     organism to utilize L-cysteine as the sole nitrogen and 
                     carbon/energy source |CITS: [35377168]|.
                     CyuA shares sequence similarity with dehydratases that have
                     an iron-sulfur (Fe-S) cluster, such as CyuA in |FRAME: 
                     TAX-2190|, but exhibits higher sensitivity to molecular 
                     oxygen exposure. Activity of CyuA purified in an anaerobic 
                     chamber was restored when incubated with Fe(II) and 
                     dithiothreitol (DTT), a treatment used with dehydratases to
                     reactivate damaged Fe-S clusters |CITS: [35377168]|.
                     The enzyme is highly specific, and D-cysteine and analogs 
                     of L-cysteine are not substrates. In competition studies 
                     <i>in vitro</i>, CyuA was found to bind compounds that 
                     contained both a thiol and a primary amine group; serine, 
                     alanine, leucine, glutathione nor &Beta;-mercaptoethanol 
                     are unable to inhibit |CITS: [35377168]|. The enzyme has a 
                     high Km for L-cysteine (200 &mu;M) that ensures that it 
                     does not deplete the cysteine pool in the cell (the 
                     concentration of L-cysteine in the cytosol is about 55 
                     &mu;M).
                     The growth-retarding effect of L-cysteine is more 
                     pronounced in a &Delta;<i>cyuA</i> strain compared to wild 
                     type |CITS: [27435271][28488255]|; a &Delta;<i>cyuA</i> 
                     strain grown in the presence of L-cysteine produces 
                     significantly less hydrogen sulfide than wild type, 
                     possibly due to adventitious desulfidation by other enzymes
                     such as tryptophanase (TnaA) and O-acetylserine 
                     sulfhydrylases |CITS: [27435271][35377168]|. Under 
                     anaerobic conditions, a &Delta;<i>cyuA</i> mutant shows 
                     little cysteine-dependent production of sulfide, and growth
                     is inhibited by added cysteine |CITS: [28607157]|. However,
                     successful outgrowth occurs after a long lag (40 hr), 
                     indicating that cysteine can serve as a sole carbon source;
                     cysteine is also efficiently used as a sole nitrogen source
                     |CITS: [35377168]|.
                     <i>cyuPA</i> is transcribed as a bicistronic mRNA; 
                     transcription is activated by |FRAME: G6247-MONOMER 
                     ""CyuR""| in the presence of high concentrations of 
                     L-cysteine (up to 5mM final concentration) but not other 
                     amino acids |CITS: [27435271][35377168]|. Transcription of 
                     <i>cyuPA</i> is significantly higher under anaerobic 
                     conditions. D-cysteine activates <i>cyuPA</i> expression at
                     ~1/3 the level of L-cysteine; several other 
                     sulfur-containing compounds also activate expression to 
                     some degree, potentially by affecting the intracellular 
                     levels of cysteine |CITS: [28607157]|. Expression of the 
                     <i>cyuPA</i> operon is significantly reduced in a 
                     &Delta;<i>crp</i> mutant |CITS: [35377168]|.
                     CyuA: ""<b>cy</b>steine <b>u</b>tilization <b>A</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [28607157]|"
