                     /product="bifunctional glycerate 2-kinase 1 / glycerate
                     2-kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.165"
                     /label=garK
                     /note="Glycerate kinase I (GKI), encoded by <i>garK</i>, 
                     catalyzes the formation of 2-phosphoglycerate from 
                     D-glycerate |CITS: [9772162][18675808]|.  Recent <i>in 
                     vivo</i> metabolic assays also showed that the product is 
                     2-phosphoglycerate |CITS: [25823014]|.
                     There are two glycerate kinases, known as GKI and GKII, in 
                     <i>E. coli</i>.  GKI is more thermostable and has a broader
                     pH optimum than GKII, and GKII activity is only induced by 
                     growth on glycolate |CITS: [4887503]|. Glycerate kinase I 
                     is induced by growth on glycolate, glucarate, glycerate, 
                     and galactarate as the carbon source and is not sensitive 
                     to catabolite repression |CITS: [4887503][10762278]|."
