     gene            3270696..3271160
                     /gene="yhaV"
                     /locus_tag="b3130"
                     /label=yhaV
     CDS             3270696..3271160
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yhaV"
                     /locus_tag="b3130"
                     /product="trifunctional ribosome-dependent mRNA
                     interferase toxin YhaV / ribonuclease / YhaV-PrlF 
                     toxin-antitoxin complex"
                     /EC_number="3.1.26.1"
                     /label=yhaV
                     /note="YhaV is a BECR-fold toxin in the RelE/ParE
                     superfamily of type II toxin-antitoxin systems |CITS: 
                     [39998207]| and the ribonuclease toxin component of the 
                     YhaV-PrlF toxin-antitoxin system |CITS: [17706670]|. The 
                     ribonuclease activity of YhaV is dependent on the presence 
                     of the ribosome |CITS: [28573789]|. YhaV associates with 
                     the 70S ribosome and the 50S ribosomal subunit in a 
                     ribosomal profiling assay |CITS: [28573789]|. Residues 
                     131-145 at the C terminus of YhaV are involved in its mRNA 
                     interferase activity |CITS: [29642286]|.
                     An analysis of the targets and site specificity of YhaV 
                     showed some cleavage site specificity, favoring a G 
                     immediately downstream of the cleavage site, a preference 
                     for cleavage in the 5' end of mRNAs, and high codon 
                     position preference. Like other <i>E. coli</i> 
                     endoribonuclease toxins, YhaV activity does not affect 
                     mature ribosomes, but inhibits ribosome biogenesis, likely 
                     by affecting the translation of a specific set of ribosomal
                     proteins |CITS: [34544284]|.
                     Expression of YhaV is translationally coupled to expression
                     of PrlF. Overexpression of <i>yhaV</i> alone leads to 
                     growth arrest; coexpression of <i>prlF</i> suppresses the 
                     growth arrest |CITS: [17706670]|. Mutations in <i>yhaV</i> 
                     appeared in an evolution experiment selecting for growth 
                     under nutrient-, nitrogen- and magnesium-limited conditions
                     |CITS: [29776341]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [22060041][31932311]|"
                     /translation="MDFPQRVNGWALYAHPCFQETYDALVAEVETLKGKDPENYQRKAA
