     gene            3286759..3288795
                     /gene="lpoA"
                     /locus_tag="b3147"
                     /label=lpoA
     CDS             3286759..3288795
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lpoA"
                     /locus_tag="b3147"
                     /product="outer membrane lipoprotein - activator of MrcA
                     activity"
                     /label=lpoA
                     /note="LpoA is an outer membrane lipoprotein that forms a 
                     complex with, and is essential for the <i>in vivo</i> 
                     function of, |FRAME: CPLX0-7717 ""penicillin binding 
                     protein 1A (PBP1A)""| |CITS: [21183074]|. LpoA stimulates 
                     the transpeptidase activity of PBP1A <i>in vitro</i> |CITS:
                     [21183073]| (see also |CITS: [24341982]|). LpoA stimulates 
                     the glycosyltransferase activity of PB1A <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [34429361]|.
                     An <i>lpoA<sup>-</sup></i> mutant is synthetically lethal 
                     in a PBP1B defective strain and this phenotype can be 
                     corrected by the expression of <i>lpoA</i> from a plasmid 
                     |CITS: [21183074]|. An 
                     <i>lpoA<sup>-</sup>lpoB<sup>-</sup></i> mutant displays a 
                     similar terminal phenotype to PBP1A<sup>-</sup> 
                     PBP1B<sup>-</sup> cells |CITS: [21183074]|. Peptidoglycan 
                     isolated from an <i>lpoA<sup>-</sup>lpoB<sup>-</sup></i> 
                     mutant prior to lysis shows a substantial decrease in 
                     peptidoglycan  cross-linking and and an increase in 
                     pentapeptide containing  muropeptides - a similar 
                     biochemistry is observed in the peptidoglycan of 
                     PBP1A<sup>-</sup> PBP1B<sup>-</sup> cells |CITS: 
                     [21183074]|. Pull-down assays using tagged LpoA indicate 
                     that it specifically associates with PBP1A; an <i>in 
                     vivo</i> stoichiometry of 1:1 has been estimated |CITS: 
                     [21183074]|.
                     LpoA localises independently of PBP1A but like PBP1A, it 
                     concentrates at the sidewall of elongating cells |CITS: 
                     [21183073]|.
                     LpoA consists of an N-terminal, predominantly helical 
                     domain, that adopts a tetratricopeptide repeat (TPR) like 
                     fold and a C-terminal domain which is responsible for 
                     binding and activation of PBP1A |CITS: [24954617]|. A 
                     partial redundancy of function between the N-terminal 
                     domain of LpoA and the |FRAME: EG12854-MONOMER ""CpoB""| 
                     protein has been reported |CITS: [25951518]|. LpoA has an 
                     extended conformation with a maximum predicted length of 
                     147 &Aring; |CITS: [24954617]|.
                     Comment: |CITS: [21183069]|
                     "
