     gene            3292333..3292851
                     /gene="yhbO"
                     /locus_tag="b3153"
                     /label=yhbO
     CDS             3292333..3292851
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yhbO"
                     /locus_tag="b3153"
                     /product="trifunctional protein/nucleic acid deglycase 2 /
                     protein deglycase / glyoxalase III"
                     /EC_number="4.2.1.130"
                     /label=yhbO
                     /note="YhbO has been identified as a protein deglycase
                     that repairs glyoxal- and methylglyoxal-glycated proteins 
                     |CITS: [26774339]| as well as a nucleotide deglycase that 
                     repairs glycated GTP as well as DNA and RNA |CITS: 
                     [28596309]|.  YhbO is able to reduce the formation of 
                     acrylamide in mixtures of glyoxal/methylglyoxal and 
                     asparagine, likely by degrading Maillard adducts |CITS: 
                     [27530919]|.
                     YhbO belongs to the diverse DJ-1/PfpI protein family |CITS:
                     [17015655]|.  It is homologous to a general stress protein 
                     from <i>Bacillus subtilis</i> |CITS: [16380269]| and shares
                     predicted functional site residues with an intracellular 
                     protease from <i>Pyrococcus furiosus</i> |CITS: 
                     [17257049]|.  However, the purified protein does not have 
                     any detectable protease activity using classical 
                     biochemical substrates |CITS: [16380269]|.  YhbO does not 
                     affect the degradation of damaged proteins and peptides in 
                     cells under oxidative stress |CITS: [17933887]|. Glyoxalase
                     III activity with both glyoxal and methylglyoxal has been 
                     measured |CITS: [26678554]|.  The glyoxalase activity may 
                     in fact reflect the protein deglycase activity of the 
                     enzyme |CITS: [26774339]|.
                     The purified protein exists as a monomer, trimer and 
                     hexamer in solution |CITS: [16380269]|.  The native protein
                     may be a homohexamer |CITS: [22228183]|.  The C104 residue 
                     is required for its function in the oxidative stress 
                     response |CITS: [17933887]|.
                     Abundance of YhbO is increased under osmotic stress imposed
                     by NaCl only under aerobic conditions |CITS: [17015655]|.  
                     Expression of <i>yhbO</i> is increased in stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [15716429]|.  <i>yhbO</i> belongs to the 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> regulon |CITS: [15716429]|, and it may 
                     be part of the Rcs regulon |CITS: [13129944]|.
                     A <i>yhbO</i> null mutant is sensitive to oxidative, 
                     temperature, UV, and pH stresses |CITS: [17933887]|.  A 
                     <i>yhbO</i> null mutant contains increased levels of 
                     glycated proteins |CITS: [26774339]| and nucleotides |CITS:
                     [28596309]| and shows somewhat decreased viability in 
                     methylglyoxal- or glucose-containing media, whereas a 
                     triple <i>hchA yhbO yajL</i> mutant shows significantly 
                     decreased viability |CITS: [26774339]|.  A <i>yhbO</i> 
                     mutant as well as an <i>hchA yhbO yajL</i> triple mutant 
                     accumulates glycated DNA and RNA and shows increased 
                     mutation frequency, with increased levels of GC&rarr;AT and
                     AT&rarr;GC transitions |CITS: [28596309]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [29147900][29147907]|"
