     gene            3304542..3304757
                     /gene="sraG"
                     /locus_tag="b4449"
                     /label=sraG
     misc_RNA        3304542..3304757
                     /gene="sraG"
                     /locus_tag="b4449"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA SraG"
                     /note="SraG is a cis-encoded antisense RNA that regulates
                     the expression of |FRAME: EG10743 <i>pnp</i>| at the 
                     posttranscriptional level by destabilizing the <i>pnp</i> 
                     mRNA and inhibiting its translation |CITS: [27495318]|. 
                     SraG was initially discovered as a small RNA of 
                     approximately 146-174 nt in length that undergoes 3' end 
                     trimming and polyadenylation |CITS: [11448770]|.  The 
                     primary transcript was shown to be 216 nt long; a 190 nt 
                     long SraG RNA population is generated by maturation at the 
                     5' or 3' end |CITS: [27495318]|.
                     <i>sraG</i> expression increases during late log phase and 
                     under temperature stress conditions |CITS: [11448770]| and 
                     appears to be induced by the stringent response |CITS: 
                     [27495318]|.  RNase III and RNase E are involved in 
                     degradation of SraG; translation of <i>pnp</i> appears to 
                     affect processing of SraG |CITS: [27495318]|.
                     SraG: ""<b>s</b>mall <b>R</b>N<b>A</b> <b>G</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [11448770]|<br>
                     PsrA: ""predicted small RNA"" |CITS: [11448770]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12762038][12654996][32213244]|"
     gene            complement(3304774..3305043)
                     /gene="rpsO"
                     /locus_tag="b3165"
                     /label=rpsO
     CDS             complement(3304774..3305043)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpsO"
                     /locus_tag="b3165"
                     /product="trifunctional 30S ribosomal subunit protein S15
                     / 30S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rpsO
                     /note="The S15 protein is a component of the 30S subunit
                     of the ribosome and also functions in the 
                     post-transcriptional regulation of its own expression.
                     The S15 protein binds to 16S rRNA in the absence of other 
                     ribosomal proteins |CITS: [4556459][4606155][1091486][1091 
                     919][7029472][6208366][8903343]|. Nucleotides essential for
                     the S15-16S rRNA interaction have been determined by 
                     mutagenesis |CITS: [6208367][11162092]| and nuclease 
                     protection |CITS: [3279390][2453025]|. S15 appears to 
                     depend on S4 for assembly |CITS: [2461734]|. Binding of S8 
                     to 16S rRNA influences the central domain organization and 
                     affects the rRNA environment of S15 |CITS: [12823975]|.
                     In addition to its function in the ribosome, the ribosomal 
                     protein S15 binds to its own mRNA, stabilizing a pseudoknot
                     secondary structure and impeding translation initiation 
                     |CITS: [2407854][2407855][2207162][9482926]|. <i>rpsO</i> 
                     mRNA and 16S rRNA compete for binding to S15 |CITS: 
                     [8041615]|; formation of a pseudoknot structure in the 
                     <i>rpsO</i> mRNA appears to be required for S15 binding 
                     |CITS: [8041615][7830558][7532857]|. Common structural 
                     determinants between the mRNA and rRNA binding sites have 
                     been investigated, showing that there is limited similarity
                     between the two targets |CITS: [12126618]|. Site-directed 
                     mutagenesis of S15 to reveal amino acids that interact with
                     RNA revealed a number of residues that are required for 
                     interaction with both the rRNA and mRNA, while others are 
                     specific for mRNA recognition |CITS: [15101974]|.
                     S15 appears to prevent the formation of a functional 
                     ternary 30S-mRNA-tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> complex, trapping the 
                     ribosome in a preinitiation complex |CITS: [7685101]|. 
                     Cryo-EM structures of S15-bound and free <i>rpsO</i> mRNA 
                     show that while S15 is bound, the start codon is buried in 
                     the pseudoknot structure and therefore inaccessible to the 
                     initiator tRNA |CITS: [17889647]|.
                     Although <i>rpsO</i> was thought to be essential for 
                     viability, it is possible to construct a strain with a 
                     precise deletion of the <i>rpsO</i> ORF |CITS: 
                     [16682557][17277072]|. The &Delta;<i>rpsO</i> strain has a 
                     significantly increased doubling time even in rich media 
                     and exhibits cold sensitivity |CITS: [16682557]|. A 
                     mutation in <i>rpsO</i> (<i>secC</i>) suppresses a 
                     <i>secA</i>(Ts) allele |CITS: [6088066]|. S15 appears to be
                     required for the optimal synthesis of lipoprotein |CITS: 
                     [2842297]|. The <i>suhD</i> allele of <i>rpsO</i> is a 
                     suppressor of the <i>rpoH11</i> mutation and leads to 
                     reduced levels of S15 in ribosomes |CITS: [2646293]|. 
                     Regulation of transcription, processing and degradation of 
                     <i>rpsO</i> mRNA have been studied extensively |CITS: [300 
                     5122][2414737][3007765][2185470][2085545][1704067][7519147]
                     [7732015][8915531][8830280][8670815][9707438][10047480][105
                     94833][10677490][10884409][10943897][12051911][12853618][14
                     622415][14654705][15885870][19103951][20167073][21685451]|.
                      RpsO: |CITS: [378941]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8722030][12768202][12787346][17194931]|, 
                     Comment: |CITS: [17889642]|"
