     gene            complement(3305192..3306136)
                     /gene="truB"
                     /locus_tag="b3166"
                     /label=truB
     CDS             complement(3305192..3306136)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="truB"
                     /locus_tag="b3166"
                     /product="tRNA pseudouridine(55) synthase"
                     /EC_number="5.4.99.25"
                     /label=truB
                     /note="TruB is a pseudouridine synthase that catalyzes the 
                     pseudouridylation of U55 in tRNA |CITS: [7489483]|.  The 
                     base modification generated by TruB activity contributes to
                     the wild-type thermal stability of tRNA |CITS: 
                     [12218021][12427942]|.  In addition to its catalytic 
                     activity, TruB also acts as a tRNA chaperone |CITS: 
                     [27849601]|; early mutagenesis results had already 
                     suggested such a role |CITS: [11142385]|.
                     tRNAs carry multiple modifications, often in nearby 
                     nucleotide residues.  The preferred substrate for the TruB 
                     methyltransferase and tRNA binding activities is unmodified
                     tRNA |CITS: [32385137]|.
                     Crystal structures of TruB have been solved |CITS: 
                     [11779468][14566049][15987897]|.  TruB recognizes the 
                     folded T-loop structure of tRNAs |CITS: [11779468]| and 
                     undergoes significant conformational changes upon tRNA 
                     binding |CITS: [14566049]|.  Recognition of the tRNA 
                     substrate requires the 53:61 base pair of the T-loop stem 
                     as well as several conserved nucleotides within the loop 
                     |CITS: [9425055]|.  TruB binds both folded and misfolded 
                     tRNAs |CITS: [27849601]|.
                     The substrate specificity of TruB has been described |CITS:
                     [7489483]|.  Site-directed mutagenesis of the conserved 
                     amino acid residue D48 |CITS: [10428788]| and Y76 |CITS: 
                     [16300397]| yields catalytically inactive enzymes.  
                     Oxidation of the D48C mutant (generating the sulfinic acid)
                     restores significant activity |CITS: [15581587]|.  In 
                     addition to the catalytic D48 residue, R181 and D90 form an
                     electrostatic network that fine-tunes the D48-R181 
                     interaction |CITS: [24371284]|.  Mutagenesis of nearly 
                     invariant lysine and proline residues in Motif I does not 
                     have severe effects on catalytic activity, but decreases 
                     thermal stability |CITS: [10924141]|.  The reaction does 
                     not appear to use a catalytic cysteine |CITS: [10529181]|. 
                     The U55 substrate base is ""flipped out"" into the enzyme 
                     during catalysis |CITS: [11779468]|. Unlike RluA, TruB 
                     activity is not inhibited by RNA containing 5-fluorouridine
                     |CITS: [14730018]|.  The reaction proceeds through 
                     formation of a glycal intermediate by deprotonation of C2' 
                     |CITS: [27292228]|. Pre-steady-state kinetic analysis 
                     showed that tRNA binding is a fast two-step process; 
                     catalysis is the rate-limiting step |CITS: [21998096]|.
                     A <i>truB</i> deletion mutant lacks the &Psi;55 
                     modification in tRNAs |CITS: [11142385]| and exhibits a 
                     competitive disadvantage in a mixed culture.  The growth 
                     phenotypes of the <i>truB</i> deletion mutant can be 
                     complemented by production of the catalytically inactive 
                     D48C mutant protein |CITS: [11142385][12427942]|, but not 
                     by the K64E mutant protein that lacks tRNA binding activity
                     |CITS: [27849601]|.  Conversely, the mutant's sensitivity 
                     to heat shock can only be complemented by the wild type 
                     gene.  A <i>trmA truB</i> double mutant exhibits more 
                     severe sensitivity to heat shock than the single 
                     <i>truB</i> mutant |CITS: [12427942]|.
                     Lack of tRNA &Psi;55 modification does not affect the 
                     frequency of -1 frameshift errors during translation |CITS:
                     [12756333]|.
                     TruB: ""<b>tR</b>NA pseudo <b>U</b> formation"" |CITS: 
                     [7489483]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17113994][17085441][25616362]|"
