     gene            complement(3306136..3306537)
                     /gene="rbfA"
                     /locus_tag="b3167"
                     /label=rbfA
     CDS             complement(3306136..3306537)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rbfA"
                     /locus_tag="b3167"
                     /product="30S ribosome binding factor"
                     /label=rbfA
                     /note="RbfA is a type II KH-domain protein that is
                     associated with the 30S subunit of the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [7535280][12628255]| and is required for efficient 
                     processing of the 16S rRNA |CITS: [9422595]| and 30S 
                     subunit assembly |CITS: [23611982]|. Release of RbfA from 
                     the 30S subunit appears to be a checkpoint for its maturity
                     |CITS: [31728529]|.
                     In the absence of RbfA, pre-30S complexes accumulate. These
                     complexes show pervasive structural differences compared to
                     mature 30S subunits and lack the ribosomal subunits S2, S3,
                     and S21 |CITS: [23611982]|. Following cold shock, a larger 
                     fraction of 30S ribosomal subunits contain RbfA |CITS: 
                     [12963368]|.
                     The GTPase |FRAME: G7841-MONOMER RsgA| was found to enhance
                     the release of RbfA from the mature 30S subunit in a 
                     GTP-dependent manner; the activity does not require GTP 
                     hydrolysis |CITS: [21102555]|. A cryo-EM structure of the 
                     30S subunit from a &Delta;<i>rbfA</i> &Delta;<i>rsgA</i> 
                     double mutant has been determined |CITS: [24671761]|. The 
                     translation initiation factor |FRAME: EG10506-MONOMER IF-3|
                     is also able to release RbfA from mature 30S subunits 
                     |CITS: [31728529]|. Methylation of the 16S rRNA helix h45 
                     by |FRAME: EG10523-MONOMER RsmA| is important for release 
                     of RbfA from the 30S subunit by both IF-3 and RsgA |CITS: 
                     [31728529]|. Like RsgA, the |FRAME: EG12098-MONOMER| 
                     interferes with the release of RbfA from the |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3953| when RluD has an E265K point mutation compared 
                     to the wild-type |CITS: [35439479]|.
                     A solution structure of the C-terminally truncated 
                     RbfA&Delta;25 variant has been determined, and a potential 
                     RNA-binding site was identified |CITS: 
                     [11824762][12628255][22389115]|. NMR resonance assignments 
                     for full length RbfA have been reported |CITS: [32671633]|.
                     Cells lacking RbfA show a cold-sensitive growth phenotype 
                     |CITS: [7535280]| and constitutive induction of the cold 
                     shock response |CITS: [8898389]|. The cold-sensitive growth
                     phenotype can be partially suppressed by overexpression of 
                     |FRAME: EG10270-MONOMER Era| |CITS: [12753192]|. Lack of 
                     RbfA affects the kinetics of translation initiation |CITS: 
                     [31586395]|. Overexpression of RbfA allows for suppression 
                     of the dominant cold-sensitive C23U mutation in 16S rRNA 
                     |CITS: [7535280]| and suppresses the slow growth rate of a 
                     <i>rimM</i> deletion mutant |CITS: [9422595][11567010]|. 
                     Several point mutations in <i>rbfA</i> suppress the growth 
                     defect of an <i>rsgA</i> deletion mutant and restore 
                     ribosome maturation |CITS: [21102555]|. An <i>rbfA</i> 
                     deletion mutant is more sensitive to the alkylating agent 
                     methyl methanesulfonate (MMS) than wild type |CITS: 
                     [18824089]|. In a &Delta;<i>rsmA</i> strain, overexpression
                     of RbfA leads to a severe growth defect and accumulation of
                     17S rRNA precursors |CITS: [23387871]|.
                     Cold shock induction of <i>rbfA</i> expression may occur 
                     through transcription antitermination mediated by CspA and 
                     other cold shock-induced proteins |CITS: [10884409]|. 
                     Studies into the toxicity of an anticonvulsant drug 
                     Lamotrigine (Ltg) on |FRAME: EG10505-MONOMER| revealed that
                     Ltg also causes accumulation of RbfA |CITS: [37676049]|.
                     RbfA: ""<b>r</b>ibosome-<b>b</b>inding <b>f</b>actor 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [7535280]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [21529161][15199224]|"
