     gene            complement(3306701..3309373)
                     /gene="infB"
                     /locus_tag="b3168"
                     /label=infB
     CDS             complement(3306701..3309373)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="infB"
                     /locus_tag="b3168"
                     /product="translation initiation factor IF-2"
                     /label=infB
                     /note="IF2 is one of three translation initiation factors
                     in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [4314905][4876939][4872321]|; 
                     together with IF1, it ensures the correct binding of 
                     fMet-tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> in the ribosomal P site |CITS: 
                     [4943554][16857585]|. IF2 together with the 50S subunit 
                     stabilizes the mRNA in the correct reading frame in the 
                     late initiation complex |CITS: [22411833]| and stabilizes 
                     the assembled 70S complex |CITS: [22722848]|.
                     The kinetically favored assembly pathway for the 30S 
                     preinitiation complex has been determined |CITS: 
                     [22562136]|. The order of assembly depends on the relative 
                     concentration of tRNA and initiation factors |CITS: 
                     [22722848]|. A cryo-EM structure of IF2 and charged 
                     initiator tRNA within the ribosome has been solved at 3.8 
                     &Aring; resolution, providing the foundation for the 
                     mechanistic understanding of translation initiation |CITS: 
                     [26973877]|. IF2 facilitates recruitment of initiator tRNA 
                     to the 30S initiation complex |CITS: [20224578]|. 
                     Observation of the IF2 conformational switch within the 30S
                     initiation complex led to a model of IF2 activation 
                     involving allosteric regulation by domain III of IF2 |CITS:
                     [29133802]|. A kinetic model of the formation of the 70S 
                     initiation complex has been generated |CITS: [26338773]|, 
                     and the formation of the translation-competent 70S ribosome
                     was investigated by time-resolved cryo-EM |CITS: 
                     [31108498]|.
                     Kinetic data show that IF2 is the primary factor 
                     responsible for recognition of the formyl group of 
                     fMet-tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> |CITS: 
                     [1104625][776966][16857585]|. IF2 also stimulates ternary 
                     complex formation of leaderless mRNAs with the 30S subunit 
                     |CITS: [10921890][11334885]| and is required for efficient 
                     translation reinitiation at the downstream open reading 
                     frame in a translationally coupled operon |CITS: 
                     [18221266]|. IF2 interacts with the 30S ribosomal subunit 
                     at two distinct sites |CITS: [16935296]|. IF2 interacts 
                     with the C-terminal domain of |FRAME: CPLX0-3955| |CITS: 
                     [4587128][17070545]|; specific point mutations were 
                     introduced to identify the residues in IF2 that are 
                     involved |CITS: [29686090]|. The interaction is important 
                     for IF2-mediated subunit association |CITS: [22102582]|; 
                     L7/L12 is required for stable binding of IF2 and GTP 
                     hydrolysis |CITS: [28342293]|. Changes in the stability of 
                     IF2 as well as the dynamics of the interaction between IF2 
                     and tRNA appear to regulate subunit joining |CITS: 
                     [25596426]|. During subunit joining, IF2 stabilizes the L1 
                     stalk domain and positions the ribosomal subunits in a 
                     semirotated orientation |CITS: [26668356]|.
                     IF2 is a large protein comprising four conserved domains: 
                     the N-terminal G domain (domain I), followed by an 
                     EF-Tu-type &beta; barrel (domain II), an 
                     &alpha;/&beta;/&alpha; sandwich (domain III), followed by 
                     an &alpha; helix that connects to a second EF-Tu-type 
                     &beta; barrel (domain IV) |CITS: [11114334]|. |CITS: 
                     [26259514]| divides the protein into N1, N2, G1, G2, G3, 
                     C1, and C2 domains. Therefore, please note that, unlike the
                     protein itself, the domain nomenclature is highly 
                     non-conserved.
                     IF2 has ribosome-dependent GTPase activity |CITS: 
                     [5246542][4337107]| with approximately 10 times higher 
                     affinity for GDP than for GTP |CITS: [3894350]|. Mutations 
                     in several amino acid residues in the GTP-binding domain 
                     render IF2 nonfunctional or cold-sensitive, suggesting that
                     GTP hydrolysis is important for IF2 function |CITS: 
                     [8170391]|[12559921]|. An E571K mutant lacks GTPase 
                     activity and has reduced affinity for the 30S subunit, but 
                     supports viability <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [22723375]|. GTP 
                     hydrolysis occurs during the formation of the 70S 
                     initiation complex |CITS: [10037688][10790378]| and has 
                     been implicated in release of IF2 from the 70S ribosome 
                     |CITS: [4909915][4566451][10037688][14532131]|. GTP-bound 
                     IF2 is required for mRNA restructuring during formation of 
                     the 30S preinitiation complex |CITS: [16600874]|. GTP 
                     hydrolysis and departure of IF2 from the ribosome gates the
                     transition to translation elongation |CITS: [22722848]|. 
                     The alarmone ppGpp can bind to IF2 at the same site as GTP 
                     and interferes with translation initiation |CITS: 
                     [16968770]|. The effect of ppGpp varies depending on the 
                     presence of a structured enhancer of translation initiation
                     (SETI) element near the translation initiation site of 
                     specific mRNAs |CITS: [31995552]|.
                     The N-terminal region of IF2 is required for optimal 
                     interaction with the 30S and 50S ribosomal subunits |CITS: 
                     [10428486]|. A solution structure of the N-terminal domain 
                     I of IF2 (IF2N) has been solved |CITS: [12600987]|. A 
                     flexible linker region connects this domain to the 
                     conserved C-terminal domains of IF2 |CITS: [14691238]|. A 
                     cryo-EM reconstruction of the initiation complex shows a 
                     conformation of IF2 that is different from that seen in the
                     crystal structure of the <i>Methanobacterium 
                     thermoautotrophicum</i> protein aIF5B |CITS: [15935757]|. 
                     The solution structure of the four C-terminal domains of 
                     IF2 also shows structural differences between IF2 and aIF5B
                     |CITS: [18442259]|. Addition of GDP or a non-hydrolyzable 
                     analog of GTP causes a structural transition |CITS: 
                     [21988058]|. Cryo-EM reconstruction of the 30S translation 
                     initiation complex shows that IF2 contacts the 16S rRNA, 
                     IF1, and S12 |CITS: [21750663]|. IF2 and EF-G appear to 
                     compete for the same binding site on the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [12051934]|.
                     IF2 exists in three isoforms, IF2&alpha; (IF2-1), IF2&beta;
                     (IF2-2), and IF2&beta;' (IF2&gamma;, IF2-3) |CITS: 
                     [4567787][1764105][2110058][3894004][1374802]|, which are 
                     generated by the use of alternative in-frame translation 
                     initiation codons: IF2&alpha; is the full-length protein, 
                     GUG at position 158 is used as the initiation codon for 
                     IF2&beta; |CITS: [7575538][30904393]|, and AUG at position 
                     165 is used for IF2&beta;' |CITS: [7575538]|. Both the 
                     &alpha; and &beta; forms are required for optimal growth; 
                     the isoforms thus have acquired specialized, although not 
                     essential functions |CITS: [1374802]|. IF2&beta; was 
                     identified as the host factor MRF&alpha;-PR required for 
                     phage Mu replication |CITS: [18028309]|. The full-length 
                     isoform functions during replication in the presence of DNA
                     damaging agents and acts as a novel facilitator of RecA's 
                     function related to repair of two-ended DNA double-strand 
                     breaks |CITS: [22536160][35343794]|. The three IF2 isoforms
                     differentially affect the efficiency of synapsis between 
                     DNA molecules during homologous recombination |CITS: 
                     [35343793]|, and their influence on the replication restart
                     system depends on the levels of (p)ppGpp |CITS: 
                     [24612328]|.
                     IF2 was shown to have chaperone-like function |CITS: 
                     [10625618]| and plays a role in ribosome maturation during 
                     adaptation to cold stress |CITS: [30916323]|. IF2 levels 
                     are increased during cold stress |CITS: 
                     [30916323][30916329]| due to increased transcription, 
                     increased mRNA half life, and increased translation 
                     efficiency |CITS: [30916329]|. IF2 levels are increased 
                     under osmotic stress conditions |CITS: [17015655]|. 
                     <i>infB</i> is an essential gene |CITS: [1830345]|. The 
                     <i>ssyG</i> class of cold-sensitive mutations localizes to 
                     <i>infB</i> |CITS: [3539591]|. Several <i>infB</i> 
                     mutations result in cold-sensitive growth, including 
                     mutations in the GTP-binding domain |CITS: [12559921]|. The
                     <i>gicD1</i> allele, resulting in a V697I substitution in 
                     IF2, confers cold sensitive growth and suppresses 
                     <i>rpsL31</i>-mediated streptomycin resistance at 15&deg;C 
                     |CITS: [19716413]|. The anticonvulsant drug lamotrigine 
                     (Ltg) induces cold sensitive growth inhibition in <i>E. 
                     coli</i>; suppressor mutations map exclusively to domain II
                     of IF2 |CITS: [25233066]|. Further studies into the 
                     toxicity of Ltg have been carried out |CITS: [37676049]|. 
                     Overexpression of IF2 in conjunction with initiator tRNA 
                     can substitute for |FRAME: G7841-MONOMER| when deleted 
                     |CITS: [38837341]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [1805969][8955898][10937868][11489118][123 
                     70015][11849551][15755955][MECHULAM11][26259514]|"
