     CDS             complement(3309398..3310885)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nusA"
                     /locus_tag="b3169"
                     /product="transcription termination/antitermination
                     protein NusA"
                     /label=nusA
                     /note="Transcription termination/antitermination L factor
                     (NusA) is a key component in both prevention and 
                     enhancement of transcriptional termination. It is important
                     in both Rho-dependent and intrinsic termination, as well as
                     in lambda and other phage antitermination systems.
                     NusA was originally identified based on its involvement in 
                     antitermination in lambda phage, as reviewed in |CITS: 
                     [1400223]|.
                     NusA is also involved in transcriptional antitermination in
                     the cell |CITS: [6265784][6154941]|. It has been shown to 
                     specifically aid in readthrough of the RNA polymerase genes
                     <i>rpoB</i> and <i>rpoC</i>, as well as in successful 
                     synthesis of the ribosomal RNA genes |CITS: 
                     [1370474][8430111][9139668][15716433]|.
                     In addition to its antitermination role, NusA is required 
                     for both Rho-dependent and intrinsic transcriptional 
                     termination. NusA is required for Rho-dependent termination
                     in lambda phage and in the cell |CITS: [6265785][2821282]|.
                     It is also frequently involved in intrinsic termination and
                     the inhibition of RNA elongation |CITS: 
                     [6758952][1285127][7844821][9139668][12711396][6199039]|. 
                     Though NusA interacts with all three subunits of RNA 
                     polymerase, its termination activity primarily depends on 
                     its interaction with the carboxy-terminus of RpoA |CITS: 
                     [1847365][8757401][8598198]|. The presence of NusA induces 
                     a conformational change in RNA polymerase prevents RNA 
                     interaction with RpoA |CITS: [7510675][7539140][7536848]|. 
                     This binding in turn activates NusA, allowing it to bind 
                     RNA and promote formation of hairpins at intrinsic 
                     termination sites |CITS: [11040219][11719185]|.
                     NusA interacts with many components of the transcriptional 
                     machinery, in addition to those interactions noted above. 
                     NusA binds Rho, and competes with sigma70 for binding to 
                     the core RNA polymerase complex |CITS: [6096352][1856861]|.
                      It does not compete with NusG for binding to either Rho or
                     the polymerase, despite modulating the same process as NusG
                     in both cases |CITS: [9571053]|.
                     NusA stimulates and stabilizes hairpin structures for 
                     pausing transcription of the thiC riboswitch,  and in this 
                     way it facilities the binding of the metabolite in the 
                     riboswitch |CITS:[31081713][35552496]|.
                     NusA contains two homology regions, S1 and KH, that are 
                     important for its activity, as well as two RNA polymerase 
                     binding regions |CITS: [10564494]|. NusA is monomeric 
                     |CITS: [1856862]|. The carboxy-terminus of NusA has been 
                     examined via NMR |CITS: [14755165][15987884]|.
                     In <i>nusA</i> mutants, total cellular RNA abundance drops 
                     due to increased termination |CITS: [6187334]|.
                     NusA downregulates its own expression, which generally 
                     mirrors the expression levels of cellular sigma factors 
                     |CITS: [2998933][2987884][3035333]|.
                     Cohen et al. (2009) suggested that NusA is directed by the 
                     translesion DNA synthesis activity of DinB to sites of 
                     active transcription. In turn, DinB might affect 
                     NusA-dependent transcription termination, which would 
                     provide another level of SOS control of gene expression 
                     (negative or positive) |CITS:[18996995]|.
                     A potential RNA G-quadruplex structure, formed by 
                     guanine-rich sequences located in the coding sequence 
                     region of the gene, was identified  for <i>nusA</i>. This 
                     structure could regulate the expression of the gene, as 
                     observed for <i>hemL</i> gene expression |CITS: 
                     [31964733]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [34570660][36154805]|"
