     gene            complement(3315431..3315517)
                     /gene="leuU"
                     /locus_tag="b3174"
                     /label=leuU
     tRNA            complement(3315431..3315517)
                     /gene="leuU"
                     /locus_tag="b3174"
                     /product="tRNA-Leu(GAG)"
                     /label=leuU
                     /note="tRNA(leuU) is one of eight leucine tRNAs.
                     tRNAs are the adapters that allow synthesis of proteins 
                     from mRNAs.
                     Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome for
                     protein synthesis.
                     There, the tRNA recognizes an RNA codon with its own 
                     three-nucleotide
                     anticodon, thus allowing synthesis of a specific peptide 
                     based on an mRNA
                     template.
                     tRNAs are processed to their active, mature forms by RNA 
                     cleavage
                     and by modification of their bases. RNA cleavage consists 
                     of
                     removal of both 5' and 3' extensions in a multistep process
                     involving
                     many RNases |CITS: [11252717]|. RNases taking part in tRNA 
                     processing include |FRAME: CPLX0-3461|,
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3601|, |FRAME: EG10858-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: EG11620-MONOMER|, and |FRAME: CPLX0-3602|. tRNAs 
                     are also subject to a
                     wide variety of base modifications catalyzed by proteins 
                     such as
                     |FRAME: EG11932-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10595-MONOMER|, |FRAME:
                     G6364-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11344-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7199-MONOMER|, |FRAME: CPLX0-1101|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11779-MONOMER|, |FRAME: G7422-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7449-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11177-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10454-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10967-MONOMER|,
                     and |FRAME: EG11022-MONOMER|.
                     Mature tRNAs are linked via a 3' CCA sequence to their 
                     cognate
                     amino acid in an ATP-dependent fashion by the appropriate 
                     amino-acid-tRNA synthetase, as shown in the |FRAME: 
                     TRNA-CHARGING-PWY|.
                     Subsequently, these charged tRNAs interact with the 
                     ribosome
                     and template mRNA to generate polypeptides. The discovery 
                     of the
                     role of tRNA in protein synthesis is reviewed in detail in 
                     |CITS: [7033244]|.
                     tRNA(leuU) is the sole translating tRNA for the the leucine
                     CUC codon |CITS: [15951035]|.
                     Along with tRNA(leuW), it is one of two tRNAs for the 
                     leucine CUU codon |CITS: [15951035][16236318]|."
     gene            complement(3315532..3315864)
                     /gene="secG"
                     /locus_tag="b3175"
                     /label=secG
     CDS             complement(3315532..3315864)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="secG"
                     /locus_tag="b3175"
                     /product="multifunctional Sec translocon subunit SecG /
                     SecYEG translocon / SecYEG-SecA complex / Sec 
                     Holo-Translocon"
                     /label=secG
                     /note="The SecG inner membrane protein is a component of
                     the heterotrimeric SecYEG preprotein translocase. SecY, 
                     SecE and an unidentified polypeptide termed 'band1' (later 
                     shown to be SecG |CITS: [8034620]|) form a stable complex 
                     which supports precursor protein translocation <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [2167176][1531482]|.
                     SecG stimulates translocation of a model secretory protein 
                     in SecYE containing proteoliposomes |CITS: [8253068]|. SecG
                     is not required for SecA binding to SecYE containing 
                     proteoliposomes; the translocation ATPase activity of SecA 
                     in SecYE containing proteoliposomes is enhanced by the 
                     presence of SecG |CITS: [8089132]|. SecG participates in 
                     preprotein signal sequence recognition |CITS: 
                     [7556084][9020118][11069681]|. SecG contributes to the 
                     stability of the translocase and to the interaction with 
                     signal sequence |CITS: [25404704]|. SecG stimulates SecA 
                     insertion at SecYE after the initiation of translocation 
                     has begun |CITS: [9184221]|. Two SecG molecules may be 
                     present in a single translocon |CITS: [10876167]|.
                     SecG and SecA may act cooperatively to facilitate 
                     preprotein translocation |CITS: [9811840][9701825]|. SecG 
                     undergoes topology inversion which may be coupled to the 
                     insertion-deinsertion cycle of SecA |CITS: 
                     [8620539][12359079][22074917]|. SecG interacts with SecA 
                     and this interaction depends on SecG topology inversion and
                     SecA membrane insertion |CITS: [12359079]|.  An inner 
                     membrane glycolipid, membrane protein integrase (|FRAME: 
                     CPD-15878 ""MPIase""|) has been implicated in the topology 
                     inversion of SecG |CITS: [23716687]|.
                     <i>prl</i> alleles of <i>secG</i> which suppress a variety 
                     of signal sequence mutations have been identified |CITS: 
                     [9020118]|. Suppressor alleles (<i>secG12, secG16</i>) 
                     which discriminate between two toxic signal sequences have 
                     been identified |CITS: [11069681]|. <i>secG</i> is not 
                     essential in sub-strain MG1655; a &Delta;<i>secG::kan</i> 
                     strain has no detectable growth defect and exhibits ony a 
                     mild defect in protein export at 20&deg;C |CITS: 
                     [10732681]|.
                     <i>secG</i>: <U>sec</U>retory<BR><i>prlH</i>: 
                     <U>pr</U>otein <U>l</U>ocalization
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [2202721]|"
