     gene            complement(3321598..3322074)
                     /gene="greA"
                     /locus_tag="b3181"
                     /label=greA
     CDS             complement(3321598..3322074)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="greA"
                     /locus_tag="b3181"
                     /product="transcription elongation factor GreA"
                     /label=greA
                     /note="GreA stimulates the mRNA cleavage activity of RNA 
                     polymerase |CITS: [7538676]|, which acts to release a 
                     polymerase complex that has stalled |CITS: 
                     [1384037][11118220]| or has incorporated an incorrect 
                     nucleotide |CITS: [8235608]|. GreA (and GreB) is also 
                     required for wild-type transcription of some regulatory 
                     regions within lambda phage |CITS: [11163202]|.
                     GreA stimulates shortening of the 3' end of the transcript 
                     by a dinucleotide, and the new 3' end is subject to 
                     elongation by RNA polymerase |CITS: [1384037]|. GreA 
                     decreases abortive initiation and increases productive 
                     initiation |CITS: [8071355][8524809]|. GreA activity 
                     requires that GreA associate with RNA polymerase before the
                     complex stalls |CITS: [8431948]|, or even before RNA 
                     transcription begins |CITS: [11380617]|. The transcription 
                     complex moves backward in conjunction with GreA activity 
                     |CITS: [8071355][8071356]|. GreA activity is influenced by 
                     multiple factors |CITS: [8071355][10860976]|.
                     GreA exhibits physical |CITS: [1384037]| and genetic |CITS:
                     [2243801]| interactions with RNA polymerase. The site of 
                     interaction on RNA polymerase has been examined |CITS: 
                     [10625443]|. The N-terminal coiled-coil domain, which 
                     includes a basic region, confers the activity |CITS: 
                     [9512710]| and determines the specificity with respect to 
                     the size of the cleaved fragment |CITS: [9054416]|. The 
                     basic region appears to maintain the interaction of the 
                     mRNA with the elongation complex |CITS: [10777576]|. The 
                     N-terminal domain is proximal to the mRNA within the 
                     transcription complex |CITS: [9054416]|. The C-terminal 
                     domain confers interaction with RNA polymerase |CITS: 
                     [9512710]|.
                     The crystal structure of GreA has been solved at at 2.2 A 
                     resolution |CITS: [7854424]|.
                     GreA has similarity to <i>E. coli</i> GreB |CITS: 
                     [8431948]|; GreB activity results in release of a longer 
                     mRNA fragment (9 nt) that released by GreA |CITS: 
                     [8431948]|. The interaction of GreB with RNA polymerase has
                     been characterized in detail |CITS: 
                     [9698562][10438515][12914698]|.  In the presence of GreA 
                     and GreB, the transcription rate in a cell-free expression 
                     system is increased, principally for strong promoters 
                     |CITS: [27186988]|.
                     GreA of <i>E. coli</i> has 65% identity to the 
                     <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> GreA |CITS: [9139926]|. GreA 
                     has similarity to proteins from <i>Neisseria 
                     meningitidis</i> and <i>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</i> |CITS: 
                     [9822829]|. GreA exhibits activity toward <i>Bacillus 
                     subtilis</i> RNA polymerase <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [11029421]|.
                     Based on overexpression, deletion, transcription profiling,
                     and RNA polymerase mutation experiments, mutual and 
                     antagonistic competition effects between GreA, GreB, and 
                     DksA on RNA polymerase activity were observed when ppGpp 
                     was absent, but they were more pronounced when DksA and 
                     ppGpp were not present |CITS: [22056927]|.
                     GreA/B factors contribute significantly to complex 
                     regulation by DksA and ppGpp. Here, GreA has a major role 
                     and GreB a minor role in the DksA-ppGpp regulatory network 
                     |CITS: [22056927]|.
                     An interaction network of GreA, GreB, DksA, ppGpp, and RNA 
                     polymerase has been proposed |CITS: [22056927]|.
                     The aspartic acid D41 is critical for the RNA cleavage 
                     reaction necessary for rescuing backtracked transcription 
                     elongation complexes |CITS: [22200485]|. On the other hand,
                     removal of a methylene group at the glutamic acid E44 
                     position (analogous to D74 in DksA), also located in the 
                     coiled-coil tip, resulted in a gain of function of both 
                     negative and positive control in each case |CITS: 
                     [22200485]|.
                     The absence of both Gre factors, GreA and GreB, greatly 
                     impairs the detoxification of nitric oxide (NO) and 
                     hydrogen peroxide (H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>) 
                     |CITS:[39248572]|. When these factors are absent, one 
                     portion of the cell population has difficulty synthesizing 
                     transcripts, while another portion does not 
                     |CITS:[39248572]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8902815][11503438][11586920]|.
                     A microarray analysis for the transcript cleavage factor 
                     GreA was performed by |CITS: [17766423]|."
