     gene            complement(3323941..3325113)
                     /gene="obgE"
                     /locus_tag="b3183"
                     /label=obgE
     CDS             complement(3323941..3325113)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="obgE"
                     /locus_tag="b3183"
                     /product="bifunctional GTPase ObgE / GTPase"
                     /label=obgE
                     /note="ObgE is an essential member of the Obg family of
                     small GTP-binding proteins |CITS: [9743119][11555285]|.  A 
                     variety of essential cellular processes, including 
                     chromosome segregation and ribosome assembly, are affected 
                     by ObgE.
                     Purified ObgE has moderate affinity and high guanine 
                     nucleotide exchange rate constants for both GTP and GDP and
                     a relatively low GTP hydrolysis rate |CITS: 
                     [11555285][15292126][19555460]|.  The physiologically 
                     relevant ligand may be |FRAME: GUANOSINE-5DP-3DP|.  During 
                     amino acid starvation, ObgE is important for cell survival 
                     and appears to alter the ratio of |FRAME: GDP-TP| to 
                     |FRAME: GUANOSINE-5DP-3DP| |CITS: [19555460]|.  ObgE also 
                     copurifies and interacts with the |FRAME: SPOT-MONOMER| 
                     |CITS: [15292126]|.  ObgE also interacts with the homodimer
                     of |FRAME: CPLX0-8184| via its C-terminal domain.  ObgE 
                     inhibits DNA binding to YbiB, indicating that it competes 
                     with DNA for binding in the positive clefts of YbiB |CITS: 
                     [36864742]|.
                     ObgE was predicted to be cation-dependent |CITS: 
                     [22750478]|, but it was later shown that ObgE is not 
                     activated by K<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [28223358]|.
                     ObgE interacts with the 50S ribosomal subunit and thereby 
                     prevents assembly and stimulates disassembly of the intact 
                     ribosome; this activity is enhanced in the presence of 
                     ppGpp, likely reflecting regulation of ribosome assembly by
                     ppGpp during the stringent response |CITS: [24844575]|. 
                     ObgE is involved in late steps of the assembly of the 50S 
                     ribosomal subunit.  In cell lysates, the ObgE protein is 
                     associated with the 50S ribosomal subunit |CITS: 
                     [15292126][16980477]|, but not during amino acid starvation
                     |CITS: [17616600]|.  The GTPase activity of ObgE is 
                     stimulated by the 70S ribosome |CITS: [28223358]|. Cryo-EM 
                     reconstructions of 50S ribosomal subunit precursors 
                     revealed a network of interacting ribosomal biogenesis 
                     factors that consists of ObgE, RluD, RsfS, and YjgA.  ObgE 
                     coordinates the maturation of the functional core of the 
                     50S subunit |CITS: [33639093]|.
                     ObgE is involved in 16S and 23S rRNA processing |CITS: 
                     [15836769][16980477]|; in the presence of GTP, purified 
                     ObgE cosediments with both 16S and 23S rRNA |CITS: 
                     [15836769]|.  Purified ObgE also cofractionates with the 
                     30S ribosomal subunit |CITS: [15836769][16980477]| and a 
                     number of ribosomal subunit proteins |CITS: [15836769]|. 
                     Earlier experimental results that showed no association of 
                     ObgE with the ribosome |CITS: [11555285]| are thought to be
                     due to instability of the association under the conditions 
                     used |CITS: [15292126]|.
                     A cryo-EM structure of ObgE bound to the 50S ribosomal 
                     subunit shows that the N-terminal domain of ObgE resides in
                     the peptidyl transfer center and is a structural mimic of 
                     the acceptor arm of A-site tRNA |CITS: [24844575]|.  A 
                     crystal structure of ObgE in the GDP-bound state shows the 
                     presence of an intrinsically disordered C-terminal domain; 
                     this domain contributes specifically to GTP binding |CITS: 
                     [28223358]|.  Analysis of the dominant negative ObgE* 
                     allele, which contains a K268I mutation located in the G 
                     domain of the protein, showed that GTP binding to the G 
                     domain appears to lead to a conformational change that is 
                     transmitted to the N-terminal domain, conferring toxicity 
                     |CITS: [31861427]|.
                     ObgE may play a role in cell survival after replication 
                     fork arrest.  A mutant containing a hypomorphic <i>obgE</i>
                     allele (<i>obgE</i>::Tn<i>5</i>) shows increased 
                     sensitivity to replication inhibitors such as hydroxyurea 
                     (HU) and
                     guanazole, and concomitant loss of <i>recA</i> or 
                     <i>recB</i> substantially enhances this phenotype.  The 
                     <i>obgE</i>::Tn<i>5</i> allele is synthetically lethal with
                     <i>seqA</i>, suggesting that ObgE plays a role in the 
                     regulation of replication or in organization of the 
                     replication fork |CITS: [15721258]|.  Cells with depleted 
                     ObgE show perturbed SeqA foci |CITS: [17578452]|.  Levels 
                     of the replication-initiator DnaA depend directly on the 
                     abundance of ObgE |CITS: [12826057][16518617]|, indicating 
                     that ObgE acts by regulating DnaA |CITS: [16518617]|.
                     ObgE mutants that specifically increase sensitivity to HU 
                     have been generated and analyzed; HU sensitivity appears to
                     be due to increased levels of cytotoxic hydroxyl radicals 
                     |CITS: [22863262]|.
                     ObgE appers to be involved in cell cycle progression and 
                     chromosome partitioning |CITS: 
                     [12402086][11555285][17578452]|.  A heat sensitive mutant 
                     exhibits a defect in chromosomal partitioning and elongated
                     cell morphology, but has no defect in DNA replication 
                     |CITS: [11555285]|.  Overproduction causes a defect in 
                     chromosomal partitioning and leads to oversized cell 
                     morphology, but does not cause gross defects in DNA 
                     replication, RNA transcription, or protein synthesis |CITS:
                     [11555285][12402086]|.  Depletion of ObgE delays chromosome
                     segregation, but is not lethal; growth resumes after 
                     expression of ObgE is restored |CITS: [17578452]|. Purified
                     ObgE associates with DNA and shows some membrane 
                     association |CITS: [11555285]|, but it does not appear to 
                     be associated with the nucleoid <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [15836769]|.  Expression of the dominant negative K268I 
                     allele of ObgE (ObgE*) results in cell death in a manner 
                     that resembles programmed cell death |CITS: [26695632]|, 
                     but does not involve reactive oxygen species |CITS: 
                     [27641546]|.  ObgE* causes irreversible inhibition of the 
                     cell cycle after the chromosome segregation step; membrane 
                     blebs form at cell division sites, followed by lysis |CITS:
                     [28702018]|.
                     ObgE controls persister cell formation.  ObgE concentration
                     correlates with the probability of transition to the 
                     persistent state in individual cells. ObgE's effect on 
                     persistence requires ppGpp and is mediated by increased 
                     expression of the |FRAME: MONOMER0-1604 HokB| toxin, 
                     leading to a decrease in the membrane potential |CITS: 
                     [26051177]|.  Most <i>obgE</i> mutant alleles that 
                     specifically lost their function in persister cell 
                     formation show reduced binding to GDP or ppGpp |CITS: 
                     [31498933]|.  Overexpression of ObgE induces protein 
                     aggregation in stationary phase and accelerates the 
                     development of persister cells |CITS: [34340538]|. ObgE is 
                     an abundant protein |CITS: [11555285]|, and the growth rate
                     correlates with the cellular level of ObgE |CITS: 
                     [15836769]|.  Levels of ObgE protein increase five-fold 
                     after exposure to UV irradiation, and moderate 
                     overexpression of ObgE leads to increased resistance to UV 
                     radiation |CITS: [12855728]|.  <i>obgE</i> is transcribed 
                     as the last gene in an operon along with <i>rplU</i>, 
                     <i>rpmA</i>, and <i>yhbE</i>.  This operon is mainly 
                     expressed during exponential growth; reports disagree on 
                     whether regulation by ppGpp/DksA is indirect |CITS: 
                     [27137500]| or direct |CITS: [27381917]|.  Furthermore, the
                     amounts of ObgE and YhbE are affected by the presence of a 
                     transcription terminator between <i>rpmA</i> and 
                     <i>yhbE</i> |CITS: [27137500]|.
                     Temperature-sensitive <i>obgE</i> mutants were found to 
                     have a lower basal level of the RecA
                     repair protein compared with wild-type; <i>recA</i> 
                     expression was not stimulated by UV irradiation in these 
                     mutants.
                     Taken together, these results strongly suggest that ObgE 
                     plays a role in the DNA repair process, most likely through
                     stimulation of the RecA-dependent DNA repair pathway |CITS:
                     [12855728]|.
                     Overproduction of ObgE suppresses the growth and ribosome 
                     assembly defects of a mutant lacking |FRAME: 
                     EG11507-MONOMER| |CITS: [11976298]|.  Depletion of ObgE 
                     does not appear to affect translation |CITS: [17578452]|. 
                     ObgE: ""<b>Obg</b> in <i><b>E</b>. coli</i>""<br>CgtA: 
                     ""<b>c</b>onserved <b>GT</b>Pase""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11200227][11489118][12732302][15737924][2 
                     2812515][23537324][24844695][26119572][26140365][28357361] 
                     |"
