                     [evidence ISS] [PMID 2670911]"
     gene            complement(3328594..3329853)
                     /gene="murA"
                     /locus_tag="b3189"
                     /label=murA
     CDS             complement(3328594..3329853)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="murA"
                     /locus_tag="b3189"
                     /product="UDP-N-acetylglucosamine
                     1-carboxyvinyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.5.1.7"
                     /label=murA
                     /note="UDP-<i>N</i>-acetylglucosamine enolpyruvoyl
                     transferase (MurA) catalyzes the first committed step in 
                     the assembly of peptidoglycan |CITS: [1512209]|. In 
                     exponentially growing cells, the intracellular 
                     concentration of UDP-<i>N</i>-acetylglucosamine is 
                     approximately 100 &micro;M, while the concentration of the 
                     MurA product, UDP-<i>N</i>-acetylglucosamine enolpyruvate 
                     is only 2 &micro;M |CITS: [6222035]|. MurA activity is 
                     feedback-inhibited by UDP-MurNAc, the product of the second
                     enzyme in the |FRAME: PWY-6387| pathway |CITS: [15751977]|.
                     The reaction mechanism of the enzyme has been investigated.
                     A tetrahedral adduct can be formed at the active site of 
                     the enzyme during catalyis |CITS: 
                     [8075064][JAmChemSoc115,10398][Kim95]|. However, covalent 
                     enzyme adduct formation is not required for catalysis 
                     |CITS: [8664284]|. The stereochemical course of the 
                     reaction has been investigated |CITS: 
                     [9485407][Lees95][9485407]|. Catalysis involves 
                     conformational transitions within the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [19899805]|.
                     MurA is the site of action for the antibiotic fosfomycin. 
                     Inactivation of MurA is dependent on the presence of the 
                     substrate UDP-GlcNAc |CITS: [8075065]|. The antibiotic 
                     covalently binds to the active site nucleophile Cys115 of 
                     the enzyme |CITS: [8075065][8994972]|. A C115D mutant is 
                     active, but resistant to fosfomycin |CITS: [8664284]|. 
                     Overexpression of MurA increases resistance to fosfomycin 
                     |CITS: [16978582][22371901]|; it is the only chromosomally 
                     encoded protein whose overexpression yields fosfomycin 
                     resistance |CITS: [22371901]|. A <i>murA</i> mutant with 
                     increased resistance to fosfomycin was identified |CITS: 
                     [4555418]|; the temperature-sensitive phenotype of that 
                     mutant may be unrelated to the <i>murA</i> mutation |CITS: 
                     [7608103]|.
                     Novel inhibitors of MurA activity have been sought and 
                     investigated; some may be potential novel antibiotics 
                     |CITS: [11600375][11897573][14684334][16945528][18261901][ 
                     20861142][35453279][35819545]|. MurA is also the primary 
                     physiological target of the nonnatural toxin bromoacetate 
                     |CITS: [20921376]|.
                     Crystal structures of wild-type and mutant MurA in various 
                     complexes have been solved |CITS: 
                     [8994972][9485407][18672863][19899805][20392080]|. 
                     Reinterpretation of existing crystal structure data 
                     suggests that under physiological conditions, MurA exists 
                     in a dormant complex containing UDP-N-acetylmuramic acid 
                     (UNAM) and the covalent PEP adduct at Cys115 |CITS: 
                     [22378791]|.
                     <i>murA</i> is an essential gene |CITS: 
                     [7608103][19557168]|. A strain containing a conditionally 
                     lethal amber mutation in <i>murA</i> has been generated 
                     |CITS: [15090508]|.
                     MurA, MurB and MurC are capable of chemo-enzymatic labeling
                     and modifying of UDP-GlcNAc-RNA <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [38716860]|.
                     This MurA protein in <i>E. coli</i> was shown to interact 
                     with the minimally characterized enzyme |FRAME: 
                     EG11894-MONOMER| in a protein-enzyme interactions (PEIs) 
                     study that examined the connection between protein-protein 
                     interactions (PPIs) and metabolomics networks studied in 22
                     different environmental conditions. This PEI may have a 
                     metabolic regulatory role in the |FRAME: PWY-6387| pathway,
                     in part because the PEI is conserved across many other 
                     bacterial taxa |CITS: [39903745]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12492849][18266853][22203377][35068599]|"
                     /translation="MDKFRVQGPTKLQGEVTISGAKNAALPILFAALLAEEPVEIQNVP
                     KLKDVDTSMKLLSQLGAKVERNGSVHIDARDVNVFCAPYDLVKTMRASIWALGPLVARF
                     GQGQVSLPGGCTIGARPVDLHISGLEQLGATIKLEEGYVKASVDGRLKGAHIVMDKVSV
