     gene            complement(3330615..3331250)
                     /gene="mlaC"
                     /locus_tag="b3192"
                     /label=mlaC
     CDS             complement(3330615..3331250)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mlaC"
                     /locus_tag="b3192"
                     /product="intermembrane phospholipid transport system -
                     periplasmic binding protein"
                     /label=mlaC
                     /note="<i>mlaC</i> encodes the periplasmic chaperone of a 
                     phospholipid intermembrane transport system that maintains 
                     outer membrane lipid asymmetry.
                     MlaC is implicated in a retrograde phospholipid trafficking
                     pathway that is thought to maintain outer membrane lipid 
                     asymmetry by removing phospholipids from the outer membrane
                     and transporting them back to the inner membrane; the Mla 
                     system comprises MlaC, the |FRAME: ABC-45-CPLX ""MlaFEDB""|
                     complex in the inner membrane and an outer membrane 
                     lipoprotein |FRAME: G7216-MONOMER ""MlaA""| |CITS: 
                     [19383799][27529189]|. MlaC is predicted to be a 
                     lipid-binding protein; MlaC interacts with the MlaFEDB 
                     inner membrane complex and the MlaA-OmpF outer membrane 
                     complex |CITS: [28388411]| (see also |CITS: [37100290]|). 
                     Mechanistic insight into retrograde phospholipid transfer 
                     from OmpC-MlaA to MlaC, and from MlaC to MlaD is reported 
                     by |CITS: [38092770][39080293]|. The 
                     OmpF<sub>3</sub>-MlaA-MlaC lipid shuttle has been 
                     reconstituted and dissected |CITS: [40168124]|.
                     Purified apo-MlaC is unable to passively bind free 
                     phospholipid but can accept phospholipid from MlaD and from
                     a reconstituted MlaFEDB complex <i>in vitro</i>; the Mla 
                     system may function for anterograde phospholipid transport 
                     with MlaFEDB exporting phospholipids from the IM, and 
                     transferring them to MlaC in an ATP-independent fashion 
                     |CITS: [31235958]|. Structural analysis indicates that MlaC
                     is an atypical substrate-binding protein (SBP) with a 
                     unique domain arrangement and ligand binding mechanism; 
                     MlaC contains a flexible binding pocket and can bind 
                     phospholipids in varying conformations |CITS: [36084896]|. 
                     Lipid-binding preferences of MlaC have been evaluated and 
                     the major phospholipids copurifying with MlaC identified; 
                     lipids copurifying with MlaC are enriched with 
                     phosphatidylglycerol (PG) |CITS: [39038171][40168124]|.
                     Deletion of <i>mlaC</i> results in permeability defects of 
                     the outer membrane; the mutant strain shows no defects in 
                     LPS or outer membrane protein levels but does have 
                     increased sensitivity to SDS-EDTA; this phenotype can be 
                     suppressed by an increase in |FRAME: EG10738 
                     ""<i>pldA</i>""| transcription |CITS: [19383799]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>mlaC</i> mutant has increased levels of 
                     phospholipid in the outer leaflet of the OM |CITS: 
                     [19383799]|.
                     <i>mla</i>: <U>m</U>aintenance of outer membrane 
                     <U>l</U>ipid <U>a</U>symmetry |CITS: [19383799]|"
