     gene            3337303..3338028
                     /gene="lptB"
                     /locus_tag="b3201"
                     /label=lptB
     CDS             3337303..3338028
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lptB"
                     /locus_tag="b3201"
                     /product="bifunctional lipopolysaccharide transport system
                     ATP binding protein LptB / lipopolysaccharide transport 
                     system"
                     /EC_number="7.5.2.5"
                     /label=lptB
                     /note="LptB is a cytoplasmic ATPase that energises the
                     transport and assembly of lipopolysaccharide (LPS) by the 
                     Lpt system.
                     Depletion of LptB results in impaired LPS transport and 
                     outer membrane biogenesis |CITS: [17056748]|. LptB forms a 
                     complex with LptF, LptG and LptC |CITS: [19500581]|. 
                     Sequence analysis suggests that LptB has an ATP binding 
                     fold |CITS: [17056748]|. Purified LptB has ATPase activity 
                     <i>in vitro</i>; purified LptB has a K<sub>M</sub> for ATP 
                     of 0.56 mM; ATP binding to LptB is cooperative |CITS: 
                     [20136079]|.
                     Crystal structures of LptB bound to ADP and a catalytically
                     inactive LptB mutant (LptBE163Q) bound to ATP have been 
                     obtained and suggest that ATP hydrolysis induces 
                     conformational change in LptB |CITS: [24639492]|. Purified,
                     crystallised LptB is an L-shaped molecule consisting of an 
                     ATPase domain containing the characteristic motifs (Walker 
                     A and B, and Q loop) and an &alpha;-helical domain |CITS: 
                     [24639492]|. LptB contains a binding site (which includes 
                     residues F90, R91, L93 and R150) which contacts the 
                     coupling helices of LptF and LptG |CITS: [27795402]|. The 
                     C-terminal domain of LptB (defined as residues 230-241) is 
                     functionally important; the region forms crucial 
                     interactions with the ATP binding site; characterization of
                     mutations which impair distinct steps of the ATP cycle 
                     support the contention that LPS transport is driven by ATP 
                     binding |CITS: [31431556]| (see further |CITS: [32236984]|)
                     LptB is found in the  inner membrane fraction of whole cell
                     lysates |CITS: [20446753]|.
                     LptB is a member of the ATP binding cassette (ABC) 
                     superfamily |CITS: [9593292]|."
