     gene            3338076..3339509
                     /gene="rpoN"
                     /locus_tag="b3202"
                     /label=rpoN
     CDS             3338076..3339509
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpoN"
                     /locus_tag="b3202"
                     /product="bifunctional RNA polymerase sigma factor RpoN /
                     RNA polymerase sigma 54"
                     /label=rpoN
                     /note="Sigma 54 controls expression of nitrogen-related
                     genes, directing |FRAME: RNAP54-CPLX| to the consensus 
                     nitrogen promoter.
                     Sigma 54 is the sigma factor controlling nitrogen-regulated
                     and other nitrogen-related promoters |CITS: 
                     [2867543][2887548]|.  It copurifies with the RNA polymerase
                     core enzyme and binds to the consensus nitrogen promoter 
                     |CITS: [2999766][2867543]|.  It is also involved in the 
                     nitric oxide stress response |CITS: [12529359]|. Mutants 
                     lacking <i>rpoN</i> had a significant defect in polyP 
                     synthesis |CITS: [32341074][35482529]|.
                     During typical exponential and stationary phase growth, the
                     amount of sigma 54 present in the cell is one tenth that of
                     the amount of sigma 70 |CITS: [8808934]|.  The metabolic 
                     context of genes dependent on sigma54 for transcription is
                     reviewed in |CITS: [11528004]|.
                     The alternative sigma factor &sigma;<sup>54</sup> (RpoN or 
                     &sigma;<sup>N</sup>) requires activation by specialized 
                     AAA<sup>+</sup> ATPases (bacterial enhancer-binding 
                     proteins, bEBPs) such as the NtrC protein |CITS:[8407777]|.
                     The bEBP mechanically translocates approximately 30 
                     residues of the &sigma;<sup>54</sup> region I 
                     (&sigma;<sup>54</sup>-RI) through the initial DNA bubble, 
                     partially unfolding the inhibitory 
                     &sigma;<sup>54</sup>-RI:extra-long helix (ELH) interface 
                     |CITS:[40759887]|. This ATP-driven remodeling disrupts the 
                     inhibition that keeps the E&sigma;<sup>54</sup> complex 
                     inactive, allowing formation of the transcriptionally 
                     competent open complex (RPo) |CITS:[40759887]|. A local 
                     switch from positive to negative charge within 
                     &sigma;<sup>54</sup>-RI facilitates disengagement of the 
                     ATPase, enabling &sigma;<sup>54</sup> disentanglement from 
                     the DNA bubble and promoter escape |CITS:[40759887]|.
                     In some sigma 54-dependent promoters, DNA bending occurs, 
                     allowing |FRAME: PROTEIN-NRIP| to reach the transcription 
                     start site |CITS: [8780778]|.  It has been suggested, 
                     however, that DNA bending related to sigma 54 is not as 
                     extreme as originally reported |CITS: [9790843]|. Sigma 54 
                     has also been alternately reported to remain bound to the 
                     promoter after RNA polymerase departs during transcript 
                     elongation, or to separate from the promoter between each 
                     round of transcription |CITS: [7557383][11809874]|.
                     The binding affinity of |FRAME: RNAP54-CPLX| for several 
                     target promoters has been evaluated |CITS: [12235394]|. 
                     Sigma 54 has a number of structural features that affect 
                     its activity.  It has a pair of leucine zipper motifs that 
                     are involved in promoter melting, as well as an acidic 
                     region that has a role in the same process and a 
                     carboxy-terminal helix-turn-helix motif that is required 
                     for promoter recognition |CITS: 
                     [2203540][1447214][8107127]|.  A strongly acidic 
                     hydrophobic repeat region is required for binding to RNA 
                     core polymerase |CITS: [8134358][8196043]|. Four leucines 
                     in the amino-terminal region are required for |FRAME: 
                     PROTEIN-NRIP| regulation of sigma 54 activity |CITS: 
                     [7481805][9076735]|. Sigma 54 binds to the core polymerase 
                     via a region that is very similar to sigma70 |CITS: 
                     [7592329][9427396]|.
                     The structure of sigma 54 has been evaluated via scanning 
                     force microscopy |CITS: [9236116]|.  Additional examination
                     via x-ray scattering microscopy shows that sigma 54 and 
                     sigma 70 share broad structural similarity |CITS: 
                     [10660585]|.  A 24 &Aring; cryo-electron structure of sigma
                     54 holoenzyme reveals major conformational changes during 
                     DNA binding |CITS: [16246367]|.
                     A global antagonistic effect on gene expression was 
                     perceived between &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, 
                     &sigma;<sup>54</sup>, and &sigma;<sup>28</sup>, based on 
                     transcriptome expression analysis, and this resulted in 
                     many physiological traits, including flagellum-mediated 
                     motility and utilization of nitrogen sources; as many as 
                     60% of genes in the RpoN regulon are under reciprocal RpoS 
                     control |CITS: [21219458]|. In addition, there is a complex
                     regulatory interaction of the three &sigma; factors: 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>, &sigma;<sup>54</sup>, and 
                     &sigma;<sup>28</sup> |CITS: [21219458]|.
                     The &sigma;<sup>54</sup> binding sites within the <i>E. 
                     coli</i> chromosome were comprehensively mapped using 
                     ChIP-seq |CITS: [26425847]|.
                     A transposon insertion mutation in the <i>rpoN</i> gene 
                     generates cellular resistance to the antibiotic 
                     trimethoprim |CITS: [34874820]|. On the other hand, 
                     inhibition of <i>rpoN</i> expression by CRISPRi enhanced 
                     cellular fitness under treatment with the antibiotics 
                     carbonyl cyanide 3-chlorophenylhydrazone (CCCP), polymyxin 
                     B, pyocyanin, rifampicin, sulfamethizole, verapamil, or 
                     erythromycin |CITS:[40352728]|.
                     The deletion of <i>rpoN</i> significantly reduces the 
                     viability of <i>E. coli</i> in soil, with the survival 
                     impact observable from day 21|CITS:[39905026]|. This 
                     finding suggests that the activation of nitrogen-related 
                     functions is critical for bacterial persistence in 
                     nutrient-poor environments such as soil.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [27789741][26010401][18331472]|"
