     gene            3339937..3340428
                     /gene="ptsN"
                     /locus_tag="b3204"
                     /label=ptsN
     CDS             3339937..3340428
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ptsN"
                     /locus_tag="b3204"
                     /product="phosphotransferase system enzyme IIA(Ntr)"
                     /label=ptsN
                     /note="<i>ptsN</i> encodes a protein paralogous to Enzyme 
                     IIA<sup>fru</sup> of the phosphoenolpyruvate 
                     (PEP)-dependent carbohydrate phosphotransferase system 
                     (PTS) |CITS: [8025669][7876255]|. PtsN regulates the 
                     activity or expression of potassium transporters |CITS: 
                     [17289841][19400808]|. The PtsN protein, also known as 
                     Enzyme IIA<sup>Ntr</sup>, is part of a phosphorelay system 
                     (PTS<sup>Ntr</sup>) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 that may have a 
                     role in the regulation of nitrogen metabolism |CITS: 
                     [23517463] [20202847]| although this has been questioned 
                     |CITS: [21742895]|. The PTS<sup>Ntr</sup> has been 
                     implicated in the control of a stress-responsive |FRAME: 
                     YCGO-MONOMER ""CvrA""| mediated K<sup>+</sup> efflux 
                     pathway |CITS: [27137496]|.
                     PtsN can be phosphorylated <i>in vivo</i>; both the energy 
                     coupling phosphotransferases of the Ntr PTS (|FRAME: 
                     EG12188-MONOMER PtsP| and |FRAME: EG12147-MONOMER Npr|) as 
                     well as the sugar PTS contribute to the phosphorylation of 
                     PtsN |CITS: [18625021]|. Dephosphorylated PtsN interacts 
                     with the potassium transporter TrkA and inhibits its 
                     activity |CITS: [17289841]|. Dephosphorylated PtsN also 
                     interacts with the sensor kinase KdpD and stimulates its 
                     activity, resulting in increased levels of phosphorylated 
                     response regulator KdpE, thereby increasing expression of 
                     <i>kdpFABC</i>, a high-affinity potassium transporter 
                     |CITS: [19400808]|. Via regulating the intracellular 
                     K<sup>+</sup> concentration, PtsN affects sigma factor 
                     selectivity and thus transcription levels for a number of 
                     genes |CITS: [21143318]|.
                     All nutrient utilization phenotypes previously attributed 
                     to lack of <i>ptsN</i> are synthetic phenotypes dependent 
                     on the presence of the <i>ilvG</i> mutation, which causes a
                     lack of |FRAME: ACETOLACTSYNII-CPLX AHASII| in the K-12 
                     MG1655 and W3110 strain backgrounds |CITS: [21531803]|. The
                     phenotypes include sensitivity of <i>ptsN</i> mutants to 
                     leucine-containing peptides |CITS: [16164569]| and 
                     inhibition of growth by a number of TCA cycle intermediates
                     and carbohydrates when a nucleoside base or amino acid is 
                     used as a source of carbon, nitrogen, and energy |CITS: 
                     [7876255]|. The intracellular K<sup>+</sup> concentration 
                     has effects on expression and activity of |FRAME: 
                     ACETOLACTSYNI-CPLX AHASI|, and thus the effect of a 
                     <i>ptsN</i> mutation on cellular metabolism depends on the 
                     strain background |CITS: [21531803]|.
                     PtsN(K75D) exhibits a phosphorylation dependent mobility 
                     shift on SDS-PAGE. Glutamine inhibits and 
                     &alpha;-ketoglutarate stimulates the <i>in vitro</i> 
                     phosphorylation of PtsN(K75D). This effect was also 
                     demonstrated in an <i>ilvG</i><sup>+</sup> revertant of 
                     <i>E. coli</i> MG1655 |CITS: [23517463]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>ptsN</i> strain has a potassium sensitive 
                     phenotype that correlates with reduced intracellular 
                     potassium content; this phenotype is suppressed by deletion
                     of |FRAME: YCGO-MONOMER <i>cvrA</i>| |CITS: [27137496]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>ptsN</i> strain is defective in swimming motility
                     and has impaired growth in minimal medium with glycerol or 
                     acetate as sole carbon source |CITS: [34563668]|.
                     A Tn<i>10</i> insertion in <i>ptsN</i> suppresses the 
                     conditionally lethal <i>era</i><sup>ts</sup> mutation 
                     |CITS: [7876255]|. <i>ptsN</i> is a multicopy suppressor of
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup> essentiality; the ability to 
                     phosphorylate PtsN is required for suppression. 
                     Overexpression of <i>ptsN</i> lowers envelope stress |CITS:
                     [18253509]|.
                     A crystal structure of PtsN has been solved at 2.35 &Aring;
                     resolution |CITS: [9636714]|, and the protein has been 
                     characterized in solution by NMR |CITS: 
                     [14512741][15741344]|. While the crystal structure shows a 
                     dimer |CITS: [9636714]|, PtsN is monomeric in solution; 
                     PtsN-phosphorylated is the the predominant form in LB-grown
                     cells |CITS: [15741344]|.
                     Comments: |CITS: [17360507][21742895]|
                     Review: |CITS: [20202847]|"
