     gene            complement(3342440..3343168)
                     /gene="mtgA"
                     /locus_tag="b3208"
                     /label=mtgA
     CDS             complement(3342440..3343168)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mtgA"
                     /locus_tag="b3208"
                     /product="bifunctional peptidoglycan glycosyltransferase
                     MtgA / peptidoglycan glycosyltransferase"
                     /label=mtgA
                     /note="MtgA is a monofunctional murein glycosyltransferase 
                     involved in polymerization of lipid II molecules into 
                     glycan strands of peptidoglycan.
                     GFP-MtgA fusions localize to the division site in 
                     <I>mrcA</I>(ts) <I>mrcB</I> mutants |CITS:[18165305]|. 
                     Bacterial two-hybrid experiments reveal MtgA interacts with
                     PBP3, FtsW, and FtsN |CITS:[18165305]|.
                     Assays for peptidoglycan synthesis activity revealed that 
                     MtgA has glycosyltransferase activity and polymerizes 
                     glycan strands with lipid II as substrate at an optimum pH 
                     of 6.0 to 6.5 |CITS:[6368264][8772200][18165305]|. This 
                     differs from the optimum of 7.5 to 8.0 for PBP1B, which is 
                     the major bifunctional glycosyltransferase for 
                     peptidoglycan synthesis in <I>E. coli</I> 
                     |CITS:[6368264][8772200]|. The glycan strands were then 
                     shown to act as substrate for lysozyme 
                     |CITS:[6368264][8772200][18165305]|. Inhibition assays show
                     that |FRAME: MOENOMYCIN moenomycin A| does not inhibit this
                     activity as it does with the high-molecular-weight 
                     penicillin-binding proteins that have glycosyltransferase 
                     activity |CITS:[8772200]|. Induction of this gene from an 
                     expression vector in wild-type W3110 caused cells to 
                     immediately stop growing, but not to lyse |CITS:[8772200]|.
                     <I>mtgA</I> mutants and <I>mtgA pbpC</I> double mutants 
                     have elevated levels of tetrapenta muropeptides 
                     |CITS:[10542235]|.
                     Ofloxacin downregulates <I>mtgA</I> expression 
                     |CITS:[14982780]|."
