     gene            3343936..3344056
                     /gene="arcZ"
                     /locus_tag="b4450"
                     /label=arcZ
     misc_RNA        3343936..3344056
                     /gene="arcZ"
                     /locus_tag="b4450"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA ArcZ"
                     /note="ArcZ is a conserved small regulatory RNA that
                     increases translation of RpoS |CITS: 
                     [11448770][19732340][20457943][20683441]| and inhibits 
                     Rho-dependent transcription termination within the 
                     <i>rpoS</i> 5' UTR upon transition to the stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [27662085]|.  The primary 120 nt <i>arcZ</i> 
                     transcript is processed to form a low-abundance 88 nt form 
                     and a stable 55 nt form derived from the 3' end of the 
                     primary transcript |CITS: 
                     [11445539][11448770][20457943][20683441]|; RNase E appears 
                     to be the major enzyme responsible for processing |CITS: 
                     [30462307]|.  In <i>hfq</i> mutant cells, the processed 
                     form is lost |CITS: [20457943][22040174]|.  The genes 
                     encoded upstream and downstream of <i>arcZ</i> are 
                     conserved |CITS: [19732340]|.
                     ArcZ binds to Hfq |CITS: [11445539][14622403][36328526]|. 
                     The full-length form of ArcZ binds at least two Hfq 
                     multimers, while the processed form of ArcZ binds Hfq less 
                     specifically.  In contrast, the processed, but not the 
                     full-length form of ArcZ binds the <i>rpoS</i> mRNA leader 
                     region |CITS: [20457943]| and is sufficient for regulation 
                     |CITS: [20683441]|.  In addition, the processed form of 
                     ArcZ downregulates the |FRAME: RYEE-RNA| by base-pairing to
                     CyaR and mediating its RNase E-dependent degradation |CITS:
                     [31552789]| or by acting as a ""sponge"" |CITS: 
                     [32356726]|.
                     ArcZ negatively affects accumulation of <i>arcB</i> mRNA. 
                     RpoS may also be involved in a complex feedback loop 
                     regulating <i>arcB</i> and <i>arcZ</i> |CITS: [20683441]|. 
                     Overexpression of ArcZ decreases swimming and swarming 
                     motility and biofilm formation |CITS: [22925049][26469694]|
                     and increases acid tolerance and |FRAME: 
                     GLUTDECARBOXA-MONOMER GadA| accumulation |CITS: 
                     [21976725][24319011]|; simultaneous overexpression of all 
                     three small RNA translational regulators of RpoS - DsrA, 
                     RprA and ArcZ - increases acid tolerance during active cell
                     growth supra-additively |CITS: [23892399]|.  Both 
                     overexpression and deletion of <i>arcZ</i> decreases 
                     biofilm formation in YESCA medium, but not in LB; 
                     overproduction of RpoS partially suppresses deletion of 
                     <i>arcZ</i> |CITS: [28470798]|.
                     Expression of ArcZ is highest in stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [11445539][11448770][20683441]|; expression is not induced 
                     by acidic growth conditions |CITS: [24319011]|.  The half 
                     life of ArcZ in stationary phase is 32 minutes |CITS: 
                     [14602901]|.  High-copy expression of <i>arcZ</i> causes 
                     increased expression of <i>rpoS</i> in minimal media |CITS:
                     [11445539]|.  The ArcAB two-component system represses 
                     expression of <i>arcZ</i> under anaerobic conditions |CITS:
                     [20683441]|.
                     ArcZ promotes dehydration tolerance through modulation of 
                     RpoS |CITS:[29457688]|.
                     A single mutation of <i>arcZ</i> slightly decreases 
                     cellular growth when D-alanine or glucosamine is used as a 
                     sole carbon source, but a double mutant, 
                     <i>arcZ</i>-<i>ssrA</i>, enhances cellular growth on 
                     pyruvate, lactate, oxaloacetate, D-alanine, or L-alanine as
                     carbon source |CITS: [35920556]|. The double mutation 
                     <i>arcZ</i>-<i>dsrA</i> increases growth on saccharate, 
                     pyruvate, lactate, fucose, and gluconate, and it was 
                     suggested that the increase is due to the lack of 
                     expression activation of RpoS by ArcZ and DsrA  |CITS: 
                     [35920556]|.  The strongest effect on growth was the 
                     decrease of it caused by the double mutation 
                     <i>arcZ</i>-<i>csrC</i> on pyruvate, lactate, fucose, or 
                     gluconate, and this effect appeared to be through the 
                     dysregulation of the sRNAs on the gene <i>ppsA</i> |CITS: 
                     [35920556]|.
                     SraH: ""<b>s</b>mall <b>R</b>N<b>A</b>"" |CITS: [11448770]|
                     PsrA16: ""<b>p</b>redicted <b>s</b>mall <b>R</b>N<b>A</b>""
                     |CITS: [11448770]|
                     Ryx: ""RNA of unknown function at X position (10 minute) in
                     the genome"" |CITS: [11445539]|
                     ArcZ: ""<b><i>arc</i></b>-associated sRNA <b>Z</b>"" |CITS:
                     [19732340]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12762038][25028968][29992897][31197038][3 
                     2213244][32475775][38839110]|"
     gene            complement(3344048..3346384)
                     /gene="arcB"
                     /locus_tag="b3210"
                     /label=arcB
     CDS             complement(3344048..3346384)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="arcB"
                     /locus_tag="b3210"
                     /product="bifunctional sensor histidine kinase ArcB /
                     protein histidine autokinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.13.3"
                     /label=arcB
                     /note="ArcB is the sensor histidine kinase of the ArcAB 
                     two-component system which mediates anaerobic repression of
                     numerous enzymes associated with aerobic metabolism |CITS: 
                     [2644240][1597416]|.
                     ArcB is an integral membrane protein with a significant 
                     cytoplasmic domain; the protein contains two transmembrane 
                     regions and three cytoplasmic modules - a primary 
                     transmitter module with a conserved histidine residue 
                     (His-292), a receiver module with a conserved aspartate 
                     residue (Asp-576) and an alternative transmitter module 
                     with a conserved histidine (His-717) |CITS: 
                     [2201868][7957084]|. Purified, soluble ArcB (lacking the 
                     two transmembrane domains) autophosphorylates in the 
                     presence of ATP and transfers a phosphate to its cognate 
                     response regulator ArcA; ArcB contains both histidyl and 
                     aspartyl phosphates |CITS: [1512197]|.  ArcB signal 
                     transduction proceeds via a multi-step His-Asp-His 
                     phosphorelay; His-292 is the site of autophosphorylation 
                     and phosphotransfer proceeds from phospho-His-292 to 
                     His-717 via Asp-576 |CITS: 
                     [8825099][9286997][9683496][10851007]|.
                     Phospho-ArcA signal decay occurs <i>in vitro</i> via a 
                     reverse phosphorelay involving phosphotransfer to His-717 
                     of ArcB and subsequently to Asp-576 with release of Pi; 
                     His-292 is not implicated in phospho-ArcA signal decay 
                     |CITS: [9830034]| (see also |CITS: [15838055]|). The 
                     fermentation products D-lactate, pyruvate and acetate 
                     accelerate ArcB autophosphorylation and phosphotransfer 
                     <i>in vitro</i> but do not affect phospho-ArcA signal decay
                     |CITS: [10585483]| (see also |CITS: [15028693]|). 
                     Autophosphorylation is an intramolecular reaction, 
                     requiring the ATP-binding site and the site of 
                     phosphorylation (His-292) to be present on the same subunit
                     of the ArcB homodimer |CITS: [20097862]| however the mode 
                     of phosphorelay and decay remains ambiguous (see |CITS: 
                     [25797699][26828204][29945971]|).
                     Under aerobic conditions, the ubiquinone electron carriers 
                     oxidise two cytosolic cysteine (Cys-180 and Cys-241) thiols
                     to form an intermolecular disulfide bond and silence the 
                     kinase activity of ArcB |CITS: [11423658][15326287]|. ArcB 
                     is oxidised specifically by quinones and is insensitive to 
                     molecular oxygen and hydrogen peroxide |CITS: 
                     [15326287][19933363]|. ArcB kinase activity is regulated by
                     the redox state of the menaquinone pool under microaerobic 
                     and anaerobic conditions |CITS: [19933363]| (see further 
                     |CITS: [24116043][23645604][27656164]|). The redox 
                     potential of ArcB is -41mV |CITS: [23645604]|.
                     Arc: <b>A</b>noxic <b>r</b>edox <b>c</b>ontrol
                     Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [8412675][7855430][9010748][16771670][20946850]|"
