     gene            complement(3366042..3366935)
                     /gene="nanA"
                     /locus_tag="b3225"
                     /label=nanA
     CDS             complement(3366042..3366935)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nanA"
                     /locus_tag="b3225"
                     /product="N-acetylneuraminate lyase"
                     /label=nanA
                     /note="<i>N</i>-acetylneuraminate lyase (NanA) is part of
                     the |FRAME: PWY0-1324| pathway, where it catalyzes the 
                     formation of <i>N</i>-acetylmannosamine and pyruvate from 
                     the linear (<i>aldehydo</i>) form of 
                     <i>N</i>-acetylneuraminate (NeuNAc) |CITS: 
                     [3902799][33895133]|.  The enzyme is classified as a Schiff
                     base-forming Class I aldolase |CITS: [1646603]|. NeuNAc, 
                     the substrate of NanA, exists predominantly in the 
                     &beta;-anomeric (cyclic) form in solution |CITS: 
                     [18063573]|.  Spontaneous mutarotation is slower at low pH 
                     |CITS: [21584336]|.  Addition of the NeuNAc anomerases NanM
                     or NanQ to the <i>in vitro</i> assay performed at low pH 
                     stimulates NanA activity.  The stimulation is due to the 
                     anomerase activity of these enzymes, indicating that the 
                     true substrate for NanA is the <i>aldehydo</i> form of 
                     NeuNAc |CITS: [33895133]|.
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme alone and in complex with 
                     hydroxypruvate or pyruvate as well as structures of enzymes
                     with altered substrate specificity have been solved |CITS: 
                     [8081752][9047371][12711733][19923724][20826 
                     162][21270125][30291262]|, providing insight into the 
                     location of the active site and the reaction mechanism.  
                     The structure of the active site was explored using 
                     C9-modified derivatives of NeuNAc |CITS: [10732983]|.  
                     Enzymes with altered substrate specificity have been 
                     obtained by <i>in vitro</i> directed evolution, 
                     structure-guided design or saturation mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [12670660][12711733][15897188][15967977][21270125]|.  All 
                     possible variants at the E192 residue have been generated 
                     and tested for substrate specificity |CITS: [20826162]|. 
                     The enzyme is a tetramer in the crystal structure; the 
                     apparent trimer found by various gel filtration experiments
                     may be explained by anomalous migration due to the 
                     asymmetric shape of the tetramer |CITS: [8081752]|. Kinetic
                     analysis of a library of proteins with point mutations in 
                     residues at the dimer-dimer interface indicated that the 
                     quarternary structure of the enzyme is essential for 
                     functional expression |CITS: [19646958]|. NanA is an 
                     obligate substrate for the GroEL chaperonin |CITS: 
                     [16051146][20360681][24813614]|.
                     There is conflicting data regarding whether |CITS: 
                     [1646603]| or not |CITS: [3909108]| the N-terminal 
                     methionine residue is cleaved from the mature protein. 
                     Expression of the enzyme is induced by growth on sialic 
                     acid in the absence of glucose |CITS: [3902799]|. 
                     Transcription of the <i>nanATEKQ</i> (sialic acid 
                     catabolic) operon is repressed by NanR |CITS: [12897000]|. 
                     Expression of <i>nanA</i> is induced by growth on cecal 
                     mucus |CITS: [15123798]|.  Expression of NanA is 
                     downregulated upon exposure to the alkylating agent 
                     methyl-methanesulfonate |CITS: [26904018]|.
                     <i>nanA</i> mutants are unable to use sialic acid |CITS: 
                     [3902799]|, and specifically <i>N</i>-acetylneuraminate, 
                     <i>N</i>-glycolylneuraminate and 
                     3-keto-3-deoxy-D-glycero-D-galactonononate |CITS: 
                     [23848303]| as the sole source of carbon and energy. 
                     Mutants were found to be defective in initiation of 
                     colonization of the mouse intestine |CITS: [15123798]|.
                     In contrast, |CITS: [20562286]| found that a <i>nanA</i> 
                     deletion mutant has only a slight effect on colonization 
                     and persistence of <i>E. coli</i> in the intestines of 
                     gnotobiotic mice.  A &Delta;<i>nanA</i> mutant shows 
                     decreased biofilm formation |CITS: [26904018]|.
                     NanA: ""<i><b>N</b></i>-<b>a</b>cetyl<b>n</b>euraminate 
                     catabolism"" |CITS: [3902799]|"
