     gene            complement(3370141..3370779)
                     /gene="sspA"
                     /locus_tag="b3229"
                     /label=sspA
     CDS             complement(3370141..3370779)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sspA"
                     /locus_tag="b3229"
                     /product="stringent starvation protein A"
                     /label=sspA
                     /note="The SspA protein is associated with RNA polymerase
                     |CITS: [8198564]| and is required for transcriptional 
                     activation of the bacteriophage P1 late promoter <i>in 
                     vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [12791143]| and for the
                     transcription of two peptidoglycan synthases |CITS: 
                     [36308522]|. SspA is essential for cell survival during 
                     acid-induced stress, acting by inhibiting accumulation of 
                     the global regulator H-NS during stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [15819627]|.
                     SspA expression is induced by starvation for glucose, 
                     nitrogen, phosphate or amino acids, and it increases with 
                     decreasing growth rate |CITS: [8022275]|.
                     The SspA protein has been crystallized, but the crystals 
                     did not allow structure determination |CITS: [12777805]|. 
                     Determination of a crystal structure was attempted with 
                     SspA proteins from <i>Vibrio cholerae</i>, <i>Pseudomonas 
                     aeruginosa</i>, and <i>Yersinia pestis</i>; only the <i>Y. 
                     pestis</i> protein diffracted to 2.0 &Aring; |CITS: 
                     [12777805]|. High sequence conservation allows modelling of
                     the <i>E. coli</i> protein on the basis of the <i>Y. 
                     pestis</i> SspA crystal structure, and amino acid residues 
                     of potential functional importance have been
                     mutagenized |CITS: [15735307]|.
                     SspA from <i>E. coli</i> shows high similarity in sequence,
                     predicted structure, and physiological function with the 
                     SspA from <i>Shewanella oneidensis</i>; these proteins 
                     appear to be interchangeable |CITS: [36308522]|. Deletion 
                     of the <i>sspA</i> gene results in decreased long-term 
                     viability under arginine starvation conditions. During 
                     exponential growth, expression of at least 11 proteins is 
                     altered in the mutant <i>relA</i> is required for the 
                     effect of <i>sspA</i> on protein synthesis |CITS: 
                     [8022275]|. Comparison of cultures with null mutants 
                     <i>sspA</i>, a noncleavable |FRAME: EG10533| 
                     (""<i>lexA3</i>"") and a double <i>lexA3</i> <i>sspA</i> 
                     versus wild-type <i>E. coli</i> with and without treatment 
                     with the DNA-damaging compound |FRAME: CPD-15110| (AZT) 
                     found SspA is as involved in inducing and repressing 
                     transcription of genes as LexA, as well as some genes that 
                     are codependent on both SspA and LexA, including many 
                     within the e14 prophage region |CITS: [38935560]|."
