     gene            3373550..3373948
                     /gene="yhcB"
                     /locus_tag="b3233"
                     /label=yhcB
     CDS             3373550..3373948
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yhcB"
                     /locus_tag="b3233"
                     /product="inner membrane protein YhcB"
                     /label=yhcB
                     /note="In a study of membrane protein complexes, YhcB
                     (later named ZapG/LapD |CITS: [33895137][36077106]|) was 
                     found together with CydA and CydB in the inner membrane and
                     was suggested to be a new subunit of the |FRAME: 
                     CYT-D-UBIOX-CPLX| |CITS: [16079137]|. However, it was later
                     shown that YhcB is not associated with the CydAB 
                     heterodimer and is not required for the assembly or 
                     function of cytochrome <i>bd</i> |CITS: [16863643]|. 
                     Proteomic analysis of membrane preparations suggests that 
                     YhcB may form a homo-oligomeric complex |CITS: [21210718]|.
                     The C-terminus of YhcB is located in the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [21210718]|.
                     Deletion of <i>yhcB</i> is lethal in a &Delta;<i>rodZ</i> 
                     background; YhcB physically interacts with |FRAME: 
                     EG10015-MONOMER ""RodZ""| and with |FRAME: EG10607-MONOMER 
                     ""MrdB""|, and with the cell-shape proteins |FRAME: 
                     EG10609-MONOMER ""MreC""| and |FRAME: EG10610-MONOMER 
                     ""MreD""|; YhcB physically interacts with |FRAME: 
                     NACGLCTRANS-MONOMER ""MurG""| and with |FRAME: 
                     G6637-MONOMER ""LapA""| |CITS: [27335665]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>yhcB</i> mutant has a growth defect under 
                     various treatments associated with envelope stress (SDS, 
                     EDTA, salt and others); the mutant strain also exhibits an 
                     increased percentage of shape and division defects compared
                     to wild type and increased susceptibility to vancomycin and
                     anti-folate antibiotics such as such as trimethoprim, 
                     sulfanilamide, and sulfamethazine |CITS: [32323199]| (see 
                     also |CITS: [34941903]|).
                     YhcB is conserved across most gamma-proteobacteria; 
                     deletion of <i>yhcB</i> results in high temperature 
                     sensitivity, filamentation and susceptibility to cell 
                     wall-acting antibiotics; &Delta;<i>yhcB</i> filaments show 
                     abnormal FtsZ location and aberrant septum formation; 
                     <i>yhcB</i> shows strong synthetic lethal phenotypes with 
                     genes involved in cell division, cell shape, and cell wall 
                     biogenesis and YhcB physically interacts with proteins of 
                     the elongasome and divisome and with several 
                     uncharacterized proteins including |FRAME: HEMX-MONOMER 
                     HemX| |CITS: [33895137]|.
                     YhcB copurifies with various proteins involved in 
                     lipopolysaccharide (LPS) and phospholipid biosynthesis; 
                     genetic and biochemical analysis suggests YhcB has an 
                     important role in LPS assembly/transport - it may act to 
                     regulate |FRAME: UDPACYLGLCNACDEACETYL-MONOMER LpxC| levels
                     and assist |FRAME: CPLX0-7704 MsbA|-mediated LPS transport 
                     |CITS: [36077106]| (see also |CITS: [37894855]|).
                     Deletion of <i>yhcB</i> in K-12 strain W3110 results in a 
                     significant growth defect and a pleomorphic phenotype; YhcB
                     is implicated in the regulation of fatty acid synthesis 
                     |CITS: [38742872]|.
                     <i>zapG</i>: <U>Z</U>-ring <U>a</U>ssociated <U>p</U>rotein
                     <U>G</U> |CITS: [33895137]|<BR><i>lapD</i>: <U>L</U>PS 
                     <U>a</U>ssembly <U>p</U>rotein D |CITS: [36077106]|"
