     gene            complement(3385817..3389617)
                     /gene="yhdP"
                     /locus_tag="b4472"
                     /label=yhdP
     CDS             complement(3385817..3389617)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yhdP"
                     /locus_tag="b4472"
                     /product="phospholipid transport factor YhdP"
                     /label=yhdP
                     /note="YhdP is implicated in the maintenance of outer
                     membrane (OM) permeability through its functional 
                     interaction with |FRAME: CPD0-2646| (cyclic ECA) |CITS: 
                     [30087168]|. YhdP is implicated in high-flux phospholipid 
                     transport |CITS: [33046656]|. YhdP may be an intermembrane 
                     bridge protein that facilitates the movement of 
                     phospholipids between the inner and outer membrane |CITS: 
                     [34781743]|.
                     <i>yhdP</i> is implicated in the stationary phase stress 
                     reponse; <i>yhdP</i> is necessary for RpoS dependent SDS 
                     resistance in carbon limited stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [27821607]|. &Delta;<i>ydhP</i> cells show increased 
                     sensitivity to SDS+EDTA and to vancomycin regardless of 
                     growth phase; disruption of ECA biosynthesis suppresses the
                     mutant phenotype of &Delta;<i>ydhP</i> cells; <i>yhdP</i> 
                     and ECA biosynthesis genes show a high level of 
                     co-occurrence in the <i>Enterobacteriaceae</i> |CITS: 
                     [30087168]|.
                     A specific functional interaction between YhdP and cyclic 
                     ECA is implicated in maintaining the OM permeability 
                     barrier: deletion of <i>yhdP</i> decreases the level of 
                     cyclic ECA; preventing cyclic ECA synthesis (deletion of 
                     <i>wzzE</i>) suppresses the envelope permeability defect of
                     &Delta;<i>ydhP</i> cells |CITS: [30087168]| (see further 
                     |CITS: [34781743]|).
                     Disruption of <i>yhdP</i> inhibits |FRAME: G7216 
                     <i>mlaA*</i>|-mediated cell death; deletion of <i>yhdP</i> 
                     slows the inner membrane shrinkage that occurs in 
                     <i>mlaA*</i> cells and delays cell death; deletion of 
                     <i>yhdP</i> disrupts the anterograde flow of phospholipids 
                     in <i>mlaA*</i> cells causing the OM to rupture before the 
                     IM |CITS: [33046656]|. Deletion of <i>yhdP</i> weakens the 
                     mechanical strength of the OM; YhdP affects both OM 
                     stiffness and permeability |CITS: [33046656]|.
                     This partially characterized enzyme was found to interact 
                     with the |FRAME: ENOLASE-MONOMER| enzyme in glycolysis in a
                     large assessment of protein-protein interactions (PPIs) 
                     |CITS: [33917325]|.
                     YhdP, |FRAME: G7875-MONOMER TamB|, |FRAME: G6703-MONOMER 
                     YdbH|, |FRAME: EG11684-MONOMER YicH|, and |FRAME: 
                     EG12251-MONOMER YhjG| are |FRAME: EG11361-MONOMER 
                     AsmA|-like paralogs predicted to contain an N-terminal 
                     inner membrane anchor and a large periplasmic region with 
                     chorein-N and AsmA-like domains; the combined loss of YhdP,
                     TamB and YdbH is lethal; the three proteins are crucial for
                     maintaining lipid homeostasis at the OM and are 
                     functionally redundant but not equivalent; TamB, YhdP and 
                     YdbH may be intermembrane bridge proteins that facilitate 
                     the movement of phospholipids between the inner and outer 
                     membrane |CITS: [34781743]| (see also |CITS: [35226662]|). 
                     Genetic interaction data suggests that the three proteins 
                     may differ in substrate specificity; YhdP may transport 
                     phospholipids with more saturated fatty acids |CITS: 
                     [38913742]|."
