     gene            complement(3393403..3394446)
                     /gene="mreB"
                     /locus_tag="b3251"
                     /label=mreB
     CDS             complement(3393403..3394446)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mreB"
                     /locus_tag="b3251"
                     /product="dynamic cytoskeletal protein MreB"
                     /label=mreB
                     /note="MreB is a homolog of the eukaryotic actin protein
                     (see |CITS: [1323828][11731313]|.
                     MreB is implicated in the formation of rod shape and 
                     mecillinam sensitivity |CITS: [3049542]|. MreB forms 
                     left-handed helical filaments beneath the surface of the 
                     cell |CITS: [14517265][12766229][22343529]|. Long MreB 
                     helical filaments are artifacts of the yellow fluorescent 
                     tag used for imaging; long filaments do not form when the 
                     tag is removed and cells expressing an MreB-mCherry fusion 
                     protein do not contain helical filaments |CITS: 
                     [22904287]|. MreB forms dynamic filaments up to 1.5 
                     &micro;m in length |CITS: [23783036]|. Purified MreB can 
                     polymerize into filament bundles; polymerization requires 
                     nucleoside triphosphate (ATP or GTP); purified MreB forms 
                     sheets in the presence of Ca<sup>2+</sup> and ATP or GTP 
                     |CITS: [23235161]|
                     Cells depleted of MreB become spherical and eventually 
                     lyse; MreB interacts with itself and with |FRAME: 
                     EG10609-MONOMER MreC|; MreB localisation is disrupted in 
                     cells depleted in |FRAME: EG10607-MONOMER MrdB|, MreC or 
                     |FRAME: EG10610-MONOMER MreD| (all of which have a 
                     spherical shape) |CITS: [15612918]|. MreB is conditionally 
                     essential; MreB<sup>-</sup> cells are viable under slow 
                     growth conditions and divide as small spheres, their death 
                     under fast growth conditions can be prevented by an 
                     increase in ppGpp levels or an increase in the FtsZ 
                     division protein. Cells depleted of MreB under fast growth 
                     conditions grow as large, non-dividing spheres with 
                     internal membrane systems |CITS: [17993535]|. Spherical, 
                     MreB<sup>-</sup> cells revert back to rod shape if MreB is 
                     expressed from a plasmid; MreB forms anti-parallel double 
                     protofilaments; mutations which disrupt MreB intra or 
                     inter-protofilament formation fail to restore rod shape 
                     |CITS: [24843005]|
                     MreB is a dynamic cytoskeletal protein - it persistantly 
                     rotates around the long axis of the cell; MreB motion is 
                     blocked in strains that cannot synthesise the essential 
                     peptidoglycan component - diaminopimelic acid, and by 
                     antibiotics that inhibit cell wall synthesis |CITS: 
                     [21903929]|. MreB localisation occurs in a curve dependent 
                     manner - MreB is enriched at negatively curved regions of 
                     the cell wall and depleted at positively curved regions. 
                     Cell wall growth is colocalised with MreB which results in 
                     bursts of localised growth |CITS: [24550515]| (consider 
                     also |CITS: [39992125]|). The curvature preference of MreB 
                     varies with growth rate and cell size; the transmembrane 
                     protein |FRAME: EG10015-MONOMER RodZ| acts to modulate 
                     MreB's curvature preference |CITS: [29599448]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [30022070]|). Correlations between MreB filaments 
                     and cell wall curvature are weak |CITS: [32077853]|.
                     MreB interacts directly with the inner membrane via a 
                     predicted N-terminal amphipathic helix; this interaction is
                     required for cell shape maintenance and viability |CITS: 
                     [21816350]|. MreB interacts with RodZ and this interaction 
                     is required for maintenance of cell shape |CITS: 
                     [19078962]|. MreB and RodZ colocalize and rotate together 
                     around the cell circumference; RodZ couples MreB to cell 
                     wall synthesis proteins (PBP2 and/or RodA) which mediates 
                     MreB processive motion as the cell wall is synthesized; 
                     MreB rotation is not absolutely required for rod shape; 
                     MreB rotation may promote robustness to cell wall stress 
                     |CITS: [26396257]|. Membrane associated elongasomes 
                     containing MreB are correlated spatially with nucleoids; 
                     effects of antibiotics on the correlation were examined 
                     |CITS: [40253395]|.
                     The role of MreB in chromosome segregation is not clear. 
                     Cells depleted of MreB segregate their chromosomes in 
                     pairs; overexpression of a defective form of MreB impairs 
                     nucleoid segregation and results in mislocalization of the 
                     chromosomal origin and terminus regions |CITS: [14517265]|.
                     MreB interacts with RNA polymerase <i>in vivo</i> and <i>in
                     vitro</i>; both are required for normal chromosome 
                     segregation in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [16391237]|. Defects 
                     in DNA segregation attributed to the inactivation or 
                     depletion of MreB are instead, a consequence of the 
                     transition from rod shape to sphere; cells treated with the
                     |FRAME: EG10606-MONOMER PBP2| inhibitor, mecillinam, are 
                     spherical and have similar defects in DNA segregation to 
                     MreB depleted cells |CITS: [17581120]|. MreB interacts with
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-2424| likely via its ParC subunit and 
                     regulates its activity <i>in vitro</i>; &Delta;mreB mutants
                     are deficient in Topo IV activity |CITS: [19187760]|.
                     The MreB cytoskeleton duplicates and segregates before the 
                     completion of cytokinesis; transverse MreB ring structures,
                     located near the midcell are intermediates in this process 
                     |CITS: [17978175]|. The ring structures contain MreB, MreC,
                     MreD, MrdB and |FRAME: EG10606-MONOMER MrdA|; assembly of 
                     these proteins is dependent on the presence of the FtsZ 
                     ring |CITS: [19220747]|. MreB appears in a punctate pattern
                     along the cell periphery and forms ring-like structures at 
                     mid-cell in cells undergoing division; MreB is recruited to
                     the Z-ring; MreB interacts with FtsZ <i>in vivo</i> and 
                     this interaction functions to deliver MrcA and MrdA to the 
                     Z ring |CITS: [23756461]|. MreB is implicated in inner 
                     membrane organization; lipid microdomains with distinct 
                     physical properties have been visualised in the <i>E. 
                     coli</i> inner membrane - MreB stabilises microdomains and 
                     modulates the dynamics of lipids and transmembrane proteins
                     |CITS: [26958890]|.
                     In response to hypoosmotic shock, MreB speed, density, and 
                     flux were not significantly affected |CITS: [24821776]|. 
                     Using flow cytometry to differentially detect tetracysteine
                     (TC)-labelled MreB and propidium iodide-labelled DNA in 
                     slow-growing cells (chromosome number &le; 2) found higher 
                     expression of MreB coincided with FtsZ expression levels 
                     after cells entered DNA replication phase of the cell 
                     cycle. Expression levels were also examined under 
                     fast-growth conditions (chromosome number &ge; 4) and under
                     different nutrient conditions |CITS: [39015018]|.
                     The assembly of MreB into a cytoskeleton has been studied 
                     in synthetic cell systems |CITS: [23651045][31932440]|. 
                     MreB interacts with |FRAME: B2170-MONOMER SetB| |CITS: 
                     [14617174]|.
                     MreB is the target of the antibacterial compound A22 
                     [S-(3,4-dichlorobenzyl)isothiourea] |CITS: [16391237]|. 
                     MreB interacts with the T7 bacteriophage encoded growth 
                     inhibitor Gp0.6 |CITS: [25512533]|
                     <i>mreB, mreC</i> and <i>mreD</i> from an operon in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12, largely transcribed as monocistronic 
                     <i>mreB</i> mRNA |CITS: [17090951]|.
                     <i>mre</i>: murein formation gene cluster E |CITS: 
                     [2822655]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [15922599][22154164][25578957][29522748][2 
                     8439631][29522748]|<br>Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [28975672][32424210][34351794]|<br>Comment: |CITS: 
                     [22961859]|"
