                     /product="bifunctional tRNA-dihydrouridine synthase B / 
                     tRNA-dihydrouridine synthase"
                     /label=dusB
                     /note="DusB and |FRAME: EG12022-MONOMER| (DusC) together
                     account for about half of the 5,6-dihydrouridine 
                     modification observed in wild-type cellular tRNA, and 
                     |FRAME: EG11932-MONOMER| (DusA) accounts for the other half
                     |CITS: [11983710]|.
                     Crystal structure of DusB in complex with a tRNA transcript
                     was obtained showing conservation the Dus fold. Despite 
                     some structural similarities DusB was found to have a new 
                     uridine binding mechanism not seen previously in the Dus 
                     family proteins |CITS: [29294097]|.
                     A <i>dusA dusB dusC</i> triple mutant exhibits a complete 
                     lack of 5,6-dihydrouridine modification in cellular tRNA, 
                     whereas each single mutant exhibits a partial reduction, 
                     compared to wild type |CITS: [11983710]|. Using a 
                     <i>&Delta;dusB</i> mutant, the specificity of DusB for 
                     dihydrouridylation of nucleotide 17 (D17) was determined in
                     |FRAME: ARG-tRNAs| (ICG), |FRAME: ILE-tRNAs| (GAU) and 
                     |FRAME: LEU-tRNAs| (CAG) |CITS: [29294097]|. Follow-up 
                     experiments with the &Delta;3 mutant found that mRNAs are 
                     not dihydrouridylated and that the dihydrouridine 
                     nucleotide in 23S rRNA is dihydrouridylated by some other 
                     dihydrouridine synthase than DusA, DusB or DusC |CITS: 
                     [34798057]|. Using a new method that blocks reverse 
                     transcriptase by using rhodamine-labelled dihydrouridine 
                     (called RhoRT-PCR), the <i>dusA dusB dusC</i> triple mutant
                     was found to not prevent the dihydrouridylation of 23S 
                     rRNA, consistent with the previous evidence; instead 
                     dihydrouridylation of 23S rRNA was found to be carried out 
                     with |FRAME: EG12229-MONOMER| |CITS: [39078675]|.
                     Analysis of each single mutant compared to wild type found 
                     reduced 5,6-dihydrouridine modification activity under 
                     paraquat-induced anaerobic conditions, with DusA being the 
                     least sensitive and DusC being the most sensitive to 
                     changes in redox best represented by a decrease in NADPH 
                     availability. Position specificity differed between the 
                     three tRNA-dihydrouridine synthases; kinetics were measured
                     under aerobic conditions |CITS: [39460624]|. 
                     Enterobacterial <i>dusB</i> genes have similarity to 
                     <i>nifR3</i> genes, and the evolution of these genes is 
                     discussed |CITS: [9738943]|. DusB was noted to be a 
                     conserved protein based on sequence and structural 
                     similarities; biochemical characterization found it to be a
                     flavoprotein that binds FMN non-covalently |CITS: 
                     [11574054][29294097]|.
                     Although <i>dusB</i> and <i>fis</i> are cotranscribed, DusB
                     protein is synthesized at very low levels compared to Fis. 
                     An <i>E. coli</i> cell is estimated to contain less than 
                     500 molecules of DusB, less than 0.5% of the level of Fis 
                     |CITS: [22389479]|.
                     DusB: ""<b>d</b>ihydro<b>u</b>ridine <b>s</b>ynthase 
                     <b>B</b>""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [35638108][35737906][40210524]|"
