                     /product="DNA adenine methyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.72"
                     /label=yhdJ
                     /note="Overexpression of YdhJ leads to methylation of
                     genomic DNA at the NsiI recognition sequence 
                     (5'-ATGCAT-3'), and partially purified YdhJ is shown to 
                     methylate this sequence to produce 
                     N<sup>6</sup>-methyladenine in the 3' adenine position of 
                     ATGC<b>A</b>T <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [17400740]|. 
                     <i>ydhJ</i> is not an essential gene, and overexpression of
                     <i>ydhJ</i> does not alter cell morphology |CITS: 
                     [16738554][17400740]|.  Overexpression of <i>yhdJ</i> from 
                     an ASKA plasmid leads to a more than 3-fold increase in the
                     MIC of hypochlorous acid |CITS: [34072091]|.
                     A <b>retracted</b> |CITS: [15342609]| publication |CITS: 
                     [15028690]| claimed that the ""CcrM"" protein is a cell 
                     cycle-regulated DNA adenine methyltransferase that is 
                     essential for viability."
