                     [9843401], [9917069], [9917073], [9973557], [10089432], 
                     [10445878], [10773075], [10814528], [10902565], [10983982],
                     [11087118], [11160893], [11453071], [11501991], [11698387],
                     [11720289], [11779873], [11976298], [12377117], [12814522],
                     [12907714], [15146074]|.
                     Regulation has been described in detail; for a review see 
                     |CITS: [12732305]|.
                     Reviews |CITS: [3326871], [3053566], [2655923], [1764510], 
                     [1720671], [1379076], [1587345], [8721993], [8722019], 
                     [8722022], [8722031], [8722035], [8594571], [9016533], 
                     [9399853], [10494619], [10902565], [11810493], [12370013], 
                     [12762014]|.
                     "
     gene            complement(3420317..3420392)
                     /gene="alaU"
                     /locus_tag="b3276"
                     /label=alaU
     tRNA            complement(3420317..3420392)
                     /gene="alaU"
                     /locus_tag="b3276"
                     /product="tRNA-Ala(UGC)"
                     /label=alaU
                     /note="tRNA(alaU) is one of five alanine tRNAs.
                     tRNA(alaU) interacts with |FRAME: SSRA-RNA| |CITS: 
                     [11387229]|.
                     tRNAs are the adapters that allow synthesis of proteins 
                     from mRNAs.
                     Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome for
                     protein synthesis.
                     There, the tRNA recognizes an RNA codon with its own 
                     three-nucleotide
                     anticodon, thus allowing synthesis of a specific peptide 
                     based on an mRNA
                     template.
                     tRNAs are processed to their active, mature forms by RNA 
                     cleavage
                     and by modification of their bases. RNA cleavage consists 
                     of
                     removal of both 5' and 3' extensions in a multistep process
                     involving
                     many RNases |CITS: [11252717]|. RNases taking part in tRNA 
                     processing include |FRAME: CPLX0-3461|,
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3601|, |FRAME: EG10858-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: EG11620-MONOMER|, and |FRAME: CPLX0-3602|. tRNAs 
                     are also subject to a
                     wide variety of base modifications catalyzed by proteins 
                     such as
                     |FRAME: EG11932-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10595-MONOMER|, |FRAME:
                     G6364-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11344-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7199-MONOMER|, |FRAME: CPLX0-1101|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11779-MONOMER|, |FRAME: G7422-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7449-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11177-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10454-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10967-MONOMER|,
                     and |FRAME: EG11022-MONOMER|.
                     Mature tRNAs are linked via a 3' CCA sequence to their 
                     cognate
                     amino acid in an ATP-dependent fashion by the appropriate 
                     amino-acid-tRNA synthetase, as shown in the |FRAME: 
                     TRNA-CHARGING-PWY|.
                     Subsequently, these charged tRNAs interact with the 
                     ribosome
                     and template mRNA to generate polypeptides. The discovery 
                     of the
                     role of tRNA in protein synthesis is reviewed in detail in 
                     |CITS: [7033244]|.
                     Bases A73 and G20 are key determinants of tRNA(ala) 
                     identity, as is the
                     G3-U70 pairing in the acceptor stem |CITS: 
                     [1799462][9153306]|."
     gene            complement(3420435..3420511)
                     /gene="ileU"
                     /locus_tag="b3277"
                     /label=ileU
     tRNA            complement(3420435..3420511)
                     /gene="ileU"
                     /locus_tag="b3277"
                     /product="tRNA-Ile(GAU)"
                     /label=ileU
                     /note="tRNA(ileU) is one of five isoleucine tRNAs.
                     tRNAs are the adapters that allow synthesis of proteins 
                     from mRNAs.
                     Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome for
                     protein synthesis.
                     There, the tRNA recognizes an RNA codon with its own 
                     three-nucleotide
                     anticodon, thus allowing synthesis of a specific peptide 
                     based on an mRNA
                     template.
                     tRNAs are processed to their active, mature forms by RNA 
                     cleavage
                     and by modification of their bases. RNA cleavage consists 
                     of
                     removal of both 5' and 3' extensions in a multistep process
                     involving
                     many RNases |CITS: [11252717]|. RNases taking part in tRNA 
                     processing include |FRAME: CPLX0-3461|,
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3601|, |FRAME: EG10858-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: EG11620-MONOMER|, and |FRAME: CPLX0-3602|. tRNAs 
                     are also subject to a
                     wide variety of base modifications catalyzed by proteins 
                     such as
                     |FRAME: EG11932-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10595-MONOMER|, |FRAME:
                     G6364-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11344-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7199-MONOMER|, |FRAME: CPLX0-1101|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11779-MONOMER|, |FRAME: G7422-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7449-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11177-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10454-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10967-MONOMER|,
                     and |FRAME: EG11022-MONOMER|.
                     Mature tRNAs are linked via a 3' CCA sequence to their 
                     cognate
                     amino acid in an ATP-dependent fashion by the appropriate 
                     amino-acid-tRNA synthetase, as shown in the |FRAME: 
                     TRNA-CHARGING-PWY|.
                     Subsequently, these charged tRNAs interact with the 
                     ribosome
                     and template mRNA to generate polypeptides. The discovery 
                     of the
                     role of tRNA in protein synthesis is reviewed in detail in 
                     |CITS: [7033244]|.
                     NMR has been used to look at tRNA(ile) structure on its own
                     and interacting
                     with |FRAME: ILES-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [6756467][6340996][3322383][8199246][35275364]|."
     gene            complement(3420580..3422121)
                     /gene="rrsD"
                     /locus_tag="b3278"
                     /label=rrsD
     rRNA            complement(3420580..3422121)
                     /gene="rrsD"
                     /locus_tag="b3278"
                     /product="16S ribosomal RNA"
                     /label=rrsD
                     /note="The 16S rRNA is the RNA component of the small
                     subunit (30S subunit) of the <i>E. coli</i> ribosome. There
                     are seven ribosomal RNA (rRNA) operons, called |FRAME: 
                     EG30084|, |FRAME: EG30085|, |FRAME: EG30086|, |FRAME: 
                     EG30087|, |FRAME: EG30088|, |FRAME: EG30089|, and |FRAME: 
                     EG30090| |CITS: [COLISALII]|.  Each operon contains a 16S 
                     rRNA gene, a 23S rRNA gene, and a 5S rRNA gene (except the 
                     rrsD operon, which contains two 5S rRNA genes) interspersed
                     with various tRNA genes |CITS: [COLISALII]|.  Regarding 
                     nomenclature, ""<i>rrs</i>"" genes encode 16S rRNAs, 
                     ""<i>rrl</i>"" genes encode 23S rRNAs, and ""<i>rrf</i>"" 
                     genes encode 5S rRNAs |CITS: [COLISALII]|. <i>rrsD</i> has 
                     the highest promoter activity of all rRNA operons in 
                     mutants lacking Fis |CITS:[ 26717514]|. <i>rrsD</i> has the
                     lowest promoter activity among rRNA operon mutants lacking 
                     HNS |CITS:[ 26717514]|.
                     The inhibition of rRNA P1 promoters due to the stringent 
                     response is not identical for all promoters; in particular,
                     <i>rrsD</i>p1 is much less reduced than the remaining six 
                     promoters |CITS:[21798983]|.  <i>rrsD</i>p1, formerly 
                     <i>rrnD</i>p1, is the only one of the seven ribosomal P1 
                     promoters that requires GTP instead of ATP as the starting 
                     nucleotide, and the reduced ppGpp sensitivity of this 
                     promoter might result from the different initiating 
                     nucleoside substrate NTP (iNTP) |CITS:[21798983]|.
                     The sequence and structure of the 16S rRNA has been 
                     described |CITS: [5340599], [4868442], [4299552], 
                     [4311070], [4903099], [4910391], [4927343], [4916974], 
                     [4932242], [4941835], [4935734], [4935735], [4935736], 
                     [4569972], [4569973], [4551094], [4562230], [4354606], 
                     [4356617], [4597768], [4620018], [4371816], [4374383], 
                     [4616170], [1106773], [1091918], [1089909], [167351], 
                     [1099457], [1103089], [809718], [780350], [818623], 
                     [787927], [786726], [322143], [379604], [347396], [351619],
                     [358146], [368799], [362531], [477513], [387717], [388420],
                     [392471], [93692], [6153384], [6159576], [6160459], 
                     [6160460], [6996708], [6784944], [6162161], [6163215], 
                     [6170942], [6170632], [7024918], [7029465], [7030394], 
                     [6285311], [6750555], [6188539], [6340066], [6194995], 
                     [6359058], [6363901], [6401121], [6200766], [6207855], 
                     [3882675], [3923479], [3907698], [3935157], [2997731], 
                     [2418415], [3720727], [3299045], [3126826], [3323531], 
                     [2853815], [2451022], [2482760], [2453025], [2457499], 
                     [2837729], [3048381], [3048388], [2457514], [2468977], 
                     [2464291], [2464693], [2642480], [2686708], [2654936], 
                     [2476177], [2675971], [2477554], [2689654], [2179567], 
                     [2408047], [2207172], [2171681], [2236056], [2001727], 
                     [1712940], [1712293], [1872848], [1960712], [1896466], 
                     [1547215], [1374556], [1280994], [1280995], [7681419], 
                     [7691600], [8241431], [7518524], [8643402], [8643405], 
                     [7543481], [7567470], [8721993], [7479839], [8658142], 
                     [8910275], [9122161], [9174095], [9222495], [9171076], 
                     [9271564], [9281424], [9310353], [9354641], [9421514], 
                     [9677294], [9784367], [9799506], [9814765], [9835591], 
                     [9893990], [10347223], [10411129], [10536142], [10747796], 
                     [10793686], [10864503], [11000271], [11090278], [11214183],
                     [11243814], [11327780], [11502181], [11552678], [11812846],
                     [11860288], [11947429], [12126618], [12126619], [12788352],
                     [12836305], [12859155]|.
                     The long leader-trailer helices on both pre-16S rRNA and 
                     pre-23S rRNA transcripts are important for biogenesis of 
                     these RNAs and understanding the leader and trailer 
                     base-pairing; these structures are found in all phyla of 
                     bacteria and archaea |CITS: [39043438]|.
                     The relationship of the 16S rRNA to ribosome assembly, 
                     structure, and function has been characterized |CITS: 
                     [4551094], [4589452], [4594437], [4592471], [4597072], 
                     [4610566], [4371816], [1090843], [1092437], [803646], 
                     [765770], [1219394], [167351], [1098691], [766751], 
