     gene            complement(3423379..3424197)
                     /gene="aroE"
                     /locus_tag="b3281"
                     /label=aroE
     CDS             complement(3423379..3424197)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aroE"
                     /locus_tag="b3281"
                     /product="shikimate dehydrogenase"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.25"
                     /label=aroE
                     /note="Shikimate dehydrogenase (AroE) catalyzes the
                     NADPH-linked reduction of 3-dehydroshikimate to shikimate 
                     |CITS: [3883995]|.  The reaction is part of the |FRAME: 
                     ARO-PWY| pathway, which provides the common precursor for 
                     the biosynthesis of aromatic amino acids, folate, quinones 
                     and enterochelin.
                     Phylogenomic analysis of the shikimate dehydrogenase family
                     showed that the two enzymes encoded in <i>E. coli</i>, AroE
                     and |FRAME: CPLX0-7462 YdiB|, belong to different 
                     subclasses; the authors suggested that YdiB was acquired by
                     horizontal gene transfer.  Common and subclass-specific 
                     catalytic residues and substrate binding motifs were 
                     identified |CITS: [18669580]|.  AroE is 
                     NADP<sup>+</sup>-specific and has much higher catalytic 
                     efficiency than YdiB, which has broader substrate 
                     specificity and can use either NADP<sup>+</sup> or 
                     NAD<sup>+</sup> as a co-substrate |CITS: [12637497]|. 
                     Mutant data and the results from metabolic engineering 
                     experiments strongly suggest that YdiB is unable to replace
                     AroE under normal physiological conditions.
                     AroE transfers hydrogen stereospecifically from the A-side 
                     of NADPH |CITS: [4155957]|.  The structure of AroE with 
                     bound NADP<sup>+</sup> was determined at 1.5 &Aring; 
                     resolution.  Although AroE and YdiB have low sequence 
                     similarity, they have high structural similarity with two 
                     &alpha;/&beta; domains separated by a wide cleft |CITS: 
                     [12637497]|.
                     The cosubstrate specificity of AroE was switched from 
                     NADP<sup>+</sup> to NAD<sup>+</sup> by first engineering a 
                     consensus Rossmann nucleotide binding domain followed by 
                     rational design towards the NAD<sup>+</sup> consensus 
                     binding domain.  A variant with four specific mutations, 
                     S131A, L135A, N149D and V152F, shows a five-fold preference
                     for NAD<sup>+</sup> over NADP<sup>+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [28633311]|.
                     In the ""reverse"" direction, AroE appears to be able to 
                     further dehydrogenate 3-dehydroshikimate to 
                     3,5-dehydroshikimate, which can spontaneously convert to 
                     gallic acid |CITS: [21279669]|.  The physiological 
                     relevance is unclear.
                     Although an <i>aroE</i> mutant is unable to grow on medium 
                     supplemented with shikimate alone, it is able to grow on 
                     medium supplemented with shikimate, phenylalanine and 
                     tyrosine |CITS: [5326114]|.  An <i>aroE</i> mutant 
                     transferred to minimal medium accumulates dehydroshikimate 
                     |CITS: [12363343]|.
                     Shikimate dehydrogenase appears to be expressed 
                     constitutively |CITS: [8426]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [25524738]|"
