     gene            complement(3424202..3424774)
                     /gene="tsaC"
                     /locus_tag="b3282"
                     /label=tsaC
     CDS             complement(3424202..3424774)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tsaC"
                     /locus_tag="b3282"
                     /product="L-threonylcarbamoyladenylate synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.87"
                     /label=tsaC
                     /note="Threonylcarbamoyl-AMP synthase (TsaC) catalyzes the 
                     synthesis of threonylcarbamoyl-AMP, an intermediate in the 
                     biosynthesis of the threonylcarbamoyladenosine 
                     (t<sup>6</sup>A) modification at position 37 of 
                     ANN-decoding tRNAs |CITS: [23072323]|.
                     Although TsaC (RimN) was initially thought to function as a
                     ribosome maturation factor |CITS: [15716138]|, new evidence
                     suggested that TsaC is a component of a heteromultimeric 
                     complex with threonylcarbamoyltransferase activity |CITS: 
                     [21775474]|.  A mixture of purified |FRAME: EG11171-MONOMER
                     TsaD|, |FRAME: G6991-MONOMER TsaB|, TsaC, and |FRAME: 
                     EG11757-MONOMER TsaE| proteins catalyzes formation of 
                     t<sup>6</sup>A in the presence of a tRNA substrate, ATP, 
                     threonine and bicarbonate <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [22378793]|.
                     A crystal structure of TsaC has been determined at 2.0 
                     &Aring; resolution |CITS: [11206077]|.  An NMR solution 
                     structure has provided insight into the catalytic mechanism
                     of the enzyme.  TsaC is monomeric under NMR conditions; key
                     residues that interact with the substrates L-threonine and 
                     ATP were identified |CITS: [26060251]|.
                     It is known that t<sup>6</sup>A biosynthesis requires ATP 
                     |CITS: [4604806][4609459]|.  TsaC was shown to bind ATP and
                     ADP, and the potential nucleotide-binding residues Arg52 
                     and Lys56 are required for function.  <i>tsaC</i> 
                     complements the t<sup>6</sup>A<sup>-</sup> phenotype of a 
                     <i>S. cerevisiae SUA5</i> mutant strain |CITS: [19287007]|.
                      TsaC binds nucleic acids, with a preference for 
                     double-stranded RNA |CITS: [11206077]|.  Specifically, it 
                     interacts with both fully and partially modified (lacking 
                     t<sup>6</sup>A37) tRNA<sup>Thr</sup> with high affinity 
                     |CITS: [19287007]|.  It was also shown to bind the 
                     unmodified anticodon stem-loop of tRNA<sup>Lys</sup>(UUU) 
                     with higher affinity than the t<sup>6</sup>A37-modified 
                     form |CITS: [21775474][23949611]|.
                     <i>tsaC</i> is an essential gene |CITS: 
                     [16738554][19287007]|.  A probable null mutant allele of 
                     <i>tsaC</i> (&Delta;<i>tsaC</i> or <i>rimN141</i>) was 
                     identified as a suppressor of the temperature-sensitive 
                     growth phenotype of the |FRAME: EG10761-MONOMER| mutant 
                     allele <i>prfA1</i> |CITS: [15126466][15716138]|.
                     However, it is not possible to separate the 
                     &Delta;<i>tsaC</i> mutation from the <i>prfA1</i> allele 
                     |CITS: [15126466]|.
                     RimN: ""<b>ri</b>bosome <b>m</b>aturation"" |CITS: 
                     [15716138]|<br>TsaC: ""<b>t</b>hreonylcarbamoyl-6(<b>s</b> 
                     ix)-<b>a</b>denosine"" |CITS: [22378793]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [22905870][25629598]|"
