     CDS             3427049..3427558
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="def"
                     /locus_tag="b3287"
                     /product="peptide deformylase"
                     /EC_number="3.5.1.88"
                     /label=def
                     /note="Peptide deformylase (PDF) releases the formyl group
                     from the amino terminal methionine residue of most nascent 
                     proteins |CITS: [ColiSalII][7896716]|. It interacts 
                     directly with the ribosome at the ribosomal exit tunnel 
                     |CITS: [18288106]| and competes with |FRAME: 
                     EG10570-MONOMER| (MAP) for the same site |CITS: 
                     [23770820]|. PDF is essential in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [8112305]|. A proteome-wide analysis of N termini in the 
                     presence and after depletion of PDF has been performed 
                     |CITS: [26017780]|.
                     The specific physiological function of the N-terminal 
                     formylated methionine residue (fMet) in proteins is 
                     uncertain. It was shown that N-terminal fMet can act as a 
                     co-translational degradation signal |CITS: [26866044]|. 
                     Using the PDF inhibitor, |FRAME: CPD0-1358|, and 
                     time-resolved analyses of proteome and translatome revealed
                     that disruption of PDF function induces different stages of
                     cellular responses with protein misfolding and membrane 
                     stress responses occurring prior to effects on metabolic 
                     pathways |CITS: [35946163]|. Oxidation of N-terminal fMet 
                     prevents deformylation by peptide deformylase (PDF) as 
                     determined using bottom-up proteomics in the presence of 
                     optimal dose of actinonin |CITS: [39499632]|.
                     PDF is a member of the zinc metalloprotease family, 
                     defining a new subfamily |CITS: [7490741][8845003]|. The 
                     physiological metal ion is Fe<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [9610360][9712848]|. As a mononuclear iron enzyme, it is 
                     damaged by micromolar hydrogen peroxide both <i>in 
                     vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i> due to oxidation of the 
                     metal-binding cysteine residue by the hydroxyl radical 
                     generated by the Fenton reaction. Even the apo-enzyme is 
                     rapidly oxidized by H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. The 
                     oxidized enzyme can be reduced back to an active form, 
                     although further exposure to H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     results in irreversible damage. Iron sequestration and 
                     substitution of Mn<sup>2+</sup> for the Fe<sup>2+</sup> 
                     cofactor can protect the enzyme |CITS: [22411989]|. 
                     Superoxide (O<sub>2</sub><sup>-</sup>) also inactivates PDF
                     |CITS: [23678969]|.
                     Systematic <i>in vitro</i> experiments showed that peptide 
                     deformylase requires at least a dipeptide as substrate 
                     |CITS: [9374870]| and has the expected broad substrate 
                     specificity, while the rate of deformylation varies between
                     different substrates |CITS: [9888804][10373378]|. A 
                     reaction mechanism involving the conserved E133 residue has
                     been proposed |CITS: [10651644]|, and theoretical studies 
                     of the catalytic mechanism and metal-ion dependence of the 
                     enzyme were performed |CITS: [17497768][18651766]|. The 
                     C-terminal domain was originally shown to be disordered in 
                     solution and not essential for activity of the enzyme 
                     |CITS: [8641475]|. However, it was shown to interact 
                     directly with the large subunit of the ribosome, and a 
                     strain containing PDF that lacks the C-terminal helix has a
                     severely diminished growth rate |CITS: [18288106]|.
                     NMR solution structures |CITS: [8845003][9665852][10353194 
                     ][10353194][19627112][19636969]| and crystal structures 
                     |CITS: [9374869][9846875][9565550][ 
                     10200158][12126617][12538898][15796505][20112455]| have 
                     been reported. A crystal structure of the C-terminal 
                     ribosome-binding helix together with the ribosome show L22 
                     as the major site of interaction |CITS: [18288106]|.
                     PDF provides an attractive target for the development of 
                     novel antibacterial agents |CITS: [16289392]|; see for 
                     example: |CITS: [17201406][17274774][18093832][27269198][2 
                     7401234][27428963][36154601]|. The enzyme was long thought 
                     to be absent from eukaryotic organisms, but genome 
                     sequences have revealed its presence even in <i>Homo 
                     sapiens</i>, where it appears to be an evolutionary remnant
                     (and thus it remains an excellent drug target) |CITS: 
                     [12924944]|.
                     Def: ""<b>def</b>ormylase"" |CITS: [8112305]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8199241][8955898][10931273][18400172]|"
