     gene            3427573..3428520
                     /gene="fmt"
                     /locus_tag="b3288"
                     /label=fmt
     CDS             3427573..3428520
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fmt"
                     /locus_tag="b3288"
                     /product="bifunctional 
                     10-formyltetrahydrofolate:L-methionyl-tRNA(fMet) 
                     N-formyltransferase / 
                     10-formyldihydrofolate:L-methionyl-tRNA(fMet) 
                     N-formyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.2.9"
                     /label=fmt
                     /note=" 
                     10-formyltetrahydrofolate:L-methionyl-tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> 
                     <i>N</i>-formyltransferase (Fmt) attaches a formyl group to
                     the free amino group of methionyl-tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> 
                     |CITS: [6989606][1624424]|. There are two types of 
                     methionine-specific tRNAs in <i>E. coli</i>; the |FRAME: 
                     Initiation-tRNAmet| is used in the initiation of 
                     translation, while |FRAME: Elongation-tRNAMet| is used 
                     during peptide chain elongation. The timely formylation of 
                     the methionine-charged initiator tRNA that is catalyzed by 
                     Fmt is important in restricting the use of the 
                     methionylated initiator tRNA specifically for initiation, 
                     rather than chain elongation |CITS: 
                     [8331078][30608556][32795532]|.
                     While the specific biological function of the formylated 
                     N-terminal fMet residue in proteins is still being studied,
                     it has been shown that N-terminal fMet can act as a 
                     co-translational degradation signal |CITS: [26866044]|, and
                     formylation has been shown to be important in the fidelity 
                     of translation initiation |CITS: [30608556][32795532]|. 
                     Another possible function of formylation of N-terminal Met 
                     residues is to prevent methionine oxidation as <i>in 
                     vivo</i> ablation of <i>fmt</i> increases oxidized 
                     methionines in proteins, which has follow-on effects |CITS:
                     [39499632]|.
                     Substrate recognition determinants in Fmt |CITS: [8887566] 
                     [9030604][9391059][9843398][9843487][9927661][10891086]| 
                     and specificity determinants within the tRNA<sup>fMet</sup>
                     |CITS: [1917939][1917940][1373194][140 
                     9632][7515283][8346229][7779813][8557626][10788620][1208716
                     8]| have been studied. Upon interaction with the acceptor 
                     stem of tRNA<sup>fMet</sup>, a previously unstructured 
                     insertion loop in the N-terminal domain of Fmt undergoes a 
                     structural change by induced fit |CITS: [9927661]|. 
                     Overexpression of Fmt can rescue the activity of tRNA 
                     mutants that are normally poor substrates for Fmt |CITS: 
                     [7538134][12718519]|. D-amino acid-charged 
                     tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> can act as a substrate for Fmt at low 
                     efficiency |CITS: [18515548]|.
                     Lysine residues in the linker region are important for 
                     enzymatic activity of Fmt, while the C-terminal domain is 
                     required for non-specific binding of tRNA |CITS: 
                     [10694387]|.
                     Fmt is monomeric |CITS: [6989606][8727328]|. Crystal 
                     structures of the enzyme alone |CITS: [8887566]| and in a 
                     complex with formyl-methionyl-tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> |CITS: 
                     [9843487]| have been solved at 2.0 &Aring; and 2.8 &Aring; 
                     resolution, respectively.  An N-terminal Rossmann-fold 
                     domain is linked to a C-terminal domain resembling an OB 
                     fold which is able to bind tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> |CITS: 
                     [8887566]|.
                     <i>fmt</i> appears to be constitutively expressed |CITS: 
                     [8432722]|. <i>fmt</i> mutations result in a growth defect 
                     and heat sensitivity |CITS: [1624424][10428776]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [8199241][7507918][8722017][8955898][11860363]|"
