     gene            3429877..3431253
                     /gene="trkA"
                     /locus_tag="b3290"
                     /label=trkA
     CDS             3429877..3431253
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="trkA"
                     /locus_tag="b3290"
                     /product="NAD-binding component of Trk potassium
                     transporters"
                     /label=trkA
                     /note="Early studies in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 identified
                     four separate K<sup>+</sup> uptake systems: two 
                     constitutive systems [TrkA (also called Trk) and TrkD (also
                     called Kup)], a high affinity system (Kdp) and a 
                     non-saturable system TrkF |CITS: [4578]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [320207][359759][6405784][3896791]|. Although initially 
                     thought to be a single system TrkA/Trk activity was later 
                     shown to result from the activity of two low affinity, high
                     rate K<sup>+</sup> transporters (|FRAME: TRKG-MONOMER 
                     ""TrkG""| and |FRAME: TRKH-MONOMER ""TrkH""|) which 
                     function in conjunction with |FRAME: TRKA-MONOMER 
                     ""TrkA""|, a peripheral membrane protein that binds NAD+ 
                     and is essential for TrkG/H activity |CITS: 
                     [2674131][1987159][8412700]|.
                     TrkA is a peripheral membrane protein that is loosely bound
                     to the cytoplasmic side of the inner membrane; when 
                     over-produced, TrkA is present mainly in the soluble 
                     fraction; no membrane bound TrkA is detected in |FRAME: 
                     EG12304 ""<i>trkE</i>""|, <i>trkG</i> or <i>trkH</i> mutant
                     strains indicating that the products of these genes are 
                     required for the normal membrane association of TrkA |CITS:
                     [2674131]|. Sequence analysis suggests that TrkA consists 
                     of two similar halves which each contain a predicted 
                     NAD<sup>+</sup> binding domain; purified, renatured TrkA 
                     binds NAD(H) |CITS: [8412700]|.
                     TrkA is a cytoplasmic, KTN (K+ transport, nucleotide 
                     binding) (also referred to as RCK - regulate the 
                     conductance of K<sup>+</sup>) domain containing protein 
                     (see |CITS: [19523906][19523906]|). A more complete 
                     understanding of Trk mediated K<sup>+</sup> transport and 
                     the role of TrkA has been obtained in <i>Vibrio</i> spp. 
                     (see for example |CITS: 
                     [21317882][23598339][25011047][31992706]|.
                     Deletion of <i>trkA</i> (BW25113 &Delta;<i>trkA::kan</i>) 
                     is associated with depolarization of membrane potential, 
                     consistent with the hypothesis that TrkA regulates gating 
                     of a non-selective, TrkH ion channel |CITS: [31992706]| 
                     <i>trk</i>: <U>tr</U>ansport of <U>K</U> |CITS: [4942756 
                     ]|"
