     gene            3431383..3431793
                     /gene="mscL"
                     /locus_tag="b3291"
                     /label=mscL
     CDS             3431383..3431793
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mscL"
                     /locus_tag="b3291"
                     /product="large conductance mechanosensitive channel"
                     /label=mscL
                     /note="MscL is a mechanosensitive channel of large
                     conductance. MscL converts mechanical tension in the cell 
                     membrane into an electrochemical response. Upon osmotic 
                     downshift water flows into a cell, increasing pressure on 
                     the membrane which activates MscL and allows solutes to 
                     exit. MscL acts as a pressure release valve, preventing 
                     cell lysis due to increased osmotic pressure.
                     The protein was originally discovered by patch clamp 
                     studies on protein fractions reconstituted into liposomes 
                     |CITS: [7511799][9074781]|. MscL conductance is 2.5 
                     nanosiemens in <i>E. coli</i> spheroplasts |CITS: 
                     [7690260]|. MscL activates at pressures close to those 
                     causing cell lysis |CITS: [8661505]|. MscL was the first 
                     cloned protein shown to respond to mechanical force by 
                     opening a large aqueous pore. The export of ions is fairly 
                     nonspecific, with slight selectivity for cations over 
                     anions; conductance is unsaturable (up to 2M KCl) |CITS: 
                     [10352145]|. MscL transfers cytoplasmic proteins including 
                     thioredoxin, elongation factor Tu (EF-Tu), and DnaK to the 
                     periplasm upon shock |CITS: [9756908][10613892]|. 
                     MscL-mediated protein excretion is positively regulated in 
                     response to osmotic and translation stress |CITS: 
                     [29382730]|. MscL will allow passage of macromolecules 
                     smaller than 6.5 kDa |CITS: [17142294]|. Gating of MscL is 
                     induced by lateral tension within the lipid bilayer |CITS: 
                     [7690260][16142922][16731564][17996439]|. The bilayer 
                     tension dependence of MscL may be modulated by changes in 
                     pH mediated through a charged cluster of amino acids 
                     located within the cytoplasmic helices of MscL |CITS: 
                     [16387769]|.
                     MscL, located within the cytoplasmic membrane, is a 
                     homooligomer. Each monomer consists of two transmembrane 
                     regions connected by a periplasmic loop with the N and C 
                     termini located in the cytoplasm |CITS: [8890153]|. MscL is
                     capable of forming complexes composed of two, three, four, 
                     or six subunits |CITS: [8890153]|, however a pentameric 
                     form is believed to be the  primary functional complex 
                     |CITS: [10501827][16322576]|. A detailed structural model 
                     of MscL (based in part, on the crystal structure of MscL 
                     from <i>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</i> |CITS: [9856938]|)  
                     has been developed and a gating mechanism proposed |CITS: 
                     [11463635][11217861]|. Extensive analyses of the gating 
                     mechanism of MscL have been performed |CITS: 
                     [8876191][9141355][9635742][9736761][10102934][10512816] 
                     [11325711][11325722][10625640][11852077][11960017][1197204 
                     7][12172537][12172538][12198539][12601086] 
                     [12670944][12963374][14507677][15024097][15041651][1511140 
                     2][15111403][15226501][15277576][15447384] 
                     [15684093][16199508][15748727][16156671][16935962][1711421 
                     7][18390626][18390625][18515388][22826215][23146938][244610
                     12][25223610][27329693][29222414][27753526][37347044][38501
                     596]|. MscL may |CITS: [21354398]| or may not |CITS: 
                     [27596282]| form clusters in the membrane. MscL fluorescent
                     fusion proteins, expressed at basal levels, are evenly 
                     distributed and mobile throughout the membrane; 
                     overexpression results in cluster formation, likely due to 
                     self-association of fluorescent tags |CITS: [27596282]|.
                     A strain lacking MscS, MscK and MscL exhibits defects in 
                     Z-ring placement in the presence of the septation 
                     inhibitor, cephalexin |CITS: [21810996]|.
                     <i>mscL</i> expression is induced by RpoS upon entry into 
                     stationary phase and by osmotic stress |CITS: [14671322]|. 
                     <i>mscL</i> expression is post-transcriptionally regulated 
                     by an <i>arfA</i>-derived |FRAME: RNA0-441 small antisense 
                     RNA| to control MscL-mediated excretion of cytoplasmic 
                     proteins (ECP activity) in response to hypoosmotic and 
                     translational stress |CITS: [37012050][29382730]|.
                     MscL is targeted to the membrane by the SRP pathway and is 
                     inserted independently of the Sec translocon |CITS: 
                     [17113597][19664056]|. Conflicting results on the 
                     requirement of YidC for membrane insertion of MscL monomers
                     have been reported |CITS: [17113597][19664056]| and a role 
                     for YidC in the assembly of homopentameric MscL has been 
                     suggested |CITS: [19664056]|.
                     Seven mechanosensitive ion channels have been characterised
                     in <i>E. coli</i> K-12: MscL, |FRAME: EG11160-MONOMER 
                     ""MscS""|, |FRAME: G6255-MONOMER ""MscK""|, |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7985 ""MscM""|, |FRAME: CPLX0-7982 ""YnaI""|, |FRAME:
                     CPLX0-7983 ""YbiO""| and |FRAME: CPLX0-7981 ""YbdG""|.  A 
                     strain lacking all seven of these proteins fails to 
                     demonstrate channel opening in patch clamp experiments 
                     |CITS: [22874652]|. <i>mscS mscL</i> double deletion 
                     mutants exhibit a lysis phenotype upon osmotic downshock 
                     |CITS: [10202137]|.
                     Parabens have been shown to induce MscL activity which 
                     allows leakage of cell contents, suggesting a mechanism for
                     their capability as antimicrobial agents |CITS: 
                     [15770478]|. MscL is implicated in transport of 
                     (dihydro)streptomycin into the cell |CITS: 
                     [25205267][27280286]|. MscL is a target for the development
                     of novel antibiotics |CITS: [30359098]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [31177589][31978161]|).
                     <i>mscL</i>: <u>m</u>echano<u>s</u>ensitive <u>c</u>hannel 
                     of <u>l</u>arge conductance
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8809939][9004483][9074781][11540118][1035 
                     2145]|<BR>Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [9916243][10066837][10322175][10498951][10 
                     548036][10625597][11275684][11369249][11746234][11988468][ 
                     12626684][12629971][12948773][15165739][17350877][22853893 
                     ][24859800]|"
