     gene            complement(3431790..3432008)
                     /gene="arfA"
                     /locus_tag="b4550"
                     /label=arfA
     CDS             complement(3431790..3432008)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="arfA"
                     /locus_tag="b4550"
                     /product="alternative ribosome-rescue factor A"
                     /label=arfA
                     /note="ArfA is a protein involved in the rescue of stalled 
                     ribosomes |CITS: [21062370]|. ArfA binds to the vacant 
                     A-site of the stalled ribosome and then recruits |FRAME: 
                     EG10762-MONOMER RF2|, which hydrolyzes peptidyl-tRNA |CITS:
                     [22857598][22922063]|.
                     The location of ArfA within the stalled ribosome has been 
                     mapped by hydroxyl radical probing |CITS: [25355516]| and 
                     cryo-EM |CITS: 
                     [27934701][27906160][27906161][28077875][28300532]|. ArfA 
                     is sandwiched between the decoding center of the ribosome 
                     and the switch loop of RF2. The C-terminus of ArfA is 
                     located in the mRNA entry channel on the 30S ribosomal 
                     subunit, a position that would normally be occupied by the 
                     3' end of a full-length mRNA |CITS: 
                     [27934701][27906160][27906161][28077875][28300532]|. ArfA 
                     recruits RF2 and induces a conformational change in RF2 and
                     the decoding center, acting as a functional equivalent of 
                     the stop codon |CITS: 
                     [26554029][27906160][27906161][28077875][28300532]|. Amino 
                     acids in ArfA that are important for its specific 
                     interaction with RF2 were investigated by site-directed 
                     mutagenesis |CITS: [28077875]|.
                     ArfA was found to be associated with the 50S subunit of the
                     ribosome |CITS: [21062370]| and binds tRNA |CITS: 
                     [22857598]|.
                     ArfA is functionally redundant with |FRAME: EG30100 
                     SsrA|-tagging and trans-translation of peptides encoded by 
                     mRNAs that lack an in-frame stop codon. Ribosome rescue by 
                     ArfA does not involve targeted degradation of the 
                     translated peptide |CITS: [21062370]|.
                     Expression of ArfA is itself regulated by the SsrA tagging 
                     system. The <i>arfA</i> mRNA contains a hairpin region 
                     within the coding region that is subject to |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3281| cleavage. RNase III processing of the 
                     <i>arfA</i> transcript produces a trucated transcript 
                     cleaved after the Gly55 codon and lacking a stop codon and 
                     a |FRAME: RNA0-441 ""54-nucleotide small RNA""| which may 
                     be processed further |CITS: [21435036][21952205]|. The 
                     <i>arfA</i>-derived small RNA acts as an antisense RNA to 
                     regulate |FRAME: EG11180 MscL|-mediated excretion of 
                     cytoplasmic proteins when cells experience hypoosmotic and 
                     translational stress; the <i>arfA</i> small RNA 
                     down-regulates <i>mscL</I> by reducing transcript half-life
                     |CITS: [37012050]|. The truncated ArfA mRNA is subject to 
                     trans-translation and degradation |CITS: 
                     [21435036][21952205]|.
                     Even in the absence of RNase III, <i>arfA</i> mRNA appears 
                     to be prematurely truncated |CITS: [21952205]|. In the 
                     absence of SsrA (tmRNA) tagging, the truncated ArfA is 
                     released from the ribosome and functions in 
                     tmRNA-independent ribosome rescue |CITS: 
                     [21435036][21952205]|. Thus, functional ArfA only 
                     accumulates when the trans-translation system is 
                     insufficiently active |CITS: [21952205]|. This regulatory 
                     strategy is conserved |CITS: [22791716]|.  Full-length ArfA
                     is unstable |CITS: [21952205]|.
                     When SsrA and ArfA are simultaneously depleted, a class of 
                     stable polypeptidyl-tRNAs accumulates, indicating that 
                     aberrant translation products are produced intrinsically 
                     |CITS: [22162769]|.
                     <i>arfA</i> RNA had been detected during efforts to 
                     identify noncoding RNAs; however, the length of the RNA 
                     detected appeared to be too short to encode the entire 
                     predicted 72 residue polypeptide |CITS: [11553332]|. 
                     <i>arfA</i> and <i>ssrA</i> are synthetically lethal |CITS:
                     [21062370]|.
                     ArfA: <b>a</b>lternative <b>r</b>ibosome rescue 
                     <b>f</b>actor <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [21062370]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [21117278][22909071][24706739][24782844][2 
                     4782856][25135187][25446863][25874843]|"
