     gene            complement(3434414..3435034)
                     /gene="rpsD"
                     /locus_tag="b3296"
                     /label=rpsD
     CDS             complement(3434414..3435034)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpsD"
                     /locus_tag="b3296"
                     /product="trifunctional 30S ribosomal subunit protein S4 /
                     30S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rpsD
                     /note="The S4 protein, a component of the 30S subunit of
                     the ribosome, functions in the assembly of the 30S 
                     ribosomal subunit, the mRNA helicase activity of the 
                     ribosome, the regulation of translation of a subset of 
                     ribosomal proteins, and transcription antitermination of 
                     rRNA operons.
                     S4 interacts directly with helical elements at the 5' 
                     domain of the 16S rRNA |CITS: 
                     [3820298][7836385][2685320][19190093][19616559]|.  The 
                     N-terminal domain of unbound S4 is dynamically disordered, 
                     which is exploited to initiate S4 binding to helix 16 in 
                     the unfolded five-way junction of 16S rRNA |CITS: 
                     [21156135]|.  S4 binding stabilizes the five-way junction 
                     and the helix 18 pseudoknot; both become tightly folded 
                     within the first minute of S4 binding.  Other components of
                     the five-way junction require more time and perhaps other 
                     30S proteins to achieve their final conformation |CITS: 
                     [21821049]|.  Single-molecule analysis of S4 binding to 16S
                     rRNA shows that the native S4-rRNA complex is formed via a 
                     stable non-native intermediate |CITS: [24522531]|.  S4 
                     binding modulates the rRNA dynamics |CITS: [24522531]|, 
                     suggesting a mechanism of conformational capture followed 
                     by mutual induced fit that allows both S4 and 16S rRNA to 
                     retain dynamic motion |CITS: [24522527]|.  The ability of 
                     both S4 and S7 to bind 16S rRNA by themselves indicates 
                     that they function as initiator proteins for the assembly 
                     of the 30S subunit of the ribosome.  The S20, S16, S15, S6,
                     and S8 subunits appear to depend on S4 for assembly |CITS: 
                     [2461734]|.
                     The S4 protein is involved in the regulation of translation
                     of the other ribosomal proteins encoded by the &alpha; 
                     operon, RpsM (S13), RpsK (S11), RplQ (L17) and S4 itself 
                     |CITS: [6445562][3309351][6211432]|.  The &alpha; operon 
                     leader region is required for translational repression by 
                     S4 |CITS: [3309351]|; S4 specifically interacts with a 
                     double pseudoknot structure which overlaps with the 
                     ribosome binding site and initiation codon for RpsM |CITS: 
                     [3912010][2443719][2443720][2470510][2112408][8643369]|. 
                     This structure has a relatively narrow phylogenetic 
                     distribution |CITS: [23396277]|.  There may be a second 
                     binding site for S4 upstream of the RplQ open reading frame
                     |CITS: [6379605]|.  The same protein domain appears to be 
                     responsible for both mRNA and rRNA binding |CITS: 
                     [7559430][3697092]|.
                     S4 can also act as a general transcription antitermination 
                     factor similar to NusA; it associates with RNA polymerase 
                     and is involved in rRNA operon antitermination |CITS: 
                     [11447122]|.
                     S4 influences translational fidelity |CITS: [325371]|. 
                     Certain mutations in <i>rpsD</i> confer a ""ribosomal 
                     ambiguity"" (ram) phenotype, which is characterized by 
                     decreased growth rate, increased streptomycin sensitivity, 
                     and increased errors in translation |CITS: 
                     [4943794][6355760][6757661][372747]|.  Ribosomes containing
                     the S4 <i>rpsD12</i> allele display the ram phenotype; 
                     these ribosomes appear to exploit only the initial phase of
                     tRNA selection, thus reducing discrimination against 
                     near-cognate aa-tRNAs |CITS: [20603079]|.  Cells carrying 
                     the <i>rpsD14</i> allele have a mutator phenotype |CITS: 
                     [12897024]|.  Many ram mutations map to the interface 
                     between S4 and S5; additional <i>rpsD</i> mutants were 
                     isolated in a random mutagenesis screen |CITS: [25548247]|.
                      The ribosome was found to have mRNA helicase activity, and
                     mutations in the S3 and S4 subunits impair this activity 
                     |CITS: [15652481]|.
                     <i>ramA</i>: ""<b>r</b>ibosomal <b>am</b>biguity""
                     Review: |CITS: [24522527]|
                     "
