     gene            complement(3435474..3435830)
                     /gene="rpsM"
                     /locus_tag="b3298"
                     /label=rpsM
     CDS             complement(3435474..3435830)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpsM"
                     /locus_tag="b3298"
                     /product="trifunctional 30S ribosomal subunit protein S13
                     / 30S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rpsM
                     /note="The S13 protein is a component of the 30S subunit
                     of the ribosome, playing a key role in subunit association 
                     and the fidelity of translocation.  The crystal structure 
                     of the ribosome |CITS: [16272117]|, cryo-EM reconstructions
                     |CITS: [12809609]|, and crosslinking experiments |CITS: 
                     [7031269]| show that the S13 protein contacts the L5 
                     protein of the large subunit of the ribosome, forming 
                     bridge B1b.  During the ratchet-like motion of the 
                     ribosome, L5 and S13 reorganize in different constellations
                     |CITS: [20643101]|.  Together with helix 38 of 23S rRNA, 
                     S13 forms bridge B1a between the subunits. S13 is thought 
                     to be involved in maintenance of pre-translocation state 
                     |CITS: [14536072][15876367]|. <i>In vitro</i>, a small 
                     subunit of the ribosome lacking S13 associates only poorly 
                     with the large subunit; the presence of tRNA and mRNA in 
                     the assay restores 70S complex formation |CITS: 
                     [15876367]|.  In the absence of S13, initiation complex 
                     formation is defective |CITS: [15876367]|, and the ribosome
                     shows increased rates of factor-independent translocation 
                     |CITS: [15876367]|.
                     <i>In vivo</i>, lack of S13 causes a deficit in polysome 
                     formation and the appearance of abundant free 30S and 50S 
                     subunits |CITS: [15876367]|.  Ribosome modulation factor 
                     binds near S13, L2, and L13 |CITS: [12473202]|.  S13 was 
                     also shown to crosslink to IF2 and IF3 |CITS: [6349681]| 
                     S13 forms a complex with S19 in order to bind to 16S rRNA 
                     at its specific site |CITS: [324809][3279034]|.  The 
                     C-terminal domain of S13 is primarily responsible for rRNA 
                     binding; its N-terminal domain may be responsible for 
                     association with S19 |CITS: [3903659]|.  S13 can be 
                     crosslinked to 16S rRNA |CITS: [2423965][2437527]|; the 
                     sites of interaction have been identified by hydroxyl 
                     radical probing |CITS: [2459390][7585249][8718688]|.  S13 
                     has been placed in the S7 assembly branch of the 30S 
                     subunit |CITS: [10376881][15525707][21030658]|.
                     S13 crosslinks to tRNA at the P site of the ribosome and 
                     weakly to tRNA at the A site |CITS: [8524654]|.
                     Structural modelling suggested that the C-terminal region 
                     of S13 interacts with the P-site tRNA.  Studies with 
                     <i>rpsM</i> mutants lacking this region show that the 
                     C-terminal tail is not essential for the function of S13, 
                     but such mutants have slower growth rates.  <i>In 
                     vitro</i>, deletion of the C-terminal tail of S13 causes a 
                     lower affinity of the 30S subunit for tRNAs |CITS: 
                     [15308780]|.
                     S13 was found to be phosphorylated |CITS: [19469554]|.
                     The |FRAME: EG10903-MONOMER S4| protein is involved in the 
                     regulation of translation of the other ribosomal proteins 
                     encoded by the &alpha; operon, S13, RpsK (S11), RplQ (L17) 
                     and S4 itself |CITS: [6445562][6251471][3309351][6211432]|.
                      The &alpha; operon leader region is required for 
                     translational repression by S4 |CITS: [3309351]|; S4 
                     specifically interacts with a double pseudoknot structure 
                     which overlaps with the ribosome binding site and 
                     initiation codon for S13 |CITS: 
                     [3912010][2443719][2443720][2470510][2112408][8643369]|. 
                     This structure has a relatively narrow phylogenetic 
                     distribution |CITS: [23396277]|.
                     <i>rpsM</i> is not essential for viability of <i>E. 
                     coli</i>, but an <i>rpsM</i> deletion strain has a severe 
                     growth defect |CITS: [15876367][17277072]|.  An S13 mutant 
                     decreases <i>in vivo</i> translation efficiency and acts as
                     antisuppressor of some suppressor tRNAs |CITS: [8193163]|. 
                     Suppressors of <i>rimM</i> point and deletion mutations 
                     localize to the C terminus of S13 |CITS: 
                     [9226267][15496525]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [26880335]|
                     "
