     gene            complement(3436125..3437456)
                     /gene="secY"
                     /locus_tag="b3300"
                     /label=secY
     CDS             complement(3436125..3437456)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="secY"
                     /locus_tag="b3300"
                     /product="multifunctional Sec translocon subunit SecY /
                     SecYEG translocon / SecYEG-SecA complex / Sec 
                     Holo-Translocon"
                     /label=secY
                     /note="The SecY inner membrane protein is the core
                     component of the heterotrimeric SecYEG preprotein 
                     translocase; SecY forms the protein conducting channel with
                     channel plug and hydrophobic seal; SecY contains a signal 
                     sequence binding pocket and cytosolic loops that interact 
                     with partner proteins (see |CITS: [16154141]| and |CITS: 
                     [24153188]|).
                     SecY is required for efficient protein translocation across
                     the cytoplasmic membrane |CITS: 
                     [6297749][6339072][6389119][2823262]|. A temperature 
                     sensitive SecY mutant (ts24) accumulates the precursor form
                     of periplasmic and envelope proteins (OmpA and MBP) in the 
                     cytoplasm when grown at the non-permissive temperature 
                     |CITS: [6370688]|. SecY supports the posttranslational 
                     translocation of proOmpA across the cytoplasmic membrane 
                     |CITS: [3017993]|. SecY is essential when translocation is 
                     reconstituted in proteoliposomes <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [2139227][1830665]|; SecY, SecE and an unidentified 
                     polypeptide termed 'band1' (later shown to be SecG |CITS: 
                     [8034620]|) form a stable complex which supports precursor 
                     protein translocation <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [2167176][1531482]|. Precursor proteins interact with SecY 
                     during translocation |CITS:[8428583]|. SecY binds synthetic
                     signal peptide <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [15476412]|.
                     The insertion of mannitol permease into the inner membrane 
                     requires SecY |CITS: [1447197]|. SecY interacts with FtsY 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS:[15815684]|; FtsY binds SecY to form 
                     a 400-kD FtsY-SecY translocon complex |CITS: [16923832]|. 
                     During cotranslational targeting, FtsY interacts with the 
                     cytosolic loops of SecY which also serve as docking sites 
                     for the ribosome and SecA |CITS: [21255212][26459600]|. 
                     SecY is an integral inner membrane protein |CITS: 
                     [3004955]|. Sec Y contains contains 10 transmembrane 
                     domains, 5 periplasmic domains and 6 cytosolic domains. The
                     N and C-termini are located in the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [2828030]|. SecY oligomers mediate translocation: one copy 
                     of the SecY complex forms the translocation pore while a 
                     nontranslocating SecY molecule provides the interaction 
                     site for SecA |CITS: [17418789]|. SecY molecules can 
                     associate with one another in the membrane but single 
                     molecules are sufficient for both co- and 
                     post-translational translocation <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [22927464]|.
                     Cardiolipin binds to SecY; SecY variants which have reduced
                     affinity for cardiolipin are associated with decreased 
                     stimulation of SecA ATPase activity and less efficient 
                     Sec-mediated protein translocation <i>in vitro</i>; 
                     additionally the PMF stimulation of translocation observed 
                     in wild type SecYEG inner membrane vesicles is lost when 
                     using these SecY variants |CITS: [30012626]|.
                     The SecY translocon is involved in CDI (contact-dependent 
                     growth inhibition) toxin import |CITS: [33531386]| (see 
                     also |CITS: [26305955]|). SecY is the receptor protein for 
                     group 2 CDI ionophore toxins |CITS: [39591464]|. 
                     <i>secY</i>: <U>sec</U>retion |CITS: 
                     [6339072]|<BR><i>prlA</i>: <U>pr</U>otein 
                     <U>l</U>ocalization: |CITS: [7011570][7040375]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [2202723][1406280]|<BR>Related reviews: 
                     |CITS: [2202721]|"
