     gene            complement(3438085..3438588)
                     /gene="rpsE"
                     /locus_tag="b3303"
                     /label=rpsE
     CDS             complement(3438085..3438588)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpsE"
                     /locus_tag="b3303"
                     /product="trifunctional 30S ribosomal subunit protein S5 /
                     30S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rpsE
                     /note="The S5 protein is a component of the 30S subunit of
                     the ribosome.  It was suggested that S5 is positioned to 
                     have access to the interface between the 30S and 50S 
                     subunits of the ribosome |CITS: [9973556]|.
                     The N-terminal half of S5 contains the sites of mutations 
                     that confer resistance to spectinomycin |CITS: 
                     [129673][4904980][4273819]| and binds non-specifically to 
                     helix 34 of the 16S rRNA |CITS: [8658142][2459389]|.  S5 
                     may be involved in modulating the conformation of the 16S 
                     rRNA |CITS: [9271564]|.  S5 can be cross-linked to mRNA 
                     |CITS: [1712292]| and tRNA |CITS: [2660905]|.
                     The S5 protein is acetylated at the N-terminus |CITS: 
                     [363452][10094780]|; mutants in the alanine 
                     acetyltransferase enzyme, |FRAME: EG10851-MONOMER RimJ|, 
                     are temperature sensitive |CITS: [385889][2828880]|. 
                     N-terminal acetylation of S5 and S18 correlates with 
                     correct folding of the platform and central pseudoknot 
                     domains of 16S rRNA |CITS: [24207057]|.  An S5 G28D mutant 
                     shows altered cell growth, folding of 16S rRNA, and 
                     translational fidelity |CITS: [17053085]|.  The G28D mutant
                     has a defect in 16S rRNA maturation |CITS: [20176963]|; 
                     overexpression of RimJ, even when lacking its 
                     acetyltransferase activity, suppresses this defect |CITS: 
                     [18466225][20176963]|.
                     S5 influences translational fidelity.  Certain mutations in
                     <i>rpsE</i> confer a ""ribosomal ambiguity"" (ram) 
                     phenotype, which is characterized by decreased growth rate,
                     increased streptomycin sensitivity, and increased errors in
                     translation |CITS: [4589342][4589343][1105158]|. Many ram 
                     mutations map to the interface between S4 and S5; 
                     additional <i>rpsE</i> mutants were isolated in a random 
                     mutagenesis screen |CITS: [25548247]|.
                     <i>spcA</i>: ""spectinomycin"""
