     gene            complement(3438966..3439499)
                     /gene="rplF"
                     /locus_tag="b3305"
                     /label=rplF
     CDS             complement(3438966..3439499)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rplF"
                     /locus_tag="b3305"
                     /product="trifunctional 50S ribosomal subunit protein L6 /
                     50S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rplF
                     /note="The L6 protein is a component of the 50S subunit of
                     the ribosome and is involved in the late stage assembly of 
                     50S ribosomal subunits.
                     L6 interacts with 23S rRNA |CITS: [1702198][9873002]|. 
                     Interaction appears to be cooperative with L3 |CITS: 
                     [9873002]|.  L6 crosslinks to the stem-loop structure in 
                     domain V of 23S rRNA |CITS: [6170935]|, but assembles to 
                     domain VI |CITS: [2465415]|.  The L6 binding site within 
                     domain VI has been identified |CITS: [10781065]|.
                     L6 is altered in a gentamicin-resistant mutant |CITS: 
                     [342908]|.  Ribosomes of gentamicin-resistant L6 mutants 
                     have altered misreading properties; the L6 alteration is 
                     thought to change a parameter of the ribosome function 
                     involved in codon recognition |CITS: [369594]|.  L6 also 
                     participates in the binding of dibekacin to ribosomes 
                     |CITS: [6268595]|.
                     Mutations in L6 cause a cold-sensitive growth defect as 
                     well as defective assembly and maturation of the 30S and 
                     50S subunits of the ribosome at low temperature |CITS: 
                     [7026977]|.  Specifically, a mutant depleted of L6 
                     accumulates 50S subunit precursor particles that lack L6 
                     |CITS: [27003253]|."
