     gene            complement(3439512..3439904)
                     /gene="rpsH"
                     /locus_tag="b3306"
                     /label=rpsH
     CDS             complement(3439512..3439904)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpsH"
                     /locus_tag="b3306"
                     /product="trifunctional 30S ribosomal subunit protein S8 /
                     30S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rpsH
                     /note="The S8 protein is a component of the 30S subunit of
                     the ribosome and also functions in the post-transcriptional
                     regulation of the ribosomal protein genes encoded in the 
                     <i>spc</i> operon.
                     The S8 protein binds to 16S rRNA |CITS: 
                     [4577755][4606155][1091486]| in the absence of other 
                     ribosomal proteins |CITS: [4937571]|.  S8 binding is not 
                     absolutely required for assembly of the 30S ribosomal 
                     subunit platform, but it influences the organization of the
                     16S rRNA central domain |CITS: [12823975]|.  Sites within 
                     16S rRNA where S8 interacts have been determined |CITS: 
                     [6338481][6208366][3016664][3279390][7689052][7515489]|, 
                     and the secondary and tertiary structure of the binding 
                     region has been investigated |CITS: 
                     [3323531][7937081][9122161][9677294]|.  The unique cysteine
                     residue present in S8 is essential for 16S rRNA binding 
                     |CITS: [3521721]|, although it may not be involved in RNA 
                     recognition |CITS: [8490021]|.  Other sequence determinants
                     important for 16S rRNA binding have been investigated 
                     |CITS: [1735715][8490021][9056763]|.  The affinity of S8 
                     for 16S rRNA and thus the stability of the complex is 
                     correlated with the optimal growth temperature of various 
                     organisms |CITS: [12914937]|.
                     In addition to its function in the ribosome, the ribosomal 
                     protein S8 binds to a site overlapping and sequestering the
                     L5 initiation codon in the <i>spc</i> operon mRNA.  It 
                     thereby represses initiation of L5 (<i>rplE</i>) 
                     translation |CITS: [2464692][6450328][6445562]|, and 
                     translational coupling ensures the coordinated regulation 
                     of genes downstream of <i>rplE</i> |CITS: [3049533]|. 
                     However, it is not known whether S8 also regulates the 
                     translation of the last two open reading frames of the 
                     <i>spc</i> operon, <i>secY</i> and <i>rpmJ</i>.
                     Expression of the first two genes of the <i>spc</i> operon,
                     encoding ribosomal proteins L14 and L24, is regulated by 
                     the same interaction of S8 with <i>spc</i> operon mRNA via 
                     ""retroregulation"" |CITS: [2643112]|. Processing of 
                     <i>spc</i> mRNA was shown to be stimulated by 
                     overproduction of S8 |CITS: [3049533]|, and this effect is 
                     dependent on polynucleotide phosphorylase and RNase II 
                     |CITS: [2643112]|.
                     The target site on mRNA for S8 feedback regulation shows 
                     structural similarity to the region of 16S rRNA that binds 
                     S8 |CITS: [6262737][2464691][2464692]|.  The affinity of S8
                     for its mRNA binding site is approximately 5-fold lower 
                     than for its rRNA binding site due to differences in the 
                     secondary structure of the sites |CITS: [7515489]|.  A 
                     crystal structure of the complex between S8 and <i>spc</i> 
                     operon mRNA has been determined at 2.8 &Aring; resolution 
                     and shows similarity to the structure of the S8/helix 21 
                     complex in the small ribosomal subunit |CITS: [15146079]|. 
                     "
