     gene            complement(3445040..3445645)
                     /gene="rplD"
                     /locus_tag="b3319"
                     /label=rplD
     CDS             complement(3445040..3445645)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rplD"
                     /locus_tag="b3319"
                     /product="trifunctional 50S ribosomal subunit protein L4 /
                     50S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rplD
                     /note="The L4 protein is a component of the 50S subunit of
                     the ribosome and also regulates the expression of S10 
                     operon at both the transcriptional and posttranscriptional 
                     level.  The functions of L4 in ribosome assembly, 
                     attenuation, and translational regulation of the operon are
                     separable |CITS: [2442760][8846294]|.
                     Addition of L4 to an <i>in vitro</i> protein synthesis 
                     reaction inhibits the synthesis of the promoter-proximal 
                     proteins in the S10 operon, suggesting that L4 may be an 
                     inhibitor of translation |CITS: [6996835]|.  <i>In 
                     vivo</i>, overproduction of L4 was found to reduce the 
                     synthesis of S10 operon mRNA |CITS: [6157482]|.  The 
                     steady-state growth rate-dependent control of the S10 
                     operon expression requires additional factors |CITS: 
                     [2152906]|.
                     L4 stimulates premature termination (transcription 
                     attenuation) at a NusA-dependent terminator site 30 bases 
                     upstream of the first structural gene of the S10 operon, 
                     and termination requires the function of NusA |CITS: 
                     [6380754][2157208]|.  The attenuator hairpin region is 
                     sufficient for NusA-dependent pausing, and a second hairpin
                     region immediately upstream of the attenuator hairpin is 
                     necessary for L4 to prolong the pause |CITS: 
                     [1285127][7844821][8636042]|.  Structural and sequence 
                     requirements for L4-mediated transcription termination 
                     within the S10 leader region have been determined |CITS: 
                     [12022224]|; the first 150 bases of the S10 leader region 
                     are sufficient for L4-mediated attenuation control |CITS: 
                     [1692593]|, and a 64-nucleotide sequence is required for L4
                     interaction with the S10 leader mRNA |CITS: [12738792]|.  
                     Binding of L4 to the leader region induces structural 
                     changes in the mRNA |CITS: [12738792]|.  A region of 110 
                     bases within domain I of 23S rRNA competes with paused 
                     transcription complex for binding of L4 |CITS: 
                     [1764518][7685080]|.
                     L4 directly interacts with the C-terminal domain of |FRAME:
                     EG10859-MONOMER| and inhibits its activity.  A number of 
                     mRNAs are stabilized by ectopic expression of L4 |CITS: 
                     [19144914]|.  L4 also interacts directly with the 
                     <i>tnaC-tnaA</i> intergenic region mRNA and represses 
                     translation of TnaA, potentially by inducing structural 
                     changes in the mRNA |CITS: [32123036]|.
                     L4 interacts with the 5' segment of 23S rRNA |CITS: 
                     [800986][3278299][6998491][7556101][9693099]|.  The L4 and 
                     L24 binding sites in 23S rRNA localize to a small fragment 
                     |CITS: [10781065]| and may be a key element for rRNA 
                     folding in the early assembly pathway for the ribosome 
                     |CITS: [11345438]|.  L4 and L24 cooperatively bind and 
                     stabilize the structure of an rRNA fragment containing 
                     their binding site |CITS: [30705137]|.  The L4 binding 
                     region within domain I of 23S rRNA has three-dimensional 
                     structural similarity to its binding region on the S10 
                     operon leader mRNA where it inhibits its own translation 
                     |CITS: [9813114][12738792]|.
                     The extended loop region of L4 (amino acids 40-88) 
                     contributes to the lining of the peptide exit tunnel in the
                     ribosome and is contacted by the growing peptide chain 
                     |CITS: [15983062]|.  Initial results suggested that 
                     deletion of this loop does not affect function of L4 |CITS:
                     [13130133]|; however, subsequent experiments showed a slow 
                     growth phenotype, cold sensitivity, altered 50S subunit 
                     assembly, and impaired translation speed |CITS: 
                     [27257065]|.  The K63E point mutation that results in 
                     erythromycin resistance alters the decoding accuracy of the
                     ribosome |CITS: [15509870]|.  Various mutations in L4 
                     affect peptide-induced pausing of translation |CITS: 
                     [18586934]|.
                     L4 is a component of the binding site for erythromycin on 
                     the ribosome |CITS: [3275651]|.  An L4 K63E mutant is 
                     resistant to erythromycin, has a cold-sensitive growth 
                     defect, and shows a defect in 50S subunit assembly |CITS: 
                     [4870510][4589347][7928988]|.  The mutation reduces the 
                     rate of erythromycin association as well as equilibrium 
                     binding |CITS: [19197244]|.  Additional L4 mutants that 
                     confer erythromycin resistance have been isolated; all map 
                     to the extended loop region of L4 |CITS: [17956547]|.  A 
                     large number of additional alleles was isolated by 
                     recombineering |CITS: [19150357]|.  L4, L5, and L21 bind to
                     erythromycin cooperatively |CITS: [2256953]|.  The sequence
                     of the L4 loop was found to contribute to the species 
                     specificity of the antimicrobial peptide phazolicin |CITS: 
                     [31594941]|."
