     gene            complement(3446318..3446629)
                     /gene="rpsJ"
                     /locus_tag="b3321"
                     /label=rpsJ
     CDS             complement(3446318..3446629)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpsJ"
                     /locus_tag="b3321"
                     /product="multifunctional 30S ribosomal subunit protein
                     S10 / NusB-S10 complex / 30S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rpsJ
                     /note="The S10 protein (NusE) is a component of the 30S
                     subunit of the ribosome.  From within its location in the 
                     ribosome, S10 plays a role in linking transcription and 
                     translation; in a separate complex with NusB, it plays a 
                     role in regulating transcription antitermination.
                     S10 can be crosslinked to tRNA in the ribosomal P site 
                     |CITS: [6759118]| and may contact 16S rRNA in two separate 
                     domains |CITS: [2459390]|.  Mutations in S10 confer 
                     resistance to the antibiotic tigecycline |CITS: 
                     [26124155]|.
                     As part of the ribosome, S10 also interacts with several 
                     non-ribosomal proteins.  The |FRAME: EG10667-MONOMER NusG| 
                     protein interacts with S10 and may thereby physically link 
                     transcription and translation |CITS: [20413501]|. 
                     Interaction of the NTD of |FRAME: EG10839-MONOMER RfaH| 
                     with its <i>ops</i> DNA target releases its CTD, which then
                     switches to a conformation that can interact with S10 and 
                     activate translation |CITS: [22817892]|.
                     Outside of the ribosome, S10 is involved in the regulation 
                     of transcription termination |CITS: 
                     [6088061][6233610][2987961]|.  S10 forms a heterodimer with
                     NusB |CITS: [1731086]| which can bind to the <i>boxA</i> 
                     sequence of <i>rrn</i> operons |CITS: [7678781][11884128]|.
                      Detailed analysis of the assembly pathway for the 
                     <i>boxA</i>-containing core antitermination complex has 
                     been performed.  S10 was shown to bind RNA non-specifically
                     and increases the affinity of NusB for <i>boxA</i> RNA 
                     |CITS: [16109710]|.  Because overexpression of S10 in a 
                     strain lacking NusB rescues transcription antitermination, 
                     S10 is the critical component of the NusB-S10 complex, 
                     while NusB appears to serve as a loading factor |CITS: 
                     [19111659]|.  An S10 mutant that lacks the extended 
                     ribosome binding loop (aa 46-67, S10<sup>&Delta;loop</sup>)
                     retains its regulatory function in transcription 
                     termination, but can not perform the essential function of 
                     S10, i.e. likely its role in the ribosome/translation.  The
                     interactions of S10 with the ribosome or with NusB are 
                     mutually exclusive |CITS: [19111659]|.  Within the NusB-S10
                     complex, S10 can interact with |FRAME: RPOB-MONOMER RpoB|, 
                     the &beta; subunit of RNA polymerase |CITS: 
                     [1831176][26043358]|. S10's extraribosomal function was 
                     first discovered in the context of bacteriophage &lambda; 
                     biology.  The <i>nusE71</i> mutation in the <i>rpsJ</i> 
                     gene was shown to affect regulation of transcription 
                     termination by the bacteriophage &lambda; antiterminator N 
                     |CITS: [6453343]|. The <i>nusE71</i> allele is a point 
                     mutation, changing a single amino acid, Ala86, to Asp.  It 
                     is the only <i>rpsJ</i> allele known to have a 
                     Nus<sup>-</sup> phenotype |CITS: [7730297]|.  The 
                     <i>nusE71</i> mutation may be specific for &lambda; 
                     antitermination; it appears to have no effect on 
                     <i>boxA</i>-mediated increase in the rate of transcription 
                     of <i>rrn</i> operons |CITS: [10383769]|.
                     NusE: ""<b>N</b> <b>u</b>tilization <b>s</b>ubstance 
                     <b>E</b>""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11018144][19111651][20413480]|"
