                     /locus_tag="b3322"
                     /label=gspB
     CDS             complement(3446867..3447286)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gspB"
                     /locus_tag="b3322"
                     /product="type II secretion system protein GspB"
                     /label=gspB
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains a cluster of genes 
                     (<i>gsp</i>) predicted to encode the components of a type 
                     II secretion system (T2SS); the cluster consists of two 
                     divergent operons |FRAME: TU0-14374 <i>gspC-O</i>| and 
                     |FRAME: TU-8433 <i>gspAB</i>| which are transcriptionally 
                     repressed by |FRAME: PD00288 nucleoid-structuring protein 
                     H-NS| under standard laboratory conditions |CITS: 
                     [8655552][11118204]|. Overexpression of the full cluster 
                     supports secretion of (plasmid expressed) |FRAME: 
                     EG11237-MONOMER| and (plasmid expressed) |FRAME: 
                     G7706-MONOMER pseudopilin GspG| in a <i>hns</i> mutant 
                     strain |CITS: [11118204][12754241]|.
                     The inner membrane proteins GspA and GspB contribute to 
                     |FRAME: G7703-MONOMER secretin| assembly and transport in 
                     some gram-negative T2SSs (see |CITS: 
                     [22466878][30767847]|).
                     <b>Comment from EcoGene:</b>
                     <i>gspB</i> was initially identified as an ORF that might 
                     be the 11.3 kDa ""initiation protein"" PinO synthesized in 
                     the absence of isoleucine in <i>relA</i> stains (|CITS: 
                     [1925011][1938934]|). But GspB is predicted to be 15.9 kDa 
                     and has isoleucine residues and there is no evidence that 
                     <i>gspB</i> encodes the PinO peptide or that it binds 
                     calcium. No homology to calmodulin or other calcium-binding
                     proteins is predicted. <i>pioO</i> was the prior gene name 
                     assigned to <i>pinO</i> since the gene name <i>pin</i> was 
                     already taken."
