     gene            complement(3459608..3460084)
                     /gene="bfr"
                     /locus_tag="b3336"
                     /label=bfr
     CDS             complement(3459608..3460084)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="bfr"
                     /locus_tag="b3336"
                     /product="bifunctional bacterioferritin / ferroxidase"
                     /EC_number="1.16.3.1"
                     /label=bfr
                     /note="<i>bfr</i> encodes the iron storage hemoprotein 
                     bacterioferritin |CITS: [2661540]|.  Bacterioferritin and 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3969 ferritin| are distantly related |CITS: 
                     [1959654][1431878]|.  Under normal growth conditions, 
                     bacterioferritin contains approximately 15% of the cell's 
                     iron, with 70% of iron found in |FRAME: 
                     SUPEROX-DISMUTFE-MONOMER SodB|; when cells are grown at 
                     high extracellular iron concentration, ferritin stores the 
                     bulk of the iron |CITS: [22094925]|.  Notably, a <i>bfr</i>
                     mutant does not exhibit a defect in iron storage |CITS: 
                     [10049371]|.
                     Bacterioferritin exists as a 24-subunit homomultimer |CITS:
                     [7032515][2648005][2644932][1904771][7664064]| that forms a
                     hollow sphere of 95 &Aring; |CITS: [7032515]| or 119 to 128
                     &Aring; |CITS: [2648005]| in diameter. Structural and 
                     physical characteristics of Bfr and Bfr complexes have been
                     examined in detail |CITS: [2644932][22 
                     76500][1783168][8477705][8389131][8388809][9867433][1707748
                     0][18946693][19439409][21488690]|. A homodimeric form of 
                     Bfr |CITS: [1904771][8477705]| is favored under low pH 
                     conditions |CITS: [1904771]|. Minimizing salt concentration
                     combined with adjusting the pH to 8.5 or lower reversibly 
                     shifts the equilibrium between dimeric and 24-meric 
                     assemblies, a property that has been used for 
                     biotechnological application of protein encapsulation 
                     within the ferritin cavity |CITS: [35969005]|.
                     Residues that affect assembly of the 24-subunit complex 
                     have been identified |CITS: 
                     [14719947][20139406][26115686]|.  Bacterioferritin contains
                     heme and also binds non-heme iron |CITS: 
                     [8477705][8389131][8388809][15299499][7559480]|.  The 
                     oxidized di-Fe<sup>3+</sup> non-heme iron located at the 
                     ferroxidase site is stable and acts as a catalytic 
                     cofactor, cycling its oxidation state and driving 
                     Fe<sup>2+</sup> oxidation |CITS: 
                     [19391621][26474305][26551523]|.
                     Bacterioferritin interacts with the ferredoxin, |FRAME: 
                     EG11181-MONOMER Bfd| |CITS: [8639572]|.
                     Entry of iron through the bacterioferritin protein shell is
                     facilitated via a B-type channel, providing access to the 
                     ferroxidase center |CITS: [25512375]|.  The dinuclear metal
                     ion binding site/ferroxidase center and the mechanism by 
                     which iron is oxidized for storage within the structure of 
                     the bacterioferritin complex have been investigated.  Iron 
                     storage proceeds in three phases. During the initial phase,
                     two Fe<sup>2+</sup> ions rapidly bind to each ferroxidase 
                     site, followed by a slower phase during which 
                     Fe<sup>2+</sup> is oxidized to Fe<sup>3+</sup>.  A third, 
                     much slower phase leads to mineralization of the iron core 
                     (storage of iron) when more than 50 Fe are added to Bfr 
                     |CITS: [8224163][8526846][8995278][9337865][10769150]|.  An
                     E128R/E135R mutant Bfr that can only assemble into the 
                     homodimeric form accumulates 8-10 Fe<sup>3+</sup> ions per 
                     molecule |CITS: [14719947]|.  Hydrogen peroxide is an 
                     intermediate product in both the ferroxidation and 
                     mineralization reactions |CITS: [12124394]|.  Core 
                     formation is most efficient at pH 6.5, is dependent on the 
                     ferroxidase reaction |CITS: [14636073]|, and is enhanced in
                     the presence of phosphate, which is incorporated into the 
                     iron core |CITS: [14673713]|.  Acidic residues on the inner
                     surface of the Bfr complex |CITS: [19439409]| and the 
                     aromatic residues Y25, Y58 and W133 near the diiron site 
                     |CITS: [26474305]| are important for iron core formation.  
                     A transient radical is formed on Y25 |CITS: [26474305]|.
                     Heme attachment to Bfr occurs via an unusual bis-methionine
                     axial ligation; methionine residues from two individual 
                     subunits coordinate one protoporphyrin IX molecule |CITS: 
                     [7664064]|.  Mutation of residue Met52 results in a 
                     bacterioferritin complex that lacks the 12 hemes normally 
                     present in the wild-type complex and causes the complex to 
                     bind more iron than the wild-type complex |CITS: 
                     [7559480]|.  The heme ligands were found to influence the 
                     rate of iron mobilization from the core, while the 
                     ferroxidase center of Bfr is not required |CITS: 
                     [21106523]|.  Binding of a flavin and electron transfer to 
                     the heme group of Bfr has been modeled |CITS: [22680309]|. 
                     Bound heme also decreases the rate of iron oxidation at the
                     ferroxidase sites and increases the rate of iron core 
                     formation |CITS: [22458666]|.
                     A <i>bfr</i> mutant exhibits wild-type iron accumulation 
                     upon entering stationary phase in the presence of exogenous
                     iron, in contrast to <i>ftnA</i> and <i>ftnA bfr</i> 
                     mutants, which show a defect in iron storage |CITS: 
                     [10049371]|.  A <i>fur ftnA</i> or <i>fur bfr</i> double 
                     mutant exhibits a defect in resistance to hydroperoxides 
                     |CITS: [10049371]|.  A strain with a transposon insertion 
                     in <i>bfr</i> shows increased fitness during selection for 
                     thiamine diphosphate (TPP) production in a competitive 
                     growth assay; a <i>bfr</i> null mutant produces a higher 
                     extracellular TPP titer |CITS: [28768189]|.
                     Bfr expression is repressed by the small RNA RyhB, and RyhB
                     itself is subject to negative regulation by the Fur protein
                     |CITS: [11917098]|.  Bacterioferritin is observed under 
                     conditions of abundant iron |CITS: [2667998]|. <i>bfr</i> 
                     mRNA stability during glucose starvation is diminished in 
                     the absence of |FRAME: CPLX0-3281| function |CITS: 
                     [16309817]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [1431878][1646016][7887220][9056851][98899 
                     81][12829269][12727864][22788214][22609271]|"
