                     /product="translation elongation factor Tu 1"
                     /label=tufA
                     /note="Elongation factor Tu (EF-Tu) is the most abundant
                     protein in <i>E. coli</i>. In its GTP-bound (active) form, 
                     EF-Tu binds aminoacylated tRNAs. At the decoding site of 
                     the ribosome, this ternary complex is ""tested"" for a 
                     codon-anticodon match; if the proper aminoacyl-tRNA has 
                     been found, GTP is hydrolyzed and EF-Tu and GDP dissociate 
                     from the ribosome, while the aminoacyl-tRNA remains bound 
                     to the ribosome.
                     In <i>E. coli</i>, EF-Tu is encoded by two genes, 
                     <i>tufA</i> and <i>tufB</i>.
                     Proteomic analyses indicate that EF-Tu is maximally 
                     expressed during stress response |CITS: [18318713]|.
                     Using a method that distinguishes N-phosphorylation from 
                     O-phosphorylation, N-phosphorylation was detected <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [33277485]|.
                     Deletion of this gene influences heterogeneity of UAG and 
                     UGA read-through |CITS: [37670559]|.
                     When exposed to dessication-rehydration stress, the main 
                     proteins found in aggregates were EF-Tu, |FRAME: 
                     TRYPTOPHAN-MONOMER| and |FRAME: EG11415-MONOMER| in 
                     addition to ribosomal proteins |CITS: [37764358]|.
                     The activity of EF-Tu is blocked when the sarcin-ricin loop
                     in the ribosome is deleted, as shown in single-turnover 
                     decoding assays |CITS: [39875174]|."
