     gene            complement(3464759..3466873)
                     /gene="fusA"
                     /locus_tag="b3340"
                     /label=fusA
     CDS             complement(3464759..3466873)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fusA"
                     /locus_tag="b3340"
                     /product="elongation factor G"
                     /label=fusA
                     /note="Elongation factor G (EF-G) is an essential protein
                     that facilitates the translocation of the ribosome by one 
                     codon along the mRNA molecule, shifting the resulting 
                     deacylated-tRNA from the ribosomal P-site to the E-site and
                     the peptidyl-tRNA from the A-site to the P-site. The 
                     activity requires GTP hydrolysis |CITS: 
                     [8985244][12762047]|. EF-G is also involved in ribosome 
                     disassembly and recycling |CITS: [12762054]|.
                     Acetylation site K618 was found to be the key site 
                     influencing translational elongation rate but not growth 
                     rate |CITS: [33128612]|.
                     Single molecule analysis revealed structural and kinetic 
                     heterogeneity in the ribosome populations. EF-G 
                     preferentially and rapidly engages with the 
                     pre-translocation (PRE) ribosome complex with a rotated 
                     small ribosomal subunit to promote translocation to the 
                     unrotated POST state |CITS: [26926435]|. In another single 
                     molecule study (on an unspecified strain of <i>E. 
                     coli</i>), a small fraction of ribosomes was found to 
                     spontaneously frameshift on mRNA with ""slippery 
                     sequences"" or with frameshifting-promoting EF-G mutants 
                     |CITS: [35869111]|. In a cell-free system of translation, 
                     high concentrations of EF-G without |FRAME:EG12099-MONOMER|
                     triggers the translocation of two tRNAs before the peptidyl
                     transfer occurs when there are consecutive |FRAME: PRO| 
                     amino acids and is referred to as 'mistranslocation'. The 
                     peptidyl-tRNA in the P-site then 'drops-off' the ribosome 
                     releasing a truncated peptide lacking the C-terminal region
                     after which translation can be reinitiated by |FRAME: 
                     EG12099-MONOMER| with the Pro-tRNA at the A-site. This 
                     process also takes place within the cell |CITS: 
                     [35188576]|.
                     EF-G mutants in the Thr48 residue in complex with the 
                     ribosome were observed to be in a highly compact form, more
                     compact than free EF-G or wild-type EF-G in complex with 
                     the ribosome, which underwent extension when GTP was 
                     hydrolyzed |CITS: [40210088]| .
                     Much of the work on <i>E. coli</i> EF-G and its 
                     translocational process has been carried out on pathogenic 
                     <i>E. coli</i> strain MRE600 (e.g. |CITS: [15448679]|). 
                     FusA: ""fusidic acid""
                     Review: |CITS: 
                     [10937868][23812707][24362468][27325008][31600135]|"
