     CDS             complement(3466901..3467440)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpsG"
                     /locus_tag="b3341"
                     /product="trifunctional 30S ribosomal subunit protein S7 /
                     30S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rpsG
                     /note="The S7 protein is a component of the 30S subunit of
                     the ribosome.
                     S7 binds to 16S rRNA |CITS: [786727][6338481][2423965][242 
                     9836][2438658][3279390][7556101][7681943][7525976][11105763
                     ]|. The ability of both S4 and S7 to bind 16S rRNA by 
                     themselves indicates that they function as initiator 
                     proteins for the assembly of the 30S subunit of the 
                     ribosome.  The S9, S13, S19, S10, S14, and S3 subunits 
                     depend on S7 for assembly |CITS: 
                     [2461734][15525707][20207951]|.  The K35 residue of S7 
                     appears to be required for efficient assembly |CITS: 
                     [10772857]|.
                     S7 crosslinks to the anticodon loop of tRNAs, indicating 
                     its location close to the decoding site of the ribosome 
                     |CITS: [2685749][9292501]|.  S7 also crosslinks to mRNA 
                     |CITS: [2690012][1651232]|; the C-terminal region of S7 is 
                     the predominant crosslinking site |CITS: [10606263]|.  S7 
                     was also shown to crosslink to IF3 |CITS: 
                     [7000779][7020604][6349681]|.  Footprinting of IF3 on 16S 
                     rRNA and modelling of IF3-30S subunit interactions 
                     suggested that IF3 is located near S7 and S11 |CITS: 
                     [11684020]|.  S7 and S11 functionally interact |CITS: 
                     [12937172]|.  In addition, S7 is the major crosslinking 
                     target of the 4.5S RNA component of the signal recognition 
                     particle |CITS: [16043501]|.
                     S7 is a translational feedback repressor |CITS: [7016341]|,
                     regulating the synthesis of itself and the other gene 
                     products encoded together with S7 in an operon: S12, S7, 
                     EF-G and possibly EF-Tu |CITS: [7507167]|. Independently 
                     expressed S7 is not subject to translational 
                     autoregulation; therefore, regulation of S7 is achieved 
                     through regulation of translation of S12 and translational 
                     coupling with S12 |CITS: [7507167]|.  Two regions located 
                     between <i>rpsL</i> and <i>rpsG</i> interact with S7 and 
                     are important for S7-mediated repression |CITS: 
                     [8289236][17027976][18620530]|.  The same determinants of 
                     S7 that are responsible for binding 16S rRNA are also 
                     involved in binding to its own mRNA |CITS: [11160889]|. The
                     antibiotic puromycin can be crosslinked to an 
                     amino-terminal peptide of S7 |CITS: [8034577]|.  A deletion
                     that truncates the &beta;-hairpin of S7, &Delta;R77-Y84, 
                     increases ribosomal frameshifting, indicating that the 
                     ribosomal E (exit) site plays a role in reading frame 
                     maintenance |CITS: [19095617]|.
                     "
