     gene            complement(3467537..3467911)
                     /gene="rpsL"
                     /locus_tag="b3342"
                     /label=rpsL
     CDS             complement(3467537..3467911)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpsL"
                     /locus_tag="b3342"
                     /product="trifunctional 30S ribosomal subunit protein S12
                     / 30S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rpsL
                     /note="The S12 protein is a component of the 30S subunit
                     of the ribosome and plays a role in translational accuracy.
                     S12 is added late in the biogenesis of the 30S subunit 
                     |CITS: [21030658]|.  The N-terminal non-globular domain of 
                     S12 appears to play a role in 30S subunit assembly |CITS: 
                     [24442609]|.  Ribosome assembly factors such as RimM and 
                     RimP influence the assembly kinetics |CITS: 
                     [20188109][25313868]|.
                     Certain mutations in S12 cause streptomycin resistance 
                     |CITS: [4868224][4181187]|.  The streptomycin resistance 
                     phenotype is recessive |CITS: [14832197]|.  Some mutations 
                     causing streptomycin resistance also lead to higher 
                     translational accuracy |CITS: [322139][10872330]| and a 
                     lower rate of protein chain elongation |CITS: [321423]|. 
                     The mutations appear to exert their effect by altering the 
                     structure of 16S rRNA |CITS: [2477554]|.  Both S12 and S5 
                     appear to facilitate changes in 16S rRNA structure during 
                     translation |CITS: [9271564]|.  S12 influences the 
                     selection of aminoacyl-tRNAs and discrimination both before
                     and after GTP hydrolysis |CITS: [387774]|, which may be 
                     explained by its influence on P site binding of charged 
                     tRNAs |CITS: [8605885]|.  S12 and S13 are also thought to 
                     be involved in maintenance of the pre-translocation state 
                     of the ribosome |CITS: [14536072]|.  The kinetic properties
                     of ribosomes with various mutant forms of S12 have been 
                     investigated |CITS: [1569565]|.  Streptomycin-resistant 
                     <i>rpsL</i> mutants show reduced A-site mRNA cleavage 
                     within paused ribosomes compared to wild type |CITS: 
                     [19776006]|.  Ribosomes containing the S12 <i>rpsL141</i> 
                     protein display the restrictive phenotype; these ribosomes 
                     increase the rejection rate of near-cognate aa-tRNAs during
                     the proofreading phase of tRNA selection |CITS: 
                     [20603079]|. S12 was shown to crosslink to IF3 |CITS: 
                     [4616691][7000779][7020604]|, IF1 |CITS: [6349681]| and 
                     EF-G |CITS: [7040673][17395204]|, interacts with |FRAME: 
                     EG10335-MONOMER| |CITS: [21596992]|, and protects the 
                     pseudoknot structure of the 530 stem/loop region |CITS: 
                     [1712293]|, the 900 stem/loop region and the 5' terminus of
                     the 16S rRNA |CITS: [2459389]| from chemical probes. S12 
                     functionally interacts with the 1409-1491 region |CITS: 
                     [1725261][17512991]|, the 912 region |CITS: [17512991]|, 
                     and G530 |CITS: [8289238]| of 16S rRNA as well as the base 
                     at position 2661 in 23S rRNA |CITS: [1765106]|.  The 
                     Tyr116-Lys119 residues of S12 appear to be located near the
                     peptidyl transferase center of the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [7559446]|.  Investigation of the ribosome structure in the
                     EF-Tu-bound state showed a bonding network that connects 
                     two sites of S12 with the anticodon and the CCA arm of 
                     aa-tRNA |CITS: [19020518]|.  Molecular dynamics simulations
                     of the coupling between S12, the ribosomal A-site, and some
                     16S rRNA elements have been performed |CITS: [25379961]|.
                     S12 can bind RNA with broad specificity and act as an RNA 
                     chaperone <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [7958841]|, although only 
                     weakly <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [10393192]|.  S12 is thought 
                     to stimulate splicing of Group I introns by resolving 
                     misfolded RNAs and assisting proper folding |CITS: 
                     [7958841]|.  S12 was found to be required for efficient 
                     splicing of the phage T4 <i>td</i> gene <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [9573049]|.
                     S12 contains a &beta;-methylthio modification at the Asp88 
                     residue of the mature protein |CITS: [8844851][10094780]|. 
                     The enzyme responsible for this modification, |FRAME: 
                     G6435-MONOMER RimO|, has been identified |CITS: 
                     [18252828][21169565]|.  An Asp88Ala mutation is dominant 
                     lethal |CITS: [17967466]|.  Proteins that interact with the
                     conserved loop that contains Asp88 have been identified 
                     |CITS: [23305560]|.
                     <i>rpsL</i> is an essential gene |CITS: [17967466]|.  A 
                     number of site-directed mutants have been evaluated for the
                     ability to substitute for the wild-type gene |CITS: 
                     [17967466]|.  Mutations in two conserved loops of S12 show 
                     increased levels of translational fidelity |CITS: 
                     [17967466]|; later, a large number of mutations in 
                     <i>rpsL</i> that affect the accuracy of translation were 
                     isolated |CITS: [21575643][24530394]|.  Although 
                     detrimental to growth, certain <i>rpsL</i> mutations convey
                     a fitness advantage for survival inside macrophages |CITS: 
                     [23089747]|.  A suppressor mutation for the 
                     temperature-sensitive ts118 allele of <i>rpsL</i> maps to 
                     |FRAME: EG10270 <i>era</i>| |CITS: [3162077]|.
                     AsuB: ""<b>a</b>nti<b>su</b>ppressor mutation"" |CITS: 
                     [3918006]|
                     Review: |CITS: [22649682]|
                     "
