     gene            complement(3471266..3471856)
                     /gene="slyD"
                     /locus_tag="b3349"
                     /label=slyD
     CDS             complement(3471266..3471856)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="slyD"
                     /locus_tag="b3349"
                     /product="trifunctional FKBP-type peptidyl-prolyl
                     cis-trans isomerase SlyD / peptidyl-prolyl cis-trans 
                     isomerase / Ni(2+) transfer to HypB"
                     /EC_number="5.2.1.8"
                     /label=slyD
                     /note="SlyD is a peptidyl prolyl
                     <i>cis/trans</i>-isomerase (PPIase) and metallochaperone. 
                     The protein has a C-terminal metal binding domain and binds
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> and Ni<sup>2+</sup> ions |CITS: [8300624]| 
                     as well as Cu<sup>+</sup> and Co<sup>2+</sup> <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [22047179]|. Its PPIase activity is 
                     regulated by Ni<sup>2+</sup> binding to the histidine-rich 
                     C-terminus |CITS: [9188461]|. Ni<sup>2+</sup> binding 
                     increases the thermodynamic stability of both SlyD domains 
                     |CITS: [21770389]|. <i>In vivo</i> under anaerobic 
                     conditions, SlyD can specifically influence the balance of 
                     nickel ions in the cell |CITS: [22047179]|. SlyD serves as 
                     a Ni<sup>2+</sup> reservoir for [NiFe]-hydrogenase 
                     biosynthesis |CITS: [22085337]|. The binding of 
                     Cu<sup>+</sup> and Ni<sup>2+</sup> to two C-terminal 
                     peptides has been studied |CITS: [22178668]|.
                     A protein consisting of the N-terminal 165 amino acids of 
                     SlyD (SlyD*) efficiently catalyzes refolding of reduced and
                     carboxymethylated RNase T1; the enzyme is inhibited by 
                     FK506, and kinetics of the refolding activity have been 
                     measured |CITS: [16388577]|. The ""insert-in-flap"" (IF) 
                     chaperone domain near the prolyl isomerase active site 
                     within SlyD* appears to be necessary and sufficient to 
                     confer chaperone activity for proline-limited folding 
                     |CITS: [17397867]|; it recognizes and binds unfolded and 
                     partially folded proteins |CITS: [19356587]|. After the IF 
                     domain forms an initial dynamic complex with unfolded 
                     proteins, they are transferred to the prolyl isomerase 
                     domain |CITS: [21147124]|. The substrate specificity of 
                     SlyD* was measured using a library of fluorescent peptides,
                     showing high activity with hydrophobic residues such as 
                     Leu, Tyr, and Phe preceding proline |CITS: [19785464]|. 
                     However, in a protein context, activity was not dependent 
                     on the amino acid residue before the proline |CITS: 
                     [19920179]|. SlyD promotes very fast prolyl isomerization 
                     in an unfolded protein; in proteins that already contain 
                     the correct prolyl isomer, SlyD thus retards conformational
                     folding |CITS: [23445547]|.
                     SlyD is involved in the insertion of Ni<sup>2+</sup> during
                     the maturation of hydrogenases and may have a role in 
                     regulating which of the three hydrogenases is synthesized 
                     |CITS: [25620052]|. SlyD has been shown to interact 
                     directly with |FRAME: EG10484-MONOMER HypB|, an accessory 
                     protein required for hydrogenase maturation |CITS: 
                     [15569666]|, and to transfer Ni<sup>2+</sup> to it |CITS: 
                     [22085337]|. A loop in the SlyD chaperone domain is 
                     required for interaction with HypB, and the interaction is 
                     important for hydrogenase maturation. The C-terminal 
                     metal-binding domain of SlyD is required for stimulating 
                     metal release from HypB and for hydrogenase activation 
                     |CITS: [17426034]|, while the PPIase activity is not 
                     required for hydrogenase maturation |CITS: [17720786]|. 
                     Contributing to the metal specificity of hydrogenase 
                     maturation, SlyD stimulates Ni<sup>2+</sup> dissociation, 
                     but prevents Zn<sup>2+</sup> dissociation from HypB |CITS: 
                     [28352890]|. Interaction with SlyD increases the GTPase 
                     activity of HypB |CITS: [22085337]|, and HypB and SlyD form
                     a tighter complex in the presence of GDP |CITS: 
                     [30758762]|. SlyD also interacts directly with |FRAME: 
                     HYCELARGE-MONOMER HycE|, the large subunit of hydrogenase 
                     3, via its IF domain |CITS: [21185288]|.  Although a 
                     truncated mutant protein, SlyD155, is able to bind a single
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup> ion and interacts with HypB, it is unable 
                     to activate release of Ni<sup>2+</sup> from HypB and does 
                     not activate hydrogenase |CITS: [22310044]|. In competitive
                     binding experiments, SlyD is able to block the interaction 
                     of HypB with HypA; a hierarchy of binary protein-protein 
                     interactions supports the nickel delivery process |CITS: 
                     [30758762]|.
                     SlyD, in cooperation with the |FRAME: G6925-MONOMER|, is 
                     also involved in regulation of the |FRAME: PD00260|. During
                     iron starvation, Fur is inactivated by RflP and SlyD in a 
                     cooperative manner. According to the current model, RflP 
                     binds to Fur, and this binding enhances the ability of SlyD
                     to modify Pro18, which converts Fur into a conformer that 
                     cannot bind to DNA. In a strain overexpressing RflP, in the
                     presence of SlyD, the DNA-binding activity of Fur decreases
                     300-fold |CITS:[32813023][37010421]|.
                     SlyD (as well as DnaK) binds to Tat signal sequences <i>in 
                     vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [17215254]|. SlyD binds
                     ATP, but does not hydrolyze it; binding is enhanced by 
                     bivalent transition metal ions, especially Zn<sup>2+</sup> 
                     and Ni<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: [10432297]|. SlyD binds with 
                     higher affinity to Tat signal sequences with positive 
                     charges; analysis of mutated SlyD variants points to 
                     specific hydrophobic amino acids within the active site and
                     negatively charged surface of the IF chaperone domain as 
                     being most important to binding positive charged substrates
                     |CITS: [38917203]|.
                     SlyD is required for phage &phi;X174-induced cell lysis 
                     |CITS: [8300625]|. Its function appears to be in the 
                     stabilization of the &phi;X174 lysis protein E |CITS: 
                     [12100551]|. An interaction of nascent lysis protein E with
                     SlyD is essential for maintaining membrane insertion 
                     competence of the toxin |CITS: [33244868]|. A peptide 
                     consisting of the N-terminal transmembrane domain of 
                     protein E inhibits the peptidyl prolyl isomerase activity 
                     of SlyD |CITS: [17005977]|.
                     A large-scale screening experiment found that SlyD targets 
                     proteins with metal cofactors, such as Fe-S clusters, and 
                     with proteins of the Fe-S biogenesis pathways, specifically
                     IscU, IscS and SufS but not the other Isc and Suf proteins 
                     |CITS: [37584558]|.
                     Gel filtration experiments indicate that SlyD is a dimer in
                     solution |CITS: [10432297]|, while an N-terminal fragment 
                     of SlyD (aa 1-165) appears to be monomeric |CITS: 
                     [16388577]|. A later study indicated that SlyD is monomeric
                     and contains multiple metal binding sites |CITS: 
                     [19947632]|. Solution structures of full-length SlyD |CITS:
                     [19645725][19760519]| and SlyD* |CITS: [19356587]| have 
                     been solved. The FKBP and IF domains do not have a fixed 
                     relative location |CITS: [19356587]|. An additional 
                     C-terminal &alpha;-helix is packed against the atypical 
                     PPIase domain and appears to be involved in nickel ion 
                     binding and conformational rearrangement of the PPIase 
                     domain |CITS: [19645725]|. The kinetics of folding of the 
                     SlyD* protein with or without the IF domain (a ""guest 
                     domain"" located in a loop of the FKBP domain) has been 
                     studied |CITS: [19136015]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>slyD</i> is toxic, causing 
                     filamentation |CITS: [8300625][9350861]|. In the <i>E. 
                     coli</i> B and C strains, <i>slyD</i> mutations cause a 
                     significant growth rate defect |CITS: [9350861]|.
                     SlyD deletion mutants resulted in reduced susceptibility to
                     ampicillin and gentamicin antibiotics as well as to 
                     hypochlorite stress under microaerobic conditions |CITS: 
                     [37584558]|.
                     SlyD: ""<b>s</b>ensitivity to <b>ly</b>sis <b>D</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [2936651]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [22970729][23585180][26919691]|"
