     CDS             3474648..3476561
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yheS"
                     /locus_tag="b3352"
                     /product="ATP-binding protein YheS"
                     /label=yheS
                     /note="YheS is a member of the ABCF family within the 
                     ATP-binding cassette (ABC) superfamily |CITS: 
                     [9593292][30597160]|. There are four ABCF family proteins 
                     within <i>E. coli</i> K-12: YheS, |FRAME: YJJK-MONOMER 
                     ""EttA""|, |FRAME: UUP-MONOMER ""Uup""| and |FRAME: 
                     G6423-MONOMER ""YbiT""|, all of which are involved in 
                     noncanonical translation of 'hard-to-translate' nascent 
                     peptides getting them through the ribosome exit tunnel. 
                     YheS releases nascent |FRAME: EG11087-MONOMER ""SecM""| 
                     from translational arrest |CITS: [38661232]|.
                     Genetic interaction data of YheS point to a role in 
                     ribosome assembly or function |CITS: 
                     [COCHRANE15][30597160]|.
                     Overexpression of YheS in a &Delta;<i>bipA</i> mutant 
                     exacerbates its cold-sensitive growth defect |CITS: 
                     [COCHRANE15]|. Using the <i>E. coli</i> CFT073 YheS 
                     protein, overexpression of an EQ<sub>2</sub> mutant of YheS
                     (carrying substitutions in the predicted ATPase active 
                     sites) inhibits protein synthesis |CITS: [30597160]|.
                     Using a reconstituted <i>in vitro</i> translation system 
                     and mutant analyses indicate the C-terminal extension and 
                     interdomain linkage, found in antibiotics-resistant 
                     (ARE)-ABCF proteins, and ATP activity of YheS are essential
                     to the release of SecM from the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [38661232]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [31563533]|"
