     gene            complement(3484812..3484979)
                     /gene="yhfG"
                     /locus_tag="b3362"
                     /label=yhfG
     CDS             complement(3484812..3484979)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yhfG"
                     /locus_tag="b3362"
                     /product="bifunctional DUF2559 domain-containing protein
                     YhfG / FicT-FicA complex"
                     /label=yhfG
                     /note="Homologs of the <i>E. coli</i> |FRAME:
                     EG10307-MONOMER Fic|-FicA proteins act as toxin-antitoxin 
                     systems.  Fic belongs to a set of enterobacterial FicT 
                     homologs with an altered FIC domain signature motif; unlike
                     other FicT proteins, expression of the <i>E. coli</i> 
                     protein does not cause a detectable growth defect |CITS: 
                     [26299961]|.
                     Crystal structures of the Fic-FicA complex have been 
                     solved.  Combined with phylogenetic analysis, the crystal 
                     structures show a conserved potential ligand-binding 
                     pocket; the ligand awaits identification |CITS: 
                     [27657533]|.
                     A meta-analysis of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655 
                     transcriptomic data sets in response to multiple stressors 
                     found 15 uncharacterized or partially characterized genes 
                     (<i>yqfA, yhfG, yhhA, ybgS, arpA, yqaE, yfdY, yaiY, yebE, 
                     ydiE, yjcB, yiiX, ycjF, yihF, yidX</i>) that were 
                     upregulated under all five stressors: heat, cold, oxidative
                     stress, nitrosative stress, and antibiotic treatment, 
                     whereas 3 uncharacterized or partially characterized genes 
                     (<i>ymfI, ydiJ, yedE</i>) that were downregulated under all
                     but nitrosative stress |CITS: [36144394]|.
                     FicA: ""<b>FIC</b> domain <b>a</b>ntitoxin"" |CITS: 
                     [26299961]|"
                     /translation="MKKLTDKQKSRLWELQRNRNFQASRRLEGVEMPLVTLTAAEALAR
                     LEELRSHYER"
                     /go_function="protein binding [goid 0005515] [evidence
                     IPI] [PMID 27657533]"
                     /go_process="positive regulation of gene expression [goid
                     0010628] [evidence HEP] [PMID 36144394]"
                     /go_process="response to stress [goid 0006950] [evidence
                     HEP] [PMID 36144394]"
     gene            complement(3485084..3485656)
                     /gene="ppiA"
                     /locus_tag="b3363"
                     /label=ppiA
     CDS             complement(3485084..3485656)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ppiA"
                     /locus_tag="b3363"
                     /product="bifunctional peptidyl-prolyl cis-trans isomerase
                     A / peptidyl-prolyl cis-trans isomerase"
                     /EC_number="5.2.1.8"
                     /label=ppiA
                     /note="PpiA is a peptidyl-prolyl
                     <i>cis-trans</i>-isomerase (PPIase), catalyzing the 
                     conformational isomerization of prolyl residues in 
                     peptides.  <i>Cis-trans</i> isomerization of prolyl peptide
                     bonds is a slow step in protein folding, and thus PpiA is 
                     thought to facilitate proper protein folding.
                     PpiA was shown to catalyze the refolding of denatured type 
                     III collagen |CITS: [1606970]|.  PpiA is a homolog of the 
                     human enzyme cyclophilin; unlike that enzyme, PpiA activity
                     is only inhibited by high concentrations of cyclosporin A 
                     |CITS: [2190212][1606970]| or FK506 |CITS: [2007139]|.  An 
                     F112W mutant, changing to the tryptophan residue conserved 
                     in eukaryotic cylcophilins, is more sensitive to inhibition
                     by cyclosporin A |CITS: [2001362]| and binds cylcosporin A 
                     in a configuration similar to the human enzyme |CITS: 
                     [8180197]|.
                     Solution structures of wild type |CITS: [8130188]| and 
                     mutant |CITS: [8180197]| PpiA as well as crystal structures
                     of a mutant form of PpiA in a complex with a peptide 
                     containing the <i>trans</i> form of proline have been 
                     determined |CITS: [15355356]|.
                     A <i>ppiA</i> null mutant shows no apparent growth defect 
                     |CITS: [8709850]|.  A strain containing null mutations in 
                     all four known periplasmic peptidyl-prolyl 
                     <i>cis-trans</i>-isomerases, <i>ppiA</i>, <i>ppiD</i>, 
                     <i>fkpA</i>, and <i>surA</i>, is viable, but shows a 
                     decreased growth rate and increased antibiotic 
                     susceptibility |CITS: [16267292]|.  Expression of PpiA is 
                     regulated by cAMP-CRP, CytR |CITS: [7715459]|, and the Cpx 
                     two-component system |CITS: [9159398]|.
                     Rot: ""rotamase"" |CITS: [2190212]|"
