     gene            complement(3508436..3509272)
                     /gene="dam"
                     /locus_tag="b3387"
                     /label=dam
     CDS             complement(3508436..3509272)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dam"
                     /locus_tag="b3387"
                     /product="DNA adenine methyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.72"
                     /label=dam
                     /note="DNA adenine methyltransferase (Dam) is responsible
                     for methylation of nearly all |CITS: [1584789][1334233]| 
                     GATC sequences in the <i>E. coli</i> genome |CITS: 
                     [368070]|. Dam activity is involved in methyl-directed 
                     mismatch repair, initiation of chromosome replication, and 
                     gene expression. The intracellular level of Dam is limiting
                     |CITS: [6233606]|; Dam occurs at about 130 molecules per 
                     rapidly growing cell |CITS: [1537808]|.
                     Dam transfers a methyl group from 
                     <i>S</i>-adenosyl-L-methionine (AdoMet) to create a 
                     N<sup>6</sup>-methyladenine at the GATC site. This is 
                     accomplished by a base-flipping mechanism in which the 
                     target residue is flipped out of the DNA strand into the 
                     active site of the enzyme |CITS: [15276835]|. AdoMet can be
                     crosslinked to a peptide corresponding to amino acids 10-14
                     of Dam |CITS: [8233797]|. Site-directed mutagenesis of the 
                     enzyme has been used to determine regions required for 
                     catalytic activity and AdoMet crosslinking |CITS: 
                     [8341592]|, to invesitigate residues involved in the 
                     base-flipping mechanism of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [15276835][16524590][17545164]|, residues that interact 
                     with the target GATC site |CITS: 
                     [15882618][16524590][19725089][19766657]|, and residues 
                     that influence processivity |CITS: [19419959]|. Crystal 
                     structures of Dam in a complex with DNA in the presence of 
                     <i>S</i>-adenosyl-L-homocysteine (AdoHcy) have been 
                     determined |CITS: [16524590][17545164][25845600]|. AdoMet 
                     also seemed to act as an allosteric effector for binding of
                     Dam to its target sequence |CITS: [2201947]|, with an 
                     allosteric binding site different from the catalytic site 
                     |CITS: [1862071]|. These results may be explained by the 
                     observed alternative conformations of AdoHcy and 
                     conformational changes during the catalytic cycle of Dam 
                     |CITS: [17545164]|. Oligonucleotide substrates, but not 
                     genomic DNA, induce dimerization of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [8589248][19580332]|.
                     DNA methylation by Dam has been shown to occur processively
                     |CITS: [2645286][12079349][12576072]|, although methylation
                     kinetics/processivity is influenced by the sequence context
                     and clustering of particular GATC sites |CITS: 
                     [15456775][16321401][18502761][22570478]|. Dam moves most 
                     efficiently between methylation sites that are separated by
                     ~500 bp, suggesting a mechanism involving DNA looping and 
                     intersegmental hopping |CITS: [25134757][25785689]|. In 
                     organisms containing Dam, the distribution of GATC sites 
                     within the genome shows distinct cluster patterns; no 
                     cluster patterns are observed in genomes that lack Dam 
                     |CITS: [27014208]|.
                     Upon replication of DNA, previously methylated genomic 
                     sites become hemimethylated and are therefore targets for 
                     Dam. Methylation of GATC sites is important for a number of
                     cellular functions including methyl-directed mismatch 
                     repair |CITS: [6225697][6308634][3029029][2185235]|, 
                     initiation of chromosome replication |CITS: [6330369][3891 
                     329][3891330][3304662][3290846][2676991][2196435][1697508][
                     2203541][9379896]|, and modulation of gene expression 
                     |CITS: [3010047][3025553][3031600][2960382]|. When base 
                     mismatches are introduced during replication, DNA mismatch 
                     repair enzymes repair the unmethylated (new) DNA strand and
                     leave the methylated (old) strand intact. When Dam 
                     methylation is blocked, mismatch repair occurs with equal 
                     frequency on both the new and old strands, leading to 
                     increased double-strand breaks and the necessity for repair
                     by recombination |CITS: 
                     [3512517][3522919][2939453][3019677][1908945]|.
                     After passage of the replication fork, the origin of 
                     replication is transiently sequestered from Dam, thus 
                     remaining in a hemimethylated state that is not capable of 
                     initiating replication |CITS: [1697508][2203541]|. |FRAME: 
                     EG12197-MONOMER SeqA|, which binds in the wake of DNA 
                     replication, effectively competes with Dam for access to 
                     hemimethylated GATC sites, leading to a delay in full 
                     methylation of these sites |CITS: [10206949]|. The length 
                     of the eclipse period is inversely correlated with the 
                     amount of Dam in the cell |CITS: [11080169]|. Methylation 
                     of GATC sites near <i>oriC</i> by Dam affects DNA 
                     conformation and modulates IHF binding |CITS: 
                     [9383152][9669330]|. Remethylation of hemimethylated sites 
                     in newly synthesized DNA occurs asynchronously: methylation
                     of the lagging replicative arm takes approximately twice as
                     long as that of the leading replicative arm |CITS: 
                     [10562566]|.
                     The methylation state of gene promoter regions can affect 
                     whether transcription of the gene occurs, in particular 
                     including <i>dnaA</i> |CITS: [1697508]|, but also other 
                     genes |CITS: 
                     [12139615][12682301][15265237][18054045][21573101]|. 
                     However, depending on the statistical analysis method that 
                     is used, no significant global changes in gene expression 
                     are observed in a <i>dam</i> mutant compared to wild type 
                     |CITS: [17342207]|. Phase-variable expression of Ag43 is 
                     regulated by competition between Dam and OxyR |CITS: [9192 
                     040][10094691][10692164][11972787][11972788][12029051][2011
                     8257]|. Interactions of Dam with non-canonical sites may 
                     explain Dam's effects on the expression of certain genes 
                     independent of its methylation activity |CITS: [25845600]|.
                     Dam affects excision of Tn10 |CITS: [6287993][2981756]|, 
                     transposition of IS10, IS50, IS3, Tn903 and Tn5 |CITS: 
                     [3000598][2451025][2832849][1650007][1645443]|, P1 
                     replication |CITS: [2826133]|, transcription of the phage 
                     Mu <i>mom</i> gene |CITS: 
                     [6752951][6218410][6229339][3536483]|, and Pap |CITS: 
                     [1357527][7906204]|, F1845 and S pilus |CITS: [7910935]| 
                     phase variation in pathogenic <i>E. coli</i>.
                     <i>dam</i> is not an essential gene |CITS: 
                     [2854098][2659593][8226701]|. Overexpression of <i>dam</i> 
                     leads to increased mutability |CITS: [7007328]|. A 
                     <i>dam</i><sup>-</sup> strain is outcompeted by the 
                     isogenic wild type |CITS: [3903484]|. <i>dam</i> mutant 
                     strains contain increased levels of DNA double-strand 
                     breaks |CITS: [16199573]| and are highly susceptible to 
                     subinhibitory levels of ampicillin and other &beta;-lactam 
                     antibiotics |CITS: [26998690]|. The antibiotic 
                     hypersensitivity of &Delta;<i>dam</i> strains is abrogated 
                     in the absence of the mutagenic polymerase |FRAME: 
                     G6115-MONOMER Pol IV| or the methyl-directed mismatch 
                     repair components |FRAME: EG10624-MONOMER| and |FRAME: 
                     EG10625-MONOMER| |CITS: [26998690]|. Genetic interactions 
                     with <i>rec</i> and <i>ruv</i> genes indicate that 
                     recombination is essential for survival of <i>dam</i> 
                     mutants |CITS: [10629194]|. Inhibitors of Dam activity have
                     been identified |CITS: [18085543]|; targeting Dam may be 
                     therapeutically useful by potentiating antibiotic drug 
                     activity |CITS: [26998690]|. Regulation of <i>dam</i> 
                     transcription is complex, involving a number of 
                     differentially regulated promoters.
                     An <i>in vitro</i> DNA replication cycle reaction system 
                     has been developed with 26 purified replication-associated 
                     <i>E. coli</i> proteins (|CITS: [29036468]|) and shown to 
                     accurately represent regulation of DNA replication 
                     initiation at <i>oriC</i> by |FRAME: EG12197-MONOMER| 
                     (SeqA) and Dam through consecutive replication cycles 
                     |CITS: [39922869]|.
                     Dam: ""<b>D</b>NA <b>a</b>denine <b>m</b>ethyltransferase""
                     |CITS: [4576399]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [26698649][24747048][20471491][19175412][1 
                     6959970][16489347][15802246][11920679][11263490][11092821] 
                     [8200522][2667217][2651430][2842065][3327459][3118961]|"
