     gene            complement(3509379..3510665)
                     /gene="damX"
                     /locus_tag="b3388"
                     /label=damX
     CDS             complement(3509379..3510665)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="damX"
                     /locus_tag="b3388"
                     /product="cell division protein DamX"
                     /label=damX
                     /note="DamX is a non-essential cell division protein; it 
                     contains a C-terminal SPOR domain which targets the protein
                     to the septal ring |CITS: [19684127][26305949]|. 
                     Recruitment of DamX to the division site is dependent on 
                     |FRAME: EG10347-MONOMER|, but not dependent on any other 
                     cell division protein tested |CITS: [19880599]|. The SPOR 
                     domain is important for <i>in vivo</i> activities of DamX 
                     |CITS: [23290046]|.
                     In the uropathogenic <i>E. coli</i> strain UTI89, DamX was 
                     shown to be required for reversible filamentation and 
                     establishment of a urinary tract infection |CITS: 
                     [27486187]|.
                     DamX interacts directly with itself, |FRAME: 
                     EG10342-MONOMER| and |FRAME: EG11529-MONOMER|, and more 
                     weakly with other cell division proteins; its SPOR domain 
                     binds peptidoglycan |CITS: [19880599]|. DamX also interacts
                     with |FRAME: EG10605-MONOMER PBP1B| and stimulates its 
                     glycosyltransferase activity |CITS: [33144379]|.
                     Relatively high binding affinity (K<sub>d</sub> ~1 
                     &micro;M) of the SPOR domain to denuded glycans in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> sacculi was measured and found to be necessary for
                     full function of DamX |CITS: [36005810]|.
                     DamX is predicted to be a bitopic inner membrane protein 
                     |CITS: [27510400]|. A solution structure of the SPOR domain
                     has been solved. The amino acid residues Q351, S354, and 
                     W416 are required for septal localization of DamX |CITS: 
                     [23290046]|. SPOR domains localize to the division site by 
                     preferentially binding to |FRAME: a-denuded-peptidoglycan 
                     denuded glycans|, which are enriched in septal 
                     peptidoglycan |CITS: [19880599][26305949]|.
                     Expression of <i>damX</i> may be translationally coupled to
                     expression of the upstream <i>|FRAME: EG10074|</i> gene 
                     |CITS: [7603433]|. The DamX protein appears to migrate as a
                     70 kDa protein in a denaturing gel |CITS: [7603433]|. 
                     Overexpression of <i>damX</i> induces cell filamentation 
                     |CITS: [7603433]|. Overexpression of <i>damX</i> from an 
                     ASKA plasmid leads to a more than 3-fold increase in the 
                     MIC of hydrogen peroxide |CITS: [34072091]|. A <i>damX</i> 
                     deletion strain in otherwise wild-type background shows no 
                     obvious defect; however, in combination with an <i>ftsN</i>
                     or <i>|FRAME: EG10218|</i> allele shows a synthetic 
                     phenotype |CITS: [19684127][19880599]|. Conversely, a 
                     <i>damX</i> null mutation alleviates the cell division 
                     defect of an <i>ftsQ1</i> mutant |CITS: [19880599]|. A 
                     transposon insertion in <i>damX</i> decreases |FRAME: 
                     PD00365|-mediated multidrug resistance |CITS: [20211899]|, 
                     and a <i>damX</i> deletion mutant shows increased 
                     susceptibility to the &beta;-lactam antibiotic cefsulodin 
                     |CITS: [33144379]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [22203377][21327035]|"
                     /translation="MDEFKPEDELKPDPSDRRTGRSRQSSERSERTERGEPQINFDDIE
                     LDDTDDRRPTRAQKERNEEPEIEEEIDESEDETVDEERVERRPRKRKKAASKPASRQYM
