     gene            complement(3511902..3512423)
                     /gene="aroK"
                     /locus_tag="b3390"
                     /label=aroK
     CDS             complement(3511902..3512423)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aroK"
                     /locus_tag="b3390"
                     /product="bifunctional shikimate kinase 1 / shikimate
                     kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.71"
                     /label=aroK
                     /note="Shikimate kinase catalyzes the fifth step in the
                     |FRAME: ARO-PWY| pathway.  Chorismate is the common 
                     precursor for the biosynthesis of the aromatic amino acids 
                     tyrosine, phenylalanine and tryptophan.
                     The existence of two shikimate kinase isozymes was 
                     initially suggested because sucrose density gradient 
                     centrifugation |CITS: [4979440]| and column chromatography 
                     |CITS: [222728]| of cell extracts showed two peaks of 
                     shikimate kinase activity.  Although shikimate kinase 1 
                     (AroK, described here) has not been assayed in pure form, 
                     |CITS: [3001029]| mention that AroK has an approximately 
                     100-fold lower affinity for shikimate than |FRAME: 
                     AROL-MONOMER| (AroL).  A fortuituously obtained L133P 
                     mutant of AroK appears to have lost shikimate kinase 
                     activity |CITS: [8682786]|.
                     The reportedly high K<sub>M</sub> value of AroK for 
                     shikimate |CITS: [3037263]| suggested that the enzyme may 
                     have a different primary function in the cell |CITS: 
                     [3001029]|.  However, using purified AroK, the 
                     K<sub>M</sub> for shikimiate is 400 &micro;M, similar to 
                     AroL's K<sub>M</sub> of 200 &micro;M |CITS: [23526945]|; 
                     the specific activity of purified AroK is less than a third
                     of that of AroL |CITS: [27558633]|.  Loss of AroK 
                     expression, but not loss of its shikimate kinase activity, 
                     confers increased resistance to mecillinam |CITS: 
                     [8682786]|.  An <i>aroK</i> deletion mutant shows increased
                     susceptibility to the cationic antibiotic peptide (CAMP) 
                     protamine; the authors hypothesize that AroK increases 
                     production of aromatic metabolites that act as signalling 
                     molecules |CITS: [25294881]|.
                     A crystal structure of shikimate kinase 1 was solved. 
                     Comparison with the structurally similar <i>Erwinia 
                     chrysanthemi</i> shikimate kinase II suggested that 
                     substitution of a stabilizing hydrophobic residue with 
                     Lys86 may cause AroK's significantly lower affinity for 
                     shikimate |CITS: [12001235]|.
                     An <i>aroK aroL</i> double mutant is unable to grow on 
                     minimal medium supplemented with shikimate |CITS: 
                     [1309529]| or shikimate, phenylalanine and tyrosine |CITS: 
                     [7883721]|.  <i>aroK</i> is important for survival under 
                     competitive planktonic growth conditions |CITS: 
                     [16849790]|.  A &Delta;<i>aroK</i> mutant shows increased 
                     sensitivity to the alkylating agent methyl methanesulfonate
                     |CITS: [18824089]|.  Overexpression of <i>aroK</i> enables 
                     enhanced tyrosine production in an engineered strain |CITS:
                     [18243023]|, although overexpression of <i>aroL</i> is more
                     efficient than overexpression of <i>aroK</i> |CITS: 
                     [22020510]|. Inactivation of <i>aroK</i> and <i>aroL</i> 
                     enhances the yield of shikimic acid in engineered strains 
                     |CITS: [19047820][20385022][22728194]|. The Keio collection
                     <i>aroK</i> mutant exhibits a 'high level' of resistance to
                     lithium exposure |CITS: [39983623]|.
                     Unlike <i>aroL</i>, expression of <i>aroK</i> is not under 
                     the control of TyrR |CITS: [222728]|.  Expression of AroK 
                     is induced at pH 8 |CITS: [12107143]|.
                     AroK: ""<b>aro</b>matic amino acid biosynthesis <b>K</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [1309529]|"
