                     /locus_tag="b3396"
                     /label=mrcA
     CDS             3516230..3518782
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mrcA"
                     /locus_tag="b3396"
                     /product="multifunctional peptidoglycan
                     glycosyltransferase / peptidoglycan DD-transpeptidase MrcA 
                     / peptidoglycan glycosyltransferase / peptidoglycan 
                     DD-transpeptidase / peptidoglycan DD-carboxypeptidase"
                     /label=mrcA
                     /note="PBP1A, the product of the <i>mrcA</i> gene, is a 
                     bifunctional peptidoglycan synthase with transglycosylase 
                     and transpeptidase activity.  PBP1A is implicated in 
                     cytoskeleton independent glycan synthesis in <i>E. coli</i>
                     K-12; PBP1A does not exhibit any |FRAME: EG10608-MONOMER 
                     MreB|-like directional motion |CITS: [27643381]|. PBP1A is 
                     required for maximal fitness in alkaline conditions (pH 
                     6.5-8.2); PBP1A activity is reduced in acidic conditions 
                     |CITS: [30963998]|.
                     Purified PBP1A produces murein with an average length of 20
                     disaccharide units and with approximately 20% of peptides 
                     involved in cross-links; purified PBP1A requires glycan 
                     polymers with monomeric peptides for transpeptidation 
                     reactions and can integrate newly synthesized glycan chains
                     into existing sacculi |CITS: [16840781]|. Purified PBP1A 
                     catalyses transglycosylation using both disaccharide 
                     molecules (Lipid II) and tetrasaccharide molecules ('Lipid 
                     IV') as sole substrates; reactions using lipid II are more 
                     processive and yield longer glycan chains; reactions using 
                     'lipid IV' proceed using a distributive mechanism (ie. 
                     products are released after each coupling) |CITS: 
                     [17704540]|. PBP1a binds penicillin in a pH range of 4-11; 
                     maximum binding occurs at pH 10 |CITS: [3090016]|.
                     Purified PBP1A has DD-carboxypeptidase activity <i>in 
                     vitro</i> which is enhanced in the presence of |FRAME: 
                     G7642-MONOMER LpoA|; this activity may be important for 
                     resolving acyl-enzyme complexes that are formed in the 
                     absence of acceptor peptides |CITS: [26370943]|. PBP1A has 
                     been reconstituted in proteoliposomes and is active in 
                     polymerizing labeled lipid II |CITS: [26370943]|. PBP1A 
                     requires LpoA to promote peptidoglycan polymerization <i>in
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [34429361]|.
                     The amino terminus may contain a non-processed signal 
                     sequence |CITS: [3882429]|.  PBP1A is able to dimerize 
                     without disulfide bonds, but doesn't form a complex with 
                     PBP1B |CITS: [12057973]|.  PBP1A fractionates with PBPs 1B,
                     2, 3, and 5 when separated through agarose, and with PBPs 
                     1B, 2, 3, 4 and 5 during chromatography through Sephacryl 
                     S-1000 |CITS: [2676988]|.  PBP1A is retained on an 
                     MltA-sepharose column only in the presence of periplasmic 
                     proteins suggesting that an unidentified periplasmic factor
                     mediates an interaction between PBP1A and the lytic murein 
                     transglycosylase |FRAME: G7457-MONOMER MltA| |CITS: 
                     [10037771]|. PBP1A forms a complex with the outer membrane 
                     lipoprotein LpoA, and LpoA is essential for the <i>in 
                     vivo</i> function of PBP1A |CITS: [21183073][21183074]|.
                     Cells lacking PBP1A do not exhibit defects in growth or 
                     cell morphology |CITS: [330236]| but an <i>mrcA</i> |FRAME:
                     EG10605-MONOMER <i>mrcB</i>| double mutation is lethal 
                     |CITS: [341159][345275][2993822][3906031]|. The interaction
                     of <i>mrcA</i> with other genes involved in murein 
                     synthesis and cell division has been widely investigated 
                     (see for example: |CITS: [7007327][7027927][3 
                     541782][3298212][2211517][2066344][10383966][11567017]|). 
                     Inhibition of PBP1A and PBP1B with cefsulodin results in 
                     osmosensitive L-form-like cells that have 7% residual 
                     peptidoglycan content and can survive in rich hypertonic 
                     medium |CITS: [17586646]|.
                     Levels of PBP1A are increased in nalidixic acid-, 
                     novobiocin-, oxolinic acid-, and nitrofurantoin-induced 
                     <i>E. coli</i> filaments |CITS: [3530676]|.
                     Transcription of peptidoglycan synthase-encoding genes, 
                     including <i>mrcA</i>, is regulated by |FRAME: 
                     EG10977-MONOMER|.
                     PBP1A is a class A high molecular mass penicillin binding 
                     protein. Class A PBPs possess both PG transglcosylase and 
                     PG transpeptidase activity. <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains 
                     two other Class A PBPs: |FRAME: EG10605-MONOMER PBP1B| and 
                     |FRAME: G7322-MONOMER PBP1C|. PBP1A and PBP1B are the major
                     transglycosylases/transpeptidases (reviewed in |CITS: 
                     [18266856]|.
                     <i>ponA</i>: <u>p</u>enicillin binding protein <u>on</u>e A
                     |CITS: [345275]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [3055170][6444131][8117464][9529891]|"
