     gene            3523133..3524011
                     /gene="hslO"
                     /locus_tag="b3401"
                     /label=hslO
     CDS             3523133..3524011
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hslO"
                     /locus_tag="b3401"
                     /product="molecular chaperone Hsp33"
                     /label=hslO
                     /note="<i>hslO</i> encodes the zinc-dependent, redox
                     regulated chaperone known as Hsp33. Hsp33 is thought to 
                     function as a  'holdase' - a chaperone that binds tightly 
                     to unfolding proteins under stress conditions with 
                     subsequent release to chaperone 'foldases' (such as the 
                     DnaK system) when non-stress conditions resume. <i>hslO</i>
                     expression is induced by heat shock (7 mins at 50&deg;C); 
                     expression is stimulated by E&sigma;<sup>32</sup> in an 
                     <i>in vitro</i> transcription-translation system |CITS: 
                     [8349564]|. Purified Hsp33 suppresses thermal aggregation 
                     of the model substrates citrate synthase and firefly 
                     luciferase <i>in vitro</i>; purified Hsp33 exists in an 
                     equilibrium between reduced and oxidized species; chaperone
                     activity is activated under oxidizing conditions |CITS: 
                     [10025400]|. Hsp33 contains 6 cysteine residues, 4 of which
                     are conserved and are implicated in zinc coordination under
                     reducing conditions; two intramolecular disulfide bonds are
                     formed between the conserved cysteines Cys230 and Cys232, 
                     and Cys263 and Cys266 upon H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     oxidation |CITS: [10025400][10764757]|. Oxidized, active 
                     Hsp33 is a dimer in solution; reduced and inactive Hsp33 is
                     monomeric |CITS: [11377197][11377198]|. Oxidizing 
                     conditions induce disulfide bond formation and loss of 
                     zinc, resulting in the formation of a dimerization 
                     competent oxidized Hsp33 monomer; subsequent dimerization 
                     is both concentration and temperature dependent |CITS: 
                     [11377198]|.
                     Upon oxidation, Hsp33 undergoes conformational change 
                     associated with increased exposure of hydrophobic surfaces 
                     |CITS: [11231006]|. Zinc binding stabilizes the reduced 
                     Hsp33 monomer in a compact fold; release of zinc is 
                     associated with unfolding of the protein and exposure of 
                     hydrophobic surfaces. Disulfide bond formation is 
                     associated with further unfolding which may be necessary 
                     for subsequent dimerization and full activation of Hsp33; 
                     dimerization results in increased affinity for unfolded 
                     client proteins possibly due to an increased area for 
                     peptide binding |CITS: [15023991]|.
                     Hsp33 is activated by H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> at 
                     43&deg;C but not at 30&deg;C; Hsp33 activation relies on 
                     two interdependent stress sensors - a redox sensitive zinc 
                     center and a thermolabile linker region - both located in 
                     the C-terminal domain of the protein |CITS: [17515905]|. 
                     Mutations in the N-terminal domain which destabilize the 
                     linker region also result in the loss of proper 
                     post-translational redox regulation <i>in vitro</i> 
                     suggesting that unfolding of the linker region plays a 
                     central role in Hsp33 activation |CITS: [20139072]|.
                     Hsp33 appears to function as a 'holdase'; addition of the 
                     reducing agent dithiothreitol (DTT) to Hsp33-luciferase 
                     complexes <i>in vitro</i> does not cause substrate release;
                     addition of DTT plus the DnaK/DnaJ/GrpE folding system to 
                     the Hsp33:luciferase complex results in the refolding of a 
                     significant proportion of luciferase molecules. 
                     &Delta;<i>trxB</i> and &Delta;<i>gor</i> cells accumulate 
                     oxidized Hsp33 suggesting that both thioredoxins and 
                     glutaredoxins can act as physiological reductants |CITS: 
                     [14685279]|. A minimal chaperone network consisting of 
                     active Hsp33, reduced TrxA and DnaK/DnaJ/GrpE is able to 
                     reactivate significant amounts of chemically denatured 
                     citrate synthase <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [14685279]|.
                     Activated Hsp33 favors binding to secondary structure 
                     elements and disfavors binding to natively disordered 
                     regions - it preferentially binds early unfolding 
                     intermediates with residual secondary structure. The 
                     unfolded linker region of activated Hsp33 is involved in 
                     substrate binding - binding may stabilize the natively 
                     disordered linker region; a return to reducing (non-stress)
                     conditions may trigger increased substrate unfolding and 
                     subsequent release to the DnaK chaperone (which has a 
                     preference for unstructured polypeptides) for refolding 
                     |CITS: [22385960]| (and see comment by |CITS: [22385952]|).
                     Residues located in the N-terminal domain and the linker 
                     region of Hsp33 are implicated in direct binding to client 
                     proteins; the client binding site consists of a flexible 
                     polar linker region and a stable hydrophobic binding 
                     surface |CITS: [26787517]|.
                     The combination of heat stress and 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> induced oxidative stress 
                     inactivates the primary chaperone |FRAME: EG10241-MONOMER 
                     ""DnaK""| and makes Hsp33 essential for survival |CITS: 
                     [15694339]|. Hsp33 protects <i>E. coli</i> against 
                     hypochlorous acid (HOCl) mediated protein aggregation; HOCl
                     activates Hsp33 at both 30&deg;C and 43&deg;C |CITS: 
                     [19013278]|.
                     <i>hsl</i>: <u>h</u>eat <u>s</u>hock <u>l</u>ocus |CITS: 
                     [8349564]|
                     Review: |CITS: [12475172]|<BR>Related reviews: |CITS: 
                     [23018052][24657586]|"
