     gene            complement(3527875..3529227)
                     /gene="envZ"
                     /locus_tag="b3404"
                     /label=envZ
     CDS             complement(3527875..3529227)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="envZ"
                     /locus_tag="b3404"
                     /product="bifunctional sensor histidine kinase EnvZ / 
                     protein-histidine autokinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.13.3"
                     /label=envZ
                     /note="EnvZ is the sensor histidine kinase of the
                     EnvZ/OmpR two-component system which is best defined by its
                     modulation of the outer membrane diffusion pores |FRAME: 
                     EG10671-MONOMER ""OmpF""| and |FRAME: EG10670-MONOMER 
                     ""OmpC""| in response to changes in medium osmolarity.
                     Purified, soluble EnvZ autophosphorylates in the presence 
                     of ATP and transfers a phosphate to its cognate response 
                     regulator OmpR; soluble EnvZ also catalyses the 
                     dephosphorylation of phospho-OmpR <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [2668953][2558046][2656684]| (see also |CITS: [9343378]|). 
                     His-243, which is the site of autophosphorylation, is 
                     important for phosphatase activity but a phosphoHis-243 
                     intermediate is not formed during the phosphatase reaction 
                     |CITS: [9023231][9171423]| (see also |CITS: [10884412]|). 
                     EnvZ is an inner membrane protein containing two 
                     trans-membrane regions, a periplasmic domain and a large 
                     cytoplasmic region containing a HAMP (histidine kinase, 
                     adenylate cyclase, methyl-accepting proteins and 
                     phosphatases) domain, a dimerization/histidine 
                     phosphorylation (DHp) domain and a C-terminal 
                     catalytic/ATP-binding (CA) domain |CITS: [2824492][9209057 
                     ][9070221][9817206][10418137][9618480][10464234][17609132][
                     12437344][12691749][15357641]|. The cytoplasmic domain of 
                     EnvZ (EnvZ<sub>cyt</sub>) interacts physically with OmpR 
                     and with phosphoOmpR <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [12453214]|. 
                     The periplasmic domain is not required for sensing 
                     osmolarity signals |CITS: [8939425]|
                     EnvZ forms a homodimer <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [9409762][10426948][19432797]|. EnvZ autophosphorylates in 
                     <i>trans</i> within an EnvZ dimer, that is, ATP binds to 
                     the CA domain from one subunit and its phosphate is 
                     transferred to the DHp domain of the other subunit |CITS: 
                     [1662380][12767831]|. The HAMP-DHp domains in the dimer 
                     form an elongated helical bundle with the two CA domains 
                     attached to each side of the bundle |CITS: [24681325]| (see
                     also |CITS: [22244755]|).
                     The cytoplasmic domain of EnvZ is capable of osmosensing 
                     <i>in vivo</i>; the helical bundle is the central 
                     osmosensing core of EnvZ; nucleotide binding is not 
                     allosterically coupled to osmosensing |CITS: 
                     [22543870][28256224]| (see also |CITS: [25937465]|).  The 
                     cytoplasmic domain of EnvZ has been fused to the 
                     periplasmic, transmembrane, and HAMP-linker domain of the 
                     aspartate receptor |FRAME: CPLX0-8102 ""Tar""|, to create 
                     the well characterized chimeric receptor Taz (Taz1) (see 
                     |CITS: [2476847][8393937][8389884][7990135][12672798][1531 
                     6026][16164563]|)
                     Autophosphorylation of the purified cytoplasmic domain of 
                     EnvZ is enhanced under conditions of high osmolality |CITS:
                     [22543870]|; autophosphorylation is further enhanced when 
                     full-length EnvZ is reconsitituted in a lipid environment 
                     (lipid nanodiscs and DDM micelles); lipid-mediated 
                     allostery impacts the kinase function of EnvZ; 
                     lipid-mediated allostery appears to be independent of  
                     lipid composition |CITS: [28256224]|. Autophosphorylation 
                     of purified EnvZ is substantially reduced at pH 5 (compared
                     to pH 8) as is EnvZ dependent dephosphorylation of 
                     phospho-OmpR; EnvZ-OmpR phosphotransfer is largely 
                     unaffected by changes in pH |CITS: [32034139]|.
                     EnvZ interacts physically with |FRAME: G7610-MONOMER 
                     ""MzrA""|; this interaction modulates the output of the 
                     EnvZ/OmpR system without affecting its ability to respond 
                     to medium osmolality |CITS: [19432797][20889743]|
                     The <i>envZ ompR</i> genes are transcribed as a single 
                     polycistronic mRNA however the amount of EnvZ per cell is 
                     significantly less than OmpR during exponential growth in 
                     medium with varying osmolarities which is indicative of a 
                     translational barrier between the two genes |CITS: 
                     [6292200][11973328]|.
                     The synthesis of both OmpC and OmpF is affected when 
                     <i>envZ</i> is deleted |CITS: [3294816]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [2846509]|).
                     <i>envZ</i>: <U>env</U>elope protein |CITS: [6799653]| (see
                     also |CITS: [7039497]|)<BR><i>tpo</i>: <U>TPo</U>ne 
                     resistance |CITS: [6997269]|<BR><i>perA</i>: |CITS: 
                     [387722]|"
