     gene            3530171..3530647
                     /gene="greB"
                     /locus_tag="b3406"
                     /label=greB
     CDS             3530171..3530647
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="greB"
                     /locus_tag="b3406"
                     /product="transcription elongation factor GreB"
                     /label=greB
                     /note="GreB stimulates the mRNA cleavage activity of RNA 
                     polymerase |CITS: [7538676]|, which acts to release a 
                     polymerase complex that has stalled |CITS: [8431948]|. GreB
                     stimulates shortening of the 3' end of the transcript by 9 
                     nucleotides or less |CITS: [8431948]|, and the new 3' end 
                     is subject to elongation by RNA polymerase |CITS: 
                     [8431948], [11118220]|. GreB decreases abortive initiation 
                     and increases productive initiation |CITS: [8524809]|. GreB
                     (and GreA) is also required for wild-type transcription of 
                     some regulatory regions within lambda phage |CITS: 
                     [11163202]|.  GreB activity during transcription from a 
                     lambda phage promoter requires that GreB associate with RNA
                     polymerase before RNA transcription begins |CITS: 
                     [11380617]|.
                     <i>In vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> analyses showed that 
                     GreB relieves backtracked pauses caused by retention of 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> at a distance of 10-20 bp from many 
                     promoters |CITS: [33568644]|.
                     The N-terminal coiled-coil domain, which includes a basic 
                     region, determines the specificity with respect to the size
                     of the cleaved fragment |CITS: [9054416]|. The basic region
                     appears to maintain the interaction of the mRNA with the 
                     elongation complex |CITS: [10777576]|. The GreB C-terminal 
                     domain confers interaction with RNA polymerase |CITS: 
                     [9698562]|.
                     The interaction of GreB with RNA polymerase has been 
                     characterized in detail |CITS: [9698562], [10438515], 
                     [11772406], [12914698]|. The structure of GreB in 
                     association with RNA polymerase has been presented at 15 A 
                     resolution |CITS: [12914698]|.
                     GreB has similarity to <i>E. coli</i> GreA |CITS: 
                     [8431948]|; GreB activity results in release of a longer 
                     mRNA fragment than that released by GreA |CITS: [8431948]|.
                     In the presence of GreA and GreB, the transcription rate in
                     a cell-free expression system is increased, principally for
                     strong promoters |CITS:[27186988]|. Based on RNA-seq data, 
                     it was reported that <i>greA</i> and <i>greB</i> genes are 
                     regulated in an opposite manner under different stress 
                     conditions |CITS: [33568644]|.
                     GreB exhibits functional similarity to the eukaryotic SII 
                     elongation factor |CITS: [9642244]|.
                     Based on overexpression, deletion, transcription profiling,
                     and RNA polymerase mutant experiments, mutual and 
                     antagonistic competition effects between GreA, GreB, and 
                     DksA on RNA polymerase activity are observed when ppGpp is 
                     absent, but the effects are more pronounced when DksA and 
                     ppGpp are not present |CITS: [22056927]|.
                     GreA/B factors contribute significantly to complex 
                     regulation by DksA and ppGpp. Here, GreA has a major role 
                     and GreB has a minor role in the DksA-ppGpp regulatory 
                     network |CITS: [22056927]|.
                     An interaction network of GreA, GreB, DksA, ppGpp, and RNA 
                     polymerase has been proposed |CITS: [22056927]|.
                     The aspartic acid D41 is critical for the RNA cleavage 
                     reaction needed for rescuing backtracked transcription 
                     elongation complexes |CITS:[22200485]|. On the other hand, 
                     removal of a methylene group at the glutamic acid E44 
                     position (analogous to D74 in DksA), also located in the 
                     coiled-coil tip, resulted in a gain of function of both 
                     negative and positive control in each case 
                     |CITS:[22200485]|.
                     The secondary channel factors (SCFs) GreB and DksA both 
                     selectively repress ribosomal RNA (rRNA) transcription by a
                     similar mechanism, because their short-lived RNAP complexes
                     do not allow sufficient time for SCFs to dissociate. Using 
                     GreB as a model to investigate the mechanism(s) of SCF-RNAP
                     binding and action at <i>rrsB</i>p1, it was revealed that 
                     GreB functions by a delayed inhibition mechanism, and the 
                     results implied that DksA functions by the same mechanism 
                     |CITS:[30720429]|. The absence of both Gre factors, GreA 
                     and GreB, greatly impairs the detoxification of nitric 
                     oxide (NO) and hydrogen peroxide 
                     (H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>) |CITS:[39248572]|. When these 
                     factors are absent, one portion of the cell population has 
                     difficulty synthesizing transcripts, while another portion 
                     does not |CITS:[39248572]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11503438], [11586920]|."
