     gene            3536526..3537404
                     /gene="rpnA"
                     /locus_tag="b3411"
                     /label=rpnA
     CDS             3536526..3537404
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpnA"
                     /locus_tag="b3411"
                     /product="bifunctional recombination-promoting nuclease
                     RpnA / recombination-promoting nuclease"
                     /label=rpnA
                     /note="<i>In vitro</i>, the RpnA protein has 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup>-dependent nonspecific DNA endonuclease 
                     activity that is stimulated by Ca<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [28096446]|.  RpnA is one of five proteins in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> that belong to the ""transposase_31"" family 
                     |CITS: [28096446]|, which is distantly related to the 
                     PD-(D/E)XK nuclease superfamily |CITS: [17584917]|.  It was
                     previously noted that RpnA has similarity to RpnC, which is
                     encoded within the <i>panBCD</i> gene cluster |CITS: 
                     [8837478]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>rpnA</i> increases RecA-independent 
                     recombination, reduces cell viability, and induces 
                     expression of the DNA damage-inducible gene <i>dinD</i>. 
                     Site-directed mutagenesis of predicted active site residues
                     in RpnA abolishes all three phenotypes. Mutagenesis of an 
                     acidic cluster within the C-terminal part of the 
                     transposase_31 domain of RpnA results in a hyperactive 
                     variant |CITS: [28096446]|.
                     <i>rpnA</i> is part of a functional interaction network 
                     that includes Fe-S cluster biosynthesis genes.  An 
                     <i>rpnA</i> mutant reaches slightly higher cell density in 
                     stationary phase than wild type when growing in media with 
                     sublethal levels of streptomycin, an aminoglycoside 
                     antibiotic |CITS: [25316676]|.
                     RpnA: ""<b>r</b>ecombination-<b>p</b>romoting 
                     <b>n</b>uclease <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [28096446]|"
