     gene            complement(3537433..3538203)
                     /gene="bioH"
                     /locus_tag="b3412"
                     /label=bioH
     CDS             complement(3537433..3538203)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="bioH"
                     /locus_tag="b3412"
                     /product="trifunctional pimeloyl-acyl carrier protein
                     methyl ester esterase / pimeloyl-[acp] methyl ester 
                     esterase / carboxylesterase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.1.85; 3.1.1.1"
                     /label=bioH
                     /note="BioH is an esterase that hydrolyzes the methyl
                     ester of pimeloyl-[acp]|, terminating the part of the 
                     biotin biosynthesis pathway that is catalyzed by the fatty 
                     acid synthesis enzymes |CITS: [20693992]|.  Although BioH 
                     is a somewhat promiscuous carboxylesterase, showing 
                     activity toward substrates with short acyl chains |CITS: 
                     [12732651][19307763][20693992]|, it was shown that 
                     pimeloyl-ACP methyl ester is the physiological substrate of
                     BioH |CITS: [23045647]|.  Thus, BioH is providing the 
                     gatekeeping function that stops further elongation of 
                     pimeloyl-ACP methyl ester by the fatty acid biosynthesis 
                     enzymes |CITS: [23045647]|.
                     Purified BioH protein interacts with CoA |CITS: 
                     [11904168]|, and a predicted CoA binding surface has been 
                     identified |CITS: [16322579]|.  The hydrolase-like active 
                     site includes a putative catalytic triad comprising the 
                     residues S82, H235, and D207 |CITS: [12732651]|.  An S82A 
                     mutation does not prevent binding of BioH to CoA |CITS: 
                     [11904168]|.  An S82A mutant lacks catalytic activity and 
                     does not support dethiobiotin formation and does not 
                     complement a <i>bioH</i> deletion mutant for growth on 
                     biotin-free medium |CITS: [20693992]|.  The importance of 
                     several surface Arg residues for the interaction of BioH 
                     with ACP was confirmed by assaying enzyme activity of 
                     mutants <i>in vitro</i> and their ability to complement a 
                     <i>bioH</i> mutant <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [23045647]|.
                     A crystal structure of BioH has been solved at 1.7 &Aring; 
                     resolution |CITS: [12732651]|, and a co-crystal structure 
                     of a complex of BioH with pimeloyl-ACP methyl ester has 
                     been solved at 2.05 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [23045647]|. 
                     Before the function of BioH was known, it was proposed that
                     its function was the condensation of CoA and pimelic acid 
                     to pimeloyl-CoA, the precursor for biotin synthesis |CITS: 
                     [12732651]|, or the transfer of a pimeloyl moiety from the 
                     active cysteinyl residue of BioC to CoA |CITS: [8830257]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [21437340]|
                     "
