     gene            3538983..3539558
                     /gene="nfuA"
                     /locus_tag="b3414"
                     /label=nfuA
     CDS             3538983..3539558
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nfuA"
                     /locus_tag="b3414"
                     /product="bifunctional iron-sulfur cluster carrier protein
                     NfuA / [Fe-S] cluster carrier protein NfuA"
                     /label=nfuA
                     /note="NfuA is a 'non-ISC, non-SUF' iron-sulfur (Fe-S)
                     cluster carrier protein. NfuA can receive Fe-S clusters 
                     from the SufBC<sub>2</sub>D and IscU/HscBA 
                     scaffold-containing complexes and can in turn transfer Fe-S
                     clusters to the A-type carrier proteins |FRAME: 
                     EG11378-MONOMER SufA| and |FRAME: EG12132-MONOMER IscA| 
                     |CITS: [22966982]|.
                     NfuA is able to transfer its iron-sulfur cluster to |FRAME:
                     ACONITASE-MONOMER apo-AcnA| |CITS: [18339628]| and |FRAME: 
                     ACONITATEDEHYDRB-MONOMER apo-AcnB| and appears to be 
                     involved in the maturation of |FRAME: EG12087 NuoG|, an 
                     iron-sulfur cluster-containing subunit of NADH 
                     dehydrogenase I |CITS: [22966982]|. Both NfuA and |FRAME: 
                     EG12181-MONOMER ""GrxD""| interact with |FRAME: 
                     G6364-MONOMER ""MiaB""|. NfuA, but not GrxD, can transfer a
                     4Fe-4S cluster to apo-MiaB <i>in vitro</i>, and both 
                     proteins affect MiaB activity <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [23543739]|. NfuA is able to replace the degraded [4Fe-4S] 
                     cluster of |FRAME: CPLX0-782 LipA| in a process that is not
                     rate-limiting, resulting in catalytic activity of LipA 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [29051382]|. Follow-up studies using
                     mutagenesis of the two cysteine residues in the C-domain 
                     indicate the Nfu-like C-terminal domain is involved in the 
                     [4Fe-4S] cluster coordination and transfer, whereas 
                     mutagenesis of the two cysteine residues in the N-terminal 
                     domain indicate this domain functions to tightly interact 
                     with LipA |CITS: [30538130]|.
                     NfuA belongs to the Nfu-I subfamily of Nfu 
                     domain-containing proteins |CITS: [22966982]|. The 
                     N-terminal domain of NfuA contains a 'degenerate' ATC 
                     domain (ATC*) because it does not contain the conserved Cys
                     residues required for iron-sulfur cluster binding found in 
                     other A-type iron-sulfur carrier proteins. The C-terminal 
                     domain is similar to the Nfu domain of <i>Azotobacter 
                     vinelandii</i> NifU as both C-terminal domains have the 
                     conserved cysteine residues C149 and C152 that are 
                     important for NfuA function |CITS: [18339628]|. The 
                     C-terminal domain alone purifies as a homodimer, is able to
                     bind a [4Fe-4S] cluster at the homodimer interface, and is 
                     able to transfer it to apo-AcnB. The N-terminal domain 
                     promotes protein-protein interactions of NfuA and its 
                     targets |CITS: [22966982]|.
                     An <i>nfuA</i> null mutant has a normal growth phenotype in
                     complex media, but is eliminated from the culture after 3 
                     days in co-culture with the wild-type strain |CITS: 
                     [16707682]|, and has a severe growth defect under oxidative
                     stress (addition of paraquat) and iron starvation (addition
                     of 2,2'-dipyridyl) conditions |CITS: [18339628]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>nfuA</i> strain shows no growth defect in M9 
                     minimal medium with glucose as the carbon source |CITS: 
                     [29051382]|.
                     Expression of <i>nfuA</i> is repressed by IscR, a 
                     transcription factor that regulates expression of genes 
                     involved in iron-sulfur cluster biogenesis. Expression of 
                     <i>nfuA</i> is lower under anaerobic conditions than under 
                     aerobic conditions |CITS: [16677314]|. Increased 
                     <i>nfuA</i> expression by paraquat and 2,2'-dipyridyl is 
                     mediated by IscR |CITS: [18339628]|.  <i>nfuA</i> 
                     expression is upregulated in response to cobalt |CITS: 
                     [20545747]|.
                     The <i>nfuA</i> gene product is required for utilization of
                     DNA as the sole source of carbon and energy; <i>nfuA</i> is
                     homologous to genes involved in natural competence and 
                     genetic transformation in other bacteria |CITS: 
                     [11591672][16707682]|. Based on complementation 
                     experiments, NfuA/GntY together with GntX were thought to 
                     be involved in high-affinity gluconate transport |CITS: 
                     [9871335]|; however, a role for NfuA alone was not tested. 
                     An <i>nfuA</i> null mutant does not have a defect in 
                     gluconate metabolism |CITS: [18339628]|.
                     NfuA: ""<b>Nfu</b>-like protein"" |CITS: [18339628]| 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [21952637][23298813][29051366]|"
