                     /label=gntT
     CDS             3539918..3541234
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gntT"
                     /locus_tag="b3415"
                     /product="high-affinity gluconate transporter"
                     /label=gntT
                     /note="GntT is one of four known transporters for
                     gluconate in <I>E. coli</I>, the others being the 
                     homologous GntU, GntP and IdnT transporters. Whole cell 
                     experiments have shown that the cloned <I>gntT</I> gene was
                     able to complement a gluconate transport negative mutant 
                     and confers high affinity gluconate transport with a Km of 
                     approx 6 &mu;M |CITS: [9045817]|. Transcriptional analysis 
                     has shown that <I>gntT</I> constitutes a monocistronic 
                     operon. Analysis of <I>gntT</I>-<I>lacZ</I> fusions has 
                     indicated that <I>gntT</I> expression is induced at low 
                     levels of gluconate, partially repressed by glucose, and 
                     increased in early logarithmic phase |CITS: [9045817]|. 
                     Expression of both <I>gntT</I> and <I>gntU</I> is 
                     controlled by the GntR repressor and by cyclic AMP-CRP 
                     |CITS: [9537375]|. GntT is a member of the Gnt family of 
                     gluconate transporters |CITS: [9119199]|. Gluconate uptake 
                     has been reported to occur via a proton-symport mechanism 
                     in <I>E. coli</I> |CITS: [4585187]|. It seems likely that 
                     GntT is a high affinity gluconate uptake system that 
                     functions via D-gluconate/proton symport.
                     Imported gluconate is metabolised primarily via the 
                     Entner-Douderoff pathway and secondarily via the pentose 
                     phosphate pathway."
