     gene            complement(3541345..3543429)
                     /gene="malQ"
                     /locus_tag="b3416"
                     /label=malQ
     CDS             complement(3541345..3543429)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="malQ"
                     /locus_tag="b3416"
                     /product="bifunctional 4-alpha-glucanotransferase /
                     amylomaltase"
                     /EC_number="2.4.1.25"
                     /label=malQ
                     /note="Amylomaltase (MalQ) was first described as a 
                     maltose-inducible enzyme that catalyzes a reversible 
                     transglycosidase reaction |CITS: [18150023][15413815]|. The
                     enzyme belongs to the glycoside hydrolase family GH77 of 
                     4-&alpha;-glucanotransferases |CITS: [26006747]| that 
                     catalyzes transglycosylation of maltose and maltodextrins. 
                     MalQ does not have maltase activity, i.e. it does not 
                     hydrolyze maltose to form two molecules of glucose |CITS: 
                     [13844669][791642]|. The specificity of MalQ for donor and 
                     acceptor molecules of the transglycosylation reaction has 
                     been difficult to ascertain. It was initially reported that
                     maltose serves as an acceptor substrate of the enzyme 
                     |CITS: [13844669]|. Further experiments using more highly 
                     purified maltose suggested that maltose is a poor acceptor 
                     |CITS: [11945237][791642]|. Later, it was demonstrated that
                     the enzyme can utilize purified <sup>14</sup>C-maltose to 
                     produce labeled maltodextrins and glucose |CITS: 
                     [9675373][16321936]|.
                     Maltotetraose appears to be the most efficient substrate 
                     |CITS: [4927806][4927807][30691252]|. Kinetic data of the 
                     utilization of maltose and higher oligomers alone or with 
                     the addition of glucose have been measured |CITS: 
                     [4927807]|. MalQ transfers glycosyl chains of 1-9 glucosyl 
                     units from the nonreducing end of donor molecules onto the 
                     nonreducing end of maltodextrin acceptors, including 
                     maltose and glucose |CITS: [4927807][791642]|.  Glucose 
                     feedback-inhibits the reaction at concentrations above 0.1 
                     mM |CITS: [19028900]|.
                     Studies using <i>malP</i>, <i>malQ</i> and <i>glgA</i> 
                     deletion mutants demonstrated that MalQ can catalyze 
                     glycogen formation from maltodextrins. <i>In vitro</i>, 
                     MalQ is able to produce glycogen from maltose when the 
                     liberated glucose was removed enzymatically and the 
                     glycogen branching enzyme GlgB was added |CITS: 
                     [21421758]|. Conversely, a Delta;<i>malQ</i> mutant grown 
                     on glucose shows increased accumulation of glycogen; <i>in 
                     vitro</i>, MalQ enhances glycogen breakdown when added to a
                     reaction mixture with GlgX and GlgP |CITS: [30691252]|. 
                     Crystal structures of apo-MalQ, MalQ in complex with 
                     maltose, and MalQ in complex with the inhibitory transition
                     state analog acarviosine-glucose-acarbose have been solved 
                     at 2.80, 2.10 and 2.10 &Aring; resolution, respectively. 
                     Each structure revealed a distinct state of the enzyme, 
                     suggesting that major conformational changes occur during 
                     catalysis. Assay data showed that the equilibrium 
                     concentration of glucose and maltodextrins produced by MalQ
                     is dependent on the initial average number of glycosidic 
                     bonds per molecule of substrate, with less glucose formed 
                     from longer substrates |CITS: [26139606]|.
                     <i>malQ</i> mutants are unable to utilize maltose as the 
                     sole source of carbon |CITS: 
                     [5332865][4891257][4943758][21421758]|. A <i>malQ</i> 
                     mutant in a <i>malB</i><sup>+</sup> background secretes 
                     acetylmaltose and acetylated maltodextrins |CITS: 
                     [7012137]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [9529892]|"
