                     /product="trifunctional RNA 5' adenylyl transferase / RNA 
                     3'-terminal phosphate cyclase / DNA 5' adenylyl 
                     transferase"
                     /EC_number="6.5.1.4"
                     /label=rtcA
                     /note="RNA 3'-terminal phosphate cyclase (RtcA) converts
                     the 3'-terminal phosphate of various RNA substrates into 
                     the 2',3'-cyclic phosphodiester in an ATP-dependent 
                     reaction |CITS: [9184239][9738023]|.  The physiological 
                     role of the enzyme is unknown.  Recently, it was shown that
                     the enzyme also has a novel polynucleotide 5' adenylylation
                     activity |CITS: [21098490]|, which raises additional 
                     possibilities for its physiological function. RtcA can also
                     act on RNA substrates with 2'-terminal phosphate ends, 
                     albeit at a dramatically lower rate.  RtcA is proposed to 
                     be part of an RNA repair pathway, converting RNA 
                     2'-phosphates, which are not substrates of RtcB, to 
                     2',3'-cyclic phosphates that can be sealed |CITS: 
                     [23945037]|.
                     The reaction mechanism of RtcA has been studied |CITS: 
                     [9184239][9738023]|.  Cyclization occurs by a three-step 
                     mechanism involving formation of a covalent 
                     protein-nucleoside monophosphate intermediate |CITS: 
                     [9738023]|.  In <i>E. coli</i>, the site of adenylation was
                     shown to be the His308 residue |CITS: [10574971]|. Crystal 
                     structures of the apoenzyme and several enzyme-substrate 
                     and enzyme-product complexes have been solved |CITS: 
                     [10673421][20399182][22167800]|.  The enzyme consists of 
                     two identifiable domains |CITS: [10673421]|.
                     A crystal structure of the RtcA-adenylate intermediate at 
                     1.7 &Aring; resolution and site-directed mutagenesis of 
                     residues in the adenylate-binding pocket revealed a 
                     potential mechanism for nucleotidyl transfer |CITS: 
                     [20399182]|, which was further investigated with the help 
                     of crystal structures of additional reaction intermediates 
                     |CITS: [22167800]|.
                     The <i>rtcA</i> gene is not essential for growth in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [9738023]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [10397337]|"
