     gene            complement(3550212..3551438)
                     /gene="rtcB"
                     /locus_tag="b3421"
                     /label=rtcB
     CDS             complement(3550212..3551438)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rtcB"
                     /locus_tag="b3421"
                     /product="trifunctional RNA-splicing ligase / 3'-PO4/5'-OH
                     RNA ligase / RNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.5.1.8"
                     /label=rtcB
                     /note="RtcB is a GTP-dependent 3'-5' RNA ligase with the
                     ability to join RNA 2',3'-cyclic-PO<sub>4</sub> or 
                     3'-PO<sub>4</sub> ends to RNA 5'-OH ends |CITS: 
                     [21224389][22045815][22320833][22474365]|.  RtcB can 
                     perform RNA repair and tRNA splicing functions when 
                     expressed in yeast host cells |CITS: [21757685]|.
                     RtcB can ligate 3'-PO<sub>4</sub> and 5'-OH DNA ends that 
                     contain a single ribonucleoside 3'-PO<sub>4</sub> or a 
                     2',3'-cyclic-PO<sub>4</sub>, and can perform a DNA splicing
                     reaction utilizing DNA 3'-PO<sub>4</sub> and 5'-OH ends via
                     a DNA<sub>3'</sub>pp<sub>5'</sub>G intermediate.  This is a
                     novel activity; ""classic"" DNA ligases are unable to 
                     ligate such substrates |CITS: [24218597]|.  The 
                     DNA<sub>3'</sub>pp<sub>5'</sub>G<sub>OH</sub> cap generated
                     by RtcB protects against degradation by |FRAME: 
                     EG10926-MONOMER ExoI| and |FRAME: EG11073-MONOMER ExoIII| 
                     |CITS: [25049385]| and inhibits proofreading by |FRAME: 
                     EG10747-MONOMER| and |FRAME: CPLX0-3803 DNA polymerase III|
                     |CITS: [25753667]|.  Conversely, the cap guanosine 3'-OH 
                     can be extended by both Pol II and Pol III.  Once embedded 
                     in DNA, the pyrophosphate-linked ribonucleotide can not be 
                     removed by RNase HII activity |CITS: [25753667]|.
                     Conserved residues that are essential for RtcB activity 
                     have been identified by site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [21757685][22474365]|.  The reaction appears to follow a 
                     three-step mechanism with initial activation of RtcB by GTP
                     hydrolysis and formation of a covalent RtcB-guanylate 
                     adduct |CITS: [22045815][22320833][22474365]|.  The 
                     guanylate group is then transferred to the 3'-phosphate of 
                     a polynucleotide, forming a polynucleotide-(3')pp(5')G 
                     intermediate.  A 5'-OH then attacks the -N(3')pp(5')G 
                     intermediate, forming the ligated product |CITS: 
                     [22474365]|.  An alternative hypothesis involves generation
                     of a 2'-3' cyclic phosphate intermediate and release of GMP
                     followed by the attack of a 5'-OH of a second RNA and 
                     subsequent ligation to the 3' phosphate |CITS: [22320833]|.
                      Further experiments indicate that the activated 
                     RNA(3')pp(5')G is an obligate intermediate even during 
                     ligation of a substrate that already contains the 2'-3' 
                     cyclic phosphate end, arguing against this alternative 
                     reaction mechanism |CITS: [22730297]|.
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme from <i>Pyrococcus 
                     horikoshii</i> show two coordination complexes for 
                     Mn<sup>2+</sup>; mutagenesis of the corresponding sites in 
                     the <i>E. coli</i> enzyme shows that they play distinct 
                     catalytic roles.  The Arg189 residue may coordinate a 
                     phosphodiester at the 5'-OH end |CITS: [26858100]|. 
                     Expression of the <i>rtcBA</i> operon is regulated by RtcR 
                     from a &sigma;<sup>54</sup>-dependent promoter |CITS: 
                     [9738023]|.
                     Comment: |CITS: [24282304]|"
