     gene            complement(3553207..3553965)
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="glpR"
                     /locus_tag="b3423"
                     /label=glpR
     CDS             complement(join(3553207..3553812,3553816..3553965))
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="glpR"
                     /locus_tag="b3423"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional repressor GlpR"
                     /label=glpR
                     /note="The <i>sn</i>-<b>Gl</b>ycerol-3-<b>p</b>hosphate 
                     <b>r</b>epressor,"" GlpR, acts as the repressor of the 
                     glycerol-3-phosphate regulon, which is organized in 
                     different operons |CITS: [3316209][825019][1556120][917984 
                     5][1400248][1372899][8955388]|. However, the array of 
                     promoters repressed by GlpR has been expanded |CITS: 
                     [32955440]|.This regulator is part of the <i>glpEGR</i> 
                     operon, yet it can also be constitutively expressed as an 
                     independent (<i>glpR</i>) transcription unit |CITS: 
                     [9524241][3881401]|.
                     Various target genes of GlpR were found to be downregulated
                     after 10 minutes of exposure to 2.5 mM 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>; in accordance, the <i>glpR</i> 
                     gene was slightly upregulated|CITS:[35453340]|. On the 
                     other hand, many genes of the GlpR regulon appear to be 
                     induced at pH 5.8, contributing to cell survival under this
                     pH condition |CITS: [37909716]|.
                     The activity of the GlpR iModulon increases in cells 
                     evolved on glycerol as a carbon source |CITS:[38636729]|. 
                     The operons regulated by GlpR are induced when 
                     <i>Escherichia coli</i> is grown in the presence of the 
                     inductor, glycerol, or glycerol-3-phosphate (G3P), and the 
                     absence of glucose |CITS:[825019]|. In the absence of the 
                     inductor, this repressor binds in tandem to inverted repeat
                     sequences that consist of 20-nucleotide-long DNA target 
                     sites |CITS: [8157609][1556120][825019]|. In contrast, 
                     |CITS: [32955440]| discovered operons whose regulation 
                     appears to be mediated by a single GlpR site per operon.
                     Binding of GlpR to DNA is diminished in the presence of the
                     inducers glycerol or G3P. Accordingly, for the 
                     GlpR-regulated operons discovered by |CITS: [32955440]|, 
                     GlpR binds strongly in the presence of glucose while all 
                     other growth conditions result in greatly diminished, but 
                     not entirely abolished, binding. With only one single site,
                     GlpR functions as an indirect glucose sensor, as only the 
                     absence of glucose is needed to relieve repression by GlpR 
                     |CITS: [32955440]|.
                     An electrophoretic mobility shift assay (EMSA) showed 
                     ribose-5-P enhanced GlpR binding to DNA, counteracting the 
                     glycerol-3-P dissociation effect |CITS: [26691989]|.
                     By using synthetic gene circuits, it was demonstrated that 
                     GlpR, contrary to what has been observed for other 
                     repressors, represses transcription without evidence of 
                     stabilization of RNA polymerase (RNAP) |CITS:[39921566]|. 
                     GlpR belongs to the DeoR family of transcriptional 
                     regulators |CITS: [8955387]|. Accordingly, this 
                     transcriptional repressor family protein is composed of two
                     domains: a conserved N-terminal domain which contains the 
                     DNA-binding region, and the carboxy-terminal domain, which 
                     is involved in oligomerization and binding of the inducer 
                     |CITS: [8955387]|.
                     Inhibition of <i>glpR</i> expression by CRISPRi reduced 
                     cellular fitness under treatment with the antibiotic 
                     pyocyanin |CITS:[40352728]|.
                     Resequencing of multiple isolates of the MG1655 strain has 
                     identified several genetic variations compared to the 
                     reference sequence, including a 1-bp deletion in 
                     <i>glpR</i> in strain ATCC700926 |CITS: [22081388]|.  <a h 
                     ref=""http://ecoliwiki.net/colipedia/index.php/Category:Str
                     ain:MG1655#Resequencing_of_MG1655_and_comparison_to_the_ref
                     erence_strain"">This EcoliWiki page</a> summarizes these 
                     sequence differences. The GlpR binding landscape was 
                     globally mapped in vivo by standardized ChIP-seq and 
                     quantitatively modeled using BoltzNet, a biophysically 
                     informed neural network that infers binding energy from 
                     sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|. The resulting ChIP-seq 
                     binding regions are available in RegulonDB (<a 
                     href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
     gene            complement(3553982..3554812)
                     /gene="glpG"
                     /locus_tag="b3424"
                     /label=glpG
     CDS             complement(3553982..3554812)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glpG"
                     /locus_tag="b3424"
                     /product="bifunctional rhomboid protease GlpG / rhomboid
                     protease"
                     /EC_number="3.4.21.105"
                     /label=glpG
                     /note="GlpG is an intramembrane protease of the rhomboid
                     family. Rhomboid proteases were first described in 
                     <i>Drosophila</i> and rhomboid-like genes are ubiquitous in
                     prokaryotic genomes; rhomboid proteases are intramembrane, 
                     non-classical serine proteases which cleave membrane 
                     proteins; GlpG catalysis has been intensively studied and 
                     described but its physiological role is not well 
                     understood.
                     GlpG cleaves the three <i>Drosophila</i> rhomboid 
                     substrates Spitz, Keren and Gurken in a mammalian cell 
                     transfection assay |CITS: [12225666]|. Purified GlpG 
                     cleaves the model substrate, <i>Drosophila</i> Gurken-TMD; 
                     cleavage does not require ATP |CITS: [15616571]|. GlpG 
                     cleaves an engineered, single spanning membrane protein of 
                     type I orientation (N<sub>out</sub>-C<sub>in</sub>) <i>in 
                     vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i>; substrate features and 
                     recognition motifs that facilitate GlpG cleavage have been 
                     explored |CITS: [16216077][17501925][20064469]|.  GlpG 
                     cleaves truncated forms of a multispanning membrane protein
                     (MdfA) <i>in vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> but intact MdfA 
                     is resistant to cleavage |CITS: [19919105]|. GlpG displays 
                     low substrate affinity and slow catalysis when analysed in 
                     real-time within a membrane environment; GlpG catalysed 
                     proteolysis is not driven by substrate affinity but is a 
                     kinetically controlled reaction |CITS: [24315097]|.
                     GlpG is a basic, polytopic inner membrane protein that 
                     belongs to the rhomboid family of membrane proteases |CITS:
                     [8955387][12225666]|. GlpG contains 6 transmembrane domains
                     with its N and C-termini located in the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [16216077]|. Crystal structures of GlpG |CITS: 
                     [17051161][17190827][17099694]| confirm the presence of six
                     transmembrane (TM) domains and show that the enzyme 
                     contains a periplasmically oriented, hydrophilic, internal 
                     cavity that contains the active site Ser-201/His-254 dyad. 
                     An isocoumarin derived inhibitor forms double covalent 
                     bonds with GlpG through the active site Ser-201 and His-254
                     residues; the alkylated acyl enzyme thus formed is 
                     extremely stable and its structure provides insights into 
                     substrate specificity and catalytic mechanism |CITS: 
                     [20890268]|. Further understanding has been achieved from 
                     structures of GlpG bound to various small inhibitors and 
                     substrate mimetics |CITS: [23665170][22130671][23359682][2 
                     2515733][23609444][25216680][26805573]| and from extensive 
                     mutational analyses |CITS: [17463085][18979634][19278654]| 
                     (and see further |CITS: [31603315][37467320]|).
                     The complete mechanism of GlpG catalysis (on a 
                     transmembrane peptide substrate), including gate opening 
                     and substrate binding, formation of intermediates, and 
                     product resolution has been delineated using time-resolved 
                     x-ray crystallography; an extracellular loop (L5) plays a 
                     key role in ushering substrate through the steps of 
                     catalysis |CITS: [31570873]|.
                     GlpG activity is modulated by its membrane environment 
                     |CITS: [36149963][39488732]|.
                     GlpG displays low thermodynamic stability; residues 
                     implicated in maintaining stability have been identified 
                     |CITS: [22797666]|. Folding and unfolding of GlpG has been 
                     studied in detail |CITS: [31780561][30470737][28808343][26 
                     858402][26999782][26479439][26056273]|.
                     Regulation has been described. The <i>glpEGR</I> genes form
                     a single complex operon with multiple promoters |CITS: 
                     [3012272][8955387][9524241]|.
                     Selected reviews: |CITS: [15326347][20070259][23585569][24 
                     099005][23432912][23518036][23937901][26621464][26716760][3
                     3210155]|<BR>Comments: |CITS: 
                     [17051148][17146459][17213330][17494772][20064458][ 
                     25027763]|"
