     CDS             complement(3559960..3561393)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glgA"
                     /locus_tag="b3429"
                     /product="glycogen synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.4.1.21"
                     /label=glgA
                     /note="Glycogen synthase is a glycosyltransferase, the
                     second enzyme in the glycogen biosynthesis pathway. It 
                     catalyzes the addition of a glucosyl unit from ADP-glucose 
                     to the non-reducing end of glycogen.
                     Lys15 may be involved in binding of ADP-glucose |CITS: 
                     [2105316][8226921]|. A conserved region around Cys379 is 
                     predicted to form a loop structure and is involved in the 
                     interaction with ADP-glucose |CITS: [14665620]|.  Lys277 
                     may be required for catalysis |CITS: [8288640]|. Further 
                     site-directed mutagenesis identified residues essential for
                     catalytic activity |CITS: [15033495]| and ADP-glucose 
                     binding |CITS: [16919233]|.
                     Crystal structures of the wild-type and mutant GlgA in 
                     various conformations have been solved, suggesting the 
                     location of catalytic residues |CITS: [19244233]|. A 
                     structure of GlgA with bound oligosaccharide indicates that
                     long-chain glycans only bind to the N-terminal domain, 
                     ensuring unencumbered interdomain movement and efficient 
                     catalysis |CITS: [19761218]|.
                     Native glycogen synthase binds to metal affinity columns 
                     and may thus bind Mg<sup>2+</sup>, like its human homolog 
                     |CITS: [16814929]|.
                     Glycogen synthase from <i>E. coli</i> B can exist in 
                     homodimeric, -trimeric and -tetrameric form |CITS: [2288]|.
                     However, all crystal structures obtained of the K-12 enzyme
                     show a monomeric form |CITS: [19244233]|. Review: |CITS: 
                     [12297270]|"
