     gene            complement(3561393..3562688)
                     /gene="glgC"
                     /locus_tag="b3430"
                     /label=glgC
     CDS             complement(3561393..3562688)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glgC"
                     /locus_tag="b3430"
                     /product="glucose-1-phosphate adenylyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.27"
                     /label=glgC
                     /note="Glucose-1-phosphate adenylyltransferase (GlgC)
                     catalyzes the rate-limiting first step in the biosynthesis 
                     of glycogen. The enzyme is allosterically activated by the 
                     glycolytic intermediate fructose-1,6-bisphosphate (FBP) and
                     inhibited by AMP and pyrophosphate, thus responding to the 
                     energy state of the cell.
                     GlgC consists of an N-terminal glycosyltransferase A-like 
                     domain and a C-terminal regulatory domain |CITS: 
                     [27545622]|. Various mutants and truncated forms of the 
                     enzyme have been studied |CITS: [2543670][9514953][9587413 
                     ][9578610][9750179][12135365][17496097]|. Co-expression of 
                     separately encoded N- and C-terminal domain polypeptides 
                     yields an active enzyme. The two domains interact strongly 
                     |CITS: [15327982]|. The Asp142 residue is important for 
                     catalytic activity |CITS: [11567027]|. Homology modelling 
                     and site-directed mutagenesis allowed the identification of
                     residues that play a role in binding of glucose-1-phosphate
                     |CITS: [17079236]|.
                     The N-terminal region is required for allosteric regulation
                     of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [2162151][11339804][16530732][17496097]|, while the 
                     C-terminal domain contributes to regulator selectivity 
                     |CITS: [12135365]|. Residues Trp113 and Gln74 have been 
                     shown to be required for activation by 
                     fructose-1,6-bisphosphate |CITS: [21741429]|. Molecular 
                     dynamics simulations further identified the Pro103-Arg115 
                     loop as part of the allosteric activation path, which was 
                     confirmed by results from site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [25620658]|. Crystal structures of the enzyme in complexes 
                     with both allosteric regulators have allowed direct 
                     identification of the AMP and FBP binding sites. AMP binds 
                     in a pocket buried between subunits, while FBP binds at the
                     very C teminus of the protein; the two sites partially 
                     overlap.  AMP binding leads to stabilization of the 
                     quarternary structure, while the FBP-bound protein is less 
                     stable and more dynamic. A regulatory mechanism has been 
                     proposed |CITS: [27545622]|.
                     Pyruvate alone is a weak activator of GlgC, but has a 
                     synergistic effect with fructose-1,6-bisphosphate, and 
                     decreases inhibition of the enzyme by AMP |CITS: 
                     [25102309]|.
                     Under liquid culture conditions, mutations in <i>glgC</i> 
                     arise spontaneously and lead to contact-dependent growth 
                     inhibition of the ancestral <i>glgC</i> wild type strains 
                     |CITS: [17956846]|. Overexpression of wild-type <i>glgC</i>
                     leads to increased glycogen accumulation |CITS: 
                     [17719034]|.
                     Expression of GlgC is regulated at the transcriptional and 
                     translational level. Transcription of <i>glgCAP</i> is 
                     activated by cAMP and CRP as well as ppGpp |CITS: 
                     [2468650][ROMEO90]| and PhoPQ under conditions of low 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup> concentration |CITS: [19702577][21029047]|.
                     CsrA, the carbon storage regulator, binds to <i>glgC</i> 
                     mRNA and inhibits initiation of translation |CITS: 
                     [12067347]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12794190][26442935]|"
