     gene            complement(3564676..3566862)
                     /gene="glgB"
                     /locus_tag="b3432"
                     /label=glgB
     CDS             complement(3564676..3566862)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glgB"
                     /locus_tag="b3432"
                     /product="1,4-alpha-glucan branching enzyme"
                     /EC_number="2.4.1.18"
                     /label=glgB
                     /note="Gycogen branching enzyme (GlgB) belongs to the
                     glycosyl hydrolase GH13 family of enzymes and catalyzes the
                     formation of the branched &alpha;-1,6-glucosidic linkages 
                     from the growing &alpha;-1,4-glucan chain during glycogen 
                     biosynthesis. When cells are grown in Kornberg medium, a 
                     threshold level of around 4 nmoles (dry weight) of ppGpp is
                     necessary to trigger net glycogen accumulation |CITS: 
                     [25188023]|.
                     Biochemical studies of the enzyme have been performed using
                     <i>E. coli</i> B |CITS: [407932]|. Gycogen branching enzyme
                     has a preference for transferring chains between 5 and 16 
                     glucose units. The minimum chain length required for 
                     branching is 12 |CITS: [9185617]|. The enzyme appears to 
                     prefer A chains as acceptors and to form branch linkages at
                     the third glucose residue from the reducing end of the 
                     acceptor chain |CITS: [25107532]|. The reaction products of
                     N-terminally truncated enzymes show an altered branching 
                     pattern |CITS: [10845715][11795883][13679080][25947105]|.
                     The Glu459 residue is important for specific activity and 
                     substrate specificity of the enzyme |CITS: [9636047]|, and 
                     the Tyr300 residue is required for enzymatic activity and 
                     thermostability |CITS: [11368019]|.
                     Crystal structures of GlgB have been solved |CITS: 
                     [12196524][26280198]|. The enzyme consists of an N-terminal
                     &beta;-sandwich Carbohydrate Binding Module 48 (CBM48) 
                     domain, a central (&beta;/&alpha;)<sub>8</sub> barrel 
                     domain containing the active site, and a C-terminal &beta; 
                     sandwich domain |CITS: [12196524][26280198]|. Crystal 
                     structures of the N-terminally truncated &Delta;112 enzyme 
                     in complex with the linear oligosaccharides maltoheptaose 
                     and maltohexaose show six distinct oligosaccharide binding 
                     sites on the surface of the enzyme. Mutations in conserved 
                     residues within binding sites I and VI significantly reduce
                     enzymatic activity |CITS: [26280198]|.
                     A phylogenomic analysis of glycogen branching and 
                     debranching enzymes has been performed |CITS: [25148856]|. 
                     Phylogenetic analysis of the <i>glgBXCAP</i> operon 
                     arrangement among the &gamma;-proteobacteria shows that it 
                     is conserved among the <i>Enterobacteriales</i> and 
                     <i>Pasteurellales</i> |CITS: [25607991]|."
