     gene            complement(3574282..3574376)
                     /gene="ryhB"
                     /locus_tag="b4451"
                     /label=ryhB
     misc_RNA        complement(3574282..3574376)
                     /gene="ryhB"
                     /locus_tag="b4451"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA RyhB"
                     /note="RyhB is a small RNA which acts to reduce iron
                     consumption under low-iron conditions by downregulating 
                     expression of iron-containing proteins, including enzymes 
                     of the TCA cycle and the aerobic respiratory chain |CITS: 
                     [11917098][16199566][16982641][22474262]|.  In addition, 
                     RyhB promotes synthesis of the siderophore enterobactin 
                     |CITS: [20696910]|.  RyhB was first identified as a small 
                     RNA of approximately 90 nt in length |CITS: 
                     [11445539][11448770]|. The role of RyhB in iron-sulfur 
                     (Fe-S) cluster biogenesis was simulated and analyzed in a 
                     mathematical model in |CITS: [37223744]|.
                     RIBOseq data suggests that in <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7 
                     EDL933, a small open reading frame within RyhB that begins 
                     with an ATT start codon may be translated to the 
                     nonapeptide MAHIASSIT |CITS: [28245801]|.
                     Structural and functional components of RyhB were 
                     identified using a library of single nucleotide mutants and
                     evaluating their effects on known regulatory targets |CITS:
                     [25262352]|.  Theoretical analysis and quantitative 
                     evaluation of regulation by RyhB has been performed |CITS: 
                     [17713988]|.  Prediction of regulatory targets of RyhB can 
                     be improved by considering accessible regions in both the 
                     sRNA and its target |CITS: [22767260]| and by simulation of
                     the effect of the sRNA on translation initiation |CITS: 
                     [23203192]|.  Additional targets have been predicted by 
                     microarray and computational analyses and comparative 
                     genomics |CITS: [16199566][16717284][23980183]| and 
                     experimentally identified by an affinity purification 
                     method |CITS: [25891076]| and by Ribo-seq |CITS: 
                     [26546513]|.  A candidate RyhB regulon was inferred by 
                     integrating information on experimentally supported targets
                     and sRNA-mRNA interactions with transcription datasets; new
                     targets with experimental support are <i>acpP, amn, cheY, 
                     fabZ, folX, gshB, mrp, rna, rsmE, tpx, ubiD</i>, and 
                     <i>ybaB</i> |CITS: [32487739]|. <b>Regulatory Targets of 
                     RyhB</b>
                     RyhB downregulates full-length <i>sdhCDAB</i> RNA abundance
                     |CITS: [11917098]|.  RyhB also mediates regulation of 
                     <i>acnA</i>, <i>fumA</i>, <i>bfr</i>, <i>sodB</i> |CITS: 
                     [11917098]| and <i>acnB</i> |CITS: [16199566][25092924]| by
                     Fur.  Indirect regulation by Fur during anaerobic growth 
                     conditions revealed a new set of targets regulated by RyhB 
                     |CITS: [26670385]|.  RyhB was shown to inhibit translation 
                     of <i>sodB in vitro</i> |CITS: [14617150]| and stimulate 
                     degradation of <i>sodB</i> mRNA by RNase E |CITS: 
                     [12975324]|.  In the absence of RNase E, gene silencing can
                     occur by translational repression alone |CITS: [16549791]|;
                     conversely, RyhB-induced mRNA cleavage at a distal site by 
                     RNase E does not require translation |CITS: [21289064]|. 
                     Quantitative analysis of the interaction between wild-type 
                     and mutant RyhB and <i>sodB</i> mRNA correlates with 
                     thermodynamic models of this interaction and show that a 
                     continuum of repression strengths is achievable. 
                     Surprisingly, a truncated form of RyhB is able to regulate 
                     certain targets independently of Hfq |CITS: [21742981]|. 
                     Phenotypic noise caused by RyhB regulation of <i>sodB</i> 
                     and <i>fumA</i> has been measured; extrinsic noise was 
                     found to be dominant |CITS: [23519613]|.
                     RyhB indirectly down-regulates Fur expression by 
                     down-regulating expression of the translationally coupled 
                     upstream reading frame Uof |CITS: [17268550]|.  The 
                     characteristics of feed-forward regulation of SodA 
                     expression by Fur and RyhB has been modeled |CITS: 
                     [24621982]|.  The <i>cysE</i> gene has also been identified
                     as a direct target of RyhB regulation. Downregulating the 
                     expression of the serine acetyltransferase CysE increases 
                     the availability of serine for the production of 
                     enterobactin |CITS: [20696910]|.  RyhB overexpression 
                     decreases expression of <i>hemB</i> and <i>hemH</i> in the 
                     engineered DALA strain |CITS: [24188714]|.
                     RyhB was shown to promote cleavage of the polycistronic 
                     <i>iscRSUA</i> mRNA between the <i>iscR</i> and <i>iscS</i>
                     open reading frames.  The IscR-encoding 5' fragment remains
                     stable, while the <i>iscSUA</i> 3' fragment appears to be 
                     degraded |CITS: [19407815]|.
                     RyhB increases expression of the shikimate transporter ShiA
                     by increasing <i>shiA</i> mRNA stability and activating 
                     translation |CITS: [17542919]|.  RyhB is required for 
                     synthesis of CirA during iron starvation: Hfq blocks 
                     translation initiation, and binding of RyhB leads to 
                     changes in the mRNA secondary structure, thereby displacing
                     Hfq |CITS: [24065131]|.
                     Although <i>ftnA</i> expression was also thought to be 
                     regulated by RyhB |CITS: [11917098]|, microarray 
                     experiments indicated that expression of <i>ftnA</i> is not
                     dependent on <i>ryhB</i> |CITS: [16199566]|.  It was later 
                     shown that <i>ftnA</i> expression is directly regulated by 
                     Fur |CITS: [20015147]|.
                     It has been demonstrated that RyhB is also able to regulate
                     at the transcriptional level, to the same targets that 
                     regulate at the translational level, and that this 
                     regulation depends on Rho and Rut sites in the target 
                     |CITS: [34592145]|.
                     <b>RyhB Interaction With Hfq and Regulation of RyhB 
                     Activity</b>
                     An efficient interaction between RyhB RNA and Hfq protein 
                     has been detected |CITS: [11445539][36328526]|; Hfq is 
                     essential for RyhB activity |CITS: 
                     [11917098][14739933][15781494][19407815]| and is limiting 
                     for small RNA function |CITS: [21189298][22040174]|. 
                     Although different small RNAs show similar binding 
                     affinities for Hfq, they show differential ability to 
                     compete for Hfq binding |CITS: [21510661]|.  Hfq mediates 
                     interaction between RyhB and RNase E |CITS: [16166379]| and
                     protects RyhB from degradation by RNase E |CITS: 
                     [12975324][14561880]|.  Regulation of a subset of RyhB 
                     targets requires <i>relA</i> due to modulation of Hfq 
                     activity by RelA |CITS: [22393021]|.  RyhB RNA and other 
                     small RNAs are more unstable in a <i>pnp</i> mutant than in
                     wild type |CITS: [21527671]|.  Converseley, RyhB that is 
                     not bound by Hfq is degraded by PNPase; in a <i>hfq pnp</i>
                     double mutant, RyhB is stabilized |CITS: [22355164]|. But 
                     if RyhB is bound to Hfq, the PNPase appears to protect RyhB
                     from degradation by RNase E, which requires its AR2 region 
                     to degrade RyhB bound to Hfq, and most probably at the same
                     time the targets of RyhB are degraded by the RNase |CITS: 
                     [36409892]|.
                     RyhB was classified as a Class I small RNA, which 
                     accumulate to higher levels in distal face mutants and to 
                     reduced levels in rim mutants of Hfq |CITS: 
                     [23318956][26373314]|.
                     Affinity purification also identified direct interactions 
                     of RyhB with the 3' external transcribed spacer (ETS) 
                     sequence of a tRNA-encoding operon, |FRAME: TU00512|.  In 
                     this context, the |FRAME: RNA0-388| is thought to function 
                     as a sponge that absorbs transcriptional noise while RyhB 
                     expression is not induced |CITS: [25891076]|.  A genetic 
                     screen identified additional regulators that affect RyhB 
                     activity, including the |FRAME: RNA0-418 AspX| sponge RNA 
                     |CITS: [34210798]|.
                     <b>Mutant Phenotypes and Expression</b>
                     An <i>ryhB</i> mutant exhibits a defect in Fur-mediated 
                     regulation of <i>sdhCDAB</i>, <i>acnA</i>, <i>fumA</i>, 
                     <i>ftnA</i>, <i>bfr</i>, and <i>sodB</i> |CITS: [11917098]|
                     and increases the viability of <i>rpoS</i><sup>+</sup> 
                     cells |CITS: [18556786]|. High-copy expression leads to a 
                     growth defect on succinate and decreased expression of 
                     <i>rpoS</i> in minimal media |CITS: [11445539]|.  Increased
                     expression of RyhB leads to increased resistance to 
                     gentamicin via its effect on the expression and activity of
                     respiratory complexes |CITS: [31009454]|.
                     RyhB is more highly expressed in minimal medium than in 
                     rich medium; expression increases in stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [11445539][11448770]|.  RyhB is expressed at higher 
                     levels when cells are grown in pyruvate compared to glucose
                     |CITS: [31363904]|.  Expression is repressed by Fur |CITS: 
                     [11917098]|.  Overexpression of RyhB leads to an increase 
                     in the concentration of free intracellular iron |CITS: 
                     [17078818]|.  RyhB is essential for growth during iron 
                     starvation |CITS: [17078818]|.  The iron homeostasis 
                     network has been investigated |CITS: [20345668]|.  
                     Overexpression of RyhB decreases swarming motility and 
                     curli expression |CITS: [26469694]|.
                     RyhB activity is also regulated at the level of 
                     transcription termination.  In normally growing cells, 
                     frequent read-through at the Rho-independent terminator is 
                     observed; this longer form of RyhB does not bind Hfq. 
                     Stress conditions enhance termination at the 
                     Rho-independent terminator |CITS: [26106215]|.  The 
                     structure of the terminator appears to be optimal for 
                     generation of functional RyhB |CITS: [28606943]|.
                     The turnover of RyhB RNA is regulated; degradation is 
                     mainly dependent on RNase III |CITS: [15781494]|, although 
                     earlier studies had implicated the RNase E/degradosome 
                     pathway |CITS: [12975324]|.  RyhB RNA degradation is 
                     inhibited by Hfq and stimulated by RNA duplex formation 
                     |CITS: [12975324][15781494]|.  |FRAME: EG10743-MONOMER 
                     PNPase| and RNase PH together stabilize RyhB during 
                     exponential growth, while PNPase accelerates degradation of
                     paired RyhB-mRNA target RNAs |CITS: [27698082]|. 
                     Paradoxically, |FRAME: EG10690-MONOMER| is required for 
                     stability of RyhB, possibly by ensuring the efficient decay
                     of the |FRAME: RNA0-388| RNA |CITS: [30061117]|. SraI: 
                     ""<b>s</b>mall <b>R</b>N<b>A</b>"" |CITS: [11448770]|<br>
