     gene            complement(3578441..3580183)
                     /gene="ggt"
                     /locus_tag="b3447"
                     /label=ggt
     CDS             complement(3578441..3580183)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ggt"
                     /locus_tag="b3447"
                     /product="glutathione hydrolase proenzyme"
                     /EC_number="3.4.19.13; 2.3.2.2"
                     /label=ggt
                     /note="<i>ggt</i> encodes a periplasmic 
                     &gamma;-glutamylhydrolase also known as 
                     &gamma;-glutamyltranspeptidase (GGT).
                     The purified enzyme has both &gamma;-glutamyl hydrolase and
                     &gamma;-glutamyl transpeptidase activity; various 
                     &gamma;-glutamyl compounds can serve as donors <i>in 
                     vitro</i> including glutathione, &gamma;-glutamylalanine, 
                     L-theanine, <i>S</i>-methyl-glutathione, 
                     &gamma;-L-glutamyl-L-tyrosine, 
                     &gamma;-L-glutamyl-L-histidine and the model compound 
                     L-&gamma;-glutamyl-<i>p</i>-nitroanilide. Glutamyl 
                     acceptors used <i>in vitro</i> include glycl-glycine, 
                     L-arginine, L-lysine, L-histidine, L-phenylalanine, 
                     L-methionine and L-dopa |CITS: [2877974]|. The purified 
                     enzyme has a low (&micro;M) K<sub>m</sub> for donors and a 
                     high (M) K<sub>m</sub> for acceptors |CITS: [2877974]|. GGT
                     is essential for cysteine (glycine) auxotrophs to utilise 
                     exogenous glutathione or &gamma;-glutamylcysteine 
                     (&gamma;-glutamylglycine) as a source of cysteine (glycine)
                     suggesting that its &gamma;-glutamyl hydrolase activity 
                     could function for amino acid salvage |CITS: 
                     [8104180][SUZUKI1995]|. A role in hydrolysing the 
                     &gamma;-glutamyl linkage in pepetidoglycan has also been 
                     suggested |CITS: [8104180]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> GGT is a heterodimer consisting of a large 
                     subunit (39 kDa) and a small subunit (20 kDa) generated 
                     from the catalytically inactive precursor protein (pro-GGT)
                     by cleavage of the peptide bond between Gln-390 and Thr-391
                     |CITS: [2877974][2570061][8720138]|. Processing is 
                     autocatalytic; Thr391 is the catalytic nucleophile for both
                     processing and the enzyme reaction |CITS: 
                     [10869181][12207027]|. In the crystal structure of GGT with
                     bound substrate (L-glutamate or GSH) the small and large 
                     subunits are intertwined; the &gamma;-glutamyl moiety of 
                     the substrate binds in a pocket which contains the 
                     catalytic Thr residue |CITS: [16618936]|. Processing is 
                     associated with conformational change and formation of a 
                     substrate binding pocket |CITS: [17135273]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> exhibits maximum GGT activity during 
                     stationary phase and when grown at 20&deg;C |CITS: 
                     [2877975]|. Cells grown at 20&deg;C contain 10 fold more 
                     <i>ggt</i> mRNA than those grown at 37&deg;C; <i>ggt</i> 
                     mRNA is more stable at 20&deg;C compared to 37&deg;C |CITS:
                     [9028034]|.
                     GGT deficient mutants have increased GSH in the culture 
                     medium compared to wild type and the concentration of 
                     extracellular GSH decreases more gradually during 
                     stationary phase; they do not exhibit cold sensitivity nor 
                     nutrient auxotrophy |CITS: [2887543]|. GGT deficient 
                     mutants show increased resistance to osmotic stress |CITS: 
                     [11703177]|.
                     GGT is a member of the N-terminal nucleophile (Ntn) 
                     hydrolase superfamily |CITS: [7477383]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> GGT has been used for the production of 
                     various &gamma;-glutamyl compounds (see review by |CITS: 
                     [17031476]|)
                     Review: |CITS: [22527720]|"
