     gene            complement(3589811..3590920)
                     /gene="livK"
                     /locus_tag="b3458"
                     /label=livK
     CDS             complement(3589811..3590920)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="livK"
                     /locus_tag="b3458"
                     /product="multifunctional L-leucine/L-phenylalanine ABC
                     transporter periplasmic binding protein / leucine / 
                     L-phenylalanine ABC transporter / L-leucine transport / 
                     L-phenylalanine transport"
                     /EC_number="7.4.2.2"
                     /label=livK
                     /note="LivK is the periplasmic binding protein of the LS
                     or leucine specific ABC transport system in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     K-12 |CITS: [4584809][323236]|. The LS system (LivKHMGF) is
                     able to transport phenylanine and plays a physiologically 
                     relevant role in phenylalanine accumulation 
                     |CITS:[14702302]|. The LS system transports both isomers of
                     leucine |CITS: [323236][6445555]|.
                     LivK is synthesized as a precursor; a 23 amino acid signal 
                     sequence is cleaved upon export to the periplasm |CITS: 
                     [6990419][6445555][3891753]|. Purified LivK* binds 
                     L-leucine (apparent K<sub>D</sub> = 0.4 &micro;M) and 
                     L-phenylalanine (apparent K<sub>D</sub> = 0.18 &micro;M) 
                     |CITS: [2007577][11206079]| but does not bind L-isoleucine 
                     or L-valine (K<sub>D</sub> > 1 mM) |CITS: [2007577]| (LivK*
                     denotes a protein that has been engineered with a StyI 
                     restriction site added at codon 124).
                     The crystal structure of LivK in both the apo- and 
                     ligand-bound form has been determined at 1.5 and 1.8 A 
                     resolution |CITS: [2649683][14672931]|. The protein 
                     consists of two domains connected by a 3-stranded hinge; in
                     the closed form, ligands (phenylalanine and leucine) are 
                     bound in a cleft formed between the two domains; binding is
                     stabilized by hydrogen bonds and van der Waals contacts."
