     gene            complement(3591915..3593018)
                     /gene="livJ"
                     /locus_tag="b3460"
                     /label=livJ
     CDS             complement(3591915..3593018)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="livJ"
                     /locus_tag="b3460"
                     /product="multifunctional branched chain amino
                     acid/phenylalanine ABC transporter periplasmic binding 
                     protein / branched chain amino acid / phenylalanine ABC 
                     transporter / transport of L-leucine / transport of 
                     L-valine / transport of L-isoleucine / transport of 
                     L-phenylalanine"
                     /EC_number="7.4.2.2"
                     /label=livJ
                     /note="LivJ is the periplasmic binding protein of the
                     LIV-I (LivJHMGF) branched chain amino acid and 
                     phenylalanine ABC transport system in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [323236]|. Early studies suggested that LIV-I served
                     as the entry point for the branched amino acids L-leucine, 
                     L-isoleucine, and L-valine and possibly L-alanine, 
                     L-threonine and L-serine  |CITS: 
                     [4972226][4908543][4584809]|. The LIV-I system is also able
                     to transport phenylanine and plays a physiologically 
                     relevant role in phenylalanine accumulation 
                     |CITS:[14702302]|.
                     LivJ is synthesized as a precursor; a 23 amino acid signal 
                     sequence is cleaved upon export to the periplasm |CITS: 
                     [328304][6445555][3891753]|. Purified LivJ* binds L-leucine
                     (K<sub>D</sub> = 0.4 &micro;M), L-isoleucine K<sub>D</sub> 
                     = 0.4 &micro;M) and L-valine (K<sub>D</sub> = 0.7 &micro;M)
                     |CITS: [2007577]| (LivJ* denotes a protein that has been 
                     engineered with a StyI restriction site added at codon 
                     124).
                     Crystal structures indicate that the protein consists of 
                     two globular domains connected by a 3-stranded hinge |CITS:
                     [6350301][2649682]|."
