     gene            complement(3593289..3594143)
                     /gene="rpoH"
                     /locus_tag="b3461"
                     /label=rpoH
     CDS             complement(3593289..3594143)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpoH"
                     /locus_tag="b3461"
                     /product="bifunctional RNA polymerase sigma factor RpoH /
                     RNA polymerase sigma 32"
                     /label=rpoH
                     /note="<i>rpoH</i> encodes &sigma;<sup>32</sup>, the
                     primary sigma factor controlling the heat shock response 
                     during log-phase growth.  It is subject to tight control 
                     via a multivalent regulatory system that reponds to 
                     temperature and the abundance of misfolded proteins within 
                     the cell.
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> levels rise, plateau and then drop 
                     following a heat shock, causing a similar induction in 
                     expression in heat shock genes |CITS: [15757896][2900239][ 
                     3306410][6092838][6380765][6387714][7038687]|. At elevated 
                     temperatures, formation of E&sigma;<sup>32</sup> is 
                     favoured over that of E&sigma;<sup>70</sup>; 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> has greater affinity for |FRAME: 
                     APORNAP-CPLX| than &sigma;<sup>70</sup> at high 
                     temperatures |CITS: [7588636]|.  Large-scale identification
                     of E&sigma;<sup>32</sup> transcriptional targets has been 
                     carried out |CITS: [15757896][16818608]|.  The consensus 
                     promoter for E&sigma;<sup>32</sup> initiation is 
                     TNtCNCcCTTGAA at -35 and CCCCATtTa at -10, where lowercase 
                     letters indicate a tendency toward, but not an absolute 
                     requirement for, a certain nucleotide at that location 
                     |CITS: [3887408][19400791]|.  Notably, recent work 
                     indicates that E&sigma;<sup>32</sup> may only be involved 
                     in the heat shock response that occurs during log-phase, 
                     aerobic growth, with entirely different systems accounting 
                     for heat shock responses during stationary phase and 
                     anaerobic growth |CITS: [16775749]|.
                     In addition to the interaction of &sigma;<sup>32</sup> to 
                     the -10 and -35 boxes of the target promoters, it interacts
                     weakly with the spacer between them via the T128 and K130 
                     residues |CITS: [37238608]|. It also interacts with the RNA
                     polymerase via its &sigma;<sub>4</sub> and 
                     &sigma;<sub>2</sub> domains |CITS: [37238608]|. A histidine
                     in &sigma;<sup>32</sup>, instead of a tryptophan, as with  
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup>, works as a wedge to start the opening
                     of DNA at the -11 and -12 positions with respect to the 
                     transcriptional start site |CITS: [37238608]|.
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> is also responsible for genetic 
                     responses to other environmental insults.  Ethanol, 
                     alkaline pH changes and hyperosmotic shock all induce the 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup>-dependent heat shock response |CITS: 
                     [3539918][3542975][10594827]|.  In addition, 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> is induced by carbon starvation |CITS:
                     [2002001]|. The regulon of &sigma;<sup>32</sup> also 
                     appears to be induced in the absence of the tmRNA SsrA 
                     |CITS: [36471624]|.
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> regulation is a multivalent process 
                     consisting of transcriptional, translational and 
                     postranslational controls.
                     <i>rpoH</i> is transcribed from two constitutive 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup>-driven promoters, as well as at least 
                     one heat-shock responsive promoter and one 
                     &sigma;<sup>24</sup>-driven promoter |CITS: 
                     [3315851][3323832][2546916][2691330]|.  Three of the 
                     <i>rpoH</i> promoters are suppressed by DnaA binding |CITS:
                     [2540187]|.  <i>rpoH</i> transcription does not depend on 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> |CITS: [2430947]|. 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> abundance is regulated at the 
                     translational level via mRNA secondary structure.  Two 
                     sequences in the <i>rpoH</i> mRNA, one near the translation
                     start site and one internal, form a secondary structure 
                     element that is disrupted by higher temperatures, allowing 
                     translation |CITS: 
                     [2050641][1961716][7505426][9882652][10090722]|. RNAse III 
                     together with the PNPase helps to stabilize the <i>rpoH</i>
                     mRNA under heat shock|CITS: [35456749]|. The transcription 
                     of <i>rpoH</i> also seems to be induced indirectly by RNAse
                     III in a CytR- and CRP-independent way |CITS: [35456749]|.
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> is regulated post-translationally via 
                     degradation and by direct inhibition of its activity |CITS:
                     [3315848]|.  Importantly, &sigma;<sup>32</sup> must be 
                     associated with the inner membrane to be properly 
                     regulated. Membrane localization of &sigma;<sup>32</sup> is
                     facilitated by the |FRAME: SRP-CPLX ""signal recognition 
                     particle (SRP) translocation system""| although 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> does not appear to contain a signal 
                     sequence nor any transmembrane regions. Fractionation 
                     experiments suggest that approximately 50% of 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> is membrane-associated |CITS: 
                     [24358019]|.
                     The protein level of the heat shock sigma factor RpoH is 
                     increased due mistranslation of the general stress response
                     sigma factor RpoS, which protects cells against heat 
                     stress. This suggests a novel regulatory role of RpoS in 
                     the heat shock response |CITS: [31419308]|. 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> is a very unstable protein which is 
                     transiently stabilised upon heat shock |CITS: 
                     [3315848][3306410][2646289][10801971]|. 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> is stabilized in the absence of the 
                     chaperone protein GroES and by the presence of abnormal 
                     proteins |CITS: [7912695][7916010]|.  Degradation of 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> depends on the ATP dependent protease 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-2982 FtsH| and |FRAME: CPLX0-1163| |CITS: 
                     [7724592][9393683][9822823]|.
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> activity is directly controlled by the
                     |FRAME: EG10241-MONOMER ""DnaK""|-DnaJ-GrpE chaperone 
                     system.  When this chaperone system is occupied by 
                     substrates such as misfolded proteins, it is not available 
                     to bind &sigma;<sup>32</sup> and limit its activity |CITS: 
                     [8599944][9822822][9822823][1534276]|.  In this way, 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> activity is tied directly to the level
                     of misfolded proteins in the cell.  |FRAME: EG12193-MONOMER
                     CbpA| and the |FRAME: CPLX0-3934| can also both limit 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> activity in the same manner |CITS: 
                     [9822823][15545634]|. &sigma;<sup>32</sup> binds DnaK <i>in
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [1565647]|; &sigma;<sup>32</sup> contains 
                     multiple DnaK binding sites |CITS: [24532774]|.  DnaK, 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> and DnaJ form a stable complex <i>in 
                     vitro</i>; efficient formation of the complex depends on 
                     the presence of ATP. DnaK and DnaJ compete with RNA 
                     polymerase core for stable binding to &sigma;<sup>32</sup> 
                     |CITS: [8248205]|. The chaperones DnaK, DnaJ and GrpE 
                     interact with &sigma;<sup>32</sup> <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [1534276]|.  FtsH degrades &sigma;<sup>32</sup> present in 
                     a DnaK-&sigma;<sup>32</sup>-DnaJ complex <i>in vitro</i> 
                     but is unable to degrade &sigma;<sup>32</sup> present in 
                     the E&sigma;<sup>32</sup> complex |CITS: [9987118]|.
                     A &sigma;<sup>32</sup> I54N mutant is defective for 
                     chaperone (DnaK/J, GroEL/S) mediated inactivation <i>in 
                     vivo</i>, but shows no altered interaction with chaperones 
                     or RNA polymerase <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [17968012]|.  A 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> I54N mutant is defective in membrane 
                     association; artificially tethering this mutant to the 
                     membrane restores homeostatic control mediated by 
                     chaperones and FtsH |CITS: [24358019]|. The hyperactive 
                     phenotype of this &sigma;<sup>32</sup> I54Nmutant is 
                     suppressed by Tn5 insertion into <i>mcrC</i> and 
                     <i>ydbA</i> genes, an effect that is not observed with 
                     <i>mcrC</i> or  <i>ydbA2</i> knockouts |CITS: [30531155]|. 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> activity can also be attenuated via 
                     phosphorylation |CITS: [12657060]| and is increased under 
                     ampicillin treatment |CITS: [33731833]|.
                     RpoH (&sigma;<sup>32</sup>) from <i>Escherichia coli</i> 
                     binds the second messenger cyclic di-GMP (c-di-GMP), 
                     supporting that RpoH is a conserved c-di-GMP receptor 
                     across Proteobacteria |CITS:[41005124]|; in <i>Pseudomonas 
                     aeruginosa</i>, c-di-GMP binding to RpoH inhibits its 
                     DNA-binding activity |CITS:[41005124]|.
                     Conserved region 2 of &sigma;<sup>32</sup> is heavily 
                     involved in its activity and regulation.  Region 2.2 is 
                     involved in interaction with the |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX|, 
                     along with other portions of the protein |CITS: 
                     [9144163][9495746]|.  Region 2 is important for 
                     FtsH-mediated degradation |CITS: [11277997]|. 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> becomes more flexible as temperature 
                     rises, and in particular region 2 appears to undergo a 
                     reversible unfolding |CITS: [14504287]|.  Region 2.1 is 
                     involved in &sigma;<sup>32</sup> stability and its 
                     transcriptional efficacy |CITS: [15516558][15901705]|. 
                     Region C (from L118 to R140) has been implicated in binding
                     of DnaK |CITS: [8601834]|, in high affinity binding to core
                     RNA polymerase |CITS: [10348869]| and in FtsH mediated 
                     proteolysis |CITS: [19025566]|. Deletion of the &sigma; 
                     finger of RpoH formed by region 3.2 decreases the overall 
                     RNAP holoenzyme activity <i>in vitro</i>, increasing the 
                     stability of the promoter complex |CITS: [33573428]|. 
                     However, this deletion makes the promoter complex less 
                     sensitive to the stringent response factor DksA in presence
                     of ppGpp |CITS: [33573428]|.
                     It was reported that RNAP holoenzyme containing RpoH 
                     &sigma; factor can incorporate NADH during transcription 
                     initiation |CITS: [33573428]|.
                     Elevated levels of RpoH disrupt the spatial co-localization
                     (""clustering"") of ribosomal RNA (rrn) operons, a genomic 
                     organization analogous to the eukaryotic nucleolus 
                     |CITS:[40245090]|. This de-clustering effect occurs even in
                     the absence of heat stress and is independent of RpoHas 
                     transcriptional activity |CITS:[40245090]|. The ability of 
                     RpoH to destabilize clustering depends on its interaction 
                     with the core RNA polymerase (RNAP) and its capacity to 
                     bind DNA |CITS:[40245090]|. RpoH-induced de-clustering can 
                     be counteracted by simultaneous expression of RpoD, 
                     indicating that RpoH and RpoD compete for RNAP 
                     |CITS:[40245090]|. Additionally, membrane localization of 
                     RpoH is relevant for its effect on <i>rrn</i> operon 
                     organization |CITS:[40245090]|.
                     <i>rpoH</i> mutants suffer a number of ills in addition to 
                     failure of the heat shock response. They are deficient in 
                     cell division at higher temperatures and sensitive to 
                     oxidative stress at any temperature |CITS: 
                     [3529094][1729253]|.  Both F and mini-F plasmid replication
                     depend on &sigma;<sup>32</sup> and fail in its absence 
                     |CITS: [3526090][2447584]|.  In <i>rpoH</i> mutants, newly 
                     synthesized protein aggregates readily even under normal 
                     growth conditions |CITS: [1936268]|.  A single nucleotide 
                     substitution in <i>rpoH</i>  (T->C) increased the abundance
                     of this protein without changes at the transcriptional 
                     level.  This mutation occurred in a successful thermal 
                     adaptation |CITS: [26373241]|. &sigma;<sup>32</sup> has 
                     been evaluated via linker insertion mutational analysis 
                     |CITS: [12700252]|.
                     Whereas E&sigma;<sup>70</sup>-directed transcription is 
                     rifampicin sensitive, E&sigma;<sup>32</sup>-directed 
                     transcription is partially rifampicin resistant |CITS: 
                     [9862449]|.
                     After cellular exposure to the aminoglycoside antibiotic 
                     kanamycin, &sigma;<sup>32</sup> accumulates to high levels 
                     and its degradation is apparently blocked; despite this 
                     accumulation, the response to heat shock normally induced 
                     by this &sigma; factor does not seem to be induced under 
                     this condition |CITS: [35486612]|. On the other hand, 
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> is not detected after exposure to the 
                     antibiotics tetracycline and chloramphenicol |CITS: 
                     [35486612]|.
                     It has been suggested that the active RpoH pathway could 
                     modulate antibiotic sensitivity  |CITS: [38391529]|.
                     A &sigma;-competition model based on comparative kinetic 
                     and thermodynamic properties was developed |CITS: 
                     [22995505]|.
                     &sigma;<sup>32</sup> appears not be able to induce pauses 
                     during transcription elongation in vitro |CITS: 
                     [31228934]|.
                     The expression of <i>rpoH</i> is increased in the presence 
                     of styrene |CITS: [33640981]|.
                     Various target genes of RpoH were found upregulated after 
                     10 min of exposure to 2.5 mM H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, 
                     despite the <i>rpoH</i> transcription level not showing 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>-dependent regulation 
                     |CITS:[35453340]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [10791710][28904250][31281142][37010795]|<BR>Comments: 
                     |CITS: [24358020][19400800]|."
