     gene            complement(3595439..3596107)
                     /gene="ftsE"
                     /locus_tag="b3463"
                     /label=ftsE
     CDS             complement(3595439..3596107)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftsE"
                     /locus_tag="b3463"
                     /product="bifunctional cell division protein FtsE /
                     divisome protein complex FtsEX"
                     /label=ftsE
                     /note="FtsE dimerizes and associates with the inner
                     membrane via interaction with FtsX, an integral membrane 
                     protein |CITS: [10048040]|.
                     FtsE and FtsX localize to the cell division site; 
                     localization is dependent on FtsZ, FtsA and ZipA, but not 
                     on FtsK, FtsQ, FtsL(W) nor FtsI; FtsEX is important for 
                     assembly or stability of the septal ring under low-salt 
                     growth conditions |CITS: [14729705]| and under low 
                     osmolarity conditions |CITS: [17071757]|. Depletion of 
                     FtsEX does not affect the abundance of FtsK, FtsQ, FtsI nor
                     FtsN in the cytoplasmic membrane |CITS: [19376877]|. The 
                     ATP-binding site mutant, FtsE(D162A) localizes to the 
                     septal ring and supports septal ring assembly but impairs 
                     septal ring constriction; FtsE may use ATP to promote 
                     septal ring constriction |CITS: [19376877]|.
                     FtsE requires FtsZ for localization to the septal ring 
                     |CITS: [19376877]|.
                     <i>ftsE</i> is non-essential when grown at high salt 
                     concentration (>0.5% NaCl); inactivation of <i>ftsE</i> 
                     (<i>ftsE::kan</i>) results in a filamented cellular 
                     morphology |CITS: [10048040]|. <i>ftsE</i> and <i>ftsX</i> 
                     are located in an operon with <i>ftsY</i> - the latter 
                     encoding the SRP receptor |CITS: [3323846][8107852]|. 
                     Overexpression of FtsEX does not interfere with 
                     SRP-mediated protein targeting; deletion of <i>ftsE</i> 
                     does not interfere with SRP-mediated protein targeting 
                     |CITS: [10048040]|. <i>ftsE</i> mutants show a defect in 
                     the translocation of potassium ion pump proteins (eg KdpA) 
                     into the cytoplasmic membrane |CITS: [9658012]|. 
                     <i>fts</i>: <U>f</U>ilamentation <U>t</U>emperature 
                     <U>s</U>ensitive (see |CITS: [10048040]|).
                     Review: |CITS: [35408901][37524757]|"
