     gene            complement(3596110..3597603)
                     /gene="ftsY"
                     /locus_tag="b3464"
                     /label=ftsY
     CDS             complement(3596110..3597603)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftsY"
                     /locus_tag="b3464"
                     /product="signal recognition particle receptor"
                     /label=ftsY
                     /note="FtsY is the receptor protein for the |FRAME:
                     SRP-CPLX ""signal recognition particle""| (SRP) in a 
                     pathway which mediates the co-translational targeting and 
                     delivery of integral membrane proteins to the |FRAME: 
                     SEC-SECRETION-CPLX Sec translocon|.
                     FtsY is found in both the soluble and inner membrane 
                     fraction; FtsY is released from the inner membrane by 
                     treatments that solubilize peripheral membrane proteins 
                     |CITS: [8194520]|. FtsY exists in two isoforms <i>in 
                     vivo</i>; full-length FtsY is found primarily associated 
                     with the membrane while a truncated form lacking the first 
                     14 amino acids is found mainly in the soluble fraction 
                     |CITS: [18281057]|. FtsY is predominantly membrane bound 
                     <i>in vivo</i>; soluble FtsY is a negligible species and 
                     soluble FtsY-SRP-ribosome nascent chain complexes are not 
                     efficiently targeted to the membrane <i>in vitro</i> |CITS:
                     [19912622]|.
                     FtsY contains an N-terminal A domain which is implicated in
                     membrane localization and conserved N and GTPase (G-) 
                     domains (often termed the NG domain) |CITS: 
                     [7518075][9373157][9177162][9002525]|. FtsY lacking the 
                     complete A domain (residues 2-196) is unable to support the
                     growth of FtsY depleted cells, however FtsY mutants with 
                     partial deletions of the A domain, including a protein 
                     lacking residues 2-195, are able to support the growth of 
                     FtsY depleted cells |CITS: [15060054]|.
                     Purified FtsY interacts directly with membrane lipids, in 
                     particular anionic lipids |CITS: [10675322]|. FtsY contains
                     two lipid-binding helices; helix I comprising the first 14 
                     amino acids of FtsY and helix II (also termed MTS 'membrane
                     targeting sequence' or &alpha;N1) comprising residues 
                     188-207 located between the A and N domains; both regions 
                     contribute to membrane interaction |CITS: 
                     [17726012][19414018][21543314]|; MTS undergoes a 
                     phospholipid-induced conformation change that is required 
                     for efficient SRP-FtsY complex formation |CITS: 
                     [21543314]|.
                     Purified, immobilised FtsY binds an SRP complex in a GTP 
                     dependent manner and this interaction stimulates GTP 
                     hydrolysis |CITS: [8107852]|. FtsY and the GTP-binding, SRP
                     protein component Ffh interact |CITS: [14724630][15383838]|
                     and reciprocally stimulate each other's GTPase activity 
                     |CITS: [7660124]|.  Purified FtsY binds GTP and has 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup> dependent GTPase activity |CITS: 
                     [7556679]|. Purified FtsY has low intrinsic GTPase activity
                     which  increases significantly upon binding to liposomes 
                     |CITS: [10675322]|. Phospholipids stimulate formation of a 
                     stable and active SRP-FtsY complex <i>in vitro</i>; 
                     formation of an active complex strengthens the FtsY-lipid 
                     interaction |CITS: [20733058]|.
                     FtsY forms a nucleotide-dependent homodimer on the 
                     membrane; homodimer formation stimulates GTPase activity; 
                     the FtsY homodimer utilizes the same interface as the 
                     FtsY/Ffh heterodimer; the physiological function of FtsY 
                     homodimerization is not known |CITS: [30146170]|.
                     FtsY binds to the SecY translocon <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [15815684][16923832][21255212]|. FtsY binds
                     to the 4th and 5th cytosolic loops of SecY which also serve
                     as binding sites for SecA and the ribosome; FtsY and SecA 
                     compete for binding to the SecY translocon |CITS: 
                     [21255212][26459600]|. FtsY-SecY interaction requires the 
                     presence of phospholipids, is nucleotide independent and 
                     persists in the presence of the SRP |CITS: [26459600]|. 
                     Ribosome nascent chain complexes (RNC) and SRP induce 
                     conformational change in the FtsY-SecY complex |CITS: 
                     [26459600]|. The central region of the FtsY A domain binds 
                     to the SecY translocon non-specifically |CITS: [27346853]|.
                     Binding to the Sec translocon induces conformational change
                     in FtsY which exposes the FtsY NG domain for binding to the
                     NG domain of Ffh; binding to the translocon activates FtsY 
                     for complex formation with ribosome bound SRP |CITS: 
                     [27355662]|.
                     FtsY actively regulates the SRP-RNC interaction; FtsY 
                     alters the energetics of SRP-RNC interaction |CITS: 
                     [24914238]|.
                     FtsY is essential; depletion of FtsY results in short 
                     filaments of cells with large unevenly distributed 
                     spherical bulges |CITS: [8194520]|. FtsY depletion inhibits
                     ribosome targeting to the membrane |CITS: [10781067]| and 
                     disrupts membrane protein biogenesis |CITS: 
                     [8999901][20161748]|.
                     FtsY has sequence similarity with the &alpha; subunit of 
                     the eukaryotic SRP receptor |CITS: [2502718][2502717]|. 
                     FtsY and the SRP are members of the GTPase superfamily; 
                     unlike classical GTPases which act as bimodal switches, 
                     FtsY and SRP are 'multi-state' GTPases which undergo 
                     multiple conformational arrangements to regulate complex 
                     processes such as protein targeting (see |CITS: [19469550]|
                     and references within). FtsY (and the SRP) belong to a 
                     class of guanine nucleotide-binding (G) proteins which are 
                     regulated by dimerization |CITS: [19424291]|.
                     Under stringent stress conditions, the (p)ppGpp alarmones 
                     directly inhibit SRP-mediated insertion of membrane 
                     proteins; (p)ppGpp binds in the nucleotide-binding pocket 
                     of the Ffh and FtsY proteins and prevents formation of the 
                     SRP-FtsY-targeting complex |CITS: [35217658]|.
                     <i>fts</i>: <u>f</u>ilamentation <u>t</u>emperature 
                     <u>s</u>ensitivity |CITS: [3025556]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9180256]|.<BR>Comments: |CITS: [8107844]|"
