     gene            3599811..3602009
                     /gene="zntA"
                     /locus_tag="b3469"
                     /label=zntA
     CDS             3599811..3602009
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="zntA"
                     /locus_tag="b3469"
                     /product="multifunctional Zn(2+)/Cd(2+)/Pb(2+) exporting
                     P-type ATPase / Zn(2+)-exporting ATPase / Cd(2+)-exporting 
                     ATPase / Pb(2+)-exporting ATPase"
                     /EC_number="7.2.2.12; 7.2.2.21"
                     /label=zntA
                     /note="<i>zntA</i> encodes a P-type ATPase that mediates
                     export of the divalent metal ions Zn<sup>2+</sup>, 
                     Cd<sup>2+</sup> and Pb<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [9364914][9405611]|. ZntA mediated metal ion export 
                     contributes to Zn<sup>2+</sup> homeostasis and confers 
                     resistance to Cd<sup>2+</sup> and Pb2+ (see |CITS: 
                     [10964935]|). The enzyme has low activity with 
                     Ni<sup>2+</sup>, Co<sup>2+</sup>, and Cu<sup>2+</sup> <i>in
                     vitro</i> but these metal ions are not physiologically 
                     relevant substrates |CITS: [12746428]|. Purified ZntA has 
                     metal (Pb<sup>2+</sup>, Zn<sup>2+</sup>, 
                     Cd<sup>2+</sup>)-induced ATPase activity; metal-thiolates 
                     may be the true substrate <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [10660539]|.
                     In the solution NMR structure of a ZntA fragment 
                     (ZntA<sub>26-118</sub>) reported by |CITS: [12417201]| 
                     mononunuclear Zn is bound through Cys-59, Cys-62 and Asp-58
                     side-chains. Asp-436 is the site of phosphorylation |CITS: 
                     [12746428]| (see also |CITS: [16245943]|). ZntA has 2 
                     high-affinity metal binding sites, one in the N-terminal 
                     domain and one in the transmembrane region; the 
                     transmembrane binding site is essential for function |CITS:
                     [15794653]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [17326661][16669635][22387457]|). Detergent-solubilized 
                     ZntA is primarily dimeric |CITS: [20962537]| (also |CITS: 
                     [33190490]|).
                     Kinetic parameters of ATP hydrolysis and formation of the 
                     phospho-enzyme intermediate for the metals Zn<sup>2+</sup>,
                     Cd<sup>2+</sup> and Pb<sup>2+</sup> and their thiolate 
                     complexes are reported in |CITS: 
                     [10660539][11527979][12746428][17326661]|.
                     <i>zntA</i> mutants are hypersensitive to zinc(II), 
                     cadmium(II) |CITS: [9364914][9405611]|, lead(II) |CITS: 
                     [9830000]|, and cobalt(II) |CITS: [9364914]| salts and 
                     weakly sensitive to nickel salts |CITS: [9364914]|. 
                     <i>zntA</i> expression is regulated by ZntR; transcription 
                     is induced by Cd<sup>2+</sup>, Zn<sup>2+</sup> and 
                     Pb<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [10048032][10608803][10802061][22459916]|.
                     ZntA is Type IB P-type ATPase |CITS: [9419228]|; ZntA is a 
                     topological Type I P-type ATPase |CITS: [20962537]|."
