     gene            complement(3602111..3602356)
                     /gene="tusA"
                     /locus_tag="b3470"
                     /label=tusA
     CDS             complement(3602111..3602356)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tusA"
                     /locus_tag="b3470"
                     /product="bifunctional sulfurtransferase TusA / IscS:TusA 
                     sulfurtransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.8.1.11"
                     /label=tusA
                     /note="TusA has pleiotrophic roles in thiomodification of
                     some tRNAs, sulfur transfer in molybdenum cofactor 
                     biosynthesis, general Fe-S cluster assembly, and iron 
                     homeostasis.
                     It functions as a sulfur mediator during tRNA synthesis of 
                     2-thiouridine of the modified wobble base 
                     mnm<sup>5</sup>s<sup>2</sup>U in tRNA by interacting with 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-248|, stimulating its cysteine desulfurase 
                     activity and accepting an activated sulfur in the form of a
                     persulfide at the cysteine residue C19. This activated 
                     sulfur appears to be transferred to the |FRAME: 
                     G7714-MONOMER ""TusD""| component of the |FRAME: CPLX-3942|
                     in a sulfur-relay system |CITS: [16387657]|. TusA increases
                     L-cysteine sulfurase activity through its ability to bind 
                     with IscS and affects directionality of sulfur transfer of 
                     IscS to a variety of sulfur-accepting molecules |CITS: 
                     [23281480]|. TusA has a role in sulfur transfer for 
                     |FRAME:PWY-6823|, transferring the sulfur it obtained from 
                     IscS to the |FRAME: EG11597-MONOMER| (MoaD) of the |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-2502| complex |CITS: [23281480]|.
                     A solution structure of TusA has been determined. 
                     Structural similarity to the C-terminal ribosome binding 
                     domain of |FRAME: EG10506-MONOMER| suggested that TusA may 
                     bind RNA |CITS: [11080457]|. However, in an <i>in vitro</i>
                     assay, TusA does not appear to bind tRNA directly |CITS: 
                     [16387657]|. Crystal structures of the TusA-IscS complex 
                     from <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7 EDL933 have been solved, and 
                     the binding interface between the two proteins was 
                     investigated by site-directed mutagenesis; Cys19 residue 
                     was also found to be the acceptor cysteine for the sulfur 
                     |CITS: [20404999]|. The TusA sequences in the two organisms
                     are identical. Two distinct bindings sites on IscS dimers 
                     were observed for TusA in <i>E. coli</i> BW25113 with three
                     surface arginines being important for the interaction. The 
                     functional cysteine residue in TusA is Cys19, however this 
                     residue does not play a role in binding to IscS. TusA 
                     binding to IscS does compete with ThiI and IscU binding to 
                     IscS due to overlapping though distinct sites |CITS: 
                     [20404999][38980029]|.
                     A <i>tusA</i> mutation has previously been shown to affect 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> abundance (stability) and to exhibit 
                     filamentous cell morphology and poor viability in rich 
                     media, but not minimal media |CITS: [9555915]|. The growth 
                     defect can be rescued by NaCl supplementation |CITS: 
                     [10830496]|. A <i>tusA</i> deletion mutant lacks the 2-thio
                     modification of mnm<sup>5</sup>s<sup>2</sup>U in tRNA and 
                     has a severe growth defect |CITS: [16387657]|. A 
                     &Delta;<i>tusA</i> mutant has a pleiotropic effect on 
                     transcription, including increased expression of molybdenum
                     cofactor biosynthesis and FeS cluster biosynthesis genes, 
                     but shows reduced activity of molybdoenzymes and 
                     accumulates the molybdopterin precursor cPMP.  A follow-up 
                     study with &Delta;<i>tusA</i> found a reduction in Fe-S 
                     cluster levels and an accumulation of |FRAME: FE+2| due 
                     mainly to a reduction in translation of |FRAME: EG10359| 
                     mRNA |CITS: [38916309]|. <i>&Delta;iscS</i>, 
                     <i>&Delta;tusA</i> and <i>&Delta;moaE</i> are 
                     hypersensitive to N-hydroxylated base analogs, such as 
                     6-N-hydroxylaminopurine, exhibit a reduced growth rate, and
                     are resistant to chlorate; this phenotype can be suppressed
                     with addition of L-cysteine or sulfide and is likely due to
                     their role in molybdenum cofactor-dependent pathways |CITS:
                     [23894086]|.
                     TusA: ""tRNA 2-<b>t</b>hio<b>u</b>ridine 
                     <b>s</b>ynthesizing protein"" |CITS: [16387657]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16547481][20405048][22905870][28098827][3 
                     1655739][32239579][38631442]|"
