     gene            3603315..3603872
                     /gene="dcrB"
                     /locus_tag="b3472"
                     /label=dcrB
     CDS             3603315..3603872
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dcrB"
                     /locus_tag="b3472"
                     /product="periplasmic bacteriophage sensitivity protein
                     DcrB"
                     /label=dcrB
                     /note="DcrB is involved in sensitivity to bacteriophages
                     C1 |CITS: [2150690][8752353]| and C6 |CITS: [12558182]|. 
                     DcrB is implicated in |FRAME: CPLX0-7963 EmrE|-mediated 
                     betaine efflux |CITS: [36748554]|.
                     DcrB and DcrA are proposed to be involved in DNA injection 
                     upon phage adsorption, stimulating development or opening 
                     of the channel through which the phage DNA enters the cell 
                     |CITS: [12558182]|.
                     DcrB is periplasmic |CITS: [8752353]|. DcrB is a probable 
                     lipoprotein |CITS: [15174130]|
                     <i>dcrB</i> exhibits a synthetic lethal phenotype with 
                     |FRAME: EG11122 ""<i>yciB</i>""|; the double deletion 
                     mutants have pleiotropic defects including morphological 
                     changes, reduced proton motive force and disrupted outer 
                     membrane protein biogenesis |CITS: [30368949]|.
                     This partially characterized protein in <i>E. coli</i> was 
                     shown to interact with the enzyme |FRAME: G6475-MONOMER| in
                     a protein-enzyme interactions (PEIs) study that examined 
                     the connection between protein-protein interactions (PPIs) 
                     and metabolomics networks studied in 22 different 
                     environmental conditions. This PEI may have a metabolic 
                     regulatory role in the |FRAME: PWY-5386| pathway involved 
                     in pyruvate metabolism, in part because the PEI is 
                     conserved across many other bacterial taxa |CITS: 
                     [39903745]|."
                     /translation="MRNLVKYVGIGLLVMGLAACDDKDTNATAQGSVAESNATGNPVNL
