     gene            3607027..3608601
                     /gene="nikA"
                     /locus_tag="b3476"
                     /label=nikA
     CDS             3607027..3608601
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nikA"
                     /locus_tag="b3476"
                     /product="trifunctional Ni(2+) ABC transporter periplasmic
                     binding protein / Ni(2+) ABC transporter / nickel ABC 
                     transporter"
                     /label=nikA
                     /note="NikA is the periplasmic binding protein of a high 
                     affinity nickel uptake system. The endogenous ligand for 
                     NikA is suggested to be a |FRAME: CPD0-2714 
                     Ni-(L-His)<sub>2</sub>| complex.
                     Purified NikA is monomeric and binds one atom of nickel per
                     molecule of protein with a K<sub>d</sub> of less
                     than 0.1&mu;M; purified NikA binds Ni<sup>2+</sup> with at 
                     least 10-fold higher affinity than for other divalent 
                     metals |CITS: [7867647]|. Purified NikA binds nickel 
                     selctively over cobalt with a K<sub>d</sub> of 1&micro;M 
                     for Ni<sup>2+</sup> and 246&micro;M for Co<sup>2+</sup> 
                     |CITS: [12960164]|. NikA mediates Tar dependent 
                     nickel-repellent chemotaxis |CITS: [7867647]|.
                     Purified, crystallized NikA consists of two lobes connected
                     by a hinge; a hydrophobic pocket shaped to fit a 
                     penta-coordinate-hydrated nickel ion 
                     [Ni(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>5</sub><sup>2</sup>] forms the 
                     ligand binding site; nickel does not form direct contacts 
                     with NikA but hydrogen bonds to a coordinating water 
                     molecule; nickel remains accessible to solvent while bound 
                     to NikA |CITS:[12960164]|. The details of this binding site
                     were later challenged by |CITS:[16011372]| who reported 
                     that NikA (aerobically overexpressed and purified in the 
                     presence of EDTA) binds Fe(III)EDTA(H2O)<sup>-</sup> with 
                     high affinity and suggested that NikA would transport 
                     nickel complexed to a chemically similar metallophore 
                     (possibly butane-1,2,4-tricarboxylate |CITS: [18759453]|) 
                     <i>in vivo</i>. Ni<sup>2+</sup> binds a natural 
                     metallophore; His416 is the only direct metal-NikA contact 
                     |CITS: [18759453][21281641]|. L-histidine is required for 
                     NikABCDE dependent nickel uptake in <i>E. coli</i>; 
                     purified NikA binds a Ni-(L-His)<sub>2</sub> complex; 
                     deletion of <i>nikA</i> decreases the uptake of both 
                     labeled Ni and labeled L-His, 4 fold |CITS: [22885853]|. 
                     Nickel, in a NikA-Ni-(L-His)<sub>2</sub> complex, is 
                     coordinated by four nitrogen atoms and one carboxylate 
                     group from the two free histidines and one nitrogen atom 
                     from His416 of NikA |CITS: [23314594]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [36255398]|).
                     Fe(III)-EDTA and Indium(III)-EDTA complexes bind tightly to
                     NikA resulting in inhibition of [Ni-Fe]-hydrogenase 
                     maturation |CITS: [40037903]|."
