     gene            complement(3620163..3622898)
                     /gene="rbbA"
                     /locus_tag="b3486"
                     /label=rbbA
     CDS             complement(3620163..3622898)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rbbA"
                     /locus_tag="b3486"
                     /product="ribosome-associated ATPase"
                     /label=rbbA
                     /note="RbbA is associated with the 30S subunit of the
                     ribosome and has ribosome-dependent ATPase activity |CITS: 
                     [10607404][11168361]|.  Stimulation of protein synthesis by
                     RbbA is dependent on ATP hydrolysis, and the ATPase 
                     activity is inhibited by hygromycin |CITS: 
                     [11168361][11557474]|.  The effect of hygromycin may be due
                     to its ability to release RbbA from the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [11557474]|.
                     RbbA specifically interacts with EF-Tu and crosslinks to 
                     the 30S ribosomal subunit protein S1 |CITS: [11168361]|. 
                     The E-site base A937 of 16S rRNA in the intact 70S ribosome
                     is protected by a truncated form of RbbA |CITS: 
                     [16495476]|.  The truncated form of RbbA may be identical 
                     to the translation factor ""Protein W"" |CITS: 
                     [3884000][3885216][7592851][8725011][10607404]|.  RbbA also
                     interacts with the membrane protein YhjD and certain 
                     lipopolysaccharide transport components |CITS: [21556145]|.
                      A genetic interaction screen discovered synthetic 
                     interactions with genes linked to membrane, protein 
                     synthesis and energy metabolism |CITS: [21556145]|.
                     RbbA contains two ATP-binding domains in its N terminus 
                     |CITS: [10607404]|.  The ATP-binding domains together with 
                     five predicted transmembrane helices led to the initial 
                     functional prediction as the ATP-binding component of an 
                     ABC transporter |CITS: [9873074]|.  A truncated form of 
                     RbbA which does not contain the predicted transmembrane 
                     domains still stimulates protein synthesis and has 
                     ribosome-dependent ATPase activity |CITS: [16495476]|. 
                     <i>rbbA yhjD</i> double deletion mutant cells are elongated
                     when grown at 15 or 32&deg;C and show impaired translation 
                     fidelity.  Both the single and double deletion mutants show
                     increased sensitivity to certain antibiotics that target 
                     the ribosome and electron transport by NDH-1 and show 
                     impaired growth on glycerol |CITS: [21556145]|. RbbA: 
                     ""<b>r</b>i<b>b</b>osomal <b>b</b>ound <b>A</b>TPase"" 
                     |CITS: [10607404]|
                     "
                     /translation="MTHLELVPVPPVAQLAGVSQHYGKTVALNNITLDIPARCMVGLIG
