     gene            3631002..3632501
                     /gene="pitA"
                     /locus_tag="b3493"
                     /label=pitA
     CDS             3631002..3632501
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pitA"
                     /locus_tag="b3493"
                     /product="bifunctional phosphate:H(+) symporter / metal 
                     phosphate:H(+) symporter PitA"
                     /label=pitA
                     /note="Early work in <i>E. coli</i> indicated that there
                     are two genetically separable transport systems for 
                     inorganic phosphate (P<sub><i>i</i></sub>) - a high 
                     affinity, low velocity Pst (phosphate specific transport) 
                     system (the |FRAME: ABC-27-CPLX|) and a low affinity, high 
                     velocity Pit (P<sub><i>i</i></sub> transport) system |CITS:
                     [6998957][328484][765745]|*. This latter system is 
                     constitutive in nature and catalyses an electrogenic metal 
                     phosphate:H<sup>+</sup> symport |CITS: 
                     [346064][328484][8110778]|. P<sub><i>i</i></sub> uptake via
                     Pit is dependent on the presence of divalent cations, like 
                     Mg2+, Ca2+, Co2+, or Mn2+, which form soluble metal 
                     phosphate (MeHP04) complexes |CITS: [8110778]|.
                     The Pit system in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 consists of two 
                     transporters - PitA and PitB - which have 81% sequence 
                     identity at the protein level; <i>pitA</i> is likely 
                     constitutive while <i>pitB</i> is repressed or inactivated 
                     through the pho regulon; both proteins may have contributed
                     to the kinetic values and substrate specificities 
                     determined in earlier studies |CITS: [11489853]|. The Pit 
                     proteins are predicted to contain 10 transmembrane helices 
                     |CITS: [11489853]|.
                     The Pit system also transports the phosphate analogue, 
                     arsenate |CITS: [328484][6998957][6998959]| while PitA (but
                     not PitB) has been implicated in the import of tellurite 
                     |CITS: [23189244]|. <i>pitA</i> insertion mutants have 
                     increased resistance to Zn(II) associated with reduced 
                     uptake of the metal ion (probably as a ZnHPO<sub>4</sub> 
                     complex) |CITS: [10713426]|. <i>pitA</i> transcription is 
                     regulated by the availability of Zn(II) and 
                     P<sub><i>i</i></sub>; Zn(II) is taken up by the PitA 
                     transporter in competition with Mg(II) |CITS: [19054109]|. 
                     PitA is regulated by the |FRAME: RNA0-348| and the small 
                     protein |FRAME: MONOMER0-766 ""MgtS""|; this regulation 
                     impacts bacterial growth under Mg<sup>2+</sup> limiting 
                     conditions |CITS: [30276893]|.
                     The PitA phosphate transporter is a member of the Inorganic
                     Phosphate Transporter (PiT) family |CITS: [24225317]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9442271][18789529]|
                     * The Pit system is lacking in many <i>E. coli</i> K-10 
                     strains |CITS: [4570598][11443103][11489853]|."
