     gene            3643597..3644022
                     /gene="arsC"
                     /locus_tag="b3503"
                     /label=arsC
     CDS             3643597..3644022
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="arsC"
                     /locus_tag="b3503"
                     /product="arsenate reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.20.4.1"
                     /label=arsC
                     /note="Based on sequence similarity with the R773-encoded 
                     enzyme, ArsC is thought to catalyze the reduction of 
                     arsenate to arsenite using glutathione with glutaredoxin as
                     electron donors.  The resulting arsenite is then removed 
                     from the cell via the ArsB transport protein. Unlike the 
                     chromosomally-encoded ArsC described here, the R773 
                     plasmid-encoded enzyme has been studied experimentally.  
                     The arsenate reductase reaction involves sequential action 
                     of three different thiolate nucleophiles that function as a
                     redox cascade |CITS: [16905151]|.  All three glutaredoxins 
                     in <i>E. coli</i> can participate in the arsenate reductase
                     reaction, but glutaredoxin 2 appears the most effective 
                     |CITS: [10593884]|.  Crystal structures of ArsC have been 
                     solved |CITS: [11709171][15295115]|, and residues involved 
                     in arsenate binding and transition-state stabilization were
                     identified |CITS: [14592722][15295115]|.
                     Based on sequence similarity with the R773-plasmid encoded 
                     arsenite resistance operon, ArsB is a transporter and ArsC 
                     is an arsenate reductase |CITS: [7721697][7860609]|.  The 
                     chromosomally encoded operon, however, is lacking 
                     <i>arsA</i>, which encodes a putative ATP-binding protein. 
                     Expression of <i>arsRBC</i> is induced by exposure to 
                     arsenite |CITS: [7721697][7860609]|; transcription is 
                     negatively regulated by ArsR |CITS: [8764681]|. 
                     <i>arsRBC</i> mutants are more sensitive to sodium arsenate
                     and arsenite than wild type, while overexpression of 
                     <i>arsRBC</i> from a plasmid leads to increased arsenate, 
                     arsenite and antimonite resistance |CITS: 
                     [7721697][7860609]|.  &Delta;<i>arsRBC</i> mutant 
                     accumulates significantly more intracellular arsenite than 
                     wild type |CITS: [7860609]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12426124][12443926][16905151]|"
