     gene            3647321..3647887
                     /gene="slp"
                     /locus_tag="b3506"
                     /label=slp
     CDS             3647321..3647887
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="slp"
                     /locus_tag="b3506"
                     /product="starvation lipoprotein"
                     /label=slp
                     /note="Slp (starvation lipoprotein) is the product of the 
                     <I>slp</I> gene |CITS:[8022277]| which forms an operon with
                     the downstream gene <I>dctR</I> |CITS:[12730179]|. Slp is 
                     believed to take part in acid resistance as expression 
                     increased when cells were grown at pH 5.5 and 4.5 under 
                     conditions known to induce glutamate-dependent acid 
                     resistance compared to pH 7.4 under the same conditions 
                     |CITS:[12426343]|.  During growth in acidic conditions, 
                     expression of Slp is negatively regulated by GadW, but 
                     positively regulated by GadXW, which are regulators of the 
                     two glutamate decarboxylase genes and the GABA APC 
                     transporter responsible for glutamate-dependent acid 
                     resistance |CITS:[12730179]|. <I>slp</I> is highly induced 
                     by overexpression of EvgA |CITS:[12399493]|, 
                     |CITS:[12670992]| indirectly through its induction of YdeO 
                     expression |CITS:[15489450]|, though <I>slp</I> was found 
                     to have a putative EvgA binding motif 542 base pairs 
                     upstream of its start codon |CITS:[12670992]|.
                     Under certain conditions, deletion of <I>slp-dctR</I> 
                     resulted in reduction of YdeO-induced acid resistance 
                     |CITS:[12694615]|, though under conditions used to induce 
                     acid resistance naturally, <I>slp-dctR</I> was not 
                     necessary |CITS:[15489450]|.  A <I>slp-dctR</I> double 
                     mutant exhibited loss of viability during growth in spent 
                     LB at pH 2.5 much faster than wild-type |CITS:[17259322]|. 
                     The <I>slp-dctR</I> double mutant was also unable to 
                     survive at pH 2.5 in minimal medium when formate, 
                     succinate, or lactate were added |CITS:[17259322]|. The 
                     fact that individual <I>slp</I> and <I>dctR</I> mutants 
                     retain resistance indicates redundant functions for Slp and
                     DctR in protection against formate, succinate, and lactate 
                     at low pH |CITS:[17259322]|.
                     <I>slp</I> is upregulated when cells are deprived of carbon
                     or during stationary phase |CITS:[8022277]|. Expression of 
                     <I>slp</I> increases 3-5 fold in stationary phase 
                     |CITS:[15489422]|. This has been shown under certain 
                     conditions to occur independently of RpoS, cAMP, and DnaK 
                     |CITS:[8022277]|, while under other conditions the promoter
                     for <I>slp</I> is RpoS sigma-dependent |CITS:[15489422]|. 
                     Expression of <I>slp</I> is reduced after 10 hours of 
                     nitrogen starvation in an <I>rpoS</I> mutant 
                     |CITS:[15289551]|. Sequence analysis suggests <I>slp</I> 
                     may also be controlled by a sigma 70 promoter 
                     |CITS:[8022277]|.
                     Slp expression is reduced by constitutive expression of 
                     <I>marRAB</I>, which is responsible for multiple antibiotic
                     resistance |CITS:[7836283]|. The Mar system affects Slp 
                     expression duringthe transition from exponential to 
                     stationary phase, though not the final stationary phase 
                     level. Cells show some increased chloramphenicol 
                     sensitivity when Slp levels increase |CITS:[11094278]|.
                     The level of Slp expression increased in response to 
                     exposure to acetate |CITS:[11244055]| and
                     acivicin |CITS:[11344143]|, in the presence of the 
                     transcriptional regulator SlyA in the enteroinvasive
                     <I>E. coli</I> strain 12860 |CITS:[12057949]|, when the 
                     leucine-responsive regulatory protein is
                     not expressed |CITS:[12374860]|, and upon deletion of 
                     <I>hns</I>, encoding the H-NS
                     transcriptional duel regulator |CITS:[14973023]|.  Slp is 
                     strongly induced under anaerobic growth,
                     but repression occurs after addition of nitrate 
                     |CITS:[15126489]|.  Other factors that reduce Slp 
                     expression include filamentous phage infection 
                     |CITS:[15782232]|, adhesion of cells to abiotic
                     surfaces |CITS:[11274103]|, and the expression of SulA, 
                     though this is suggested to be more
                     likely the result of a response to physiological 
                     alterations due to blockage of cell division 
                     |CITS:[14729718]|.
                     Slp was found as higher order oligomers tethered to the 
                     outer membrane |CITS:[16079137]|.
                     "
