     gene            3648043..3648573
                     /gene="dctR"
                     /locus_tag="b3507"
                     /label=dctR
     CDS             3648043..3648573
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dctR"
                     /locus_tag="b3507"
                     /product="putative DNA-binding transcriptional regulator
                     DctR"
                     /label=dctR
                     /note="DctR is a predicted transcriptional regulator whose 
                     target may be <i>dctA</i>.  A <i>dctR</i> mutation is the 
                     mechanism by which Suc<sup>+</sup> reversion mutations 
                     suppress the growth defect on C<sub>4</sub>-dicarboxylates 
                     (such as succinate) that is exhibited by <i>atp</i> mutants
                     |CITS: [9811641]|.
                     Like other transcriptional regulators, DctR forms 
                     heterodimers with |FRAME: RCSB-MONOMER RcsB|, but does not 
                     appear to form homodimers.  No regulatory targets have been
                     identified yet |CITS: [26635367]|.
                     A <i>slp dctR</i> double mutation suppresses the increased 
                     acid resistance of a strain overproducing YdeO |CITS: 
                     [12694615]| and affects survival in acidified spent minimal
                     E glucose medium, indicating a role in protection against 
                     metabolic endproducts that are toxic at low pH |CITS: 
                     [17259322]|.  A <i>slp-dctR</i> double mutant exhibited 
                     loss of viability during growth in spent LB at pH 2.5 much 
                     faster than wild-type |CITS: [17259322]|.  The 
                     <i>slp-dctR</i> double mutant was also unable to survive at
                     pH 2.5 in minimal medium when formate, succinate, or 
                     lactate were added |CITS: [17259322]|.  The fact that 
                     individual <i>slp</i> and <i>dctR</i> mutants retain 
                     resistance indicates redundant functions for Slp and DctR 
                     in protection against formate, succinate, and lactate at 
                     low pH |CITS: [17259322]|.
                     Overproduction of YdeO |CITS: [12694615]| or GadE |CITS: 
                     [14702398]| result in increased expression of <i>dctR</i>. 
                     Overexpression of DctR affects cellular morphology and 
                     causes filamentous biofilm formation |CITS: [12900028]|.
                     A <i>yhiF</i> mutation in an enterohemorrhagic <i>E. 
                     coli</i> strain causes increased type III secretion and 
                     increased cellular adhesion, compared to wild type |CITS: 
                     [12704134]|.
                     DctR: ""<b>d</b>i<b>c</b>arboxylate <b>t</b>ransport 
                     <b>r</b>egulator"" |CITS: [9811641]|"
