     gene            complement(3649768..3650100)
                     /gene="hdeA"
                     /locus_tag="b3510"
                     /label=hdeA
     CDS             complement(3649768..3650100)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hdeA"
                     /locus_tag="b3510"
                     /product="periplasmic acid stress chaperone HdeA"
                     /label=hdeA
                     /note="HdeA is an energy-independent chaperone that
                     protects periplasmic proteins from acid-induced 
                     aggregation; HdeA is an acid-activated conditionally 
                     disordered chaperone (see |CITS: [23018052]|).
                     HdeA is estimated to be highly abundant in both the growth 
                     and stationary phase; HdeA appears to form multimers |CITS:
                     [9298646]|. <i>hdeA</i> is non-essential for growth in both
                     minimal and rich media; the phenotype of <i>hdeA</i> 
                     insertion mutants varies depending on the specific position
                     and/or orientation of the marker |CITS: [9335267]|.
                     Purified HdeA prevents aggregation of the model substrate 
                     rhodanese at low pH (pH 2); the growth of an <i>hdeA</i> 
                     deletion mutant is severely compromised following exposure 
                     to acid (pH 2); HdeA is activated by a low pH-induced dimer
                     to monomer transition; the HdeA monomer is involved in 
                     protecting periplasmic proteins from acid-induced 
                     denaturation and aggregation |CITS: [10623550]|. 
                     <i>hdeA</i> is acid inducible (pH 4.5-5.5) |CITS: 
                     [12426343]|. Purified HdeA suppresses the aggregation of 
                     typical chaperone substrates at pH values below 2.5; 
                     purified HdeA forms soluble complexes with denatured 
                     substrate proteins at low pH which dissociate upon return 
                     to neutral pH |CITS: [15911614]|. Purified HdeA adopts a 
                     disordered conformation at pH values less than 3 and 
                     exposes hydrophobic surfaces that are involved in binding 
                     denatured substrates |CITS: [15911614][18271752]|. HdeA 
                     undergoes rapid, pH-induced partial unfolding and 
                     monomerization which activates its chaperone function; 
                     hydrophobic residues that form the dimer interface in 
                     inactive HdeA serve to bind substrate in active HdeA |CITS:
                     [19321422]|. HdeA prevents protein aggregation at low pH 
                     and supports folding upon return to neutral pH; HdeA 
                     releases substrate in a non-native but folding-competent 
                     state; slow release of substrate following pH 
                     neutralization may function to suppress aggregation while 
                     refolding occurs |CITS: [20080625]|. <i>hdeA</i> is 
                     implicated in protection from metabolites that become toxic
                     under extreme acid stress |CITS: [17259322]|. Abundance of 
                     HdeA is increased in response to nitrosative stress |CITS: 
                     [35427827]|.
                     Native HdeA clients under acid stress include lipoproteins,
                     enzymes, chaperones, metalloproteins and many transport 
                     proteins; the periplasmic chaperones SurA and DegP are HdeA
                     client proteins under acid conditions |CITS: [21892184]|. 
                     SurA or DegP assist HdeA in re-folding acid denatured 
                     alkaline phosphatase <i>in vitro</i> and a cooperation 
                     between the chaperones may exist <i>in vivo</i>; HdeA might
                     protect SurA and DegP to ensure that they remain functional
                     for the refolding of other proteins during pH recovery 
                     |CITS: [21892184]|.
                     HdeA's folded state is brittle - removing or slightly 
                     shifting the native disulfide bond is cytotoxic |CITS: 
                     [37267908]|.
                     HdeA and HdeB are structural homologues and the two 
                     proteins may form a heterodimer |CITS: [10623550]|.
                     HdeA: <i><U>h</U>ns</i>-<U>d</U>ependent <U>e</U>xpression 
                     |CITS: [8244952]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [22459131][34046432]|"
