     gene            complement(3651345..3651414)
                     /gene="arrS"
                     /locus_tag="b4704"
                     /label=arrS
     misc_RNA        complement(3651345..3651414)
                     /gene="arrS"
                     /locus_tag="b4704"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA ArrS"
                     /note="ArrS is a small RNA that is an antisense RNA to the 
                     |FRAME: TU0-14736 ""T3""| transcript of <i>gadE</i> and may
                     lead to processing of this transcript to the T2 form |CITS:
                     [21501346][24600026]|.  ArrS appears to stabilize both the 
                     T2 and T1 transcripts of <i>gadE</i> |CITS: [24600026]| and
                     positively regulates Cfa expression |CITS: [31308070]|.
                     ArrS interacts with the protein YfiF, which is a 
                     ncRNA-binding protein |CITS:[38531971]|.
                     Transcription of ArrS is induced by acidic growth 
                     conditions and in stationary phase and is upregulated by 
                     GadE |CITS: [21501346]|.  Overexpression of <i>arrS</i> 
                     leads to increased acid resistance |CITS: [24600026]|. 
                     ArrS: ""<b>a</b>cid <b>r</b>esistance-<b>r</b>elated 
                     <b>s</b>mall RNA"" |CITS: [21501346]|
                     Review: |CITS: [32213244]|"
     gene            3651726..3652253
                     /gene="gadE"
                     /locus_tag="b3512"
                     /label=gadE
     CDS             3651726..3652253
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gadE"
                     /locus_tag="b3512"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA-binding transcriptional
                     activator GadE / GadE-RcsB DNA-binding transcriptional 
                     activator"
                     /label=gadE
                     /note="The transcriptional activator GadE, for
                     ""<b>G</b>lutamic <b>a</b>cid <b>d</b>ecarboxylase,"" is 
                     positively autoregulated |CITS:[15489450][14702398]| and 
                     controls the transcription of genes involved in the 
                     maintenance of pH homeostasis, including the principal acid
                     resistance system |CITS:[12686161][11976288][11555293 
                     ][14702398][12940989][12399493][12694615][12426343]|, 
                     glutamate dependent (GAD), also referred as the GAD system,
                     and genes involved in multidrug efflux, among others 
                     |CITS:[12446650][18808381][12730179][16980449][1829 
                     7445][14702398]|. GadE also controls the expression of two 
                     transcription factors related to acid resistance, GadW and 
                     GadX, and for this reason it is considered the central 
                     activator of the acid response system 
                     |CITS:[14702398][12940989]|. GadE is encoded by the 
                     <i>gadE-mdtEF</i> operon, inducible by low pH 
                     |CITS:[12426343]|, which is located in the region called 
                     the acid fitness island |CITS:[18808381]|. Expression of 
                     <i>gadE</i> is controlled by an unusually large 798-bp 
                     upstream intergenic region, termed the sensory integration 
                     locus |CITS:[19220752]|. At least six regulators related to
                     the acid resistance system, GadE, GadX, GadW, EvgA, YdeO, 
                     and MnmE, are involved in the direct regulation of 
                     <i>gadE</i> |CITS:[19220752][17660422][14702398]|.
                     Ma et al. proposed that the binding targets for GadE 
                     consist of 20-nucleotide sequences that possess 
                     nonconserved motifs, called the GAD box, and suggested that
                     it is a regulator that may form a complex with GadX 
                     |CITS:[12940989]|.
                     As a member of the LysR family, this transcription factor 
                     is composed of two domains: the N-terminal domain, which 
                     has a potential helix-turn-helix DNA-binding motif in the 
                     DNA-binding region, and the C-terminal inducer-binding 
                     domain |CITS:[16218453][8257110]|.
                     In cells induced for chlorhexidine (CHX) resistance, the 
                     abundance of GadE and part of its regulon is increased 
                     |CITS: [34095221]|. It has been observed that deletion of 
                     <i>gadE</i>, together with a |FRAME: G7216| 
                     single-nucleotide mutant, can modestly contribute to CHX 
                     resistance |CITS: [34095221]|. On the other hand, 
                     inhibition of <i>gadE</i> expression by CRISPRi enhanced 
                     cellular fitness under treatment with the antibiotics 
                     carbonyl cyanide 3-chlorophenylhydrazone (CCCP) or 
                     pyocyanin |CITS:[40352728]|.
                     The  gadE  gene expression could be  repressed  in a direct
                     or indirect way by the OmpR protein. Since, using a gene 
                     array system,  a repression of this gene was observed as a 
                     result of  an up mutation in the ompR gene |CITS: 
                     [14523117]|.
                     The protein BglG binds to the <i>gadE</i> transcript to 
                     stabilize it |CITS: [35397010]|. The <i>gadE</i> gene has 
                     the sequence TTGTTAGCCGCAAAGAAAGTATCAAAAATC, which is the 
                     putative sequence that is recognized and bound by BglG 
                     |CITS: [35397010]|.
                     In the absence of acid stress, the <i>gadE</i> gene is 
                     expressed in a heterogeneous mode, and consequently a 
                     subpopulation of the cells shows an increased expression of
                     its regulon and resistance to acid stress |CITS: 
                     [36453903]|. On the other hand, an <i>evgS</i> 
                     gain-of-function mutant constitutively regulates positively
                     the expression of <i>gadE</i>; this is consistent with the 
                     fact that <i>gadE</i> is positively regulated by EvgA 
                     |CITS: [36453903]|. However, in an unknown way, the 
                     loss-of-function mutation of the gene <i>serC</i> also 
                     enhances the expression of <i>gadE</i> |CITS: [36453903]|.
                     Transcription of the <i>gadE</i> gene is downregulated in 
                     cells cultivated in LB broth in the presence of the 
                     antibiotic kanamycin |CITS:[36853057]|, and is also 
                     downregulated by the aldehydes cinnamaldehyde, 
                     2-pyridinecarboxaldehyde, crotonaldehyde, glyoxal, and 
                     formaldehyde |CITS:[40086616]|.
                     A <i>gadE</i> knockout mutant affects the cellular 
                     permeability |CITS: [35397010]|, and under low pH it 
                     affects type I pili expression |CITS: [35316170]| and 
                     cellular survival |CITS: [37909716]|."
