     gene            3658224..3658329
                     /gene="gadY"
                     /locus_tag="b4452"
                     /label=gadY
     misc_RNA        3658224..3658329
                     /gene="gadY"
                     /locus_tag="b4452"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA GadY"
                     /note="GadY acts as a positive regulator of <i>gadX</i>
                     and <i>gadW</i> by directing processing of the <i>gadXW</i>
                     transcript, which stabilizes both of the processing 
                     products.  RNase III and an unidentified nuclease are 
                     responsible for processing |CITS: [21147125]|.  <i>gadY</i>
                     is encoded on the antisense strand between <i>gadX</i> and 
                     <i>gadW</i> and thus possibly functions by base-pairing 
                     between the 3' UTR of <i>gadX</i> and the complementary 3' 
                     sequences of GadY |CITS: [15466020]|.  RNase E may be 
                     involved in regulation by GadY |CITS: [17213667]|.
                     GadY is a small RNA that is highly expressed during entry 
                     into stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [12069726][15466020][16885437]| and upregulated by low pH 
                     |CITS: [17026754]|.  Full length GadY (GadY(105)) may be 
                     processed at the 5' end, resulting in two smaller 90 
                     (GadY(90)) and 59 (GadY(59)) nt RNA species with identical 
                     3' ends.  Only GadY(90) remains present during late 
                     stationary phase.  All three forms of GadY are able to bind
                     Hfq, which is suggested to stabilize the small RNA |CITS: 
                     [15466020]|.
                     GadY expression is dependent on the stationary phase 
                     alternative sigma factor &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: 
                     [15466020]|.  <i>gadY</i> transcription is negatively 
                     regulated by GadW and positively regulated by GadX |CITS: 
                     [18808381]|.  Overexpression of GadY results in increases 
                     expression of a number of genes and proteins, including 
                     DnaK and GroEL |CITS: [15466020]|, <i>gadA</i>, 
                     <i>gadB</i>, and <i>gadC</i> |CITS: [26383169]|, reduces 
                     expression of TnaA |CITS: [15466020]|, and decreases 
                     swarming motility |CITS: [26469694]|.  Overexpression of 
                     GadXY rescues the growth defect of YidC-depleted cells 
                     |CITS: [21216990]|.  Constitutive expression of GadY 
                     improves growth at pH 6.0 and decreases acetate production 
                     |CITS: [26383169]|.  Overexpression of GadY increases 
                     biofilm formation in LB, but not YESCA medium; this appears
                     to be due to its inhibitory effect (similar to that of 
                     McaS) on CsrA's negative regulation of <i>pgaA</i> |CITS: 
                     [28470798]|.
                     The transcription of the gene <i>gadY</i> increase under pH
                     4.4 but not at pH 5.8, and it appears to contribute to cell
                     survival at pH 4.4 |CITS: [37909716]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [32213244][29137641][21646430][15466017][1 
                     5063850][12654996]|"
     gene            complement(3658346..3659170)
                     /gene="gadX"
                     /locus_tag="b3516"
                     /label=gadX
     CDS             complement(3658346..3659170)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gadX"
                     /locus_tag="b3516"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional dual regulator GadX"
                     /label=gadX
                     /note="The transcriptional activator GadX, for
                     ""<b>G</b>lutamic <b>a</b>cid <b>d</b>ecarboxylase,"" is 
                     positively autoregulated and controls the transcription of 
                     pH-inducible genes, including the principal acid resistance
                     system |CITS: [12686161][11976288][11555293]|, is glutamate
                     dependent (GAD), is also referred to as the GAD system, and
                     its genes are involved in multidrug efflux |CITS: 
                     [12446650][18808381][12730179][16980449][18297445]|. In 
                     addition GadX also activates the transcription of the 
                     central activator involved in the acid response |CITS: 
                     [17660422]|. The physiological inducer is unknown. Richard 
                     et al. proposed that GadX can sense intracellular 
                     Na<sup>+</sup> concentrations, but the mechanism is not 
                     known |CITS: [17768258]|.
                     GadX is one of the regulators of the acid resistance system
                     and is encoded by the unusual <i>gadXW</i> operon, which is
                     located in the region called the acid fitness island |CITS:
                     [18808381]|. This operon encodes two transcriptional 
                     regulators, GadX and GadW, both of which are members of the
                     AraC/XylS family of transcriptional regulators |CITS: 
                     [9409145][11282467][18808381]|.
                     The activities of GadX and GadW are indispensable upon 
                     entry into the stationary phase in response to acid pH 
                     |CITS: [11976288][12446650]|. In addition Tramonti et al. 
                     provided evidence that the transcription of the 
                     <i>gadXW</i> operon is regulated to a posttranscriptional 
                     level by a GadY small RNA |CITS: [18808381][15466020]|. 
                     GadX is highly homologous to GadW (42%), and apparently 
                     both are capable of cross talk to regulate expression of 
                     the genes of this system |CITS: [18808381]|. Although 
                     little is known about the regulating mechanism of GadX, 
                     Tucker et al. proposed that this regulator and GadW have 
                     distinct molecular mechanisms |CITS: [12730179][16980449]|.
                     These regulators form homodimers |CITS: [9409145]| and 
                     heterodimers |CITS: [12446650]| <i>in vivo</i>.
                     Currently, the  GadW/GadX-dependent circuit, involved in 
                     the GAD system, is under discussion and study. Tramonti et 
                     al.  showed that GadX alone activates the <i>gadA</i> and 
                     <i>gadB</i> promoters |CITS: [11976288]|; Ma et al. added 
                     to this regulatory interaction the GadW protein, showing 
                     that it inhibits GadX and that in some cases it activates 
                     in the absence of GadX  |CITS: [12446650]|. Trucker et al. 
                     provided evidence that GadW can work as a coactivator of 
                     GadX or it can inhibit the GadX-dependent activation, along
                     with evidence of more target genes for GadX/GadW regulation
                     |CITS: [12730179]|. About their interactions, Tramonti et 
                     al. showed the direct GadX binding at the promoters of the 
                     <i>gadB</i> (two sites) and <i>gadA</i> (four sites) 
                     operons |CITS: [11976288]|; also, Ma et al. showed that 
                     GadW forms a homodimer and that it also binds to the DNA of
                     the <i>gadA</i> and <i>gadB</i> promoters |CITS: 
                     [12446650]|.
                     As a member of the AraC/XylS family, this transcription 
                     factor is composed of two domains: the C-terminal domain 
                     (60% homologous to the C terminal of GadW), which contains 
                     two potential helix-turn-helix DNA-binding motifs in the 
                     DNA-binding region, and the amino-terminal domain (30% 
                     homologous to the N terminal of GadW), which is responsible
                     for dimerization |CITS: [9409145][8451183][12446650]|.
                     Tramonti et al. speculated that GadX binds in tandem to two
                     directed repeat sequences, in the same orientation, in the 
                     intergenic regions to activate or represses the 
                     transcription of the genes regulated |CITS: [18808381]|. 
                     The binding targets for GadX consist of 21-nucleotide-long 
                     directed repeat sequences that possess conserved motifs, 
                     called the GAD box, which is proposed to be the binding 
                     site for GadX and GadW |CITS: 
                     [12730179][12730179][18808381]|. This proposal was not 
                     unexpected, because the identity and similarity of the 
                     C-terminal domains are 41% and 66%,  respectively |CITS: 
                     [18808381]|. Each monomer of GadX binds to one of these 
                     conserved sequences |CITS: [18808381]|.
                     By using synthetic gene circuits, it was demonstrated that 
                     GadX acts as a repressor under the tested conditions, with 
                     stronger repression when its binding site is located at 
                     position -57 relative to the promoter compared to positions
                     closer (+1) to the transcription start site (TSS)  
                     |CITS:[39921566]|.
                     The <i>gadX</i> gene expression was downregulated in a 
                     mutant deficient for the |FRAME: EG11495-MONOMER| HdeD 
                     |CITS:[34694904]|. The transcription and translation of 
                     <i>gadX</i> increase under pH 4.4 but not at pH 5.8, and it
                     appears to contribute to cell survival at pH 4.4 |CITS: 
                     [37909716]|.
                     GadX was transcriptionally upregulated in iron limitation 
                     over iron excess at 63% dissolved oxygen as determined by 
                     RNA-seq |CITS:[ 35777694]|.
                     The GadX binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>gadX</i> mutant shows increased persister cell 
                     formation under antibiotic stress |CITS: [22221537]|."
