     gene            complement(3661151..3662548)
                     /gene="ccp"
                     /locus_tag="b3518"
                     /label=ccp
     CDS             complement(3661151..3662548)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ccp"
                     /locus_tag="b3518"
                     /product="trifunctional cytochrome c peroxidase /
                     menadiol:hydrogen peroxide oxidoreductase / 
                     hydroquinone:hydrogen peroxide oxidoreductase"
                     /label=ccp
                     /note="<i>ccp</i> encodes a periplasmic cytochrome
                     <i>c</i> peroxidase (CCP) that enables <i>E. coli</i> to 
                     respire using H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> as an anaerobic 
                     terminal electron acceptor; Ccp may support growth in 
                     environments where H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> is produced 
                     and oxygen levels are diminished; Ccp activity does not 
                     protect the cytoplasm from exogenous 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>; its physiological role is to 
                     exploit H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> for respiratory gain 
                     while the cytoplasmic peroxidase and catalase activities 
                     protect the cell from the toxic effects of 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> |CITS: [28696311]|. 
                     Hydroperoxide negative (Hpx<sup>-</sup>) mutants (ie. 
                     strains lacking KatG and KatE catalases plus NADH 
                     peroxidase AhpCF) retain a significant ability to degrade 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> under anaerobic conditions; 
                     Hpx<sup>-</sup> strains lacking <i>ccp</i> (Hpx<sup>-</sup>
                     &Delta;<i>ccp</i>) show a substantial loss of this ability 
                     which can be complemented by overexpression of <i>ccp</i> 
                     from a plasmid |CITS: [28696311]|. Ccp receives electrons 
                     directly from the membrane quinone pool for the reduction 
                     of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> in the periplasm; Ccp 
                     supports anaerobic growth with glycerol as a sole carbon 
                     source and H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> as the terminal 
                     electron acceptor |CITS: [28696311]|.
                     Like Hpx<sup>-</sup> &Delta;<i>ccp</i> mutants, 
                     Hpx<sup>-</sup> &Delta;<i>ccmABCDEFGH</i> mutants show a 
                     significant loss in the ability to degrade 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> under anaerobic conditions 
                     suggesting that Ccp uses the |FRAME: ABC-35-CPLX ""Ccm 
                     pathway""| for heme cofactor attachment |CITS: [28696311]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> Ccp is a member of a sub-group of bacterial 
                     CCPs that contain three heme-binding motifs |CITS: 
                     [17027371]|. Ccp contains the two heme binding sites 
                     (CXXCH) which are characteristic of bacterial cytochrome 
                     <i>c</i> peroxidases (the electron transferring or E heme 
                     and the peroxidatic or P heme) plus an additional 
                     <i>c</i>-type heme binding motif in the N-terminal domain 
                     (the NT heme); the P heme is His/OH 6-coordinated; the NT 
                     and E hemes are both His-Met 6-coordinated |CITS: 
                     [29550214]| (see also |CITS: [37375153][40669070]|).
                     A <i>ccp</i> mutant (W3110 &Delta;<i>ccp</i>) shows 
                     increased sensitivity to exogenous peroxides |CITS: 
                     [17464064]|.
                     Purified recombinant Ccp, lacking its transmembrane domain 
                     is a monomer in solution |CITS: [29550214]|.
                     Expression of <i>ccp</i> is increased under anaerobic 
                     conditions and is dependent on FNR and OxyR
                     |CITS: [17464064]|; strong expression of a <i>ccp-lacZ</i> 
                     transcriptional fusion requires anoxia plus the presence of
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>; dual regulation by both OxyR 
                     and FNR ensures that <i>ccp</i> is expressed when oxygen is
                     absent and H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> is present |CITS: 
                     [28696311]|
                     Ccp is predicted to be a bitopic inner membrane protein 
                     |CITS: [27510400]|. Ccp contains a putative signal 
                     peptidase II cleavage site |CITS: [17464064]| ."
