     gene            complement(3675521..3676807)
                     /gene="dctA"
                     /locus_tag="b3528"
                     /label=dctA
     CDS             complement(3675521..3676807)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dctA"
                     /locus_tag="b3528"
                     /product="multifunctional C4 dicarboxylate/orotate:H(+)
                     symporter / transport of a C4-dicarboxylate / transport of 
                     L-aspartate / transport of orotate / transport of fumarate 
                     / transport of malate / transport of succinate / DcuS-DctA 
                     sensor complex"
                     /label=dctA
                     /note="DctA is required for dicarboxylate transport 
                     |CITS:[357845]|. DctA is a proton motive force-dependent 
                     C<sub>4</sub>-dicarboxylate transporter, responsible for 
                     the uptake of fumarate, succinate, L-aspartate and L- and 
                     D-malate under aerobic conditions, and also for the uptake 
                     of orotate, a pyrimidine precursor |CITS:[11946977][554151 
                     0][4346810][11803016][17088549][8955389]|.
                     The substrate recognition site of DctA is located on the 
                     outer surface of the inner membrane 
                     |CITS:[1092298],[359555]|.
                     DctA and DcuB function as co-sensors with |FRAME: 
                     DCUS-MONOMER ""DcuS""| - the sensor histidine kinase of the
                     two-component system that controls the expression of genes 
                     required for C4-dicarboxylate degradation. DcuS requires 
                     the transport proteins DctA or DcuB for normal function. 
                     DctA interacts specifically with DcuS <i> in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [22780562]|. DctA is predicted to contain eight 
                     transmembrane helices with the N and C-termini located in 
                     the cytoplasm. Residues important for interaction with DcuS
                     are located in &alpha;-helix 8b |CITS: [22780562]|. DctA's 
                     role in the DctA-DcuS sensor complex is not dependent on 
                     transport function - DctA mutants with uncoupled regulatory
                     and transport function can be isolated and DctA-DcuS sensor
                     function can be activated by substrates such as citrate 
                     that are not transported by DctA |CITS: [25135747]|.
                     Transcription of <I>dctA</I> is weakly controlled by the 
                     DcuSR two-component system in response to dicarboxylates | 
                     CITS:[15995204],[15073297],[10482502],[9973351],[9765574]|.
                      DcuR binds upstream of <I>dctA</I> |CITS:[14996819]|. 
                     <I>dctA</I> expression increases during stationary phase 
                     |CITS:[10482502]|.  <i>dctA</i>, among other genes involved
                     in carbon source transport and metabolism, was 
                     downregulated in two MG1655 lysogens carrying closely 
                     related Stx2a phages O104 and PA8 |CITS: 31208335|. 
                     <I>dctA</I> is strongly down-regulated by CRP interacting 
                     with E&sigma;<sup>54</sup> and is therefore subject to 
                     catabolite repression 
                     |CITS:[4346810],[9451003],[15150239],[15066832],[10482502] 
                     ,[8955389]|. <I>dctA</I> is repressed by ArcA under 
                     anaerobic conditions |CITS:[10482502]|. <I>dctA</I> is also
                     repressed by |FRAME: EG11889-MONOMER ""DctR""| in an 
                     <I>atp</I> deletion strain preventing growth on 
                     C<sub>4</sub>-dicarboxylates |CITS:[9811641]|.
                     <I>dctA</I> is transiently induced upon cold shock 
                     |CITS:[15466053]|.
                     Mutants defective in <I>dctA</I> were unable to grow on 
                     fumarate or malate, showed decreased growth on succinate 
                     and were unable to utilize orotate as a pyrimidine source 
                     |CITS:[11946977],[8955389],[10482502]|.  These defects 
                     could be complemented by the cloned <I>dctA</I> gene 
                     |CITS:[10482502]|.  There appears to be another 
                     transporter, possibly a monocarboxylate carrier, that is 
                     able to import succinate at acidic pH in sufficient amounts
                     for aerobic growth on succinate, but cannot exchange 
                     fumarate and succinate |CITS:[11511871]|. The |FRAME: 
                     YCHM-MONOMER ""DauA""| protein was characterised by |CITS: 
                     [23278959]| as a succinate/fumarate/aspartate transporter 
                     active in aerobic and acidic environements. DctA is a 
                     member of the Dicarboxylate/Amino Acid:Cation Symporter 
                     (DAACS) family of  transporters |CITS:[8031825]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [27134023][27415771]|"
