     gene            3689818..3691389
                     /gene="bcsE"
                     /locus_tag="b3536"
                     /label=bcsE
     CDS             3689818..3691389
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="bcsE"
                     /locus_tag="b3536"
                     /product="c-di-GMP-binding protein BcsE"
                     /label=bcsE
                     /note="BcsE has been identified as a c-di-GMP-binding
                     protein. The central DUF2819 domain, renamed GIL for 
                     <b>G</b>GDEF <b>I</b>-site-<b>l</b>ike domain, is 
                     sufficient for c-di-GMP binding |CITS: [24942809]|. 
                     Products of the <i>bcs</i> gene cluster are involved in 
                     cellulose biosynthesis, a characteristic of the rdar (red, 
                     dry and rough) morphotype; the K-12 laboratory strain of 
                     <i>E. coli</i> does not show a rdar morphotype and does not
                     produce cellulose |CITS: [11260463][24097954]|.
                     In the cellulose producing K-12 strain AR3110, BcsE 
                     interacts with the cellulose phosphoethanolamine (pEtN) 
                     transferase |FRAME: EG12265-MONOMER ""BcsG""| suggesting 
                     that pEtN cellulose modification is controlled by the 
                     biofilm-promoting second messenger c-di-GMP |CITS: 
                     [29348238]|; pEtN cellulose modification is strongly 
                     reduced in an E. coli K-12 AR3110 &Delta;<i>bcsE</i> mutant
                     |CITS: [29348238]|. BcsE also contributes to cellulose 
                     production - deleting <i>bcsE</i> in <i>E. coli</i> strain 
                     AR3110 &Delta;<i>csgBA</i> (which produces cellulose but no
                     curli) results in decreased cellulose production |CITS: 
                     [29348238]|.
                     In <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strains such as W3110 and MG1655, a 
                     ""domesticating SNP"" consists of a point mutation that 
                     introduces a stop codon after the first 5 amino acids of 
                     the wild-type <i>bcsQ</i> ORF.  This domesticating SNP 
                     lowers expression of both <i>bcsQ</i> and the downstream 
                     <i>bcsA</i> gene.  After repairing the SNP, the resulting 
                     ""de-domesticated"" W3110 derivative strain produces 
                     cellulose and has dramatically altered biofilm morphology. 
                     A &Delta;<i>bcsEFG</i> mutation introduced into this strain
                     leads to significantly reduced cellulose biosynthesis 
                     |CITS: [24097954]|.
                     In the cellulose producing strain <i>E. coli</i> 1094, the 
                     inner membrane and cytosolic Bcs proteins - BcsA, BcsB, 
                     BscQ, BcsG, BcsE, BcsR and BcsF - organize in a 
                     multisubunit, stable macrocomplex; two-hybrid assays 
                     identified multiple direct interactions between the 
                     subunits and a low resolution structure has been obtained 
                     |CITS: [29234007]| (see also |CITS: [33712813]|).
                     BcsE: ""<b>b</b>acterial <b>c</b>ellulose 
                     <b>s</b>ynthesis""
                     Related review: |CITS: [26077867]|"
