     gene            complement(3693340..3693447)
                     /gene="ldrD"
                     /locus_tag="b4453"
                     /label=ldrD
     CDS             complement(3693340..3693447)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ldrD"
                     /locus_tag="b4453"
                     /product="small toxic polypeptide LdrD"
                     /label=ldrD
                     /note="The |FRAME: TAX-83333| genome contains four genes 
                     encoding Long Direct Repeat (LDR) peptides - |FRAME: 
                     G0-9602|, |FRAME: G0-9605|, |FRAME: G0-9607|, and |FRAME: 
                     G0-9041|. The first three are clustered together as tandem 
                     repeats, while |FRAME: G0-9041| is located elsewhere on the
                     genome. Deletions of <i>ldrABC</i>, <i>ldrD</i> and 
                     <i>ldrABDC</i> have no effect under laboratory conditions 
                     |CITS: [12123448]|. Four <i>ldr</i>-like regions, three 
                     near <i>ldrD</i> and one near <i>ldrABC</i>, may represent 
                     recombination spots for LDRs |CITS: [11939563]|.
                     All four <i>ldr</i> genes encode small toxic peptides, and 
                     each gene is matched with a neighboring <i>rdl</i> gene 
                     that encodes an antisense regulatory RNA that is 
                     complementary to a region of the <i>ldr</i> gene, forming 
                     four Type I toxin-antitoxin (TA) pairs. Type I TA systems 
                     consist of a stable toxin-expressing mRNA, which is 
                     counteracted by an unstable RNA antitoxin.
                     While many plasmid-encoded TA systems behave as selfish 
                     elements and favour their own maintenance at the expense of
                     their host, the role of chromosomally-encoded TA systems is
                     less clear |CITS: [19325885]|.
                     The LdrD peptide is matched with the |FRAME: RDLD-RNA 
                     ""RdlD antisense regulatory RNA""|. The LdrD mRNA is 
                     stable, whereas the RdlD antisense RNA is unstable. While 
                     endogenous expression of the LDR toxin peptides has not 
                     been reported, LdrD overexpression leads to fast and 
                     irreversible cell growth arrest, nucleoid condensation and 
                     eventually cell death. The RdlD RNA is complementary to the
                     upstream region of the <i>ldrD</i> gene, and leads to 
                     degradation of the <i>ldrD</i> mRNA, preventing its 
                     translation |CITS: [12123448]|.
                     All <i>ldr</i> loci contain an additional <i>mok</i>-like 
                     reading frame that overlaps with both the 5'-end of the 
                     antisense RNA gene and part of, or the entire, <i>ldr</i> 
                     toxin reading frame. This reading frame overlapping the 
                     <i>ldrD</i> gene has been named <i>ldrX</i> |CITS: 
                     [17376733]|.
                     Whole-genome gene expression analysis after induction of 
                     LdrD expression showed changes in expression of many stress
                     response and membrane proteins |CITS: [18710431]|. A later 
                     study has shown that the toxicity of LdrD originates from a
                     direct inhibition of protein translation. LdrD interacts 
                     with the ribosomal exit tunnel and interferes with protein 
                     translation, leading to a decrease of translation 
                     efficiency |CITS: [37229877]|. LdrD has a propensity to 
                     adopt an alpha helical conformation. An L14A mutation 
                     renders LdrD toxin non-functional |CITS: [37229877]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17376733][19210615][21342783][23131729]|."
                     /translation="MTFAELGMAFWHDLAAPVIAGILASMIVNWLNKRK"
                     /go_component="plasma membrane [goid 0005886] [evidence
                     RCA] [PMID 27510400]"
                     /go_component="plasma membrane [goid 0005886] [evidence
                     IEA] [GO_REF 0000044]"
     gene            3693496..3693559
                     /gene="rdlD"
                     /locus_tag="b4454"
                     /label=rdlD
     misc_RNA        3693496..3693559
                     /gene="rdlD"
                     /locus_tag="b4454"
                     /product="antisense regulatory RNA RdlD"
                     /note="The LdrD peptide and RdlD antisense regulatory RNA
                     are encoded within one of a set of four Long Direct Repeats
                     (LDRs); RdlD acts as the antitoxin of a Type I 
                     toxin-antitoxin pair |CITS: [12123448]|.
                     Overproduction of LdrD causes toxicity, manifesting as 
                     condensation of the nucleoid and cell death.  RdlD RNA is 
                     complementary to the upstream region of the <i>ldrD</i> 
                     gene, and it acts to regulate LdrD translation.  The 
                     <i>ldrD</i> mRNA is stable, whereas the RdlD antisense RNA 
                     is unstable |CITS: [12123448]|.  |CITS: [17376733]| 
                     proposed the hypothesis that translation of LdrD is 
                     regulated by RdlD RNA via regulation of translation of the 
                     larger ORF <i>ldrX</i>.
                     RdlD interacts with the protein |FRAME: EG12866-MONOMER| 
                     |CITS: [36328526]|.
                     RdlD: ""<b>r</b>egulator <b>d</b>etected in 
                     <b>L</b>DR-<b>D</b>"" |CITS: [12123448]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [15487940][17376733][23131729][32213244]|"
     gene            3693923..3695194
                     /gene="yhjV"
                     /locus_tag="b3539"
                     /label=yhjV
     CDS             3693923..3695194
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yhjV"
                     /locus_tag="b3539"
                     /product="putative transporter YhjV"
                     /label=yhjV
                     /note="The YhjV protein is an uncharacterised member of
                     the Hydroxy/Aromatic Amino Acid Permease (HAAAP) Family 
                     within the Amino Acid-Polyamine-Organocation (APC) 
                     Superfamily |CITS: [24225317]|.
                     YhjV is implicated in melatonin transport; a <i>yhjV</i> 
                     knockout strain has decreased growth in the presence of 
                     melatonin (4g/L) and overexpression of <i>yhjV</i> improves
                     melatonin production in a strain engineered for its 
                     synthesis |CITS: [35794920]|.
                     YhjV has 11 transmembrane helices and the C-terminus is 
                     located in the periplasm |CITS:[15044727]|.
                     A <i>yhjV</i> deletion mutant is 2-fold more sensitive to 
                     both X-radiation and UV-radiation than wild type |CITS: 
                     [27718375]|."
