     gene            complement(3701976..3702052)
                     /gene="proK"
                     /locus_tag="b3545"
                     /label=proK
     tRNA            complement(3701976..3702052)
                     /gene="proK"
                     /locus_tag="b3545"
                     /product="tRNA-Pro(CGG)"
                     /label=proK
                     /note="tRNA(proK) is one of three proline tRNAs (|FRAME: 
                     EG30066|, |FRAME: EG30067| and |FRAME: EG30068|) and is 
                     monocistronic.
                     tRNAs are the adapters that allow synthesis of proteins 
                     from mRNAs. Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the 
                     ribosome for protein synthesis. There, the tRNA recognizes 
                     an RNA codon with its own three-nucleotide anticodon, thus 
                     allowing synthesis of a specific peptide based on an mRNA 
                     template.
                     tRNAs are processed to their active, mature forms by RNA 
                     cleavage and by modification of their bases. RNA cleavage 
                     consists of removal of both 5' and 3' extensions in a 
                     multistep process typically involving many RNases |CITS: 
                     [11252717]|. Both the monocistronic <i>proK</i> and 
                     <i>proL</i> tRNA precursors are processed via the |FRAME: 
                     PWY-8468| which starts with removal of the gene by 
                     endonucleolytic cleavage of the 5' end and the complete 
                     removal of the 3' Rho-independent transcription terminator 
                     by RNase E. The 5' end undergoes additional cleavage by 
                     RNase P |CITS: [27288443]| resulting in the mature tRNA 
                     precursor ready for charging. When RNase E is absent, both 
                     RNase G and RNase Z can serve as a weak substitute for 
                     RNase E |CITS: [27288443]|.
                     tRNAs are also subject to a wide variety of base 
                     modifications catalyzed by proteins such as |FRAME: 
                     EG11932-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10595-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     G6364-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11344-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     G7199-MONOMER|, |FRAME: CPLX0-1101|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11779-MONOMER|, |FRAME: G7422-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     G7449-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11177-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10454-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10967-MONOMER| and |FRAME: 
                     EG11022-MONOMER|.
                     Mature tRNAs are linked via a 3' CCA sequence to their 
                     cognate amino acid in an ATP-dependent fashion by the 
                     appropriate amino-acid-tRNA synthetase, as shown in the 
                     |FRAME: TRNA-CHARGING-PWY|. Subsequently, these charged 
                     tRNAs interact with the ribosome and template mRNA to 
                     generate polypeptides. The discovery of the role of tRNA in
                     protein synthesis is reviewed in detail in |CITS: 
                     [7033244]|.
                     The relationship between tRNA(pro) structure and its 
                     recognition by |FRAME: PROS-CPLX| has been extensively 
                     evaluated |CITS: [7522561][8643363][7870582][12903242]|. 
                     Following UVA irradiation, tRNA(pro) is acylated at 
                     one-tenth the normal rate, causing a growth defect |CITS: 
                     [2449211]|."
     gene            complement(3702144..3703835)
                     /gene="eptB"
                     /locus_tag="b3546"
                     /label=eptB
     CDS             complement(3702144..3703835)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="eptB"
                     /locus_tag="b3546"
                     /product="multifunctional phosphoethanolamine transferase
                     EptB / phosphoethanolamine transferase (KdoII) / Kdo2-lipid
                     A phosphoethanolamine 7''-transferase / Kdo2-lipid VIA 
                     phosphoethanolamine 7''-transferase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.8.42"
                     /label=eptB
                     /note="EptB catalyses the addition of phosphoethanolamine
                     (pEtN) to the 3-deoxy-d-manno-oct-2-ulosonate (KdoII) sugar
                     of the inner core of lipid A-core oligosaccharide. 
                     Lipopolysaccharide, isolated from an <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     W3110 derived |FRAME: A-DEEP-ROUGH-FORM-LIPOPOLYSACCHARIDE 
                     deep rough| (Re) mutant (<i>E. coli</i> WBB06) contains 
                     non-stoichiometric pEtN substitutions at position 7 of the 
                     second Kdo residue |CITS: [9266718]|. Addition of the pEtN 
                     moiety to the Kdo region of Re LPS is absolutely dependent 
                     upon the presence of CaCl<sub>2</sub> in the growth medium;
                     LPS isolated from strain WBB06 grown in rich media does not
                     contain detectable pEtN substitutents; membrane 
                     preparations from wild-type W3110 and mutant WBB06 grown in
                     the presence of 5-50 mm CaCl<sup>2</sup> both contain an 
                     enzyme activity that modifies the outer Kdo sugar of the 
                     LPS core with a pEtN moiety; calcium-induced pEtN 
                     transferase activity is not seen in membranes of an <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |FRAME: EG10781 <i>pssA</i>| null mutant 
                     suggesting that phosphatidylethanolamine is the donor 
                     substrate for the pEtN moiety |CITS: [11042192]|. 
                     <i>eptB</i> encodes Ca<sup>2+</sup>-induced 
                     phosphoethanolamine transferase; EptB requires 
                     Ca<sup>2+</sup> and phosphatidylethanolamine (PE) for 
                     activity <i>in vitro</i>; EptB catalyses pEtN transferase 
                     activity <i>in vitro</i> with both hexa-acylated |FRAME: 
                     KDO2-LIPID-A (Kdo)<sub>2</sub>-lipid A| and tetra-acylated 
                     |FRAME: KDO2-LIPID-IVA (Kdo)<SUB>2</SUB>-lipid 
                     IV<SUB>A</SUB>| as aceptor |CITS: [15795227]|. EptB is an 
                     inner membrane protein whose active site likely faces the 
                     periplasm |CITS: [15795227]|.
                     Incorporation of pEtN on the second Kdo is seen in 
                     glycoform I and glycoform V LPS derivatives |CITS: 
                     [22021036][12819207]|.
                     An in-frame deletion of <i>eptB</i> in a strain lacking 
                     <i>waaC</i> and <i>waaF</i> (a heptose-deficient strain) 
                     causes sensitivity to Ca<sup>2+</sup>, while deletion of 
                     <i>eptB</i> in a wild-type background does not |CITS: 
                     [15795227]|.
                     Under normal laboratory growth conditions EptB expression 
                     is silenced by the |FRAME: RNA0-348| |CITS: [19889087]|; 
                     <i>eptB</i> expression is regulated at multiple levels 
                     |CITS: [23659637]|.
                     Using a method that distinguishes N-phosphorylation from 
                     O-phosphorylation, N-phosphorylation was detected <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [33277485]|."
