     CDS             3706452..3707015
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tag"
                     /locus_tag="b3549"
                     /product="bifunctional 3-methyl-adenine DNA glycosylase I, 
                     constitutive / 3 methyl-adenine DNA glycosylase"
                     /EC_number="3.2.2.20"
                     /label=tag
                     /note="Alkylating agents such as 
                     N-methyl-N'-nitro-N-nitrosoguanidine (MNNG) or methyl 
                     methanesulfonate (MMS) can produce potentially mutagenic 
                     lesions in DNA.
                     Alkylation damage may also occur spontaneously in living 
                     organisms through the action of methyl donors such as 
                     S-adenosylmethionine |CITS:[7188181]|.
                     The product of the <I>tag</I> gene in <I>Escherichia 
                     coli</I> is one of two (along with AlkA) 
                     3-methyladenine-DNA-glycosylases present in the genome. 
                     Like AlkA, it acts preferentially on double-stranded DNA 
                     containing 3-methyladenine |CITS:[3536857]| by catalyzing 
                     the hydrolysis of the N-glycosylic bonds linking the 
                     methylated base to the deoxyribose -phosphate backbone. 
                     This enzymatic action results in the formation of an 
                     apurinic (AP) site which requires further repair through 
                     the action of an AP endonuclease, DNA polymerase and 
                     DNA-ligase.  Unlike AlkA, the <I>tag</I> gene product is 
                     not inducible through the adaptation response."
                     /translation="MERCGWVSQDPLYIAYHDNEWGVPETDSKKLFEMICLEGQQAGLS
