     gene            3707012..3707452
                     /gene="yiaC"
                     /locus_tag="b3550"
                     /label=yiaC
     CDS             3707012..3707452
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yiaC"
                     /locus_tag="b3550"
                     /product="trifunctional peptidyl-lysine
                     N-acetyltransferase YiaC / peptidyl-lysine 
                     N-acetyltransferase / peptidyl-lysine N-lactyltransferase"
                     /label=yiaC
                     /note="YiaC is an <i>N&epsilon;</i>-lysine
                     acetyltransferase that targets 391 unique lysine residues 
                     in 251 proteins |CITS: [30352934]|.
                     Overexpression of YiaC in an acetylation-gutted strain 
                     (&Delta;<i>|FRAME: EG20173| |FRAME: G7350| |FRAME: EG11448|
                     |FRAME: G6577|</i>) leads to the appearance of acetylated 
                     proteins in an anti-acetyllysine Western blot. Mutagenesis 
                     of a predicted catalytic residue in YiaC, Y115A, eliminates
                     the acetylation signal observed with the wild-type protein 
                     |CITS: [30352934]|.
                     YiaC was the only GCN5-related N-acetyltransferase (GNAT) 
                     family protein that resulted in lactylation of lysines when
                     overexpressed, as measured in whole <i>E. coli</i> cell 
                     lysates by immunoblotting with a pan-lysine lactylation 
                     (Kla) antibody. &Delta;<i>yiaC</i> experiments confirmed 
                     the lactylation by YiaC and found over 1000 unique lysine 
                     sites in 478 proteins. Kinetic analysis found that YiaC has
                     strong binding affinity to both lactyl-CoA, formed by 
                     |FRAME: EG12432-MONOMER|, and to acetyl-CoA (K<sub>D</sub> 
                     = 6.83 and 5.57, respectively) but did not bind to 
                     succinyl-CoA. YiaC's lactylation activity was found to be 
                     more efficient than its acetylation activity. Proteomics 
                     analysis of the lactylome of <i>E. coli</i> found the 
                     target proteins ranged from metabolic and biosynthetic 
                     proteins to translation and ribosome assembly proteins, 
                     with many enzymes in glycolysis, TCA cycle and fatty acid 
                     biosynthesis being both lactylated and targeted for 
                     delactylation by |FRAME: G6577-MONOMER| |CITS: [36333310]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>yiaC</i> reduces motility of the K-12 
                     strain BW25113 in soft agar |CITS: [30352934]|. The YiaC 
                     lysine lactylation and CobB delactylation regulates 
                     enzymatic activities of the target proteins |FRAME: 
                     CITSYN-MONOMER|, |FRAME: NICOTINATEPRIBOSYLTRANS-MONOMER|, 
                     |FRAME: GPMA-MONOMER| and |FRAME: PKI-MONOMER| <i>in 
                     vitro</i>, pointing to the Kla-based molecular mechanism 
                     for regulation of glycolysis |CITS: [36333310]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [34721364]|"
                     /translation="MIREAQRSELPAILELWLESTTWGHPFIKANYWRDCIPLVRDAYL
                     ANAQNWVWEEDGKLLGFVSIMEGRFLAAMFVAPKAVRRGIGKALMQYVQQRHPHLMLEV
                     YQKNQPAINFYQAQGFHIVDCAWQDETQLPTWIMSWPVVQTL"
