     gene            complement(3713808..3713960)
                     /gene="hokA"
                     /locus_tag="b4455"
                     /label=hokA
     CDS             complement(3713808..3713960)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hokA"
                     /locus_tag="b4455"
                     /product="small toxic polypeptide"
                     /label=hokA
                     /note="Sequence analysis indicates that the <I>hokA</I>
                     gene is a homologue of the <I>hok</I> (host killing) gene 
                     which is responsible for plasmid stabilization in plasmid 
                     R1. <I>hok</I> encodes a stable mRNA whose translation is 
                     inhibited by a less stable mRNA encoded by <I>sok</I>. The 
                     stable mRNA from <I>hokA</I> encodes a polypeptide that 
                     induction experiments have shown to be toxic to 
                     <I>Escherichia coli</I> host cells, resulting in cell 
                     growth arrest, morphological changes, and rapid cell 
                     killing. The <I>hokA</I> system is inactive due to 
                     insertion of the insertion element IS150 32 bp upstream of 
                     the start codon of the gene. Sequence analysis shows that 
                     after the removal of the IS150 element sequence, the 
                     <I>hokA</I> system contains regulatory elements described 
                     for the <I>hok/sok</I> system from plasmid R1 including a 
                     fold-back inhibition element (<I>fbi</I>), a translational 
                     activation element (<I>tac</I>), a promoter, and an 
                     overlapping open reading frame named <I>mokA</I>. Northern 
                     analysis using the <I>hokA</I> system from E. coli C which 
                     does not contain the IS150 element has shown that the 
                     <I>fbi</I> sequence is exonucleolytically removed during 
                     post-transcriptional processing, and that the <I>hokA</I> 
                     locus also encodes a small, less stable antisense RNA 
                     complimentary to the <I>hokA</I> mRNA leader in the 
                     <I>mokA</I> open reading frame. The antisense RNA is 
                     homologous to Sok-RNA from plasmid R1, and is believed to 
                     be designed to regulate <I>hokA</I> translation by 
                     inhibiting translation of the overlapping <I>mokA</I>. It 
                     has been shown that hokA is unable to mediate PSK. Missing 
                     from the K-12 sequence are the promoter for the antisense 
                     RNA and the start of the <I>mokA</I> reading frame, 
                     consistent with a 39 base-pair deletion caused by insertion
                     of the IS150 element |CITS:[10361310]|."
