     CDS             complement(3715688..3717757)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glyS"
                     /locus_tag="b3559"
                     /product="glycine--tRNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.1.1.14"
                     /label=glyS
                     /note="The isolated &beta; subunit of GlyRS is monomeric
                     in solution |CITS: [6992865]| and binds tRNA<sup>Gly</sup>;
                     there is one binding site per monomer |CITS: [6374618]|.  
                     The N-terminal domain of the &beta; subunit contains 
                     residues required for adenylate synthesis, while the 
                     C-terminal domain appears to be dispensable for catalytic 
                     activity |CITS: [2404005]|.
                     A crystal structure of GlyRS has been solved at 2.68 
                     &Aring; resolution, revealing an unusual X-shaped 
                     architecture with an &alpha; subunit dimer at the center. 
                     The &alpha; subunit contains binding sites for glycine and 
                     ATP, while the &beta; subunit interacts with the 
                     &gamma;-phosphate of ATP.  Site-directed mutagenesis 
                     supported the proposed roles for the &alpha; and &beta; 
                     subunits |CITS: [34390350]|.
                     Site-directed mutagenesis of the cysteine residues C98, 
                     C395 and C450 established that &beta; subunit cysteine 
                     thiols are not required for catalytic activity of GlyRS; 
                     however, C395 can be alkylated by NEM, resulting in loss of
                     GlyRS activity |CITS: [3536904]|.
                     There is a general discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [37224536]|."
