     gene            3730857..3732887
                     /gene="malS"
                     /locus_tag="b3571"
                     /label=malS
     CDS             3730857..3732887
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="malS"
                     /locus_tag="b3571"
                     /product="alpha-amylase"
                     /EC_number="3.2.1.1"
                     /label=malS
                     /note="MalS is a periplasmic enzyme that degrades linear
                     |FRAME: Dextrins dextrins| of at least three glucose 
                     residues.  |FRAME: CPD-3781| and |FRAME: CPD-3783| serve as
                     substrates, leading to classification of the enzyme as an 
                     &alpha;-amylase hydrolyzing internal 1,4-glucosidic 
                     linkages |CITS: [3005273]|.  MalS is one of two 
                     &alpha;-amylases present in <i>E. coli</i>.  The second 
                     enzyme, |FRAME: ALPHA-AMYL-CYTO-MONOMER ""AmyA""|, is 
                     cytoplasmic.
                     MalS is an endo hydrolase that recognizes its substrates 
                     from their non-reducing end and preferentially liberates 
                     |FRAME: MALTOHEXAOSE| |CITS: [3275641]|, which can then be 
                     transported through the cytoplasmic membrane via the 
                     |FRAME: ABC-16-CPLX|.  MalS is thought to provide a growth 
                     advantage by degrading periplasmic maltodextrins between 7 
                     and 15 glucose units in length |CITS: [3005273]|.
                     MalS is a monomer in solution; two intramolecular disulfide
                     bonds have been mapped |CITS: [9268356]|. A crystal 
                     structure was reported at a resolution of 2.7 &Aring;, 
                     showing that the enzyme has unique structural features of 
                     circularly permutated domains.  The N-domain was predicted 
                     by AlphaFold2 be a CBM69 (carbohydrate-binding module 69), 
                     serving to bind the polysaccharide substrates.  The domain 
                     is highly flexible and is not visible in the crystal 
                     structure. The enzyme has a 6-glucosyl unit pocket, into 
                     which the non-reducing end of the substrate fits. MalS has 
                     two Ca<sup>2+</sup> binding sites that contribute 
                     significantly to its thermostability |CITS: [37241718]|.
                     Expression of <i>malS</i> is under the control of the 
                     |FRAME: PD00237| |CITS: [3005273]|."
