     gene            3745352..3746326
                     /gene="yiaU"
                     /locus_tag="b3585"
                     /label=yiaU
     CDS             3745352..3746326
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yiaU"
                     /locus_tag="b3585"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional activator YiaU"
                     /label=yiaU
                     /note="YiaU is a LysR-type transcription factor involved
                     in regulation of bacterial envelope biogenesis, antibiotic 
                     resistance, biofilm formation, and complement sensitivity 
                     |CITS: [35438626][35508583]|. This protein contains a 
                     helix-turn-helix motif to bind DNA in its N-terminal 
                     domain, and it was predicted to regulate genes related to 
                     toxins and cell fitness |CITS:[35880217]|.
                     In systematic studies of oligomerization, it was shown that
                     some members of the LysR family, like YiaU, interact with 
                     other members of the family to form heterodimers, but the 
                     physiological significance of this is unknown |CITS: 
                     [21187915]|.
                     Genome-wide YiaU binding sites were determined <i>in 
                     vivo</i> by the chromatin immunoprecipitation method 
                     combined with lambda exonuclease digestion (ChIP-exo) in 
                     glucose M9 minimal medium |CITS:[ 34428301]|, and <i>in 
                     vitro</i> by genomic systematic evolution of ligands using 
                     exponential enrichment (gSELEX-chip) |CITS: [35438626]|.
                     A total of five YiaU binding regions were identified by 
                     gSELEX-chip and confirmed by gel shift assay; these binding
                     regions flank six operons encoding envelope components or 
                     proteins involved in its modification |CITS: [35438626]|. 
                     The <i>in vivo</i> transcriptional regulation of some of 
                     the binding targets identified by gSELEX-chip was 
                     determined by RT-qPCR. A YiaU binding site was identified 
                     by gSELEX-chip in the <i>yiaU-yiaT</i> spacer, suggesting 
                     autoregulation |CITS: [35438626]|.  A palindromic AT-rich 
                     18-bp-long sequence was identified in the YiaU binding 
                     regions |CITS: [35438626]|. Based on gSELEX data, it was 
                     proposed to rename YiaU as CsuR (regulator of cell surface)
                     |CITS: [35438626]|.
                     The transcriptional response for the deletion of YiaU was 
                     characterized by RNA-seq in M9 minimal medium supplemented 
                     with 7 mM l-threonine |CITS:[35508583]|, and differentially
                     expressed genes were found related to cell envelope 
                     biogenesis, carbohydrate transport, energy production, and 
                     amino acid metabolism |CITS:[35508583]|. The direct YiaU 
                     regulatory targets identified by integrative analysis of 
                     ChIP-exo and RNA-seq data are involved in 
                     lipopolysaccharide (LPS) biosynthesis, and authors proposed
                     renaming YiaU as LpsR (LPS biosynthesis regulator) 
                     |CITS:[35508583]|.
                     YiaU is essential for growth microaerobically at high 
                     concentrations of oxacillin in RPMI_10LB medium 
                     |CITS:[35508583]|.
                     A strain overexpressing YiaU showed increased resistance to
                     chlortetracycline, amoxicillin, colistin, rolitetracycline,
                     oxytetracycline, and captan, while resistance to patulin 
                     was reduced in comparison with the wild type strain |CITS: 
                     [35438626]|. Moreover, this overexpressing YiaU strain 
                     showed increased biofilm formation and enhanced complement 
                     sensitivity |CITS: [35438626]|.
                     It was hypothesized that YiaU has an effector molecule; 
                     however, this molecule has not been determined |CITS: 
                     [35438626]|.
                     Upstream of the <i>yiaU</i> gene, a sequence was found that
                     could work as a transcriptional promoter, and the 
                     transcription of the <i>yiaU</i> gene is affected by 
                     conditions that generate general transcriptional 
                     termination |CITS: [35880217]|.
                     A knockout strain in <i>yiaU</i> reached stationary phase 
                     at a lower OD 600 than the wild-type strain 
                     |CITS:[35508583]|."
