     gene            complement(3751377..3753221)
                     /gene="selB"
                     /locus_tag="b3590"
                     /label=selB
     CDS             complement(3751377..3753221)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="selB"
                     /locus_tag="b3590"
                     /product="trifunctional selenocysteyl-tRNA-specific
                     translation elongation factor / GTPase / 
                     SelB-L-selenocysteinyl-tRNA(sec)"
                     /EC_number="3.6.5.3"
                     /label=selB
                     /note="SelB is a specialized translation factor that takes
                     the place of elongation factor EF-Tu for the insertion of 
                     selenocysteine into a peptide chain at the site of a UGA 
                     codon.  Most UGA codons signal chain termination; a UGA 
                     codon that encodes selenocysteine insertion is 
                     distinguished from those that signal chain termination by a
                     structured sequence, called SECIS 
                     (<b>SE</b>leno<b>C</b>ysteine <b>I</b>nsertion 
                     <b>S</b>equence), immediately downstream of the UGA codon 
                     |CITS: [1396569][8226830]|.  SelB recognizes and binds to 
                     identical bases within the loop region in the SECIS hairpin
                     structure of both <i>fdhF</i> and <i>fdnG</i> mRNAs, and 
                     thereby brings the charged |FRAME: 
                     modified-charged-selC-tRNA 
                     selenocysteyl-tRNA<sup>Sec</sup>| (Sec-tRNA<sup>Sec</sup>) 
                     into the neighborhood of the UGA codon |CITS: 
                     [8483932][8314089][8634916]|.
                     Structures of several intermediates during delivery of 
                     Sec-tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> to the ribosome have been solved by 
                     single-particle cryo-electron microscopy, elucidating the 
                     dynamics of codon recognition and GTPase activation |CITS: 
                     [27842381]|.
                     SelB is found at a level of approximately 1100 copies per 
                     cell |CITS: [2140572]|.
                     The N-terminal domain of SelB shares extensive sequence 
                     similarity with EF-Tu and IF2 |CITS: [2531290]|; this 
                     domain binds specifically to Sec-tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> |CITS: 
                     [8893853]|.  Its structure was modeled based on that of 
                     <i>Thermus thermophilus</i> EF-Tu |CITS: [9150874]|.  Like 
                     EF-Tu, SelB binds GTP and exhibits a low GTPase activity 
                     which is stimulated by the presence of 70S ribosomes, the 
                     UGA codon and the SECIS element |CITS: 
                     [9454578][27842381]|.  An additional C-terminal domain of 
                     SelB consisting of four tandem winged-helix motifs is 
                     responsible for binding to the SECIS mRNA structure |CITS: 
                     [8893853]|.  A crystal structure of a fragment of SelB in 
                     complex with the hairpin SECIS element has been solved at 
                     2.3 &Aring; resolution |CITS: 
                     [17565186][18029744][17537456]|.  Suppressor mutations 
                     within <i>selB</i> that suppress the effect of defined 
                     mutations within the SECIS element have been isolated 
                     |CITS: [10628863][11053373]|.
                     The kinetics of the interactions of SelB with guanosine 
                     nucleotides, the SECIS element, and Sec-tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> 
                     have been studied, shedding light on the mechanism of SelB 
                     action compared to the standard elongation factor EF-Tu 
                     |CITS: [10781605][19940162][22740700]|.  Measurement of 
                     dissociation constants suggest that SelB-GTP first 
                     interacts with tRNA-Sec before recognition of the SECIS 
                     element |CITS: [34624294]|.
                     The length of the aminoacyl-acceptor stem of 
                     tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> is a specificity determinant for SelB 
                     binding |CITS: [1939093]|, and the selenol group of the 
                     aminoacylated tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> is required for stable 
                     interaction between SelB and Sec-tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> |CITS: 
                     [1839607]|.  Providing specificity, the linker region 
                     between domains 3 and 4 of SelB interacts with and distorts
                     the long variable arm of tRNA<sup>Sec</sup>, and an 
                     extended loop in domain 3 of SelB interacts with the 
                     antideterminant box of tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> |CITS: 
                     [27842381]|.
                     A <i>selB</i> mutant accumulates 
                     Sec-tRNA<sup>Sec</sup><sub>UCA</sub> and does not 
                     incorporate selenocysteine into selenoproteins |CITS: 
                     [2524495]|.
                     SelB is able to autoregulate the expression of the 
                     <i>selAB</i> operon at the posttranscriptional level.
                     SelB binds to a SECIS-like element upstream of the 
                     <i>selA</i> ribosome binding site, affecting the stability 
                     and translation of the <i>selAB</i> mRNA |CITS: 
                     [12486013]|.
                     SelB: ""<b>sel</b>enium metabolism"" |CITS: [2962989]| 
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [1832153][1838215][9315303][11568434][11568440][29447106]| 
                     "
