     gene            complement(3753218..3754609)
                     /gene="selA"
                     /locus_tag="b3591"
                     /label=selA
     CDS             complement(3753218..3754609)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="selA"
                     /locus_tag="b3591"
                     /product="selenocysteine synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.9.1.1"
                     /label=selA
                     /note="Selenocysteine synthase (SelA) catalyzes the
                     conversion of serine to selenocysteine on serine-charged 
                     tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> |CITS: [2007584]|. SelA is a complex of 
                     10 subunits with five-fold symmetry |CITS: [1474891]|; the 
                     homodecameric structure is assembled via stepwise addition 
                     of dimers to intermediate oligomeric states |CITS: 
                     [23266652]|. Formation of a productive active site requires
                     homodecamerization |CITS: [24456689]|.
                     The pyridoxal 5'-phosphate cofactor is present at a 
                     stoichiometry of one per monomer |CITS: [2007584]| and is 
                     attached at Lys295 |CITS: [9688279]|. The 
                     seryl-tRNA<sup>Sec</sup><sub>UCA</sub> substrate was 
                     thought to be present at a stoichiometry of one per two 
                     monomers |CITS: [2007585]|, but was later shown to be at a 
                     one-to-one stoichiometry |CITS: [23428847]|. The serine 
                     residue of seryl-tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> first forms a Schiff 
                     base with the PLP cofactor, followed by dehydration of 
                     serine to generate the enzyme-bound 
                     aminoacrylyl-tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> intermediate |CITS: 
                     [2007585]|.
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-7957 SelD| forms a ternary complex with the 
                     SelA-tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> complex |CITS: [26378233]|. 
                     Structural analysis of a single-particle cryoEM 
                     reconstruction of SelA covalently bound to PLP at a 
                     resolution of 2.69 &Aring; confirms the symmetrical, 
                     ring-like pentamer of dimers structure and estimates that 
                     10 tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> molecules can potentially bind. A gap
                     is present between the C-terminal domain and the core, 
                     facilitating the interaction between adjacent dimers; this 
                     structure differs from that observed in other fold-type-1 
                     PLP-dependent enzymes and is unique to bacteria |CITS: 
                     [38681238]|.
                     Site-specific mutants at the dimer-dimer interface are 
                     catalytically inactive |CITS: [24456689]|. A <i>selA</i> or
                     <i>selD</i> mutant exhibits a defect in 
                     selenocysteinyl-tRNA formation, whereas a <i>selB</i> 
                     mutant does not |CITS: [2524495]|. Extracts of cells 
                     overproducing SelA and SelD exhibit <i>in vitro</i> 
                     production of selenocysteinyl-tRNA<sub>UCA</sub> from 
                     seryl-tRNA<sub>UCA</sub> |CITS: [2405383]|.
                     Transcription of <i>selAB</i> appears to be constitutive 
                     |CITS: [1650339]|. A SECIS-like element in the 5' UTR of 
                     the <i>selAB</i> transcript binds a complex of 
                     SelB-GTP-selenocysteyl-tRNA<sup>Sec</sup> and negatively 
                     regulates efficiency of translation initiation, thus 
                     coupling the biosynthesis of selenocysteine to the 
                     availability of selenium |CITS: [12486013]|. SelA protein 
                     levels are decreased when <i>E. coli</i> is grown in media 
                     with high levels of zinc |CITS: [17122063]|.
                     SelA: ""<b>sel</b>enocysteine <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [2962989]| 
                     Review: |CITS: [12098758]|"
