     gene            3768932..3769519
                     /gene="mtlR"
                     /locus_tag="b3601"
                     /label=mtlR
                     /note="There are several palindrome sequences within
                     <i>mtlR</i> gene that may work to regulate transcription or
                     translation |CITS:[8300537]|."
     CDS             3768932..3769519
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mtlR"
                     /locus_tag="b3601"
                     /product="transcriptional repressor MtlR"
                     /label=mtlR
                     /note="In 1994, MtlR, for ""<b>M</b>anni<b>t</b>o<b>l</b> 
                     <b>R</b>egulator,"" was described as a transcriptional 
                     regulator of the <i>mtlADR</i> operon, and potential 
                     TF-binding sites were proposed to be recognized for the 
                     regulator |CITS: [8300537]|. However, in 2009 Tan <i>et 
                     al.</i> |CITS: [19840941]| demonstrated that MtlR is unable
                     to bind to the <i>mtlADR</i> operon regulatory region, and 
                     they furthermore demonstrated that MtlR does not possess a 
                     DNA-binding motif. Because a regulatory effect of MtlR has 
                     been demonstrated (mutants have an effect on <i>mtlADR</i> 
                     expression), it is proposed that this regulation is 
                     indirect, through the involvement of some other DNA-binding
                     regulator, in <i>E. coli</i>.
                     It is noteworthy that in other organisms, MtlR contains a 
                     DNA-binding motif; in addition, it has been shown to 
                     function as a DNA-binding regulator of transcription |CITS:
                     [26159071]|.
                     MtlR belongs to the Pfam05068 family, a new class of 
                     transcription factors in bacteria that may regulate gene 
                     expression indirectly as part of a larger transcriptional 
                     complex |CITS: [19840941][14681378]|.
                     The crystal structures of MtlR from <i>Vibrio 
                     parahaemolyticus</i> and its homolog, the fumarase E 
                     protein from <i>Shigella flexneri</i>, have been determined
                     |CITS: [19840941]|.
                     The  dephosphorylated HPr protein interacts with MtlR in a 
                     1:1 ratio and forms a heterooctamer |CITS: [28225088]|. The
                     preferential utilization of glucose over mannitol is 
                     dependent upon this protein complex; dephosphorylated HPr, 
                     which accumulates during glucose transport, interacts 
                     specifically with MtlR and enhances its repressor activity,
                     resulting in the inhibition of mannitol utilization |CITS: 
                     [28225088]|. When HPr is phosphorylated at His15, 
                     electrostatic repulsion between MtlR and HPr causes 
                     disassociation of the MtlR-HPr complex, which induces the 
                     <i>mtl</i> operon |CITS: [31558743]|.
                     The crystal structures of MtlR-HPr have been determined 
                     |CITS: [31558743]|.
                     "
