                     /label=lldP
     CDS             3770759..3772414
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lldP"
                     /locus_tag="b3603"
                     /product="multifunctional lactate/glycolate:H(+) symporter
                     LldP / (S)-lactate transporter / (R)-lactate transporter / 
                     glycolate transporter / (S)-2-hydroxybutanoate transporter"
                     /label=lldP
                     /note="LldP is an inner membrane permease that functions
                     in the the uptake of L-lactate, D-lactate and glycolate 
                     |CITS: [8407843], [11283302], [11785976]|. The <I>lldP</I> 
                     gene is located in a lactate-inducible operon with the 
                     <I>lldD</I> and <i>lldR</I> genes encoding a lactate 
                     dehydrogenase and a regulatory protein, respectively |CITS:
                     [8407843]|.
                     <i>E.coli</i> strains lacking both 2-hydroxymonocarboxylate
                     transporters LldP and |FRAME: B2975-MONOMER ""GlcA""| are 
                     unable to grow with D-lactate, L-lactate or glyoxylate as 
                     sole carbon source. Overexpression of a plasmid carrying 
                     <i>lldP</i> restores growth of the double mutant on all 
                     three substrates |CITS: [11283302], [11785976]|.
                     Transport is abolished by addition of the proton ionophore,
                     carbonyl cyanide <i>m</i>-chlorophenylhydrazone (CCCP) 
                     |CITS: [11785976]|.
                     Disruption of the <I>lld</I> operon decreased lactate 
                     transport, which could be restored by complementation with 
                     the cloned <I>lldP gene</I> |CITS: [8407843]|. Glycolate 
                     does not induce expression of the <i>lld</i> operon - basal
                     expression of <i>lldP</i> is sufficient to transport this 
                     substrate at a rate which supports growth |CITS: 
                     [11283302]|.
                     "
