     gene            3774575..3775048
                     /gene="trmL"
                     /locus_tag="b3606"
                     /label=trmL
     CDS             3774575..3775048
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="trmL"
                     /locus_tag="b3606"
                     /product="multifunctional tRNA
                     (cytidine/uridine-2'-O)-ribose methyltransferase / tRNA 
                     (cytidine-2'-O)-ribose methyltransferase / tRNA 
                     (5-carboxymethylaminomethyluridine)-ribose 
                     methyltransferase / tRNA 
                     (5-methoxycarbonylmethoxyuridine)-ribose methyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.207"
                     /label=trmL
                     /note="TrmL is the methyltransferase responsible for 
                     2'-<i>O</i>-methylation of the wobble nucleotide 34 
                     (cytidine or uridine) ribose in both tRNA<sup>Leu</sup> 
                     isoacceptors |CITS: [20855540]|.  It also 
                     2'-<i>O</i>-methylates a minor fraction of 
                     5-methoxycarbonylmethoxy-modified U34 of |FRAME: serT-tRNA|
                     |CITS: [26681692]|.
                     TrmL showed no methyltransferase activity with certain 
                     synthetic tRNA substrates |CITS: [16848900]|; the enzyme 
                     requires the presence of the ms<sup>2</sup>i<sup>6</sup> 
                     modification at the A37 nucleotide for activity |CITS: 
                     [20855540][23804755]|.  In addition, the A35 nucleotide 
                     functions as an identity element for substrate recognition 
                     by TrmL |CITS: [20855540]|.  TrmL only accepts pyrimidine 
                     nucleotides at the wobble position; additional identity 
                     elements within the anticodon loop were identified |CITS: 
                     [26106808]|.
                     TrmL belongs to the SPOUT superfamily of methyltransferases
                     |CITS: [8265370][11763972]| and is a dimer in solution 
                     |CITS: [16848900][20855540]|.  Crystal structures of the 
                     enzyme have been solved, allowing the identification of the
                     SAM co-substrate binding site and the predicted active 
                     site.  Residues predicted to be involved in tRNA binding 
                     were investigated by site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [23804755]|.
                     A <i>trmL</i> mutant does not have a growth defect in rich 
                     medium.  However, experiments using several cycles of 
                     competitive growth revealed a defect compared to wild type,
                     which is possibly related to recovery from stationary phase
                     |CITS: [20855540]|.
                     TrmL: ""<b>tR</b>NA <b>m</b>ethyltransferase <b>L</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [20855540]|
                     Review: |CITS: [25607529]|"
