     gene            complement(3777551..3777802)
                     /gene="grxC"
                     /locus_tag="b3610"
                     /label=grxC
     CDS             complement(3777551..3777802)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="grxC"
                     /locus_tag="b3610"
                     /product="bifunctional reduced glutaredoxin 3 /
                     glutaredoxin"
                     /EC_number="1.8.4.2"
                     /label=grxC
                     /note="<b>General Information</b><br>Glutaredoxins are 
                     ubiquitous proteins that catalyze the reduction of 
                     disulfides.  Glutaredoxins are similar to thioredoxins; 
                     both are usually small (9-12 kDa), and both are capable of 
                     catalyzing thiol-disulfide exchange reactions. 
                     Representatives of at least one of these two protein 
                     families have been found in all organisms studied, 
                     indicating that proteins of this type are essential for 
                     cellular functions.  A main difference between the two is 
                     the mechanism for their reduction.  Glutaredoxins are 
                     generally reduced by the compound |FRAME: GLUTATHIONE| 
                     (GSH), while thioredoxins are reduced by the specific 
                     flavoenzyme |FRAME: THIOREDOXIN-REDUCT-NADPH-CPLX|.
                     While thioredoxins are found to be general reductants of 
                     protein disulfides, glutaredoxins are better at reducing 
                     mixed disulfides between proteins (or low molecular weight 
                     thiol-containing compounds) and GSH.  The substrate 
                     specificities of glutaredoxins and thioredoxins appear to 
                     depend on geometric and electrostatic complementarity and 
                     long-distance electrostatic interactions between the 
                     redoxin and its target |CITS: [29861944]|.
                     <b>About This Protein</b>
                     Grx3 is one of several glutaredoxins in <i>E. coli</i>. 
                     |FRAME: RED-GLUTAREDOXIN Grx1|, |FRAME: GRXB-MONOMER Grx2| 
                     and |FRAME: GRXC-MONOMER Grx3| all carry the classic 
                     dithiol active site CPYC, while |FRAME: EG12181-MONOMER 
                     Grx4| has a monothiol (CGFS) active site |CITS: 
                     [7937896][8636094][15833738]|.
                     Glutaredoxin 3 has been reported to be able to function as 
                     a disulfide reductase, but not as well as glutaredoxin 1. 
                     Coupled with cellular glutathione it may be the third 
                     hydrogen donor system in the absence of thioredoxin and 
                     glutaredoxin 1.  It was proposed that under normal 
                     conditions it is likely that glutaredoxin 3 has other 
                     functions |CITS: [7937896] [8636094]|.
                     An experiment using the fluorescent disulfide probe |FRAME:
                     CPD-26606| to measure thioredoxin and glutaredoxin reducing
                     activity <i>in vivo</i> concluded that the thioredoxin 
                     system was the predominant disulfide reductase for fast 
                     disulfide reduction in |FRAME: TAX-562| |CITS: [35204259]|.
                     Contrary to these findings, an experiment utilizing a 
                     different probe, |FRAME: CPD-26605|, concluded that Grx2 
                     and Grx3 exhibited higher activity in reducing the 
                     disulfide probe than the other redoxins (Grx1 and 
                     thioredoxins 1 and 2) |CITS: [35107099]|."
