     gene            3780198..3781457
                     /gene="envC"
                     /locus_tag="b3613"
                     /label=envC
     CDS             3780198..3781457
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="envC"
                     /locus_tag="b3613"
                     /product="murein hydrolase activator EnvC"
                     /label=envC
                     /note="EnvC is a divisome associated factor which
                     activates the peptidoglycan (PG) hydrolases |FRAME: 
                     NACMURLALAAMI1-MONOMER ""AmiA""| and |FRAME: 
                     NACMURLALAAMI2-MONOMER ""AmiB""| required for septal 
                     splitting |CITS: [20300061]|. EnvC-mediated amidase 
                     activation is regulated by direct interaction with the 
                     |FRAME: ABC-54-CPLX FtsEX| transmembrane complex (reviewed 
                     in |CITS: [31376483]|). EnvC contains a structural element 
                     - the 'restraining arm' - that is implicated in a 
                     self-inhibition mechanism; FtsEX interaction with EnvC may 
                     drive conformational change that leads to amidase 
                     activation |CITS: [33097670]| (see further |CITS: [ 
                     37276423]|).
                     EnvC is not essential for viability; an <i>envC</i> null 
                     mutant has cell division and cell envelope integrity 
                     defects, an FtsZ ring defect, and is unable to form 
                     colonies at 42<sup>o</sup>C |CITS: 
                     [12113939][11976287][19525345]|. Temperature sensitivity 
                     can be rescued by addition of 5% betaine to the medium 
                     |CITS: [11976287]|. EnvC does not degrade peptidoglycan in 
                     solution |CITS: [20300061]| but does have murein hydrolytic
                     activity in a zymogram assay |CITS: [15165230]| and also 
                     shows weak protease activity toward beta-casein |CITS: 
                     [11976287]|.
                     EnvC was initially shown to localize to the inner membrane 
                     and to the cytoplasm |CITS: [11976287]|; however, a 
                     periplasmic localization was shown elsewhere |CITS: 
                     [12113939]|, and an EnvC-green fluorescent protein fusion, 
                     when exported to the periplasm, localizes to the septal 
                     ring apparatus and functionally complements 
                     EnvC<sup>-</sup> defects |CITS: [15165230]|. An EnvC 
                     mCherry fusion localises to the cell division site |CITS: 
                     [19525345]|.
                     EnvC contains three functional domains: a globular 
                     C-terminal LytM domain with similarity to the M23/M37 
                     peptidase family, a central regulatory domain and an 
                     N-terminal FtsEX-interacting coiled coil domain |CITS: 
                     [12113939][11976287][33097670]|. The coiled-coil domain is 
                     required for recruitment of EnvC to the divisome |CITS: 
                     [20300061]|. EnvC contains a LytM-like (Pfam: peptidase 
                     M23) domain (as do |FRAME: EG12111-MONOMER NlpD|, |FRAME: 
                     G7484-MONOMER YgeR| and |FRAME: EG10013-MONOMER MepA|). An 
                     <i>E. coli</i> strain lacking all LytM containing proteins 
                     shows severe defects in septal peptidoglycan (PG) splitting
                     and forms long cell chains |CITS: [19525345]|. The LytM 
                     domain of EnvC is sufficient for enhancing amidase activity
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [20300061]|. The LytM-like domain 
                     has a protein fold that is similar to other LytM proteins 
                     but the active site region lacks a catalytic metal ion and 
                     a metal binding motif. Site directed mutagenesis indicates 
                     that residues important for function cluster in and around 
                     the 'degenerate' active-site. This is consistent with the 
                     hypothesis that EnvC does not itself function to cleave PG 
                     but acts to activate other PG hydrolases |CITS: 
                     [23796240]|.
                     The original PM61 mutant strain has an H366Y mutation in 
                     EnvC |CITS: [12113939]|. Overproduction of both wild-type 
                     EnvC and the H366Y mutant protein causes a growth defect 
                     and cell lysis |CITS: [12113939]|. An <i>envC</i> mutant 
                     exhibits wild-type sensitivity to sodium dodecyl sulfate, 
                     ethidium bromide, ethylmethane sulfonate, or nalidixic acid
                     |CITS: [11976287]|. The <i>envC</i> gene was also 
                     identified in a screen for synthetic lethality with 
                     <i>minCDE</i>, further arguing for a function of EnvC in 
                     cell division |CITS: [15165230]|.
                     Initial cloning of genes complementing the <i>envC</i> 
                     defect in strain PM61 resulted in a plasmid containing the 
                     <i>acrE</i> and <i>acrF</i> genes |CITS: [1720861]|; this 
                     is now thought to be the result of multicopy suppression of
                     <i>envC<sup>-</sup></i> by <i>acrEF</i> |CITS: [12113939]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [15491352]|"
