     gene            3789339..3790298
                     /gene="waaC"
                     /locus_tag="b3621"
                     /label=waaC
     CDS             3789339..3790298
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="waaC"
                     /locus_tag="b3621"
                     /product="trifunctional ADP-heptose:LPS
                     heptosyltransferase 1 / ADP-heptose:Kdo2-lipid A 
                     heptosyltransferase / ADP-heptose:Kdo2-lipid 
                     IVA-heptosyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.4.99.23"
                     /label=waaC
                     /note="ADP-heptose:LPS heptosyltransferase I (HepI) is the 
                     enzyme responsible for transfer of the first heptose sugar 
                     onto the Kdo<sub>2</sub> moiety of the lipopolysaccharide 
                     inner core |CITS: [9446588][11054112]|.
                     HepI is able to catalyze heptose transfer to underacylated 
                     and fully deacylated Kdo<sub>2</sub>-lipid A analogs; this 
                     activity does not require addition of detergent.  Thus, the
                     enzyme appears to only recognize the Kdo sugar region of 
                     these acceptor molecules |CITS: [22059588]|.
                     Crystal structures of WaaC from the pathogenic strain RS218
                     have been solved, providing insight into the catalytic 
                     mechanism |CITS: [16963083]|.  WaaC is considered to be an 
                     antimicrobial drug target, and small molecule inhibitors 
                     have been identified |CITS: [18571407][22147564]|.
                     A <i>waaC</i> mutant has a defect in the LPS core heptose 
                     region |CITS: [7024253]|, causing a deep-rough phenotype; 
                     the lipopolysaccharide of the mutant strain is heptoseless 
                     and contains 2-keto-3-deoxyoctulosonic acid (Kdo) as its 
                     only core sugar |CITS: [3882666]|.  A &Delta;(<i>waaC</i> 
                     <i>lpxL</i> <i>lpxM</i> <i>lpxP</i>) strain is viable under
                     slow growth conditions in minimal medium at low temperature
                     (23&deg;C) and synthesizes only Kdo<sub>2</sub>-lipid 
                     IV<sub>A</sub>.  Overexpression of <i>msbA</i> partially 
                     rescues the growth defect of that strain.  Lack of 
                     <i>waaC</i> induces the &sigma;<sup>E</sup> 
                     extracytoplasmic stress response |CITS: [19346244]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>waaC</i> leads to formation of 
                     biofilms with abnormal architecture |CITS: [12900028]|. In 
                     <i>waaC</i>, <i>waaE</i>, <i>waaF</i> and <i>waaG</i> 
                     mutants biofilm formation was significantly increased 
                     relative to the parental strain |CITS: [23284671]|.
                     The <i>waaC</i> gene is located in the first of three 
                     operons in the <i>rfa</i> locus |CITS: [1577693]|. In <i>E.
                     coli</i> K-12 W3110 inactivation of both chromosomal 
                     <i>waaC</i> and the heptosyltransferase <i>waaF</i> by 
                     allelic-replacement mutagenesis resulted in Re-LPS with 
                     highly 2-aminoethanol phosphate substituted tetrasaccharide
                     |CITS: [9266718]| (Re-LPS is a rough LPS mutant |CITS: 
                     [15932345]|). In <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strains deficient in 
                     <i>waaC</i> and <i>waaF</i> heterologously expressing 
                     Chlamydial <i>waaA</i>, it was shown that 
                     Kdo-(2->8)-Kdo(2->4), but not Kdo-(2->4)-Kdo(2->4) could 
                     serve as a heptose acceptor for <i>E. coli</i> WaaC |CITS: 
                     [19201821]|.
                     In <i>E. coli</i> K-12 W3110 two <i>waaC</i> mutants were 
                     shown to be resistant to infection by bacteriophage mEp213 
                     |CITS: [23103976]|. Mutant selection on the antibiotic 
                     tigecycline included a mutant in <i>waaC</i> with low-level
                     resistance |CITS: [23843301]|. WaaC was also shown to 
                     directly interact with LapB <i>in vivo</i>, the latter 
                     being a heat shock protein involved in lipopolysaccharide 
                     assembly |CITS: [24722986]|.
                     The chromosomal <i>waa</i> region (formerly <i>rfa</i>) 
                     contains the major core-oligosaccharide assembly operons in
                     <i>E. coli</i>  |CITS: [12045108]||CITS: [17362200]|. The 
                     current nomenclature system was proposed originally in 
                     |CITS: [9004408]| and |CITS: [9791168]| and followed 
                     thereafter.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12045108][9791168][7504166]|"
