     gene            3790308..3791567
                     /gene="waaL"
                     /locus_tag="b3622"
                     /label=waaL
     CDS             3790308..3791567
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="waaL"
                     /locus_tag="b3622"
                     /product="O-antigen ligase"
                     /EC_number="2.4.99.26"
                     /label=waaL
                     /note="In <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655, <i>waaL</i> encodes 
                     O-antigen ligase or perhaps more correctly 'surface 
                     polymer:lipid A-core ligase' (see |CITS: [9157235]|). 
                     Purified WaaL is an ATP hydrolysis-independent, metal 
                     ion-independent inverting glycosyltransferase |CITS: 
                     [21983211][30047569]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strains are phenotypically rough - 
                     their lipopolysaccharide has a complete core structure but 
                     does not have an O-specific polysaccharide (O-PS). <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 strains do not produce O-PS to attach to the 
                     LPS core due to mutations in the <i>rfb</i> gene cluster; 
                     in MG1655, the <i>rfb-50</i> allele, an IS<i>5</i> 
                     insertion, interrupts <i>wbbL</i> encoding rhamnose 
                     transferase; an engineered strain of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     with all <i>rfb</i> genes intact synthesizes O16 antigen 
                     |CITS: [7512872][7517390][7517391]|.
                     WaaL from <i>E. coli</i> K-12 interacts with and 
                     efficiently ligates a reporter O-PS, D-galactan I, to the 
                     K-12 core oligosaccharide molecule |CITS: [9535865]|. WaaL 
                     is also able to attach colanic acid, or M-antigen 
                     (traditionally considered to be an exopolysaccharide) to 
                     the LPS core producing M<sub>LPS</sub> - a novel LPS 
                     glycoform that occurs in a highly mucoid strain of <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [17227761]|. Purified WaaL catalyses 
                     transfer of O-antigen from its isoprenoid carrier to <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 lipid A-core |CITS: [21983211]|. WaaL 
                     displays specificity for the terminal core acceptor site 
                     and lacks specificity for the carrier-linked O-PS; purified
                     WaaL can ligate O-PS substrates from different bacterial 
                     species to <i>E. coli</i> lipid A-core |CITS: [30047569]|; 
                     the GlcNAc-HepIV &beta;(1 &rarr; 7) linkage of the outer 
                     core oligosaccharide defines specificity of WaaL in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [10574995]|.
                     WaaL is predicted to be an inner membrane protein with 12 
                     transmembrane domains; experimental topology analysis 
                     suggests its C terminus is located in the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [15919996]| (see also |CITS: [19019161]|). WaaL contains a 
                     large periplasmic loop which is required for function 
                     |CITS: [19019161]|. WaaL can form oligomers and likely 
                     functions as a dimer <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [30047569]|. 
                     Inactivation of <i>waaL</i> does not generally cause a 
                     detectable morphological phenotype; this is not surprising,
                     because K-12 strains lack O antigen |CITS: [1577693]|. 
                     Inactivation of <i>waaL</i> prevents formation of smooth 
                     LPS in a K-12 strain engineered to produce O-PS |CITS: 
                     [9535865]| (see also |CITS: [21983211]|) and prevents 
                     formation of M<sub>LPS</sub> when colanic acid synthesis is
                     induced |CITS: [17227761]|. &Delta;<i>waaL</i> cells lack 
                     |FRAME: CPD0-2294 lipopolysaccharide-linked enterobacterial
                     common antigen| (ECA<sub>LPS</sub>) |CITS: [34809459]|.
                     waaL (formerly rfaL) was initially implicated as an O-PS 
                     ligase by virtue of a shared synteny between core 
                     oligosaccharide biosynthesis genes in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     and <i>Salmonella typhimurium</i> |CITS: 
                     [1624462][1385388]|
                     Related reviews: |CITS:[12045108][9791168][7504166]|"
