     gene            complement(3791599..3792672)
                     /gene="waaU"
                     /locus_tag="b3623"
                     /label=waaU
     CDS             complement(3791599..3792672)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="waaU"
                     /locus_tag="b3623"
                     /product="bifunctional putative ADP-heptose:LPS
                     heptosyltransferase 4 / ADP-heptose:LPS heptosyltransferase
                     4"
                     /label=waaU
                     /note="WaaU is a putative ADP-heptose:LPS
                     heptosyltransferase which may be responsible for the 
                     attachment of the fourth heptose residue (HepIV) residue to
                     the third glucose (GlcIII) residue in the outer core of 
                     K-12 lipopolysaccharide; WaaU contains 2 
                     glycosyltransferase motifs and shares regions of local 
                     similarity with the |FRAME: EG11189-MONOMER ""WaaC""| and 
                     |FRAME: EG12210 ""WaaF""| heptosyltransferases |CITS: 
                     [9791168][9535865]|.
                     O-antigen cannot be detected in the <i>waaU</i> (formerly 
                     <i>rfaK</i>) knockout mutant 
                     (<i>rfaK2</i>::&Omega;KM<sup>r</sup>) of an <i>E. coli</i> 
                     K-12 strain engineered to express <i>Shigella 
                     dysenteriae</i> O-antigen |CITS: [1385388]|.
                     WaaU is implicated in phage specificity: in contrast to the
                     parental strain, an <i>E. coli</i> K-12 <i>waaU</i> 
                     knockout mutant is sensitive to the rough specific phage 
                     Br2; WaaU may be a transferase which adds <i>N</i>-acetyl 
                     D-glucosamine (GlcNAc) to an inner core heptose |CITS: 
                     [1385388]|.
                     It is also possible that WaaU mediates the non 
                     stoichiometric substitution of HepIV with an 
                     <i>N</i>-acetyl D-glucosamine residue as shown |FRAME: 
                     CPD0-939 ""here""| (see |CITS: [9791168]|). However, later 
                     work |CITS: [10574995]| reported that a single sugar bound 
                     to Und-PP can be translocated across the inner membrane in 
                     a Wzx dependent fashion and that a terminal <i>N</i>-acetyl
                     D-glucosamine residue in the structure of LPS from a 
                     <i>glf</i> mutant of E. coli K12 W3110 is an incomplete 
                     O-antigen donated from |FRAME: 
                     ACETYL-D-GLUCOSAMINYLDIPHOSPHO-UNDECAPRE Und-PP bound 
                     <i>N</i>-acetyl D-glucosamine|. This suggests that terminal
                     non stoichiometric <i>N</i>-acetyl D-glucosamine 
                     substitutions in K-12 LPS are not part of the core molecule
                     but represent an incomplete O-antigen structure (see also 
                     |CITS: [39992125]|).
                     The chromosomal <i>waa</i> region (formerly <i>rfa</i>) 
                     contains the major core-oligosaccharide assembly operons in
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [12045108]||CITS: [17362200]|. The 
                     current nomenclature system was proposed originally in 
                     |CITS: [9004408]| and |CITS: [9791168]| and followed 
                     thereafter.
                     Reviews: |CITS:[12045108],[9791168],[7504166]|"
