     gene            complement(3793627..3794325)
                     /gene="waaY"
                     /locus_tag="b3625"
                     /label=waaY
     CDS             complement(3793627..3794325)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="waaY"
                     /locus_tag="b3625"
                     /product="lipopolysaccharide core heptose (II) kinase"
                     /label=waaY
                     /note="WaaY is a heptose specific lipopolysaccharide (LPS)
                     core kinase which catalyses phosphorylation of the second 
                     heptose residue in the inner core of LPS |CITS: [9756860]|.
                     Non-polar inactivation of <i>waaY</i> in the non K-12 <i>E.
                     coli</i> strain F470 (|FRAME: CPD-21352 ""R1 core type""|) 
                     results in a minor increase in sensitivity to novobiocin 
                     and SDS and the core oligosaccharide of this mutant is not 
                     phosphorylated on heptose II |CITS: [9756860]|. WaaY 
                     activity is dependent on prior WaaP, WaaQ (and possibly 
                     WaaG |CITS: [10986272]|) function; the core oligosaccharide
                     of both <i>waaP</i> and <i>waaQ</i> mutants does not have a
                     phosphoryl substituent on heptose II despite the presence 
                     of functional <i>waaY</i> |CITS: [9756860]|.
                     Inactivation of <i>waaY</i> produces a phenotype of 
                     decreased susceptibility to the human cathelicidin 
                     antimicrobial peptide (LL-37); reduced peptide binding to 
                     the cell surface and a diminished capacity to compromise 
                     the inner membrane were observed |CITS: [26100635]|.
                     In <i>Salmonella typhimurium</i> LT2 a screen for 
                     resistance to the antimicrobial peptides LL-37, CNY100HL 
                     and wheat germ histones yielded a <i>waaY</i> mutant among 
                     others |CITS: [23894360]|.
                     The chromosomal <i>waa</i> region (formerly <i>rfa</i>) 
                     contains the major core-oligosaccharide assembly operons in
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [12045108][17362200]|.  The current 
                     nomenclature system was proposed originally in |CITS: 
                     [9004408]| and |CITS: [9791168]| and followed thereafter.
                     Reviews: |CITS:[12045108][9791168][7504166]|"
