     gene            complement(3794343..3795359)
                     /gene="waaJ"
                     /locus_tag="b3626"
                     /label=waaJ
     CDS             complement(3794343..3795359)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="waaJ"
                     /locus_tag="b3626"
                     /product="bifunctional UDP-glucose:(glucosyl)LPS 
                     alpha-1,2-glucosyltransferase / lipopolysaccharide 
                     glucosyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.4.1.58"
                     /label=waaJ
                     /note="WaaJ (also known as WaaR and formerly RfaJ) is a 
                     UDP-glucose:(glucosyl)LPS &alpha;-1,2-glucosyltransferase 
                     which adds the third glucose (GlcIII) to the second glucose
                     (GlcII) in the outer core of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 LPS |CITS:
                     [10234827][1624461]|.
                     <i>waaJ</i> in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 is also referred to in 
                     the literature by the synonym WaaR. Early studies 
                     characterized the <i>waa</i> (<i>rfa</i>) locus and 
                     identified <i>waaJ</i> in both <i>E. coli</i> and 
                     <i>Salmonella typhimurium</i> |CITS: 
                     [2168379][1938935][1577693]|. Later work renamed 
                     <i>waaJ</i> to <i>waaR</i> in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 to 
                     differentiate its activity (formation of 
                     &alpha;-D-Glc<i>p</i>-(1&rarr;2)-&alpha;-D-Glc<i>p</i> in 
                     the LPS outer core) from that of WaaJ in <i>S. 
                     typhimurium</i> (formation of 
                     &alpha;-D-Glc<i>p</i>-(1&rarr;2)-&alpha;-D-Gal<i>p</i>) 
                     (see |CITS: [9535865][9792656]|).
                     <i>waaJ</i> is predicted to encode a soluble or peripheral 
                     membrane protein; WaaJ activity requires functional 
                     <i>waaB</i>; WaaJ function is essential for Mu phage 
                     sensitivity |CITS: [1624461]|.
                     The substrate donor and acceptor specificities of several 
                     recombinantly produced outer core glycosyltransferases from
                     different <i>E. coli</i> strains have been studied, 
                     including the WaaR UDP-glucose:(glucosyl) LPS 
                     alpha-1,2-glucosyltransferase of the non K-12 <i>E. 
                     coli</i> strain F632 (|FRAME: CPD-21354 ""R2 core type""|).
                     The enzyme belongs to glycosyltransferase CAZy family 8. 
                     Recombinantly produced His<sub>6</sub>-WaaR is 64% 
                     membrane-associated (cited as data not shown) and shows a 
                     preference for its specific donor substrate, but a relaxed 
                     specificity for alternate LPS acceptor substrates and does 
                     not discriminate between 1,3-linked Glc (its native 
                     acceptor) or 1,3-linked Gal |CITS: [17631498]|. Suppression
                     of WaaR activity is implicated in the production of various
                     <i>E. coli</i> LPS glycoforms; &Delta;<i>waaR</i> strains 
                     show enhanced incorporation of a third Kdo residue leading 
                     to accumulation of LPS glycoforms IV and V and implying 
                     that the preferred substrate for incorporation of a third 
                     Kdo is a backbone that lacks GlcIII and HepIV |CITS: 
                     [22021036]| (see also |CITS: [26618164]|).
                     The chromosomal <i>waa</i> region (formerly <i>rfa</i>) 
                     contains the major core-oligosaccharide assembly operons in
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [12045108][17362200]|.  The current 
                     nomenclature system was proposed originally in |CITS: 
                     [9004408]| and |CITS: [9791168]| and followed thereafter.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12045108][9791168][7504166]|"
