     gene            complement(3795399..3796418)
                     /gene="waaO"
                     /locus_tag="b3627"
                     /label=waaO
     CDS             complement(3795399..3796418)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="waaO"
                     /locus_tag="b3627"
                     /product="UDP-D-glucose:(glucosyl)LPS 
                     alpha-1,3-glucosyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.4.1.73"
                     /label=waaO
                     /note="The lipopolysaccharide of <i>E. coli</i> K-12
                     consists of two major components: the hydrophobic lipid A 
                     moiety inserted into the outer membrane and the 
                     phosphorylated core oligosaccharide |CITS: [12045108]|. 
                     WaaO is a nonprocessive &alpha;-1,3 glucosyltransferase 
                     involved in the synthesis of the R core of 
                     lipopolysaccharide |CITS: [9765561]|. WaaO adds the second 
                     glucose (GlcII) to the first glucose (GlcI) of the outer 
                     core of LPS |CITS: [1624461]|.  Activity of WaaO requires a
                     functional <i>waaB</i> gene |CITS:[1624461]|.
                     Note that the <i>E. coli</i> K-12 gene is named <i>waaO</i>
                     but is also referred to in the literature by its synonyms. 
                     In <i>Salmonella typhimurium</i> it is named <i>waaI</i> 
                     (<i>rfaI</i>). The history of this nomenclature is 
                     explained in the comment section of the EcoGene GenePage in
                     the Unification Link.
                     The results of site-directed mutagenesis analysis of WaaO 
                     indicate that Asp-131 and -133 (DXD motif in region I) and 
                     Asp-220 and -222 (DXD motif in region III) may play a 
                     crucial role in the catalytic function of <i>E. coli</i> 
                     K-12 WaaO protein |CITS: [9765561]|.
                     An <i>E. coli</i> K-12 <i>waaO</i> (<i>rfaI</i>) deletion 
                     mutant is more susceptible to the antibiotic ciclopirox 
                     compared to the parent strain |CITS: [23936064]|.
                     WaaO from <i>E. coli</i> K-12 has been cloned, 
                     overexpressed, purified and its activity characterized. The
                     enzyme requires Mg<sup>2+</sup> and utilizes UDP-glucose as
                     sugar donor. In the absence of the lipid acceptor, WaaO can
                     slowly hydrolyze the UDP-glucose sugar donor. It cannot 
                     hydrolyze UDP-galactose. It was also shown <i>in vitro</i> 
                     that the enzyme can add multiple glucosyl residues to 
                     analogs of the WaaG and WaaB acceptors, forming 
                     multiglucosylated products over time |CITS: [24479701]|.
                     In addition to components of the main LPS biosynthetic 
                     pathway, some nonstoichiometric substitutions can be found 
                     in the inner core such as phosphoethanolamine, rhamnose and
                     an additional Kdo, resulting in additional glycoforms. Both
                     <i>waaO</i> and <i>waaB</i> deletion mutants contained LPS 
                     with a Kdo<sub>3</sub>-rhamnose branched tetrasaccharide. A
                     double deletion mutant in these genes could not incorporate
                     the third Kdo residue |CITS: [22021036]|.
                     The chromosomal <i>waa</i> region (formerly <i>rfa</i>) 
                     contains the major core-oligosaccharide assembly operons in
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [12045108][17362200]|.  The current 
                     gene nomenclature system was proposed originally in |CITS: 
                     [9004408]| and |CITS: [9791168]| and followed thereafter.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12045108][9791168][7504166]|"
