     gene            complement(3798513..3799310)
                     /gene="waaP"
                     /locus_tag="b3630"
                     /label=waaP
     CDS             complement(3798513..3799310)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="waaP"
                     /locus_tag="b3630"
                     /product="lipopolysaccharide core heptose (I) kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.235"
                     /label=waaP
                     /note="WaaP is a heptose specific lipopolysaccharide (LPS)
                     core kinase; it is required for the addition of phosphate 
                     to O-4 of the first heptose residue in the core 
                     oligosaccharide of LPS |CITS: 
                     [1348243][9756860][11069912]|.
                     <i>waaP</i> is highly conserved in <i>E. coli</i> strains 
                     with K-12, R1, R2, R3 and R4 core types and between <i>E. 
                     coli</i> and <i>Salmonella typhimurium</i> |CITS: 
                     [9756860]|. Non-polar inactivation of <i>waaP</i> in the 
                     non K-12 <i>E. coli</i> strain F470 (|FRAME: CPD-21352 ""R1
                     core type""|) results in hypersensitivity to novobiocin and
                     SDS; the core oligosaccharide of this mutant (which 
                     contains functional <i>waaQ</i> and <i>waaY</i>) lacks all 
                     phosphoryl substituents and a heptose III residue 
                     indicating that WaaP function is a prerequisite for |FRAME:
                     EG11341-MONOMER ""WaaQ""| and |FRAME: EG11425-MONOMER 
                     ""WaaY""| activity |CITS: [9756860]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [1348243]|). Non-polar inactivation of |FRAME: EG11339 
                     ""<i>waaG</i>""| (encoding LPS glucosyltransferase) in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> strain F470 also decreases core 
                     phosphorylation suggesting that WaaP activity may be 
                     partially dependent on WaaG |CITS: [10986272]|. Mutants 
                     lacking WaaP exhibit a deep rough phenotype but do not have
                     altered outer membrane profiles |CITS: [10986272]|.
                     Purified WaaP from E. coli strain F470 catalyses the 
                     addition of labeled phosphate from 
                     [&gamma;<sup>33</sup>P]ATP] to an LPS acceptor; the enzyme 
                     requires Mg2+ for activity and has a pH optimum between 8.0
                     and 9.0 |CITS: [11069912]|.
                     Inactivation of <i>waaP</i> suppresses the lethality of a 
                     &Delta;<i>seqA</i>/<i>recA629</i>(Cs) double mutation 
                     |CITS: [19432803]|.
                     The chromosomal <i>waa</i> region (formerly <i>rfa</i>) 
                     contains the major core-oligosaccharide assembly operons in
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [12045108][17362200]|. The current 
                     nomenclature system was proposed originally in |CITS: 
                     [9004408]| and |CITS: [9791168]| and followed thereafter.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12045108][9791168][7504166]|"
