     gene            complement(3799303..3800427)
                     /gene="waaG"
                     /locus_tag="b3631"
                     /label=waaG
     CDS             complement(3799303..3800427)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="waaG"
                     /locus_tag="b3631"
                     /product="bifunctional lipopolysaccharide
                     glucosyltransferase I / lipopolysaccharide 
                     glucosyltransferase"
                     /label=waaG
                     /note="The lipopolysaccharide of <i>E. coli</i> K-12
                     consists of two major components: the hydrophobic lipid A 
                     moiety inserted into the outer membrane and the 
                     phosphorylated core oligosaccharide |CITS: [12045108]|. 
                     UDP-glucose:(heptosyl)lipopolysaccharide 
                     &alpha;-1,3-glucosyltransferase (WaaG) adds the first 
                     glucose (GlcI) of the outer core of LPS from UDP-glucose to
                     the HepII residue of the inner core |CITS: 
                     [368061][1348243]|. WaaG is a glycosyltransferase family 4 
                     (GT4) enzyme.
                     Early studies characterized the <i>rfa</i> locus and 
                     identified the <i>waaG</i> gene (formerly <i>rfaG</i>) 
                     |CITS: [2168379][1938935][1732225][1577693]|. 
                     <i>waaG</i><sup>+</sup> restores flagella and pili to a 
                     <i>waaGPSBI</i> deletion mutant |CITS: [1348243]|. Mutation
                     of <i>waaG</i> reduces phosphorylation of the inner core 
                     heptoses HepI and HepII |CITS: [10986272]|. An <i>E. 
                     coli</i> DH5&alpha; <i>waaG</i>::Tn5 mutant has a 
                     hypervesiculation phenotype |CITS: [16855227]|.
                     Studies of <i>waaG</i> mutants showed altered behavior in 
                     biofilms |CITS: [16321928][10398745]| and demonstrated an 
                     effect of LPS core synthesis on fimbrial biogenesis |CITS: 
                     [7988890]|. In <i>waaC</i>, <i>waaE</i>, <i>waaF</i> and 
                     <i>waaG</i> mutants biofilm formation was significantly 
                     increased relative to the parental strain |CITS: 
                     [23284671]|. Mutants in <i>waaG</i> from strain Nissle1917 
                     showed impaired colonization of mouse gut and increased 
                     susceptibility to bile acids |CITS: [15727837]|.
                     Crystal structures of WaaG have been solved, providing 
                     insight into binding of the nucleotide-sugar substrate at 
                     the active site. WaaG is a retaining glycosyltransferase, 
                     catalyzing a reaction in which the &alpha;-stereochemistry 
                     of the donor glucose residue is retained in the product 
                     |CITS: [17113996]|.
                     NMR spectroscopy and molecular dynamics simulations of 
                     substrate binding have been performed. These techniques 
                     were used to investigate the binding of natural substrates,
                     enzymatic activity, and donor substrate selectivity. 
                     Molecular docking studies identified ligands that compete 
                     with UDP-&alpha;-D-glucose for binding and can serve as 
                     scaffolds for further inhibitor design |CITS: [22711644]|. 
                     A 30-residue peptide fragment of WaaG was identified as a 
                     putative membrane-interacting region. Its interaction with 
                     lipid bilayers was analyzed by biophysical and NMR 
                     techniques (PDB solution structure 2N58) and its role in 
                     the anchoring of WaaG to the inner membrane was proposed 
                     |CITS: [26244737]|. Point mutation studies found that lipid
                     substrates interact with a loop region in the N-terminal 
                     domain, that UDP and UDP-glucose substrates cause 
                     conformational changes, and that the binding of lipid and 
                     substrate are coupled. Additionally, the UDP product of 
                     UDP-glucose hydrolysis was found to have an affinity for 
                     WaaG and can act as an inhibitor |CITS: [37558175]|.
                     WaaG from <i>E. coli</i> K-12 has been cloned, 
                     overexpressed and purified. For the <i>in vitro</i> assay 
                     an inner core analog 
                     [<sup>32</sup>P]heptose<sub>2</sub>-1-dephospho 
                     Kdo<sub>2</sub>-lipid A was prepared and used as the 
                     acceptor substrate. Several nucleotide sugar donors were 
                     tested and the reaction was shown to be specific for 
                     UDP-glucose as sugar donor. Triton X-100 at concentrations 
                     of 0-0.2% increased the activity although higher 
                     concentrations were inhibitory. Enzymatic activity 
                     decreased in the presence of a number of divalent cations. 
                     Similar studies were performed for WaaB and WaaO from this 
                     organism |CITS: [24479701]|.
                     In the absence of the lipid acceptor, WaaG can slowly 
                     hydrolyze the UDP-glucose sugar donor. It was not able to 
                     hydrolyze UDP-galactose |CITS: [24479701]|.
                     The chromosomal <i>waa</i> region (formerly <i>rfa</i>) 
                     contains the major core-oligosaccharide assembly operons in
                     <i>E. coli</i>  |CITS: [12045108]||CITS: [17362200]|. The 
                     current nomenclature system was proposed originally in 
                     |CITS: [9004408]| and |CITS: [9791168]| and followed 
                     thereafter.
                     WaaG is considered a drug target, since its inactivation 
                     would lead to compromising the integrity of the outer 
                     membrane, making the bacterium susceptible to antibiotics 
                     that normally cannot enter the cell. A preliminary study 
                     looking for fragment molecules that act as inhibitors has 
                     been published |CITS: [35215321]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12045108][9791168][7504166]|"
