     gene            complement(3800424..3801458)
                     /gene="waaQ"
                     /locus_tag="b3632"
                     /label=waaQ
     CDS             complement(3800424..3801458)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="waaQ"
                     /locus_tag="b3632"
                     /product="bifunctional lipopolysaccharide core
                     heptosyltransferase 3 / lipopolysaccharide core 
                     heptosyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.4.99.25"
                     /label=waaQ
                     /note="WaaQ is a heptosyltransferase that is responsible
                     for addition of the side-branch heptose of the inner core 
                     of LPS |CITS: [9756860]|. It catalyzes the transfer of 
                     L-<i>glycero</i>-D-<i>manno</i>-heptose to the second Hep 
                     of Hep<sub>2</sub>-Kdo<sub>2</sub>-Lipid A via an 
                     &alpha;(1&rarr;7) glycosidic linkage |CITS: [25957775]|.
                     A non-polar <i>waaQ</i> mutant lacks the HepIII sugar of 
                     the inner core region of LPS |CITS: [7517388]| as well as 
                     phosphorylation at HepII |CITS: [23372159]|. WaaP activity 
                     (phosphorylation at HepI) is a prerequisite for the 
                     efficient addition of the HepIII residue to the inner core 
                     by WaaQ |CITS: [9756860]|. A <i>waaQ</i> transposon 
                     insertion mutant was shown to grow in mouse cecal mucus, 
                     but cell clumping appeared to prevent it from colonizing 
                     mouse large intestine. The mutant was also hypersensitive 
                     to sodium dodecyl sulfate, bile salts and novobiocin, as 
                     expected for deep-rough mutants |CITS: [12654836]|. 
                     Transposon insertion mutants in <i>waaQ</i> showed 
                     increased tolerance to lactoperoxidase |CITS: [16000755]| 
                     and more sensitivity than wild-type to the antibacterial 
                     plant-derived compound thymol |CITS: [17949301]|. In the 
                     facultative pathogen <i>E. coli</i> KT1094 serotype K1, a 
                     nonpolar <i>waaQ</i> mutant resulted in a reduction in 
                     cell-bound K1 polysaccharide |CITS: [22522903]|.
                     A <i>waaQ</i> nonsense mutation contributes to colistin 
                     resistance in a laboratory evolved strain |CITS: 
                     [35680894]|
                     The chromosomal <i>waa</i> region (formerly <i>rfa</i>) 
                     contains the major core-oligosaccharide assembly operons in
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [1732225][12045108][17362200]|. The 
                     current nomenclature system was proposed originally in 
                     |CITS: [9004408]| and |CITS: [9791168]| and followed 
                     thereafter.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17362200][12045108][9791168][7504166]|"
