     gene            3803185..3803664
                     /gene="coaD"
                     /locus_tag="b3634"
                     /label=coaD
     CDS             3803185..3803664
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="coaD"
                     /locus_tag="b3634"
                     /product="pantetheine-phosphate adenylyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.3"
                     /label=coaD
                     /note="Phosphopantetheine adenylyltransferase (PPAT)
                     catalyzes the transfer of an adenyly group from ATP to 
                     4'-phosphopantetheine |CITS: [10480925]|.  This is the 
                     penultimate reaction and a secondary rate-limiting step in 
                     CoA biosynthesis |CITS: [6370952]|.
                     Coenzyme A binds to the enzyme, but did not appear to 
                     influence catalytic activity of the reverse reaction |CITS:
                     [10480925]|.  However, CoA is a competitive inhibitor of 
                     the forward reaction |CITS: [17873050]|.
                     The enzyme is homohexameric in solution, behaving as a 
                     dimer of trimers |CITS: [10480925]|.  Crystal and solution 
                     structures of PPAT have been solved, and the catalytic 
                     mechanism and substrate interactions have been 
                     characterized |CITS: 
                     [10205156][10329792][11812124][12837781][27130242]|.  The 
                     reverse reaction appears to proceed via a ternary complex 
                     mechanism |CITS: [10480925]|; product inhibition studies of
                     the forward reaction are consistent with a random bi-bi 
                     kinetic mechanism |CITS: [17873050]|.  The functional 
                     landscape of PPAT was investigated by broad mutational 
                     scanning, a technique whereby homologs from hundreds of 
                     different organisms are evaluated for the ability to 
                     complement an <i>E. coli</i> mutant |CITS: [29301959]|. 
                     <i>coaD</i> is essential for growth in complex media |CITS:
                     [11361082][12142426]|.  CoaD is not similar to the 
                     mammalian PPAT and is therefore a potential antimicrobial 
                     drug target; inhibitors of the enzyme have been found and 
                     tested |CITS: [12750020]|.
                     "
