     gene            complement(3803703..3804512)
                     /gene="mutM"
                     /locus_tag="b3635"
                     /label=mutM
     CDS             complement(3803703..3804512)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mutM"
                     /locus_tag="b3635"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA-formamidopyrimidine glycosylase
                     / DNA-5'-terminal deoxyribophosphodiesterase"
                     /EC_number="3.2.2.23"
                     /label=mutM
                     /note="MutM (also known as Fpg or formamidopyrimidine 
                     glycosylase) is a bifunctional DNA glycosylase which 
                     cleaves the N-glycosidic bond of redox-damaged purines and 
                     incises the phosphodiester backbone to yield single strand 
                     breaks with 3' and 5'-phosphoryl ends; the reaction 
                     proceeds via formation of an enzyme-substrate Schiff base 
                     intermediate with subsequent &beta;,&delta;-elimination 
                     reactions (see |CITS: [15102448]|). The glycosylase 
                     activity of MutM generates blocked 3' termini that are then
                     processed by one of two AP endonucleases (|FRAME: 
                     EG11073-MONOMER ""Exonuclease III""| and |FRAME: 
                     EG10651-MONOMER ""Endonuclease IV""|) to generate the 3' OH
                     ends that are required for DNA polymerase repair synthesis 
                     (see |CITS: [10946230]|). MutM is one of three enzymes of 
                     the 'GO system' which counters the incorporation and 
                     accumulation of 8-oxoguanine in the genome (see |CITS: 
                     [1328155][11554297]|).
                     Purified MutM excises the secondary alkylation product of 
                     7-methylguanine (that is, 
                     2,6-diamino-4-hydroxy-5-N-methyl-formamidopyrimidine or 
                     Fapy-7MeG) |CITS: [3319582][2651883]|. Purified MutM is a 
                     monomeric zinc-binding protein which has 
                     formamidopyrimidine-DNA (Fapy-DNA) glycosylase activity and
                     an associated strand nicking activity 
                     |CITS:[2664776][1689309]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [8253809][8473347]|). MutM nicks both phosphodiester bonds 
                     3' and 5' of the lesion site leaving a gap with 
                     3'-phosphate and 5'-phosphate ends |CITS 
                     [2679549][8555240]|. MutM also catalyzes the excision of 
                     5'-terminal deoxyribose phosphate (dRp) from abasic DNA 
                     releasing an unsaturated dRp |CITS:[1378443]|.
                     MutM is active on the mutagenic lesion 
                     7,8-dihydro-8-oxoguanine (8-oxoG) and this lesion, which is
                     readily generated by endogenous oxidative processes, may 
                     represent the physiological substrate of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [2052552][1649454]|. MutM has a preference for removal of 
                     8-oxoG from 8-oxoG:C over 8-oxoG:A and this preference is 
                     important for proper functioning of the GO system |CITS: 
                     [7515054][17209553]| (and see |CITS: [15939442]|). MutM 
                     excises 8-oxoguanine lesions in both double-stranded and 
                     single-stranded DNA |CITS: [9159874][10683766]|. Purified 
                     MutM excises 2,6-diamino-4-hydroxy-5-formamidopyrimidine 
                     (FapyG), 4,6-diamino-5-formamidopyrimidine (FapyA) and 
                     8-oxoG lesions generated in DNA by photosensitization or 
                     &gamma;-irradiation |CITS: [1731864]|. Purified MutM 
                     removes the pyrimidine base lesions, 5-hydroxycytosine 
                     (5-OHC) and 5-hydroxyuracil (5-OHU) |CITS: 
                     [8034633][10079077]|. Purified MutM excises the 
                     DNA-hydantoin lesions, spiroiminodihydantoin (Sp) and 
                     guanidinohydantoin (Gh), which result from the further 
                     oxidation of  8-oxoguanine lesions |CITS: 
                     [11101315][11328881][17655276]|.
                     Analysis of the repair of oxidative DNA damage induced in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> cells treated with hydrogen peroxide 
                     suggests that MutM is the primary DNA glycosylase that 
                     removes oxidised purines and some oxidized pyrimidines 
                     (5-OHC and 5-OHU) <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [21601577]|. 
                     Purified MutM is able to cleave 8-oxoguanine and 
                     5,6-dihydrouracil containing single strand and 'bubble' DNA
                     substrates (dsDNA with several consecutive mismatched 
                     nucleotides); the enzyme may be active in the repair of 
                     lesions in transcription intermediates |CITS: [31784740]|. 
                     Purified MutM does not excise 8-oxoG from 8-oxoG:|FRAME: 
                     CPD0-2060 Z| mispairs; the GO system does not efficiently 
                     repair oxidative DNA damage when |FRAME: CPD0-2060 Z base| 
                     is present in DNA  |CITS: [38042029]|.
                     The molecular mechanisms for lesion recognition, formation 
                     of a productive complex and lesion excision have been 
                     widely studied and elucidated; the studies have used MutM 
                     from a variety of bacteria including <i>Geobacillus 
                     stearothermophilus</i> |CITS: [12055620][16497933][2001068 
                     1][22219368][23404556][26553802]| and <i>E. coli</i> |CITS:
                     [1741272][9030608][9862987][2166 
                     6255][24799677][25520195][26392572]| The ability of MutM to
                     increase the capacity of mammalian cells to repair 
                     oxidative DNA damage has been studied (see |CITS: 
                     [16217765][20026203][24498234]| and a review by |CITS: 
                     [16432839]|)
                     <i>mutM</i>: <U>mut</U>ator locus M; the <i>mutM</i> 
                     mutator strain reverts Cupples strain CC104 to 
                     Lac<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [3053667]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [11554299][19751748]|<BR>Related reviews: 
                     |CITS: [12893082][20178755][27903453]|"
