     gene            3806091..3807311
                     /gene="dfp"
                     /locus_tag="b3639"
                     /label=dfp
     CDS             3806091..3807311
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dfp"
                     /locus_tag="b3639"
                     /product="trifunctional fused
                     4'-phosphopantothenoylcysteine decarboxylase and 
                     phosphopantothenoylcysteine synthetase / 
                     phosphopantothenoylcysteine synthetase / 
                     4'-phosphopantothenoylcysteine decarboxylase"
                     /EC_number="6.3.2.5; 4.1.1.36"
                     /label=dfp
                     /note="The <i>dfp</i> (<i>coaBC</i>) gene encodes an
                     enzyme with two domains, each catalyzing one of two 
                     sequential reactions in the coenzyme A biosynthetic pathway
                     |CITS: [10922366][11278255][11358972]|.
                     The C-terminal (""CoaB"") domain of the protein confers 
                     phosphopantothenoylcysteine synthetase activity |CITS: 
                     [12140293]|.  When expressed separately, the CoaB domain 
                     forms dimers in solution.  Point mutants in conserved 
                     residues have been generated and analyzed, and the 
                     existence of a 4'-phosphopantothenoyl-CMP intermediate of 
                     the two half reactions has been confirmed |CITS: 
                     [12140293][14686929]|.  Crystal structures of this domain 
                     have been solved, and a reaction mechanism has been 
                     proposed |CITS: [15530362]|.  Small molecule inhibitors 
                     have been designed and tested |CITS: [19902973]|.
                     The N-terminal (""CoaC"") domain of the protein binds FMN 
                     and confers 4'-phosphopantothenoylcysteine decarboxylase 
                     activity |CITS: [10922366][11358972]|.  The domain can be 
                     expressed separately and forms dodecamers.  A number of 
                     point mutants in conserved residues have been generated and
                     analyzed |CITS: [11358972]|.  A pathway for formation of 
                     the substrate-activating thioaldehyde for the 
                     decarboxylation reaction has been proposed |CITS: 
                     [11427085]|, and the stereochemistry of the reaction has 
                     been studied |CITS: [12565925]|.
                     <i>dfp</i> is an essential gene |CITS: 
                     [12142426][21551303]|.  A temperature-sensitive allele, 
                     <i>dfp-707</i> (initially called <i>dna-707</i>), has been 
                     isolated |CITS: [333440][2999089]|.  The <i>dfp-707</i> 
                     mutation was later shown to result in a F11D change in the 
                     CoaC domain of Dfp, which leads to decreased levels of 
                     soluble protein at 37&deg;C |CITS: [11358972]|.  The 
                     <i>dfp-1</i> allele results in a A275T change in a 
                     conserved motif of the CoaB domain of Dfp |CITS: 
                     [14686929]|.  A &Delta;<i>dfp</i> mutant is viable in the 
                     presence of |FRAME: CPD-16617| |CITS: [21551303]|.
                     Dfp: ""<b>D</b>NA"" and ""<b>f</b>lavo<b>p</b>rotein"" 
                     |CITS: [2999089]|
                     "
