     gene            complement(3809223..3809909)
                     /gene="rph"
                     /locus_tag="b3643"
                     /label=rph
     CDS             complement(3809223..3809909)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rph"
                     /locus_tag="b3643"
                     /product="trifunctional truncated RNase PH /
                     phosphate-dependent exonuclease / 3'-exonuclease"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.56; 3.1.13.4"
                     /label=rph
                     /note="In the <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strains MG1655
                     (represented in EcoCyc) and W3110, the <i>rph</i> gene 
                     contains a frameshift mutation (<i>rph-1</i>) resulting in 
                     a shortened open reading frame. As characterized in strain 
                     W3110, Rph is truncated at the C-terminus and lacks the 
                     weak poly(A) phosphorylase activity associated with RNase 
                     PH. The strains also show a pyrimidine starvation phenotype
                     due to a PyrE deficiency that results from the polar effect
                     of the <i>rph</i> frameshift mutation on the downstream 
                     <i>pyrE</i> gene (|CITS: [8501045]| and discussed in |CITS:
                     [9278503]|).
                     In the absence of the |FRAME: G7459-MONOMER| RppH, the 
                     <i>rph-1</i>-encoded truncated RNase PH inhibits |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-1382|-mediated maturation of certain pre-tRNAs. It is
                     thought that the truncated RNase PH protein creates a 
                     steric hindrance and reduces access of RNase P to the 
                     pre-tRNA |CITS: [28808133]|.
                     <b>Further descriptions of RNase PH here refer to the 
                     wild-type version of the enzyme present in other <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 strains, or to a <i>rph</i><sup>+</sup> 
                     derivative designated MG1655(Seq)* described in |CITS: 
                     [21135037]|.</b> The <i>rph</i> gene was previously named 
                     <i>orfE</i> |CITS: [1885537][1925027]|.
                     Ribonuclease PH (RNase PH) is a divalent cation-dependent 
                     and inorganic phosphate-dependent exoribonuclease that acts
                     in processing of the tRNA 3' terminus |CITS: [2455297]|. 
                     There is also evidence for its role in the processing of 
                     rRNAs, small stable RNAs, tRNAs and mRNA fragments 
                     containing secondary structure |CITS: 
                     [23532845][22609921][9501180][]|. Like polynucleotide 
                     phosphorylase (PNPase), RNase PH catalyzes phosphorolysis 
                     rather than hydrolysis of RNA substrates, yielding 
                     nucleoside diphosphate products |CITS: 
                     [2455297][1512253][1644789]|. The enzyme also has a weak 
                     poly(A) phosphorylase activity relative to PNPase |CITS: 
                     [2455297]|.
                     Recombinant Rph has been overexpressed, purified and its 
                     RNase PH activity characterized. It has helix-turn-helix 
                     sequence motifs and binds nonspecifically to DNA |CITS: 
                     [1885537][1512253][1512252]|. It is a multimeric enzyme of 
                     undefined subunit stoichiometry |CITS: [1512252][1512253]|.
                     Crystal structures of RNase PH from other organisms reveal 
                     a homohexamer |CITS: [12746447][19153445][14767080]|. RNase
                     PH is a member of the PDX family of 3' to 5' 
                     phosphate-dependent exoribonucleases and has homologs in 
                     many other organisms containing RNase PH domains.
                     The enzyme activity has been studied <i>in vitro</i> |CITS:
                     [1512252][1512253]|. The reaction is reversible <i>in 
                     vitro</i>, but probably unidirectional <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [1707683][1512253]|.
                     <i>In vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> evidence for an 
                     interaction between a basic stretch of 80 amino acids in 
                     wild-type RNase PH and an acidic stretch of 16 amino acids 
                     in RNase E has been presented |CITS: [24766456]|.
                     An <i>rph</i> mutation causes synthetic phenotypes, 
                     including synthetic lethality, in combination with various 
                     other RNase mutations |CITS: [1644789]|. Quintuple mutation
                     of exoribonucleases RNase II, D, BN, T, and PH is lethal, 
                     whereas quadruple mutants are viable, but not wild-type 
                     |CITS: [1400219][8422961]|.
                     Mutant and gene expression data suggest that RNase PH and 
                     RNase T are involved in the regulation of tRNA processing 
                     by competing with poly(A) polymerase I for the 3'-ends of 
                     tRNAs |CITS: [22287637][23241393]|. In the case of the 
                     three proline tRNAs, RNase PH is not involved; instead the 
                     endoribonuclease |FRAME: EG10859-MONOMER| appears to be 
                     responsible for their maturation |CITS: [27288443]|. RNase 
                     II, RNase PH and PNPase exonucleolytically remove 3' tRNA 
                     sequences with and without poly(A) tails in the Glu2, Ile1 
                     and Ala1B tRNA processing II pathways following initial 
                     cleavage by |FRAME: CPLX0-3281|. RNase PH also removes the 
                     last 3' nucleotides in these processes |CITS: [35652235]|. 
                     See |FRAME: PWY0-1628| and |FRAME: PWY0-1625| as well as 
                     individual tRNA processing pathways.
                     Double mutation of PNPase and RNase PH causes ribosomal 
                     defects |CITS: [920905]|. In the absence of the 
                     exoribonucleases RNase II, R and PNPase, RNase PH can carry
                     out the 3' processing of 16S rRNA at a slow rate |CITS: 
                     [23532845]| and has a role in the rRNA starvation 
                     degradation pathway |CITS: [21135037][27298395]|. A strain 
                     containing deletions of the RNase PH and RNase II genes 
                     shows accumulation of precursor 23S rRNA and ribosome 
                     assembly defects |CITS: [22190745][23065976]|. Strains with
                     mutations in RNases I, PH and R, or RNases I, II, PH and 
                     PNPase genes show increased sensitivity to the 
                     aminoglycoside antibiotics neomycin and paromomycin that 
                     affect translation and inhibit ribosome subunit assembly 
                     |CITS: [22930249]|.
                     Mutants lacking RNase PH and PNPase accumulate secondary 
                     structure-containing mRNA fragments, and RNase PH can 
                     utilize duplex RNA as a substrate |CITS: [22609921]|.
                     Both RNase PH and PNPase are involved in the stabilization 
                     of Hfq-bound small noncoding RNAs (sRNAs) which regulate 
                     target mRNAs |CITS: [27698082]|. RNase PH may also have a 
                     role in the maturation of small stable RNAs |CITS: 
                     [9501180]|.
                     Regulation of the rph-pyrE operon has been described |CITS:
                     [1375912]|.
                     RNase PH is named for <b>ph</b>osphate-dependent nuclease 
                     |CITS: [2455297]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [11222749][29676246][35787606]|"
