     gene            3811120..3811944
                     /gene="dinD"
                     /locus_tag="b3645"
                     /label=dinD
     CDS             3811120..3811944
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dinD"
                     /locus_tag="b3645"
                     /product="DNA damage-inducible protein D"
                     /label=dinD
                     /note="<I>dinD</I> expression increases in response to
                     agents that induce the SOS response such as the DNA 
                     damaging agent
                     |FRAME: CPD-17518|, and UV treatment |CITS:[6771759]|. 
                     Induction also occurs in response to bile salts 
                     |CITS:[10398829]| and hydrogen peroxide |CITS:[11443091]|. 
                     Its expression fails to respond to DNA damage in 
                     <I>recA</I> and <I>lexA</I> mutants |CITS:[6771759]|. 
                     <I>dinD</I> expression is repressed in uninduced cells by 
                     LexA |CITS:[7029544],[6818350]|. Expression of <I>dinD</I> 
                     is reduced upon photoreactivation after UV induction of the
                     SOS response, identifying the cyclobutane pyrimidine dimer 
                     as the possible primary inducer of SOS response genes upon 
                     UV exposure |CITS:[2991189]|. A specific <I>dinD</I> mutant
                     was shown to have a cold-sensitive phenotype, forming long 
                     filaments with large nucleoid masses in the central region 
                     |CITS:[8002613]|. The mutation also leads to the induction 
                     of the SOS response at low temperature even without DNA 
                     damaging agents |CITS:[8002613]|. This cold-sensitivity is 
                     suppressed in <I>recA</I> and <I>lexA</I> mutants 
                     |CITS:[8002613]|, as well as by multicopy expression of 
                     <I>lexA</I>, <I>dinG</I>, and <I>dinI</I> |CITS:[8682790]|.
                     Deletion of <I>dinD</I> resulted in no discernible 
                     phenotype |CITS:[8002613]|."
