     gene            complement(3812848..3814530)
                     /gene="ligB"
                     /locus_tag="b3647"
                     /label=ligB
     CDS             complement(3812848..3814530)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ligB"
                     /locus_tag="b3647"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA ligase B / DNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.5.1.2"
                     /label=ligB
                     /note="LigB is an NAD<sup>+</sup>-dependent DNA ligase 
                     |CITS:[11812821]|; LigB is implicated in base excision 
                     repair and/or mismatch repair |CITS: [28700629]|.
                     Purified LigB ligates a duplex DNA substrate containing a 
                     single nick; the reaction requires NAD<sup>+</sup> and 
                     divalent cations which can be magnesium, manganese, or less
                     effectively cobalt |CITS:[11812821]|. A ligase-adenylate 
                     intermediate is formed when purified LigB is incubated with
                     NAD<sup>+</sup> ([<sup>32</sup>P-AMP]NAD<sup>+</sup>) and 
                     magnesium |CITS:[11812821]|.
                     LigB has sequence similarity to |FRAME: EG10534-MONOMER 
                     ""LigA""|, the other NAD<sup>+</sup>-dependent DNA ligase 
                     in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 |CITS:[11812821]|. LigB contains the
                     conserved KXDG motif (motif I) which is a signature feature
                     of the ligase/capping superfamily of covalent nucleotidyl 
                     transferases |CITS: [8559059]|; LigB contains a nucleotidyl
                     transferase domain and oligomer-binding fold (OB-fold) but 
                     lacks the BRCT domain and 2 of the 4 zinc-binding cysteines
                     that are characteristic of bacterial NAD<sup>+</sup> 
                     ligases |CITS: [11812821]|.
                     LigB is significantly less active than LigA <i>in 
                     vitro</i>; this difference in activity is due to a 
                     differential response to the NAD<sup>+</sup> cofactor 
                     |CITS: [11812821]|.
                     A K126A mutation eliminates the enzyme activity 
                     |CITS:[11812821]|.
                     <i>ligB</i> maintains a conserved gene neighbourhood 
                     (<i>ligB, gmk, spoT</i> and <i>recG</i>) across 14 
                     representative Enterobacteriaceae genomes |CITS: 
                     [28700629]|.
                     <i> ligB</i> expression alters in response to various cell 
                     stressors (see |CITS: [28700629]| and references within). A
                     &Delta;<i>ligB</i> mutant is more sensitive to hydrogen 
                     peroxide and mitomycin C and deletion of <i>ligB</i> 
                     increases the mutation rate in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655, 
                     delays recovery from cadmium-induced damage and reduces 
                     biofilm formation under static, batch-growth conditions; 
                     LigB influences cell tolerance to certain antibiotics 
                     |CITS: [28700629]|. Overexpression of <i>ligB</i> blocks 
                     DNA replication and kills cells; this phenotype is not 
                     strictly dependent on ligase activity |CITS: [28700629]|. 
                     <I>ligB</I> mRNA transcripts show an unusual decrease in 
                     polyadenylation in a |FRAME: EG10690-MONOMER ""PAP I""| 
                     overexpressing strain |CITS:[17040898]|."
