                     /label=rpoZ
     CDS             3815466..3815741
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpoZ"
                     /locus_tag="b3649"
                     /product="RNA polymerase subunit omega"
                     /label=rpoZ
                     /note="RpoZ copurifies with RNA polymerase and may play a 
                     structural role in that complex.
                     RpoZ copurifies with |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX| and, when added 
                     during renaturation studies, maximizes the yield of 
                     functional polymerase |CITS: [9288911]|. RpoZ binds to 
                     |FRAME: RPOC-MONOMER|, preventing its aggregation during 
                     renaturation and assisting in its addition to the partially
                     formed polymerase complex |CITS: [11531998]|. The 
                     initiating methionine of RpoZ is cleaved |CITS: [9868784]|.
                     Recombinant RpoZ is also cleaved by an endogenous protease,
                     most likely by |FRAME: EG10673-MONOMER| |CITS: [2152188]|.
                     Under exponential growth conditions with an <i>rpoZ</i> 
                     mutant, &sigma;<sup>38</sup> transcription is enhanced in 
                     parallel with overall DNA relaxation. Overproduction of 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> in an <i>rpoZ</i> mutant increased 
                     both overall DNA supercoiling and the transcription of 
                     genes utilizing high levels of negative superhelicity 
                     |CITS: [21810966]|.
                     An increased E&sigma;<sup>38</sup>/E&sigma;<sup>70</sup> 
                     ratio was associated with global relaxation of DNA in an 
                     exponentially growing <i>rpoZ</i> mutant |CITS:[21810966]|.
                     Deletion of the &omega;-subunit alters the &sigma; factor 
                     selectivity of promoter recognition |CITS: [21810966]|. The
                     &omega;-subunit of RNAP optimizes the transcription of 
                     highly negatively supercoiled DNA |CITS:[21810966]|.
                     It is possible that the &omega;-subunit modulates the 
                     relative affinities of &sigma;<sup>38</sup> and 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> for the core polymerase 
                     |CITS:[21810966]|.
                     Couplons analysis of transcriptional profiles with an 
                     <i>rpoZ</i> mutant suggested cross talk between 
                     E&sigma;<sup>38</sup> and <b>n</b>ucleus-<b>a</b>ssociated 
                     <b>p</b>roteins (NAPs) regarding the relaxation of DNA 
                     |CITS:[21810966]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [33238579]|"
