     gene            3815760..3817868
                     /gene="spoT"
                     /locus_tag="b3650"
                     /label=spoT
     CDS             3815760..3817868
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="spoT"
                     /locus_tag="b3650"
                     /product="multifunctional bifunctional (p)ppGpp
                     synthase/hydrolase SpoT / guanosine 3'-diphosphate 
                     5'-triphosphate 3'-diphosphatase / GDP diphosphokinase / 
                     guanosine-3',5'-bis(diphosphate) 3'-diphosphatase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.7.2"
                     /label=spoT
                     /note="SpoT is a key enzyme involved in the stringent
                     response in <i>Escherichia coli</i>. It is a bifunctional 
                     enzyme with both hydrolase and synthetase activities |CITS:
                     [2005135]|. The ppGpp hydrolase activity of SpoT has a 
                     major physiological role in ppGpp degradation |CITS: 
                     [372753]| and is inhibited under conditions of 
                     physiological stress. Its amino acid sequence has been 
                     shown to be extensively related to that of RelA |CITS: 
                     [2542299]|. RelA is involved in the <i>E. coli</i> 
                     stringent response triggered by amino acid starvation, 
                     activating (p)ppGpp synthesis via a ribosomal mechanism 
                     |CITS: [12419222]|. SpoT provides the hydrolase activity 
                     for this RelA-dependent synthetase activity.
                     The physiological role of the RelA-independent, 
                     <i>spoT</i>-encoded synthetase activity is less clear. This
                     activity is also referred to as ppGpp synthetase II or 
                     PSII, whereas the RelA synthetase activity is referred to 
                     as PSI |CITS: [2005135] [8730877]|. PSII activity was 
                     demonstrated in <i>relA</i> null mutants and it was shown 
                     that <i>relA</i>/<i>spoT</i> null mutants did not 
                     synthesize ppGpp |CITS: [2005134]|. The SpoT PSII activity 
                     has been proposed to have a role in maintaining the basal 
                     level of ppGpp during exponential growth. PSII is 
                     relatively inactive during amino acid starvation, is 
                     activated by other sources of stress, and is active during 
                     exponential growth. In contrast, the  RelA-encoded PSI 
                     activity is activated by amino acid starvation and has 
                     little activity during exponential growth |CITS: 
                     [8648647]|.
                     Although incompletely understood, the regulation of ppGpp 
                     levels by the <i>spoT</i>-encoded hydrolase and PSII 
                     synthetase activities involves sensing sources of nutrient 
                     stress other than amino acid starvation. These include a 
                     limitation in fatty acid synthesis |CITS: [17078815]|, 
                     phosphate starvation (which elevates (p)ppGpp levels by a 
                     mechanism involving IraP, RssB and RpoS) |CITS: 
                     [17640895]|, and iron limitation |CITS: [15853883]|. SpoT 
                     PSII activity may be necessary for increasing the ppGpp 
                     levels in these conditions, although how it detects them is
                     still under investigation. In the case of fatty acid 
                     starvation, there is <i>in vivo</i> and <i>in vitro</i> 
                     evidence for a model in which acyl carrier protein 
                     specifically interacts with SpoT and may trigger a SpoT 
                     conformational change leading to (p)ppGpp accumulation. The
                     interaction between ACP and SpoT was demonstrated in 
                     co-purification experiments and in a bacterial two-hybrid 
                     system |CITS: [17078815]|.
                     SpoT has been found to play a role in sensing translational
                     elongation rate, and thus growth rate, under different 
                     growth conditions. In cells with <i>relA</i> deleted, ppGpp
                     synthesis during steady-state growth but not during 
                     transient shifts between different states of the ribosome, 
                     i.e. dwelling and translocating ribosomes, is attributed to
                     SpoT; however, the mechanism of this sensing role is not 
                     known |CITS: [35544692]|.
                     Another interaction has been reported between SpoT and the 
                     50S ribosomal subunit-associated GTPase, CgtA<sub>E</sub> 
                     (ObgE). The two proteins were shown to co-purify. The 
                     possible involvement of CgtA<sub>E</sub> in the control of 
                     SpoT function, or an effect of SpoT on CgtA<sub>E</sub> 
                     activity were suggested |CITS: [15292126]|.
                     In addition to the ppGpp hydrolase and synthetase activity 
                     of SpoT, SpoT also has pppGpp hydrolase activity |CITS: 
                     [365225]| and possibly pppGpp synthetase activity. The 
                     putative pppGpp synthetase activity is based the 
                     observation that in &Delta;<i>relA</i>/&Delta;<i>gpp</i> 
                     mutants starved for glucose pppGpp accumulates, which 
                     suggests phosphoryl group transfer to GTP as well as GDP, 
                     although the phosphoryl donor is not known (cited as 
                     unpublished data in Cashel et. al. (1996) ""The Stringent 
                     Response"", chapter 92, in |CITS: [ColiSalII]|).
                     SpoT has been studied both mutationally and biochemically. 
                     Deletion mutants of <i>spoT</i> were analyzed for their 
                     ability to complement chromosomal mutations defective in 
                     synthetase or hydrolase activities. This allowed mapping of
                     ppGpp hydrolase activity to the first 203 amino acids and 
                     the PSII activity to an overlapping region comprising amino
                     acids 67-374. This suggested separate, but closely linked 
                     catalytic sites in the amino acid sequence |CITS: 
                     [8730877]|. A site-directed mutagenesis study suggested 
                     that Asp293 is required for ppGpp synthetase activity 
                     |CITS: [12596879]|. Proteolysis studies of partially 
                     purified, recombinant SpoT identified two major domains 
                     (Met1-Phe373 and Glu374-Asn702) each containing two minor 
                     sub-domains. <i>In vivo</i> expression studies suggested 
                     that ppGpp synthetic activity was associated with the 
                     N-terminal domain |CITS: [12224635]|.
                     In comparison, proteolysis studies done on the bifunctional
                     Rel/Spo homolog from <i>Streptococcus equisimilis</i> 
                     localized hydrolase and synthase activities to the 
                     N-terminal half of the protein, whereas the C-terminal half
                     appeared to have a regulatory function |CITS: [12003927]|. 
                     The crystal structure of a fragment containing the 
                     catalytic region of this <i>S. equisimilis</i> protein was 
                     determined at 2.1 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [15066282]|.
                     A <i>relA spoT</i> mutant was found to form biofilms with 
                     reduced catalase activity and elevated levels of hydroxyl 
                     radicals; the biofilms of mutants were more sensitive to 
                     the antibiotics ofloxacin and tobramycin than were biofilms
                     formed by wild type |CITS: [22096200]|. SpoT mutant strains
                     with missense mutations in the <i>relA</i> gene showed an 
                     increase in glycogen content and elevated levels of 
                     glycogen gene expression when these strains were grown in 
                     Kornberg medium |CITS: [25188023]|.
                     Mutant strains lacking SpoT functions are incapable of 
                     growth in minimal medium M9 |CITS: [25188023]|.
