     gene            3818570..3820651
                     /gene="recG"
                     /locus_tag="b3652"
                     /label=recG
     CDS             3818570..3820651
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="recG"
                     /locus_tag="b3652"
                     /product="ATP-dependent DNA helicase RecG"
                     /EC_number="5.6.2.4"
                     /label=recG
                     /note="RecG is a DNA helicase involved in double-strand
                     break repair and protecting against aberrant DNA 
                     replication following replication fork collisions.
                     RecG catalyzes branch migration on forked DNA structures 
                     |CITS: [14616075][7957087]|. Studies in which double-strand
                     breaks are induced show that double-strand break repair can
                     proceed via two recombination pathways. Both pathways 
                     require RecBCD and RecA, but one depends on the resolvase 
                     enzyme, RuvABC, while the other relies on RecG.
                     RecG helicase binds the junctions formed as recombinational
                     intermediates (|FRAME: a-DNA-Holliday-junction Holliday 
                     junctions|) and resolves them by promoting branch migration
                     in a process termed ""strand extrusion"" |CITS: 
                     [10585965]|. RecG shows an affinity for three-way junctions
                     and is thought to resolve them by translocation, disrupting
                     RecA-promoted pairing |CITS: [9236123]|.
                     The regulation of <i>recG</i> has been described. 
                     Transcription is induced by the CreBC two component system 
                     in minimal medium growth conditions |CITS: [11350954]|. In 
                     <i>ruv</i> mutants, the RecG junction resolution pathway 
                     can be stimulated by the expression of RusA, a resolvase 
                     whose gene resides on a cryptic prophage |CITS: 
                     [8331065][10585965]|.
                     Mutants lacking RecG function allow constitutive stable DNA
                     replication (cSDR or SDR), i.e. DNA replication in the 
                     absence of protein synthesis. They also exhibit an absolute
                     requirement for Pol I DNA polymerase activity. They are 
                     lethal in combination with mutants in <i>rnhA</i>, another 
                     gene whose absence allows cSDR |CITS: [7774596]|. 
                     <i>recG</i> mutants are somewhat more susceptible to DNA 
                     damage |CITS: [7957087][14616075][36985274]|. In the 
                     specific case of UV irradiation, <i>recG</i> mutants lead 
                     to a PriA-dependent pathological DNA replication cascade, 
                     most likely through repeated generation of new replication 
                     forks |CITS: [19538444]|. <i>&Delta;recG</i> mutants have a
                     high incidence of extra replication initiation in the 
                     terminus area by preventing illegitimate reinitiations 
                     |CITS: [23892781][25368150]|, and this over-replication can
                     occur anywhere replication forks converge |CITS: 
                     [29982635]|. A mutation in RecG decreases the <i>in 
                     vivo</i> mutation rate influencing genetic instability 
                     associated with some G-Quadraplex sequence structures 
                     |CITS: [37761860]|. Double <i>ruvABC recG</i> mutants 
                     exhibited lower survivability, impairment in chromosome 
                     segregation and cell division defects in response to 
                     various agents causing double-stranded DNA breaks; some of 
                     the responses were mitigated by recB |CITS: [36985274]|.
                     A study of the antimutagenic effect of |FRAME: 
                     Abscisic-Acid| has found that exposure to this compound 
                     results in significant (19.1-fold) up-regulation of RecG. 
                     While the antimutagenic effect of |FRAME: Abscisic-Acid| on
                     wild type cells exposed to |FRAME: CPD-26608| (EMS) was 
                     ~78%, it was only 9% in a &Delta;<i>recG</i> mutant, 
                     suggesting that RecG contributes significantly to the 
                     observed antimutagenic effect |CITS: [35874072]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [20093100][25613916][27282357][32351461]|"
