                     /label=yicI
     CDS             complement(3825579..3827897)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yicI"
                     /locus_tag="b3656"
                     /product="trifunctional alpha-D-xyloside xylohydrolase /
                     xylan alpha-xylosidase / isoprimeverose alpha-xylosidase"
                     /EC_number="3.2.1.177"
                     /label=yicI
                     /note="YicI is a member of the glycosyl hydrolase 31
                     family and has &alpha;-xylosidase activity.  The enzyme is 
                     specific for &alpha;-xyloside linkages and is most active 
                     with &alpha;-xylosyl fluoride as the substrate, with a 
                     K<sub>m</sub> of 0.17 mM |CITS: [15294295]|.  YicI 
                     hydrolyzes <i>p</i>-nitrophenyl &alpha;-D-xylopyranoside 
                     and &alpha;-D-xylopyranosyl fluoride rapidly |CITS: 
                     [15501829]|.
                     YicI can also catalyze transxylosylation, providing 
                     information on the substrate interactions at the aglycone 
                     binding site.  The enzyme prefers aldopyranosyl sugars with
                     an equatorial 4-OH as the acceptor substrate |CITS: 
                     [17970751]|.
                     Site-directed mutagenesis, introducing the C307I and F308D 
                     substitutions, changed the substrate specificity of the 
                     enzyme from an &alpha;-xylosidase to an &alpha;-glucosidase
                     |CITS: [16631751]|.
                     Crystal structures of free YicI and a reaction intermediate
                     |CITS: [15501829][16510986]| and with a nonhydrolyzable 
                     substrate analog |CITS: [16478160]| have been solved.
                     <i>yicJI</i> is predicted to be a member of the |FRAME: 
                     EG20253-MONOMER ""XylR""| regulon; putative XylR and CRP 
                     binding sites are identified upstream of <i>yicJI</i> 
                     |CITS: [11750821]|. The <i>yicJI</i> operon appears to be 
                     involved in fitness of the pathogenic <i>E. coli</i> strain
                     BEN2908 |CITS: [21477255]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [22146713]|"
                     /translation="MKISDGNWLIQPGLNLIHPLQVFEVEQQDNEMVVYAAPRDVRERT
