     gene            complement(3839090..3839355)
                     /gene="uhpU"
                     /locus_tag="b4829"
                     /label=uhpU
     misc_RNA        complement(3839090..3839355)
                     /gene="uhpU"
                     /locus_tag="b4829"
                     /product="small RNA UhpU"
                     /note="The Hfq-dependent small RNA UhpU and 111 potential 
                     targets were identified by applying a small RNA prediction 
                     algorithm to RIL-seq data |CITS: [34093460]|.
                     UhpU is most highly expressed during exponential growth and
                     under iron limitation |CITS: [34093460][37843988]|, and its
                     initial transcript undergoes processing |CITS: [37843988]|.
                     Interestingly, UhpU expression is reduced in the presence 
                     of glucose phosphate (G6P), while the processed transcript 
                     is enhanced under this growth condition |CITS: [37843988]|.
                     Computational analysis suggests the presence of strong 
                     stem-loops at the 3A' end of UhpU |CITS: [37843988]|. 
                     Notably, overexpressing UhpU leads to a significant 
                     increase in bacterial motility, despite a slight rise in 
                     the number of flagella |CITS: [37843988]|. RIL-seq analysis
                     highlights a regulatory role of UhpU with potential targets
                     encompassing various cellular processes such as flagella 
                     synthesis, carbon metabolism, and redox homeostasis |CITS: 
                     [37843988]|. Review: |CITS: [32213244]|"
     gene            complement(3839136..3840527)
                     /gene="uhpT"
                     /locus_tag="b3666"
                     /label=uhpT
     CDS             complement(3839136..3840527)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="uhpT"
                     /locus_tag="b3666"
                     /product="multifunctional hexose-6-phosphate:phosphate
                     antiporter / glucose-6-phosphate:phosphate antiporter / 
                     mannose-6-phosphate:phosphate antiporter / 
                     fructose-6-phosphate:phosphate antiporter / 
                     L-glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate:phosphate antiporter"
                     /label=uhpT
                     /note="UhpT is a hexose phosphate transporter that is a
                     member of the Major Facilitator Superfamily (MFS) 
                     |CITS:[8438231]|. UhpT has been purified and reconstituted 
                     into liposomes and demonstrated to transport hexose 
                     6-phosphates via an inorganic phosphate antiport mechanism 
                     |CITS: [3522583]|. The transport system was also shown to 
                     catalyze a reversible phosphate: phosphate exchange |CITS: 
                     [3522583]|. Using phosphate-loaded proteoliposomes, and in 
                     the absence of any imposed cation motive gradient, hexose 
                     6-phosphate accumulated in the proteoliposomes, and the 
                     K<sub>m</sub> for the transport of 
                     2-deoxy-glucose-6-phosphate via UhpT was determined to be 
                     approximately 20 &mu;M |CITS: [3522583]|.
                     Substrate specificity of UhpT has been studied |CITS: 
                     [10037698], [11349129], [15556940]|.
                     Size separation chromatography and reconstitution at low 
                     lipid/protein ratios suggest that UhpT has a monomeric 
                     functional unit |CITS: [2197272]|. Hydropathy analysis and 
                     PhoA fusions suggest that UhpT has a 12 transmembrane 
                     segment topology with the amino and carboxy termini facing 
                     the cytoplasm |CITS:
                     [2156798]|.
                     UhpT and |FRAME: GLPT-MONOMER GlpT| are homologous in <i>E.
                     coli</i> |CITS: [15260472]|. UhpT and GlpT are responsible 
                     for uptake of |FRAME: CPD0-1113| (see |CITS: 
                     [27029300][36009997][37926273]|)."
