                     0008652] [evidence IEA] [GO_REF 0000043]"
                     /go_process="L-valine biosynthetic process [goid 0009099]
                     [evidence IMP] [PMID 17463081]"
     gene            complement(3846478..3846617)
                     /gene="istR"
                     /locus_tag="b4616"
                     /label=istR
     misc_RNA        complement(3846478..3846617)
                     /gene="istR"
                     /locus_tag="b4616"
                     /product="small RNA IstR-2"
                     /note="The IstR-2 RNA is is induced by the DNA damaging
                     agent mitomycin C, and expression may be regulated by LexA.
                     IstR-2 and IstR-1 share a sequence that can base-pair with 
                     a short region of the <i>tisAB</i> mRNA, leading to
                     RNase III-dependent cleavage of the message and thereby 
                     inactivating translation of the toxic TisB peptide. 
                     Induction of the SOS response causes depletion of the 
                     IstR-1 pool and thus causes accumulation of the 
                     <i>tisAB</i>
                     mRNA, the TisB peptide, and slowing growth |CITS: 
                     [15620655]|.
                     An <i>istR-2</i> promoter mutant produces a reduced number 
                     of persister cells that are tolerant to ciprofloxacin, an 
                     antibiotic that causes DNA damage, similar to a <i>tisB</i>
                     mutant.  It is possible that the <i>istR-2</i> promoter 
                     region contains a regulatory site that is essential for 
                     <i>tisB</i> expression |CITS: [20186264]|. IstR: 
                     ""<b>i</b>nhibitor of <b>S</b>OS-induced <b>t</b>oxicity by
                     <b>R</b>NA"" |CITS: [15620655]|"
     misc_RNA        complement(3846478..3846550)
                     /gene="istR"
                     /locus_tag="b4616"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA IstR-1"
                     /note="IstR-1 is part of a Type I toxin-antitoxin system 
                     consisting of a toxic protein and a small RNA antitoxin. It
                     is present throughout growth and regulates translation of 
                     the toxic peptide TisB.
                     The <i>istR</i> locus is transcribed from two promoters, 
                     producing small RNAs of 75 (IstR-1) and 140 (IstR-2) 
                     nucleotides |CITS: [15620655]|.  Initially, both promoters 
                     were assigned separate genes.  Because it is fully 
                     contained within IstR-2, the gene previously encoding 
                     IstR-1, |FRAME: G0-10202|, has been changed to a phantom 
                     gene.
                     The <i>tisAB</i> locus encodes a toxic peptide, TisB.  The 
                     <i>tisAB</i> mRNA first undergoes a processing event that 
                     cleaves 41 nt from the 5' end.  Only this processed mRNA is
                     translatable, and only the TisB open reading frame is 
                     translated.  Binding of IstR-1 to this mRNA inhibits 
                     <i>tisB</i> translation, although the IstR-1 binding site 
                     is distant from the <i>tisB</i> ribosome binding site.
                     The mechanism most likely involves inhibition of ""standby 
                     ribosomes"" |CITS: [17499044]|.  Decreasing IstR-1 levels 
                     are correlated with increasing membrane depolarization 
                     after ciprofloxacin treatment.  Both its 5' UTR (nt 1-41) 
                     and IstR-1 binding contribute to the prevention of 
                     inappropriate TisB expression; a &Delta;1-41 <i>tisB</i> 
                     &Delta;<i>istR</i> double mutant strain shows a high 
                     persistence phenotype |CITS: [27997707]|.  Binding of 
                     IstR-1 also leads to further processing of the <i>tisAB</i>
                     mRNA by RNase III, yielding an inactive mRNA fragment 
                     |CITS: [15620655][17499044]|.
                     Induction of the SOS response causes depletion of the 
                     IstR-1 pool and thus causes accumulation of the 
                     <i>tisAB</i> mRNA and the TisB peptide, thereby slowing 
                     growth |CITS: [15620655]|.  An <i>istR-1</i> deletion 
                     mutant produces an increased number of persister cells that
                     are tolerant to ciprofloxacin, an antibiotic that causes 
                     DNA damage |CITS: [20186264]|.
                     The function of IstR-1 is not dependent on Hfq |CITS: 
                     [17499044]|.  Consistent with this observation, IstR-1 is a
                     very weak competitor for binding of Hfq among a set of 
                     small RNAs |CITS: [25582129]|.
                     IstR: ""<b>i</b>nhibitor of <b>S</b>OS-induced 
                     <b>t</b>oxicity by <b>R</b>NA"" |CITS: [15620655]| Reviews:
                     |CITS: [17376733][19210615][20528688][21342783][2 
                     2060041][23093802][32213244][32849403]|"
