                     /gene="tisB"
                     /locus_tag="b4618"
                     /product="membrane-depolarizing toxin TisB"
                     /label=tisB
                     /note="TisB is the toxic peptide of a Type I
                     toxin-antitoxin (TA) system consisting of a toxic protein 
                     and a small RNA antitoxin encoded by |FRAME: G0-10201|. The
                     TisB/IstR TA system appears to be found only in the 
                     <i>Enterobacter-Escherichia</i> clade and may be a recent 
                     arrival to this clade within the <i>Enterobacterales</i> 
                     order |CITS: [35377167]|.
                     TisB is involved in the formation of persister cells, which
                     show tolerance to multiple antibiotics |CITS: [20186264]|. 
                     TisB may act by forming anion channels in the plasma 
                     membrane, leading to membrane depolarization |CITS: 
                     [22728134][27997707]|, although |CITS: [23062338]| report 
                     only minimal leakage of a dye molecule from lipid vesicles 
                     unless high concentrations of TisB are used. The TisB/IstR 
                     TA system is integrated into the SOS stress response to 
                     major DNA damage by transiently blocking the import of host
                     and/or microbial-produced genotoxic compounds which in turn
                     allows for increased survival in the mammalian gut |CITS: 
                     [35377167]|.
                     TisB is predicted to be a bitopic inner membrane protein 
                     |CITS: [27510400]|. The secondary structure of TisB in 
                     lipid vesicles was investigated by circular dichroism. A 
                     mechanism for membrane insertion was proposed |CITS: 
                     [23062338]|, and a ""charge zipper"" consisting of four 
                     salt bridges was postulated for assembly of an antiparallel
                     dimer in the membrane |CITS: [23062338][23332763]|. TisB 
                     appears to increase the thickness of the lipid bilayer and 
                     the bilayer-bilayer repeat distance |CITS: [27595096]|.
                     A ""<i>tisA</i>"" open reading frame was originally 
                     annotated upstream of <i>tisB</i> |CITS: [15620655]|. The 
                     <i>tisAB</i> mRNA first undergoes a ribonucleolytic 
                     processing event that cleaves 41 nt from the 5' end. Only 
                     this processed mRNA is translatable, and only the TisB open
                     reading frame is translated (referred to as ""+42 
                     <i>tisB</i>""). Binding of the cognate antitoxin small RNA 
                     isoform IstR-1 to this mRNA inhibits <i>tisB</i> 
                     translation, although the IstR-1 binding site is distant 
                     from the <i>tisB</i> ribosome binding site. The mechanism 
                     most likely involves inhibition of ""standby ribosomes"" 
                     |CITS: [17499044]|. The +42 <i>tisB</i> ribosome standby 
                     site has been characterized and consists of both a 5' end 
                     stem-loop structure next to a single-stranded region that 
                     is approximately 100 nt from the ribosome binding site. 
                     Standby-dependent translation initiation requires binding 
                     of |FRAME: EG10900-MONOMER| or the 30S ribosome to the 
                     standby site |CITS: [31320593]|. Point mutations in the 
                     stem-loop structure reduce binding of protein S1, and loss 
                     of binding resulted in no 30S ribosome initiation complex 
                     forming <i>in vitro</i> and alleviated TisB toxicity <i>in 
                     vivo</i>. Compensatory mutations restore the protein S1 
                     binding and TisB toxicity levels, and point to the 5'-end 
                     structure being an RNA pseudoknot |CITS: [33231643]|. 
                     Decreasing IstR-1 levels are correlated with increasing 
                     membrane depolarization after ciprofloxacin treatment. Both
                     the 5' UTR (nt 1-41) and IstR-1 binding contribute to the 
                     prevention of inappropriate TisB expression; a &Delta;1-41 
                     <i>tisB</i> &Delta;<i>istR</i> double mutant strain shows a
                     high persistence phenotype |CITS: [27997707]|. Binding of 
                     IstR-1 also leads to further processing of the <i>tisAB</i>
                     mRNA by RNase III, yielding an inactive mRNA fragment 
                     |CITS: [15620655][17499044]|.
                     A <i>tisB</i> null mutant produces a sharply decreased 
                     number of persister cells that are tolerant to 
                     ciprofloxacin, an antibiotic that causes DNA damage. 
                     Conversely, mild overproduction of TisB induces persister 
                     formation |CITS: [20186264]|. When overexpressed at a 
                     higher level, insertion of TisB into the inner membrane 
                     leads to rapid decrease in ATP concentration and cell death
                     |CITS: [18761622]|. In studies of the response of 
                     <i>tisB</i> null mutant cells to trimethoprim, vancomycin 
                     and colicin M treatments, TisB was found to disrupt the 
                     proton motor force (PMF) through membrane depolarization, 
                     which in turn inhibits or modulates the activity of 
                     PMF-dependent transporters, affecting activities such as 
                     thymine and sugar import and reducing the uptake of toxic 
                     compounds, including minimizing the uptake of colicins when
                     cells are strongly stressed. Competition studies of wild 
                     type and <i>&Delta;tisB</i> strains in mouse gut indicate 
                     that TisB improves <i>E. coli</i>colonization and fitness. 
                     TisB was found to affect the timing of LexA-controlled gene
                     induction but did not affect the DNA repair function of the
                     SOS stress response until cells are exposed to strong 
                     genotoxic stresses such as exposure to colicins |CITS: 
                     [35377167]|.
                     Induction of the SOS response causes increased 
                     transcription of <i>tisAB</i> and depletion of the IstR-1 
                     pool, thus leading to accumulation of the <i>tisAB</i> 
                     mRNA, translation of the TisB peptide, and slowed growth 
                     |CITS: [15620655]|. Deletion of <i>tisB</i> does not affect
                     the induction of the SOS regulon
                     Whole-genome gene expression analysis after induction of 
                     TisB expression shows changes in expression of many stress 
                     response and membrane proteins |CITS: [18710431]|. 
                     Induction of TisB expression leads to growth inhibition and
                     a rapid decrease in plating efficiency |CITS: [18761622]|. 
                     After UV exposure, overexpression of TisB leads to higher 
                     levels of LexA protein, delayed induction of <i>recA</i> 
                     expression, and processing of the <i>uxaA</i> mRNA |CITS: 
                     [18832374]|.
                     TisB: ""<b>t</b>oxicity-<b>i</b>nduced by <b>S</b>OS"" 
                     |CITS: [15620655]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [19210615][20528688][21342783][22060041][2 
                     3093802][32849403]|"
