     gene            3877853..3878212
                     /gene="rnpA"
                     /locus_tag="b3704"
                     /label=rnpA
     CDS             3877853..3878212
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rnpA"
                     /locus_tag="b3704"
                     /product="multifunctional RNase P protein component /
                     RNase P / 3'-ribonuclease / 5'-ribonuclease"
                     /EC_number="3.1.26.5"
                     /label=rnpA
                     /note="RnpA protein and RnpB RNA together comprise the 
                     ribonuclease P (RNase P) holoenzyme |CITS: 
                     [386349][6164392]|. RNase P acts in processing of tRNA 
                     precursor molecules |CITS: [1104100][167017][4560501]| and 
                     the stable 4.5 S RNA precursor |CITS: [794064]|. The RnpB 
                     RNA subunit alone exhibits catalytic activity toward tRNA 
                     precursors <i>in vitro</i> under high ionic strength 
                     conditions, but requires the protein subunit under 
                     physiological conditions and for efficient activity toward 
                     a 4.5 S RNA precursor substrate |CITS: 
                     [6197186][6199841][1717693][16163391]|. The protein subunit
                     stabilizes the global structure of the RNA component, 
                     allowing it to exhibit the properties of the holoenzyme 
                     complex |CITS: [16163391]|. RNase P also exhibits activity 
                     toward C4 repressor RNA of bacteriophages P1 and P7, which 
                     may indicate a role for RNase P in lysogeny/lysis 
                     regulation |CITS: [7597035]|, and toward CI RNA, which is 
                     involved in immunity to bacteriophage P4 |CITS: 
                     [12051840]|. RNase P, and its RNA subunit alone, can cleave
                     a tRNA-like structure in turnip yellow mosaic virus |CITS: 
                     [3359488]|. RNase P can also initiate tRNA precursor 
                     processing at the 3' ends of some long primary transcripts 
                     by endonucleolytic cleavage of the Rho-independent 
                     transcription terminator |CITS: 
                     [25183518][17981836][27288443]|.
                     Activity of the RNase P holoenzyme and RnpB RNA on 
                     precursor tRNA substrates has been extensively 
                     characterized |CITS: [1104100][167017][1099089][1099090][1 
                     103144][2474662][765341][2199767][1701142][6197186][6199841
                     ][3023854][2423112][3359488][2468523][2266141][1718000][306
                     1798][7680114][7535857][7730292][8628683] 
                     [7527466][8341661][8799129][9558342][9571638][9790839][111 
                     70376][11871663][1371349][10339557][10637323][10653690][108
                     43849][11226427][11926814][12904300][19932118][22315063]|. 
                     Substrate interaction and specificity for <i>E. coli</i> 
                     RnpB RNA and holoenzyme have been examined in detail. 
                     Approaches have included: studies using native <i>E. 
                     coli</i> or heterologous precursor tRNA substrates |CITS: 
                     [6380759][770465][6209276][3516691][1371348][2447080][30625
                     78][3276688][1706437][1279179][6351914][16932744][2453026][
                     19906695][23362254][27336323]|; chemical modification of 
                     precursor tRNA substrates and RnpB RNA |CITS: 
                     [1901990][2480641][1707681][8441616][87564 
                     10][7681942][7688143][7521296][7540978][8972769][9860878][9
                     917070][10026268][11345434][11453068][17570824][19793868]|;
                      analysis of mutations in precursor tRNA substrates, RnpB 
                     RNA and the holoenzyme |CITS: [2474662][2843531][3081499][ 
                     3123492][6298744][7680119][7685824][7688247][7525271][77975
                     95][8144499][8634910][8665413][8947568][9558342][9790839][1
                     0652227][10359770][12756331][14510490][17135488][16822954]|
                     ; studies of model substrates for RnpB RNA |CITS: 
                     [2443980][ 
                     2266140][2266141][1379304][1281315][7688454][7694652][86502
                     23][8758997][12457557][12507473][11377419][11454206][127612
                     91][12951523][14510487][14510496][14586127][15477392][15644
                     198][16244456][16332695][17719605][18452950][24097434][1974
                     9247][19008262]|; and tertiary structure modeling |CITS: 
                     [7515186][9174092]|. Additional substrates studied include 
                     a bacteriophage phi80-induced RNA |CITS: [778851]|, the 
                     <i>E. coli</i> <i>btuB</i> encoded coenzyme B12 riboswitch 
                     |CITS: [16061811]|, turnip yellow mosaic virus RNA |CITS: 
                     [3359488]|, and polycistronic tRNA transcripts |CITS: 
                     [17981836][18033800][25183518]|. RnpA also participates in 
                     substrate binding and recognition |CITS: 
                     [19932118][24056935][25273134][27693057]|.
                     The association of RnpB RNA and RnpA protein has been 
                     studied |CITS: [3526344][2459398][2480345][8312258][831225 
                     9][9135114][10026248][10652223][10656815][11557817][1250747
                     1][12466529][16750220][16932744][17652407][19114042][202116
                     79]|. RnpB RNA is important for the folding and stability 
                     of RnpA protein |CITS: [26517763]|. The enzyme complex 
                     exhibits a 1:1 stoichiometry of RnpB RNA to RnpA protein 
                     |CITS: [2459398][8312258][9558342]|.
                     RNase P fractionates with the inner membrane |CITS: 
                     [1722976]|. Overproduced RNase P enzyme is also localized 
                     to the inner membrane, whereas overproduced RnpB RNA is 
                     localized to the cytoplasm |CITS: [1943711]|.
                     Structural properties of RnpA have been investigated |CITS:
                     [9135109]|. The N-terminus plays a role in the catalytic 
                     activity of RNase P |CITS: [10652223]|.  The RNR motif, 
                     centrally located in the RnpA protein, and the C-terminal 
                     region, are both required for interaction with the RnpB RNA
                     |CITS: [10652223]|. RnpA is very basic |CITS: [2415431]|. 
                     The RnpA protein has been crystallized for structural 
                     studies |CITS: [12554950]| and a NMR solution structure is 
                     presented in the PDB link on this page. RnpA may interact 
                     with additional, non-RnpB RNAs |CITS: [12490703]|.
                     An <i>rnpA49</i> mutation results in a recessive heat 
                     sensitivity phenotype |CITS: [6156104]|, a reduced RNase P 
                     <i>K</i><sub>cat</sub> |CITS: [2474662]|, and shows defects
                     in interaction with RnpB RNA |CITS: [2480345]|. An 
                     <i>rnpA49</i> mutant harboring a ColE1-type episome 
                     exhibits buildup of RNA I-derived RNAs |CITS: [1380803]| 
                     and an <i>rnpA49</i> mutant shows defective arginine 
                     tRNA(CCG) processing |CITS: [9891848]|. The <i>rnp-241</i> 
                     mutation also causes heat sensitivity |CITS: [2415431]|. 
                     Multiple ways in which <i>E. coli</i> compensates for the 
                     <i>rnpA49</i> mutant temperature sensitive phenotype have 
                     been studied and involve mutations in |FRAME: CPLX0-2881| 
                     or |FRAME: EG11259-MONOMER| |CITS: [38688559]|.
                     Mutations in RnpA affect RNase P substrate specificity 
                     |CITS: [9135114]|. The large-scale gene expression profile 
                     of an <i>rnpA</i> mutant has been evaluated |CITS: 
                     [12702811]|. Mutations in the RnpA protein subunit and in 
                     RnpB RNA of RNase P exhibit different effects on tRNA 
                     precursor processing |CITS: [2464697]|.
                     The <i>rnpA</i> gene has been identified in a variety of 
                     bacteria including <i>Bacillus subtilis</i> |CITS: 
                     [3004954]|, <i>Borrelia burgdorferi</i> |CITS: [8359672]|, 
                     <i>Synechocystis</i> sp. PCC 6803 |CITS: [8898883]|, 
                     <i>Thermotoga maritima</i> |CITS: [11160919]|, 
                     <i>Mycoplasma capricolum</i> |CITS: [8233831]|, <i>Proteus 
                     mirabilis</i> |CITS: [2172087]|, <i>Micrococcus luteus</i> 
                     |CITS: [2172090]|, <i>Buchnera aphidicola</i> |CITS: 
                     [9516544]|, <i>Streptomyces coelicolor</i> |CITS: 
                     [1577691]|, <i>Streptomyces bikiniensis</i> var. 
                     zorbonensis |CITS: [1379566]|, <i>Mycobacterium leprae</i> 
                     |CITS: [8969512]|, <i>Xanthomonas campestris</i> pv. 
                     campestris 17 |CITS: [12039751]|, <i>Thermus 
                     thermophilus</i> |CITS: [12719542]|, and <i>Thermus 
                     filiformis</i> |CITS: [12719542]|. Evolutionary 
                     implications of the <i>rnpA</i> gene have been considered 
                     |CITS: [1714569]|. Heterologous replacement of RnpA in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> results in a loss of fitness as reflected in
                     growth rate parameters |CITS: [22448220][25266807]|. The 
                     RnpA protein exhibits antigenic similarity to proteins from
                     human cells and from <i>Bacillus subtilis</i> |CITS: 
                     [2479027]|. <i>E. coli</i> <i>rnpA</i> mutant phenotypes 
                     are functionally complemented by RnpA from 
                     <i>Synechocystis</i> sp. PCC 6803 |CITS: [8898883]| or 
                     <i>Streptomyces bikiniensis</i> var. zorbonensis |CITS: 
                     [1379566]|, or by <i>Brevibacterium albidum</i> arginine 
                     tRNA |CITS: [9180701]|.
                     Regulation has been described |CITS: [2415431][1397490]|."
