     gene            3878436..3880082
                     /gene="yidC"
                     /locus_tag="b3705"
                     /label=yidC
     CDS             3878436..3880082
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yidC"
                     /locus_tag="b3705"
                     /product="bifunctional membrane protein insertase YidC /
                     Sec Holo-Translocon"
                     /label=yidC
                     /note="YidC mediates membrane insertion/assembly of inner 
                     membrane proteins |CITS: [10675323][10913619][10949305][11 
                     415986][12107184][12724529][12950181]|. YidC associates 
                     with both the |FRAME: SECE-G-Y-CPLX ""SecYEG translocon""| 
                     and the |FRAME: SECD-SECF-YAJC-YIDC-CPLX ""SecDF-YajC 
                     accessory complex""| |CITS: [12068816]| and is part of the 
                     multisubunit Sec 'holo-translocon' (HTL) |CITS: 
                     [24550475][27435098][27924919][31053257]| that mediates the
                     transport of nascent polypeptides across, or their 
                     integration into, the cytoplasmic membrane. In addition to 
                     its Sec associated function YidC can also insert some 
                     membrane proteins such as |FRAME: CPLX-166 ""MtlA""|, 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-7627 ""MscL""|, the |FRAME: ATPE-MONOMER 
                     ""F<sub>0</sub>-c""| subunit of ATP synthase, and some 
                     phage proteins, independently |CITS: 
                     [10949305][11751917][15096523][17113597][22160593]| (and 
                     see |CITS: [39329451]|).
                     Purified, reconstituted YidC mediates the 
                     post-translational insertion of F<sub>0</sub>-c. YidC is 
                     required for F<sub>0</sub>-c to adopt its correct 
                     oligomerisation state |CITS: [23872420]|. YidC suppresses 
                     misfolding and assists folding of the |FRAME: LACY-MONOMER 
                     ""LacY""| transporter in <i>in vitro</i> folding 
                     experiments; YidC and SecYEG insert and fold LacY both 
                     alone and cooperatively |CITS: [27595331][30801000]| (and 
                     see further |CITS: [37708891]|). YidC mediates 
                     posttranslational insertion of the C-terminal transmembrane
                     helices (cTMs) of multispanning membrane proteins |CITS: 
                     [39587101]|. YidC mediates stepwise insertion of MscL by a 
                     non-strict co-translational mechanism |CITS: [40058572]|.
                     YidC and SecYEG are implicated as receptors in an mRNA 
                     targeting pathway that acts in parallel to the canonical 
                     SRP-dependent pathway for membrane protein insertion |CITS:
                     [36842086]|.
                     YidC is essential |CITS: [10949305]|. YidC depletion causes
                     induction of the Psp stress response |CITS: 
                     [12724529][14594846]| resulting from defects in respiratory
                     chain complex biogenesis |CITS: [12724529]|. YidC function 
                     is implicated in the biogenesis of integral membrane 
                     subunits of anaerobic respiratory complexes |CITS: 
                     [18635537]|.
                     YidC consists of an N-terminal signal-anchor sequence (TM1)
                     followed by a large periplasmic domain, and five 
                     additional, closely spaced C-terminal transmembrane regions
                     |CITS: [9804807][18234665]|. The crystal structure of YidC 
                     comprises a core transmembrane region (TM2-6) containing a 
                     functionally important hydrophilic groove that is common to
                     YidC proteins, plus protruding periplasmic and cytoplasmic 
                     regions |CITS: [25466392]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [28844594][30241934]|).
                     Microscopy of 2D crystals suggests that full-length 
                     membrane bound YidC is dimeric |CITS: [18054957]|. The 
                     cryo-EM structure of YidC in complex with a translating 
                     ribosome suggests that the active form of the insertase is 
                     dimeric |CITS: [19450532]|. Momomeric YidC represents the 
                     functional unit <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [23933010]|. Protein
                     purification and cross-linking studies indicate that YidC 
                     is physically and functionally connected to |FRAME: 
                     EG11506-MONOMER ""FtsH""| and that this complex may be 
                     involved in quality control of inner membrane proteins 
                     (IMPs) during biogenesis |CITS: [18387365]|. The C-terminus
                     of YidC can interact with the ribosome, the |FRAME: 
                     SRP-CPLX| (SRP) and |FRAME: EG10346-MONOMER ""FtsY""| 
                     |CITS: [22160593]|. <i>E. coli</i> ribosomes do not 
                     associate with YidC unless they are synthesizing YidC 
                     substrates |CITS: [23933010]|. A molecular model of 
                     ribosome-bound YidC during co-translational insertion of 
                     the substrate F<sub>0</sub>-c has been determined |CITS: 
                     [25012291]|.
                     Purified, reconsitituted YidC forms ion-conducting channels
                     when activated by ribosomes; a YidC dimer is likely the 
                     channel-forming entity |CITS: [38136645]|. Defects of an 
                     <i>E. coli</i> yidC mutant are functionally complemented by
                     production of chloroplast Alb3 |CITS: [11891220]|.
                     YidC localizes to the inner membrane, particularly at the 
                     cell poles |CITS: [11821429]|. YidC membrane insertion has 
                     been described |CITS: [11777926][11821429]|.
                     A potential RNA G-quadruplex structure, formed by 
                     guanine-rich sequences is located in the coding sequence 
                     region of the gene; this structure could regulate the 
                     expression of the gene, as observed for <i>hemL</i> |CITS: 
                     [31964733]|.
                     YidC is a bacterial member of the Oxa1 superfamily of 
                     membrane protein insertases; homologs of YidC operate in 
                     the mitochondria, the chloroplast, the ER membrane and in 
                     Archaea (reviewed in |CITS: [33664512]|).
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11457446][12191770][12452432][14507478][1 
                     4528263][15802250][15364957][17425940][18658156][24418623][
                     27879020][29800285][36427551][40221051]|.<BR>Comments: 
                     |CITS: [10949283][23933056]|"
