     gene            3880188..3881552
                     /gene="mnmE"
                     /locus_tag="b3706"
                     /label=mnmE
     CDS             3880188..3881552
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mnmE"
                     /locus_tag="b3706"
                     /product="trifunctional
                     5-carboxymethylaminomethyluridine-tRNA synthase GTPase 
                     subunit / 5-carboxymethylaminomethyluridine-tRNA synthase /
                     5-aminomethyluridine-tRNA synthase"
                     /label=mnmE
                     /note="MnmE is required for wild-type 
                     5-methylaminomethyl-2-thiouridine modification of tRNA 
                     |CITS: [6427754]|.  The additional modification of 
                     m<sup>5</sup>U stabilizes the U&middot;G pairing at the 
                     wobble position and thus plays a role in decoding NNG 
                     codons |CITS: [18456657]|.
                     Together with MnmG, MnmE is involved in maintenance of the 
                     correct reading frame |CITS: 
                     [9237903][11532950][11544186][12756333]|.  MnmE also 
                     appeared to play a role in oxidation of thiophene and furan
                     compounds |CITS: [1917835]| and regulates 
                     glutamate-dependent acid resistance |CITS: 
                     [15522079][19220752]|.
                     MnmE is a GTP-binding protein that also exhibits GTPase 
                     activity, showing rapid GTP hydrolysis and low nucleotide 
                     affinity.  The nucleotide binding and hydrolysis activities
                     are localized within the central 17 kDa GTPase domain 
                     |CITS: [10601028]|.  The GTPase activity as well as the 
                     Cys451 residue in the C-terminal domain are required |CITS:
                     [12730230]|, but not sufficient |CITS: [15983041]| for the 
                     wild-type tRNA modification function.
                     Dimerization of the GTPase domain is potassium 
                     ion-dependent; subsequent GTP hydrolysis activity is 
                     dependent on dimerization |CITS: [16763562]|.  Low pH 
                     inhibits the GTP hydrolysis activity |CITS: [17143896]|. 
                     Unlike other GTPases, MnmE does not appear to use an 
                     ""arginine finger"" for catalysis |CITS: 
                     [16763562][17143896]|.
                     While the GTPase domain is required for homodimerization, 
                     the helical domain of MnmE is responsible for the 
                     interaction with MnmG |CITS: [18565343]|.  Nucleotide 
                     binding leads to a conformational change in MnmE, and 
                     GTP-bound MnmE has a much higher affinity for MnmG |CITS: 
                     [19591841]|.  Conversely, MnmG binding induces large 
                     conformational changes in MnmE |CITS: [20353943]|.  MnmG 
                     stabilizes the MnmE homodimer and stimulates GTP hydrolysis
                     |CITS: [19591841][20353943]|.
                     Solution and crystal structures of the G-domain of MnmE 
                     have been solved |CITS: [14752268][16763562][17143896]|. 
                     MnmE can homomultimerize and localizes to the cytoplasm, 
                     showing some association with the cytoplasmic membrane 
                     |CITS: [10601028]|.  MnmE interacts specifically with MnmG 
                     |CITS: [17062623]|.  A fraction of MnmE protein appears to 
                     be associated with the plasma membrane |CITS: [10601028]|. 
                     Viability of an <i>mnmE</i> mutation is dependent on the 
                     strain background |CITS: [10601028]|.  <i>mnmE</i> mutants 
                     are defective in the tRNA modification 
                     5-methylaminomethyl-2-thiouridine; tRNA anticodons that are
                     modified with 5-methylaminomethyl-2-thiouridine in the wild
                     type show 2-thiouridine modification in the mutant, and 
                     mutants exhibit a defect in UAG readthrough |CITS: 
                     [6427754]|.  Unexpectedly, the hypomodified 
                     tRNA<sup>Lys</sup> of an <i>mnmE</i> mutant leads to 
                     decreased misreading of the anticodon |CITS: [9811540]|. An
                     <i>mnmE</i> deletion enhances the slow growth phenotype of 
                     a &Delta;<i>yjeQ</i> mutant |CITS: [18223068]| and 
                     partially supresses the cold-sensitive growth phenotype and
                     |FRAME: G6364-MONOMER ""MiaB""| activity defect of a 
                     |FRAME: G7511 <i>ygfZ</i>| null mutant |CITS: [22081392]|. 
                     <i>mnmE</i> is involved in glutamate-dependent acid 
                     resistance |CITS: [15522079]| and growth at the moderately 
                     low pH 4.5 |CITS: [27826291]|.  Certain transposon 
                     insertion mutants that make cells hypersensitive to 
                     azacytidine-induced DNA-protein crosslinks map to tRNA 
                     modification genes, including <i>mnmE</i> and <i>mnmG</i>. 
                     The azacytidine hypersensitivity is explained by their 
                     tmRNA tagging defect |CITS: [25731940]|.
                     Expression of <i>mnmE</i> is increased during stationary 
                     phase, but independent of the stationary phase sigma factor
                     RpoS.  Expression is also subject to catabolite repression 
                     and is decreased in the absence of oxygen |CITS: 
                     [10738982]|.
                     ThdF: ""<b>th</b>iophene <b>d</b>egradation"" |CITS: 
                     [1917835]|
                     TrmE: ""<b>tR</b>NA <b>m</b>odification"" |CITS: [6427754]|
                     MnmE: ""<b>m</b>ethylami<b>n</b>o<b>m</b>ethyl 
                     modification""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11200227][11489118][21885683][22386868][2 
                     2905870][25310651][25607529][36986305]|"
