     gene            3881795..3881869
                     /gene="tnaC"
                     /locus_tag="b3707"
                     /label=tnaC
     CDS             3881795..3881869
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tnaC"
                     /locus_tag="b3707"
                     /product="tnaAB operon leader peptide"
                     /label=tnaC
                     /note="The TnaC leader peptide is required for regulation
                     by attenuation of the |FRAME: TU00085| operon. The 
                     <i>tnaCAB</i> operon codes for two key enzymes in 
                     tryptophan metabolism, including tryptophanase. It is 
                     regulated by attenuation within its leader region in 
                     response to tryptophan abundance. When tryptophan is 
                     present, transcription continues through a Rho-dependent 
                     terminator, and <i>tnaA</i> and <i>tnaB</i> are 
                     transcribed.
                     Attenuation of <i>tnaCAB</i> occurs via ribosomal pausing 
                     at the final codon, P24, of the TnaC leader peptide. As 
                     translation of the leader peptide nears completion, it 
                     halts with the terminal proline of TnaC still attached to 
                     its tRNA and stuck within the ribosome. In the absence of 
                     tryptophan, peptide chain release factor RF-1 and peptide 
                     chain release factor RF-2 cleave TnaC from the tRNA and 
                     free the ribosome. This, in turn, allows access by |FRAME: 
                     EG10845-MONOMER Rho| to a Rho-dependent terminator that 
                     then prematurely terminates transcription of <i>tnaCAB</i>.
                     In the presence of tryptophan, release of the nascent 
                     peptide chain is prevented, and the ribosome remains in 
                     place, blocking access by Rho to the terminator and 
                     allowing continued transcription of the operon |CITS: 
                     [3902796][10869076][11050101][11470925][11880383]|. 
                     Eventually, the stalled ribosome is cleared away by 
                     ribosome recycling factor and peptide chain release factor 
                     RF-3 |CITS: [17293419]|. As a natural consequence of this 
                     mechanism, attenuation at <i>tnaCAB</i> depends on both 
                     translation of the leader peptide and the presence of Rho 
                     |CITS: [2345136][7543478][8522534]|.
                     The close coupling of transcription and translation is 
                     achieved via an RNA polymerase-pausing mechanism. There is 
                     a pause site toward the latter end of the <i>tnaC</i> 
                     sequence. The RNA polymerase pauses there, allowing time 
                     for the ribosome to attach to the nascent RNA and begin 
                     translation. When the ribosome reaches the paused RNA 
                     polymerase, transcription continues. This pausing mechanism
                     ensures that the ribosome is in the proper position to 
                     regulate the Rho-dependent terminator in response to 
                     tryptophan abundance |CITS: [14563884][14712716]|. A 
                     tryptophan-independed ribosome stalling event near the C 
                     terminus of TnaC appears to be responsible for the 
                     relatively high basal level of <i>tnaAB</i> transcription, 
                     while the 5' end of the <i>tnaC</i> mRNA appears to control
                     synchronization between RNA polymerase and the ribosome 
                     |CITS: [31873224]|.
                     Stalling of the ribosome during translation of TnaC depends
                     on a complex interaction between TnaC and the ribosome. 
                     Attenuation is completely dependent on the sole tryptophan 
                     within the leader peptide sequence. The precise position of
                     this tryptophan codon within <i>tnaC</i>, and thus the 
                     position of Trp within the ribosomal exit tunnel, is 
                     important |CITS: [12228716]|. Mutations at this position 
                     eliminate tryptophan induction of <i>tnaCAB</i> 
                     transcription |CITS: [2345136][12228716]|. Interactions of 
                     the nascent TnaC peptide with the ribosomal exit tunnel, 
                     most notably Asp16 |CITS: [18424524][22110039]|, Pro24 
                     |CITS: [18424524]| and Ile19 |CITS: [24137004]|, are 
                     essential for inhibiting clearing of the TnaC-tRNA complex 
                     from the peptidyl transferase center within the ribosome 
                     and for altering the conformation of the 23S RNA in a 
                     manner that may help generate a tryptophan-binding site on 
                     the ribosome |CITS: [18424524]|. |CITS: [31873224]| 
                     constructed 1450 <i>tnaC</i> variants and tested the 
                     response to three different concentrations of tryptophan 
                     <i>in vivo</i>. Nucleotides within ribosomal RNA and amino 
                     acid residues within ribosomal proteins that interact with 
                     TnaC and influence ribosome pausing have been identified 
                     |CITS: [16061180][17293420][19329641][22110039][24137004]|.
                     Free tryptophan inhibits |FRAME: EG10762-MONOMER 
                     RF-2|-mediated cleavage of TnaC-tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> within 
                     the peptidyl transferase center of the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [11470925]|. Trp12 |CITS: [16505360]| and other residues of
                     TnaC |CITS: [18424524]| introduce changes in the peptidyl 
                     transferase center (PTC) that enable tryptophan binding. 
                     The R23F mutant of TnaC exhibits increased sensitivity to 
                     tryptophan |CITS: [34504068]|. Cryo-EM reconstructions of 
                     the stalled ribosome suggested that two molecules of free 
                     tryptophan are present in the ribosomal exit tunnel, and 
                     that the PTC adopts a conformation that can not accommodate
                     release factors |CITS: [19933110][25310980]|. Cryo-EM 
                     structures of TnaC-stalled ribosomes with and without RF-2 
                     |CITS: [34403461]| and with wild-type and R23F mutant TnaC 
                     |CITS: [34504068]| later showed that TnaC forms a secondary
                     structure that coordinates a single tryptophan within the 
                     ribosomal exit tunnel, while RF-2 is prevented from 
                     adopting its active conformation in the PTC |CITS: 
                     [34403461][34504068]|. The presence of tryptophan blocks 
                     the function of RF-2 more efficiently than that of RF-1 
                     |CITS: [31712310]|.
                     In the absence of tryptophan, Rho-dependent termination 
                     within the <i>tnaC</i> sequence depends on both a 
                     <i>rut</i>- and a <i>boxA</i>-like sequence within the 
                     leader region of the operon |CITS: 
                     [2420781][10869076][14712716]|. The role of the 
                     <i>boxA</i>-like sequence in promoting termination is 
                     peculiar, as the <i>boxA</i> sequence normally acts as an 
                     antiterminator |CITS: [11880383]|. GreA stimulates 
                     expression of <i>tnaCAB</i> |CITS: [17766423]|, while HolE 
                     and Cnu downregulate its expression |CITS: [24375106]|. 
                     Multiple additional amino acids can induce expression of 
                     <i>tnaAB</i>; deletion of <i>nusB</i> reduces this 
                     ""cross-talk"" and enables tighter control in engineered 
                     systems |CITS: [33835775]|.
                     Overexpression of TnaC under inducing conditions leads to 
                     accumulation of TnaC-|FRAME: proM-tRNA| within the 
                     ribosome, depletion of uncharged |FRAME: proM-tRNA|, and 
                     thus a reduced growth rate |CITS: [16484200]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [23746254][20117091][19965743][18369182][1 
                     2228706][11395401][9150880][40210524]|"
                     /translation="MNILHICVTSKWFNIDNKIVDHRP"
                     /go_function="RNA binding [goid 0003723] [evidence IEA]
                     [GO_REF 0000043]"
                     /go_function="rRNA binding [goid 0019843] [evidence IEA]
                     [GO_REF 0000043]"
                     /go_process="regulation of termination of DNA-templated
                     transcription [goid 0031554] [evidence IEA] [GO_REF 
                     0000002]"
                     /go_process="L-tryptophan catabolic process [goid 0006569]
                     [evidence IEA] [GO_REF 0000043]"
                     /go_process="positive regulation of L-tryptophan metabolic
                     process [goid 0090358] [evidence IMP] [PMID 2345136]"
                     /go_process="transcriptional attenuation by ribosome [goid
                     0031556] [evidence IMP] [PMID 10869076]"
                     /go_process="transcriptional attenuation by ribosome [goid
                     0031556] [evidence IDA] [PMID 11050101]"
                     /go_process="transcriptional attenuation by ribosome [goid
                     0031556] [evidence IEA] [GO_REF 0000002]"
     gene            3882090..3883505
                     /gene="tnaA"
                     /locus_tag="b3708"
                     /label=tnaA
     CDS             3882090..3883505
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tnaA"
                     /locus_tag="b3708"
                     /product="bifunctional tryptophanase / L-cysteine
                     desulfhydrase"
                     /EC_number="4.1.99.1; 4.4.1.1"
                     /label=tnaA
                     /note="Tryptophanase or tryptophan indole-lyase (TnaA) is
                     an extremely well-studied pyridoxal phosphate 
                     (PLP)-dependent enzyme that catalyzes the cleavage of 
                     L-tryptophan to indole, pyruvate and 
                     NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>. Together with the tryptophan 
                     transporter TnaB, it enables utilization of L-tryptophan as
                     sole source of nitrogen or carbon for growth. In recent 
                     years, it has become clear that one of the reaction 
                     products, indole, plays a significant role as an 
                     extracellular |CITS: 
                     [11418561][14569285][19797360][20868745][22101045]| and 
                     intracellular |CITS: [24695245]| signal, even acting as a 
                     cell cycle regulator |CITS: [22172706]|. Indole production 
                     by TnaA depends directly on the amount of exogenous 
                     tryptophan, and the enzyme does not appear to degrade 
                     internal anabolic tryptophan |CITS: [23397453]|. 
                     Tryptophanase is a major contributor towards the cellular 
                     L-cysteine desulfhydrase (CD) activity |CITS: 
                     [12883870][16000837]|. <i>In vitro</i>, tryptophanase also 
                     catalyzes &alpha;,&beta; elimination, &beta; replacement, 
                     and &alpha; hydrogen exchange reactions with a variety of 
                     L-amino acids |CITS: [334760]|.
                     Tryptophanase is one of the most S-sulfhydrated proteins in
                     <i>E. coli</i>; at least six of its seven Cys residues can 
                     be S-sulfhydrated. S-sulfhydration reduces the enzymatic 
                     activity of tryptophanase. In a mouse model, a diet high in
                     sulfur-containing amino acids reduces indole levels in the 
                     gut and mitigates kidney damage |CITS: [32943527]|.
                     Molecular and biochemical properties of tryptophanase have 
                     been studied in depth by several groups; only a small 
                     number of papers can be cited here, with preference given 
                     to work performed on the K-12 enzyme. Tryptophanase 
                     consists of four identical subunits |CITS: [4551942]|, each
                     of which contains one molecule of pyridoxal phosphate (PLP)
                     |CITS: [1091651]|. The dissociated dimeric form of the 
                     enzyme appears to be inactive |CITS: [770473][321224]|. 
                     Dissociation has been studied by site-directed mutagenesis 
                     of residues within the subunit interaction interfaces 
                     |CITS: [26627645]|. Crystal structures of wild-type and 
                     mutant TnaA apo- and holoenzyme have been solved |CITS: 
                     [16790938][17704565][19 
                     814824][25760702][26527264][26627645]|. The tetramer 
                     contains four bound K<sup>+</sup> ions |CITS: [16790938]|. 
                     The apo form of the enzyme, lacking PLP, appears to be more
                     flexible than the holo form |CITS: [25760702]|. Using a 
                     method that distinguishes N-phosphorylation from 
                     O-phosphorylation, N-phosphorylation was detected <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [35636362]|.
                     PLP forms an aldimine bond with Lys270 of the enzyme |CITS:
                     [4551944]|; binding of PLP induces a conformational change 
                     |CITS: [1091651][770472]|. The enzyme requires 
                     K<sup>+</sup> for its activity and for tight PLP binding 
                     |CITS: [1091651][2502187][9744811]|. Analysis of 
                     site-directed mutants, e.g. in |CITS: 
                     [2260981][9744811][11900544]|, has identified a number of 
                     residues that are required for interaction with PLP, 
                     orienting the substrate-PLP intermediates in the optimal 
                     conformation for catalysis, and are important for overall 
                     enzyme activity and substrate specificity. The enzyme may 
                     react by a rare S<sub>E</sub>2-type mechanism. Some of this
                     work is reviewed in |CITS: [12686128]|.
                     Different benzimidazole analogs of the indolenine 
                     intermediate/transition state are either substrates or 
                     inhibitors of tryptophanase |CITS: [23438036]|. 
                     L-bishomotryptophan is a potent and selective competitive 
                     inhibitor of the enzyme |CITS: [25088962]|.
                     Tryptophanase localizes to a single focus at one of the 
                     cell poles, mainly during the logarithmic growth phase. The
                     Min system is involved in localization of the protein 
                     |CITS: [22380631]|. Amino acid residues that affect 
                     assembly and localization of the enzyme were identified by 
                     site-directed mutagenesis. Sequestration of the enzyme may 
                     be an additional mechanism to regulate its activity |CITS: 
                     [25650045]|. When the level and distribution of 
                     tryptophanase is analyzed under a variety of concentrations
                     of glucose and tryptophan, formation of foci appears to be 
                     more complex |CITS: [30931970]|.
                     In a strain selected for increased isobutanol tolerance, a 
                     <i>tnaA</i> mutation contributes significantly to the 
                     tolerance phenotype |CITS: [21179021]|. A <i>tnaA</i> 
                     mutant has decreased viability during long-term stationary 
                     phase |CITS: [24695245]|. Reports disagree on whether 
                     indole signaling increases or decreases persister cell 
                     formation; |CITS: [22426114][26483762]| report decreased 
                     persister cell formation of a <i>tnaA</i> mutant when grown
                     in rich medium. In contrast, |CITS: [25041421]| report 
                     decreased persister cell formation when indole is added to 
                     a <i>tnaA</i> mutant and increased persister cell formation
                     when tryptophanase activity is indirectly downregulated by 
                     the phosphodiesterase activity of |FRAME: G6783-MONOMER 
                     DosP| |CITS: [25219496]|. The different results may be due 
                     to differences in the experimental conditions and strains 
                     used.
                     Regulation of expression of tryptophanase has been of 
                     interest for a long time and was shown to be induced by 
                     L-tryptophan and regulated by catabolite repression |CITS: 
                     [16747382][16747528][16747655][4860581][4322348][4335309][ 
                     4339643][164897][170248][1107479][323244][6284718][7921262]
                     |. Glucose depletion triggers synthesis of tryptophanase 
                     |CITS: [26332864]|. Regulation by L-tryptophan is exerted 
                     via antitermination in the leader region |CITS: 
                     [3902796][2420781]| that is facilitated by the TnaC leader 
                     peptide |CITS: [3522554][2345136]|. For a description of 
                     the attenuation mechanism, please see |FRAME: TU00085| and 
                     the review by |CITS: [17601995]|. <i>tnaA</i> mRNA levels 
                     are significantly downregulated in <i>E. coli</i> cells 
                     inoculated on lettuce leaves |CITS: [22247152]|. During 
                     exponential growth under anaerobic nitrate respiration 
                     conditions, transcription of <i>tnaCAB</i> is repressed by 
                     the NarXL and NarPQ two-component systems via FNR, thereby 
                     avoiding the conversion of indole into mutagenic 
                     nitrosative derivatives |CITS: [27631134]|.
                     TnaA protein levels are significantly increased by growth 
                     at high external pH |CITS: [10094700][12107143]| and growth
                     on glycerol |CITS: [22513097]|. <i>tnaA</i> mRNA levels are
                     significantly decreased under moderately acidic conditions 
                     |CITS: [32346014]|. Increased TnaA expression via TorRS is 
                     involved in survival of alkaline stress conditions due to 
                     TMAO respiration |CITS: [12657056]|. Increased <i>tnaA</i> 
                     expression at the onset of stationary phase is dependent on
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> |CITS: [15489429]| and Crl |CITS: 
                     [17224607][18158608]|. Expression of the toxin |FRAME: 
                     G6109-MONOMER YafQ| reduces TnaA levels, possibly by 
                     cleavage of the <i>tnaA</i> mRNA at the in-frame YafQ 
                     cleavage cites |CITS: [25041421]|. Addition of glucose and 
                     other carbohydrates inhibits TnaA function, suggesting an 
                     additional level of carbohydrate-dependent 
                     post-translational regulation |CITS: [25061041]|.
                     The binding of the transcription factor |FRAME: CPLX0-226 
                     CRP| to a predicted CRP DNA binding site around the start 
                     of the <i>tnaA</i> gene has been demonstrated; however an 
                     effect of this binding on gene expression has not been 
                     analyzed |CITS: [37114659]|.
                     Increased degradation of <i>tnaA</i> mRNA upon a shift to 
                     acidic growth conditions is dependent on RNase E |CITS: 
                     [32346014]|. RNase E-dependent degradation of <i>tnaCAB</i>
                     mRNA is modulated by |FRAME: EG10867-MONOMER|; the 
                     interaction of L4 with RNase E leads to stabilization of 
                     the mRNA |CITS: [19144914][32123036]|. Conversely, L4 
                     interacts directly with the <i>tnaC-tnaA</i> intergenic 
                     region mRNA and represses translation of TnaA, potentially 
                     by inducing structural changes in the mRNA |CITS: 
                     [32123036]|.
                     Tryptophanase is the regulatory target of the small RNA 
                     Rcd, which ensures stable maintenance of the ColE1 plasmid.
                     Upregulation of tryptophanase activity increases indole 
                     production; indole then appears to act as an intracellular 
                     signaling molecule to induce a cell division delay |CITS: 
                     [17163976]|.
                     In |FRAME: TAX-6239| pretreated with |FRAME: CPD-22691| 
                     (FUdR), TnaA production of |FRAME: HS| in the presence of 
                     reduced |FRAME: GLUTATHIONE| or |FRAME: CYS| rapidly killed
                     the <i>C. elegans</i>; H<sub>2</sub>S production by TnaA in
                     colonic epithelial cell lines incubated in the presence of 
                     FUdR had significantly reduced intracellular ATP |CITS: 
                     [35279884]|.
                     When exposed to dessication-rehydration stress, the main 
                     proteins found in aggregates were TnaA, |FRAME: 
                     EG11036-MONOMER| and |FRAME: EG11415-MONOMER| in addition 
                     to ribosomal proteins |CITS: [37764358]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [12686128][17163965][17601995][25446541][40329925]|"
