     gene            complement(3905199..3907028)
                     /gene="glmS"
                     /locus_tag="b3729"
                     /label=glmS
     CDS             complement(3905199..3907028)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glmS"
                     /locus_tag="b3729"
                     /product="L-glutamine--D-fructose-6-phosphate
                     aminotransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.6.1.16"
                     /label=glmS
                     /note="L-glutamine:D-fructose-6-phosphate
                     aminotransferase, or GFAT, catalyzes the first step in 
                     hexosamine
                     biosynthesis.  The enzyme uses a nucleophilic attack by the
                     thiol group of Cys1 on the &delta;-carbonyl group of 
                     glutamine to form ammonia, which is then channeled through 
                     the enzyme to the acceptor group on
                     fructose-6-phosphate.  The enzyme can not use exogenous 
                     ammonia as the nitrogen donor |CITS: [3297136]|.
                     The N-terminal domain contains the glutamine amidohydrolase
                     activity, and the C-terminal domain contains
                     the ketose/aldose isomerase activity |CITS: [1898018]|. 
                     Crystal structures of the isolated domains and the
                     full length enzyme have been solved |CITS: 
                     [8805567][9739095][10091662][11700065][16339762] 
                     [18295797]|.  An 18 &Aring; hydrophobic channel provides a 
                     conduit for the transfer of ammonia from the
                     glutamine amidohydrolase active site to the isomerase 
                     active site |CITS: [11700065]|.  Molecular dynamics 
                     simulations and kinetic analysis of mutants identified 
                     roles for W74, A602 and V605 in the opening and
                     closing of the ammonia channel |CITS: [17559838]|.  Major 
                     structural changes occur during the catalytic
                     cycle |CITS: [18295797]|.
                     Mutants of GlmS that show reduced sensitivity to product 
                     inhibition by glucosamine-6-phosphate have been
                     isolated |CITS: [16871653]|.
                     Expression of GlmS is controlled at several levels. 
                     <i>glmS</i> mRNA and protein levels are decreased by growth
                     on amino sugars |CITS: [8349539]|.  <i>glmS</i> is 
                     cotranscribed together with <i>glmU</i> in the <i>glmUS</i>
                     operon.  Expression of GlmS is uncoupled from the 
                     expression of GlmU in various ways.
                     RNase E processes the <i>glmUS</i> transcript at sites 
                     between <i>glmU</i> and <i>glmS</i>, generating a 
                     monocistronic 1.9 kb <i>glmS</i> transcript.  This 
                     transcript is very unstable, with a half life of 0.72 
                     minutes
                     |CITS: [17395638]|.  The small RNAs GlmY |CITS: [17854828]|
                     and GlmZ |CITS: [17824929]| regulate <i>glmS</i> expression
                     posttranscriptionally.  Via a regulatory cascade involving 
                     processing and
                     stabilization of transcripts, the unprocessed form of 
                     |FRAME: SRAJ-RNA| binds to <i>glmS</i> mRNA,
                     uncovering the ribosome binding site and allowing 
                     translation |CITS: [18334534][18351803]|.
                     Although |CITS: [17040898]| reported that <i>glmS</i> mRNA 
                     is polyadenylated,
                     epistasis experiments and direct assays for polyadenylation
                     show that <i>glmS</i> mRNA is not polyadenylated, and the 
                     effect of |FRAME: EG10690-MONOMER| |CITS: [17395638]| on 
                     the stability of
                     <i>glmS</i> mRNA is indirect, via polyadenylation of 
                     |FRAME: TKE1-RNA| |CITS: [18334534][18351803]|.
                     A <i>glmS</i> mutant requires glucosamine or 
                     N-acetylglucosamine for growth |CITS: [5541523]|. 
                     Overexpression of
                     the catabolic D-glucosamine-6-phosphate isomerase, NagB, 
                     can suppress the requirement for <i>glmS</i>
                     |CITS: [2687246]|.
                     The transcription terminator region of <i>glmS</i> contains
                     the unique insertion site for the transposon Tn7 |CITS: 
                     [3010949]|.
                     GlmS: ""<b>gl</b>ucosa<b>m</b>ine""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [11902440][24667238]|"
