     gene            complement(3907190..3908560)
                     /gene="glmU"
                     /locus_tag="b3730"
                     /label=glmU
     CDS             complement(3907190..3908560)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glmU"
                     /locus_tag="b3730"
                     /product="trifunctional fused
                     N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphate uridyltransferase and 
                     glucosamine-1-phosphate acetyltransferase / 
                     N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphate uridyltransferase / 
                     glucosamine-1-phosphate acetyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.23; 2.3.1.157"
                     /label=glmU
                     /note="<i>glmU</i> encodes a fused enzyme with two
                     enzymatic activities that catalyze sequential steps in the 
                     biosynthesis of UDP-<i>N</i>-acetyl-D-glucosamine 
                     (UDP-GlcNAc), an essential precursor of cell wall 
                     peptidoglycan, lipopolysaccharide and enterobacterial 
                     common antigen |CITS: [8083170]|.
                     The enzymatic activities of GlmU are present in two 
                     independently folding and functional domains |CITS: 
                     [11084021]|.  The uridylyltransferase activity, which 
                     catalyzes the formation of UDP-GlcNAc, localizes to the 
                     N-terminal domain, and the acetyltransferase activity, 
                     which catalyzes the formation of 
                     N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphate (GlcNAc-1-P), is located in
                     the C-terminal domain.  Kinetic data shows that the acetyl 
                     transfer precedes the uridylyl transfer.  The intermediate 
                     of the two sequential reactions, 
                     N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphate, appears to be released 
                     from the enzyme |CITS: [8555230]|.  The trimeric 
                     organization of the enzyme is required for activity |CITS: 
                     [11084021]|.  In solution, the enzyme is in a 
                     trimer/hexamer equilibrium.  Two trimers can interact by a 
                     loop in their N-terminal domains that also undergoes a 
                     large conformational change during the uridyltransferase 
                     reaction |CITS: [21370307]|.
                     Inhibitors of GlmU activity, such as iodoacetamide and 
                     N-ethyl maleimide, inhibit biofilm formation in 
                     urophathogenic <i>E. coli</i> P18 |CITS: [16641457]|. 
                     Inhibitors that are specific for the acetyltransferase 
                     activity of GlmU have been identified |CITS: [19349513]|. 
                     Crystal structures of a truncated form of the enzyme have 
                     been solved and show that the protein folds into two 
                     distinct domains connected by an &alpha;-helical arm |CITS:
                     [10428949]|.  Crystal structures of full-length GlmU 
                     together with substrates have been solved, revealing the 
                     substrate binding sites and suggesting reaction mechanisms
                     |CITS: [11329257][17473010]|.
                     A C307A mutation results in a 1,350-fold decrease in 
                     acetyltransferase activity of GlmU |CITS: [9733680]|.  A 
                     conditional <i>glmU</i> mutant at the restrictive 
                     temperature shows morphological changes and cell lysis 
                     |CITS: [8407787]|.  Both activities of GlmU are required 
                     for viability |CITS: [10428949][11084021]|.  A 1-bp 
                     insertion into BoxG1 upstream of |FRAME: TU00280| was 
                     identified as a beneficial adaptation in one population of 
                     a long-term evolution experiment |CITS: [20040094]|.
                     GlmU: ""<b>gl</b>ucosa<b>m</b>ine 
                     <b>u</b>ridyltransferase"" |CITS: [8407787]|
                     Review: |CITS: [18266853]|
                     "
