     gene            complement(3914310..3914549)
                     /gene="atpE"
                     /locus_tag="b3737"
                     /label=atpE
     CDS             complement(3914310..3914549)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="atpE"
                     /locus_tag="b3737"
                     /product="multifunctional ATP synthase Fo complex subunit
                     c / ATP synthase Fo complex - subunit c / ATP synthase Fo 
                     complex / ATP synthase / thiamin triphosphate synthase"
                     /EC_number="7.1.2.2"
                     /label=atpE
                     /note="The c subunit of the F<sub>o</sub> complex of ATP 
                     synthase is absolutely required for proton translocation 
                     and is
                     also necessary for binding of the F<sub>1</sub> complex. 
                     The number of subunit c monomers determines the
                     number of transported ions per revolution. The 
                     stoichiometry of the subunits has been obtained within the 
                     range of 9 to 18 |CITS: 
                     [1337099][1385726][1834653][2141983][9792682][30442809]|; 
                     the preferred number of c
                     subunits is 10 |CITS: [11320246][19181809]|. Quantitation, 
                     cross-linking and reconstitution experiments with wild-type
                     and mutant F<sub>0</sub> show that the stoichiometry of 
                     subunit-c is independent of its rate of synthesis |CITS: 
                     [18540679]|. This is the subunit affected by the inhibitor 
                     dicyclohexylcarbodiimide.
                     Mutational and cross-linking studies indicate that 
                     transmembrane helices (TMH) 4 and 5 of subunit a pack in 
                     close
                     proximity to TMH2 of subunit c. Additional studies suggest 
                     that pH-dependent conformational change may be related to 
                     the proposed role of TMH5 of subunit-a in gating H(+) 
                     access from the periplasm to TMH2 of subunit-c |CITS: 
                     [18786930]|.
                     The initiating methionine is cleaved |CITS: [9868784]|. 
                     Chemical modification studies suggest that the ion exit 
                     channel lies between the interface of subunit-c and 
                     subunit-a. Other models suggest that ions exit through an 
                     aqueous channel in subunit-c or a second half channel in 
                     subunit-a |CITS: [14595019]|.
                     It has been suggested that the structural divergence of the
                     c-subunit ring and the a-subunits between bacterial species
                     may allow development of species specific drugs which 
                     target these subunits |CITS:[18431549]|.
                     Insertion of F<sub>o</sub>-c into proteliposomes depends on
                     the glycolipid known as |FRAME: CPD-15878| and is 
                     stimulated by |FRAME: YIDC ""YidC""| |CITS: [28431927]|.
                     "
                     /translation="MENLNMDLLYMAAAVMMGLAAIGAAIGIGILGGKFLEGAARQPDL
