     gene            complement(3917104..3918993)
                     /gene="mnmG"
                     /locus_tag="b3741"
                     /label=mnmG
     CDS             complement(3917104..3918993)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mnmG"
                     /locus_tag="b3741"
                     /product="trifunctional
                     5-carboxymethylaminomethyluridine-tRNA synthase subunit 
                     MnmG / 5-carboxymethylaminomethyluridine-tRNA synthase / 
                     5-aminomethyluridine-tRNA synthase"
                     /label=mnmG
                     /note="MnmG is required for wild-type 
                     5-methylaminomethyl-2-thiouridine modification of tRNA 
                     |CITS: [6427754]|. The additional modification of 
                     m<sup>5</sup>U stabilizes the U&middot;G pairing at the 
                     wobble position and thus plays a role in decoding NNG 
                     codons |CITS: [18456657]|.
                     MnmG and MnmE both act in a tRNA modification pathway that 
                     reduces +2 frameshift errors in translation |CITS: 
                     [11544186]|. Transcription of <i>mnmG</i>, which is 
                     adjacent to <i>oriC</i>, appeared to affect DNA replication
                     |CITS: [1745229][7968535]|. Subsequently it was shown that 
                     transcription of <i>mnmG</i> is only required for 
                     <i>oriC</i> activation under suboptimal conditions |CITS: 
                     [9356478]|. However, MnmG does appear to promote cell 
                     division |CITS: [26074904]|.
                     The MnmG protein is a dimer in solution, interacts 
                     specifically with MnmE, and binds FAD. Mutations in the 
                     conserved G13 and G15 residues of the proposed FAD binding 
                     site lead to loss of FAD binding and loss of 
                     methylaminomethyl modification of tRNAs |CITS: [17062623]|.
                     Crystal structures of MnmG have been solved |CITS: 
                     [18565343][19801413]|, showing the location of the FAD 
                     binding site and the dimerization interface, as well as 
                     suggesting sites for the interaction with tRNA |CITS: 
                     [18565343][19801413]|. The C-terminal domain is required 
                     for the interaction with MnmE |CITS: [18565343]|. 
                     Nucleotide binding leads to a conformational change in 
                     MnmE, and GTP-bound MnmE has a much higher affinity for 
                     MnmG |CITS: [19591841]|. Conversely, MnmG binding induces 
                     large conformational changes in MnmE |CITS: [20353943]|. 
                     MnmG stabilizes the MnmE homodimer and stimulates GTP 
                     hydrolysis |CITS: [19591841][20353943]|.
                     <i>mnmG</i> is allelic with <i>trmF</i> |CITS: 
                     [11544186][17062623]|. tRNA isolated from a <i>trmF</i> 
                     mutant carries 2-thiouridine instead of 
                     5-methylaminomethyl-2-thiouridine modifications in the 
                     wobble position of the anticodon in certain tRNAs |CITS: 
                     [6427754][17062623]|. <i>mnmG</i> complements the 
                     temperature-sensitive growth defect of a mutant that 
                     requires the normally non-essential tRNA<sup>leuX</sup> for
                     growth |CITS: [9603884]|.
                     The effect of an <i>mnmG</i> null mutation is dependent on 
                     the strain background; an <i>mnmG</i> null mutation can not
                     be introduced into the V5701 strain background, but only 
                     leads to a reduced growth rate in the MG1655 background 
                     |CITS: [17062623]|. Overexpression of MnmEG exacerbates the
                     growth defect and |FRAME: G6364-MONOMER ""MiaB""| activity 
                     defect of a |FRAME: G7511 <i>ygfZ</i>| null mutant |CITS: 
                     [22081392]|. Certain transposon insertion mutants that make
                     cells hypersensitive to azacytidine-induced DNA-protein 
                     crosslinks map to tRNA modification genes, including 
                     <i>mnmE</i> and <i>mnmG</i>. The azacytidine 
                     hypersensitivity is explained by their tmRNA tagging defect
                     |CITS: [25731940]|.
                     Regulation of transcription during the cell cycle has been 
                     described |CITS: 
                     [2836709][2464126][1745229][7968535][9426127][8981994]|. 
                     GidA: ""<b>g</b>lucose-<b>i</b>nhibited <b>d</b>ivision 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [6185823]|
                     TrmF: ""<b>tR</b>NA <b>m</b>odification"" |CITS: [6427754]|
                     MnmG: ""<b>m</b>ethylami<b>n</b>o<b>m</b>ethyl 
                     modification""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [22386868][22905870][25310651][25607529][3 
                     6986305][40210524]|"
