     rep_origin      3919104..3919335
                     /locus_tag="G0-10506"
                     /label=oriC
                     /note="oriC"
     gene            complement(3919372..3919815)
                     /gene="mioC"
                     /locus_tag="b3742"
                     /label=mioC
     CDS             complement(3919372..3919815)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mioC"
                     /locus_tag="b3742"
                     /product="flavoprotein MioC"
                     /label=mioC
                     /note="MioC is an FMN binding protein |CITS: 
                     [10913144][16963438]| that is involved in regulating cell 
                     division |CITS: [26074904]|.  In an <i>in vitro</i> system,
                     MioC was found to be required for activity of biotin 
                     synthase |CITS: [10913144]|.  However, a <i>mioC</i> mutant
                     does not require biotin for growth (D. Bates, unpublished 
                     observation noted in |CITS: [26074904]|).
                     MioC, the protein, is not required for wild-type DNA 
                     replication at <i>oriC</i> |CITS: [3294807]|, but was later
                     found to promote cell division |CITS: [26074904]|. MioC has
                     similarity to flavodoxin family proteins |CITS: 
                     [7756978][10913144]|; its structure was predicted to 
                     exhibit an &alpha;/&beta; twisted open-sheet fold |CITS: 
                     [7756978]|.  Solution structures of the apo- and holo forms
                     of MioC have been solved, confirming the flavodoxin-like 
                     &alpha;/&beta; sandwich structure.  FMN binding induces 
                     structural stabilization |CITS: [16963438]|.
                     Mutations in <i>mioC</i> do not cause defects in cell 
                     growth or size, in the amount of DNA or number of origins 
                     of DNA replication measured per cell, or in timing of 
                     <i>oriC</i> replication initiation |CITS: [1620598]|.  A 
                     promoter mutation that blocks transcription of <i>mioC</i> 
                     results in cells that are 35% longer than wild type, but 
                     has no effect on DNA replication and chromosome segregation
                     |CITS: [26074904]|.
                     Reports differ about whether |CITS: 
                     [3294807][1745229][1555241]| or not |CITS: 
                     [2249664][1620598][9356478]| transcription originating at 
                     the <i>mioC</i> promoter contributes to wild-type DNA 
                     replication at <i>oriC</i>.  Transcription of <i>mioC</i> 
                     appears to inhibit replication initiation |CITS: 
                     [7968535][8132454][12940821]|, but other reports indicate 
                     that this is not the case |CITS: [8655499]|.  Differences 
                     in replication between chromosomal and plasmid contexts may
                     confuse the integration of interpretations of various 
                     experiments |CITS: [1620598]|.  The DnaA binding site (DnaA
                     box) upstream of <i>mioC</i> is involved in DNA replication
                     at <i>oriC</i> |CITS: [2830261][3294807]|. Transcription 
                     from the <i>mioC</i> promoter crosses <i>oriC</i> |CITS: 
                     [2836709]|.
                     Regulation has been described |CITS: 
                     [2546917][9426127][11889089]|.  DnaA regulates <i>mioC</i> 
                     transcription via an upstream binding site (DnaA box) 
                     |CITS: [3294807]|.  Transcription from <i>mioC</i> is 
                     subject to stringent control |CITS: [1745229][1555241]| and
                     decreases at the time of initiation of DNA replication 
                     |CITS: [7968535][8132454][8655499]|.  Transcription is 
                     repressed by MraZ |CITS: [24659771]|.
                     The <i>mioC</i> mRNA has a half life of 1.51 minutes |CITS:
                     [2546917]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [1555241]|
                     "
