     gene            complement(3922957..3924453)
                     /gene="ravA"
                     /locus_tag="b3746"
                     /label=ravA
     CDS             complement(3922957..3924453)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ravA"
                     /locus_tag="b3746"
                     /product="bifunctional regulatory ATPase RavA / ATPase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.4.10"
                     /label=ravA
                     /note="RavA and ViaA together may function as a regulatory 
                     chaperone for |FRAME: FUMARATE-REDUCTASE| (Frd) during 
                     anaerobic fumarate respiration |CITS: [27979649]|.  RavA 
                     belongs to the MoxR family of AAA+ ATPases.  In the 
                     presence of ATP, RavA forms a hexamer |CITS: [16301313]|. 
                     RavA interacts with the |FRAME: LYSDECARBOX-CPLX 
                     ""inducible lysine decarboxylase (CadA)""|, but not |FRAME:
                     LDC2-CPLX ""LdcC""|; interaction with CadA stimulates the 
                     ATPase activity of RavA |CITS: [16301313]|. Interaction 
                     with RavA reduces the inhibitory effect of the alarmone 
                     ppGpp on CadA activity |CITS: [21148420]|.  3D 
                     reconstructions and modeling explain why only CadA, but not
                     LdcC, can interact with RavA |CITS: [27080013]|. Together 
                     with ViaA, RavA negatively modulates the activity of the 
                     Frd complex |CITS: [27979649]|.
                     A crystal structure of RavA with ADP bound has been solved 
                     at 2.9 &Aring; resolution |CITS: [21148420]|.  RavA 
                     consists of a conserved N-terminal AAA+ domain and a poorly
                     conserved C-terminal domain |CITS: [16301313]| which is 
                     formed by a discontinuous triple-helical domain and the 
                     LARA domain, which mediates the interaction with CadA 
                     |CITS: [21148420]|.  An 11 &Aring; resolution cryo-EM map 
                     of the RavA-CadA complex reveals that the LARA domain of 
                     RavA re-arranges itself to match the five-fold symmetry of 
                     CadA |CITS: [25097238]|.
                     At high temperatures under acidic conditions, the LARA 
                     domain of RavA is amyloidogenic.  Structural and molecular 
                     dynamics simulation results suggest that the 
                     &beta;-sandwich folded core of LARA is normally protected 
                     by its N-terminal loop, which is disrupted by low pH and 
                     high temperatures, allowing amyloid formation by the core 
                     region |CITS: [27265850]|.
                     Analysis of microarray and synthetic genetic array data 
                     suggests a functional link between RavA and Fe-S cluster 
                     protein homeostasis/assembly as well as bacterial 
                     respiration |CITS: [24454883][24586182]|.
                     Expression of RavA is under control of the alternative 
                     sigma factor &sigma;<sup>S</sup>; RavA levels increase in 
                     late log/early stationary phase |CITS: [16301313]|.  A 
                     <i>ravA</i> mutant reaches a higher cell density in 
                     stationary phase than wild type in the presence of 
                     sublethal levels of the aminoglycosides kanamycin or 
                     streptomycin; conversely, overexpression of RavA sensitizes
                     cells to kanamycin.  Deletion of certain subunits of 
                     |FRAME: NADH-DHI-CPLX|, |FRAME: SUCC-DEHASE SDH| and 
                     |FRAME: CYT-O-UBIOX-CPLX cytochrome bo| suppress this 
                     effect |CITS: [24454883]|.
                     RavA: ""<b>r</b>egulatory <b>A</b>TPase <b>v</b>ariant 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [16301313]|
                     Review: |CITS: [22491058]|"
