     gene            3924676..3926544
                     /gene="kup"
                     /locus_tag="b3747"
                     /label=kup
     CDS             3924676..3926544
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="kup"
                     /locus_tag="b3747"
                     /product="K(+):H(+) symporter Kup"
                     /label=kup
                     /note="Early studies in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 identified
                     four separate K<sup>+</sup> uptake systems: two 
                     constitutive systems [TrkD (also called Kup) and TrkA* 
                     (also called Trk)], a high affinity system (|FRAME: 
                     ATPASE-1-CPLX ""KdpFABC""|) and a non-saturable system TrkF
                     |CITS: [4578]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [320207][359759][6405784][3896791]|.
                     Expression of cloned <i>kup</i> supports growth of strain 
                     TKW4205 (<i>kdp<sup>-</sup> trkG<sup>-</sup> 
                     trkH<sup>-</sup> kup<sup>-</sup></i>) at 0.1mM 
                     K<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [8226635]|. The Kup protein is 
                     composed of two domains - an integral membrane domain that 
                     has 12 putative trans-membrane spanners and a hydrophilic 
                     C-terminal domain; both termini of the protein are located 
                     at the cytoplasmic side of the cell membrane and deletion 
                     of most of the hydrophilic domain reduces, but does not 
                     abolish Kup mediated K<sup>+</sup> uptake |CITS:[8226635]| 
                     (see also |CITS: [24519967]|). Kup also transports the 
                     cations Cs<sup>+</sup> and Rb<sup>+</sup> |CITS:[2649491]|.
                     Kup (TrkD) is implicated in potassium ion uptake under 
                     hyper-osmotic stress at low pH (below 6) |CITS:[10214935]|.
                     At pH 5.5, K<sup>+</sup> uptake by wild-type <i>E. coli</i>
                     disappears in the presence of the protonophore CCCP |CITS: 
                     [10214935]|); Kup likely operates as a 
                     K<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> symporter |CITS: [11682179]|. 
                     Kup mediated uptake of Cs<sup>+</sup> sustains cell growth 
                     under K<sup>+</sup> limiting conditions; in minimal medium 
                     with 0.1mM K<sup>+</sup>, Cs<sup>+</sup> concentrations up 
                     to 25mM support growth (with an optimum at 5mM) while 
                     concentrations greater than 100 mM impair growth; Kup 
                     mediated Cs<sup>+</sup> uptake and Kdp mediated 
                     K<sup>+</sup> uptake sustain cell growth under 
                     K<sup>+</sup> limiting and Cs<sup>+</sup> excess conditions
                     |CITS: [28522840]|. Kup also contributes to Rb<sup>+</sup> 
                     uptake which sustains growth in minimal medium supplemented
                     with Rb<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [28522840]|.
                     <i>trk</i>: <U>tr</U>ansport of <U>K</U> |CITS: 
                     [4942756]|<BR><i>kup</i>: K<sup>+</sup> uptake |CITS: 
                     [2649491]|
                     * although initially thought to be a single system TrkA/Trk
                     activity was later shown to result from the activity of two
                     low affinity, high rate K<sup>+</sup> transporters (|FRAME:
                     TRKG-MONOMER ""TrkG""| and |FRAME: TRKH-MONOMER 
                     ""TrkH""|)."
