     gene            3926711..3927130
                     /gene="rbsD"
                     /locus_tag="b3748"
                     /label=rbsD
     CDS             3926711..3927130
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rbsD"
                     /locus_tag="b3748"
                     /product="D-ribose pyranase"
                     /EC_number="5.4.99.62"
                     /label=rbsD
                     /note="RbsD is a ribose pyranase; it binds specifically to
                     the &beta;-furan and &beta;-pyran forms of ribose and 
                     increases the exchange rate between the two forms |CITS: 
                     [15060078]|.
                     A preliminary crystallographic analysis of RbsD was 
                     reported, but no structure coordinates were deposited with 
                     PDB |CITS: [11320319]|.  Based on analytical centrifugation
                     analysis, RbsD was initially reported to be an octamer in 
                     solution |CITS: [11320319]|; analysis of the crystal 
                     structure showed it to be a decamer, similar to the 
                     <i>Bacillus subtilis</i> homolog |CITS: [12738765]|, while 
                     gel permeation chromatography indicated that the protein 
                     was a heptamer |CITS: [15060078]|.  Under urea-induced 
                     denaturation conditions, the homodecamer undergoes stepwise
                     disassembly and non-stepwise reassembly |CITS: 
                     [16731978][19937196]|.  The Lys2 residue appears to be 
                     involved in stabilization of the oligomeric state |CITS: 
                     [31588591]|.
                     The RbsD protein is required for efficient utilization of 
                     ribose when ribose is transported into the cell via a 
                     mutated form of PtsG, the glucose transporter.  A mutation 
                     in <i>rbsD</i> does not abolish ribose transport |CITS: 
                     [10318813]|.  An <i>rbsD</i> point mutant that abolishes 
                     pyranase activity is impaired in the utilization of 
                     D-ribose as a source of carbon and energy |CITS: 
                     [15060078]|.
                     Overproduction of RbsD inhibits growth on D-ribose due to 
                     accumulation of methylglyoxal |CITS: [15489434]|. 
                     <i>rbsD</i> was found to be downregulated in a MG1655 
                     lysogen carrying the Stx2a phage PA8 |CITS: [31208335]|."
