     gene            3930405..3930638
                     /gene="rbsZ"
                     /locus_tag="b4804"
                     /label=rbsZ
     misc_RNA        3930405..3930638
                     /gene="rbsZ"
                     /locus_tag="b4804"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA RbsZ"
                     /note="RbsZ is a small regulatory RNA encoded within the
                     3' end and 3' UTR of <i>rbsB</i>; it was discovered in an 
                     RNA-RNA interactome screen, where RybB-RbsZ was the most 
                     abundant pair found on the RNA chaperone ProQ.  RbsZ 
                     appears to act as a sponge that lowers the leves of RybB 
                     RNA |CITS: [31761494]|.  RbsZ and 4 potential targets were 
                     also identified by applying a small RNA prediction 
                     algorithm to RIL-seq data |CITS: [34093460]|. 
                     Overexpression of RbsZ leads to reduced levels of RybB; 
                     converseley, RybB levels are increased in cells lacking 
                     RbsZ |CITS: [31761494]|.
                     |CITS: [31761494]| showed that levels of RbsZ are similar 
                     in wild type and &Delta;<i>proQ</i> or &Delta;<i>hfq</i> 
                     strains, while |CITS: [34093460]| showed that RbsZ levels 
                     are decreased in a &Delta;<i>hfq</i> strain.  RbsZ is most 
                     highly expressed during early- and mid-stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [34093460]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [32213244][32475775]|"
     gene            3930654..3931583
                     /gene="rbsK"
                     /locus_tag="b3752"
                     /label=rbsK
     CDS             3930654..3931583
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rbsK"
                     /locus_tag="b3752"
                     /product="ribokinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.15"
                     /label=rbsK
                     /note="RbsK is a ribokinase that belongs to the ribokinase 
                     subfamily of sugar kinases |CITS: [8382990]|.
                     Crystal structures of ribokinase alone and in ternary 
                     complexes have been solved |CITS: 
                     [9519409][10438599][11786021]|.  They suggest an ordered 
                     reaction mechanism |CITS: [9519409]| with a critical role 
                     for conformational changes |CITS: [10438599]|; monovalent 
                     cation binding leads to a conformational change and 
                     activation of the enzyme |CITS: [11786021]|.
                     Ribokinase specifically binds to the &alpha;-furanose form 
                     of ribose, as shown in the crystal structure |CITS: 
                     [9519409]| and by NMR techniques |CITS: [15060078]|.
                     The rare codon AGG, encoding the Arg307 and Arg309 
                     residues, together with the inefficient termination codon 
                     UGA lead to tagging of 10-25% of newly synthesized 
                     molecules of RbsK by the SsrA system |CITS: 
                     [11891313][12139620]|.
                     An <i>rbsK</i> mutant can not utilize D-ribose as the sole 
                     source of carbon and energy |CITS: 
                     [4889152][6327616][6327617]|.  In an <i>rbsK</i> mutant, 
                     D-ribose is able to leak from the cell |CITS: [15060078]|. 
                     The gene was identified by a computational analysis as a 
                     candidate for encoding resistance to the antiobiotic 
                     |FRAME: CPD0-2068|. Resistance was confirmed by wet-lab 
                     experiments that compared the minimum inhibitory 
                     concentration (MIC) of a knock-out strain to that of the 
                     wild-type strain |CITS: [35487919]|.
                     <i>rbsK</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.  <i>rbsK</i> was found to be 
                     downregulated in a MG1655 lysogen carrying the Stx2a phage 
                     PA8 |CITS: 31208335|."
                     /translation="MQNAGSLVVLGSINADHILNLQSFPTPGETVTGNHYQVAFGGKGA
