     gene            3943920..3945564
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="ilvG"
                     /locus_tag="b4488"
                     /label=ilvG
     CDS             join(3943920..3944903,3944902..3945564)
                     /pseudo
                     /gene="ilvG"
                     /locus_tag="b4488"
                     /product="acetolactate synthase II subunit IlvG"
                     /label=ilvG
                     /note="<i>ilvG</i> is not expressed in the canonical,
                     sequenced <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strain due to an internal 
                     frameshift mutation that effectively cuts it in half |CITS:
                     [765758][6460909]|. Mutations that remove a single 
                     nucleotide or add two nucleotides can revert a strain to 
                     allow wild-type production of IlvG |CITS: 
                     [7015336][7033211]|. Based on a similar example in <i>S. 
                     typhimurium</i>, it has been suggested that this disabling 
                     mutation in <i>ilvG</i> is specific to the lab isolate, and
                     is not representative of natural <i>E. coli</i> strains 
                     |CITS: [7557326]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 with the <i>ilvG</i> mutation undergo 
                     oscillating cycles of isoleucine starvation due to aberrant
                     regulation of isoleucine biosynthesis. This is alleviated 
                     by restoration of <i>ilvG</i> expression |CITS: 
                     [11536144]|.
                     <i>ilvG</i> expression increases during growth on acetate 
                     |CITS: [10753455]|.
                     <i>ptsN</i> knockout phenotypes relating to nitrogen source
                     utilization regulation were observed only in strains 
                     lacking functional <i>ilvG</i> |CITS: [21531803]|. In 
                     non-K-12 strains, site-directed mutagenesis and 
                     crystallographic studies of IlvG have helped define the 
                     catalytic mechanism |CITS: 
                     [20504042][21370850][21636904]|."
     gene            3945561..3945824
                     /gene="ilvM"
                     /locus_tag="b3769"
                     /label=ilvM
     CDS             3945561..3945824
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ilvM"
                     /locus_tag="b3769"
                     /product="trifunctional acetolactate synthase II subunit
                     IlvM / acetohydroxybutanoate synthase / acetolactate 
                     synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.2.1.6"
                     /label=ilvM
                     /note="Acetohydroxyacid synthase II (AHAS II) encoded by 
                     <i>ilvG</i> and <i>ilvM</i> catalyzes the biosynthesis of 
                     &alpha;-aceto-&alpha;-hydroxybutyrate and 
                     &alpha;-acetolactate. AHAS II is not found in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 due to a mutation in <i>ilvG</i>, although it
                     is found in other <i>E. coli</i> strains. See the entry for
                     |FRAME: G8221-MONOMER| for more information.
                     In non-K-12 strains AHAS II is a bifunctional enzyme that 
                     catalyzes the biosynthesis of 
                     &alpha;-aceto-&alpha;-hydroxybutyrate for the isoleucine 
                     pathway and of &alpha;-acetolactate for the valine pathway 
                     |CITS: [3511034]|. Both the large catalytic subunit IlvG 
                     and the small regulatory subunit IlvM are required for this
                     activity, though the exact stoichiometry of the IlvM 
                     subunit is unclear |CITS: [9581571][3027038]|. The kinetics
                     of AHAS II have been evaluated |CITS: [9581571]|. "
