     gene            3959121..3959450
                     /gene="trxA"
                     /locus_tag="b3781"
                     /label=trxA
     CDS             3959121..3959450
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="trxA"
                     /locus_tag="b3781"
                     /product="bifunctional thioredoxin 1 /
                     thioredoxin:disulfide oxidoreductase"
                     /label=trxA
                     /note="Thioredoxins are small electron transfer proteins
                     that contain a cysteine disulfide/dithiol active site with 
                     the amino acid sequence motif Cys-X-X-Cys (where X is any 
                     amino acid).  They are members of the thioredoxin protein 
                     family.  Members of this family/superfamily contain the 
                     thioredoxin fold, a characteristic and stable protein fold 
                     consisting of four &beta;-sheets surrounded by three 
                     &alpha;-helices (reviewed in |CITS: [17008712]|).  The 
                     proteins function in a wide variety of cellular processes. 
                     Thioredoxin is reduced by NADPH in a reaction catalyzed by 
                     thioredoxin reductase. The conversion between the oxidized 
                     and reduced forms results in a subtle change of 
                     conformation.  The functional properties differ between the
                     two forms of thioredoxin.  The reduced form of thioredoxin 
                     is a protein disulfide reductase, and catalyzes 
                     dithiol-disulfide exchange reactions. <i>Escherichia 
                     coli</i> thioredoxin contains the active site amino acid 
                     sequence Trp-Cys-Gly-Pro-Cys.  In |CITS: 
                     [7812718][2187529][2193685][8098620][2181145][6378624]| and
                     reviewed in |CITS: [3896121] [3152490]|. <i>Escherichia 
                     coli</i> has two thioredoxins: |FRAME: 
                     RED-THIOREDOXIN-MONOMER ""thioredoxin 1""|, encoded by the 
                     |FRAME: EG11031| gene, and |FRAME: RED-THIOREDOXIN2-MONOMER
                     ""thioredoxin 2""|, encoded by the |FRAME: EG11887| gene. 
                     Thioredoxin 1 is involved in reducing important cytoplasmic
                     enzymes such as |FRAME: RIBONUCLEOSIDE-DIP-REDUCTI-CPLX|, 
                     |FRAME: PAPSSULFOTRANS-CPLX|,  |FRAME: EG11433-MONOMER| and
                     |FRAME: EG12394-MONOMER|.  <i>E. coli</i> thioredoxin also 
                     binds to bacteriophage T7 DNA polymerase and serves as its 
                     processivity factor for polymerization during phage growth 
                     (|CITS: [20080681]| and in |CITS: [9440688]|).  In 
                     addition, many other proteins that may interact with 
                     thioredoxin have been identified |CITS: [15502869] 
                     [15004283]|.  Thioredoxin also functions in a pathway 
                     involving a trans-membrane transfer of reducing potential 
                     from the cytoplasm to the periplasm.  This pathway 
                     transfers electrons from cytoplasmic thioredoxin, through 
                     the cytoplasmic membrane protein DsbD, to reduce the 
                     periplasmic protein disulfide isomerase DbsC |CITS: 
                     [9352906] [19258316]|.  Reviewed in |CITS: [17659286]|. The
                     solution structures of reduced |CITS: [7812718] [2193685]| 
                     and oxidized |CITS: [7812718]| wild-type thioredoxin have 
                     been determined by NMR spectroscopy.  The crystal structure
                     of oxidized wild-type thioredoxin has also been solved at 
                     2.8 &Aring; |CITS: [1094461]| and 1.68 &Aring; |CITS: 
                     [2181145 ]| resolution.  Crystal structures are also 
                     available for mutant thioredoxins |CITS: [12364576] 
                     [10489448] [15987909] [8098620]| and chimeric |CITS: 
                     [12135359]| or fusion |CITS: [15967998]| proteins with 
                     thioredoxin.  Crystal structures of thioredoxin in complex 
                     with thioredoxin reductase |CITS: [10947986]|, 
                     3'-phosphoadenosine-5'-phosphosulfate reductase |CITS: 
                     [17352498]|, and bacteriophage T7 DNA polymerase |CITS: 
                     [15235589] [9440688] [15297882] [15528277] [16271888]| have
                     also been solved.
                     Thioredoxin function <i>in vivo</i> can depend upon 
                     cellular location.  Although thioredoxin acts as a 
                     disulfide bond reductant in the reducing environment of the
                     cytoplasm, it has been shown that if thioredoxin is 
                     experimentally exported to the oxidizing environment of the
                     periplasm it can function as an oxidant, promoting 
                     disulfide bond formation |CITS: [9724776]|.  The <i>in 
                     vivo</i> importance of the redox potential of thioredoxin 
                     was demonstrated using thioredoxin active site mutants 
                     which had increased redox potentials |CITS: [10464247]|. 
                     Many other genetic, biochemical and biophysical studies of 
                     <i>E. coli</i> thioredoxin structure and function have been
                     done, examples include |CITS: [18003618 ] [16357193] 
                     [16853329] [15752590] [15049690][36190807]|. As a model 
                     system it was used to show that the rate-limiting step in 
                     its folding is the <i>cis-trans</i> isomerization of a 
                     conserved <i>cis</i> prolyl peptide bond at Ile75-Pro76. 
                     Acceleration of this step by replacement of 
                     <i>cis</i>-Pro76 with (2<i>S</i>,4<i>S</i>)-fluoroproline 
                     was demonstrated |CITS: [26382254]|.
                     An experiment using the fluorescent disulfide probe |FRAME:
                     CPD-26606| to measure thioredoxin and glutaredoxin reducing
                     activity <i>in vivo</i> concluded that the thioredoxin 
                     system was the predominant disulfide reductase for fast 
                     disulfide reduction in |FRAME: TAX-562| |CITS: [35204259]|.
                     Contrary to these findings, an experiment utilizing a 
                     different probe, |FRAME: CPD-26605|, concluded that Grx2 
                     and Grx3 exhibited higher activity in reducing the 
                     disulfide probe than the other redoxins (Grx1 and 
                     thioredoxins 1 and 2) |CITS: [35107099]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [26149400]|"
