     gene            3962391..3963437
                     /gene="wzzE"
                     /locus_tag="b3785"
                     /label=wzzE
     CDS             3962391..3963437
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="wzzE"
                     /locus_tag="b3785"
                     /product="enterobacterial common antigen polysaccharide 
                     co-polymerase"
                     /label=wzzE
                     /note="The assembly of enterobacterial common antigen
                     (ECA) occurs by a Wzx/Wzy-dependent pathway (see |CITS: 
                     [32788387][25358682]|).
                     <i>wzzE</i> is located within a gene cluster that encodes 
                     the enzymes required for ECA synthesis and assembly; WzzE 
                     functions to modulate the length of |FRAME: CPD-21605 
                     ECA<sub>PG</sub>| polysaccharide chains |CITS:[10515954]|. 
                     Typically, ECA<sub>PG</sub> chain lengths are 1 to 14 
                     repeats long with a modal value of 6 or 7; <I>wzzE</I> 
                     mutants display a random, non-modal distribution of 
                     ECA<sub>PG</sub> polysaccharide chain lengths 
                     |CITS:[10515954]|. <I>wzzE</I> is also required for the 
                     synthesis of cyclic ECA (|FRAME: CPD0-2646 
                     ECA<sub>CYC</sub>|) |CITS:[16199561]|. The lipid III 
                     flippase |FRAME: EG11486-MONOMER WzxE|, ECA polymerase 
                     |FRAME: FUC4NACTRANS-MONOMER WzyE|, and WzzE may function 
                     as a multiprotein complex |CITS:[16816184]|. WzyE and WzzE 
                     were shown in |FRAME: TAX-29471| to form a complex <i>in 
                     vivo</i> with stoichiometry of approximately eight WzzE to 
                     one WzyE. WzyE is positioned in the center of a 
                     transmembrane cavity, or lumen, formed by the transmembrane
                     helices of the eight WzzE molecules. The nascent polymer 
                     forms inside the lumen, and the length of the polymer is 
                     limited by the lumen's depth |CITS: [36944376]|.
                     Polymerisation of a pentasaccharide repeat unit (Gal-&alph 
                     a;1,3-(Fuc-&alpha;1,2)-Gal-&beta;1,3-GalNAc-&alpha;1,3-GalN
                     Ac-PP-Und) has been reconstituted using purified Wzy and 
                     Wzz proteins from <i>E. coli</i> O86; Wzz proteins, derived
                     from O86:B7 and O86:H2 impart defined modal distributions 
                     to the polysaccharides generated <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [20418877]|."
