     gene            3968506..3969636
                     /gene="wecE"
                     /locus_tag="b3791"
                     /label=wecE
     CDS             3968506..3969636
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="wecE"
                     /locus_tag="b3791"
                     /product="dTDP-4-dehydro-6-deoxy-D-glucose transaminase"
                     /EC_number="2.6.1.59"
                     /label=wecE
                     /note="dTDP-4-dehydro-6-deoxy-D-glucose transaminase is
                     involved in the biosynthesis of enterobacterial common 
                     antigen (ECA).  This enzyme catalyzes the conversion of 
                     dTDP-4-keto-6-deoxy-D-glucose to dTDP-D-thomosamine |CITS: 
                     [2166030][15271350]|.
                     Crystal structures of the enzyme have been solved |CITS: 
                     [26023720]|.  While gel filtration showed a molecular 
                     weight of 180 kDa, suggesting a homotetrameric structure 
                     |CITS: [15271350]|, the crystal structure showed a 
                     homodimeric configuration |CITS: [26023720]|.  The 
                     conserved active site Lys181 residue forms the internal 
                     aldimine with the PLP cofactor.  The stereochemical 
                     specificity of the enzyme for amination of the C4 
                     stereocenter, forming the C4-<i>R</i> configuration, is 
                     determined by a mode of nucleotide binding (a ""base flip""
                     mechanism) that differs from that of other C4 sugar 
                     aminotransferases and results in reorientation of the sugar
                     moiety |CITS: [26023720]|.
                     <i>wecE</i> mutants do not produce ECA |CITS: [2166030]| 
                     and accumulate the pathway intermediate |FRAME: 
                     C55-PP-GLCNAC-MANNACA ECA-lipid II|, leading to a 
                     sensitivity to bile salts and triggering cell envelope 
                     stress responses |CITS: [9811644]|.  A <i>wecE</i> mutation
                     was also identified in a screen for mutations that cause 
                     altered cell shapes.  This phenotype is due to ECA-lipid II
                     accumulation, indicating that there is competition between 
                     metabolic pathways for a common pool of undecaprenyl 
                     phosphate |CITS: [26593043]|.
                     "
