     gene            3971961..3973313
                     /gene="wzyE"
                     /locus_tag="b3793"
                     /label=wzyE
     CDS             3971961..3973313
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="wzyE"
                     /locus_tag="b3793"
                     /product="enterobacterial common antigen polymerase"
                     /label=wzyE
                     /note="The assembly of enterobacterial common antigen
                     (ECA) occurs by a Wzx/Wzy-dependent pathway (see |CITS: 
                     [32788387][25358682]|).
                     <i>wzyE</i> is located immediately downstream of 
                     <i>wecF</i> (encoding the 4-alpha-L-fucosyltransferase 
                     which catalyses the final step in biosythesis of an ECA 
                     trisaccharide) and is suggested to encode a polymerase 
                     involved in ECA polysaccharide chain elongation |CITS: 
                     [11673418]|. WzyE is required for the assembly of 
                     glycerophosphatidyl-linked ECA (|FRAME: CPD-21605 
                     ECA<sub>PG</sub>|) and cyclic ECA (|FRAME: CPD0-2646 
                     ECA<sub>CYC</sub>|) |CITS: [16199561]|; WzyE catalyses 
                     polymerization of repeat unit Und-P linked saccharides at 
                     the periplasmic face of the inner membrane via a 
                     'block-transfer' mechanism with modulation of 
                     polysaccharide chain length dependent on |FRAME: 
                     EG11295-MONOMER WzzE|.
                     The lipid III flippase |FRAME: EG11486-MONOMER WzxE|, ECA 
                     polymerase WzyE, and co-polymerase |FRAME: EG11295-MONOMER 
                     WzzE| may function as a multiprotein complex 
                     |CITS:[16816184]|. WzyE and WzzE were shown in |FRAME: 
                     TAX-29471| to form a complex <i>in vivo</i> with 
                     stoichiometry of approximately eight WzzE to one WzyE 
                     |CITS: [36944376]|.
                     WzyE is highly conserved. Alignment of 248 WzyE sequences 
                     from across Enterobacteriales identified 105 residues that 
                     are completely conserved. Topology mapping of WzyE from 
                     |FRAME: TAX-623| identified 13 transmembrane segments, four
                     or five periplasmic loops, and six small cytoplasmic loops 
                     |CITS: [35470793]|.
                     Polymerization of a penta-saccharide repeat unit (Gal-&alp 
                     ha;1,3-(Fuc-&alpha;1,2)-Gal-&beta;1,3-GalNAc-&alpha;1,3-Gal
                     NAc-PP-Und) has been reconstituted using purified Wzy and 
                     Wzz proteins from the enteropathogenic <i>E. coli</i> O86 
                     strain; Wzy is both necessary and sufficient to catalyze 
                     polymerization of the repeat units <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [20418877]|."
