     gene            3975735..3975811
                     /gene="argX"
                     /locus_tag="b3796"
                     /label=argX
     tRNA            3975735..3975811
                     /gene="argX"
                     /locus_tag="b3796"
                     /product="tRNA-Arg(CCG)"
                     /label=argX
                     /note="tRNA(argX) is one of seven arginine tRNAs.
                     tRNAs are the adapters that allow synthesis of proteins 
                     from mRNAs.
                     Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome for
                     protein synthesis.
                     There, the tRNA recognizes an RNA codon with its own 
                     three-nucleotide
                     anticodon, thus allowing synthesis of a specific peptide 
                     based on an mRNA
                     template.
                     tRNAs are processed to their active, mature forms by RNA 
                     cleavage
                     and by modification of their bases. RNA cleavage consists 
                     of
                     removal of both 5' and 3' extensions in a multistep process
                     involving
                     many RNases |CITS: [11252717]|. RNases taking part in tRNA 
                     processing include |FRAME: CPLX0-3461|,
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3601|, |FRAME: EG10858-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: EG11620-MONOMER|, and |FRAME: CPLX0-3602|. tRNAs 
                     are also subject to a
                     wide variety of base modifications catalyzed by proteins 
                     such as
                     |FRAME: EG11932-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10595-MONOMER|, |FRAME:
                     G6364-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11344-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7199-MONOMER|, |FRAME: CPLX0-1101|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11779-MONOMER|, |FRAME: G7422-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7449-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11177-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10454-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10967-MONOMER|,
                     and |FRAME: EG11022-MONOMER|.
                     Individually, DksA and ppGpp activate and inhibit, 
                     respectively, the transcription started from the 
                     <i>argX</i> promoter (<i>argX</i>p). On the other hand, 
                     <i>argX</i>p also is regulated negatively by DksA, and this
                     response occurs in the absence of ppGpp |CITS:[ 27915292]|.
                     RNase E processing of the <i>argX</i> operon is modulated 
                     by Hfq binding to the mRNA |CITS: [14622403]|.
                     Mature tRNAs are linked via a 3' CCA sequence to their 
                     cognate
                     amino acid in an ATP-dependent fashion by the appropriate 
                     amino-acid-tRNA synthetase, as shown in the |FRAME: 
                     TRNA-CHARGING-PWY|.
                     Subsequently, these charged tRNAs interact with the 
                     ribosome
                     and template mRNA to generate polypeptides. The discovery 
                     of the
                     role of tRNA in protein synthesis is reviewed in detail in 
                     |CITS: [7033244]|."
     gene            3975869..3975945
                     /gene="hisR"
                     /locus_tag="b3797"
                     /label=hisR
     tRNA            3975869..3975945
                     /gene="hisR"
                     /locus_tag="b3797"
                     /product="tRNA-His(GUG)"
                     /label=hisR
                     /note="tRNA(hisR) is the sole histidine tRNA.
                     tRNAs are the adapters that allow synthesis of proteins 
                     from mRNAs.
                     Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome for
                     protein synthesis.
                     There, the tRNA recognizes an RNA codon with its own 
                     three-nucleotide
                     anticodon, thus allowing synthesis of a specific peptide 
                     based on an mRNA
                     template.
                     tRNAs are processed to their active, mature forms by RNA 
                     cleavage
                     and by modification of their bases. RNA cleavage consists 
                     of
                     removal of both 5' and 3' extensions in a multistep process
                     involving
                     many RNases |CITS: [11252717]|. RNases taking part in tRNA 
                     processing include |FRAME: CPLX0-3461|,
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-3601|, |FRAME: EG10858-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: EG11620-MONOMER|, and |FRAME: CPLX0-3602|. tRNAs 
                     are also subject to a
                     wide variety of base modifications catalyzed by proteins 
                     such as
                     |FRAME: EG11932-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10595-MONOMER|, |FRAME:
                     G6364-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11344-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7199-MONOMER|, |FRAME: CPLX0-1101|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11779-MONOMER|, |FRAME: G7422-MONOMER|,
                     |FRAME: G7449-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11177-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10454-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10967-MONOMER|,
                     and |FRAME: EG11022-MONOMER|.
                     Mature tRNAs are linked via a 3' CCA sequence to their 
                     cognate
                     amino acid in an ATP-dependent fashion by the appropriate 
                     amino-acid-tRNA synthetase, as shown in the |FRAME: 
                     TRNA-CHARGING-PWY|.
                     Subsequently, these charged tRNAs interact with the 
                     ribosome
                     and template mRNA to generate polypeptides. The discovery 
                     of the
                     role of tRNA in protein synthesis is reviewed in detail in 
                     |CITS: [7033244]|.
                     tRNA(hisR) differs from other tRNAs in having an extra 5' G
                     that
                     is required for effective charging of the tRNA with 
                     histidine |CITS: [2678006][3023854]|.
                     Specifically, the phosphate group of this G is required for
                     aminoacylation |CITS: [10747795]|.
                     NMR and MD studies on wild-type and mutant tRNA(hisR)
                     show that in the absence of the extra 5' G, the acceptor 
                     stem microhelix
                     is less structured |CITS: [14654706]|.
                     tRNA(hisR) also has a unique G1:C73 base pairing in its 
                     acceptor helix, of which
                     C73 plays an important part proper histidinylation |CITS: 
                     [8144499]|.
                     RNase E processing of the <i>argX</i> operon that includes 
                     <i>hisR</i>
                     is modulated by Hfq binding to the mRNA |CITS: 
                     [14622403]|."
