     gene            3976095..3976171
                     /gene="proM"
                     /locus_tag="b3799"
                     /label=proM
     tRNA            3976095..3976171
                     /gene="proM"
                     /locus_tag="b3799"
                     /product="tRNA-Pro(UGG)"
                     /label=proM
                     /note="tRNA(proM) is one of three proline tRNAs (|FRAME: 
                     EG30066|, |FRAME: EG30067| and |FRAME: EG30068|) and is 
                     part of the <i>argX</i> polycistronic operon (|FRAME: 
                     EG30017| |FRAME: EG30042| |FRAME: EG30049| |FRAME: 
                     EG30068|).
                     tRNAs are the adapters that allow synthesis of proteins 
                     from mRNAs. Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the 
                     ribosome for protein synthesis. There, the tRNA recognizes 
                     an RNA codon with its own three-nucleotide
                     anticodon, thus allowing synthesis of a specific peptide 
                     based on an mRNA template.
                     tRNAs are processed to their active, mature forms by RNA 
                     cleavage and by modification of their bases. RNA cleavage 
                     consists of removal of both 5' and 3' extensions in a 
                     multistep process typically involving many RNases |CITS: 
                     [11252717]|. The |FRAME: PWY-8469| of <i>proM</i> is RNase 
                     E-dependent: the separation of <i>proM</i> from the other 
                     genes in the <i>argX</i> operon is by multiple RNase E 
                     cleavages at 3, 4 and 15 nt upstream of the final mature 5'
                     end |CITS: [11871663][12000793]| with the 3' 
                     Rho-independent transcription terminator being completely 
                     removed by RNase E cleavage without involvement of other 
                     RNases |CITS: [27288443]|. However, RNase G and RNase Z can
                     serve as weak substitutes in the absence of RNase E |CITS: 
                     [27288443]|.
                     tRNAs are also subject to a wide variety of base 
                     modifications catalyzed by proteins such as |FRAME: 
                     EG11932-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10595-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     G6364-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11344-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     G7199-MONOMER|, |FRAME: CPLX0-1101|, |FRAME: 
                     EG11779-MONOMER|, |FRAME: G7422-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     G7449-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG11177-MONOMER|, |FRAME: 
                     EG10454-MONOMER|, |FRAME: EG10967-MONOMER| and |FRAME: 
                     EG11022-MONOMER|.
                     Mature tRNAs are linked via a 3' CCA sequence to their 
                     cognate amino acid in an ATP-dependent fashion by the 
                     appropriate amino-acid-tRNA synthetase, as shown in the 
                     |FRAME: TRNA-CHARGING-PWY|. Subsequently, these charged 
                     tRNAs interact with the ribosome and template mRNA to 
                     generate polypeptides. The discovery of the role of tRNA in
                     protein synthesis is reviewed in detail in |CITS: 
                     [7033244]|.
                     The relationship between tRNA(pro) structure and its 
                     recognition by |FRAME: PROS-CPLX| has been extensively 
                     evaluated |CITS: [7522561][8643363][7870582][12903242]|. 
                     Following UVA irradiation, tRNA(pro) is acylated at 
                     one-tenth the normal rate, causing a growth defect |CITS: 
                     [2449211]|.
                     The <i>argX</i> operon which includes <i>proM</i> is 
                     modulated by the major global regulator of cell physiology,
                     |FRAME: EG10438-MONOMER|, binding to the mRNA |CITS: 
                     [14622403]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [31036345][34995554]|"
     gene            3976318..3977553
                     /gene="aslB"
                     /locus_tag="b3800"
                     /label=aslB
     CDS             3976318..3977553
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aslB"
                     /locus_tag="b3800"
                     /product="putative anaerobic sulfatase maturation enzyme
                     AslB"
                     /label=aslB
                     /note="AslB is a member of the anaerobic sulfatase
                     maturation enzyme subfamily of the Radical SAM superfamily 
                     of enzymes |CITS: [11222759][16766528]|.
                     AslB from <i>E. coli</i> BL21(DE3), which is 96% identical 
                     to AslB of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655 (shown here), has 
                     been studied experimentally.  Co-expression of AslB with 
                     recombinant human sulfatases results in increased sulfatase
                     activity |CITS: [28882567]|.
                     Both an <i>aslB</i> single and <i>aslB ydeM</i> double null
                     mutant is still able to mature a heterologous sulfatase 
                     from <i>Clostridium perfringens</i>.  Maturation of the 
                     <i>C. perfringens</i> enzyme appears to be dependent on a 
                     different enzyme that is present only under aerobic 
                     conditions |CITS: [17303125]|.
                     Expression of <i>aslB</i> is activated by the 
                     transcriptional regulator Rob |CITS: [10850996]|."
