     gene            3979792..3979963
                     /gene="glmZ"
                     /locus_tag="b4456"
                     /label=glmZ
     misc_RNA        3979792..3979963
                     /gene="glmZ"
                     /locus_tag="b4456"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA GlmZ"
                     /note="GlmZ is a small RNA involved in posttranscriptional 
                     regulation of <i>glmS</i> accumulation and translation in 
                     response to the intracellular concentration of glucosamine 
                     6-phosphate |CITS: [17824929]|.  The full-length form of 
                     GlmZ is thought to interact directly with the 5' UTR of the
                     <i>glmS</i> mRNA, unmasking the ribosome binding site and 
                     thus activating translation |CITS: [18351803]|.
                     An efficient interaction between GlmZ RNA and Hfq protein 
                     has been detected |CITS: [11445539]|, and Hfq is required 
                     for the GlmZ-mediated increase in GlmS synthesis |CITS: 
                     [17824929][18351803]|.
                     GlmZ was detected as a small RNA that appears in two forms,
                     a full-length species of approximately 210 nt and a 
                     processed species, GlmZ*, of approximately 172 nt in length
                     |CITS: [11445539][11448770]|.  Posttranscriptional 
                     processing of GlmZ is dependent on RNase III |CITS: 
                     [11448770]| and on |FRAME: CPLX0-7998 RapZ| |CITS: 
                     [17824929]|.  RapZ acts as an adaptor protein that guides 
                     processing of GlmZ by RNase E |CITS: [23475961]|.  The 
                     |FRAME: TKE1-RNA GlmY| small RNA antagonizes processing of 
                     GlmZ |CITS: [18351803][18334534]| by binding and 
                     sequestering RapZ, thereby inhibiting RapZ-enabled 
                     processing of GlmZ |CITS: [23475961]|.  RapZ binds 
                     glucosamine-6-phosphate (GlcN6P); the GlcN6P-free form of 
                     RapZ stimulates phosphorylation of the two-component system
                     kinase |FRAME: CPLX0-8275 GlrK|, which subsequently causes 
                     increased expression of <i>glmY</i>.  Both GlmY and GlmZ 
                     can counteract RapZ's effect on GlrK, resulting in a 
                     negative feedback loop |CITS: [32065419]|.
                     Because the GlmZ* cleavage product retains the ability to 
                     bind RapZ, it competes with full-length GlmZ for RapZ 
                     binding and thereby counteracts processing.  GlmZ is 
                     therefore never completely processed <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [31116083]|.
                     Domain-swapping experiments between GlmZ and GlmY have 
                     allowed identification of the specific RNA regions that are
                     responsible for their different functions and fates despite
                     their high sequence similarities.  A single-stranded region
                     and the oligo(U) tail at the 3' end of GlmZ are responsible
                     for the specific interaction with Hfq, which protects GlmZ 
                     from RapZ-enabled RNase E cleavage.  The central stem-loop 
                     2 (SL2) is specifically required for RapZ-dependent RNase E
                     cleavage, while the sequence of the actual cleavage site, 
                     located in the single-stranded region following SL2, is not
                     sequence-specific |CITS: [26531825][36504162]|.
                     Complex formation of GlmZ-RapZ-Rnase E has been 
                     demonstrated |CITS:[36504162]|. The SLI and SLII stem-loop 
                     structures of GlmZ are bound by two molecules of two RapZ 
                     tetramers,  and the region near the intersection of SLI and
                     SLII is bound by one tetramer of RNAse E |CITS:[36504162]|.
                     Using a network biology approach, both GlmZ and McaS (IsrA)
                     were predicted to be involved in the DNA damage response.  
                     A <i>glmZ mcaS</i> double mutant is less sensitive to DNA 
                     damaging agents and has a lower basal mutation rate than 
                     wild type |CITS: [21876160]|.  The GlmY/GlmZ system 
                     provides resistance to inhibitors of GlmS activity, such as
                     bacilysin and Nva-FMDP.  <i>glmY</i> or <i>glmZ</i> mutants
                     do not overexpress <i>glmS</i> in response to GlmS 
                     inhibitors, leading to increased susceptibility to these 
                     antibiotics |CITS: [27379045]|. GlmZ may also contribute to
                     the stabilization of <i>rnb</i> mRNA |CITS: [31744883]|.
                     GlmZ is expressed in the early logarithmic growth phase and
                     is slightly induced by cold shock |CITS: [11448770]|. The 
                     unprocessed form is prevalent in LB medium, while the 
                     shorter, processed form predominates in minimal medium 
                     |CITS: [11445539]|.  The half life of GlmZ in exponentially
                     growing cells is 2 minutes |CITS: [14602901]|.
                     SraJ: ""<b>s</b>mall <b>R</b>N<b>A</b>"" |CITS: [11448770]|
                     PsrA20: ""<b>p</b>redicted <b>s</b>mall <b>R</b>N<b>A</b>""
                     |CITS: [11448770]|
                     Ryx: ""RNA of unknown function at X position (10 minute) in
                     the genome"" |CITS: [11445539]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12762038][15487940][18981470][19880344][2 
                     4667238][26618164][32213244][32391395]|"
