     gene            3984513..3987059
                     /gene="cyaA"
                     /locus_tag="b3806"
                     /label=cyaA
     CDS             3984513..3987059
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cyaA"
                     /locus_tag="b3806"
                     /product="adenylate cyclase"
                     /EC_number="4.6.1.1"
                     /label=cyaA
                     /note="Adenylate cyclase catalyzes the synthesis of cyclic
                     AMP (cAMP) by an intramolecular transfer of the adenylyl 
                     group of ATP to the 3'-hydroxy group, releasing 
                     pyrophosphate; thus, CyaA helps regulate the cellular 
                     concentration of cAMP. cAMP is an important signaling 
                     molecule; the role of cAMP in regulating the production of 
                     metabolic enzymes was first summarized in |CITS: 
                     [4317896]|. The absence of cAMP affects the expression of 
                     alternative carbon source uptake and degradation systems 
                     involved in central metabolism |CITS:[ 26204448]|. Via 
                     binding to the CAP/CRP protein (see |FRAME: CPLX0-226|), it
                     participates in the regulation of transcription of many 
                     genes.
                     Adenylate cyclase is present at very low levels of 
                     approximately 15 molecules/cell |CITS: [6300054]|. Its rare
                     UUG initiation codon, which was confirmed by N-terminal 
                     sequencing of the purified protein |CITS: [6427472]|, 
                     limits the translation of <i>cyaA</i> |CITS: [3898067]|. 
                     Polyamines stimulate synthesis of adenylate cyclase at the 
                     level of translation initiation |CITS: [11278825]|.
                     The regulation of adenylate cyclase has been studied for 
                     many years, but the results were often contradictory. 
                     Proteins of the PEP:sugar phosphotransferase system (PTS) 
                     were implicated early, and regulation of adenylate cyclase 
                     in cell extracts could be reconstituted by addition of PTS 
                     proteins |CITS: [3909141]|. Experiments with various 
                     mutants finally showed that the phosphorylated form of 
                     |FRAME: CRR-MONOMER| appears to be responsible for 
                     activating adenylate cyclase |CITS: [9084162][9457881]|, 
                     although some results remained unexplained; see, for 
                     example, |CITS: [9202467]|. An experiment attempting to 
                     reconstitute regulation of adenylate cyclase <i>in 
                     vitro</i> showed that a membrane-targeted adenylate cyclase
                     interacts directly with IIA<sup>Glc</sup>, but regulation 
                     of the enzyme still requires additional factor(s) present 
                     in cell extract |CITS: [16407219]|. Other possible 
                     regulators of adenylate cyclase activity, such as 
                     inhibition by CAP/CRP |CITS: [6283317]| and stimulation by 
                     EF-Tu |CITS: [3017931]|, have been reported.
                     Expression of <i>cyaA</i> is repressed by cAMP-CRP |CITS: 
                     [6267421][2982847]|. This transcriptional regulation is 
                     only partly responsible for downregulation of cAMP levels 
                     by CRP; the majority of the effect is due to 
                     posttranscriptional regulation |CITS: [9003304]| via 
                     reducing the level of phosphorylated IIA<sup>Glc</sup> 
                     |CITS: [9749675]|.
                     The enzyme is composed of an N-terminal catalytic domain 
                     and a C-terminal regulatory domain
                     |CITS: [6302283][6393056]|. Amino acid residues that are 
                     important for catalytic activity have been identified by 
                     site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [1540166][7983047][18620542]|, and a two-metal ion 
                     catalytic mechanism has been proposed |CITS: [18620542]|. 
                     The C-terminal 48 residues are required for 
                     posttranscriptional regulation of adenylate cyclase by CRP 
                     |CITS: [9003304]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>cyaA</i> is lethal in a <i>crp</i> 
                     wild-type background |CITS: [2557591]|. A <i>cyaA</i> 
                     mutant shows a slow-growth phenotype when grown aerobically
                     on glucose, but appears to have no effect on overall 
                     metabolic flux |CITS: [15838044]|. Growth of a <i>cyaA</i> 
                     mutant is negatively affected by increased external pH 
                     |CITS: [2841287][2557591]|. <i>cyaA</i> mutants are 
                     resistant to fosmidomycin due to regulation of expression 
                     of the GlpT transporter by cAMP |CITS: [14519998]|. 
                     &Delta;<i>cyaA</i> mutants are significantly more resistant
                     to oxidative stress from exposure to |FRAME: CPD-12799| and
                     |FRAME: HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| than wild-type cells |CITS: 
                     [19372151]|, though this resistance phenotype has not been 
                     seen consistently |CITS: [3045081][9324269]|. <i>cyaA</i> 
                     mutants showed similar survival rates in the presence of 
                     antibiotics and carbon source consumption as cell 
                     persisters, indicating a key component in metabolism of 
                     these phenotypic variants |CITS:[ 25712354]|. 
                     &Delta;<i>cyaA</i> cells also show protection (i.e. reduced
                     death) from phenol- and ciprofloxacin-mediated killing 
                     without affecting the mimimum inhibitory concentration 
                     (MIC) for each antibiotic; complementation by insertion of 
                     plasmid-containing wild-type <i>cyaA</i> occurred for this 
                     phenotype as well as killing from diverse other lethal 
                     stressors |CITS: [35648826]|. Deletion of <i>cyaA</i> 
                     drives heterogeneity of UGA readthrough and biogenesis of 
                     rRNAs and |FRAME: TRP-tRNAs| |CITS: [37670559]|. 
                     <i>cyaA</i> mutants could replace the native<i> Pantoea</i>
                     sp. as essential gut symbionts of the insect |FRAME: 
                     TAX-106108| |CITS: [35927448]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9871918][2699242][2699243][2844115]|"
                     /translation="MYLYIETLKQRLDAINQLRVDRALAAMGPAFQQVYSLLPTLLHYH
