                     /gene="cyaY"
                     /locus_tag="b3807"
                     /product="frataxin CyaY"
                     /label=cyaY
                     /note="CyaY is a protein with similarity to human
                     frataxin, mutations in which are responsible for the 
                     neurodegenerative disease Friedreich's ataxia.  CyaY is 
                     involved in iron-sulfur cluster assembly, where its 
                     function is still not fully understood.  Recent data 
                     support a regulatory role of CyaY, slowing the rate of Fe-S
                     cluster formation by regulating |FRAME: CPLX0-248 IscS| 
                     function.  <i>In vivo</i> under iron-rich growth 
                     conditions, CyaY has a positive effect on Fe-S cluster 
                     biogenesis |CITS: [25430730]|.
                     CyaY specifically interacts with IscS |CITS: 
                     [16603772][20981023]|; this interaction is required for its
                     function in Fe-S cluster formation |CITS: [25430730]|. 
                     There are conflicting results regarding whether |CITS: 
                     [23682711]| or not |CITS: [19305405][20981023]| this 
                     interaction competes with the IscS-IscU interaction.
                     CyaY, IscX |CITS: [20404999][24810328]|, and Fdx |CITS: 
                     [23682711][23839945]| compete for overlapping binding sites
                     on IscS.  <i>In vitro</i>, CyaY can inhibit the formation 
                     of [2Fe-2S] clusters on IscU |CITS: 
                     [19305405][21799759][25688831]| and inhibit IscS cysteine 
                     desulfurase activity |CITS: [21799759]|.  CyaY's ability to
                     inhibit Fe-S cluster formation is dependent on the specific
                     IscS enzyme; both <i>E. coli</i> CyaY and human frataxin 
                     stimulate the human cysteine desulfurase, and both human 
                     frataxin and CyaY inhibit Fe-S cluster formation by the 
                     <i>E. coli</i> ISC system |CITS: [22352884]|.
                     <i>In vitro</i> in the presence of |FRAME: CPLX0-248 IscS| 
                     and cysteine, the Fe<sup>3+</sup>-bound soluble oligomeric 
                     form of CyaY can serve as an iron donor to the scaffold 
                     protein IscU |CITS: [16603772]|.  However, under conditions
                     emulating the intracellular redox potential, CyaY does not 
                     bind iron; only in the presence of 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, CyaY was found to bind iron and
                     attenuate the production of hydroxyl radicals |CITS: 
                     [17244611]|.
                     CyaY interacts with iron and exhibits salt-sensitive 
                     protein aggregation in the presence of iron |CITS: 
                     [12140189]|.  At least two ferrous ions are bound per CyaY 
                     monomer; binding and oxidation kinetics have been studied. 
                     Binding of Fe<sup>3+</sup> promotes aggregation |CITS: 
                     [15276847]|.  The interactions of CyaY with iron and other 
                     metal ions was further studied by NMR and X-ray 
                     crystallography |CITS: [17651435][23344952]|.  In the 
                     presence of a thioredoxin reductase system, CyaY does not 
                     bind iron and does not form aggregate complexes |CITS: 
                     [17244611]|.  CyaY does not bind iron <i>in vivo</i> under 
                     aerobic growth conditions, when |FRAME: EG12132-MONOMER 
                     IscA| does |CITS: [20942799]|.
                     Crystal |CITS: [10908679]| and solution |CITS: [15530368]| 
                     structures of CyaY have been solved.  Features of the CyaY 
                     structure are discussed with respect to corresponding 
                     regions of human frataxin (see <a 
                     href=""https://www.omim.org/entry/606829"">OMIM</a>), 
                     mutations in which are responsible for Friedreich's ataxia 
                     |CITS: [10908679]|.  Comparison with frataxins of other 
                     species shows that the C-terminus is important for thermal 
                     stability of the protein |CITS: [15157084]|.  The iron 
                     binding surface consists of a patch of conserved negatively
                     charged residues |CITS: [15530368]|.
                     CyaY does not have an obvious role in iron homeostasis in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [10452520]|.  CyaY/frataxin-related 
                     proteins in many organisms are phylogenetically associated 
                     with chaperones HscA and HscB, suggesting a conserved role 
                     in assembly of iron-sulfur clusters |CITS: [11689493]|. 
                     Mitochondria-targeted CyaY can complement yeast depleted of
                     its endogenous frataxin |CITS: [17727661]|.  Changing 
                     Ile108 of |FRAME: G7324-MONOMER IscU| to Met, a conserved 
                     amino acid in eukaryotic IscU proteins, renders the Isc 
                     system dependent on CyaY (as it is in eukaryotes) |CITS: 
                     [25996492]|.
                     A <i>cyaY</i> null mutant does not exhibit defects in 
                     growth, iron accumulation, or sensitivity to oxidant 
                     (H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>), compared to wild type |CITS: 
                     [10452520]|.  Only a competitive growth experiment revealed
                     that a &Delta;<i>cyaY</i> mutant is outcompeted by wild 
                     type cells |CITS: [25430730]|.  A <i>cyaY</i> deletion 
                     strain contains reduced amounts of |FRAME: NADH-DHI-CPLX| 
                     and |FRAME: SUCC-DEHASE| in its membrane |CITS: 
                     [17650323]|, and reduced activities of a diverse set of 
                     Fe-S cluster-containing proteins |CITS: [25430730]|.
                     <i>cyaY</i> was shown to be transcribed |CITS: [8874804]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16911956][23298813][25996679][25510311]|; 
                     Comment: |CITS: [20405048]|"
