     gene            3999224..4001053
                     /gene="recQ"
                     /locus_tag="b3822"
                     /label=recQ
     CDS             3999224..4001053
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="recQ"
                     /locus_tag="b3822"
                     /product="bifunctional ATP-dependent DNA helicase RecQ / 
                     ATP-dependent 3' -> 5"" helicase"
                     /EC_number="5.6.2.4"
                     /label=recQ
                     /note="A component of the RecF recombinational pathway,
                     RecQ is a helicase that functions in blocking illegitimate 
                     recombination, enhancing topoisomerase activity, initiating
                     SOS signaling and clearing blocked replication forks.
                     RecQ is an ATP-dependent DNA helicase |CITS: [2164680]|. 
                     Its activity also depends on magnesium ion and 
                     single-strand binding protein (SSB) |CITS: 
                     [11024023][8387604]|. Its Km for dsDNA is 0.5-0.9 &mu;M 
                     (measured in concentration of base pairs), with a Kcat of 
                     2.3-2.7 base pairs/s/RecQ monomer and optimal activity at 
                     0.05 mM magnesium ion |CITS: [11024023]|. Cooperativity of 
                     ATP binding suggests a multimeric active form, but ample 
                     evidence indicates that RecQ functions as a monomer |CITS: 
                     [11024023][12805371]|. Based on fluorescence titration, one
                     RecQ binds per ten nucleotides of substrate DNA, and 
                     single-stranded and duplex DNA bind competitively |CITS: 
                     [14665634]|. Fluorescence evaluation has also been used to 
                     determine the dependence of DNA unwinding rate on the 
                     concentration of RecQ and magnesium ion |CITS: [16143813]|.
                     As it carries out its helicase activity, RecQ also 
                     stimulates topoisomerase III, leading to enhanced 
                     supercoiling and catenation of covalently closed DNA |CITS:
                     [10360177][12909639]|.
                     RecQ suppresses illegitimate recombination that occurs via 
                     regions of short homology, as well as spontaneous and 
                     UV-induced recombination |CITS: 
                     [15476890][9108069][10811888]|. <i>In vitro</i>, RecQ can 
                     initiate homologous recombination in the company of RecA 
                     and SSB, but also unwinds RecA-formed recombinational 
                     junctions, disrupting recombination |CITS: [9553043]|. RecQ
                     binds blocked replication forks, specifically binding forks
                     with leading strand gaps more efficiently than those with 
                     lagging strand gaps or Holliday junctions. It then unwinds 
                     the duplex DNA template and the lagging strand, generating 
                     a gap on the lagging strand |CITS: 
                     [10589843][12543983][15289460]|. RecQ appears to be the 
                     major factor controlling replication fork reversal at a 
                     stalled replication fork; <i>recQ</i> mutants 
                     (&Delta;<i>recQ</i>) show a diminished ability to reverse 
                     replication forks at a nucleoprotein block <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [30466158]|.
                     RecQ consists of a large (59 kD) amino-terminal domain that
                     contains its helicase activity and a smaller (9 kD) 
                     carboxy-terminal domain that contains its DNA-binding 
                     activity |CITS: [12771204]|. Crystal structures of the 
                     catalytic core have been determined to 1.8 &Aring; 
                     resolution in the unbound state and to 2.5 &Aring; 
                     resolution in the ATP-bound state. The core consists of 
                     four subdomains, two of which make up the helicase region, 
                     the other two forming zinc-binding motifs |CITS: 
                     [14517231]|. This zinc-binding region is required for 
                     protein stability and DNA binding, but not for enzymatic 
                     activity |CITS: [15292213]|. The crystal structure of the 
                     carboxy-terminal Helicase-and-RNase-D-C-terminal (HRDC) 
                     domain has also been determined, and has a globular fold 
                     similar to other DNA binding domains |CITS: [16084389]|. 
                     The DNA binding domains support sensing and response to DNA
                     geometry during DNA restructuring reactions and contribute 
                     to quality control of homologous recombination by 
                     disruption of harmful illegitimate recombination 
                     (recombination between non-allelic loci) |CITS: 
                     [28069956]|.
                     Loss of <i>recQ</i> yields thymineless death and deficiency
                     in the RecF recombination pathway, as well as inhibition
                     of the SOS response |CITS: [2993821][15289460]|.  A triple 
                     mutant lacking the helicases RecQ, HelD and UvrD is 
                     deficient in homologous recombination and recombinational 
                     DNA repair |CITS: [7868608]|. A mutation in RecQ increases 
                     the <i>in vivo</i> mutation rate influencing genetic 
                     instability associated with some G4 structures |CITS: 
                     [37761860]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [15922367][25131811]|"
