     gene            4012984..4013589
                     /gene="ubiJ"
                     /locus_tag="b3834"
                     /label=ubiJ
     CDS             4012984..4013589
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ubiJ"
                     /locus_tag="b3834"
                     /product="bifunctional ubiquinone biosynthesis accessory
                     factor UbiJ / Ubi complex"
                     /label=ubiJ
                     /note="UbiJ is an accessory factor in ubiquinone
                     biosynthesis that is part of the Ubi complex metabolon 
                     |CITS: [30686758]|.  The C terminus of UbiJ interacts 
                     directly with UbiK; the isolated proteins appear to form a 
                     2:1 UbiK:UbiJ heterotrimer that is able to bind 
                     palmitoleate |CITS: [28559279]|, ubiquinone-8, 
                     2-octaprenylphenol, and C6-demethoxy-ubiquinone-8 |CITS: 
                     [30686758]|.  The N-terminal SCP2 domain of UbiJ is 
                     responsible for lipid binding |CITS: [30686758]|.
                     UbiJ predominantly localizes to the cytosol.  The seven 
                     ubiquinone biosynthesis proteins UbiE, UbiF, UbiG, UbiH, 
                     UbiI, UbiJ, and UbiK form a 1 MDa complex in the cytosol; 
                     UbiJ appears to be required for stability of the complex 
                     |CITS: [30686758]|.  The purified SCP2 domain of UbiJ 
                     homodimerizes; crystal structures of this domain have been 
                     solved |CITS: [30686758]|.
                     Although |CITS: [16738554]| did not recover a <i>yigP</i> 
                     (<i>ubiJ</i>) null mutant strain, |CITS: [24142253]| was 
                     able to construct such a mutant.  Under aerobic growth 
                     conditions, this <i>ubiJ</i> mutant has a growth defect and
                     does not contain any ubiquinone-8.  A plasmid encoding the 
                     C-terminal 50 amino acids of UbiJ can rescue the growth 
                     defect |CITS: [24142253]|.  A <i>ubiJ</i> mutant 
                     accumulates octaprenylphenol |CITS: [28559279]|. 
                     <i>ubiJ</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     The <i>ubiE</i>, <i>ubiJ</i>, and <i>ubiB</i> genes are 
                     cotranscribed |CITS: [10960098]|.  <i>ubiJ</i> appears to 
                     be expressed constitutively |CITS: [28559279]|.
                     Mutants that separate the potential functions of 
                     <i>ubiJ</i> and |FRAME: G0-10737 <i>esrE</i>|, a small RNA 
                     encoded within the 3' terminus of <i>ubiJ</i>, have been 
                     constructed |CITS: [31553492]|."
