                     /label=tatA
     CDS             4015305..4015574
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tatA"
                     /locus_tag="b3836"
                     /product="bifunctional twin arginine protein translocation
                     system - TatA protein / twin arginine protein translocation
                     system"
                     /label=tatA
                     /note="TatA is an inner membrane component of the twin
                     arginine translocation (Tat) complex for the export of 
                     folded proteins. Homo-oligomeric TatA may form the 
                     translocation pore of the complex although other models 
                     have been also suggested |CITS: [12608575][23471988]|. TatA
                     is predicted to consist of an N-terminal transmembrane 
                     domain followed by an amphipathic helix and a cytoplasmic 
                     domain |CITS: [9649434]|. The topology of TatA has been 
                     debated with studies variously suggesting that TatA is 
                     exposed at the cytoplasmic face of the membrane |CITS: 
                     [16973610][12427031]|, that the N-terminus is located in 
                     the periplasm while the C-terminal region adopts a dual 
                     topology |CITS: [14701831]|, that the N-terminus is located
                     in the cytoplasm and the C-terminus adopts a dual topology 
                     |CITS: [17536842]| or that it has a fixed 
                     N<sub>out</sub>:C<sub>in</sub> topology |CITS: [22399293]|.
                     TatA is an integral inner membrane protein; overexpressed 
                     TatA lacking the transmembrane domain does not associate 
                     with the inner membrane; TatA forms homo-oligomers; higher 
                     order Tat complexes may be assembled from homo-oligomeric 
                     TatA (and TatB) units |CITS: [11591389]|. The N-terminal 
                     &alpha; helix is required for stable TatA 
                     self-interactions; TatA lacking the N-terminal &alpha; 
                     helix is able to interact with a phospholipid monolayer 
                     |CITS: [12427031]|.
                     TatA complexes purified from a TatA overproducing strain 
                     form ring-shaped particles of variable diameter enclosing a
                     central channel which is open at one end; the complexes 
                     contain a variable number of subunits ranging from 12 to 35
                     depending on the method of calculation |CITS: [16027357]|. 
                     In cells overexpressing <i>tatABC</i>, TatA forms 
                     homo-multimeric tube structures in the cytoplasm which can 
                     interact with one another to form higher order structures; 
                     TatA needs TatC to form detectable amounts of tube 
                     structures |CITS: [18644791]|. When expressed from its 
                     native promoter TatA forms homo-oligomeric complexes of 
                     variable stoichiometry (average number of subunits is 35); 
                     large complexes do not form in cells lacking <i>tatBC</i>; 
                     complex formation is not affected upon disruption of the 
                     proton motive force |CITS: [18832162]|. TatA can exist in 
                     both dispersed and higher order oligomer structures; TatA 
                     association is triggered by substrate availability; TatA 
                     assembly requires the PMF and the presence of TatBC |CITS: 
                     [24003141]|
                     In the oligomeric TatA complex, TatA subunits form a ring 
                     of transmembrane helices around a central channel with the 
                     amphipathic helices extending outwards |CITS: [23471988]|. 
                     Inter-subunit contacts between the transmembrane helices 
                     and between neighbouring amphipathic helices contribute to 
                     the TatA oligomeric interface |CITS: 
                     [16973610][23471988][25090434]|.
                     TatA can bind Tat substrate molecules both <i>in vivo</i> 
                     and <i>in vitro</i>; binding is not dependent on the 
                     presence of a twin-arginine motif and does not require the 
                     TatBC components |CITS: [25774531]|.
                     TatA (and TatE) interacts with |FRAME: EG10776-MONOMER 
                     ""phage shock protein A""|; PspA is not directly required 
                     for Tat transport - it may act to counterbalance membrane 
                     instability caused by Tat transport |CITS: [22689583]| 
                     <U>m</U>embrane <U>t</U>argeting and <U>t</U>ranslocation 
                     |CITS: [9546395]|<BR><U>t</U>win <U>a</U>rginine 
                     <U>t</U>ranslocation |CITS: [9649434]|"
