     gene            4015578..4016093
                     /gene="tatB"
                     /locus_tag="b3838"
                     /label=tatB
     CDS             4015578..4016093
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tatB"
                     /locus_tag="b3838"
                     /product="bifunctional twin arginine protein translocation
                     system - TatB protein / twin arginine protein translocation
                     system"
                     /label=tatB
                     /note="TatB is an inner membrane component of the twin
                     arginine translocation (Tat) complex for the export of 
                     folded proteins; TatB and TatC form a functional unit that 
                     is thought to act as the substrate receptor for the Tat 
                     complex.
                     TatB is predicted to contain an N-terminal transmembrane 
                     domain followed by a cytoplasmic domain |CITS: [9649434]|. 
                     TatB is an integral inner membrane protein; overexpressed 
                     TatB lacking the transmembrane domain remains peripherally 
                     associated with the inner membrane; TatB forms 
                     homooligomers; higher order Tat complexes may be assembled 
                     from homooligomeric TatB (and TatA) units |CITS: 
                     [11591389]|. Purified TatB (residues 1-101) consists of 4 
                     &alpha; helices (one transmembrane helix, one amphipathic 
                     helix and two solvent exposed helices) and adopts an 
                     extended 'L-shaped' structure |CITS: [24699374]|.
                     TatB interacts with a Tat substrate signal sequence, this 
                     interaction is dependent on the presence of an RR motif and
                     TatC |CITS: [14580344]| <i>In vitro</i> assays suggest that
                     TatB forms an oligomeric binding site that contacts folded 
                     Tat substrates in an intermediate binding step that 
                     precedes translocation |CITS: [20926683]|.
                     <U>m</U>embrane <U>t</U>argeting and <U>t</U>ranslocation 
                     |CITS: [9546395]|<BR><U>t</U>win <U>a</U>rginine 
                     <U>t</U>ranslocation |CITS: [9649434]|
                     Note: the D-43 mutation identified by |CITS: [9546395]| 
                     which prevents targeting and translocation of several 
                     periplasmic redox enzymes is located in <i>tatB</i> |CITS: 
                     [9649434]|"
