     gene            4016914..4017696
                     /gene="tatD"
                     /locus_tag="b4483"
                     /label=tatD
     CDS             4016914..4017696
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tatD"
                     /locus_tag="b4483"
                     /product="trifunctional 3' -> 5' ssDNA/RNA  exonuclease
                     TatD / 3' -> 5' RNA exonuclease / 3' -> 5' ssDNA 
                     exonuclease"
                     /EC_number="3.1.11.1"
                     /label=tatD
                     /note="TatD is a magnesium dependent 3' - 5' exonuclease
                     with a preference for single strand DNA and RNA. A 
                     <i>tatD</i> knockout mutant is more sensitive to 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> than wild type. Purified TatD 
                     has efficient exonuclease activity on ssDNA containing a 3'
                     terminal deaminated base (uracil or hypoxanthine). TatD may
                     be involved in the repair of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     induced DNA damage |CITS: [25114049]|.
                     Purified, crystallised TatD has a TIM barrel fold (an eight
                     stranded &alpha; &beta; barrel). The conserved residues 
                     Glu91, Glu201 and Asp 203 are involved in metal binding at 
                     the active site |CITS: [25114049]|.
                     TatD exhibits magnesium-dependent DNase activity |CITS: 
                     [10747959]|.  A <i>tatD</i> mutant shows increased RecA-GFP
                     foci formation |CITS: [18034795]|.
                     Deletion of <i>tatD</i> has very little effect on growth 
                     |CITS: [11389150]|.  A <i>tatD ycfH yjjV</i> triple mutant 
                     does not exhibit phenotypes that would suggest involvement 
                     of the corresponding proteins in the Sec-independent Tat 
                     protein export system.  Like a <i>tatD</i> deletion, TatD 
                     overproduction does not cause transport phenotypes |CITS: 
                     [10747959]|.  In a <i>tatD</i> mutant, mutant forms of Tat 
                     export system substrates were seen to accumulate instead of
                     being degraded rapidly |CITS: [19343049]|; however, this 
                     result may have been due to inadvertent overexpression of 
                     the Tat export substrates |CITS: [20659466]|.  A 
                     <i>tatD</i> deletion mutant is more sensitive to 
                     kasugamycin than wild type |CITS: [23308060]|.
                     <i>tatD</i> appears to be transcribed at lower levels than 
                     <i>tatABC</i> |CITS: [10747959]|.
                     MttC: ""<b>m</b>embrane <b>t</b>argeting and 
                     <b>t</b>ranslocation"" |CITS: [9546395]|
                     TatD: ""<b>t</b>win <b>a</b>rginine <b>t</b>ranslocation"" 
                     |CITS: [9649434]|"
