     gene            complement(4017693..4018181)
                     /gene="rfaH"
                     /locus_tag="b3842"
                     /label=rfaH
     CDS             complement(4017693..4018181)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rfaH"
                     /locus_tag="b3842"
                     /product="transcription antiterminator RfaH"
                     /label=rfaH
                     /note="RfaH acts as a transcriptional antiterminator for a 
                     subset of operons required for the production of 
                     extracellular components such as the lipopolysaccharide 
                     core and other virulence factors |CITS: 
                     [7007352][8606157][8951819][35258322]|. It is an allosteric
                     modulator of RNA polymerase (RNAP), reducing polymerase 
                     pausing at certain positions |CITS: [17711918]|. RfaH 
                     binding to the |FRAME: RPOC-MONOMER &beta;'| clamp helices 
                     (&beta;'CH) and the |FRAME: RPOB-MONOMER &beta;| subunit 
                     gate loop (&beta; GL) of RNAP results in a stabilizing 
                     bridge with the DNA that allows the RNAP to resist pausing 
                     and termination signals |CITS: [21777814]|.
                     RfaH is a so-called ""transformer protein"": its C-terminal
                     domain can assume an &alpha;-helical or a &beta;-barrel 
                     structure, and the two conformations have different 
                     functions |CITS: [23131843][39324667]|.
                     RfaH homologs are found in Gammaproteobacteria, with the 
                     highest abundance in Enterobacteriaceae |CITS: [35258322]|.
                     RfaH is a specialized paralog of the essential |FRAME: 
                     EG10667-MONOMER|; it modulates a subset of genes that 
                     counter NusG's transcriptional pausing and termination 
                     activity |CITS: [8951819]|. RfaH and NusG compete for the 
                     same binding site on RNAP <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [19096362]|. RfaH interaction with RNAP abolishes sigma 
                     factor-dependent pausing, likely because it interacts with 
                     a site adjacent to the &beta;' subunit clamp helix site 
                     that may interact with &sigma; during elongation |CITS: 
                     [18195372]|. RfaH is able to reverse a non-productive RNAP 
                     trigger loop conformation that stabilizes paused RNAP 
                     complexes |CITS: [23769674]| and stabilizes the closed 
                     clamp conformation of RNAP, inhibiting formation of an exit
                     channel RNA duplex that would lead to RNAP pausing |CITS: 
                     [24275665]|. RfaH decreases &rho;-mediated polarity |CITS: 
                     [21777814]|.
                     The so-called JUMPStart sequence which is present in the 5'
                     UTRs of RfaH-enhanced operons is associated with 
                     transcriptional pausing |CITS: [9171395]|. The <i>ops</i> 
                     (operon polarity suppressor) element, a short conserved 
                     sequence, is a Class II pause signal |CITS: [10860976]| 
                     that directs recruitment of RfaH into a paused RNAP complex
                     that subsequently resists transcription termination |CITS: 
                     [10660066][12007406]|. The <i>ops</i> sequence on the 
                     partially accessible nontemplate strand forms a hairpin 
                     structure with a flipped-out T residue that is required for
                     recognition by RfaH |CITS: [12007406][29741479]|. RfaH then
                     binds to RNAP while inhibiting escape from the <i>ops</i> 
                     pause site, but subsequently suppresses pausing downstream 
                     |CITS: [12007406]| by stabilizing the upstream edge of the 
                     transcription bubble |CITS: [29757477]|. RfaH also binds 
                     the small RNA |FRAME: RNA0-390 RirA| in the presence of 
                     RNAP; this interaction depends on intact <i>ops</i> sites 
                     within RirA |CITS: [27629414]|.
                     RfaH countersilences genes that are silenced by |FRAME: 
                     PD00288|, |FRAME: EG10439-MONOMER| and the |FRAME: 
                     G6869-MONOMER| (YdgT) in a Rho-dependent way |CITS: 
                     [35258322]|. RfaH and YdgT may be a specific 
                     silencing/countersilencing pair of proteins that regulate 
                     xenogene transcription elongation, because both are 
                     commonly found in many species |CITS: [35258322]|.
                     A crystal structure of RfaH has been solved |CITS: 
                     [17012804][17434131]|. The protein consists of a globular 
                     N-terminal domain (NTD) with similarity to NusG, and an 
                     &alpha;-helical C-terminal domain (CTD) that caps the 
                     hydrophobic cavity of the NTD. Cap opening is required for 
                     RfaH activity. The NTD mediates RNAP pausing, while the CTD
                     is required for <i>ops</i> sequence specificity |CITS: 
                     [17434131]|. Cryo-EM structures of RfaH with the RNA 
                     polymerase elongation complex enabled visualization of its 
                     interaction with the <i>ops</i> sequence and modeling of 
                     its effect on transcription |CITS: [29887376]|.
                     Analysis of single-residue substitutions in the NTD allowed
                     separation of its functional regions for interaction with 
                     the <i>ops</i> site, pausing at <i>ops</i> sites, and 
                     subsequent anti-pausing activity <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [20132437]|. Interaction of the NTD with its <i>ops</i> DNA
                     target releases the CTD, which then switches from its 
                     &alpha;-helical conformation to an all-&beta; conformation 
                     that is similar to the NusG CTD. In the all-&beta; 
                     conformation, the CTD can interact with |FRAME: 
                     EG10909-MONOMER S10| and activate translation |CITS: 
                     [22817892]|.
                     The conformation of the CTD is determined by the 
                     availability and ability to interact with the NTD |CITS: 
                     [23990324]|. The reversible, conformational switch of the 
                     CTD has been investigated by molecular dynamics, all-atom 
                     Monte Carlo simulations and contact-based analysis of local
                     energetic frustration dynamics |CITS: [24758259][254 
                     83894][26230837][26033324][26374226][29323497][30537824][31
                     810657][32996201][39324667]|. Simulation of the 
                     &alpha;-to-&beta; conformational transition in the context 
                     of the full protein showed that weakening of the 
                     interdomain contacts triggers the structural transition, 
                     which proceeds through an obligate intermediate |CITS: 
                     [26230837][26374226]|. The intrinsically disordered linker 
                     region between the NTD and CTD plays a role in stabilizing 
                     the closed conformation |CITS: [27555285]|. The conserved 
                     residues Ile93 and Phe130 at the interface between the NTD 
                     and CTD stabilize their interaction; replacement with 
                     residues specific for NusG proteins destabilizes the 
                     interaction and relaxes RfaH's requirement for <i>ops</i> 
                     sequence interaction |CITS: [28605514]|. NMR spectroscopy 
                     confirmed a functional cycle for RfaH: the interaction with
                     <i>ops</i>-paused RNAP causes opening of RfaH and refolding
                     of the CTD into the all-&beta; conformation, which enables 
                     interaction with the ribosome; dissociation of the RNAP 
                     elongation complex and release of RfaH leads to refolding 
                     of the CTD into the &alpha; configuration |CITS: 
                     [30742024][37991741]|. The backbone dynamics of the core 
                     Tudor domain of RfaH were determined by NMR, showing 
                     mobility across all timescales |CITS: [34197736]|. The 
                     transition path of the CTD between all-&alpha; to 
                     all-&beta; undergoes a global unfolding transition through 
                     a molten globule-like nucleus formation |CITS: [35438983]|.
                     Coevolutionary sequence analysis predicted contacts for the
                     metamorphic behavior of the autoinhibited (&alpha;) and 
                     active (&beta;) folds in the RfaH protein subfamily |CITS: 
                     [35634768]|. Structural modelling analysis focused on a 
                     change in a hairpin conformation within the NTD affected 
                     CTD stability and reverse fold switching as well as 
                     pointing to the potential optimal temperature for fastest 
                     fold switching |CITS: [39400465]|. RfaH participates in 
                     controlling several genes involved in the biosynthesis, 
                     assembly, and export of the lipopolysaccharide core |CITS: 
                     [6230355][1655711]|, F-factor sex pili |CITS: [383690]|, 
                     hemolysin |CITS: [1584020][8606157]|, and indirectly (via 
                     MicA and &sigma;<sup>E</sup>) the |FRAME: EG10309-MONOMER 
                     FimB| recombinase |CITS: [24163336]|. Loss of RfaH 
                     attenuates virulence of pathogenic strains of <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [12117951][15727837]|. An <i>rfaH</i> 
                     mutant has an increased-biofilm phenotype in both the 
                     nonpathogenic K-12 strain and in the uropathogenic strain 
                     536 |CITS: [16452414]| and shows decreased persistence in 
                     the presence of tosufloxacin |CITS: [33117736]|. Extragenic
                     suppressor mutations that enable a &Delta;<i>rfaH</i> 
                     mutant to grow in the presence of sodium dodecyl sulfate 
                     map to <i>rho</i>, <i>rpoC</i>, and <i>hns</i> |CITS: 
                     [28559482]|. Unlike NusG, RfaH occurs mostly in the 
                     <i>Proteobacteria</i> and regulates the expression of a 
                     limited number of genes |CITS: [33109766]|.
                     Small-molecule inhibitors of RfaH binding to RNA polymerase
                     have been identified |CITS: [30069925]|.
                     SfrB: ""<b>s</b>ex <b>f</b>actor <b>r</b>egulation"" |CITS:
                     [383690]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [7504166][9426123][11983161][22817886][230 
                     95672][24632072][25640595][30808693][31137816][33176159][34
                     570660]|<br> Comment: |CITS: [29757477]|"
