     gene            4019887..4020588
                     /gene="fre"
                     /locus_tag="b3844"
                     /label=fre
     CDS             4019887..4020588
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fre"
                     /locus_tag="b3844"
                     /product="multifunctional NAD(P)H-flavin reductase /
                     riboflavin reductase / FMN reductase / FAD reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.5.1.30; 1.5.1.41; 1.5.1.39; 1.5.1.37"
                     /label=fre
                     /note="Flavin reductase (Fre) catalyzes the reduction of
                     free flavins (riboflavin, FMN, or FAD) by NADPH or NADH 
                     |CITS: [3305505]|.  The enzyme appears to be an important 
                     physiological source of superoxide radicals |CITS: 
                     [8132544]|.
                     The enzyme follows a sequential ordered reaction mechanism,
                     with binding of NADPH followed by flavin binding |CITS: 
                     [8530465]|.  After both NADPH and riboflavin are bound, a 
                     charge-transfer complex of NADPH and riboflavin is formed 
                     as the first intermediate, followed by a second 
                     charge-transfer intermediate of enzyme-bound 
                     NADP<sup>+</sup> and reduced riboflavin |CITS: [9718311]|. 
                     The enzyme is A-side-specific, selectively transfering the 
                     pro-R hydrogen from the C-4 position of the nicotinamide 
                     group |CITS: [10373427]|. Flavin reductase utilizes flavins
                     only as substrates, not as cofactors.  The interaction of 
                     the enzyme with its flavin substrates has been studied 
                     |CITS: [8530465]|.  FAD appears to interact with Fre at a 
                     site overlapping with the NAD(P)H-binding site, binding 
                     more tightly than either FMN or riboflavin.  In the 
                     presence of both FMN and FAD, Fre preferentially reduces 
                     FAD; FAD thereby inhibits Fre activity at low NADH 
                     concentrations |CITS: [12177066]|. Fre interacts with the 
                     |FRAME: G7060-MONOMER|; Fre uses the MsrQ FMN cofactor as a
                     substrate to catalyse reduction of the MsrQ heme in the 
                     presence of NADH; the Fre-MsrQ interaction is a 
                     two-component protein system that together with |FRAME: 
                     G7059-MONOMER MsrP| serves to repair the oxidized 
                     methionine residues of functionally diverse periplasmic 
                     proteins |CITS: [34550690][28028176][26641313]|.
                     Although Cys5, Cys149 and Cys214 are not required for 
                     enzymatic activity, they are responsible for 
                     <i>N</i>-ethylmaleimide inactivation of the enzyme |CITS: 
                     [8647136]|.  The Ser49 residue is important for recognition
                     of the isoalloxazine ring of flavins |CITS: [8663185]|.  A 
                     crystal structure of the enzyme has been solved, showing 
                     structural similarity to the ferredoxin reductase family of
                     flavoproteins |CITS: [10353815]|.  The conformational 
                     dynamics of the Fre-FAD complex has been studied |CITS: 
                     [14551431][16880391]|.
                     Flavin reductase is part of the ribonucleoside-diphosphate 
                     reductase activating system that is able to regenerate the 
                     tyrosyl radical of |FRAME: 
                     RIBONUCLEOSIDE-DIP-REDUCTI-CPLX|.  The system is composed 
                     of three proteins, flavin reductase, |FRAME: 
                     SUPEROX-DISMUTMN-CPLX| and a protein fraction named 
                     fraction b whose function is poorly defined |CITS: 
                     [3517866][3305505]|.  In a <i>fre</i> mutant, |FRAME: 
                     SULFITE-REDUCT-CPLX| can substitute for this activity 
                     |CITS: [8360156]|.
                     In the presence of a flavin, Fre also acts as a ferric 
                     reductase, reducing Fe<sup>+++</sup> to Fe<sup>++</sup>; 
                     however, it is the reduced flavin rather than the Fre 
                     enzyme that donates the electron for reduction of 
                     Fe<sup>+++</sup> |CITS: [3305505][2186712][8436123]|. Again
                     in the presence of a flavin, Fre is able to reduce chromate
                     to a soluble and stable Cr(III)-NAD<sup>+</sup> complex 
                     |CITS: [12054743]|.  Overexpression of Fre leads to 
                     increased sensitivity to H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> under 
                     anaerobic conditions.  Addition of cyanide has a 
                     synergistic effect, which is due a block in respiration 
                     that leads to accumulation of NADH.  NADH can then be used 
                     by Fre to produce FADH<sub>2</sub>, which in turn 
                     efficiently reduces Fe<sup>+++</sup> to Fe<sup>++</sup>. 
                     Fe<sup>++</sup> reacts with H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> to 
                     produce hydroxyl radicals which damage DNA |CITS: 
                     [12080063]|.
                     <i>fre</i> was initially thought to be identical to 
                     <i>ubiB</i> |CITS: [1379743]|, but it was later shown that 
                     <i>fre</i> is not involved in ubiquinone synthesis |CITS: 
                     [10960098]|.
                     <i>fre</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     <i>fre</i> shows differential codon adaptation, resulting 
                     in differential translation efficiency signatures, in 
                     aerotolerant compared to obligate anaerobic microbes.  It 
                     was therefore predicted to play a role in the oxidative 
                     stress response.  An <i>fre</i> deletion mutant was shown 
                     to be more sensitive than wild-type specifically to 
                     hydrogen peroxide exposure, but not other stresses |CITS: 
                     [24580753]|.
                     Fre: ""<b>f</b>lavin <b>re</b>ductase"" |CITS: [2050627]| 
                     Review: |CITS: [8118169]|; Comment: |CITS: [14551425]|"
