     gene            complement(4022142..4024331)
                     /gene="fadB"
                     /locus_tag="b3846"
                     /label=fadB
     CDS             complement(4022142..4024331)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fadB"
                     /locus_tag="b3846"
                     /product="multifunctional multifunctional fatty acid
                     oxidation complex subunit alpha / 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA 
                     dehydrogenase / enoyl-CoA hydratase / 
                     Delta(3)-cis-Delta(2)-trans-enoyl-CoA isomerase / 
                     3-hydroxyacyl-CoA epimerase / trifunctional fatty acid 
                     oxidation complex"
                     /EC_number="1.1.1.35; 4.2.1.17; 4.2.1.74; 5.3.3.8; 5.1.2.3"
                     /label=fadB
                     /note="FadB is a multifunctional enzyme that is involved
                     in the degradation of fatty acids via the &beta;-oxidation 
                     cycle. Four enzymatic activities are associated with FadB: 
                     enoyl-CoA hydratase, 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA epimerase, 
                     3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase, and 
                     &Delta;<sup>3</sup>-<i>cis</i>- 
                     &Delta;<sup>2</sup>-<i>trans</i>-enoyl-CoA isomerase |CITS:
                     [3286611]|.
                     The 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA epimerase (EC 5.1.2.3) reaction 
                     occurs by a dehydration/hydration mechanism |CITS: 
                     [1748662]|.  The enoyl-CoA hydratase (EC 4.2.1.17) and 
                     3-hydroxyacyl-CoA epimerase reactions are catalyzed by the 
                     same active site within the N-terminal domain of FadB. A 
                     Gly116Phe mutation eliminates both activities |CITS: 
                     [8454629]|. The protonated &gamma;-carboxylate of Glu139 
                     |CITS: [7756275]| and deprotonated &gamma;-carboxylate of 
                     Glu119 |CITS: [9283097]| provide acid/base functional 
                     groups for dehydration. A reaction mechanism was proposed 
                     |CITS: [9283097]|.
                     The L-3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase (EC 1.1.1.35) 
                     activity maps to the C-terminal domain of FadB. A His435Gln
                     mutation specifically eliminates 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA 
                     dehydrogenase activity |CITS: [8755745]|. Alanine-scanning 
                     mutagenesis of additional acidic residues showed that 
                     Glu462 is required for catalysis |CITS: [8993342]|. The 
                     <i>fadB64</i> allele also leads to a specific defect in the
                     3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase activity of FadB |CITS: 
                     [6402028]|.
                     FadA and FadB form a multifunctional enzyme complex |CITS: 
                     [334745][374410][6402028][3286611][8454629]| (data from 
                     <i>E. coli</i> B in |CITS: [322129][7012144][6350283]|). 
                     Overexpression of FadA and FadB can be utilized for the 
                     production of long-chain fatty acids in an engineered 
                     reversal of the &beta;-oxidation cycle |CITS: 
                     [21832992][23656231]|.
                     Expression of the enzymes involved in &beta;-oxidation is 
                     normally induced by fatty acids of chain length 14 and 
                     longer |CITS: [4861587][4886296][18317523]|. Regulation is 
                     at the transcriptional level and involves repression by 
                     FadR |CITS: [6271734][1569108]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10709637][11263873]|"
