     gene            4026505..4027956
                     /gene="trkH"
                     /locus_tag="b3849"
                     /label=trkH
     CDS             4026505..4027956
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="trkH"
                     /locus_tag="b3849"
                     /product="K(+) transporter TrkH"
                     /label=trkH
                     /note="TrkH is a Na<sup>+</sup>-independent potassium ion 
                     transporter that functions as a major K<sup>+</sup>-uptake 
                     system; TrkH-mediated potassium uptake is able to support 
                     growth in low K<sup>+</sup> medium (0.1mM K<sup>+</sup>) in
                     the absence of other potassium uptake systems |CITS: 
                     [36586436]|.
                     Early studies in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 identified four 
                     separate K<sup>+</sup> uptake systems: two constitutive 
                     systems [TrkA (also called Trk) and TrkD (also called 
                     Kup)], a high affinity system (Kdp) and a non-saturable 
                     system TrkF |CITS: [4578]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [320207][359759][6405784][3896791]|. Although initially 
                     thought to be a single system TrkA/Trk activity was later 
                     shown to result from the activity of two K<sup>+</sup> 
                     transporters which were named |FRAME: TRKG-MONOMER 
                     ""TrkG""| and |FRAME: TRKH-MONOMER ""TrkH""| |CITS: 
                     [1987159][7896723]|.
                     <i>trkG trkH</i> double null mutations abolish saturable 
                     K<sup>+</sup> uptake in a strain lacking |FRAME: 
                     ATPASE-1-CPLX ""Kdp""| and |FRAME: KUP-MONOMER ""TrkD""| 
                     mediated potassium transport |CITS: [1987159]|. TrkG and 
                     TrkH show 41% amino acid identity; inactivation of either 
                     <I>trkH</I> or <I>trkG</I> (in a &Delta;<i>kdpFABC 
                     trkD1</i> background) affects the kinetics of potassium 
                     uptake |CITS: [7896723]|. TrkH and TrkG are both active as 
                     low affinity, high rate potassium ion transporters |CITS: 
                     [1987159][7896723]| and function in conjunction with 
                     |FRAME: TRKA-MONOMER ""TrkA""|, a peripheral membrane 
                     protein that binds NAD<sup>+</sup> and is essential for 
                     TrkH/G activity |CITS: [2674131] [8412700]|. |FRAME: 
                     EG12304 ""TrkE""| (also known as SapD), an ATP binding 
                     protein, is required for TrkH activity and affects the 
                     kinetics of TrkG activity |CITS: [1987159], [11700350]|. In
                     the Transporter Classification Database TrkH and TrkG are 
                     members of the K<sup>+</sup> transporter (Trk) family 
                     within the VIC superfamily |CITS: [26546518]|.
                     TrkH is an inner membrane protein with 10 predicted 
                     transmembrane regions |CITS: [7896723]|. TrkH is predicted 
                     to contain 4 sequential MPM (membrane-pore-membrane) 
                     regions plus two additional TM regions |CITS: [10423425]| 
                     (see also |CITS: [40662853]|).
                     Trk mediated K<sup>+</sup> transport in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     requires both ATP and the proton motive force |CITS: 
                     [320207]| however Trk systems are not energised directly by
                     ATP |CITS: [3886836]|.
                     A strain lacking all 4 major K<sup>+</sup> uptake systems 
                     (|FRAME: ATPASE-1-CPLX ""Kdp""|, |FRAME: KUP-MONOMER 
                     ""Kup""|, |FRAME: TRKG-MONOMER ""TrkG""|, and |FRAME: 
                     TRKH-MONOMER ""TrkH""|) can only grow in medium 
                     supplemented with 30mM K<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [36586436]|.
                     A full understanding of the mechanism of Trk mediated 
                     K<sup>+</sup> transport remains outstanding; structural and
                     functional studies of the TrkH K<sup>+</sup> transporter 
                     from <i>Vibrio parahaemolyticus</i> (see for example |CITS:
                     [21317882][23598339][25011047][31992706]| indicate that it 
                     assembles with the regulatory protein TrkA and operates as 
                     an ion channel.
                     <i>trk</i>: <U>tr</U>ansport of <U>K</U> |CITS: [4942756 
                     ]|"
