     gene            4036778..4037404
                     /gene="dsbA"
                     /locus_tag="b3860"
                     /label=dsbA
     CDS             4036778..4037404
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dsbA"
                     /locus_tag="b3860"
                     /product="thiol:disulfide oxidoreductase DsbA"
                     /label=dsbA
                     /note="DsbA is a periplasmic thiol:disulfide
                     oxidoreductase that promotes protein disulfide bond 
                     formation in <i>E. coli</i> K-12. DsbA contains a redox 
                     active disulfide bond (Cys30-Cys33) that is catalytically 
                     transferred via disulfide exchange to a diverse range of 
                     newly translocated protein substrates.
                     Purified DsbA catalyses disulfide bond formation in <i>in 
                     vitro</i> translated alkaline phosphatase and stimulates 
                     the refolding of reduced bovine RNaseA |CITS: [1429594]|. 
                     DsbA catalyses disulfide reduction in the presence of mild 
                     reducing agents <i>in vitro</i> - purified DsbA accelerates
                     the reduction of insulin in the presence of dithiothreitol 
                     |CITS: [1934062]|. <i>dsbA</i> null mutants 
                     (<i>dsbA</i>::kan1; <i>ppfA</i>::Tn5) show defects in 
                     protein disulfide bond formation |CITS: 
                     [1934062][1740115]|. A <i>dsbA</i> null mutant is defective
                     in the synthesis of anaerobically induced <i>c</i>-type 
                     cytochromes |CITS: [7487336]|. <i>dsbA</i> is non-essential
                     in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [1934062]|. DsbA may have 
                     >300 <i>in vivo</i> substrates; experimentally determined 
                     substrates include PhoA, DppA, LivJ, HisJ, RNase I, DegP, 
                     FlgI, MepA, YggN and GltI |CITS: [14726535]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [8503954][14739460]|). The intramolecular disulfide 
                     bond of DsbA is very reactive and thus disulfide-bonded 
                     DsbA is a strong oxidant |CITS: [8494885]|. Reduced DsbA is
                     more stable than oxidised DsbA and the energetic difference
                     drives disulfide transfer |CITS: [8494885][8411164]|. DsbA 
                     has a redox potential of -0.122 V |CITS: 
                     [9562546][8495194]|. The two cysteine residues in the 
                     active site are not equivalent: Cys30 is accessible and 
                     reactive and forms a very unstable mixed disulfide with 
                     substrate while Cys33 is inaccessible and presumably buried
                     |CITS: [8110795]| (see also |CITS: [8180227]|). The strong 
                     oxidizing power of DsbA results from both the low 
                     pK<sub>a</sub> of Cys30 and the increased stability of 
                     reduced DsbA compared to oxidized DsbA |CITS: [8521518]|. 
                     Reduced DsbA requires DsbB for reoxidation |CITS: 
                     [8430071][7750543][7615498][7568240][95 
                     53083][9140064][10428033][10854438][12072444][24145032]|. 
                     DsbA catalysed oxidative folding occurs via formation of an
                     unstable mixed disulfide complex; resolution of the 
                     intermediate complex is retarded in a DsbA(P151T) mutant 
                     |CITS: [14739460]|. DsbA mediated oxidative folding of PhoA
                     has been characterized <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [19766568]|.
                     Crystal structures of both reduced and oxidized DsbA have 
                     been obtained |CITS: [8413591][9194175][9655827]|. Crystal 
                     structures of DsbA in complex with DsbB and in complex with
                     a peptide residue from <i>Shigella flexneri</i> are also 
                     available |CITS: [19389711][17110337]|.
                     Dsb mutant strains (&Delta;<i>dsbA</i>, &Delta;<i>dsbB</i> 
                     and &Delta;<i>dsbAB</i>) have unimpaired growth under 
                     aerobic conditions but show a severe growth defect under 
                     anaerobic conditions which can be suppressed by 
                     supplementation with cystine; the anaerobically grown 
                     <i>dsb</i> mutant strains also display a perturbed 
                     morphology and a &Delta;<i>dsbA</i> strain accumulates the 
                     reduced, inactive form of the |FRAME: EG11569-MONOMER| 
                     |CITS: [28559299]|. The |FRAME: Polysulfides polysulfide| 
                     compound, octasulfur, can complement the anaerobic growth 
                     defect of a <i>dsbA</i> deletion mutant |CITS: [39918318]|.
                     Reoxidation of a genetically encoded redox probe targeted 
                     to the periplasm is diminished, but not absent, in a 
                     &Delta;<i>dsbA</i> strain suggesting the presence of an 
                     alternative (possibly glutathione-dependent) system for 
                     disulfide bond formation |CITS: [37413765]|.
                     <i>dsbA</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     DsbA is a target for the development of novel antibiotic 
                     adjuvants |CITS: [40107029]|.
                     <i>dsbA</i>: disulfide bond formation |CITS: 
                     [1934062]|<BR><i>ppfA</i> <U>p</U>eriplasmic 
                     <U>p</U>hosphatase <U>f</u>ormation |CITS: [1740115]| 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [21241169]|<BR>Selected related reviews: 
                     |CITS: [7830566][10535729][12524212][15546661][16446111][1 
                     8082634][19567379]|"
