     gene            4040326..4043112
                     /gene="polA"
                     /locus_tag="b3863"
                     /label=polA
     CDS             4040326..4043112
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="polA"
                     /locus_tag="b3863"
                     /product="multifunctional DNA polymerase I / 5' to 3'
                     exonuclease / 3' to 5' proofreading exonuclease / DNA 
                     polymerase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.7"
                     /label=polA
                     /note="DNA Polymerase I (Pol I) is a multifunctional
                     enzyme that combines a DNA polymerase activity, 
                     ""flap-directed"" 5' nuclease activity, and a 3' to 5' 
                     proofreading exonuclease activity. It is required for 
                     several types of DNA repair and is the primary enzyme 
                     responsible for Okazaki fragment maturation, i.e. removing 
                     RNA primers from newly-synthesized DNA and replacing them 
                     with DNA.
                     Pol I is involved in several DNA repair pathways. It is 
                     required for excision repair, displacing the UvrABC 
                     nuclease and patching the gap it leaves behind |CITS: [169 
                     227][12705824][1103827][1103828][1530937][2931721][7012116]
                     |. Pol I is also required in MutHLS-mediated very short 
                     patch repair |CITS: [2671653]|. Pol I can excise and 
                     replace pyrimidine dimers directly |CITS: [340455]|. It 
                     also cleaves the faulty nucleotide from abasic lesion sites
                     following nicking by endonuclease III |CITS: [6273443]|. 
                     Finally, Pol I is generally involved in postreplication 
                     repair of DNA gaps and double-strand breaks |CITS: 
                     [3308845]|. Participation of fluorescently labeled Pol I in
                     the base excision repair (BER) of single nucleotide gaps 
                     generated in live <i>E. coli</i> cells has been visualised 
                     and quantified |CITS: [23630273]|.
                     Pol I primer removal and subsequent DNA gap filling has 
                     been shown directly in phiX174 phage DNA synthesis |CITS: 
                     [6262323]|. A similar role for Pol I in <i>E. coli</i> is 
                     supported by the observations that Pol I can initiate 
                     synthesis at a DNA nick, that Okazaki fragment joining is 
                     only 10% of normal in mutants lacking <i>polA</i> and that 
                     normal replication depends on Pol I |CITS: 
                     [4904090][4943548][4620020]|.
                     Pol I consists of two domains. The larger domain, commonly 
                     known as the Klenow fragment when it is proteolytically 
                     separated, contains the polymerase and 3' to 5' exonuclease
                     activities |CITS: [4982877][4552924]|. The N-terminal 
                     smaller domain contains the 5' to 3' nuclease activity that
                     is structure specific rather than nucleotide specific 
                     |CITS: [4552925][7683443]|. The Klenow domain itself has a 
                     large and a small subdomain, with its carboxy-terminal 
                     large domain containing the polymerase but not the 3' to 5'
                     exonuclease function |CITS: [3329725]|. The Klenow domain 
                     also contains a ""thumb"" structure that is required for 
                     DNA binding, processivity and frameshifts and a J-helix 
                     region that regulates both the polymerase and 3' to 5' 
                     exonuclease functions |CITS: 
                     [8798775][10818095][15924429]|. The Klenow portion 
                     undergoes conformational changes on binding template, then 
                     again on the subsequent binding of dNTPs |CITS: 
                     [10630996]|.
                     Pol I and its subdomains have been crystallized several 
                     times. The initial crystallization was to 3.5 &Aring; 
                     resolution |CITS: [6101086]|. Crystal structures have been 
                     determined for Klenow fragment bound to dNTP, 
                     pyrophosphate, monophosphates, ssDNA and dsDNA |CITS: 
                     [3883192][3194400][8260491]|. Crystal structures of Pol I 
                     bound to dNMP and ssDNA have been determined to 2.6 &Aring;
                     and 3.1 &Aring; resolution, respectively |CITS: [1989886]|.
                     Pol I binds DNA via hydrogen bonding between the minor 
                     groove an a hydrogen-bonding track on the protein |CITS: 
                     [11258875][12522214][15210707][3194400]|. Pol I binds only 
                     one oligonucleotide at a time and only binds dsDNA at nicks
                     or strand ends |CITS: [4890764]|. It has a higher affinity 
                     for primers containing template mismatches or hairpin-like 
                     elements, and has a separate binding site for the 
                     3'-hydroxyl end of substrates |CITS: [1547884][4918843]|. 
                     The binding of DNA by the Pol I 5' to 3' exonuclease 
                     function has been examined in detail |CITS: [11349126]|.
                     Polymerization by Pol I is processive, typically covering 
                     stretches of 20-40 nucleotides but potentially going up to 
                     hundreds of nucleotides |CITS: [338607][1102539]|. The 
                     kinetics of polymerization have been extensively evaluated 
                     |CITS: [1102540][1092683][3902078][3280024][1645180]|. The 
                     nucleotide-dependence of polymerization termination has 
                     also been examined |CITS: [2971662]|.
                     Pol I polymerization is also specific; though error rates 
                     <i>in vitro</i> of 1 in 8,000-80,000 have been measured, 
                     the estimated rate on natural DNA is between 1 in 680,000 
                     and 1 in 6.3 million |CITS: [363714][6448843]|. The 
                     mechanisms behind specificity have been examined, as well 
                     as the role of differing metal cofactors in specificity 
                     |CITS: [9571040][9520378][363715][6371712]|.
                     The two exonuclease activities of Pol I have also been 
                     evaluated. The 3' to 5' exonuclease, which is responsible 
                     for proofreading, does not identify base-pair mismatches. 
                     Instead, Pol I lingers when a mismatch occurs, allowing 
                     more time for the exonuclease to act on the mismatch |CITS:
                     [3058205][6391956]|. The transfer of DNA from the 
                     polymerase to the 3' to 5' exonuclease active site can 
                     occur either intra- or intermolecularly, with mismatches 
                     favoring the latter |CITS: [2659595]|. The 5' to 3' 
                     exonuclease activity requires a free 5' end at a 
                     ssDNA-dsDNA junction and is slower than the 3' to 5' 
                     exonuclease activity |CITS: [9159471][4890763]|. Cryo-EM 
                     structures of Pol I and of the Klenow fragment bound to DNA
                     and primer substrates revealed that Pol I displaces the 
                     primer in a way similar to monomeric helicases and cuts at 
                     the RNA-DNA junction leaving a 5' end for |FRAME: 
                     EG10534-MONOMER| to use |CITS: [37620586]|.
                     <i>polA</i> mutants are more vulnerable to UV and X-rays 
                     and experience more deletions, duplications and frameshifts
                     |CITS: [2999864][11805041][368586]|. <i>polA mutU4</i> 
                     double mutants are inviable, as are <i>recA polA</i> double
                     mutants which accumulate reactive oxygen species (ROS) 
                     |CITS: [4347245][4551758][35705748]|. Plasmid-based 
                     <i>polA</i> mutants in each PolA region responsible for its
                     different activities found that the template-strand bias 
                     associated with clustered DNA damage requires 
                     strand-displacement and 5a²-flap endonuclease activities 
                     but not the 3a²-to-5a² exonuclease
                     activity; however, when 5a²-flap endonuclease activity
                     is not present, there is a different mode of template 
                     strand bias, implying possible backup mechanisms for 
                     repairing or tolerating single-stranded breaks that occur 
                     at clustered DNA damage sites |CITS: [39155334]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [15533035]|"
