     misc_RNA        4043259..4043369
                     /gene="spf"
                     /locus_tag="b3864"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA Spot 42"
                     /note="The small regulatory RNA Spot 42 (Spf) was first 
                     identified as an abundant, non-translated RNA in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [4577761][4577762][390161][229230][2440852]|. Its first 
                     regulatory target, <i>galK</i>, was identified much later 
                     |CITS: [12101127][17264113][26045496]|, and additional 
                     regulatory targets were identified by |CITS: 
                     [21292161][22388518][23980183][27849174]|. Three conserved 
                     unstructured regions of Spot 42 are able to basepair with 
                     different mRNA targets |CITS: [22388518]|.
                     Spot 42 was initially thought to be responsible for 
                     selective inhibition of translation of <i>galK</i> within 
                     the <i>gal</i> operon mRNA by occluding interaction of the 
                     ribosome with the <i>galK</i> Shine-Dalgarno sequence 
                     |CITS: [12101127]|. Later, it was shown that Spot 42 
                     regulation of <i>galK</i> expression by Spot 42 at this 
                     site occurs after RNase cleavage of mRNAs transcribed from 
                     the <i>galE</i> promoter, which generates the so-called mK2
                     mRNA with a new 5' end |CITS: [24794565]|. Binding of Spot 
                     42 leads to degradation of mK2 |CITS: [26045496]|. Spot 42 
                     also affects polarity of gene expression; its binding site 
                     overlaps with a putative |FRAME: CPLX0-2441 Rho|-binding 
                     site and may enhance Rho-mediated transcription termination
                     |CITS: [26045496]|.
                     SpoT 42 sRNA expedites the natural processes of mRNA decay 
                     |CITS: [36936984]|. The region III of Spot 42 appears to be
                     involved in mRNA degradation in the <i>gal</i> operon 
                     |CITS: [36936984]|. This sRNA causes degradation of the 
                     full <i>galETKM</i> mRNA and the <i>galTKM</i> and 
                     <i>galKM</i> mRNAs, but it does not degrade <i>galE, 
                     galET</i>, or <i>galETK</i> mRNAs.  When the SpoT 42 causes
                     degradation of <i>galKM</i> mRNA, the <i>galET</i> mRNA is 
                     produced; therefore an important role of this sRNA is to 
                     produce a specific transcript |CITS: [36936984]|. Spot 42 
                     inhibits RNase E cleavage occurring before the <i>galK</i> 
                     translation initiation site |CITS: [36936984]|.
                     The interaction of Spot 42 with Hfq has been demonstrated 
                     |CITS: [36328526]|.
                     Spot 42 does not inhibit translation of the <i>sdhCDAB</i> 
                     operon directly. Instead, Spot 42 binds upstream of the 
                     translation initiation site and recruits Hfq to its binding
                     site, which occludes the site for binding of the 30S 
                     subunit of the ribosome |CITS: [22474262]|. Spot 42 
                     downregulates expression of the high-affinity arabinose 
                     transporter at low inducer (arabinose) concentration, and 
                     thereby indirectly regulates expression from the 
                     p<sub>BAD</sub> promoter |CITS: [27849174]|.
                     A candidate Spot 42 regulon was inferred by integrating 
                     information on experimentally supported targets and 
                     sRNA-mRNA interactions with transcription datasets; new 
                     targets with experimental support are <i>fabA, fadL, lpd, 
                     lysS, mdh, mglB, rbsB, tktA, yjiA</i>, and <i>yjjK</i> 
                     |CITS: [32487739]|.
                     An <i>spf</i> deletion mutant exhibits a mild growth defect
                     in some, but not all, strain backgrounds |CITS: [2940230]|.
                     Overexpression of Spf RNA results in slow growth, 
                     particularly on succinate |CITS: [6183252]| as well as 
                     other carbon sources |CITS: [21292161]|, and a pronounced 
                     growth defect upon transfer from minimal to rich media, 
                     compared to wild type |CITS: [6183252]|. Overexpression 
                     also leads to a two-fold increase in 6S RNA abundance 
                     |CITS: [6183252]| and increased DNA polymerase I activity 
                     |CITS: [2452153]|.
                     Transcription of <i>spf</i> is negatively regulated by 
                     cAMP-CRP |CITS: [2454912]| and is highest during early 
                     exponential phase |CITS: [26045496]|. Spot 42 and CRP form 
                     a multi-output feedforward loop during catabolite 
                     repression. Both the steady-state level and dynamics of 
                     gene regulation are affected |CITS: [21292161][21788732]|. 
                     Spot 42 RNA accumulates to 150-200 copies per cell in 
                     glucose-grown cells |CITS: [229230]|. The Hfq protein 
                     stabilizes Spot 42 and stimulates pairing of Spf regions 
                     with complementary RNA sequences |CITS: [11804583]|. Spot 
                     42 appears to be unable to compete effectively for Hfq 
                     binding |CITS: [22040174]|. The small RNA PspH is able to 
                     base-pair with Spot 42; overexpression of PspH leads to an 
                     almost 5-fold reduction in the level of Spot 42 |CITS: 
                     [27588604]|.
                     Spf: ""<b>sp</b>ot <b>f</b>ourty-two""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [2450678][10068996][12732300][15063850][15 
                     487940][17381274][18981470][19239884][21292156][21843668][ 
                     26327359][32213244]|"
     gene            4043277..4043324
                     /gene="spfP"
                     /locus_tag="b4837"
                     /label=spfP
     CDS             4043277..4043324
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="spfP"
                     /locus_tag="b4837"
                     /product="dual-function small protein SpfP"
                     /label=spfP
                     /note="The small protein SpfP coding sequence was found
                     within a small RNA coding sequence called |FRAME: EG30098|.
                     The <i>spf</i> transcription unit is a dual-function RNA 
                     which can act as an mRNA for translation to SpfP or as the 
                     Spot 42 regulatory small RNA. The base-pairing activity of 
                     Spot 42 and the protein-coding activity of SpfP are 
                     genetically separated; however, SpfP expression at higher 
                     temperatures (42 &deg;C) were found to interfere with the 
                     base-pairing activity and repression of Spot 42 regulatory 
                     RNA |CITS: [35239441]|.
                     SpfP interacts tightly with CRP, the |FRAME: CPLX0-226|; 
                     overexpression of SpfP blocks some CRP-dependent genes, 
                     specifically |FRAME: EG10554| and |FRAME: EG10362|, but not
                     others. The modulation of <i>galE</i> by SpfP was found 
                     affect the ability of CRP to activate transcription of the 
                     galactose operon. SpfP interacts with specific residues of 
                     CRP to inhibit CRP activation |CITS: [35239441]|."
                     /translation="MFYLSDLLLHVIGFG"
                     /go_function="regulatory RNA binding [goid 0061980]
                     [evidence IDA] [PMID 35239441]"
                     /go_process="regulation of carbohydrate utilization [goid
                     0043610] [evidence IMP] [PMID 35239441]"
