     gene            4044396..4044641
                     /gene="csrC"
                     /locus_tag="b4457"
                     /label=csrC
     misc_RNA        4044396..4044641
                     /gene="csrC"
                     /locus_tag="b4457"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA CsrC"
                     /note="CsrC is one of two small regulatory RNAs that
                     modulate regulation by the |FRAME: EG11447-MONOMER| CsrA 
                     |CITS: [9211896][12694612]|.
                     The CsrA protein cooperatively binds to the CsrC RNA, 
                     forming an RNA-protein complex.  9 potential CsrA-binding 
                     elements are located in loop regions within the CsrC RNA 
                     |CITS: [12694612][16131593]|.  The structure of CsrC RNA 
                     and the possible role of the repeat regions in binding to 
                     CsrA is discussed |CITS: [12694612]|.  CsrC RNA was 
                     reported to be polyadenylated |CITS: [11448770]|. 
                     Degradation of both CsrB and CsrC is controlled by |FRAME: 
                     EG10018-MONOMER CsrD|.  CsrD itself does not function as a 
                     ribonuclease |CITS: [16980588]|; instead, it enables the 
                     RNase E-mediated degradation of CsrB RNA by counteracting 
                     the protection against degradation that is due to the 
                     CsrB-CsrA interaction |CITS: [27235416]|.
                     The dynamics and genetic dependencies of the Csr signaling 
                     cascade have been investigated both by modeling and <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [23878244][29599472]|.
                     Decay of CsrB and CsrC is stimulated when unphosphorylated 
                     |FRAME: CRR-MONOMER EIIA<sup>Glc</sup>| binds to the EAL 
                     domain of CsrD.  The phosphorylation state of 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> depends on the carbon source; growth on 
                     preferred sources, including glucose, leads to a net 
                     dephosphorylation of EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> and other PTS 
                     proteins.  Thus, growth on a preferred carbon source 
                     promotes decay of CsrB/CsrC |CITS: [26507976]|.
                     A <i>csrC</i> mutation leads to increased transcription of 
                     <i>csrB</i>, and a <i>csrB</i> mutation leads to increased 
                     transcription of <i>csrB</i> |CITS: [12694612]|.
                     CsrC accumulates during the transition to stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [12694612]|; expression is significantly higher 
                     during growth in minimal medium compared to rich medium 
                     |CITS: [19538511]|.  CsrA activates <i>csrC</i> 
                     transcription indirectly |CITS: [12694612]|, potentially 
                     via the BarA/UvrY two-component system |CITS: 
                     [12694612][26673755]|; CsrA does not alter the half-life of
                     CsrC |CITS: [12694612]|, which was variously reported to be
                     8 minutes |CITS: [14602901]|, 3.9 minutes |CITS: 
                     [27551019]| or 2 minutes |CITS: [12694612]|. 
                     &sigma;<sup>E</sup> also indirectly activates <i>csrC</i> 
                     transcription |CITS: [28924029]|.  Expression of 
                     <i>csrC</i> is positively regulated by UvrY |CITS: 
                     [21488981][26673755]|.  ppGpp and DksA enhance <i>csrC</i> 
                     transcription |CITS: [21488981]|.  cAMP-CRP negatively 
                     regulates transcription of <i>csrC</i> |CITS: [27551019]|. 
                     Overexpression of CsrC increases curli expression and 
                     biofilm formation and decreases swarming motility |CITS: 
                     [12694612][26469694]|. A stronger effect on growth was the 
                     decrease of it caused by the double mutation 
                     <i>arcZ</i>-<i>csrC</i> on pyruvate, lactate, fucose, or 
                     gluconate, and this effect appeared to be through the 
                     dysregulation of the sRNAs on the gene <i>ppsA</i> |CITS: 
                     [35920556]|.
                     Evidence for the existence of CsrC was reported previously:
                     SraK |CITS: [11448770]|, Tpk2 |CITS: [11553332]|, RyiB 
                     |CITS: [11445539]|, and IS198 |CITS: [12069726]|.  SraK and
                     RyiB are reported to be the same RNA |CITS: [12762038]|.  
                     The IS198 gene is reported to encode a 13 residue peptide 
                     |CITS: [12069726]|.
                     SraK: ""small RNA"" |CITS: [11448770]|; PsrA21: ""predicted
                     small RNA"" |CITS: [11448770]|; RyiB: ""RNA of unknown 
                     function at X position (10 minute) in the genome"" |CITS: 
                     [11445539]|.
                     CsrC: ""<b>c</b>arbon <b>s</b>torage <b>r</b>egulator 
                     <b>C</b>"" |CITS: [12694612]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12762038][12732300][15814430][17383221][2 
                     0446015][22672726][32213244]|
                     Comment: |CITS: [26593223]|"
