     gene            4045405..4046778
                     /gene="hemN"
                     /locus_tag="b3867"
                     /label=hemN
     CDS             4045405..4046778
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hemN"
                     /locus_tag="b3867"
                     /product="coproporphyrinogen III dehydrogenase"
                     /EC_number="1.3.98.3"
                     /label=hemN
                     /note="Oxidative decarboxylation of coproporphyrinogen III
                     to protoporphyrinogen IX is catalyzed by two enzymes in 
                     <i>E. coli</i>.  Under aerobic conditions, the |FRAME: 
                     EG12189 <i>hemF</i>| gene product is active.  Under 
                     anaerobic conditions, HemN catalyzes the coproporphyrinogen
                     III dehydrogenase reaction |CITS: 
                     [7768836][9325429][12114526]|.
                     HemN is monomeric when overproduced and purified under 
                     anaerobic conditions and might be membrane-associated <i>in
                     vivo</i>.  The enzyme contains an oxygen-sensitive [4Fe-4S]
                     iron-sulfur cluster; mutations in specific residues 
                     important for iron-sulfur cluster coordination and 
                     catalysis have been analyzed and a catalytic mechanism has 
                     been proposed |CITS: [12114526]|.  HemN has been classified
                     as a ""Radical SAM"" enzyme |CITS: [11222759]|. 
                     <i>S</i>-adenosylmethionine (SAM) is consumed during 
                     catalysis; the role of a second SAM binding site in HemN 
                     has been investigated by site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: 
                     [15967800]|.  The catalytic mechanism has been studied in 
                     detail |CITS: [16606627]|.  Recently, |CITS: [30884058]| 
                     proposed a revised catalytic mechanism elucidating the 
                     function of the second SAM molecule present in the active 
                     site.
                     A crystal structure of HemN has been solved at 2.07 &Aring;
                     resolution.  A 4Fe-4S cluster is coordinated by three
                     conserved cysteine residues; two molecules of SAM are 
                     present, only one of which has coordination bonds with the 
                     4Fe-4S cluster |CITS: [14633981]|.  Both SAM binding sites 
                     are required for catalytic activity |CITS: [15967800]|. 
                     Expression of <i>hemN</i> is increased under anaerobic 
                     growth conditions and decreased under conditions of limited
                     iron availability |CITS: [7768836]|.  <i>hemN</i> can 
                     complement a <i>Salmonella typhyimurium hemF hemN</i> 
                     double mutant under both aerobic and anaerobic growth 
                     conditions |CITS: [7768836]|.  A <i>hemN</i> mutant is 
                     defective for cytochrome <i>b</i> and cytochrome <i>c</i> 
                     synthesis only under anaerobic growth conditions |CITS: 
                     [9325429]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [15450488][16218869][18307109][20506125][2 
                     2097883][31290896]|"
