     gene            4051767..4053590
                     /gene="bipA"
                     /locus_tag="b3871"
                     /label=bipA
     CDS             4051767..4053590
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="bipA"
                     /locus_tag="b3871"
                     /product="bifunctional 50S ribosomal subunit assembly
                     factor BipA / GTPase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.5.3"
                     /label=bipA
                     /note="BipA is a member of the ribosome-binding
                     translational GTPase (trGTPase) superfamily and is 
                     considered a global stress-responsive regulator at low 
                     temperatures. BipA is necessary for growth under low 
                     temperature conditions |CITS: [11683274][11738827]|. It 
                     plays a role in ribosome assembly, has 
                     nucleotide-independent chaperone activity, and has been 
                     associated with capsule production, biofilm formation, and 
                     LPS core biosynthesis and maturation, the latter of which 
                     contributes to its ability to colonize the mouse large 
                     intestine |CITS: [12654836][18820021][30305394]|.
                     Crystal structures of BipA in various forms, including the 
                     apo-, GDP-, and ppGpp-bound forms, have been solved; the 
                     conformation of the protein does not change upon binding of
                     either nucleotide |CITS: [26163516][26283392]|. A cryo-EM 
                     structure of BipA-GTP bound to the ribosome shows large 
                     differences compared to the free form of BipA and reveals 
                     the unique interactions with the ribosome and A-site tRNA 
                     |CITS: [26283392]|. The N-terminal G-domain of BipA is 
                     required for its nucleotide-independent chaperone-like 
                     activity, similar to other trGTPases, such as |FRAME: 
                     EG10505-MONOMER IF2|, |FRAME: EG11036-MONOMER EF-Tu|, 
                     |FRAME: EG10360-MONOMER EF-G|, |FRAME: EG10529-MONOMER|, 
                     and |FRAME: EG12114-MONOMER| |CITS: [27325008][30305394]|.
                     GTPase activity of BipA was confirmed when mutation of 
                     Asn-128 to Asp inhibited GTP hydrolysis; cells with this 
                     mutation also result in ribosome abnormalities implying GTP
                     hydrolysis is required for the ribosome assembly function 
                     of BipA |CITS: [30305394]|.
                     A <i>bipA</i> mutation suppresses the defect in 
                     lipopolysaccharide core biosynthesis, the SDS sensitivity, 
                     and the defect in mouse intestinal colonization of a 
                     <i>waaQ</i> mutant |CITS: [12654836]|. A strain containing 
                     a transposon insertion in <i>bipA</i> is more sensitive to 
                     chloramphenicol than wild-type |CITS: [18807027]|. The 
                     variety of phenotypes of a <i>bipA</i> deletion, as well as
                     the genetic interaction with the |FRAME: EG11118-MONOMER| 
                     RluC suggests that BipA plays a role in modulating the 
                     structure or function of the ribosome |CITS: [18820021]|. 
                     Deletion of <i>bipA</i> partly suppressed |FRAME: 
                     EG10504-MONOMER IF1|-dependent cold sensitivity |CITS: 
                     [20477873]|. The cold sensitivity and altered ribosome 
                     profiles of a <i>bipA</i> mutant are suppressed by deletion
                     of <i>rluC</i> |CITS: [18820021][25777676]| and exacerbated
                     by deletion of <i>deaD</i> |CITS: [25777676]|. When 23S 
                     rRNA is not modified by RluC, ribosome assembly becomes 
                     BipA-independent |CITS: [25777676]|.
                     Expression of <i>bipA</i> and its involvement with the 50S 
                     ribosome subunit in ribosome assembly are induced at low 
                     (20&deg;C) but not high (43&deg;C) growth temperatures 
                     |CITS: [30305394]|. A variety of subunit aberrations 
                     occurred in a &Delta;bipA mutant (strain ESC19) at low 
                     temperature, particularly in those 50S ribosomal subunit 
                     particles that lacked ribosomal protein L6 |CITS: 
                     [30305394]|. A series of genomic library screening 
                     experiments with strain ESC19 found that BipA was 
                     positively regulated by |FRAME: CPLX0-226| |CITS: 
                     [30305394]| and that 3 different regulators could restore 
                     the cold sensitivity: |FRAME: EG10881| (50S ribosomal 
                     subunit protein L20), |FRAME: EG11137|, and |FRAME: 
                     EG11078| |CITS: [31998269][33240252][37152896]|.
                     BipA does not appear to play a role in translational 
                     fidelity, but it does influence tmRNA tagging of a model 
                     non-stop mRNA |CITS: [19925844]|.
                     <i>bipA</i> shows differential codon adaptation, resulting 
                     in differential translation efficiency signatures, in 
                     thermophilic microbes. It was therefore predicted to play a
                     role in the heat shock response. A <i>bipA</i> deletion 
                     mutant was shown to be more sensitive than wild-type, 
                     specifically to heat shock, but not to other stresses 
                     |CITS: [24580753]|.
                     Whereas BipA of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 is reported not to be 
                     tyrosine phosphorylated |CITS: [9642082][11683274]|, BipA 
                     of enteropathogenic <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [9622352][9642082]| and wall-less L-form <i>E. coli </i> 
                     |CITS: [9884220]| is phosphorylated on tyrosine.
                     BipA has been characterized in pathogenic bacteria |CITS: 
                     [8559071][9622352][9642082][9765944][10760151][10781541][12
                     675808][14560797]|. <i>Salmonella</i> typhimurium BipA is a
                     ribosome-associated GTPase |CITS: [8559071][18621905]|. 
                     BipA of enteropathogenic <i>E. coli</i> is a regulatory 
                     protein |CITS: [9642082][10781541][9622352][12675808]| 
                     involved in pathogenesis |CITS: [9622352][12675808]|, is 
                     required for wild-type translation |CITS: [9642082]|, and 
                     shows genetic interactions with Uup and YheS |CITS: 
                     [30597160]|.
                     A publication by Owens et al. (|CITS: [15297874]|) has been
                     retracted |CITS: [17971824]|; certain results unrelated to 
                     the purported regulation of Fis expression, such as BipA 
                     interaction with the ribosome and its ribosome-dependent 
                     GTPase activity, may remain valid. BipA: 
                     ""<b>B</b>PI-<b>i</b>nducible <b>p</b>rotein <b>A</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [8559071]|
                     TypA: ""<b>ty</b>rosine <b>p</b>hosphoprotein"" |CITS: 
                     [9642082]|
                     Review: |CITS: [29235176]|"
