     gene            4068029..4068814
                     /gene="csqR"
                     /locus_tag="b3884"
                     /label=csqR
     CDS             4068029..4068814
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="csqR"
                     /locus_tag="b3884"
                     /product="bifunctional DNA-binding transcriptional dual
                     regulator CsqR / CsqR-6-sulfo-D-quinovose"
                     /label=csqR
                     /note="CsqR, formerly known as YihW and hypothesized to be
                     a transcriptional regulator |CITS: [24463506]|, functions 
                     as a repressor for genes of catabolism of sulfoquinovose 
                     (SQ), a hydrolysis product of sulfoquinovosyl 
                     diacylglycerol (SQDG) |CITS: [30372406]|. CsqR belongs to 
                     the DeoR family of transcription factors and contains a 
                     helix-turn-helix motif to bind DNA in its N-terminal domain
                     |CITS: [35880217]|. It acts as a carbon source-dependent 
                     dual regulator involved in sustaining baseline growth in 
                     the absence of the <i>lac</i> operon |CITS: [29453395]|. 
                     CsqR is likely involved in regulation of lactose metabolism
                     and functions either in a complementary or opposite manner 
                     to a global regulator of carbohydrate metabolism, cAMP-CRP,
                     and functions as a sugar-dependent dual regulator |CITS: 
                     [29453395]|. Genome-wide CsqR binding sites were determined
                     <i>in vivo</i> by the chromatin immunoprecipitation method 
                     combined with lambda exonuclease digestion (ChIP-exo) in 
                     glucose M9 minimal medium |CITS: [34428301]|.
                     Although the oligomeric state of CsqR is not entirely 
                     understood, it is speculated to function as an oligomer 
                     when bound to DNA |CITS:[38570624]|
                     CsqR is part of a cluster of genes involved in the 
                     degradation of sulfoquinovose (SQ); see the |FRAME: 
                     PWY-7446| pathway.  This cluster is conserved in certain 
                     Actinobacteria and Proteobacteria |CITS:[38570624]|. The 
                     activity of the CsqR transcriptional regulator is 
                     controlled by SQ and sulfoquinovosyl glycerol (SQG) during 
                     the exponential growth phase. Both SQ and SQG act as 
                     inducers for the <i>yihUTS</i> operon and <i>yihV</i> gene,
                     and also for <i>csqR</i> expression |CITS: [30372406]|. The
                     compounds sulforhamnose and lactose also appear to act as 
                     effectors of CsqR, and a binding pocket for the effectors 
                     has been identified in CsqR. |CITS:[38570624]|.
                     It was determined by Genomic SELEX screening that CsqR 
                     binds strongly only between <i>yihV-csqR</i> 
                     (<i>squV-csqR</i>) and <i>yihUTS</i> (<i>squUTS</i>) 
                     operons, and CsqR binds with minor strength close to the 
                     <i>trxB</i>, <i>yeaC</i>, <i>hyfB</i>, and <i>accC</i> 
                     genes |CITS: [34220787]|. CsqR was classified as a 
                     single-target transcription factor |CITS: [34220787]|.
                     The gene <i>csqR</i> is responsible for producing two forms
                     of proteins: a longer form, CsqR-l, which is 28.5 kDa, and 
                     a shorter form, CsqR-s, which is approximately 26 kDa 
                     |CITS:[38570624]|. The shorter form is notably activated 
                     during the use of sulfoquinovose (SQ) as the sole carbon 
                     source. CsqR-s, which begins at a conserved methionine at 
                     position 25, is more conserved in the Enterobacteriales 
                     group of bacteria |CITS:[38570624]|. CsqR-l is 
                     predominantly synthesized during the exponential growth 
                     phase when various carbon sources are accessible, whereas 
                     CsqR-s is produced in response to SQ during the stationary 
                     phase |CITS:[38570624]|. CsqR-s may function as an 
                     inhibitor of CsqR-l during this phase |CITS:[38570624]|. 
                     Structurally, CsqR-s lacks the first &alpha;-helix and 
                     &beta;-strands of the N-terminal DeoR-type winged 
                     helix-turn-helix (wHTH) motif found in CsqR-l, potentially 
                     affecting its ability to bind DNA and interact with 
                     effectors |CITS:[38570624]|. Interestingly, CsqR-s exhibits
                     new interactions between its N-terminal and C-terminal 
                     domains and loses the interdomain linker, while CsqR-l 
                     maintains more contacts within the interdomain linker 
                     |CITS:[38570624]|.
                     The transcription of the <i>csqR</i> gene is affected by 
                     heat stress and glucose-lactose shift |CITS: [35880217]|. 
                     The CsqR binding landscape was globally mapped in vivo by 
                     standardized ChIP-seq and quantitatively modeled using 
                     BoltzNet, a biophysically informed neural network that 
                     infers binding energy from sequence data |CITS:[40335485]|.
                     The resulting ChIP-seq binding regions are available in 
                     RegulonDB (<a href=""https://regulondb.cc 
                     g.unam.mx/ht/dataset/TFBINDING/source=GALAGAN"">Galagan 
                     collection</a>), and the corresponding energy matrix and 
                     genomic binding profiles can be accessed at <a 
                     href=""http://boltznet.bu.edu"">boltznet.bu.edu</a> 
                     |CITS:[40335485]|."
                     /translation="MSLTELTGNPRHDQLLMLIAERGYMNIDELANLLDVSTQTVRRDI
                     RKLSEQGLITRHHGGAGRASSVVNTAFEQREVSQTEEKKAIAEAVADYIPDGSTIFITI
