                     deacylase / D-tyrosyl-[tRNA(Tyr)] deacylase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.1.96"
                     /label=dtd
                     /note="D-aminoacyl-tRNA deacylase (Dtd) is an
                     aminoacyl-tRNA editing enzyme that removes mischarched 
                     D-amino acids from tRNAs.  It has D-Tyr-tRNA<sup>Tyr</sup> 
                     deacylase activity, releasing tRNA<sup>Tyr</sup> from 
                     misaminoacylation with D-tyrosine (in contrast to proper 
                     aminoacylation with L-tyrosine) |CITS: [10383414]|.  It is 
                     also able to edit mischarged Gly-tRNA<sup>Ala</sup> more 
                     efficiently than |FRAME: ALAS-CPLX AlaRS| |CITS: 
                     [28362257]|.
                     <i>In vitro</i>, the enzyme mis-edits tRNA charged with the
                     achiral amino acid glycine; the enzyme's specificity for 
                     D-amino acids appears to be achieved by rejecting L-amino 
                     acids rather than by actively recognizing D-amino acids.  
                     The binding mode of Gly-tRNA<sup>Gly</sup> was 
                     characterized in the crystal structure of the enzyme from 
                     <i>Plasmodium falciparum</i> and by mutagenesis of the 
                     conserved residues Phe125 and Ala102.  EF-Tu from 
                     <i>Thermus thermophilus</i> apparently competes for binding
                     Gly-tRNA<sup>Gly</sup>, thereby protecting this charged 
                     tRNA from misediting by Dtd |CITS: [27224426]|. Dtd has 
                     higher activity towards mischarged Gly-tRNA<sup>Ala</sup> 
                     than towards Gly-tRNA<sup>Gly</sup>, at least partly due to
                     recognition of the G3&middot;U70 identity element of 
                     tRNA<sup>Ala</sup> |CITS: [28362257]|.
                     The enzyme is a dimer in solution |CITS: [10383414]|.  A 
                     crystal structure of Dtd has been solved at 1.55 &Aring; 
                     resolution |CITS: [11568181]|.
                     A <i>dtd</i> mutant exhibits a growth defect, compared to 
                     wild type, in the presence of exogenous D-tyrosine |CITS: 
                     [10383414]|.  In a <i>dtd</i> deletion mutant grown in the 
                     presence of D-tyrosine, 40% of the tRNA<sup>Tyr</sup> pool 
                     is converted into D-Tyr-tRNA<sup>Tyr</sup>, while 
                     D-Tyr-tRNA<sup>Tyr</sup> is not found in the wild type. The
                     low level of L-tyrosine-charged tRNA may therefore account 
                     for the enhanced toxicity of D-tyrosine in the <i>dtd</i> 
                     deletion mutant |CITS: [15292242]|. Overexpression of 
                     <i>dtd</i> is deleterious, likely due to mis-editing of 
                     Gly-tRNA<sup>Gly</sup> |CITS: [27224426]|. If an 
                     <i>alaS</i> mutant that is unable to edit mischarged 
                     Ser-tRNA<sup>Ala</sup> and Gly-tRNA<sup>Ala</sup> is 
                     combined with a <i>dtd</i> deletion, the strain shows a 
                     synthetic growth defect and becomes sensitive to added 
                     glycine |CITS: [28362257]|.
                     A <i>dadA</i> (D-amino acid dehydrogenase) <i>dtd</i> 
                     double mutant exhibits a more severe growth defect in the 
                     presence of exogenous D-tyrosine than a <i>dtd</i> single 
                     mutant |CITS: [10383414]|.
                     <i>dtd</i> shows differential codon adaptation, resulting 
                     in differential translation efficiency signatures, in 
                     thermophilic microbes.  It was therefore predicted to play 
                     a role in the heat shock response.  A <i>dtd</i> deletion 
                     mutant was shown to be more sensitive than wild-type 
                     specifically to heat shock, but not other stresses |CITS: 
                     [24580753]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [28764927]|
                     "
