     gene            complement(4086808..4087350)
                     /gene="rhaD"
                     /locus_tag="b3902"
                     /label=rhaD
     CDS             complement(4086808..4087350)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rhaD"
                     /locus_tag="b3902"
                     /product="rhamnulose-1-phosphate aldolase"
                     /EC_number="4.1.2.19"
                     /label=rhaD
                     /note="Rhamnulose-1-phosphate aldolase is a class II
                     aldolase that catalyzes the third step in the L-rhamnose 
                     degradation pathway.
                     The enzyme contains 2 molecules of zinc per enzyme complex 
                     |CITS: [SCHWARTZ72]|.  If cobalt or selected other divalent
                     metal ions are artificially substituted for zinc, the 
                     enzyme has an oxygenase activity in the presence of 
                     dihydroxyacetone phosphate |CITS: [8774862]|.
                     The enzyme has been crystallized |CITS: 
                     [4975916][4208690]|, and crystal structures have been 
                     solved |CITS: [11976494][12962479]|.  A catalytic mechanism
                     was proposed based on results from site-directed 
                     mutagenesis in combination with structural information 
                     |CITS: [12962479]|.  Anisotropic mobility of the N-terminal
                     ""antenna domain"" supports catalysis |CITS: [18085797]|.  
                     Stereospecificity of RhaD has been investigated |CITS: 
                     [10992239]|.
                     For the aldol addition reaction, RhaD accepts a range of 
                     aldehydes in place of L-lactaldehyde; this property has 
                     been used for synthesis of a variety of compounds of 
                     interest |CITS: 
                     [22428999][22735612][23749229][25060350][30386427]|.
                     RhaD (there called Rua) was used in a study of designed 
                     protein-protein associations; certain mutations generated a
                     new contact surface and allowed formation of an octamer 
                     |CITS: [18187656]|.
                     Expression of RhaD is induced by L-rhamnose as well as 
                     L-lyxose |CITS: [1650346]|.  Expression is downregulated 
                     under simulated microgravity conditions |CITS: [23472115]|.
                     <i>rhaD</i> mutants can not utilize rhamnose as a source of
                     carbon |CITS: [5341476]|."
