     gene            4090738..4091358
                     /gene="sodA"
                     /locus_tag="b3908"
                     /label=sodA
     CDS             4090738..4091358
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sodA"
                     /locus_tag="b3908"
                     /product="bifunctional superoxide dismutase (Mn) /
                     superoxide dismutase"
                     /EC_number="1.15.1.1"
                     /label=sodA
                     /note="SodA, or MnSOD, is one of three superoxide
                     dismutases in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [330499]|. The three 
                     enzymes differ in their metal cofactor requirement; MnSOD 
                     contains manganese. Superoxide dismutase is implicated in 
                     the response to a large number of environmental changes 
                     that lead to the generation of superoxide radicals.
                     MnSOD and the iron-containing enzyme |FRAME: 
                     SUPEROX-DISMUTFE-MONOMER FeSOD| (SodB) are not functionally
                     equivalent; MnSOD is more effective than FeSOD in 
                     preventing damage to DNA, while FeSOD seems more effective 
                     in protecting a cytoplasmic superoxide-sensitive enzyme 
                     |CITS: [1447175]|. However, both enzymes were shown to 
                     protect dihydroxy acid dehydratase from inactivation by 
                     oxidative stress |CITS: [7771772]|. MnSOD binds DNA 
                     non-specifically |CITS: [7961811]| with a K<sub>a</sub> of 
                     3 x 10<sup>5</sup> M<sup>-1</sup> |CITS: [15620896]|. The 
                     superoxide dismutases contribute to reducing iron toxicity 
                     |CITS: [7730258]|.
                     The enzyme is highly specific for the manganese cofactor; 
                     an iron-substituted MnSOD is catalytically inactive |CITS: 
                     [9548935]|, although it was recently suggested that 
                     iron-substituted MnSOD has peroxidase/catalase activity 
                     |CITS: [25964067]|.
                     The binding constant for Mn(II) is 3.2 x 10<sup>8</sup> 
                     M<sup>-1</sup> |CITS: [15082717]|, and the process of 
                     manganese acquisition has been studied |CITS: [16258041]|. 
                     Metal uptake appears to be cooperative and requires dimer 
                     dissociation |CITS: [21044611]|. The apo-form of a 
                     Gln146Glu mutant is much more stable than the apo-form of 
                     the wild type enzyme; conversely, the wild type, but not 
                     the mutant enzyme is stabilized by metal binding. This 
                     suggests that enzyme stability was sacrificed for enhanced 
                     metal binding ability during evolution of MnSOD |CITS: 
                     [28704037]|. The Glu170 residue is essential for metal 
                     selectivity and is thought to form a hydrogen bond with 
                     His171 of the second monomer in the homodimeric enzyme 
                     |CITS: [9712831]|. A Gly165Thr mutation confers some 
                     activity using Fe as the metal cofactor |CITS: [20451673]|.
                     Crystal structures of wild type and mutant apo- and MnSOD 
                     have been solved |CITS: [EDWARDS98][1068609 
                     4][11141052][11294629][20417642][21044611]|. The catalytic 
                     mechanism and involvement of the metal cofactor have been 
                     studied |CITS: [11669646][11800609][124 
                     75351][12538055][15260508][15453782][15683235][15839704][16
                     460038]|. Superoxide dismutase is also part of the 
                     ribonucleoside-diphosphate reductase activating system. The
                     system is composed of three proteins, flavin reductase, 
                     superoxide dismutase and a protein fraction named fraction 
                     b whose function is poorly defined |CITS: 
                     [3517866][3305505]|. MnSOD was identified as the enzyme 
                     present in the system |CITS: [3517866]|, but other species 
                     of the enzyme could substitute |CITS: [7588767]|. 
                     Production of MnSOD is not dependent on the SOS system 
                     |CITS: [2997150]|. The complex transcriptional and 
                     post-transcriptional regulation of <i>sodA</i> expression 
                     has been studied in detail |CITS: [3131302][2127370][21809 
                     12][2181443][2041478][1565612][1332615][8449876][8444893][8
                     460139][8387743][8509428][8412671][8277251][8241258][813554
                     0][7982626][7862094][8825776][8626315][11932448][20075074][
                     20070527][22393021][24621982][27757702]|. An <i>sodA</i> 
                     mutant is more sensitive to paraquat than wild type |CITS: 
                     [3011417]| and shows an increased mutation rate that is 
                     dependent on the presence of oxygen |CITS: [3022287]|. A 
                     <i>sodA sodB</i> double mutant is especially sensitive to 
                     DNA damage induced by hydrogen peroxide |CITS: [3298208]|. 
                     Under aerobic conditions, a <i>sodA sodB</i> double mutant 
                     is able to grow slowly on rich medium, but not on minimal 
                     glucose medium. The double mutant is able to grow under 
                     anaerobic conditions or by supplementing the minimal medium
                     with a full complement of amino acids |CITS: [3011417]|. 
                     The amino acid auxotrophic phenotypes can be explained by 
                     oxidative inactivation of dihydroxy acid dehydratase |CITS:
                     [7771772]| and transketolase |CITS: [9933617]|. A <i>sodA 
                     sodB</i> double mutant is more susceptible to killing by 
                     human serum and neutrophils |CITS: [7872803]|.
                     Persistant, sublethally injured <i>E. coli</i> induced by 
                     lactic acid stress exhibited slower resuscitation rates in 
                     <i>&Delta;sodB</i>, <i>&Delta;gshA</i> and 
                     <i>&Delta;gshB</i> mutants, and even more slowly in a 
                     <i>&Delta;sodA</i> mutant, relative to wild-type |CITS: 
                     [35395105]|.
                     Overexpression of <i>katG, ahpCF, sodA</i> or <i>oxyR</i> 
                     in nutrient shift assays in the presence of ampicillin, 
                     specifically when switching from glycerol to oleic acid, 
                     alleviated the reactive oxygen species induced killing of 
                     persister cells |CITS: [39470288]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [1779751][7917419][14641053][19914407][220 
                     79668][22247543]|
                     Commentary: |CITS: [35477006]|"
                     /translation="MSYTLPSLPYAYDALEPHFDKQTMEIHHTKHHQTYVNNANAALES
