     gene            complement(4103654..4104400)
                     /gene="fpr"
                     /locus_tag="b3924"
                     /label=fpr
     CDS             complement(4103654..4104400)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fpr"
                     /locus_tag="b3924"
                     /product="trifunctional flavodoxin/ferredoxin-NADP(+)
                     reductase / flavodoxin-NADP(+) reductase / 
                     ferredoxin-NADP(+) reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.19.1.1; 1.18.1.2"
                     /label=fpr
                     /note="Flavodoxin/ferredoxin-NADP<sup>+</sup> reductase
                     (Fpr) participates in chains of redox reactions; it 
                     contains a noncovalently bound FAD cofactor that 
                     facilitates the reversible electron transfer between 
                     NADP(H) and ferredoxins or flavodoxins.  Fpr is involved in
                     the activation of anaerobic ribonucleoside reductase |CITS:
                     [1460049][8449868]|, pyruvate-formate lyase (PFL) |CITS: 
                     [7042345]|, methionine synthase |CITS: [4154078]| and 
                     biotin synthase |CITS: [7642583]|.  Ferredoxin can not 
                     substitute for flavodoxin as an electron donor for 
                     activation of PFL |CITS: [7042345]| and methionine synthase
                     |CITS: [8993326]|.  Because an <i>fpr</i> mutant has no 
                     growth defect on rich or minimal medium under aerobic or 
                     anaerobic growth conditions, a second flavodoxin reductase 
                     activity, likely |FRAME: G6701-MONOMER|, is able to fulfill
                     Fpr's role in activating these enzymes |CITS: [7635836]|.  
                     Fpr together with ferredoxin assists in the assembly of 
                     2Fe-2S clusters on IscU |CITS: [25688831]|.
                     Fpr is able to reduce |FRAME: OX-FERREDOXIN|, |FRAME: 
                     OX-FLAVODOXIN1| and |FRAME: OX-FLAVODOXIN2|; however, 
                     ferredoxin is kinetically and thermodynamically preferred 
                     |CITS: [12234497]|.  Fpr appears to reduce the flavodoxin 
                     substrate by successive one-electron transfer processes 
                     |CITS: [7042345]|.  The redox potential of the semiquinone 
                     and hydroquinone state of Fpr was measured to be -308 mV 
                     and -268 mV, respectively |CITS: [9765990][9839946]|. While
                     Fpr containing the oxidized FAD cofactor is very stable, 
                     the reduced form loses activity due to partial unfolding of
                     the protein and dissociation of FADH<sub>2</sub> in a 
                     temperature- and pH-dependent manner |CITS: [12372607]|.
                     Crystal structures of Fpr have been solved |CITS: 
                     [9149148][21306142]|.  The protein comprises a 
                     &beta;-sandwich FAD-binding domain and an &alpha;/&beta; 
                     NADPH-binding domain |CITS: [9149148]|.  Site-directed 
                     mutagenesis of arginine residues within the NADPH binding 
                     site has confirmed their importance for catalytic activity 
                     |CITS: [11085917]|.  Loss of the C-terminal tryptophan 
                     residue can improve the catalytic efficiency |CITS: 
                     [21306142]|.
                     Fpr also has paraquat diaphorase |CITS: [8108411]| and 
                     ferric reductase |CITS: [20407804]| activities.
                     <i>fpr</i> mutants show increased sensitivity to methyl 
                     viologen |CITS: [2834327]| and paraquat |CITS: [7635836]|; 
                     Fpr may contribute to NAD(P)H homeostasis in stressed cells
                     |CITS: [11844783]|.
                     <i>fpr</i> is a member of the SoxRS regulon; expression can
                     be induced by exposure to paraquat |CITS: 
                     [8108411][8825776]| and hydrogen peroxide |CITS: 
                     [11073934]|.  <i>fpr</i> expression is also activated by 
                     MarA |CITS: [8522515]| and Rob |CITS: [8626315]|.
                     MvrA: ""<b>m</b>ethyl <b>v</b>iologen <b>r</b>esistance 
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [2834327][1400248]|
                     Fpr: <b>f</b>erredoxin NAD<b>P</b><sup>+</sup> 
                     <b>r</b>eductase |CITS: [8449868]|"
