     gene            complement(4105642..4107150)
                     /gene="glpK"
                     /locus_tag="b3926"
                     /label=glpK
     CDS             complement(4105642..4107150)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glpK"
                     /locus_tag="b3926"
                     /product="glycerol kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.30"
                     /label=glpK
                     /note="Glycerol kinase (GlpK) is involved in the
                     utilization of the glycerol moiety of phospholipids and 
                     triglycerides after their breakdown into usable forms such 
                     as glycerophosphodiesters, <i>sn</i>-glycerol-3-phosphate 
                     (G3P) or glycerol.  GlpK catalyzes the MgATP-dependent 
                     phosphorylation of glycerol to yield sn-glycerol 
                     3-phosphate |CITS:
                     [5335908]|.  This reaction is the rate-limiting step in 
                     glycerol utilization |CITS: [4914079][17086184]|.  GlpK is 
                     a member of a superfamily of proteins that share a common 
                     ATPase domain |CITS: [1323828]|.
                     Inhibition studies suggested an ordered catalytic 
                     mechanism, with glycerol binding first |CITS: [4575199]|, 
                     but later experiments support a random catalytic mechanism 
                     |CITS: [2148683]|.  The crystal structure of a putative 
                     productive conformation of the enzyme is consistent with 
                     ""half-of-the-sites"" reactivity and the importance of 
                     domain motion for catalytic activity |CITS: [10090737]|.
                     A nucleophilic in-line transfer mechanism for the kinase 
                     reaction has been proposed |CITS: [10364471]|.  Molecular 
                     dynamics modeling suggested that the proposed domain motion
                     regulates the binding of glycerol by preventing competition
                     with water |CITS: [12829462]|.
                     GlpK is a key enzyme in glucose control of glycerol 
                     metabolism; its activity is subject to inhibition by 
                     fructose 1,6-bisphosphate (FBP) |CITS: [5328677][4575199]| 
                     and the nonphosphorylated form of |FRAME: CRR-MONOMER| 
                     |CITS: [2985549][3013838]|.  Inhibition by FBP is the 
                     dominant regulatory mechanism for repressing glycerol 
                     utilization in the presence of glucose |CITS: [11344141]|. 
                     At low concentrations, the enzyme can undergo a reversible 
                     subunit dissociation between tetramer and dimer |CITS: 
                     [215194][215195]|.  The dimeric form does not bind FBP 
                     |CITS: [215195]|; the tetramer-dimer equilibrium plays a 
                     role in desensitization of GlpK to inhibition by FBP |CITS:
                     [31903]|.  Crystal structures of GlpK in various complexes 
                     with substrates and inhibitors have been solved |CITS: 
                     [2547969][8430315][8170944][9538005][9817843][98434 
                     23][10090737][10364471][17441732]|.
                     Mutant enzymes with decreased inhibition by FBP show a 
                     shift of the dimer-tetramer equilibrium towards the dimer; 
                     enzymatic activity and inhibition by IIA<sup>Glc</sup> are 
                     not affected |CITS: [8060980]|.  The crystal structure of 
                     GlpK-glycerol in the presence and absence of FBP shows that
                     a tetramer of GlpK binds two molecules of FBP, with half of
                     each binding site donated by one monomer |CITS: [9843423]|.
                      The Ser58Trp mutation sterically blocks formation of the 
                     dimer-dimer interface near the FBP binding site and 
                     eliminates inhibition by FBP |CITS: [10090737]|.  The G230D
                     mutant with increased catalytic activity and decreased 
                     regulation by FBP has an altered binding loop conformation 
                     that interferes with the FBP binding site |CITS: 
                     [17441732]|.  Tetramer assembly and FBP binding has been 
                     quantified; the positive cooperativity for FBP inhibition 
                     is due to a 4000-fold higher affinity of the GlpK tetramer 
                     for FBP |CITS: [12680779]|.
                     The interaction between GlpK and Enzyme IIA<sup>Glc</sup> 
                     requires the presence of glycerol |CITS: [3013838]|.  In 
                     crystal structures of GlpK in complex with unphosphorylated
                     IIA<sup>Glc</sup>, IIA<sup>Glc</sup> binds far from the 
                     active site |CITS: [8430315]|; the interaction creates an 
                     intermolecular zinc coordination site which increases 
                     binding and inhibition |CITS: [8170944]|.  Mutation of the 
                     zinc-liganding residue contributed by GlpK abolishes the 
                     enhanced IIA<sup>Glc</sup> inhibition by zinc |CITS: 
                     [9538005]|. The conformation of the IIA<sup>Glc</sup> 
                     binding site influences the regulation of GlpK activity 
                     |CITS: [9817843]|.  ""Transplanting"" regulation by 
                     IIA<sup>Glc</sup> to the <i>Haemophilus influenzae</i> 
                     enzyme showed that residues 427-429 couple 
                     IIA<sup>Glc</sup> binding to the catalytic core |CITS: 
                     [12161559][17924663]|.  Amino acid substitutions in the 
                     coupling locus do not affect allosteric coupling parameters
                     |CITS: [19056335]|.  Binding site mutants have been 
                     analyzed |CITS: [11714284]|
                     A single point mutation at the domain-domain interface of 
                     GlpK, Gly305Ser, results in complete loss of glucose 
                     control over glycerol utilization |CITS: 
                     [5328677][8631672]|.  This single mutation affects both the
                     catalytic and regulatory properties of the enzyme, 
                     suggesting a central role for domain movement in these 
                     functions |CITS: [8631672]|.  Mutagenesis of conserved 
                     active site aspartate residues alters catalytic parameters 
                     of the enzyme and affects allosteric regulation 
                     differentially |CITS: [9448710]|.
                     GlpK also appeared to be regulated by adenine nucleotides 
                     |CITS: [3021201]|; however, regulation does not involve a 
                     separate regulatory binding site |CITS: [2148683]|.  GlpK 
                     activity may be stimulated by interaction with the glycerol
                     transporter GlpF; however, no stimulation was observed 
                     <i>in vitro</i>, and a direct interaction could not be 
                     demonstrated |CITS: [8432702]|.
                     In an adaptive evolution experiment for growth on glycerol,
                     the majority of mutants were found to be in <i>glpK</i> 
                     |CITS: [15574828][17086184][18487343][21550976][24481126]|.
                     Altered kinetic and regulatory properties of GlpK 
                     apparently contribute to rapid growth on glycerol |CITS: 
                     [17086184]|.  Four of the adaptive mutations reduce the 
                     enzyme's affinity for FBP and formation of the tetramer 
                     |CITS: [21550976]|.
                     <i>glpK</i> is required for growth on glycerol as the sole 
                     source of carbon and energy |CITS: [13930693][17012394]|. 
                     <i>glpK</i> was found to be downregulated in a MG1655 
                     lysogen carrying the Stx2a phage O104 |CITS: 31208335|. 
                     Transcription of <i>glpK</i> is regulated by catabolite 
                     repression and by GlpR |CITS: [4580569][6250693][1372899]|.
                      Expression of GlpK is induced by spermine at both high and
                     low pH |CITS: [16223443]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [825019][7781919]|
                     The <i>E. coli</i> GlpK structure was used to predict the 
                     impact of human gene variants found in glycerol kinase 
                     deficiency, an X-linked recessive disorder |CITS: 
                     [11479736][16025401]|.
                     "
