     gene            complement(4107173..4108018)
                     /gene="glpF"
                     /locus_tag="b3927"
                     /label=glpF
     CDS             complement(4107173..4108018)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="glpF"
                     /locus_tag="b3927"
                     /product="multifunctional glycerol facilitator / diffusion
                     of glycerol / diffusion of sugar alcohols / diffusion of 
                     glyceraldehyde / diffusion of glycine / diffusion of urea /
                     diffusion of arsenite / diffusion of antimonite"
                     /label=glpF
                     /note="The glycerol facilitator, GlpF, allows the
                     facilitated diffusion of glycerol across the inner membrane
                     for utilization inside the cell.  It is a member of the 
                     major intrinsic protein (MIP) family of transmembrane 
                     channel proteins, along with AqpZ, a water channel.
                     GlpF is predicted to be monomeric 
                     |CITS:[10633068],[9852047]|.  A projection map at a 
                     resolution of 3.7 &Aring; reveals GlpF forms a tetramer of 
                     four channels |CITS:[11265760],[14630323]|.  Interactions 
                     between purified GlpF show it forms multiple oligomeric 
                     complexes |CITS:[11226336]|.  The tetrameric structure has 
                     a funnel on its periplasmic surface which help in 
                     recruitment of glycerol to the transporter 
                     |CITS:[11709172]|.  The structure of GlpF with glycerol has
                     been determined by X-ray crystallography to a resolution of
                     2.2 &Aring; |CITS:[11039922]|.  The structure of GlpF has 
                     also been determined using cryo-electron microscopy of two 
                     dimensional crystals to a resolution of 6.9 &Aring; 
                     |CITS:[11162735]|.  The GlpF channel contains hydrophobic 
                     residues on one side and hydrophilic residues on the other 
                     side, possibly limiting water permeation |CITS:[11162735]|.
                      Real time molecular dynamics (MD) simulations indicate 
                     GlpF is a two stage filter |CITS:[11743202]|.  GlpF 
                     prevents proton transport either by orienting water 
                     molecules in a bipolar water file 
                     |CITS:[11964478],[14581193]| or by an electrostatic 
                     mechanism |CITS:[15377535]|.  In order to select for 
                     glycerol over water
                     transport, it is believed GlpF has a glycerol-mediated 
                     induced fit gating motion |CITS:[11743202]|.  MD 
                     simulations show water is necessarily cotransported with 
                     glycerol because competition with water for hydrogen 
                     bonding sites is required for
                     movement of glycerol through the pore |CITS:[11709172]|. 
                     Glycerol is transported equally well in either direction 
                     |CITS:[14581200]|.
                     <I>glpF</I> mutants have impaired growth on low 
                     concentrations of glycerol |CITS:[4563976]|.  <I>glpF</I> 
                     mutants also had reduced uptake of  DL-glyceraldehyde, 
                     glycine, urea, erythritol, D-arabitol, L-arabitol, ribitol,
                     xylitol, D-galactitol, D-mannitol, D-sorbitol 
                     |CITS:[6998951]|, AsOH<sub>3</sub> |CITS:[14970228]|, and 
                     SbOH<sub>3</sub> |CITS:[9150238]|.  Complementation studies
                     have demonstrated that the cloned <I>glpF</I> gene can 
                     complement glycerol transport mutants |CITS:[2152911]|.  
                     Voltage clamp experiments showed that GlpF was not 
                     voltage-activated for ion transport; it mediates glycerol 
                     diffusion via a pore type mechanism |CITS: [9421629]|.
                     GlpF interacts with glycerol kinase (GlpK) leading to 
                     activation of GlpK |CITS:[8432702]|.
                     Expression of <I>glpF</I> is regulated by GlpR and cAMP-CRP
                     |CITS:[1372899]|.
                     Reviews:  |CITS:[1715617][10707060][11101882][12027015][11 
                     952238][12440697][11988468][11937042][12629974],[12948772][
                     14630323][14630331][14646142][16837191][19096772]| "
