     gene            4108443..4108688
                     /gene="zapB"
                     /locus_tag="b3928"
                     /label=zapB
     CDS             4108443..4108688
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="zapB"
                     /locus_tag="b3928"
                     /product="cell division factor ZapB"
                     /label=zapB
                     /note="The ZapB protein is a cell division factor that is 
                     required for proper Z ring formation |CITS: [18394147]| and
                     is recruited to the inner face of the Z ring by ZapA |CITS:
                     [20487275]|.  The N terminus of ZapB is required for its 
                     interaction with ZapA |CITS: [22056926]|.  ZapB interacts 
                     with |FRAME: EG12855-MONOMER MatP|, thereby anchoring the 
                     Ter macrodomain in mid-cell |CITS: [22580828][26762981]|.  
                     In the absence of functional Min and nucleoid exclusion 
                     (SlmA) systems, the MatP-ZapB-ZapA system positions the Z 
                     ring over the nucleoid in mid-cell |CITS: [25101671]|.  The
                     protein network formed by the divisome proteins FtsZ, ZapA,
                     ZapB and MatP has been studied using superresolution 
                     imaging, showing that these proteins form a multi-layered 
                     protein network that extends from the cell membrane to the 
                     chromosome and that stabilizes the FtsZ ring |CITS: 
                     [25848771]|.  The ZapA and ZapB proteins are able to form 
                     an FtsZ-independent structure at midcell which appears to 
                     provide positional information to FtsZ; ZapB appears to be 
                     recruited to midcell by MatP |CITS: [28249098]|.  
                     Interaction with ZapB localizes DapE to midcell and may 
                     enhance the Ter signal, thereby promoting functional Z ring
                     formation |CITS: [28470834]|.
                     Before septum formation, a complex of the early-division 
                     proteins ZipA, ZapA, and ZapB oscillates along the cell 
                     with a periodicity that is opposite that of MinC.  The 
                     oscillation is dependent on the presence of FtsZ filaments 
                     and on the Min system |CITS: [24327341]|.
                     The components of the divisome appear to follow a ""first 
                     in, first out"" principle, i.e. FtsZ and ZapA are the first
                     proteins to depart the divisome |CITS: [27096604]|; 
                     however, ZapA remains within a diffuse structure at 
                     mid-cell after addition of a cell division inhibitor.
                     This structure requires the presence of ZapB |CITS: 
                     [28249098]|.
                     ZapB interacts with FtsZ and itself in a two-hybrid assay. 
                     A crystal structure of ZapB has been solved at 2.8 &Aring; 
                     resolution, showing that the protein forms antiparallel 
                     coiled-coil dimers.  <i>In vitro</i>, ZapB self-assembles 
                     into filaments that are enhanced in the presence of 
                     divalent cations |CITS: [18394147]|.
                     In the absence of ZapB, localization of FtsZ is more 
                     diffuse, and an <i>ftsZ84 zapB</i> double mutant has a 
                     synthetic growth defect |CITS: [18394147][22056926]|. 
                     Overexpression of ZapB leads to abnormal condensation of 
                     the nucleoid and formation of ectopic cell division septa. 
                     A <i>zapB</i> deletion strain shows delayed cell division, 
                     but is viable |CITS: [18394147]|.  Single-molecule based 
                     superresolution imaging shows an abnormal cell division 
                     septum and a disordered arrangement of FtsZ clusters |CITS:
                     [23859153]|.  A &Delta;<i>zapB</i> mutant is more 
                     susceptible to the cationic antimicrobial peptides (CAMPs) 
                     LL-37 and HNP-1 |CITS: [26810659]|.
                     ZapB: ""<b>Z</b> ring-<b>a</b>ssociated <b>p</b>rotein 
                     <b>B</b>"" |CITS: [18394147]|"
