     gene            complement(4108773..4109258)
                     /gene="rraA"
                     /locus_tag="b3929"
                     /label=rraA
     CDS             complement(4108773..4109258)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rraA"
                     /locus_tag="b3929"
                     /product="ribonuclease E inhibitor protein A"
                     /label=rraA
                     /note="RraA inhibits |FRAME: CPLX0-3461| activity by
                     binding to and masking the C-terminal RNA binding domain of
                     RNase E.  The interaction of RraA with the degradosome is 
                     facilitated by protein-RNA remodeling via the ATPase 
                     activity of RhlB |CITS: [20106955]|.
                     RraA physically interacts with RNase E, but does not 
                     interact with the RNA substrates |CITS: [13678585]|. 
                     High-affinity binding of RraA to RNase E requires the 
                     C-terminal domain (CTD) of RNase E |CITS: 
                     [13678585][16771842]|.  RraA interacts with both 
                     RNA-binding sites of RNase E and interferes with their 
                     interaction with RNA |CITS: [20106955]|.  RraA also 
                     interacts with the RhlB helicase component of the 
                     degradosome, and a ternary complex of RraA, RNase E and 
                     RhlB can be observed |CITS: [20106955][24045937]|.
                     Binding of RraA or RraB, a second modulator of RNase E 
                     activity, differently affect the composition of the 
                     degradosome |CITS: [16771842]|.  RraA also interacts with 
                     the DEAD-box protein SrmB and modulates its RNA-dependent 
                     ATPase activity |CITS: [24045937]|.
                     RNase II, RNase E, Orn, Hfq, PAP I, RNase III, RppH, RraA, 
                     and RraB are components of the RNA processing and 
                     degradation network and appear to colocalize to cellular 
                     structures that coil around the cell periphery |CITS: 
                     [24266791]|.
                     A crystal structure of RraA is presented at 2.0 &Aring; 
                     resolution.  RraA forms a homotrimer; the complex is shaped
                     like a ring with a hole of 12 &Aring; across.  RraA is 
                     structurally related to a family of aldolases |CITS: 
                     [14499605]|.
                     The regulatory interaction between RraA and RNase E and 
                     their orthologs appears to be evolutionarily conserved 
                     |CITS: [18510556][18756093][19194749]|.  Proteins with 
                     structural similarity to RraA from organisms such as 
                     <i>Pseudomonas putida</i> are class II aldolases; RraA 
                     itself lacks detectable C-C lyase activity.  Restoration of
                     the G-X<sub>20</sub>-R-D-X<sub>2</sub>-E/D motif by 
                     site-directed mutagenesis of RraA led to catalytic activity
                     |CITS: [24359411]|.
                     Transcription of <i>rraA</i> is 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent and increased upon entry into
                     stationary phase.  The stability of <i>rraA</i> mRNA itself
                     is dependent on the activity of RNase E |CITS: [16621818]|.
                      Overproduction of RraA causes pleiotropic phenotypes due 
                     to increased abundance of RNAs that are usually substrates 
                     of RNase E |CITS: [13678585]|. Overexpression of RraA 
                     rescues cells overexpressing RNase E from growth arrest 
                     |CITS: [16725107]| and phenocopies RNase E deficiency 
                     |CITS: [22719885]|.  Coexpression of <i>rraA</i> with 
                     recombinant membrane proteins alleviates their toxic effect
                     on the cell |CITS: [27797488]| and involves RNase E 
                     activity |CITS: [28501587]|.
                     RraA was originally mis-annotated as a SAM-dependent 
                     methyltransferase predicted to act in menaquinone 
                     biosynthesis, and given the name MenG (Hudspeth et al., 
                     unpublished; GenBank record U56082); however, the protein 
                     was found to lack both structural or functional indications
                     of methyltransferase activity |CITS: [13678585][14499605]|.
                     RraA: ""<b>r</b>egulator of <b>r</b>ibonuclease 
                     <b>a</b>ctivity <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [13678585]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [19215772][19667755][24438330]|"
