     gene            complement(4112308..4113267)
                     /gene="ftsN"
                     /locus_tag="b3933"
                     /label=ftsN
     CDS             complement(4112308..4113267)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ftsN"
                     /locus_tag="b3933"
                     /product="cell division protein FtsN"
                     /label=ftsN
                     /note="FtsN is an essential cell division protein present
                     at 3000-6000 molecules per cell; inactivation of 
                     <i>ftsN</i> results in filamentation and cell death |CITS: 
                     [8509333][15466024]|.
                     FtsN contains a small N-terminal cytoplasmic domain, a 
                     membrane-spanning region and a large periplasmic C-terminal
                     domain |CITS: [8631709]|. FtsN localizes to the septum 
                     during division; the periplasmic domain of FtsN is 
                     sufficient for correct localization |CITS: 
                     [9282742][19684127]|. The cytoplasmic domain and membrane 
                     spanning region determine membrane localization, but are 
                     not otherwise required for FtsN function |CITS: [8631709]|.
                     The FtsN periplasmic domain contains an &alpha;-helical 
                     region located near to the membrane, followed by a long 
                     unstructured region and a C-terminal SPOR domain which 
                     binds peptidoglycan |CITS: [15101973][15466024]| and is 
                     required for efficient accumulation of FtsN at constriction
                     sites |CITS: [19684127]| (see further |CITS: [24056104]|). 
                     SPOR domains bind to |FRAME: a-denuded-peptidoglycan 
                     denuded glycans| which are enriched in septal PG |CITS: 
                     [26305949]|. The binding affinity (K<sub>d</sub> ~10 
                     &micro;M) of the FtsN SPOR domain to denuded glycans in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> sacculi was lower than that measured for 
                     DamX SPOR domain; swapping the DamX SPOR domain for the 
                     FtsN SPOR domain did not change the activity |CITS: 
                     [36005810]|.
                     FtsN is recruited to the septum late in the cell cycle; 
                     localization to the Z ring is dependent on |FRAME: 
                     EG10347-MONOMER FtsZ|, |FRAME: EG10339-MONOMER FtsA|, 
                     |FRAME: G7258-MONOMER ZipA|, |FRAME: EG10341-MONOMER FtsI| 
                     (PBP3), and |FRAME: EG10342-MONOMER FtsQ| |CITS: 
                     [9282742][11703663][11948172][14702319]| (and see further 
                     |CITS: [15630023][16824093]|). Conversely, |FRAME: 
                     G7458-MONOMER AmiC| requires FtsN for localization to the Z
                     ring |CITS: [12787347]|.
                     FtsN interacts with the peptidoglycan (PG) synthase |FRAME:
                     EG10605-MONOMER MrcB| (PBP1B) <i>in vitro</I> and <i>in 
                     vivo</i>; FtsN stimulates MrcB-catalysed PG synthesis <i>in
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [17938168]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [30622193][33109614]|). <i>In vivo</i> FRET experiments 
                     indicate that FtsN forms homo-oligomers and interacts with 
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-7538 ZapA|, |FRAME: EG10344-MONOMER FtsW| and
                     FtsI |CITS: [20497333]|. FtsN interacts directly with FtsA 
                     <i>in vivo</i>; the cytoplasmic and transmembrane region of
                     FtsN is sufficient for this interaction |CITS: [22328664]|.
                     FtsN-FtsA interaction facilitates midcell localization of 
                     FtsN and precedes SPOR-dependent midcell recruitment |CITS:
                     [24750258]| (see further |CITS: [25496259][29967164]|). 
                     FtsN-FtsA interaction induces FtsA polymerization <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [36123441]|.
                     Deprivation of FtsN results in disassembly of divisome 
                     components |CITS: [20345660]|. FtsN is implicated in the 
                     regulation of |FRAME: EG10605-MONOMER MrcB| and |FRAME: 
                     EG10341-MONOMER FtsI| within the divisome |CITS: 
                     [30622193]|. High-resolution imaging of labeled FtsN and 
                     FtsZ supports a contention that the Z-ring proteins and the
                     peptidoglycan-synthesizing machinery are spatially 
                     separated during envelope constriction |CITS: [29193432]| 
                     (and further |CITS: [36180460]|. FtsN and |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7934| are implicated in a mechanism for activating 
                     the septal peptidoglycan synthase FtsWI |CITS: [ 
                     37524712]|.
                     <i>ftsN</i> was first identified as a multicopy suppressor 
                     of the <i>ftsA12</i>(Ts) mutation; overexpression of 
                     <i>ftsN</i> also suppresses the <i>ftsI23</i>(Ts) and 
                     <i>ftsQ1</i>(Ts) mutations, suggesting interactions between
                     these proteins |CITS: [8509333]|. In addition, FtsN 
                     overproduction can partially suppress thermosensitive or 
                     deletion mutants of <i>ftsK</i> |CITS: 
                     [9721304][16194242]|. Overproduction of FtsN does not 
                     affect the maturation time of the divisome |CITS: 
                     [15752189]|.
                     The main driver of rate-limitation for the initiation of 
                     constriction at the septum is FtsZ cellular concentration 
                     and accumulation within the Z-ring, rather than FtsN and 
                     FtsA; a new model is proposed |CITS: [39550358]|.
                     Selected reviews: |CITS: [15491352][12626683][16824090][25 
                     571948][28396350][28419603][35408901][37524757]|"
                     /translation="MAQRDYVRRSQPAPSRRKKSTSRKKQRNLPAVSPAMVAIAAAVLV
                     TFIGGLYFITHHKKEESETLQSQKVTGNGLPPKPEERWRYIKELESRQPGVRAPTEPSA
