     gene            complement(4114540..4116738)
                     /gene="priA"
                     /locus_tag="b3935"
                     /label=priA
     CDS             complement(4114540..4116738)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="priA"
                     /locus_tag="b3935"
                     /product="bifunctional replication restart protein PriA /
                     DNA replication restart primosome"
                     /label=priA
                     /note="PriA is a multifunctional protein; it has
                     single-stranded DNA-dependent ATPase activity, is a 3' 
                     &rarr; 5' DNA translocase, serves as a scaffold for 
                     recruitment of other proteins to form the |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3922|, and is a helicase that opens duplex DNA in its
                     role in restarting stalled replication forks. PriA, along 
                     with |FRAME: EG10747-MONOMER|, is required to restart DNA 
                     synthesis immediately following UV exposure |CITS: 
                     [11929519]|. Based on genetic interactions, there are 
                     multiple replication restart pathways, several of which 
                     require PriA, such as one using the |FRAME: EG10829-MONOMER
                     ""helicase RecG""| and another using the |FRAME: 
                     RUVABC-CPLX resolvasome RuvABC""| |CITS: 
                     [10835375][15049815][11864599][34423481]|. PriA is required
                     for inducible and constitutive stable DNA replication in 
                     the absence of protein synthesis |CITS: [7525276]|.
                     PriA binds to the 3' terminus of nascent DNA at stalled 
                     replication forks <i>in vitro</i>, leading to assembly of 
                     the full primosome at the binding site |CITS: [12917421]|. 
                     PriA preferentially binds to stalled forks with D loops, 
                     which are intermediates in recombination-based recovery 
                     from a stall |CITS: [10097074][9236123]|. Larger 
                     single-stranded gaps at the stall site favor |FRAME: 
                     EG10765-MONOMER PriC|-mediated restart rather than PriA 
                     involvement |CITS: [15749022]|. PriA binding is most 
                     effective when the lagging strand is duplex and the leading
                     strand is single stranded; the PriA specifically unwinds 
                     the lagging strand |CITS: [11580240]|. D-loop binding leads
                     to assembly of the full primosome at the stall site |CITS: 
                     [10455182]|.
                     In many plasmids and phages, PriA and the primosome 
                     function in initiation of normal DNA replication. In this 
                     context, PriA binds to the primosome assembly site (PAS), a
                     double-hairpin DNA structure that acts as the origin of 
                     replication for replicative DNA synthesis |CITS: 
                     [2931433][6130524][6273849][6109282][8366072][10356325]|. 
                     Following PAS binding, PriA translocates 3' to 5' along 
                     ssDNA in an ATP-dependent fashion, after which it can move 
                     onto and unwind duplex DNA, a process that also uses ATP 
                     |CITS: [2170365][2825188]|. This PriA-mediated unwinding 
                     moves just as fast as unwinding catalyzed by the normal 
                     replicative helicase, |FRAME: EG10236 DnaB|, and is 
                     stimulated by interaction with |FRAME: EG10976 
                     ""single-strand binding protein""| (SSB) via the SSB 
                     carboxy-terminus |CITS: [2824502][15576682]|. PriA helicase
                     activity is blocked by |FRAME: EG11038 Tus| bound at 
                     <I>ter</i> replication termination sites |CITS: 
                     [1317865][1533620]|.
                     PriA binding is the first step in assembly of the 
                     primosome, a multiprotein complex that restarts stalled 
                     replication forks in <i>E. coli</i> and initiates 
                     replication in various plasmids and phages |CITS: 
                     [8663104]|. The primosomal protein |FRAME: EG10764 PriB| 
                     helps PriA bind |FRAME: EG10244 DnaT|, as well as 
                     stimulating the processivity and helicase activity of PriA 
                     |CITS: [8663106][16188886]|. PriA activity doubles in the 
                     completed primosome |CITS: [8366072]|. Notably, PriA that 
                     lacks its helicase and translocation activities can still 
                     catalyze primosome assembly, and this alone appears to be 
                     sufficient to correct defects in homologous recombination 
                     and double-strand-break repair that are normally present in
                     <i>priA</i> null mutants |CITS: [1313026][8631700]|. The 
                     amino-terminal domain of PriA has been crystallized |CITS: 
                     [16226927]| and has a 3'-terminal nucleotide recognition 
                     site |CITS: [17464287]| with specific conserved amino acids
                     that bind the phosphodiester, ribose ring and nucleotide 
                     base |CITS: [30201718]|. PriA binds ssDNA as a monomer via 
                     a single DNA-binding site, binding to as few as 8 
                     nucleotides |CITS: [10956036][10875934]|. This binding 
                     occurs in the PriA amino-terminal domain, though the 
                     helicase domain works together with it synergistically to 
                     allow high-affinity binding of D-loops at replication forks
                     |CITS: [12151393][15252043]|. Helicase activity is not 
                     required for this binding |CITS: [12622722]|. The kinetics 
                     of PriA binding to ssDNA have been examined in detail 
                     |CITS: [15323559]|.
                     PriA has been shown to interact extensively with each arm 
                     of the branched DNA at a replication fork. The interactions
                     of the N-terminus with double-stranded and single-stranded 
                     DNA has been examined and found to have significant 
                     double-stranded DNA affinity |CITS: [21888358]|. An 
                     aromatic rich loop in PriA binds to the DNA replication 
                     fork where parental, leading and lagging strands intersect 
                     and allosterically activates ATP hydrolysis |CITS: 
                     [27484483]|. A crystal structure of the 
                     PriA/replication-fork complex and single-site variants 
                     revealed specific amino acid residues within the 
                     winged-helix domain that orients PriA to specific sites on 
                     the parental leading and lagging strands. A mutant with the
                     winged-helix domain deleted (&Delta;WHD) didn't unwind the 
                     lagging strand to the same extent as the wildtype and 
                     selectively inactivated the PriA-PriC replication restart 
                     pathway |CITS: [30201718]|. The interactions trigger 
                     structural changes that reshape PriA, creating a pore 
                     encircling the single-stranded lagging-strand DNA. In 
                     addition, the reshaping exposes a surface of PriA with 
                     which PriB docks |CITS: [37169801]|.
                     <i>priA</i> null mutants are unable to generate phiX174 
                     phage or maintain plasmids that use an <i>E. coli</i> 
                     origin of replication. They also have growth defects and a 
                     filamentous phenotype |CITS: [1826559]|. The SOS response 
                     is induced in these mutants |CITS: [1938875]|. 
                     Site-directed mutagenesis analyses suggested metal binding 
                     in a cysteine-rich region, revealed Zn<sup>2+</sup> is 
                     involved in its helicase activity, and that the ATPase 
                     function can be uncoupled from the helicase and DNA binding
                     activities |CITS: [8440719]|. Loss of PriA helicase 
                     activity suppresses the deleterious effects of the loss of 
                     RecG recombinational helicase function |CITS: [8955297]|. A
                     mutant that contains only the N-terminus and a mutant that 
                     contains the C terminus (split at the winged-helix domain) 
                     are both required for near full PriA activity |CITS: 
                     [28607160]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [10506828][10754552][10844643][20097140][25 
                     136561][28320884][29202195][32120891]|"
