     gene            4123763..4125943
                     /gene="katG"
                     /locus_tag="b3942"
                     /label=katG
     CDS             4123763..4125943
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="katG"
                     /locus_tag="b3942"
                     /product="trifunctional catalase/hydroperoxidase HPI /
                     catalase / hydroperoxidase"
                     /EC_number="1.11.1.21"
                     /label=katG
                     /note="There are two distinct catalases in <i>E. coli</i>.
                     The KatG enzyme is the bifunctional hydroperoxidase I 
                     (HPI), with both catalase and peroxidase activity. A 
                     monofunctional catalase, HPII, is encoded by <i>katE</i> 
                     |CITS: [3019370]|. Endogenously produced 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> is primarily scavenged by a 
                     third enzyme, |FRAME: CPLX0-245|, while catalase is the 
                     primary scavenger at high H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     concentrations |CITS: [11717276][11717277]|.
                     The catalase activity uses one molecule of 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> as the electron donor and a 
                     second molecule of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> as the 
                     electron acceptor, producing oxygen and water. At lower 
                     concentrations of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, the 
                     peroxidase activity is able to utilize a suitable electron 
                     donor other than H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> |CITS: 
                     [10801338]|. For both activities, the initial step is the 
                     reduction of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> to H<sub>2</sub>O 
                     by a ferric heme, producing the ferryl porphyrin cation 
                     radical
                     intermediate known as compound I. In the catalase reaction,
                     compound I is returned directly to the ferric state by 
                     oxidizing a second molecule of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     to O<sub>2</sub>.
                     Under certain conditions, KatG associates with thioredoxin,
                     which hypothetically could act as the external electron 
                     donor |CITS: [15004283]|. In the presence of 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, overexpressed |FRAME: 
                     EG11404-MONOMER ""YchF""| was found to interact directly 
                     with KatG and to inhibit its activity |CITS: [23139412]|.
                     KatG was initially thought to be periplasmic |CITS: 
                     [3293564]|, but was later localized to the cytoplasm |CITS:
                     [10068295]|.
                     KatG consists of two large domains that may have arisen 
                     from a gene duplication event |CITS: [1954228][11257490]|. 
                     Although the C-terminal domain of KatG is not predicted to 
                     bind heme or have catalytic activity itself, it is required
                     for KatG activity |CITS: [15240123]|. Crystal structures of
                     the C-terminal domain of KatG have been solved |CITS: 
                     [11976501][15388929]|. The C-terminal domain may serve as a
                     platform where its I'-helix directs structural adjustments 
                     of the N-terminal domain |CITS: [16752901][23084782]|.
                     The dimeric form of KatG is the smallest active species of 
                     the enzyme |CITS: [3886630]|. Under some conditions, KatG 
                     was observed to exist as an isoenzyme pair, HPI-A and HPI-B
                     |CITS: [3886630][3904630]|. KatG activity is much more 
                     sensitive to elevated temperature than KatE |CITS: 
                     [10194300]|.
                     The catalytic activity of site-directed mutants in 
                     predicted active site residues has been investigated |CITS:
                     [10801338]|. A W105F mutant has severely reduced catalase 
                     activity, but slightly increased hydroperoxidase activity 
                     |CITS: [10801338][11356135]|. Monofunctional and 
                     bifunctional peroxidases share highly similar active sites.
                     A 35 amino acid structure connecting the F and G helices in
                     KatG is unique to the bifunctional enzyme; deletion of this
                     loop causes loss of 99.8% of catalase activity, while the 
                     mutant enzyme retains 50% of its peroxidase activity |CITS:
                     [15147967]|. Mutagenesis of the LL1 loop similarly reduces 
                     catalase activity and enhances peroxidase activity |CITS: 
                     [23018273]|.
                     KatG production can be induced by ascorbate |CITS: 
                     [3886630]| or H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> |CITS: 
                     [3886630][3904630][9209028]|. Expression of <i>katG</i> 
                     increases at stationary phase |CITS: [8157598]|, but 
                     regulation does not appear to be dependent on RpoS |CITS: 
                     [8157598]|. KatG is required for rapid recovery of 
                     transport function in H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>-treated 
                     |CITS: [2832383]| or near-UV exposed |CITS: [2222441]| 
                     cells. Overexpression of <i>katG</i> suppresses 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> sensitivity of an <i>oxyR</i> 
                     mutant strain |CITS: [2847922]|. An <i>ahpCF katG</i> 
                     double mutant is extremely sensitive to the increased 
                     endogenous production of peroxides during phosphate 
                     starvation |CITS: [11251823]|. Hpx<sup>-</sup> mutants 
                     lacking all three H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>-scavenging 
                     enzymes (<i>katG katE ahpCF</i>) grow poorly under aerobic 
                     conditions |CITS: [15967999]|. Overexpression of 
                     <i>katG</i> from an ASKA plasmid leads to a more than 
                     3-fold increase in the MIC of hydrogen peroxide |CITS: 
                     [34072091]|. The amount of KatG was increased in a 
                     proteomics study in a &Delta;|FRAME: CPLX0-245 Ahp| mutant 
                     |CITS: [37725887]|.
                     Persistant, sublethally injured <i>E. coli</i> induced by 
                     lactic acid stress exhibited slower resuscitation rates in 
                     <i>&Delta;katG</i> and <i>&Delta;katE</i> mutants, though 
                     not as slow as <i>&Delta;sodC</i> and <i>&Delta;rpoS</i> 
                     mutants, relative to wild-type |CITS: [31487417]|. 
                     Overexpression of <i>katG, ahpCF, sodA</i> or <i>oxyR</i> 
                     in nutrient shift assays in the presence of ampicillin, 
                     specifically when switching from glycerol to oleic acid, 
                     alleviated the reactive oxygen species induced killing of 
                     persister cells |CITS: [39470288]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [7557318][9010748][22209752][22330759][22609271]|"
