     gene            complement(4138460..4140193)
                     /gene="eptC"
                     /locus_tag="b3955"
                     /label=eptC
     CDS             complement(4138460..4140193)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="eptC"
                     /locus_tag="b3955"
                     /product="bifunctional phosphoethanolamine transferase
                     EptC / phosphoethanolamine transferase"
                     /label=eptC
                     /note="EptC is required for the incorporation of 
                     phosphoethanolamine (P-EtN) onto the phosphorylated heptose
                     I residue of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 lipopolysaccharide (LPS) 
                     |CITS: [23372159]|. P-EtN modifications are absent on the 
                     LPS of <i>E. coli</i> grown under phosphate-rich conditions
                     but are enriched in the LPS from growth under 
                     phosphate-limiting conditions |CITS: [23372159]|.
                     <i>eptC</i> deleted strains have severe growth defects in 
                     the presence of SDS (0.25% - 1%) and are more sensitive to 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> than the wild-type |CITS: [23372159]|. 
                     Transcription of an <i>eptC-lacZ</i> fusion is reduced 4-5 
                     fold in a <i>phoR</i> deletion mutant and reduced 2-fold in
                     a <i>basR</i> deletion mutant. Two putative PhoB binding 
                     sites are located in the promoter region of <i>eptC</i> 
                     |CITS: [23372159]|.
                     LPS from an <i>E. coli</i> strain lacking all three 
                     <i>ept</i> genes (<i>eptA, eptB</i> and <i>eptC</i>) does 
                     not contain any P-EtN modifications |CITS: [23372159]|. 
                     EptC (formerly YijP) has similarity to YijP of <i>Ecoli</i>
                     . K1, which is required for that strain to invade brain
                     microvascular endothelial cells and thereby cross the 
                     blood-brain barrier |CITS: [10456927]|.  Reviews discuss 
                     the
                     role of YijP in meningitis caused by pathogenic <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [11008113][11298651]|.
                     EptC was identified as a component of a complex in the 
                     inner membrane together with an unidentified 36 kDa protein
                     |CITS: [16079137]|."
                     /translation="MHSTEVQAKPLFSWKALGWALLYFWFFSTLLQAIIYISGYSGTNG
