     gene            complement(4140375..4143026)
                     /gene="ppc"
                     /locus_tag="b3956"
                     /label=ppc
     CDS             complement(4140375..4143026)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ppc"
                     /locus_tag="b3956"
                     /product="phosphoenolpyruvate carboxylase"
                     /EC_number="4.1.1.31"
                     /label=ppc
                     /note="Phosphoenolpyruvate carboxylase (Ppc) is an
                     anaplerotic enzyme that replenishes oxaloacetate in the 
                     tricarboxylic acid cycle. The carboxylation of 
                     phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) to form oxaloacetate and 
                     P<sub>i</sub> proceeds in two steps: formation of 
                     carboxyphosphate and the enolate form of pyruvate, followed
                     by carboxylation of the enolate and release of phosphate 
                     and oxaloacetate.
                     Ppc activity is allosterically regulated by many kinds of 
                     effectors |CITS: [4872182]| that interact with several 
                     distinct binding sites. The <i>in vivo</i> concentrations 
                     of the various regulators differ depending on the growth 
                     conditions, resulting in different levels of enzyme 
                     activity |CITS: [6987214]|. The primary activator was 
                     thought to be acetyl-CoA; fructose-1,6-bisphosphate (FBP) 
                     and GTP can act synergistically with acetyl-CoA |CITS: 
                     [7040354]|. A mechanism for synergistic activaton of the 
                     enzyme by different effectors was proposed |CITS: 
                     [6986377]|. In a proteome-wide screen, Ppc was found to 
                     bind citrate, ATP, and FBP. Citrate inhibits Ppc activity 
                     <i>in vitro</i>; the presence of FBP or acetyl-CoA had no 
                     effect on the inhibition |CITS: [29307493]|.
                     Analysis of metabolomics and metabolic flux data after 
                     removal of glucose shows a buildup of PEP and a 99% 
                     decrease of Ppc activity; this effect is due to the 
                     decreased level of FBP and the ultrasensitive response of 
                     Ppc to FBP levels in the presence of acetyl-CoA and 
                     aspartate. The highly sensitive regulation of Ppc activity 
                     enables adaptation to variable carbon availability and 
                     prevents a futile cycle during gluconeogenesis |CITS: 
                     [22522319]|.
                     Crystal structures for Ppc complexed with L-aspartate, 
                     Mn<sup>2+</sup>, Mn<sup>2+</sup> and/or a PEP analog were 
                     determined |CITS: [2677392][9927652][10481043][12467579]|. 
                     Ppc is a homotetramer in a dimer-of-dimers arrangement 
                     |CITS: [9927652]|. The binding site for the inhibitor 
                     L-aspartate is located approximately 20&Aring; away from 
                     the catalytic site |CITS: [9927652]|. Additional crystal 
                     structures with Mn<sup>2+</sup> and a PEP analog enabled 
                     identification of the active site and proposals for the 
                     reaction mechanism and the mechanism for allosteric 
                     regulation |CITS: [10481043][12467579]|.
                     Ppc may be a target for the antimicrobial peptide 
                     Lactoferricin B |CITS: [22164243]|.
                     Various Ppc mutants have been isolated or generated by 
                     site-directed mutagenesis |CITS: [3902793][2016273]|. A 
                     <i>ppc</i> point mutant (Thr188Ile) grows similarly to wild
                     type on TCA cycle intermediates as the sole carbon source, 
                     but grows very slowly on glucose. This mutant contains Ppc 
                     that is primarily in the homodimeric form; it can be 
                     activated by acetyl-CoA, but not by various co-activators 
                     |CITS: [7012114][3536922]|. An Arg438Cys mutant is also 
                     predominantly homodimeric |CITS: [9927652]|. An Arg313Gln 
                     mutant is desensitized to activation by FBP |CITS: 
                     [22522319]|. A Lys620Ser mutant is less sensitive to 
                     inhibition by aspartate and malate |CITS: [9249011]|. Two 
                     mutations, Ile829Ser and Arg849Ser, were identified by 
                     adaptive laboratory evolution and also cause decreased 
                     sensitivity to inhibition by L-aspartate |CITS: 
                     [29457640]|. Both the conserved His138 |CITS: [1765093]| 
                     and Arg587 |CITS: [7490260]| residues are required for 
                     catalysis of the second half-reaction, the carboxylation of
                     the enolate form of pyruvate |CITS: [1765093]|.
                     Overexpression of Ppc improves the growth yield on glucose 
                     |CITS: [8285716]|, increases production of succinate from 
                     glucose by fermentation |CITS: [8633880]|, and reduces 
                     acetate excretion |CITS: [9251207]|. Gene expression, 
                     enzyme activities, metabolite concentrations and metabolic 
                     flux have been measured in a <i>ppc</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [14963616][15158257][15781419]|. Metabolic modeling of 
                     <i>ppc</i> mutants is enhancing comprehension of the 
                     metabolic network and enabling predictions for metabolic 
                     engineering |CITS: [20679215][21092096]|. In a 
                     high-throughput experiment evaluating growth of single-gene
                     deletion mutants on a variety of carbon sources, the 
                     &Delta;<i>ppc</i> mutant was only able to grow efficiently 
                     with acetate, &alpha;-ketoglutarate, fumarate, malate, 
                     succinate, or ribose as the sole source of carbon |CITS: 
                     [32994326]|.
                     Ppc activity was initially measured to be highest during 
                     growth on glucose |CITS: [7030789]|. Further comparisons 
                     showed that it is highest during growth on glycerol as the 
                     sole source of carbon, followed by gluconate and glucose; 
                     growth on acetate represses the activity |CITS: 
                     [12655459]|. Using an engineered promoter library, the 
                     optimal expression level for <i>ppc</i> resulting in the 
                     highest biomass yield during growth on glucose minimal 
                     media was determined |CITS: [16123130]|. <i>rne</i> mutants
                     produce significantly less Ppc than wild type |CITS: 
                     [23275245]|.
                     Both deletion and increased expression of <i>ppc</i> has 
                     been used to increase the yield of a variety of products in
                     metabolic engineering studies |CITS: [30012131][36532595]|.
                     An evolved mutant of <i>icd</i>, specifically the amino 
                     acid substitutions G205D, was able to enhance the |FRAME: 
                     GLYOXYLATE-BYPASS| much better than |FRAME: EG10491| when 
                     <i>ppc</i> is deleted |CITS: [39870377]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [12781768][15725057][24688678]|"
