                     /product="acetylornithine deacetylase"
                     /EC_number="3.5.1.16"
                     /label=argE
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> acetylornithine deacetylase (ArgE)
                     is a homodimer that catalyses the deacylation of 
                     N<sup>2</sup>-acetyl-L-ornithine to yield ornithine and 
                     acetate |CITS: [1551850]|. Ornithine is an obligatory 
                     intermediate in the arginine biosynthetic pathway and a 
                     branch point in the synthesis of polyamines |CITS: 
                     [10684608]|. It is a metalloenzyme that is activated by 
                     cobalt and inorganic phosphate |CITS: [1628835]|. The 
                     enzyme contains two metal binding sites: a high-affinity 
                     site that shows a preference for Zn(II) and a second lower 
                     affinity site that shows a strong preference for Co(II) 
                     |CITS: [10684608]|. ArgE can also be activated by Mn(II) 
                     ions |CITS: [16201833]|. Two Mn(II) ions bind to ArgE to 
                     form a dinuclear site in ArgE |CITS: [17333302]|."
