     gene            complement(4148206..4148322)
                     /gene="oxyS"
                     /locus_tag="b4458"
                     /label=oxyS
     misc_RNA        complement(4148206..4148322)
                     /gene="oxyS"
                     /locus_tag="b4458"
                     /product="small regulatory RNA OxyS"
                     /note="OxyS is an Hfq-dependent small RNA that plays a 
                     regulatory role in the oxidative stress response and has an
                     antimutator effect |CITS: [9230301]|. Expression of OxyS in
                     response to oxidative stress impairs cell division enabling
                     more efficient recovery |CITS: [29237698]| and regulates 
                     Fe-S cluster biogenesis which includes providing resistance
                     to aminoglycosides |CITS: [39495911]|. OxyS is involved in 
                     regulation of <i>fhlA</i> |CITS: 
                     [9230301][9774350][20927406]|, <i>rpoS</i> |CITS: 
                     [9230301][9478196][9774349][20683441]|, and <i>flhDC</i> 
                     |CITS: [22925049]|. Additional regulatory targets were 
                     computationally predicted and verified by Northern analysis
                     |CITS: [16717284]|. In |CITS: [38071477]|, 18 target genes 
                     of OxyS were computationally predicted.
                     OxyS also regulates genes involved in the generation of 
                     intracellular H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> |CITS: 
                     [10368161]|, genes involved in cellular elongation |CITS: 
                     [38071477]|, and regulates translation of the iron-sulfur 
                     cluster transcriptional regulator |FRAME: CPLX0-8083 
                     ""IscR""| mRNA under oxidative stress conditions |CITS: 
                     [39495911]|.
                     OxyS interacts with the Hfq protein |CITS: 
                     [9774349][11804582][21510661][23804244]|, which stimulates 
                     pairing of regions of OxyS RNA with its mRNA target |CITS: 
                     [11804582]|[39495911]. Interaction with Hfq alters the 
                     secondary structure of OxyS via a multi-step process |CITS:
                     [23804244][35934049]|. Crystal structures of the A-rich 
                     linker region of OxyS binding at the distal side of Hfq 
                     have been solved |CITS: [25670676]|. OxyS RNA binds to 
                     <i>fhlA</i> mRNA and inhibits translation by interfering 
                     with the binding of the mRNA by the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [9774350][10903857]|. The Hfq-mediated interaction between 
                     OxyS and <i>flhA</i> appears to be between the N-site bases
                     of (ARN)<sub>X</sub> motifs |CITS: [28852099]|.
                     The mechanism of <i>rpoS</i> regulation by OxyS was long 
                     unclear. The 5' end of a stem-loop region in OxyS may base 
                     pair with the <i>rpoS</i> mRNA |CITS: [16039523]|; direct 
                     interaction of OxyS with <i>rpoS</i> mRNA has been shown 
                     |CITS: [21793590]|. However, while its interaction with Hfq
                     is strong, OxyS only shows weak affinity for the 
                     <i>rpoS</i> leader region. Regulation by small RNAs can be 
                     affected by competition for Hfq |CITS: [21189298]|; 
                     regulation of <i>rpoS</i> translation by OxyS may thus be 
                     due to sequestration of Hfq rather than binding to 
                     <i>rpoS</i> mRNA |CITS: [21889623]|. Indeed, expression of 
                     OxyS at physiologically relevant levels reduces the 
                     abundance of other small RNAs including DsrA (a positive 
                     regulator of <i>rpoS</i>) and reduces their association 
                     with Hfq. Increased levels of Hfq overcome this competition
                     effect |CITS: [22040174]|.
                     Overexpression of OxyS decreases growth due to increased 
                     repression of <i>nusG</i> expression, which in turn allows 
                     increased expression of <i>kilR</i>. KilR interferes with 
                     cell division, thus enabling recovery from oxidative stress
                     |CITS: [29237698]|.
                     The secondary structure and three-dimensional models of 
                     OxyS have been mapped by various methods |CITS: 
                     [21793590][37380360]|. It was found that OxyS has four 
                     stem-loop structures (SL1 to 4) and that this sRNA adopts 
                     an elongated boomerang-like shape |CITS: [37380360]|. The 
                     SL1 spans nucleotides 1-47, SL2 50-63, SL3 90-109, and SL4 
                     72-89 of OxyS |CITS: [37380360]|. In the case of 
                     interaction of OxyS with <i>fhlA</i> mRNA, SL1 and SL3 
                     interact with the mRNA and Mg<sup>2+</sup> is required for 
                     that interaction |CITS: [37380360]|. Additional 
                     interactions of OxyS with ribosomal protein S1 and the 
                     &beta; subunit of RNA polymerase were identified |CITS: 
                     [18388495]|.
                     OxyS RNA is abundant and relatively stable, with a 
                     half-life measured to be 12-15 minutes |CITS: [9230301]| or
                     approximately 22 minutes |CITS: [20016254]|. Growth phase 
                     does not affect the stability of OxyS in the MG1655 strain 
                     background. However, in a <i>hfq</i> mutant strain the 
                     stability of OxyS in stationary phase is significantly 
                     decreased |CITS: [20016254]|. The |FRAME: RNA0-412| appears
                     to be able to act as a sponge to decrease OxyS levels 
                     |CITS: [33460557]|.
                     The orthologues of OxyS and their targets in other 
                     organisms have been analyzed in |CITS: [38071477]|. 
                     Transcription of <i>oxyS</i> is induced by hydrogen 
                     peroxide stress |CITS: [9230301]|. Levels of OxyS are 
                     higher in a |FRAME: EG10542| mutant than in wild type 
                     |CITS: [16750174]|. Degradation of OxyS RNA is probably 
                     stimulated by RNA duplex formation between the sRNA and its
                     target RNA |CITS: [12975324]|. Overexpression of OxyS 
                     decreases swimming and swarming motility and curli 
                     expression |CITS: [26469694]| and inhibits growth by 
                     impairing cell division |CITS: [29237698]|.
                     Mutation studies along with electrophoretic mobility shift 
                     assays (EMSAs) identified the base-pairing site in the 
                     5'-UTR of IscR mRNA; the interaction is improved by Hfq 
                     |CITS: [39495911]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9830102][10068996][11804580][12753181][12 
                     732300][15063850][15487940][23070552][29137641][29992897][ 
                     32213244][40015371]|"
     gene            4148418..4149335
                     /gene="oxyR"
                     /locus_tag="b3961"
                     /label=oxyR
     CDS             4148418..4149335
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="oxyR"
                     /locus_tag="b3961"
                     /product="DNA-binding transcriptional dual regulator OxyR"
                     /label=oxyR
                     /note="OxyR, ""oxidative stress regulator,"" is the 
                     transcriptional dual regulator for the expression of 
                     antioxidant genes in response to oxidative stress, 
                     particularly, elevated levels of hydrogen peroxide. The 
                     OxyR regulon includes genes involved in peroxide 
                     metabolism, redox balance, and peroxide protection by, for 
                     example, manganese uptake |CITS: 
                     [2256675][11443091][12100544][19400769]|. Moreover, OxyR 
                     activates the synthesis of the small, noncoding <i>oxyS</i>
                     RNA. This allows OxyR to regulate as many as 40 additional 
                     gene products indirectly by affecting mRNA stability or 
                     translation efficiency |CITS: [9230301]|. This 
                     transcription factor also appears to regulate the 
                     replication initiation process |CITS: [37482259]|.  OxyR 
                     acts as a repressor for its own synthesis in both the 
                     oxidized and reduced forms |CITS: [2471187][1864839]|. 
                     Accordingly, point mutations observed in <i>oxyR</i> as a 
                     result of adaptive evolution under high iron conditions 
                     lead to increased expression levels of OxyR-regulated 
                     genes, also termed the OxyR i-modulon |CITS: [31651953]|. 
                     For more information on i-modulons, see |CITS: [31797920]|.
                      Based on microarray assays, a new function as a regulator 
                     of S-nitrosylation (a nitrosative stress regulon) under 
                     anaerobic conditions has been determined for OxyR |CITS: 
                     [22539721]|, and it is distinct from the OxyR regulon 
                     activated by oxidative stress |CITS: [22539721]|. The role 
                     of OxyR in iron-sulfur (Fe-S) cluster biogenesis was 
                     simulated and analyzed in a mathematical model in |CITS: 
                     [37223744]|.
                     OxyR appears to be indirectly involved in the formation of 
                     persister cells through the upregulation of proteins 
                     involved in the detoxification of reactive oxygen species 
                     (ROS) |CITS:[39470288]|.
                     It has been demonstrated that OxyR is necessary for the 
                     resumption of the replication process following hydrogen 
                     peroxide stress, through its role as a regulator of genes 
                     related to manganese uptake |CITS: [37493920]|.
                     Various target genes of OxyR were found upregulated after 
                     10 min of exposure to 2.5 mM H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> in 
                     comparison with controls, despite <i>oxyR</i> not being 
                     found to be differentially transcribed|CITS:[35453340]|. It
                     has been demonstrated that the OxyR regulon is 
                     differentially regulated under oxidative stress 
                     |CITS:[39541985]|. One set of the regulon is downregulated,
                     while another set is rapidly upregulated after induction, 
                     and then the upregulation decays rapidly |CITS:[39541985]|.
                     Meanwhile, other genes are gradually upregulated, and this 
                     regulation is more sustained |CITS:[39541985]|. It has been
                     suggested that differences in the dissociation constants of
                     the OxyR promoter and the effects of stress on cell growth 
                     explain these divergent patterns in gene regulation 
                     |CITS:[39541985]|.
                     In a gene expression study during the transition from 
                     aerobic to anaerobic conditions, part of the regulatory 
                     cascade involving the protein OxyR was analyzed |CITS: 
                     [38891842]|.
                     OxyR consists of 305 amino acid residues and is a member of
                     the LysR family of bacterial transcription factors |CITS: 
                     [2471187][2511419]|. It is a tetramer in solution |CITS: 
                     [7868603]|. OxyR consists of two domains, the N-terminal 
                     domain, carrying a helix-turn-helix motif, and the 
                     C-terminal regulatory domain. A flexible linker connects 
                     both domains. The C-terminal domain contains the 
                     determinants for oligomerization and two redox-active 
                     cysteines, Cys199 and Cys208 |CITS: 
                     [7868603][15543158][12595552]|. OxyR functions as a 
                     reversible cellular redox switch. In the presence of 100 nM
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, activation occurs by the 
                     formation of an intramolecular disulfide bond between 
                     Cys199 and Cys208 |CITS: [2183352][15543158]|. It was 
                     determined that concentrations of >2 &mu;M extracellular 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> trigger the intracellular OxyR 
                     stress response, and >5 &mu;M begins to impair cell growth 
                     in minimal medium |CITS: [29550333]|. Consistent with this,
                     in minimal medium dose-dependent growth retardation 
                     occurred in the wild type and in a Glucose Minimal Media 
                     Optimized Strain (GMOS), but no significant impact of 
                     H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> on the growth profile of 
                     iron-evolved strains was noted, as overexpression of 
                     OxyR-regulated ROS-detoxifying genes rendered the strains 
                     tolerant to peroxide |CITS: [31651953]|. In contrast with 
                     activation, inactivation of OxyR involves reduction of the 
                     disulfide bond by glutaredoxin 1 and glutathione |CITS: 
                     [9497290]|. Oxidation is significantly faster than 
                     reduction, allowing for transient activation in a reducing 
                     environment |CITS: [10339558]|. OxyR is also activated 
                     under nitrosative stress; this reaction involves 
                     S-nitrosylation of OxyR on Cys25 in planktonic cells under 
                     aerobic and anaerobic growth conditions |CITS: 
                     [8797819][33850153]|. A mutant in Cys25 causes an increment
                     of biofilm formation |CITS: [33850153]|. On the other hand,
                     OxyR is not activated by an excess of copper, a metal that 
                     could adhere to the cysteines of the protein |CITS: 
                     [36756756]|.
                     It has been demonstrated that oxidized acetosyringone (AS) 
                     induces the expression of the transcriptional regulator 
                     OxyR |CITS: [38671069]|.
                     The crystal structures of the regulatory domain of OxyR in 
                     both its reduced and oxidized forms have been solved. A 
                     comparison of both structures reveals that oxidation is 
                     accompanied by a significant structural change in the 
                     regulatory domain |CITS: [11301006]|. OxyR also senses 
                     sulfane sulfur (H<sub>2</sub>S<sub>n</sub>), which is toxic
                     under both anaerobic and aerobic conditions at high levels.
                     OxyR is required to deal with H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> 
                     only under aerobic conditions, whereas it responds to 
                     H<sub>2</sub>S<sub>n</sub> under both aerobic and anaerobic
                     conditions |CITS: [31421411]|. Sulfane sulfur modifies OxyR
                     at Cys<sup>199</sup> to form a protein persulfide that in 
                     turn activates the expression of thioredoxin, glutaredoxin,
                     and catalase. The Cys residues C199 and C208 are required 
                     for such induction, and OxyR Cys<sup>199</sup> 
                     persulfidation is likely the mechanism of sensing 
                     H<sub>2</sub>S<sub>n</sub> |CITS: [31421411]|.
                     Under ampicillin treatment, the activity of OxyR appears to
                     be affected |CITS: [33731833]|.
                     In systematic studies of oligomerization, it was shown that
                     some members of the LysR family, like OxyR, interact with 
                     other members of the family to form heterodimers, but the 
                     physiological significance of this is unknown |CITS: 
                     [21187915]|. OxyR homologs are widely present in bacteria; 
                     4772 homologs of OxyR were identified and determined to be 
                     distributed in 4494 bacterial genomes |CITS: [31421411]|. 
                     Although their interchangeability of function between 
                     species might not occur, for example, the OxyR of <i> E. 
                     coli</i> is unable to substitute for the function of its 
                     homolog in <i>Shewanella oneidensis</i>, that is probably 
                     due to the fact that OxyR in <i>S. oneidensis </i> forms 
                     larger oligomers than in <i> E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [35073744]|. A comparison of structure and oligomerization 
                     of OxyR among some species has been reported |CITS: 
                     [35073744]|.
                     OxyR can bind as a pair of dimers bending to the DNA |CITS:
                     [12706716][22692422]| and recognizes a motif comprised of 
                     ATAGnt elements spaced at 10-bp intervals |CITS: 
                     [8087856]|. The binding to DNA differs significantly for 
                     the oxidized and reduced forms |CITS: [8087856]|. Reduced 
                     OxyR binds DNA at two pairs of major grooves separated by 
                     one helical turn, while oxidized OxyR occupies four 
                     consecutive major grooves. When bound to DNA, oxidized OxyR
                     stimulates transcription by interacting with the C-terminal
                     domain of the &alpha;-subunit of RNA polymerase |CITS: 
                     [7592462]|.
                     A review of the function of OxyR in protecting against 
                     nitrosative stress under anaerobic conditions revealed 
                     parallels between regulation of S-nitrosylation and hypoxic
                     signaling by nitric oxide (NO) in mammalian cells, and in 
                     mitochondria in particular |CITS: [31691575]|. 
                     Interconnectivity between the <i>oxyR</i> gene and the 
                     genes encoding the TFs YgfI and YddM has been reported 
                     |CITS:[35835781]|.
                     The expression of <i>oxyR</i> is positively regulated by 
                     cAMP-activated Crp protein during exponential growth and 
                     negatively regulated by RpoS when cells enter stationary 
                     phase |CITS: [9324269]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [22381957][31691575][36972330][40583719]|"
