     gene            complement(4149318..4150718)
                     /gene="sthA"
                     /locus_tag="b3962"
                     /label=sthA
     CDS             complement(4149318..4150718)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="sthA"
                     /locus_tag="b3962"
                     /product="multifunctional soluble pyridine nucleotide 
                     transhydrogenase / NAD(P)H oxidase (H2O2-forming) / 
                     pyridine nucleotide transhydrogenase / NAD(P)H oxidase 
                     (superoxide-forming)"
                     /EC_number="1.6.1.3; 1.6.3.1"
                     /label=sthA
                     /note="<i>E. coli</i> contains both a soluble and a 
                     membrane-bound pyridine nucleotide transhydrogenase. The 
                     soluble pyridine nucleotide transhydrogenase is the 
                     <i>sthA</i> gene product; its primary physiological role 
                     appears to be the reoxidation of NADPH |CITS: 
                     [11731130][14660605]|. The membrane-bound 
                     proton-translocating transhydrogenase is the <i>pntAB</i> 
                     gene product; |FRAME: PYRNUTRANSHYDROGEN-CPLX PntAB| is a 
                     major source of NADPH |CITS: [14660605]|.
                     SthA contains noncovalently bound FAD and is present in a 
                     form consisting of seven or eight monomers |CITS: 
                     [9922271]|. While the <i>in vivo</i> function of the enzyme
                     is to oxidize excess of NADPH forming NADH, the enzyme is 
                     able to catalyze the reaction in the opposite direction, 
                     and most metabolic engineering reports employing the enzyme
                     benefit from that activity |CITS: [23794523][30962822]|.
                     The enzyme contains a CXXXXT motif that is well conserved 
                     in pyridine nucleotide transhydrogenase and distinguishes 
                     these enzymes from the related family of flavoprotein 
                     disulfide reductases, in which a CXXXXC motif is conserved 
                     |CITS: [35933012]|.
                     Moderate overexpression of SthA allows an increased maximal
                     growth rate of a phosphoglucose isomerase mutant |CITS: 
                     [11731130]|, and a <i>pgi sthA</i> double mutant is not 
                     viable |CITS: [14660605]|. These phenotypes may be due to 
                     the ability of SthA to restore the cellular redox balance 
                     under conditions of excess NADPH formation |CITS: 
                     [11731130][14660605]|. Mutations in <i>sthA</i> appear 
                     during adaptation of a <i>pgi</i> mutant strain to growth 
                     on glucose minimal medium |CITS: [21079674]|. Transcription
                     of <i>sthA</i> is downregulated by growth on glycerol 
                     |CITS: [14660605]|.
                     In <i>E. coli</i> mutant strains grown on different carbon 
                     sources, such as glycerol and glucose for an <i>lrp</i> 
                     mutant and glucose for an <i>arcA</i> mutant strain, the 
                     <i>udhA</i> promoter activity was increased, suggesting a 
                     role as a repressor for those regulators |CITS:[27488847]|.
                     A 2022 study described an undocumented minor oxidase 
                     activity under aerobic conditions, during which the enzyme 
                     transfers the electrons from reduced pyridine nucleotides 
                     to molecular oxygen, producing hydrogen peroxide and 
                     superoxide radical. Under the experimental conditions, 98% 
                     of the NADH substrate molecules were used for reduction of 
                     thio-NADP+, with only 2% of the reducing equivalents 
                     transferred to molecular oxygen, which was mostly converted
                     into hydrogen peroxide |CITS: [35933012]|."
