     gene            complement(4152098..4153198)
                     /gene="trmA"
                     /locus_tag="b3965"
                     /label=trmA
     CDS             complement(4152098..4153198)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="trmA"
                     /locus_tag="b3965"
                     /product="bifunctional tRNA m(5)U54 methyltransferase / 
                     tRNA(m(5)U54)methyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.35"
                     /label=trmA
                     /note="TrmA is the methyltransferase that catalyzes
                     methylation of U54 in the T&Psi; loop of tRNAs |CITS: 
                     [1100618]| as well as methylation of the corresponding U341
                     residue within the tRNA-like domain of |FRAME: SSRA-RNA| 
                     |CITS: [23603891]|. In addition to its catalytic activity, 
                     TrmA also acts as a tRNA chaperone |CITS: [32597953]|.
                     tRNAs carry multiple modifications, often in nearby 
                     nucleotide residues. The preferred substrate for the TrmA 
                     methyltransferase activity is unmodified tRNA, while its 
                     binding activity is highest with &Psi;55-modified tRNA 
                     |CITS: [32385137]|.
                     The catalytic mechanism involves formation of a covalent 
                     complex between the catalytic Cys324 residue of the enzyme 
                     and tRNA |CITS: [3327525][1911760]| and occurs by single 
                     SN2 displacement |CITS: [1754383]|. Stereochemically, the 
                     reaction proceeds by <i>cis</i> addition followed by 
                     <i>trans</i> elimination |CITS: [7532997]|. Random and 
                     site-directed mutagenesis of conserved residues allowed 
                     identification of residues involved in formation of the 
                     TrmA-tRNA intermediate, enzymatic activity |CITS: 
                     [17459887]|, as well as SAM and tRNA binding |CITS: 
                     [32597953]|. Glu358 acts as a general base, abstracting a 
                     proton from C5 of the methylated reaction intermediate 
                     |CITS: [17459887][18451029]|. TrmA is also able to 
                     methylate 16S rRNA at the U788 residue <i>in vitro</i>, 
                     possibly due to the sequence and structural similarity of 
                     that rRNA region to the T&Psi; stem-loop |CITS: [8117682]|.
                     Specificity determinants within the tRNA encompass both the
                     primary and secondary structure of the T-arm |CITS: 
                     [2007136]|; a 2-5 bp stem and 7 nt loop as well as the U54 
                     methyl acceptor and C56 are required |CITS: [8794745]|. The
                     crystal structure of a complex between TrmA E358Q and a 
                     19-mer RNA fragment corresponding to the covalently bound 
                     reaction intermediate has been solved at 2.4&Aring; 
                     resolution. The U54 base is flipped out of the T loop, and 
                     other bases adopt a specific fold that differs from the 
                     mature tRNA fold |CITS: [18451029]|.
                     The enzyme is present in both a free form and strongly 
                     bound to tRNA and 16S rRNA <i>in vivo</i>, but is not 
                     associated with the ribosome |CITS: [8419334]|.
                     The <i>trmA5</i> point mutant that lacks all m<sup>5</sup>U
                     modification of tRNA has essentially no detectable 
                     phenotype, although it is outcompeted by wild type in mixed
                     populations |CITS: [1100618]|. Various <i>trmA</i> point 
                     mutants unable to bind or methylate tRNA are also 
                     outcompeted by wild type |CITS: [32597953]|. Introduction 
                     of the <i>trmA5</i> to a double <i>truB trmH</i> mutant, 
                     resulting in lack of m<sup>5</sup>U54, &psi;55, and Gm18 
                     modifications to tRNA, only slightly worsens the growth 
                     rate defect of the <i>truB trmH</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [12218021]|. An earlier report suggested that the 
                     <i>trmA</i> gene is essential for viability, implying a 
                     second, vital function in addition to m<sup>5</sup>U 
                     modification of tRNA |CITS: [1373891]|, but <i>trmA</i> 
                     deletion strains were later obtained |CITS: [16738554]|. 
                     The double deletion mutant 
                     &Delta;&Delta;<i>trmA</i><i>truB</i> reduces aminoacylation
                     and other modifications of tRNAs on a global level, but 
                     only significantly affects translation of a subset of 
                     codons and proteins; this results in an overall impairment 
                     of cellular fitness |CITS: [38889150]|. Expression of 
                     <i>trmA</i> is growth rate-dependent |CITS: [1999392]|.
                     TrmA: ""<b>t</b>ransfer <b>R</b>NA <b>m</b>ethylation"" 
                     |CITS: [4921251]|"
