     gene            4153567..4155411
                     /gene="btuB"
                     /locus_tag="b3966"
                     /label=btuB
     CDS             4153567..4155411
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="btuB"
                     /locus_tag="b3966"
                     /product="bifunctional cobalamin outer membrane
                     transporter / cobalamin outer membrane transport complex"
                     /label=btuB
                     /note="<i>btuB</i> mutants, defective in cobalamin
                     (vitamin B12) transport/binding and resistant to E colicins
                     and phage BF23, were originally isolated from a 
                     methionine-coenzyme B12 auxotroph (K-12 strain KBT001; see 
                     |CITS: [4326741][4196588][4579870][4579869][1254550][33660 
                     7][3882670][2982793]|).
                     BtuB is an outer membrane protein that mediates binding and
                     TonB-dependent active transport of cobalamin across the 
                     outer membrane (see |CITS: [342525][6768753][3294107][ 
                     2193917][7665457][10485884][10700278][11029413][11705387][1
                     1705387][12578351][16953570][16741124][22497281][23517233][
                     27806272][27693114][28429713]|). The system has high 
                     affinity for various cobalamins including |FRAME: 
                     ADENOSYLCOBALAMIN ""adenosylcobalamin""|, cobinamide, and 
                     the commercial product cyanocobalamin |CITS: [342525]| (and
                     see |CITS: [8905078]|). Cobalamin binding by BtuB is 
                     calcium dependent |CITS: [3512539][2168369]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [14499604]|).
                     Crystal structures of BtuB with and without bound 
                     cyanocobalamin have been reported |CITS: 
                     [12595710][12652322]|: the protein forms a 22-stranded 
                     &beta;-barrel with an N-terminal hatch domain (sometimes 
                     referred to as the channel cork) embedded in the barrel 
                     lumen; long extracellular loops participate in cobalamin 
                     binding and also ligate two calcium ions (see also |CITS: 
                     [15716445][17908684]|). Calcium binding serves to position 
                     and stabilize the extracellular loops which facilitates 
                     high affinity cobalamin binding |CITS: [19747487]| (see 
                     also |CITS: [17028020][19927326][27705764]|).
                     BtuB is required for the activity of many colicins (see for
                     example |CITS: [2699893][7047502][2699893]|). BtuB binds 
                     colicins E3 and E2 but does not serve as the translocation 
                     channel |CITS: [14528295][17548346]|. OmpF is recruited to 
                     the BtuB-colicin E complex to form a 'colicin translocon'  
                     |CITS: [16166265]| (see also |CITS: [18986168][21156129]|).
                     For further information on colicin import across the <i>E. 
                     coli</I> outer membrane see |CITS: [30541793]|. BtuB is the
                     terminal receptor for a number of small and large 
                     siphoviruses |CITS: [34784345]|.
                     Synthesis of BtuB is repressed when cells are grown in the 
                     presence of high concentrations of cobalamins |CITS: 
                     [363685][3027510]|. Expression is regulated at the 
                     translational level by an adenosylcobalamin responsive 
                     riboswitch located in the 5' untranslated region of 
                     <i>btuB</i> mRNA; adenosylcobalamin interacts with the 
                     <i>btuB</i> aptamer, inducing a structural switch in the 
                     downstream expression platform that prevents ribosome 
                     binding |CITS: 
                     [1847525][9190822][9852020][10852957][12323379]| (see also 
                     |CITS: [22331895]|). The <i>btuB</i> riboswitch binds 
                     adenosylcobalamin selectively |CITS: 
                     [12323379][18506875][23064232]| (further |CITS: 
                     [20830434][24243114][26370077][28377919][38922714]|). The 
                     <i>btuB</i> riboswitch also controls |FRAME: CPLX0-2441 
                     Rho|-dependent transcription termination; following 
                     adenosylcobalamin-dependent translation inhibition, Rho may
                     target the 81-210 nucleotide region of <i>btuB</i> to 
                     terminate transcription |CITS: [38742638]|.
                     Expression of <i>btuB</i> is regulated by the small RNAs 
                     |FRAME: SRAE-RNA OmrA| and, to a lesser extent, |FRAME: 
                     RYGB-RNA OmrB|; this sRNA-mediated control is independent 
                     of the riboswitch regulation and occurs via a distinct 
                     mechanism; OmrA interacts directly with <i>btuB</i> mRNA 
                     and, unlike the riboswitch, relies on |FRAME: CPLX0-1461 
                     Hfq| and the |FRAME: CPLX0-2381| to modulate translation 
                     inititation |CITS: [38742638]|.
                     <i>btuB</i> expression is transcriptionally repressed under
                     acidic conditions (pH 5.5) by the acid responsive genes 
                     <i>gadX</i> and <i>gadY</i> |CITS: [21314918]|.
                     OMP localisation and turnover has been studied using 
                     labelled, modified colicin E9 which forms a complex with 
                     BtuB but is blocked for import: OMPs cluster in islands 
                     which are stabilised by promiscuous protein-protein 
                     interactions; OMPs engage in both homologous and 
                     heterologous associations that slow their diffusion in the 
                     outer membrane; OMP biogenesis is greatest at the mid-cell 
                     and diminishes towards the poles |CITS: [26061769]|.
                     In a laboratory evolution experiment, increased expression 
                     of BtuB, is beneficial for the growth of a 
                     cobamide-dependent  strain with pseudocobalamin |CITS: 
                     [39873498]|.
                     <i>btuB</i> exhibits mosaic evolution patterns in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [40444982]|.
                     <i>bfe</i>: resistance to phage <U>BF</U>23 and the 
                     <U>E</U> group of colicins (see |CITS: 
                     [4561717]|)<BR><i>btu</i>: B<sub>12</sub> uptake"
