     gene            complement(4164004..4164954)
                     /gene="coaA"
                     /locus_tag="b3974"
                     /label=coaA
     CDS             complement(4164004..4164954)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="coaA"
                     /locus_tag="b3974"
                     /product="bifunctional pantothenate kinase / pantetheine
                     kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.1.33; 2.7.1.34"
                     /label=coaA
                     /note="Pantothenate kinase catalyzes the first step in the 
                     biosynthesis of coenzyme A, an essential cofactor that is 
                     involved in many reactions.  This reaction is also the most
                     highly regulated step in the pathway |CITS: 
                     [6796563][6370952]|.
                     Pantothenate kinase is sensitive to feedback inhibition by 
                     the CoA pool |CITS: [7929447][3029083]|.  Mutants that are 
                     refractory to feedback inhibition, but retain catalytic 
                     activity, have been isolated; these mutants have a higher 
                     intracellular CoA content and secrete 4'-phosphopantetheine
                     |CITS: [12754240]|.
                     The compounds |FRAME: CPD0-1152| and |FRAME: CPD0-1153| are
                     competitive inhibitors of pantothenate kinase |CITS: 
                     [15136582]| and can be utilized as substrates of the CoA 
                     biosynthesis pathway |CITS: [12372838]|.  The 
                     phosphopantothenimide moiety is transferred to ACP, 
                     rendering it incapable of supporting fatty acid 
                     biosynthesis.  Thus, |FRAME: CPD0-1152| and |FRAME: 
                     CPD0-1153| are pro-antibiotics |CITS: [15459190]|, 
                     explaining earlier results |CITS: [4909169]|.  Further 
                     inhibitors/substrates of pantothenate kinase have been 
                     designed and tested |CITS: [16213731][24814884]|.
                     Growth rescue of a |FRAME: EG10004 <i>dfp</i>| null mutant 
                     by addition of pantethine requires the pantetheine kinase 
                     activity of CoaA |CITS: [21551303]|.
                     Utilization of two different translation start sites of 
                     <i>coaA</i> results in proteins that differ by eight amino 
                     acids at the N terminus |CITS: [1328157][7929447]|. Crystal
                     structures of pantothenate kinase in complex with AMPPNP (a
                     nonhydrolyzable analog of ATP) or CoA have been solved, 
                     showing that the phosphate binding sites of CoA and ATP 
                     overlap, which explains their kinetic competition |CITS: 
                     [10862768]|.  The N-terminal region forms much of the dimer
                     interface |CITS: [10862768]|.  The crystal structure of the
                     ternary complex with ADP and patothenate shows induced fit 
                     binding of pantothenate; a mechanism of phosphoryl transfer
                     was proposed |CITS: [15136582]|. <i>coaA</i> is an 
                     essential gene |CITS: [12142426]|. Temperature-sensitive 
                     alleles have been isolated |CITS: 
                     [2824448][4866408][16701556]|.
                     "
