     gene            4167286..4167669
                     /gene="secE"
                     /locus_tag="b3981"
                     /label=secE
     CDS             4167286..4167669
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="secE"
                     /locus_tag="b3981"
                     /product="multifunctional Sec translocon subunit SecE /
                     SecYEG translocon / SecYEG-SecA complex / Sec 
                     Holo-Translocon"
                     /label=secE
                     /note="The SecE inner membrane protein is a component of
                     the heterotrimeric SecYEG preprotein translocase. SecY, 
                     SecE and SecG form a stable complex which supports 
                     precursor protein translocation <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [2167176][1531482][8034620]|.
                     A cold sensitive SecE mutant (<i>SecEcsE501</i>) 
                     accumulates the precursor form of periplasmic and envelope 
                     proteins (MBP, LamB and RBP) when grown at the 
                     non-permissive temperature |CITS: [3284784][2673920]|; the 
                     <i>SecEcsE501</i> allele is an alteration to the concensus 
                     ribosome binding site that results in decreased expression 
                     of <i>secE</i> |CITS: [2050112]|. SecE is an inner membrane
                     protein with three predicted transmembrane regions |CITS: 
                     [2673920]|. SecE is essential for growth; a truncated SecE 
                     (<i>secE&Delta;7-78</i>) functions effectively <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [2050112]|. <i>prl</i> alleles of 
                     <i>secE</i> which suppress a variety of signal sequence 
                     mutations have been characterized |CITS: 
                     [2673921][2050112]|. Overexpression of <i>secE</i> 
                     stabilizes over-expressed SecY <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [8253665]|. A truncated form of SecE lacking amino acid 
                     residues 7-66 (SecE-C) is functional for protein 
                     translocation <i>in vitro</i>; this C-terminal region may 
                     represent the site of interaction with SecY |CITS: 
                     [1551922]|.
                     Cross-linking studies suggest that the third transmembrane 
                     region of SecE interacts with both SecY and with a 
                     neighbouring SecE molecule |CITS: [11445571]|.
                     <i>secE</i> expression increases at low temperature |CITS: 
                     [2050112]|
                     <i>secE</i>: <U>sec</U>retory<BR><i>prlG</i>: 
                     <U>pr</U>otein <U>l</U>ocalization
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [2202721]|"
