     gene            4167671..4168216
                     /gene="nusG"
                     /locus_tag="b3982"
                     /label=nusG
     CDS             4167671..4168216
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nusG"
                     /locus_tag="b3982"
                     /product="transcription termination/antitermination factor
                     NusG"
                     /label=nusG
                     /note="The transcription termination factor NusG has many 
                     functions in the cell. It is required for inhibiting 
                     pausing during transcription, coupling transcription with 
                     translation, Rho-dependent termination, and transcription 
                     antitermination |CITS: [38387461]|.
                     NusG is involved in antitermination during lambda phage 
                     transcription, as reviewed in |CITS: [1400223]|.
                     NusG is required for some Rho-dependent termination |CITS: 
                     [1547498][7761393]|. One NusG monomer binds per Rho 
                     hexamer, increasing the efficiency of Rho-dependent 
                     termination, possibly by overcoming a kinetic barrier 
                     |CITS: [8422985][10820031][7761393]|. In addition, NusG 
                     shifts the termination point relative to the site of 
                     termination when only Rho is present |CITS: [7505669]|. 
                     NusG only associates with the elongation complex in the 
                     presence of Rho |CITS: [7525972]|. Overexpression of NusG 
                     relative to Rho leads to inhibition of Rho-dependent 
                     termination |CITS: [8801418]|.
                     NusG is also involved in antitermination. NusG is required 
                     for antitermination in transcription of ribosomal RNA, as 
                     well as readthrough of an attentuator preceding <i>rpoB</i>
                     |CITS: [10383769][14973028][1370474]|. NusG stimulates 
                     transcription generally, apparently by suppressing 
                     transcriptional pausing |CITS: [7868616]|. NusG binds 
                     weakly to the core RNA polymerase (RNAP) |CITS: [1532577]|.
                     Two domains of NusG mediate distinct interactions with RNAP
                     elongation complexes: the N-terminal domain with RNAP and 
                     the C-terminal domain with Rho, Nus factors, or RNA; 
                     interactions of both domains with the elongation complexes 
                     are required for enhancing Rho-dependent termination or 
                     supporting antitermination |CITS: [19500594]|. NusG does 
                     not compete with NusA for binding to either Rho or the 
                     polymerase, despite modulating the same process as NusA in 
                     both cases |CITS: [9571053]|. Local and non-local 
                     structural dynamics and interactions of NusG and its 
                     paralog, RfaH, with RNAP transcription elongation complexes
                     have been examined |CITS: [35670666]|.
                     NusG is required for viability |CITS: [1547498]|. This 
                     requirement is removed in <i>E. coli</i> strains that have 
                     had their complement of phage-acquired genes significantly 
                     reduced, suggesting that a major role for NusG, in 
                     collaboration with |FRAME: CPLX0-2441|, is blocking 
                     transcription of potentially toxic ""passenger"" genes 
                     |CITS: [18487194]|.
                     Most of NusG is located complexed to the chromosome through
                     binding to RNA polymerase |CITS: [38387461]|. Only a small 
                     portion is bound to the 30S ribosomal subunit, and 
                     approximately 10% of this protein is free in the cell 
                     |CITS: [38387461]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [34570660][36154805][39374889]|"
