     gene            4168375..4168803
                     /gene="rplK"
                     /locus_tag="b3983"
                     /label=rplK
     CDS             4168375..4168803
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rplK"
                     /locus_tag="b3983"
                     /product="trifunctional 50S ribosomal subunit protein L11
                     / 50S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rplK
                     /note="The L11 protein is a component of the 50S subunit
                     of the ribosome.
                     L11 is posttranslationally modified by methylation |CITS: 
                     [4610403][1089427]|.  The N-terminal alanine residue is 
                     methylated to N-trimethylalanine |CITS: [332162][6778808]|,
                     and two lysine residues are methylated to N&epsilon;, 
                     N&epsilon;, N&epsilon;-trimethyllysine |CITS: 
                     [7004866][6447699][6778808][10094780]|.
                     Methylation of L11 does not appear to be essential for its 
                     function |CITS: [7715456]|.  L11 is a target for the 
                     |FRAME: EG10443-MONOMER| |CITS: [30206139]|.
                     L11 participates in joining the 30S initiation complex to 
                     the 50S subunit |CITS: [765132][2666133]|.  Ribosome core 
                     particles lacking L11 do not display EF-G-dependent GTPase 
                     activity |CITS: [165973]|.  The N-terminal domain of L11 
                     participates in the formation of the arc-like connection 
                     between EF-G and L7/L12 during tRNA translocation |CITS: 
                     [16337596]|.  The universally conserved Pro22 residue 
                     undergoes a <i>cis-trans</i> isomerization.  Molecular 
                     dynamics simulations showed that the <i>cis</i> 
                     configuration of Pro22 enables interaction of the 
                     N-terminal domain of L11 with the C-terminal domain of L12 
                     |CITS: [22407015]|.  Site-directed mutagenesis of conserved
                     residues in L11 and L12 elucidated their role in the 
                     interactions between L11, L12, and ribosome-associated 
                     GTPases |CITS: [22965132]|.
                     L11 directly interacts with 23S rRNA |CITS: [321250]|.
                     Its binding site has been identified |CITS: [6271782]| and 
                     is adjacent to the L8 complex binding site in 23S rRNA 
                     |CITS: [6373761][1692883]|.  Binding of the L8 complex and 
                     L11 appears to be cooperative |CITS: [8263910][7630717]|. 
                     L11 recognizes and stabilizes the three-dimensional 
                     structure of its binding site in 23S rRNA |CITS: 
                     [1942050][10734197][15492007][16716074]|.  A crystal 
                     structure of L11 bound to a 58 nucleotide fragment of 23S 
                     rRNA has been determined; the C-terminal domain of L11 
                     binds RNA tightly, while the N-terminal domain makes only 
                     limited contacts with RNA |CITS: [10338213]|.
                     The L11 binding region of 23S rRNA appears to be important 
                     for translation termination |CITS: [12051910]|.  |FRAME: 
                     EG10335-MONOMER RRF| initially contacts L11 |CITS: 
                     [22388519]|.  The N-terminal domain of L11 is critical for 
                     modulation of release factor binding |CITS: 
                     [6355097][6383864][6373771][3511060][12818198]|.  Deletion 
                     of the amino terminus reduces the termination efficiency of
                     RF1, but not RF2, thus increasing nonsense suppression at 
                     UAG codons, while changes in L11 reduce dissociation of RF2
                     from ribosome, causing a decrease in nonsense suppression 
                     at UGA codons |CITS: [16371360]|.  Strains lacking L11 
                     exhibit UAG stop codon suppression, defective growth, and 
                     high-temperature lethality; strains lacking only the 
                     N-terminal domain of L11 are only defective in 
                     UAG-dependent termination |CITS: [12818198]|.  However, 
                     later experiments show that L11 affects RF1 and RF2 
                     activity similarly |CITS: [16556224]|.
                     L11 is required for binding of the thiazole antibiotic 
                     thiostrepton to the ribosome |CITS: [1089652]|.
                     L11 plays a role in regulating the stringent response; an 
                     <i>rplK</i> (<i>relC</i>) mutant has a relaxed phenotype 
                     |CITS: [4610577][772409]|.  Both a proline-rich helix in 
                     the N-terminal domain of L11 and the linker region between 
                     the N- and C-terminal domains are required for regulating 
                     the activity of (p)ppGpp synthetase I (|FRAME: RELA-MONOMER
                     RelA|) |CITS: [11673421][17095013]|, although no direct 
                     interaction between the two proteins has been detected 
                     |CITS: [11673421]|.  ppGpp synthesis by RelA requires both 
                     uncharged tRNA at the A site of the ribosome and the 
                     presence of L11 |CITS: [12419222]|.  The L11 N-terminal 
                     domain alone can activate RelA in the presence of ribosomes
                     and uncharged tRNA |CITS: [17095013]|.
                     RNA polymerase stalls at a site near the <i>rplK</i> 
                     promoter |CITS: [18333883]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [22649682]|"
