     gene            4171173..4175201
                     /gene="rpoB"
                     /locus_tag="b3987"
                     /label=rpoB
     CDS             4171173..4175201
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpoB"
                     /locus_tag="b3987"
                     /product="multifunctional RNA polymerase subunit beta /
                     RNA polymerase, core enzyme / RNA polymerase / RNA 
                     polymerase sigma 24 / RNA polymerase sigma 19 / RNA 
                     polymerase sigma 28 / RNA polymerase sigma S / RNA 
                     polymerase sigma 32 / RNA polymerase sigma 54 / RNA 
                     polymerase sigma 70"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.6"
                     /label=rpoB
                     /note="Along with its &beta;' partner, the &beta; subunit
                     is integrally involved in the enzymatic function of RNA 
                     polymerase.
                     Both the &beta; and &beta;' subunits interact with DNA and 
                     may contribute to the polymerase active site |CITS: 
                     [7040939][387258][8248780][1693014][2253882]|. Two 
                     conserved areas near the carboxy-terminus of RpoB are 
                     required for RNA polymerase assembly |CITS: [9245594]|. 
                     RpoB has a ""flexible flap"" element that contacts sigma 
                     factors and is either involved in or required for 
                     transcription by various RNA polymerase holoenzyme 
                     complexes |CITS: [11823642][12424241]|. These interactions 
                     are mediated via a hydrophic patch on the flap element 
                     |CITS: [15465046]|.
                     A mutation in the <i>rpoB</i> gene compensates for 
                     mutations in the genes encoding the major antibiotic efflux
                     pumps AcrAB and AcrEF through a pleiotropic effect |CITS: 
                     [33972351]|. The effect of this <i>rpoB</i> mutant depends 
                     on the presence of the protein DksA |CITS: [33972351]|.
                     Mutations in <i>rpoB</i> and <i>rpoC</i> are often found in
                     adaptive laboratory evolution experiments using wild-type 
                     MG1655 and derivative strains grown in media with excess 
                     substrate and maintained in exponential growth compared to 
                     growth in minimal media |CITS: 
                     [17086184][19849850][25304508][27135538]|. Follow up 
                     experiments using mutations in <i>rpoB</i> and ribosomal 
                     operons (<i>rrn</i>CEH and <i>rrn</i>BCH) increased growth 
                     rate and resource allocation to ribosome synthesis but 
                     decreased 'hedging functions', i.e. the capacity to adapt 
                     to changing carbon source |CITS: [35243241]|. Other 
                     adaptive laboratory evolution experiments for cells exposed
                     to excess iron in combination with antibiotic exposure 
                     revealed a variety of secondary effects from mutations in 
                     <i>rpoB</i> |CITS: [36978471]|.
                     Cellular exposure to quaternary ammonium compounds (QACs), 
                     which are used as antimicrobial agents, causes mutation in 
                     the <i>rpoB</i> gene |CITS: [35398118]|.
                     Mutations within the rifampicin binding site of <i>rpoB</i>
                     identified an alpha helix spanning region that resulted in 
                     either increased or decreased susceptibility to rifampicin 
                     and identified additional single-residue mutations that 
                     produced faster transcription rates |CITS: [37648864]|."
