     gene            4175278..4179501
                     /gene="rpoC"
                     /locus_tag="b3988"
                     /label=rpoC
     CDS             4175278..4179501
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpoC"
                     /locus_tag="b3988"
                     /product="multifunctional RNA polymerase subunit beta' /
                     RNA polymerase, core enzyme / RNA polymerase / RNA 
                     polymerase sigma 24 / RNA polymerase sigma 19 / RNA 
                     polymerase sigma 28 / RNA polymerase sigma S / RNA 
                     polymerase sigma 32 / RNA polymerase sigma 54 / RNA 
                     polymerase sigma 70"
                     /EC_number="2.7.7.6"
                     /label=rpoC
                     /note="Along with its &beta; partner, the &beta;' subunit
                     of RNA polymerase is integrally involved in the enzymatic 
                     function of RNA polymerase, especially at the promoter 
                     melting stage.
                     Both the &beta; and &beta;' subunits interact with DNA and 
                     may contribute to the polymerase active site |CITS: 
                     [7040939][387258][8248780][10198039]|.
                     Though &beta;' is not required for interaction with the 
                     initiating nucleotide, it binds |FRAME: RPOD-MONOMER| and 
                     makes contact at the same points on the nontemplate strand 
                     as |FRAME: RPOD-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [11278820][9077436][9813048][10652363]|.  The minimal set 
                     of RNA polymerase components needed for promoter melting 
                     consists of the RpoC amino-terminus and a portion of a 
                     sigma factor |CITS: [14988563]|.  The ""jaw"" domain of 
                     RpoC stabilizes the open promoter complex |CITS: 
                     [16123036]|.
                     Based on studies of single-molecule tracking, it was 
                     determined that RpoC (as part of RNAP) spends most of its 
                     search time bound to DNA |CITS: [33621478]|. On average, at
                     any time 45% of RpoC molecules are in long-lived DNA-bound 
                     complexes at target sites, 48% are transiently, 
                     nonspecifically DNA-bound, and only 7% are in free 
                     diffusion between DNA strands;  a diffusion coefficient of 
                     0.36 &mu;m<sup>2</sup>s<sup>-1</sup>  was calculated |CITS:
                     [33621478]|.
                     RpoC is involved in chelating a magnesium ion that is 
                     required for RNA polymerase catalysis |CITS: [8658176]|. 
                     RpoC also coordinates a zinc ion that is required for it to
                     maintain its functional conformation |CITS: [10500100]|.
                     The carboxy-terminus of RpoC interacts with |FRAME: 
                     EG11013-MONOMER|, which must act on DNA to prevent 
                     excessive negative supercoiling in the wake of RNA 
                     polymerase |CITS: [12788950]|.
                     Mutations in <i>rpoB</i> and <i>rpoC</i> are often found in
                     adaptive laboratory evolution experiments using wild-type 
                     MG1655 and derivative strains grown in media with excess 
                     substrate and maintained in exponential growth compared to 
                     growth in minimal media |CITS: 
                     [17086184][19849850][25304508][27135538]|.
                     Cellular exposure to quaternary ammonium compounds (QACs), 
                     which are used as antimicrobial agents, causes mutation in 
                     the <i>rpoC</i> gene |CITS: [35398118]|."
