     CDS             complement(4181793..4182563)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="thiG"
                     /locus_tag="b3991"
                     /product="bifunctional 1-deoxy-D-xylulose
                     5-phosphate:thiol sulfurtransferase / thiazole synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.8.1.10"
                     /label=thiG
                     /note="1-deoxy-D-xylulose 5-phosphate:thiol
                     sulfurtransferase (ThiG) participates in the synthesis of 
                     the thiazole moiety of thiamine |CITS: [8432721]|.  ThiG is
                     responsible for a complex cyclization reaction; formation 
                     of |FRAME: CPD-13575| requires 1-deoxyxylulose 5-phosphate,
                     a sulfur atom from cysteine which is transferred via ThiS, 
                     and |FRAME: CPD-12279| (dehydroglycine) produced by the 
                     tyrosine lyase ThiH |CITS: [14757766][17403671]|.
                     ThiG purifies in a large multimeric complex with ThiH 
                     |CITS: [12650933]|.  Because |FRAME: CPD-12279| is unstable
                     and undergoes hydrolysis to glyoxylate and ammonium, 
                     complex formation between ThiG and ThiH may ensure that it 
                     is passed directly from ThiH to ThiG |CITS: [17969213]|.
                     Growth of a <i>thiG</i> insertion mutant requires the 
                     presence of thiazole or thiamine |CITS: [8432721]|.
                     A computational screen for potential suppressors of a 
                     &Delta;|FRAME: G0-9461 <i>pdxB</i>| mutation identified 
                     ThiG as an enzyme with similarity to <i>Bacillus 
                     subtilis</i> PdxS, the glutamine amidotransferase component
                     of the pyridoxal 5'-phosphate synthase complex. 
                     Overexpression of <i>thiG</i> in a &Delta;<i>pdxB</i> 
                     mutant can indeed suppress the growth defect on minimal 
                     medium, and mutations in predicted active site residues of 
                     ThiG eliminate suppression |CITS: [26821166]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10382260][26443755]|"
