     gene            complement(4182565..4182765)
                     /gene="thiS"
                     /locus_tag="b4407"
                     /label=thiS
     CDS             complement(4182565..4182765)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="thiS"
                     /locus_tag="b4407"
                     /product="sulfur carrier protein ThiS"
                     /label=thiS
                     /note="ThiS is a sulfur carrier protein that provides the
                     sulfur source for the thiazole moiety in thiamine 
                     biosynthesis.
                     In a reaction catalyzed by |FRAME: CPLX0-8558 ThiF|, the 
                     C-terminal cysteine residue of ThiS is adenylated, yielding
                     ThiS-COAMP |CITS: [9632726][15896804]|.  |FRAME: 
                     Sulfurylated-ThiI| may then transfer its sulfur to 
                     ThiS-COAMP, releasing AMP and forming a covalent disulfide 
                     bond, |FRAME: ThiS-ThiI-disulfides| intermediate.  ThiS 
                     then appears to be transferred to ThiF, again forming a 
                     covalent disulfide bond, |FRAME: ThiS-ThiF-disulfides| 
                     intermediate |CITS: [11438688]|.  However, it has not been 
                     experimentally determined when and how the disulfide bond 
                     is reduced to enable the final sulfur transfer to form 
                     thiazole.
                     A solution structure of ThiS shows a ubiquitin-like fold 
                     |CITS: [11135670]|.  The crystal structure of the ThiS-ThiF
                     protein complex shows a dimer of heterodimers, where the 
                     dimer interface is provided by ThiF.  The ThiS C-terminus 
                     is located near the active site |CITS: [16388576]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10382260][26443755]|
                     Note: |CITS: [35344569]| indicates that |CITS: [23638106]| 
                     is retracted."
