     gene            complement(4184132..4186027)
                     /gene="thiC"
                     /locus_tag="b3994"
                     /label=thiC
     CDS             complement(4184132..4186027)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="thiC"
                     /locus_tag="b3994"
                     /product="phosphomethylpyrimidine synthase"
                     /EC_number="4.1.99.17"
                     /label=thiC
                     /note="HMP-P synthase (ThiC) catalyzes the conversion of 
                     5-aminoimidazole (AIR) into 
                     4-amino-hydroxymethyl-2-methylpyrimidine phosphate (HMP-P) 
                     |CITS: [15326535]|. ThiC was demonstrated to be a novel 
                     member of the radical S-adenosylmethionine (SAM) 
                     superfamily, also known as AdoMet superfamily |CITS: 
                     [18953358]|. The role of the radical SAM proteins in the 
                     ThiC-catalyzed complex rearrangement reaction has been 
                     investigated |CITS: [21771780][22579873][19113839]|.
                     The labelling pattern of the conversion of AIR to HMP-P has
                     been studied in detail, and potential reaction mechanisms 
                     were proposed. ThiC activity was enhanced by SAM and 
                     nicotinamide cofactors. SAM is neither used as a methyl 
                     donor, nor is it cleaved to the adenosyl radical; its role 
                     in the reaction is thus unclear. The reaction is dependent 
                     on the presence of an additional protein, which has not 
                     been identified yet |CITS: [15326535]|.
                     ThiC has been extensively studied in <i>Salmonella 
                     enterica</i>. ThiC contains a CXXCXXXXC motif, similar but 
                     not exactly the same as the motif found in all AdoMet 
                     enzymes. Mutational studies showed that replacement of any 
                     cysteine residues in the motif abolished thiamine 
                     biosynthesis |CITS: [16849799]|. In AdoMet enzymes this 
                     motif nucleates a [4Fe-4S] cluster. Experimental evidence 
                     showed the motif in ThiC to bind the [4Fe-4S] cluster 
                     |CITS: [18686975]|.
                     The crystal structure of ThiC from <i>Caulobacter 
                     crescentus</i> was determined and revealed to be a 
                     homodimer |CITS: [18953358]|.
                     The <i>thiC</i> riboswitch was shown to be kinetically 
                     regulated |CITS: [26932506][31081713]|. Negative regulation
                     of translation by the <i>thiC</i> riboswitch may trigger 
                     Rho-dependent termination of transcription |CITS: 
                     [28071751][28520932]|. Multiple consecutive elongation 
                     complex pause sites are present in the riboswitch region; 
                     different stem-loop structures occur at various pause sites
                     representing a stepwise transcription process |CITS: 
                     [28071751]|. Three major RNAP transcriptional pause sites 
                     are affected by the transcriptional regulator, NusA, which 
                     stimulates and stabilizes hairpin structures for pausing 
                     transcription; pause half-life measurements, particularly 
                     with a photolabile detection system, have been determined, 
                     and the 5' most proximal pause site found to be primarily 
                     affected by the RNA hairpin structure |CITS: 
                     [31081713][35552496]|. Using a variety of probing assays of
                     the nascent <i>thiC</i> riboswitch RNAs with and without 
                     ThiC identified the nucleotides where ThiC binds in the 
                     riboswitch aptamer region. The ThiC binding causes 
                     rearrangement of the nascent RNA structure such that the P1
                     stem forms and the AUG is sequestered, thus translation 
                     initiation is prevented in response to sensing ThiC. ThiC 
                     also exposes a site for Rho binding and additional 
                     repression of translation |CITS: [38864595]|."
