     gene            complement(4186260..4186736)
                     /gene="rsd"
                     /locus_tag="b3995"
                     /label=rsd
     CDS             complement(4186260..4186736)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rsd"
                     /locus_tag="b3995"
                     /product="regulator of sigma D"
                     /label=rsd
                     /note="Rsd exerts its effect via its ability to
                     specifically interact with a number of proteins, including 
                     |FRAME: RPOD-MONOMER &sigma;<sup>70</sup>| |CITS: 
                     [9560209]|, |FRAME: SPOT-MONOMER SpoT| |CITS: [29915072]| 
                     and dephosphorylated |FRAME: PTSH-MONOMER HPr| |CITS: 
                     [24324139]|.
                     Rsd was first identified as an anti-&sigma; factor.  It 
                     binds specifically to the major &sigma; factor |FRAME: 
                     RPOD-MONOMER &sigma;<sup>70</sup>| |CITS: [9560209]| with a
                     stoichiometry of 1:1 |CITS: [14687566][19763331]|.  Rsd 
                     sequesters &sigma;<sup>70</sup>, making RNAP core enzyme 
                     accessible for &sigma;<sup>S</sup> and possibly other 
                     &sigma; factors in stationary phase |CITS: 
                     [9560209][17351046]|.  The dephosphorylated form of |FRAME:
                     PTSH-MONOMER HPr| is able to form a tight 1:1 complex with 
                     Rsd and thereby inhibit its interaction with 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup>, linking Rsd anti-&sigma; factor 
                     activity to nutrient availability |CITS: [24324139]|.
                     Rsd interacts directly with the TGS domain of |FRAME: 
                     SPOT-MONOMER SpoT| and stimulates its (p)ppGpp hydrolase 
                     activity; it does not interact with RelA.  The stimulatory 
                     effect of Rsd on SpoT (p)ppGpp hydrolase activity is 
                     negated by the presence of dephosphorylated.  Rsd thus 
                     functions as a link between PTS-dependent carbon source 
                     utilization and the stringent response HPr |CITS: 
                     [29915072]|.
                     Rsd can interact simultaneously with regions 4 |CITS: [956 
                     0209][11292818][11591686][14687566][15561138][18359804]| 
                     and 2 |CITS: [18826409]| of &sigma;<sup>70</sup>.  Region 4
                     is involved in recognition of the -35 promoter element, 
                     while region 2 is involved in the recognition of the -10 
                     promoter element.  Both regions also contain critical 
                     determinants for the interaction with core RNA polymerase 
                     (RNAP) |CITS: [11931761]|.  The crystal structure of Rsd 
                     bound to region 4 of &sigma;<sup>70</sup> shows that Rsd 
                     binding directly interferes with both the core RNAP-binding
                     and promoter-binding functions of region 4 |CITS: 
                     [17681541]|.  A crystal structure of Rsd bound to HPr shows
                     that the binding interfaces on Rsd for &sigma;<sup>70</sup>
                     and HPr partially overlap |CITS: [26457424]|.
                     Rsd was initially isolated in a complex with 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> from stationary-phase cell extracts 
                     |CITS: [9560209]|.  Rsd increases expression from a 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent promoter and reduces 
                     expression from certain &sigma;<sup>70</sup>-dependent 
                     promoters |CITS: [10368152][17351046][24324139]|. 
                     Increasing amounts of Rsd can physically displace 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup> from holo-RNAP <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [14962387]|.
                     Rsd protein levels increase as cell growth begins to 
                     decrease |CITS: [9560209][19763331]|.  Pull-down 
                     experiments have demonstrated that Rsd levels are only 
                     twofold higher in stationary phase, with approximately 
                     6,000 molecules per cell, compared to levels in 
                     exponentially growing cells.  Therefore, the concentration 
                     of Rsd should be high enough in exponentially growing cells
                     to allow binding to &sigma;<sup>70</sup>.  Yet, the complex
                     between Rsd and &sigma;<sup>70</sup> can only be detected 
                     in stationary-phase extracts |CITS: [19763331]|. Expression
                     of <i>rsd</i> is under the control of two promoters, P1 and
                     P2, where P1 is &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent and P2 is 
                     &sigma;<sup>70</sup>-dependent |CITS: 
                     [10368152][19763331]|.  The P2 promoter is stronger than P1
                     and active in all phases of growth |CITS: [19763331]|. 
                     Transcriptional regulators of <i>rsd</i> expression have 
                     been investigated |CITS: [30225374]|.  6S RNA appears to 
                     regulate Rsd post-transcriptionally |CITS: [29686109]|.
                     In a high-throughput approach to identify dsRNA fragments 
                     that resulted from cleavage by RNase III, <i>rsd</i> mRNA 
                     was found to be regulated by RNase III |CITS: [32518184]|. 
                     Global gene expression at different growth stages of 
                     wild-type cells compared to &Delta;<i>rsd</i> and 
                     &Delta;<i>rpoS</i> mutants showed that ~75% of genes under 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup> control were also affected by loss of 
                     Rsd |CITS: [29686109]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>rsd</i> mutation suppresses the growth defect 
                     of a &Delta;<i>relA</i> mutant under amino acid starvation 
                     conditions, indicating that Rsd stimulates SpoT (p)ppGpp 
                     hydrolase activity <i>in vivo</i>.  A &Delta;<i>rsd</i> 
                     mutant also shows a longer diauxic lag phase and less 
                     efficient growth on casamino acids post-lag than wild-type 
                     |CITS: [29915072]|.
                     Rsd: ""<b>r</b>egulator of <b>s</b>igma <b>D</b>"" |CITS: 
                     [9560209]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [10320479][18375176][20491934][31867037]|<br>
                     Comment: |CITS: [29970419]|"
