     gene            4188718..4189389
                     /gene="nfi"
                     /locus_tag="b3998"
                     /label=nfi
     CDS             4188718..4189389
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nfi"
                     /locus_tag="b3998"
                     /product="bifunctional endonuclease V / deoxyribonuclease
                     V"
                     /label=nfi
                     /note="<i>nfi</i> encodes Endonuclease V, a DNA repair
                     enzyme which catalyses the initiating reaction in an 
                     alternate excision repair (AER) pathway that targets a 
                     variety of DNA lesions including the purine deamination 
                     products, deoxyinosine and deoxyxanthosine.
                     Early studies characterised a Mg<sup>2+</sup> dependent 
                     endonuclease which nicked duplex DNA with the production of
                     3'-hydroxy and 5'-phosphate termini; the enzyme, named 
                     Endonuclease V, had a preference for various damaged 
                     substrates including duplex phage (PBS-2) DNA which 
                     contained uracil instead of thymine |CITS: 
                     [14159][10793655][6277916]|.
                     Purified endonuclease V (Endo V), recognizes deoxyinosine, 
                     urea, and AP sites in DNA; Endo V nicks duplex DNA at the 
                     second phosphodiester bond 3' to the damage site, leaving 
                     behind the intact lesion |CITS: [8206931]|. The purified 
                     enzyme has high affinity for both the deoxyinosine 
                     containing substrate and the nicked product indicating that
                     the enzyme remains bound to DNA after the cleavage reaction
                     |CITS: [7499377]|. Mn<sup>2+</sup> and Co<sup>2+</sup> can 
                     partially replace Mg<sup>2+</sup> and support Endonuclease 
                     V activity |CITS: [8206931]|. The endonucleolytic activity 
                     of purified endonuclease V on deoxyuridine-containing DNA 
                     is much lower compared with that on deoxyinosine-containing
                     DNA |CITS: [9388217]|. Purified EndoV is active on double 
                     stranded DNA containing deoxyxanthosine |CITS: [10725661]| 
                     and on double and single-stranded DNA containing 
                     deoxyoxanosine |CITS: [15289580]|.
                     The purified enzyme can cleave heteroduplex DNA containing 
                     hairpin or loop structures and can cleave the 
                     5'-single-stranded tail of 5'-flap and pseudo-Y DNA |CITS: 
                     [8940043]|. Endo V is also active on duplex DNA containing 
                     mismatched base pairs; the enzyme has a much weaker 
                     interaction with this substrate and incised product, and 
                     may dissociate from mismatched DNA after cleavage |CITS: 
                     [7989304]|; cleavage activity on DNA containing mismatches 
                     or deoxyuridine is higher in the presence of 
                     Mn<sup>2+</sup> compared to Mg<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [9388217]|.
                     The mechanism for processing deoxyinosine removal post 
                     EndoV cleavage is not fully understood. The repair pathway 
                     initiated by EndoV produces a repair patch of 5 or more 
                     nucleotides |CITS: [17981100]|. A minimal EndoV-mediated 
                     repair reaction consisting of endonuclease V, DNA 
                     polymerase I, DNA ligase, and four deoxynucleoside 
                     triphosphates has been reconstituted <i>in vitro</i>; 
                     polymerase I proof-reading 3-5' exonuclease activity is 
                     implicated in processing of the 3' penultimate deoxyinosine
                     |CITS: [20696623][24012058][29522920]|.
                     Endo V is active on various inosine-containing RNA 
                     substrates <i>in vitro</i>; the turnover rate for incision 
                     of RNA substrates is significantly lower than that for 
                     inosine-containing DNA substrates |CITS: [23912683]|. 
                     <I>nfi</I> insertion mutants (<i>nfi-1::cat</i>) show an 
                     increased frequency of nitrous-acid induced mutations plus 
                     (paradoxically) an increased rate of survival 
                     |CITS:[9422591]| (see further |CITS: [10606815]|). The 
                     elevated mutation rate of an <i>nfi</i> insertion mutant, 
                     exposed to nitrous acid, nitrate, or nitrite is caused by a
                     defect in the removal of hypoxanthine and xanthine from DNA
                     |CITS: [11104906]|. Inactivation of Endo V restores the 
                     viability of <i>rdgB recA</i> double mutants 
                     |CITS:[12791149]|.
                     <i>nfi</i>: e<U>n</U>donuclease <U>fi</U>ve |CITS: [14159]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [26404388][25824682][23263163]|<BR>Related 
                     reviews: |CITS: [12361800][24754999]|
                     "
