     gene            4191854..4193251
                     /gene="zraS"
                     /locus_tag="b4003"
                     /label=zraS
     CDS             4191854..4193251
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="zraS"
                     /locus_tag="b4003"
                     /product="bifunctional sensor histidine kinase ZraS / 
                     protein-histidine auto-kinase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.13.3"
                     /label=zraS
                     /note="ZraS is the histidine kinase (HK) of the atypical
                     Zra two-component signaling pathway - an envelope stress 
                     response pathway that contributes to antibiotic resistance 
                     |CITS: [30389436][26438879]|. ZraS undergoes 
                     autophosphorylation at a conserved histidine residue and 
                     then serves as a phosphodonor, transferring the phosphoryl 
                     group to an aspartate residue of the response regulator 
                     ZraR; the exact mechanism of signal sensing by ZraS remains
                     unclear.
                     ZraS is an inner membrane protein with two predicted 
                     transmembrane segments |CITS: [11243806]|. 
                     Autophosphorylation of the purifed cytoplasmic domain of 
                     ZraS and subsequent transfer of the phosphoryl group to its
                     cognate response regulator - the transcription factor ZraR,
                     has been demonstrated <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [15522865]|. 
                     ZraS is a trans-acting histidine kinase; the purified 
                     cytoplasmic domain of ZraS forms a functional homodimer 
                     |CITS: [40062583]|.
                     <i>zraS</i>, <i>zraR</i> and |FRAME: EG11918 
                     ""<i>zraP</i>"" | constitute a divergent operon; the 
                     expression of <i>zraSR</i> and <i>zraP</i> is induced by 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup> through the ZraSR phosphorelay; ZraR, the 
                     regulator protein, binds the <i>zraP&ndash;zraSR</i> 
                     intergenic region; ZraP (most probably in the zinc-bound 
                     form) is a repressor of <i>zraPSR</i> expression |CITS: 
                     [11243806][26438879]|. Expression of <i>zraS</i> is induced
                     in the presence of the antimicobials, benzethonium 
                     chloride, mecillinam, polymyxin B, tetracycline, and 
                     lomefloxacin |CITS: [30389436]|. The purified periplasmic 
                     domain of ZraS binds Zn<sup>2+</sup> and zinc binding to 
                     this domain induces conformational change |CITS: 
                     [33309686]|.
                     The binding of the transcription factor |FRAME: CPLX0-226 
                     CRP| to a predicted CRP DNA binding site around the start 
                     of the <i>zraS</i> gene has been demonstrated; however an 
                     effect of this binding on gene expression has not been 
                     analyzed |CITS: [37114659]|.
                     The Zra proteins are not involved in metal resistance; 
                     deletion of <i>zraS</i> does not significantly affect the 
                     minimum inhibitory concentrations (MICs) of zinc and copper
                     |CITS: [26438879]|.
                     Phylogenetic analysis separates the HK genes of <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 into 5 branches; ZraS is classified as a Type
                     I HK |CITS: [11320123]|
                     <i>Note</i>: bacterial histidine kinases function as 
                     homodimers; both <i>cis</i>- and 
                     <i>trans</i>-autophosphorylation mechanisms have been 
                     documented (see |CITS: [19575571][25982528]|). In the 
                     absence of experimental evidence ZraS is represented as a 
                     monomer in the EcoCyc database."
