     CDS             4205406..4207007
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aceB"
                     /locus_tag="b4014"
                     /product="malate synthase A"
                     /EC_number="2.3.3.9"
                     /label=aceB
                     /note="Malate synthase catalyzes the Claisen condensation
                     of glyoxylate and acetyl-CoA, initially forming a malyl-CoA
                     intermediate that is hydrolyzed to the products malate and 
                     CoA.
                     There are two isozymes of malate synthase in <i>E. coli</i>
                     |CITS: [14336062][7925370]|. Malate synthase A is a key 
                     enzyme in the |FRAME: GLYOXYLATE-BYPASS|. It metabolizes 
                     glyoxylate formed in the dissimilation of acetate. The 
                     isozyme |FRAME: MALSYNG-MONOMER| is responsible for almost 
                     all of the malate synthase activity in cells metabolizing 
                     glyoxylate formed during growth on glycolate |CITS: 
                     [7925370]|.
                     Malate synthase A catalyzes the irreversible condensation 
                     of |FRAME: ACETYL-COA| with |FRAME: GLYOX| to produce 
                     |FRAME: MAL| and |FRAME: CO-A|. The formation of 
                     (<i>S</i>)-malate is a key reaction in the |FRAME: 
                     GLYOXYLATE-BYPASS|. This cycle is similar to the TCA cycle 
                     (see |FRAME: TCA|) but it bypasses the TCA cycle reactions 
                     that lead to a loss of |FRAME: CARBON-DIOXIDE|, thus 
                     providing TCA cycle intermediates for cell carbon 
                     biosynthesis (see |FRAME: TCA-GLYOX-BYPASS|). The 
                     glyoxylate cycle has been extensively studied in connection
                     with growth on acetate which is used in the synthesis of 
                     these biosynthetic precursors.
                     <i>E. coli</i> malate synthase A has been structurally 
                     characterized |CITS: [18714089]|. Its encoding gene 
                     <i>aceB</i> is located in the <i>ace</i>BAK operon which is
                     transcriptionally regulated by <i>iclR</i> and <i>fadR</i>.
                     The operon is inducible by growth on acetate and is 
                     repressed on most other carbon sources |CITS: [4275 
                     310][7009561][7033207][3297049][2826399][17426033][17238290
                     ][21481254]|. Crystal structures of recombinant malate 
                     synthase A have been solved at high resolution, the apo 
                     form at 1.04 &Aring;, and in complex with acetyl-CoA and 
                     competitive inhibitor pyruvate (or oxalate) at 1.40 &Aring;
                     resolution. The crystals contained one monomer per 
                     asymmetric unit for the apo form and two for its complex 
                     with acetyl-CoA and pyruvate. Residues Glu250 and Asp278 
                     and a highly conserved Cys438 were identified at the active
                     site. When compared to the G isoform, malate synthase A is 
                     a smaller molecule that lacks the &alpha;/&beta; domain 
                     found in isoform G. However when substrate is bound, the 
                     two active sites are nearly identical and inhibitors bind 
                     with similar affinities |CITS: [18714089]|.
                     A series of vectors inducibly expressing paired-terminus 
                     antisense RNAs was constructed to silence central carbon 
                     metabolism in host <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655. A vector 
                     that silenced <i>aceB</i> mRNA at 98% efficacy resulted in 
                     a defect in carbon catabolite repression |CITS: 
                     [24212579]|.
                     In order to understand the role of natural selection in 
                     bacterial regulation, the <i>aceB</i> gene, among other 
                     genes, has undergone an analysis that compared the level of
                     expression, plasticity, and noise of random versus 
                     segregating variants of many environmental isolates of 
                     <i>E. coli</i>, including <i>E. coli</i> MG1655 |CITS: 
                     [35726087]|.
                     The amount of AceB was increased in a proteomics study in a
                     &Delta;|FRAME: CPLX0-245 Ahp| mutant |CITS: [37725887]|. 
                     Review: |CITS: [2512996]|"
