     gene            4208524..4210260
                     /gene="aceK"
                     /locus_tag="b4016"
                     /label=aceK
     CDS             4208524..4210260
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="aceK"
                     /locus_tag="b4016"
                     /product="trifunctional isocitrate dehydrogenase
                     kinase/phosphatase / isocitrate dehydrogenase kinase / 
                     isocitrate dehydrogenase phosphatase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.11.5"
                     /label=aceK
                     /note="Isocitrate dehydrogenase kinase/phosphatase (AceK) 
                     controls a branch point between two pathways of central 
                     metabolism, the |FRAME: TCA| and the |FRAME: 
                     GLYOXYLATE-BYPASS|, shown in the |FRAME: TCA-GLYOX-BYPASS|.
                      It controls the flux between the two cycles by controlling
                     the activity of |FRAME: ISOCITHASE-CPLX| (IDH), an enzyme 
                     of the TCA cycle |CITS: [6378912][6389540][2861202]|.  By 
                     phosphorylating and thus inactivating IDH |CITS: 
                     [6385963]|, its substrate isocitrate is diverted to the 
                     glyoxylate cycle enzyme, isocitrate lyase.  By 
                     dephosphorylating and thus activating IDH, this enzyme's 
                     stronger affinity for isocitrate feeds it into the TCA 
                     cycle.
                     Models that attempt to explain the robustness of the IDH 
                     regulatory system have been published |CITS: [19266029]| 
                     and have provided potential explanations |CITS: 
                     [23192354]|.
                     Phosphorylation and dephosphorylation of IDH were found to 
                     be carried out by the same enzyme, AceK |CITS: [6292732]|. 
                     A conformational switch that is induced by AMP appears to 
                     determine whether AceK acts as a kinase or a phosphatase 
                     |CITS: [20505668]|.  AceK also exhibits a high intrinsic 
                     ATPase activity |CITS: [2824478][8620880][8702587]|.
                     AceK acts on a serine residue at the active site of IDH 
                     |CITS: [3112144]|; the enzyme recognizes only the intact 
                     IDH |CITS: [2557094]|.  Regulators of the 
                     kinase/phosphatase activities act either via binding to the
                     active site of the protein substrate IDH (such as NADPH and
                     isocitrate) or via binding to the kinase/phosphatase itself
                     (such as AMP, 3-phosphoglycerate and pyruvate) |CITS: 
                     [10625615]|.  A computational model of the kinase reaction 
                     suggested a dissociative reaction mechanism |CITS: 
                     [23977203]|; the phosphatase reaction follows an 
                     associative general acid-base catalytic mechanism |CITS: 
                     [25272278]|.  The single Mg<sup>2+</sup> ion in the 
                     catalytic site appears to be a key factor, balancing the 
                     kinase and phosphatase reactions |CITS: [25272278]|.  Later
                     experiments showed catalytic activity with Mn<sup>2+</sup> 
                     in place of Mg<sup>2+</sup> ions, as well as the presence 
                     of two Mn<sup>2+</sup> ions in the active site; the 
                     phosphatase activity is higher in the presence of 
                     Mn<sup>2+</sup> than in the presence of Mg<sup>2+</sup>.  
                     ADP within the active site plays a crucial role for 
                     divalent metal ion binding |CITS: [31235769]|.
                     Crystal structures of wild type or mutant AceK alone or in 
                     complexes have been solved |CITS: 
                     [19407397][19923739][20505668][25272278][31235769]|; 
                     structures published by the Jia lab utilized AceK isolated 
                     from <i>E. coli</i> O157:H7, which is 98.8% identical to 
                     that from K-12.  Except for the ATP binding motif, AceK 
                     lacks sequence homology with eukaryotic kinases and 
                     phosphatases; nevertheless, the structure of the C-terminal
                     kinase domain of AceK is similar to eukaryotic protein 
                     kinases.  In addition, an N-terminal regulatory domain with
                     a novel fold was discovered, with AMP binding to an 
                     allosteric site between the kinase and the regulatory 
                     domain.  The substrate recognition loop of AceK was found 
                     to be inserted deeply inside the active site cleft of IDH, 
                     enabling access to the otherwise buried active site Ser113 
                     residue |CITS: [20505668]|.
                     Mutagenesis of the kinase domain regulatory loop 
                     &beta;3&alpha;C showed that it is important for both 
                     structure and function of AceK |CITS: [27528271]|.
                     There appears to be only one ATP-binding site per subunit 
                     |CITS: [2834232][8620880]|.  A mutation in the predicted 
                     ATP binding site leads to loss of both the kinase and 
                     phosphatase activities of the enzyme |CITS: [2547774]|. The
                     phosphatase reaction does not proceed in the presence of a 
                     non-hydrolyzable ATP analog |CITS: [8702587]|.
                     Mutant alleles separating the kinase and phosphatase 
                     activities of the enzyme have been obtained |CITS: 
                     [1847910][1310504]|.  A kinetic model for the mechanism of 
                     the kinase and phosphatase activities of the enzyme with a 
                     single active site has been proposed |CITS: [8702587]|. 
                     Site-directed mutagenesis of the predicted ""catalytic 
                     triad"" residues found that only one, Asp371, is essential 
                     for activity, while Asn377 and Asp403 appear to be involved
                     in Mg<sup>2+</sup> binding |CITS: [11258918]|. Under 
                     non-reducing conditions, an intermolecular disulfide bond 
                     involving the C67 residue of both monomers is formed |CITS:
                     [10231385]|.
                     <i>aceK</i> expression is much lower than expression of the
                     <i>aceB</i> and <i>aceA</i> genes upstream in the same 
                     operon |CITS: [2826408]|; this effect appeared to be due to
                     inefficient translation of <i>aceK</i> |CITS: [8331088]|.  
                     The <i>aceK</i> mRNA contains a cleavage site for RNase 
                     III; cleavage appears to lead to rapid degradation of the 
                     <i>aceK</i>-coding part of the transcript |CITS: 
                     [28351917]|.  <i>aceK</i> is essential for growth on 
                     acetate |CITS: [3297049]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [2557093][8381789][16415587][22889914]|"
