     gene            4213756..4217439
                     /gene="metH"
                     /locus_tag="b4019"
                     /label=metH
     CDS             4213756..4217439
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="metH"
                     /locus_tag="b4019"
                     /product="cobalamin-dependent methionine synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.1.1.13"
                     /label=metH
                     /note="Cobalamin-dependent methionine synthase (MetH) is a
                     large modular protein that catalyzes the transfer of a 
                     methyl group from |FRAME: 5-METHYL-THF-GLU-N 
                     5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate| to |FRAME: HOMO-CYS| to form 
                     |FRAME: MET| in the final step of the |FRAME: 
                     HOMOSER-METSYN-PWY| pathway.
                     The reaction can be catalyzed by either of two 
                     transmethylases, |FRAME: HOMOCYSMET-MONOMER MetE| or MetH. 
                     MetH, described here, is dependent on |FRAME: COB-I-ALAMIN|
                     (vitamin B12) as a cofactor, whereas MetE is not.  MetH can
                     utilize either the mono- or triglutamate forms of folate, 
                     while MetE can use only the triglutamate form |CITS: 
                     [5319971]|.  MetE and MetH lack sequence similarity, 
                     suggesting that these proteins arose by convergent 
                     evolution |CITS: [1339288]|.
                     MetH is composed of four functionally distinct modules that
                     are arranged linearly with single interdomain linkers.  The
                     N-terminal module binds homocysteine, while the second 
                     module binds methyl-tetrahydrofolate (methylTHF) |CITS: 
                     [9201956]|.  The third module binds the cobalamin cofactor 
                     |CITS: [2668277][8369296]|; the C-terminal module binds 
                     |FRAME: S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE| (AdoMet) and is necessary for
                     the initial reductive methylation of the cobalamin cofactor
                     |CITS: [8369297]|. The cobalamin cofactor serves as a 
                     methyl group carrier and is initially activated by transfer
                     of a methyl group from AdoMet, which forms |FRAME: 
                     CPD-9037| |CITS: [5337044][4886251][4886252]|.  Kinetic 
                     studies provided evidence for an ordered sequential 
                     mechanism in which methylTHF binding precedes binding of 
                     homocysteine, and release of methionine precedes release of
                     tetrahydrofolate |CITS: [2271698]|.  MetH first catalyzes 
                     methyl transfer from enzyme-bound |FRAME: CPD-9037| to 
                     homocysteine to produce methionine and |FRAME: 
                     COB-I-ALAMIN| |CITS: [2853966]|.  MethylTHF then 
                     remethylates cob(I)alamin within MetH to regenerate 
                     methylcobalamin |CITS: [2271698]|.  Although exogenously 
                     supplied cobalamin can be utilized for catalysis of the two
                     methylation half reactions by the isolated N-terminal half 
                     of MetH |CITS: [9201956]|, MetH requires the bound cofactor
                     for efficient catalysis |CITS: [11305922]|.  The movement 
                     of a proton during catalysis has been investigated |CITS: 
                     [9398303]|. Zinc is required for methyl transfer from 
                     methylcobalamin to homocysteine |CITS: [9398304]|; the zinc
                     ion is coordinated by the cysteine residues 247, 310 and 
                     311 and the L-homocysteine substrate |CITS: 
                     [PEARISO98][11170420]|.
                     The cobalamin cofactor appears to be oxidized once every 
                     approximately 2,000 catalytic cycles to |FRAME: CPD-1829| 
                     |CITS: [8369297]| and must be reactivated by the uptake of 
                     an electron from reduced |FRAME: FLAVODOXIN1-MONOMER| 
                     acting together with |FRAME: FLAVONADPREDUCT-MONOMER Fpr| 
                     |CITS: [4154078][13738]|.  Ferredoxin can not substitute 
                     for flavodoxin as an electron donor |CITS: [8993326]|.
                     The presence of the oxidized cob(II)alamin cofactor appears
                     to lead to a global conformation change in MetH that allows
                     the interaction of cob(II)alamin with flavodoxin and 
                     AdoMet, reactivating the cofactor by reductive methylation 
                     |CITS: [9548919]|.  Reduction of cob(II)alamin to 
                     cob(I)alamin is followed by methyl transfer from AdoMet and
                     a slow change from a five-coordinate methylcobalamin to the
                     active six-coordinate methylcobalamin |CITS: [9730838]|.  
                     The Tyr1139 active site residue within the reactivation 
                     domain mediates lengthening of the Co-OH<sub>2</sub> bond 
                     in the presence of AdoMet |CITS: [19006389]|.  Glu61 of 
                     flavodoxin interacts with Lys959 of the C-terminal 
                     activation domain of the enzyme |CITS: [10978155]|.
                     The enzyme undergoes global conformational changes to allow
                     the cobalamin-binding domain to to distinguish its two 
                     methyl donors, methyltetrahydrofolate and AdoMet |CITS: 
                     [8369297][8879545][12832615]|.  The His759 residue |CITS: 
                     [17567043][18332423]| as well as the presence of the 
                     ligands methylTHF, flavodoxin and the &beta;-ligand of 
                     cobalamin |CITS: [17924667]| influence the transition 
                     between different conformations.  Small-angle X-ray 
                     scattering (SAXS) combined with cryoelectron microscopy 
                     suggested that the different oxidation states share a 
                     common resting-state conformation. However, flavodoxin 
                     preferentially binds the Cob(II) state, ensuring that only 
                     the Cob(II) enzyme can enter the reactivation cycle |CITS: 
                     [37339208]|.
                     A crystal structure of the cobalamin-binding domain of MetH
                     revealed a conformational change in cobalamin upon binding 
                     to MetH, as well as a cap domain that appears to protect 
                     the cofactor |CITS: [1593636][7992050]|.  A structure 
                     containing both the cobalamin- and AdoMet-binding domains 
                     in the activation conformation shows displacement of the 
                     cap domain and formation of a new interface |CITS: 
                     [11731805]|.  Crystal structures of the C-terminal 
                     AdoMet-binding domain have been solved |CITS: [8939751]|.  
                     Disulfide crosslinking was used to lock a C-terminal 
                     fragment of MetH in the reactivation conformation; its 
                     crystal structure showed that the His759 residue 
                     dissociated from cobalamin and formed contacts within the 
                     AdoMet-binding domain |CITS: [18332423]|. Additional 
                     structures elucidated the steps in reactivation, involving 
                     the transition between coordination states of the cobalt 
                     atom |CITS: [19846791]|. A number of crystal structures of 
                     the reactivation domain enabled identification of residues 
                     involved in substrate binding |CITS: [29733595]|.
                     Mutation of Cys310 or Cys311 in the N-terminal catalytic 
                     domain to either alanine or serine result in loss of 
                     catalytic activity |CITS: [9201956]|.  Site-directed 
                     mutagenesis of residues in the cobalamin-binding domain 
                     showed that a His759Gly mutation abolishes catalytic 
                     activity |CITS: [8652589]| by blocking turnover |CITS: 
                     [8652590]|.  Mutagenesis of Asp757 or Ser810 decrease 
                     cobalamin reactivity, but increase AdoMet-dependent 
                     reactivation of the enzyme |CITS: [8652590]|.  Mutations in
                     the cap domain increase photolysis of methylcobalamin 
                     |CITS: [8879545]|.  Specific recognition of AdoMet within 
                     the C-terminal reactivation domain is promoted by the 
                     Glu1097 and Glu1128 residues |CITS: [29733595]|. 
                     <i>metH</i> mutants are methionine auxotrophs and are 
                     unable to utilize vitamin B<sub>12</sub> supplied in 
                     minimal medium |CITS: [4579870][6284705]|.  Negative 
                     complementation of the wild type with a truncated 
                     <i>metH</i> allele suggested formation of multimeric 
                     complexes |CITS: [2830470]|.  Expression of <i>metH</i> is 
                     positively regulated by MetR |CITS: [3549685][2543976]|; 
                     the presence of homocysteine decreases the positive 
                     regulation by MetR |CITS: [2656646][2673243]|. 
                     MetR-dependent transcription activation requires different 
                     C-terminal domain residues and orientation of the &alpha; 
                     subunit of RNA polymerase for <i>metE</i> vs. <i>metH</i> 
                     promoters |CITS: [7836282][10986259]|.  Expression also 
                     appeared to be positively regulated by B<sub>12</sub> and 
                     negatively regulated by methionine |CITS: [2830470]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [2407589][1943695][7712296][8736549][96678 
                     65][9587027][11513576]|"
