     gene            complement(4230707..4232182)
                     /gene="xylE"
                     /locus_tag="b4031"
                     /label=xylE
     CDS             complement(4230707..4232182)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="xylE"
                     /locus_tag="b4031"
                     /product="D-xylose:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=xylE
                     /note="XylE is a D-xylose/proton symporter, one of two
                     systems in <I>E. coli</I> K-12 responsible for the uptake 
                     of D-xylose - the other being the ATP-dependent ABC 
                     transporter XylFGH. The cloned <I>xylE</I> gene has been 
                     shown to complement <I>xylE</I> mutants in <I>vivo</I> 
                     |CITS: [2820984]|. XylE-mediated transport in whole cells 
                     is inhibited by protonophores and elicits an alkaline pH 
                     change |CITS: [6995439]|. Experiments using <I>xylE</I> and
                     <I>xylF</I> mutants have established that XylE has a Km of 
                     63-169 &mu;M for D-xylose |CITS: [8581399]|.
                     Crystal structures of XylE bound to the ligands D-xylose, 
                     D-glucose and the chemically synthesized glucose 
                     derivative, 6-bromo-6-deoxy-D-glucose have been obtained at
                     2.6 - 2.9 &Aring;. Structures were captured in an outward 
                     facing conformation. XylE contains 12 transmembrane (TM) 
                     helices organised into two distinct domains with amino and 
                     carboxy termini located on the cytosolic side of the 
                     membrane. XylE also contains an intracellular domain 
                     consisting of 4 helices - 3 of these connect the TM 
                     domains, the fourth is located at the C-terminal end of the
                     protein |CITS: [23075985]|. Crystal structures of XylE in 
                     an inward open conformation are also available |CITS: 
                     [23624861], [24060988]|.
                     D-glucose is a strong inhibitor of xylose transport in 
                     <i>in vitro</i> sugar competition assays. Other hexose and 
                     pentose sugars (D-galactose, L-fucose, L-arabinose, 
                     D-mannose and L-rhamnose) had no effect |CITS: [23075985]|.
                     D-glucose does not induce the XylE transport system |CITS: 
                     [6995439]|.
                     XylE mutants with neutral replacements at Asp27 (XylE D27N 
                     and XylE D27A) do not catalyse D-xylose:H<sup>+</sup> 
                     symport although binding and counterflow activity remain 
                     similar to the wild-type suggesting that Asp27 is involved 
                     in proton transport. A XylE double mutant Q175I / L297F 
                     transports D-glucose although at a lower level than 
                     D-xylose |CITS: [24550316]|. Specific lipid-protein 
                     interactions affect the conformational dynamics of XylE; 
                     phosphotidylethanolamine disrupts a charge-relay network 
                     present on the intracellular side of XylE and triggers a 
                     conformational shift towards the inward facing state <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [30297844]|.
                     XylE is a member of the major facilitator superfamily (MFS)
                     of transporters |CITS: [1419050]|. The <I>xylE</I> gene 
                     probably constitutes a monocistronic operon whose 
                     expression is inducible by D-xylose. Imported xylose is 
                     catabolised to xylulose-5-phosphate by the action of the 
                     XylA and XylB enzymes."
                     /translation="MNTQYNSSYIFSITLVATLGGLLFGYDTAVISGTVESLNTVFVAP
