     gene            complement(4233459..4235003)
                     /gene="malF"
                     /locus_tag="b4033"
                     /label=malF
     CDS             complement(4233459..4235003)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="malF"
                     /locus_tag="b4033"
                     /product="multifunctional maltose ABC transporter membrane
                     subunit MalF / maltohexaose ABC transporter / maltose ABC 
                     transporter / maltotetraose ABC transporter / maltotriose 
                     ABC transporter"
                     /EC_number="7.5.2.1"
                     /label=malF
                     /note="MalF is an integral membrane component of the
                     maltose ABC transporter |CITS: 
                     [6765939][6088520][3317413]|. A MalF-MalG heterodimer forms
                     the translocation channel of the maltose ABC transporter; 
                     the MalF-MalG channel contacts the MalK dimer at the 
                     cytoplasmic face of the membrane and the MalE binding 
                     protein at the periplasmic face of the membrane |CITS: 
                     [18033289]|
                     MalF contains 8 transmembrane (TM) helices; in a crystal 
                     structure of the maltose transporter in complex with MalE, 
                     maltose and ATP, the MalF periplasmic loop (P2) located 
                     between TM 3 and 4 is a major area of contact with the 
                     maltose binding protein MalE, while an EAA loop located 
                     between TM 6 and 7 is a major area of contact with the MalK
                     dimer |CITS: [18033289]|.
                     <I>malF</I> mutations that alter the substrate specificity 
                     of the maltose ABC transporter have been isolated and 
                     characterised |CITS:[8636026][9401026][9442094]|. Insertion
                     of MalF into the cytoplasmic membrane is SecE- and 
                     SecA-dependent |CITS:[8647843],[7795711]|; MalF can 
                     assemble in the membrane independently of the bacterial 
                     secretion machinery |CITS:[1939136]|."
