                     /product="multifunctional maltose ABC transporter
                     periplasmic binding protein / maltohexaose ABC transporter 
                     / maltose ABC transporter / maltotetraose ABC transporter /
                     maltotriose ABC transporter"
                     /EC_number="7.5.2.1"
                     /label=malE
                     /note="<i>malE</i> encodes the periplasmic
                     substrate-binding component of the maltose ABC transporter.
                     MalE, or maltodextrin binding protein (MBP) can bind linear
                     maltodextrins, cyclic maltodextrins and various 
                     maltodextrin analogues although only linear maltodextrins 
                     up maltoheptaose are substrates for transport |CITS: 
                     [4215651][6997044][6358208][3524683]|. Non-polar 
                     <i>malE</i> deletion mutants do not show appreciable growth
                     on media containing maltose concentrations up to 25 mM 
                     |CITS: [7040366]|.
                     MBP consists of two globular domains separated by a deep 
                     groove which contains the maltodextrin binding site. MBP 
                     adopts two distinct conformations - an open unliganded form
                     in which the two globular domains are far apart and the 
                     binding groove is accessible and a closed liganded 
                     structure. Bound maltose is almost completely enclosed and 
                     is held in place by hydrogen bonds and Van der Waals 
                     interactions |CITS: [2002054][1420181][9309217]|. Crystal 
                     structures of MalE bound to cyclomaltoheptaose |CITS: 
                     [8399200][10864509]|, of MalE bound to the modified sugars 
                     maltotriitol and maltotetraitol |CITS: 
                     [11237621][11790091]|, of MalE complexed with xenon |CITS: 
                     [12217701]| and of various mutant MalE proteins |CITS: 
                     [7853407][8951382][12592028][12794084]| are available. 
                     Maltose bound MBP has a strong stimulatory effect on the 
                     ATPase activity of the maltose transporter 
                     (MalFGK<sub>2</sub>) <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [1549599], 
                     [18725638]|. Both liganded and unliganded forms of MBP 
                     interact with the membrane components of the maltose 
                     transporter <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [8558904], [7891451]| 
                     and unliganded MBP is also able to stimulate the ATPase 
                     activity of MalFGK<sub>2</sub> |CITS: [19630440]|. The 
                     interaction of both liganded and unliganded MalE with 
                     MalFGK<sub>2</sub> in different nucleotide states (apo, ADP
                     and ATP) has been investigated |CITS: [23509285]|. 
                     Dissociation of MBP is not a prerequisite for substrate 
                     translocation |CITS: [23509285]|.
                     MBP is expressed at very high levels in the periplasm of 
                     <i>E. coli</i> growing on maltose |CITS: [4215651], 
                     [358938]|.
                     In addition to being the periplasmic substrate-binding 
                     component of the maltose ABC transporter, MalE with bound 
                     substrate is also able to bind to the chemoreceptor Tar to 
                     induce chemotaxis toward maltose. MalE mutants have been 
                     identified which are chemotaxis negative for maltose 
                     |CITS:[1091624],[1429629], [9927672]|.
                     Export of proMalE to the periplasm is dependent on the 
                     cytoplasmic chaperone |FRAME: EG10937 ""SecB""| and this 
                     process has been extensively studied |CITS:[2834066][26442 
                     37][2670890][2188948][8226642][8050582][18048690]| (and 
                     reviewed by |CITS: [2202725]|) . Reviews: |CITS: [ 
                     2124143], [17950243]|"
