     gene            4236712..4237827
                     /gene="malK"
                     /locus_tag="b4035"
                     /label=malK
     CDS             4236712..4237827
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="malK"
                     /locus_tag="b4035"
                     /product="multifunctional maltose ABC transporter ATP
                     binding subunit / maltohexaose ABC transporter / maltose 
                     ABC transporter / maltotetraose ABC transporter / 
                     maltotriose ABC transporter"
                     /EC_number="7.5.2.1"
                     /label=malK
                     /note="MalK is the ATP-binding component of the maltose
                     ABC transporter |CITS:[6778869][ 8226895][9287001]|. 
                     Opening and closing of the MalK dimer which is associated 
                     with ATP binding and hydrolysis is thought to be a key 
                     event in the transport cycle.
                     Crystal structures are available for MalK with bound ATP 
                     |CITS: [14527411]|; MalK without nucleotide (in both an 
                     open and semi-open conformation) |CITS: [14527411]| and 
                     MalK with bound ADP |CITS: [16326809]|. In computer 
                     simulations, the introduction of MgATP into the active site
                     of MalK is associated with transition from semi-open to 
                     closed conformation and from open to semi-open conformation
                     |CITS: [16877382]|. Computer simulations also suggest that 
                     the closed form of MalK is stable when it contains two 
                     bound ATP molecules and that a single ATP hydrolysis will 
                     induce opening of the dimer |CITS: [18790847]|.  ATP binds 
                     along the dimer interface between the nucleotide binding 
                     domains (NBDs), positioned between the Walker A motif of 
                     one subunit and the signature LSGGQ motif of the other 
                     subunit |CITS: [12093921][14527411]|; ATP binding tightly 
                     tethers the NBDs of the MalK dimer; the &gamma; phosphate 
                     of ATP interacts with the Walker A motif, the Q loop, the 
                     switch region histidine of one NBD and the LSGGQ motif of 
                     the other NBD |CITS: [21825153]|. ATP hydrolysis is 
                     catalysed by a mechanism in which Glu159 acts as the 
                     general base to accept a proton from the attacking water; 
                     it is not clear whether one or two ATPs are hydrolyzed 
                     during a single transport cycle |CITS: [21825153]| (and see
                     comment by |CITS: [21876189]|) Conformational changes in 
                     MalK, associated with ATP binding and hydrolysis, modulate 
                     its interation with MalF and MalG <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [9791180]|. Closure of the MalK dimer interface coincides 
                     with opening of MBP and maltose release to the transporter 
                     |CITS: [15117946]|.
                     ATP binding to MalK is associated with conformational 
                     change in MalG and MalF periplasmic loops |CITS: 
                     [16352608]|.
                     The N-terminal portion of MalK is the ATP-binding portion 
                     which interacts with MalF and MalG, and the C-terminal 
                     portion is a regulatory domain that interacts with the 
                     transcriptional activator, |FRAME: PD00237 ""MalT""| 
                     |CITS:[3926486],[11709552],[12813052],[14527411]|. Over 
                     expression of <i>malK</i> prevents induction of the 
                     <i>mal</i> regulon by interfering with the activity of MalT
                     |CITS: [3049541]|. MalK interacts with MalT <i>in vivo</i> 
                     and <i>in vitro</i>; the ability to down regulate MalT 
                     activity is associated with an inactive, ATP-bound form of 
                     MalK |CITS: [9822819]|. MalK inhibits transcription 
                     activation by MalT <i>in vitro</i> and this effect is 
                     relieved by maltotriose; MalK and maltotriose compete for 
                     MalT binding |CITS: [15180985]|. Inhibition involves 
                     sequestration of MalT by the resting form of the MalFGK 
                     transporter, not by free MalK |CITS: [22715926]|. MalK is 
                     also the site of interaction with |FRAME: CRR-MONOMER 
                     ""EIIA<sup>Glc</sup>""|; under inducer exclusion MalK binds
                     to the dephosphorylated form of EIIA<sup>Glc</sup>; the 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> binding site is separate to the site of 
                     ATP binding |CITS: [23770568][27571040]|. Two molecules of 
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> interact with the MalK subunits and 
                     stabilise an inward facing conformation of the transporter.
                     This interaction would prevent the structural 
                     rearrangements required for ATP hydrolysis and maltose 
                     transport. A hydrogen bond is formed between MalK(E122) and
                     EIIA<sup>Glc</sup>(H90); phosphorylation of H90 is 
                     predicted to disrupt the interface |CITS: [23770568]|. In a
                     reconstituted system EIIA<sup>Glc</sup> interacts with MalK
                     during all steps of the transport cycle; EIIA<sup>Glc</sup>
                     binding inhibits transport by preventing closure of the 
                     MalK dimer |CITS: [27571040]|.
                     MalK accumulates recurrent mutations under glucose 
                     limitation that likely relieve repression of <i>lamB</i> 
                     transcription, enhancing glucose uptake under 
                     nutrient-limited conditions |CITS:[40830465]|.
                     Commenst: |CITS: [12122215][22742494]|"
