     gene            4242944..4243816
                     /gene="ubiA"
                     /locus_tag="b4040"
                     /label=ubiA
     CDS             4242944..4243816
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ubiA"
                     /locus_tag="b4040"
                     /product="4-hydroxybenzoate octaprenyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.5.1.39"
                     /label=ubiA
                     /note="4-Hydroxybenzoate octaprenyltransferase is the
                     second enzyme in the pathway of ubiquinone biosynthesis. It
                     is
                     responsible for the prenylation of 4-hydroxybenzoate using 
                     a polyprenyldiphosphate as a side chain precursor. While 
                     <i>E. coli</i> produces an octapreny diphosphatel natively,
                     UbiA is not specific to that compound and can accept 
                     polyprenyl diphosphates of different lengths |CITS: 
                     [24558159][26186907]|.
                     UbiA is an inner membrane protein with seven predicted 
                     transmembrane domains.  The C terminus is located in the 
                     periplasm |CITS: [15919996]|.  Earlier work experimentally 
                     localized UbiA to the membrane fraction |CITS: 
                     [4552989][8155731]|.
                     The structure of the enzyme and its active site have been 
                     modelled |CITS: [15597200]|.  In subsequent work a 3D 
                     structural model was developed that places UbiA in SCOP 
                     family 48 583.  The model was experimentally supported by 
                     site-directed mutagenesis studies |CITS: [18338424]|. 
                     <i>ubiA</i> mutants can grow on glucose as the sole source 
                     of carbon, but can not grow on succinate
                     |CITS: [4870277][4552989][8409922]|.  A 
                     <i>ubiA</i><sup>-</sup> mutant grows more slowly on glucose
                     and
                     accumulates acetate and lactate |CITS: [195602]|.
                     The <i>sdgG10</i> mutation, which suppresses a conditional 
                     mutation in <i>dnaG</i> (DNA primase), has been
                     localized to either the <i>ubiC</i>, <i>ubiA</i> or 
                     <i>yjbI</i> gene |CITS: [9093842]|.
                     <i>ubiA</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     Radiotracer studies have suggested that UbiA, UbiD, and 
                     UbiI can utilize <i>p</i>-aminobenzoate, or intermediates 
                     derived from it, as substrates |CITS: [25681964]|. Reviews:
                     |CITS: [24480387][11583838][11153266]|"
