     gene            complement(4243971..4246394)
                     /gene="plsB"
                     /locus_tag="b4041"
                     /label=plsB
     CDS             complement(4243971..4246394)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="plsB"
                     /locus_tag="b4041"
                     /product="multifunctional glycerol-3-phosphate
                     1-O-acyltransferase / acyl-acp:sn-glycerol-3-phosphate 
                     1-O-acyltransferase / acyl-acp:sn-glycerol-3-phosphate 
                     1-O-acyltransferase (glycerol-3-phosphate acyltransferase) 
                     / acyl-CoA:sn-glycerol-3-phosphate 1-O-acyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.15"
                     /label=plsB
                     /note="Membrane-bound glycerol-3-phosphate acyltransferase 
                     (PlsB) catalyzes the first committed step in phospholipid 
                     biosynthesis and is thought to function in close proximity 
                     to the succeeding enzyme |FRAME: 
                     1-ACYLGLYCEROL-3-P-ACYLTRANSFER-MONOMER| (PlsC) |CITS: 
                     [6351928]|. It is specific for acylation at position 1 of 
                     |FRAME: GLYCEROL-3P| and can utilize either fatty acyl-acyl
                     carrier protein (acyl-ACP) or fatty acyl-coenzyme A 
                     (acyl-CoA) thioesters as acyl donors to form |FRAME: 
                     ACYL-SN-GLYCEROL-3P|. Fatty acids that are endogenously 
                     synthesized are attached to ACP and exogenously added fatty
                     acids are attached to CoA |CITS: [ 
                     10989][324973][6997313][6251087][7005223][7026564]6355102[]
                     [3525537][3294813]|. In <i>E. coli</i> phospholipids the 
                     <i>sn</i> 1 position is occupied mainly by either 
                     palmitate, or <i>cis</i>-vaccenate, whereas the <i>sn</i> 2
                     position is predominantly palmitoleate, or 
                     <i>cis</i>-vaccenate. This is thought to result from the 
                     substrate preferences of the PlsB and PlsC enzymes |CITS: 
                     [7005223][6998967]|.
                     The <i>plsB</i> gene has been shown to be regulated by 
                     stress response regulators such as |FRAME: RPOE-MONOMER| 
                     and |FRAME: GUANOSINE-5DP-3DP| |CITS: 
                     [7929384][16336047][19798669][21463370]|. PlsB is part of a
                     protein network for phospholipid synthesis and interacts 
                     with |FRAME: ACP| (ACP), |FRAME: EG11110-MONOMER| (YbgC) 
                     and |FRAME: PHOSPHASERSYN-MONOMER| (PssA) to form a complex
                     at the cytoplasmic side of the inner membrane |CITS: 
                     [16294310]|.
                     <i>plsB</i> is essential for growth |CITS: 
                     [16738554][17645809]|.
                     Site-directed mutagenesis and chemical modification studies
                     have demonstrated catalytically important amino acid 
                     residues in PlsB, including an invariant histidine residue 
                     that is essential for catalysis |CITS: 
                     [9515909][10231527]|. Genetic studies have identified the 
                     <i>plsB</i> locus as involved in the formation of multidrug
                     tolerant persister cells |CITS: [16816185]|. |FRAME: 
                     EG11437-MONOMER| and |FRAME: EG11674-MONOMER PlsY| together
                     can substitute for PlsB in synthesis of |FRAME: 
                     2-Acyl-sn-glycerol-3-phosphates| in <i>E. coli</i> and most
                     other Gram negative proteobacteria |CITS: 
                     [17645809][37601446][39475235]|.
                     The properties of the <i>E. coli</i> B enzyme were studied 
                     in earlier work |CITS: [4552475][4352195][350288]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [23500459][22981714][18369234][9370310][MathewsvanHolde]|"
