     gene            4251597..4252634
                     /gene="dusA"
                     /locus_tag="b4049"
                     /label=dusA
     CDS             4251597..4252634
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dusA"
                     /locus_tag="b4049"
                     /product="bifunctional tRNA-dihydrouridine synthase A / 
                     tRNA-dihydrouridine synthase"
                     /label=dusA
                     /note="DusA accounts for about half of the
                     5,6-dihydrouridine modification observed in wild-type 
                     cellular tRNA; |FRAME: EG11311-MONOMER| (DusB) and |FRAME: 
                     EG12022-MONOMER| (DusC) together account for the other 
                     half. DusA is solely responsible for the 5,6-dihydrouridine
                     modification observed in tRNA<sub>2</sub><sup>fMet</sup> 
                     |CITS: [11983710]|.
                     A <i>dusA dusB dusC</i> triple mutant (&Delta;3) exhibits a
                     complete lack of 5,6-dihydrouridine modification in 
                     cellular tRNA, whereas each single mutant exhibits a 
                     partial reduction, compared to wild type |CITS: 
                     [11983710]|. Amino acids that are required for DusA 
                     activity have been identified |CITS: [16962594]|. Using a 
                     <i>&Delta;dusA</i> mutant, the specificity of DusA for 
                     dihydrouridylation of nucleotide 20 and 20a was determined 
                     in |FRAME: ARG-tRNAs| (ICG), |FRAME: ILE-tRNAs| (GAU) and 
                     |FRAME: LEU-tRNAs| (CAG) |CITS: [29294097]|. Follow-up 
                     experiments with the &Delta;3 mutant found that mRNAs are 
                     not dihydrouridylated and that the dihydrouridine 
                     nucleotide in 23S rRNA is dihydrouridylated by some other 
                     dihydrouridine synthase than DusA, DusB or DusC |CITS: 
                     [34798057]|. Using a new method that blocks reverse 
                     transcriptase by using rhodamine-labelled dihydrouridine 
                     (called RhoRT-PCR), the <i>dusA dusB dusC</i> triple mutant
                     was found to not prevent the dihydrouridylation of 23S 
                     rRNA, consistent with the previous evidence; instead 
                     dihydrouridylation of 23S rRNA was found to be carried out 
                     with |FRAME: EG12229-MONOMER| |CITS: [39078675]|.
                     Analysis of each single mutant compared to wild type found 
                     reduced 5,6-dihydrouridine modification activity under 
                     paraquat-induced anaerobic conditions, with DusA being the 
                     least sensitive and DusC being the most sensitive to 
                     changes in redox best represented by a decrease in NADPH 
                     availability. Position specificity differed between the 
                     three tRNA-dihydrouridine synthases; kinetics were measured
                     under aerobic conditions |CITS: [39460624]|. DusA: 
                     ""<b>d</b>ihydro<b>u</b>ridine <b>s</b>ynthase <b>A</b>""
                     Reviews: |CITS: [35638108][35737906]|"
