     gene            complement(4264688..4264969)
                     /gene="yjcB"
                     /locus_tag="b4060"
                     /label=yjcB
     CDS             complement(4264688..4264969)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yjcB"
                     /locus_tag="b4060"
                     /product="uncharacterized protein YjcB"
                     /label=yjcB
                     /note="A meta-analysis of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 MG1655 
                     transcriptomic data sets in response to multiple stressors 
                     found 15 uncharacterized or partially characterized genes 
                     (<i>yqfA, yhfG, yhhA, ybgS, arpA, yqaE, yfdY, yaiY, yebE, 
                     ydiE, yjcB, yiiX, ycjF, yihF, yidX</i>) that were 
                     upregulated under all five stressors: heat, cold, oxidative
                     stress, nitrosative stress, and antibiotic treatment, 
                     whereas 3 uncharacterized or partially characterized genes 
                     (<i>ymfI, ydiJ, yedE</i>) that were downregulated under all
                     but nitrosative stress |CITS: [36144394]|.
                     <i>yjcB</i> was in the top 20 genes with increased 
                     translation in response to extreme acid stress (pH 4.4 
                     compared to pH 7.6) as measured by ribosome coverage of its
                     transcripts using RNA-Seq |CITS: [37909716]|."
                     /translation="MATLTTGVVLLRWQLLSAVMMFLASTLNIRFRRSDYVGLAVISSG
                     LGVVSACWFAMGLLGITMADITAIWHNIESVMIEEMNQTPPQWPMILT"
                     /go_component="plasma membrane [goid 0005886] [evidence
                     IEA] [PMID 26602691]"
                     /go_process="positive regulation of gene expression [goid
                     0010628] [evidence HEP] [PMID 36144394]"
                     /go_process="response to stress [goid 0006950] [evidence
                     HEP] [PMID 36144394]"
                     /go_process="cellular response to acidic pH [goid 0071468]
                     [evidence EXP] [PMID 37909716]"
     gene            4265399..4266985
                     /gene="pdeC"
                     /locus_tag="b4061"
                     /label=pdeC
     CDS             4265399..4266985
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="pdeC"
                     /locus_tag="b4061"
                     /product="bifunctional cyclic di-GMP phosphodiesterase
                     PdeC / cyclic di-GMP phosphodiesterase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.4.52"
                     /label=pdeC
                     /note="PdeC is a c-di-GMP-specific phosphodiesterase whose 
                     activity is implicated in modulating matrix formation in 
                     the deeper layers of <i>E. coli</i> biofilm. Overexpression
                     of <i>pdeC</i> reduces biofilm formation |CITS: 
                     [19460094]|. PdeC is an inner membrane protein with two 
                     predicted transmembrane domains which flank a predicted 
                     periplasmic CSS domain containing two highly conserved 
                     cysteines, followed by a cytoplasmic C-terminal EAL domain 
                     with phosphodiesterase activity |CITS: 
                     [15919996][26148715][29514851]|. Purified PdeC 
                     reconstituted into nanodiscs exhibits c-di-GMP 
                     phosphodiesterase activity only in the presence of reducing
                     agent (DTT) |CITS: [29514851]|.
                     The periplasmic CSS domain contains two cysteine residues 
                     which form a disulphide bond; the redox state of the 
                     periplasmic CSS domain controls phosphodiesterase activity;
                     PdeC is highly active when the CSS domain is in the free 
                     thiol state |CITS: [29514851]|. PdeC in the free thiol 
                     state can dimerize; PdeC in the free thiol state is subject
                     to DegP/DegQ proteolytic processing and the product of this
                     processing (a membrane attached EAL domain) can also 
                     dimerize; in both cases dimerization is associated with 
                     increased PDE activity |CITS: [29514851]|. Moderate 
                     expression of cloned PdeC in a K-12 strain that produces 
                     both curli fibres and cellulose results in a macrocolony 
                     morphology that reflects low cellular c-di-GMP 
                     phosphodiesterase activity; expression of PdeC variants 
                     which are unable to form the CSS domain disulphide bond 
                     results in unstructured macrocolonies reflecting enhanced 
                     phosphodiesterase activity |CITS: [29514851]|.
                     Formation of the periplasmic PdeC disulfide bond is 
                     promoted by the DsbA-DsbB system; microaerobic conditions 
                     in the deeper layers of a biofilm matrix may limit activity
                     of the DsbA-DsbB system, increasing disulfide bond 
                     formation in PdeC with a consequent decrease in 
                     phosphodiesterase activity |CITS: [29514851]|.
                     A screen for suppressors of the motility defect of a 
                     |FRAME: EG12252 &Delta;<i>pdeH</i>| mutant identified 
                     activating mutations in <i>E. coli</i>'s alternative 
                     c-di-GMP phosphodiesterases (PDEs), including PdeC. This 
                     supports the view that many of the candidate c-di-GMP 
                     phosphodiesterases encoded in the <i>E. coli</i> genome 
                     require an appropriate stimulus to become active. The 
                     suppressor mutation in <i>pdeC</i> consisted of an IS 
                     element insertion in its promoter region, leading to 
                     strongly increased levels of PdeC protein, increased 
                     swimming motility, and reduced levels of intracellular 
                     c-di-GMP |CITS: [26553851]|.
                     Expression of <i>pdeC</i> is induced during the transition 
                     from exponential to stationary phase and is 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-dependent |CITS: [19332833]|. PdeC can 
                     be detected in wild-type cells grown in tryptone broth at 
                     37 &deg;C |CITS: [26553851]|.
                     <i>pdeC</i> belongs to a network of genes which facilitate 
                     stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [23224554]|.
                     PdeC: ""<b>p</b>hospho<b>d</b>i<b>e</b>sterase <b>C</b>"" 
                     |CITS: [26148715]|"
