     gene            complement(4275341..4277299)
                     /gene="acs"
                     /locus_tag="b4069"
                     /label=acs
     CDS             complement(4275341..4277299)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="acs"
                     /locus_tag="b4069"
                     /product="bifunctional acetyl-CoA synthetase (AMP-forming)
                     / propanoate:CoA ligase (AMP-forming)"
                     /EC_number="6.2.1.1; 6.2.1.17"
                     /label=acs
                     /note="Acetyl-CoA synthetase (Acs) activates acetate to 
                     acetyl-CoA in an ATP-dependent manner |CITS: 
                     [21941][7751300]|. Acs activity constitutes one of two 
                     distinct pathways by which <i>E. coli</i> activates acetate
                     to acetyl-CoA. The Acs pathway (|FRAME: PWY0-1313|) 
                     functions in a mainly anabolic role, scavenging acetate 
                     present in the extracellular medium. Induction of 
                     <i>acs</i> expression functions as the metabolic switch 
                     activating this pathway |CITS: [10894724][21122111]|.
                     Genetic interactions suggest that Acs is acetylated and 
                     thereby inactivated through the action of |FRAME: 
                     G7350-MONOMER|, and that |FRAME: G6577-MONOMER CobB| 
                     catalyzes deacetylation of Acs |CITS: 
                     [22059728][29253849]|. Acetylation of Acs by |FRAME: 
                     G7350-MONOMER| has been shown <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [26251518]|. Acetylation of Acs can occur non-enzymatically
                     by acetyl-phosphate |CITS: [24835953]|. Acetylated Acs has 
                     a significantly decreased k<sub>cat</sub> for both acetate 
                     and propionate |CITS: [24835953]|. When cells are grown in 
                     propionate-containing medium, Lys111, Lys221, and Lys609 of
                     Acs are propionylated. A Lys609Arg mutant, potentially 
                     mimicing propionylation of the Lys609 residue, shows 
                     decreased enzymatic activity |CITS: [27804304]|.
                     Acs was thought to be involved in acetylation of |FRAME: 
                     CHEY-MONOMER CheY| |CITS: 
                     [2901103][1390767][9473056][9560203][11359578][15327941]|. 
                     Acetylation of CheY by Acs <i>in vitro</i> is reversible 
                     and cooperative, with a Hill coefcient of 3 |CITS: 
                     [15327942]|. However, acetylation of CheY <i>in vivo</i> is
                     largely a result of autoacetylation |CITS: [18234227]|. An 
                     <i>acs</i> mutant has a defect in chemotaxis |CITS: 
                     [11359578]|, which was not seen by |CITS: [20345663]|. 
                     Based on sequence similarity, Acs was predicted to be a 
                     hydroxycinnamate-CoA ligase |CITS: [12952533]|.
                     An <i>acs</i> mutant strain grows poorly on solid media 
                     with low concentrations of acetate as the sole source of 
                     carbon |CITS: [7751300]| and does not grow on propanoate as
                     the sole source of carbon |CITS: [24835953]|. In a 
                     long-term glucose-limited continuous culture experiment, 
                     mutants in the <i>acs</i> promoter region that overexpress 
                     Acs appeared |CITS: [9656481][19893610]|.
                     Acs activity is induced by growth on acetate |CITS: 
                     [21941]|. Gene expression is observed during growth on 
                     acetate or propionate |CITS: [12473114]| and at stationary 
                     phase |CITS: [8997713][10629211]|. Expression of <i>acs</i>
                     is increased in glucose-limited chemostat cultures compared
                     to batch cultures |CITS: [21881893]|. Decreased expression 
                     of Acs at high growth rates is primarily controlled by 
                     transcript stability |CITS: [24243845]|. CsrA positively 
                     regulates Acs expression |CITS: [10692369]|. A genome-wide 
                     screen to identify all direct or indirect regulators of 
                     <i>acs</i> expression has been performed, showing a tight 
                     link to metabolic enzymes |CITS: [23892289]|. <i>acs</i>, 
                     among other genes involved in carbon source transport and 
                     metabolism, was downregulated in two MG1655 lysogens 
                     carrying closely related Stx2a phages O104 and PA8 |CITS: 
                     31208335|. Reviews: |CITS: [15755952][24636882][34721364]|"
