     gene            4284409..4285722
                     /gene="gltP"
                     /locus_tag="b4077"
                     /label=gltP
     CDS             4284409..4285722
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="gltP"
                     /locus_tag="b4077"
                     /product="trifunctional glutamate/aspartate : H(+)
                     symporter GltP / L-aspartate:H(+) symporter / 
                     L-glutamate:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=gltP
                     /note="<I>E. coli</I> contains four transporters for the
                     uptake of glutamate (GltIJKL, GltP, GltS, and GadC) and 
                     five transporters for the uptake of aspartate (GltIJKL, 
                     GltP, DctA, DcuA, and DcuB). GltP accounts for 
                     approximately 60% of the total wild-type transport velocity
                     of glutamate, and 52% of the total wild-type transport 
                     velocity of aspartate in aerobic growth with succinate and 
                     40 mM NaCl |CITS:[336628]|.
                     GltP is a proton dependent transporter for glutamate and 
                     aspartate. <I>E. coli</I> K12 cannot grow on glutamate as a
                     sole carbon and nitrogen source, selection of mutants which
                     can grow on glutamate (Glt+) are typically due to mutations
                     which overexpress either the GltS or GltP glutamate 
                     transporters |CITS:[2576660]|. The cloned <I>gltP</I> gene 
                     has been shown to confer the ability to utilize and 
                     transport glutamate and aspartate |CITS:[1971622]|. A 
                     mutation leading to the overexpression of <I>gltP</I> 
                     results in increased sensitivity to cysteate and 
                     DL-threo-&beta;-hydroxyaspartate |CITS:[2576660]|. Whole 
                     cell transport experiments have shown that GltP is a proton
                     dependent transporter which can transport glutamate and 
                     aspartate with Km of approx 5 &mu;M |CITS:[336628]|. This 
                     has been confirmed in <I>E. coli</I> membrane vesicles, 
                     where GltP was shown to function via an electrogenic 
                     symport mechanism where L-glutamate was co-transported with
                     at least two protons |CITS:[8596452]|. GltP is a member of 
                     the DAACS family of proton or sodium-dependent amino acid 
                     or dicarboxylate transporter family.
                     GltP was found to have a reversible equilibrium of a 
                     trimeric and hexameric form in solution after purification 
                     |CITS:[17042498]|."
                     /translation="MKNIKFSLAWQILFAMVLGILLGSYLHYHSDSRDWLVVNLLSPAG
