     gene            4320319..4321821
                     /gene="proP"
                     /locus_tag="b4111"
                     /label=proP
     CDS             4320319..4321821
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="proP"
                     /locus_tag="b4111"
                     /product="trifunctional osmolyte:H(+) symporter ProP / 
                     L-proline:H(+) symporter / glycine betaine:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=proP
                     /note="ProP is an osmosensing transporter which mediates
                     the uptake of zwitterionic osmolytes such as proline and 
                     glycine betaine; ProP function protects cells from 
                     dehydration under conditions of hyperosmotic stress.
                     The <i>proP</i> locus was first defined in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     K-12 (strain RM2 &Delta;<i>putPA</i>) by mutations which 
                     eliminate an inducible proline uptake activity and increase
                     resistance to the toxic proline analaog, 3,4-dehydroproline
                     |CITS: [6355059]|. ProP mediated proline uptake is induced 
                     and stimulated by osmotic stress |CITS: [3514577]|; ProP 
                     has similar affinity for proline and glycine betaine |CITS:
                     [3312483]|. ProP catalyses active proline uptake in 
                     cytoplasmic membrane vesicles; ProP activity measured in 
                     whole cells is eliminated by the uncoupling agent CCCP and 
                     the respiration inhibitor KCN |CITS: [3049595]|. ProP 
                     functions as a proline:H<sup>+</sup> symporter |CITS: 
                     [10446288]|; purified ProP reconstituted in liposomes 
                     catalyses the active accumulation of proline, this activity
                     requires both membrane potential and an osmotic upshift and
                     maximum uptake occurs in the presence of a pH downshift 
                     |CITS: [10026245]|.
                     ProP also transports the osmoprotectants taurine, ectoine, 
                     carnitine, pipecolate, proline betaine, 
                     <i>N,N</i>-dimethylglycine, and 1-carboxymethylpyridinium 
                     |CITS: [1884995][1629159][7952193][9871325][10446288]|; 
                     ProP transports the L-proline analog, 5-hydroxy-L-pipecolic
                     acid |CITS: [3082857]| and the toxic proline analog 
                     3,4-dehydroproline |CITS: [6355059]|. ProP transports 
                     glycine betaine, dimethylsulfoniopropionate (DMSP) and 
                     dimethylsulfonioacetate (DMSA) but (unlke ProU) it does not
                     provide osmoprotection at very low concentrations of these 
                     substrates (1 nM to 1 &micro;M) |CITS: [10427011]|. 
                     Choline, alanine, trigonelline and sarcosine are all 
                     inhibitors of proline import but are not transport 
                     substrates |CITS: [10446288]|.
                     ProP activity is stimulated by exogenous K<sup>+</sup> 
                     |CITS: [10446288]| although this may represent a 
                     K<sup>+</sup> requirement for respiration rather than 
                     direct involvement in transport |CITS: [18620422]|. As a 
                     component of the pmf, membrane potential (&Delta;&Psi;) 
                     contributes to ProP catalysed symport; &Delta;&Psi; also 
                     controls osmosensing - ProP appears to progress from an off
                     to an on and then an osmolality-sensitive state as the 
                     membrane potential increases from 0 to -137mV |CITS: 
                     [18620422]|. Conformational change of ProP (as measured by 
                     the accessibility of introduced cysteine residues to 
                     alkylation) has been observed in response to osmolality 
                     |CITS: [19049385]|.
                     ProP consists of 11 or 12 predicted transmembrane regions 
                     and a cytoplasmic C-terminal domain which is required for 
                     activity |CITS: [15823022][10992293]|. Comparative 
                     modelling suggests that ProP functions via the alternating 
                     access model of transport |CITS: [15823022]|. ProP may 
                     exist in monomer-dimer equilibrium <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [16042394]|;  the C-terminal domain forms an 
                     intermolecular, antiparallel &alpha;-helical coiled-coil 
                     structure which is implicated in osmosensing |CITS: [10992 
                     293][14643666][16042394][16239220][17441691][30448037][3283
                     8524]|. Membrane phospholipid composition (more 
                     specifically the proportion of cardiolipin) is also 
                     implicated in the control of ProP function; ProP 
                     concentrates with cardiolipin at the cell poles |CITS: 
                     [16239220][17504273][18326496]| (and see reviews by |CITS: 
                     [17555431][19539601]|). The dimeric coiled-coil domain is 
                     implicated in cardiolipin dependent polar localization of 
                     ProP <i>in vivo</i>; mutations which affect coiled-coil 
                     structure also impact subcellular localization |CITS: 
                     [27802568]|.
                     BACTH (Bacterial Adenylate Cyclase Two-Hybrid System) 
                     assays indicate that ProP interacts with |FRAME: 
                     B1657-MONOMER YdhP|, |FRAME: EG11928-MONOMER YjbJ| and 
                     |FRAME: CARDIOLIPSYN-MONOMER ""cardiolipin synthase A""| 
                     (ClsA) |CITS: [29280215]|.
                     ProP is a member of the Metabolite:H<sup>+</sup> Symporter 
                     (MHS) family within the Major Facilitator Superfamily (MFS)
                     of transporters |CITS: [1419050][26546518]|. <i>proP</i> 
                     expression is responsive to both osmotic and stationary 
                     phase signals; <i>proP</i> is transcribed from two 
                     promoters - P1 which is subject to transient CRP-mediated 
                     derepression under osmotic upshift and P2 which is 
                     implicated in Fis mediated RpoS dependent stationary phase 
                     induction |CITS: [8002611][7545153][90799 
                     29][9237902][10610762][10894725][11866515][17302803]|. The 
                     |FRAME: EG12866-MONOMER| amplifies the osmolality response 
                     of ProP by an unknown mechanism |CITS: 
                     [2536686][10049386][15476391][21381725]|. |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-3281| cleaves a stem-loop in the 5' untranslated 
                     region of <i>proP</i> mRNA that is synthesized from the P1 
                     promoter; RNase III cleavage of <i>proP</i> mRNA is 
                     downregulated in hyperosmotic media |CITS: [25645556]|. 
                     Expression of <i>proP</i> from a multicopy plasmid supports
                     growth of K<sup>+</sup> transport deficient mutants on 
                     minimal medium containing 5 mM K<sup>+</sup>; uptake is 
                     linearly dependent on the external concentration of 
                     K<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [15205426]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [17555431][17047223][11913457][10066837]| "
                     /translation="MLKRKKVKPITLRDVTIIDDGKLRKAITAASLGNAMEWFDFGVYG
                     FVAYALGKVFFPGADPSVQMVAALATFSVPFLIRPLGGLFFGMLGDKYGRQKILAITIV
