     gene            complement(4321998..4323089)
                     /gene="basS"
                     /locus_tag="b4112"
                     /label=basS
     CDS             complement(4321998..4323089)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="basS"
                     /locus_tag="b4112"
                     /product="trifunctional sensor histidine kinase BasS / 
                     protein-histidine autokinase / protein phosphoaspartate 
                     phosphatase"
                     /EC_number="2.7.13.3"
                     /label=basS
                     /note="BasS (also known as PmrB) is the sensor histidine
                     kinase of the BasSR two-component system.
                     BasS is a predicted integral membrane protein with sequence
                     similarity to the sensor kinase family of proteins 
                     including a conserved histidine (His-152) which is presumed
                     to be the site of autophosphorylation; overproduced BasS 
                     autophosphorylates in the presence of ATP and transfers a 
                     phosphate to its cognate response regulator, BasR <i>in 
                     vitro</i>; it is also able to phosphorylate purified OmpR 
                     with low efficiency |CITS: [8282725]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [15522865][21811415]|). The purified cytoplasmic domain of 
                     BasS (Arg-89 &rarr; Ile-363) has phospho-BasR phosphatase 
                     activity <i>in vitro</i>; variation in the protein 
                     phosphatase activity of BasS (PmrB) between <i>E. coli</i> 
                     and <i>Salmonella enterica</i> determines the ability of 
                     |FRAME: G7172-MONOMER ""PmrD""| to activate the BasSR 
                     (PmrAB) system in the two species |CITS: [21811415]|.
                     BasSR is required for the Fe(III)-dependent induction of 
                     the <i>arnBCADTEF</i> operon; a &Delta;<i>basSR</i> strain 
                     exhibits mild acid-sensitivity when grown aerobically in 
                     medium containing a high (1.5mM) concentration of 
                     FeSO<sub>4</sub> |CITS: [15322361]|. <i>basS</i> is 
                     upregulated 2.4 fold by growth in 0.2mM ZnSO<sub>4</sub> 
                     and a &Delta;<i>basS</i> strain is hypersensitive to 
                     ZnSO<sub>4</sub> |CITS: [15659689]|.
                     BasS is homologous to the well characterized PmrB sensor 
                     kinase of <i>Salmonella enterica</i>. BasS contains a 
                     putative iron binding motif |CITS: [11051552]|.
                     A PmrB point mutation (L14R) is associated with acquisition
                     of a |FRAME: CPD-13158 colistin| resistance phenotype in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> ATCC 25922 |CITS: [34234474]|; mutations in 
                     <i>basS</i> and <i>basR</i> have also been found associated
                     with de novo colistin resistance in adaptive laboratory 
                     evolution (ALE) experiments with <i>E. coli</i> MG1655 
                     |CITS: [35680894]|.
                     <i>Note</i>: bacterial histidine kinases function as 
                     homodimers; both <i>cis</i>- and 
                     <i>trans</i>-autophosphorylation mechanisms have been 
                     documented (see |CITS: [19575571][25982528]|). In the 
                     absence of experimental evidence BasS is represented as a 
                     monomer in the EcoCyc database.
                     A consitutively active <i>basS</i> mutant allele resulting 
                     in a D312N change was identified as the previously reported
                     <i>dgkR-1</i> mutant allele |CITS: [3015952][21463370]|.
                     <i>basS</i>: <u>b</u>acterial <u>a</u>daptive 
                     re<u>s</u>ponse |CITS: [8282725]|"
