     gene            4331728..4333083
                     /gene="melA"
                     /locus_tag="b4119"
                     /label=melA
     CDS             4331728..4333083
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="melA"
                     /locus_tag="b4119"
                     /product="alpha-galactosidase"
                     /EC_number="3.2.1.22"
                     /label=melA
                     /note="&alpha;-galactosidase is required for utilization
                     of &alpha;-galactosides as nutrients |CITS: [2831880]|. 
                     &alpha;-galactosidase was previously thought to be a 
                     tetramer |CITS: [5543331]|, but is now believed to be a 
                     dimer |CITS: [2831880]|.  The enzymatic activity is very 
                     unstable upon purification, but can be stabilized by the 
                     addition of NAD<sup>+</sup> |CITS: [5543331]|.  The Aes 
                     protein can bind to and inhibit the activity of 
                     &alpha;-galactosidase |CITS: [12374803]|.
                     <i>melA</i> mutants are unable to utilize melibiose as the 
                     sole source of carbon |CITS: [5329906][4877127]|.  MelA 
                     activity can be induced by three &alpha;-D-galactosides: 
                     melibiose, melibiitol and galactinol |CITS: [4877127]|. 
                     Although <i>melA</i> and <i>melB</i> are co-transcribed, 
                     the expression of <i>melB</i> is much lower than that of 
                     <i>melA</i>.  This appears to be due to a NusA binding site
                     between the two ORFs, leading to transcription attenuation.
                      In addition, the <i>melA</i> mRNA is more stable than the 
                     <i>melAB</i> mRNA |CITS: [7982902]|."
