     gene            4333186..4334607
                     /gene="melB"
                     /locus_tag="b4120"
                     /label=melB
     CDS             4333186..4334607
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="melB"
                     /locus_tag="b4120"
                     /product="trifunctional melibionate:H+ symporter / 
                     melibiose:H(+)/Na(+)/Li(+) symporter / 
                     methyl-alpha-D-galactpyranoside transport"
                     /label=melB
                     /note="MelB is a melibiose-cation cotransport protein
                     belonging to the Glycoside-Pentoside-Hexuronide:Cation 
                     Symporter Family (GPH) |CITS: [8031825]|. Proteins in the 
                     GPH family facilitate sugar-cation symport |CITS: 
                     [2987195]|. MelB has been purified, solubilized and 
                     reconstituted into liposomes and demonstrated to mediate 
                     melibiose symport with sodium, lithium, or proton as the 
                     coupling ion |CITS: [6319412][2185831][3316227]|. The 
                     effects of sodium ion, lithium ion, and proton on the sugar
                     binding constants of MelB were studied in de-energized 
                     membrane vesicles incubated in media containing various 
                     concentrations of the co-transported cations |CITS: 
                     [3316227]|. It was observed that, in a similar fashion, 
                     sodium ions and lithium ions activate sugar binding by 
                     selectively increasing the permease affinity for the 
                     co-transported sugar. This suggests that the two monovalent
                     cations have comparable activation strengths |CITS: 
                     [3316227]|. Hydropathy analysis, proteolytic digestion, and
                     PhoA fusions suggest a 12 transmembrane segment topology, 
                     with the N and C termini located on the cytoplasmic face of
                     the membrane |CITS:[9814854][8672452][9214297]|.
                     The susbtrate specificity of MelB was studied in a lactose 
                     transport negative strain of <i>E. coli</i> possessing an 
                     inducible melibiose transport system. 
                     &alpha;-galactopyranosides (methyl 
                     &alpha;-D-galactopyranoside, raffinose and 
                     <i>p</i>-nitrophenyl-&alpha;-galactoside) are cotransported
                     with either H<sup>+</sup> or Na<sup>+</sup> while 
                     &beta;-galactopyranosides (lactulose, lactose, methyl 
                     &beta;-D-galactopyranoside and 
                     <i>o</i>-nitrophenyl-&beta;-D-galactoside) are 
                     cotransported with Na<sup>+</sup>. The monosaccharides 
                     D-fucose, L-arabinose and D-galactosamine are cotransported
                     with  Na<sup>+</sup> while D-galactose can be cotransported
                     with either H<sup>+</sup> or Na<sup>+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [3311166]|.
                     Two-dimensional crystallization of MelB permease was 
                     achieved, and a projection map at 8.0 &Aring; resolution 
                     was derived by electron crystallography, suggesting an 
                     asymmetric protein unit hosting 12 potential transmembrane 
                     alpha -helices that are distributed in two domains lining a
                     central and curve-shaped cleft |CITS:[12110569]|.
                     The melibiose transport system is temperature sensitive - 
                     active at 30<sup>o</sup>C but not at 37 <sup>o</sup>C 
                     |CITS: [14347955]|.
                     Reviewed in |CITS:  [1970646], [8099233], [11248194]|."
