     gene            complement(4335497..4337143)
                     /gene="fumB"
                     /locus_tag="b4122"
                     /label=fumB
     CDS             complement(4335497..4337143)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fumB"
                     /locus_tag="b4122"
                     /product="bifunctional fumarase B / D-tartrate
                     dehydratase"
                     /EC_number="4.2.1.2; 4.2.1.81"
                     /label=fumB
                     /note="Fumarase B (FumB) is one of three characterized
                     fumarase isozymes in <i>E. coli</i> (encoded by |FRAME: 
                     EG10356|, |FRAME: EG10357|, and |FRAME: EG10358|), all of 
                     which participate in the TCA cycle.  FumB belongs to the 
                     class I fumarases; like FumA, it is a homodimeric 4Fe-4S 
                     cluster-containing protein |CITS: [3282546][23405168]|. 
                     Fumarase B is required for anaerobic growth on D-tartrate 
                     |CITS: [17643228]| and appears to be involved in biofilm 
                     formation |CITS: [16385049]|.
                     The cell adapts to changing environmental oxygen conditions
                     by utilizing different isozymes.  Both FumA and FumB 
                     contain iron-sulfur centers; exposure to oxidative agents 
                     such as superoxide results in damage to the metal cofactor 
                     and loss of enzyme activity |CITS: [8226748]|. Although 
                     FumB was previously reported to have a higher affinity for 
                     malate than for fumarate |CITS: [3282546]|, recent kinetic 
                     data using a purified enzyme have shown it to be similar to
                     FumA |CITS: [23405168]|.  FumB is thought to function as an
                     alternative enzyme during anaerobiosis |CITS: 
                     [WOODS87][11133938]|.  In contrast, FumC is an 
                     iron-independent enzyme and is insensitive to oxidative 
                     damage |CITS: [8203917]|.  FumC is made by the cells 
                     primarily as a backup enzyme if the FumA or FumB enzymes 
                     are damaged by reactive oxygen species.  |CITS: 
                     [9418241][8460946][11133938][16187334]|
                     Expression of all three fumarase genes is anaerobically 
                     controlled.  Expression of <i>fumB</i> gene is four fold 
                     elevated under anaerobic cell growth conditions versus 
                     aerobic conditions |CITS: [9418241]|.  In contrast, 
                     <i>fumA</i> expression is highest only during aerobic 
                     growth and where <i>fumC</i> is only weakly expressed. When
                     cells encounter oxidative stress conditions, <i>fumC</i> 
                     gene expression is then significantly induced by the SoxRS 
                     regulatory system |CITS: [7592392]|. The aerobic/anaerobic 
                     transcriptional regulators ArcA and Fnr function to repress
                     <i>fumA</i> as well as <i>fumC</i> gene expression under 
                     anaerobic conditions.
                     Overexpression of <i>fumB</i> complements the <i>fumA1</i> 
                     mutation |CITS: [6296045][3005475]| and a <i>fumAC</i> 
                     deletion |CITS: [2834509]|.  The expression level of 
                     <i>fumB</i> is highest under anaerobic growth on glycerol 
                     and fumarate |CITS: [WOODS87]|.  <i>fumB</i> expression is 
                     regulated in response to oxygen, iron and heme availability
                     |CITS: [8000525][9418241]| and increases 9-fold upon 
                     deletion of <i>tqsA</i>, which increases biofilm formation 
                     |CITS: [16385049]|.
                     Fumarase B interacts specifically with the |FRAME: 
                     DCUB-MONOMER|.  The interaction has been proposed to form a
                     metabolon, where the transporter catalyzes the uptake of 
                     L-malate while FumB converts it to fumarate for fumarate 
                     respiration.  The transpoerter also removes succinate, the 
                     product of the respiration process |CITS: [36044995]|."
