     gene            complement(4337221..4338561)
                     /gene="dcuB"
                     /locus_tag="b4123"
                     /label=dcuB
     CDS             complement(4337221..4338561)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dcuB"
                     /locus_tag="b4123"
                     /product="multifunctional anaerobic C4-dicarboxylate
                     transporter DcuB / fumarate:succinate antiporter / 
                     malate:succinate antiporter / L-aspartate:succinate 
                     antiporter / fumarate:H(+) symporter / D-tartrate:succinate
                     antiporter / DcuS-DcuB sensor complex"
                     /label=dcuB
                     /note="DcuB is a C4-dicarboxylate transporter; DcuB
                     functions primarily as a fumarate:succinate antiporter 
                     during anaerobic fumarate respiration.
                     <i>dcuB</i> and <i>dcuA</i> encode predicted inner membrane
                     proteins with high sequence similarity; <i>E. coli 
                     dcuB</i>::Kan or <i>dcuA</i>::Spc single mutants do not 
                     have impaired growth under anaerobic fumarate, malate or 
                     aspartate respiration; the double mutants (<i>dcuB</i>::Kan
                     <i>dcuA</i>::Spc) have severely impaired growth under these
                     same conditions |CITS: [7961398]|. DcuB catalyses the 
                     export of succinate coupled to the uptake of fumarate, 
                     malate or aspartate |CITS: [7961398]|.
                     When grown under anaerobic conditions <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     displays transport activities for uptake, efflux and 
                     exchange of C4-dicarboxylates. Fumarate:succinate exchange 
                     is required for fumarate respiration while succinate efflux
                     (without exchange) occurs during glucose fermentation; net 
                     uptake of C4-dicarboxylates is observed experimentally when
                     no counter substrate is present. The exchange reaction is 
                     electroneutral while uptake and efflux are electrogenic 
                     symport reactions probably of the dicarboxylate with 3 
                     H<sup>+</sup> (|CITS: [1512189][8020497]| and see review by
                     |CITS: [11803016]|). <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains 3 
                     C4-dicarboxylate transporters (DcuA, DcuB and DcuC) that 
                     are active under anaerobic conditions; a strain lacking all
                     three (<i>dcuA</i>::Spc <i>dcuB</i>::Kan 
                     <i>dcuC</i>::mini-Tn10) is not able to grow anaerobically 
                     on fumarate plus glycerol; all three are active in both 
                     exchange and uptake; DcuB favours an exchange reaction over
                     uptake |CITS: [8955408]|.
                     DcuB is predicted to have 10 transmembrane helices with the
                     N- and C-terminal residues in the periplasm |CITS: 
                     [9733683]|.
                     Expression of <i>dcuB</i> is induced anaerobically by FNR 
                     and by C-4 dicarboxylates |CITS: [9852003]|. <i>dcuB</i> is
                     repressed by CRP-cAMP and by nitrate acting through the 
                     NarXL regulatory system |CITS: [9852003]|. Expression of 
                     <i>dcuB</i> is induced by C4-carboxylates through the 
                     |FRAME: PWY0-1468 ""DcuSR two-component system""| |CITS: 
                     [9765574][9973351][29203472]|. Deletion of <i>dcuB</i> 
                     results in constitutive expression of a <i>dcuB'-'lacZ</i> 
                     reporter gene fusion in the absence of fumarate. This 
                     activation is dependent on the presence of DcuS and 
                     suggests that DcuB has a regulatory function in addition to
                     a transport function. Specific point mutations have been 
                     identified which differentiate between these two functions 
                     |CITS: [18957436]|. DcuB interacts physically with DcuS 
                     <i>in vivo</i>; the complex is present in both the absence 
                     and presence of fumarate; the DcuB-DcuS complex is 
                     responsible for the concentration dependent sensing of 
                     C4-dicarboxylates under anaerobic conditions |CITS: 
                     [27318186]| (and further |CITS: [29203472]|).
                     DcuB interacts specifically with |FRAME: FUMARASE-B FumB| 
                     and with |FRAME: ASPARTASE-CPLX AspA|; these interactions 
                     may represent the formation of 'metabolons' for the 
                     coordination of reactions in fumarate respiration |CITS: 
                     [36044995]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [27134023][27415771]|
                     "
