     gene            4342401..4342697
                     /gene="ghoS"
                     /locus_tag="b4128"
                     /label=ghoS
     CDS             4342401..4342697
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ghoS"
                     /locus_tag="b4128"
                     /product="bifunctional antitoxin of the GhoTS
                     toxin-antitoxin system / sequence-specific endoribonuclease
                     GhoS"
                     /label=ghoS
                     /note="GhoS is the antitoxin of a novel type V
                     toxin-antitoxin system.  GhoS is a sequence-specific 
                     endoribonuclease that specifically cleaves sites within the
                     <i>ghoT</i> coding region of the <i>ghoST</i> transcript, 
                     thereby limiting translation of the GhoT toxin |CITS: 
                     [22941047]|.
                     <i>ghoT</i> is one of a small set of coding regions that do
                     not contain cleavage sites for the |FRAME: G7572-MONOMER 
                     MqsR| toxin, whereas the <i>ghoS</i> coding region contains
                     three consensus MqsR cleavage sites.
                     Thus, in the presence of MqsR, the <i>ghoS</i>-encoding 5' 
                     region of the <i>ghoST</i> transcript is degraded, thereby 
                     enriching for the <i>ghoT</i>-encoding 3' region |CITS: 
                     [23289863]|.
                     A solution structure of GhoS has been determined and showed
                     structural similarity to sequence-specific 
                     endoribonucleases that preferentially cleave 
                     single-stranded RNA |CITS: [22941047]|.
                     Phenotype microarray experiments using scarless 
                     &Delta;<i>ghoST</i> and <i>ghoT</i> mutants showed 
                     differential response to a set of antimicrobial compounds 
                     |CITS: [24373067]|.
                     GhoS: ""<b>gho</b>st cells"" |CITS: [22941047]|
                     Comment: |CITS: [22987009]|"
                     /translation="MEGKNKFNTYVVSFDYPSSYSSVFLRLRSLMYDMNFSSIVADEYG
                     IPRQLNENSFAITTSLAASEIEDLIRLKCLDLPDIDFDLNIMTVDDYFRQFYK"
                     /go_function="nuclease activity [goid 0004518] [evidence
                     IEA] [GO_REF 0000043]"
                     /go_function="hydrolase activity [goid 0016787] [evidence
                     IEA] [GO_REF 0000043]"
                     /go_function="endonuclease activity [goid 0004519]
                     [evidence IEA] [GO_REF 0000043]"
                     /go_function="RNA endonuclease activity [goid 0004521]
                     [evidence IEA] [GO_REF 0000002]"
                     /go_function="RNA endonuclease activity [goid 0004521]
                     [evidence IDA] [PMID 22941047]"
     gene            4342725..4342898
                     /gene="ghoT"
                     /locus_tag="b4559"
                     /label=ghoT
     CDS             4342725..4342898
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ghoT"
                     /locus_tag="b4559"
                     /product="toxin of the GhoTS toxin-antitoxin system"
                     /label=ghoT
                     /note="GhoT is the toxin of a novel type V toxin-antitoxin 
                     system.  GhoT is a peptide that damages the membrane, 
                     reduces cellular ATP levels and disrupts membrane 
                     potential, causing ghost cell formation and increasing 
                     persistence |CITS: [22941047][24373067]|.  The GhoS 
                     antitoxin acts by specifically cleaving <i>ghoT</i> mRNA 
                     |CITS: [22941047]|.
                     <i>ghoT</i> is one of a small set of coding regions that do
                     not contain cleavage sites for the |FRAME: G7572-MONOMER 
                     MqsR| toxin, whereas the <i>ghoS</i> coding region contains
                     three consensus MqsR cleavage sites.
                     Thus, in the presence of MqsR, the <i>ghoS</i>-encoding 5' 
                     region of the <i>ghoST</i> transcript is degraded, thereby 
                     enriching for the <i>ghoT</i>-encoding 3' region |CITS: 
                     [23289863]|.
                     A <i>ghoT</i> deletion mutant shows decreased MqsR-mediated
                     persister cell formation |CITS: [22941047]|. An F38R mutant
                     maintains polar localization, but is no longer toxic |CITS:
                     [24373067]|.
                     Phenotype microarray experiments using scarless 
                     &Delta;<i>ghoST</i> and <i>ghoT</i> mutants showed 
                     differential response to a set of antimicrobial compounds 
                     |CITS: [24373067]|.
                     GhoT: ""toxin producing <b>gho</b>s<b>t</b> cells"" |CITS: 
                     [22941047]|
                     Comment: |CITS: [22987009]|"
                     /translation="MALFSKILIFYVIGVNISFVIIWFISHEKTHIRLLSAFLVGITWP
                     MSLPVALLFSLF"
                     /go_component="cell pole [goid 0060187] [evidence IDA]
                     [PMID 24373067]"
                     /go_component="plasma membrane [goid 0005886] [evidence
                     IEA] [GO_REF 0000044]"
                     /go_component="plasma membrane [goid 0005886] [evidence
                     IEA] [PMID 19054114]"
                     /go_component="membrane [goid 0016020] [evidence IDA]
                     [PMID 24373067]"
     gene            complement(4343017..4344534)
                     /gene="lysU"
                     /locus_tag="b4129"
                     /label=lysU
     CDS             complement(4343017..4344534)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lysU"
                     /locus_tag="b4129"
                     /product="multifunctional lysyl-tRNA synthetase /
                     L-homoserine cyclase / L-homocysteine cyclase / L-ornithine
                     cyclase / lysine--tRNA ligase / Ap4A synthetase / Ap3A 
                     synthetase / Ap4 cap addition"
                     /EC_number="6.1.1.6"
                     /label=lysU
                     /note="The lysine-tRNA ligase LysU is a member of the
                     family of aminoacyl tRNA synthetases, which interpret the 
                     genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their 
                     specific tRNA molecules.  The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis.  LysU belongs to the subclass IIB of 
                     aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases |CITS: 
                     [2203971][1852601][23075299]|.  <i>E. coli</i> contains 
                     both a constitutive (|FRAME: LYSS-MONOMER LysS|) and an 
                     inducible lysyl-tRNA synthetase; <i>lysU</i> encodes the 
                     inducible, more thermostable enzyme |CITS: 
                     [7012120][6337114][2183178][7782306]|.  LysU may be more 
                     error-prone than LysS |CITS: [25918376]|.
                     LysU catalyzes misacylation of tRNA<sup>Lys</sup> with 
                     arginine, threonine, methionine, leucine, alanine, serine, 
                     and cysteine, although even the most efficient noncognate 
                     substrate, arginine, is used with 1600-fold lower catalytic
                     efficiency than the cognate amino acid lysine |CITS: 
                     [10387054]|.  The enzyme does not appear to possess a 
                     post-transfer editing mechanism, but it does possess an 
                     efficient pre-transfer editing function, discriminating 
                     against ornithine, homocysteine, and homoserine by 
                     cyclization of the respective adenylates, forming lactams 
                     |CITS: [9287150][10387054]|.
                     Residues within LysU that are important for N73 
                     discrimiation of tRNA<sup>Lys</sup> were analyzed by 
                     site-directed mutagenesis, showing that the loop of motif 2
                     within the C-terminal catalytic domain as well as 
                     additional residues are involved |CITS: [10213598]|.
                     LysU catalyzes the formation of diadenosine tetra- and 
                     triphosphate (|FRAME: ADENOSYL-P4 Ap<sub>4</sub>A|, |FRAME:
                     ADENOSINE5TRIPHOSPHO5ADENOSINE Ap<sub>3</sub>A|), which are
                     most likely damage metabolites |CITS: [28516732]|, both 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [6756470][3325036][2554306]|.  Both the tRNA charging 
                     reaction and the synthesis of Ap<sub>4</sub>A and 
                     Ap<sub>3</sub>A share a common lysyl-adenylate 
                     intermediate; the subsequent formation of Ap<sub>4</sub>A 
                     in the presence of Zn<sup>2+</sup> and ATP is reversible 
                     and allows the later appearance of Ap<sub>3</sub>A in the 
                     presence of Zn<sup>2+</sup> and ADP |CITS: [16884494]|. 
                     Mutations in E264, R269, and other amino acids in the motif
                     2 loop were generated by alanine scanning and site directed
                     mutagenesis, and their effects on the catalytic activity of
                     LysU were analyzed |CITS: [23121660]|.  LysU is able to add
                     an Ap<sub>4</sub> cap to the <i>yeiP</i> mRNA <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [31178354]|.
                     LysU is able to accept |FRAME: CO-A| as an alternative 
                     substrate to tRNA <i>in vitro</i>, aminoacylating it with 
                     lysine, leucine, threonine, alanine, valine, and isoleucine
                     |CITS: [10995737]|.  LysU can adenylate |FRAME: 
                     EG12172-MONOMER HinT| in the presence of Mg<sup>2+</sup> 
                     and lysine, indicating a dependence on the formation of the
                     lysyl-AMP intermediate; the reaction is inhibited by 
                     Zn<sup>2+</sup>, Ap<sub>4</sub>A and Ap<sub>3</sub>A and 
                     enhanced by the presence of pyrophosphatase |CITS: 
                     [17158446]|.
                     A phosphorylated form of LysU containing a single 
                     phosphothreonine residue can be isolated; it does not show 
                     significant differences in catalytic activity, but may be 
                     more stable than the nonphosphorylated form |CITS: 
                     [16884494]|. Using a method that distinguishes 
                     N-phosphorylation from O-phosphorylation, N-phosphorylation
                     of a His residue was detected <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [33277485]|.
                     The ATP binding site of LysU was investigated by affinity 
                     labeling |CITS: [7852265][7852266]|.  Crystal structures of
                     the homodimeric enzyme in a variety of active site states 
                     have been solved, and a reaction mechanism was deduced 
                     |CITS: [7735833][10913247]|.  Biochemical and computational
                     molecular dynamics studies of the reaction mechanism have 
                     been performed |CITS: [12787471][16317719]|.
                     LysU may |CITS: [2188953]| or may not |CITS: 
                     [2001999][1744045]| be required for normal growth at 
                     elevated temperature.  Evolved highly thermoresistant cells
                     were reported to be dependent on the presence of LysU 
                     |CITS: [20406805]|, but later work has shown that the 
                     thermotolerant phenotype of those cells was due to the 
                     presence of a  GroESL plasmid, and was not related to the 
                     presence of <i>lysU</i> |CITS: [36973666]|.  <i>lysU</i> 
                     expression is induced by elevated temperature, 
                     anaerobiosis, and low pH |CITS: 
                     [3325036][3280549][2187401][1744045][1514820]|. 
                     Overexpression of <i>lysU</i> in the presence of excess 
                     serine leads to a growth defect in the absence of |FRAME: 
                     G6973-MONOMER| |CITS: [25918376]|.  Increased 
                     Ap<sub>4</sub>A levels in a LysU-overexpressing 
                     &Delta;<i>apaH</i> mutant increase the cells' sensitivity 
                     to aminoglycoside antibiotics |CITS: [31004054]|.
                     LysU is negatively regulated by |FRAME: PD00288| |CITS: 
                     [7961513]| and is a member of the Lrp (leucine-responsive 
                     regulatory protein) regulon |CITS: 
                     [1346534][1555652][1569010][7678344]|.  Cooperative binding
                     of Lrp to the <i>lysU</i> promoter region represses 
                     transcription |CITS: [8064854]|.  Overexpression of 
                     <i>lysU</i> suppresses the low temperature growth defect of
                     a <i>lysS</i> null mutant |CITS: [1321323]|. ArcA appears 
                     to activate <i>lysU</i> gene expression under anaerobiosis.
                      A putative ArcA binding site was identified 201 bp 
                     upstream of this gene |CITS: [15699038]|; but it is not 
                     known which of the two promoters transcribing the gene is 
                     affected.
                     There is a general discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [37224536]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [7934813][7576245][23075299]|"
