     gene            complement(4348514..4349848)
                     /gene="cadB"
                     /locus_tag="b4132"
                     /label=cadB
     CDS             complement(4348514..4349848)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cadB"
                     /locus_tag="b4132"
                     /product="bifunctional lysine:cadaverine antiporter / 
                     cadaverine:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=cadB
                     /note="CadB, a lysine:cadaverine antiporter and CadA, a
                     lysine decarboxylase constitute a lysine-dependent acid 
                     resistance system (LDAR) which functions to protect the 
                     cell from mild acid stress; the LDAR system is one of a 
                     number of amino acid dependent AR mechanisms in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> (reviewed in |CITS: [23701194]|). CadA catalyzes 
                     the decarboxylation of lysine to cadaverine and CO2 (a 
                     process that counteracts acid stress through consumption of
                     cytosolic protons) while CadB exchanges the internal 
                     product, cadaverine for the external substrate, L-lysine. 
                     Cadaverine accumulates in the external medium when cells 
                     are grown anaerobically at pH 5.5 in the presence of 
                     L-lysine; this accumulation is negligible in a strain 
                     lacking both CadA and CadB and significantly reduced in a 
                     strain lacking CadB |CITS: [1556085]|. Under acidic 
                     conditions in the presence of lysine, CadB functions as a 
                     lysine:cadaverine antiporter; induction of the <i>cadBA</i>
                     operon under these conditions promotes decarboxylation of 
                     lysine to cadaverine by CadA, plus the CadB facilitated 
                     excretion of cadaverine and uptake of L-lysine; CadB 
                     antiport function generates a membrane potential (interior 
                     negative) |CITS: [14982633]|. At neutral pH CadB catalyses 
                     the uptake of cadaverine, likely via a proton symport 
                     mechanism; cadaverine uptake by CadB results in some 
                     stimulation of growth in a polyamine-deficient strain 
                     however expression of the <i>cadBA</i> operon is low at 
                     neutral pH and thus the physiological relevance of uptake 
                     activity is negligible |CITS: [14982633]|.
                     The LDAR system is implicated in the lysine mediated 
                     protection afforded to cells under anaerobic 
                     P<sub><i>i</i></sub> limitation - conditions which mimic 
                     those occurring in the human gut; the LDAR system helps 
                     cells tolerate the effect of weak acids produced under 
                     these conditions |CITS:[17209032]|.
                     CadB mediates the promiscuous import of muropeptides, 
                     specifically freed peptidoglycan peptides; the 
                     physiological relevance of this activity is not clear 
                     |CITS: [38169298]|.
                     CadB is a member of the Basic Amino Acid/Polyamine 
                     Antiporter (APA) Family within the Amino 
                     Acid-Polyamine-Organocation (APC) superfamily of 
                     transporters |CITS: [24225317]|. A model of CadB has been 
                     developed, based in part on the crystal structure of the 
                     agmatine-arginine antiporter AdiC |CITS: [21796432]|.
                     The <i>cadBA</i> operon is is positively regulated by 
                     |FRAME: PD00436 ""CadC""|; expression is induced 
                     anaerobically under acidic conditions and in the presence 
                     of exogenous L-lysine |CITS: 
                     [8195083][8808945][1370290][18086202]|."
