     gene            complement(4355289..4356590)
                     /gene="dcuA"
                     /locus_tag="b4138"
                     /label=dcuA
     CDS             complement(4355289..4356590)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="dcuA"
                     /locus_tag="b4138"
                     /product="multifunctional C4-dicarboxylate transporter
                     DcuA / L-aspartate:fumarate antiport / succinate:fumarate 
                     antiport / succinate:malate antiport / 
                     succinate:L-aspartate antiport / fumarate:H(+) symport"
                     /label=dcuA
                     /note="DcuA is a C4-dicarboxylate transporter which is
                     required for the uptake of L-aspartate as a nitrogen source
                     under aerobic conditions; DcuA mediates an 
                     L-aspartate:fumarate antiport reaction which results in the
                     net uptake of nitrogen without carbon uptake; DcuA is the 
                     major transporter for L-aspartate uptake under aerobic 
                     conditions |CITS: [29995997]| (see further |CITS: 
                     [33012071]|).
                     <i>dcuA</i> and <i>dcuB</i> encode predicted inner membrane
                     proteins with high sequence similarity; <i>E. coli 
                     dcuB</i>::Kan or <i>dcuA</i>::Spc single mutants do not 
                     have impaired growth under anaerobic fumarate, malate or 
                     aspartate respiration; the double mutants (<i>dcuB</i>::Kan
                     <i>dcuA</i>::Spc) have severely impaired growth under these
                     same conditions |CITS: [7961398]|. DcuA catalyses the 
                     export of succinate coupled to the uptake of fumarate, 
                     malate or aspartate |CITS: [7961398]|.
                     When grown under anaerobic conditions <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     displays transport activities for uptake, efflux and 
                     exchange of C4-dicarboxylates. Fumarate:succinate exchange 
                     is required for fumarate respiration while succinate efflux
                     (without exchange) occurs during glucose fermentation; net 
                     uptake of C4-dicarboxylates is observed experimentally when
                     no counter substrate is present. The exchange reaction is 
                     electroneutral while uptake and efflux are electrogenic 
                     symport reactions probably of the dicarboxylate with 3 
                     H<sup>+</sup> (|CITS: [1512189][8020497]| and see review by
                     |CITS: [11803016]|). <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains 3 
                     C4-dicarboxylate transporters (DcuA, DcuB and DcuC) that 
                     are active under anaerobic conditions; a strain lacking all
                     three (<i>dcuA</i>::Spc <i>dcuB</i>::Kan 
                     <i>dcuC</i>::mini-Tn10) is not able to grow anaerobically 
                     on fumarate plus glycerol; all three are active in both 
                     exchange and uptake; DcuA favours an exchange reaction over
                     uptake |CITS: [8955408]|.
                     DcuA is thought to contain 10 transmembrane regions with 
                     the N- and C-termini located in the periplasm; an 80 
                     residue cytoplasmic loop is located between TM 5 and 6 
                     |CITS: [9733683]|. DcuA interacts with |FRAME: 
                     ASPARTASE-CPLX AspA| and this interaction may represent the
                     formation of a metabolon for coordinating reactions in 
                     fumarate respiration |CITS: [36044995]|.
                     <i>dcuA</i> is constitutively expressed under both aerobic 
                     and anaerobic conditions and may function in 
                     C4-dicarboxylate transport during the microaerobic to 
                     anaerobic transition |CITS: [9852003]|.
                     Related reviews: |CITS: [27415771]|
                     "
