     CDS             complement(4364446..4365474)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="epmB"
                     /locus_tag="b4146"
                     /product="lysine 2,3-aminomutase"
                     /label=epmB
                     /note="Co-expression of EpmB enhances the lysylation of
                     EF-P by EpmA <i>in vivo</i>.  EpmB may catalyze the 
                     conversion of &alpha;-lysyl-EF-P to &beta;-lysyl-EF-P 
                     |CITS: [20729861]|, but preliminary evidence indicates that
                     it acts before EpmA |CITS: [22128152]|.
                     The <i>epmB</i> gene product is similar to the lysine 
                     2,3-aminomutase of <i>Clostridium subterminale</i>, 
                     although phylogenetic clustering has shown that EpmB 
                     belongs to a different subfamily than the lysine 
                     2,3-aminomutases |CITS: [20070887]|.  The purified protein 
                     has low lysine 2,3-aminomutase activity and produces 
                     (R)-&beta;-lysine, the enantiomer of the product of the 
                     <i>Clostridium</i> enzyme.  The low activity of the <i>E. 
                     coli</i> enzyme compared to the clostridial enzyme may 
                     indicate that L-lysine is not its natural substrate, or 
                     that &beta;-lysine is required in low amounts |CITS: 
                     [17042480]|.  EpmB is a member of the ""radical SAM"" 
                     family of enzymes, and the lysyl-free radical intermediate 
                     of the reaction has been characterized |CITS: [17042480]|. 
                     Physical clustering and phylogenetic analysis have 
                     implicated EpmB in a pathway for the modification of a 
                     lysine residue in |FRAME: EG12099-MONOMER| |CITS: 
                     [20070887]|.
                     EpmB: ""<b>E</b>F-P <b>p</b>ost-translational 
                     <b>m</b>odification <b>B</b>"" |CITS: [22706199]|"
