     gene            4365516..4366082
                     /gene="efp"
                     /locus_tag="b4147"
                     /label=efp
     CDS             4365516..4366082
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="efp"
                     /locus_tag="b4147"
                     /product="protein chain elongation factor EF-P"
                     /label=efp
                     /note="EF-P is an elongation factor that stimulates the 
                     synthesis of peptide bonds |CITS: 
                     [1105576][383483][3881259][3884000][3549294][10987361]|. 
                     EF-P specifically functions to enhance translation of 
                     polyproline-containing proteins by alleviating the stalling
                     of ribosomes at polyproline stretches |CITS: 
                     [23239623][23239624]| and for a specific subset of proteins
                     that contain diprolyl motifs (XPP/PPX) |CITS: [24003132]|. 
                     The presence of EF-P only affects the production of a 
                     polyproline-containing protein if the translation 
                     elongation rate is more affected than translation 
                     initiation |CITS: [25148683]|. A paralog of EF-P, |FRAME: 
                     EG12035-MONOMER|, can also resolve this type of polyproline
                     stalling |CITS: [39622818]|.
                     EF-P is also involved in maintaining the reading frame 
                     during the translation of slippery sequences |CITS: 
                     [26009254]| and is a factor in transcription-translation 
                     coupling, preventing premature termination of transcription
                     by ensuring efficient synthesis of polyproline-containing 
                     proteins |CITS: [27624127]|.
                     The EF-P binding site on the ribosome overlaps the 
                     peptidyltransferase center |CITS: [9195040]|; interactions 
                     between EF-P and 70S ribosomes have been mapped |CITS: 
                     [18201202]|. It was previously thought that EF-P might 
                     facilitate proper positioning of fMet-tRNA<sup>fMet</sup> 
                     for initial peptide bond formation |CITS: 
                     [18201202][19696344]|, but |CITS: [23277358]| showed no 
                     involvement of EF-P in forming the initiation complex. The 
                     D-stem-loop structure of tRNA<sup>Pro</sup> was shown to be
                     a recognition determinant for EF-P, helping to speed up the
                     inherently slow Pro-Pro bond formation |CITS: [27216360]|. 
                     Enhanced incorporation of consecutive non-proteinogenic 
                     D-amino acids by EF-P also occurred with tRNAs engineered 
                     in the D-arm motif |CITS: [29155943]|. Incorporation of 
                     diverse non-canonical amino acids have been further 
                     explored with a |FRAME: DIHYDROFOLATEREDUCT-MONOMER| model 
                     system |CITS: [37871253]|.
                     A screen of <i>Salmonella enterica</i> proteins whose 
                     translation is affected by EF-P led to the identification 
                     and verification of additional protein sequence motifs 
                     present in the affected proteins |CITS: [23611909]|. 
                     However, EF-P does not affect ribosomal stalling at other 
                     short peptide motifs that were identified in a screen in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [23431150]|. In certain 
                     polyproline-containing proteins, translation does not stall
                     and thus does not depend on EF-P, revealing that certain 
                     amino acids upstream of the PPP |CITS: [25143529]| and PPX 
                     |CITS: [25144653]| motifs influence ribosomal pausing. 
                     Ribosome pausing at proline-rich motifs in the absence of 
                     EF-P was characterized by ribosome profiling |CITS: 
                     [25843707]|. After the peptidyl-tRNA in the P-site 
                     'drops-off' releasing a truncated peptide due to 
                     mistranslocation of consecutive |FRAME: PRO| amino acids by
                     |FRAME: EG10360-MONOMER|, EF-P can reinitiate translation 
                     by promoting peptidyl transfer with Pro-tRNA at the A-site 
                     |CITS: [35188576]|. Together, EF-P and an SKIK peptide tag 
                     introduced into a desired protein can enhance translation 
                     and increase protein production <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [39573840]|.
                     The Lys34 residue of EF-P is modified; the modification was
                     initially thought to be a spermidine residue |CITS: 
                     [18201202]|, but was later predicted to be a &beta;-lysine 
                     residue |CITS: [20070887]|. Further experiments showed that
                     Lys34 is modified with a lysyl residue, which requires both
                     |FRAME: CPLX0-7889 ""EpmA""| and |FRAME: G7836-MONOMER 
                     ""EpmB""|. The &beta;-lysyl-modified form is the active 
                     form <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [20729861][21841797][22128152][23239623][23239624]|. A K34A
                     mutant of <i>efp</i> is not lysylated, showing that K34 is 
                     the only lysylation site in EF-P. The site was also 
                     identified by mass spectrometry |CITS: [22128152]|. |FRAME:
                     G7201-MONOMER ""EpmC""| was subsequently shown to catalyze 
                     the hydroxylation of the &gamma; (C4) or &delta; (C5) 
                     position of Lys34 in EF-P |CITS: [22706199]|. The 
                     5-hydroxyllysine form of EF-P appears to be present <i>in 
                     vivo</i> |CITS: [22706199]|, although hydroxylation does 
                     not alter EF-P activity |CITS: [23239624][23277358]|.
                     EF-P may form a complex in solution; its molecular weight 
                     as measured on a Sephadex column is 50 kD |CITS: 
                     [1105576]|. The activity of EF-P is dependent on 
                     Mg<sup>2+</sup> ions |CITS: [1105576]|. EF-P is 
                     phosphorylated in bacteriophage T7-infected cells |CITS: 
                     [8022276]|. A cryo-EM structure of polyproline-induced 
                     stalled ribosomes has been obtained with and without 
                     modified EF-P |CITS: [29100052]|.
                     EF-P has been reported to be essential for viability by 
                     |CITS: [9405429]|, but deletion |CITS: [23591475]| and 
                     transposon insertion |CITS: [16738554]| mutants were later 
                     constructed and shown to have a slow growth phenotype 
                     |CITS: [16738554][20729861][23591475]|. |FRAME: 
                     VALS-MONOMER| contains a conserved proline triplet that is 
                     critical for its activity and requires EF-P for expression,
                     which may explain evolution of the EF-P system |CITS: 
                     [25310979]|. The combination of <i>rplI</i> and <i>efp</i> 
                     mutations causes a nearly synthetic lethal phenotype |CITS:
                     [25879934]|. Mutations in <i>efp</i> were found to suppress
                     the lethality of a <i>rep uvrD</i> double mutant |CITS: 
                     [27956500]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> contains approximately one molecule of EF-P 
                     per 10 ribosomes |CITS: [7011506]|. The EF-P-stimulated 
                     synthesis of peptide bonds is one of several targets of the
                     oxazolidinone class of antibiotics |CITS: [11897593]|. 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [23288527][26416626][28886684]|"
