                     [GO_REF 0000002]"
                     /go_component="membrane [goid 0016020] [evidence IDA]
                     [PMID 9677290]"
                     /go_process="response to toxic substance [goid 0009636]
                     [evidence IEA] [GO_REF 0000002]"
     gene            4366370..4366516
                     /gene="ecnB"
                     /locus_tag="b4411"
                     /label=ecnB
     CDS             4366370..4366516
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ecnB"
                     /locus_tag="b4411"
                     /product="bacteriolytic entericidin B lipoprotein"
                     /label=ecnB
                     /note="<i>ecnAB</i> encodes an antidote/toxin system which
                     may function as a starvation adaptation that supports 
                     further cell growth at the expense of dying subpopulations;
                     the functions of EcnAB are consistent with a programmed 
                     cell death system |CITS: [9677290]|. <i>ecnB</i> encodes a 
                     bacteriolytic lipoprotein that is expressed in stationary 
                     phase under high osmolarity conditions from an |FRAME: 
                     RPOS-MONOMER ""RpoS""|-dependent promoter and repressed by 
                     the |FRAME: PWY0-1500 ""OmpR/EnvZ two component regulatory 
                     system""| |CITS: [9677290]|
                     <i>ecnB</i> transcript can be detected in wild type cells 
                     grown in high salt conditions (300mM NaCl) but is absent in
                     an <i>rpoS</i> transposon insertion mutant; <i>ecnB</i> 
                     transcript can be detected in <i>envZ</i> and <i>ompR 
                     envZ</i> transposon insertion mutants regardless of 
                     osmolarity |CITS: [9677290]|.
                     The |FRAME: MONOMER0-1562 ""entericidin A lipoprotein""| 
                     (EcnA) functions as an antidote to entericidin B 
                     |CITS:[9677290]|.
                     The EcnB lipoprotein is predicted to localise to the outer 
                     membrane; EcnB may adopt an amphipathic &alpha;-helical 
                     conformation |CITS: [9677290]|.
                     The entericidin locus is non-essential in <i>E. coli</i> 
                     K-12 |CITS: [9677290]|.
                     "
