     gene            complement(4367628..4368761)
                     /gene="ampC"
                     /locus_tag="b4150"
                     /label=ampC
     CDS             complement(4367628..4368761)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ampC"
                     /locus_tag="b4150"
                     /product="beta-lactamase"
                     /EC_number="3.5.2.6"
                     /label=ampC
                     /note="The <I>ampC</I> &beta;-lactamase gene encodes a
                     serine cephalosporinase |CITS:[6795623]|. AmpC was shown to
                     be active against penicillin G with a Km of 12 &mu;M, 
                     D-ampicillin with a Km of 6 &mu;M, and cephalosporin C with
                     a Km of 217 &mu;M |CITS:[4983650]|. AmpC has been shown to 
                     bind <sup>125</sup>I-penicillin-X and aztreonam 
                     |CITS:[9324260]|. AmpC is also believed to have an 
                     additional function as a peptidoglycan hydrolyzing enzyme 
                     |CITS:[8288112]|.
                     The <I>ampA1</I> mutation, located in the promoter or 
                     operator region for <I>ampC</I>, causes increased 
                     expression of the <I>ampC</I> gene resulting in increased 
                     &beta;-lactam resistance 
                     |CITS:[16562043],[334718],[6251026]|.
                     <I>ampC</I> mutants exhibit aberrant cell morphology in a 
                     <I>mrcA dacA</I> background |CITS:[9324260]|. 
                     Overexpression of <I>bolA</I> increases <I>ampC</I> 
                     expression in addition to <I>dacA</I> and <I>dacC</I> 
                     expression |CITS:[12354237]|.
                     The acquisition of de novo amoxicillin resistance is 
                     associated with a variety of genomic alterations including 
                     amplification of <i>ampC</i> |CITS: [30591014]| (see 
                     further |CITS: [40698818]|). Inducing de novo amoxicillin 
                     resistance is associated with production of an 
                     <i>ampC</i>-containing transposon |CITS: [34502136]|. 
                     During the <i>de novo</i> evolution of amoxicillin 
                     resistance, <i>ampC</i> promoter mutations occur earlier in
                     the time-course than <i>ampC</i> amplification |CITS: 
                     [39704543]|. In &Delta;|FRAME: EG10509|, &Delta;|FRAME: 
                     G6115| and wild-type <i>E. coli</i> exposed to sub-lethal 
                     concentrations of amoxicillin, multiple and consistent 
                     mutations were found in <i>|FRAME: EG10040|</i>, its direct
                     upstream gene <i>|FRAME: EG10333|</i> and in |FRAME: 
                     EG10896|, pointing to their roles in amoxicillin resistance
                     |CITS: [39970152]|.
                     Review: |CITS:[19136439]|."
