     gene            complement(4379209..4380177)
                     /gene="psd"
                     /locus_tag="b4160"
                     /label=psd
     CDS             complement(4379209..4380177)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="psd"
                     /locus_tag="b4160"
                     /product="phosphatidylserine decarboxylase proenzyme"
                     /EC_number="4.1.1.65"
                     /label=psd
                     /note="Phosphatidylserine decarboxylase catalyzes the
                     formation of phosphatidylethanolamine, the most abundant 
                     phospholipid in <i>E. coli</i> membranes. 
                     Phosphatidylserine decarboxylase is one of a small class of
                     enzymes that use a covalently bound pyruvoyl prosthetic 
                     group.  The pyruvoyl group is thought to act analogously to
                     pyridoxal phosphate cofactor by forming a Schiff base 
                     between its carbonyl residue and the amino group of the 
                     substrate and then serving as an electron sink to 
                     facilitate the decarboxylation.  The pyruvoyl group is 
                     essential for catalytic activity and can be inactivated by 
                     carbonyl reagents |CITS: [338609]|.  <i>E. coli</i> encodes
                     two more members of this class of enzymes, |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-2901| and |FRAME: SAMDECARB-CPLX|. 
                     Pyruvoyl-containing enzymes are expressed as proenzymes, 
                     so-called ""zymogens"", which undergo a post-translational 
                     self-maturation/cleavage called serinolysis |CITS: 
                     [3042771]|.  In phosphatidylserine decarboxylase, the 
                     hydroxyl group of the Ser254 residue is essential for the 
                     cleavage of the peptide bond between Gly253 and Ser254, and
                     for subsequent conversion of Ser254 into the pyruvoyl 
                     prosthetic group at the amino terminus of the &alpha; 
                     subunit |CITS: [3042771][2406271][33707636]|.  Asp90, 
                     Asp142 and His144 also play a role in the auto-cleavage 
                     reaction |CITS: [33707636]|.
                     Crystal structures of the apo- and 
                     phosphatidylethanolamine-bound C-terminally truncated 
                     enzyme have been solved |CITS: [32402247][33707636]|.  Psd 
                     froms a homodimer from two &alpha;/&beta; monomers.  A 
                     hydrophobic N-terminal helical region within the &beta; 
                     chain forms a positively charged pocket surrounding the 
                     active site |CITS: [32402247]|.  Higher-resolution 
                     structures allowed the identification of interaction sites 
                     with the diacyl chains of the phospholipid molecule |CITS: 
                     [33707636]|.  Site-directed mutagenesis of conserved 
                     residues within the active site pocket showed that Tyr137 
                     and His144 are involved in catalysis |CITS: 
                     [32402247][33707636]|; the second and third N-terminal 
                     helices are required for membrane binding and catalytic 
                     activity |CITS: [32402247]|.
                     Temperature-sensitive <i>psd</i> mutants accumulate 
                     phosphatidylserine at the restrictive temperature |CITS: 
                     [1093166][796663][361737]| and are defective in flagellar 
                     motility and chemotaxis |CITS: [8244943]|.  The filamentous
                     phenotype and loss of viability of ts <i>psd</i> mutants at
                     the restrictive temperature can be partially suppressed by 
                     the addition of Mg<sup>2+</sup> or Ca<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [361737]|.
                     Expression of <i>psd</i> is regulated by CpxAR |CITS: 
                     [11953442]| and the alternative sigma factor |FRAME: 
                     RPOE-MONOMER &sigma;<sup>E</sup>| |CITS: [12900013]|.
                     Psd: ""<b>p</b>hosphatidyl<b>s</b>erine 
                     <b>d</b>ecarboxylase"" |CITS: [1093166]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [22534166][19165886][9370338][9211308][2197977][6089649]|"
