     gene            complement(4380274..4381326)
                     /gene="rsgA"
                     /locus_tag="b4161"
                     /label=rsgA
     CDS             complement(4380274..4381326)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rsgA"
                     /locus_tag="b4161"
                     /product="bifunctional ribosome small subunit-dependent
                     GTPase A / GTPase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.5.3"
                     /label=rsgA
                     /note="RsgA, a member of the HAS-GTPase
                     (<b>H</b>ydrophobic <b>A</b>mino acid <b>S</b>ubstituted 
                     for catalytic glutamine <b>GTP</b>ases) family, plays a 
                     role in the late stages of maturation of the 30S subunit of
                     the ribosome |CITS: 
                     [18223068][21303937][21960487][28482099]|. RsgA induces 
                     local conformational changes in the 30S subunit and may 
                     thereby destabilize kinetically trapped assembly 
                     intermediates |CITS: [28482099]|. It may also function as a
                     checkpoint protein that tests the decoding center of the 
                     30S subunit |CITS: [28396444]|.
                     RsgA exhibits GTPase activity |CITS: [12220175]|, which is 
                     stimulated 160-fold by association of RsgA with 
                     stochiometric amounts of the 30S subunit of the ribosome 
                     |CITS: [14973029]|. Addition of aminoglycosides that bind 
                     to the A site of the 30S ribosomal subunit inhibit that 
                     effect |CITS: [15466596][15828870][18029743]|.
                     RsgA is a member of a family of P-loop-containing GTPases 
                     with an unusual arrangement of GTPase motifs |CITS: 
                     [12220175]|. The N terminus has an OB-fold RNA-binding 
                     domain, the central region comprises the GTPase motifs, and
                     the C terminus has a potential zinc knuckle domain |CITS: 
                     [12220175]|. The conserved His248 in the switch 1 region 
                     functions as the catalytic residue |CITS: [28482099]|.  The
                     C-terminal zinc finger domain is required for RsgA 
                     stability and its interaction with the 30S subunit |CITS: 
                     [25904134]|.
                     The RsgA protein copurifies with ribosomes at a ratio of 
                     1:200. Recombinant RsgA interacts most strongly with the 
                     30S subunit in the presence of 5'-guanylylimidodiphosphate 
                     (GMP-PNP), a nonhydrolyzable GTP analog |CITS: [14973029]|.
                     Binding of RsgA to the 30S subunit induces conformational 
                     changes at the A and P site as well as helix 44; GTP 
                     hydrolysis may induce partial conformational restoration 
                     and dissociate RsgA from the small subunit |CITS: 
                     [18588897]|. The ribosome binding factor |FRAME: 
                     EG11178-MONOMER RbfA| promotes association of RsgA with the
                     30S subunit. RsgA in turn enhances the release of RbfA in a
                     GTP-dependent manner |CITS: [21102555]|; the C-terminal 
                     &alpha; helix of RsgA is important for this activity |CITS:
                     [25904134]|. RsgA is able to bind both mature and immature 
                     30S subunits; its affinity for the mature subunit is much 
                     stronger |CITS: [27382067]|. Binding of Era to the 30S 
                     subunit destabilizes helix 44 and prevents binding of RsgA 
                     |CITS: [31265110]|.
                     RsgA was thought to be essential for growth |CITS: 
                     [9743119]|, but a deletion mutant was shown to have a slow 
                     growth phenotype and accumulate 30S and 50S ribosomal 
                     subunits as well as a 17S rRNA precursor |CITS: 
                     [15466596][18223068]|. The structure of the 30S ribosomal 
                     subunit of a &Delta;<i>rsgA</i> mutant was solved by 
                     cryo-EM microscopy reconstruction, showing the presence of 
                     unprocessed 17S rRNA and absence of some late ribosomal 
                     proteins |CITS: [21303937]|; similar structural defects 
                     exist in a &Delta;<i>rimM</i> mutant |CITS: [23611982]|. 
                     These immature 30S subunits are able to mature slowly 
                     |CITS: [27382067]|. A cryo-EM co-structure shows that RsgA 
                     interacts with helix 44, the head and the platform of the 
                     30S subunit, overlapping with the binding sites of |FRAME: 
                     EG10505-MONOMER IF2| and |FRAME: EG10506-MONOMER IF3| 
                     |CITS: [21960487]|. A comparison of higher-resolution 
                     cryo-EM structures of the mature 30S subunit with and 
                     without bound RsgA show stabilization of helix 44 in the 
                     presence of RsgA |CITS: [28396444]|. The protein 
                     composition |CITS: [23293003]| and cryo-EM structure |CITS:
                     [24671761]| of the 30S subunit from a &Delta;<i>rbfA</i> 
                     &Delta;<i>rsgA</i> double mutant have been determined.
                     In a genetic interaction screen, two GTPases, |FRAME: 
                     EG10270-MONOMER Era| and |FRAME: EG10505-MONOMER IF2|, were
                     found to be multicopy suppressors of the slow growth 
                     phenotype, and several synthetic lethal mutants were 
                     isolated |CITS: [18223068]|. Overexpression of both IF2 and
                     initiator tRNAs can rescue the &Delta;<i>rsgA</i> slow 
                     growth phenotype |CITS: [38837341]|.
                     Certain mutations in <i>rbfA</i> also suppress the growth 
                     and 30S subunit maturation defects |CITS: [21102555]|, and 
                     salt stress suppresses the ribosome assembly and 16S rRNA 
                     processing defects |CITS: [19620234]| of a 
                     &Delta;<i>rsgA</i> mutant. Salt shock leads to a transient 
                     decrease in the intracellular level of RsgA; a 
                     &Delta;<i>rsgA</i> mutant is more resistant to high salt 
                     stress than wild type |CITS: [19620234]|. Salt stress 
                     resistance appears to be a general feature of many ribosome
                     maturation mutants |CITS: [23741511]|.
                     An S221A mutation within the central GTPase motif causes a 
                     catalytic defect |CITS: [12220175]|. N-terminal truncation 
                     mutants showed that the OB-fold region is essential for 
                     ribosome interaction and GTPase stimulation; the N-terminal
                     amino acids 1-20 are essential for GMP-PNP-dependent 
                     interaction with the 30S subunit |CITS: [14973029]|.
                     Direct inactivation of ribosomes by RsgA inhibits lytic 
                     phage T4 and can result in cells entering the persister 
                     state |CITS: [37670987]|.
                     RsgA: ""<b>r</b>ibosome <b>s</b>mall subunit-dependent 
                     <b>G</b>TPase <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [15466596]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16890019][21529161][21675921]|"
