     gene            4381421..4381966
                     /gene="orn"
                     /locus_tag="b4162"
                     /label=orn
     CDS             4381421..4381966
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="orn"
                     /locus_tag="b4162"
                     /product="oligoribonuclease"
                     /EC_number="3.1.13.3"
                     /label=orn
                     /note="Oligoribonuclease (Orn) is a processive 3'-to-5' 
                     exoribonuclease specific for short oligoribonucleotides 
                     |CITS: [240824][170260][9573169]|.
                     Short RNAs, from 2-7 nucleotides in length, accumulate in 
                     the cell during RNA degradation, because they are not good 
                     substrates for general exoribonucleases. Orn recycles these
                     short RNA oligonucleotides into mononucleotides. The 
                     activity on very short  oligoribonucleotides is not shared 
                     with other exoribonucleases in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [7608090]|. Orn is the only known exoribonuclease enzyme 
                     that is essential for growth in <i>E. coli</i>, indicating 
                     the importance of its function |CITS: [10200269]|.
                     Orn from |FRAME: TAX-287| was shown to cleave the 
                     dinucleotide |FRAME: L-DI-GMP|, which is formed from the 
                     second messanger |FRAME: C-DI-GMP| by |FRAME: EC-3.1.4.52| 
                     |CITS: [26305928][26305945]|.
                     Orn binds adenosine-3',5'-bisphosphate (pAp), but does not 
                     hydrolyze it under the conditions tested
                     |CITS: [16682444]|.
                     Crystal structures of Orn have been solved |CITS: 
                     [15039570]|. A study of recombinant Orn from |FRAME: 
                     TAX-666| has found that the enzyme exhibits a strong 
                     substrate preference for diribonucleotides, and the crystal
                     structure revealed an active site optimized for 
                     diribonucleotides |CITS: [31225796]|. This finding has led 
                     to the proposal that Orn might be a specific diribonuclease
                     rather than an enzyme that acts on oligonucleotides, as has
                     been originally presumed |CITS: [36026528]|. However, 
                     further studies are needed to resolve this question.
                     Orn: ""<b>o</b>ligo<b>r</b>ibo<b>n</b>uclease"" |CITS: 
                     [9573169]|
                     Review: |CITS: [17560162][35787606]|"
