     gene            4383883..4385430
                     /gene="nnr"
                     /locus_tag="b4167"
                     /label=nnr
     CDS             4383883..4385430
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nnr"
                     /locus_tag="b4167"
                     /product="multifunctional NAD(P)HX epimerase / NAD(P)HX
                     dehydratase / (S)-NADHX dehydratase / (S)-NADPHX 
                     dehydratase / NADHX epimerase / NADPHX epimerase"
                     /EC_number="4.2.1.136; 5.1.99.6"
                     /label=nnr
                     /note="The Nnr protein is a bifunctional enzyme that
                     catalyzes both the epimerization between the <i>R</i> and 
                     <i>S</i> forms of NAD(P)HX and the ADP-dependent repair of 
                     (<i>S</i>)-NAD(P)HX to NAD(P)H.  The N-terminal domain 
                     appears to be responsible for the epimerase activity, while
                     the C-terminal domain is responsible for the dehydratase 
                     activity |CITS: [21994945]|.
                     Comparative genomics and metabolomics experiments suggest 
                     that Nnr, and in particular the epimerase domain, may have 
                     a moonlighting function that involves pyridoxal phosphate 
                     metabolism |CITS: [29654173]|.
                     Studies in the 1950s have shown that eukaryotic |FRAME: 
                     GAPOXNPHOSPHN-RXN ""glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate 
                     dehydrogenase""| slowly catalyzes the formation of a 
                     hydrated form of NADH, called |FRAME: NADHX|, from NADH 
                     |CITS: [13174589]|.  The equilibrium of this hydration 
                     reaction is in favor (100/1) of the hydrated form |CITS: 
                     [3061454]|, making the reaction virtually irreversible.
                     In addition, NADHX can be formed spontaneously from NADH 
                     under slightly acidic conditions.  NADHX and NADPHX are 
                     known to inhibit dehydrogenases in other organisms |CITS: 
                     [9851831]|, and it may thus be important to eliminate these
                     damaged metabolites.
                     The highly conserved K197 residue (K192 in the publication,
                     where a different start site is used) within the N-terminal
                     epimerase domain is required for the epimerase activity of 
                     the enzyme |CITS: [29654173]|. Neither a &Delta;<i>nnr</i> 
                     deletion mutant nor a K197A point mutant show a growth 
                     defect in rich or minimal medium.  However, the deletion 
                     mutant accumulates large amounts of the <i>R</i>, <i>S</i>,
                     and cyclic forms of NADHX and NADPHX.  Surprisingly, the 
                     general changes in the metabolome of the K197A point mutant
                     clustered further away from wild type than those of the 
                     deletion mutant.  In particular, the pools of amino acids 
                     showed larger changes in the point mutant |CITS: 
                     [29654173]|.
                     Nnr: ""<b>n</b>icotinamide <b>n</b>ucleotide 
                     <b>r</b>epair"" |CITS: [21994945]|"
