     gene            4385402..4385863
                     /gene="tsaE"
                     /locus_tag="b4168"
                     /label=tsaE
     CDS             4385402..4385863
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tsaE"
                     /locus_tag="b4168"
                     /product="bifunctional N(6)-L-threonylcarbamoyladenine
                     synthase, TsaE subunit / N(6)-L-threonylcarbamoyladenine 
                     synthase"
                     /EC_number="2.3.1.234"
                     /label=tsaE
                     /note="TsaE is involved in the biosynthesis of the 
                     threonylcarbamoyladenosine (t<sup>6</sup>A) residue at 
                     position 37 of ANN-decoding tRNAs.  A mixture of purified 
                     |FRAME: G7698-MONOMER TsaC|, |FRAME: EG11171-MONOMER TsaD|,
                     |FRAME: G6991-MONOMER TsaB|, and TsaE proteins catalyzes 
                     formation of t<sup>6</sup>A in the presence of a tRNA 
                     substrate, ATP, threonine and bicarbonate <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [22378793][25578970]|.
                     The three essential proteins TsaE, TsaB and TsaD form an 
                     interaction network.  TsaB can interact with both TsaE and 
                     TsaD directly, and the interactions appear to be mutually 
                     exclusive |CITS: [19376873]|.  Purified TsaE forms a 
                     heterogeneous mixture of oligomers <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [19246765][19376873]|.
                     The protein is broadly conserved in bacteria and contains 
                     motifs characteristic of P-loop ATPases |CITS: [15324301]|.
                      A crystal structure of <i>Haemophilus influenzae</i> TsaE 
                     shows a nucleotide binding fold |CITS: [12112691]|.  
                     Measurements of TsaE's low intrinsic ATPase activity and 
                     ADP binding vary; |CITS: [15324301]| measured ATPase 
                     activity with a K<sub>m</sub> of 1.4 mM and a 
                     k<sub>cat</sub> of 12 h<sup>-1</sup> and ADP binding with a
                     K<sub>d</sub> of 32 &micro;M; |CITS: [19376873]| measured 
                     ATPase activity with a K<sub>m</sub> of 169 &micro;M and a 
                     K<sub>cat</sub> of 0.0026 s<sup>-1</sup>; |CITS: [25578970 
                     ]| measured ADP binding with a K<sub>d</sub> of 6.54 
                     &micro;M.  The ATPase activity of TsaE is activated by the 
                     TsaB-TsaD heterodimer. Site-directed mutants in the 
                     predicted ATP binding site and switch regions were 
                     generated and biochemically characterized |CITS: 
                     [25578970]|.  TsaE belongs to the widely conserved 
                     Ubiquitous bacterial Kinase (UbK) family of protein kinases
                     and is able to autophosphorylate <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [28890133]|.
                     TsaE is essential for growth in complex media under aerobic
                     conditions |CITS: 
                     [11361082][15324301][16738554][19376873]|, but not under 
                     anaerobic conditions |CITS: [19101473]|.  Depletion of the 
                     protein results in the formation of enlarged, elongated 
                     cells with an unusual distribution of DNA |CITS: 
                     [19376873]|.  |FRAME: G6864 <i>rstA</i>| was identified as 
                     a multicopy suppressor of the lethal phenotype of a strain 
                     with a conditional <i>tsaE</i> deletion |CITS: [17293428]|.
                      Other multicopy suppressors indicate a link between purine
                     biosynthesis and TsaE function |CITS: [19376873]|.  Other 
                     genetic interactions were found |CITS: [21873492]|.  A 
                     deletion/insertion mutant containing an extra copy of 
                     <i>tsaE</i> suppresses the effects of a 
                     <i>tsaD</i><sup>ts</sup> allele |CITS: [23909935]|. 
                     Expression of <i>tsaE</i> is induced by norfloxacin and 
                     other fluoroquinolone antibiotics |CITS: [19101473]|. TsaE:
                     ""<b>t</b>hreonylcarbamoyl-6(<b>s</b>ix)-<b>a</b>den 
                     osine"" |CITS: [22378793]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16333325][19465656][22905870]|"
