     gene            4385882..4387219
                     /gene="amiB"
                     /locus_tag="b4169"
                     /label=amiB
     CDS             4385882..4387219
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="amiB"
                     /locus_tag="b4169"
                     /product="bifunctional N-acetylmuramoyl-L-alanine amidase
                     B / N-acetylmuramoyl-L-alanine amidase"
                     /EC_number="3.5.1.28"
                     /label=amiB
                     /note="<i>amiB</i> encodes a protein with 
                     N-acetylmuramoyl-L-alanine amidase activity. Murein sacculi
                     isolated from a strain overexpressing <i>amiB</i> shows a 
                     significant decrease in the amount of high molecular weight
                     murein; expression of <i>amiB</i> from a plasmid 
                     complements the chaining phenotype associated with deletion
                     of the three N-acetylmuramoyl-L-alanine amidases |FRAME: 
                     NACMURLALAAMI1-MONOMER ""AmiA""|, AmiB and |FRAME: 
                     G7458-MONOMER ""AmiC""| |CITS: [11454209]|. Murein amidase 
                     activity produces stemless or |FRAME: 
                     a-denuded-peptidoglycan denuded glycans|.
                     Cells containing double amidase gene deletions 
                     (&Delta;<i>amiAB</i>, &Delta;<i>amiAC</i> and 
                     &Delta;<i>amiBC</i>) form septal peptidoglycan rings - 
                     thick dark bands of peptidoglycan between adjacent cells 
                     seen in the purified sacculi from chained cells |CITS: 
                     [17483214]|. The murein amidases are necessary for the 
                     continued incorporation of peptidoglycan into the 
                     developing septum in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [17483214]|. The
                     divisome associated factor, |FRAME: EG12297-MONOMER 
                     ""envC""|, activates AmiB and AmiA catalysed peptidoglycan 
                     cleavage <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [20300061]|. AmiB is 
                     recruited to the division site by an N-terminal targeting 
                     domain; recruitment is |FRAME: EG11529-MONOMER ""FtsN""| 
                     and |FRAME: EG10341-MONOMER ""FtsI""| dependent and 
                     correlates with the onset of envelope constriction; 
                     recruitment is independent of EnvC |CITS: [21764913]|. 
                     Auto-inhibition is a crucial feature of amidase regulation 
                     |CITS: [22715947]|. Structures of AmiA in an autoinhibited 
                     resting state and of AmiB in an EnvC LytM domain-bound 
                     activated state have been determined and a mechanism for 
                     amidase activation presented |CITS: [37276423]|.
                     |FRAME: G7484-MONOMER ActS| activates AmiB <i>in vitro</i> 
                     |CITS: [33660879]|. AmiB activity is pH-dependent; EnvC, 
                     |FRAME: EG12111-MONOMER NlpD|, and ActS stimulate AmiB 
                     activity in acidic environments |CITS: [33666292]|.
                     Unlike AmiA and AmiC, AmiB is not a Tat substrate and is 
                     thought to be transported to the periplasm by the Sec 
                     system |CITS: [12787347]|. It is predicted to have a signal
                     peptide from residues 1 to 22, an unknown domain from 
                     residues 23 to 253, and an amidase domain from residues 254
                     to 445 |CITS: [12787347]|."
