     gene            4389069..4390019
                     /gene="miaA"
                     /locus_tag="b4171"
                     /label=miaA
     CDS             4389069..4390019
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="miaA"
                     /locus_tag="b4171"
                     /product="tRNA dimethylallyltransferase"
                     /EC_number="2.5.1.75"
                     /label=miaA
                     /note="Dimethylallyl diphosphate:tRNA
                     dimethylallyltransferase (DMAPP-tRNA transferase, MiaA) 
                     catalyzes the first step in the pathway for 
                     hypermodification of the A37 base of certain tRNAs such as 
                     tRNA<sup>Tyr</sup> and tRNA<sup>Phe</sup>. Further 
                     thiomethylation of the A37 position is dependent on the 
                     presence of the isopentenyl modification |CITS: [379002]|. 
                     MiaA transfers the dimethylallyl moiety of DMAPP to the 
                     N<sup>6</sup> position of A37, which is adjacent to the 
                     anticodon |CITS: [4341485][4326583]|. This modification is 
                     required for efficient binding of tRNA<sup>Tyr</sup> to 
                     ribosomes |CITS: [4938812]| and is important in preventing 
                     frameshifts and other mutations |CITS: [11532950]|. 
                     MiaA-catalyzed tRNA modification is involved in regulating 
                     the translation efficiency of modification-tunable 
                     transcripts (MoTTs) |CITS: [26979278]|.
                     MiaA can act on isolated tRNA stem-loops, as long as the 
                     helix stem and the A36-A37-A38 motif are both present 
                     |CITS: [9214656][10828971]|. Co-crystal structures of MiaA 
                     in complex with tRNA<sup>Phe</sup> have been solved, 
                     showing a mutually induced fit mechanism of recognition 
                     involving structural rearrangement of the anticodon loop of
                     the tRNA |CITS: [19158097][19435325]|. No substantial 
                     change in the structure of the catalytic domain of MiaA is 
                     seen upon tRNA binding |CITS: [19435325]|. Both reports 
                     rely for comparison on apo-MiaA structures of the protein 
                     from other organisms. An ordered sequential mechanism of 
                     substrate binding and catalysis has been suggested |CITS: 
                     [9012675][11004538]|.
                     Modification of tRNAs by MiaA is important in preventing 
                     various mutations. <i>miaA</i> mutants show increased +1 
                     (but not -1) frameshifting as well as GC to TA 
                     transversions, the latter requiring active recombination 
                     |CITS: [11532950][12756333][1999389][11160115]|. 
                     <i>miaA</i> mutations also increase the general rate of 
                     spontaneous mutations and alter readthrough and suppression
                     of nonsense codons |CITS: [2656644][6410151]|. Deletion of 
                     this gene influences heterogeneity of UAG and UGA 
                     readthrough |CITS: [37670559]|.
                     In <i>miaA</i> mutants, the altered efficiency of 
                     translation by tRNAs lacking the dimethylallyl modification
                     affects regulation by attenuation of several operons, 
                     including <i>pheA</i>, <i>trp</i>, <i>pheMST</i> and 
                     <i>tnaCA</i> |CITS: 
                     [7042684][6317866][6379198][2345136][2231729][2231731]|. A 
                     mutation in <i>miaA</i> inhibits growth and, when combined 
                     with a mutation in <i>rpsL</i>, leads to streptomycin 
                     dependence |CITS: [3312947][3517592]|. Deletion of 
                     <i>miaA</i> increases the evolvability of resistance to 
                     ciprofloxacin |CITS: [23669383]|. A <i>miaA</i> insertion 
                     mutant causes hypersensitivity to azacytidine-induced 
                     DNA-protein crosslinks; the phenotype may result from a 
                     defect in azacytidine-induced tmRNA tagging |CITS: 
                     [25731940]|.
                     Mutation of <i>miaA</i> is lethal in a strain lacking 
                     functional tRNA<sub>6</sub><sup>Leu</sup> |CITS: 
                     [9603884]|; a <i>miaA</i> insertion mutant shows decreased 
                     levels of |FRAME: RPOS-MONOMER RpoS| |CITS: [24296670]|. 
                     MiaA-catalyzed tRNA modification is required for efficient 
                     decoding of UUX-leucine codons, and thus at least in part 
                     responsible for efficient translation of RpoS and IraP 
                     |CITS: [26979278]|.
                     MiaA appears to have an indirect role in biofilm formation,
                     perhaps through its regulation towards RpoS |CITS: 
                     [35025506]|.
                     The <i>miaA</i> gene is part of a superoperon with complex 
                     patterns of transcription and mechanisms of regulation 
                     |CITS: [7748952]|. The superoperon includes a number of 
                     heat shock promoters, and <i>miaA</i> is essential for 
                     growth at high temperatures |CITS: [8824618]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [22905870][40210524]|"
