     gene            4390105..4390413
                     /gene="hfq"
                     /locus_tag="b4172"
                     /label=hfq
     CDS             4390105..4390413
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hfq"
                     /locus_tag="b4172"
                     /product="bifunctional RNA-binding protein Hfq / Hfq"
                     /label=hfq
                     /note="Hfq is an RNA chaperone and major global regulator
                     of cell physiology. The <i>E. coli</i> K-12 Hfq was 
                     discovered as a host factor (HF-I) required for wild-type 
                     bacteriophage Q beta (Q&beta;) replication |CITS: 
                     [4874917][2020545][8345521][9007075][10623514]|. It is 
                     structurally and functionally similar to eukaryotic 
                     Sm/Sm-like proteins with its donut-shaped, homohexamer 
                     structure and conserved core region. It plays a major role 
                     in post-transcriptional regulation through stimulating 
                     base-pairing between small RNAs (sRNAs) and their target 
                     messenger RNA (mRNA), thus helping regulate translation and
                     mRNA stability. Additional roles include binding to 
                     ribosomal RNA (rRNA) affecting rRNA biogenesis and 
                     function, to transfer RNAs (tRNAs) influencing the accuracy
                     of protein synthesis, to double-stranded (ds) DNA possibly 
                     playing a role in regulation of DNA replication and 
                     compaction, and to a variety of other proteins related to 
                     its various regulatory roles, such as ribosomal proteins 
                     and poly(A) polymerase I.
                     Studies with <i>hfq</i> mutants exhibit pleiotropic 
                     phenotypes, such as decreased growth rate, increased cell 
                     length and sensitivity to UV light, in large part to its 
                     role in regulating expression of |FRAME: RPOS-MONOMER| 
                     (&sigma;<sup>S</sup>) in addition to 
                     &sigma;<sup>S</sup>-independent genes (initial studies 
                     include |CITS: [7984093][8654929][8982015][10529396][10600 
                     545][14526009]|). <i>hfq</i> belongs to a network of genes 
                     which facilitate stress-induced mutagenesis (SIM) in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [23224554][28794186]|. Hfq also seems 
                     to be involved in phage T7 resistance |CITS: [36978471]|.
                     Hfq is primarily found in the cytosol associated with 
                     ribosome- and nucleoid-associated proteins |CITS: 
                     [8288550][10551881][10947847]|. Hfq is one of the most 
                     abundant proteins in the nucleoid in growing cells |CITS: 
                     [10515926]|. Significant amounts of metallothionein 
                     (MT)-tagged Hfq protein visualized using electron 
                     microscopy were observed at the cell periphery and possibly
                     moving into the periplasm, pointing to submembrane 
                     localization of translational regulation by Hfq and 
                     stress-related sRNAs |CITS: [20011543]|. Hfq and the other 
                     components of the RNA degradation and processing machinery 
                     in <i>E. coli</i> are not diffusely distributed throughout 
                     the cytoplasm, but rather they are compartmentalized within
                     composite cellular elements within membrane-associated 
                     coiled structures that require the C-terminal region 
                     amyloid fibrils |CITS: [24266791][25772301]|. During stress
                     conditions, Hfq is located near the poles of the cell, is 
                     accompanied by polar accumulation of most small RNAs 
                     (sRNAs) |CITS: [31540875]| and is dependent on the 
                     pole-localizer |FRAME: G7087-MONOMER TmaR| |CITS: 
                     [36577380]|.
                     Hfq is able to bind ATP and has been associated with ATPase
                     activity |CITS: [12834354][17660259]|; however other 
                     analyses found ATPase activity is not required for its 
                     function as a chaperone |CITS: [23226421]|. <b>Regulatory 
                     function with sRNAs</b>
                     A variety of approaches have been used to identify the 
                     small RNA species that associate with Hfq to determine the 
                     binding mechanisms, such as those used by |CITS: 
                     [11445539][14622403][20348540][27588604][36328526]|. Hfq 
                     helps sRNAs base-pair with their target mRNAs <i>in 
                     vitro</i> |CITS: [11804583][11804582]|; many sRNAs require 
                     binding of Hfq for their activity |CITS: 
                     [11222598][11917098]|. Additional sRNA targets of Hfq were 
                     identified in a study using the <u>R</u>NA 
                     <u>i</u>nteraction by <u>l</u>igation and <u>seq</u>uencing
                     (RIL-seq) method |CITS: [27588604]|. The RIL-seq approach 
                     subsequently found that the Hfq-dependent RNA-RNA 
                     interactome was much more extensive than the |FRAME: 
                     EG12866-MONOMER|-dependent RNA-RNA interactome, though 
                     there was overlap between the two interactomes |CITS: 
                     [31761494]|. Another study using RIL-seq along with other 
                     methods for verification identified global Hfq-dependent 
                     RNA-RNA interactome (sRNA:sRNA, sRNA:mRNA and sRNA:tRNA) 
                     that adapted depending on the nitrogen (N) growth 
                     conditions: N-replete, N-starved, N-starved for 24 hrs, and
                     2 hrs after N added to 24 hr N-starved cultures. This study
                     also showed the extensive interactions that the 
                     Hfq-dependent sRNA |FRAME: RYEB-RNA| exhibits in long term 
                     (24 hrs) N-starved conditions. Further examination of the 
                     Hfq-dependent post-transcriptional regulation of N 
                     starvation confirmed its independence from the 
                     transcriptional regulation of |FRAME: PROTEIN-NRI NtrC| and
                     |FRAME: G7072-MONOMER Nac| but the Hfq-dependent |FRAME: 
                     RNA0-434| interaction with <i>nac</i>, which is also 
                     activated by NtrC, also points to the potential coupling of
                     transcription and translational regulatory interactions 
                     |CITS: [32532816][38142457]|.
                     Three different locations on the Hfq homohexamer structure 
                     have been found to bind RNAs: the proximal face, the distal
                     face and the rim region. Testing wild-type and missense 
                     mutants of Hfq for DsrA-dependent activation of <i>rpoS</i>
                     mRNA revealed at least two independent RNA-binding sites on
                     the Hfq complex: one located around the proximal face of 
                     the Hfq hexamer that recognizes and binds to uridine 
                     (U)-rich sequences at the 3' end of sRNAs, and the other at
                     the distal face on the C-terminus of Hfq, which extend 
                     laterally away from the Hfq hexameric core, associated with
                     binding to poly(A) sequences on mRNAs |CITS: 
                     [12554874][15030475][15531892][19889981]|. <i>E. coli</i> 
                     Hfq derivatives with the nonconserved C-terminal extension 
                     ('tail') removed could still regulate RNAs |CITS: 
                     [20888338]|. Simultaneous binding of Hfq to <i>rpsO</i> 
                     mRNA and DsrA sRNA fragments on the distal and proximal 
                     faces have been detected and verified <i>in vivo</i> |CITS:
                     [23605038]|. Binding affinities (K<sub>d</sub>) for DsrA 
                     sRNA, the <i>rpoS</i> mRNA 5'-untranslated region (UTR) and
                     different size poly(A) RNAs were measured in the range of 
                     20-50 nM per hexamer |CITS: [15531892]|. 3' U-rich 
                     sequences on the lateral rim of the Hfq homohexamer ring 
                     also interact with sRNA |CITS: [22454537][22645344]|. Using
                     deletion mutants and chimeras, sRNAs have been found to 
                     fall into two distinct Hfq binding groups with functional 
                     implications: Class I sRNAs bind to proximal and rim faces 
                     and pair with mRNA targets that bind the distal face for 
                     stability and rapid turnover, thus acting as ""emergency 
                     responders""; Class II sRNAs bind to proximal and distal 
                     faces and pair with targets that bind to the rim, acting as
                     ""silencers"" |CITS: [23318956][26373314]|. Further studies
                     to determine binding characteristics both <i>in vitro</i> 
                     and <i>in vivo</i> have been carried out |CITS: 
                     [23771143][27049793][28826489][35104863][35934049]|.
                     Using immobilized Hfq hexamers, sRNA competition was 
                     directly observed with two primary competitive scenarios 
                     observed: 'active competition' in which some resident sRNAs
                     (already associated with Hfq) becoming rapidly displaced 
                     from Hfq by an incoming sRNA, and 'stable coexistence' in 
                     which two sRNAs reside on an Hfq for >20sec; rarely was 
                     'passive' competition observed in which a resident sRNA was
                     rapidly displaced after interacting with another incoming 
                     sRNA |CITS: [35508531]|.
                     The dynamics of Hfq interactions with sRNAs is a multi-step
                     process starting with Hfq binding causing conformational 
                     change, as shown with OxyS; the intrinsically disordered 
                     C-terminal domains (CTDs) of the sRNA appears also to play 
                     a direct role in the restructuring dynamics |CITS: 
                     [35934049]|. Using single-molecule fluorescence microscopy 
                     the small ribonucleoprotein Hfq-DsrA was found to 
                     recursively ""sample"" the nascent, elongating mRNA at the 
                     (AAN)<sub>4</sub> motif ensuring the mRNA target site is 
                     rapidly located as it emerges from the |FRAME: APORNAP-CPLX
                     ""RNA Polymerase""| (RNAP) |CITS: [37116495]|.
                     Hfq's function as an RNA chaperone was confirmed from a 
                     study showing it binds and induces structural changes in 
                     <i>ompA</i> mRNA |CITS: [12634847]|. Using fluorescence 
                     resonance energy transfer (FRET) assay with the sRNA, DsrA,
                     and its regulatory target <i>rpoS</i> mRNA, Hfq was found 
                     to be capable of both RNA annealing and strand displacement
                     |CITS: [17259214]|, whereas another FRET-based assay with 
                     two RNA 21-mers showed Hfq's chaperone activity was found 
                     to promote RNA annealing but not strand displacement |CITS:
                     [17901153][17907573]|.
                     Hfq also regulates post-transcriptionally through 
                     regulating the length of mRNA polyadenylation by increasing
                     the processivity of poly(A) polymerase I to synthesize and 
                     elongate poly(A) tails on the 3' ends, as shown for 
                     <i>rpsO</i> mRNA |CITS: [10677490][12853618][14654705]|.
                     Another major role of <i>E. coli</i> Hfq as a 
                     post-transcriptional regulator involves mRNA degradation, 
                     both inhibition and promotion of mRNA degradation. Hfq 
                     binding to poly(A) tails was suggested to prevent mRNA 
                     degradation |CITS: [12853618][14654705][15522076]|, and its
                     ability to bind to the RNase E cleavage site on some sRNAs 
                     as well as to form ribonucleoprotein complexes with sRNAs 
                     and ribonucleases protects mRNA targets of sRNAs from 
                     degradation by RNaseE |CITS: [12554874][12975324][145 
                     61880][15522088][16166379][20167073]|. Hfq interactions can
                     lead to degradation of RNAs, as shown by its ability to 
                     bind and altering the structure of <i>sodB</i> mRNA which 
                     allows the sRNA RyhB to bind and lead to degradation of 
                     both RyhB and <i>sodB</i> mRNAs |CITS: [14739933]|.
                     An ""active cycling"" model has been proposed for Hfq 
                     interactions with sRNAs. Cycling of Hfq-RNA complexes was 
                     initially proposed from a study of binding and release of 
                     Hfq to DsrA sRNA and <i>rpsO</i> mRNA that involved a 
                     leader sequence with an upstream AAYAA sequence |CITS: 
                     [15561140][18658123]|.
                     Hfq interacts with Rho-independent transcription terminator
                     polyU tails of some sRNA rather than AU-rich regions of the
                     terminator hairpin loop. Initially it was observed that 
                     shortening the polyU tail eliminates the ability of Hfq to 
                     bind and regulate SgrS and RyhB sRNAs |CITS: [21788484]|. 
                     Subsequent studies showed that both the 3' region of SgrS 
                     sRNA consisting of an internal hairpin structure preceded 
                     by a U-rich sequence and the terminator hairpin followed by
                     a long poly(U) tail are the binding locations for Hfq 
                     regulation of SgrS sRNA under stress conditions. This study
                     provided insights into Hfq modulation of both termination 
                     and generation of functional sRNAs |CITS: 
                     [22454537][26106215][28606943]|. Many more studies have 
                     been carried out showing the role of Hfq in both positive 
                     and negative regulation of sRNAs: |CITS: 
                     [8654929][9774349][9826663][10809669][11804582][118 
                     04583][16204185][17267410][34046019][36328526]|. Studies 
                     related to stress conditions include |CITS: [15872 
                     186][24065131][25649688][28794186][29294046][35920556][3686
                     0088]|. <b>Other functions associated with Hfq</b> Early in
                     the studies of Hfq, it was found to interact with ribosomes
                     and 16S rRNA |CITS: [807580][6162477]|. More recently a 
                     deletion mutant of <i>hfq</i> exhibited a variety of 
                     defects related to ribosome biogenesis, such as 
                     down-regulation of translation-related and ribosome 
                     biogenesis-related proteins but not reduced mRNA levels, 
                     accumulation of improperly processed pre-16S rRNA and 
                     reduced levels of functional 70S ribosomes. Amino acid 
                     residues of Hfq that are associated with the <i>E. coli</i>
                     17S rRNA precursor are located in the distal face of Hfq 
                     rather than its proximal or rim surfaces |CITS: 
                     [29669858][29764978]|.
                     Interactions with various tRNAs suggests that Hfq may have 
                     a role in tRNA processing. Hfq was found to 
                     co-immunoprecipitate with several tRNAs, such as |FRAME: 
                     proM-tRNA| |CITS: [14622403]|. The proximal face of Hfq 
                     interacts with sites on the T-stem and D-stem regions of 
                     the tRNAs with binding affinities (K<sub>d</sub>) ranging 
                     between 20-50 nM per Hfq hexamer |CITS: [18230766]|. 
                     <i>&Delta;hfq</i> mutants had reduced translational 
                     fidelity |CITS: [18230766]|.
                     Hfq binds dsDNA, both linear and supercoiled, in a sequence
                     non-specific manner within the nucleoid; this may influence
                     regulation of DNA replication and compaction. Hfq shows 
                     preference for curved DNA but had a higher dissociation 
                     constant (K<sub>d</sub> = 250 nM) with non-curved DNA 
                     |CITS: [9245691][10551881]|. Interactions between Hfq and 
                     DNA was studied using ColE1 and lambda plasmids as 
                     replicons and <i>hfq</i> mutants |CITS: [24811974]|. 
                     Binding of dsDNA occurs at the C-terminal domain of Hfq but
                     within the eleven amino acid region where amyloid 
                     self-assembly occurs |CITS: [20619373][37082015]|, whereas 
                     disordered dsDNA with A-tracts was found to bind to the 
                     proximal face of the Hfq hexamer |CITS: [32133526]|. The 
                     C-terminal domain also induces strong local alignment of 
                     the Hfq:DNA fibers which may indicate it has a role as a 
                     bacterial chromatin organizer |CITS: [31816864]|. 
                     Additionally, dsDNA with A-tracts was able to interact with
                     Hfq when polyA RNA was already bound to Hfq's distal face, 
                     pointing to a potential mechanism for sequence-independent 
                     binding of Hfq to dsRNA stems |CITS: [32133526]|.
                     A mutant of Hfq lacking a C-terminus domain was tracked as 
                     it moved along a dsDNA and found to have accelerated 
                     mobility relative to wild-type Hfq, and the mobility of the
                     Hfq had a reverse dependence on the internal motion of the 
                     DNA molecule |CITS: [32930601][35166115]|. Hfq's mobility 
                     along dsDNA is slowed by enhanced binding affinity to 
                     methylated nucleotides as determined by fluorescence 
                     imaging in nanofluidics device |CITS: [39363842]|.
                     In addition to interactions with nucleic acids, Hfq also is
                     thought to have protein-protein and protein-lipid 
                     interactions. It co-purifies with possibly as an accessory 
                     protein of RNA polymerase |CITS: [12834354]|. 
                     Co-sedimentation and direct contact with lipid bilayers and
                     membranes has been observed; the amyloid region of Hfq's 
                     C-terminal domain interacts with the membrane, with both 
                     the membrane and the amyloid region undergoing 
                     conformational changes |CITS: [28878270][35955871]|. One 
                     conformational change observed for the membrane <i>in 
                     vitro</i> was Hfq induced transient pores in the membrane 
                     hypothesized to be associated with sRNA export |CITS: 
                     [28878270]|.
                     <i>E. coli's</i> Hfq forms the ringed, homohexamer 
                     structure observed in Sm/Sm-like proteins |CITS: 
                     [11804582][11804583][12095248][12853626]|. In a comparative
                     structural study the less conserved C-terminal domain of 
                     Hfq is predicted to be unstructured |CITS: [12202750]|. Hfq
                     can polymerize <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i> into 
                     well-ordered fibers, that are associated with these 
                     unstructured amyloid regions |CITS: 
                     [16337963][25772301][30889801]|. The 11 amino acid region 
                     within the C-terminus domain forms amyloid-like 
                     &beta;-strand filaments without affecting the N-terminal 
                     conserved core region |CITS: [37865695]|. Crystal 
                     structures of Hfq |CITS: [12777774][12853626]|, of Hfq 
                     bound to the poly(A) RNA and the oligoribonucleotide 
                     A<sub>15</sub> |CITS: [19889981]|, to DsrA |CITS: 
                     [21979921][22718981]|, to RydC |CITS: [25551292]| and of 
                     the Hfq core bound to a 30-bp dsDNA |CITS: [32133526]| have
                     been studied. Crystals superstructures of 
                     (Hfq<sub>6</sub>)<sub>n&ge;8</sub> with |FRAME: 
                     EG10905-MONOMER| and RNA polymerase have been obtained; the
                     C-termini plays a role in the formation of these structures
                     |CITS: [38869079]|.
                     When <i>hfq</i> or it's C-terminal domain is deleted, 
                     single cell analysis found the antibiotic ciprofloxacin 
                     accumulated within the cells even when |FRAME: EG10670| 
                     and/or |FRAME: EG10671| are also deleted, indicating that 
                     Hfq plays a role in mediating the modulation of 
                     sRNA-independent antibiotic transport |CITS: [39920540]|. 
                     Hfq accumulates recurrent mutations under glucose 
                     limitation, clustered on its distal face, disrupting RNA 
                     binding to the <i>rpoS</i> leader, diminishing RpoS 
                     translation, and indirectly diminishing expression of 
                     glucose-scavenging genes |CITS:[40830465]|.
                     Regulation of Hfq has been described |CITS: [7748952][8288 
                     550][7984093][8824618][10515926][16818608][25649688]|. 
                     Potential antibiotics that target the amyloid region of Hfq
                     have been studied |CITS: [30513780][33806663]|.
                     Hfq: ""<u>h</u>ost <u>f</u>actor for phage <u>Q</u> beta"" 
                     |CITS: [2020545]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10744991][11115103][11804580][12208995][1 
