     CDS             4390489..4391769
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hflX"
                     /locus_tag="b4173"
                     /product="bifunctional ribosome rescue factor HflX /
                     GTPase"
                     /EC_number="3.6.5.3"
                     /label=hflX
                     /note="HflX is a heat shock-induced ribosome-dependent
                     GTPase that rescues stalled ribosomes by dissociating 
                     vacant and mRNA-associated ribosomes with deacylated tRNA 
                     in the peptidyl site |CITS: [26458047][26733579]|. 
                     Structural data suggests that HflX disrupts the central 
                     bridges between the ribosomal subunits and thereby promotes
                     their dissociation |CITS: [26458047]|.
                     The HflX protein is a GTPase |CITS: [19181811]| that 
                     interacts with the 50S subunit of the ribosome in the 
                     presence of both GTP, GDP, ATP and ADP |CITS: 
                     [19109926][26458047]|.  The intrinsic GTPase activity of 
                     HflX is very slow and can be stimulated 1000-fold by 
                     interaction with the 50S subunit |CITS: 
                     [19109926][19824612]| as well as 70S ribosomes and 
                     poly(U)-programmed 70S ribosomes |CITS: 
                     [19824612][22554723]|.  GTP hydrolysis is required for 
                     ribosome dissociation |CITS: [26733579]| or the release of 
                     HflX from the 50S subunit |CITS: [26458047]|.
                     HflX binds both GTP and ATP |CITS: [19181811][19824612]|. 
                     Both the N-terminal and the C-terminal domain can bind and 
                     hydrolyze GTP and ATP |CITS: [23956218]|.  Purine 
                     nucleotide binding was studied by equilibrium and 
                     pre-steady-state fluorescence techniques.  K<sub>D</sub> 
                     values indicate a higher affinity for guanine nucleotides 
                     as well as for diphosphates compared to triphosphates; GTP 
                     appears to be the physiologically relevant substrate |CITS:
                     [19824612]|.  GTPase activity of HflX is not 
                     K<sup>+</sup>-stimulated |CITS: [23650596]|.  HflX was 
                     found to bind (p)ppGpp |CITS: [29511080]|.
                     A cryo-EM structure of the 50S ribosomal subunit in a 
                     complex with HflX and GMP-PNP shows that HflX binds to the 
                     intersubunit face of the 50S subunit.  The N-terminal 
                     domain of HflX contacts several rRNA helices near the 
                     peptidyl transferase center, while the C-terminal domain 
                     interacts with the L12 stalk base.  The 50S subunit itself 
                     undergoes conformational changes in the regions involving 
                     intersubunit bridges |CITS: [26458047]|.
                     Although <i>hflX</i> is part of the ""<i>hflA</i>"" locus 
                     which was originally found to change the frequency of 
                     &lambda; lysogeny, it does not play a role in this process 
                     or in transposition frequency |CITS: [19181811]|.  HflX was
                     thought to be involved in regulation of HflC and HflK 
                     activity |CITS: [8248183]|, but it does not interact with 
                     HflK or HflC |CITS: [19181811]|.  Autophosphorylation of 
                     HflX in the presence of Mn<sup>2+</sup> was observed |CITS:
                     [24794564]|;  based on mutagenesis results, the S211 
                     residue within the P-loop is the GTP-dependent 
                     autophosphorylation site |CITS: [27398305]|.  High 
                     concentrations of Mn<sup>2+</sup> appear to inhibit growth 
                     of a &Delta;<i>hflX</i> mutant |CITS: [24794564]|.
                     The <i>hflA</i> locus contains the <i>hflC</i>, 
                     <i>hflK</i>, and <i>hflX</i> genes |CITS: [3040675]|.  It 
                     affects the &lambda; phage lysis/lysogeny switch by playing
                     a role in proteolysis of the &lambda; cII protein |CITS: 
                     [6218885]|.  A strain containing an in-frame deletion of 
                     <i>hflX</i> shows no change in lysogenic frequency of 
                     &lambda; or in frequency of transposition |CITS: 
                     [19181811]|.
                     <i>hflX</i> is part of a complex superoperon |CITS: 
                     [3040675][7748952][7984093][8824618]|.  Expression is 
                     increased by heat shock |CITS: [8349564][26458047]|, and a 
                     <i>hflX</i> mutant shows a growth defect at increased 
                     temperatures |CITS: [26458047]|.
                     Hfl: ""<b>h</b>igh <b>f</b>requency of <b>l</b>ysogeny"" 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16333325][21885683][21675921][30448945]|"
