     gene            4396471..4398912
                     /gene="rnr"
                     /locus_tag="b4179"
                     /label=rnr
     CDS             4396471..4398912
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rnr"
                     /locus_tag="b4179"
                     /product="RNase R"
                     /label=rnr
                     /note="RNase R is a ribonuclease that has been implicated
                     in rRNA maturation, mRNA degradation during stationary 
                     phase, degradation of polyadenylated mRNAs, and 
                     tmRNA-mediated degradation of non-stop mRNAs.
                     RNase R is a processive hydrolytic 3' to 5' exoribonuclease
                     that releases 5'-nucleoside monophosphates, producing limit
                     end products of di- and trinucleotides. Although it can 
                     cleave several RNA substrates, it shows greatest activity 
                     toward rRNA |CITS: [11948193]|. Unlike RNase II, RNase R is
                     able to digest double-stranded RNA as long as a 
                     single-stranded region at the 3' end is available; it was 
                     shown that RNase R is involved in the decay of structured 
                     mRNAs |CITS: [15664199]|. The C-terminal S1 domain does not
                     appear to be responsible for this ability |CITS: 
                     [17242308]|, while the nuclease domain is sufficient for 
                     degradation of double-stranded RNA |CITS: [19004832]|. A 3'
                     overhang of at least 7 nucleotides is required for RNase R 
                     binding and activity; the structure of the 3' sugar residue
                     only plays a small role in substrate recognition |CITS: 
                     [16893880]|. The enzyme does not bind or degrade 
                     single-stranded DNA |CITS: [16893880]|.
                     RNase R contains two N-terminal cold shock domains (CSDs) 
                     which appear to play a role in substrate recruitment, a 
                     central nuclease (RNB) domain, followed by an S1 domain 
                     that may position substrates for efficient catalysis, and a
                     C-terminal basic domain |CITS: [19004832]|. The CSDs, 
                     especially CSD2, is responsible for an RNA helicase 
                     activity that is independent of the RNase activity of the 
                     protein and that is required for complementation of the 
                     cold-shock function of CsdA |CITS: [20023028]|. The Asp280 
                     residue in the RNB domain is required for RNase activity, 
                     but is not involved in substrate binding |CITS: 
                     [19630750]|, while Arg572 is required for degradation of 
                     structured RNA |CITS: [19361424]|.  Tyr324 |CITS: 
                     [19630750]| and Arg572 |CITS: [19361424]| influences the 
                     length of the final degradation product. Both CSDs and the 
                     S1 domain function as RNA-binding domains and are 
                     responsible for the selective degradation of 
                     double-stranded RNA substrates containing a single-stranded
                     3' overhang of five or more nucleotides |CITS: [19630750]|.
                     Swapping the S1 and RNB domains from RNase R into an RNase 
                     II context enables the degradation of ds RNA substrates and
                     the appearance of the 2 nt final degradation product |CITS:
                     [21465561]|.
                     RNase R appears to be specifically involved in the 
                     maturation of |FRAME: SSRA-RNA|, a small RNA involved in 
                     rescue of stalled ribosomes, at low growth temperatures 
                     |CITS: [14622421]|, and in the tmRNA-mediated degradation 
                     of non-stop mRNAs |CITS: [17087776]|. Targeted non-stop 
                     mRNA decay requires interaction with SmpB/tmRNA and 
                     engagement with the stalled ribosome and involves the 
                     C-terminal basic domain of RNase R |CITS: [21091502]|. 
                     RNase R also decreases |FRAME: EG10669| mRNA stability at 
                     stationary phase |CITS: [16556233]| and degrades 
                     oligoadenylated |FRAME: EG10914| mRNA |CITS: [19103951]|. 
                     RNase R is involved in the degradation of ribosomal RNA 
                     both during starvation and for ribosomal quality control 
                     |CITS: [21135037]|.
                     RNase R levels are induced 7- to 8-fold by cold shock, 
                     mainly as a result of increased mRNA stability. |FRAME: 
                     EG10743-MONOMER ""PNPase""| is involved in regulating RNase
                     R levels |CITS: [14622421]|. RNase R is also induced during
                     entry into stationary phase and starvation |CITS: 
                     [16135521][16556233]|. Maturation of the operon mRNA 
                     containing <i>rnr</i> and RNase R levels are dependent on 
                     RNase E |CITS: [16563345]|. RNase R levels are slightly 
                     increased at high temperatures and ~5-fold increased at 
                     high temperatures in an RNase E mutant |CITS: [19103951]|. 
                     The RNase R protein is highly unstable during exponential 
                     growth; the protein is stabilized in stationary phase and 
                     under cold shock and minimal media growth conditions, 
                     accounting for increased RNase R levels despite decreased 
                     levels of <i>rnr</i> mRNA |CITS: [20185542]|. The 
                     trans-translation system (tmRNA and SmpB) is responsible 
                     for the short half life of RNase R during exponential 
                     growth via its interactions with the C-terminal domains of 
                     RNase R |CITS: [20688916]|. During exponential phase, 
                     Lys544 is acetylated by |FRAME: G7350-MONOMER ""Pka""|, 
                     resulting in tighter binding of tmRNA-SmpB to RNase R 
                     followed by proteolytic degradation |CITS: [21981926]|. 
                     tmRNA-SmpB stimulates binding of the proteases |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-1163 ""HslUV""| and |FRAME: CPLX0-2881 ""Lon""| to 
                     the N terminus of RNase R |CITS: [22879590]|. Pka is not 
                     present in late exponential and stationary phase, resulting
                     in increased stability of newly synthesized RNase R |CITS: 
                     [22124017]|.
                     VacB (later shown to be identical to RNase R |CITS: 
                     [9603904]|) of enteroinvasive <i>E. coli</i>, <i>Shigella 
                     flexneri</i>, and other <i>Shigella</i> is important for 
                     the virulence of these organisms |CITS: [1400189]|.
                     An <i>rnr</i> mutant has a growth defect during growth at 
                     low temperatures |CITS: [14622421]|. An <i>rnr pnp</i> 
                     double mutant is not viable |CITS: [9603904]| and 
                     accumulates high levels of mRNAs containing REP sequences 
                     |CITS: [15664199]|. Overexpression of RNase R complements 
                     the cold-sensitive phenotype of a <i>deaD</i> mutant |CITS:
                     [17557820]|, but not that of a <i>pnp</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [18606734]|. Overexpression of RNase R rescues the growth 
                     defect of a &Delta;<i>rph</i> &Delta;<i>pnp</i> mutant 
                     |CITS: [19617368]|.
                     An <i> in vivo</i> genome-wide targetome investigation that
                     the stability of the stem-loops formed at the 3' end of 
                     processed mRNAs from polycistronic operons affected the 
                     termination of the 3' to 5' exonuclease activity of PNPase,
                     RNase II and RNase R |CITS: [37845007]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [8335617][19215773][21418661][35787606][36272229]|"
