     CDS             4415656..4415883
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rpsR"
                     /locus_tag="b4202"
                     /product="multifunctional 30S ribosomal subunit protein
                     S18 / S6:S18 ribosomal subunit complex / 30S ribosomal 
                     subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rpsR
                     /note="The S18 protein is a component of the 30S subunit
                     of the ribosome.
                     The N-terminal alanine residue of S18 is acetylated by the 
                     |FRAME: EG10850-MONOMER RimI| enzyme |CITS: 
                     [6991870][10094780]|.  N-terminal acetylation of S18 and S5
                     correlates with correct folding of the platform and central
                     pseudoknot domains of 16S rRNA |CITS: [24207057]|. S18 
                     interacts with the central domain of 16S rRNA; the 
                     interaction is dependent on S8 and S15 |CITS: 
                     [6208366][2449359][2459389]|.  S18 may be involved in 
                     binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the P site of the ribosome 
                     |CITS: [4585975]|.  S18 is photoaffinity labeled by 
                     puromycin, an analog of the 3' end of aminoacylated tRNA 
                     |CITS: [8034577]|.
                     S18 was also shown to crosslink to IF3 |CITS: 
                     [7000779][7020604]|, IF1 |CITS: [6349681]| and 4.5S RNA 
                     |CITS: [16043501]|.
                     Expression analysis of <i>rpsF</i>p indicates that it may 
                     be autoregulated by one or more of its operon components 
                     |CITS: [23292777]|.  Coexpressed S6:S18 were found to bind 
                     to the <i>rpsF</i> 5'-UTR in a region with structural 
                     similarity to their binding site in 16S rRNA |CITS: 
                     [23980204]|.  The S6:S18 complex was shown to repress 
                     translation of <i>rpsF</i> |CITS: [26447183]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [22649682]|"
