     gene            4415925..4416374
                     /gene="rplI"
                     /locus_tag="b4203"
                     /label=rplI
     CDS             4415925..4416374
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="rplI"
                     /locus_tag="b4203"
                     /product="trifunctional 50S ribosomal subunit protein L9 /
                     50S ribosomal subunit / ribosome"
                     /label=rplI
                     /note="The L9 protein is a component of the 50S subunit of
                     the ribosome.  L9 is universally conserved in eubacteria, 
                     but not in other domains of life; surprisingly, it is not 
                     essential.  L9 appears to be involved in maintenance of the
                     reading frame during translational pauses |CITS: 
                     [19258537]|.  A model for the physiological function of L9 
                     has been proposed |CITS: [25879934]|.
                     L9 is part of the first reconstitution intermediate of the 
                     50S subunit |CITS: [3298242][8308887]|.  L9 can be 
                     crosslinked to L2 and L28 |CITS: 
                     [3541786][2653827][2665813]| and is exposed on the ribosome
                     surface |CITS: [9371771]|.  A solution structure determined
                     by cryo-EM showed that the L9 protein consists of 
                     N-terminal and C-terminal domains connected by a long 
                     &alpha; helical domain that is wrapped around the L1 stalk 
                     |CITS: [10647188]|.  An atomic model of L9 within the 
                     ribosome was obtained in a cryo-EM structure, revealing 
                     contacts with the 30S subunit |CITS: [25707802]|.
                     The binding region of L9 on domain V of 23S rRNA was mapped
                     |CITS: [9813114][10625493]|.  The N- and C-terminal domains
                     of L9 interact with two separate sites in domain V of 23S 
                     rRNA |CITS: [8780779][10764578]|.  Crosslinking of tRNA in 
                     the A site of the ribosome to L9 depends on the state of 
                     the tRNA |CITS: [2660905]|.
                     L9 may normally be involved to limit ""ribosomal hopping"" 
                     during translation of bacteriophage T4 gene <i>60</i> 
                     |CITS: [7809071][8780779][10835364][11399077]|.  In a 
                     strain lacking <i>rplI</i>, frameshifting, stop-hopping 
                     |CITS: [11399077]|, as well as recoding at stalled 
                     ribosomes |CITS: [21757758]| is increased.
                     Strains that contain certain mutations in the essential 
                     ribosomal stability factor |FRAME: G7319-MONOMER Der| are 
                     dependent on L9 for survival.  The N-terminal domain of L9 
                     is both necessary and sufficient for complementation of the
                     <i>derT57I</i> mutant allele |CITS: [23772068]|.  A second 
                     class of mutations causing synthetic phenotypes map to 
                     |FRAME: EG12099-MONOMER EF-P| and enzymes responsible for 
                     its post-translational modification.  L9 improves subunit 
                     maturation in <i>der</i> and <i>efp</i> mutant backgrounds 
                     |CITS: [25879934]|.
                     An <i>rplI</i> mutant with a temperature-sensitive growth 
                     defect has been isolated |CITS: [ISONO78][366612]|.  An 
                     <i>rplI</i> deletion shows only a slight growth defect 
                     |CITS: [23772068]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [19258537]|"
