     gene            4418752..4419372
                     /gene="fklB"
                     /locus_tag="b4207"
                     /label=fklB
     CDS             4418752..4419372
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fklB"
                     /locus_tag="b4207"
                     /product="bifunctional peptidyl-prolyl cis-trans isomerase
                     FklB / peptidyl-prolyl cis-trans isomerase"
                     /EC_number="5.2.1.8"
                     /label=fklB
                     /note="The FklB protein is an FKBP (FK506 binding
                     protein)-type peptidyl-prolyl <i>cis-trans</i> isomerase 
                     (PPIase) that may be located in the periplasm.  Sequence 
                     analysis indicates that FklB is related to the Mip 
                     (macrophage infectivity potentiator)-like FKBPs which occur
                     in many intracellular pathogens |CITS: [8703024]|. 
                     Substrates were identified by pull-down assay and include 
                     Rob, Crl, PcnB, and ObgE |CITS: [32545723]|.
                     Purified FklB (from <i>E. coli</i> K-12 HB101) is a dimer 
                     in solution; the enzyme shows a preference for substrate 
                     with a hydrophobic side chain in position P1 (eg 
                     Suc-Ala-<u>Leu</u>-Pro-Phe) and also for a lysyl residue at
                     this position (Suc-Ala-<u>Lys</u>-Pro-Phe);  PPIase 
                     activity is inhibited <i>in vitro</i> by the 
                     immunosuppressive drug FK506 |CITS: [8703024]|.
                     Based on sequence similarity, FklB appears to have a domain
                     structure consisting of an N-terminal dimerization domain, 
                     a long &alpha;-helix linker, and a C-terminal domain that 
                     is implicated in binding FK506 and rapamycin |CITS: 
                     [22263615][22989269][25072141][29632827][26944658]|. 
                     Mutations in the linker helix affect the chaperone 
                     activity, structure and stability of FklB, but have little 
                     effect on rapamycin binding |CITS: [22989269][29632827]|. 
                     The Tyr15 residue of the N-terminal domain is additionally 
                     required for dimerization |CITS: [26944658]|.  Mutants in 
                     putative active site residues were assayed for potential 
                     substrate binding and PPIase activity |CITS: [32545723]|. 
                     The putative periplasmic localization of FklB was shown 
                     using a polyclonal antibody against FKBP25mem from 
                     <i>Legionella pneumophila</i> Philadelphia I |CITS: 
                     [8703024]|; it may have thus detected a different PPIase of
                     similar molecular weight.
                     <i>fklB</i> appears to be either poorly expressed or 
                     tightly controlled |CITS: [8703024]|.  A strain containing 
                     non-polar deletions of all six cytoplasmic PPIases 
                     (&Delta;6<i>ppi</i>) shows a growth defect at 37&deg;C in 
                     rich media and at 43&deg;C in both rich and minimal media 
                     |CITS: [32545723]|."
