     gene            4419681..4421093
                     /gene="cycA"
                     /locus_tag="b4208"
                     /label=cycA
     CDS             4419681..4421093
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="cycA"
                     /locus_tag="b4208"
                     /product="multifunctional
                     D-serine/alanine/glycine:H(+)symporter / D-alanine:proton 
                     symporter / D-serine:proton symporter / glycine:proton 
                     symporter / L-alanine:proton symporter / 
                     beta-alanine:proton symporter / D-cycloserine:proton 
                     symporter"
                     /label=cycA
                     /note="CycA is an inner membrane protein which mediates
                     the uptake of D-serine, D-alanine, and glycine; it also 
                     contributes to L-alanine uptake, is the major transporter 
                     for &beta;-alanine uptake and is implicated in the uptake 
                     of D-cycloserine.
                     <i>cycA</i> mutants are defective in the the uptake of 
                     D-serine, D-alanine and glycine and slightly impaired in 
                     L-alanine uptake; <i>cycA</i> mutants are unable to utilize
                     D-alanine as a carbon source |CITS: [4583203][4574696]|. 
                     CycA mediates the uptake of &beta;-alanine and is the main 
                     &beta;-alanine transporter in strain MG1655; &beta;-alanine
                     transport is dependent on electrochemical H<sup>+</sup> 
                     potential |CITS: [15221223]|. CycA mediated transport of 
                     glycine is implicated in the regulation of the |FRAME: 
                     GCVMULTI-CPLX ""glycine cleavage system""| |CITS: 
                     [7894704]|.
                     CycA is implicated in the transport of D-cycloserine (a 
                     D-alanine analog and broad spectrum antibiotic) when cells 
                     are grown in minimal media; CycA does not play a role in 
                     D-cycloserine transport in complex media - this is probably
                     due to competition from other CycA substrates |CITS: 
                     [23316042]|. The <i>cyc<sup>r1</sup></i> strain 
                     characterized by |CITS: [4926674]| contains a nonsense 
                     mutation in the carboxy terminal region of CycA |CITS: 
                     [23316042]|.
                     In the Transporter Classification Database |CITS: 
                     [26546518]| CycA is a member of the Amino Acid Transporter 
                     (AAT) family within the Amino Acid-Polyamine-Organocation 
                     (APC) superfamily.
                     Early studies described four distinct systems mediating the
                     uptake of neutral amino acids in <i>E. coli K-12</i> 
                     including a system (termed System I) specific for alanine, 
                     serine and glycine; no stereospecificity for isomers was 
                     observed |CITS: [4880290]| (see also |CITS: 
                     [4919992][4926674]|).
                     The small RNA GvcB, and Hfq negatively regulate <i>cycA</i>
                     expression during growth in complex media |CITS: 
                     [19118351]|. <i>cycA</i> expression significantly decreases
                     when cells are exposed to human serum and loss of 
                     <i>cycA</i> increases cell viablility in the presence of 
                     serum |CITS: [31346171]|.
                     <i>dagA</i>: <U>d-a</U>lanine <U>g</U>lycine |CITS: 
                     [4574696]|<BR><i>cycA</i>: <U>cyc</U>loserine (see |CITS: 
                     [4583203]|)
                     Related review: |CITS: [4613254]|"
