     gene            complement(4421138..4421800)
                     /gene="ytfE"
                     /locus_tag="b4209"
                     /label=ytfE
     CDS             complement(4421138..4421800)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ytfE"
                     /locus_tag="b4209"
                     /product="bifunctional [Fe-S] cluster repair protein YtfE
                     / nitrite reductase"
                     /label=ytfE
                     /note="YtfE belongs to a conserved and widely distributed
                     family of hemerythrin-like proteins. Purified YtfE contains
                     a non-heme di-iron center of the histidine-carboxylate type
                     |CITS: [16553864][18357473][24740378]|. Based on 
                     spectroscopic, mass spectrometric and kinetic approaches, 
                     YtfE is considered to belong to a new class of nitrite 
                     reductases |CITS: [35416044]|.
                     YtfE appears to be involved in the repair of iron centers 
                     |CITS: [17289666][18203837]|. YtfE is able to donate iron 
                     for the assembly of an Fe-S cluster into apo-|FRAME: 
                     G7324-MONOMER IscU| |CITS: [24740378]| but only under 
                     conditions that degrade its di-iron center |CITS: 
                     [35416044]|. YtfE can also act as a nitric oxide scavenger 
                     that catalyzes the reduction of nitric oxide (NO) to 
                     nitrous oxide (N<sub>2</sub>O) |CITS: [27246459]|. Genetic 
                     interaction experiments suggest that YtfE releases NO from 
                     nitrosylation-damaged metalloproteins, with subsequent 
                     reduction of NO to N<sub>2</sub>O by the |FRAME: 
                     G6457-MONOMER hybrid cluster protein| |CITS: [29493496]|. 
                     Similarly, in the absence of |FRAME: EG11415-MONOMER Dps|, 
                     YtfE increases the intracellular levels of reactive oxygen 
                     species |CITS: [30249704]|.
                     Exposure of YtfE to oxygen leads to dimerization via the 
                     formation of intermolecular disulfide bridges between C30 
                     or C31 in the N-terminal domain of the protein |CITS: 
                     [27246459]|. Ligands for the di-iron center were predicted 
                     by homology-based modelling; site-directed mutagenesis of 
                     conserved residues showed that H129, E133 and E208 are 
                     required for YtfE activity |CITS: [25583388]|. Crystal 
                     structures of monomeric YtfE have been solved |CITS: 
                     [27246459][32940468][33995335]|. The structure of the 
                     di-iron center was elucidated; as isolated, the di-iron 
                     center is in the Fe<sup>II</sup>-Fe<sup>III</sup> state 
                     |CITS: [27246459]|. Two conformational states (C and C' 
                     states) have been identified depending on the osmotic 
                     stress conditions |CITS: [32940468]|. Based on 
                     site-directed mutagenesis data, the two glutamate residues 
                     E125 and E159 are involved in the binding/release of iron, 
                     although they are not part of the histidine-carboxylate 
                     iron coordination site |CITS: [33995335]|.
                     The C-terminal hemerythrin-like domain of YtfE interacts 
                     with Dps |CITS: [30249704]|; YtfE also directly interacts 
                     with IscU and |FRAME: G7325-MONOMER IscS| |CITS: 
                     [33995335]|.
                     YtfE promotes the reduction of NO to N<sub>2</sub>O. In its
                     fully reduced Fe<sup>II</sup>-Fe<sup>II</sup> state, 
                     addition of NO leads to the formation of a di-mononitrosyl 
                     complex, {Fe(NO)}<sup>7</sup>-{Fe(NO)}<sup>7</sup>, and 
                     subsequent two-electron reduction to form N<sub>2</sub>O 
                     and recovery of the Fe<sup>II</sup>-Fe<sup>III</sup> 
                     di-iron site in the presence of an electron donor |CITS: 
                     [27246459]|.
                     Levels of <i>ytfE</i> mRNA are increased in cells treated 
                     with NO under anaerobic growth conditions.  An <i>E. 
                     coli</i> strain lacking a functional <i>ytfE</i> gene is 
                     more sensitive to NO and oxidative stress than wild type 
                     |CITS: [15546870][17289666]| and has increased sensitivity 
                     to iron starvation and decreased activity of iron-sulfur 
                     proteins |CITS: [16553864][20138195]|. Reports on the 
                     growth of &Delta;<i>ytfE</i> mutants under stress 
                     conditions disagree; |CITS: [29493496]| shows similar 
                     sensitivity of &Delta;<i>ytfE</i> and wild type strains to 
                     NO, while |CITS: [30249704]| shows increased sensitivity of
                     &Delta;<i>ytfE</i> to NONOate and hydrogen peroxide 
                     compared to wild type.
                     Expression of <i>ytfE</i> is regulated by the NO-sensitive 
                     transcriptional repressor NsrR |CITS: [16428390][17449618]|
                     and, possibly indirectly, by Fnr and Fur |CITS: 
                     [16553864]|.
                     The nitrite reduction and its kinetics have been measured; 
                     two cysteine residues, Cys30 and Cys31, play a role in the 
                     electron transfer for the nitrite reduction |CITS: 
                     [35416044]|.
                     RIC: ""<b>r</b>epair of <b>i</b>ron <b>c</b>enters"" |CITS:
                     [18203837]|
                     Review: |CITS: [22029434][35807291]|"
