     gene            complement(4431355..4431993)
                     /gene="msrA"
                     /locus_tag="b4219"
                     /label=msrA
     CDS             complement(4431355..4431993)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="msrA"
                     /locus_tag="b4219"
                     /product="trifunctional methionine sulfoxide reductase A / 
                     protein-methionine-S-oxide reductase / methionine sulfoxide
                     reductase"
                     /EC_number="1.8.4.11; 1.8.4.13"
                     /label=msrA
                     /note="MsrA is one of several methionine sulfoxide
                     reductases in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: [12504094][15601707]|. 
                     Methionine residues in proteins, including the N-terminal 
                     methionine, can be damaged by oxidation and can be repaired
                     (reduced) by methionine sulfoxide reductases |CITS: 
                     [15057280]|. Modification of chemically oxidized N-terminal
                     methionines by formylation or acetylation significantly 
                     increases the catalytic efficiency of reduction by MsrA and
                     |FRAME: EG12394-MONOMER MsrB| in redox kinetic studies 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [39499632]|. MsrA and MsrB are also 
                     important for keeping |FRAME: EG10823-MONOMER RecA| active 
                     under oxidative stress conditions |CITS: [33605213]|. 
                     Conversely, the transcription factor |FRAME: G7924 HypT| is
                     activated by methionine oxidation and inactivated by 
                     MsrA/MsrB activity |CITS: [23690622]|.
                     MsrA appears to use |FRAME: RED-THIOREDOXIN-MONOMER 
                     thioredoxin 1| (Trx1) as the electron donor <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [3533930][11604533][21570393]|. Overexpressed 
                     |FRAME: RED-THIOREDOXIN2-MONOMER thioredoxin 2| (Trx2) can 
                     substitute for Trx1 |CITS: [21570393]|.
                     Cys51, Cys198, and Cys206 are involved in catalysis. A 
                     three-step ping-pong reaction mechanism involving a 
                     sulfenic acid intermediate on Cys51 has been proposed 
                     |CITS: [10964927][11604533]|. The N and C termini of MsrA 
                     are not essential for reductase activity (with DTT), though
                     the C terminus is required for thioredoxin-dependent 
                     reduction, and a mutant protein lacking both termini 
                     displays kinetic defects |CITS: [11604533]|. The 
                     rate-limiting step is associated with recycling of 
                     thioredoxin |CITS: [24632144]|. A crystal structure is 
                     presented at 1.9 &Aring; resolution |CITS: 
                     [10957644][11080639]|. Solution structures of both the 
                     reduced and oxidized forms of MsrA provide further insight 
                     into the catalytic mechanism |CITS: [17157315]|.
                     Some organisms produce a methionine sulfoxide reductase 
                     composed of two domains with similarity to MsrA and MsrB, 
                     respectively, whereas <i>E. coli</i> produces these two 
                     distinct polypeptides. MsrB exhibits 1000-fold lower 
                     catalytic efficiency than MsrA toward free methionine 
                     sulfoxide |CITS: [11677230]|. MsrA and MsrB exhibit similar
                     activity (quantitatively) toward an oxidized peptide 
                     substrate, though they appear to exhibit some differences 
                     in specificity for substrate sites within the peptide 
                     |CITS: [11677230][15057280]|. The <i>E. coli</i> MsrA and 
                     MsrB polypeptides do not interact with each other <i>in 
                     vitro</i> or in a yeast two-hybrid test |CITS: [11677230]|.
                     MsrA appears to have some specificity for the <i>S</i> 
                     enantiomer of methionine sulfoxide |CITS: 
                     [10799493][12504094]|. Additional methionine sulfoxide 
                     reductase activities have been observed in a <i>msrA 
                     msrB</i> double mutant, including activities toward both 
                     the met-<i>R</i>-(o) and met-<i>S</i>-(o) enantiomers of 
                     free (non-peptide) methionine sulfoxide (fRMsr and fSMsr 
                     activities) |CITS: [12504094]|, a soluble NADPH-dependent 
                     activity toward the met-<i>S</i>-(o) enantiomer of 
                     peptide-linked methionine sulfoxide (MsrA1 activity) |CITS:
                     [12504094][12604343]|, and a membrane-associated 
                     NADPH-dependent activity toward both met-<i>S</i>-(o) and 
                     met-<i>R</i>-(o) enantiomers of peptide-linked and free 
                     methionine sulfoxide |CITS: [12504094][12604343]|. 
                     Production of MsrA protein is regulated by growth phase 
                     |CITS: [7836279]|. An <i>msrA</i> mutant shows increased 
                     sensitivity to oxidative stress |CITS: [7836279][11481433]|
                     as well as increased sensitivity to nitrite and 
                     <i>S</i>-nitrosoglutathione, which are released by 
                     activated macrophages during infection |CITS: [11481433]|. 
                     <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i>, <i>Neisseria 
                     gonorrhoeae</i>, and <i>E. coli</i> (strain MC1061) MsrA 
                     proteins are involved in host cell adhesion |CITS: 
                     [8755589]|.
                     A methionine auxotroph can utilize |FRAME: CPD0-1959| for 
                     growth |CITS: [37234][3533930]|.
                     MsrA: ""<b>m</b>ethionine <b>s</b>ulfoxide <b>r</b>eductase
                     <b>A</b>"" |CITS: [8113203]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [11795868][15680228][15680230][18302927][25963551]|"
