     gene            4432199..4433932
                     /gene="tamA"
                     /locus_tag="b4220"
                     /label=tamA
     CDS             4432199..4433932
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tamA"
                     /locus_tag="b4220"
                     /product="bifunctional translocation and assembly module
                     subunit TamA / translocation and assembly module"
                     /label=tamA
                     /note="TamA, a member of the Omp85 superfamily 
                     |CITS:[17090219][35650668]|, localizes to the outer 
                     membrane in <I>E. coli</I> K-12 |CITS:[16522795], 
                     [22466966]|.
                     TamA forms a complex (the translocation and assembly module
                     or TAM) with the inner membrane protein, TamB. TAM is 
                     responsible for the efficient secretion of the |FRAME: 
                     G7080-MONOMER adhesin protein Ag43| in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [22466966]|. The TAM complex may facilitate the 
                     movement of phospholipids between the inner and outer 
                     membrane |CITS: [34781743]|.
                     TamA consists of three periplasmic POTRA (polypeptide 
                     transport associated) domains in tandem joined to a 
                     16-stranded &beta;-barrel in the outer membrane. The three 
                     POTRA domains adopt a C-shape arrangement extending into 
                     the periplasm; they may use a strategy of &beta;-strand 
                     augmentation to guide substrate molecules toward the 
                     barrel. The TamA &beta;-barrel is partly occluded at the 
                     periplasmic face and completely closed at the extracellular
                     face. A cleft in the &beta;-barrel located near the POTRA3 
                     attachment site may represent the access gate for TamA 
                     substrate |CITS: [24056943]|. TamA contains a lateral gate 
                     located between the first and last &beta;-strands in the 
                     barrel domain; the Tam substrate FimD, interacts with Tam 
                     at the lateral gate region |CITS: [28752534]|. The lateral 
                     gate of TamA interacts directly with TolC |CITS: 
                     [35061668]|. TamA lacking the three POTRA domains fails to 
                     bind TamB |CITS: [25341963]|.
                     A <i>tamA</i> knockout mutant shows a strong reduction in 
                     growth, but no identifiable defects in membrane composition
                     or integrity |CITS:[17214547]|."
