     gene            4433929..4437708
                     /gene="tamB"
                     /locus_tag="b4221"
                     /label=tamB
     CDS             4433929..4437708
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="tamB"
                     /locus_tag="b4221"
                     /product="bifunctional translocation and assembly module
                     subunit TamB / translocation and assembly module"
                     /label=tamB
                     /note="TamB is an inner membrane protein that forms a
                     complex (the translocation and assembly module or TAM) with
                     the outer membrane protein, TamA. TAM is responsible for 
                     the efficient secretion of the adhesin protein Ag43 in 
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 |CITS: [22466966]|. The TAM complex may
                     facilitate the movement of phospholipids between the inner 
                     and outer membrane |CITS: [34781743]|.
                     TamB contains a signal sequence and two predicted 
                     transmembrane segments. TamB retains the N-terminal signal 
                     anchor sequence that includes N-terminal formylmethionine 
                     |CITS: [22466966]|. TamB is an elongate molecule spanning 
                     the periplasmic space to contact the POTRA domains of TamA 
                     |CITS: [25341963]|. The very C-terminal portion of TamB may
                     form several amphipathic &beta;-strands |CITS: [25994932]|.
                     TamB may act as a conduit for membrane proteins, shielding 
                     them from the aqueous environment of the periplasm |CITS: 
                     [29129383]|.
                     TamB was present very early in the evolution of bacteria; 
                     the TamB family is defined by conserved features including 
                     a signal anchor domain, &beta;-helical structure and a 
                     C-terminal region that may form several amphipathic 
                     &beta;-strands |CITS: [25994932]|. <i>tamB</i> exhibits 
                     mosaic evolution patterns in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [40444982]|.
                     TamB, |FRAME: G7690-MONOMER YhdP|, |FRAME: G6703-MONOMER 
                     YdbH|, |FRAME: EG11684-MONOMER YicH| and |FRAME: 
                     EG12251-MONOMER YhjG| are |FRAME: EG11361-MONOMER 
                     AsmA|-like paralogs predicted to contain an N-terminal 
                     inner membrane anchor and a large periplasmic region with 
                     chorein-N and AsmA-like domains; the combined loss of TamB,
                     YhdP, and YdbH is lethal; the three proteins are crucial 
                     for maintaining lipid homeostasis at the OM and are 
                     functionally redundant but not equivalent; TamB, YhdP and 
                     YdbH may be intermembrane bridge proteins that facilitate 
                     the movement of phospholipids between the inner and outer 
                     membrane |CITS: [34781743]| (see also |CITS: [35226662)]|. 
                     Genetic interaction data suggests that the three proteins 
                     may differ in substrate specificity; TamB may transport 
                     phospholipids with more unsaturated fatty acids |CITS: 
                     [38913742]|."
