     gene            complement(4437793..4438062)
                     /gene="yzfA"
                     /locus_tag="b4223"
                     /label=yzfA
                     /note="<i>yzfA</i> was originally described as the
                     <i>ileR</i> locus |CITS: [3525538]|.  However, in a 
                     personal communication to Kenn Rudd (see the EcoGene 
                     database entry), Ron Somerville stated that <i>yzfA</i> is 
                     not the IleR structural gene.
                     <i>yfzA</i> was deaccessioned in ASAP in 2007.  Comment in 
                     ASAP: ""yzfA (b4223) is no longer thought to be an actual 
                     gene and has been deaccessioned; this reading frame is on 
                     the opposite strand of ytfP""
                     Eric Neeno-Eckwall, personal communication 11/7/2018: YtfP 
                     is highly conserved among the enterobacteria, with 
                     conservation of genome context as far out as Vibrio. I 
                     looked at a couple of examples, and the coding sequence of 
                     yzfA is not conserved. It would be a pseudogene in Dickeya,
                     and would have a long C-terminal extension in Salmonella 
                     (and is not predicted by GeneMark). A version in 
                     Escherichia albertii (87% nucleotide identity for ytfP) 
                     would require a different start codon and is also not 
                     predicted by GeneMark.
                     Previous UniProt ID: P08339"
                     /note="/phantom"
