                     /label=mpl
     CDS             4445602..4446975
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mpl"
                     /locus_tag="b4233"
                     /product="trifunctional UDP-N-acetylmuramate--L-alanyl-gam 
                     ma-D-glutamyl-meso-2,6-diaminoheptanedioate ligase / 
                     UDP-N-acetylmuramate:L-alanyl-gamma-D-glutamyl-me 
                     so-diaminopimelate ligase / 
                     UDP-N-acetylmuramate:L-alanyl-gamma-D-glutamyl-me 
                     so-diaminoheptanedioate-D-alanine ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.3.2.45"
                     /label=mpl
                     /note="UDP-<i>N</i>-acetylmuramate:L-alanyl-&gamma;-D-glut 
                     amyl-<i>meso</i>-diaminopimelate ligase (Mpl) acts in 
                     murein recycling by enabling ligation of the tripeptide 
                     L-Ala-D-Glu-<i>meso</i>A<sub>2</sub>pm with UDP-MurNAc and 
                     reincorporation into peptidoglycan. 
                     L-Ala-D-Glu-<i>meso</i>A<sub>2</sub>pm is released while 
                     remodelling the cell wall during growth |CITS: [8808921]|. 
                     The enzyme can utilize tri-, tetra-, and pentapeptide <i>in
                     vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i>, but utilizes either the 
                     dipeptide L-Ala-D-Glu or L-Ala alone at a much lower rate. 
                     The substrate specificity of Mpl has been investigated 
                     |CITS: [16377691][17384195][23270797]|.
                     An <i>mpl</i> null mutant shows no obvious growth defect, 
                     but has decreased levels of UDP-MurNAc-pentapeptide and 
                     increased levels of
                     L-Ala-D-Glu-<i>meso</i>A<sub>2</sub>pm |CITS: [8808921]|. 
                     Overexpression of <i>mpl</i> can complement the conditional
                     growth defect of a |FRAME: EG10619 <i>murC</i>| mutant, 
                     thus showing that Mpl is able to utilize L-alanine as a 
                     substrate both <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: 
                     [17384195]|.
                     Tpl: ""<b>t</b>ri<b>p</b>eptide <b>l</b>igase""<br>Mpl: 
                     ""<b>m</b>urein <b>p</b>eptide <b>l</b>igase"" |CITS: 
                     [8808921]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [9158731][18535144][26443726]|"
