     gene            complement(4450339..4452477)
                     /gene="nrdD"
                     /locus_tag="b4238"
                     /label=nrdD
     CDS             complement(4450339..4452477)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="nrdD"
                     /locus_tag="b4238"
                     /product="multifunctional anaerobic
                     ribonucleoside-triphosphate reductase / 
                     ribonucleoside-triphosphate reductase / ATP reductase / GTP
                     reductase / UTP reductase / CTP reductase / anaerobic 
                     nucleoside-triphosphate reductase activating system"
                     /EC_number="1.1.98.6"
                     /label=nrdD
                     /note="Ribonucleotide reductases (RNRs) catalyze the
                     conversion of ribonucleotides into deoxyribonucleotides, 
                     which are required for DNA synthesis and repair. Nucleotide
                     reduction involves a transient protein-based thiyl radical 
                     that abstracts a hydrogen atom from the 2'-position of the 
                     nucleotide. Three classes of RNR enzymes have been 
                     identified; they differ in the metallocofactor required for
                     generating the thiyl radical. <i>E. coli</i> contains three
                     RNRs, the class Ia |FRAME: 
                     RIBONUCLEOSIDE-DIP-REDUCTI-CPLX|, the class Ib |FRAME: 
                     RIBONUCLEOSIDE-DIP-REDUCTII-CPLX|, and the class III 
                     anaerobic ribonucleoside-triphosphate reductase (NrdD) 
                     described here. The existence of an RNR specifically active
                     under anaerobic conditions was first shown by |CITS: 
                     [2833197][2648390][2673261]|.
                     The NrdD reductase is activated by a tightly associated 
                     activase enzyme (NrdG) under anaerobic conditions |CITS: 
                     [7852304]| and is inactivated by oxygen. Exposure to oxygen
                     results in C-terminal truncation of the protein |CITS: 
                     [8421692]| by cleavage on the N-terminal side of the glycyl
                     radical at position 681 |CITS: [7826394]|. Activation of 
                     the enzyme involves generation of a glycine radical at the 
                     C2 position of Gly681 |CITS: [8421692][8636106]|. A G681A 
                     mutant lacks the glycyl radical and has no enzymatic 
                     activity |CITS: [8636106]|. A multi-component system is 
                     responsible for activation of the enzyme.  Electrons are 
                     transferred from NADPH by |FRAME: FLAVONADPREDUCT-MONOMER| 
                     to flavodoxin |CITS: [8267617][8449868]|.
                     NrdD was initially thought to contain an iron-sulfur 
                     cluster |CITS: [8381402]|; however, it was later found that
                     the signal was likely due to the presence of an iron-sulfur
                     cluster in the NrdG protein |CITS: [8621608]|. NrdD reduces
                     all four common ribonucleotides; its catalytic activity is 
                     allosterically regulated. Competition experiments suggest 
                     two binding sites for modulators |CITS: [7929317]|. The 
                     reaction mechanism of NrdD has been investigated |CITS: 
                     [7929323][7669047]|. The hydrogen donor of the reductase 
                     reaction is formate |CITS: [7568012]|.
                     This enzyme is a class III ribonucleotide reductase that is
                     essential for anaerobic growth. In <i>E. coli</i> strain 
                     DHB4, construction of deletion mutants and analysis of 
                     their growth characteristics showed that the main 
                     ribonucleotide reductase activity in strict anaerobiosis is
                     provided by the products of <i>nrdDG</i>. Under highly 
                     restricted oxygen condtions, |FRAME: 
                     RIBONUCLEOSIDE-DIP-REDUCTI-CPLX| can support some growth in
                     a strain containing an <i>nrdDG</i> deletion. Either 
                     thioredoxin reductase or glutathione reductase are required
                     for anaerobic growth, suggesting that these reductive 
                     pathways contribute to enzyme activity |CITS: [16771665]|.
                     Binding of Zn<sup>2+</sup> to NrdD protects the protein 
                     from proteolytic cleavage and plays a role in the formation
                     of the glycyl radical |CITS: [19381696]|.
                     An <i>nrdD</i> null mutant does not grow under entirely 
                     anaerobic conditions, but grows under aerobic or 
                     microaerophilic conditions due to the activity of NrdAB 
                     |CITS: [8954104]|.
                     Ribonucleotide reductase catalyzes the rate-limiting step 
                     in DNA biosynthesis. Its central role in DNA
                     replication and repair makes its regulation important to 
                     ensure appropriate pools of deoxyribonucleotides for these 
                     processes. Three major classes I, II and III have been 
                     designated that share similar catalytic mechanisms. 
                     Enterobacteria, including <i>Escherichia coli</i> and 
                     <i>Salmonella enterica</i> serovar Typhimurium contain 
                     class Ia (encoded by |FRAME: EG10660| and |FRAME: 
                     EG10661|), class Ib (encoded by |FRAME: EG20257|
                     and |FRAME: EG12381|) and class III (encoded by |FRAME: 
                     EG11417|) enzymes. Class Ia and Ib enzymes are
                     active under aerobic conditions, while class III enzymes 
                     are inactivated by oxygen and function under strictly 
                     anaerobic conditions. Although there are differences in 
                     structure and cofactor use, their catalytic mechanisms 
                     involve a transient cysteinyl radical at the active site 
                     that inititates ribonucleotide reduction. Regeneration of 
                     the
                     enzymes is accomplished by corresponding reductive enzyme 
                     systems |CITS: [16771665]| and discussed
                     in |CITS: [17496099]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8473279][12206460][20494104]|"
