     gene            complement(4470799..4473654)
                     /gene="valS"
                     /locus_tag="b4258"
                     /label=valS
     CDS             complement(4470799..4473654)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="valS"
                     /locus_tag="b4258"
                     /product="multifunctional L-homocysteine cyclase / 
                     L-threonine--tRNA(Val) ligase / L-threonyl-[tRNA(Val)] 
                     deacylase / valine--tRNA ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.1.1.9"
                     /label=valS
                     /note="Valine-tRNA ligase (ValRS) is a member of the
                     family of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, which interpret the 
                     genetic code by covalently linking amino acids to their 
                     specific tRNA molecules.  The reaction is driven by ATP 
                     hydrolysis.  ValRS belongs to the Class I aminoacyl-tRNA 
                     synthetases |CITS: [2203971][7647112]|.  ValRS contains a 
                     conserved proline triplet that is critical for activity; it
                     therefore requires |FRAME: EG12099-MONOMER EF-P| for 
                     expression, which may explain evolution of the EF-P system 
                     |CITS: [25310979]|.
                     ValRS is a monomer in solution |CITS: [4897802]|.  A single
                     binding site for tRNA<sup>Val</sup> was thought to exist 
                     |CITS: [4897800]|, but evidence for a second binding site 
                     has been reported |CITS: [11434781]|.  The reaction 
                     mechanism of ValRS includes the formation of an aminoacyl 
                     adenylate intermediate, which then serves as the animo acid
                     donor in the aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase reaction |CITS: 
                     [165186][320199][199234][391274]|.  The enzyme rapidly 
                     forms a valyl-adenylate and binds a second molecule of 
                     valine slowly |CITS: [199234]|.  Residues in contact with 
                     valine and residues that may be part of the editing site 
                     have been determined |CITS: [10903513]|.  The ATP binding 
                     site of ValRS was determined by affinity labeling |CITS: 
                     [2271710]|.  Investigation of pre-steady-state and single 
                     turnover kinetics of ValRS show that the enzyme exhibits 
                     burst kinetics and is rate-limited by release of 
                     aminoacyl-tRNA, distinguishing it from class II aminoacyl 
                     tRNA synthetases |CITS: [16843487]|.
                     Specificity determinants within tRNA<sup>Val</sup> that are
                     important for recognition by ValRS have been identified 
                     |CITS: [1847071][2049085][8071341][7937960][939 
                     6792][10387013][11434781]|. The anticodon is sufficient for
                     distinguishing tRNA<sup>Met</sup> and tRNA<sup>Val</sup> 
                     |CITS: [3055296][2023934]|.  ValRS specifically acylates 
                     the 2'-OH of tRNA<sup>Val</sup>, while the 3'-OH is 
                     important for preventing misacylation |CITS: [11923317]|.  
                     The U73 mutant of tRNA<sup>Val</sup> is valylated at either
                     the 2' or the 3'-OH group |CITS: [15826650]|.
                     Many aminoacyl tRNA synthetases have been shown to have 
                     editing functions.  ValRS from  E. coli was shown to 
                     misactivates threonine and &alpha;-aminobutyrate |CITS: 
                     [4897802][329276][371673]| and the enzyme from yellow lupin
                     was shown to activate cysteine |CITS: [6257275]|, but these
                     misacylated tRNAs are hydrolyzed by a post-transfer editing
                     mechanism |CITS: [371673][2261451]|. Editing occurs almost 
                     exclusively via post-transfer hydrolysis of the mischarged 
                     aminoacyl-tRNA within the editing domain |CITS: 
                     [12034843][20498377]|.  The editing site of ValRS 
                     recognizes adenosine at the 3' end of tRNA |CITS: 
                     [11434781]|; further requirements for editing have been 
                     determined |CITS: [14596614][14970394]|.  The nature of the
                     mischarged tRNA does not influence its transfer to the 
                     editing site on ValRS |CITS: [10792042]|.  Mutations that 
                     allow charging of tRNA<sup>Val</sup> with cysteine have 
                     been identified and lie in the editing site of ValRS |CITS:
                     [11313495]|; the Lys277 residue is important for the 
                     editing activity |CITS: [12475234]|.  D286A, K277P, and 
                     K277P/D286A mutants within the post-transfer editing domain
                     are unable to deacylate misacylated tRNA |CITS: 
                     [20498377]|.  Isoleucine is selected against by a steric 
                     exclusion mechanism |CITS: [375976]|.  The 
                     &beta;-phosphate-&gamma;-phosphate interchange reaction of 
                     ATP contributes to the prevention of tRNA aminoacylation by
                     non-cognate amino acids |CITS: [6258639]|.
                     During infection of <i>E. coli</i> with bacteriophage T4, 
                     ValRS is modified by the tau peptide, changing its 
                     properties |CITS: [19475][330535][6992274]|.
                     There is a general discrepancy between k<sub>cat</sub> 
                     values of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases that were measured 
                     <i>in vitro</i> and values that were calculated as needed 
                     to support measured growth rates |CITS: [6373741]|. 
                     Modeling of these parameters in <i>E. coli</i> has found 
                     that the required k<sub>cat</sub> values are on average 
                     7.6-fold higher than those measured <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: 
                     [37224536]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [10966471][11330299][23075299]|"
