     gene            complement(4507531..4508052)
                     /gene="fecI"
                     /locus_tag="b4293"
                     /label=fecI
     CDS             complement(4507531..4508052)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fecI"
                     /locus_tag="b4293"
                     /product="bifunctional RNA polymerase sigma factor FecI /
                     RNA polymerase sigma 19"
                     /label=fecI
                     /note="FecI (&sigma;<sup>19</sup>) belongs to the
                     subfamily of ""iron starvation sigmas"" within the 
                     Extracytoplasmic Function (ECF) family of sigma factors 
                     |CITS: [8596456][12207687]|.  The only known target for 
                     transcription initiation by |FRAME: CPLX0-221| is the 
                     <i>fecABCDE</i> operon promoter |CITS: [10648550]|.  This 
                     limited role is typical for the minor sigma factors.
                     FecI was first described as a protein that regulates the 
                     expression of genes involved in the transport of ferric 
                     citrate |CITS: [2836368]| and was later shown to be the 
                     sigma factor that enables transcription from the <i>fec</i>
                     transport operon promoter upstream of <i>fecA</i> |CITS: 
                     [2254251][7752886][7557462][8596456]|. <i>In vitro</i>, 
                     transcription by |FRAME: CPLX0-221| is highest at 25&deg;C 
                     and is stimulated by the presence of potassium glutamate 
                     |CITS: [10648550]|.
                     Three proteins, |FRAME: EG10286-MONOMER FecA| in the outer 
                     membrane, |FRAME: EG10292-MONOMER FecR| in the cytoplasmic 
                     membrane, and FecI in the cytoplasm, form a signaling 
                     pathway that responds to the presence of iron(III) 
                     dicitrate and induces expression of ferric citrate 
                     transport genes (most recently reviewed in |CITS: 
                     [31187912][17673165]|).  The periplasmic N terminus of FecA
                     interacts with the C terminus of FecR, and the cytoplasmic 
                     N terminus of FecR binds FecI |CITS: [10633096]|.  
                     Mutations in FecR can be compensated by missense mutations 
                     in the putative RNA polymerase-binding region 2.1 of FecI; 
                     these mutations don't restore the FecR-FecI interaction 
                     |CITS: [11114913]|.  The FecI-FecR interaction site maps to
                     region 4 of FecI |CITS: [12057967]|.  The cytoplasmic 
                     domain of FecR stimulates the interaction of FecI with 
                     |FRAME:  RPOC-MONOMER| |CITS: [12618442]|.
                     Overexpression of FecI promotes a partial destabilization 
                     of ribosomal RNA (rrn) operon clustering, although with 
                     less intensity than RpoH |CITS:[40245090]|. This effect is 
                     reversed by the simultaneous expression of RpoD, suggesting
                     that FecI competes with sigma<sup>D</sup> for the core RNA 
                     polymerase (RNAP) |CITS:[40245090]|. Similar to RpoH, FecI 
                     is associated with the inner membrane, supporting a model 
                     in which membrane localization is relevant for the spatial 
                     organization of <i>rrn</i> operons |CITS:[40245090]|.
                     Transcription of <i>fecI</i> is not autoregulated |CITS: 
                     [8602163]|, but may be upregulated by RpoS during 
                     exponential growth |CITS: [18158608]|.  Under iron-replete 
                     conditions, expression of <i>fecIR</i> and <i>fecABCDE</i> 
                     is repressed by Fur-Fe<sup>2+</sup> |CITS: 
                     [9575233][17921503]|.  FecI levels appear to decrease in 
                     stationary phase cells |CITS: [10648550]|.  FecI belongs to
                     the polyamine modulon; its expression is enhanced by 
                     polyamines at the level of translation |CITS: 
                     [15326188][17220219]|.
                     <i>fecI</i> mutants are unable to grow with iron(III) 
                     dicitrate as the sole source of iron |CITS: [2254251]| and 
                     do not express the <i>fecABCDE</i> ferric citrate transport
                     genes |CITS: [8602163]|.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [31187912][17673165][17370038][16718597][1 
                     6428314][16333751][16102597][12732308][12207687][9148773]| 
                     "
