     gene            4530774..4531376
                     /gene="fimB"
                     /locus_tag="b4312"
                     /label=fimB
                     /note="<i>fimB</i> transcription is regulated by growth
                     phase and its repression require the alternative sigma 
                     factor, RpoS during enter stationary phase. Although the 
                     mechanism by which RpoS achieves negative control is 
                     unknown it is possible that the effect on <i>fimB</i> is 
                     exerted indirectly |CITS:[9108285]|.
                     By making use of microarray analysis, Constantinidou et al 
                     |CITS: [16377617]| concluded that FNR represses <i>fimB</i>
                     gene expression, but it is not know which of the three 
                     promoters (<i>fimB</i>p1, <i>fimB</i>p2, <i>fimB</i>p3) is 
                     affected. They also identified a putative FNR binding site 
                     upstream of the gene, but the sequence was not shown."
     CDS             4530774..4531376
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fimB"
                     /locus_tag="b4312"
                     /product="Type 1 fimbriae regulatory protein FimB"
                     /label=fimB
                     /note="FimB, along with FimE, is a recombinase in
                     <I>Escherichia coli</I> which catalyzes the site-specific 
                     recombination required for inversion of a 314-bp invertible
                     DNA element, the <I>fim</I> switch (<I>fimS</I>), to 
                     control transcription of the type I fimbrial structural 
                     genes--a process known as phase-variation switching. 
                     <I>fimS</I> contains the promoter for expression of the 
                     fimbrial structural genes. The promoter is positioned to 
                     allow expression of <I>fimAICDFGH</I> when <I>fimS</I> is 
                     in the ON orientation, but not when it is in the OFF 
                     orientation. FimB is responsible for both OFF-to-ON and 
                     ON-to-OFF switching at equal rates. FimE is responsible for
                     primarily ON-to-OFF switching.
                     FimB and FimE share significant homology with the lambda 
                     integrase family of site-specific recombinases 
                     |CITS:[2886490]|.
                     Analyses of <I>fimB</I> and <I>fimE</I> mutants and direct 
                     assays indicate that <I>fimB</I> is able to mediate 
                     recombination in both directions (on-to-off and off-to-on) 
                     while <I>fimE</I> only stimulates recombination in the 
                     on-to-off direction |CITS:[2874022],[2571915],[1679430],[8 
                     898385],[8101185],[8878036]|. Extensive analysis of 
                     <I>fimS</I> has been performed showing the specificity of 
                     FimB and FimE for <I>fimS</I> sequences, the effect of 
                     sequence alterations on the ability of FimB and FimE to 
                     catalyze inversion, the effects of temperature and media on
                     inversions, and the effects of alterations in the way other
                     accessory proteins like Lrp, IHF, and H-NS are able to 
                     interact with this system 
                     |CITS:[2863818],[2886490],[2888114],[1348054],[1362 
                     708],[8093239],[8104927],[7510658],[7916011],[7715458],[915
                     7242],[9515701],[10792731],[16159759],[16855224],[18048927]
                     ,[18034794]|. Protein fusion studies indicate that 
                     <I>fimB</I> expression is suppressed by the lysosomal 
                     enzyme <I>N</I>-acetyl-<I>B</I>-glucosaminidase 
                     |CITS:[15534208]|. Expression of <I>fimB</I> is regulated 
                     by N-acetylglucosamine (GlcNAc) by preventing NagC 
                     activation of transcription from two NagC binding sites 
                     located upstream of <I>fimB</I> |CITS:[15534208]|. IHF 
                     binds at <I>ibs</I> between the NagC sites and enhances 
                     cooperative binding of NagC at the two NagC binding sites 
                     |CITS:[17238917]|. Sialic acid also regulates expression of
                     <I>fimB</I> by preventing NanR and NagC activation of 
                     transcription from binding sites upstream of <I>fimB</I> 
                     |CITS:[12890032],[15534208],[17238917]|. Mutation of these 
                     binding sites reduces <I>fimB</I> expression 
                     |CITS:[17238917]|.  Expression of <I>fimB</I> is also 
                     controlled by (p)ppGpp, and mutants lacking (p)ppGpp have 
                     reduced transcription of the gene |CITS:[16796685]|. 
                     <I>fimB</I> and <I>fimE</I> are repressed by H-NS 
                     |CITS:[9352908],[9595659],[9922259]|. RpoS is also involved
                     in negative regulation of <I>fimB</I>.
                     Expression of <I>fimB</I> and <I>fimE</I> are controlled by
                     RcsB during growth at neutral pH by increasing <I>fimB</I> 
                     transcription or decreasing <I>fimE</I> transcription 
                     depending on salt concentration |CITS:[17644608]|. The 
                     effects of pH and osmolarity on <I>fimB</I> and <I>fimE</I>
                     expression have also been studied in uropathogenic <I>E. 
                     coli</I> |CITS:[11854225]|."
