     CDS             4531854..4532450
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fimE"
                     /locus_tag="b4313"
                     /product="regulator for fimA"
                     /label=fimE
                     /note="FimE, along with FimB, is a recombinase in
                     <I>Escherichia coli</I> which catalyzes the site-specific 
                     recombination required for inversion of a 314-bp invertible
                     DNA element, the <I>fim</I> switch (<I>fimS</I>), to 
                     control transcription of the type I fimbrial structural 
                     genes--a process known as phase-variation switching.  
                     <I>fimS</I> contains the promoter for expression of the 
                     fimbrial structural genes. The promoter is positioned to 
                     allow expression of <I>fimAICDFGH</I> when <I>fimS</I> is 
                     in the ON orientation, but not when it is in the OFF 
                     orientation. FimB is responsible for both OFF-to-ON and 
                     ON-to-OFF switching at equal rates.  FimE is responsible 
                     for primarily ON-to-OFF switching.
                     FimB and FimE share significant homology with the lambda 
                     integrase family of site-specific recombinases 
                     |CITS:[2886490]|.
                     Analyses of <I>fimB</I> and <I>fimE</I> mutants and direct 
                     assays indicate that <I>fimB</I> is able to mediate 
                     recombination in both directions (on-to-off and off-to-on) 
                     while <I>fimE</I> primarily stimulates recombination in the
                     on-to-off direction |CITS:[2874022],[2571915],[1679429 
                     ],[1679430],[8101185],[8878036],[10096084]|. <I>fimE</I> 
                     mutants do arise which are able to perform OFF-to-ON 
                     switching |CITS:[10594822],[10620686]|. Extensive analysis 
                     of <I>fimS</I> has been performed showing the specificity 
                     of FimB and FimE for <I>fimS</I> sequences, the effect of 
                     sequence alterations on the ability of FimB and FimE to 
                     catalyze inversion, the effects of temperature and media on
                     inversions, and the effects of alterations in the way other
                     accessory proteins like Lrp, IHF, and H-NS are able to 
                     interact with this system 
                     |CITS:[2863818],[2886490],[2888114],[1348054],[1362 
                     708],[8093239],[8104927],[7510658],[7916011],[7715458],[915
                     7242],[9515701],[16159759],[18034794],[18048927],[10792731]
                     |. <I>fimS</I> also acts as a phase-variable switch with 
                     respect to <I>fimE</I> mRNA stability 
                     |CITS:[10096084],[11703669],[12180928],[16321930]|.  In the
                     OFF position, <I>fimE</I> transcripts are terminated by a 
                     Rho-dependent terminator within <I>fimS</I> and are much 
                     less stable than transcripts produced with <I>fimS</I> in 
                     the ON position |CITS:[12180928],[16321930]|.  The 
                     reduction in FimE allows FimB to increase OFF-to-ON 
                     inversions and induce fimbrial gene expression.  Expression
                     of <I>fimE</I> is positively regulated by LrhA preventing 
                     fimbrial gene expression |CITS:[16207912]|.  <I>fimB</I> 
                     and <I>fimE</I> are repressed by H-NS 
                     |CITS:[9595659],[9922259]|. Expression of <I>fimB</I> and 
                     <I>fimE</I> are controlled by RcsB during growth at neutral
                     pH by increasing <I>fimB</I> transcription or decreasing 
                     <I>fimE</I> transcription depending on salt concentration 
                     |CITS:[17644608]|.  The effects of pH and osmolarity on 
                     <I>fimB</I> and <I>fimE</I> expression have also been 
                     studied in uropathogenic <I>E. coli</I> |CITS:[11854225]|."
