     gene            4532932..4533480
                     /gene="fimA"
                     /locus_tag="b4314"
                     /label=fimA
     CDS             4532932..4533480
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fimA"
                     /locus_tag="b4314"
                     /product="bifunctional type 1 fimbriae major subunit /
                     fimbrial complex"
                     /label=fimA
                     /note="FimA, the major subunit of the <i>Escherichia
                     coli</i> type 1 fimbriae (pili), has been identified and 
                     its gene sequence determined |CITS: [6148338][2858471]|.
                     A typical pilus is composed of around 1,000 subunits of 
                     FimA which form a right-handed helical rod |CITS: 
                     [1355769]| connected to a short tip fibrillum composed of 
                     the adaptor proteins FimG and FimF, and the adhesin FimH 
                     attached at its distal end |CITS: [7892228]|.  The 
                     <i>fimA</i> promoter is contained within <i>fimS</i>, the 
                     <i>fim</i> switch, which controls transcription of 
                     <i>fimA</i> and the genes for the other fimbrial structural
                     proteins by undergoing a site-specific inversion |CITS: 
                     [2863818]|.
                     Monomeric FimA is the dominant protein in K-12 MG1655 outer
                     membrane vesicles (OMVs); disruption of fimbrial assembly 
                     alters OMV cargo |CITS: [33643229]|. Deletion of 
                     <i>fimA</i> impairs pili-dependent surface motility (PDSM) 
                     in <i>E. coli</i> strain W3110 |CITS: [40178519]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains 12 chaperone-usher fimbrial 
                     operons, including <i>fimAICDFGH</i>; a fimbria-lacking 
                     mutant - strain WQM026 - created by deleting all 12 operons
                     has been characterised |CITS: [33863704]|."
