     gene            4534913..4537549
                     /gene="fimD"
                     /locus_tag="b4317"
                     /label=fimD
     CDS             4534913..4537549
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="fimD"
                     /locus_tag="b4317"
                     /product="type I fimbriae usher protein"
                     /label=fimD
                     /note="FimD is a member of the Fimbrial Usher Porin (FUP)
                     family |CITS: [12192075]|. FimD is the usher constituent in
                     the type 1 pilus (fimbrial) chaperone-usher pathway in 
                     <i>Escherichia coli</i>. During |FRAME: CPLX0-3401 ""type 1
                     pilus""| assembly, the chaperone-subunit complex composed 
                     of the FimC chaperone and the FimA, FimF, FimG or FimH 
                     subunit is targeted to the FimD usher which facilitates 
                     assembly of the pilus and subsequent translocation across 
                     the outer membrane |CITS: [12700251]|. Type 1 pili mediate 
                     recognition of and adhesion to, host tissues and much of 
                     our knowledge regarding type 1 pili in <i>E. coli</i> is 
                     derived from studies using uropathogenic strains.
                     FimD consists of 5 functional domains: an N-terminal domain
                     (NTD), a pore domain that, in the resting state, is 
                     occluded by a plug domain and two carboxy-terminal domains 
                     (CTD1 and CTD2) |CITS: [17066380][12842468][18485872]|. The
                     purified N-terminal 139 residues of FimD binds FimC-FimH 
                     and FimC-FimF complexes <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [12842468]|.
                     Cryo-electron microscopy of FimD bound to translocating 
                     FimC:F:G:H suggests that the functional usher forms twinned
                     translocation channels |CITS: [18485872]|.
                     Deletion of <i>fimD</i> results in cells that either lack 
                     fimbrial structures or the occasional presence of a 
                     singular abnormal fimbrium |CITS: [1970114]|. Following 
                     pre-incubation with the FimC-FimH (chaperone-adhesin) 
                     complex, purified FimD from <i>E. coli</i> K-12 strain 
                     W3110, assembles native-like pili from purified FimC-FimA 
                     proteins <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [18369105]|. <i>In 
                     vitro</i> assembly of pili from purified proteins proceeds 
                     independently of cellular energy |CITS: [18369105]| Subunit
                     polymerisation at FimD occurs via a mechanism known as 
                     donor strand exchange (DSE) whereby the N-terminal 
                     extension of an incoming subunit replaces the FimC 
                     chaperone strand that transiently occupies a groove in the 
                     previously assembled subunit |CITS: [12437927]|. Crystal 
                     structures of a pilus biogenesis initiation complex 
                     (FimD:C:H) |CITS: [12437927]| and a post initiation 
                     elongation complex (FimD:C:F:G:H) |CITS: [23579681]| have 
                     been obtained. In the first case FimH is captured inside 
                     the FimD pore and the usher plug is displaced from the 
                     barrel to the periplasm. The FimC-FimH complex is held in 
                     place by interactions with the two periplasmic carboxy 
                     terminal domains of FimD. The crystal structure reported by
                     |CITS: [23579681]| captures secretion of the pilus tip 
                     consisting of FimF:G:H through the usher pore. In this 
                     structure FimH is on the extracellular side of the pore, 
                     FimG is in the lumen of the pore and FimF is at the pore 
                     entry and remains in complex with the chaperone FimC.
                     A folding-unfolding cycle of the N-terminal tail of FimD 
                     (which precedes the NTD) is implicated in the recruitment 
                     and handover of chaperone-subunits that occurs at the usher
                     during pilus assembly |CITS: [30283140]|.
                     FimD is homologous with the PapC protein of the P pili 
                     usher protein of uropathogenic <i>E. coli</I> |CITS: 
                     [10066482]|. Assembly of the FimD usher is dependent on 
                     |FRAME: EG10985-MONOMER| and the |FRAME: CPLX0-3933 Bam 
                     complex| |CITS: [21784935]|.
                     <i>fimD</i> exhibits mosaic evolution patterns in <i>E. 
                     coli</i> |CITS: [40444982]|.
                     <i>E. coli</i> K-12 contains 12 chaperone-usher fimbrial 
                     operons, including <i>fimAICDFGH</i>; a fimbria-lacking 
                     mutant - strain WQM026 - created by deleting all 12 operons
                     has been characterised |CITS: [33863704]|. Reviews: |CITS: 
                     [19820722][21557063][22411982][22402496]|"
                     /translation="MSYLNLRLYQRNTQCLHIRKHRLAGFFVRLVVACAFAAQAPLSSA
                     DLYFNPRFLADDPQAVADLSRFENGQELPPGTYRVDIYLNNGYMATRDVTFNTGDSEQG
