     gene            complement(4557104..4558336)
                     /gene="mdtM"
                     /locus_tag="b4337"
                     /label=mdtM
     CDS             complement(4557104..4558336)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mdtM"
                     /locus_tag="b4337"
                     /product="multifunctional multidrug efflux pump / bile
                     salt:H(+) antiporter / Na(+):H(+) antiporter / K(+):H(+) 
                     antiporter / multidrug efflux pump MdtM / Na(+):H(+) 
                     antiporter MdtM / K(+):H(+) antiporter MdtM / bile 
                     salt:H(+) antiporter MdtM"
                     /label=mdtM
                     /note="The MdtM protein is a multidrug efflux protein that 
                     belongs to the major facilitator superfamily (MFS) of 
                     transporters |CITS:[10926494]|.
                     Overexpression of the cloned <I>mdtM</I> gene in a 
                     drug-sensitive strain results in a four-fold increase in 
                     resistance to acriflavine and a two-fold increase in 
                     resistance to chloramphenicol, ethidium bromide and 
                     tetraphenylphosphonium bromide (TPP) |CITS:[11566977]|. 
                     Strains lacking <i>mdtM</i> show impaired growth in the 
                     presence of ethidium bromide and chloramphenicol compared 
                     to wild type. Overexpression of cloned <i>mdtM</i> in both 
                     a wild type background and in an <i>mdtM</i> null 
                     background results in increased resistance to ethidium 
                     bromide and chloramphenicol |CITS: [22426385]|. MdtM is 
                     functional in whole cell ethidium bromide transport assays 
                     and efflux is disrupted by the addition of the ionophore, 
                     carbonyl cyanide 3-chlorophenylhydrazone (CCCP) |CITS: 
                     [22426385]|. MdtM is able to export a broad range of 
                     quaternary ammonium compounds (QACs) and contributes to the
                     intrinsic resistance of <i>E. coli</i> to these 
                     antimicrobial compounds |CITS: [23221628]|.
                     MdtM catalyses the efflux of unconjugated bile salts and 
                     contributes to cholate and deoxycholate resistance in <i>E.
                     coli</i> - this may be the natural physiological role of 
                     the transporter. Deletion of <i>mdtM</i> results in 
                     increased susceptibility to cholate and deoxycholate 
                     compared to wild-type. Purified MdtM binds cholate and 
                     deoxycholate with high affinity. MdtM catalyses 
                     electrogenic bile salt:H<sup>+</sup> exchange in inverted 
                     membrane vesicles at pH7.2. MdtM acts in synergy with the 
                     AcrAB/TolC multidrug efflux system to provide enhanced 
                     efflux of unconjugated bile salts in <i>E. coli</i> |CITS: 
                     [24684269]|.
                     MdtM also catalyses Na<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> and 
                     K<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> exchange and contributes to 
                     <i>E. coli's</i> adaptive response to alkaline 
                     environments. Strains lacking <i>mdtM</i> show growth 
                     inhibition at pH9 and pH9.75 compared to wild type. 
                     <i>mdtM</i> expressd from a plasmid confers 
                     alkali-tolerance to strains lacking chromosomally encoded 
                     <i>mdtM</i> or <i>mdfA</i>. MdtM also catalyses 
                     Rb<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> and 
                     Li<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> antiport, but not 
                     Ca<sup>2+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> antiport, <i>in vitro</i>. 
                     MdtM catalysed Na<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> and 
                     K<sup>+</sup>:H<sup>+</sup> exchange at alkaline pH is 
                     electrogenic but the exact stoichiometry is unknown |CITS: 
                     [23701827]|.
                     Purified MdtM is monomeric in 
                     dodecyl-&beta;-D-maltopyranoside solution and binds 
                     chloramphenicol with a dissociation constant 
                     (K<sub>d</sub>) of 0.17&micro;M |CITS: [22426385]|. The 
                     charged amino acid, aspartic acid 22 (D22), is important 
                     for function. Expression of a plasmid encoded D22A mutant 
                     protein in cells lacking chromosomally encoded MdtM results
                     in impaired growth in the presence of ethidium bromide and 
                     disrupts whole cell ethidium bromide transport assays. A 
                     D22A mutation disrupts chloramphenicol efflux, however a 
                     purified D22A mutant protein retains the ability to bind 
                     chloramphenicol (K<sub>d</sub> = 0.69 &micro;M) suggesting 
                     that the functional significance of D22 is not related to 
                     substrate recognition |CITS: [22426385]|.
                     MdtM expressed from a plasmid can compensate for the loss 
                     of the related multidrug efflux protein, |FRAME: 
                     CMR-MONOMER ""MdfA""| |CITS: [22426385]|.  Overexpression 
                     of <i>mdtM</i> increases the MICs for puromycin and 
                     lincomycin |CITS: [21173244]|.
                     MdtM is predicted to contain 12 transmembrane segments with
                     both the the N-termini and C-termini located in the 
                     cytoplasm |CITS: [22426385]|.
                     MdtM is implicated in the uptake of proline-rich 
                     antimicrobial peptides (PrAMPS) in cells lacking the 
                     peptide uptake permease, |FRAME: CPLX0-8097 ""SbmA""| 
                     |CITS: [26169420]|.
                     <i>mdtM</i> is one of 35 efflux-encoding genes that have 
                     been deleted or inactivated in 'Efflux KnockOut-35' 
                     (EKO-35) -  a K-12 BW25133-derived strain that lacks 35 
                     efflux pumps and can be used as a platform to study their 
                     function |CITS: [36065018][36318669]|.
                     Review: |CITS: [28962921]|"
