     gene            4576766..4577680
                     /gene="mrr"
                     /locus_tag="b4351"
                     /label=mrr
     CDS             4576766..4577680
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="mrr"
                     /locus_tag="b4351"
                     /product="Type IV methyl-directed restriction enzyme
                     EcoKMrr"
                     /label=mrr
                     /note="Mrr is a type IV restriction endonuclease involved
                     in restriction of <i>N<sup>6</sup></i>-adenine-methylated 
                     DNA |CITS: [3036779][1649819][1650347]| and 
                     <i>C<sup>5</sup></i>-cytosine-methylated DNA |CITS: 
                     [1650347][1830580]|.  Studies in which Mrr led to the 
                     induction of the SOS response suggest Mrr is an 
                     endonuclease that creates SOS-inducing double strand breaks
                     in DNA containing <i>N<sup>6</sup></i>-adenine-methylated 
                     DNA |CITS: [3036779]|.
                     Mrr is involved in the RecB-dependent high pressure 
                     induction of the SOS stress response in <i>E. coli</i>. 
                     <i>mrr</i> mutants were unable to induce the SOS response 
                     when exposed to high pressure while the SOS response 
                     resulting from direct damage to DNA remained unaffected. 
                     Expression of <i>mrr</i> does not increase upon high 
                     pressure treatment.  Overexpression of <i>mrr</i> resulted 
                     in an approximately 500-fold increase in high pressure 
                     sensitivity |CITS: [16313623]|.
                     Mrr colocalizes with the nucleoid.  Exposure to increased 
                     hydrostatic pressure leads to nucleoid condensation and to 
                     cellular blebbing; expression of the HhaII 
                     methyltransferase leads to formation of a more stable 
                     Mrr-nucleoid complex.  <i>mrr</i> mutants that respond 
                     differently to these two conditions have been isolated 
                     |CITS: [24423871]|.
                     Mutations that inactivate Mrr have been isolated, and a 
                     structural model has been proposed |CITS: [18951880]|. Mrr:
                     ""<b>m</b>ethylated adenine <b>r</b>ecognition and 
                     <b>r</b>estriction"" |CITS: [3036779]|
                     McrF: ""<b>m</b>odified <b>c</b>ytosine 
                     <b>r</b>estriction"" |CITS: [1830580]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16934473][23990325]|"
