     gene            complement(4578946..4581096)
                     /gene="btsT"
                     /locus_tag="b4354"
                     /label=btsT
     CDS             complement(4578946..4581096)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="btsT"
                     /locus_tag="b4354"
                     /product="pyruvate:H(+) symporter"
                     /label=btsT
                     /note="BtsT is a high-affinity, pyruvate:H<sup>+</sup>
                     symporter which mediates the uptake of pyruvate under 
                     nutrient limiting conditions. Extrachromosomal expression 
                     of <i>btsT</i> in a &Delta;<i>btsT</i> mutant and in a 
                     strain with decreased pyruvate metabolism increases the 
                     uptake rate of labeled pyruvate compared to the control; 
                     transport is highest at pH 7.5, decreases at pH 6 and is 
                     not detected at pH 4.5 and 3; transport is abolished upon 
                     addition of the protonophores 2,4-dinitro-phenol (DNP) or 
                     carbonyl cyanide <i>m</i>-chlorophenyl hydrazone (CCCP) 
                     |CITS: [29061664]|. Purified BtsT, reconstituted into 
                     proteoliposomes mediates the active accumulation of labeled
                     pyruvate; transport is dependent on both membrane potential
                     and a proton gradient (interior negative and alkaline) 
                     |CITS: [29061664]|.
                     BtsT (YjiY) is predicted to be an inner membrane protein 
                     with sixteen |CITS: [15919996]| or 18 |CITS: [29061664]| 
                     transmembrane domains; topology analysis suggests both 
                     termini are located in the cytoplasm |CITS: 
                     [15919996][29061664]|. BtsT belongs to the CstA family of 
                     putative peptide transporters |CITS: [24225317]|. BtsT 
                     shares high sequence similarity and identity with the 
                     peptide transporter and carbon starvation associated 
                     protein |FRAME: EG10167-MONOMER ""CstA""| |CITS: 
                     [22685278]|.
                     Expression of <i>btsT</i> is induced at the onset of 
                     stationary phase in media containing peptides or amino 
                     acids as the source of carbon |CITS: [22685278][24659770]| 
                     (see also |CITS: [29038258]|). Induction of <i>btsT</i> 
                     requires |FRAME: CPLX0-226 ""cAMP-CRP""|. Expression of 
                     <i>btsT</i> is controlled by the |FRAME: PWY0-1559|. A BtsR
                     binding site exists upsteam of the <i>btsT</i> 
                     transcription start site. Overproduction of BtsR results in
                     increased <i>btsT</i> promoter activity <i>in vivo</i> 
                     |CITS: [22685278]|. <i>btsT</i> is subject to |FRAME: 
                     CPLX0-7956 ""CsrA""| derived post-transcriptional 
                     regulation. <i>btsT</i> mRNA contains a CsrA binding site 
                     and deletion of <i>csrA</i> results in constitutive 
                     expression of a <i>btsT</i> translational fusion |CITS: 
                     [24659770]|.  <i>btsT</i>, among other genes involved in 
                     carbon source transport and metabolism, was downregulated 
                     in two MG1655 lysogens carrying closely related Stx2a 
                     phages O104 and PA8 |CITS: 31208335|.
                     Ribosomal protein L4 inhibits RNase E-dependent decay of 
                     the <i>btsT</i> transcript |CITS: [19144914]|.
                     BtsT interacts with the sensor kinases |FRAME: 
                     EG12007-MONOMER ""BtsS""| and |FRAME: G7243-MONOMER 
                     ""PyrS""| and with the membrane transporter |FRAME: 
                     YHJX-MONOMER ""YhjX""|; BtsT is capable of 
                     homo-oligomerisation |CITS: [24659770]|.
                     A &Delta;<i>btsT</i> mutant shows no growth defects in LB 
                     nor in M9 minimal medium supplemented with pyruvate |CITS: 
                     [29061664]|.
                     <i>btsT</i>: <u>b</u>enz<u>t</u>rauben<u>s</u>&auml;ure 
                     <u>T</u>ransporter |CITS: [29061664]|"
