     gene            4598463..4599140
                     /gene="yjjG"
                     /locus_tag="b4374"
                     /label=yjjG
     CDS             4598463..4599140
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="yjjG"
                     /locus_tag="b4374"
                     /product="trifunctional pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase YjjG / 
                     pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase / 5-fluorouridine 
                     5'-nucleotidase"
                     /EC_number="3.1.3.5"
                     /label=yjjG
                     /note="YjjG is a monophosphatase whose activity is
                     essential for thymine salvage in a <i>thyA</i> (|FRAME: 
                     THYMIDYLATESYN-CPLX|) mutant.  The dUMP phosphatase 
                     activity of YjjG generates deoxyuridine, which can be used 
                     by DeoA to liberate the deoxyribose-1-phosphate required 
                     for salvage of thymine |CITS: [17189366][21736641]|.
                     YjjG was first identified as a pyrimidine nucleotidase 
                     belonging to the superfamily of haloacid dehalogenase 
                     (HAD)-like hydrolases.  YjjG has phosphatase activity 
                     towards UMP, dUMP, and dTMP, and the activity is inhibited 
                     by nucleoside di- and triphosphates |CITS: 
                     [15489502][16990279]|.  The enzyme shows a low level of 
                     substrate discrimination among its preferred substrates 
                     |CITS: [16990279]|.  The phosphatase activity of YjjG was 
                     also shown in a high-throughput screen of purified proteins
                     |CITS: [15808744]|.
                     The general nucleotidase activity of YjjG allows it to 
                     protect cells against the potentially mutagenic 
                     noncanonical pyrimidine nucleotides, preventing their 
                     incorporation into DNA |CITS: [17286574]|.
                     YjjG is oligomeric in solution |CITS: [15489502]|.
                     The enzyme is likely identical to a partially purified 
                     5'-nucleotidase from <i>E. coli</i> B |CITS: [4571806]|.
                     A <i>yjjG thyA</i> double mutant is unable to use thymine 
                     for growth on glucose minimal medium |CITS: [17189366]|.
                     A <i>yjjG</i> mutant is sensitive to toxic noncanonical 
                     pyrimidine derivatives such as 5-fluorodeoxyuridine |CITS: 
                     [17286574]|.
                     "
