     gene            4599231..4600820
                     /gene="prfC"
                     /locus_tag="b4375"
                     /label=prfC
     CDS             4599231..4600820
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="prfC"
                     /locus_tag="b4375"
                     /product="peptide chain release factor RF3"
                     /label=prfC
                     /note="Release factor 3 (RF3), a class II release factor,
                     is a ribosome-dependent GTPase that stimulates the release 
                     of RF1 and RF2 from the ribosome after peptide chain 
                     termination. The action requires nucleotide exchange and 
                     hydrolysis of GTP.
                     |FRAME: EG10762-MONOMER RF2| of <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     contains threonine at position 246, which decreases its 
                     termination efficiency. Some physiological defects 
                     associated with the loss of RF3 discussed below appear to 
                     be due to the variant RF2 present in <i>E. coli</i> K-12 
                     |CITS: [25636454]|. The presence of RF3 alleviates the 
                     defects associated with the reduced-function RF2 allele 
                     |CITS: [28301469]|.
                     It was initially found that RF3 stimulates the release of 
                     the growing polypeptide chain at stop codons; unlike RF1 
                     and RF2, it is not codon-specific |CITS: 
                     [4909514][5289007]|. Rather, RF3 stimulates the activities 
                     of RF1 and RF2 |CITS: [6383864][8016068]|. It was reported 
                     that RF3 increases the affinity of RF2 to the UGA 
                     termination complex, but not that of RF1 to the UAG 
                     termination complex |CITS: [7737996]|. Later experiments 
                     showed that RF3-GTP catalyzes the removal of either RF1 or 
                     RF2 from the ribosome after termination |CITS: [9233821]|. 
                     RF3 promotes ribosome recycling |CITS: [9233822]| by 
                     accelerating the release of RF1 |CITS: [9344747]| or RF2 
                     |CITS: [9826500]|, thereby enabling |FRAME: EG10335-MONOMER
                     RRF| binding. RF3 in combination with RRF and EF-G 
                     increases dissociation of peptidyl-tRNA from the ribosome 
                     |CITS: [9451005][10452886]|. RF3 also plays a role in a 
                     post-peptidyltransfer quality control function of the 
                     ribosome |CITS: [19092806][22000017]|. RF3 is recruited to 
                     the ribosome by the presence of aberrant nascent 
                     polypeptide chains and mediates chain termination together 
                     with RF2 |CITS: [34107307]|.
                     The GTP-bound form of RF3 has a higher affinity for 
                     ribosomes than the GDP-bound form |CITS: [9436907]|. While 
                     |CITS: [11595190]| showed that free RF3 binds GDP much more
                     strongly than GTP, |CITS: [24214994][24667215]| later 
                     showed that RF3 binds GTP and GDP with similar affinity. 
                     Removal of the terminated polypeptide chain is the 
                     essential step regulating the sequence of steps during 
                     termination |CITS: [12419223]|.  Peptidyl-tRNA in the 
                     ribosomal P site inhibits binding of RF3-GTP |CITS: 
                     [12859902]|. The N-terminal domain of RF1 and RF2 is 
                     required for interaction with RF3 and stimulating 
                     nucleotide exchange |CITS: [14651631]|. Ribosomal protein 
                     |FRAME: EG10873-MONOMER L7/L12| is required for stable 
                     binding of RF3-GTP to the ribosome |CITS: [28342293]|.
                     The initial model for RF3 function postulated that RF3 
                     enters the ribosome in its GDP-bound form; the ribosomal 
                     complex containing RF1or RF2 and hydrolysis of 
                     peptidyl-tRNA by RF1 or RF2 stimulates nucleotide exchange 
                     in RF3, and RF3-GTP then stimulates the release of RF1 or 
                     RF2 from the ribosome. GTP hydrolysis subsequently allows 
                     release of RF3 itself from the ribosome |CITS: [11595190]|.
                     A refined model postulates that RF3-GTP stably binds to the
                     ribosome following peptide release; a conformational change
                     of the ribosome then promotes dissociation of class I 
                     release factors |CITS: [24667215]| after GTP hydrolysis by 
                     RF3 |CITS: [27779391]|. Cryo-electron microscopy studies of
                     RF3 trapped on the ribosome show two different states of 
                     RF3 binding and involving a large conformational 
                     rearrangement of the ribosome itself |CITS: [14985767]|. 
                     Crystal structures of GDP-bound RF3, a cryo-EM structure of
                     the post-termination ribosome with GDPNP-bound RF3 and 
                     analysis of mutant RF3 proteins elucidated the function of 
                     RF3 and its ability to induce conformational changes in the
                     ribosome |CITS: [17540173]|. Subsequently, crystal and 
                     cryo-EM structures of RF3 within the ribosome provided 
                     further detail on the function of RF3 |CITS: 
                     [22187675][23755360][30076302]|. The RF3-induced 
                     conformational changes of the ribosome were investigated in
                     detail using TIRF microscopy |CITS: [29889659]|. A more 
                     recent cryo-EM structure of apo-RF3 bound to the 70S 
                     ribosome termination complex with RF1 revealed a 'peptide 
                     flip' in the phosphate-binding loop of the ribosome causes 
                     the GDP to dissociate from RF3. Furthermore, GTP binding to
                     RF3 causes a domain rotation that results in RF1 
                     dissociating from the ribosome |CITS: [39030416]|.
                     A transposon insertion mutant in <i>prfC</i> leads to 
                     suppression of UGA stop codons; this phenotype allowed 
                     cloning of the <i>prfC</i> gene |CITS: [8016068]|. Another 
                     transposon insertion mutant suppresses a leaky <i>miaA</i> 
                     mutation |CITS: [1999389][8016077]|. Inactivation of 
                     <i>prfC</i> stimulates synthesis of RF2 |CITS: [7737996]|, 
                     while increased expression of wild-type |CITS: [8636994]| 
                     and expression of RF3<sup>DDI</sup> alleles of <i>prfC</i> 
                     |CITS: [20043913]| suppress the temperature-sensitive 
                     <i>prfB286</i> allele.  Several point mutations in 
                     <i>prfC</i> suppress the temperature-sensitive <i>prfA1</i>
                     allele |CITS: [8636994]|. &Delta;<i>prfC</i> strains show 
                     increased sensitivity to protein synthesis errors |CITS: 
                     [22000017]|, including by treatment with canavanine |CITS: 
                     [34107307]|.
                     Tos: ""<b>t</b>ransposon-induced <b>o</b>pal 
                     <b>s</b>uppressor"" |CITS: [8016068]|<br>PrfC: 
                     ""<b>p</b>rotein <b>r</b>elease <b>f</b>actor <b>C</b>"" 
                     Reviews: |CITS: [7969105][9150871][9179839][10937867][1251 
                     4123][18206363][18544041][22345149]|<br>Comment: |CITS: 
                     [19129838]|"
