     gene            4607140..4607919
                     /gene="deoC"
                     /locus_tag="b4381"
                     /label=deoC
     CDS             4607140..4607919
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="deoC"
                     /locus_tag="b4381"
                     /product="deoxyribose-phosphate aldolase"
                     /EC_number="4.1.2.4"
                     /label=deoC
                     /note="DeoC is a deoxyribose-phosphate aldolase which is
                     part of the pathway that utilizes pyrimidine 
                     deoxyribonucleoside as a carbon and energy source. 
                     Deoxyribose-phosphate aldolase catalyzes the aldol reaction
                     between the acetaldehyde donor and glyceraldehyde 
                     3-phosphate acceptor to generate deoxyribose 5-phosphate. 
                     It belongs to the class I aldolases which form Schiff base 
                     intermediates.  |CITS: [6749498]|
                     The purified enzyme may exist as both a monomer and a 
                     dimer. The enzyme exists as a monomer in Tris/HCl 
                     containing EDTA and as a dimer in phosphate buffer.
                     |CITS: [6749498]|
                     The crystal structure of a covalent complex of deoxyribose 
                     phosphate aldolase with deoxyribose 5-phosphate was 
                     resolved to 1.05 &Aring;. Site directed mutagenesis of the 
                     Schiff base Lys<sup>167</sup> and Lys<sup>201</sup> were 
                     found to be critical to catalysis.  |CITS: [11598300]| 
                     Mutations in <i>deoC</i> suppressed thymine requirement for
                     growth in thymidylate synthase (<i>thyA</i>) mutants. The 
                     growth of <i>deoC</i> mutants were inhibited by 
                     deoxyribonucleosides, thymidine, or deoxyadenosines. The 
                     mutants were sensitive to deoxyribonucleosides as a result 
                     of deoxyribose 5-phosphate accumulation.  |CITS: 
                     [4873559][4923156]|.
                     The <i>deo</i> designation is used to indicate a gene 
                     determining an enzyme necessary for growth on 
                     deoxythymidine or deoxyuridine as the sole carbon source. 
                     |CITS: [5340684]|
                     The <i>deo</i> operon consists of four enzymes: 
                     deoxyriboaldolase, thymidine phosphorylase, 
                     phosphodeoxyribomutase and purine nucleoside phosphorylase 
                     |CITS: [4904508 ]|. This operon has an unusual and complex 
                     pattern of regulation |CITS: [171553][231107]| including 
                     two regulatory proteins encoded by the genes <i>cytR</i> 
                     and <i>deoR</i> |CITS: [4559263 ]|.
                     "
