     gene            4608046..4609368
                     /gene="deoA"
                     /locus_tag="b4382"
                     /label=deoA
     CDS             4608046..4609368
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="deoA"
                     /locus_tag="b4382"
                     /product="bifunctional thymidine phosphorylase / uracil 
                     phosphorylase"
                     /EC_number="2.4.2.4"
                     /label=deoA
                     /note="DeoA is a thymidine phosphorylase which is part of
                     the pathway that utilizes pyrimidine deoxyribonucleoside as
                     a carbon and energy source. Thymidine phosphorylase 
                     catalyzes the phosphorolysis of thymidine and other 
                     pyrimidine 2-deoxyribosides. This enzyme is highly specific
                     for deoxyribose 1-phosphate |CITS: [14213352]|. The crystal
                     structure of DeoA has been determined at 2.8 and 2.6 
                     &Aring; resolution |CITS: [2199449][3546318]|. The enzyme 
                     is an S-shaped dimer with identical subunits that are 
                     related by a crystallographic 2-fold axis |CITS: 
                     [2199449]|.
                     Mutants lacking <i>deoA</i> readily incorporates exogenous 
                     thymidine into deoxyribonucleic acid, but not ribonucleic 
                     acid or other macromolecules |CITS: [4905532]|. Thymidine 
                     phosphorylase mutants fail to grow on deoxycytodine, 
                     deoxyuridine and thymidine |CITS: [ColiSalII]|. More 
                     recently, a &Delta;<i>deoA</i> mutant was found to be 
                     required for <u>t</u>hymine-starvation <u>i</u>nduced 
                     <u>c</u>hromosome <u>f</u>ragmentation (TiCF) and may 
                     encode a critical anti-fragmentation factor. Conversely, 
                     fast removal of thymidine by DeoA can ""snap-starve"" 
                     replication forks for  dTTP resulting in replication forks 
                     freezing in place and not undergoing disintegration, 
                     possibly blocking TiCF. However, DeoA did not affect 
                     thymineless death (see |FRAME: EG11002|) |CITS: 
                     [35324030]|.
                     The <i>deo</i> designation is used to indicate a gene 
                     determining an enzyme necessary for growth on 
                     deoxythymidine or deoxyuridine as the sole carbon source. 
                     |CITS: [5340684]|
                     The <i>deo</i> operon consists of four enzymes: 
                     deoxyriboaldolase, thymidine phosphorylase, 
                     phosphodeoxyribomutase and purine nucleoside phosphorylase 
                     |CITS: [4904508]|. This operon has an unusual and complex 
                     pattern of regulation |CITS: [171553][231107]| including 
                     two regulatory proteins encoded by the genes <i>cytR</i> 
                     and <i>deoR</i> |CITS: [4559263]|.
                     Research focused on optimizing production of modified 
                     nucleosides has been carried out on PpnP |CITS: 
                     [35269937]|."
