     gene            4609420..4610643
                     /gene="deoB"
                     /locus_tag="b4383"
                     /label=deoB
     CDS             4609420..4610643
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="deoB"
                     /locus_tag="b4383"
                     /product="trifunctional phosphopentomutase / deoxyribose 
                     1,5-phosphomutase / alpha-D-ribose 1,5-phosphomutase"
                     /EC_number="5.4.2.7"
                     /label=deoB
                     /note="Phosphopentomutase is a catabolic enzyme which
                     catalyzes the transfer of a phosphate group between the C1 
                     and the C5 carbon atoms of ribose and deoxyribose, 
                     respectively |CITS: [4992818][1089430]|.
                     A mutation in <i>deoB</i> suppresses the high thymine 
                     requirement for growth of <i>thy</i> mutants |CITS: 
                     [4873559][4877128]| and improves the survival of 
                     <i>thyA</i> mutants in stationary phase |CITS: [14612251]|.
                      Transposon insertion mutations in <i>deoB</i> suppress the
                     growth defect of a <i>tktA tktB</i> mutant |CITS: 
                     [26691989]|.  Deletion of <i>deoB</i> increases glycerol 
                     consumption as well as hydrogen and ethanol production 
                     compared to wild type |CITS: [26122736]|, and increases 
                     lycopene production in an engineered strain |CITS: 
                     [24658737]|.
                     The <i>deo</i> operon has a complex pattern of regulation 
                     |CITS: [171553][231107][6087276]|.  Expression of 
                     <i>deoB</i> is downregulated by nitrogen starvation |CITS: 
                     [15289551]|.
                     The <i>E. coli</i> phosphopentomutase appears to be 
                     biochemically and structurally distinct from mammalian 
                     phosphopentomutase |CITS: [17804405]|, making it a 
                     potential target for antibiotic development.
                     DeoB: ""necessary for growth on <b>deo</b>xythymidine or 
                     deoxyuridine as the sole source of carbon"" |CITS: 
                     [5340684]|"
