     gene            4611586..4612917
                     /gene="hipH"
                     /locus_tag="b4385"
                     /label=hipH
     CDS             4611586..4612917
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="hipH"
                     /locus_tag="b4385"
                     /product="serine/threonine-protein kinase toxin HipH"
                     /label=hipH
                     /note="HipH is a serine/threonine kinase similar to the
                     toxin |FRAME: EG10443-MONOMER HipA| |CITS: [34061596]| and 
                     is toxic upon overexpression, which leads to formation of 
                     elongated cells and a reduced number of colony-forming 
                     units after plating |CITS: [28430938]|. Consequences of 
                     HipH induction include arrest of cell division and DNA 
                     segregation, increased glycogen synthesis, and altered 
                     ribosome assembly.  Unlike HipA, HipH has no direct impact 
                     on antibiotic tolerance |CITS: [36537800]|.
                     The primary protein substrates of HipH are the |FRAME: 
                     EG10889-MONOMER| (RpmE), which is phosphorylated at Ser69, 
                     and the |FRAME: CPLX0-7956| (CsrA), which is phosphorylated
                      at Ser56. HipH also has autophosphorylase activity 
                     (phosphorylating Ser200, Ser201 and Ser217). 
                     Phosphorylation of both RomE and CsrA negatively affects 
                     their function, and in the case of RpmE, impacts ribosome 
                     assembly |CITS: [36537800]|.
                     Overexpression of HipH activates HipA and other kinases, 
                     suggesting that there is a cross talk between Ser/Thr 
                     kinases in |FRAME: TAX-562| |CITS: [36537800]|.
                     No cognate antitoxin is encoded near hipH; however, 
                     overexpression of HipA's cognate antitoxin, |FRAME: 
                     EG10442-MONOMER HipB|, can counteract the toxic effect of 
                     overexpressed YjjJ |CITS: [28430938]|.
                     Deletion or silencing of the hipH gene did not have any 
                     significant effect on the cell growth or proteome dynamics 
                     during exponential growth. It was suggested that the enzyme
                     is involved in regulatory mechanisms during stationary 
                     phase |CITS: [36537800]|."
