     gene            complement(4612918..4613934)
                     /gene="lplA"
                     /locus_tag="b4386"
                     /label=lplA
     CDS             complement(4612918..4613934)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="lplA"
                     /locus_tag="b4386"
                     /product="multifunctional lipoate--protein ligase A /
                     lipoate-ATP adenylate transferase / 
                     lipoyl-adenylate--protein ligase / lipoate-AceF ligase / 
                     lipoate-SucB ligase / octanoate-non-lipoylated apo domain 
                     ligase"
                     /EC_number="6.3.1.20"
                     /label=lplA
                     /note="LplA is a lipoyl-protein ligase |CITS: [8206909]|. 
                     Lipoate modification of the E2 subunits is important for 
                     the function of |FRAME: PYRUVATEDEH-CPLX| |CITS: 
                     [814874][6794598][8444795]|, |FRAME: 2OXOGLUTARATEDEH-CPLX 
                     ""&alpha;-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase""| |CITS: 
                     [814874][6794598][8444795]|, and the |FRAME: GCVMULTI-CPLX|
                     |CITS: [1655709][8444795]|.
                     It was initially thought that LplA only utilizes lipoate 
                     imported from outside the cell, in contrast to the other 
                     lipoyl-protein ligase, LipB, which utilizes octanoyl-ACP. 
                     However, <i>E. coli lipB</i> mutants are able to grow with 
                     externally supplied octanoate instead of lipoate. Octanoate
                     growth requires both LplA and |FRAME: CPLX0-782|, the 
                     enzyme that converts octanoyl side chains to lipoyl side 
                     chains, suggesting that LplA attaches octanoate to the E2 
                     domain, and |FRAME: CPLX0-782| converts the octanoate to 
                     lipoate |CITS: [14700636]|.
                     The two-part reaction requires ATP and Mg<sup>2+</sup> 
                     |CITS: [8206909][7639702]| and proceeds through a 
                     lipoyl-AMP intermediate |CITS: [20089862]|. The conserved 
                     Lys133 residue is required for both the lipoate adenylation
                     and the lipoate transfer reaction |CITS: [20089862]|. LplA 
                     can also utilize lipoyl-ACP or octanoyl-ACP, albeit very 
                     inefficiently |CITS: [12591875]|. Determinants of substrate
                     recognition |CITS: [10329614]| and catalysis are discussed,
                     including structural similarity between lipoylating and 
                     biotinylating enzymes and implications with respect to the 
                     catalytic site |CITS: [11106165]|.
                     LplA is a monomeric enzyme that is present at low 
                     abundance, with fewer than 10 monomers in each cell |CITS: 
                     [7639702]|. Crystal structures of LplA alone, in complex 
                     with lipoate, the post-lipoate adenylation intermediate 
                     state and the pre-lipoate transfer intermediate state have 
                     been solved |CITS: [16043486][20089862]|. Lipoate 
                     adenylation is followed by large conformational changes to 
                     accommodate the apoprotein substrate. A reaction mechanism 
                     has been proposed |CITS: [20089862]|. Limited trypsin 
                     proteolysis of LplA leads to cleavage of a central loop 
                     region; cleavage eliminates catalytic activity |CITS: 
                     [16384580]|.
                     An <i>lplA</i> null mutant has a defect in utilization of 
                     externally supplied lipoate for modification of proteins 
                     and is resistant to selenolipoate |CITS: [8206909]|. The 
                     <i>slr1</i> (<i>lplA1</i>) selenolipoate-resistant mutant 
                     shows a G76S change in the LplA protein, which causes 
                     increased specificity for lipoate over the selenolipoate 
                     analog |CITS: [8002607]|. Genetic interactions have been 
                     observed between LplA and the LipA to LipB |FRAME: 
                     PWY0-501|: phenotypes of a <i>lipB</i> mutant are 
                     suppressed by overproduction of LplA, and <i>lplA lipA</i> 
                     or <i>lplA lipB</i> double mutants exhibit growth defects, 
                     whereas an <i>lplA</i> single mutant does not |CITS: 
                     [8002607]|. A knockout mutant of <i>lplA</i> does not 
                     impact lipoylation by LipA or LipB at the target 
                     lipoylation sites on the target proteins for each |CITS: 
                     [35648456]|. Missense mutations in <i>lplA</i> that reduce 
                     the enzyme's K<sub>m</sub> for octanoate can scavenge 
                     cytosolic octanoate and thereby suppress the auxotrophic 
                     phenotype of a <i>lipB</i> mutant |CITS: [19684135]|. 
                     Engineered LplA derivatives are utilized for fluorescent 
                     labeling of proteins <i>in vivo</i>; e.g. |CITS: 
                     [20534555][25313043]|.
                     Slr: <i>s</i>eleno<i>l</i>ipoic acid-<i>r</i>esistant 
                     |CITS: [8170976]|
                     LplA: <i>l</i>ipoate-<i>p</i>rotein <i>l</i>igase <i>A</i> 
                     |CITS: [8206909]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [16221579][19734307][27074917][38624243]|"
