     gene            4615729..4617111
                     /gene="radA"
                     /locus_tag="b4389"
                     /label=radA
     CDS             4615729..4617111
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="radA"
                     /locus_tag="b4389"
                     /product="DNA recombination protein"
                     /label=radA
                     /note="RadA is involved in the processing of DNA
                     recombination intermediates |CITS: [12446634]|.
                     Purified RadA binds ssDNA [poly(dT)<sub>30</sub>] in the 
                     presence of ADP, exhibits DNA-stimulated ATPase activity 
                     and enhances the branch migration phase of RecA-mediated 
                     3-strand exchange reactions in a forward direction (towards
                     the 3' end of the donor ssDNA) <i>in vitro</i>; RadA 
                     stimulates branch migration of both 3-strand and Holliday 
                     junctions |CITS: [26845522]|.
                     A <i>radA</i> null mutant exhibits a modest defect in 
                     conjugational DNA recombination |CITS: [12446634]|. 
                     <i>radA</i> mutants exhibit greater sensitivity to 
                     methylmethane sulfonate |CITS: [1327967], [8759876], 
                     [12446634]|, phleomycin |CITS: [12446634]|, ciprofloxacin 
                     |CITS: [25484163]| and to UV radiation than wild type 
                     |CITS: [8759876]|.  Mutations in <i>radA</i> are 
                     synergistic with mutations in <i>recG</i> and <i>ruvABC</i>
                     for recombination and tolerance to DNA damage which 
                     suggests RadA may function in the processing of DNA 
                     recombination intermediates |CITS: [12446634] [25484163]|. 
                     Mutations in <i>radA</i> are synergistic with mutations in 
                     DNA helicases (eg.<i> lhr, dinG, priA, rep, uvrD, radD, 
                     yoaA</i>) for sensitivity to the DNA damaging agents 
                     ciprofloxacin and azidothymidine |CITS: [25484163]|.
                     RadA has a zinc finger motif (CX2CXnCX2C) located in the 
                     N-terminal region of the protein and an ATP/GTP-binding 
                     motif. RadA shares regions of sequence similarity with Lon 
                     protease and RecA |CITS: [1327967]|.
                     <i>radA</i> and <i>serB</i> are cotranscribed |CITS: 
                     [1327967]|.
                     <i>sms</i>: <U>s</U>ensitivity to <U>m</U>ethylmethane 
                     <U>s</U>ulfonate<BR><i>rad</i>: <U>rad</U>iation 
                     sensitivity
                     "
