     gene            complement(4618672..4620339)
                     /gene="ettA"
                     /locus_tag="b4391"
                     /label=ettA
     CDS             complement(4618672..4620339)
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="ettA"
                     /locus_tag="b4391"
                     /product="energy-dependent translational throttle protein
                     EttA"
                     /label=ettA
                     /note="EttA is a translation factor that gates ribosome
                     entry into the translation elongation cycle through a 
                     nucleotide-dependent interaction that is sensitive to the 
                     ATP/ADP ratio. EttA is a member of the ATP-binding cassette
                     F (ABC-F) protein family |CITS: [24389466]|, of which there
                     are four in <i>E. coli</i> K-12: YheS, |FRAME: YJJK-MONOMER
                     ""EttA""|, |FRAME: UUP-MONOMER ""Uup""| and |FRAME: 
                     G6423-MONOMER ""YbiT""|. All of these ABC-F proteins are 
                     involved in noncanonical translation of 'hard-to-translate'
                     nascent peptides getting them through the ribosome exit 
                     tunnel. EttA represses poly-acidic sequence-dependent 
                     intrinsic ribosome destabilization, stalling and premature 
                     termination |CITS: [38661232]|. Higher amounts of ATP 
                     relieve ADP-dependent inhibition of protein synthesis by 
                     EttA, suggesting that the elevated ADP/ATP ratio found in 
                     energy-depleted cells leads to stabilization of 70S 
                     translation initiation complexes in a hibernating 
                     conformation |CITS: [24389466]|. Mechanistically, EttA 
                     modulates the movements of the ribosome and tRNAs that are 
                     required for polypeptide elongation. A model for regulation
                     of translation by EttA has been proposed |CITS: 
                     [24389465]|.
                     Members of the ABC-F protein family have two ABC domains, 
                     each containing an insertion in the loop after the first of
                     the three &alpha; helices in the ABC&alpha; subdomain, and 
                     separated by an 81 residue linker domain |CITS: 
                     [24389466]|. A cryo-EM structure of EttA bound to the 
                     ribosome showed that the linker domain is a P-site 
                     tRNA-interaction domain |CITS: [24389465]|. A crystal 
                     structure of EttA has been solved at 2.4 &Aring; 
                     resolution, showing a domain-swapped dimer. Soluble EttA 
                     exists in a slowly reversible monomer-dimer equilibrium 
                     that favors the monomeric form at concentrations found 
                     <i>in vivo</i> |CITS: [24389466]|.
                     The ATPase activity of wild type EttA is stimulated by the 
                     presence of ribosomes. The EttA-EQ<sub>2</sub> mutant, 
                     which is expected to prevent ATP hydrolysis and trap EttA 
                     in its ATP-bound conformation, has a dominant negative 
                     effect on growth by inhibiting protein synthesis after 
                     formation of the first peptide bond |CITS: [24389466]|. 
                     ATP-bound EttA-EQ<sub>2</sub> binds to the ribosomal E site
                     and kinetically traps the ribosomal PRE complex in the MS-I
                     state |CITS: [24389465]|. An <i>ettA</i> deletion strain 
                     shows decreased survival of long-term stationary phase 
                     |CITS: [24389466]|.
                     Using a reconstituted in vitro translation system and <i>in
                     vivo</i> mutant analyses indicate the C-terminal extension,
                     interdomain linkage and ATP activity of EttA are essential 
                     to it's ribosome release activity. But unlike YbiT, EttA 
                     alleviates destabilization of the ribosome <i>in vivo</i> 
                     when the poly-acidic sequence are in the N-terminal of the 
                     nascent protein |CITS: [38661232]|.
                     EttA: <b>e</b>nergy-dependent <b>t</b>ranslational 
                     <b>t</b>hrottle <b>A</b> |CITS: [24389466]|
                     Reviews: |CITS: [30476446][31563533][33135152]|
                     Comment: |CITS: [24500425]|"
