     gene            4620550..4622487
                     /gene="slt"
                     /locus_tag="b4392"
                     /label=slt
     CDS             4620550..4622487
                     /codon_start=1
                     /gene="slt"
                     /locus_tag="b4392"
                     /product="trifunctional soluble lytic murein
                     transglycosylase / peptidoglycan lytic endotransglycosylase
                     / peptidoglycan lytic exotransglycosylase"
                     /label=slt
                     /note="<i>slt</i> encodes a soluble lytic murein 
                     transglycosylase (known as Slt70) which cleaves the 
                     &beta;-1,4 glycosidic bond between 
                     <I>N</I>-acetylglucosamine (GlcNAc) and 
                     <I>N</I>-acetylmuramic acid (MurNAc) residues of 
                     peptidoglycan (PG) with the concomitant formation of a 
                     1,6-anhydro ring at the MurNAc residue. Slt70 may function 
                     as a PG quality control enzyme - the primary physiological 
                     function of Slt70 may be the degradation of uncrosslinked 
                     glycan strands thus preventing their aberrant incorporation
                     into the PG matrix |CITS: [25480295]|. Purified Slt70 
                     degrades murein into non-reducing low-molecular weight 
                     fragments and converts the glycosidic bond between GlcNAc 
                     and MurNAc into an internal 1,6-anhydroMurNAc bond; the 
                     enzyme has a K<sub>m</sub> of 200mg murein sacculi/L and 
                     activity is optimal at pH4.5 |CITS:[357]|. Slt70 is present
                     in the soluble fraction after mechanical breaking of the 
                     cells |CITS: [357]|; Slt70 localises to the cell envelope 
                     where it binds to the outer surface of the murein sacculus 
                     |CITS: [1885544]|. Slt70 functions as an 
                     exotransglycosylase <i>in vitro</i> |CITS: [7018908]|. 
                     Purified Slt70 (residues 19-645) degrades the purified cell
                     wall sacculus to produce the following major products (in 
                     order of abundance): tetra-A1 (GlcNAc-1,6-anhydro-MurNAc 
                     monomer with tetrapeptide stem); tetra2-A1 
                     (GlcNAc-1,6-anhydro-MurNAc monomer with cross-linked 
                     tetrapeptide stems); tri-A1 (GlcNAc-1,6-anhydro-MurNAc 
                     monomer with tripeptide stem) plus other variations in 
                     smaller amounts. The reaction generates cross-linked and 
                     non-crosslinked products (the former in relatively small 
                     amounts); it also generates products that are indicative of
                     an endolytic transglycosylase reaction and some products 
                     containing a reducing end (ie containing a terminal MurNAc 
                     residue rather than a 1,6-anhydro-MurNAc residue) |CITS: 
                     [23421439]|
                     Purified Slt70 binds |FRAME: EG10341-MONOMER ""PBP3""| and 
                     |FRAME: EG12015-MONOMER ""PBP7/8""| <i>in vitro</i>; PBP7/8
                     stimulates the lytic transglycosylase activity of Slt70 
                     <i>in vitro</i> |CITS:[8063800]|.
                     Slt70 activity is modulated by the stringent response; 
                     specific inhibition of Slt70 activity by ppGpp is not 
                     observed |CITS:[1970319]|.
                     The structure of Slt70 in it's native form and as a complex
                     with 1,6-anhydromuropeptide has been determined by X-ray 
                     crystallography |CITS: 
                     [2184239],[8107871][7479697][10452894]|; an N-terminal 
                     &alpha;-superhelical U domain plus a linker domain form a 
                     closed ring structure with a central hole which is capped 
                     by a C-terminal globular structure that contains the active
                     site. The structure has also been determined as a complex 
                     with bulgecin A |CITS:[7548026]|, its inhibitor 
                     |CITS:[1400320]|.
                     Overproduction of Slt70 (>30 fold) results in growth 
                     inhibition and lysis of some cells |CITS:[1938883]|. 
                     <i>slt</i> deletion mutants have significantly reduced 
                     murein hydrolytic activity in the soluble fraction of the 
                     cell; deletion of <i>slt</i> does not affect growth rate or
                     cell morphology but alters the antibiotic sensitivity of 
                     the cell |CITS: [1400320]|. Mecillinam treatment of 
                     &Delta;<i>slt</i> cells leads to a severely altered 
                     morphology due to the aberrant incorporation of 
                     peptidoglycan into the PG matrix; Slt70 is responsible for 
                     destroying the un-crosslinked glycan strands produced by 
                     &beta;-lactam targeted PG synthetic enzymes |CITS: 
                     [25480295]|. Deletion of <i>slt</i> increases sensitivity 
                     to &beta;-lactams (ampicillin, oxacillin, ceftazidime, and 
                     meropenem) and increases resistance to vancomycin (the 
                     so-called ""seesaw effect"") |CITS: [40418177]|. 
                     Constitutive activation of the Cpx two component pathway 
                     results in upregulation of <i>slt</i> (although no 
                     corresponding changes in peptidoglycan length could be 
                     observed); <i>slt</i> is a member of the CpxR regulon 
                     |CITS: [25422305]|
                     <I>E. coli</I> K-12 contains other lytic murein 
                     transglycosylases including |FRAME: G7457-MONOMER 
                     ""MltA""|, |FRAME: G7410-MONOMER ""MltB""|, |FRAME: 
                     G7533-MONOMER ""MltC""|, |FRAME: EG10246-MONOMER ""MltD""|,
                     |FRAME: G6622-MONOMER ""EmtA""| and |FRAME: EG11373-MONOMER
                     ""MltF""|. |FRAME: EG11494 ""MltG""|, an inner membrane 
                     protein and potential PG terminase, also has lytic murein 
                     transglycosylase activity.
                     Reviews: |CITS: [8765311][7712284]|<BR>Comment: |CITS: 
                     [25534806]|"
